SGO_COMMENTARY.push({"id":"jerome-more-ezk-rev","author":"St Jerome","work":"Commentary on Matthew; Commentary on Isaiah; Commentary on Ezekiel; Homilies on Psalms; and other works","translator":"New English translations from the Historical Christian Faith Writings Database, made from Migne's Greek and Latin text or the 19th-century Russian and dedicated to the public domain, many of them machine-assisted; plus public-domain ANF/NPNF texts","lang":"en","license":"Public Domain","source":"https://github.com/HistoricalChristianFaith/Commentaries-Database (Jerome/)","versification":"kjv","tradition":"west","notes":{"EZK.1.1":["The Prophet Ezekiel was led captive with Joachin king of Judah to Babylon, and he prophesied there to those who were captives with him, to those repenting that they had willingly handed over the prophecy of Jeremiah to enemies, and yet saw the city Jerusalem to stand, which he had predicted would fall. And in his thirtieth year of age, and in the fifth year of the captivity, he began to speak to his fellow captives. And at the same time, though later, this one in Chaldea and Jeremiah in Judea prophesied. His style is neither greatly eloquent nor excessively rustic, but properly proportioned between both. And he was a priest, as also was Jeremiah, the beginning and ending of the book being wrapped in great obscurities. But also the common edition of him does not differ much from the Hebrew one. Because of that I greatly wonder what was the cause, that when we have the same translators in all the books, in some they translated the same things, in others, different things. Therefore, read this also according to our translation because, by being written in words with spaces, it gives a clearer meaning to readers. And if my friends also mock this, say to them that no one restrains them from writing. But I do not respect him who follows them, which is more clearly said in Greek, as they are called insult-swallowers (φαγολοιδοροι).\n— Prologue to Ezekiel","Having finished the eighteen volumes of explanations on Isaiah, I was eager to pass on to Ezekiel, which I had often promised to you and to your mother Paula of holy memory, O Eustochium, virgin of Christ, and to put the final hand, as the saying goes, to the prophetic work; and behold, suddenly the death of Pammachius and Marcella, the siege of the city of Rome, and the falling asleep of many brothers and sisters were announced to me. And I was so dismayed and stunned that day and night I thought of nothing else but the safety of all, and considered myself a captive in the captivity of the saints, nor could I open my mouth until I learned something more certain, while I hang anxious between hope and despair and torment myself with the misfortunes of others. But after the brightest light of all the earth was extinguished, nay rather, after the head of the Roman empire was cut off and, to speak more truly, the whole world perished in one city, I became mute and was humbled and kept silent from good things, and my sorrow was renewed; my heart grew hot within me, and in my meditation a fire will blaze up; nor did I think that saying should be neglected: Music in mourning is an untimely tale. But since you too demand this unceasingly, and a scar is gradually drawn over the great wound, and the Scorpion is pressed down beneath the soil of Trinacria between Enceladus and Porphyrion, and the Hydra of many heads has at last ceased to hiss against us, and a time has been given in which we ought to devote ourselves not to answering the snares of heretics but to the exposition of the Scriptures, I shall take up the prophet Ezekiel, whose difficulty the tradition of the Hebrews proves. For among them, unless someone has completed the age of priestly ministry, that is, the thirtieth year, he is permitted to read neither the beginnings of Genesis, nor the Song of Songs, nor the beginning and end of this volume, so that the full time of human nature may come to perfect knowledge and to mystical understandings. If by the mercy of the Lord I bring this work through to the finish, I shall pass on to Jeremiah, who in his Lamentations, under the type of Jerusalem, laments the four quarters of the world with a fourfold alphabet.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","And it came to pass in the thirtieth year, in the fourth, on the fifth of the month. The thirtieth year is spoken of not, as most suppose, with reference to the age of the prophet, nor to the jubilee, which is the year of remission, but counting from the eighteenth year of Josiah king of Judah, when the book of Deuteronomy was found in the temple of God, up to the fifth year of the captivity of Jehoiachin, surnamed Jeconiah, who was led into Babylon with his mother and with Daniel and the three youths and Ezekiel — this is the first captivity of the tribe of Judah, when the wrath of God dripped down upon Jerusalem —. According to the ἀναγωγὴν [anagogy, the higher sense], however, the Lord and Savior is prefigured, who at thirty years of age came to baptism, which is the perfect age in a man. Hence also in the book of Numbers, according to the Hebrews, the priests begin to minister in the tabernacle not from the twenty-fifth year of age, as is contained in the Seventy, but from the thirtieth. As a sign of this Joseph too went before, when in Egypt he bestowed grain on the hungry people, and John the Baptist came to the streams of the Jordan and preached the baptism of repentance. In what is written, 'in the fourth', with the sentence left hanging, 'month' is understood; for at once there follows 'on the fifth of the month'. To make this plainer, the Seventy added it above.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","When I was in the midst of the captives beside the river Chebar, the heavens were opened and I saw visions of God. On the fifth of the month, that is the fifth year of the exile of King Jehoiachin. That the captive people sat by the river of Babylon, David prophesies in the spirit: By the rivers of Babylon, there we sat and wept; Chebar, moreover, is either the name of a river or certainly, according to its interpretation, by which it is rendered 'heavy', it signifies the Tigris and the Euphrates and all the great and very heavy rivers which are said to be in the land of the Chaldeans; and understand the opened heavens not by a division of the firmament but by the faith of the believer, because the heavenly mysteries were unlocked to him. Hence also at the baptism of the Savior, when the Holy Spirit descended upon him in the form of a dove, we read 'the heavens opened'; and when these are unlocked, visions are laid open, not one vision but many, as the Lord says through the prophet: I will multiply visions, and in the hands of the prophets I have been likened. These are the visions which the whole prophecy of Ezekiel has woven together.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","This too is to be understood: that the Lord came to baptism in the thirtieth year of his age; in the fourth month, which among us is called January and stands first at the beginning of the year, apart from Nisan, the month of new things, in which the Passover is celebrated — for among the eastern peoples, after the gathering of the crops and the winepresses, when the tithes were brought into the temple, October was the first month and January the fourth —; and he adds the fifth day of the month, and it signifies the baptism in which the heavens were opened to Christ, and the day of the Epiphany is venerable to this day — not, as some think, his birth in the flesh, for then he was hidden and did not appear. This fits that time when it was said: This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.1.2":["And more meaningfully, according to the Hebrews and the other translators, Jehoiachin's is called an 'exile', and not a 'captivity', as the Seventy translated; for he was not captured when the city was overcome, but, surrendering himself of his own will, was led to Babylon: therefore let that of Jehoiachin, that is, of Jeconiah, be called the first 'exile', and that of Zedekiah the second or final 'captivity'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.1.3":["The word of the Lord came to Ezekiel the son of Buzi, the priest, in the land of the Chaldeans beside the river Chebar. Both to Daniel and to Ezekiel, who were in Babylon beside the rivers, the mysteries of things to come are laid open above the waters, nay rather in the purest waters, so that the power of baptism might be shown. Besides, the apostle Paul too, when he was washed in the Lord by Ananias, scales having first covered his eyes, was freed of blindness. And in Genesis the first things that lived came forth from the waters.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","Further, 'Buzi' is rendered in our language 'spurned' and 'despised', 'Hiezechiel' [Ezekiel] 'strengthened by God'. This will be fitted to the Lord thus, that we may say that the Creator of the world, because he is the Father of the Savior, is spurned and despised by all the heretics who do not receive the Old Testament. Nor is it surprising that the Lord is the strong one of God, since he himself is the power of God and the wisdom of God.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","And the hand of the Lord came upon him there. (LXX: \"And the hand of the Lord came upon me.\") In order that we may be able to discern and understand the visions of God, we need the hand and strength of God upon us. By this hand and arm the people of Israel was led out of Egypt; its mighty works even the magicians partly understood, saying: This is the finger of God; and the Savior in the Gospel: If I cast out demons by the finger of God, in place of which it is written in another Gospel: If I cast out demons by the Spirit of God.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.1.4-28":["Now the whole vision is this: the spirit lifting up, and the great cloud, and the four living creatures, and as many wheels following the living creatures and the spirit, which were deemed worthy to be under the firmament of God; and these, after they had been lifted up and had heard a voice as of many waters and as of the word of the sublime God, and the sound of a camp and of an army, let down their wings and showed their amazement by silence; and there appeared, seated upon the likeness of sapphire, as it were the likeness of a man, who from the loins upward had the appearance of electrum and from the loins downward the appearance of fire round about; after which a surer sign of mercy is given, like the look of the bow when it is in the cloud on the day of rain. We have spoken at greater length about this vision because it is both obscure and expounded in various ways by many. In what remains, so far as can be done without loss to the sense, we shall strive for brevity because of the size of the volume.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.1.4":["And I saw, and behold, a wind of whirlwind was coming from the north, and a great cloud, and a fire enfolding, and brightness round about it. (LXX: \"And I saw, and behold, a spirit carrying away — or lifting up — was coming from the north, and a great cloud in it, and fire flashing, and brightness round about it.\") For the consolation of the exiled people and the revelation of the sentence of God, the prophet saw a very great vision. In its interpretation all the synagogues of the Jews are mute, saying that it is beyond man to wish to attempt anything either concerning this or concerning the building of the temple, which is written at the end of this prophecy. We, however, entrust to kindly and faithful readers what we have received from our elders and what, according to the small measure of our poor talent, comparing spiritual things with spiritual, we can surmise rather than explain, begging pardon, that they may look with favor on our rashness — nay rather, on a faith that does not know its own measure — rather than be angry. And first it must be known that the 'spirit lifting up or carrying away', which we, following Aquila, have translated 'wind of whirlwind', and, following Symmachus and Theodotion, 'blast and spirit of tempest', is taken by some in a good sense, by others in the contrary sense. For the Hebrew word 'ruha', according to the character of the passages, is taken either as 'spirit' or as 'soul' or as 'wind'. Spirit, as there: You will send forth your spirit and they will be created; soul: His spirit will go forth, and he will return to his earth; wind: With a violent spirit you will shatter the ships of Tarshish, and elsewhere: Fire, sulphur and the spirit of storms, the portion of their cup. Those who read 'wind' and 'blast of tempest' understand this: that the wrath and fury of God comes from the north, that is, Nebuchadnezzar, and that Jerusalem was to be taken six years after this vision. For the vision is seen in the fifth year of the exile of King Jehoiachin, which was also the fifth of Zedekiah, reigning in Jerusalem, who, we read, likewise after six years, that is, in the eleventh year of his rule, when the city was taken, was led into Babylon. To those, therefore, who dwelt beside the river Chebar and had surrendered themselves to the king of their own accord, it is revealed that they did well to obey the sentence of God: for in a short time both the province of Judea and the city of Jerusalem would be taken. And as for a great cloud being described, we understand that it carries showers and rains over Judea; and the enfolded fire shows the punishments to come and the evils of captivity; and the brightness round about it signifies the open judgments of God. But those who take it in the contrary sense, that is, the good one, understand the spirit carrying away or lifting up as the Holy Spirit, who carries away vices and sins from men, or lifts up those who lie low to the heights, and makes them withdraw from the north, that most frigid wind, from which: Evils blaze up upon all the earth; and in Jeremiah that terrible pot is kindled from the face of the north. The great cloud too they refer to the person of Christ, who came into the Egypt of this world upon a light cloud, and they say that it is properly called great by comparison with lesser ones, namely the prophets and the apostles and all the saints, of whom it is written: And your truth even to the clouds, and: I will command the clouds not to rain a shower upon Israel, and elsewhere: The clouds, the dust of his feet, and again: Clouds and darkness round about him. The flashing fire also, and the brightness round about it, they take according to what is written: God is a consuming fire. To send this upon the earth the Savior says that he came, and he desires it to burn in us and in all believers; and though it brings terror and punishments upon sinners, yet it glows red with brightness and is full of light and radiance. Therefore it refines us, so that it may bestow more joyful things on us when we are pure and cleansed.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","And from the midst of it as it were the appearance of electrum, that is, from the midst of the fire. (LXX: \"And in the midst of it as it were a vision of electrum in the midst of the fire.\" And what follows: ÷ And brightness in it ⁚, is to be marked beforehand with an obelus.) Had Scripture not added, saying: That is, from the midst of the fire, we could have erred because of the ambiguity of the word, so as to think that the appearance or vision of electrum was in the midst of the wind or spirit. This, therefore, is to be understood: that in the midst of the fire, or of the torments of God, there is the likeness of electrum, which is more precious than gold and silver; and that after judgment and torments, which seem rough and hard to those who suffer them, the gleam of electrum appears more precious, since all things are governed by the providence of God, and what is thought a punishment is a medicine.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.1.5":["And in the midst of it the likeness of four living creatures, and this their appearance: the likeness of a man in them. In the midst 'of it': 'of the electrum' is indeed understood, but it is better that we understand 'of the fire', which is light to believers, punishment to unbelievers. In the midst of this fire, therefore, was the likeness of four living creatures — a likeness, not a nature —, and of the four living creatures, which are afterwards called fourfold in form, there is one likeness, that of a man, so that all the rational beings in the world may be pointed out: Let us make, he says, man after our image and likeness — now the image and likeness of God is not a form of the body but of the mind, drawn after the likeness of the true image, Christ, which is the image of the invisible God —. These rational creatures dwell in four places, either because of the four hinges of the world by which the globe is enclosed, or because of the four places of the heavenly, the earthly, the infernal and the supercelestial beings, of which the apostle Paul also speaks: That in the name of Jesus every knee should bow, of things heavenly, earthly and infernal. For three there is the testimony of Paul; let us see the fourth too: Praise God, heavens of heavens, and let the water that is above the heavens praise the name of the Lord; and again the Apostle speaks of heavenly things and of others that are supercelestial.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.1.6-8":["Four faces to one and four wings to one; and their feet, straight feet, and the sole of their foot as the sole of a calf's foot, and sparks as the appearance of glowing bronze. And the hand of a man under their wings on the four sides. (LXX: \"And four faces to one and four wings to one, and their legs straight, and their feet winged. And sparks as flashing bronze, and their wings light. And the hand of a man under their wings on their four sides.\") Concerning the four living creatures whose appearance was the likeness of a man, but in such a way that each had four faces and four wings, and straight feet, and the sole of the foot 'as the footprint of a calf's foot' or, as Aquila translated, 'round' — which the Seventy omitted entirely —; concerning the sparks also, glowing red with the brightness of bronze, and their light wings, which is not found in the Hebrew; concerning the hand of a man too under their wings on the four sides, and the rest that the prophetic discourse describes, we shall try to say what seems right to us, once we have briefly summed up the opinions of individuals. Some, whom we too followed in the preface of the Commentaries on Matthew, think that the four Gospels are designated by the names of these living creatures: Matthew's, because he described as it were a man: The book of the generation of Jesus Christ, the son of David, the son of Abraham; that of the lion they refer to Mark: The beginning of the gospel of Jesus Christ, the Son of God. As it is written in Isaiah the prophet: The voice of one crying in the desert: Prepare the way of the Lord, make his paths straight; that of the calf to the Gospel of Luke, which begins with the priesthood of Zechariah; that of the eagle to the opening of John, who, flying up to the height, began: In the beginning was the Word, and the Word was with God, and God was the Word: on which we said in the above-mentioned work what seemed right to us; and more fully in the Revelation of John the forms and names of these living creatures are referred to the four Gospels: how the description of all the living creatures can be fitted to them, we shall try to say in its place. Others, however, who follow the foolish wisdom of the philosophers, surmise the two hemispheres in the two cherubim of the temple, and ourselves and the antipodes as men lying, as it were, on their backs and falling. Most, following Plato, refer the rational, the irascible and the concupiscible part of the soul, which he calls λογικὸν and θυμικὸν and ἐπιθυμητικὸν, to the man and the lion and the calf: placing reason and thought and mind and counsel, the same being virtue and wisdom, in the citadel of the brain; ferocity, however, and wrath and violence in the lion, which resides in the gall; further, lust, luxury and the desire of all pleasures in the liver, that is, in the calf, which clings to the works of the earth; and they posit a fourth, which is above these and beyond these three, which the Greeks call συνείδησιν — the spark of conscience which is not extinguished even in the breast of Cain, after he was cast out of paradise, and by which, when we are overcome by pleasures or by fury, and at times deceived by the very likeness of reason, we perceive that we sin —; this they assign properly to the eagle, not mingling itself with the three but correcting the three when they stray; and we read that in the Scriptures it is sometimes called spirit, which intercedes for us with unspeakable groanings. For no one knows the things that are of a man, except the spirit that is in him; and Paul, writing to the Thessalonians, prays that it be kept whole together with soul and body. And yet we see this very conscience too, according to what is written in Proverbs: The wicked man, when he has come into the depth of sins, despises, cast down in some and losing its place, who have not even shame and modesty in their offenses and deserve to hear: The face of a harlot has become yours, you do not know how to blush. This four-horse chariot, then, God guides after the manner of a charioteer; and when it runs with disordered steps he reins it in, makes it docile and compels it to obey his command. This discussion of the parts of the soul, that is, of man, who is called the lesser world by the same philosophers, we too shall touch upon. There are those who think, simply, that in the four living creatures, according to the opinion of Hippocrates, the four elements are shown of which all things consist: fire, air, water, earth; how these are mixed with one another and, though each is single, are seen joined to one another and touch one another, and have in the one person of the living creatures four forms and figures, is not the business of the present work. The four wheels too, rising from earthly things to the heights, joined to the single and fourfold living creatures, they reckon to be either the mingling of these same elements, or the cycle of the four seasons, each completed in three months, and the turning year, which has taken its name from the fact that it is always turning and returns into itself; of which it was beautifully said in a single little verse: Spring, summer, autumn, winter, and month, and year. And what is said, 'There was a wheel in a wheel', they think signifies 'a year in a year', of which another poet says: And the year rolls back into itself along its own footsteps. The likeness of the firmament too, which is compared to crystal, they would have understood as this heaven which we look up at, under which the four living creatures roll and pass. And the throne of the color of sapphire, and the man seated upon it in human likeness, they assert describe the dominion of him who rules all things and has all things under his feet, of the Omnipotence; and that at the end it is said: This, the vision of the likeness of the glory of the Lord, through which, as through a kind of painting and image, providence is shown. And as to the feet of the living creatures being called straight, and the sole of the foot a calf's, or round, they hold that every earthly thing flies up to heavenly things and, all angles being cut away, follows roundness, which is the most beautiful of all figures; that the glowing sparks indicate that all things are full of light; and that the hand of a man under the wings, of the man himself as well as of the lion and the calf and the eagle, means that reason should sustain all things and lift them up from earthly lowliness to heavenly things. These things we can refer both to the Gospels and to all that we have set out above. I remember having heard that the four passions, which Cicero discusses most fully in the Tusculans — of joy, of distress, of desire and of fear, of which two concern things present, two things to come —, are signified by the four living creatures — of which Vergil too says briefly: Hence they fear and desire, grieve and rejoice —; that these ought to be ruled by reason and the power of God; and that against them are set — nay rather, set over them — four virtues: prudence, justice, fortitude, temperance, so that they may be governed by their judgment; but how these are fitted to the faces of the man, the lion, the calf and the eagle, they have said nothing at all; on these the same philosopher and orator discourses in his three books of Duties addressed to his son. I have read too a short little discussion of a certain Catina, whom the Syrians call λεπτὸν, that is, 'sharp' and 'ingenious', who thinks that the order of the camp of the twelve tribes in the wilderness is described, to the east, west, north and south, which are joined to one another both by love and by kinship, and that this is the wheel in a wheel; which are led by the spirit and protected by the cloud in the desert and lit by the pillar of fire at night, so that they may not return to Egypt but may always hasten to go to the land of promise. The likeness of electrum in the midst he interprets as the Holy of Holies, and he refers the face of the man to all Israel, that of the lion to the royal scepter of Judah, that of the calf to the priestly and Levitical tribe; outside these is the eagle, the vengeance and retribution of God, looking out over all things from heaven and ready to tear the sinner, of which it is said also in Hosea: As an eagle over the house of God, that is, the temple; and he holds that in this same prophet the great eagle with great wings and talons, which has the course for entering into Lebanon, is understood as Nebuchadnezzar, whom it signifies even now to be about to come; and that God sits upon a chariot of this kind like a charioteer and commands what is to be done and what is not to be done. This, moreover, he says, is said to the people who live in Babylon: that if it submits its neck to God and obeys his reins, it will again merit his help and recover the land which it had lost.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.1.9":["And they had faces and wings on the four sides; and its wings were joined, one to the other. They did not turn back when they advanced, but each one proceeded before its face. The Gospels are joined to one another and cling together, and, flying through the whole world, they run hither and thither; they have no end to their flight, nor are they ever overcome and retreat, but they always advance to higher things; hence Paul also says: Forgetting what is past and stretching myself out toward what is to come. This same thing we can say both of the virtues of the mind and of the flight of the seasons and the mingling of the elements: that, leaving the past behind, they always hasten toward what lies ahead. That the seasons glide and flee is shown in a short little verse: But meanwhile it flees, irretrievable time flees, and in a lyric poem: Alas, Postumus, Postumus, the fleeting years glide by ...\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.1.10":["Now the likeness of their countenance: the face of a man, and the face of a lion on the right of the four of them, and the face of an ox on the left of the four of them, and the face of an eagle above the four of them. By the faces he means the beginnings of the Gospels, of which the man and the lion, that is, the birth of Christ and the voice of the prophet thundering in the desert, hold the right side; but that of the calf, that is, of the victims and the priesthood of the Jews, is on the left, because, abolished, it has passed over to the spiritual priesthood of which it was said: You are a priest for ever according to the order of Melchizedek; yet only in such a way that all cling to one another and are reckoned as one body; the eagle, however, which is above the birth, is also above the prophecy which is fulfilled by the coming of the Lord, and above the priesthood which has passed away, and is outside all these, telling of the spiritual birth, how the Father is in the Son and the Son in the Father, of which it is most rightly said: His generation who shall declare?. These are, according to the Revelation, as we have already said before: Four living creatures full of eyes before and behind, of which one living creature like a lion, and the second like a calf, and the third having a face as of a man, and the fourth like a flying eagle; and there they are said, after the manner of the seraphim, to have six wings each, covering their face with two and their feet with two, and flying with two, and ceasing neither by day nor by night, and saying: Holy, holy, holy, Lord God almighty, who was and who is and who is to come.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","That these four Gospels, then, were foretold long before, the book of Ezekiel too proves, in which the first vision is woven together thus: And in the midst as it were the likeness of four living creatures, and their countenance the face of a man and the face of a lion and the face of a calf and the face of an eagle. The first, the face of a man, signifies Matthew, who began to write as if about a man: The book of the generation of Jesus Christ, the son of David, the son of Abraham; the second signifies Mark, in whom is heard the voice of a lion roaring in the desert: The voice of one crying in the desert: Prepare the way of the Lord, make his paths straight; the third, that of the calf, prefigures that the evangelist Luke took his beginning from Zechariah the priest; the fourth signifies John the evangelist, who, taking up the wings of an eagle and hastening to higher things, discourses on the Word of God. The rest that follows tends to the same sense: their legs straight, and their feet winged, and wherever the spirit went, they went and did not turn back, and their backs full of eyes, and sparks and lamps running to and fro in the midst, and a wheel within a wheel, and in each one four faces. Hence the Apocalypse of John too, after its account of the twenty-four elders, who, holding harps and bowls, worship the Lamb of God, brings in lightnings and thunders and seven spirits running to and fro and a sea of glass and four living creatures full of eyes, saying: The first living creature like a lion, and the second like a calf, and the third like a man, and the fourth like a flying eagle; and a little after: They were full, it says, of eyes, and they had no rest day and night, saying: Holy, holy, holy, Lord God almighty, who was and who is and who is to come. By all these things it is plainly shown that only four Gospels ought to be received, and that all the dirges of the apocrypha are to be sung by dead heretics rather than by living churchmen.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"EZK.1.11":["And their faces and their wings stretched out above; two wings of each were joined, and two covered their bodies. We said above too that the Gospels and the seasons and all rational creatures and the four virtues are joined to one another, so that whoever lacks one lacks all. That two are stretched out and lift themselves on high signifies the heavenly preaching, and that all things tend toward the majesty of God. By the two, however, by which the bodies are covered, human knowledge is shut out, and no perfect view is granted, as the Apostle says: We know in part and we prophesy in part. But when what is perfect has come, the things that are in part will be destroyed.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.1.12":["And each one walked before its face. Wherever the impulse of the spirit was, there they went, and they did not turn back when they walked. He who holds the handle of the plough ought not to look back behind him, nor imitate Lot's wife, lest he fall into the plague of Deuteronomy and perish by an incurable ὀπισθοτόνῳ [opisthotonos, a spasm that bends the body backward], by which Eli too fell, because he had offended God through the fault of his sons; how much more the four living creatures, which were full of light and winged, which follow the Holy Spirit going before them, which so fly through the world and raise themselves to the heights that they cover their bodies with the wings of history and do not grant us a fuller view? That it is said a second time: They did not turn back when they advanced, points to the mystery of both Testaments: that in these four living creatures both the Law and the Gospel hasten toward things to come and never take a backward motion.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.1.13":["The coals of burning fire, however, and the lamps running about in the midst of the living creatures are to be interpreted from that passage in Isaiah: And the fire devoured the matter like hay, and he will sanctify them in burning fire; of which it is also written elsewhere: Coals of fire will fall upon them; and against deceitful lips it is said in another psalm: What shall be given to you, or what shall be added to you, against the deceitful tongue? Sharp arrows of the mighty, with desolating coals; and in another place: You have coals of fire, you will sit upon them; these will be a help to you. Whatever creature we look at flashes forth the knowledge of God, since from the creatures the Creator is recognized; and from the midst of the living creatures the brightness of fire and lightning goes forth: for if the Gospels have struck against one another, in the midst of the letter and of the lowly history you will find the mysteries of the Holy Spirit.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.1.14":["And the likeness of the living creatures: their appearance as of burning coals of fire, and as the appearance of lamps. This was the vision running about in the midst of the living creatures: the brightness of fire, and lightning going forth from the fire. And the living creatures went and returned in the likeness of flashing lightning. (LXX: \"And in the midst of the living creatures a vision, as of burning coals of fire, as the face of lamps running about in the midst of the living creatures; and the brightness of fire, and from the fire there went forth as it were lightning.\" And what follows: And the living creatures ran and returned as the appearance of 'bezec', was added in the Seventy from the edition of Theodotion; for they, lest the prophet should seem to utter two statements rather contrary to each other, thought that what they supposed to be contrary, that is: And the living creatures ran and returned, should be passed over in silence, lest it cause the reader to stumble; but it is better in the divine books to translate what has been said, even if you do not understand why it was said, than to take away what you do not know; otherwise many other things too, which are ineffable and which the human mind cannot grasp, will be deleted by this license.) We, however, say this: that, just as in Proverbs it is written in the same place: Do not answer a fool according to his folly, lest you become like him, and again there is set down what seems to us to be contrary: Answer a fool according to his folly, lest he seem wise to himself, and both agree according to the difference of times and persons, since the fool is both despised because he does not receive wisdom, and foolish pride is knocked down by another folly, according to what the Apostle also says: I have become foolish; you compelled me, so in this place too we ought to ask why above it was said twice: The living creatures did not turn back when they advanced, and now once: The living creatures ran and returned; and there would be a problem were it not followed by: In the likeness of flashing lightning, which in the Hebrew tongue is called 'bezec', and which Symmachus translated: As the appearance of a ray of lightning. As the sky flashes with frequent fires, and in the twinkling of an eye and in a moment the lightnings dart about and return, not losing their womb and, so to speak, the source and matter of the fire, so these living creatures too, when they proceed with unstumbling foot, hasten toward what lies ahead. But if they see something set against their effort, they do not so much turn back as draw themselves in, to stretch out again and to offer the light which they had hidden for a little while, so that they may give the servants their rations in their season, and not give what is holy to dogs, nor cast pearls before swine; hence Paul too says: I gave you milk to drink, not food, for you were not yet able to take it; and the choosing of Judas and the anointing of Saul do not convict God of ignorance of things to come, but show that he is the judge of things present; hence also the apostles are commanded that, if they perceive a house to be unworthy of their greeting, they should first shake off their running feet, and the peace which they had given to the house should return to them.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.1.15-18":["All things heavenly and earthly, and whatever falls under human understanding, roll on their own wheels; the moon runs through the yearly circuit of the sun in each month; the Morning Star, which is itself the Evening Star, while it gleams ruddy in the east and in the west and tempers the darkness of night with a small light, completes the same course in two years; and the four other stars which are called wandering, and whatever shines in the sky, and the varieties too of crops and trees and herbs, run on their own wheels through the four seasons; and we see nothing that was not before: Circling in circles the spirit goes, and returns into its circuits. All torrents go into the sea, and the sea is not filled. Why? Because they return to their springs from the womb of the abyss. But if anyone considers the wheel and course of the Gospels, that is, of the four living creatures, which breathe, live and understand, he will see that in a short time the world was filled with the apostolic word. The wheel in a wheel, too, is either the joining of the two Testaments, which Jacob's ladder and Isaiah's tongs and the twice-sharpened sword point to, or the Gospels cohering with one another, whose course and stature reach toward heaven, and touch anything on the earth only a little, as, ever hurrying, it hastens to the heights; of which it is also said elsewhere: Holy stones roll upon the earth, from which the heavenly Jerusalem is built; I think that this same thing is signified also by what is sung in the psalm: The voice of your thunder in the wheel, and elsewhere: Which sets on fire the wheel of birth; what sense these testimonies have in their own places is not a matter for this time.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.1.15":["And when I looked at the living creatures, there appeared one wheel upon the earth beside the living creatures that had four faces. And the appearance of the wheels and their work as the vision of the sea, and one likeness of the four of them; and their appearance and works as if it were a wheel in the midst of a wheel. Going by their four sides they went, and they did not turn back when they walked. The wheels had also stature and height and a dreadful appearance, and the whole body full of eyes round about the four of them. (LXX: \"And I saw, and behold, one wheel upon the earth following the four living creatures. And the appearance of the wheels and their making as the appearance of tharsis. And a likeness in the four, and their work as a wheel is wont to be in a wheel. On their four sides they walked, and did not turn back when they walked. And their backs, and they had height. And I saw them, and their backs full of eyes round about the four of them.\") Thus far the picture of the four living creatures that had four faces each is described, following the spirit and the cloud which was in the midst of the spirit; now for each living creature a single wheel is set, which were not joined to the living creatures but followed them; or one wheel appeared upon the earth, which, divided into four, had as many faces as the living creatures it followed.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.1.16":["And so great was the likeness of the four wheels following the four living creatures that they were most truly believed to be one wheel. And their work and making was as the vision of 'tharsis', which we have rendered 'sea'; Aquila put 'hyacinth', a stone which has the likeness of the sky. And their appearance was as a wheel in a wheel, not so that you would believe it one wheel, but one coupled to another.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.1.17":["They went on the four sides and were not dragged, nor did they turn backward; for how could it happen that they turned back, when they were following the living creatures, which always stretched themselves toward what lay ahead?\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.1.18":["The stature too and height of the wheels was so great that it caused wonder in those who saw them. And the whole body and the backs were full of light round about, so that you would see no member that did not have eyes of light — of whatever kind the fables of the poets describe Argus to have been, hundred-eyed or of many eyes, whom Juno turned into a peacock for his negligent guarding —, so that what is a marvel of God the Creator was the condemnation of an unavenged adultery.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","That the whole body and the backs are full of eyes will be confirmed by anyone who sees that there is nothing in the Gospels that does not shine and illumine the world with its splendor, so that even the things thought small and lowly gleam with the majesty of the Holy Spirit.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.1.19-21":["And when the living creatures walked, the wheels too walked together beside them. And when the living creatures were lifted up from the earth, the wheels too were lifted up at the same time. Wherever the spirit went, there, as the spirit went, the wheels too were lifted up together, following it; for the spirit of life was in the wheels. With those going they went, and with those standing they stood; and with those lifted up from the earth the wheels too were lifted up together, following them, because the spirit of life was in the wheels. The four living creatures followed the spirit and the cloud which was in the spirit. And again the wheels, though they followed the living creatures as they lifted themselves from the earth, were by no means following the living creatures but the spirit, so as to show their own will: Because the spirit of life was in the wheels. Three things, moreover, are indicated equally both in the living creatures and in the wheels: when they stood, when they walked, when they were lifted up — things which both the living creatures and the wheels did in common; for neither could the wheels walk while the living creatures stood, nor could the wheels lift themselves up while the living creatures walked upon the earth; but those whose preaching was one had also one rest and course and elevation. And a second time it is said: Because the spirit of life was in the wheels, so that we should by no means think of some contrivance of wheels, such as we see on carriages, wagons, coaches and chariots, but of living beings, nay rather of what is above living beings; for the animal man does not perceive the things that are of the spirit. These wheels, then, in which was the spirit of life, do all things with order and measure, and are in concord with the living creatures, following them and through them the Holy Spirit; nay rather, passing over the intermediaries, rejoicing in the fellowship of the Holy Spirit. All these things, according to the earlier understanding, the prudent reader will be able to fit to the varied interpretation.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.1.22":["And over the head of the living creatures the likeness of a firmament, as the appearance of dreadful crystal, and stretched out over their heads above. Under the firmament, moreover, their wings straight, one toward the other. Each one veiled its body with two wings, and the other was likewise veiled. And I heard the sound of the wings, as the sound of many waters, as the sound of the sublime God. When they walked, there was as it were the sound of a multitude, like the sound of a camp, and when they stood, their wings were let down. For when a voice came from above the firmament that was over their head, they stood and lowered their wings. And above the firmament that was overhanging their head, as the appearance of a sapphire stone, the likeness of a throne, and above the likeness of the throne a likeness as the appearance of a man above. (Where we put: Their wings straight, and: As the sound — or voice — of the sublime God, the Seventy translated: Their wings stretched out and flying, and: As the voice of the strong one, which in the Hebrew is called 'saddai'. And many things, both in this and in other chapters, were passed over by them, which I have refrained from setting down because of their length. As for what we have translated more plainly: One toward the other; each one veiled its body with two wings, and the other was likewise veiled, in place of which in the Hebrew is written: 'Of a woman toward her sister; a man veiled his body with two wings, and a man was likewise veiled', he put man after woman in the same person for this reason, lest we should suppose sex among heavenly beings, since in one and the same, according to the Hebrew idiom, the same is called both man and woman.) Now above the four living creatures and as many wheels is seen the likeness of the firmament, which we call heaven, having the appearance of crystal, which is most pure and is said to congeal from clean and shining waters through excessive cold, so much so that water bound by frost is also called in the Greek tongue 'crystallus' [ice]; and it was fitting that in the higher regions there should be an exceptional purity which protects all things, that is, the rational and wise powers, and the course of the four seasons, and the regions of the world, and the order of all things, and the preaching of the Gospel, which is understood in part and veiled in part.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.1.24":["And the voice of the flying wings is heard, as the voice of many waters, which according to the Revelation of John signify peoples; and, advancing little by little, it is said to be as the voice of a camp and as the voice of the sublime God, which in Hebrew is called 'saddai', and according to the Seventy 'voice of the word', so that we may believe that all that is preached in the world is the voice of the Son of God.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.1.25-26":["When the living creatures stood, their wings were let down: for they could not bear the voice of almighty God resounding in the heavens, but stood and marveled, and by their silence showed forth the power of God, who sat above the firmament; and this firmament had, for those who were below, the likeness of crystal, but to those who were above it seemed like a sapphire stone; and this likeness of sapphire was the throne of him who sat in the likeness of a man. From this we understand that the firmament and the crystal and the sapphire and the man are shown in likeness, not in reality.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.1.26":["That the man ought to be understood as God the Father many testimonies teach, among which is this in the Gospel: A man, a certain one, planted a vineyard and let it out to husbandmen, and a little after: He sent his servants and, above all, his son, and again: A man made a marriage feast for his son — not that the Son is excluded from the kingdom, of whom Isaiah wrote: I saw the Lord sitting upon a throne high and lifted up, and John: These things, moreover, he says, Isaiah said when he saw the glory of the Son of God, but that the Son too reigns in the Father —; and all things of the Son are the Father's, who is the image of the invisible God the Father: for in Daniel too God the Father is introduced sitting, and the Son of man is brought before him to receive the kingdom; and in the Revelation of John the same things are described of the Son; and at the contest of the first martyr Stephen he is seen standing at the right hand of the Father, he of whom it is sung in the Psalms: The Lord said to my Lord: Sit at my right hand, until I make your enemies the footstool of your feet. And just as in the crystal all the purest and shining things in the heavens are shown, which illumine our eyed body, so in the sapphire, that is, in the throne of God, and above the firmament, which we take as heaven, the hidden and secret and incomprehensible mysteries of God are shown, who made darkness his hiding place and is seen in the cloud and in the thick darkness: hence also it is written in Exodus: And under the feet of God there was as it were a work of sapphire brick, and like the appearance of the firmament of the clearest heaven; hence also the bride describes the beauty of the bridegroom in the Song: His belly as it were an ivory tablet upon a sapphire stone; and in the Revelation of John the first foundation is written to be of jasper, the second of sapphire; and on the breastplate of the high priest, in the row and order of the individual stones, the second row has the carbuncle and the sapphire and the jasper; of which stones we have spoken in part in the Explanations of Isaiah.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.1.27-28":["And I saw as it were the appearance of electrum, like the look of fire, within it round about, from his loins and upward; and from his loins downward I saw as it were the appearance of fire shining round about, like the look of the bow when it is in the cloud on the day of rain: this was the look of the brightness round about. This is the vision of the likeness of the glory of the Lord. The likeness of the man who sat upon the likeness of the throne (the throne which had the likeness of sapphire stone) is compared from the loins and upward to electrum, and this electrum had both within and without as it were the look of fire. But from the loins and downward there was fire shining round about, to show that the things which are above the loins, where perception and reason dwell, have no need of fire or of flames but of the most precious and purest metal; whereas the things that are from the loins downward, where intercourse is, where generation is, where the incitements of the vices are, need the purgation of flames, so that, once they have been purged, they may have the likeness of the bow, which is commonly called iris, when it is in the cloud on the day of rain — for the ἴρις [rainbow], which in Holy Scripture is called the bow, and in the Revelation of John is called by the same name ἴρις, cannot appear except in rain and in a watery cloud, being of diverse and most beautiful colors that pass gradually into one another; whence the poet too says: It snatches a thousand varied colors from the light opposite; and the same poet also, following the usage of the common people: When the bow, he says, drinks, by which he signifies that the bow never appears except in a cloud and in waters —. This bow is the sign of the clemency of God and of the covenant which he made with men, so that, whenever it appears in the cloud, we may know, according to the example of antiquity, that we shall by no means perish by a flood. From this it is shown that after penalties and torments and the purgation of sins there will be mercy, at least for those who have deserved to see God reigning. Hence it is said: This was the look of the brightness round about, round about God, or the throne, or all things that are perfect; and this is the vision of the glory of the likeness of God: not that he saw the glory of the Lord, but the likeness of his glory.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.1.28":["And I saw, and I fell upon my face, and I heard the voice of one speaking. He is not lifted up by the greatness of the visions, but from awareness of human frailty he falls down upon his face. Whence also the apostle Paul, after his ascent to heaven and to paradise and his hearing of unspeakable words, says that an angel of Satan was given to him to buffet him, lest he be exalted; and Abraham fell upon his face after he had heard the words of God. And yet, because he had fallen down not through sin but through humility, he is raised up by the Lord and is commanded to hear his words. We should know also that it is one thing to fall upon the face, another to fall backward: Abraham, after he had heard of the birth of Isaac, fell upon his face and laughed; but Eli, who had sinned, fell backward; and of Dan it is written in Genesis: Let Dan be a serpent in the path, biting the hoof of the horse, and his rider shall fall backward, awaiting the salvation of God: for since he had fallen from the horse at the bite of the serpent, he therefore awaits the coming of the Savior; and in the Gospel according to John, those who had come to seize the Lord, after he answered, 'I am', fell backward to the ground, not at all enduring the word of him who had said to Moses: Go, say to the sons of Israel: He who is has sent me to you.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.2.1":["And he said to me: Son of man, stand upon your feet, and I will speak to you. Lying down, he could not hear the word of God; but he hears, with Moses: But you, stand here with me, and Daniel too records that this happened to him.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.2.2":["And the spirit entered into me after he had spoken to me, and set me upon my feet, and I heard him speaking to me and saying. (LXX: \"And the spirit came to me, and took me up, and lifted me, and set me upon my feet, and I heard him speaking to me, and he said to me.\") The divine word had instructed the prophet and commanded: Stand upon your feet, but without the help of God and the coming of the Holy Spirit he could not stand; therefore the Spirit enters into him, or takes him up, and raises him, so that he may be of firm step and may be able to say: He set my feet upon a rock. And we read of no sinner standing, but to the saints it is said: Stand therefore, girded about your loins; whereas the sinner deserves to hear that word of Deuteronomy: The footstep of your foot shall not be stable.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.2.3":["Frequently it is said to Ezekiel:\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","Son of man, and to Daniel rarely; each of them, in the person of him who had said: But the Son of man has nowhere to lay his head, consoles the captive people and draws them back to repentance.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","I send you to the sons of Israel, to apostate nations which have withdrawn from me; they and their fathers have transgressed my covenant up to this day. He by no means calls them the people of God, Israel, but apostate nations, those who have withdrawn from the Lord, not only the fathers but the sons as well: lest the sons should seem liable for the sin of the fathers, but rather so that the impiety of fathers and sons alike might be equal. Moreover, as for the Seventy's translation 'irritating me' or 'turning into bitterness', it signifies that God, who is by nature kind and sweet, is changed into bitterness by our vices: for just as it is said to the saints: Taste and see that the Lord is sweet, so sinners find him bitter; whence the Apostle too refers the goodness and severity of God to the saints and to the sinners, and of the sinners who lay fallen it is written: The Lord raises up those who are dashed down, the Lord loves the righteous, the Lord looses those in fetters, while to the saints who stand he promises rewards.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.2.4":["And they are sons of hard face and of untamable heart to whom I send you. (This is not found in the Seventy.) And it is a mark of great clemency for God to send to such men and not to despair of their salvation, and a mark of prophetic confidence that he is not afraid to go even to such men. Now the untamable heart and the hard face we ought to understand in accordance with what is said to the sinner: Your neck is an iron sinew, and your brow is bronze; and these men in what follows are also charged with a heart of stone, which God says he will tear out and in its place give back one of flesh, so that by its softness it may receive the precepts of God.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.2.5":["And you shall say to them: Thus says the Lord God; if perhaps even they will hear, and if perhaps they will be quiet. Something similar is written in Jeremiah too: If perhaps they will hear and repent, and in the Gospel: Perhaps they will respect my son. Now God speaks these words with the disposition of one who is in doubt, so that they may show the free choice of man, lest the foreknowledge of future things make immutable, whether for evil or for good, what God knew would come to be: for it is not because he knows what is to come that we must necessarily do what he foreknew; rather, what we are going to do by our own will, he, as God, knows will come to be.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.2.6":["You therefore, son of man, do not fear them, nor be afraid of their words, for they are unbelieving and subverters with you. (LXX: \"And you, son of man, do not fear them, nor dread before their face, for they will rage and will gather against you round about.\") Although they are hard of neck and of untamable heart, yet my precepts are stronger. And do not think yourself deceived if you are sent to those who will not hear you when you speak. Behold, I foretell to you that they will rage and gather against you and surround you, leaving you no escape; and they will do this because they are unbelieving and despise the commands of God.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","And you dwell with scorpions. Such as can strike, such as can wound both with an arched blow and with a hollow sting, so that the same stroke both opens the skin and pours in the venom. Each one receives his name according to his character: to the scribes and Pharisees it is said: Brood of vipers, and of Herod, who was a shifty turncoat: Go and tell this fox; and the false prophets in this same prophet are compared to foxes, and now, for their untamable heart, they are said to be scorpions; whence in the Gospel too we read: All who came before me were thieves and robbers, and the sheep did not hear them — and how did the lost sheep of the house of Israel hear the adversaries? —, from which it is shown that those who heard the thieves and robbers lost the name of sheep and took on other names, so that they did not perish as sheep but as vipers, foxes and scorpions.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.2.7":["And you shall speak my words to them; perhaps they will hear and fear (or be quiet). We must therefore not cease, although those to whom we speak are wicked, from preaching the word of God in season and out of season, according to the Apostle, because it can happen both that the hard man is corrected to softness and that he who is obedient, his will changed, does not hear.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.2.8":["Do not be provoking, as the house is provoking. We have said once already that for 'provocation' or 'irritation' the Seventy put 'bitterness'. What he says, then, is this: You must not imitate those whom you are sent to correct, lest like sin should also earn like punishment.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","Open your mouth and eat what I give you. 'Show yourself worthy', he says, 'of my words, and take up spiritual food', so that, just as it is said in the Gospel: He who has ears to hear, let him hear, so here too it may be said: 'He who has a mouth open to eat, let him eat'. Whence also through the psalmist the Lord speaks: Open your mouth and I will fill it, and he answered: I opened my mouth and drew in the spirit; and the apostle Paul, who had in himself the treasures of wisdom and knowledge and in whom Christ the Lord spoke, writes to the Corinthians: My mouth is open to you, O Corinthians; and of the Savior Matthew records: Opening his mouth he taught the disciples.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.2.9":["And I saw, and behold, a hand sent to me. For the hand 'sent' the Seventy translated 'stretched out'; it is sent and stretched out to those who obtain the benefits of the Lord, but is drawn back from those to whom he speaks through the prophet: Is the hand of the Lord shortened and drawn back? and in the psalm: Why have you turned away your hand, and your right hand from the midst of your bosom, to the end? Finally, in the passion he stretched out his wings, and took up the disciples and carried them on his shoulders, and said: All the day long I have spread out my hands to an unbelieving people, that he might gather the children of Jerusalem as a hen gathers her chicks under wings of her own, and that by the stretching out of his hands Israel might overcome Amalek.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","In which was a rolled-up book, and he spread it out before me, and it was written within and without; there were written in it lamentations and song and woe. For 'rolled-up book' the Seventy translated 'the heading of the book', in the hand of the Lord who sits upon the throne, the hand which was sent forth and stretched out; of this book it is also said in the Psalms: In the heading of the book it is written of me — let us understand 'heading' as the beginning —. This book, rolled up and sealed, and written within and without, or before and behind, was of such difficulty that no one could, either in heaven or on earth or under the earth, open it or read it, except only the lion of the tribe of Judah, the root of David and of Jesse; the book which John in the Revelation said he had devoured, not whole but in its heading, that is, in its beginning. For it cannot be that human nature should devour all the treasures of this book.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.2.10":["And he spread it out before the prophet and offered it to be read and known, the book which in Isaiah is said to be sealed to a people that does not believe — for to this day a veil has been laid upon the Old Testament for the Jews —. And it was written before and behind, concerning things to come and things past; or certainly without, in the letter of the history, and within, in the spiritual understanding, of which the psalmist also says: All the glory, he says, of the king's daughter is from within. And there were written in it, both within and without, lamentations and song and woe: lamentations over those who are called to repentance, as Samuel and the apostle Paul bewailed and mourned Saul and the Corinthians, whom they longed to save; song over those who are worthy of God's testimony and to whom the psalmist gives the command: Sing to the Lord a new song; and woe over those who are utterly despaired of and who, when they have come into the depth of sins, despise.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","But as far as concerns the spiritual understanding, the prophet takes up a lament over every Israel which used to discern God with the mind and afterwards ceased to serve him, according to that which is commanded to Ezekiel, that he should devour the book in which there was written, both within and without, lamentation, and song, and woe. Understand by within the Song of Solomon that follows, who says: The king has brought me into his chamber. And the forty-fourth (45th) psalm, in which it is written: All the glory of the king's daughter is within. But without is whatever is read in the letter, and is seen in the bark, and is not held in the marrow of the spirit. And so, both according to the letter and according to tropology, in all the books of the prophets a lament is written over those who repent after sins; a song over those who, stained by no filth of sins, are worthy of canticle and praise; woe over those who do not repent, but according to the hardness of their heart treasure up for themselves wrath in the day of wrath.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"EZK.3.1":["Eat this scroll, and go, speak to the sons of Israel. Unless we first eat the opened scroll, we cannot teach the sons of Israel; and so David too, after he himself had obtained mercy: I will teach, he says, the unjust your ways, and the wicked shall be converted to you.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.3.2":["And I opened my mouth, and he fed me with that scroll. 'I', he says, 'opened my mouth, because it was said to me: Open your mouth and eat'. And when the mouth was opened, the Lord bestowed food, so that the beginnings of the will may be in us and we may obtain the perfection of blessedness from the Lord: For it is not of him who wills nor of him who runs, but of God who shows mercy; and yet both to will and to run belong to our own free choice. For 'I opened' the Seventy translated 'he opened', so that God is understood, who himself opened the prophet's mouth and fed him.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.3.3":["Your belly shall eat, and your bowels shall be filled. (LXX: \"Your mouth shall eat, and your belly shall be filled\".) The beginnings of reading and of the simple history are the eating of the scroll; but when by constant meditation we have stored up the book of the Lord in the treasury of memory, our belly is spiritually filled and our bowels are satisfied, so that we have, with the apostle Paul, bowels of mercy, and that belly is filled of which Jeremiah speaks: My belly, my belly, I am in pain; and the thoughts of my heart trouble me.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","And I ate it, and it became in my mouth like sweet honey. David also says: How sweet to my throat are your words, beyond honey to my mouth, and elsewhere: The judgments of the Lord are true, more to be desired than gold and very precious stone, and sweeter than honey and the honeycomb; and Samson found a honeycomb in the mouth of the lion, and the Lord after the resurrection ate part of a broiled fish and a honeycomb, and in Proverbs it is said of the bee, although the Hebrew copies do not have this: Go to the bee and learn how she is a worker, and makes her work chaste: whose labors kings and the unlearned use for their health; this Moses and the prophets and the evangelists and the apostles have wrought, so that whoever is a king, whose heart is in the hand of God, may enjoy sweet food, while he who is simple and, without the cunning of the serpent, has the innocence of doves, may believe with simple faith and be saved; for snares are everywhere, and often the devil transforms himself into an angel of light, and honey drips from the lips of a harlot woman, who promises sweet things so as to slip in poisons.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.3.4":["And he said to me: Son of man, go and enter to the house of Israel, and you shall speak my words to them. He to whom it is said: Go and enter to the house of Israel is shown not to have been with the sinful people, although he seemed to dwell within the same borders by the river Chebar — for it is written: 'And I was in the midst of the exile by the river Chebar' —: for the prophet's way of life was separate, and he was offended by the sight of the wicked. Moses also did this same thing, pitching his tabernacle far from the camp of the multitude: and when he had entered it, the pillar of fire came down and stood before the door of the tabernacle.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.3.5-6":["For you are not sent to a people of deep speech and of a difficult tongue, to the house of Israel; nor to many peoples of unknown speech, whose speech you cannot hear: and if I were to send you to them, they would hear you. 'Since they will not come to you, go on to them: for not indeed do the healthy have need of a physician, but those who are ill; nor can you say: I cannot speak to them because they are of another tongue, and we are barbarians to one another, nor can we hear each other's speech. Why should I speak of one people? If I were to send you to diverse nations, still my authority and power would overcome every difficulty. And would that the time were at hand when I am to send to all the nations, when I am also to give the graces of tongues, so that my apostles may preach and may bring the whole world, out of its diversity of tongues, under my yoke by one faith; more easily would those hear who are of deep and lofty speech, who have nothing of Jewish levity but walk with a grave and solid foot, and who, though their tongues are unknown, are of known faith'; whence it follows: And if I were to send you to them, they would hear you. And in fact he afterward sent, and all alike believed; whence Paul and Barnabas say to the Jews: To you indeed the word had to be preached, but because you would not receive the word, behold, we turn to the Gentiles; and of the centurion it is said: Not even in Israel have I found such faith.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.3.7":["But the house of Israel will not hear you, because they will not hear me. For all the house of Israel is of a brazen forehead and a hard heart. 'Do not be saddened', he says, 'if they will not hear you. For this reason they will not hear you: because they will scorn to hear me', according to what the Savior said: He who does not receive you does not receive me; in which free will is openly shown. And two causes are given why they do not hear: 'Because they are', he says, 'of perverse will, and of a brazen forehead' — or, as the Seventy rendered, 'they are contentious' —, and it is understood that, though often called to salvation, they would not hear.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.3.8-9":["Behold, I have made your face stronger than their faces, and your forehead harder than their foreheads; like adamant and like flint I have made your face. 'The house of Israel is of a brazen forehead and of impudent audacity, and so hard of heart that it is compared to scorpions. Therefore I have given you a very hard countenance and a forehead that is overcome by no shame'. From this we learn that it is at times of the grace of God to resist impudence and, when the occasion demands, to crush forehead with forehead. And this is granted lest our modesty and human shame should be terrified at the snares of rivals.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.3.10":["Son of man, all my words which I speak to you, take into your heart and hear with your ears. This is the food of the scroll, and these are the words in which, according to the diversity of the hearers, we speak to men either laments or song or woe. And yet it must be noted that the words of God must first be stored in our heart, and heard and understood with great care, and so brought forth to the people.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.3.11":["Go, enter to the exile (or captivity), to the sons of your people. And our Lord came to the people of the Jews, the Father sending him, to preach release to the captives, and that there might be fulfilled in him what is written: Ascending on high he led captivity captive, he gave gifts among men.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.3.12":["And the spirit took me up, and I heard behind me a voice of great commotion: Blessed be the glory of the Lord from his place. God, of whom it is written: The Lord taking up the meek, himself took up the prophet too, who was not in the flesh but in the spirit and, walking in the spirit, did not fulfill the works of the flesh, for the Spirit of God was in him; for which reason, despising earthly things, he is also caught up by the spirit and hears behind him a voice of great commotion, forgetting the things that are past and stretching himself toward what is to come, so that he might leave the snares of his enemies behind his back — something of this kind we also read in Exodus, when Pharaoh and all his army were pursuing Israel, and the angel in the pillar of cloud left the front of the camp and came behind their backs and stood all night between Israel and the Egyptians, so that, the adversaries being terrified, Israel might hear: Blessed be the glory of the Lord from his place; and the place of the Lord is every one in whom he finds lodging, or certainly the Son is the place of the Father, just as the Father too is the place of the Son, as the Lord Savior says: I in the Father, and the Father in me; Micah too signifies this same thing: Behold, the Lord will go forth from his place, and will come down upon the high places of the earth; and the mountains will be shaken beneath him, and the valleys will melt like wax before the fire, and like water that is carried down a steep place: for when the Lord Savior came down and set out from the Father, the high places of the earth and the mountains were shaken, and the lower parts of the valleys were melted; the place of the glory of the Lord can also be the inaccessible light of which the Apostle speaks: Who alone has immortality, and dwells in light inaccessible —. When we turn over in our mind the past judgments of the Lord, we hear behind our back the voice of great commotion; when we meditate in our heart on the things to come, a voice is heard from what lies before us and from those things that are ahead.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.3.13":["And the voice of the wings of the living creatures striking one against another, and the voice of the wheels following the living creatures, and the voice of great commotion. (Whereas according to the Hebrew there is understood ἀπὸ κοινοῦ [in common, supplied from the preceding clause]: I heard behind me a voice of great commotion, and 'I heard' the voice of the wings of the living beings, and the voice of the wheels, the Seventy added: And 'I saw' the voice of the wings of the living beings, the voice of them striking one against another, and the rest, in keeping with what is read in Exodus: And all the people saw the voice of God, so that the prophet heard the voice that came behind his back, but saw the one that was before him.) But it is better and truer to have understood the voice as heard rather than seen, both that of the wings striking one another and that of the wheels, of which we spoke above, and that of the great commotion, which showed that the wrath of God was to come upon the people of Israel.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.3.14-15":["For the hand of the Lord was with me, strengthening me. And I came to the exile, to the heap of new grain, to those who dwell by the river Chebar; and I sat where they were sitting, and I remained seven days mourning in the midst of them. (LXX: \"And the hand of the Lord came upon me strong. And I entered to the captivity aloft; and I went around those dwelling by the river Chebar who were there, and I sat there seven days dwelling in the midst of them.\") Where we translated 'heap of new grain', Aquila, Symmachus and Theodotion set down the Hebrew words themselves, 'thel abib', which the Seventy translated 'aloft, and I went around', believing it better to say something than to set down what would raise a question for the reader. But we have learned from the Hebrews that 'thel abib' signifies this: when new wheat or barley is gathered, and after famine and want, before it is threshed on the floor, it promises some hope of food; so too Israel, small and slender, dwelling on the banks of the river Chebar, as if reviving and sprung up from the earth, promised a seed-sowing of the Jewish people. And the hand of the Lord comes upon the prophet, strengthening him, so that he may receive the name of watchman and teach what he has learned. And first for seven days he moves among them, seeing all that they did, so that afterward he might know what to rebuke. And he mourns, or dwells, in the midst of them, seeing their crimes and the righteous sentence of God for the iniquity of their sins — where we said 'mourning' and in the Hebrew it is written 'masmim', Theodotion translated 'marveling', to express the stupor of the prophet as he beheld their iniquities; but the second edition of Aquila, which the Hebrews call κατὰ ἀκρίβειαν [according to exactness], translated ἠρεμάζων, that is 'resting' and 'set apart', to show that he was indeed in the midst of the captives, but separated from them in mind —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.3.14":["The spirit also lifted me and took me up, and I went away bitter in the indignation of my spirit. (LXX: \"And the spirit raised and received me, and I went away aloft in the rush of my spirit.\") After he was raised by the spirit and taken up by it, then in indignation and in the bitterness of his own spirit he went away whither he was being borne, understanding the indignation of God and turning over in his mind what the vision signified; or else, aloft, he was borne by the very rush of his own spirit, desiring to go where the Lord commanded — but let us understand his spirit not as the Holy Spirit but as the spirit of a man, of which it is spoken in many places, and in Isaiah: By night my spirit rises to you, O God —. And the prophet is carried over, not, as some think, in spirit, but in the body itself, as we also read of Habakkuk according to Theodotion.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.3.16-17":["And when the seven days had passed, the word of the Lord came to me, saying: Son of man, I have given you as a watchman to the house of Israel, and you shall hear the word from my mouth, and you shall announce it to them from me. He who is to be a watchman and is to tell the words of God to the people ought to be still for a long time, and to grieve at the things he sees, and to have in his conscience nothing of those things which he is going to rebuke in others.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.3.18-19":["If, when I say to the wicked: You shall die the death, you do not announce it to him nor speak so that he may turn from his wicked way and live, that wicked man shall die in his iniquity, but his blood I will require from your hand. But if you announce it to the wicked, and he is not converted from his iniquity and from his wicked way, he indeed shall die in his iniquity, but you shall deliver your soul. There are two 'wicked', or 'unjust' as the Seventy translated: one who hears nothing from the watchman and dies in his wickedness, whose blood is sought from the watchman's hands; the other, to whom the watchman announces it, and who, scorning to hear, dies by his own fault, in such a way only that the watchman is free from blame. From this we understand that the Lord threatens the wicked and says: You shall die the death for this reason, that he may turn from his wicked way and live; for the threat is not against men but against sins, nor against those who turn from their vices but against those who remain in sin. And it is a great peril to keep silent the words of God, for a threefold cause: either from fear or from sloth or from flattery; whence Isaiah too: Woe, he says, is me, wretched, because I was silent. And what follows: But you have delivered your soul, echoes that saying of the Apostle: If anyone's work burns, he will suffer loss; but he himself will be saved, yet so as through fire, so that it may test whether the watchman is outside the cause of the death of him who perished, or guilty of it; for the work of the teacher is the salvation of the disciple.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.3.20-21":["But also if the righteous, turning from his righteousness, does iniquity, I will set a stumbling block before him. He shall die because you did not announce it to him: in his sin he shall die, and his righteous deeds which he did shall not be in remembrance; but his blood I will require from your hand. But if you announce it to the righteous man that the righteous man should not sin, and he does not sin, living he shall live because you announced it to him, and you have delivered your soul. Just as we read of two wicked or unjust men: one who did not hear and perished, and the other who heard and persevered in evil, so there are two righteous: one who did not hear and perished, the other who heard and was converted to repentance and saved his soul. And it is to be noted that the righteous can fall and, if he has a teacher toward better things, may draw back his foot; and the wicked or unjust man indeed, if he is not converted, will die in his wickedness and iniquity. But the righteous, if he commits wickedness and sin, does not die at once, but a 'stumbling block' is set in his sight, or 'torment', and as Theodotion said 'infirmity', so that he may be tormented and not find a level way, and may understand that he is sick; of such the Apostle also says: Therefore many among you are infirm and many sleep; for it profits the righteous to understand his offense through the experience of torments, and to say with the psalmist: I was turned in my misery while the thorn is fastened in me. And as his iniquities do not stand in the way of the wicked if he turns from his wicked way and lives, so his old righteous deeds do not profit the righteous if he is overwhelmed by new crimes. And as for what he added: He shall die, because you did not announce it to him, it is understood that he could have lived if the watchman and teacher had taught him.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.3.22":["And the hand of the Lord was there upon me, and he said to me: Arise, go out into the plain, and there I will speak with you. What we translated from the Hebrew as 'there', which is not found in the Seventy, is set down with significance, and there is no doubt that it signifies the place; for above we read: And I came to the exile, to the heap of new grain, to those who dwell by the river Chebar. And rightly he who had said: I sat, is commanded to rise; and he who had entered to the exile hears: Go out, not into rugged valleys, not onto sheer crags, but into the breadth of the plain, which can contain the glory of the Lord — whence it is also said to the Corinthians: Be enlarged —. When he goes in to the captives, of whom it was said that they rage and persecute him, he needs a strong hand so that he can resist and endure the fury of his persecutors; but when he goes out into the plain, the 'hand' of the Lord does come upon him, because without his help he could not go out, but 'strong' is not added: for he goes out to see the glory of God, not to fight. And this must be observed, that the prophet, while sitting himself in the midst of the captives who were sitting, did not see the glory of the Lord.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.3.23-24":["And I fell on my face. And the spirit entered into me and set me upon my feet, and spoke, and said to me: Go in and be shut up in the midst of your house. Not able to bear the standing glory of the Lord, he fell on his face, so that he might be raised up by the spirit entering into him. This spirit set him upon his feet and spoke, saying: Enter and be shut up in the midst of your house; and the sense is: 'Because you have been strengthened by the sight of the Lord's majesty, fear no one and dread no one, but return into your house — either for the needs of the body, as some think, or as a sign of the siege to come — and, just as Isaiah, barefoot and naked, announced the three years' captivity and nakedness of the people, so you too, shut up in your house, prophesy by the very deed the impending siege of the city of Jerusalem'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.3.23":["And I arose and went out into the plain, and behold, there the glory of the Lord was standing, like the glory which I had seen by the river Chebar. At the Lord's command he goes in and at his command he goes out, and he sat with those who were sitting, because the number of the captives could not hear him while he stood. And having gone out into the breadth of the plain, he saw the glory of the Lord standing, which before he had seen walking, and lifting itself up, and at times standing — for it was by the river of the world, 'Chobar' [Chebar], which means 'most heavy', that the glory was seen, which signifies that all the glory of this world flows past and has no stable footing; but the glory of the Lord, standing and abiding, is seen in the plain with the prophet standing, the glory which in the midst of the captives could neither stand nor be seen —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.3.25":["And you, son of man, behold, bonds have been put upon you, and they shall bind you with them, and you shall not go out from their midst; and I will make your tongue cleave to your palate (or throat), and you shall be dumb and not as a man who rebukes, because it is a provoking house. That he is shut up in his house, that he is tied in bonds, that he does not go out to those among whom he dwells, but amid a multitude of captives suffers the solitude of a prison, is, as I said above, a sign of the siege, which, when Jerusalem is shut up and surrounded by the army of the Chaldeans, permits none of the inhabitants to go out; this is the pot of which Jeremiah speaks, and this prophet himself in what follows, in which the flesh of the people is consumed.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.3.26":["But as for the prophet's tongue cleaving to his palate or throat, and his being made dumb and not having the authority of one who rebukes, the cause is plain: Because it is, he says, a provoking house; and the sense is: So great are their bitternesses and so great their contentions against God that they do not deserve to hear one who rebukes them. From this it is plain that, where there is a multitude of sins, the sinners are unworthy to be corrected by the Lord.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.3.27":["But when I have spoken to you, I will open your mouth, and you shall say to them: Thus says the Lord God: He who hears, let him hear, and he who refrains, let him refrain, because it is a provoking house. What we read in Ecclesiastes: A time to keep silent and a time to speak, is borne out in the prophetic word as well: that it belongs to the same wisdom both to keep silent and to speak according to the time, and to give fellow servants their rations in due season; whence Isaiah too says to the unbelieving people: 'I have kept silent; shall I keep silent forever? says the Lord'. He, therefore, who on account of the multitude of sins long kept his mouth shut, because he saw that some could be converted, of whom it is said: He who hears, let him hear, and he who from evil refrains, let him refrain and cease, for this reason, with his mouth opened, and not by his own will but by the Lord's command, speaks to the people. Now that which we have set down: He who hears, let him hear, and he who refrains, let him refrain, for which the Seventy translated: He who hears, let him hear, and he who is unbelieving, let him be unbelieving, the second edition of Aquila translated thus: He who hears shall be heard, and he who leaves shall be left; and the sense is: He who has, to him it shall be given; but he who has not, even what he seems to have shall be taken away from him.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.4.1-2":["And you, son of man, take for yourself a brick, and you shall set it before you, and you shall portray on it the city of Jerusalem. And you shall set in order a siege against it, and you shall build siege-works and heap up a mound, and you shall set camps against it and place battering rams round about. Just as we said before — Go in and be shut up in the midst of your house, and: Behold, bonds have been put upon you, and they will bind you, and you will not go out — that the prophet is a type of the coming siege of the city of Jerusalem, so now he is commanded to portray it on a brick by the geometric art, and the brick itself is to be placed in the prophet's sight, so that, after he has portrayed Jerusalem in the dust, he may draw the whole siege against it, sketching the siege-works and the heaped-up mounds and the ring of the besieging army and the battering rams round about, all the things by which cities are usually taken. Siege-works is the name for the works by which a city is shut in, so that none of the besieged can escape; heaped-up mounds, those by which the rampart and the ditches are filled; camps, the guard-posts of soldiers all around; battering rams, those by which the foundations of walls are shaken and the joining of the stones is loosened. This is said so that the approaching captivity of the city of Jerusalem under King Zedekiah may be signified, in whose eleventh year both the king and the city were taken. For 'brick', which in Greek is called πλίνθος, in the feminine gender, Symmachus translated more clearly πλινθίον, which we can call a 'little brick' and an 'abacus'; in its dust geometers are accustomed to draw γραμμάς, that is, 'lines' and 'radii'. From this some hold that it is not absurd to have knowledge of this discipline as well, recalling those examples: that Joshua son of Nun sent spies to describe the land, which is properly called geometry; and that the angel in Zechariah had a geometer's measuring line for measuring Jerusalem. And as for the prophet now being commanded to portray Jerusalem in the dust — which among them is properly called σκηνογραφία [scenography, drawing in perspective] —, we can also take the brick as a reproach to Israel, because it served Pharaoh in clay and brick; or as meaning that the city which they thought firm and impregnable is compared to a most fragile brick, which at the touch of water dissolves at once, so that she of whom it was said before: Is the hardest rock (or of the desert) the mountain of the daughter of Zion?, may be turned into a brick to be ruined by the Babylonian flood, according to what is written: Therefore the Lord will bring upon you waters many and strong, the king of the Assyrians.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.4.3":["And you, take for yourself an iron pan, and you shall set it as an iron wall between you and the city, and you shall set your face firmly toward it, and it shall be in siege, and you shall surround it: it is a sign to the house of Israel. What we said above, that both the prophet himself and the portrayal on the brick — surrounded by siege-works, mound and battering rams — go before as a sign of Jerusalem's coming siege, is now said more clearly, after many things in between: It is a sign to the house of Israel. The iron pan, which is set like a wall between the prophet and the city, shows the great wrath of God, which is worn down by no prayers and is not bent to mercy: for just as iron subdues all metals and nothing is harder than it, so the incredible crimes of Jerusalem by their own fault made God, who is gentle by nature, most hard. The pan is also said to be a wall set in the middle between the people and God, to show that the whole multitude is shortly to be fried and reduced to nothing. And the setting firm of the face against the city is a sign of severity, according to what we read written elsewhere: The face of the Lord is upon those who do evil, to destroy their memory from the earth.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.4.4-6":["And you shall sleep on your left side, and you shall place the iniquities of the house of Israel upon it by the number of the days in which you sleep upon it, and you shall take up their iniquity. But I have given you the years of their iniquity by the number of days, three hundred and ninety days, and you shall bear the iniquity of the house of Israel. And when you have completed these, you shall sleep on your right side a second time, and you shall take up the iniquity of the house of Judah for forty days; a day for a year, a day, I say, for a year I have given you. We ask what the three hundred and ninety years are, which are reckoned in place of as many days, during which the prophet slept on his left side, bound and constrained so that he would not move onto his other side, showing the captivity and miseries of the ten tribes, that is, of Israel; and what the other forty years are, during which he lay on his right side for Judah and its iniquities, or, as Holy Scripture relates, slept.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","— Just as, therefore, in Israel, that is, in the ten tribes, from Pekah king of Israel, under whom Shalmaneser laid waste much of the Israelite people, to the fortieth year of Ahasuerus, when the persecution of the whole Jewish people was eased, we reckon three hundred and ninety years, so from the first year of Jeconiah, when a great part of Jerusalem was carried over to Babylon, to the first year of Cyrus king of the Persians, which was the thirtieth year of the temple's desolation, forty years are reckoned; under him the captivity of the Jews was relaxed and liberty given back to the people. — Some, joining the three hundred and ninety years of Israel and the forty of Judah, make four hundred and thirty years, and hold that they are fulfilled from the baptism of the Savior to the consummation of the world. Others, however, and above all the Jews, want the four hundred and thirty years, during which the people is set in tribulation and distress and under the yoke of captivity, to be reckoned from the second year of Vespasian, when Jerusalem was taken by the Romans and the temple overthrown; and that then the people will return to its former state, so that, as the sons of Israel were in Egypt four hundred and thirty years, so the last captivity too may be ended in the same number; and that it is written in Exodus: Now the dwelling of the sons of Israel, in which they dwelt in the land of Egypt, was four hundred and thirty years, and again: It came to pass after four hundred and thirty years, all the army of the Lord went out by night.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","But if some contentious interpreter will not accept what we have said, but understands that the harlot named Gomer, daughter of Diblaim, bore the first and the third as males and the second, the middle one, as a female, wanting the Scripture to mean just what is read, let him answer how he explains that passage in Ezekiel where he is commanded by the Lord to bear the iniquities of the house of Israel, that is, of the ten tribes, and for three hundred and ninety days to sleep continually on one side, the left, although in the LXX one hundred and ninety are written, and to sleep so that he never wakes nor changes his side, unless perhaps, sated with sleep, he opened his eyes a little to take the most filthy food of ash-baked bread, made of wheat and barley, and bean and lentil and millet, and baked on human dung. For the nature of things does not allow that any man should sleep always on one side for three hundred and ninety days. And again he says: You shall take up the iniquities of the house of Judah, and for forty days you shall sleep on the right side. These days, however, will be reckoned as years, during which Israel and Judah will be pressed down by siege and a very long captivity, so that, conquered and motionless, they cannot turn themselves from one side to the other. If he cannot prove that these things, and others like them which we read in the holy Scriptures, were done, but contends that they signify something else, then this harlot woman too, and the other woman, the adulteress, who were either joined to the prophet or kept by the prophet, indicate not a shameful union of lust but the mysteries of things to come.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"EZK.4.5":["— Concerning Israel this must be said: that under King Pekah son of Remaliah, who reigned in Israel twenty years, Tiglath-pileser king of Assyria came, and took Ijon, and Abel house of Maacah, and Janoah, and Kedesh, Hazor, and Gilead, the whole land of Naphtali; and he carried them over to the Assyrians. After him Hoshea son of Elah reigned in Israel nine years, and he was taken with all Samaria by Shalmaneser king of the Assyrians, and carried over to Halah and Habor, the rivers of Gozan, in the cities of the Medes. Now Hoshea was taken in the sixth year of King Hezekiah, as the holy history of Kings relates; and from him, if we reckon in order, we shall be able to find in this way for how many years Israel was in distress and pressed by the yoke of captivity. From the sixth year to the twenty-ninth year — for that many years Hezekiah reigned — twenty-four years are reckoned. Manasseh succeeded him and reigned fifty-five years. After him Amon reigned two years. After him Josiah, thirty-two years. Jehoiakim, who was also surnamed Eliakim, succeeded him, and reigned eleven years. After him Jehoiachin, surnamed Jeconiah, who was at once led into captivity; and in his place Zedekiah reigned eleven years, under whom Jerusalem was taken and the temple destroyed. So from the first captivity of Israel, which took place under King Pekah, to the eleventh year of Zedekiah, in which the temple was laid waste, there are one hundred and sixty-four years. From the second, when Hoshea was taken and all Samaria overthrown, one hundred and thirty-five years. And there were seventy years of the temple's desolation, which, added to the first captivity, make two hundred and thirty-four years. For in the second year of Darius king of the Persians the temple was built by Zerubbabel son of Shealtiel and Jeshua son of Jozadak, while Haggai and Zechariah prophesied; Darius reigned thirty-six years, and, one being taken away, his thirty-five years are added. After him Xerxes son of Darius reigned twenty years. After him Artabanus, seven months. And Xerxes, who was surnamed Μακρόχειρ [Long-hand], forty years. After him Xerxes, two months, and Sogdianus, seven months. Darius, surnamed Nothus, succeeded him, and reigned nineteen years. After him Artaxerxes, surnamed Memor, son of Darius and Parysatis, reigned forty years; he is called Ahasuerus by the Hebrews, and under him is related the history of Mordecai and Esther, when the whole people of the Jews, freed from the danger of death, recovered its former liberty. And from the second year of Darius to the last year of Ahasuerus there are one hundred and fifty-five years and four months, which, added to the two hundred and thirty-four years above, make three hundred and eighty-nine years and four months.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","— And I greatly wonder why the common copies have 'one hundred and ninety years', and in some it is written 'one hundred and fifty', when plainly both the Hebrews and Aquila, Symmachus and Theodotion hold 'three hundred and ninety years', and among the Seventy themselves — and yet they have not been corrupted by the fault of copyists — the same number is found. A most difficult question, and, not to speak proudly, one explained by no one, we believe we have expounded not so much by our own knowledge as by the grace of the Lord, that being fulfilled which he himself promised: Seek and you will find, ask and you will receive, knock and it will be opened to you. — But whoever wishes to reckon from the first captivity, which took place under Menahem son of Gadi king of Israel, who reigned in Samaria ten years, when Pul king of the Assyrians came into the land of Israel and received a thousand talents of silver, and after him the other two years of Pekah that he reigned in Israel, will find the three hundred and ninety years completed in the twenty-eighth year of King Ahasuerus, when the history of Esther is said to have taken place; and this is also more credible, for it was not when the reign of Ahasuerus was ended, but while he was still reigning, that Israel cast off the yoke of the heaviest servitude.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.4.6":["— The sleeping on the right side, that is, of the forty years, is easily reckoned. For after Eliakim, who was surnamed Jehoiakim, his son Jehoiachin, surnamed Jeconiah, reigned three months. While he was reigning, the servants of Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon went up to Jerusalem, and the city was surrounded with siege-works. And Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon came to the city, while his servants were attacking it. And Jehoiachin king of Judah went out to the king of Babylon, he and his mother and his servants and his prince and his eunuchs, and the king of Babylon received him in the eighth year of his reign. And he carried out from there all the treasures of the house of the Lord and the treasures of the royal house. And shortly after: And he carried over all Jerusalem and all the princes and all the mighty men of the army, ten thousand, into captivity, and every craftsman and smith; and nothing was left, except the poor of the people of the land. When he had been taken, with a countless multitude of the people and all the wealth of Jerusalem, Zedekiah reigned eleven years, under whom the city was taken and the temple destroyed; and its desolation lasted until the second year of Darius, for seventy years. Now in the thirtieth year of the temple's desolation, Cyrus reigned among the Persians, having overthrown Astyages king of the Medes; and he, according to the prophecy of Isaiah, sent back to Jerusalem about fifty thousand men of the tribe of Judah, and the vessels of the temple which Nebuchadnezzar had carried off, and the rest that the history of Ezra relates.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.4.7":["And toward the siege of Jerusalem you shall turn your face, and your arm shall be bared, and you shall prophesy against it. (LXX: \"And in the shutting-in of Jerusalem you shall prepare your face, and you shall strengthen your arm, and you shall prophesy upon it.\") There is need of a preparing of the face, and of the strength and firmness of an arm bared and stripped, so that the siege of the city may be shown not only by the voice but also by the gesture and bearing of the one prophesying.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.4.8":["Behold, I have surrounded you with bonds, and you shall not turn from your side to the other side, until you complete the days of your siege (or shutting-in). The left parts, because they had been without the temple and the knowledge of God in Samaria, are assigned to Israel; the right, to Judah, in which were the worship and religion of God; and it must be noted that on the one is the punishment of sinners, on the other the exercise of virtue. Nor does he turn from one side to the other, so that no respite from torments is indicated, until the complete conclusion of the aforesaid days is fulfilled. What a day is for the prophet, a year is for those who suffer; and the years Laban counted were to Jacob as a few days. Not only is there variety in punishments according to the difference of merits, but in the recompense of the good as well the lambs stand on the right and the goats on the left; hence also in another place it is written: The heart of the wise is in his right hand, but the heart of the fool in his left. The bonds of the Lord, by which we are bound unto salvation, are one thing; those of the devil, by which he had bound the woman in the Gospel for eighteen years, are another; hence also: Each one is bound fast by the cords of his own sins, cords which the Lord loosed through the type of Lazarus raised up, who had lain in the tomb bound with bands and wrappings.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.4.9-10":["Now the prophet is commanded to show by deed the coming famine and want of the people of Israel: for just as in a scarcity of all things no one looks for varieties and delicacies of food, but for how the belly may be filled, so now the prophet puts wheat and barley, beans, lentils and millet and oats into one vessel, and makes three hundred and ninety loaves to eat one each day; and these loaves weigh twenty shekels each — that is, staters; and a shekel, that is, a stater, has four drachmas; and eight drachmas make a Latin ounce —, so that one loaf is said to weigh ten ounces, by which life is dragged out rather than sustained.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.4.9":["And you, take for yourself wheat and barley and beans and lentils and millet and vetch, and you shall put them in one vessel, and you shall make yourself loaves by the number of the days in which you sleep on your side: for three hundred and ninety days you shall eat it. And your food, which you eat, shall be by weight twenty staters a day; from time to time you shall eat it. And you shall drink water by measure, the sixth part of a 'hin'; from time to time you shall drink it. And you shall eat it like a cake baked under the ashes, and you shall cover it with human dung before their eyes. For what we have translated 'vetch', for which in the Hebrew is said 'chasamim', the Seventy and Theodotion put ὄλυραν, which some think is 'oats' and others 'rye'; but the first edition of Aquila and Symmachus translated 'zeas' or 'zeias', which we call either 'far' [emmer] or, in the native speech of Italy and Pannonia, 'spica' [ear of grain] and 'spelta' [spelt]; to 'in a vessel' the Seventy also added 'of earthenware'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","It must also be noted that, according to the number of the days of the left side, three hundred and ninety loaves baked under the ashes are ordered to be made and eaten one each day, while about loaves for the forty days of the right side there is complete silence, so that Holy Scripture may signify in a hidden way that the punishment is not the same for a sinning people, provided it still has the knowledge of God, and for one that has wholly departed from the religion of the true God. We can also say this, according to the spiritual understanding: that the Jewish people, since its offense against God, eats to this day loaves baked under the ashes, mixed of wheat, barley, beans, lentils, millet and oats, of which the one is the food of men, the other of beasts of burden: for in that they seem to believe in one God, they have something of wheat too; but in that they deny the Son, they feed on the barley of beasts of burden and on beans, by which the belly of those who eat them is puffed up and the mind is said to be weighed down, so much so that it is a food abhorred even by the Pythagoreans. For lentils Esau lost his birthright; millet is the food of rustics and countrymen and of fattened fowl; on oats, or vetch and olyra, brute animals are fed.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.4.10-11":["The scantiness of the food is increased by the scantiness of the water: for he is commanded to drink each day the sixth part of the Hebrew measure called 'hin' — now a 'hin' makes two Attic 'chois' [choes, an Attic liquid measure], which we can call two Italian 'sextarii', so that the 'hin' is the measure of the Jewish sextarius and of our military one, whose sixth part makes a third of an Italian sextarius —; and this food and drink, in the words of the renowned orator, gives no strength, but keeps off death. As for his saying: From time to time you shall eat (or drink) it, it means from evening to evening, although some wrongly think it is to be understood from year to year.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.4.12":["And the loaves themselves are made like a barley loaf baked under the ashes; and he is told to cover them with human dung, not with those who are to eat them unaware — for in such a situation ignorance usually tempers miseries — but with them seeing, and before their eyes, so that one may endure the horror of the sight before the nausea of the taste.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","And that it is written, according to the Seventy, that Elijah, fleeing Jezebel, found a ὀλυρίτην [made of olyra] loaf, 'baked under the ashes', is a sign of persecution and want; hence he is also fed by ravens, and, because he had found no food in Israel, he was fed with the nourishment of the nations, which were unclean. The Law itself too, which they read and do not understand, is a loaf baked under the ashes, and it is covered with human dung; for which reason Paul the Apostle too says that he counted the gains of the Law and the observance of the former ceremonies as dung, that he might gain Christ.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.4.13":["And the Lord said: Thus shall the sons of Israel eat their bread polluted among the nations to which I will cast them out. And I said: Ah, ah, ah, Lord God, behold, my soul is not polluted, and what died of itself and what was torn by beasts I have not eaten from my infancy until now, and no unclean flesh has entered my mouth. And he said to me: Behold, I have given you the dung of oxen instead of human excrement, and you shall make your bread in it. That the prophets, according to Zechariah, are men of portent, who portend things to come by their deeds, and of whom God says: In the hands of the prophets I have been likened, we read very often in many places. Just as, therefore, Ezekiel eats bread in dung, so the sons of Israel too, or the whole people of the Jews, or, as others think, the ten tribes, will eat polluted bread among the nations; although the threat is not addressed to those who have already been cast out, but to those who were still to be cast out of the land of promise.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.4.14":["When the prophet had learned this, according to Aquila he expressed his abhorrence three times: 'Ah, ah, ah', but according to Symmachus and the Seventy he answered: Μηδαμῶς, which in Latin is said 'By no means', for which Theodotion translated: 'O Lord God'; lest we should think that he is contradicting the Lord's command, rather than giving reasons, or rather pleading, why he cannot do this; and in the end he obtains what he asked, and the severity of the sentence was tempered by a milder command. It is asked why Ezekiel refused easier things, while Hosea was at once joined to a harlot and did not object, or rather reply, that he had a chaste body and ought not to be stained by union with a prostitute, as the Apostle says: He who cleaves to a prostitute becomes one body with her; from this it is shown that it was a figure of the Synagogue or of the Church, not truly carried out according to the letter, as we discussed more fully when expounding that prophet himself. 'What died of itself' is the name for that which loses its life without the shedding of blood, and in which the soul dies; 'torn by beasts', for what in Greek is called θηριάλωτον.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.4.15":["And that the dung of oxen is granted in place of human dung signifies lighter evils, since he is indeed commanded to eat bread baked in ox dung, but to be far from the uncleanness of human dung; and to this day this sentence is kept among the people of the Jews: they do not eat their bread in human dung — for they neither serve idols nor worship the various monstrous forms of demons — but in ox dung, since they do everything for the sake of the flesh and the belly and the good things of this earth, according to what is said: He who does them shall live in them. But we who despise earthly things not only trample on the foods of human dung and count earthly delights as nothing, but eat the bread which came down from heaven and enjoy that food of which the psalmist sings: Man ate the bread of angels, living not at all on Egyptian meats but on the fineness of manna.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.4.16-17":["What he had shown by deed was to come, he also declares by word; and the whole sleeping on the left and the right side, and the bread baked under the ashes and mixed from a variety of six kinds, signifying the evils of the world, tends to this: to proclaim the want of food in Jerusalem and an incredible scarcity of water, so that each one collapses upon his brother, hoping from another for the help that he does not see to be in himself — for it is the nature of men that, when evils press and the weight of distress, they put their confidence in their neighbor rather than in themselves —, and they waste away in their iniquities, while they suffer all things for their iniquities. And I fear that this breaking of bread may be found in our Jerusalem too, in which the vision of peace is discerned: bread which the Lord breaks when he is angry and judges us unworthy of his bread; and would that we might at least deserve to receive it by weight and in anxiety, and that the tip of Lazarus's finger might moisten our tongue, parched with excessive dryness. But when the bread and water of the Church fail, a man will collapse against his brother, and everywhere there is discord, as we tear the tunic of Christ which not even the soldiers dared to tear at the passion of the Savior, and waste away in our iniquities, while we do not have the righteousness of God.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.4.16":["And he said to me: Son of man, behold, I will break the staff of bread in Jerusalem, and they shall eat bread by weight and in anxiety, and they shall drink water by measure and in distress, so that, bread and water failing, each one may collapse upon his brother, and they may waste away in their iniquities. The Hebrew word 'mate' the first edition of Aquila translated 'staff', while the second, and Symmachus and Theodotion, translated στήριγμα, that is, 'support'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","It is written in Jeremiah that the little ones, that is, the common people of the Church, asked for bread, and there was none to break it for them; but Paul, who was a man and knew that Christ had broken the loaves of the Law and given them to the disciples to be distributed, speaks confidently. And those who need the milk of infancy and are not able to take solid food cannot eat the staff or strength of bread; and nothing so strengthens the mind of the one who eats as the bread of life, of which it is written: And bread strengthens the heart of man.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.5.1-4":["Moreover, just as in the hair of the head and the beard there is a token of beauty and manhood, and if they are shaved off a shameful nakedness appears, and the outermost and, so to speak, dead part of the whole body is in the hair of the head and the hairs of the beard, so Jerusalem and its people, dead and separated from the living body of God, is handed over to famine and pestilence, to slaughter and the sword, to captivity and dispersion. From this dispersion, under the figure of the hairs, some part is bound in the height of the cloak, so that a small amount is again handed over to burning, from which an infinite flame and the whole devastation of punishment goes forth into all the house of Israel.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.5.2-4":["And you, son of man, take for yourself a sharp sword like a barbers' razor (or shaving the hairs), and draw it across your head and your beard, and you shall take for yourself a balance of weight and divide them. A third part you shall burn with fire in the midst of the city, according to the completion of the days of the enclosure; and a third part you shall cut down with the sword round about it; but the other third you shall scatter into the wind, and I will bare the sword after them. And you shall take from there a small number, and you shall bind them in the top of the cloak; and from them again you shall take, and cast them into the midst of the fire, and burn them; from it a fire shall go forth into all the house of Israel. Instead of the 'three' parts of the hair of the head and of the beard, of which one is burned in the midst of the city, another is cut down with the sword round about it, and the third is scattered, to be snatched hither and thither by the wind, from which a little is taken and bound in the edge of the cloak, and again some small amount of the third part is cast into the fire, from which a flame goes forth into all the house of Israel, the Seventy translated 'four' parts; and when they had said: A fourth part you shall burn with fire in the midst of the city, and a fourth you shall slay with the sword round about it, and a fourth you shall scatter into the wind, because another fourth part remained to them, they added of their own: And a fourth part you shall take and burn it in the midst of the city, as if it were not the same as the first, and he had said one thing in the first and another in this one which has been added. Finally, in what follows too, the Lord himself explains through the prophet the αἴνιγμα [riddle] of the hairs divided into three parts, saying: A third part of you shall die by pestilence and be consumed by famine in the midst of you, signifying that famine and pestilence are the fire; and a third part of you shall fall by the sword round about you, describing the slaughters and wars outside; but the third part of you, he says, I will scatter into every wind, showing that these are the ones who are to be led into captivity. After these he says that he bares or pours out his sword, so that not even captivity should be the last of evils, and that he takes from those very ones who were scattered and made captive, and binds at the top of his cloak those who are to return from captivity to Jerusalem, and from them too takes some part and consumes it with fire and flame, signifying the Macedonians, under whom the inhabitants of Judea, and especially of Jerusalem, suffered harsh things.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.5.4":["But as to his saying: From it (that is, from the people of the Jews, or according to the Seventy: From her, so that the city of Jerusalem is understood) a fire shall go forth into all the house of Israel, the history of the Maccabees relates it: that a certain part of the Jews gave themselves over to Antiochus Epiphanes and stirred him up to the persecution of the people, and many other things which are written in the same history and in the volumes of Josephus, and especially the dissension of Hyrcanus and Alexander, through the occasion of which Gnaeus Pompey took Jerusalem and subjected it to Roman dominion; and afterwards, under Titus and Vespasian, the city was taken and the temple overthrown, and, after fifty years, under Aelius Hadrian the city was burned down to the ground and destroyed, so that it lost even its former name.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.5.5-6":["She was set, therefore, in the midst of the nations so that, since God was known in Judea, and in Israel his name was great, all the nations round about might follow her examples; but she, following the impiety of the nations placed around her, surpassed even them in her wickedness. Symmachus translated this beautifully: This Jerusalem, which I set in the midst of the nations, and the regions around her, has exchanged my judgments for impieties which she learned from the nations, and my justifications from the regions which are round about her, because they have rejected my ordinances, and in my judgments they have not walked. But that what the Seventy said: My justifications unjustly from the nations, and my ordinances from the regions which are round about her, has no coherence is clear even if I keep silent.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.5.5":["Thus says the Lord God: This is Jerusalem; in the midst of the nations I have set her, and round about her lands; and she despised my judgments, so as to be more impious than the nations, and my precepts more than the lands which are round about her: for they have cast away my judgments, and in my precepts they have not walked. That Jerusalem is situated in the middle of the world this same prophet testifies, showing that she is the navel of the earth; and the psalmist, expressing the nativity of the Lord: Truth, he says, has sprung from the earth; and then, the passion: He has wrought, he says, salvation in the midst of the earth. For on the side of the east she is girded by the region which is called Asia; on the side of the west, by that which is called Europe; on the south and the south wind, by Libya and Africa; on the north, by the Scythians, Armenia and Persia and all the nations of Pontus.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.5.7-9":["'Why,' he says, 'should I complain that you have not walked in my precepts and have not done my judgments, when you have even surpassed all the nations round about in your wickedness, and have not done what they often did by the natural law written in their hearts? Therefore not through angels nor through any ministers, but I myself will execute judgments in you, with all looking on, such as I have not done in you before and will not do again'. And by saying 'judgments' he shows the truth of the sentence, lest his wrath should seem to exceed the measure of the punishments.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.5.7":["Therefore thus says the Lord God: Because you have surpassed the nations that are round about you, you have not walked in my precepts, and have not done my judgments, and have not acted according to the judgments of the nations that are round about you, therefore thus says the Lord God: Behold, I am against you, and I myself will execute judgments in the midst of you in the eyes of the nations. And I will do in you what I have not done, and the like of which I will not do again, because of all your abominations. (In place of what we translated: Because you have surpassed the nations that are round about you, Symmachus translated: Because your multitude was from the nations that are round about you. Aquila's second edition: Because you have been numbered among the nations that are round about you; and further, LXX: \"Because your occasion is from the nations that are round about you\".) And the sense is: either that Jerusalem by her crimes surpassed all the nations round about, or that her multitude is to be called not the people of Israel but a crowd of the other nations, or certainly that they were turned into the number of the other nations which are round about, or that they took every occasion from the nations whom they ought rather to have taught toward the good.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.5.9":["Nor did he do in any nation such things as he did in Jerusalem; and the mighty shall suffer torments mightily; and: The like, he says, I will not do again. He said well: I will not do the like, for after the killing of Christ he was going to do much harsher things: for it is one thing to have worshiped idols at times, for which they had been punished before, and another to have slaughtered the Son of God.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.5.10":["Therefore fathers shall eat sons in the midst of you, and sons shall eat their fathers, and I will execute judgments in you, and I will winnow all your remnants into every wind. To what he had said above: Because you have surpassed the nations that are round about you (or Because your occasion is from the nations that are round about you), you have not walked in my precepts, and have not done my judgments, he has referred 'therefore' three times. First thus: Therefore thus says the Lord God: Behold, I am against you, and I myself will execute judgments in the midst of you; secondly: Therefore fathers shall eat sons in the midst of you; thirdly: Therefore, as I live, says the Lord God, unless because you have violated my holy place with all your offenses, and the rest. Yet it must be noted that where things are seemly and honorable, and in accordance with the merit of those who undergo them, God professes that he does them himself; but where they are sad and not worthy of God, he says indeed that they happen, but not by him, as in the present passage: Therefore fathers shall eat sons in the midst of you, and sons shall eat their fathers; for he did not say: 'I will make fathers eat their sons in the midst of you, and sons eat their fathers'; but what was seemly and did not seem unworthy of the majesty of God, he says he does himself, for there follows: And I will execute judgments in you, and I will winnow all your remnants into every wind. Something of the sort we shall be able to find also in the curses of Deuteronomy, and especially in that passage: Those who glorify me I will glorify; but those who despise me shall be brought to nothing; for those who glorify him he himself glorifies, but those who despise him will not be despised or brought to nothing by the Lord — for this is what would have followed for him to say —, but they will be brought to nothing absolutely, not by God but for their own deserts and sins. We read in the volume of Kings that, under the compulsion of famine, a son was devoured by his mother; Josephus too records many things of this kind done in the siege of Jerusalem; but when fathers ate their sons or sons their fathers, no history relates, unless perhaps it is to be believed that in many evil necessities these things too were done. This very thing can also be referred to our Jerusalem, when masters against disciples, that is, fathers against sons, and disciples against masters, that is, sons against fathers, are stirred up in mutual strife, and that is fulfilled which is said through the Apostle: But if you bite and accuse one another, see that you are not consumed by one another. Hence the prophet too sang in mystical speech: When those who afflict me drew near against me to eat my flesh, and Job says something similar: If indeed my maidservants have said: Who will give us of his flesh, that we may eat?, and concerning masters who pursue gain from their disciples and have no care for their salvation it is said: Who devour my people like a meal of bread, of whom the Apostle too says: And they eat, he says, the houses of widows. We can understand the fathers who ate sons and the sons who ate fathers not only in the Babylonian but also in the Roman siege; what is said: And I will winnow all your remnants into every wind, pertains rather to the Roman captivity, when they were scattered throughout the whole world, and this is the dispersion of the hair of head and beard into every wind.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.5.11":["Therefore, as I live, says the Lord God, unless because you have violated my holy place with all your offenses and with all your abominations, I too will break (or according to the Seventy I will cast you off), and my eye shall not spare, and I will not have pity. This is said properly to Jerusalem: 'Because you have violated my holy things by deserting me, and have worshiped idols in my temple, I too will break all your idols and crush and cut them down, or I will cast you off because you first cast me off, and my eye will not spare when I see you submitting your neck to famine, the sword and slavery, nor will I have pity, because you do not deserve mercy', for it is written: Blessed are the merciful, for they shall obtain mercy. This same thing must be understood in the Church too, that God could daily say to masters and priests who have acted negligently: 'Because you have violated my holy things with all your offenses and with all your abominations which you have committed, I too will crush and break you, and I will repay through myself what you perpetrated against my people. Concerning this the same prophet speaks more fully against the shepherds: that they clothe themselves with the wool of the sheep and feed on the milk and the cheese, and do not seek out the broken and sick flock.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.5.12":["A third part of you shall die by pestilence and be consumed by famine in the midst of you, and a third part of you shall fall by the sword round about you, but the third part of you I will scatter into every wind, and I will unsheathe the sword after them. And I will fulfill my fury and make my indignation rest upon them, and I will be consoled; and they shall know that I the Lord have spoken in my zeal, when I have fulfilled my indignation upon them. (The Seventy, because above they had set down four parts, whereas the prophetic word proved that there are only three, in the ravaging by burning and by sword and by dispersion, so that superfluously the one part of burning was divided into two parts, in this place too divided that same first part into two, so as to say: A fourth part of you shall be worn away by death, and a fourth part of you shall be consumed by famine in the midst of you, and a fourth part of you shall fall round about you, and a fourth part of you I will scatter into every wind; although what we have set down: And a fourth part of you shall fall by the sword has been added from the edition of Theodotion under asterisks.) But it is clear, as the Hebrew truth contains, that there are three parts, of the first of which it is said: And a third part of you shall die by pestilence and be consumed by famine in the midst of you. And it would have been much better to translate what is written than to seek a defense for a thing badly translated; nor do we say that this was done by those to whom antiquity has given authority, but that it was corrupted over many centuries by the fault of copyists and readers, although both Aristeas and Josephus and the whole school of the Jews assert that only the five books of Moses were translated by the Seventy; and since we have spoken of this point above, it seems that it should now be passed over.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.5.13":["This only I will add, which is necessary, regarding what he says: And I will fulfill my fury and make my indignation rest upon them, and I will be consoled; and they shall know that I the Lord have spoken in my zeal, when I have fulfilled my indignation upon them: how the fury and indignation and zeal of God are to be understood, we have often explained, namely that God speaks with human affections, not that he himself is angry, but that we, through punishments and torments, may feel God to be angry. Zeal, moreover, is to be taken under the μετάφορᾳ [metaphor] of a husband and wife: a husband, as long as he loves his wife, is jealous; if he has neglected her, he says what we shall say in what follows: My zeal shall depart from you; and I will no longer be angry with you. And as to what is joined: And they shall know that I the Lord have spoken in my zeal: not those who have been consumed by famine and pestilence, nor those who have fallen by the sword round about the city, but those who will be scattered into every wind will, by the deaths of others and by their own miseries, feel God angry, whom they were unwilling to feel merciful.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.5.14":["And I will give you into a desert, (and what follows:) and into a reproach to the nations which are round about you (is not found in the Seventy, in place of which they added of their own: and your daughters round about you; and again according to both editions:) in the sight of everyone passing by. Now it profits Jerusalem that all the vices in her should be made desolate and reduced to a desert, and that she should recognize her own sins, so that she who ought to have been an example of virtues to the other nations may be an example of miseries. Her daughters, moreover, we can understand as cities or villages, or as the churches scattered throughout the whole world, so that whoever is a pilgrim of this age, and says with the psalmist: I am a sojourner and a pilgrim, as all my fathers, and of whom it is said: Those who passed by did not say: The blessing of the Lord be upon you, may see her reproach and grieve.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.5.15":["And you shall be a reproach and a blasphemy, an example and an astonishment among the nations which are round about you (in place of which in the Seventy is read: And she shall be στενακτὴ — that is, 'worthy of groaning'; and from Theodotion there has been added: — καὶ δηλαϊστὴ, a word of which we have no knowledge; in place of which the three other translators rendered 'blasphemy', which in the Hebrew is called 'geddupha'. And there follows:), when I have executed judgments in you in fury and indignation and in rebukes of wrath, which I the Lord have spoken. That Jerusalem is worthy of groaning when she has offended God, and he has executed judgments in her in fury and indignation, Paul too testifies, who says: We were by nature children of wrath, even as the rest, and again: We who are in this tabernacle groan, being burdened; and we speak with Jerusalem: I will bear the wrath of the Lord, because I have sinned against him. Δηλαϊστὴν or δηλαίαν some wish to be understood as 'unhappy' and 'wretched', others as 'conspicuous' and 'exposed to miseries'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.5.16":["When I send the most evil arrows of famine, which will be deadly, and which I will send to destroy you, and I will gather famine upon you, and I will crush the support (or staff) of bread. That famine and pestilence and most evil beasts and whatever other evils we endure in this world come because of our sins is manifest. Hence also that famine which is recorded to have happened in Ruth, and in the volume of Kings under Elijah, when the heaven was shut for three years and six months, and under Elisha, whose incredible want a sudden abundance made good, was sent by the judgment of God. And this was not only done then in Jerusalem, but is done also in our Jerusalem, in which, because of the greatness of our sins, first a famine of hearing the word of God is sent; then death follows the famine, so that we who did not hear: Taste and see that the Lord is sweet, may afterwards experience: The death of sinners is most evil. Of the support and staff of bread we have spoken above.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.5.17":["And I will send upon you famine and most evil beasts, even to utter destruction, and pestilence and blood shall pass through you, and I will bring the sword upon you; I the Lord have spoken. That most evil beasts are brought upon a land of desolation which has been laid waste by famine, sword and pestilence, the present times also show, when dogs, domestic animals, are roused to madness against the flesh of their masters, and the land is filled with bears and wolves and all other kinds of beasts; and by pestilence and blood passing through it he signifies disease and the sword. But into our Jerusalem too most evil beasts are sent, when we are given over to passions of ignominy and to a reprobate mind and to the consciousness of sins, which torment and tear apart our soul. Dissensions, heresies, schisms, rivalries, envies, sorrows, detractions, many desires, avarice — which is the root of all evils —, these are most evil beasts; and when they are in us, we deserve to hear: 'Your transgression shall chastise you', and we pray and say: Do not deliver to the beasts a soul that confesses to you.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.6.2-3":["And the word of the Lord came to me, saying: Son of man, set your face toward the mountains of Israel, and you shall prophesy to them, and you shall say: Mountains of Israel, hear the word of the Lord God. Thus says the Lord God to the mountains and the hills, to the crags and the valleys. Since 'Adonai' the Lord is often said in this prophet, it seems that it should be briefly explained for the sake of the Greeks and Latins who have no knowledge of the Hebrew language: 'Adonai' is one name among the ten names of God, and it means 'lord', which we often use of men as well — indeed Sarah too, calling Abraham her 'lord', called him by this name; and where it is said: My lord king, 'Adonai' is written — ; when therefore two names, 'lord' and 'Lord', are joined, the first name is common, the second is properly God's, the one which is called ἄρρητον, that is, 'ineffable', and which was written on the golden plate that was on the forehead of the high priest. Now the mountains of Israel, which had been occupied by the images of demons, hear and understand their Creator not with ears but by the command and power of their Maker — just as the sea saw and fled, the Jordan was turned back, surely not with eyes of flesh, which it lacked; and to the sea it is said: Be still, be silent, and the blasts of the winds are commanded, and the worm that struck Jonah's shelter is given its order; and of this same earth it is written: Who looks upon the earth and makes it tremble — ; to them the prophet speaks, indicating that the idols are to be destroyed, and that the altars and all the ceremonies with which they had previously served the idols are to be crushed. We can take the mountains of Israel as both the princes and those who excelled in power and wisdom and knowledge and riches. And this must be noted: that the face is indeed set firm and placed against the mountains, but he speaks by no means to the mountains alone, but also to the hills and to the crags and to the valleys, in which a lower rank and degree of prudence and knowledge is recognized. Tropologically, it seems to me, the mountains are those who have arrived at perfect knowledge; the hills, those who are a little lower; the crags, those who, having no knowledge and trusting only in their manner of life, presume something for themselves concerning the interpretation of the Scriptures, having, according to the Apostle, a zeal for God, but not according to knowledge; and that those are called valleys who are the last in the Church, who, though lower both in life and in knowledge, nevertheless do not withdraw from the gathering of the Lord's household; all of whom are commanded to hear the word of God, so that each one may understand, according to his own small measure and sense, what the Lord commands.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.6.3-4":["But otherwise, upon those also who are lifted up into pride, and of whom the Apostle speaks: Knowledge puffs up, but love builds up, a sword is brought, so that all their images, which they have fashioned from their own mind, may be crushed, and they may lie slain and perceive that they are dead, seeing that their images and fictions profit them nothing.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.6.3-6":["Behold, I will bring a sword upon you, and I will destroy your high places; and I will demolish your altars, and your images shall be broken, and I will cast down your slain before your idols. And I will lay the corpses of the sons of Israel before the face of your images, and I will scatter your bones around your altars, in all your dwellings. It is said to the mountains of Israel that God will destroy their high places, on which men worshiped fashioned gods, and will break the altars and images, and will cause their worshipers to be slain in the sight of the shrines, so that the corpses of the dead may lie where the victims formerly lay, and the bones may be scattered — not of the mountains, but of those who lay on the mountains — round about the altars and in all the cities and villages of Israel. This according to the letter.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.6.5":["And as for his saying that he scatters the bones of the mountains around the altars, he indicates that all the strongest of them are to be separated from one another — for it profits perverse knowledge that the wicked be divided from the wicked — ; and as those who, with a most wicked consensus, were building a tower were, for their own benefit, scattered and their tongues divided, lest the most wicked consensus make them worse, so the bones of the mountains are scattered, that they may understand their vain endeavors. (This which we have put: And I will lay the corpses of the sons of Israel before the face of your images, is not found in the Seventy.)\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.6.6-7":["The cities shall be deserted, and the high places shall be demolished, and your altars shall be scattered and shall perish, and your idols shall be broken and shall cease, and your shrines shall be crushed, and your works shall be blotted out; and the slain shall fall in your midst, and you shall know that I am the Lord. The reason is plain why the cities of the mountains are to be deserted: namely, that the high places and altars may be demolished, and the idols destroyed, and the shrines perish, and all worship of images cease; that the slain may fall in the midst of the mountains, and they may know that he himself is the Lord.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.6.6":["Through these words an occasion is given us rather to follow the spiritual understanding, so that the cities of the heretics, which are interpreted as churches, and the high places of their pride and the altars of their perverse doctrines may be broken, and the idols may perish which they had feigned for themselves out of their own heart, and the shrines may be crushed — for these are the temples which had risen up against the temple of the Lord, and which in the Greek tongue are called τεμένη, that is, 'fanes' and 'shrines' — , and all the works of the mountains may be crushed which are not works of God but of the masters of false knowledge.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.6.7":["And when the slain have fallen in the midst of the mountains — either those whom the mountains themselves had slain, or the men of the Church who had slain them, living wickedly as they were, unto their salvation — then there will be an end of the proud mountains, so that they may know that he himself is the Lord. It may also happen that, out of the one people of the mountains of Israel, when some have been slain, the others come to know: for when the pestilent man is scourged, the fool will be wiser.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.6.8-9":["And I will leave among you those who escape the sword among the nations, when I have scattered you into the lands; and your freed ones shall remember me among the nations to which they have been led captive. That which the Lord says to Elijah: I have left for myself seven thousand men who have not bowed the knee to Baal, and which the apostle Paul writes: And now in this time a remnant according to the election of grace has been saved, can fit this passage. For the apostles too, who were of the seed of Israel and had escaped the sword, were preserved for this reason: that they might be scattered among the nations, and preach the Gospel in the lands, and remember God among the nations in which they were dwelling in the time of captivity. This can also be said: that some from the Jewish captivity were preserved for this reason, that among the nations they might remember his name and proclaim his power. Of the peoples of the heretics also, those who have been able to escape the sword of their perdition and the most wicked faction which had been stirred up against God, scattered hither and thither, will remember the name of God, doing penance, and will exult that they have been taken captive by men of the Church.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.6.9-10":["It profits the heretics too that their heart be crushed, that most evil treasury of perverse doctrines, because it has departed from the Lord, and their eyes, fornicating after their own inventions; and they, when they have remembered the Lord, will be displeased with themselves in all their abominations, and, understanding the truth of the true faith, will know that he himself is the Lord. And for this reason he often brings on tribulation, which seems ruinous and most grievous to those who suffer it: that they may be turned to penance.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.6.9":["Because I have crushed their heart, fornicating and departing from me, and their eyes fornicating after their idols; and they shall be displeased with themselves over the evils which they had done in all their abominations, and they shall know that I the Lord have not spoken in vain, that I would do this evil to them. (Much of this passage has been passed over by the Seventy, and this has been changed: I swore to their fornicating heart, in place of which we have put: I have crushed their heart, fornicating.) Now he swore and decreed that he would do what he showed in deed; or else, he crushed the heart fornicating and departing from God — the heart of those to whom he speaks through Hosea: They have been led astray by a spirit of fornication — , and the eyes fornicating after idols — through whose windows death is said to have entered: for which reason in Daniel too the two elders ordered Susanna to be unveiled, that they might enjoy the beauty of her bared body — .\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.6.10":["Nor has the Lord spoken in vain, that he would do evil or evils, when at his threat those who lay under the sentence do penance; but if the heart is hard and untamable, not perceiving, even through punishments, the benefits of the one who corrects, it is said to them: In vain have I struck your sons; you have not received discipline.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.6.11":["Thus says the Lord God: Strike with your hand and stamp your foot, and say: Alas, for all the abominations of the evils of the house of Israel. The prophet is commanded to show the magnitude of the sins with hands and feet and speech, so that, by the gesture of his body and the lowering of his voice, he may display the bearing of one stunned and amazed and weeping. For just as, whenever we behold something new and astonishing, we shudder in body as much as in mind, so that we clap our hands and strike our feet on the ground and lower our voice to weeping, so now the prophet, discerning with the eyes of the mind the punishments to come, speaks concerning all the abominations of the evils of the house of Israel which follow. Spiritually, we strike with the hand when we separate ourselves from evil works; we stamp the foot when we do not walk in the way of sinners; we lament and weep when the things that are done displease us, which is shown by the word 'alas' — for which Aquila translated ἀᾶ [ah!], Symmachus σχετλίασον, which means 'lament' or 'mourn', the Seventy or Theodotion εὖγε [well done!], which belongs rather to one who mocks than to one who mourns — .\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","Who are to fall by sword, famine, pestilence. This is the cause of mourning: that the inhabitants of the mountains and the worshipers of the altars of the idols are to fall by sword, famine, pestilence. Those who are led about by various errors and change their faith daily and are carried about by every wind of doctrine, these are cut down by the sword and divided into parts, abandoning the unity of the Church's faith. But he who does not have spiritual food dies of famine, not knowing him who said: I am the life. Further, he who, given over either to single vices or to several, cannot be separated from them, falls by pestilence, as if by a kind of lethargy.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.6.12":["He who is far off shall die by pestilence, but he who is near shall fall by the sword, and he who is left and besieged shall die by famine, and I will fulfill my indignation upon them. He who escapes the siege of the city and moves away to the deserts will die by pestilence; he who is near will fall by the sword of the enemy; but he whom the hostile army surrounds will perish by famine and want; and in all these the indignation of God will be fulfilled, so that those who remain may know that he himself is the Lord. Whoever also withdraws from the Church will at once die by pestilence; he who seems to himself a man of some learning and diligent will, unless he takes care, be struck by the sword of the enemy; he who is content with simple faith — like the brute animals he does indeed spend his time in the Church, yet he brings nothing to completion in any good work, nor is he an imitator of the ant, which in harvest prepares food for itself and carries it in — , he will die of famine; and in all these the wrath of the Lord will be fulfilled.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.6.13":["And you shall know that I am the Lord, when your slain are in the midst of your idols, round about your altars, on every lofty hill and on all the summits of the mountains, and under every shady tree, and under every leafy oak, the place where they burned sweet-smelling incense to all their idols. It is plain according to the letter and needs no interpretation, that on the mountains of Israel and on the lofty hills, among the very altars of the idols and the groves, which are called shady trees, the army of Israel was cut down, so that where they had sinned, there they would be punished. According to ἀναγωγὴν [anagogy, the higher sense] let us follow the order set forth. When the masters of the heretics have been crushed and overcome by men of the Church who are instructed in the knowledge of the Scriptures, we see the slain of the perverse doctrines lying among the images which they had fashioned, and those who lifted themselves up against the knowledge of God laid low on the mountains and hills under every shady tree: they had pompous words and the shade of leaves, and did not have the fruits of good works; they were under every leafy oak, which bears fruit not for men but for swine, where they burned sweet-smelling incense, not to God but to their idols; nor could they say what is written of Noah: The Lord smelled the odor of good fragrance, and that which Paul says: We are the good odor of Christ to God in every place — but just as the saint delights God with his odor, so the sinner, in whose person that psalm is sung: My scars have rotted and become corrupt before the face of my folly, burns stinking incense to his idols — . (That which we have translated: On all the summits of the mountains, and a little after: And under every leafy oak, the Seventy left out.)\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.6.14":["I will stretch out my hand over them, and I will make the land desolate and forsaken from the desert of Deblatha in all their dwellings; and they shall know that I am the Lord. O mountains of Israel, when your slain have fallen in the midst of the altars of your idols, on every lofty hill, on all the summits of the mountains, and I have filled with the corpses of the dead your groves, in which they once burned sweet-smelling incense to their idols, then I will stretch out my hand for vengeance, which until now had been held back, and I will reduce the whole land of Israel to a wilderness, from the desert of Deblatha — which is in the land of Hamath, which today is called Ἐπιφάνεια [Epiphania] of Syria — in all their dwellings, so that it may be plain to all that there is nothing between the wilderness and the Great Sea which the enemy's blade has not consumed — many think that it is the same place of which it is written in Jeremiah: And they seized Zedekiah in the desert which is near Jericho, and all his retinue fled from him. And when they had taken the king, they brought him to the king of Babylon at Reblatha, which is in the land of Hamath, and that, on account of the nearness, or rather the likeness, of the Hebrew letters 'daleth' and 'res', which are distinguished by a small stroke, it is called either Deblatha or Reblatha — . But according to the mystical senses, the Lord stretches out his hand over all who had been deceived by heretical error, to make their land, which is interpreted as the Church, desolate from the desert of Deblatha — which in our language means παλάθη, that is, 'a mass of figs' 'and of dried figs pressed together' — , so that, after they have found bitterness in a feigned sweetness, which belonged not to cultivated land but to the wilderness, they may then know that he himself is the Lord: For honey drips from the lips of a harlot woman, which for a time fattens the throats of those who eat, and afterward is found more bitter than gall. This is also signified by the two baskets of figs which were set in Jeremiah before the face of the temple, one of very good figs and one of very bad, of which the one refers to the Church of Christ, the other to the congregation of the wicked.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.7.2":["And the word of the Lord came to me, saying: And you, son of man, thus says the Lord God to the land of Israel: The end has come, the end upon the four 'wings of the earth'. These we have translated 'regions of the world': namely the east and the west, the south and the north; of which wings Isaiah also speaks: Lord, from the wings (or ends) of the earth we have heard wonders, which were being wrought throughout the whole world by the apostles; and of the saints it is written: If you sleep in the midst of the lots, wings of a dove silvered over, and its hinder parts in the greenness of gold; whence the Lord too, when he had drawn near and seen Jerusalem, wept and said: How often I would have gathered your children as a hen gathers her chicks under her wings, and you would not?; he it is who, in the song of Deuteronomy, spread out his wings, took them up and bore them on his shoulders. A certain writer sets down as the spiritual wings of the earth, by which we fly up to heavenly things, four kinds of believers: the house of Aaron and the house of Levi and the house of Israel and those who fear the Lord — of whom the psalmist sings: House of Israel, bless the Lord; house of Aaron, bless the Lord; house of Levi, bless the Lord; you who fear the Lord, bless the Lord —, understanding by Aaron the priesthood, by Levi the temple-keepers and ministers, by Israel the whole people, by those who fear the proselytes. We, however, think it should be explained simply: that after the threat against the mountains of Israel the prophetic word is directed to the whole land of Israel, whether of the ten or of the twelve tribes, and that the prophet by no means announces a captivity to come but sees one already bearing down. For in the fifth year of Zedekiah Ezekiel began to prophesy to the captives in Babylon, and in the ninth year Nebuchadnezzar came and besieged Jerusalem, and he took it in the eleventh year of Zedekiah. From this it is clear that the end had come and drawn near, not upon mountains and hills and crags and valleys, but upon the four regions of the land on every side of Israel; not of the whole earth — for had he said that, it could have been believed to concern the whole world —, but simply of the land, which signifies Israel; for thus he had begun: Thus says the Lord God to the land of Israel: The end has come, the end upon the four regions of the land.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.7.3":["Now the end is upon you, and I will send my fury against you, and I will judge you according to your ways, and I will set against you all your abominations. And my eye will not spare you, and I will not have mercy, but I will set your ways upon you, and your abominations will be in your midst, and you shall know that I am the Lord. (In this chapter, in the Seventy translators, the order has been changed and confused, so that the first things are last and the last either first or in the middle, and the middle things themselves are moved now to the end, now to the beginning; wherefore we, following both the Hebrew itself and the other translators, have set down the order of the truth.) To the land of Israel, then, the word is directed: that the end and consummation has come upon it, and that the Lord sends forth his fury against it, a fury not unjust nor coming merely from indignation, but full of equity and reason, so that he may judge it according to its ways and show it all its abominations, that it may remember its works and understand what wrong it has done.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.7.4":["And as for his saying: My eye will not spare you, and I will not have mercy, like a most merciful physician who wishes to cut away rotten flesh and to burn out decayed wounds with the cautery, he does not spare so that he may spare, he does not have mercy so that he may have mercy the more, lest anything rotten remain in the body that would corrupt the living flesh by its nearness. But the Lord strikes the one whom he loves, and chastises every son whom he receives; to this understanding belongs that saying: I will strike and I will heal, for every medicine has bitterness for a time, but afterwards the fruit of the pain is shown in health; something of this kind God speaks through Amos: Since I have known you out of all the tribes of the earth, therefore I will avenge upon you all your sins; for the Lord knows those who are his. 'And her ways', he says, 'and the course of her whole life, and her abominations and stains I will set in her midst', so that the former sins may be set before the eyes of the sinful woman, who is punished for her healing; and when the Lord has done this, those who have been tormented will know that he himself is the Lord. That testimony too sounds this very thing: I will avenge Jacob according to his ways, and according to his devices I will repay him.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.7.5-6":["Thus says the Lord God: One affliction, an affliction, behold, comes. The end comes, the end comes; it has awakened against you, behold, it comes. Crushing comes upon you who dwell in the land. (These words are not found in the Seventy, but in their edition they were added under asterisks from the translation of Theodotion; let us, however, follow the Hebrew.) In place of 'affliction', which we have put for the sake of clarity following Symmachus, who translated κάκωσιν [ill-treatment], both in the Hebrew and in the Greek κακία or πονηρία is written, that is, 'malice'. And it is said that the time of the final sin of the land of Israel has arrived, so that it no longer escapes the sentence of God which it had long been deserving. And because it had not yet filled up its sins, the punishment was for that reason being deferred, according to that which is written: For the sins of the Amorites are not yet complete; whence the Lord too says to the Jews: You also, he says, fill up the measure of your fathers. Misery, then, has come upon you, affliction has come, and captivity has come. And lest you think that I am again threatening things to come, I point with my finger and show it: Behold, it comes, the end comes, the end comes; he who until now seemed to you to be dozing and at rest has suddenly awakened against you and risen up.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.7.6-7":["We can also interpret this place spiritually against the people of the Jews, upon whom came the end and consummation from the blood of Abel the righteous to the blood of Zechariah son of Barachiah, whom they slew between the temple and the altar. For as long as they did not lay hands on the Son of God, the Lord's sentence dozed for them and was put off; but when they killed the heir so that the inheritance might perish, then it awoke against them, and the whole order and tangle of miseries rose up, because they did not seek heavenly things but desired to cling to the earth.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.7.7":["'Crushing' has come — which in the Hebrew is called 'sephphira', and which Aquila translated προσκόπησιν, that is, 'contemplation' and 'looking forward' — which you, ever fearful, dreaded would come upon you — and which Theodotion translated πλοκὴν, that is, the 'order and connected web' of all evils —. Further, what is said: Upon you who dwell in the land, we ought to take according to that saying of the Ἀποκαλύψεως [Revelation] of John: Woe upon all who dwell on the earth — for the saint is not a dweller on the earth but a stranger and sojourner, and he says: I am a stranger and a sojourner, as all my fathers were. For this reason Abraham too is called 'a Hebrew', that is, περάτης, and 'sojourner' and 'passer-through' and 'wayfarer', hastening to pass from the present age to the things to come —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","The time has come, the day of slaughter is near, and not of the glory of the mountains. The Hebrew word 'adarim', which we, dividing it into two words, the first 'ad', the second 'arim', have translated following Theodotion as 'glory of the mountains', Symmachus turned into 'postponement' and said: And the time is near, a day of haste and not of postponement. (But the Seventy translated thus: The time has come, the day has drawn near, not with disturbance nor with pains.) Theodotion: The time has come, the day of famine is near, and not of the glory of the mountains. And the time of slaughter and of famine is proven even now in the Jewish people, which has neither prophets nor the word of God by which the soul of believers is fed. By the mountains understand the teachers and those who have knowledge of the Scriptures, of whom it is said in another place too: You give light wonderfully from the everlasting mountains. But the Seventy, who said: Not with disturbance and not with pains, signify this: that they have such ignorance of God and blindness of soul that they are neither disturbed in their crimes nor tormented by the pain of repentance.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.7.8":["Now from near at hand I will pour out my wrath upon you, and I will unfold my fury upon you, and I will judge you according to your ways, and I will lay (or give) upon you all your abominations. If we wish to reckon from the fifth year of the exile of King Jeconiah to the ninth year of the reign of Zedekiah, when Nebuchadnezzar came and besieged Jerusalem, there will be three years in between. Hence it is rightly said: 'Now from near at hand I will pour out my wrath upon you; I will by no means foretell things to come to you nor threaten things that will happen long afterwards; now I will fulfil my fury upon you: and this fury and wrath is not without judgment, but is so that it may repay your ways upon your head and make you feel all your abominations'. We can also speak of the final captivity: that after the killing of Christ, when the end came upon them and sin awoke against them, there was fulfilled that which the apostle Paul writes: Now the wrath of God has come upon them to the end; when Jerusalem was encircled by Titus and Vespasian and its desolation came and there was fulfilled: Behold, your house will be left to you deserted, then they were judged according to their ways and the blasphemies by which they denied the Lord, and they felt the fury of God, and all his indignation was poured out upon them and they received back their crimes, so that the destruction of the temple remains until the consummation.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.7.9":["And my eye will not spare, nor will I have mercy, but your ways I will lay (or give) upon you, and your abominations will be in your midst, and you shall know that I am the Lord who strikes. The causes of the severity and harshness, or, as the heretics think, of the cruelty of God, in that 'my eye will not spare, nor have mercy', he adds, saying: 'I will give you your ways, and your abominations will be in your midst, so that you may feel what you have done and, seeing your abominations set either before your eyes or in the midst of all, may correct your error by repentance, and, withdrawing from your former works, when you hate what you have done, then you will know that he himself is the Lord who strikes for this reason: to correct, and: He chastises every son whom he receives'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.7.10-11":["Behold the day, behold, it comes, contraction has gone forth; the rod has blossomed, pride has budded; iniquity has risen up in the rod of impiety: not from them, and not from the people, nor from their noise; and there will be no rest for them. (LXX: \"Behold, the day gives birth, behold, the end comes, the encompassing has gone forth, and the rod has blossomed, pride has budded, injury has been roused, and it will crush the strength of the wicked, and not with disturbance nor with haste, and they are not of them, nor is there beauty in them.\") 'The day', he says, 'has been contracted and shortened, and the captivity presses close at hand; the rod which for a long time threatened you has blossomed, and from the blossom it is bringing forth the fruit of blows; your pride has budded what you deserved, so that over the rod of your impiety, with which you impiously beat those subject to you, there rises up iniquity which is not from the wretched people, nor from those who are led like brute animals by their teachers, nor from their clamor and noise with which they shout in vain, led astray by the priests and Pharisees, so that they cried out with one voice and said: Crucify, crucify such a one; hence there will be no rest for them, but everlasting captivity'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","For this reason, then, the Lord visits and strikes: that all the pride which long lay hidden, shut in, may spring up and lie open, and injury may be roused against the pride that has sprung up and is budding, and the strength of the wicked may be crushed, not with disturbance nor with haste; for the end of the blossoming rod is health and correction, which comes not by the merit of those who are corrected, nor by a beauty which they do not have in themselves, but by the mercy of the Lord. A difficult place, and one in which the Hebrew and the Seventy differ greatly; many things have been added to the Seventy from the edition of Theodotion, so that they might seem to have some coherence.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.7.10":["But the Seventy, according to that sense in which it is written: For you do not know what the coming day may bring forth, said: 'Behold, the day gives birth to what it had conceived long before, and the end comes, and the encompassing has gone forth, which is to encompass and hold all your evils (or, as Symmachus translated, inspection, so that the Lord may see all that you have done and consider your works and render to each what he deserves)'. And on what they likewise translated: The rod has blossomed, we can use that testimony: Do not take the rod away from your son, and that of the apostle: What do you want? Shall I come to you with a rod, or in love and a spirit of gentleness?; and God too resounds through the mouth of the psalmist: I will visit their iniquities with a rod, and their sins with scourges; but my mercy I will not take away from them.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.7.12":["The time has come, the day has drawn near. It is not only to Jerusalem that it is said that the time of its captivity has come and the day has drawn near on which it is to be walled in by the Babylonian army; but to him too who exulted over his barns built and enlarged, the Lord speaks: Fool, this night your soul will be taken from you; and the things you have prepared, whose will they be?; whence the apostle too: The time, he says, has been shortened; and in another place: For the figure of this world is passing away; and it must be noted that he did not defer it to the future but spoke of the present, 'is passing away', and that daily 'the figure of the world' slips by: for it never remains in the same state, but the figure of things growing and diminishing ever flies past and is changed; whence the Lord too: Heaven, he says, and earth will pass away. But if these things, by which all that is in the world is contained, pass away and go by, what in human affairs can be everlasting?\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","Let him who buys not rejoice, and let him who sells not mourn. It is natural to rejoice in the purchase of possessions and to mourn in their sale. But when slavery and captivity are pressing, both joy and sorrow are vain in either case. Hence the apostle too: The time has been shortened; for the rest, let those who have wives be as though they had none, and those who weep as though they did not weep, and those who rejoice as though they did not rejoice, and those who buy as though they did not possess, and those who use this world as though they did not make full use of it; for the flood too overwhelmed those buying and selling, as the Lord says in the Gospel.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","For wrath is upon all its people. (This is not found in the Seventy.) For 'people' one may also take 'multitude' and 'crowd' according to the Hebrew: for this is what 'amona' signifies. And the reason is clear why he who buys ought not to rejoice and he who sells should cease to weep: because the wrath of God is shortly to come upon the whole multitude of the land of Judea, or of Jerusalem; for this is understood from the preceding words, in which it is written: Thus says the Lord God to the land of Israel, and the rest.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.7.13":["For he who sells will not return to what he has sold. He speaks according to the Hebrew custom: for every purchase used to return to the seller in the fiftieth year, the year of release, which among them was called the jubilee; before the year of release comes, then, when possessions return to their former owners, captivity will burst in, which will take away the whole accustomed life of the city. But what the Seventy translated: For he who buys will not return to the seller, though according to the history it does not hang together at all, according to anagogy can have this sense, so that we may say that he who had been deceived by heretical error, when he has understood the fraudulence of his teacher, by no means returns to the seller, that is, to the teacher, but despises and scorns him.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","And still their life is among the living: for the vision to all its multitude will not go back. Just as the possession will not return to its former owner, since the overthrow of the city is impending, so the vision and prophetic threat, which is directed to the whole multitude of the city, will by no means go back and become void, but will be fulfilled in fact, while those to whom the prophetic word is directed are still living. And he says this to show the captivity is imminent, lest according to their custom they should say: 'This vision is for many days hence and will be fulfilled after long times'. We can also say this: that after the prophetic vision withdrew from the Jews — The law, indeed, and the prophets until John the Baptist —, after the killing of the Savior it by no means goes back to them, nor do they any longer deserve to have prophets. And significantly: 'To all', he says, 'the multitude the vision will not go back'; therefore it will go back to those of the Jews who believed in the Lord, namely the apostles, and the remnant of the Jewish people which has been saved out of Israel. (But neither are these two verses found in the edition of the Seventy.)\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","And a man in the iniquity of his life will not be strengthened. (LXX: \"And a man in the eyes of his life will not obtain.\") And the sense according to the Hebrew is: his iniquity will not profit a man, nor will it furnish him any strength; according to the Seventy: even the man who desired what he thought precious in the world will not obtain it, but, as liberty perishes, all that is beautiful will be lost. Now the ambiguity of the Hebrew letters 'iod' and 'uau', which are distinguished only by size, caused some to translate 'iniquity' and others 'eyes'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.7.14":["Sound the trumpet, let all be made ready. (LXX: \"Blow the trumpet and judge all things.\") Of the blast and sound of trumpets we read in many places, as there: Lift up your voice like a trumpet, and elsewhere: Blow the trumpet at the new moon, on the notable day of our solemnity, and in the apostle: For the Lord himself, at the command and at the voice of the archangel and at the trumpet of God, will descend from heaven, and in the Gospel: Do not blow a trumpet before you; and the history of Numbers tells of beaten and silver trumpets, which are compared to the prophetic word and to the apostolic teaching. And now it is commanded that all be made ready for wars at the signal of the blast of the horn. But as for what the Seventy said: And judge all things, no one doubts that it befits the power of the apostles, who, after the trumpet of the angels has sounded, are to judge the twelve tribes of Israel on twelve thrones; one of whom, Paul, said: We shall judge angels, and elsewhere: In you the world will be judged. But according to the present sense, those who are bound to this office are bidden, by irony, to blow the trumpet, so that all may be made ready against the Babylonian army.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","And there is no one who goes to battle; for my wrath is upon all its multitude. (These words too are not found in the Seventy.) God had commanded that they sound the trumpet to make ready an army which could resist the strength of the Babylonians, but it did no good to have commanded it, since there is no one among the people who dares to go forth to wars. And the people has been unnerved and lacks the strength to fight for this reason: because the whole wrath of God is upon all its multitude, that is, understood, of the city of Jerusalem or of the land of Judea; but also of the Jerusalem of our land: when either public persecution or the incitements of various vices strive to overcome us, in vain do the teachers sound the trumpet and hasten to make us ready for wars, since there is no strength in the people, which has been withdrawn for this reason, that present sins have deserved the wrath of the Lord.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.7.15-16":["The sword without, pestilence and famine within; he who is in the field will die by the sword, and those who are in the city will be devoured by pestilence and famine. And those of them who flee will be saved, and they will be on the mountains like doves of the valleys, all trembling, each in his iniquity. (The Seventy were entirely silent about 'doves of the valleys', or, as Theodotion translated, 'meditating'.) And what we have translated: All trembling, each in his iniquity, Theodotion translated thus: All murmuring, each one in her iniquity, so that under the metaphor of meditating doves he signifies that each one of the people weeps for his own sin and understands and perceives why he suffers these things. Now the three parts — of some who are to die in the city by pestilence and famine, and of others whom the sword will kill outside, and of those who have escaped captivity by flight — we read of above; of these, he who is saved will pass over to the mountains and, like murmuring doves, will trembling bewail his sins. Tropologically, however, it is to be understood thus: that he who is in the field and the plain, outside the bounds of the Lord's city, which means the Church, is struck by the adversary's blade; but he who in the city has acted negligently and has not prepared for himself the foods of which it is written in Proverbs: He who works his land will be filled with bread, he will die by famine and pestilence; while the few who have escaped either the sword of the heretics or the famine and death of their own sloth will not be saved except on the mountains, and if they have taken the wings of a dove and flown away and found rest — doves which, as long as they are in the valleys, tremble and dread every single thing, whose innocence we are commanded to imitate, and of which it is written in the psalm: If you sleep in the midst of the lots, wings of a dove silvered over, and its hinder parts in the paleness of gold, in whose form the Holy Spirit descended and remained upon the Lord Savior; and beautifully, following Theodotion, we shall call a meditating dove him who meditates on the law of the Lord day and night, and of whom it is written: The mouth of the righteous will meditate wisdom.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.7.17-18":["All hands will be weakened and all knees will flow with waters. And they will gird themselves with sackcloth, and dread will cover them. (For 'knees flowing with waters' the Seventy translated: Thighs will be defiled with moisture.) When all have trembled and fled to the mountains, the hands of all will be weakened, and no one will be able to resist the enemy, and through the greatness of their fear urine will defile their knees, nor will the bladder be able to hold back the waters flowing forth; they will gird themselves with sackcloth, and trembling will possess all things; granted that this was done in that people not only under the Babylonians but also under the Romans, when the wrath of God came upon them to the end. But also in our Judea, in which is the confession of the Lord, when iniquities have multiplied and the love of many has grown cold, all hands of good works will be weakened, and all knees, or thighs, will flow with waters, those of the men who seek unlawful couplings and whom Scripture in the law calls γονορρυεῖς, that is, 'suffering a flow of seed' and 'unclean'; hence those who are such ought to gird themselves with the sackcloth of repentance and to dread the judgment to come, and he who does this will deserve to hear Isaiah saying: Be strengthened, weakened hands and feeble knees, be made strong.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.7.18":["On every face shame, and on all heads baldness. The blush of the face is a sign of shame, and the consciousness of sins shines out in the face, and there is hope of salvation when shamefacedness follows wrongdoing; hence to her who with hard heart gloried in her sins it was said: The face of a harlot has become yours, you do not know how to blush. Baldness of the head too signifies mourning, when the hair loses its comeliness and we lose the beauty of our locks; accordingly it is said to Jerusalem: For the ornament of your head you have baldness, because of your works, and another prophet: All heads, he says, in every place will be shorn, and every beard will be shaved; Micah also to the same Jerusalem: Make yourself bald, he says, and be shorn for the children of your delights; enlarge your baldness like the eagle; and over the dead baldness is commanded to be made, but only the holy, that is, the Nazirites, and those who have been found worthy to attain the high priesthood of the Lord, do not shave their heads, for they neither have works of death nor are unclean, because they are Nazirites, that is, holy ones of the Lord; and if anyone dies beside them, all the former days of their sanctification will not be reckoned; Samuel was a holy one of God, and therefore possessed everlasting ornaments of the head, and he heard that saying from the Song of Songs: Your locks are black as a raven; but Samson, because he lost his hair, lost his strength, and as his hair gradually grew back his former strength returned, so that in dying he killed many more than while alive; Elisha, though he had baldness of body, nevertheless, because he was a Nazirite of the Lord, enjoyed the locks of the head; hence the little boys, because they were little and had not yet reached a man's age, mocking his baldness and saying: Go up, baldhead, go up, baldhead, were torn by the bites of wild beasts, whose dwelling is the glades and forests.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.7.19":["Their silver will be cast outside, and their gold will be in the dunghill. (LXX: \"Their silver will be cast into the streets, and their gold will be for contempt.\") As flight and captivity compel them, they will cast their riches, the weights of gold and silver, into the streets, desiring only to save their lives, so that what were formerly their luxuries may not be a burden. Otherwise, according to anagogy, all the silver of the captives and of those fleeing from Jerusalem will be cast into the streets, onto the broad and spacious way that leads to death, because they have abandoned the narrow path of salvation; but all the gold will be for contempt and will be reckoned as a dunghill and uncleanness: for what is outside the Church of the Lord cannot have cleanness.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","Their silver and their gold will not be able to deliver them on the day of the fury of the Lord. (And this is not found in the Seventy.) No one doubts that in the time of siege and famine gold and silver do not deliver the hungry, and that teeth dashed against the most precious metal are blunted as on the hardest stone. Accordingly there follows:\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","They will not satisfy their soul, and their bellies will not be filled. By present evils we have learned that many rich men, amid silk, gems and weights of gold and silver, worn out by want, have had the end of beggars. But that the silver and gold of those who are outside the Church do not deliver the souls of their possessors on the day of the fury of the Lord, but that they have everlasting hunger and are tormented with an empty belly, is shown by that testimony in which it is said: The ransom of a man's soul is his own riches. His own riches are those which the truth of Christ has taught us, who commanded us to make for ourselves friends of the mammon of iniquity, who may receive us into the eternal tabernacles.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","For it has become the stumbling block of their iniquity. 'Therefore', he says, 'neither will the soul of those who possess gold and silver be satisfied nor their belly be filled: because this very gold and silver has become the stumbling block of their crimes'. And he signifies the idols which, fashioned of gold and silver, condemn their own makers. (But the Seventy translated: For torment was their iniquity, so that the wicked may be tormented in their own error and understand that they have wickedly turned the gifts of God into blasphemy).\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.7.20-21":["For this reason I have given it to them for uncleanness. And I will give it into the hands of strangers to be plundered, and to the impious of the earth for a prey, and they will defile it. (LXX: \"For this reason I have given them to them for uncleanness. And I will hand them over into the hands of strangers that they may plunder them; and to the pestilent of the earth for a prey, and they will pollute them.\") 'Because from the gold and silver, that is, from the ornaments of the necklaces which I had given them, they made for themselves images of their abominations and idols, for this reason I have given those things to them for uncleanness and for dung', for which Symmachus translated nausea, wishing to express the filth of idols. 'And I will hand them over', he says, 'into the hands of enemies that they may plunder everything, and so that I may not so much defile them as show to have been defiled the things which earlier seemed most holy'. We too are handed over into the hands of enemies and of those estranged from God, when we make our ornaments into images of demons, and all our glory is possessed by the impious or the pestilent of the earth, so that they may subject us to their power.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.7.20":["And the ornament of their necklaces they set for pride, and they made of it the images of their abominations and idols. (LXX: \"The choice things of the world they set for pride, and the images of their abominations and their stumbling blocks they made of them.\") 'What I', he says, 'had given as an ornament and as riches to those who possessed them, they turned to pride, so that from the very things by which they could have freed their soul through almsgiving and good works they took material for arrogance; then from the gold and silver they made idols and turned my gifts into images of demons'. But as for what the Seventy said: The choice things of the world they set for pride, it is to be referred to gold and silver, than which nothing in the world is thought more precious; and as for what follows: Their stumbling blocks, let us know that it was added from the edition of Theodotion. And the tropology is easy: that the gold and silver, the senses and words of the Scriptures, which are the choice things of the world and have been given to us for an ornament, the heretics have set as tinder and material of pride, and have made of them images of their various doctrines and of their abominations and stumbling blocks, so that through the very things by which they could have worshiped and adored God, by these they offended God.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.7.22":["And I will turn away my face from them, and they will violate my secret place; and there will enter into it emissaries (or, as the Seventy translated, rashly, or, as Symmachus and Theodotion, 'those bursting in' and 'the pestilent') and they will defile it. 'When', he says, 'for the causes given above, the abominations of the people, I turn my face away from them and by no means judge them worthy of my eyes, then they will violate my secret place (which signifies 'the Holy of Holies', for which the Seventy translated vision, to show that the presence of God had been in the holy places), and the impious and pestilent of the earth will burst into those places which, apart from the priests and the high priest alone, no one else dared to enter'. This we know was done both by the Babylonians, and by King Antiochus and Gnaeus Pompey, and, finally, under Vespasian and Titus, when the temple was taken and overthrown, and all things were perpetrated which the prophet's following discourse embraces. At our evil works too God turns away his face, and because his face is turned away, therefore the secret place of God is violated, so that in place of the priests and saints of God the pestilent enter and defile all things, so that what ought to have been a place of holiness becomes a place of uncleanness, according to that Gospel saying: My Father's house will be called a house of prayer; but you have made it a den of robbers.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.7.23":["Make a conclusion, for the land is full of the judgment of bloods, and the city is full of iniquity. (LXX: \"And I will bring disturbance, for the land full of the judgment of bloods, and the city full of iniquities.\") The Hebrew word 'arethic' Aquila translated 'conclusion', Symmachus and Theodotion καθήλωσιν, that is, 'nailing fast', for which the Seventy put φυρμόν, which we have rendered as 'disturbance'. 'Therefore, O prophet, conclude my wrath upon the land of Judea and upon the city of Jerusalem in a brief word: so that, as the land is full of the judgment of bloods — for all are judged in blood, shedding innocent blood, as Scripture records the Jews also did in the person of Naboth —, so their own gore too may be shed and the city shown to be full of iniquity — for under Manasseh too the city of Jerusalem was filled from gate to gate with the blood of the prophets —. And conclusion and disturbance and nailing fast will come upon them, so that no one can escape the impending miseries'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.7.24":["And I will bring the worst of the nations, and they will possess their houses. (And this is not found in the Seventy.) 'In place of priests and prophets I will bring the Babylonians, the worst of all the nations, that they may possess your houses and subject you to slavery'. But our houses too, that is, our souls — for we are the temple of God, and the Holy Spirit dwells in us —, the worst demons will possess, if our land is full of bloods and our dwelling full of iniquity.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","And I will make the pride of the mighty cease, and they will possess their sanctuaries. (LXX: \"And I will turn away the roaring of their strength, and their holy things will be polluted.\") All pride offends God. Therefore the worst of the nations will possess the houses of the mighty and the proud and will take hold of their sanctuaries, because the unclean man enters the place of holiness and defiles it with his uncleanness. And significantly, because the holy things of God had been polluted and the secret place violated, he did not say: 'They will possess my sanctuaries', but: 'their' sanctuaries, which after their defilement ceased to be 'mine'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.7.25":["Anguish is at hand. (LXX: \"Propitiation will come.\") — According to the preceding order Aquila and Theodotion rightly translated that 'anguish' is at hand, and Symmachus 'grief'; but the Seventy, in the midst of all the sorrowful things, put a word of gladness, so that the mind of the mourner may be sustained and may hope for better things amid adversities, and for the Lord propitious, of whom it is written: The Lord raises up those who are dashed down. — We, however, following Symmachus, have joined the former sentence to the latter, so as to say: As anguish comes upon them, they will seek peace and there will be none, which according to the Seventy cannot stand at all: for if 'propitiation will come', how 'will they seek peace and not find it'? And he will seek peace and not find it who heard from the apostles: Peace to this house, and did not keep it nor make it rest in his mind; but, put to flight by evil works, it cannot be found. And it is that peace which surpasses all understanding, which the Savior, ascending victorious to the Father, left to the apostles.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.7.26":["Trouble upon trouble will come, and report upon report. (LXX: \"Woe upon woe will be, and messenger upon messenger.\") Just as it is said to the saints: Rejoice, again I say: rejoice, and of them it is written: They will go from strength to strength, so that they may heap future goods upon present goods, so also to these, upon whom anguish has come and who have sought peace and not found it, there will come trouble upon trouble, or woe upon woe — according to what is written in Revelation too: One woe has passed, and another woe will come —, and report will come upon report, and messenger upon messenger — according to that saying of blessed Job: While one was still speaking, another messenger came, adding evils to evils, and describing the roaring and the throng of the approaching Babylonian —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","And they will seek a vision from the prophet, and the law will perish from the priest, and counsel from the elders. Properly, each thing is sought from each: the prophecy of things to come is sought from the prophet; the interpretation of the law is the priest's office; prudent counsel is what mature age searches out, according to that which is written: In the counsel of the saints and in the congregation, great are the works of the Lord. And these things were sought from the prophets and elders and priests not only at that time, when the Babylonian army was coming against Jerusalem, but they are required daily in the churches, which, if they have lost vision, law and counsel, boast in vain that they have prophets and priests and elders.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.7.27":["The king will mourn and the prince will be clothed with grief (or, according to the Seventy, with destruction; who, intending I know not what, were entirely silent about what we have expressed from the Hebrew speech: The king will mourn.) That Zedekiah mourned, and that all the princes of the people of the Jews were covered with grief, Holy Scripture records. But we can — although at first sight this may seem blasphemous — say that the king who mourns our vices and sins is Christ, who speaks in the Psalms: What profit is there in my blood, while I go down into corruption? and who, drawing near to Jerusalem, wept over it and said: Jerusalem, Jerusalem, you who kill the prophets and stone those sent to you, how often I would have gathered your children as a hen gathers her chicks under wings, and you would not? The princes too will be either, in a good sense, the apostles who mourn sinners, or, in the contrary sense, those to whom it is said: Hear the word of the Lord, princes of Sodom. And he will be clothed, according to the Seventy, with destruction, who does not have the armor of God, nor has put on Christ: For as many as have been baptized in Christ have put on Christ.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","And the hands of the people of the land will be troubled. (Or, according to the Seventy, will be weakened.) When there is no vision in the prophet and no knowledge of the law in the priest and no counsel in the elders, and the king has mourned and the prince is in grief or in destruction, consequently the hands of the people too will be troubled, losing their order, or will be weakened, no longer having their ancient strength. And beautifully the people whose hands will be troubled, and whose citizenship is not in heaven, is called not the people of God but the people of the land, of whom it is written: Let those who depart be written upon the earth.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","According to their way I will do to them, and according to their judgments I will judge them. (Or, as the Seventy translated, I will avenge myself on them.) To sinners God renders according to their ways, so as to keep the truth of judgment; but toward the saints he exceeds the measure of mercy: The sufferings of this time, indeed, are not worthy of the future glory which will be revealed in us. For the wages of sin is death, which the sinner receives according to his ways and his works; but the virtue of the righteous enjoys the Lord's bounty, of which Paul speaks: The gift of God is eternal life. And what he added: And according to their judgments I will judge them, sounds that Gospel saying: With what judgment you judge, it will be judged of you, and that apostolic one: In that in which you judge another, you condemn yourself; for you do the same things that you judge.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","And they shall know that I am the Lord. This little verse is often set down in this prophet, because the knowledge of God follows punishments and torments, so that him whom they do not understand through benefits they may come to know through torments.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.8.1-2":["And the hand of the Lord God fell upon me there, and I saw: and behold, a likeness as the appearance of fire; from the appearance of his loins and downward, fire, and from his loins and upward as the appearance of brightness, as the vision of electrum. (LXX: \"And the hand of Adonai the Lord came upon me there, and I saw, and behold, a likeness as the form of a man; from his loins and downward, fire; from his loins and upward ※ as the vision of a breeze: as the vision of electrum.\") The Hebrew text does not contain 'man'; and the 'vision' 'of a breeze', which is not found in the Seventy, we have added from Theodotion; and where we said: The hand of the Lord fell upon me, Symmachus translated: The hand of the Lord befell me, which in the Hebrew is said 'thephphol' [it fell]; and the hand signifies ἐνέργειαν, that is, 'working', so that he might be able to know the mysteries of the vision.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.8.1":["And it came to pass in the sixth year, in the sixth month, on the fifth of the month: I was sitting in my house, and the elders of Judah were sitting before me. We read that the preceding vision came to the prophet in the fifth year of the exile of Jeconiah, in the fourth month, on the fifth of the month. But this one, which we now wish to expound, is recorded to have taken place in the sixth year of the same king, in the sixth month, on the fifth of the month. From this it is clear that these things which are written took place a year and two months later, and that either the prophecy was interrupted for a year and two months, or the things which the preceding discourse wove together were carried out over the whole fourteen months. Now, that the prophecy was made in the sixth year and the sixth month and on the fifth day of the month (numbers which refer to the making of the world and to the fleshly senses, which look upon earthly things and do not yet contemplate heavenly ones) will not surprise anyone who has understood that the discourse is directed to the presbyters of Judah, and to those among whom seventy were holding censers and twenty-five were worshiping the sun in the temple. And the prophet was sitting in his house, fleeing the throng of the multitude, and the elders of Judah were sitting before him, either eager to hear the prophet's words or lying in wait for his sayings. And it is significant that they are called the elders of Judah, so that we may understand that nothing is said to Israel, that is, to the ten tribes which had been taken captive earlier, but to those of the tribe of Judah who had been led into captivity with Jeconiah.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.8.2":["And this must be considered: that he does not see the truth itself but the likeness of fire, since from the loins downward fire appeared, and from the loins upward as the appearance of brightness, as the vision of electrum. For 'brightness', which in the Hebrew is called 'zor', Theodotion translated 'breeze', so as to show the refreshments of God in the upper parts. Indeed, in the volume of Kings too, blessed Elijah beholds the coming of the Lord not in the storm and the whirlwind, not in the blazing of fire, but in the appearance of a breeze, thin and most gentle, so as to prove the mildness of almighty God. Now the works of intercourse, which are signified by the loins, downward, are purged by fire, and the works of the virtues, which rise from the loins to the higher parts, have brightness and splendor; and yet neither is the fire said to reach to the feet, nor the brightness of electrum to the head, but only 'downward' and 'upward', so that, in sinners as well as in saints, according to the diversity of merits, one may reach as far as this or that member, another as far as these or those. And just as those who hold the perfection of holiness reach all the way to the crown of the head, so those who are in the depth of sins must be believed to hold the soles of the fiery feet.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.8.3":["And the likeness of a hand, sent forth, took hold of me by a lock of my head (LXX: \"And he stretched out the likeness of a hand and took me up by the fringe of my crown.\") Here too it is the likeness of a hand, not the hand itself, that is stretched out: for there is nothing bodily in God; and a hand is seen, as a member of the human body, so that by its familiarity it may in no way terrify him who is taken up and seized: for if it had touched the prophet in the likeness of a snake or of any other of the beasts, the very unlikeness of the members would have terrified the one taken up. And a part of the hair is seized, because human nature cannot bear the grasping of the whole head. For 'lock' the Seventy translated κράσπεδον, that is, 'fringe', of which the one is usually taken of hair, the other of garments.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","And the spirit lifted me up between earth and heaven. First he is seized by the likeness of a hand; afterwards he is lifted up by the spirit, and the spirit does not raise him at once to heaven, but between earth and heaven, so that, leaving earthly things behind for the time being, he may hasten toward heaven. We can also say this: that the prophet was seized by the likeness of God's hand on account of good works, and is lifted up by the spirit on account of the knowledge of spiritual things.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","And he brought me to Jerusalem, in the vision of God, near the inner door that looked toward the north, where the idol of jealousy had been set up to provoke rivalry. And behold, there was the glory of the God of Israel, according to the vision that I had seen in the plain. (LXX: \"And he brought me into Jerusalem, in the vision of God, to the vestibule of the gate that looks toward the north, where was the statue of the possessor.\" But this, which is found in certain codices, ※ And in the statue the image of jealousy: has been added from Theodotion. The rest likewise.) When he says: He brought me to Jerusalem in the vision of God, he shows that he was carried over not in the body, but in the spirit. According to this kind of vision we too can behold both the heavens and the depths of the sea and the lower regions, while we embrace in thought the principles of each. First, however, he comes to the vestibule that looks toward the north, so as to see there the statue of the idol of jealousy, to provoke rivalry, so that from that place he may penetrate to the inner parts and discern the rest, which are described afterward; and history relates that a statue of the idol Baal was placed in the temple of God; and it is aptly called 'the idol of jealousy', because it provokes the Lord to rivalry and jealousy, according to what is said in Deuteronomy: They provoked me to jealousy with that which was not God; and I will provoke them to jealousy with a nation which is not; further, because 'jealousy' and 'possession' are called by the same name, which in Hebrew is said 'cena', for 'the statue of jealousy' the Seventy translated 'the statue of the possessor'; indeed, when Cain was born, his parent said: I have possessed, a man through God, alluding by the possession of a man to the name Cain.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.8.4":["And the glory of the God of Israel was there, not because it took delight in such a neighbor, but so that by its presence it might destroy the idol of jealousy and the temple; hence the overthrow of the city and of the temple also follows after a short time; and it is written in Isaiah that a narrow bed can by no means hold two, and a short cloak cannot cover both, Scripture signifying that saying of the Apostle: What agreement has Christ with Belial? The temple of God with an idol?\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.8.5":["And he said to me: Son of man, lift up your eyes toward the way of the north. And I lifted up my eyes toward the way of the north, and behold, to the north of the gate of the altar, the idol of jealousy in the very entrance. (Where we have put: To the north of the gate of the altar, the idol of jealousy in the very entrance, the Seventy translated: From the north toward the eastern gate, which is not found in the Hebrew, leaving out the other things which are written there.) 'He said to me,' he says, '(either the glory of God which I had seen in the plain, or the spirit which lifted me up between heaven and earth), and he spoke to me after he had carried me over into Jerusalem and made me see either the city or the temple, and he commanded me to lift up my eyes toward the way of the north; for there the statue of Baal had been set, which he calls the idol of jealousy, in the very entrance of the gate'. But those who refer the idol of jealousy, or of the possessor, to the glory of God or to the spirit act impiously, interchanging the person of the idol with the majesty of God.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.8.6":["And he said to me: Son of man, do you think you see what these are doing, the great abominations which the house of Israel is doing here, so that I should withdraw far from my sanctuary? And turning again you will see greater abominations. 'Lest you think,' he says, 'that I abandon the temple unjustly and leave the city to be overthrown, look at what they do in the temple; and when you have beheld those things, do not think that this is the end of their crimes: you will behold still greater things'. This can indeed also be understood of our Jerusalem and our temple, when, after the example of Hophni and Phinehas, we work the crimes of idols in the tabernacle of God, and commit fornication with those women who have vowed themselves to the warfare and ministry of God, and pluck off the first portions of his sacrifices, giving them for the use of clients and servants, and do everything for the sake of gain. But if these things which we see are so great, how much greater must those be reckoned which escape human awareness?\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.8.7-9":["And he brought me in to the door of the court, and I saw, and behold, one hole in the wall; and he said to me: Son of man, dig through the wall; and when I had dug through the wall, one door appeared. And he said to me: Go in, and see the most wicked abominations which these are doing here. (This which I translated: And I saw, and behold, one hole in the wall, is not found in the Seventy.) And because everything is shown as if in an image and a painting, he says that he saw one hole in the wall, and that he was ordered to dig it through and make it larger, so that, the hole having been opened, he might enter more widely and see what he could not see while placed outside. By this it is shown that, in the churches as well as in each of us individually, greater vices are revealed through small ones, and that through certain holes, as it were, one arrives at the greatest abominations: for by the fruits the tree is known, and out of the abundance of the heart the mouth speaks. Words that burst out are a sign of the inner man, just as a foul word sometimes exposes the lustful man who cunningly hides his vices, and desire for a trifling thing betrays the avarice lurking within: for greater things are revealed by lesser ones, and the conscience cannot be disguised by face and eyes, since a wanton and lascivious mind shines out in the face, and the secrets of the heart are disclosed by the movement of the body and by gestures.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.8.10":["And I went in and saw, and behold, every likeness of creeping things and of animals, an abomination, and all the idols of the house of Israel were painted on the wall round about, throughout. Not only did the idol of jealousy stand at the entrance of the north gate, but all the walls of the temple too were painted with various images of idols, so that there was no beast which the painting on the wall did not show. This we see to this day in the shrines of idols and the temples of the Gentiles: that a foolish religion worships every kind of beast; whence Vergil too: And of every sort, he says, of gods the monsters and barking Anubis — as if those which he praises were not monsters too —, against Neptune and Venus and against Minerva. We too can point out idols painted on the walls of our temple, when we are subject to all vices and paint in our heart the consciousness of sins and various images, of which it is said in the psalm too: O Lord, in your city you will scatter their image, and in another place: Yet man walks about in an image. That is, there is no one among men who does not have some image, whether of holiness or of sin; for which reason it is said of the furious and the wrathful: Their fury is after the likeness of a serpent, like a deaf asp that stops its ears, which will not hear the voices of the charmers; and of wicked men: Generation of vipers, who showed you to flee from the wrath to come?; and of the shifty and deceitful: Go, tell this fox; and of lovers of women: They have become to me horses mad for females; each one neighed after his neighbor's wife; and also of the brutish and senseless: Do not become like the horse and the mule, which have no understanding; and again: With bit and bridle bind fast their jaws, who do not draw near to you; and many things of this kind. (But this which we have put: Behold, every likeness of creeping things and of animals, is not found in the Seventy.)\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.8.11":["And seventy men of the elders of the house of Israel, and Jaazaniah son of Shaphan was standing in the midst of them, who were standing before the paintings, and each one had a censer in his hand, and a vapor of cloud was rising up from the incense. When he says that there were seventy men of the elders of the house of Israel who held censers in their hands, he shows that there were many other presbyters too who were not doing this, but were perhaps held fast by other sins, and that one, Jaazaniah son of Shaphan by name, stood in their midst, as the prince of the crime and sacrilege of those who, having abandoned the worship of God, were worshiping idols, and in the temple were adoring not God, whose temple it was, but the paintings on the walls. And it is aptly said: Jaazaniah son of Shaphan was standing, that is, of his own 'judgment' and 'condemnation', in the midst of the standing presbyters, because both the prince and those whose prince he was stood with a firm step in their crimes, and did not waver in evil, but persisted most stoutly; and the vapor of cloud, of confusion and of whirlwind, which rose from the incense, displayed the sacrileges of those who offered it. Let this have happened at that time according to the letter. But we too must pray that the elders of the house of Israel, multiplying the number seven, which is holy, into seven decades, may not stand in their errors and adore the paintings of idols, and that the vapor of sacrilege may not ascend, or rise up, resisting God. When we see a most wicked people gathered together, of which it is written: I have hated the church of evildoers, and overseers worse than the people, and a still more wicked prince, who has been set over the people and the presbyters, let us say that Jaazaniah is standing before the paintings, and that each one has a censer in his hands, adoring not the majesty of God but their own opinions, and that it is by no means an odor of good fragrance that reaches God, but a stench that reaches the idols.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.8.12":["And he said to me: Surely you see, son of man, what the elders of the house of Israel are doing in the darkness, each one in the secret of his bedchamber; for they say: The Lord does not see us, the Lord has forsaken the earth. 'From what they do in the temple when gathered together,' he says, 'it is shown what each one does in his own chamber'. But according to the ἀναγωγὴν [anagogy, the higher sense] it can better be understood that some princes of the churches do in darkness things which are shameful even to speak of. This is the hidden man of the heart, of whom it is said: Enter into your chamber, and again: He who sees in secret will repay you. Let each one call his conscience to mind and recall the works of darkness in darkness, and let him know that it is written: Everyone who does evil hates the light, lest he be reproved by the light, and he will understand that this has been fulfilled in himself: He who is drunken is drunken by night, which cannot stand according to the letter: for how many are drunken at luncheons and banquets by day?; but because the drunkenness of the heart, which offends God, belongs to darkness and not to light, therefore: Whoever is drunken is drunken in the night. And when the elders of the house of Israel have done evil in darkness and in the secret of their chambers, and have thought that they could hide from God, then they will consequently say: The Lord does not see us, the Lord has forsaken the earth. And many are sinners, walking according to their own thoughts, who do not reckon that God cares for mortal things, or that our vices concern him. Certainly, when we sin, if we thought that God sees and is present, we would never do what displeases him. Further, what follows, The Lord has forsaken the earth, is also the opinion of certain philosophers, who, from the course and substance of the stars, suppose that there is providence in heavenly things, but that it despises earthly things, since nothing right or according to order is done on earth.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.8.14":["And he said to me: Turning again you will see greater abominations which these are doing. And he brought me in through the door of the gate of the house of the Lord which looked toward the north, and behold, women were sitting lamenting Adonis. What we have translated 'Adonis', both the Hebrew and the Syriac language call 'thamuz'; hence, because according to the pagan fable the lover of Venus, a most beautiful youth, is said to have been slain in the month of July and afterwards to have come back to life, they call that same month of July by the same name and celebrate an annual festival for him, in which he is lamented by women as though dead, and afterwards, coming back to life, he is sung of and praised. And consistently, after it has been shown what the princes and elders of the house of Israel did in the temple and in darkness and in their chambers, the vices of the women too are described, who lament when deprived of the company of their lovers, and exult if they have been able to obtain them. And since that same paganism interprets subtly fables of the poets of this kind, which contain shamefulness, accompanying the slaying and the resurrection of Adonis with lamentation and with joy (of which it thinks the one is shown in the seeds that die in the earth, the other in the crops in which the dead seeds are born again), let us too call women those who are either saddened or exultant at the evils and goods of the world, being of a soft and effeminate mind, and let us say that they lament 'thamuz', that is to say, those things which are reckoned most beautiful in the affairs of the world.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.8.15-16":["And since we read above, after the idol of jealousy which appeared at the north gate: Turning again you will see greater abominations, and, after the paintings of all the beasts had been shown along the wall, which the seventy presbyters and Jaazaniah son of Shaphan were adoring, holding censers in their hands, it is said a second time: Turning again you will see greater abominations which these are doing, because women were sitting lamenting Adonis, so after the third sin too it is said: Turning again you will see abominations greater than these. But what abomination is greater than the three preceding crimes? Clearly the fourth, which follows: And behold, at the door of the temple of the Lord, between the vestibule and the altar, about twenty-five men with their backs toward the temple of the Lord and their faces toward the east, and they were worshiping the rising of the sun, because, despising the Lord, that is, the Creator, they were adoring the sun, that is, the Lord's creature, although the Lord himself had commanded through Moses that they should by no means adore God facing the east after the custom of the Gentiles, but, in whatever part of the world they might be, whether to the east, or to the west, or to the south, or to the north, they should adore facing the temple, where the Lord was believed to inhabit the Holy of Holies; which indeed we know Daniel too did in Babylon, who, with the windows of his upper room open, adored the Lord facing the temple which stands in Jerusalem. No one doubts that this, according to the letter, was a matter of sacrilege. According to the ἀναγωγὴν, that all heretics are worse than the earlier things, that is, than the idol of jealousy and the figures of living creatures painted on the wall and the lamentation for Adonis, through which idolatry and pleasure are shown, he will be able to know who has understood the prophet saying: But you have hated discipline, and have cast my words behind you, and in another place: They turned their shoulders against me, departing; or are we ignorant that Marcion and the other heretics who tear the Old Testament to pieces, despising the Creator, that is, the righteous God, worship and adore some other, good God whom they have fashioned out of their own heart, and that all the heretics of our time, who preach the Son of God as a creature and yet adore him, having left the temple of the divinity and holding it behind their back, say by their own confession that they adore a creature? But we so adore the sun of righteousness that we adore God in the temple of the Old Testament, where the Law and the prophets are, where the cherubim and the mercy seat are. Where we translated 'twenty-five' men, the Seventy put 'twenty', and in certain copies the 'five' have been added from Theodotion.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.8.16":["And he said to me: Surely you have seen, son of man; turning again you will see abominations greater than these. And he brought me into the inner court of the house of the Lord, and behold, at the door of the temple of the Lord, between the vestibule and the altar, about twenty-five men with their backs toward the temple of the Lord and their faces toward the east, and they were worshiping toward the rising of the sun. What we have translated 'vestibule' following Symmachus, who put πρόπυλον [gateway], where the Seventy and Theodotion translated the Hebrew word itself, 'aelam', Aquila's first edition translated προστάδα [porch] and the second the 'aelam' of the temple; this we can express as the 'portico' of the temple, or the unroofed 'court' which was between the temple and the altar.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.8.17":["And he said to me: Surely you have seen, son of man; is it a light thing to the house of Judah that they should do these abominations which they have done here, because, filling the earth with iniquity, they have turned to provoke me? And behold, they put the branch to their nostrils. (Where we said: And behold, they put the branch to their nostrils, the Seventy translated: And behold, they themselves like men sneering, to which there was added from Theodotion: They stretch out the branch, so that the whole together reads: And behold, they themselves stretch out the branch as if sneering, for which Symmachus translated: As if sending out a sound in the likeness of a song through his nostrils.) It signifies that the twenty-five men, who are set firm in a square in a solid station and, from the five senses, by five times five form the figure of a square, not only have the temple behind their back in figure, but, after the manner of idols, put the branch to their nostrils — no doubt a branch of the palms which in the Greek language they call βαΐα —, so that by this it may signify that they adore idols. Indeed Job too says that among his other virtues he had this as well: that never, looking upon the sky and the sun and the moon and the shining stars, had he kissed his hand, that is, adored creatures. But Symmachus' translation signifies a foul and hoarse sound proceeding from the nostrils in contempt of God. And all heretics, who have filled the earth with iniquity and have turned to the knowledge falsely so called, so as to provoke the Lord to anger, must be reckoned, when they have wished to praise God, rather to blaspheme with a foul sound, or to hold the God of the Old Testament in low esteem, to deride, to despise.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.8.18":["Therefore I too will act in fury: my eye will not spare, nor will I have mercy, (and what follows:) and when they cry to my ears with a loud voice, I will not hear them (is not found in the Seventy). 'For all these things,' he says, 'which they have done, I too will act in fury and my eye will not spare, nor will I have mercy'. Hearing this, the heretics of old slander the Creator as cruel and bloodthirsty, and do not consider that the apostle Paul, who is surely the apostle of the good God — as they themselves would have it —, writes to the Corinthians: If I come again I will not spare, so as to chastise offenders with the rod and draw back the erring to salvation; for those who do not understand what is profitable for them, and often pray for contrary things, it is expedient that they not be heard by the Lord. Hence in the prayer too we say: Your will be done; not our will, which is accustomed to err, but your will, which knows the things to come; and it is sometimes a great happiness not to merit mercy for the present. These, therefore, who are of the house of Judah and have withdrawn from the confession of the Church, the Lord will not spare; and when they cry out with a loud voice, of which the Lord said: Their cry has come to me, the Lord will nevertheless not hear them, so that, compelled by evils, they may understand what they have done.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.9.1":["And he cried in my ears with a loud voice, saying: The visitations of the city have drawn near. For 'visitations' the Seventy translated 'vengeance': for Mine indeed is vengeance, and I will repay, says the Lord, and every vengeance is a visitation, as of one who is sick, as of one who has wounds, as of one who awaits the hands of physicians, according to what is written elsewhere: I will visit their iniquities with the rod, and their sins with scourges; but my mercy I will not take away from them. And rightly, as we said above, visitation or vengeance is said to draw near, with the captivity close at hand.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","And each one has a vessel of slaughter in his hand. He did not say 'had', as the Seventy translated, for he is not narrating things past, but shows things present and future. Whoever, therefore, strikes the wicked in that they are wicked, and has vessels of slaughter to kill the worst, is a minister of the Lord.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.9.2-3":["And behold, six men were coming from the way of the upper gate which looks toward the north, and each one a vessel of destruction in his hand; and one man in their midst clothed in linen, and a writer's inkhorn at his reins; and they went in and stood beside the bronze altar. And the glory of the Lord of Israel was taken up from the Cherub over whom it was, to the threshold of the house; and he called the man who was clothed in linen and had a writer's inkhorn at his loins. For 'linen', which we translated following Symmachus, the Seventy translated ποδήρη [a robe reaching to the feet], Theodotion the Hebrew word itself, 'baddim', Aquila 'choice things'; again, where we said: And a writer's inkhorn at his reins, the Seventy put: And a sapphire girdle upon his reins, Theodotion: Κάστυ [inkhorn] of the scribe in his hand, Aquila: Μελανοδοχεῖον (that is, Inkhorn) of the scribe at his loins, Symmachus: He had a writer's tablets at his reins; and where we translated 'threshold of the house', the Seventy and Theodotion put τὸ αἴθριον, that is, a 'vestibule' under the open sky; for 'vessel' also, which each of the men had in his hands, the Seventy alone translated 'axe'. To blot out, therefore, the sins of men, who were made on the sixth day of the fashioning of the world, six men come and descend from the upper gate (or, as the Seventy translated, 'the high one') toward the north; and each one had an axe in his hands, of which John speaks: For already the axe has been laid to the roots of the trees; every tree that does not bear fruit will be cut down and sent into the fire. And in the midst of the six men was one, that is, a seventh, in the attire of a high priest, clothed in linen garments, and he had a writer's inkhorn at his reins, with which to write down the sins of all and to separate the number of the saints from the sinners; or a sapphire girdle, of the color of the sapphire stone which is among the ornaments of the chief of the priests. And the six men went out and stood beside the bronze altar — for there were two altars: one of incense, of gold, within, and the other before the temple, the bronze altar of holocausts. They are said to stand beside the altar ready for the command of the one giving orders, so that whoever they saw had not had his sins forgiven there, they might know him to be subject to the sentence of the Lord and to slaughter —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","Some interpret the six men as six angels who obey the will of God, and understand that one, who had the high priest's garments, as the Savior, according to the fact that the angel of great counsel is a priest, and to what is said: You are a priest forever according to the order of Melchizedek. Now ποδήρη, which the Seventy translated, is one of the eight garments of the high priest, which in Latin is called 'talaris' [ankle-length]; and from the fact that it comes down to the feet, it was allotted this name — I know that many years ago I published a book on the vesture of the high priest, to whose explanation I send the reader, for I cannot say everything in every place —. And as to what Aquila's first edition and Theodotion called κάστυ, for which in the Hebrew one reads 'cesath': when I asked a Hebrew what it meant, he answered me 'that in the Greek tongue it is called καλαμάριον [pen-case], from the fact that pens are stored in it'; we say 'atramentarium' [inkhorn], from the fact that it holds atramentum [ink]; many, more expressively, call them 'thecae' [cases], from the fact that they are cases for writers' pens. Of the four Cherubim, one Cherub is sent from the glory of the Lord's majesty, to speak to him who was clothed in the vestments of the high priest — and although most think that τά χερουβεὶμ [the cherubim] is said in the neuter gender and the plural number, we ought to know that 'Cherub' is in the singular number, of the masculine gender, and in the plural, of the same gender, 'Cherubim'; not that there is sex among the ministers of God, but that each thing is called by different genders according to the property of its own language: angels are called in the plural number 'Malachim', and 'Cherubim' and 'Seraphim', of the same gender and number; whereas 'sabaoth', which means 'of warfares' or 'of hosts' and 'of powers', are said in the feminine gender and the plural number, and end in the final syllable 'oth'. — Those, however, who wish the Savior to be understood under the figure of the high priest use an example from the Revelation of John, in which it is written: And I turned and saw the voice that was speaking with me; and having turned I beheld seven golden lampstands, and in the midst of the lampstands one like a son of man.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.9.3":["The glory of the Lord of Israel also went up, or was taken up, from the one Cherub, and went to the threshold, or to the open-air part of the court of the house and of the vestibule, which is in no way pressed down by a roof but enjoys the freedom of the air. This very glory of the Lord called the man who was in the attire of a high priest and had the inkhorn at his loins, and said what Scripture attests.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.9.4":["And the Lord said to him: Pass through the midst of the city, in the midst of Jerusalem, and mark a tau upon the foreheads of the men who groan and grieve over all the abominations that are done in its midst. And to those he said, in my hearing: Pass through the city following him, strike; let your eye not spare, nor have pity: old man, youth and virgin, little child and women, kill to utter destruction; anyone upon whom you see the tau, do not kill, and begin from my sanctuary. He who was clothed in an ankle-length garment, or a linen one, and displayed priestly attire, is commanded to put a sign upon the foreheads of the men who groan and grieve over all the abominations that are done in the midst of Jerusalem. For 'sign', which the Seventy, Aquila and Symmachus translated, Theodotion put the Hebrew itself, 'tau', which among the Hebrews is the last of the twenty-two letters, so as to show perfect knowledge in the men who groan and grieve; or, as the Hebrews assert, because 'law' is called 'thora' among them, which is written with this letter at the beginning of its name, those received this seal who had fulfilled the precepts of the law. And to come to our own: in the ancient letters of the Hebrews, which the Samaritans use to this day, the last letter, 'tau', has the likeness of a cross, which is painted on the foreheads of Christians and is signed by the frequent tracing of the hand; there are those who think that, from the fact that this is the last letter according to the Hebrew alphabet, it is shown that in the multitude of sinners a remnant of the saints survives. Those who groan and grieve, therefore, are saved, who not only did not consent to evil works but also lamented the sins of others, as Samuel too lamented over Saul, and the apostle Paul over those who after sinning did not do penance; whence he himself also said: We who are in the tabernacle groan, and elsewhere: I have great sorrow, and unceasing pain of my heart.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.9.5-6":["And the six men are commanded, apart from those who can say: The light of your countenance has been signed upon us, O Lord, to kill all: to spare not the old man, not the young man, not the virgin, not the little child, not the woman — according to the mystical senses, the old men are those in the Church of whom it is said: A man's gray hairs are his prudence; the young men, those who are most ready for the battles of faith; the virgins, those who keep chastity with their whole mind; the little children, those who are given the milk of infancy to drink and do not yet take solid food; the women, those who attest the frailty of their sex by the weakness of their soul — ; none of whom is spared if they believe themselves to be anything apart from the seal of Christ.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.9.6-7":["They began, therefore, with the elder men who were before the face of the house. And he said to them: Defile the house and fill the courts with the slain. For 'courts', which in Hebrew are called 'aseroth', the Seventy put 'ways'. Now the elder men are slain, of whom we read above that they had censers and burned incense to idols. Nor is there any sanctity of the temple left, because, the God of religion having been offended, all things have been defiled, so that whence came the sin, thence may come the judgment. And the courts in the temple are filled with corpses, and not the ways, which surely were outside, unless perhaps we can understand the ways as the streets of the city. Nor could the angels have dared to defile with the blood of the dead the temple in which the glory of God had formerly dwelt, had not the Lord of the departing glory commanded this. Everyone who is dead through sin defiles the courts of the temple and the ways of the city of the Lord in which he lives; he who rises with Christ ceases to be dead.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.9.6":["And what follows: And begin from my sanctuary (or, as the Seventy translated: And begin from my holy ones) signifies either the priests who busied themselves in the temple and worshiped idols, or those who, by the merit of their priesthood, were called holy among the people and who had been the cause of sin among the peoples, and these were the first to deserve punishment: for it is time for judgment to begin from the house of God.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","You who are first in riches will be the first to bear the yoke of captivity, according to what is written in Ezekiel: Begin from my sanctuary. Not from the saints, as most people think, but from the overthrow of the temple, which was holy.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"EZK.9.7":["And they went out, and struck those who were in the city. (Or, as others translated: They struck the city, naming the city itself in place of those who were in the city.)\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.9.8":["And when the slaughter was complete, I remained, and I fell upon my face and, crying out, I say: Alas, Lord God; will you then destroy all the remnant of Israel, pouring out your fury upon Jerusalem? When all who did not have the seal had been laid low in the slaughter, the prophet fell on his face, thinking, because of the multitude of the slain, that no one but himself had remained. Because this seemed to be contrary to the sentence by which the Lord had ordered that the men with signed foreheads were not to be slain, it was for that reason removed in the common edition; but we, following the Hebrew truth, have put: I remained; and it must be noted that he had not said 'alone', for had he said that, it would have seemed contrary, but: I remained, so that 'with the others' who had sealed foreheads may be understood — and that we may know that this was said pointedly: I remained: in the volume of Kings, when Elijah speaks to God: They have torn down your altars, and I have been left alone, and they seek to take away my life, he put 'alone' because he did not know that others had remained —. Some think that this can also be understood from the person of the Lord, in whose type Ezekiel went before, concerning the people of the Jews: When all turned aside, together they became useless; and the prophet testifies: Save me, O Lord, for the holy one has failed; for the Lord was found 'alone' who did no sin, nor was guile found in his mouth. And as to what he added: Pouring out your fury upon Jerusalem, the word of pouring out shows the greatness of the punishments, as we read elsewhere: Contempt has been poured out upon princes, and again: My steps had almost been poured out, and in a good sense: The love of God has been poured out in our hearts, and: Grace has been poured out on your lips, and in the contrary sense: Pour out your fury upon the nations that have not understood you, and upon the kingdoms that have not called upon your name, and: Pour out the spear and close in from the opposite side against those who persecute me.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.9.9":["And he said to me: The iniquity of the house of Israel and of Judah is great, exceedingly, very much; and the land is filled with bloods, and the city is filled with turning away. For they said: The Lord has forsaken the land, and: The Lord does not see; therefore my eye too will not spare, nor will I have pity: I will repay their ways upon their head. The Lord, seeing his prophet weep for the people and marvel at the greatness of the indignation, gives the reasons, not, as he thought, of a punishment unjust or exceeding measure, but of a deserved and just sentence: 'The iniquity,' he says, 'of the ten tribes of Israel and of the two tribes of Judah is great, and not only great but exceedingly great'; nor was it enough to have said this, but he added 'very much', so that the greatness of the punishment might match the greatness of the iniquity. From this we learn that sins are not equal, as many think and especially the Stoics, but either great or small, and that according to the quality and measure of those who sin there is diversity in the punishing judgment. The land, he says, is filled with bloods (or, as the Seventy translated, with peoples), and the city is filled with turning away (or, as the common edition has, with iniquity and uncleanness); no moderate amount of blood was shed, but from gate to gate, and the whole city turned aside from the worship of God, and in its place it is full of uncleanness, namely the filth of idolatry. The cause of such great crimes is this: that they thought there was no providence over the earth, and that God did not care for mortal things, according to what we read elsewhere: Of course this is a labor for those above, this is a care that troubles them in their quiet! ...\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.9.10":["Since, therefore, they thought either that there was no providence, or that the providence which had formerly been among the peoples had at this time abandoned its own people, for that reason God's eye too will not spare, nor will he have pity: so that, raging against vices, he may be placable to virtues, and may repay their ways and sins upon their heads, either upon the ruling part of the heart or upon the princes of the people, according to the book of Numbers, in which the heads of the peoples are said to be princes.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.9.11":["And behold, the man who was clothed in linen, who had the inkhorn at his loins, answered, saying: I have done as you commanded me. (LXX: \"And behold, the man who was clothed in a podere — that is, an ankle-length garment —, and girded as to his loins with a girdle, and he answered a word, saying: I have done as you charged me.\") He is the man who was ordered to pass through the midst of Jerusalem and to put a sign on the foreheads of the men groaning and grieving over all their iniquities. He says, therefore, that he has fulfilled the Lord's commands and has armed the foreheads of those who mourn with the seal of the letter 'tau'. But the six men, whom the Lord had commanded, saying: Pass through the midst of the city following him, and strike; and have pity on no one, to utter destruction, report nothing of the kind; for to carry out a sentence that is proved not by word but by deed is a matter not of gladness but of grief. And in this place, for 'podere', that is, 'ankle-length garment', which the Seventy translated, Theodotion put the Hebrew word itself, 'baddim', Symmachus 'linen', Aquila 'choice' or 'robe'; for 'girdle' also Symmachus put 'tablets', Aquila 'inkhorn', Theodotion κάστυ.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.10.1":["And I looked, and behold, in the firmament that was above the head of the Cherubim, as it were a sapphire stone, as it were the appearance of the likeness of a throne appeared above it. And he spoke to the man who was clothed in linen, and said: Go in into the midst of the wheels that are beneath the Cherub, and fill your hands with coals of fire that are between the Cherubim, and pour them out upon the city. Concerning the firmament that was above the Cherub, and the sapphire stone that had the likeness of a throne, and the wheels that followed the living creatures, let it suffice to have spoken above, and let the reader be content with the explanation of those things.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.10.2":["But now, as for the command given to the man who was clothed in linen — for which the Seventy in this place translated 'robe' — that he should take coals of fire from the midst of the Cherubim and pour them out, or scatter them, upon the city, it seems to me to show this: that after the slaying and slaughter of many, and the corpses of the dead in the streets, coals of fire are taken up to purge, or cleanse, Jerusalem, according to what is written in Isaiah: He will sanctify him in burning fire, and will devour the material like hay. These are the coals which, as a remedy for the tongue and for lying, laying waste vices and sins, the prophet, when questioned, longs for: What shall be given to you, and what shall be added to you against a deceitful tongue? The sharp arrows of the mighty, with desolating coals; and we shall be able to find many things of this kind in the holy Scriptures, of which we have often spoken. This only is to be noted: that in Isaiah, who had sinned only in speech and had said that he had unclean lips, a single coal is taken up with tongs to purge him; here, however, since the city was full of iniquity and, after the punishments, of the corpses of the dead, many coals of fire are taken up, and not with one hand but with both, so that all Jerusalem may be purged — although this too may be understood: that the coals of fire are to be taken up not for purging but for punishments and torments, to be poured out upon Jerusalem —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","And he went in in my sight. Now the Cherubim were standing on the right side of the house when the man went in, and a cloud filled the inner court. And the glory of the Lord was lifted up from above the Cherub to the threshold of the house, and the house was filled with the cloud, and the court was filled with the brightness of the glory of the Lord. And the sound of the wings of the Cherubim was heard as far as the outer court, like the voice of God almighty speaking. And when he had commanded the man who was clothed in linen, saying: Take fire from the midst of the wheels that are between the Cherubim, he went in and stood beside the wheel; and a Cherub stretched out his hand from the midst of the Cherubim to the fire that was between the Cherubim, and took it, and gave it into the hand of him who was clothed in linen; who, receiving it, went out. And there appeared in the Cherubim the likeness of a man's hand beneath their wings. While the prophet looks on, the man goes in who was clothed in a garment reaching to the feet, or in linen — and, as the Seventy now change their rendering, in a 'holy robe', although among the Hebrews there is one and the same word, 'baddim' [linen garments], and the other translators persist in the rendering they began with —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.10.3-5":["Now as the man who was clothed in linen garments went in, the Cherubim were standing on the right side of the house, so that the holy powers on high might be seen to hold the right-hand part of the house of God, and those that are sent for punishments — of whom it is written: A sending through most evil angels — may be believed to occupy the left-hand parts. And when the glory of the Lord had been lifted up from the Cherub who was about to hand the coals of fire to the man, and had passed over to the threshold of the house, at once the inner court is filled with cloud, darkness and gloom; for punishments are not carried out while the majesty of the Lord is present, and the glory of the Lord is seen in the court, of which it is written: The sound of the wings of the Cherubim was heard as far as the outer court. For 'sound' the Hebrew has 'voice', and this voice had the likeness of the voice of God almighty speaking — for which the Seventy translated 'like the voice of God saddai', which Aquila, Symmachus and Theodotion translated as ἱκανόν, which signifies 'strong and mighty' —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.10.6-7":["And he goes in, as is said a second time, into the midst of the wheels that are beneath the Cherubim, to fill his hands with coals of fire. When he had gone in, he only stood beside the wheel and, stunned either by sorrow for Jerusalem that was to be punished or by the greatness of the glory, did not himself put his hand to the fire; but one of the Cherubim, that is, a Cherub, put out his hand and took the fire that was in the midst of the Cherubim and gave it into the hand of him who was clothed in linen garments; who, receiving it, went out; and yet Scripture does not say what he did when he had gone out, so as to leave the narration of a sad event to our understanding rather than set it before our eyes.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.10.8":["Note, reader, in what points the former vision and this one agree or disagree, so that from the comparison of the two, without any prompting from us, you may understand the divine mysteries; note also what I pointed out before: how in the firmament, and in the throne, and in the hand of a man, not the reality but the likeness is spoken of; for we do not contemplate the glory of the Lord face to face with unveiled countenance, but we see all things in likeness and image, as long as we are encompassed by a frail and corruptible body.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.10.9":["And I looked, and behold, four wheels beside the Cherubim; one wheel beside one Cherub, and another wheel beside one Cherub, and the appearance of the wheels was like the sight of a chrysolite stone, and their aspect was one likeness for the four, as if a wheel were in the midst of a wheel. And when they went in four directions, they went and did not turn back as they went, but to the place to which the first one turned to go, the others also followed and did not turn about. And their whole body and necks and hands and wings and rims were full of eyes round about the four wheels; and these wheels he called whirling, in my hearing. Now one had four faces: the one face the face of a Cherub, and the second face the face of a man, and in the third the face of a lion, and in the fourth the face of an eagle. And the Cherubim were lifted up. Observe that earlier, according to the Seventy, the appearance of the wheels was Like the sight of 'tharsis', but now, according to the same: Like the sight of 'carbuncle', so that by the fire that is handed over by the Cherub a likeness of the punishments may be shown — although in the Hebrew, both above and here, 'tharsis' is written, which Aquila translated 'chrysolite' and Symmachus 'hyacinth' —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.10.12":["And again, where we rendered: And their whole 'body', with 'of the wheels' understood, in the Hebrew it is written: And all their 'fleshes', and necks and hands and wings and rims; note that flesh is spoken of in the heavenly powers, so that, when we read: Flesh and blood shall not possess the kingdom of God, nor corruption incorruption, we may know that not the substance of the flesh but its works are condemned: for 'basar', which is written in this place, signifies not 'body' but 'flesh'. Now by the rims of the wheels he meant the iron tires by which the roundness of the wood is bound and held tight. These wheels, moreover, have not only fleshes, or a body, or members, but also necks, hands and wings, so that in each of these we may perceive the workings of things, not images of members.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.10.13-15":["Further, what follows: In my hearing, up to the place where it is written: The Cherubim were lifted up, is not found in the Seventy but has been added from the Hebrew.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.10.13":["The 'wheels' themselves, too, are called in the Hebrew tongue 'gelgel', which Symmachus translated 'whirling' and Aquila 'wheel'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.10.14":["In this it must be observed that it is by no means as is said above: The face of a man, and the face of a lion four, and the face of a calf on the left four, and the face of an eagle four on the right, so that, of course, some might be believed to be on the left and others on the right; rather, an equal order of them all is described, as Scripture says: Now one had four faces: the one face the face of a Cherub, and the second face the face of a man, and in the third the face of a lion, and in the fourth the face of an eagle, so that the first and second and third and fourth face is described not by a difference of place, right and left, but in a single order, by degrees. Now the one face, that is, the first, he called Cherub, where above he had said the face of a calf; from which it is understood that the face of a Cherub is the face of a calf. The sense of all these things is contained in the explanation of the former vision.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.10.15":["But as for what is said: This is the living creature that I had seen beside the river Chebar, Scripture shows that in the exposition of the living creature we ought to understand the same things that we understood above.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.10.17":["This is the living creature that I had seen beside the river Chebar. And when the Cherubim went, the wheels also went alongside them; and when the Cherubim lifted their wings to be raised up from the earth, the wheels did not hold back, but they too were beside them. When those stood, these stood, and when those were lifted up, these were lifted up, for the spirit of life was in them. All things live to God and perceive their own Creator: He is not, you see, the God of the dead, but of the living. Hence also all the bodies of the wheels are full of eyes. And in the psalm it is said: The heavens tell the works of God, and the firmament proclaims the works of his hands. Day to day pours forth a word, and night to night shows knowledge; the sun too and the moon, the sea and the rivers, the mountains and the hills praise God, and the elements revolve in their own circuit year by year, and we discern on earth the order of the heavens, so that, keeping the order of the ancient law, they revolve and run, and we see nothing new upon the earth.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.10.18-19":["And the glory of the Lord went out from the threshold of the temple, and stood above the Cherubim; and the Cherubim, lifting their wings, were raised up from the earth before me, and as they went out the wheels also followed; and it stood at the entrance of the eastern gate of the house of the Lord, and the glory of the God of Israel was above them. This is the living creature that I saw beneath the God of Israel beside the river Chebar, and I understood that they were Cherubim. Four by four faces to one, and four wings to one, and the likeness of a man's hand under their wings; and the likeness of their faces, the very faces that I had seen beside the river Chebar, and their gaze, and the onrush of each to go forward before its face. The glory of the Lord, or the majesty that had stood at the threshold of the temple, stood above the Cherubim, which, lifting their wings, were raised up while the prophet watched, the wheels following alike, and it stood at the entrance of the eastern gate of the house of the Lord, and little by little the protection of God left Israel. First it was in the temple; then it stood in the court of the temple, or at the threshold, and as it withdrew from the temple, cloud and gloom filled the inner recesses of the temple; and again the glory itself sat upon the Cherubim and, as they at once flew off and went away to the outer parts, the wheels followed; and the glory stood at the eastern gate of the house of the Lord, not at the door of the temple, but beyond the court and the threshold, at the entrance of the eastern gate of the house of the Lord.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.10.20-22":["When the prophet had seen this: This is, he says, the living creature that I saw beneath the God of Israel beside the river Chebar, and I understood that they were Cherubim. For what new thing did he see, that he should understand that they were Cherubim, which he had not understood before? 'Cherubim' in our language is 'multitude of knowledge', the knowledge of the mysteries of God, and his throne and resting place; hence also in the psalm it is said: You who sit upon the Cherubim, be made manifest; for the glory of the Lord always sits in the multitude of knowledge, and it is made manifest then: when all things are shown to be ruled by providence, and nothing to happen by chance, and no one to say: The Lord has forsaken the earth, and the Lord does not see; hence also the gaze of the Cherubim and their desire is to forget the things that are past and to stretch themselves forward to the things to come.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.11.1":["And the spirit lifted me up, and brought me in to the eastern gate of the house of the Lord, which looks toward the rising of the sun. Since the glory of the Lord had gone forth from the threshold of the temple and stood above the Cherubim, and afterward, as the Cherubim flew away, the glory itself stood at the entrance of the eastern gate of the house of the Lord, the prophet too — he could not go to it by himself — was lifted up by the spirit and led to the same eastern gate of the house of the Lord, which looks toward the sun of righteousness, of whom it is written: Behold a man, the Orient is his name, so that he should by no means remain in the temple, which was confounded by the cloud of ignorance, but, standing in the gate of the house of the Lord on the east, might come to know the mysteries that follow.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","And behold, at the entrance of the gate twenty-five men, and I saw in their midst Jaazaniah the son of Azur and Pelatiah the son of Benaiah, princes of the people. And he said to me: Son of man, these are the men who devise iniquity, and handle a most wicked counsel in this city, saying: Were not the houses built long ago? This is the cauldron, and we are the flesh. Therefore prophesy concerning them; prophesy, son of man. And the spirit of the Lord rushed upon me and said to me: Speak. Thus says the Lord: So have you spoken, house of Israel, and the thoughts of your heart I know. You have killed very many in this city, and you have filled its streets with the slain. Therefore thus says the Lord God: Your slain, whom you have laid in its midst, they are the flesh, and this is the cauldron; and I will bring you out of its midst. You have feared the sword, and the sword I will bring upon you, says the Lord God. And I will cast you out of its midst, and I will give you into the hand of enemies, and I will execute judgments upon you. By the sword you shall fall; in the borders of Israel I will judge you, and you shall know that I am the Lord. This shall not be to you for a cauldron, and you shall not be in its midst for flesh: in the borders of Israel I will judge you; and you shall know that I am the Lord. (What follows is not found in the Seventy but has been added from the Hebrew:) Because you have not walked in my precepts, and you have not done my judgments, but you have acted according to the judgments of the nations which are round about you. The prophet is taken up, according to what is written: The Lord taking up the meek, or he is lifted up from the earth and brought in to the eastern gate of the house of the Lord, so that he may come to know the mysteries of the twenty-five men who were at the entrance of the gate, and of Jaazaniah the son of Azur and Pelatiah the son of Benaiah, princes of the people.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","And since what is said is plain according to the letter, setting aside for a little while the names of Jaazaniah and Azur, Pelatiah and Benaiah, which we reserve for another time, this must be said: that to this day in the Church, which is the house of God, both before the eastern gate and at the entrance, or in the way, of the gate, which signifies the Savior, through whom we enter to the Father, there are twenty-five men, referring all things to the senses — and, so far as it does not escape my memory, I have never been able to find this number in a good sense, although in Leviticus men are chosen for the priestly ministry from twenty-five years of age; for in the Hebrew it does not have this number which is given in the Seventy, but the number thirty, which is contained in the opening of this prophet and in the age of the Lord when he came to the streams of the Jordan and was baptized by John; but if in the Gospel five wise virgins and five foolish are found, let us know that this number is placed in the middle and, according to the quality of those who use it, can be applied either to the good or to the bad side —. These men, then, who refer all things to the senses, and have two princes, of whom we spoke above, are contained in the dual number, which splits unity and, in the number of the second day, is not seen by the Lord, according to the Hebrew truth — whence also into the ark of Noah the unclean are brought in two and two; but the Lord, drawing the division together into one, made both one and loosed the middle wall, condemning the enmities in his flesh; whence also he says something more august and more sublime: I and the Father are one, so that he may call us back from the Jewish duality into the union of the Christian faith; finally, when the high priesthood of the Jews had been lost, the Lord suffers by no means under one prince, but under two, Annas and Caiaphas, so that he might show the divided error of their religion —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.11.2-12":["There are many at the entrance of this gate, before the eastern house of the Lord, who by their vices are compared to the nations, and from the consciousness of their sins despair of salvation, and say: 'The city in which we dwell is a cauldron, and we are all flesh, and we shall be consumed by the Babylonian burning, whose darts are fiery', unwilling to do penance and despairing of salvation; therefore they hear that they themselves are not the flesh of the past and lost city, but those whom they have scandalized, whom they have killed; and for that reason the sword is brought upon them: so that, after they have been judged in the borders of Israel, by no means among the nations but among Christians, then they may know that he himself is the Lord, according to what is read in the Psalms: When he slew them, they sought him, so that him whom they had not perceived through benefits, they may come to know through torments.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.11.2-3":["These, then, who were at the entrance of the eastern gate of the house of the Lord, despairing of salvation and, from the consciousness of their crimes, ready for destruction, and unwilling to correct their sins through repentance, say: 'Although the houses which had earlier collapsed have lately, after the first captivity, been built, yet we know that this city is like a cauldron and we are for flesh, so that in it we may be consumed and burned up together, according to what is written in Isaiah: The tempest, if it passes through, will not take us'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.11.4-10":["As they say these things, the spirit of the Lord rushed upon the prophet, and commanded him a second time to prophesy and to say: 'Since you have said these things, and the secrets of your hearts do not escape me, I will interpret to you how the city is to be reckoned a cauldron and you flesh, not according to the sense in which you spoke, but according to another which you do not fear: the city is indeed in the likeness of a cauldron, but it will not be filled with your flesh, but with that of those whom you have killed; you, however, who think that you will die in this city, I will bring out of the midst of the city and hand over into the hands of enemies, and I will execute judgments upon you, so that, when you have fallen by the sword, by no means in the city nor outside the bounds of Israel but in the borders of your own province, then too you may know that I am the Lord'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.11.11-12":["And a second time it is said: 'In the borders of Israel I will judge you, and you shall know that I am the Lord; and all these things you will suffer, because you have not walked in my precepts and have not done my judgments, but have wrought the crimes of the other nations round about'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.11.13":["And it came to pass, while I was prophesying, Pelatiah the son of Benaiah died; and I fell on my face, crying out with a loud voice, and I said: Alas, alas, alas, Lord God, you are making a consummation of the remnant of Israel. As the prophet prophesies, one of the two princes, Pelatiah the son of Benaiah, dies, whose name means 'a fall turning aside', and he is the son of a 'builder' or 'mason' — for οἰκοδόμος cannot be rendered into our language otherwise —; the other of the princes, Jaazaniah, is kept safe — Jaazaniah signifies 'his ear', by which 'of the soul' is understood; Azur, however, 'support' or 'help' —. He, then, who by falling had turned aside from the Lord and was of a most wicked building, rightly falls while he whom God strengthened is prophesying; but he who obeyed the precepts of God and was sustained and supported by his help rightly remains in command of the people — we read in Exodus that, when Pharaoh king of Egypt had died, the sons of Israel groaned from the works of clay, of straw and of brick, and cried out to the Lord, upon whom, while he lived, they could not call; Isaiah also, when Uzziah the leprous king had died, saw the Lord sitting upon a throne high and lifted up, and the Seraphim standing around him, and, understanding that he had unclean lips and dwelt among a people having unclean lips, he merits that a coal be sent from the altar and purge his unclean lips; here, however, as the strength of God prophesies, the prince who had turned aside from the service of the Lord falls, so that he may be raised up by Christ: for if the Lord is the resurrection, the devil is the fall; in which it is asked how it is said of the Savior: Behold, this one is set for the fall and the resurrection of many in Israel: for the fall of those who stood most wickedly, and the resurrection of such as had fallen —. When the prophet had seen this, he fell on his face, not by the desert of one who sins but with the feeling of one who mourns, and with a loud voice, which came from the great ardor of faith, he cried out three times and said: Alas, alas, alas, Lord God, you are making a consummation of the remnant of Israel; and the sense is: 'Even the very remnant which seemed to be among the people is being destroyed in your indignation'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.11.14-16":["The word of the Lord, therefore, came to Ezekiel, who had said: Alas, alas, alas, Lord God, you are making a consummation of the remnant of Israel, namely in the death of Pelatiah and of those who were killed with him: that by no means were those the remnant which the prophet supposed in Jerusalem, but those who were seen to be captives in Babylon; and the sense is: 'O son of man, your brothers, begotten of the same stock with you, your brothers, I say, to whom the inhabitants of Jerusalem said: You have gone away from the Lord, because you are captives and have handed yourselves over with Jeconiah, but to us the land of Israel has been given for an everlasting possession — say, therefore, to them that I have indeed made them far off from the land of Israel and dispersed them into the nations and ordered them to be in foreign lands, but, because they have obeyed my command, I shall be among them, even in strange and hostile places, for a little sanctification, while very many are found among them who by no means submit their necks to idolatry but are mindful of the commandments of God', as Daniel and the three youths were, and it must be believed that others were, after the likeness of such great princes as Scripture now shows. From all of which we learn that we must not insult those who by the judgment of God have been handed over to punishments, nor reproach a man converted from his sins, nor ever say to those who do penance and have gone out of the Church: You have gone far away from the Lord; to us the land has been given for a possession, because thus says the Lord: 'Although I have separated them for a little while from my Church and dispersed them among the nations, yet I will be to them for a small sanctification while they remember their sins, and those who had departed from the land of God because of their vices, may hasten, because of repentance, to return to their former seats'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.11.15":["And the word of the Lord came to me, saying: Son of man, your brothers, your brothers, the men your kinsmen and the whole house of Israel, all to whom the inhabitants of Jerusalem said: Go far away from the Lord; to us the land has been given for a possession. Therefore thus says the Lord God: Because I have made them far off among the nations, and because I have scattered them into the land, I will be to them for a small sanctification in the lands to which they have come. (LXX: \"And the word of the Lord came to me, saying: Son of man, your brothers, your brothers and the men of your captivity and the whole house of Israel is consummated, to whom the inhabitants of Jerusalem said: You have gone far away from the Lord; to us the land has been given for an inheritance. Therefore say: Adonai the Lord says: Because I will drive them away into the nations, and I will sow them abroad into every land, and I will be to them for a little sanctification in the regions into which they shall have entered there.\") First we must speak of the variety in translation. Where the Hebrew has 'men your kinsmen' — for this is what 'goolathach' means —, the Seventy translated 'men of your captivity'; then, where there follows: All to whom the inhabitants of Jerusalem said, in place of 'all' they put 'is consummated', because of the ambiguity of the letters and of the word: for 'chollo', which Aquila and Symmachus and Theodotion translated 'all', they rendered 'is consummated', which does not accord with the sense at all.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.11.17-21":["Therefore speak: Thus says the Lord God: I will gather you from the peoples, and I will assemble you from the lands in which you have been scattered, and I will give you the soil of Israel. And they shall enter there, and shall take away all the offenses, and all the abominations from it; and I will give them one heart (or another), and a new spirit I will bestow, in their inward parts; and I will take away the stony heart from their flesh, and I will give them a heart of flesh, that they may walk in my precepts, and keep my judgments and do them, and be to me for a people and I be to them for God. But those whose heart walks after their stumbling blocks and their abominations, their way I will set upon their own head, says the Lord God. The divine word speaks to those who were captives in the land of Babylon, and to the brothers of Ezekiel the prophet and to his kinsmen, to whom the inhabitants of Jerusalem said: You have gone far away from the Lord; to us the land has been given for a possession; and it speaks these things which follow: 'I will gather you from the peoples, and I will restore to you the land of Israel; and when you have entered, you will take away all the idols on account of which you had offended God, and I will give you one heart, of the fear and the service of God, so that you may by no means serve diverse idols, or another than the one you had before; and I will bestow a new spirit in your inward parts, according to what is written: Create a clean heart in me, and renew a holy spirit in my inward parts; and I will take away from you the stony heart — that is, a hard heart, according to what Stephen, the first martyr in Christ, says: Hard of neck and uncircumcised in heart —; and the heart of Pharaoh is hardened, so that he may not let the people of Israel go; and I will give you', he says, 'a heart of flesh, soft and tender, and one which can receive the commandments of God, so that they may be written on the fleshly tablets of the heart' — for this reason a soft heart is given and the hard heart is taken away: so that they may walk in his precepts and keep his judgments and be in the people of God, and the Lord, who before had been an adversary, may be to them for God —; 'but those who have by no means corrected their former sins by repentance, but have walked after their abominations, I will repay', he says, 'with what they deserve: let their ways be set upon their heads'. Most think that these things happened under Zerubbabel the son of Shealtiel, and under Joshua the son of Jehozadak, the great priest, and under Ezra and Nehemiah, to the tribe of Judah and to those who returned with them, and that the inhabitants of Jerusalem who were captured under Zedekiah king of Judah, or who fled with Jeremiah into Egypt, were dispersed into all lands, so that they by no means returned to the city of Jerusalem. But the full conversion, of those who were captives and of the remnant of Israel, is understood in Christ, when the remnant was saved, and in one day three thousand believed, and again five thousand, and others, of whom James speaks to the apostle Paul: You see, brother, so many thousands of believing Jews. These are all zealous for the law. But also daily the proud inhabitants of the city of Jerusalem, whose heart walks after their stumbling blocks and their abominations, earn the displeasure of God, and those who were outside, the stony heart having been taken away and a most soft heart received, return through repentance to the Church, and walk in the precepts of the Lord, and keep his judgments, and become the people of the Lord, and the Lord, whom they had previously offended, becomes God to them.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.11.22-23":["And the Cherubim lifted up their wings, and the wheels with them, and the glory of the God of Israel was above them; the glory of the Lord went up from the midst of the city, and stood upon the mountain which is to the east of the city. Little by little the glory of the Lord withdraws from Jerusalem: first, deserting the temple, it stands in the court or at the threshold of the house, and afterward at the entrance of the eastern gate; lastly, its wings lifted and the wheels following, it stands upon the mountain which is to the east of the city — no doubt it signifies the Mount of Olives, from which the Savior ascended to the Father; and the glory of the Lord, which had departed from the city of Jerusalem, stood upon the Mount of Olives as a sign of resurrection and of light, so that from there it might behold Jerusalem about to perish and about to burn —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.11.23":["And as for his saying: The glory went up from the midst of the city, in other words the Lord speaks to the disciples: Arise, let us go hence, and to the Jews: Your house will be left to you desolate; Josephus also relates that there was heard in the temple a voice of the angels and of the heavenly strength, which had previously been the city's defense: Let us go away from these seats. And, in a wondrous manner, to the present day the glory of the Lord, which deserted the temple, stands upon the Mount of Olives and, gleaming in the sign of the cross, looks upon the once Jewish temple, dissolved into embers and ashes.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.11.24-25":["And the spirit lifted me up, and brought me into Chaldea, to the exile, in a vision, in the spirit of God; and the vision which I had seen was taken away from me. And I spoke to the exile all the words of the Lord which he had shown me. Where we translated from the Hebrew truth: And the vision which I had seen was taken away from me, the Seventy translated: And I went up from the vision which I had seen; and both signify that the prophet was carried over to Jerusalem from Babylon not in the body but in the spirit, and, when the vision had been taken away which had led him in the spirit to Jerusalem and had shown him all the things which the preceding discourse narrated, he returned from himself, and spoke to the exile all the things which had been shown to him, namely to those of whom it is written above: I was sitting in my house, and the elders of Judah were sitting beside me, and the hand of the Lord God fell upon me there, and I saw. And, in a wondrous manner, while those who had come to visit him were sitting there, the prophet was beholding mystical visions, and he was absent from those who sat before him: absent in spirit, present in body. And all things happen so that those who were captives might receive consolation, in that they are to be led back into the land of Israel, and are to walk in the precepts of the Lord, and to be to him for a people, and he is to be to them for God; but those who have not been converted to repentance, but have walked after their abominations, are to receive what they have done. That in the holy Scriptures words are spoken in place of things, we have often pointed out.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.12.2":["And the word of the Lord came to me, saying: Son of man, you dwell in the midst of a provoking house, who have eyes to see and do not see, and ears to hear and do not hear, for it is a provoking house. (Where we have rendered: You dwell in the midst of a provoking house, the Seventy put: You dwell in the midst of their iniquities; but from what follows: For it is a provoking house, we understand that the former clause too fits this little verse.) We have often shown, too, that 'bitterness' is said in place of 'provocation', so that the sense is: 'You dwell in the midst of a house that turns God to bitterness, which, by the bitterness of its vices, makes God, who is by nature sweet and merciful, bitter and cruel'; otherwise we read of God: Taste and see that gracious (or sweet) is the Lord. The prophet, then, dwells in the midst of a people provoking God to bitterness, which has eyes and does not see, ears and does not hear, not through a defect of the senses but through the obstinacy of depravity; whence the Lord also says to the Jews: If you were blind, he says, you would not have sin, censuring and condemning in them a voluntary blindness. 'Since, therefore, they have eyes to see and do not see, ears to hear and do not hear, and they despise your words, teach them through an image and a picture, and show them by bodily figures, so that they may recognize the coming captivity not only by hearing but also with their eyes'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.12.3":["You therefore, son of man, make yourself vessels of exile, and you shall migrate by day before them; and you shall migrate from your place to another place in their sight, if perhaps they may look, for it is a provoking house. (LXX: \"And you, son of man, make yourself vessels of captivity, and be taken captive by day before them; and be taken captive from your place to another place in their sight, that they may see, for it is a provoking house.\") No one doubts that the whole content of this passage points to the future captivity of King Zedekiah of the tribe of Judah, who was taken together with Jerusalem. But since some would have the captivity of Zedekiah, which means 'righteous Lord', go before as a type of the Savior, who, descending from heavenly things to earthly, took on a human body, and would have these be the vessels of captivity, I therefore think the prudent and cautious reader must be forewarned: that we set these things down, lest we seem to pass over anything, but do not approve them; for a wicked king cannot go before as a figure of him who is the example of all piety. That Zedekiah was taken by the Babylonians and Chaldeans both Jeremiah the prophet and the history of Kings relate, and that he was led from place to place, that is, from Jerusalem to Babylon, and the rest of what Ezekiel says in what follows. But those who refer it to the Lord Jesus Christ think that his captivity and exile from place to place signify the descent from heavenly things to earthly.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.12.4-6":["And you shall carry out your vessels like the vessels of one going into exile by day in their sight, and you shall go out in the evening before them, as one migrating goes out. Before their eyes dig through the wall for yourself, and you shall go out through it; in their sight you shall carry on your shoulders, in the darkness you shall flee: you shall veil your face, and you shall not see the land, for I have given you as a portent to the house of Israel. (LXX: \"And you shall bring forth your vessels, vessels of captivity, by day in their eyes, and you shall go out in the evening in their sight, as a captive goes out. Dig for yourself in the wall, and you shall pass through it; in their sight you shall be carried on shoulders, and you shall go out hidden: you shall cover your face, and you shall not see the land, for I have given you as a portent to the house of Israel.\") The prophet is commanded to do in figure what Zedekiah is going to do in truth: 'Gather,' he says, 'your baggage — for these are the vessels of one going into exile — while they watch, and after they have seen what you are going to do, you shall go out in the evening in the likeness of one going into exile'; we read that Zedekiah fled by night, the wall having been dug under, to the deserts of the Jordan, and was there seized by the Babylonians, and that this is what the wall dug through signifies; and as for his saying: You shall carry on your shoulders, there is understood: your baggage and whatever those who flee are accustomed to carry for the comfort of the road; in the darkness you shall carry out: so that you may not be seen by anyone; you shall veil your face: either by the blindness that befell him, when his eyes were put out by Nebuchadnezzar, or so that it might not be recognized that he himself was the king and the vigilance of his guards become greater; and what follows: And you shall not see the land, signifies this: that he was led blind into Babylon and did not see it; for I have given you, he says, as a portent to the house of Israel: for both the words and the deeds of the prophets are for a sign and a figure — whence in Zechariah too they are called 'men of portent', because they portend figures; and in Hosea God says: By the hands of the prophets I have been likened —. Those, however, who want Christ to be understood in the person of Ezekiel take the vessels of captivity as the human body; and the wall dug through as that by which he broke through the bars of the underworld and, rising again, was seen by believers alone; and his being carried on shoulders as when he ascended as victor to heaven with the angelic powers; and that he goes out hidden and veils his face, so that the divine majesty may not be seen in mortal flesh; and that he does not see the land, lest that land be illumined by his eyes which does not deserve his gaze: for the eyes of the Lord are upon the righteous, and he turns his face away from the wicked. 'And all these things,' they say, 'he is related to have done so that, through the likeness of a man, he might turn the hard hearts of the Israelite people to faith; and he is given as a portent to the house of Israel, according to what is written: Behold, this one is set for the resurrection and for the ruin of many, and for a sign that will be spoken against'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.12.7":["So I did as he had commanded me: I brought out my vessels like the vessels of one migrating by day, and in the evening I dug through the wall for myself with my hand; in the darkness I went out, and I carried on my shoulders in their sight. (LXX: \"And I did so according to all that he had commanded me: and I brought out vessels like vessels of captivity by day, and toward evening I dug through the wall for myself with my hand, and I went out hidden, I was taken up upon shoulders in their sight.\") What he was commanded above to do, he testifies that he accomplished in deed, and it needs no interpretation, according to either sense, since it was expounded above. But this must be noted: that at one and the same time Jeremiah was prophesying in Jerusalem and Ezekiel in Babylon, and the prophecy of the former was sent to the captives, and that of the latter to those who dwelt in Jerusalem, so that the providence of the one God in different regions might be proven, and the hearers might understand that whatever befell the people came about by no means through the power of idols but by the command of the Lord.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.12.9-10":["And the word of the Lord came to me in the morning, saying: Son of man, have not the house of Israel, the provoking house, said to you: What are you doing? Say to them: Thus says the Lord God. 'If the provoking house asks you: Why are you doing these things? — or, according to the Seventy, even if they do not ask, you thrust it upon them, so that they may know why you have done these things —, say to them: Thus says the Lord God.'\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.12.10-16":["This burden is upon the prince who is in Jerusalem, and upon all the house of Israel which is in their midst. Say: I am your portent; as I have done, so shall it be done to them: into exile and into captivity they shall go; and the prince who is in their midst shall be carried on shoulders, he shall go out in the darkness; they shall dig through the wall to lead him out: his face shall be covered, so that he may not see the land with his eyes. And I will spread my net over him, and he shall be taken in my dragnet; and I will bring him to Babylon, into the land of the Chaldeans, and he shall not see it; there he shall die. And all who are around him, his guard and his troops, I will scatter to every wind; and I will draw the sword after them. And they shall know that I am the Lord, when I have scattered them among the nations and sown them in the lands. And I will leave of them a few men from the sword and famine and pestilence, that they may tell all their crimes among the nations to which they shall enter; and they shall know that I am the Lord. First let us expound the history: 'Say,' he says, 'O son of man, to those who either ask or do not ask what you are doing, this: that this burden and vision is upon the leader who is in Jerusalem — by the leader signifying Zedekiah —. And say: I am your portent (or, as the Seventy translated: I who make portents, so that future things may be known from present ones), as I have done, so shall it be done to them, to the leader and to all who are in the leader's retinue: they shall be led captive into the Babylonian exile, and the leader who is in their midst shall be carried on the shoulders either of beasts of burden or of those who had fled with him, and he shall go out in the darkness and in the shadows of night; and his companions shall dig under the wall to lead him out: his face shall be veiled, either by the fear of one fleeing or by the injury of blindness, so that, blind, he may not see the land of Babylon. And I will spread my net over him, and he shall be taken in my dragnet — for it was with the Lord's permission that he was taken by the Babylonians, and what he suffered to be done by the enemy, he testifies that he himself did — ; and, led into Babylon, he shall not see the land of the Chaldeans, and there he shall die a captive. And all his companions and helpers and the comrades of his flight, once he has been seized, I will scatter hither and thither, nor will I allow them to get away safe, but I will draw my sword in the hands of the enemy. And when they have been scattered among the nations, let them understand that I am the Lord and that all things were done by my will. And I will leave,' he says, 'of his companions who fled with him and were able to escape, a few who escaped the sword and the famine and the pestilence, so that, when by flight or by captivity they have come to diverse regions, they may tell all their crimes, either by word or by example, on account of which they deserved such great evils; and let them understand that I myself am the Lord'. But he who tries to expound the tropology and the explanation begun concerning the Savior interprets the leader who is in the midst of Jerusalem as him who went before as a portent and a sign, in that both he himself and his companions are captives of this world, and, ascending as victor from the underworld, he was carried on the shoulders of angels, went out in the darkness, unknown to unbelievers, and, the middle wall having been destroyed, made both one; his face is covered, so that he may not look upon the land of the Jews — nor is it a wonder if the Lord has hidden his face, since to this day a veil is placed before the face of Moses for the people that does not believe —. And the Lord spread his net over him — that which the bride says of him in the Song of Songs: Behold, he is behind our wall, looking out through the windows, appearing through the nets; of whom Jeremiah too says: The Spirit of our face, Christ the Lord, was taken in our sins; and: Though he had not done sin, he was made sin for us — ; and he will be brought, he says, into Babylon, the confusion of this world, into the land of the Chaldeans, which means 'as it were demons' — or else, that he might draw back to salvation those who had been enslaved to demons and lead captive the former captivity —, and that very land of the Chaldeans, he says, he will not see, and there he will die, so that by his death he may give life to us all. And all his helpers and companions, the apostles and apostolic men, he scattered into the whole world; and he drew his sword after them, so that they might be crowned with various martyrdoms. And then they might understand, either they themselves or those who had believed through them, that he himself is the Lord, and that he had scattered them among the nations and sown them in the lands for this reason, that they might bear much fruit. And he left of them a few men, such as John the apostle and the others, delivered from sword and famine and pestilence and escaping the onslaught of persecutions, so that they might tell the crimes of the Jews and the iniquities of all the nations to which they had entered, so that, having done penance, they might know that he himself was the Lord. We have said these things leaving the judgment to the reader's discretion; for the rest, there is no doubt that it is dangerous to compare the captivity and death of a wicked king with the mystery of the Lord.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.12.18-20":["And the word of the Lord came to me, saying: Eat your bread in disturbance, and drink your water in haste and grief. And say to the people of the land: Thus says the Lord God to those who dwell in Jerusalem in the land of Israel: They shall eat their bread in anxiety, and drink their water in desolation, so that the land may be made desolate of its multitude because of the iniquity of all who dwell in it; and the cities that are now inhabited shall be desolate, and the land deserted, and you shall know that I am the Lord. (LXX: \"And the word of the Lord came to me, saying: Son of man, you shall eat your bread with pain, and you shall drink your water with torment and distress. And you shall say to the people of the land: Thus says Adonai the Lord to the inhabitants of Jerusalem upon the land of Israel: They shall eat their loaves with want, and drink their water with devastation, so that the land and its fullness may be devastated; for in impiety are all who dwell in it, and their cities which are inhabited shall be in desolation, and the land shall be devastated, and you shall know that I am the Lord.\") After the prophecy about the prince and his companions, which was shown under a bodily image, he comes to the people, and whatever is said to the prophet is referred through the prophet to the inhabitants of Jerusalem: that in the evil of the siege they will eat their bread in want and distress, and drink their water by no means in abundance, but with torment and tribulation. 'These things,' he says, 'O prophet, are said to you, so that you may speak to the people of your land and say: Thus says the Lord to the inhabitants of Jerusalem, which is in the land of Israel: Besieged by Nebuchadnezzar and the Chaldeans, you shall endure the evil of hunger and thirst, so that the whole land with its fullness may be destroyed', signifying the inhabitants by that which is inhabited. And lest they think this comes upon them without cause: 'Tell them,' he says, 'that because of their crimes and impieties all the cities of Judea are to be reduced to solitude, and the cultivation of the whole land is to perish, so that they may come to know as raging the God whom they would not know as merciful'. According to the ἀναγωγὴν [anagogy, the higher sense], we can call the inhabitants of Jerusalem those believing in Christ who dwell in the church; and if through sins they have deserved his displeasure, let them eat bread with want and drink water with grief and distress, enduring not a lack of the food of bread or of the drink of water, but a famine of the word and teaching of God. For when, either through the fault of the princes who are to be captured and handed over to the Babylonians or through our own hardness, we have lost the word of God and have not deserved to have useful waters, then we shall take our food in pain and penury, and the land will lose the multitude of believers, and the cities, which are understood as the churches throughout the whole world, will be made desolate, and the land will be deserted, so that all may know that this is the Lord's displeasure. This indeed we can also understand of the time of persecution.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.12.22-28":["And the word of the Lord came to me, saying: Son of man, what is this proverb of yours in the land of Israel, of those saying: The days shall be put off far, and every vision shall perish? Therefore say to them: Thus says the Lord God: I will make this proverb cease, nor shall it be commonly said any longer in Israel; and speak to them that the days have drawn near, and the word of every vision. For no longer shall any vision be empty, nor divination ambiguous, in the midst of the sons of Israel; for I the Lord will speak, whatever word I speak, and it shall be done; it shall not be prolonged any more: but in your days, provoking house, I will speak the word and do it, says the Lord God. And the word of the Lord came to me, saying: Son of man, behold the house of Israel, of those saying: The vision that this man sees is for many days, and for distant times he prophesies; therefore say to them: Thus says the Lord God: None of my words shall be prolonged any longer; the word that I speak shall be fulfilled, says the Lord God. What we, following Symmachus, have called 'proverb', all the other translators translated 'parable', which in Hebrew is called 'masal'. (Again, where we have published: Nor will there be ambiguous divination, the Seventy rendered: Nor one divining according to favor, for which all translated 'slippery', what we have called 'ambiguous', so that we may recognize the words of the prophets that deceive their hearers.) And this is the sense of the whole chapter: above he had prophesied against the prince who was in Jerusalem, then he had announced to the people that famine and the burning of thirst were to come; and as the multitude did not believe it, an old and well-worn proverb is taken up: The threat of the prophets is put off for a long time, and every vision will perish, for in vain is it 'seen' — or, according to Symmachus, διαπνεύσει, that is, 'it will be dissolved into wind and a thin breeze' —. 'Tell them, therefore,' he says, 'that my threat is by no means put off, nor is a false and ambiguous divination put forth among the peoples, one with an uncertain end, so that it is spoken at one time and fulfilled at another; but now, while you who speak and those who hear are alive, the word that I have spoken will be fulfilled'. He signifies the impending captivity of the city of Jerusalem, and Zedekiah, with the people of Judah, about to be taken at any moment. Not only at that time, however, but to this day the unbelieving crowd and the hard hearts of mortals do not receive the prophecy of God, but all that the prophets threaten, that the Gospel proclaims, they reckon is to be put off to another time. Let us note that here 'parable', which is used by the Seventy, is taken for 'proverb', according to what we read in the psalm: And I became a parable to them, and in this same prophet: What is this parable to you among the people of Israel, of those saying: The fathers have eaten the sour grape, and the teeth of the sons have been set on edge. We have touched briefly on the obvious things, so that we may linger over the more obscure.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.13.2":["And the word of the Lord came to me, saying: Son of man, prophesy to the prophets of Israel who prophesy; and you shall say to those who prophesy from their own heart: Hear the word of the Lord. Thus says the Lord God. (This which we have set down: Who prophesy; and you shall say to those who prophesy from their own heart, the Seventy omitted, and in its place they added what is not found in the Hebrew: And you shall prophesy, and you shall say to them.) Now this speech is against the false prophets, who were deceiving the people and prophesying something else, contrary to the commandments of God. Nor should it trouble anyone that they are called prophets, for holy Scripture has this custom, that it names each one the prophet of his own prophesying and speech, just as there are called prophets of Baal and prophets of idols and prophets of confusion; whence also the apostle Paul calls a Greek poet a prophet: A certain one of them, their own prophet, said: Cretans are always liars, evil beasts, idle bellies, and in Hosea we read: As a raving prophet, a man bearing the spirit. But whatever was said at that time to the Israelite people is now referred to the Church, so that the holy prophets are the apostles and apostolic men, while the lying and frenzied prophets are all the heretics, whose leaders invent things from their own heart, while those who have been seduced by them acquiesce in the lies of others.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.13.3":["Woe to the senseless prophets, who follow their own spirit and see nothing. (LXX: \"Woe to those who prophesy from their own heart and do not see at all\", leaving out that which has been set down by us: Who follow their own spirit). Since the name of prophets, according to the rule of the Scriptures, is common to the good and the bad, they differ in this, that the good prophets are said to be wise, but the bad stupid and senseless; of these the one is referred to the men of the Church, the other to all the heretics, who, abandoning the Spirit of God, follow their own spirit, because they prophesy not at all by divine inspiration but from their own heart: whence also they see nothing. But he who is wise follows not the thoughts of his own heart but the Spirit of God, according to that passage in which, earlier on, the living creatures and the wheels followed the Spirit of God; and the prophet says: After the Lord your God you shall walk, and in another place: Lead me in the right path, and again: Your Spirit, he who is good, will lead me into the land; and for the people of God the pillar of fire and the cloud was the leader in the wilderness; and Jeremiah says: I have not labored following you. But which of the heretics, who follows his own spirit, does not stumble into darkness and, blind, lead the blind into a pit? Hence, although in their mysteries, or rather their orgies, they seem to themselves to see more than the teachers of the Church, still they see nothing at all, because they have lost the sun of righteousness, and in vain they imitate those who were formerly called 'seers' and to whom it is said: You who see, go; withdraw into the land of Judah, and in Bethel you shall not prophesy; for which reason the 'vision' of Isaiah and of Obadiah is placed in the titles, and the Lord commanded the apostles: Lift up your eyes and see, and the prophet humbly entreats: Unveil my eyes, and I will consider the wonders of your law.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.13.4-5":["Like foxes in the deserts were your prophets, Israel. You do not go up from the opposite side, nor did you set up a wall for the house of Israel, that you might stand in battle on the day of the Lord. They see vain things and divine a lie, saying: Says the Lord, when the Lord has not sent them; and they persisted in confirming the word. Have you not seen an empty vision, and spoken a lying divination, and do you say: Says the Lord, when I have not spoken? (LXX: \"As foxes in the deserts are your prophets, Israel. They did not stand on the firmament, and they gathered flocks upon the house of Israel; they did not rise up, those who speak on the day of the Lord, seeing lies, divining vain things, who say: The Lord says, and the Lord did not send them; and they began to raise up the word. Have you not seen a false vision, and spoken vain divinations, ※ and you were saying: The Lord says, and I have not spoken?⁚\") Let us speak first according to the Hebrew: the prophets of Israel are like foxes, daily committing thefts of domestic fowl, who dwell, according to Aquila and the Seventy, 'in the deserts', according to Symmachus and Theodotion 'in broken walls' and 'ruins'; who cannot go up from the opposite side, nor set up a wall for the house of Israel — we read that Aaron ran to meet the fire that was devouring the people of Israel, and stood in the midst, and set up a wall for the safety of the people; to Jeremiah also it is said that he should not run to meet the Lord, nor stand against his wrath by perseverance in prayer: for just as a wall is set against an enemy, and one is accustomed to meet an adversary from the opposite side and to confront one who is coming on, so the sentence of God is broken by the prayers of the saints; whence also it is said to Moses: Let me go, and I will blot out this people: for in the one to whom it is said: Let me go, it is shown that he has the power of holding back —. These cannot stand in battle on the day of the Lord, so as to fight for the people with prayers and to resist the sentence of God with supplications.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.13.4":["Moreover, according to the Seventy the sense here is this: all the heretics, because of their deceitfulness and wickedness, are like foxes, of whom the Savior speaks: Foxes have holes, and the birds of the sky nests; and of Herod it is written: Tell this fox; and of these same foxes, which deceive the little and simple ones and lay waste the vineyard of Christ, Solomon speaks: Catch us the little foxes that destroy the vineyards; and in the psalm it is sung concerning those who are tripped up by their deceitfulness: They will go into the lower parts of the earth, they will be handed over into the hands of the sword, they will be the portions of foxes.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.13.5":["These prophets, who are like foxes, did not stand on the firmament; nor did they deserve to hear with Peter: You are Peter, and upon this rock I will build my Church; nor are they like Moses, to whom it was said: But you, stand here with me, nor like him who says: He set my feet upon a rock, but they are tossed about by every wind of doctrine; nor did they stand on the firmament of which we read in Psalm 18 (19): The heavens tell the glory of God, and the firmament announces the works of his hands. These gathered all sorts of irrational people, who on account of their excessive innocence are like cattle, against the house of God; hence it is now said: And they gathered flocks upon the house of Israel; nor did they themselves rise up, nor could they raise up others, but whatever they have done and are doing is full of lies.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.13.6-7":["Since, therefore, they behold vain things and divine a lie and say that they have been sent by the Lord, when they have not been sent by him, and persist in their errors and desire to make their own word firm, they are convicted that all the things they preach are empty, since they say that their own lie is the word of the Lord.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","And what follows: And they began to raise up the word, signifies this: that they wish indeed to rise up and to lift themselves to the heights, but cannot fulfill what they desire; and it is said to them: Have you not seen a false vision, and spoken vain divinations? — and what follows: And you were saying: The Lord says, and I have not spoken, is not found in the Seventy —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.13.8-9":["Therefore thus says the Lord God: Because you have spoken vain things and seen a lie, therefore behold, I am against you, says the Lord God; and my hand shall be upon the prophets who see vain things and divine a lie: in the council of my people they shall not be, and in the writing of the house of Israel they shall not be written, nor shall they enter into the land of Israel, and you shall know that I am the Lord God. (LXX: \"Therefore say: Thus says Adonai the Lord: Since your words are lies and your divinations vain, therefore behold, I am over you, says the Lord God; and I will stretch out my hand upon the prophets who see a lie and who speak vain things: in the discipline of my people they shall not be, nor shall they be written in the writing of the house of Israel, and into the land of Israel they shall not enter, and they shall know that I am Adonai the Lord.\") Under the person of the prophets, the speech is against the heretics who speak vain things and see a lie, and persist in making their own doctrine firm: that the Lord himself comes against them and rises up and stretches out his hand over them to strike, and does not draw it back to spare; and he threatens that they will not be in the council of the people of the Lord, nor in his Church but in the synagogue of the devil, nor will they be written in the house of Israel — of whom it is said in another place: Let them be written upon the earth: not into the land of Israel, which is the land of the living, of which it is now said: Nor shall they enter into the land of Israel, but into the land of the dead and the shadow of death — so that, after they have suffered these things, separated from the assembly of the people of God, they may understand that he himself is the Lord. Where we have rendered: In the council of my people they shall not be, the Seventy translated: In the discipline (or in the correction) of my people they shall not be: for the correction of adversaries is one thing, that of sons another; whence also Jeremiah: Correct, he says, us, O Lord, but in judgment, and not in fury, so that you may by no means make us few. Pour out your fury upon the nations which have not known you, and upon the kingdoms which have not called upon your name.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.13.10":["Because they have deceived my people, saying: Peace, peace, and there is no peace; and he himself was building a wall, but they were daubing it with mud without straw. Say to those who daub without tempering that it will fall; for there will be a flooding shower, and I will give very great stones rushing down from above, and a wind of tempest scattering it. For behold, the wall has fallen; will it not be said to you: Where is the daubing with which you daubed? Therefore thus says the Lord God: And I will make the spirit of tempests burst forth in my indignation, and there will be a flooding shower in my fury, and great stones of wrath in consummation; and I will destroy the wall which you daubed without due mixture, and I will level it with the earth, and its foundation will be uncovered, and it will fall and be consumed in its midst, and you shall know that I am the Lord. And I will fulfill my indignation on the wall and on those who daub it without due mixture, and I will say to you: There is no wall, and there are none who daub it; the prophets of Israel who prophesy to Jerusalem, and see for her a vision of peace, and there is no peace, says the Lord God. (LXX: \"Because they deceived my people, saying: Peace, peace, and there was no peace; and this one builds a wall, and these daub it with folly. Say to those who daub with folly: It will fall; there will be a flooding rain, and I will give great stones into their joints and they will fall, and a spirit carrying away, and it will be broken apart. And behold, the wall has fallen; and will they not say to you: Where is your daubing with which you daubed? Therefore thus says Adonai the Lord: And I will make a spirit carrying away break forth in my fury, and there will be a flooding rain in my wrath, and I will bring great stones in fury unto consummation; and I will dig under the wall which you daubed, and it will fall, and I will set it upon the earth, and its foundations will be uncovered, and it will fall, and you will be consumed with rebukes, and you will know that I am the Lord. And I will fulfill my fury upon the wall and upon those who daub it it will fall, and I said to you: There is no wall, nor those who daub it; the prophets of Israel who prophesy over Jerusalem, and who see peace for her, and there is no peace for them, says the Lord God.\") The prophets of Israel, who will not be in the council of the people of God, nor be written in the writing of the house of Israel, nor enter the land of promise, will suffer these things because they deceived my people, wrongfully announcing to them peace and prosperity instead of the rigor of penitence and conversion, according to that which is said elsewhere: Peace, peace: and where is peace? God, then, was building for them μεταφορικῶς [metaphorically], through the commandments of the law and the utterances and threats of the holy prophets, an enclosure — for this is what the Hebrew word 'his' signifies — so as to keep from them every incursion of beasts and every hostile assault, as long as, converted to God, they were girded by this as by a bulwark and a most solid wall —; but the false prophets promised them peace not once but often: for this is what the repeated word of peace signifies. And that very wall, which in what follows is called 'cir', the false prophets were daubing 'without due mixture' — as Symmachus translated, that is, 'with pure mud', which had no straw and could not supply any strength; but as the Seventy and Theodotion have it, they daub it 'with folly'; while Aquila translated the Hebrew word 'thaphel' by ἀνάλῳ, which means 'without salt', passing from one metaphor to another metaphor, so that, just as food without salt has no flavor, so also mud without the straw by which it is made firm and bound together can provide no strength to the wall —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","Also that which we read above: He himself was building (or builds) a wall, many refer to the people of Israel, which was promising itself vain help, either from the Egyptians or from peace.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.13.11-12":["'Say,' he says, 'to those who promise them vain things and pledge empty help, that this wall, this enclosure, will fall, and I will send a most violent shower, that is, most savage enemies — and he means the Babylonians and Chaldeans —, and these same enemies as stones of hail rushing down from above', all by μεταφορὰν [metaphor], so that a sudden whirlwind may overturn and undermine the unstable enclosure; and that afterward it may be said to them — that is, to the false prophets —: 'Where is the daubing with which you daubed? and where is the help you promised?'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.13.11":["That which the Seventy translated: And upon their joints, is not found in the Hebrew; and it signifies the joints of the stones in a wall, or the supports of timber by which walls are strengthened.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.13.13-15":["But whatever we have said of the false prophets can be referred also to the heretics: that all their fabrications are dissolved by the wrath of God, and the building of the worst dogmas, which does not have the seasoning of the Holy Spirit, collapses and slides down to its foundations and to dust, and the enclosure itself is leveled with the earth and falls, and both the prophets who were promising and the people to whom it was promised are consumed in its midst, so that, after he has fulfilled his indignation and has said to them: There is no such wall — that is, 'you have no defense' — and there are none who daub it, but all things are vain and come to nothing, then you will know by deed that I am the Lord.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.13.16":["And so that we may know who those are who daub the wall, there follows: The prophets of Israel — that is, the false prophets — who prophesy to Jerusalem, and see for her a vision of peace; these are the cause of sin and ruin and foolish security: For there will be no peace, says the Lord, because the Lord did not send them nor did he speak to them.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.13.17-18":["And you, son of man, set your face against the daughters of your people who prophesy from their own heart, and prophesy over them, and say: Thus says the Lord God: Woe to those who sew cushions under every elbow of the hand, and make pillows under the head of every age to catch souls; and when they caught the souls of my people, they made their souls alive. And they profaned me before my people for a handful of barley and a fragment of bread, to kill souls which do not die, and to make alive souls which do not live, lying to my people who believe lies. Therefore thus says the Lord God: Behold, I am against your cushions, with which you catch flying souls, and I will tear them from your arms, and I will let go the souls which you catch, souls to fly, and I will tear your pillows, and I will free my people from your hand, and they shall no longer be in your hands for plunder; and you shall know that I am the Lord. Because you made the heart of the righteous grieve falsely, whom I did not sadden, and strengthened the hands of the wicked, so that he should not turn back from his evil way and live, therefore you shall not see vain things, and you shall divine divinations no more, and I will rescue my people from your hand, and you shall know that I am the Lord. (LXX: \"And you, son of man, make your face firm upon the daughters of your people who prophesy from their own heart, and prophesy over them, and you shall say: Thus says the Lord God: Woe to those who sew pillows under every elbow of the hand, and make veils over every head of every age to pervert souls; the souls of my people have been overturned, and they were saving souls. And they defiled me before my people for a handful of barley and a fragment of bread, to slay souls which ought not to die, and to save souls which ought not to live, while they speak vain words to a people that listens. Therefore thus says the Lord God: Behold, I am over your pillows, upon which you have gathered souls, and I will tear them from your arms, and I will let go the souls which you pervert, their souls into dispersion, and I will tear your veils, and I will free my people from your hands, and they shall no longer be in your hands for gathering; and you shall know that I am the Lord. Because you were perverting the soul of the righteous unjustly, and I was not perverting him, and you were strengthening the hands of the unjust, so that he should not be converted at all from his most evil way and live, therefore you shall see lies no more, and you shall by no means divine divinations any longer, and I will free my people from your hand, and you shall know that I am the Lord.\") Above, the divine word was directed to the prophets, who were daubing the wall with mud, because it had no straw and could give no strength to the wall or enclosure; now he is ordered to set his face, or to direct it, or, as the Seventy translated, to make it firm, against the daughters of the people, the prophetesses. And just as other, false prophets were inspired against the prophets by a diabolical spirit, to overturn the commandments of God, so against the prophetesses — such as were Deborah and Huldah and, in the Acts of the Apostles, the four prophesying daughters of Philip the evangelist — other women of the same sex were inspired by a demonic spirit, such as were Prisca and Maximilla, to overturn the faith of the truth by the prophesying of a lie. These, the Hebrews say, were trained in magic arts through necromancies and a pythonic spirit, such as was she who seemed to have raised up the soul of Samuel, and, in the Acts of the Apostles, she whose divination brought much income to her masters, out of whom the unclean spirit was cast at the command of the apostle Paul. But we say this: that some heretics, by the falsity of their dogmas, preach virtue — of whom were Pythagoras, and Zeno, from whom come the Stoics, the Brahmans of the Indians and the Gymnosophists of the Ethiopians, who because of the continence of their diet win the admiration of the nations — and rightly are they said to daub a wall and to promise some strength —, but because they lack the seasoning of Christ, their labor is vain and their building will perish: Unless the Lord builds the house, those who build it have labored in vain; others, however, teachers of pleasures and of lust, are said to sew cushions and to place them under every elbow of the hand: the Epicureans and the Pyrrhonists, and among us Jovinian and Eunomius, who say: Let us eat and drink, for tomorrow we shall die.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.13.17":["The prophet, then, is ordered to set, or to make firm, his face against the daughters of his people. And first we must say what the face 'set' or 'directed' and 'made firm' means: namely that which is written of the Lord: The face of the Lord is upon those who do evil, to destroy their memory from the earth. As indeed wax melts before the face of the fire, so let sinners perish before the face of God; according to which sense it is said in this same prophet: Son of man, make your face firm upon Theman and Darom and Nageb, and again: The word of the Lord came to me, saying: Son of man, make your face firm upon Jerusalem, and a little after: The word of the Lord came to me, saying: Son of man, make your face firm upon the Ammonites, and again: Make your countenance firm upon Pharaoh, king of Egypt, and again: Son of man, make your face firm upon Gog and Magog, and in another place: I will make my face firm upon that man, and I will put him into the desert and into dissipation, and I will take him away from the midst of the people, and you shall know that I am the Lord.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.13.18-19":["Woe, then, to these heresies and to these doctrines, which, promising rest, deceive every age and sex, to catch the souls of the wretched and to defile me before my people, while I am believed to love pleasure; and this not for a corus and a hemicorus of barley, as we read in Hosea, but for a handful of barley, on which brute animals feed, and a fragment of bread: not a whole loaf, nor the solid testimonies of the Scriptures, but those which have been broken and cut short and diminished by heretical depravity, so as to deceive all the holy and drag them to death, while they strive to make sinners alive with vain promises.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.13.20-23":["Therefore the clement and merciful God does not slay the prophetesses themselves, but will tear their cushions, which like nets catch flying souls, so that, after these have been torn, the souls may have free power of flying; and he will rend the veils, or pillows, on which the ruling part of the soul was reclining and with which the heads of the deceived were covered; since the apostle teaches that the heads of men are not to be veiled, but that the glory of the Lord is to be contemplated with unveiled face: 'For you,' he says, 'were breaking the spirits of those who serve God with false terrors, and were keeping the wicked shut in with fraudulent promises, lest by doing penance they should recover the life which they had lost; therefore you shall by no means see vain visions, nor will I call your lies prophecies, but divinations, of which it is written: There is no augury in Jacob, nor divination in Israel, so that I may rescue my people from your hands, and you may know by deed that I am the Lord, who have freed the lost'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.14.1-4":["And men, elders of Israel, came to me and sat before me. And the word of the Lord came to me, saying: Son of man, these men have set uncleannesses in their hearts, and have set up the stumbling block of their iniquity against their face; shall I, being asked, answer them? Therefore speak to them, and say to them: Thus says the Lord God: A man, a man of the house of Israel who sets his uncleannesses in his heart, and sets up the stumbling block of his iniquity against his face, and comes to the prophet inquiring of me through him, I the Lord will answer him according to the multitude of his uncleannesses, that the house of Israel may be caught in their heart, in which they have withdrawn from me in all their idols. Therefore say to the house of Israel: Thus says the Lord God: Turn and withdraw from your idols, and turn away your faces from all your defilements. For a man, a man of the house of Israel and of the proselytes, whoever is a stranger in Israel, if he is estranged from me and sets his idols in his heart, and sets up the stumbling block of his iniquity against his face, and comes to the prophet to inquire of me through him, I the Lord will answer him by myself; and I will set my face upon that man, and I will make him an example and a proverb, and I will destroy him from the midst of my people, and you shall know that I am the Lord. And the prophet, when he errs and speaks a word, I the Lord have deceived that prophet, and I will stretch out my hand upon him, and I will blot him out from the midst of my people Israel. And they shall bear their iniquity: as the iniquity of the one inquiring, so shall the iniquity of the prophet be, that the house of Israel may no longer stray from me, nor be polluted in all their transgressions, but may be to me for a people, and I may be to them for God, says the Lord of hosts. (LXX: \"And the word of the Lord came to me, saying: Son of man, these men have set their thoughts in their hearts, and have set the torment of their iniquities before their face; shall I, answering, answer them? Therefore speak to them and say to them: Thus says Adonai the Lord: A man, a man of the house of Israel who sets his thoughts in his heart, and sets the torment of his iniquity before his face, and comes to the prophet to inquire of him in me, I the Lord will answer him in those things by which his mind is held, so as to turn away the house of Israel according to their hearts, which have been estranged from me in their thoughts. Therefore say to the house of Israel: Thus says the Lord God: Turn and withdraw from your inventions and from all your impieties, and turn your faces. For a man, a man of the house of Israel and of the strangers who sojourn in Israel, who is estranged from me and sets his thoughts in his heart, and sets the torment of his iniquity before his face, and comes to the prophet to inquire of him in me, I the Lord will answer him in that by which he is held; and I will make my face firm upon that man, and I will make him a desert and an extermination, and I will take him away from the midst of my people, and you shall know that I am the Lord. And the prophet, if he errs and speaks a word, I the Lord have made that prophet err, and I will stretch out my hand upon him, and I will exterminate him from the midst of my people Israel. And they shall receive their iniquity: according to the iniquity of the one inquiring, the iniquity of the prophet also shall likewise be, that the house of Israel may by no means stray from me, and that they may not be polluted any more in all their offenses, and they shall be to me for a people, and I will be to them for God, says Adonai the Lord.\") After he had spoken to the prophets who prophesied out of their own heart and daubed the wall without tempering, and to the prophetesses who sewed cushions and put them under every elbow of the hand, and made veils, and covered the heads of every age — by which the teaching of perverse doctrines is shown —, there came to the prophet not all the old men, lest all should seem to err, but certain of the elders of Israel, and they sat before the prophet, meanwhile deferring in silence the reason for which they had come; and at once the word of God came to the prophet, indicating why they were present — for it is not in the nature of man, however holy, to know the secrets of the heart; hence it is said of the Savior alone: But Jesus, seeing their thoughts and why do you think evil things in your hearts —. And the sense is: 'Son of man, these men who sit before you have set their uncleannesses in their hearts — or thoughts, and, according to Symmachus and Theodotion, idols —, and they have set the stumbling block — that is, the ruin and torment — of their iniquity against their face, and so they have come to inquire after the word of the Lord. Ought I to answer men of this sort, who come to me with their former thoughts, not abandoning the crimes of their impiety even at this time, but believing in idols and deceitful divinations, and having the ruin and torment of iniquity against their face, while they despair of better things and are ready for punishment, and so desire to learn my words through you? Since, therefore, they have come to you with a perverse heart, you answer not in your own person — lest the authority of the one answering be slight —, but by my command, and you shall say to them: Thus says the Lord God'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.14.4-8":["Now the meaning of the whole passage, to comprehend a broad explanation in a brief discourse, is this: 'A man, a man not of the other nations — whose error can have pardon —, but of the house of Israel, who comes to the prophet to inquire with his former vices — of which we have spoken above —, I will answer him according to his heart and his uncleannesses, so that he may hear according to what he wishes and believes: for he who asks not in a spirit of learning but of testing does not deserve correction. For they have withdrawn from me and have followed idols; and this is a brief threat, so that they may withdraw from idols and perverse thoughts and turn to me, abandoning their former crimes'; for he who asks deceitfully does not deserve to hear the truth, but is to be caught by his own heart, just as the scribes and Pharisees, questioning the Lord, or rather testing him, hear: Why do you test me?, and again: Neither will I tell you by what power I do these things; but whoever is such a man and asks in this way, God sets his face upon him, or makes it firm, so that by the severity of his countenance the hardness of the brow may be softened and he may be for an example and a proverb, or for a solitude and a destruction, so that, when he has been destroyed out of the people of God, the rest may know that he himself is the Lord, who knows the secrets of the heart and understands the perversity of the mind, and considers not the words of those who speak but their hearts.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.14.7":["And what is said a second time: A man, a man of the sons of Israel and of the strangers who are joined to them, shows, according to the Apostle, a twofold man in us: the outer and the inner — for many, having the bodily face of a man, take on the images of various beasts, whose destruction the prophet desires when he prays: Lord, in your city you will destroy their image; those of whom it is written: Man, when he was in honor, did not understand; he was compared to senseless beasts of burden, and became like them, are not men who are men, but men who are beasts of burden; again, those who hear in the Gospel: Serpents, brood of vipers, who showed you to flee from the wrath to come?, are not men who are men, but men who are serpents as well; and those of whom it is written: Foxes have holes, and the birds of heaven nests, and of Herod: Tell this fox, are not men who are men, but men who are foxes —; but those who possess both words 'man', if they err, are corrected by punishments for this reason, that they may understand that he himself is the Lord. Moreover, what we have set down according to the Seventy: A man who is estranged from me and sets his thoughts in his heart, and the torment (or punishment) of his iniquity before his face, agrees with that testimony which is written in Isaiah: Walk in the light of your fire, and in the flame which you have kindled; for each one kindles for himself the blazing of flames and prepares punishments for himself, while he is unwilling to correct his errors by repentance, but remains in errors that deserve the burning of flames.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.14.9-11":["Then he stretches out his hand upon him — the prophet is understood — and he is blotted out from the midst of his people, so that he may bear his iniquity, because for like error the punishment too is alike, so that both he who asks and he who is asked may bear their iniquity, and the house of Israel, that of those who wish to hear the words of God, may by no means be mocked by perverse prophesyings, but may be the people of God, and may itself deserve to have the Lord as God. With what he said: I the Lord have deceived him, the words of Solomon agree, who, discoursing of God, says: And he will mock the mockers, and that testimony: If they walk perversely toward me, I too will walk against them in perverse fury.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.14.9":["And what follows also seems to raise a question: The prophet, when he errs and speaks a word, I the Lord have deceived that prophet; let us not think that this is said of a true prophet, but of a false prophet, who is called a prophet συνωνυμῶς [by a shared name]. And it can be resolved from that passage of the Scriptures where Ahab king of Israel, going to battle, will not listen to Micaiah the prophet, but acquiesces in the counsels of the false prophets, and Micaiah says that he saw the Lord sitting on his throne, and that a spirit of error offered itself of its own accord to deceive the king; the devil too, in the book of Job, going around the earth, is said to stand in the sight of God and to receive power from the Lord, first over Job's substance, then over his body; and Balaam the soothsayer also is sent by the Lord to deceive Balak the son of Beor. All this, however, is said so that it may not be reckoned to the strength of the false prophets that the people is deceived and would rather hear a lie than lend its ear to the truth, but that it is of the wrath of God that a perverse and unbelieving people listens to false prophets rather than to prophets.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.14.13-20":["And the word of the Lord came to me, saying: Son of man, when a land sins against me so as to transgress, transgressing, I will stretch out my hand upon it, and I will break the staff of its bread, and I will send famine into it and kill from it man and beasts; and if these three men are in its midst, Noah and Daniel and Job, they by their righteousness shall deliver their own souls, says the Lord of hosts. But if I also bring most evil beasts upon the land to lay it waste, and it becomes impassable because there is no one passing through on account of the beasts, if these three men are in it, as I live, says the Lord, they shall deliver neither sons nor daughters, but they alone shall be delivered, and the land shall be made desolate. Or if I bring a sword upon that land, and say to the sword: Pass through the land, and I kill from it man and beast, and these three men are in its midst, as I live, says the Lord God, they shall not deliver sons or daughters, but they alone shall be delivered. But if I also send pestilence upon that land, and pour out my indignation upon it in blood, to take away from it man and beast, and Noah and Daniel and Job are in its midst, as I live, says the Lord God, they shall not deliver son and daughter, but they by their righteousness shall deliver their own souls. For thus says the Lord God: But if I also send my four most evil judgments, sword and famine and evil beasts and pestilence, against Jerusalem, to kill from it man and cattle, yet there shall be left in it a deliverance of those who bring out sons and daughters: behold, they shall come out to you, and you shall see their way and their inventions, and you shall be consoled over the evil which I brought upon Jerusalem, in all that I have brought in upon it. And they shall console you, when you see their way and their inventions, and you shall know that not in vain have I done all that I did in it, says the Lord God. Four plagues are brought upon the 'transgressing' land — or, as the Seventy translated, the 'sinning' land —: famine, beasts, sword, pestilence; and if these are brought one at a time, piece by piece, upon individual regions, and these three men, Noah and Daniel and Job, whose righteousness is proclaimed by the voices of the Scriptures, pray for the sinning land, not only will they be unable to preserve it, but not even their own sons and daughters, if these have committed the works of the sinning land; they will be able to deliver themselves alone.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.14.13-21":["But upon our land too, to which it is said: You are earth, and into earth you shall go, and upon our Jerusalem, each and every one of the believers, if they despise the commandments of God and deserve to be called transgressors, famine will be brought: Not a famine of bread nor a thirst for water, but a famine of hearing the word of God; the sword too will be brought, of which we read: They shall be delivered into the hands of the sword, they shall be the portions of foxes, and to Mary: A sword shall pierce through your own soul also; beasts too will be brought, to which the righteous man asks not to be handed over: Do not hand over to beasts the soul that confesses to you; and pestilence will be brought — for which the Seventy translated 'death' —, of which it is written: The death of sinners is very evil, and: The soul that sins, it shall die, and: So bitter is death. From all of which neither the teaching of spiritual parents — whom we understand to be teachers — nor princes will be able to deliver us, unless the sons give their assent and assist the parents' entreaties by their own efforts; for the righteousness of the righteous shall be upon him, and the iniquity of the wicked shall abide in him: Each shall die for his own sin, and shall be saved by his own righteousness; and in vain do the Jews say: Abraham is our father, since they do not have the works of Abraham; but if there is confidence in anyone, let us trust in the Lord alone: Cursed, for, is every man who puts his hope in man, however holy they may be, however much they may be prophets; we read: Do not trust in men, and again: It is good to trust in the Lord rather than to trust in princes, not in the princes of the world only but in the princes of the churches, who will deliver only their own souls, if they are righteous, but the sons and daughters whom they have begotten in the Church they will not be able to save, if these are negligent; and yet the Apostle says: If anyone's work is burned up, he himself will be saved, yet so as through fire.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.14.13":["And the hand is stretched out over the transgressing land so that the staff, or strength, of bread may be broken; and that hand, stretched out, threatens plagues, since he who stretches it out and lifts it up does not wish to strike sinners, but by its stretching out to terrify them and to preserve them once terrified.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.14.14-20":["But if Noah and Daniel and Job, gathered together and placed in one place, cannot withdraw the sinning land, that is, those who dwell in the land, from the wrath of God, what is to be said of those who think that sinful sons can be delivered from the fires of Gehenna by the merit and virtues of their parents? Hence neither will a martyr father be able to deliver a transgressing son, nor will a mother of holy life give the rewards of chastity to an unchaste daughter; and on the contrary the vices of parents will not harm their children, but: The soul that sins, it shall die. Lot, dwelling in Sodom but a stranger both in spirit and in flesh, did not deliver his wife, who had looked back behind her, but only his daughters, who perhaps had not sinned; and Josiah, a holy man, not only did not save a sinful people by his virtues, but he himself also died in its sins. But if at times the Lord promises his mercy to their posterity on account of Abraham and David, it must be noted that he does not spare those who persevere in crimes, but those who do penance, so that the conduct of the sons assists the merits of the fathers.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.14.14":["— It is asked, since Abraham and Isaac and Jacob, Moses too and the other patriarchs and prophets, were righteous, why mention is made of these alone; which is easily resolved: for Noah could not put off the flood impending over the world, because all the earth had polluted the ways of the Lord, but he had his sons preserved, who perhaps were of the same virtue, and also for the sake of a seedbed of the human race; Daniel too softened by no weeping the captivity impending over the people of the Jews; and Job as well, not on account of sins but for the sake of testing, delivered neither his house nor his sons. Others, however, say that because these three men alone beheld both prosperity and adversity and prosperity again, they are for that reason named together, and that this is secretly signified: that, just as they saw both good things and evil and again joyful things, so also the people of Israel, which had first enjoyed good things and afterward bore the yoke of captivity, if it does penance, returns to its former happiness. —\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.14.21-23":["'Since,' he says, 'these things are so, and the prayer of such great men does not save a sinning land from single plagues, thus says the Lord: Because Jerusalem has sinned abominably, I will bring four plagues upon it at once: the sword by which it is slaughtered by its enemies, and famine which it endures under siege, and beasts by which those who flee are devoured in deserts and forests, and pestilence, which always follows famine and want — for which the common edition translated 'death' —. And these things,' he says, 'I will bring upon it, so that neither men remain in it nor beasts; and yet, in my clemency, while the four plagues impend at once and lay everything waste, I will leave in it some who bring out their sons and daughters, and let them come here as captives — that is, to Babylon and to Chaldea —; and when you see them and have looked closely at their works, then you will understand that they were justly taken captive and that my sentence was just, and your captivity will have consolation, while you understand that I delivered them from sword and famine and beasts and pestilence for this reason: that from their perversity you may learn that Jerusalem was deservedly overthrown and the temple burned and the people led captive, except those whom sword, famine, beasts and pestilence consumed'. These things were said of that Jerusalem upon which, while Ezekiel was prophesying among the Chaldeans, captivity was drawing near.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.15.2-6":["And the word of the Lord came to me, saying: Son of man, what will become of the wood of the vine, out of all the woods of the groves that are among the trees of the forests? Is wood taken from it to make a work, or will a peg be fashioned from it, so that any vessel may hang from it? Behold, it has been given to the fire for food; the fire has consumed both its parts, and its middle has been reduced to ashes; will it be useful for a work? Even when it was whole it was not fit for a work; how much more, when the fire has devoured it and burnt it up, will no work be made of it! Therefore thus says the Lord God: As the wood of the vine among the trees of the forests, which I have given to the fire to devour, so will I give up the inhabitants of Jerusalem; and I will set my face against them: they will go out from the fire, and the fire will consume them, and you will know that I am the Lord, when I have set my face against them and have made the land pathless and desolate, because they have proved transgressors, says the Lord God. (Where we translated: The fire has consumed both its parts, and its middle has been reduced to ashes, the Seventy translated: The fire consumes its yearly cleansing, and lets it go to the end. Again, where we said: When I have set my face against them, they rendered: When I have set firm. 'Transgression', too, they translated as 'offense'.) By various similitudes, then, which the Greeks call parables, the overthrow of the city of Jerusalem is foretold; and just as above, in the frying pan in which the flesh of the people was fried, and in the brick on which the siegeworks and mounds and the engines of rams and of mantlets were drawn, the siege and famine of that same city were described, and as we shall read later in this same prophet — in Jeremiah too it is written that the 'pot' which is kindled from the face of the north signifies the city of Jerusalem; and the forest of Nageb and Theman and Darom [three Hebrew words for the south], cut down, foretells this very thing —, so in the present passage the same Jerusalem is compared to a vineyard and a fruitful vine — of which it is written in another place: I planted you a fruitful vine, wholly true; how have you been turned into bitterness, O alien vine?, and in Isaiah: I planted, he says, a vine 'sorec', which means 'chosen' and 'beautiful'; and in the Psalms we read: You brought a vine out of Egypt, you cast out the nations and planted it; you were the guide of its way before it; and we learn it more plainly when Scripture says: But the vineyard of the Lord Sabaoth is the house of Israel —. As long as this vine bears fruit, among all the trees of the woodlands nothing is more precious than it; but if it ceases to have grapes, it is useful for nothing except to be given to the fire with its root and its layered shoots. The other trees of the forests — to say nothing of the fruit-bearing trees, of which Scripture here makes no mention —, though they have no fruit, once cut down provide a great deal of usefulness and serve both all of agriculture and the making of arms, while they are thinned into shields and shaved into spear-shafts; they close doorways, they hold things as cupboards, as chests and coffers, and they furnish all the equipment of a house. But the vine, once it has ceased to bear grapes, is so useless that not even a peg can be made from it on which something might hang; and when you have cut off its shoots, the fire first seizes both their parts, then the flame consumes them; and it is of no use whatever once it has been reduced to ashes, seeing that even when it is whole it offers no usefulness without its fruit.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.15.2-8":["According to the spiritual understanding, we can understand the vineyard and Jerusalem as the multitude of believers, which, if it has been negligent and has lost the cultivation of its former religion, and has not borne the fruits of the virtues nor made wine that gladdens the heart of man, will be given up to everlasting flames and reckoned as nothing: for the servant who knows the will of his master, and does not do it, will be beaten with many blows. The Lord too says something of the kind in the Gospel according to John: I am the true vine, and my Father is the husbandman. Every branch that abides in me and does not bear fruit, the Father will take it away; and every one that bears fruit he will cleanse, that it may bear more fruit; but if the Savior is the vine and we the branches and God the Father the husbandman, as long as we keep God's commandments we are continually cultivated, and our cultivation is a cleansing: for it is not of him who wills, nor of him who runs, but of God who shows mercy; and if, bearing fruit, we are cleansed by the Father so that we may bear more abundant fruit, it is shown that all our effort, by God's help, reaches its end and yields the fruit of fullness; but the branch that has not borne fruit will be taken away by the Father, and once it has been taken away, what the Father does with it Scripture does not say, unless perhaps separation from the body of Christ is everlasting punishment and destruction. What we have understood of the vineyard and the vine, the Savior teaches under another figure in the Gospel: Salt is good; but if the salt has lost its savor, it is useful for nothing except to be thrown out and trodden underfoot by men; and the eye, which gives light to the whole body, if it has been blinded, hears from the Lord: If the light that is in you is darkness, how great will the darkness itself be? For in comparison with the transgressor, and with him who has denied the God he first confessed, lighter is the punishment of those who never had knowledge of God. The trees of the woodlands or forests, however, according to the variety of believers, have some usefulness in a great house, in which there are not only vessels of gold and silver but also of wood and earthenware, some for honor, and some for dishonor.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.15.4-8":["Now the ambiguity of the Hebrew word 'sene', which is written with three letters, 'sin' and 'nun' and 'iod', is this: if it is read 'sene' it means 'two', if 'sane', 'years'; hence it came about that for its two extremities, which we translated 'both its parts', the Seventy translated 'its yearly cleansing', whereas Aquila said 'its two last parts', Symmachus and Theodotion 'its two extremities'; and the sequence is this: 'As a fruitless vine is given to the fire to no use, so Jerusalem too, or rather its inhabitants, will be given up to the flames, so that they go out from the fire and the fire consumes them, that is: even if they escape from the city, outside they will be consumed by the enemy's sword, and those who remain, crushed by the very weight of their miseries, will recognize that I am the Lord, when I have set, or set firm, my face against them and have given them up to everlasting desolation: for they proved not sinners like the other nations, but transgressors' — for it is one thing to neglect what you do not know, and another to despise what you have worshiped —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.16.1-2":["And the word of the Lord came to me, saying: Son of man, make known to Jerusalem her abominations, and you shall say: Thus says the Lord God to Jerusalem. (LXX: \"And the word of the Lord came to me, saying: Son of man, bear witness to Jerusalem of her iniquities, and you shall say to her: Thus says Adonai the Lord.\") Where the compass of the individual prophecies was brief, we set down whole passages, adding at once beneath them what seemed right to us; here, however, because a long discourse is directed to Jerusalem by one who bears witness to her and teaches her the sins she has committed, and the prophecy extends to nearly two hundred lines, namely up to that place in which it is written: When I have been appeased toward you in all that you have done, says the Lord God, we must set out the whole prophecy in parts, and fit the due explanations to the parts we have set out. Under the person of a harlot woman, who was first joined in union with a husband, there is woven together the birth and upbringing and youth and marriage and adultery and divorce and recall again of Jerusalem, so that both the clemency and the judgment of the husband and the crimes of the adulteress and harlot may be recognized, when after all the punishments he raises up for her an everlasting covenant, that she may remember her iniquity and be confounded, and it may no longer be hers to open her mouth because of her confusion, when he has been appeased toward her in all that she had done; and it greatly profits sinners to know what they have done: whence the penitent too says: For I know my iniquity, and my sin is ever against me. Now Jerusalem can be understood in four ways: either this one which was set on fire by the Babylonian and the Roman flame, or the heavenly one of the firstborn, or the Church, which means 'vision of peace', or the souls of individuals who by faith behold God. And what many think should be interpreted of the heavenly Jerusalem the Church does not accept, lest we be compelled to take all that is woven together in the present prophecy as referring to the fall and torments of the heavenly powers, and to their restoration to their former state.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.16.3":["Your root and your generation from the land of Canaan, your father an Amorite and your mother a Hittite. Great is the faith, great the boldness of the prophets, that one man should charge a whole city with ignoble birth; admirable is Daniel who, to an elder who was sinning and joining murder to adultery, dared as a boy to say: Seed of Canaan and not of Judah, beauty has deceived you; great too is Isaiah, who cries out to the princes and to the people of the Jews: Hear the word of the Lord, princes of Sodom; give ear to the words of the Lord, people of Gomorrah; but no less is Ezekiel, who speaks confidently to the whole city: Your root and your generation from the land of Canaan, your father an Amorite and your mother a Hittite. Although Stephen too, the first martyr of the Gospel, spoke thus to the raging people: Stiff of neck, and uncircumcised in heart, you have always resisted the Holy Spirit, and although we can take the root of Jerusalem and her generation, the land of Canaan, in the sense that the people was called in Egypt and dwelt there a long time — for Ham, the father of Canaan, was the founder of the Egyptian nation —, yet, according to the likeness of their crimes, let us say that the root of Jerusalem is the land of Egypt; whence also her father is called 'amorrhaeus' [Amorite], which means διαδούμενος, that is, 'celebrated by much talk', and her mother 'chethaea' [Hittite], that is, ἐξιστῶσα, 'she who' either 'is herself mad' or 'drives others into madness': Everyone, indeed, who commits sin is born of the devil, whence also in the whole world the name of this ancient father is notorious, and it turns many into madness. And Jerusalem is commanded, whether according to the letter or according to the spirit, to leave her ancient father, and it is said to her: Hear, daughter, and see, and incline your ear, and forget your people and your father's house; and the king will desire your beauty, and it is promised her, if after sin she is willing to return to her true father: I will restore you as you were when you were born. Such is the nobility of the city of Jerusalem, which, descending from the stock of Abraham, Isaac and Jacob, had God as her father, and yet, because she sinned and left her true parent, deserves to hear: Your root and your generation from the land of Canaan, your father an Amorite and your mother a Hittite! If she heard this, what will become of us, who, called out of the filth of the nations, lost every stain in the washing of the Savior, if we defile the garment of Christ and do not have the wedding garment at the banquet? Surely this: that, with hands and feet bound, we be handed over to everlasting fires, in which there is weeping of eyes and gnashing of teeth.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.16.4":["And when you were born, on the day of your birth, your navel was not cut, and you were not washed in water for salvation, nor salted with salt, nor wrapped in swaddling cloths. No eye spared you, to do for you one of these things, having pity on you, but you were cast out upon the face of the earth in the abjection of your soul on the day on which you were born. (LXX: \"And your generation: on the day on which you were born, they did not bind your breasts, and you were not washed in water for salvation, nor salted with salt, and you were not wrapped in swaddling cloths. Nor did your eye spare you, that I should do for you one of all these things, and that it should suffer anything over you, and you were cast out upon the face of the field for the depravity of your soul on the day on which you were born.\") Let us discuss each point in the order of the reading. When Jerusalem was born of an Amorite father and a Hittite mother and poured forth from the womb, her navel was not cut, the navel by which fetuses are nourished in the belly, like trees and shrubs which are nourished through their roots by the hidden moisture of the earth — and just as the seed-bed of men is signified in the loins, so the genitals of women, by a decent expression, according to the custom of the Scriptures, are called the navel, on the testimony of Job who, under the figure of the dragon, speaks of the devil: His strength is in his loins, and his might in the navel of his belly; for this dragon, the ancient serpent who is called the devil and Satan and deceived the whole world, has his strength against men in the loins, against women in the navel —, and this signifies of Jerusalem that she did not at once receive the Law, nor were the beginnings of her shameful birth cut away from her, but she first lived a Gentile life; for which the Seventy translated: On the day on which you were born, they did not bind your breasts, since Holy Scripture has this custom, that for the heart it names the little breast, or the breast, and the breasts, as it sees fit for each place — the priests, in whom there ought to be doctrine, and from whose mouth they seek the Law, receive the little breast; John reclines upon the breast of the Lord, to draw the streams of wisdom from the richest fountain; the virgin's two breasts in the Song of Songs are like two fawns, twins of a gazelle, which feed among the lilies, until the day breathes and the shadows are removed; a careful mother, as soon as her baby girl is born, binds her breasts, so that they may refrain from tender swelling and keep their virginal beauty, but when she has come to the age of puberty, it is said of her: Will a bride forget her adornment, or a virgin her breast-band? —. There follows: And you were not washed in water for salvation; the bloody bodies of infants are usually washed as soon as they are sent forth from the womb; so too the spiritual birth needs the saving washing: For no one is clean from filth, not even if his life has been of one day, and in the Psalms we read: In iniquities I was conceived, and in sins my mother conceived me; the second birth undoes the first birth, for it is written: Unless one is reborn of water and the Spirit, he cannot enter into the kingdom of God; and there are many washings which the pagans, in their mysteries, and the heretics promise, who all wash but do not wash for salvation; therefore it is added: And you were not washed in water for salvation, which indeed can be understood not only of heretics but also of churchmen who do not receive saving baptism with full faith, of whom it must be said that they received the water but did not receive the Spirit — just as that Simon the magician, who wished to buy the grace of God with money, was indeed baptized in water but was by no means baptized for salvation —. Thirdly it is said: Nor salted with salt; the tender bodies of infants, while they still keep the warmth of the womb and with their first wail bear witness to the beginnings of a toilsome life, are usually touched with salt by the midwives, so that they may be drier and be firmed; but Jerusalem, born of evil parents, obtained no savor, no care; to those, however, who are reborn in Christ it is said: You are the salt of the earth, and they are commanded by the Apostle: Let your speech always be seasoned with salt in grace — whence also in common speech the wise are called 'salty' and the foolish 'unsalted', and in Leviticus it is enacted by law: Every sacrifice of yours shall be salted with salt, the salt of the covenant of the Lord shall not cease from your sacrifices: in all your gifts you shall offer salt; he who has been seasoned with this salt and by its admixture has dried up all stench and harmful moisture will no longer say: My scars have putrefied and become corrupt before the face of my foolishness. I know that I have read in a certain volume about the Lord Savior that he himself is the heavenly salt, and has seasoned with his savor not only earthly and infernal things but heavenly ones too, and that what is written is fulfilled: Glory to God in the highest, and on earth peace to men of good will —. Fourth is: Nor wrapped in swaddling cloths; the Savior too was wrapped in the swaddling cloths of infancy, and everyone who is born needs the protection of God through the wrappings of swaddling cloths. It is natural, where at least the care of the parents is not lacking, for newborn infants first to have the navel cut, then to be washed with water to wash off the blood, thirdly for the moisture of the little bodies to be dried by the application of salt, fourthly for the tender bodies of infants to be wrapped in swaddling cloths, for a twofold reason: both so that the body may be dried by the salt, the body being kept and held tight by the cloths so that it does not run off, and so that the most tender limbs may not easily become misshapen — whence also the bodies of the barbarians are straighter than Roman bodies: for up to the second and third year they are always wrapped in swaddling cloths —; but not so Jerusalem, whose navel was not cut off, nor were her breasts bound, nor was she washed with water for salvation, nor salted with salt, nor wrapped and bound in the swaddling cloths of infancy.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.16.5":["But why she deserved none of these things, the following Scripture testifies: No eye spared you, to do for you one of these things, having pity on you, for which the Seventy put: Your eye did not spare you, that I should do for you one of all these things, and the twofold edition must have a twofold understanding; the former signifies this: No eye spared you, to do for you one of these things, having pity on you, and the sense is: 'No one had pity on you, since God was offended; no one's heart was moved over you, so as to do for you at least one of the four things above, since you did not deserve to receive them all together'; in the other it is said: Your eye did not spare you, that I should do for you one of all these things, and it has this sense: 'You acted so, and were so born in sins, and in sins your mother conceived you, that not even you yourself had pity on yourself. And since you behaved so as to be cruel against yourself through evil works, what could I do for you, who did not deserve to receive even one of the things above? Since, then, none of these things which are usually done for infants was done in you, and this not without cause nor without judgment but through your own fault and sin, since not even you yourself had pity on yourself, therefore: You were cast out on the face of the earth (or of the field), and cast out because of the depravity of your soul on the day on which you were born' — let us pray that we be not cast out through depravity of soul onto the face of the field, in which wide and spacious is the way that leads to death, in which the cavalry of the Chaldeans runs riot —. And at the same time it must be considered that no one, on the day on which he is born, can commit anything depraved, except at the time of the washing, when a wise generation is taken up by the faith of the believer.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.16.6":["But passing by you I saw you trampled in your blood, and I said to you, when you were in your blood: Live. I said, I say, to you: In your blood live. I made you multiplied like the sprout of the field, and you went in and came to womanly finery, your breasts swelled and your hair sprouted; and you were naked and full of confusion. (LXX: \"And I passed by you and saw you mixed in your blood, and I said to you: From your blood, life; be multiplied. Like the growth of the field I made you, and you were multiplied and were magnified; and you entered into the cities of cities, your breasts were raised and your hair sprang up; but you were naked, full of disgrace.\") 'After you were cast out on the face of the field, or upon the face of the earth, because of the depravity of your soul, you no longer deserved my help. Nevertheless: Passing by you I saw you trampled (or mixed) in your blood, that is, guilty of deadly crimes, and I summoned you, granting repentance, and I said to you: Although you are in blood, yet live, converted.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.16.7":["And so great was the abundance of all things in you, that you had the likeness of a most fertile field — and the blessing of Jacob with which his father blessed him, saying: Behold, the smell of my son is as the smell of a full field which the Lord has blessed —, and that you entered the inner chambers of the Lord — and said with the bride: The king has brought me into his chamber —, and came to womanly finery'; for which the Seventy translated: And you entered into the cities of cities, so that, just as one speaks of the Song of 'Songs' and of an age of 'ages', so cities are called cities of 'cities', of which it is said in the Gospel: 'Have authority over ten (or over five) cities, so that you are called not only a city but a metropolis city', according to what is written in Isaiah: Faithful Zion, mother of cities; moreover, what Symmachus translated: And you came to the adornment of women, or Theodotion: And you entered to the adornment of adornments, for which we, following the Hebrew sense, said: And you came to womanly finery, shows the time of puberty, when girls are called 'marriageable' and can be joined in the embraces of husbands. Then there follows: Your breasts swelled and your hair sprouted: the maturity of young girls is decently indicated, when the breasts swell, or are raised, and the hair sprouts. And although she was ready for marriage and had beauty of body, she did not have the covering of garments nor was she protected by the help of God; if anyone does not have the garment of Christ, he is naked; if anyone is not clothed with the bowels of mercy, of goodness, of humility, of modesty, of meekness, of patience, he lies upon the face of the earth, and his beauty is disfigured by confusion and nakedness.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.16.8-9":["And you became mine. And I washed you with water, and cleansed your blood from you, and anointed you with oil. 'Because I spread my wings and covered your disgrace and swore to you and entered into a pact with you, therefore you have become my possession, so that you, who were another's when you were seen unclean, began to be mine when I confirmed my pact with you by an oath', or, according to the custom of the prophets: 'You became mine', he says, 'stands for: you were joined to me in marriage; and I washed you', he says, 'with the water of saving baptism': Unless, indeed, one is reborn of water and the Spirit, he cannot enter into the kingdom of God; and elsewhere we read: He will baptize you in the Holy Spirit and fire, of which baptism Isaiah too speaks, saying: The Lord will wash away the filth of the sons and daughters of Zion, and will cleanse the blood from their midst, by a spirit of judgment and a spirit of burning. And when he has washed, and cleansed her of the blood — the blood from which, by the touch of his fringe, he also healed the αἱμορροοῦσαν [woman with a flow of blood] who for twelve years had been running with gore —, it is not enough to wash with water and cleanse from blood, unless he also anoints with oil, according to which the Samaritan, which means 'guardian', first soothed the wounds of the half-dead man by pouring in oil, then checked them with the sharpness of wine; but our Lord was anointed with another oil, which would not soothe the pain of wounds but grant gladness, as the Holy Spirit says to him: Therefore God, your God, has anointed you with the oil of exultation above your fellows. The opposing powers too have the smoothness of a counterfeit oil, promising sweet things through the heretics: things which are turned into bitterness, and which the prophet detests, recalling: But let not the oil of the sinner fatten my head. But if from anointing the anointed one, that is, Christ, is so named, see how far Jerusalem had advanced, that she too, anointed with spiritual oil, received the name of Christ, according to what we read in the Psalms of Abraham: Do not touch my christs, and do no evil among my prophets.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.16.8":["And I passed by you and saw you; and behold, your time, the time of lovers. And I spread my cloak over you, and covered your disgrace. (LXX: \"And I passed by you and saw you; and behold, your time, the time of those turning aside. And I spread my wings over you, and covered your disgrace.\") What great clemency! It had not been enough to have seen her once trampled in blood and to have called her to repentance, but when she sins again he visits her and, because she was naked and full of confusion, he comes again and visits; and behold, her time, the time 'of lovers' — or according to the Seventy 'of those turning aside', and according to the second translation of Aquila, συναλλαγῆς, namely 'of betrothal' and 'of the time of the nuptials', but according to the first edition of the same and Theodotion μαστῶν, which means 'of breasts', for which Symmachus rendered ἀγαπῆς, which signifies 'love' —; that, then, was the time when women can be joined and sustain marital intercourse. Nevertheless the merciful God spread his wings, took them up, and carried them on his shoulders — according to what is written in the Gospel: How often I wished to gather your children as a hen gathers her chicks under her wings, and you would not —, or he spread a 'cloak' in place of 'wings' — for this is what πτερυγίῳ [wing; also the skirt of a garment] means, with which Ruth too was covered as she slept at the feet of Boaz; and on the border, that is, the πτερυγίῳ, of our garment we are commanded to fasten ὑακίνθινας [hyacinth-blue] fringes —; and he did these things to cover the disgrace of Jerusalem — indicating by this name the genital body of women; for indeed, according to the Apostle, we surround the uncomely parts of our body with greater honor, whence also the psalmist sings: Blessed are they whose iniquities are forgiven, and whose sins are covered; and because Ham did not cover these in his father, he is marked with an everlasting curse —. What we have said of Jerusalem, let us refer to our soul, which, as long as it is set in infancy, is without sin; but when the time of its age has come, there are many lovers — of demons and of heretics and of perverse dogmas — who desire to turn aside to it; they are repelled by the protection of the Lord, so that it may receive not the satellites of the devil but the ministers of the Savior. For 'of those turning aside', 'love' and 'of breasts', the Hebrew has 'dodim', which, according to the peculiar character of their language, signifies all the things translated above. The time 'of love' and 'of breasts', or 'of the lovers of God', can be understood as Abraham and Isaac and Jacob, when the Lord promised them that his people would be freed after the Egyptian servitude.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","And I swore to you, and entered into a pact with you, says the Lord God. (LXX: \"And I swore to you, and entered into a covenant with you, says Adonai the Lord.\") 'Because I spread my cloak, or my wings, over you and covered your confusion, therefore I swore to you and entered into a pact, or covenant, with you, so that you might be joined to me rather than to the others who wished to turn aside to you' — according to that: The Lord has sworn and will not repent: You are a priest forever, according to the order of Melchizedek; for an oath is the end of every dispute, and: As I live, says the Lord, 'I would rather have the repentance of the sinner than his death' —. At the time of the nuptials, then, Israel received the oath of God in place of every dowry gift — and because David had said of himself: Remember, O Lord, David and all his meekness, who swore to the Lord, vowed a vow to the God of Jacob, the Lord himself also swears to him, as Scripture says: The Lord has sworn truth to David, and will not frustrate him.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.16.10":["And I clothed you in parti-colored garments. (LXX: \"And I dressed you in varied garments.\") In the ceremonies of the Law, so that, occupied in the study of them, you would forsake idols. These are the varied garments with which Joseph was clothed, and his brothers could not sell him until they had first stripped him of the variety of his tunic; and of the bride it is said in Psalm 44 (45): The queen stood at your right hand in gilded clothing, surrounded with variety; this is the garment of which the Apostle too speaks: Having put on the new man, who is renewed in knowledge according to the image of the Creator. Do we want to know what the varied garments are? Let the same Apostle teach us, writing to believers: Put on therefore the bowels of mercy, of goodness, of humility, of meekness, of patience — what is more beautiful than this variety? —, of which Job too spoke: Who gave women the wisdom of weaving or the skill of variety?, and the high priest, once a year, when he burned incense for the people and entered the Holy of Holies, used varied vestments. Certainly, if riches, and not holiness and diversity of virtues, made the variety of garments, Pharaoh and Nebuchadnezzar would be written to have had these garments, and not Joseph, who under a sojourning and shepherd father could not have parti-colored and royal garments.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","And I shod you with violet. (LXX: \"And I shod you with hyacinth.\") Aquila and Symmachus translated 'violet', the Seventy and Theodotion 'hyacinthine', for which in the Hebrew is written 'thas'. And beautifully, because the discourse is directed to Jerusalem and, under the person of a woman, all her adornment is set out, ὑακίνθινα [hyacinth-colored] or ἰάνθινα [violet] shoes are spoken of, which do not suit the person of men — indeed, those who are to keep the Passover are commanded, without any variety of color, to have their loins girded and their feet shod; these are the shoes of which the Apostle writes: Feet shod in the preparation of the Gospel of peace; Philo, the most eloquent man among the Jews, compares the ὑάκινθον [hyacinth] in the vestments of the high priest to the air, by which he thinks the higher and heavenly things are signified —. Nor is Jerusalem shod with hyacinthine shoes until she has been washed with water — whence also the apostles, whom the Lord had earlier commanded to shake off the dust of their feet, have their feet washed by the Savior; and the bride says in the Song of Songs: I have washed my feet, how shall I defile them?; and the divine word testifies to Moses and Joshua that they should loose the latchet of their shoe, because the place in which they stand is holy —; but when the feet have been washed and purged of all filth, they are shod with ὑακίνθινις or ἰάνθινις — both of which are of an ἀέριον [air-like] and κυανέου [dark blue] color — so that they may be caught up to meet the Lord in the air, and hasten to the heavenly kingdoms.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","And I girded you with fine linen. Jerusalem is not only clothed in parti-colored garments and shod with ὑακίνθινις, but is also girded with fine linen, from which the finest threads are woven in the vestment of the high priest; and the wife in Proverbs, who wove two cloaks for her husband, of the present age and of the future, is said to have made garments for herself of fine linen and purple. With this fine linen the reins are girded whenever the rich incitements of lust are to be thinned, and nothing of gross moisture is left in them, and we fulfill the precept of the Apostle, who says: Stand therefore with your loins girded in truth — as though he said in other words 'with fine linen' —. And the Lord says to the apostles: Let your loins be girded, and lamps in your hands: for unless we bind up the flowing humors of the loins, we cannot have lamps in our hands; whence God also says to Job: Gird up your loins like a man. And those who are to eat the lamb, with feet shod, holding a staff, standing in the truth of the Gospel and prepared for the blood of Christ, are said to have their loins girded; with this belt, which mortifies the loins, Elijah too and John the Baptist are girded; of these the penitent too groans tearfully, saying: For my loins are filled with illusions.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","And I clothed you with fine stuffs. (LXX: \"And I surrounded you with trichaptum.\") For which Aquila put 'flowery' or 'palpable', Symmachus 'garment', Theodotion the Hebrew word itself, 'messe'; and when I was diligently inquiring what the word 'trichaptum', which the Seventy translated, meant, and could find from none of the Greeks either the usage or the ἐτυμολογίαν [etymology] of the word, at last I learned that it was coined by the Seventy — for new names must be fixed to new things —, because the garment was of such fineness that it was believed to have the thinness of hairs and of the hair of the head; whence I too, wishing to express the thinness of the garment, translated 'subtilibus' [fine stuffs] for 'trichapto', because it was woven with fine warp and weft. And fittingly, to those whom he had rebuked, saying: The heart of this people has grown fat, and Jacob ate and drank and was filled and grew fat, and the beloved kicked and forsook God his maker, fineness of garments is now granted; for in the book of Wisdom too, which by some is ascribed to Solomon, the spirit of wisdom is called only-begotten and manifold, fine and mobile; and the manna, of which it is written: Man ate the bread of angels, is said to have been very fine, in the likeness of hoarfrost and of coriander seed. We can, according to the interpretation of the second edition of Aquila, who translated 'flowery', understand the 'garment' through a μεταφορὰν [metaphor] as the fertility of the land of Judea, or as the beauty of divine worship.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.16.11":["And I adorned you with adornment. Here he sets down the adornments in general, which in what follows he enumerates one by one: bracelets and torque, earrings and crown, and the rest, which adornments he bestowed on Jerusalem, and in Isaiah it is said that by her own fault she lost them all: for it is written: Because the daughters of Jerusalem were lifted up (that is, were proud) and walked with lofty neck, and the rest: instead of a girdle they were girded with ropes, and instead of the adornment of the head disfigured with baldness, and instead of beautiful and soft clothes surrounded with the roughness of sackcloth.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","And I put bracelets on your hands. (LXX: \"And I surrounded your hands with bracelets.\") When God gives us good works, he puts bracelets around our hands. Whence also he says that he was likened in the hands of the prophets, and we frequently read that the word came in the hand of Haggai or Jeremiah and the others; in Job too the lip of the dragon is pierced with a bracelet, and all his venomous hissings, which are understood in perverse dogmas, are pierced by the ring of good works.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","And a torque around your neck. For 'torque', which we translated following the second edition of Aquila and Symmachus, the Seventy and Theodotion translated κάθεμα [pendant] — which is written not only here but also in Isaiah: The Lord will take away the glory of their garment, and the little lamprey-chains and the clusters and the rings, and the κάθεμα —; and I think that by κάθεμα they meant a necklace of various gems descending onto the breast of women, which itself too is a most beautiful adornment of women.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.16.12":["And I gave an earring over the mouth (or your nostrils). The Hebrew word 'nezem', except for Symmachus, who translated ἐπιρρίνιον [nose-ring], all translated 'earring', not that earrings are put in the nostrils — they are called 'inaures' [earrings] from the fact that they hang from the ears [aures] —, but that a ring, made in the likeness of earrings, is called by the same word; and to this day, among the other adornments of women, golden rings are wont to hang from the forehead over the mouth and to overhang the nostrils. These we receive according to mystical understanding when with the Apostle we can say: We are the good odor of Christ in those who are being saved, and in those who perish: to some an odor of death unto death, to others an odor of life unto life; when we live and it can be said of us: The dead shall not praise you, O Lord, but we the living, then God smells the odor of our sacrifice of good fragrance, and we have the golden ring of divine senses and dogmas in our nostrils; but when we are dead and wallow in the mire and in the filth of lusts, we have in our nostrils that earring of which Solomon wrote: As golden earrings in the nostrils of a swine, so is beauty to an ill-mannered woman.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","And rings for your ears. (LXX: \"And little wheels upon your ears.\") He who stops his ears so as not to hear the judgment of blood, and hedges them with thorns so as not to become a partaker with the envious and the detractors — of whom it is written: Do not mingle with detractors; for their destruction will come suddenly, and who knows the ruin of both? —, namely both of him who detracts and of him who gladly listens, this man has rings and little wheels in his ears — not the wheels which follow the Cherubim: for that belongs to the evangelical powers, but little wheels, in the diminutive —, which befit human frailty. Therefore it is also said to God: The voice of your thunder in the wheel: for in those who pass over earthly things and touch the earth with scarcely the slightest footprints — of whom it is written: Holy stones are rolled upon the earth —, the voice of the Lord's thunder and of lofty dogmas resounds; but those who are led by human errors and have no solid footing but are carried about by every wind of doctrine and, cast out of the paradise of firmness, dwell in the region of Naid [Nod], which means 'fluctuation', deserve to bear the sentence of a lying tongue, which sets on fire the wheel of our birth.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","And a crown of beauty on your head. (LXX: \"And a crown of boasting upon your head.\") We translated 'beauty' following Symmachus, for the elegance of the sense; otherwise both in the Hebrew, 'thopherth', and in the other translators, καύχησις, it is called 'boasting'. The other adornments belong to individual members; the adornment of the head is the dignity of the whole body; and he calls crown the diadem of women, by which the abundance of the hair is bound and adorned. Do we want to know what the crown of beauty on the head of Jerusalem is? Let us unroll Exodus, in which it is written: And he made a plate of gold, and wrote on it letters formed with the seal of the sanctification of the Lord, and put it upon the head of Aaron; 'the sign of the Lord's holiness' is the name of almighty God, which is written with four Hebrew letters and among them is called 'ineffable', since his name cannot be spoken — whose majesty even paganism is not ignorant of, and the altar of the Athenians bears witness, 'To the Unknown God', whence also the Apostle: What you, he says, worship as unknown, this we proclaim to you —. This I think is the crown of which he also speaks thus in the Psalms: The light of your countenance is sealed upon us, O Lord, and the Apostle says: I have finished the course, I have kept the faith; for the rest there is laid up for me the crown of righteousness, which the righteous judge will render to me, and in another place to believers: My joy and my crown: for the glory of fathers is their sons; and in Solomon it is written: Old age is a crown of glory, namely of those who have destroyed the things that belong to little ones and, after the age of the perfect man, have come to old age and to the name of fathers, to whom John writes after the children and the young men, who can say: When I was a little one, I spoke as a little one, I understood as a little one, I thought as a little one; when I became a man, I destroyed the things that belong to a little one.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.16.13":["And you were adorned with gold and silver. We have often said that gold pertains to the mind, silver to speech. And may the Lord grant us that we may deserve to receive the divine sense and wisdom, and to bring forth with grace of eloquence what we conceive in the mind, and by no means to make idols of these, as the heretics do, of whom it is written: I gave them silver and gold; but they made Baal of the silver and gold. And this, according to the spiritual understanding, is the more fitting for the adornment of women; otherwise silver, the cheaper material, is the adornment of country women and of poor women and maidservants, which does not befit Jerusalem, who is said to have advanced to be queen.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","And you were clothed with fine linen and many-threaded stuff and many colors. (LXX: \"And your cloaks of fine linen and trichapta and variegated things.\") The Hebrew word 'mesi' Theodotion here too set down as it stands, as above, Aquila ἄνθινον [flowered], Symmachus 'polymitum' [many-threaded], and we, what above we called 'subtile' [fine], here, following Symmachus, called 'polymitum'; the Seventy translated 'trichapta', keeping their former interpretation, so as to show garments fine like hairs. Now the whole adornment of women is described: that she is washed, that she is cleansed, that she is anointed with oil, and is clothed in parti-colored garments, is shod with ὑακίνθινις, is girded with fine linen, is clothed with trichapta or fine stuffs, receives bracelets, and the golden torque, rings hanging over the mouth, and little wheels in the ears, a diadem on the head, is decorated with gold and silver — although silver is the cheaper material —, and after all this there is added: You were clothed with fine linen and many-threaded stuff and variegated colors — or, as the Seventy translated, 'with cloak and mantles', for this is what περιβόλαια signifies —, so that she was not only adorned in the care of her body and had all the necklaces of women, but the husband's diligence was also solicitous for her bedding and her couch, so that, when he had completed these things, he might come to the delights of foods, and would not allow anything to be lacking to her even in these. All of which, following the explanation above, we have fitted to the spiritual understanding: that she is covered with fine linen and fine stuffs, so that the delicate limbs of the wife may not be weighed down by the heavy burden of mantles, but may display usefulness and beauty alike.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","Fine flour and honey and oil you ate, and you became beautiful greatly, exceedingly. After the clothing he takes care of food, that Jerusalem may eat fine flour and honey and oil, either separately, as many reckon, or, by a mixture of the three, a sweeter bread, the bread which came down from heaven; and under three names, as some think, it indicates the mystery of the Trinity, not that there is one substance and another, but that by the diverse names of fine flour and honey and oil the one sweetness of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit is shown. And this is to be noted, that in many sacrifices fine flour is offered, the marrow of the wheat and the purest, of which it is written: And he satisfied them with the fat of wheat, not of barley nor of meal, which is offered only when a husband is moved by a spirit of jealousy. In the volume of Judges we read of 'Debboram' [Deborah], which means 'bee', whose prophecy is the sweetest honey, and this is referred to the Holy Spirit, who among the Hebrews is called by a feminine name: in the Gospel of the Hebrews too, which the Nazarenes read, the Savior is introduced speaking: Just now my mother, the Holy Spirit, took hold of me; moreover the fine flour, which is made from wheat, is referred to the Lord, who says of himself: Unless a grain of wheat falls into the earth and dies, it remains alone; but if it dies, it bears much fruit; and the oil, which sustains and refreshes the limbs of the weary, and is the nourishment of light, and with which the light of the lamp is kindled continually in the tabernacle of God, is said to befit the Father; and all these grant one grace to believers, as the Apostle says: There are divisions of graces, but one Spirit; and divisions of ministries, but one Lord; and divisions of operations, but one and the same God. He who has eaten such food, and has been fed and nourished with this sweetest and most delightful bread and the splendor of shining oil, will be turned into beauty and become most beautiful: not once, but repeated: 'exceedingly, exceedingly'; for the doubled adverb gives the speech this weight, that nothing further could be added to the greatness of the beauty.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","And you advanced to a kingdom. — This is not found in the Seventy, who perhaps, translating the Scriptures at Alexandria from the Hebrew truth into the Greek language, feared to publish it, lest they seem to offend the king of Egypt by recording that a kingdom was owed to Jerusalem by God; although the learned prove that only the five books of Moses were translated by them. — After manifold adornment, then, after the crown, after the abundance of gold and silver, after the most beautiful garments and mantles, after incredible beauty, she advances to be queen, because she is the city of the great king, of whom it is written: O God, give your judgment to the king, and your righteousness to the king's son; and in the Revelation of John Jerusalem is described as a bride adorned and arrayed for her husband; in the Psalms also it is said of her: The queen stood at your right hand in gilded clothing, surrounded with variety. But all that we say of Jerusalem, lest we dwell on each detail and the size of the books bring weariness to the readers, let us refer to the Church; for if Jerusalem, according to the Apostle, is our mother, and our mother is the Church, consequently Jerusalem is the Church, the mother of the firstborn who are enrolled in heaven.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.16.14":["And your name went forth among the nations because of your fairness; for you were perfect in my beauty, which I set upon you, says the Lord God. (LXX: \"And your name went out among the nations in your fairness; which was made complete in loveliness, which I set upon you, says the Lord God.\") Symmachus translated this passage more clearly: And your name went forth among the nations; which was perfect because of my dignity, which I had set upon you, says the Lord God. And the sense is: 'By my benefits and my incredible bounty you came to the title of queen, so that the talk of all the nations spoke of you, and you were perfect, not by your own merits and virtues but by my liberality': Unless, indeed, the Lord builds the house, they have labored in vain who build it. Unless the Lord guards the city, he has watched in vain who guards it; for he who plants and he who waters is nothing, unless the Lord gives the increase, because: It is not of him who wills nor of him who runs, but of God who has mercy, so that, when we have done all things, we may say: We are unprofitable servants; we have done what we ought to do; not that the free choice of man is taken away by the beneficence of God, but that this very freedom ought to have the Lord as its helper.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.16.15":["And having confidence in your loveliness you fornicated in your name, and you laid out your fornication to everyone passing by, to become his. Great is the peril of trusting not in the clemency of God but in one's own beauty; and the more exalted anyone has been, the more he ought to fear lest he fall and fornicate in his own name. For the adverse powers despise ordinary foods and desire foreign nourishment; whence it is said of the devil: His foods are choice; he does not want to deceive just anyone: it is King Saul, who was chosen by the Lord, and Judas the apostle that he hastens to trip up. Let us not, therefore, trust in our own loveliness, nor think the liberality of God to be our own virtue, but rather let us hear: Do not boast about tomorrow, for you do not know what the coming day may bring forth, and in another place: Brothers, if a man is overtaken in any sin, you who are spiritual, perfect such a one in a spirit of meekness, considering yourself, lest you too be tempted; and let us not lay out, or pour out, our fornication to everyone passing by, so as to become not God's, in whom we believe, but his by whom we are defiled: he who receives all vices, and takes into his bosom the passing spirit of diverse sins, and spreads his feet for everyone passing by, he pours out his fornication, and begins to endure the dominion of him whose host he has become.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.16.16":["And taking some of your garments you made for yourself high places sewn together here and there; and you fornicated upon them, as has not been done nor will be. (LXX: \"And you took some of your garments and made yourself idols sewn together; and you fornicated upon them, and you will not enter nor shall it be.\") Symmachus translated this passage thus: And when you had taken some of your clothes, you made yourself many-colored high places; and you fornicated in them, things not done nor to be. For 'sewn together' and 'variegated' and 'high places' or 'idols', Aquila and Theodotion translated ἐμβολίσματα, which signifies 'diverse rags sewn together here and there' and 'an idol made like a plaster', as it were 'a πολύρραφον [much-stitched] garment'. 'I clothed you', he says, 'in variegated garments, and you were dressed in fine linen and many-threaded stuff and the variety of many forms; but you took your garments, with which you were gifted by my bounty, and made for yourself idols, or high places, which in Hebrew are called 'bamoth', a thing which never was done before nor will be done, that what you had received for adornment you should turn to the injury of the giver'. This can be understood of Jerusalem according to the letter thus: that she assigned all the precepts of the Law and her riches and the abundance of all things to idols, which no other nation has done or will do; for all the nations which from the beginning served idols are not guilty of so great a crime. But our Jerusalem, which means 'vision of peace', is torn by the heretics, when, plucking one testimony of the Scriptures or another from their proper places, they try to sew them onto places to which they cannot be fitted; to whom, as we read, it was also said above in this same prophet: Woe to those who sew pillows under every elbow of the hand, and make a covering over the head of every age, and the Lord, forbidding this to be done, says in the Gospel: No one puts a patch of rough cloth on an old garment; otherwise it takes away from its fullness, and a worse tear is made. Nor do they put new wine into old wineskins; such are the Nazarenes, who try to fit the observance of the old Law to the grace of the Gospel, and all the heretics who strive to sew a testimony that fits one sense in its own place onto another, whereas the Lord's tunic, woven from the top throughout, cannot be torn, but, woven by the Holy Spirit, admits no tearing. But what is said according to the Seventy: And you will not enter nor will it be, signifies this: 'When you have made for yourself idols of perverse dogmas which you take for high places, and have fornicated upon them, believing in the things which you yourself have fashioned, you will not be able to enter the temple of God, and your images will have no substance nor will they exist forever', as the Apostle says: We know, indeed, that an idol is nothing in the world.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.16.17":["And you took the vessels of your beauty, from my gold and from my silver which I gave you, and you made yourself male images, and you fornicated with them. According to the letter, the Lord commanded in the Law that there be made censers, bowls, a lampstand, the ark of the Covenant: all either of gold or gilded, and others of silver, all of which Jerusalem melted down and turned into idols of Bel or Baal, Chemosh, Ashtaroth and Milcom. According to the spiritual understanding, we make idols of the gold and silver of the holy Scriptures when we corrupt the grace of sense and eloquence by heretical depravity, and set our mouth in heaven, and our tongue passes through the earth; and male images is the name given to those in which every heresy fornicates, from the fact that each one thinks that what he worships and holds, which he has fashioned, is most robust and cannot be overthrown by any assault; these are the images which we have fashioned from our own heart and which are to be destroyed in the heavenly Jerusalem, of which the prophet foretells: O Lord, in your city you will scatter their image; for whoever is a man and has lost the name of God, it is said of him: Nevertheless man walks in an image; nevertheless he is disquieted in vain; but let us have one husband and venerate one image, which is the image of the invisible and almighty God.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.16.18-19":["And my oil and my perfume you set before them. And my bread (or, as the Seventy translated, my loaves which I gave you), fine flour, honey and oil with which I nourished you, you set in their sight for an odor of sweetness. The oil of which we spoke above, and the perfume or incense of which the psalmist cries out: Let my prayer be directed as incense in your sight, and the loaves of proposition which we are commanded to offer to God, the fine flour too, the purest sense of the Scriptures, and the honey which Jonathan tasted, and his eyes were opened and he was strengthened, wretched Jerusalem set in the sight of the idols — or before the male images —, that they might be for an odor of sweetness to them, things which by their nature are sweet and most delightful but, when they are offered to idols and false doctrines, are turned into bitterness.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.16.18":["And you took your many-colored garments, and covered them. 'The male images' is understood, so that she turned all the finery she had received for use into blasphemy. And we do this whenever we surround heretical depravity with prudence, temperance, fortitude, righteousness and all the virtues and, under their pretext, deceive all the simple, so that, seeing the honey of the virtues, they do not beware of the poisons of the vices.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.16.20-22":["And it came to pass, says the Lord God. And you took your sons and your daughters whom you bore to me, and you sacrificed them to them to be devoured — is your fornication a small thing, that you sacrificed my sons? —, you gave them up, consecrating them to them. And after all your abominations and fornications, you did not remember the days of your youth, when you were naked and full of shame, trampled in your blood. (LXX: \"And it came to pass, says Adonai the Lord. And you took your sons and your daughters whom you bore, and you sacrificed them to them for consumption, as though you had committed little fornication, and you killed your sons and gave them up when you offered them to them. This over and above all your fornication and your abominations; and you did not remember the day of your infancy, when you were naked and disgraced, you lived mingled in your blood.\") That Jerusalem sacrificed her sons and daughters to idols — the sons and daughters of whom it is written: Sons I have begotten and exalted, but they have despised me — holy Scripture records: They sacrificed their sons and their daughters to demons, and again: They poured out innocent blood, the blood of their sons and of their daughters, whom they sacrificed to the carved images of Canaan. Now according to the Hebrew he calls them 'his own' — for he himself had said of them: Israel is my firstborn son — or, according to the Seventy, 'yours', those whom you begot of fornication. And in that place where we have put: And you gave them up, consecrating them to them, for which the Seventy translated: And you gave them up for appeasing (or for expiating), Aquila, Symmachus and Theodotion put: You carried them across and led them through, by which the heathens, through fire, either carry their sons across while they are little or compel them, when grown, to pass through. 'Although you have done these things so, you did not remember your infancy, when I took you up, spattered with blood, and washed you, and — after the many things which the prophetic discourse has related — joined you to myself'. Our Jerusalem too, if she has been tripped up by heretical deceit, takes her sons who are stronger in faith and her daughters who do not have such great strength of faith — or certainly the sons who know every mystical thing, the daughters who follow the simple history — and hands them over to demons to be devoured, and while she kills them she believes that she is giving life and appeasing the images, which are glutted with their slaughter. And what is said according to the Seventy: This is over and above all your fornication and your abominations, signifies that the teaching of the heretics is worse than all sins and fornications, for it makes the sons of God sons of demons, nay rather it kills those whom she had either begotten for God with much labor or had made her own sons, those whom she begot in fornication.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.16.23-25":["And it happened after all your wickedness, woe, woe to you, says the Lord God, and you built yourself a brothel, and you made yourself a place of prostitution in all the streets; at every head of the way you built the sign of your prostitution, and you made your beauty abominable, and you parted your feet to every passer-by, and you multiplied your fornications. And you committed fornication with the sons of Egypt, your neighbors, of great flesh, and you multiplied your fornication to provoke me. (LXX: \"And it came to pass after all your wickednesses, ※ woe, woe to you, ⁚ says Adonai the Lord, and you built yourself a dwelling of fornication, and you made yourself prostitution in every street, and at the beginning of every way you built your fornications, and you corrupted your beauty, and you spread your legs to every passer-by, and you multiplied your fornication. And you committed fornication with the sons of Egypt, your neighbors, who are of great flesh, and you committed fornication manifoldly to provoke me to anger.\") This, which is said a second time: Woe, woe to you, the common edition does not have, but it was added from the translation of Theodotion; and again, where we said: And you built yourself a brothel, for which the Seventy translated: And you built yourself a dwelling of fornication, Symmachus and Theodotion translated πορνεῖον [brothel], while Aquila, wishing to express the ἐτυμολογίαν [etymology] of the Hebrew word 'gob', put βόθυνον, which we could call 'a pit', to signify a den of vice and a dug-out, dark cave in which the lust of prostitutes has its haunt; and what follows: And you made yourself a place of prostitution in all the streets, for which the Seventy said ἔκθεμα [a thing set out for show], has in the Hebrew 'rama', and Aquila as well as Symmachus and Theodotion translated it 'high place' or 'lofty'; and then in what follows it is also written: At every head of the way you built the sign of your prostitution — for 'sign' and 'fornication' the Hebrew again has 'rama', which we have translated either 'place of prostitution' or 'sign', since among the Hebrews it is a single word —, and this is set up, or is made rather high, so that the place of fornication may be seen from afar by those who wish to fornicate and there may be no need to search for it.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.16.23":["To our Jerusalem too, whether the Church or the souls of believers, if they have been negligent and have lain open to all vices, a twofold word of cursing is addressed, so that they hear: Woe, woe to you, says the Lord God, according to what is written in the Revelation of John: Woe upon all the inhabitants of the earth. For if a holy man says: I am a stranger and a sojourner, as all my fathers were, why should not the sinner be said to be an inhabitant of the earth?\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.16.24-26":["What we have said once, and again and again, let it suffice to have said for good: that under the person of a woman who commits fornication after her husband's many benefits, the fornication of Jerusalem is described: how she withdrew from God and was joined to idols and was not content with hidden fornication, but built herself a brothel and in every way set up a sign for passers-by — that they might come to the harlot and sate their lust, not so much their own as hers who spread her legs to every passer-by —, and disfigured the greatness of her beauty by the greatness of her fornication; and among other things she prostrated herself to the Egyptians too, her neighbors, out of love for great flesh, and, to display every kind of foulness, was disfigured by a manifold kind of filth.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.16.24":["Now he builds himself a brothel who sins with complete brazenness of brow, according to what is written: The sinner, when he has come into the depth of impiety, despises. And he raises a very high place in all the streets: For wide and spacious is the way that leads to death.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.16.25-26":["And all his ways are full of foulness, and to all the thoughts that supply the incitements of vices he opens his heart and spreads his legs, and he commits fornication with the Egyptians his neighbors, whose examples he follows, namely the heathens, who glory in their foulnesses, and he is so wicked as to surpass even them in foulness. And all this he does to provoke God to anger. Now the Egyptians are said to be of great flesh, either because of the greatness of their foulness or because of the deformity of their sins: hence the holy man is said to be of little flesh, which in him decreases daily and is thinned away by the virtues, so that it is no longer called flesh but spirit; and he says in the psalm: My soul has thirsted for you, O God, how manifoldly my flesh for you (or, as some copies have, how my flesh has been consumed), in a desert land, pathless and waterless, so in the holy place I have appeared to you; for this reason the Corinthian fornicator too is handed over: For the destruction of the flesh, that the spirit may be saved; of this flesh it is written: All flesh is grass, and all its glory like the flower of grass; the grass has withered and the flower has fallen; but the word of the Lord abides forever, of which the Apostle also spoke: Flesh and blood shall not possess the kingdom of God, and in Genesis God says: My spirit shall not remain in these men, because they are flesh, and to believers it is said: Those who are in the flesh cannot appease God. But you are not in the flesh but in the spirit; but the flesh of the saints is another, of which it is written: All flesh shall see the salvation of God.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.16.25":["And he builds at every head of the way, so as to leave no kind of sin untried, but, imitating the harlot of Proverbs, openly in the streets and at the corners of the streets he invites the foolish young men passing by to his embraces, and defiles the beauty of the soul which he received as a gift from God the creator.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.16.26":["Now no one doubts that Palestine is a neighbor of Egypt, since the Lord says to Abraham: I will give you all the land, from the river of Egypt to the great river Euphrates, so that on one side it has the Egyptians, on the other the Assyrians, with whom too she is said further on to have committed fornication; and the fornication of Jerusalem with the Egyptians is the imitation of their idolatry.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.16.27":["Behold, I will stretch out my hand over you, and I will take away your justification, and I will give you up in the souls of those who hate you, of the Palestinian daughters, who blush at your wicked way. (LXX: \"But if I stretch out my hand over you, and I will take away your ordinances, and I will give you into the souls of those who hate you, of the daughters of the foreigners, who made you turn aside from your way.\" And what follows: Ἠσέβησας, that is: You acted impiously, is fitted according to the Seventy to the following section, according to the others it is the end of the preceding one.) Now the Lord stretches out his hand over Jerusalem, to correct her in her sinning and to take away her ordinances, which, as long as she kept the precepts of God, were called the ordinances of the Lord, but when she worshiped idols and exchanged religion for impiety, are called her ordinances — which is to be understood not only of the law and the ceremonies, but also of the sabbaths and the feast days and the new moons: for when he gives the law he says: My sabbaths and: My feast days, but when they withdrew from God, it is said to them: Your new moons and sabbaths and the great day I will not receive; your fasting and rest and your solemnities my soul has hated; which is written of the people as well: for those who were formerly called the people of God, after they withdrew from the Lord, it is said of them to Moses: Your people have sinned, whom you led out of the land of Egypt —. What is here called the stretching out of the hand is elsewhere called lifting up or visitation: for when God does not rebuke the sinner, he draws back his hand, but if he sins and begins to sicken in the law of God, a visitation is sent to him, according to what is sung in the Psalms: If his sons forsake my law and do not walk in my judgments, I will visit their iniquities with the rod, and in this prophet it is written: The land, if it sins against me so as to commit an offense, I will stretch out my hand over it, and I will break the staff of its bread, and the rest; in which we ask what difference there is between the stretching out of the hand and the sending of it: for the devil says to the Lord: Send your hand, and touch all that he has; unless he blesses you to your face, and it seems to me that the hand is sent for the testing of those to whom it is sent, but stretched out for the punishment of those who deserve torments; and not only the stretching out and the sending, but also the spreading out of the hand of God is spoken of, as Isaiah proclaims in the person of the Lord: All day long I have spread out my hands to a people unbelieving and contradicting; the saints too are related to lift up their hands, as Scripture says: The lifting up of my hands an evening sacrifice. Now since the ordinances of God have been taken away from the people of the Jews, let us ask them what observance of the law they have: victims they cannot offer, since the temple has been taken away and overthrown, nor can the stoning of adulteresses and of the other crimes, granted by the law, be carried out, nor the rest of the land in the year of remission, and the other things of this kind; and she is given into the souls of those who hate her, of the Palestinian daughters, when she is handed over to the Palestinians, whom the Seventy indiscriminately call 'foreigners', by which we may understand the cities or towns of Palestine. Our Jerusalem too, if we pass over the ceremonies of God, and his hand has been stretched out over us, and, the observance of the whole law having been taken away, she will be handed over to the daughters of Palestine and not to the sons; for we are not handed over first to the heavier punishments but, according to the quality of our sins, to the lesser ones, so that, chastised in the smaller, we may escape the greater torments of tortures. 'Philistim', that is 'Palaestini' [Palestinians], with the first part of the letters changed, means 'falling by the cup — or by the making —', by which it is signified that we are to be handed over to the adverse powers, which have drunk from the cup of Babylon and have fallen, and whose works are perverse; and so great will be the chastisement and disgrace of wretched Jerusalem that even the adverse powers themselves blush at the greatness of our sins. What we have translated, following Symmachus, 'wicked', Theodotion put as the Hebrew word itself, 'zemma'. And since the ceremonies of God have been taken away from the people of the Jews, the giving of the law passes over to us together with the priesthood, as Scripture says: Appoint, O Lord, a lawgiver over them, and in another place: Set me a law, O Lord, in your way.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.16.28-29":["And you committed fornication with the sons of the Assyrians, because you had not yet been filled; and after you committed fornication, not even so were you satisfied. And you multiplied your fornication in the land of Canaan with the Chaldeans, and not even so were you satisfied. (LXX: \"And you acted impiously, and you committed fornication with the sons of Assyria, and not even so were you satisfied; and you committed fornication, and you were not filled. And you multiplied your covenants toward the land of Canaan of the Chaldeans, and not even in these were you filled.\") Above it was written that Jerusalem committed fornication with the sons of Egypt her neighbors, who are of great flesh; here the other neighborhood, that of the Assyrians, is indicated; and therefore they are handed over to both nations: because they venerated the images of both, and, entering Chaldea, which is the land of Canaan, they took on in exchange the errors of those to whose rule they were subject. According to tropology we often pass from some sins to others; and when we have not done penance for our former crimes, we add offense to offenses and are sated by no error, but as with a long rope and the strap of a heifer's yoke we drag along our sins and our covenant, or we multiply fornication in the land of 'Chanaan' [Canaan], which means 'as it were a tossing', with the Chaldeans, who themselves also sound to us 'as it were demons'; and we always have our footprints in the sand and can never say: He has set my feet upon a rock; and we are subjected to the depravities of the heretics, who have the likeness of demons, or to the demons themselves, of whom Paul writes: Our fight is not against flesh and blood, but against the rulers of these darknesses, against the spiritual things of wickedness in the heavenly places.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.16.30":["In what shall I cleanse your heart, says the Lord God, when you do all these works of a harlot and wanton woman? For you have built your brothel at the head of every way, and you have made your high place in every street; nor have you become like a harlot who increases her price by disdain. (LXX: \"What shall I do to your heart, says Adonai the Lord, when you have done all these works of a fornicating and shameless woman? ÷ and you committed fornication threefold in your daughters ⁚ when you built your brothel at the beginning of every way, and made your base in every street; and you were not like a harlot gathering wages.\") And what the Seventy said: And you committed fornication threefold in your daughters, is not found in the Hebrew, nor did any of the other translators put it. Now God says all these things to Jerusalem Ἐπαπορητικῶς [as one at a loss], because he cannot find the remedy by which he might heal her, sick and half dead, according to what Isaiah writes: What is there that I ought to have done further for my vineyard, and have not done for it?, and Hosea: What shall I do to you, Ephraim? What shall I do to you, Judah? Your mercy is like a morning cloud, and like the dew passing away early.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","And this which, as we said above, is not found in the Hebrew: You committed fornication threefold in your daughters, is said either to Jerusalem according to the letter: that she committed fornication in every way in her towns and villages, and no corner or street remained where she did not set up the signs of idolatry; or to the Church and to the believers who were deceived: who did not heed that word of the Apostle: May the God of peace himself sanctify you in all things, and may your spirit and soul and body be kept whole without complaint at the coming of our Lord Jesus Christ, but committed fornication in every way, both in body and in spirit and in soul. We read in Proverbs: But you, describe them threefold, that you may answer words of truth which are proposed to you, and we are commanded to understand the utterances of truth, that is, the holy Scriptures, threefold: first, according to the letter; second, in a middle way, through tropology; third, more loftily, so that we may know all mystical things. According to the letter is this: Neither let us commit fornication, as some of them committed fornication, and twenty-three thousand fell in one day, and: Do not murmur, as some of them murmured, and perished by the destroyer; in the middle way and according to tropology, when we withdraw from the letter and climb a little to higher things, as the Apostle says: It is written: You shall not muzzle the mouth of the ox that treads out the grain, and immediately there follows: Is it for oxen that God cares? Or did he speak surely for our sake?; but the last, that is, the third, sublime and sacred understanding, according to that word of the same Apostle: For this reason a man shall leave father and mother and shall cleave to his wife. This mystery is great; but I speak in Christ and in the Church. Now the daughters of the fornicating Church are the souls of those who first believed and afterwards were deceived into heresy, whose guilt is referred back to the mother.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.16.31":["'For your idolatry was committed not in secret but with full license, so that at all the beginnings of the ways and at the crossroads you set up altars, and made your base, or your high place, of which we spoke above, in every street; nor did you imitate the cunning harlots, who are accustomed to increase the price of lust by difficulty and thereby provoke their lovers the more to madness — according to the Seventy: You were not like a harlot gathering wages —, but you gave what you ought to have received, so that not even in this, that you committed fornication and followed demons, were you protected by their help, but, oppressed by various captivities, you felt your calamities'. Which indeed can also be referred to every Christian soul that, having deserted the worship of God, has given itself up to vices and luxury and, having followed the worldly life, has not fared happily even in it, but has both lost the riches of religion and not received the riches of the world, nor was there any difficulty in debauching her, but she thrust herself upon your lovers. There is also another, spiritual fornication, when, deserting the Church, we are joined to heretics and build our brothel at the head of every way, the ways of which the divine word commands: Stand in the ways and seek the everlasting paths of the Lord, and see which is the good way, and walk in it, namely the books of Moses, of Joshua, of Judges and of Kings, and Isaiah and Jeremiah and the other prophets. He builds his brothel at all the beginnings of the ways who, for the perversity of his doctrine, wickedly abuses the testimonies of the Scriptures and says: 'Thus says Isaiah': 'Thus said Hosea': 'Thus spoke Moses' and: 'Thus Daniel'; and fittingly he places his brothel not in the middle of the ways nor at the ends of the ways, but at the beginning: for if he had come to the knowledge and the depths of the divine books, he could not go astray. His high place too, or base, he makes in every street, giving himself up to the wantonness and vices of heathens and impious men, even among the heretics themselves, when she has been defiled by them, having no favor but lying open to contempt, because she easily lost her former chastity of faith.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.16.32-34":["Whatever we have said of Jerusalem, refer also to the Church and to the souls of believers, who give their marriage gifts — namely gold in understanding and silver in speech and the garments by which our foulness and disgrace are veiled — to their lovers, either to the contrary powers or to the teachers of perverse doctrines, when they assert that luxury does no harm, and that indiscriminate lust, according to the genitals of the body, demands intercourse by natural law, that all foods are to be used without distinction, since they were made for this, to be eaten, that prudence is necessary only in so far as it profits oneself, and that righteousness has no place, for if it prevailed one would have to beg, and other things of this kind; receiving which, wretched Jerusalem, in whom there ought to have been a vision of peace, turned the sharpness of her intellect and the charm of her eloquence to foulness; and for her lovers she went forth against her husband adorned with those ornaments with which she had been adorned by her husband, to the abuse of the good. And what follows: And you made gifts to them that they might come in to you from every side to commit fornication with you, signifies every kind of sin, so that she is not content with one offense, but endures a hunger for sinning and, against the laws of nature, defiled in all her members, is polluted even to the crown of her head.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.16.32":["But as an adulterous woman who brings in strangers over her husband. To all harlots wages are given, but you have given wages to all your lovers, and you made gifts to them that they might come in to you from every side to commit fornication with you. And it has happened in you contrary to the custom of women in your fornications, and after you there will be no fornication; for in that you gave wages and did not receive wages, the contrary has happened in you. (LXX: \"An adulterous woman ÷ like you ⁚ receiving wages from her husband. To all who committed fornication with her she gave wages, and you gave wages to all your lovers, and you loaded them that they might come to you round about in your fornication. And there happened in you a perverse thing against women in your fornication, and after you they have not committed fornication; in that you gave wages and wages were not given to you, there also happened in you a perverse thing.\") This which is written in the Seventy: Like you, is not found in the Hebrew; and it disturbs the order of the reading, and raises no small question: what woman, and like whom she is, since Jerusalem herself is said to be the adulterous woman. Jerusalem, then, took the abundance of all things with which she had been endowed by her husband's liberality, and gave it to strangers, namely to demons and idols, according to what is written in Hosea: And this I did not know, that I gave her wheat and wine and oil, and multiplied silver for her; but she made silver and golden Baalim, whence, angered, he at once adds: Therefore I will turn back, and take away my wheat in its season and my wine in its season; and I will take away my garments and my linens, so that I may no longer cover her disgrace; and now I will uncover her disgrace in the sight of her lovers, and no one shall rescue her from my hand.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.16.33":["And whereas all fornicating women are accustomed to receive wages from their lovers, Jerusalem did the contrary, giving rather than receiving; and to show the lavishness of her wages: She loaded them, he says, that they might come to her round about, lest there be any neighbor who was not mingled in her disgrace.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.16.34":["And indeed Jerusalem surpassed the other harlots in this too, that she not only gave her own things, which she had acquired by labor in the practice of foulness, but also bestowed her marriage gifts on her lovers and debauchers. And so great was the adulteress's exposure to every magnitude of foulness that she is ranked above not only present but also future harlots; hence we too translated according to Symmachus: 'And after you there will be no fornication; for in comparison with you every fornication from now on will be reckoned lighter'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.16.35-42":["Therefore, harlot, hear the word of the Lord. Thus says the Lord God: Because your bronze has been poured out, and your disgrace has been uncovered in your fornication over your lovers, and over the idols of your abominations in the blood of your sons whom you gave to them, behold, I will gather all your lovers with whom you mingled, and all whom you loved, together with all whom you hated; and I will gather them against you from every side, and I will lay bare your disgrace before them, and they will see all your foulness. And I will judge you with the judgments of adulteresses and of those who shed blood, and I will give you into the blood of fury and jealousy. And I will give you into their hands, and they will destroy your brothel, and they will demolish your place of prostitution, and they will strip you of your garments, and they will take away the vessels of your beauty; and they will leave you naked, and full of disgrace. And they will bring a multitude upon you, and they will stone you with stones, and they will cut you down with their swords. And they will burn your houses with fire, and they will execute judgments upon you in the eyes of very many women; and you will cease to commit fornication, and you will no longer give wages. And my indignation will rest in you, and my jealousy will be taken away from you; and I will be quiet, and will be angry no more. Because you did not remember the days of your youth, and provoked me in all these things, therefore I too have given your ways upon your head, says the Lord God, and I have not done according to your crimes in all your abominations. (LXX: \"Therefore, harlot, hear the word of the Lord. Thus says the Lord God: Because you poured out your bronze, and your disgrace will be uncovered in your fornication to all your lovers, and into all the thoughts of your iniquities and in the blood of your sons whom you gave to them, therefore behold, I will gather against you all your lovers with whom you mingled, and all whom you loved together with all whom you hated; and I will gather them against you round about, and I will uncover your wickednesses to them, and all will see your disgrace. And I will take vengeance on you with the vengeance of adulteresses and of those who shed blood, and I will set you in the blood of fury and jealousy. And I will deliver you into their hands, and they will dig under your brothel, and they will destroy your base, and they will strip you of your garments, and they will take away the vessels of your glorying; and they will leave you naked, and full of disgrace. And they will bring a multitude upon you, and they will stone you with stones and cut you down with their swords. And they will set fire to your houses, and they will execute vengeances upon you before very many women; and I will turn you away from fornication, and you will no longer give wages. And I will send my fury upon you, and my jealousy will be taken away from you; and I will rest, and I will no longer be anxious. Because you did not remember the day of your infancy, and you grieved me in all these things, I too, behold, will give your ways upon your head, says Adonai the Lord, and thus you have done iniquity above all your iniquities.\") Let us first lay the foundations of the history. 'Because you have done these things and those which the preceding discourse has set out, therefore hear, harlot, what you have done and what you are going to suffer: You poured out your bronze, which you had received from me, and gave to your lovers the wages which you ought to have received, and you killed your sons, so that, in offering them to idols, you have proved to be not only an adulteress but also a parricide. Therefore I will gather against you all your lovers with whom you committed fornication, both those whom you loved and those whom you hated, and I will strip you by the law of adulteresses, so that all may see your foulness and the genitals for whose sake they were formerly carried away by the madness of love'. Now all these things, through the μεταφορὰν [metaphor] of an adulterous and murderous woman who not only committed fornication against her husband but also killed her sons, are said to Jerusalem: both that all the nations are to be gathered against her, whose idols she worshiped and into whose worship she turned all the gifts of God, and that the temple of Baal is to be destroyed, and the altars of the whole city overthrown by fire, and nothing is to remain in her; and that, just as all are accustomed to throw stones at an adulteress and to slaughter a harlot, so that she is killed by the wounds of each one, in such a way that all women see the punishment of the fornicatress, so Jerusalem is to be abandoned in the sight of the other cities round about and of all the nations; and that this is done so that she may cease to commit fornication and may no longer give wages to her lovers, and so that the indignation of God may rest and he may not be angry with her whom he has ceased to love; from which we perceive that it is a great mark of displeasure to be in no way an object of God's care, but for a man to be left to his crimes and sins. My jealousy, he says, will be taken away from you; and I will be quiet, and will be angry no more, as with one estranged, who has withdrawn from me and whom I have handed over to everlasting nakedness — but if Jerusalem suffered this because she committed fornication with idols, what do we think she will suffer when she has slaughtered the Son of God? —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.16.36-43":["According to tropology, every soul has received spiritual money from God, according to that passage of the Gospel which is told in the parables of the five, the two and the one talents, and of the ten servants receiving a mina each, who, when they have acted negligently, will become debtors of fifty denarii and of five hundred; and for this reason, in the presence of the lovers, whom we understand to be the demons and the contrary powers, the disgrace of Jerusalem is uncovered, either on the day of judgment or in the time of penance, when they are rebuked; and at first, indeed, God almighty alone discerns hidden things, as the word of the Gospel says: And the Father who sees in secret, and in another place: God searching hearts and reins, and in the volume of Kings: You alone know the hearts of all men; but when there has been fulfilled: There is nothing hidden that will not be made manifest and covered that will not be revealed, and in another place: Do not judge before the time, until the Lord comes, who will both bring to light the hidden things of darkness and make manifest the counsels of hearts, and then each one will have praise from God, and when the time of rebuke has come, then that word of Hosea is fulfilled: Now their thoughts have surrounded them, and in another place: Their thoughts among themselves accusing or else defending, on the day when God judges the secrets of men, and again: Behold the man, and his works before his face. And those who formerly committed fornication with her will see all her foulness, and God will give her into the blood of fury and jealousy; for the fury of a husband against an adulterous wife is full, and can be redeemed by no price. The blood of the sons, moreover, can be understood thus, that we call the sons of Jerusalem λογισμούς καλούς, that is, 'good thoughts', which have been implanted in men by God, and which the adulteress killed when she turned aside to evil works. It also profits Jerusalem that her brothel should be dug under and every seedbed of fornication destroyed: for when this has been done, she will no longer give wages, and the indignation of God will rest, and he will in no way be jealous over her, being secure about her chastity; that is, according to those who take what is said in a good sense. Others, however, as we said above, refer it to the contrary sense, so that it belongs to God's great anger not to be angry with them, once he has despised the fornicatress and despaired of her salvation; but if the heretics who do not receive the Old Testament should, according to the edition of the Seventy, find fault in this place with what is said: And you grieved me in all these things, on the ground that God not only admits anger but is also subject to grief and sadness, let us ask them how they accept this, which is certainly a command of the good God: Do not grieve the Holy Spirit of God, in whom you were sealed in the day of redemption; for whatever they say in defense of that testimony, we shall apply in satisfaction of the present passage.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.16.43":["'And all these things', he says, 'you have done, forgetful of former benefits, and you have provoked me to anger, or grieved me, whom you ought to have provoked to joy by good works. Therefore I too have rendered your works upon your head. And although I overthrew you, O adulteress, yet I will exercise less of my anger on you than you deserved'; so that in this too the divine clemency may be shown, that the sins are greater than the punishments.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.16.44":["Behold, everyone who speaks a common proverb will take it up against you, saying: As the mother, so also her daughter. You are the daughter of your mother, who cast off her husband and her sons; and you are the sister of your sisters, who cast off their husbands and their sons. (LXX: \"These are all the things which they said against you in a parable, saying: As the mother, also the daughter. You are the daughter of your mother, who leaves her husband and her sons, and sisters of your sisters, who left their husbands and their sons.\") After the κατάλογον [catalogue] of the vices and of the chastisement of Jerusalem, by which she is drawn back to salvation, there is also applied a proverb worn by the common speech of the people, or, as the Seventy translated, a 'parable': Such as the mother, such also the daughter.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.16.45-46":["The heretics who nevertheless accept the Old Testament are accustomed to understand three natures from this passage: spiritual, animal and earthly, and they refer the spiritual to Jerusalem, the animal to Samaria, the earthly to Sodom; let us briefly ask them how three natures, spiritual, animal and earthly, which are certainly different from one another, are said to have one mother and one father — which does not agree with their fiction —, and how the animal and the earthly nature, according to this same prophet, are restored to their ancient state, that is, to the spiritual — which is itself also contrary to their reasonings —. Now that Samaria is to be understood of the heresies is proved both in the prophet Hosea and in many other places, and especially by that testimony: Woe to those who despise Zion and who trust in the mountain of Samaria; they have gathered as a vintage the chiefs of the nations; for all heretics despise 'Sion' [Zion], which means 'watchtower' and is referred to the Church, and they trust in the mountain of Samaria, namely in the pride of perverse doctrines which they think sublime, and through these frauds of preaching they gather as a vintage and lay waste the chiefs of the nations, so that, by the marvel of their doctrines, they draw all the powerful among the Gentiles into heretical error.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.16.45":["Now the mother of Jerusalem, as is written both above and in what follows, is called 'chethaea' [Hittite], and it means 'raving' or 'turning into madness', by which are shown the incitements of this world, which lead the captive soul to destruction and separate her from her husband — there is no doubt that by him the word of God and doctrine are meant —; and she is the sister of her sisters, who, as we shall read a little later, are called Sodom and Samaria, of whom the one signifies the life and luxury of the Gentiles, the other the snares of the heretics. Furthermore, what is read in the Seventy: Sisters of your sisters, who repulsed their husbands and their sons, makes no sense — for what other sisters did Sodom and Samaria have, who are the sisters of Jerusalem? And this too must be asked, what husbands Sodom and Samaria sent away and what sons they cast off —, unless perhaps we can say this: that the feet of those who go astray always waver, and theirs are not the solid footsteps that properly belong to the truth, but they run hither and thither and are carried about by every wind of doctrine, while they pass from falsehood to another falsehood and, when they have realized that they toiled in vain over the first, cross over to the second and the third.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","Your mother a Hittite, and your father an Amorite. And your greater sister is Samaria, she — and her daughters — who dwells at your left hand; but your sister lesser than you, who dwells at your right hand, is Sodom and her daughters. But you have not walked in their ways, nor done a little less according to their crimes; you have done things almost more wicked than they in all your ways. (LXX: \"Your mother a Hittite, and your father an Amorite. Your elder sister is Samaria, she — and her daughters — who dwells on your left; and your sister younger than you, who dwells on your right, is Sodom and her daughters. And not even so did you walk in their ways, nor did you act a little less according to her iniquities; and you surpassed them in all your ways.\") Above he had said: Your father an Amorite and your mother a Hittite; here, with the order and the number changed: Your mother a Hittite and your father an Amorite. For when crimes have advanced, they are split into parts, and, unity forsaken, they advance into a crowd and a multitude, which cannot climb to the mountains with Jesus.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.16.46":["Now that Samaria and Sodom are the sisters of Jerusalem, of whom one is on the left and the other on the right, you will confirm even according to the bodily understanding, if you look toward the east from the temple of Jerusalem; and Samaria is called greater because she sinned first and was led into captivity by the Assyrians, and Sodom lesser and younger, which is referred to the crowd of the Gentiles; otherwise, at that time Sodom did not exist, for before we read of Jerusalem in the Scriptures, it had been destroyed by divine fire together with Gomorrah, Admah and Zeboiim.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.16.47":["And Jerusalem, in the middle between the two sisters, which by another name is called Judah, was led by the Chaldeans into Babylon, and Jerusalem sinned far more wickedly than Samaria and Sodom, worshiping in the temple the idol of Jealousy and afterwards killing the Son of God. Furthermore, according to tropology, Samaria and Sodom, that is, the heretics and the heathens, often commit lighter things than those who are reckoned to be Jerusalem, that is, the churchmen; hence it is said to the Corinthians, who believed indeed in Christ but were weighed down by evil works: Fornication is actually heard of among you; and such fornication as is not even among the Gentiles, so that someone has his father's wife, and the rest.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.16.48":["As I live, says the Lord God, Sodom your sister, she and her daughters, has not done as you and your daughters have done. Behold, this was the iniquity of Sodom your sister: pride, fullness of bread and abundance, and the idleness of her and of her daughters, and they did not stretch out a hand to the needy and the poor; and they were lifted up and committed abominations before me, and I took them away as you saw. And Samaria did not commit half of your sins; but you have surpassed her by your crimes, and you have justified your sisters in all your abominations which you have wrought. (LXX: \"As I live, says Adonai the Lord, if Sodom your sister has done, she and her daughters, as you and your daughters have done. Nevertheless the iniquity of Sodom your sister was pride in fullness of breads and in abundance. She abounded in delights, she and her daughters ÷ this she had, she and her daughters ⁚ and she did not take hold of the hand of the poor and needy; and they gloried magnificently and committed iniquities in my sight, and I took them away as you saw. And Samaria did not commit half of your sins; and you multiplied your iniquities beyond them, and you justified your sisters in all your iniquities which you have wrought.\") What the Seventy put: This she had, she and her daughters, is not found in the Hebrew. In the Old Testament the oath of God is: As I live, says the Lord God, but in the New: Amen, amen I say to you; but if this is common with others: The God of Abraham, the God of Isaac, the God of Jacob; he is not the God of the dead, but of the living, and in another place: I will please the Lord in the land of the living, we ask by what reason God claims a common word as properly his own; but just as a good tree is spoken of, and a good man, and a good shepherd, and a good servant — yet no one is absolutely good but the one God —, so, although the angels and the other powers, and also the patriarchs and prophets and apostles, are living, in comparison with God almighty they are called dead: Who is the man who shall live and not see death?; hence the apostle Paul too says of God: Who alone, he says, has immortality, and dwells in light inaccessible, and of the fountain of the living: Our life, he says, is hidden with Christ in God.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.16.49":["He therefore who swears and says: As I live, says the Lord, in describing the crimes of Sodom and her daughters, set first pride, which is properly the devil's, and the first sin; hence the same Apostle: Lest, puffed up, he says, with pride, he fall into the judgment of the devil, on account of which he fell from heaven: For he had said: I will make strength and wisdom of understanding, I will take away the boundaries of the nations and lay waste their strength and shake the inhabited cities, and I will seize the whole world with my hand like a nest, and like broken eggs I will carry it off, and: God resists the proud, but gives grace to the humble; we read also in another place: Why is earth and ashes proud?; and the Gospel relates the pride of the Pharisee overcome by the humility of the publican; whose seedbed is fullness of bread and abundance of all things and idleness, or, as the Seventy translated, an opulence of delights and luxury; that rich man in the Gospel, clothed in purple, is related to have had no other crime except that, abounding in wealth and riches, he had broken out into such pride that he did not stretch out his hand to the needy and poor Lazarus, and so forgot his own condition that he did not give to the wretched man even what was to be thrown away; for which reason it is also written in another place: Everyone who exalts himself will be humbled, and he who humbles himself will be exalted. Pride, fullness, abundance of all things, idleness and delights: this is the sin of Sodom, and on account of it there follows forgetfulness of God, which thinks present goods everlasting and that it will never lack necessities; for which reason it is also commanded in the law: Take heed lest, eating and drinking and being filled, having built very fine houses, and having sheep and oxen, silver and gold, you forget the Lord your God, and in another place it is written of Israel: He ate and drank, and was filled and grew fat, and the beloved kicked; knowing which, Solomon too, the wisest of all, prays in Proverbs: Grant me what is necessary and what suffices, lest, being filled, I become a liar and say: Who will see me? or, being made poor, I steal and forswear the name of God.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","Furthermore, as to the fact that for 'delights' the Hebrew contains 'idleness', it should be known that it belongs to this sense: every soul of the idle man is in desires; that is, something must always be being done, lest the field of our breast, when the hand ceases, be overrun by the brambles of evil thoughts.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.16.51":["And what follows: And you have justified your sisters in all your abominations which you have wrought, does not declare Sodom and Samaria simply righteous, but by comparison with those who are worse, just as the publican too, of whom we spoke above, is called righteous not absolutely but by comparison with one worse. And yet, though Sodom and Samaria are such, they have not sinned even half, in comparison with Jerusalem: The servant, for, who knows the will of his master and does not do it will be beaten with many stripes, and: The mighty will mightily suffer torments.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.16.52":["Therefore you too, bear your shame, you who have surpassed your sisters by your sins, acting more wickedly than they; for they have been justified by you. (LXX: \"And you, endure your torment, in that you corrupted your sisters in your sins, by which you acted iniquitously beyond them, and you justified them beyond yourself.\") The second plank after shipwreck is, when you have sinned, to blush, and not to fall under that reproach which is spoken against Jerusalem: The face of a harlot has become yours; you do not know how to blush. Now he bears his torment who is tortured by his own conscience, and in this world endures torture of his own will, so that he may not endure eternal torments; and we corrupt our brothers or sisters by our sins when through our sins they are provoked to greater crimes — let what I say be made clearer thus: imagine someone set on the summit of the priesthood not living well and disfiguring his dignity by his works: is not the lay brother corrupted by the imitation of his vices? —; for he too who has scandalized one of the least is cast headlong into the deep with a millstone tied to his neck. The sisters of Jerusalem too, Sodom and Samaria, are justified, not because they are righteous in themselves, but, as we said, in comparison with one who is worse.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","Therefore you too, be ashamed and bear your disgrace, you who have justified your sisters. (LXX: \"And you, be ashamed and take up your disgrace, because you have justified your sisters.\") Shame is followed by disgrace, disgrace by chastisement, chastisement by consolation, consolation by salvation, according to that word of the Apostle: Tribulation works patience, patience proof, proof hope. And hope does not put to shame — no doubt with respect to the future, since in the present it has blotted out its sins through shame —; something of the same kind is sounded by that word of the Gospel too: There is a shame that leads to death, and there is a shame that leads to life; the Holy Spirit too exhorts sinners in the Psalms: Let all my enemies be put to shame and be afraid; let them be turned back, and be put to shame very swiftly; it is written also in another place: Tell your sins first, that you may be justified, and again: The righteous man is his own accuser at the beginning of his speech. It is no wonder, then, if Jerusalem is called to shame and disgrace, she who sinned to such a degree that she justified her sisters, and to whom it is said thereafter: Both you and your daughters, return to your ancient state.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.16.53-54":["And I will turn them, restoring them, with the turning of Sodom with her daughters, and with the turning of Samaria and of her daughters; and I will turn your return in the midst of them, that you may bear your disgrace, and you will be ashamed in all that you have done, consoling them. (LXX: \"And I will turn their turnings with the turning of Sodom and of her daughters, and I will turn the turning of Samaria and of her daughters; and I will turn your turning in the midst of them, that you may bear your torment, and have disgrace from all that you have done to provoke me to anger.\") Great is the progress of Jerusalem, that, after the shame and disgrace brought upon her by the judgment of God, which she willingly accepted and bore, saying: I will bear the wrath of the Lord, because I have sinned against him, restoration to her ancient state should be promised her. But yet, since by comparison with her crimes Sodom and Samaria were justified, of whom one is on the right and the other on the left, first the 'turning', or 'captivity', of Sodom is restored, as Aquila translated, second the 'captivity' of Samaria, as the same Aquila and Symmachus translated, and last of all Jerusalem is restored, which had been weighed down by greater iniquity and had shown her sinful sisters to be righteous in comparison with herself. For who doubts that among three sinners, nay rather impious men — the Gentile, the heretic, the churchman — he is worthy of much greater punishments who has been of greater dignity? : For the mighty, as we said, will mightily suffer torments; but he who has been least is worthy of mercy, and: The servant who knows the will of his master and does not do it will be beaten with many stripes. Hence Peter too: It is time, he says, for judgment to begin from the house of the Lord; and in this same prophet it is commanded to those who hold axes: Begin from my holy ones, so that Jerusalem last of all, after her sisters have first been turned back and restored to their former state, may bear her disgrace and be ashamed and blush over the things in which she had sinned and in all things console her sisters, while she endures heavier things; or certainly she is ashamed and blushes for this reason, that she provoked God to anger. From which it is plain that anger is not natural to God, but that God, most clement and most gentle, is provoked to anger by our vices, as the Apostle says: Or do you despise the riches of his goodness and forbearance and patience, not knowing that the goodness of God calls you to penance? But according to your hardness and impenitent heart you treasure up wrath for yourself, which the Lord does not have by nature; whence it is also written in another place: You sent forth your wrath, and it devoured them like stubble — for what is joined and united in one body cannot be sent, but only what is outside the body, for example a lance, a javelin, an arrow, a sword —; we read in the Gospel that it will be more tolerable for the land of Sodom on the day of judgment than for the land that has not received the apostles.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.16.55":["And your sister Sodom and her daughters will return to their antiquity, and Samaria and her daughters will return to their antiquity, and you and your daughters, return to your antiquity. (LXX: \"And your sister Sodom and her daughters will be restored as they had been from the beginning, and Samaria and her daughters will be restored as they were from the beginning, and you and your daughters will be restored as you were from the start.\") The Jews, among the other fables and endless genealogies and ravings which they invent, dream this too: that at the coming of their Christ, whom we know to be Antichrist, and in the kingdom of a thousand years, Sodom is to be restored to its ancient state, so that it will be like the paradise of God and like the land of Egypt, and that Samaria will receive its former happiness, so that they will return from the Assyrians to the land of Judea — for we read that the ten tribes were taken captive by Pul and Shalmaneser and Tiglath-pileser, kings of the Assyrians, and are captives there to this day —; that Jerusalem too is then to be built, and all her daughters, namely the cities and villages and fortresses that are to be under her power, will flourish as they flourished before, and Jerusalem herself is to be built of gold and silver and precious stones, of which Isaiah too is said to prophesy: I will establish your judges as before and your counselors as from the beginning, and after this you shall be called the city of righteousness, the faithful mother of cities, Zion, and our Revelation to speak, and David to sing: Deal kindly, O Lord, in your good will with Zion, and let the walls of Jerusalem be built, and in another place: The cities of Judea shall be built, and they shall dwell and abide there, and their seed shall be directed forever. But we, leaving the perfect knowledge of these things to the judgment of God, nay rather clearly confessing that after the second coming of the Lord Savior there will be nothing lowly, nothing earthly, but the heavenly kingdoms which are first promised in the Gospel, say this: that in the state of the Church all things have been fulfilled and are fulfilled daily. Sodom returns to its ancient state when the soul, formerly Gentile and impious, restored to its own nature, understands the Creator. Samaria receives her ancient blessedness, the error of the heretics despised, and joined to Christian doctrine and faith. And when they have returned, Jerusalem too, 'vision of peace', which is interpreted as the Church, will return to her former state — of which it is written: His place has been made in peace, and: Jerusalem, which is built as a city whose sharing thereof is in the selfsame, and in the Apostle: But the Jerusalem which is above is free, which is our mother, and in the same: You have come to Mount Zion and the city of the living God, the heavenly Jerusalem, and thousands of angels in throngs —, and she will return with her daughters, who are scattered over the whole world; of which we have treated more fully in the explanations of Isaiah.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.16.56-58":["But Sodom your sister was not heard in your mouth in the day of your pride, before your wickedness was uncovered, as at this time, to the reproach of the daughters of Syria, and of all the Palestinian daughters round about you, who surround you on every side. Your crime and your disgrace you have borne, says the Lord God. (LXX: \"If Sodom your sister had not been for hearing in your mouth in the days of your pride, before your wickednesses were uncovered, as now is the case with the daughters of Syria, and if this had not been, what would have been done to you, and to all round about you, the daughters of the foreigners who surround you on every side? Your impieties and your iniquities, you, bear them.\") Symmachus translated this passage thus: Because Sodom your sister was not in hearing through your mouth in the day of your pride, before your disgrace was uncovered, as in the time of the reproach of the daughters of Syria, and of all the Palestinian daughters who are round about you, who surround you on every side. Your crime and your wickedness you will bear; Theodotion in this way: And Sodom your sister was not in the hearing of your mouth in the day of your pride, before your wickedness was uncovered, as the time of the reproach of the daughters of Syria, and of all the daughters of the foreigners round about her, who abhor you on every side. Your fornication and your abominations you have borne, says Adonai the Lord; our translation agrees with the edition of Aquila. All these I have set down so that from the comparison of them all we may be able to find a trace of some sense; and meanwhile, without prejudice to others, the order of the reading seems to us to be rendered thus: 'In the days of your pride, when you were sinning, you did not remember the overthrow of Sodom your sister, before your wickedness lay open, which at this time has been uncovered to such a degree that you became a reproach to all the cities of Syria and Palestine which are round about you; since, therefore, you surpassed your sister in crime, and were not frightened by her example so as not to suffer like things and to hold back your foot, but now your disgrace has been uncovered, hear the sentence of God: either 'bear' — according to the Seventy —, or 'you have borne' — according to Aquila and Theodotion, or 'you are going to bear' — according to Symmachus — your crime and disgrace, so that, after you have endured the punishment for sacrilege, you may obtain pardon, not from your covenant but from my clemency'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.16.57":["'Syria' in the Hebrew tongue is called 'aram', which means 'loftiness'; and according to the explanation of that passage of the prophet Isaiah in which Aram and Ephraim conspire against Judah and Jerusalem, the subtle wisdom of the heathens, which promises itself the loftiness of knowledge, in all the wickedness of the heretics, attacks Judah, in which is the true confession, and cannot prevail; here too the same arrogance of Syria, that is, of the philosophers, together with the daughters of the foreigners, in whom is shown the manifold error of the diverse doctrines among the nations, mocks Jerusalem and holds in reproach her by whose vices it has been surpassed.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.16.59-63":["For thus says the Lord God: And I will do to you as you despised the oath, so as to make void the covenant. And I will remember my covenant with you in the days of your youth, and I will raise up for you an everlasting covenant. And you will remember your ways, and you will be ashamed when you receive your sisters with you, the greater with your lesser ones; and I will give them to you as daughters, but not by your covenant. And I will raise up for you my covenant with you, and you will know that I am the Lord, that you may remember and be ashamed, and that it may no longer be yours to open your mouth because of your shame, when I have been appeased toward you in all that you have done, says the Lord God. (LXX: \"Thus says Adonai the Lord: And I will do in you as you have done, as you despised these things, so as to transgress my testament. And I will remember my testament which I made with you in the days of your youth, and I will raise up for you an everlasting testament. And you will remember your way, and you will be despised when you receive your sisters greater than you together with your younger ones; and I will give them to you for proving, and not by your testament. And I will raise up my testament with you, and you will know that I am the Lord, and you may remember and be ashamed, and it may no longer be yours to open your mouth before the face of your disgrace, when I have been propitiated toward you according to all that you have done, says the Lord God.\") 'For this reason I said to you: Your crime and your disgrace, you, bear them (or you have borne or you are going to bear), : that you may receive what you deserve, because you despised my oath and made void my covenant. But when there has been fulfilled: I will kill and I will make alive, I will strike and I will heal, then I will remember my covenant which I once had with you. And I will raise up for you a covenant, by no means that of the law, which has passed away, but the everlasting covenant of the Gospel, so that, when you have remembered your ways and received your sisters, greater or lesser, Samaria and Sodom and their companions, I may grant them to you as daughters — or for proving: For there must also be heresies, so that those who are approved may be made manifest to each —, not from your merit, but from my mercy; and then you may know that I am the Lord, and remember my benefits and be ashamed, and say with the Apostle: I who am not worthy to be called an apostle, because I persecuted the Church of God'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.16.63":["And that it may no longer be yours to open your mouth because of your shame: for it belongs to the saints to open the mouth — according to the Apostle, who says: My mouth is open to you, O Corinthians —, and to the Lord Savior — who, opening his mouth, taught them, and who says in the psalm too: I will open my mouth in parables —; but to the sinner it is said: You have sinned, be still, and: Why do you take up my testament through your mouth?, and: Praise is not seemly in the mouth of a sinner; and the holy man deserves to hear: Open your mouth and I will fill it. From this we understand that even when, through the mercy of the Lord, we have received back our former glory, nay rather have received the everlasting covenant of the Gospel, after the Lord has been appeased toward us in all that we have done, we keep the memory of past sin and always close our mouth, because we have been saved not by our works but by the grace of God.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.17.2":["And the word of the Lord came to me, saying: Son of man, propound a riddle and tell a parable to the house of Israel, and you shall say: Thus says the Lord God: A great eagle with great wings, long in the extent of its limbs, full of feathers and of variety, came to Lebanon and took the marrow of the cedar; it plucked off the top of its foliage and carried it over into the land of Canaan; in a city of merchants it set it. And it took of the seed of the land and set it in the ground for seed, that it might make firm its root upon many waters; on the surface it set it. And when it had sprouted, it grew into a broader vine of low stature, its branches looking toward it, and its roots were under it; so it became a vine, and bore fruit in vine-shoots and sent out offshoots. (LXX: \"And the word of the Lord came to me, saying: Son of man, narrate a narration and speak a parable to the house of Israel, and you shall say: Thus says Adonai the Lord: A great eagle with great wings, of long extent, full of talons, which has the leading to enter into Lebanon, and it took the choice parts of the cedar; it plucked off the tops of the tenderness and brought them into the land of Canaan; in a walled city it set it. And it took of the seed of the land and gave it in a plain as a planting upon many waters, that it might be looked upon, and it set it, and it sprang up and became a weak vine and small in size, so that only its branches appeared under it, and its roots were beneath it; and it became a vine, and made offshoots and stretched out its branches.\") When it is said to the prophet: Propound a riddle, tell a parable (or, as the Seventy translated: Narrate a narration and speak a parable), it is shown that what is said is obscure; for no one doubts that both a riddle and a parable put forward one thing in the words and hold another in the sense: for the Savior too spoke to the people in parables, which he explained privately to the apostles; therefore we must understand the riddle and the parable in just this way, as a riddle and a parable.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.17.3-19":["According to anagogy — although the interpretation may seem forced — this must be known: that, just as the other living creatures are customarily referred both to a good and to a bad sense, so also is the eagle. Of the lion it is said in a good sense: A lion's whelp is Judah; in the contrary sense: Our adversary the devil goes about like a roaring lion, and that in the psalm: He lies in wait in secret, like a lion in his den. He lies in wait to seize the poor. In a good sense it is said of the eagle that the righteous man, having become rich, may make himself wings like an eagle, that he may be able to return to the house of his superior, and in Isaiah it is written that the righteous may put forth feathers like eagles, may run and not grow weary, may walk and not hunger; but rightly he who had said: I will do strength and wisdom of understanding, I will remove the boundaries of the nations and plunder their strength, and I will shake the cities that are inhabited, and I will grasp the whole world in my hand like a nest and like broken eggs, is now described in the person of an eagle: in that he by no means wishes to strip from Lebanon — which means 'whiteness' and is referred to incense — the small and low shrubs, but the tops of the cedars, and of the princes and the royal stock, according to that saying of Habakkuk: His food is choice. And he makes a covenant with those whom he has captured, that they may bow their necks to the Babylonian king and fulfill what the Apostle says: Whom I have delivered to Satan, that they may learn not to blaspheme, and in another place: Whom I have delivered, he says, to Satan for the destruction of the flesh, that the spirit may be saved. We ought not, then, if perhaps on account of some sin we are cast out of the congregation of the brethren and out of the house of God, to struggle against it, but to bear with an even mind the sentence brought against us and to say with the prophet: I will bear the wrath of the Lord, because I have sinned against him, until he justifies my cause, and so on. And it often happens that, being subject to one, we go over to another who promises us his help and does not make us abide in the former sentence; which must be avoided by every means, lest our vine-shoots be dried up and our sprouts wither, and we be believed to act against the compact of God.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.17.3-4":["Now in the present prophecy of Ezekiel two eagles are set forth; of the first we must speak now, of the other we shall treat in what follows. And meanwhile let us gather the simple history. A great eagle with great wings, long in the extent of its limbs, full of feathers and of variety (or, as the Seventy rendered, full of talons) is Nebuchadnezzar, king of the Chaldeans, of whom Hosea also speaks: As an eagle over the house of the Lord. He, ruling over many nations and hedged about by a countless army, came upon the house of God — no doubt it means the temple — or, as Scripture now says, to Lebanon, of which Zechariah speaks: Open your gates, O Lebanon; and let fire devour your cedars. Howl, fir tree, for the cedar has fallen, since the magnificent have been laid waste; and often the temple, which was renowned and lofty, is called Lebanon in the holy Scriptures. And it took the marrow of the cedar, and plucked off the top of its foliage and carried it over into the land of Canaan; in a city of merchants it set it (for 'merchants', or 'commerce', the Seventy translated 'walled'); and it indicates Jeconiah king of Judah, whom Nebuchadnezzar captured together with his mother and the princes of the people and all the wealth of Jerusalem and the vessels of the temple, and carried over to Babylon, which is in the land of Canaan, and there he grew old; of whose line afterward, as the writing of Matthew the evangelist testifies, our Lord and Savior was born, through Shealtiel and Zerubbabel.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.17.5-6":["In his place he took of the seed of the same land, that is, of the royal stock, Mattaniah, the paternal uncle of Jeconiah, whose name he changed, and he called him Zedekiah and set him as king in Jerusalem, and put him over many peoples. And yet he set him on the surface, and did not make his power firm with a deep root of rule, but set him so that he might be looked upon and be under Babylonian power, or: Of low stature, its branches looking toward it, so that he would indeed rule over the peoples of the Jews, but would look to the authority of the Babylonian who gave the orders: for this is what Scripture says: Of low stature, its branches looking toward it — understand: toward the eagle — which the Seventy rendered more plainly: And it sprang up — what had been planted — and became a weak and small vine, so that only its branches appeared, and he would seem indeed to have a kingdom, but his kingdom itself, low and weak, would be ruled by the will of the Babylonian prince. So much for the present passage for now; let us join the rest to it as well.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.17.7":["And there was another great eagle with great wings and many feathers; and behold, this vine, as if sending its roots to it, stretched out its vine-shoots to it, that it might water it from the beds of its growth. In good land upon many waters he planted it, that it may make foliage and bear fruit, and become a great vine. Say: Thus says the Lord God: Shall it then prosper? Will he not pull up its roots and strip off its fruit, and dry up all the vine-shoots of its growth, and it will wither? And not with a great arm nor with many peoples, so as to pull it up by the roots. Behold, it is planted; shall it then prosper? Will it not, when the burning wind touches it, be dried up, and wither in the beds of its growth? (LXX: \"And there was another great eagle with great wings, with very many talons; and behold, this vine was entwined toward it, and its roots toward it, and it stretched out its branches toward it, that it might water it with the clod of its planting. In a good plain upon much water it was fattened, that it might make sprouts and bring forth fruit, and become a great vine. Therefore thus says Adonai the Lord: Shall it be guided aright? Will not the roots of its tenderness and its fruit rot, and all that has sprung from it wither? And not with a great arm nor with a numerous people, so as to pull it up from its roots. And behold, it is fattened; shall it be guided aright? Will it not, as soon as the burning wind touches it, wither, and be dried up with dryness? With the clod of its growth it will wither.\") The second eagle, that is, the other one, itself also great, full of feathers and of many talons, because of its rapacity and its devastation of very many nations, is Pharaoh king of Egypt. And behold, this vine — understand: King Zedekiah, who had been set up in Jerusalem by Nebuchadnezzar — began, it says, to send its offshoots to it, that is, to direct envoys to the king of the Egyptians and to ask him for help against the king to whom he was subject — for this is what Scripture now says: That it might water it from the beds of its growth —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.17.8-10":["It had been planted in good land by Nebuchadnezzar, that it might make foliage and bring forth fruit and grow into a broader vine; therefore the prophet is commanded to speak to the vine which had been planted by Nebuchadnezzar and had sent its branches to the Egyptian eagle: 'Shall it prosper because it has attempted to do this, and will not all its offshoots and vine-shoots at once be dried up? And not,' it says, 'with a great arm nor with a numerous people'; for Zedekiah, fleeing and left destitute of the help of the Egyptian king, was caught by the leaders of Nebuchadnezzar in the deserts of Jericho, and all his companions were scattered here and there, as is written in the volume of Kings and in Παραλιπομήνῳ [Chronicles] and in Jeremiah.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.17.12-15":["And the word of the Lord came to me, saying: Say to the provoking house: Do you not know what these things signify? Say: Behold, the king of Babylon comes to Jerusalem, and he will take its king and its princes, and lead them to himself into Babylon; and he will take of the seed of the kingdom, and strike a covenant with him, and receive an oath from him; but he will also take away the mighty of the land, that the kingdom may be lowly and not lift itself up, but keep his compact and observe it. But he, withdrawing from him, sent messengers to Egypt, that it might give him horses and a numerous people; shall he prosper or obtain safety who has done these things? And shall he who has broken the compact escape? As I live, says the Lord God, surely in the place of the king who made him king, whose oath he made void and whose compact, which he had with him, he broke, in the midst of Babylon he shall die. And not with a great army nor with a numerous people will Pharaoh make war against him, in the casting up of a mound and the building of ramparts, to kill many souls. For he had despised the oath so as to break the covenant, and behold, he gave his hand; and when he has done all these things, he shall not escape. This is the riddle and this is the parable: that under two eagles and a vine Scripture was showing two kings, of Babylon and of Egypt, and King Zedekiah, who, against his oath, abandoning his friendship with Nebuchadnezzar, transferred himself to the Egyptian king: And he will strike, it says, a covenant with him, and receive an oath from him, that he may keep his compact and observe it. He who has dissolved, it says, the compact, shall he escape? From this we learn that faith must be kept even among enemies, and that one must consider not to whom but by whom you have sworn; for far more faithful is he found who, on account of the name of God, trusted you and was deceived, than you who, under pretext of the divine majesty, plotted snares against your enemy, nay rather, by now your friend.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.17.16":["Surely, it says, in the place of the king who made him king, whose oath he made void and whose compact, which he had with him, he broke, in the midst of Babylon he shall die; for we read that Zedekiah was captured, led to Riblah, and there, after his sons had been killed, blinded, and, shut in a cage like a wild beast, carried over to Babylon — whence also Scripture, though it seems to be contrary to itself, is nevertheless most true in both: it had been said to Zedekiah: And you shall enter Babylon and: You shall not see it: for he entered, because he was led to Babylon, and he did not see it, because he had been blinded —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.17.17":["But what follows: And not with a great army nor with a numerous people will Pharaoh make war against him, in the casting up of a mound and the building of ramparts, to kill many souls, is understood in two ways: either the Egyptian king, coming against Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon, will not be able to give battle nor with a small army resist so great a multitude; or Zedekiah himself will be conquered by Pharaoh, from whom he hoped for help, not that Pharaoh conquered him — or that Scripture anywhere attests this —, but that Nebuchadnezzar's conquest was brought about on the occasion given by the king of Egypt; and that not he who was besieging seemed to conquer Jerusalem, but he in whom Zedekiah had hoped in vain.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.17.18":["Behold, it says, he gave his hand to the king of Egypt, and handed himself over, and committed the sacrilege of perjury against God: 'Will it,' it says, 'profit him?'. And when he has done all these things, he shall not escape.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.17.19-20":["Therefore thus says the Lord God: As I live, surely the oath that he despised and the covenant that he transgressed I will lay upon his head, and I will spread my net over him and he shall be caught in my dragnet, and I will bring him into Babylon, and I will judge him there in the transgression by which he despised me. And all his fugitives with the whole column shall fall by the sword; and the rest shall be scattered to every wind, and you shall know that I the Lord have spoken. It is a worldly maxim: Deceit or valor, who would ask, in an enemy?, which those who say that enemies are to be deceived by fraud are wont to set against us; yet even if we were to assent to it, Zedekiah did much worse: for he did not deceive an enemy, but a friend to whom he had been bound by a covenant of the Lord. Therefore, so long as you do not swear and do not enter into a compact under the name of God, it belongs to prudence and courage either to deceive or to overcome the adversary in whatever way you can; but when you have bound yourself by an oath, he who trusted you is by no means an adversary but a friend, and he was deceived under pretext of an oath, that is, of the invocation of God. That is why Scripture now says: The oath that he despised and the covenant that he transgressed I will lay upon his head; and lest we should think that the oath and the covenant and the compact belonged to the king of Babylon or to Zedekiah who had made it, there follows: In the transgression by which he despised me. So he who scorns an oath despises him by whom he swore, and does wrong to him in whose name the adversary trusted; for which reason: I will spread, it says, my net over him and he shall be caught in my dragnet, and I will bring him into Babylon, and I will judge him there. Whatever, then, Nebuchadnezzar did against Zedekiah, he did not do by his own strength, but by the wrath of God, in whose name perjury had been committed.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.17.22":["Thus says the Lord: And I myself will take of the marrow of the lofty cedar, and will set it; from the top of its branches I will strip off a tender shoot, and I will plant it upon a high and eminent mountain. On the lofty mountain of Israel I will plant it, and it will break forth into a sprout and bear fruit, and it will become a great cedar, and all birds will dwell under it, every winged creature nesting under the shade of its foliage. And all the trees of the region shall know that I the Lord have brought low the lofty tree and raised up the low tree, and dried up the green tree and made the dry tree flourish: I the Lord have spoken and have done it. (LXX: \"For thus says Adonai the Lord: And I will take away of the choice parts of the cedar from the top ※ and I will give from the head of its branches ⁚, from their heart I will take away, and I will plant upon a high mountain; and I will hang it on the lofty mountain of Israel, and I will plant, and it will bring forth a sprout and bear fruit and will become a great cedar, and every bird will rest under it, and every winged creature will rest under its shade ÷ and its branches will be restored ⁚. And all the trees of the field shall know that I am the Lord who bring low the lofty tree and exalt the low tree, and make the green tree dry and make the dry tree sprout: I the Lord have spoken and I do it.\") What is read in the Seventy: And I will give from the head of its branches, has been added from the edition of Theodotion; and again what follows: And its branches will be restored, must be marked with an obelus, because it is not found in the Hebrew. The real Nebuchadnezzar takes of the marrow of the lofty cedar, from the top of its branches, for this purpose: That the kingdom may be lowly and not lift itself up; but the Lord God, the Father almighty, who spoke to Ezekiel, takes from the royal stock and from the line of David: And plants it upon a high and eminent mountain; he it is who speaks in the psalm: But I have been set up as king by him upon Zion, his holy mountain. For the prince had failed from Judah and the leader from Israel, until he should come for whom it is reserved: And he shall be the expectation of the nations.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.17.23-24":["He broke forth into a sprout and bore fruit, and surpassed all the cedars by his loftiness, so that all the birds of heaven dwell under him, and all winged creatures are protected by his shade — he of whom Habakkuk says: Horns in his hands: there his strength is hidden, and who desires to gather, like a hen, the chicks under his wings —, so that all the trees of the region may know that he himself is the Lord; τροπικῶς [figuratively] speaking of believers: he who brought low Israel, once a lofty tree, and exalted the lowly people of the Gentiles; who dried up the green tree of the Jews, flowering and sprouting in the Law and the Prophets, and made the dry tree of the nations flourish, so that what he had always spoken he might fulfill in deed; the same thing is signified also by the words of Simeon in the Gospel, saying: Behold, this one is set for the ruin and for the resurrection of many, and by the grain of mustard seed which, though it is the smallest of all seeds, once it has grown is a dwelling place for birds.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.17.24":["Some interpret it otherwise, referring the lofty tree brought low and the low tree exalted to the passion of the Lord Savior: Who, though he was in the form of God, did not count it robbery to be equal with God, but emptied himself, taking the form of a servant; and after the resurrection the very tree was afterward raised on high which, at first green, was dried up in death and afterward, reviving, recovered its former greenness. Others, in Jewish fashion, refer both to Israel: that at the first coming it was brought low and dried up, and at the second it will be restored to its former state, when that is fulfilled which the apostle Paul says: When the fullness of the Gentiles has come in, then all Israel will be saved.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.18.2":["And the word of the Lord came to me, saying: What is it that you turn a parable into this proverb among you in the land of Israel, saying: The fathers have eaten a sour grape, and the teeth of the sons have been set on edge? (LXX: \"And the word of the Lord came to me, saying: ÷ Son of man, ⁚ what is the parable among the sons of Israel, of those who say: The fathers have eaten a sour grape, and the teeth of the sons have been set on edge?\") What the Seventy said, Son of man, is not found in the Hebrew. Now divine Scripture warns that what was said in Exodus, I am the Lord your God; a jealous God, who render the sins of the fathers upon the sons to the third and fourth generation for those who hate me, and show mercy to thousands for those who love me and keep my precepts, and again, The Lord descended in a cloud and stood beside Moses, and Moses called on the name of the Lord, and the Lord passed before his face, and he called on him, saying: Lord God, compassionate and merciful, patient and of much mercy and true, and keeping righteousness and mercy to thousands, taking away iniquities and injustices and sins, and he will not cleanse the guilty, rendering the iniquities of the fathers upon the sons and upon the sons of the sons to the third and fourth generation, ought to be taken thus, as a parable and a proverb, so that it sounds one thing in the words and holds another in the sense; which we also said above on the parable of the two eagles. Hence the Lord in Psalm 77 (78): I will open, he says, my mouth in parables, I will utter propositions from the beginning; and in the Gospel he sets forth the parable of the sowing, and of the darnel, and of the mustard seed, which, though it is the least of all seeds, rises up into a great tree, in such a way that he presents one thing in the words and holds another in the sense; and we, up to the present day, think that the two testimonies of Exodus which we set down above are not a parable, but set out a simple statement. And although we would not dare to say anything, nor would the earthen vessel speak against the potter, 'why have you made me thus or thus', yet we suffered a hidden stumbling block, because it seemed an injustice on God's part that one should sin and another pay for the sins. For if he renders the sins of the fathers upon the sons to the third and fourth generation, it seems unjust that one should sin and another be punished; but from what follows, for those who hate me, the stumbling block of the threat, or of the precept, is resolved: for they are not punished to the third and fourth generation because their fathers transgressed, since it is rather the fathers, who were the sinners, who ought to have been punished, but because they proved to be imitators of their fathers and hated God, by an inherited evil, the impiety growing from the root into the branches as well. At this place the heretics who do not receive the Old Testament are accustomed to say against the Creator: How good and righteous is the God of the Law and the Prophets, who, resting and silent at the sins of the fathers, repays those who have not sinned; nay rather, how great is the cruelty in him, that he should extend his wrath even to the third and fourth generation! To them we shall reply that even in this the clemency of God the Creator is shown: for it is not a mark of ferocity and severity to hold back wrath until the third and fourth generation, but a sign of mercy to defer the punishment of sin; for when he says, The Lord God, compassionate and merciful, patient and of much compassion, and adds, rendering the iniquities of the fathers upon the sons and the sons of the sons, he indicates this: that he is of such great mercy that he does not punish at once but defers the sentence of punishment. But if the vengeance on sinners is deferred to the third and fourth generation, what more will he do with the righteous and the holy? There follows: And keeping righteousness and mercy to many thousands for those who keep his commandments and do his precepts. It is written in Proverbs: As a sour grape is harmful to the teeth and smoke to the eyes, so is iniquity to those who practice it; from which it is clear that it is not the teeth of others that ache and are set on edge, but the teeth of those who have eaten the sour grape. And this is the sense of this passage: just as, if anyone should wish to say, The fathers have eaten a sour grape, and the teeth of the sons have been set on edge, it is ridiculous and has no logical consequence, so it is unjust and perverse that the fathers should sin and the sons and grandsons be tormented. There are those who expound what is written in Exodus, rendering the iniquities of the fathers upon the sons to the third and fourth generation, in such a way as to refer the statement to the human soul, saying that the father in us is the light prick of the senses and the incitements of the vices; the son, if the thought has conceived the sin; the grandson, if you carry out in deed what you have thought and conceived; and the great-grandson, that is, the fourth generation, if you not only do what is evil and wicked but glory in your crimes, according to what is written: The impious man, when he has come into the depth of evils, despises. God therefore by no means punishes the first and second goads of thoughts, which the Greeks call προπαθείας [pre-passions, the first movements of the mind], without which no human being can exist, but only if someone has resolved to do what he has thought, or has been unwilling to correct by repentance the very things he has done; whence it is written: No human being is without sin, not even if his life be of a single day. But the years of his life are numbered; and in another place: Who will boast that he has a chaste heart?; and again: The stars too are not clean in his sight, and against his angels he has devised something perverse. But if that sublime nature is not free of sin, what is to be said of human beings who, surrounded by fragile flesh, must say with the Apostle: Wretched man that I am! Who will deliver me from the body of this death?, and that word, that when we have done all things we must say, We are unprofitable servants; we have done what we ought to have done, and: Unless the Lord builds the house, those who build it labor in vain. Unless the Lord guards the city, he who guards it has kept watch in vain? Now in proof of this point, that the first impulse of thoughts, or rather the slight prompting of the mind, is by no means punished by God, but only if you complete in deed what you have conceived in the mind, that passage of Genesis must be brought forward: Ham sinned in mocking his father's nakedness, and the sentence was received not by him who laughed but by his son Canaan: Cursed, he says, be Canaan; a servant he shall be to his brothers. For what righteousness is there in this, that the father should have sinned and the sentence be pronounced against the son? Likewise what the Apostle sets down in the opposite direction, that the woman is saved if her son remains in faith and holiness and chastity, would seem not to have the righteousness of a sentence, namely that if the sons and grandsons are good, the parents are saved; for how many parents are holy and have wicked sons, and, on the contrary, how many are sinners and beget holy sons? Therefore all that we said above is to be taken according to this sense: that the sins of parents and begetters are punished in the branches, not in the root. Let it suffice for the present to have said this about the proverb, or the parable: that the Law and the Prophets, that is, Exodus and Ezekiel, or rather God himself, who spoke both here and there, should by no means seem to disagree in their statements, or to correct here what he had said badly there. But if anyone can find either a better sense or another one, which removes the stumbling block of testimonies contrary to one another, it is rather his opinion that is to be accepted.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.18.3-4":["But if they wish to say that after the return from Babylon these nations were subjected to the people of Israel, first we shall demand the authority of the Scriptures by which they may prove that this happened; then, when they are quite unable to demonstrate it, we shall grant it to them out of superfluity and say: Suppose that what you say happened; what justice of God would it be that the grandfathers blasphemed and the forefathers reproached, and afterwards it was repaid to the grandsons? Especially since that sentence by which it was formerly said in the law that the sins of the fathers are repaid upon the sons in the third and the fourth generation has been loosed through Ezekiel: As I live, says the Lord; this parable shall no longer be spoken, but the soul that sins, it shall die. And at the same time notice that what was said is a parable, and does not mean what they bring forward on the surface of the letter.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"EZK.18.3":["As I live, says the Lord, if this parable shall be any longer to you as a proverb in Israel. Behold, all souls are mine; as the soul of the father, so also the soul of the son is mine: the soul that sins, it shall die. (LXX: \"As I live, says the Lord Adonai, if it shall be any longer that this parable is spoken in Israel; for all souls are mine; as the soul of the father, so the soul of the son is mine: the soul that sins, it shall die.\") What As I live, says the Lord means, and parable or proverb, we have said more fully above; it will by no means be spoken in Israel, but among those who do not have the knowledge of God and cannot discern the truth.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.18.4":["All, he says, souls are mine, according to creation, not according to merit, as Moses was called a man of God, of whom it is written, A prayer of Moses, the man of God, and Elijah, who said to the captain of fifty soldiers, And if I am a man of God, let fire come down upon you and upon the fifty men; but the man of sin and son of iniquity is not called a man of God, just as those are called servants and attendants of God of whom it cannot be said, Everyone who commits sin is the servant of sin, and again, For by whom anyone is overcome, to him he is also a servant. Just as the sins of the sons do not harm the fathers, so the sins of the fathers do not overflow onto the sons, but the soul that sins, it shall die: not by the abolition of its substance, but out of the fellowship of him who says, I am the life, and who says elsewhere, Everyone who lives and believes in me shall not die for ever, and, Amen, amen I say to you, whoever keeps my word shall not see death for ever; for our life indeed is hidden with Christ in God, the life by which we shall live when Christ, our life, appears in glory, and that which is written will be fulfilled: Amen, amen I say to you, he who hears my word and believes him who sent me has eternal life, and does not come into judgment, but passes from death to life. But what is said by Balaam, Let my soul die the death of the righteous, has this sense: that he desires to die to the world and to sin, and to live with the souls of the righteous, whose life is Christ, and who can sing, I will please the Lord in the land of the living; he is not indeed the God of the dead, but of the living; and if Balaam, translated into our language, means 'a vain people', which is most true, it is clear that the formerly vain people of the nations desired to have fellowship with the souls of the righteous Abraham, Isaac and Jacob, who are called εὐθεῖς, that is, 'upright' and 'righteous'; whence also the book of Genesis received its name from their designation.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.18.5-9":["And a man, if he is righteous and does judgment and righteousness, does not eat on the mountains and does not lift up his eyes to the idols of the house of Israel, and does not violate his neighbor's wife, and does not approach a menstruous woman, and does not grieve a man, returns the pledge to the debtor, seizes nothing by force, gives his bread to the hungry, and covers the naked with a garment, and does not lend at usury, and does not take more, turns his hand away from iniquity, does true judgment between man and man, walks in my precepts, and keeps my judgments so as to do truth: this man is righteous and shall live with life, says the Lord God. (LXX: \"But a man who is righteous, who does judgment and righteousness, does not eat upon the mountains and does not lift up his eyes to the thoughts of the house of Israel, and does not defile his neighbor's wife, and does not approach a woman flowing with blood, and does not oppress a man, returns the pledge owed, and does not seize plunder, gives his bread to the hungry, and covers the naked with a garment, and does not give his money at usury, and does not take more, turns his hand away from iniquity, will do righteous judgment between a man and his neighbor, and walks in my precepts, keeps my justifications so as to do them: this man is righteous, he shall live with life, says Adonai the Lord.\") 'Do you wish', he says, 'to know that what was said, I will render the sins of the fathers to the third and fourth generation, does not mean what most people suppose, and is not like this saying, The fathers have eaten a sour grape, and the teeth of the sons have been set on edge? Hear what I am about to add: if a father is righteous, who has done these things and has not done those, and has a very wicked son who, abandoning his father's virtues, gives himself over to crimes, will not the one live with life because he is righteous, and the other die by death because he has committed all the things by avoiding which his father was made righteous?' Let us look at the catalogue of the father's virtues, which seem to me to be divided into seventeen parts; of which the first is: to have done judgment; the second, similar to this: to have joined righteousness to judgment; the third: not to have eaten on the mountains; the fourth: not to have lifted the eyes to the idols — or, as the Seventy translated, the thoughts — of the house of Israel; the fifth: not to have violated his neighbor's wife; the sixth: to have avoided the embraces of a menstruous wife; the seventh: not to have grieved a man — or, as the Seventy rendered it, oppressed through power —; the eighth: to have returned the pledge to the debtor; the ninth: to have seized nothing by force — or, according to the Seventy, not to have seized plunder —; the tenth: to have given his bread to the hungry; the eleventh: to have covered the naked with a garment; the twelfth: not to have given money at usury; the thirteenth: to have taken nothing more from what you have given; the fourteenth: to have turned his hand away from iniquity; the fifteenth, which seems similar to the first but is different in part: to have done true judgment between man and man — or, his neighbor —; the sixteenth: to have walked in the precepts of the Lord; the seventeenth: to have kept his judgments and justifications. What each of these means we shall say in what follows.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.18.5":["·i· If, he says, a man is righteous and does judgment. It is written in Proverbs: The thoughts of the righteous are judgments. He who possesses this virtue, so as to do nothing without reason and judgment, can say that prophetic word, The judgments of the Lord are true, justified in themselves; and when he has judged all things rightly, so as not to accept the person of the poor in judgment, he will fulfill the precept of the Lord, As the lesser, so also shall you judge the greater, saying boldly, My soul has longed to desire your judgments at all times, and again, I have chosen the way of truth, I have not forgotten your judgments, and in the same psalm, I have known that your judgments are righteousness; and he will advance to such great blessedness that he understands the judgments of the Lord, which are a great deep, and says with the Apostle, O the depth of the riches of the wisdom and knowledge of God! How inscrutable are his judgments, and his ways unsearchable!, and recalls in prayer, For your judgments are delightful. ·ii· After judgment follows 'righteousness', and he who has it will clearly possess Christ, who, according to the Apostle, was made for us righteousness and sanctification and redemption, so that he does true righteousness and does not regard persons in judgment, but knows that, in his judgment of others, he must be judged on his own righteousness.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.18.6":["·iii· The third is: He does not eat on the mountains, which the Jews think pertains to the sin of idolatry; for we read frequently in the books of Kings and of Chronicles: Nevertheless he did not depart from the high places; the people still sacrificed and burned incense on the heights, Scripture signifying this: that on mountains and in groves they sacrificed victims to idols and kindled incense. We, however, say that he eats on the mountains who says with the Pharisee: I give you thanks, God, that I am not like this tax collector. I fast twice in the sabbath, I give tithes of all my substance, and so on; but on the contrary the tax collector, hearing him who had said, Learn from me, for I am meek and humble of heart, struck his breast with his hand, that is, the treasury of the worst thoughts, and did not dare to raise his eyes to heaven. But that too which is said elsewhere, Do not seek things greater than you, and do not search out things stronger than you, convicts all heretics of eating on the mountains of pride, and of despising the simplicity of the Church, and of not knowing that it is written of them: God resists the proud, but gives grace to the humble. ·iiii· In the fourth place is set: And does not lift up his eyes to the idols of the house of Israel, for which the Seventy translated 'thoughts'. Now idols, that is, images which they have fashioned from their own heart, all heretics make, and to them they lift up the eyes of the heart, since they have supposed their falsehood and lie to be the truth. And those are called images of the house of Israel which are found in the Church and, by occasion of knowledge falsely so called, seduce all the simple, as they bring the dogmas of the philosophers into the house of Israel, that is, of those who behold God with the mind. ·v· In the fifth place is set: And does not violate his neighbor's wife (or defile), which plainly forbids adultery; but from the fact that it says 'his neighbor's', unless every human being is understood as a neighbor, the precept would seem to be that we abstain from the wives of friends, but freely pollute the spouses of enemies and strangers. Every human being, therefore, is to be taken as the neighbor of every human being, according to the Gospel parable set forth by the Savior about a certain man who, going down to Jericho, was wounded by robbers, when the Pharisee asks, 'who was his neighbor?', teaching that he is the neighbor who has done good. According to the mystical understanding, the wife of the holy man can be understood as wisdom, as Solomon says: Love her and she will embrace you, cherish her and she will guard you; and he desires to defile her who finds fault with the good words of others and, kindled by the torches of envy, violates holy things, corrupts chaste things, defiles pure things. ·vi· The sixth is: And does not approach a menstruous woman. Each month the heavy and sluggish bodies of women are relieved by the outflow of unclean blood, and if at that time a man has intercourse with a woman, the fetuses conceived are said to contract a defect of the seed, so that lepers and elephantiacs are born from this conception, and corrupted gore makes bodies, foul in either sex, degenerate through the smallness or the enormity of their limbs. Men are therefore commanded, not only with respect to other men's women but also with their own, to whom they are seen to be joined by law, Scripture saying, Increase and multiply and fill the earth, to know fixed times for intercourse, when they must come together and when they must abstain from their wives; which indeed both the Apostle and Ecclesiastes express: A time to embrace and a time to be far from embraces. Let the wife therefore beware lest, overcome by the desire for intercourse, she conceal it from her husband, and the husband lest he do violence to his wife, thinking that the pleasure of marriage ought to be subject to him at every time; whence Paul too: That each one, he says, may know how to possess his own vessel in sanctification and chastity. It is finely said in the little maxims of Sextus the Pythagorean: An adulterer is the too ardent lover of his own wife; a book which a certain man, translating it into the Latin language, wished to adorn with the name of the martyr Sixtus, not considering that in the whole volume, which he pointlessly divided into two parts, the name of Christ and of the apostles is entirely passed over in silence; nor is it surprising if he turned a pagan philosopher into a martyr and bishop of the city of Rome, since he also changed the first book of Eusebius of Caesarea in defense of Origen to the name of the martyr Pamphilus, so that with such a eulogist he might more easily commend the most impious books Περὶ Ἀρχῶν [On First Principles] to Roman ears.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.18.7":["·vii· There follows in the seventh place: And does not grieve a man (or, as the Seventy translated, does not oppress through power). I do not know who is free of this vice and sin; for the Egyptians too oppressed the Hebrews through power, whence Habakkuk also complains that the impious oppresses the righteous. And would that this were said only of those who are outside, and not of those who are within! For the princes of the churches too are accustomed to oppress the people through pride, of whom it is written: They have made you prince: do not be lifted up; be among them as one of them, and the Savior commanded: Whoever wishes to be first among you, let him be the least of all. But what is said according to the Hebrew, And does not grieve a man, agrees with the apostolic testimony, Do not grieve the Holy Spirit of God, who dwells in you; and in the Gospel according to the Hebrews, which the Nazarenes are accustomed to read, it is placed among the greatest crimes: he who has grieved his brother's spirit. But if the sadness of another kills the one who causes the grief, what must be said of iniquity and a tyrannical mind, when that saying fits, Why do earth and ashes glory?, so that a man, forgetful of his condition, how he is full of phlegm, bile and dung, and shortly to be ploughed through by worms, sets his mouth in heaven, and his tongue passes through even to the earth, and he says with the true Nebuchadnezzar, I will ascend into heaven, above the stars of heaven I will set my throne and I will be like the Most High? ·viii· The eighth is: If he returns the pledge to the debtor; not to every debtor, otherwise for many the occasion of receiving back their pledges would become a means to riches, but to that debtor of whom it is written in the Law that he is poor and has put up his own garment, and ought to receive back his covering before sunset, lest, tormented by cold, he cry out to the Lord, who is the avenger of his injury. But if, according to what follows, we ought to give bread to the hungry and cover the naked with a garment, how much more to return what is his own, provided the debtor's poverty is beyond doubt? We can also return the pledge to the debtor when, to one to whom we are joined in love and who owes us mutual charity, we return his pledge, retaining with us nothing further of his debt. ·viiii· The ninth place is held by: Seizes nothing by force (or, according to the Seventy, does not seize plunder). The Apostle too speaks of plunderers, that among the other sinners one must not even eat with such men; and every plunder is mixed with violence, for unless force has been applied, plunder does not succeed. But there is also a holy violence and a desirable plunder, of which the Gospel writes: From the days of John the Baptist the kingdom of heaven suffers violence, and the violent tear it away; of which Jude the brother of James also speaks: And some indeed seize from the fire; but have mercy on others who are judged. On the contrary, the adverse powers hasten to seize their prey to the destruction of those whom they seize, which Jacob too signifies when he says: An evil beast has devoured him, an evil beast has seized Joseph. Hence also the sheep of the Lord which follow him are not seized from his hands, and he himself says: What the Father has given me is greater than all, and no one can seize it from the hand of the Father; from which is seen the one power, might and substance of the Father and the Son: for if no one can seize from the Son's hand what the Father has given, and these same things are in the Father's hand and are not seized from him, it is clearly proved that all things are common to the Father and the Son, and that the Father holds in the Son's hand, just as the things that are the Son's are held in the Father's hand. ·x· The tenth is: Gives his bread to the hungry; by which we are taught that alms are to be given not to the sated but to the hungry, and that bread is to be given not to those who belch with fullness but to those who are tormented by emptiness. In bread, moreover, every food is contained, and 'his' is said significantly, lest we turn into an act of mercy bread acquired from plunder and usury and the misfortune of others: for the redemption indeed of a man's soul is his own riches; which we see many doing who oppress their clients and the poor and the farmers through power, or commit thefts against them, to say nothing of the violence of soldiers and judges, so that out of much they bestow a little on the poor and glory in their crimes; and the deacon reads out publicly in the churches the names of the offerers, 'She offers so much, he has promised so much'; and they are pleased with themselves at the applause of the people, while their conscience torments them; and we give the wretches occasion to rejoice over what they bestow and not to mourn over what they have seized. But it is better for us to understand that the bread of righteousness is he who said, I am the bread who came down from heaven, and whom we pray in prayer to be granted to us: Our substantial bread (or that is to come) give us, so that him whom we shall afterwards receive always, we may deserve to receive daily in the present age. This bread the righteous man gives to the hungry, of whom it is written, Blessed are those who hunger and thirst for righteousness, for they shall be filled, because he who is righteous makes the common bread of all his own, the bread which had failed in Judea, as the prophet says: I will take away from them the strength (or the staff) of bread. What we are saying, provided that we are Christ's, or rather what the prophet records, is the bread of believers and of the hungry, which is by no means to be given to those who have eaten and drunk and been sated and, grown fat, have kicked, of whom it is said, Woe to you who are sated now, for you will hunger, lest they vomit it up, as Solomon says: For he will vomit it up and will defile your good words; which the Savior says in other words: Do not give what is holy to dogs, nor cast your pearls before swine. ·xi· The eleventh place is held by: And has covered the naked with a garment, a saying which, according to the explanation of the preceding little verse, must be expounded in two ways: that we both give covering to the naked, as the Savior says, I was naked, and you covered me, and give to those who are naked of faith and virtues the garment of Christ, of whom it is written, For as many of you as were baptized in Christ have put on Christ. Naked of this garment was he who, not having a wedding garment, was thrown out of the banquet. Of this nakedness the Lord also speaks to Jerusalem: You moreover were naked and full of confusion.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.18.8":["·xii· The twelfth number is held by: And does not lend at usury (or, as the Seventy translated, does not give his money at usury). In the Hebrew usury on all kinds of goods is forbidden, in the Seventy only on money, according to what is also written in Psalm 14 (15), Who has not given his money at usury, and as it is said, To your brother you shall not lend at interest, but to a foreigner you shall lend at interest. But see the progress: at the beginning of the Law interest is removed only from brothers; in the prophet usury is forbidden from all, as Ezekiel says, He has not given his money at usury; and further, in the Gospel there is an increase of virtue, as the Lord commands, 'Lend to those from whom you do not hope to receive back'. ·xiii· There follows in the thirteenth place: And does not take more. Some think that usury applies only to money; foreseeing this, divine Scripture removes the surplus on everything, so that you may not receive back more than you gave. In the country it is customary for 'usury', or, as the divine word calls it, 'abundance', to be exacted on grain and millet, wine and oil, and other kinds of goods; for example, that in winter we give ten modii and at harvest receive back fifteen, that is, more by half. He who thinks himself most just will take a fourth part more; and they are accustomed to argue and say: 'I gave one modius, which, when sown, made ten modii. Is it not just that I should receive half a modius more of what is mine, when he, by my generosity, has nine and a half from what is mine?' Do not err, says the Apostle: God is not mocked. For let the merciful moneylender answer us briefly whether he gave to one who had or to one who did not have. If to one who had, he certainly ought not to have given; but he gave as if to one who did not have: why then does he exact more as if from one who has? Others, in return for money lent at interest, are accustomed to accept little gifts of various kinds, and do not understand that whatever it is is called 'usury' and 'surplus' if they have received more than what they gave. ·xiiii· The fourteenth step: From iniquity, he says, he turns away his hand, so that he flees iniquity in every work. For iniquity is committed not only with the hand but with the other members too, as Solomon says, Remove iniquitous lips far from you, and in the Psalms, They have spoken iniquity on high; the foot too runs to iniquity, and the eye, if it covets another man's wife; nor would he be an imitator of him of whom it is said, He did no iniquity and no deceit was found in his mouth. Hence also we are commanded to make friends for ourselves from the unrighteous mammon, who may receive us into the eternal tabernacles. ·xv· The fifteenth is: Does true judgment between man and man (or his neighbor), which seems to mean the same as the first, where it is written, If he is righteous and does judgment; but it adds the 'truth' of the judgment which he does between man and man, or his neighbor: it is seen to have an ἐπίτασιν [intensification] of the virtues. Hence also at the opening of Proverbs, after many precepts, the 'correction' of judgment is added: To know, it says, wisdom and discipline, and to understand the words of prudence, to receive the subtleties of words, and to know true righteousness, and after all things to correct judgment. For this reason the Apostle too rebukes those who are established in the Church, because they have lawsuits among themselves, and the least and despised one is chosen, who according to the Gospel is first, to judge between man and man, he who has put away the things of a child and has attained to the perfect man; and yet he needs a greater discernment to be able to attain to the 'truth' of judgment.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.18.9":["·xvi· There follows in the sixteenth place: Walks in my precepts, ·xvii· and in the seventeenth: Judgments (or my justifications) he keeps, so as to do them, and keeps them once done; each of which has a manifold understanding, if we wish to go over all the commandments of the Law, in which there are said to be precepts of the Lord and in which justifications. Psalm 118 (119) is full of precepts and justifications, and in part Psalm 18 (19), in which it is written, The righteousnesses of the Lord are right, gladdening hearts and the precept of the Lord is bright, enlightening the eyes; in this the question is raised how God said in this same prophet, I gave them justifications not good, in which they should not live in them; and the answer is easy, and fuller in what follows: that the Jews, following the killing letter, do not live, and the Christians, understanding the life-giving spirit, do live. It would be long, if we wished to prove by testimonies where there are said to be precepts of the Lord and where justifications, and in what differences or obscurities they are each wrapped. Hence too in the present place it is added: This man is righteous, he shall live with life, says the Lord God; he who has done these things and has not done those will by no means be punished for the offenses of his father, but will live by his own virtues.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.18.10-13":["And let us speak first according to the history, so that you may know that the iniquities of the fathers do not overflow onto the sons. If the righteous man does what the preceding discourse wove together in order, he shall live with life; but if he begets a son who departs from the service of the Lord and exchanges his father's virtues for vices, so that he does what the father did not do and does not do what the father wrought, will he be able to live? Certainly he will not live, but will be guilty of his own blood. Further, according to the spiritual understanding, if a righteous man of the Church has preached the evangelical faith, and his son and disciple has been deceived by heretical error, the son will be called pestilent, of whom it is written also in Psalm 1, And has not sat in the chair of pestilence, and in Proverbs, The bold and insolent and proud man is called pestilent. He has shed the blood of the deceived and doubles all sins upon himself, eating on the mountains of pride and polluting his neighbor's church, grieving the needy and the poor in the knowledge of the Scriptures, oppressing and supplanting them, seizing as plunder those whom he has seduced from the Church, not returning the pledge which he had received from his teacher so as to fulfill what is written, Freely you have received, freely give, and lifting up his eyes to the idols and images which he has fashioned from his own heart, and committing all abominations, and giving money at usury, so as to increase the teacher's error by the diligence of the disciples, and demanding back from those to whom he gave more than he had given: certainly he will not be able to live, but will die in his own blood.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.18.10":["But if he begets a son who is a robber, shedding blood, and does one of these things (or, as the Seventy translated, and does sins, does not walk in the way of his righteous father), and who does not do all these things, but eats on the mountains, and pollutes his neighbor's wife, grieves the needy and the poor (or oppresses), seizes plunder, does not return the pledge, and lifts up his eyes to idols, commits abomination, gives at usury and takes more: shall he live? He shall not live; since he has done all these detestable things, he shall die by death, his blood shall be upon him. For 'robber' in the Hebrew is written 'pharis', which the second edition of Aquila translated 'sinner', Symmachus 'transgressor', the Seventy and Theodotion 'pestilent'. Just as a pestilence creates diseases and is wont to lay waste far and wide the regions on which it has settled, so the pestilent man ravages everything.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.18.14-18":["But if he begets a son who, seeing all the sins of his father which he did, fears and does not do likewise, does not eat upon the mountains and does not lift up his eyes to the idols of the house of Israel, and does not violate his neighbor's wife, and does not grieve a man, does not retain the pledge, and does not seize plunder, gives his bread to the hungry, and covers the naked with a garment, turns his hand away from injury to the poor, does not take usury and surplus, does my judgments, walks in my precepts: this man shall not die in the iniquity of his father, but shall live with life. His father, because he practiced calumny and did violence to his brother, and wrought evil in the midst of his people, has died in his iniquity. 'And do not wonder', he says, 'if the son of a righteous man, turning aside to vices and sins, dies by death, since, on the contrary, the son of a sinner and impious man, if he sees the perversity of his father's ways and turns himself so as not to do evil things and to do good things, is not held guilty of his father's crimes'. This can be taken also of us, to whom it is said in the Psalms, Hear, daughter, and see, and incline your ear, and forget your people and the father's house; and the king will desire your beauty, and who have been born of the stock of the nations: that we should leave behind the crimes of our parents and do judgment and righteousness and live in it. He therefore repeats what we have treated more broadly above, and so we run through everything briefly, wishing to pass on to the things that are more obscure and new.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.18.19-20":["And you say: Why has the son not borne the iniquity of the father? Clearly because the son has wrought judgment and righteousness, has kept all my precepts, and has done them: he shall live with life. The soul that sins, it shall die: the son shall not bear the iniquity of the father, and the father the iniquity of the son; the righteousness of the righteous shall be upon him, and the impiety of the impious shall be upon him. He solves the question which the hearer could raise as an objection: 'You are accustomed', he says, 'to say: Why has the righteous son not borne the iniquity of the father?'; to which he himself replied: 'Clearly because the son has done well and has not committed his father's offenses'. And it is just that, as the sinner dies in his crime, so the righteous man should live in his virtues; and that the soul that sins should die, and he who keeps the precepts of God should live.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.18.21-22":["But if the impious man repents of all his sins which he has wrought, and keeps all my precepts, and does judgment and righteousness, he shall live with life and shall not die. Of all his iniquities which he has wrought I will not be mindful. 'To such a degree', he says, 'do the sins of the fathers not overflow onto the sons, nor does a criminal father weigh down a righteous son, nor are some punished for the crimes of others, that one and the same man who was formerly impious and a sinner, if afterwards he repents and, turned to better things, blots out his former sins, is not judged by the oldness of his sin, but is received into my flock by the renewal of virtue'. And at the same time it must be considered what kind of impious man and sinner he receives as a penitent: 'If from all his sins', he says, 'which he has wrought he turns himself away, and keeps all the precepts of the Lord, so, namely, that he abandons all crimes and follows all virtues; if he does all good things and forsakes all evil things, I too will forget all his iniquities which he has wrought'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.18.22":["In his righteousness which he has wrought he shall live. 'He shall live not so much by my righteousness as by his own, although it belongs to my righteousness to render good things to the good and evil things to the evil'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.18.23":["Is the death of the impious man of my will, says the Lord God, and not that he should turn from his ways and live? Therefore it is of the Lord's will that all be saved and come to the knowledge of the truth; and wherever the sentence of God seems severe and ferocious, he condemns not men but sins.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.18.24":["But if the righteous man turns himself away from his righteousness, and does iniquity according to all the abominations which the impious man is accustomed to work, shall he live? All his righteousnesses which he had done shall not be remembered; in the transgression by which he transgressed and in his sin which he sinned, in it he shall die. Just as old offenses do not weigh down the righteous man who was formerly a sinner, so former righteousnesses do not help the sinner who was formerly righteous; each one is judged in that in which he is found.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.18.25":["And you have said: The way of the Lord is not equitable. Hear, house of Israel: Is my way not equitable, and are not rather your ways crooked? He gives the reasons why the sentence of the Lord is just: 'Do you think me unjust', he says, 'in rendering the sins of the fathers to the sons, so that while some eat the sour grape, the teeth of others are set on edge? Behold, each one dies in his own sin and is given life in his own righteousness; in both cases, not the things past but the things present. Rather it is your sentence that is unjust, you who think that the parable is not a parable, but take it as though it were the truth of history, that the crimes of some are punished in others'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.18.26":["For when the righteous man turns himself away from his righteousness and does iniquity, he shall die in them: in the iniquity which he has wrought he shall die. This can be understood thus: the people of Israel, formerly righteous, turned itself away from its righteousness, because it abandoned the author of righteousness, and did iniquity by denying the Son of God; in the sin and crime which it wrought it will die, not in many but in one, killing the heir so that it lost the inheritance.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.18.27-28":["And when the impious man turns himself away from his impiety which he has wrought, and does judgment and righteousness, he has given life to his own soul; for, considering, he has turned himself away from all his iniquities which he has wrought; he shall live with life, and shall not die. 'On the contrary', he says, 'the people of the nations, not having the knowledge of God, and impious, if it turns itself away from its impiety which it formerly wrought in idolatry, and does the things which were commanded to Israel in the Law, has itself, formerly dead, given life to its own soul; and seeing that it had perished in the iniquities which it wrought, it will believe in him who says, 'I am the way and the life and the truth'; it shall live with life, and shall not die'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.18.29":["And the sons of Israel say: The way of the Lord is not equitable. Are my ways not equitable, house of Israel, and are not rather your ways crooked? To this day Israel blasphemes God: why has he abandoned his people and taken up the multitude of the nations? The Lord rebukes them because their ways are crooked, while the sentence of the Lord is just: having despised or destroyed the former tenants, he sends other farmers into his vineyard; understanding which, in the parable of the Gospel, the Jews said: This shall not be.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.18.30-31":["Turn, and repent of all your iniquities, and iniquity shall not be a ruin to you. Cast away from you all your transgressions by which you have transgressed. This word is properly directed to Israel, that they should repent and abandon their iniquities, or transgressions, by which they transgressed against God. But it can also be understood of both peoples, so that both Israel and the crowd of the nations, abandoning their vices, may turn to him who is able to heal their bruises.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.18.30":["Therefore I will judge each one of you according to his ways, house of Israel, says the Lord God. Whether those who are judged are from the multitude of the Gentiles or from the people of Israel, there is no respect of persons with God, but each one will be crowned by his own faith, and condemned by impiety and unbelief.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.18.31":["'And make for yourselves a new heart and a new spirit, so that, abandoning the oldness of the letter, you may live in the newness of the spirit'. The new heart of Israel is to believe in him whom it had formerly denied; the new heart of the nations is to abandon idols and, having despised the cult of the dead, to believe in him who is the God of the living.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","And why will you die, house of Israel? It is better, as we said above, to take this exhortation, in which it is written, Turn, and repent, as addressed to the person of the Jews, whom he does not wish to die and to whom he is now speaking: 'Why will you die, house of Israel, you who have as fathers Abraham, Isaac and Jacob — of whom it is written, The God of the living and not of the dead —? Why will you die by your own fault, you who ought to live by the merit of your fathers and by my mercy?'\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.18.32":["For I do not want the death of the dying sinner, says the Lord God; return and live. 'I do not want you to die', he says, 'whom I begot in salvation: for sons indeed I have begotten and exalted, but they have despised me; return therefore and live'. Return is not said except to those who were formerly with God and afterwards deserted his company. And live through repentance, you who are dead through sin. Therefore Israel, because it does not return to its former state, must be believed to be dead.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.19.1-14":["From this it is plain that both the earlier reading, in which it is said: Why did your mother, a lioness, lie down among lions?, and this one, in which it is written: Your mother is like a vine in your blood, planted upon water, pertain to Jerusalem, which lost her lions and her shoots, and there did not remain in her a rod that might rise up into a tribe or into the scepters of rulers. For it begins from a lament and ends in a lament: Take up a lament over the princes of Israel, that is, at the beginning, and now at the end: It is a lament; and the royal stock is to be attended by the lamentation and lament of the parable. Moreover, according to the ἀναγωγὴν [anagogy, the higher sense], what others refer to the heavenly Jerusalem — so as to say that from it very many fell down into this valley of tears, and that kings ceased to be, and that the once most beautiful vine was dried up by the burning wind, so that none of its green shoots remained in it, the vine which Jeremiah afterwards laments under the figure of the city — we understand of the Church — because in the last time, iniquity having been multiplied, the love of many grows cold, so that if it can be, even the elect of God are tested, and her princes are caught in the nets of the devil, who is not content with the capture of one king but hastens daily to capture kings and princes, according to what is written: His foods are choice —. The shoots of the Church too, which formerly were equaled to flowers and to the redness of blood, are afterwards dried up by the burning wind; and this exposition the parable of the Gospel also sounds forth, in which, when the sun had risen, the things that had sprouted were scorched and suddenly dried up, so that scarcely one of the shoots remains to rise up into a rod and become a worthy prince of the people, which Isaiah says in other words: I will bring famine and thirst upon the land, and not a famine of bread and a thirst for water, but a famine of hearing the word of God. Hence we must lament and wail over the princes of Israel, through whose vice and pride Judea was deserted, and Jerusalem was captured.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.19.1-9":["And you, take up a lament over the princes of Israel, and you shall say: Why did your mother, a lioness, lie down among lions, in the midst of young lions rear her whelps? And she brought out one of her young lions, he became a lion, and he learned to catch prey and to devour man. And the nations heard of him, and not without wounds of their own they caught him, and they brought him in chains into the land of Egypt. And when she had seen that she was weakened and her expectation had perished, she took one of her young lions, she made him a lion. He went about among the lions and became a lion, he learned to catch prey and to devour men; he learned to make widows, and to bring their cities into a desert, and the land and its fullness was made desolate by the sound of his roaring. And the nations gathered against him from every side, from the provinces, and they spread their net over him; in their wounds he was caught. And they put him into a cage, in chains, and they brought him to the king of Babylon; and they put him into prison, so that his voice should no longer be heard upon the mountains of Israel. (LXX: \"And you, take a lamentation over the prince of Israel, and you shall say: Why has your mother become a whelp in the midst of lions, in the midst of lions she multiplied her whelps? And one of her whelps sprang forth, he became a lion, and he learned to seize spoils, he devoured men. And the nations heard against him, in their corruption he was caught, and they brought him in a muzzle into the land of Egypt. And she saw that he had been taken away from her and that her substance had perished, and she took another of her whelps, she made him a lion. And he went about in the midst of the lions, he became a lion, and he learned to seize spoils, he devoured men; and he fed in his boldness, and he brought their cities to a desert, and he laid waste the land and its fullness by the sound of his roaring. And he set nations against him round about from the regions, and they spread their nets over him, in their corruption he was caught. And they put him in a muzzle and in a cage, he came to the king of Babylon; and they brought him into prison, so that his voice should not be heard any longer upon the mountains of Israel.\") I know that I have read in this place a manifold explanation, and one entangled in such great obscurities that it did not so much open up the reading as wrap it up, since it refers everything that is written to the opposing powers and describes lions and wars in the heavenly Jerusalem, how one of them was captured and another was in turn set up in his place; and it sets out many testimonies that the devil and his companions are customarily called lions, such as that saying of the apostle Peter: Our adversary the devil goes about like a roaring lion and seeks how he may enter, and that of the Psalms: Do not hand over to the beasts the souls that confess to you; certainly, when it comes to the history, it says that Johanan the son of Kareah was led into Egypt (how he, fleeing with a few men, is to be called a lion, I am unable to know), and that the other lion is Zedekiah, who was himself also led into Babylon. But we, leaving explanations of this kind to the judgment of the reader, say that, with the captivity close at hand, the prophet is not so much foretelling things to come as narrating things past. For after the sixth year of King Zedekiah — since immediately in what follows we read: And it came to pass in the seventh year, in the fifth month, on the tenth of the month — a lament is taken up over the princes — or the prince — of Israel, and it is said: Why did your mother, a lioness, lie down among lions? The lament, then, is over the princes, namely all who were begotten of the stock of Josiah. Jerusalem, the mother of the princes, is called a lioness, who bore and reared young lions, and brought out one of her young lions and made him a lion, signifying Jehoahaz the son of Josiah, whom Pharaoh Neco led into Egypt, and in his place he set up Jehoiakim; and when he had died, Jeconiah his son, having been made king, was led to Babylon by Nebuchadnezzar together with his mother and the nobles of the city; of him is born Shealtiel, the father of Zerubbabel, who received his name from the fact that he was born in Babylon. And it is plain that under the μεταφορᾷ [metaphor] of the lioness, of the young lions and of the lion, and again of another lion, those things are described which had already been done at the time when the prophet was saying these things.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.19.3":["For after Jerusalem had brought out Jehoahaz the son of Josiah from the royal stock and made him king, he proved so cruel in a short time that he is related, tropologically, to have caught prey and devoured men.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.19.4":["When the Egyptian had come against him, he caught him not without wounds — or in a pit, as is properly contained in the Hebrew, so as to preserve the tropological capture of lions, which are always caught in pits — and he brought him in a muzzle, or in chains, into the land of Egypt, and there he died.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.19.5":["When his mother Jerusalem, who had borne him, who had reared him, who had set him in the kingship, had seen that her expectation had perished, she took from among the other kings Jeconiah, the son of Jehoiakim, and set him up as king.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.19.6-9":["He too, having imitated the cruelty of his predecessor, is described under the ferocity of a lion, in that he devoured men, made many widows, brought cities to a desert, and at the sound of his roaring the whole province was terrified, so that the nations gathered against him and spread their net and caught him in a pit and put him in a cage and in chains — not that this was done to Jeconiah: for he gave himself up to the king of Babylonia and was carried off into Chaldea; but so that the metaphor of the lion may be preserved, which is caught in pits and bound with chains and kept in cages —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.19.9":["Otherwise, according to the history, we read this of Zedekiah, who was set up as king of Jerusalem after Jeconiah — and this is what gave occasion for most people to understand Zedekiah rather than Jeconiah, because chains are read of, and cages, and a prison is named —; he was, then, kept in prison and not put to death, and was only removed from the kingship. But the history relates that Zedekiah was led blind into Babylon and there immediately killed.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.19.10-11":["Your mother is like a vine in your blood, planted upon the water; its fruits and its leaves grew from many waters; and it had solid rods for the scepters of rulers, and its stature was exalted into leaves, and it saw its height in the multitude of its shoots. And it was plucked up in wrath, and cast down to the ground, and the burning wind dried up its fruits; the rods of its strength withered and were dried up, fire devoured it. And now it is transplanted into the desert, in a land impassable and thirsty. And fire went out from the rod of its branches, which devoured its fruit; and there was not in it a strong rod, a scepter of rulers. It is a lament, and it shall be for a lament. (LXX: \"Your mother is like a vine, like a flower on pomegranates, planted in water; its fruit and its sprout came from much water; and it had a rod of strength for a tribe of leaders, and it was exalted in its greatness in the midst of its offshoots, and it saw its greatness in the multitude of its shoots. And broken in fury, it was cast to the ground, and the burning wind dried up its choice parts; they were avenged, and the rod of its strength was dried up, fire consumed it. And now they have planted it in the desert, in a land without water. And fire went out from the rod of its choice ones, and devoured it, and there was not in it a rod of strength. A tribe is in a parable of lamentation, and it shall be for a lament.\") One thing is said in different words; and just as in the earlier passages we read that Jerusalem was compared to a beautiful woman, and again to a lioness who reared lions in her lair, so now she is compared to a vine-stock or a most beautiful vine planted upon many waters; and that therefore its shoots, nourished by the moisture, grew so much that one shoot, which Scripture calls a most solid or most strong rod, advanced 'into the scepters of rulers' — for which the Seventy rendered 'into a tribe of leaders' — so, namely, that kings should be made from it. And the vine itself was of such beauty that the splendor of its greenness was made equal to the flowers of the pomegranate, which is what is said in the Hebrew, like a vine in your blood, the redness of its flowers being compared to blood.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.19.12":["The vine, then, was exalted and raised up; because God resists the proud, but gives grace to the humble, it was plucked up in the fury of the Lord and cast down to the ground, so that the elegant metaphor of the vine-stock might preserve the ruins of Jerusalem; for however beautiful a vine may be and however far it stretches out with long runners, unless you raise up stakes and reeds, joined to the earth it dries up from the heat, especially if the burning wind, which the Greeks call καύσωνα [the scorching wind], has dried up its fruits, so that it withers and is burned as though by the blazing heat of fire. But as for what has been added in the Seventy, And they were avenged, in place of what is written in the Hebrew, They withered, what coherence it can have I am unable to know.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.19.13-14":["That vine, then, of such abundance and of such fruits and of such beauty, how is it now carried over into the desert, in a land impassable and thirsty? And it signifies the land of Babylon, to which they were carried over, or Egypt, where they fled for refuge, or the land of Judea itself, in which a few poor people remained, over whom Gedaliah was set to gather and rule the remnant of the people; against him there rose up, from the royal stock and from the rod of the branches of the vine, Ishmael, who killed him at Mizpah and devoured all the fruit of the vine; and from then on there was not in that vine a strong rod and a scepter of rulers: for none of the kings remained to govern the people, but with Johanan, the son of Kareah, they fled to Egypt. Therefore it is a lament and a parable of lamentation, so that the royal line may be lamented, which will thenceforth cease in Judea until he should come for whom it is reserved: And he was the expectation of the nations.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.20.1":["And it came to pass in the seventh year, in the fifth, on the tenth of the month, there came men of the elders of Israel to inquire of the Lord, and they sat before me. Eleven months and five days after the previous vision, the word again comes to the prophet, after some of the elders of the house of Israel had come to inquire of the Lord and had sat before him. That it was the custom of the people of Israel to seek from the Lord through the prophets whatever they desired to know, many examples testify: Saul, looking for the asses, goes to Samuel and received advice from the servant boy to offer the prophet the fourth part of a shekel; when the son of Jeroboam fell ill, his wife is sent to Shiloh to the prophet Ahijah, bringing little gifts, loaves and cakes, grapes and a small jar of honey, to learn about her son's sickness; David too, a prophet, inquires of another prophet, Nathan, whether he ought to build a temple to the Lord; and Ahab asks whether he should go up to Ramoth-gilead or not; in Isaiah also and in the volume of Kings the things that were to come upon Sennacherib are announced. Nor is it surprising if in the Old Testament the prophets announce to individuals the things that are to come, since we read that Agabus prophesied the things that were to come upon Paul; for it is written in Deuteronomy: The nations which the Lord is about to destroy in your sight, these will listen to dreams and divinations; but to you the Lord your God has not granted it so: for it belongs to the nations to resort to Chaldeans, soothsayers, haruspices, diviners, lot-casters, the oracles of demons, by which their error is mocked. Hence now too the elders of Israel approach the prophet in order to inquire of the Lord through him, and yet they are silent about their question, while he who was to be questioned knew what they were asking, so that in this too there might be a miracle: to know why they had come and to answer the things that a silent speech held within their mind.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.20.3":["And the word of the Lord came to me, saying: Son of man, speak to the elders of Israel, and you shall say to them: Thus says the Lord God: Have you come to inquire of me? As I live, I will not answer you, says the Lord God. To the saints and to those who ask fitting things a promise is given: 'While you are still speaking I will say: Behold, I am here'; but to sinners, such as the elders of Israel were, whose crimes the prophet describes in what follows, no answer is given, but a rebuke for their sins, and he adds an oath: As I live, so that the sentence of the one who refuses may be the firmer. What the Seventy said: If I answer you, Symmachus translated more plainly: I will not answer you.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.20.4":["If you judge them, if you judge, son of man. (LXX: \"If I avenge them with vengeance, son of man.\") 'They indeed came to inquire, wishing to learn the future concerning the things about which they are in doubt. But you, son of man, judge them, so that there may be no prophet's answer, but the sentence of one who judges, for the iniquities which they have committed, and they have followed the crimes of their fathers'; or certainly: If I avenge them with vengeance, so that the sense is: 'They are covered with such great iniquity that they are not worthy even of correction and reproof', according to what is said in the prophet: I will no longer visit your daughters and your daughters-in-law, when they have committed adultery. Hence sinners too, who have come into the depth of sin, are let go to do the desires of their heart.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","Show them the abominations of their fathers. And you shall say to them. (LXX: \"Testify to them the iniquities of their fathers, and you shall say to them.\") If the sins of the fathers do not overflow onto the sons, how is it that the abominations and iniquities of the fathers are now set before the elders? For this reason, evidently: that they may be shown to act like their parents and, by a hereditary evil, to drag a very long rope of sins, so that they may dread the punishments of those whose vices they imitate.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.20.5-6":["Thus says the Lord God: On the day when I chose Israel, and lifted up my hand for the stock of the house of Jacob, and appeared to them in the land of Egypt, and lifted up my hand for them, saying: I am the Lord your God. On that day I lifted up my hand for them, to lead them out of the land of Egypt into the land which I had provided for them, flowing with milk and honey, which is excellent among all lands. (LXX: \"Thus says the Lord God: From the day on which I chose the house of Israel, and became known to the seed of the house of Jacob, and was known to them in the land of Egypt, and took them up, saying: I am the Lord your God. On that day I took hold of them with my hand, saying, to lead them out of the land of Egypt into the land which I prepared for them, a land flowing with milk and honey, it is a honeycomb beyond all lands.\") For what the Seventy said: It is a honeycomb beyond all lands, for which we translated: It is excellent among all lands, the first edition of Aquila put 'firm support', the second 'renowned', Symmachus 'region', Theodotion 'strength', namely because this land is the foundation of all lands, because in it are the religion of God and the temple and the ceremonies and the knowledge of God; and so far was the people of Israel, settled in Egypt, from knowing God, that Moses, sent to them: If they ask, he says, me: Who sent you?, what shall I say to them? Say, he says, to them: He who is, sent me. In Egypt, then, he became known to the people and to the seed of the house of Jacob, when he lifted up his hand for them against the Egyptians, and said: I am the Lord your God, and he chose them to lead them out of the land of Egypt to a land flowing with milk and honey; for they were not yet able to take solid food, but like little children and sucklings they needed the honey and milk of infancy. According to the letter, however, that the land of Judea is renowned and more fertile than all lands, no one will be able to doubt who has considered the whole land from Rhinocorura as far as Mount Taurus and the river Euphrates, and the power of its cities and the pleasantness of its regions, namely Palestine and Phoenicia, Arabia, Coele-Syria, Cilicia, and the other regions which the Lord promised to Israel if he had kept the precepts of God; and that he did not receive them was the fault of his unbelief. For even in Palestine itself and the province of Judea very many nations remained which were not driven out; for the guarantor is not to blame if the one to whom the promise is made has made himself unworthy of the pledge, especially since the one who promises sets out a choice: If you are willing and listen to me, you shall eat the good things of the land. But if you are unwilling, the sword shall devour you. They are chosen, then, according to the present, and it does not follow that one who is chosen cannot be tempted or perish, since both Saul, chosen as king, and Judas, chosen as apostle, afterward fell by their own fault. The lifting up of the hand, or its stretching out, shows the posture of one who strikes: that he might strike the Egyptians for them and free the people of Israel from Egypt.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.20.7":["And he said to them: Let each one cast away the offenses of his eyes, and do not be defiled with the idols of Egypt: I am the Lord your God. (LXX: \"And he said to them: Let each one cast away the abominations of his eyes, and let him not be defiled with the inventions of Egypt: I am the Lord your God.\") For 'offenses', which in Hebrew are called 'seccuse', Symmachus translated 'nauseating things', the second edition of Aquila 'cuttings-off', so as to signify that bleariness must be cast away from the eyes, and a kind of nausea which not only hinders the sight but also produces vomiting in those who look at it; 'gelule' too is a Hebrew word, which the Seventy translated 'inventions', the first edition of Aquila 'defilements', the second, and Symmachus and Theodotion, 'idols', whom we too have followed for the present. He commanded them, as they were first going out of Egypt, to forget the idols which they had served for a long time; to cast them away from their eyes, so as not to hold them worthy even of a glance, and no longer to give consent to the things by which they had long been defiled. I, he says, am the Lord your God, not the portents of Egypt, not the fabrications of various monsters. But we too, when we go out of Egypt, are commanded to cast away the offenses of our eyes, namely so that we may not take delight in the things in which we formerly took delight in the world, lest we be defiled with the images of Egypt, that is, with the inventions of the philosophers and of the heretics, which are rightly called idols. Let us also turn our eyes away from the spectacles, or rather the offenses, of Egypt: of the arena, the circus, the theaters, and all the things that defile the purity of the soul and enter the mind through the senses; and what is written is fulfilled: Death will come in through your windows.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.20.8":["And they provoked me. (LXX: \"And they withdrew from me.\") Or, as Symmachus translated: They did not consent to me; and as soon as they are called, they withdraw from God, so that there is no interval between the calling and the withdrawal. And this is to be noted, that no one withdraws from God except one who was formerly with him; hence the dragon too in the book of Job is called ἀποστάτης, a transgressor and one who withdraws.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","And they would not listen; each one did not cast away the abominations of his eyes, nor did they abandon the idols of Egypt. (LXX: \"And they would not listen to me; they did not cast away the abominations of their eyes, and did not abandon the inventions of Egypt.\") 'For this reason', he says, 'they provoked me and withdrew from me: because they would not listen, while man's free will is preserved, and they did all the things which I commanded in the law that they should not do, at once despairing of salvation and muttering against Moses, and distrusting the promises of God'. There follows:\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","And I said that I would pour out my indignation upon them, and fulfill my wrath on them in the midst of the land of Egypt. 'As soon as I commanded them to leave or cast away the offenses of their eyes and by no means to be defiled with the idols of Egypt, they provoked me and would not listen to what I said, but on the contrary did what I had commanded should not be done. Therefore I had decreed to pour out on them my fury and my indignation and to fulfill my wrath, which I had conceived in my mind, in the midst of Egypt, upon those who had not yet gone out of Egypt'. For there follows: I therefore cast them out of the land of Egypt and led them down into the desert, from which it is shown that they had not yet gone out of the land of Egypt when he wished to pour out his indignation and fulfill his wrath upon them and to strike them in the midst of the land of Egypt.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.20.9":["And I acted for my name's sake, that it might not be profaned before the nations in whose midst they were, and among whom I appeared to them, to lead them out of the land of Egypt. 'What I was thinking of doing on account of justice I did not do on account of the greatness of my clemency, lest my name be defiled and I should seem not to have been able to fulfill what I had promised to Israel. I spared my name, then, lest the nations among whom I became known to them in the land of Egypt should have occasion to blaspheme'. Up to this point the prophet describes what he promised them while they were settled in the land of Egypt, and how they at once gave offense, and what the Lord thought of doing against them and yet did not do; what follows he spoke to them after they had gone out of Egypt.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.20.10-12":["I therefore cast them out of the land of Egypt, and led them out into the desert. And I gave them my precepts, and showed them my judgments, which a man should do and live in them. Moreover I also gave them my sabbaths, that it might be a sign between me and them, and that they might know that I am the Lord who sanctifies them. And the house of Israel provoked me in the desert: they did not walk in my precepts, and they cast away my judgments, which, if a man does them, he shall live in them, and they violated my sabbaths exceedingly. I said therefore that I would pour out my fury upon them in the desert and consume them. And I acted for my name's sake, that it might not be profaned before the nations from which I cast them out in their sight. These things are said to those who were led out of Egypt into the desert, so that, freed from the vices of the Egyptians, they might more easily fulfill the precepts of God in the wilderness and do his judgments and keep the sabbaths, which were given as a sign between the one who gave them and those to whom they were given, as Scripture says: The Lord spoke to Moses, saying: Command the children of Israel and you shall say to them: See that you keep my sabbaths, for it is a sign between me and you throughout your generations, that you may know that I am the Lord who sanctifies you; and keep the sabbath, for it is holy to you: whoever violates it shall surely die; everyone who does work on it, that soul shall be put to death from the midst of its people. So the sabbath and circumcision were given as a sign of the true sabbath and the true circumcision: that we may know that we must rest from earthly works in the perfect and true sabbath, and that we should circumcise not the foreskin but the heart. Hence, working for six days, we rest on the seventh day, so that day and night we may do nothing else than recognize that all that we live is owed to the Lord, and, as the week comes round again, consecrate ourselves wholly to his name, so that through the sanctification of the day we may remember the Lord who sanctifies us.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.20.13-14":["These precepts and ordinances and the observance of the sabbath the Lord gave in the desert, so that doing them they might live in them, not that they should pass from them to greater things, as is promised in the Gospel; and these they violated, not once nor a little but exceedingly, so that the ἐπίτασις [intensification] of the violation might be shown. He said, therefore, and decreed in his mind, that he would pour out his fury upon them in the desert and consume them, when he spoke to Moses: Let me alone, and, angered with fury against them, I will destroy them; but he would not do it, sparing the Egyptians and the other nations, lest they be scandalized, and them as well, on whom he had mercy, awaiting the repentance of those who would turn back. And it is to be noted that, when he was speaking to them while they were settled in Egypt, after the offense: I acted, he says, for my name's sake, that it might not be profaned before the nations, but now he says: I acted for my name's sake, that it might not be profaned before the nations from which I cast them out in their sight; for then they were still in their midst, not yet having gone out, but now, after they have gone out, it is said: From which I cast them out in their sight.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.20.15-16":["I therefore lifted up my hand over them into the desert, not to bring them into the land which I gave them, flowing with milk and honey, the foremost of all lands; because they cast away my judgments, and did not walk in my precepts, and violated my sabbaths, for their heart went after idols. And my eye spared them, so that I did not kill them; nor did I consume them in the desert. (LXX: \"And I lifted up my hand over them in the desert, so as not at all to bring them into the land which I gave them, a land flowing with milk and honey, it is a honeycomb beyond every land; because they rejected my ordinances, and did not walk in my precepts, and violated my sabbaths, and they walk after the thoughts of their hearts. And my eye spared them, so that I did not wipe them out; and I did not kill them to consumption in the desert.\") 'I had indeed decreed to pour out all my fury upon them and to wipe them out and consume them, but I acted for my name's sake, that it might not be profaned among the nations — namely the Amalekites and the rest, which fought against them in the wilderness —, so that I would by no means bring them into the land which I promised them, flowing with milk and honey, which is the foremost of all lands — or a honeycomb —'. The cause of the punishment and of this sentence is: Because they cast away my judgments, and did not walk in my precepts, and violated the sabbaths; and the cause of that cause is this: that they did not do these things because their heart followed the idols of Egypt.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.20.17":["'But my eye', he says, 'spared them, so that I did not kill them and wipe them out: so that I did not consume them altogether'; in which it is asked how he spared those whose corpses lay in the wilderness and, except for Joshua son of Nun and Caleb son of Jephunneh, no one entered the land of promise. From this we understand that they live, and are not reserved for eternal punishments, nor blotted out of the book of the living, nor consumed before the face of the Lord; for if, because they were not brought into the land of promise, they must be believed to have perished, then Moses too perished, who only saw the land of promise and did not enter it, of whom the Lord speaks in the book of Joshua: Moses my servant is dead, and in Malachi: Remember the law of Moses my servant, and in Jeremiah God speaks of him as of his friend and most intimate: If Moses and Samuel should stand in my sight? Otherwise we must also believe that the prayers of Moses were heard, when, interceding for the people, he says: If you forgive them their sin, forgive; but if you will not, blot me too out of your book. And that the sentence of the Lord against sons and servants is not perdition but correction, let us hear the testimonies of the Scriptures: Son, do not be saddened at the discipline of the Lord, nor faint when you are rebuked by him: for whom the Lord loves he corrects, and he scourges every son whom he receives, and in another place: When he slew them, then they sought him, and in the song of Deuteronomy: I will kill and I will make alive. We can also say this according to the letter: that he did not wipe them out nor consume them along with their stock and offspring, but, when the fathers had been killed, he spared the sons, whom he brought into the land of promise.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.20.18-20":["But he said to their sons in the wilderness: In the precepts (or statutes) of your fathers do not walk, and neither with their idols nor keep their judgments (or be defiled by thoughts). I am the Lord your God: walk in my precepts, and keep my judgments and do them, and sanctify my sabbaths, that it may be a sign between me and you, and that it may be known (or that you may know) that I am the Lord your God. After the slaying of the fathers who fell in the wilderness, he gives precepts to the sons, in whose salvation he had mercy on the fathers: 'And the eye of the Lord', he says, 'spared them, so as not to kill them altogether and wipe them out, but to preserve the fathers in the sons'; and he testifies to them the same things that he also spoke to the fathers: that, after they have walked in his precepts and kept his judgments and observed the sanctification of the sabbath, which was given as a sign, they may then know that he is the Lord their God. By the precepts and statutes of the fathers and their judgments he signifies ingrained error, so that they may not imitate the sins of those whose torments they behold.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.20.21-22":["And the sons exasperated me; they did not walk in my precepts, and did not keep my judgments to do them, which, when a man has done them, he shall live in them, and they violated my sabbaths; and I threatened to pour out my fury upon them, and to fulfill my wrath on them in the desert. But I turned back my hand and acted for my name's sake, that it might not be profaned before the nations from which I cast them out in their eyes. 'But the sons too', he says, 'followed the crimes of their parents and did all that those had done; hence they too deserved a like sentence; but they were preserved by the same greatness of my mercy as their fathers too, and for the same reasons for which I also had mercy on the fathers, so that I, the one and the same Creator of both, might soften my wrath with a like patience'. We run through the plain things so as to pass on to the more obscure.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.20.23-26":["Again I lifted up my hand against them in the wilderness, to scatter them among the nations and winnow them into the lands, because they had not done my judgments, and had rejected my precepts, and had violated my sabbaths, and their eyes had been after the idols (or thoughts) of their fathers. Therefore I too gave them precepts not good, and judgments in which they shall not live; and I defiled them in their gifts, when they offered (or made pass over) everything that opens the womb, for their transgressions (for which the Seventy translated that I might destroy them. And what they passed over:), and they shall know that I am the Lord. Where in the Old Testament the Lord lifted up his hand against the sons of those who fell in the wilderness, wishing to scatter them among the nations, Scripture does not say, but from what he relates here we must believe that it happened; or else this signifies that, after they had entered the land of promise, at various times on account of many sins they were handed over to diverse nations and kings, and at that time the precepts of the Lord and the judgments, which were good by their nature and in which believers could live, were made not good for them, since in captivity they were by no means able to keep the precepts of the law and do what the divine word commanded; nor did he say: I gave them 'evil' precepts, but 'not good' — for it does not at once follow that what is not good is evil, as the Apostle too teaches: that it is good for a man not to touch a wife or a woman, but, because of incontinence, for each one to possess his own vessel in sanctification and chastity; and if he does this, it is neither good nor evil —; so God gave them, scattered among the nations, precepts not good — that is, he gave them up to their own thoughts and desires —, so that they did what is not fitting; and he defiled them in their gifts, like a priest separating the lepers from the people and declaring them to be defiled, while they offer to idols what they ought to offer to God; and they make everything that opens the womb, that is, the firstborn, pass through the fire to Baal, so that, after they have been abandoned by God and handed over to the worship of idols, they may then understand that he is the Lord whom by their own fault they roused to anger. Symmachus translated this passage more plainly, putting the future for the past: Therefore I too will give them precepts not good, and judgments on account of which they shall not live, and I will defile them on account of their gifts; because they consecrate and make pass over what opens the womb, that I may destroy them, and they may know that I am the Lord; and the sense is: 'Because I saw that the sons were equaling the crimes of their fathers and doing the same things for which those had offended God, I wished to divide them among the nations, and scatter them through the whole world, and give them precepts not good, and judgments in which they would not live, so as to defile them in their gifts, because they were consecrating all the firstborn to idols, and to destroy them forever, and that they might know that I am the Lord'. He shows that he did not give the precepts not good to those who were staying in the desert, but that to those whom he wished to scatter among the nations and make strangers throughout the whole world he had thought of giving what he did not give, so that there, by their own fault, they would make the good precepts of God not good, while they present to idols what God had commanded to be presented to himself.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","Wherefore speak to the house of Israel, son of man, and you shall say to them: Thus says the Lord God: Still in this too your fathers blasphemed me, when they had spurned me with contempt, and I had brought them into the land over which I lifted up my hand to give it to them; they saw every high hill and every leafy tree, and they sacrificed their victims there, and there they gave the provocation of their offering, and there they set their odor of sweetness and poured out their libations. And I said to them: What is the high place to which you go in? And its name was called 'high place' to this day. (LXX: \"Therefore speak to the house of Israel, son of man, and you shall say to them: Thus says Adonai the Lord: Even to this your fathers provoked me to anger in their transgressions by which they fell away against me, and I brought them into the land over which I lifted up my hand to hand it over to them; and they saw every high hill and every leafy tree, and they sacrificed there to their gods and set the fury of their gifts, and set there the odor of their fragrance, and poured out there their libations. And I said to them: What is ἀβανὰ [abana, a transliteration], that you go in there? And they called its name ἀβανὰ to this present day.\") 'I wished', he says, 'to scatter them among all the nations when they sinned in the wilderness and to give them precepts not good, so that they would sacrifice to idols what they ought to have offered to me, and consecrate to them all their firstborn through fire, so that I might kill them and wipe them out'. — When he says 'I wished', he shows that he did not do what he wished. And what follows: They shall know that I am the Lord, is not found in the Seventy; for it did not seem to them consistent that after the slaying they should know that he is the Lord —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.20.25":["And Ezekiel, in the person of God: \"I led out\", he says, \"them\", that is, the people of the Jews, \"from the land of Egypt: and I led them into the desert: and I gave them my precepts, and showed them my justifications: which a man shall do, and live in them\" (Ezek. 20:10). But concerning that law which works wrath, to which the Apostle too died, he adds afterward: \"And I gave them precepts that were not good, and justifications in which they shall not live in them\" (ibid., 25).\n— Commentary on Galatians","But let us not think that to live in them are the words of the Apostle, but of the prophet Ezekiel, who says: \"And I led them out into the desert, and I gave them my precepts, and showed them my ordinances, which a man shall do, and shall live in them\" (Ezek. 20:10, 11). And when he had said that those who had walked in the precepts and ordinances live, he added: \"And I gave them precepts not good, and ordinances in which they should not live in them\" (ibid., 25). How much care in the words! Where he said: \"I gave them precepts and ordinances in which they should live in them\", he did not add \"good\". But where he put \"in which they should not live in them\", he added: \"And I gave them precepts not good, and ordinances in which they shall not live in them\". But these things more fully in Ezekiel: now let us return to the order of the Epistle.\n— Commentary on Galatians","In another way, however, we shall affirm that the weak and needy elements are the traditions of the Jews and the base understanding according to the letter, which are statutes that are not good and precepts that are not good. For the spiritual understanding of the Law is strong and rich (Ezek. 20), so that either it ought not to be called an element at all, or it is indeed an element by comparison with the age to come and with the life in Christ Jesus, by which the angels and the heavenly powers now live. But compared with the Jewish sense, it should be called not so much an element, that is, a beginning, as perfection.\n— Commentary on Galatians","This too can be said: that before the offense they received only the Decalogue, but after the idolatry and blasphemy the manifold ceremonies of the law, so that they would rather offer to God the victims which they were offering to demons, and, by comparison with sacrilege, what was not good in itself would become lighter: by no means evil, because they were offered to God, and yet not good, because they had offended the author of good.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.20.27-29":["'But you, son of man, speak again to them, that is, to the elders of the house of Israel who came to inquire of you: Your fathers, from whose stock you are descended, blasphemed me in this too and held me for nothing: after I had brought them into the land, which they possessed by my strength, which I had given them to possess, they turned it to provoking me; for when they had seen every high hill and leafy tree, they sacrificed on the mountains and in the groves and in the woodlands, and sacrificed victims to idols, and poured out offerings; and when I saw this, I said to them: What is 'bama' — for this means 'high place' —, or why do you go into such a place, which you have chosen for yourselves on all the hills, so that to this day places of this kind are called 'bamoth' and the ancient error keeps its former name?'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.20.29":["For 'bama', which we rendered as 'high place', an error has prevailed in the edition of the Seventy, so that ἀβανὰ is written, which means nothing in the Hebrew language; 'bama', if you divide its two syllables into two words, can mean 'in what', but that sense does not fit the present passage; but wherever it is written in the books of Kings and Chronicles: Still the people burned incense and sacrificed on the high place, 'bama' in the singular and 'bamoth' in the plural mean 'high places'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.20.30-31":["Therefore say to the house of Israel: Thus says the Lord God: You are defiled in the way of your fathers, and you commit fornication after their stumbling-blocks, and in the offering of your gifts, when you make your sons pass through the fire, you are defiled with all your idols to this day; and shall I answer you, house of Israel? As I live, says the Lord God, I will not answer you. 'You imitate the vices of your fathers and walk by the same ways, so that like offenses deserve a like punishment too; and you have advanced so far in crimes that you consecrate your sons to demons through fire, nor is it enough to have done this once, but even at present you do the same things; not that the elders in captivity do these things, but that those who were staying in Jerusalem and over whom captivity was hanging do not cease to do all these things. And when you are bound by such great outrages, do you seek', he says, 'my answer? As I live, says the Lord, and I swear by my own self, I will not answer you'. And what follows:\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.20.32":["Nor shall the thought of your mind come to pass, of you who say: We will be like the nations and like the families of the earth, to worship wood and stones. As I live, says the Lord God, with a strong hand and with an outstretched arm and with fury poured out I will reign over you, has this sense: 'Do not think that your thoughts, by which you blaspheme against me, can be fulfilled; for you say: We do not wish to be under the Lord nor to be called his people, but, as all the nations in the whole world are, and each people lives by its own will, so as to worship wood and stones and serve idols, we too will be one nation out of many'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.20.33":["To which God replies, and swears by himself, and says: 'I will not leave you nor despise you, as negligent masters are accustomed to despise runaway slaves, but I will drag you back to my rule, and with arm outstretched and striking I will reduce you to your former servitude and reign over you, so that, whether you will or not, you may have me as king, and may feel as an angry king him whose clemency you neglected'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.20.34":["And I will lead you out from the peoples, and gather you from the lands in which you are scattered; with a mighty hand, and an outstretched arm, and with fury poured out I will reign over you. 'I will not suffer you', he says, 'to be among the nations nor to be held in eternal captivity, but I will lead you out from the peoples and gather you from the lands into which hostile necessity has scattered you here and there to be slaves; and I will do these things not to destroy you and wipe you out, but to be your king'. From this we understand that even what the heretics call the cruelty of the Creator sounds of clemency, since he is angry and rages and pours out all his fury for this purpose: to draw back to his kingdom those who had chosen the tyranny of demons, and that to be its slaves.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.20.35-37":["And I will bring you into the desert of the peoples, and there I will enter into judgment with you face to face. As I contended in judgment against your fathers in the desert of the land of Egypt, so will I judge you, says the Lord, and I will subject you to my scepter, and bring you into the bonds of the covenant. And I will pick out from you the transgressors and the ungodly, and I will lead them out of the land of their sojourning, and they shall not enter the land of Israel, and you shall know that I am the Lord. 'I will do', he says, 'to you who are in Babylon and now serve idols what I did to your fathers in Egypt: I will lead you out into the desert of the peoples and there enter into judgment with you face to face, as I contended in judgment against them when they went out of Egypt; and after I have judged you, I will subject you to my scepter and rule, and enter into a covenant with you, and bring you into your land in the bonds of love, so that, bound by my love, you may by no means be able to depart from me.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.20.38":["But the transgressors and the ungodly, who in hardness of heart persevere in evil works, I will pick out from you, not to possess them but to cast them away; and I will indeed lead them out of the land of their sojourning, so that, once led out, they may not enter the land of Israel but perish in diverse regions, and that, by the distinction of the good and the evil, you may know that I am the Lord who do all things with judgment'. The discourse hastens on to what remains, and we run briefly through each point, so as to offer readers only the sense.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.20.39-40":["And you, house of Israel, thus says the Lord God: Walk each of you after your idols, and serve them. But if in this too you do not listen to me, and pollute my holy name any more with your gifts and with your idols. (LXX: \"And you, house of Israel, thus says Adonai the Lord: Let each one take away his inventions; and after this, if you listen to me, and do not pollute my holy name any more with your gifts and with your pursuits.\") Symmachus translated this passage more plainly: Each of you, go and serve your idols, since you would not listen to me. But my holy name do not pollute any more through your gifts and images. But on my holy mountain, on the high mountain of Israel, says the Lord God, there all the house of Israel shall serve me. Symmachus' translation has this meaning: 'Since you do not wish to serve me, go, serve idols and tread in the footsteps of your images; for you do not wish to listen to me: by no means sacrifice victims to me, nor burn incense, nor be called my people — for my worshipers will by no means serve me in groves and shrines and the places of idols, as you do, but on my holy mountain, on the high mountain of Israel —'. The Seventy, for their part, unfold a sense of their own: 'Abandon your former thoughts, and take away the evil sins of your inventions; and if after this you listen to me and do not pollute my name with your gifts and images, then on my holy mountain you shall offer me victims and serve me, all the house of Israel'. Aquila, however, agreeing in part with Symmachus, disagrees in part: Go, he says, after your idols and serve them, since you are unworthy of my rule, nor does your worship delight me; but if in this too you by no means listen to me, but pollute my holy name, while you pretend to offer to me what you offer to idols, and do such blasphemy to my name that, being worshipers of idols, you say that you are mine, know this: that on my holy mountain, on the high mountain of Israel, there all the house of Israel shall serve me, not you who serve idols, but all the house of Israel which is afterward to believe.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.20.40-42":["On my holy mountain, on the high mountain of Israel, says the Lord God, there all the house of Israel shall serve me: all, I say, in the land in which they shall please me; and there I will require your firstfruits and the beginning of your tithes in all your sanctifications. In an odor of sweetness I will receive you, when I have led you out from the peoples and gathered you from the lands into which you were scattered, and I shall be sanctified in you in the eyes of the nations. And you shall know that I am the Lord. Of this mountain, on which all the house of Israel is to serve God, Isaiah and Micah sang with one voice: In the last days the mountain of the house shall be manifest on the summit of the mountains and shall be raised above the hills; and all the nations shall flow to it, and many peoples shall go and say: Come, let us go up to the mountain of the Lord and to the house of the God of Jacob, and he will teach us his ways and we will walk in his paths; for out of Zion shall go forth the law, and the word of the Lord from Jerusalem; and again Isaiah: Go up onto a high mountain, he says, you who proclaim to Zion; lift up and exalt your voice, you who bring good tidings to Jerusalem. This mountain, Zion, we understand either as the Church, which means 'watchtower' and is set on the height of holy doctrines, or as the Lord Savior himself, in whom the firstfruits and the tithes and all worship are turned into an odor of sweetness, so that all the nations round about, seeing that the people of the Lord has been saved, may glorify God, and those who have been saved may know that he is the Lord.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.20.42":["When I bring you into the land of Israel, into the land for which I lifted up my hand to give it to your fathers. 'Then you shall know that I am the Lord, when I bring you', he says, 'into the land of Israel, for which I lifted up my hand to bring your fathers into it, which they lost by their own fault and which you have received back not so much by your own merit as by my clemency'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.20.43":["And you shall remember your ways and all your crimes, with which you were defiled in them; and you shall be displeasing to yourselves in your own sight in all your wickednesses which you have done. And you shall know that I am the Lord, when I have done good to you for my name's sake, not according to your evil ways nor according to your most wicked crimes, house of Israel, says the Lord God. We cannot remember our crimes and vices unless we have been brought into the land of Israel and, set there, say with the Apostle: I who am not worthy to be called an apostle, because I persecuted the Church of God. And you shall be displeasing, he says, to yourselves in your own sight in all your wickednesses which you have done (or, as Symmachus translated: And you shall seem little to yourselves because of all your wickednesses which you have wrought), so that, after they have been exalted, they may believe themselves to be lowly, because: The Lord gives grace to the humble, and in another place it is written: Before crushing the heart of a man is exalted, and before height it is humbled: for crushing follows pride, and exaltation follows humility.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.20.44":["And you shall know that I am the Lord, when I have done good to you: and we came to know the Lord Savior the more at that time when he did good to us and suffered for our sins and bore our iniquities and grieved for us, not for any merit of those who are saved but for his name's sake; otherwise our ways and most wicked crimes deserved not mercy but punishment. But whatever we have said of the people of Israel, which, freed from Egypt, committed many things in the wilderness and offended God, and afterward, brought into the land of promise, venerated idols and worshiped wood and stones, and afterward was preserved by the clemency of God, let us refer to those who have been freed from the Egypt of this world by the high hand of the Lord and, brought into the wilderness of vices, sighed again for Egypt and did the things for which they ought to have been punished, but nonetheless, through repentance, not by their own merit but by the clemency of the Lord, have been preserved.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.20.46-48":["And beautifully, at the beginning: Drip, he says, toward the southwest, so that not the whole wrath of God may seem to be poured out, but a kind of drip and a part. But if there is such savagery in a drip, what must be reckoned of whole downpours, so that all flesh, which is to see the salvation of God, may know through the burning of the woods and through the flame that is extinguished by no one's help that he himself is the Lord?\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","Tropologically, however, Theman and Nageb and Darom can be understood as Egypt; for we read in Daniel that the south is frequently taken for Egypt; and Egypt refers to the straits of this world. Ezekiel therefore prophesies the evils that are to come upon the world, which he calls a forest, one that has no fruit-bearing trees but is a dwelling-place of beasts, of which it is also said in Psalm 28 (29): The voice of the Lord perfecting the stags, and he will uncover the thickets of the woods. These are the woods and forests that devoured more of Absalom's army in the battle than the sword killed. And in the forests the green wood is kindled first, and then the dry: those who live in evil, and those who are dead to righteousness. And what is said, And every face shall be burned in it from the south to the north, says this: from those who seemed to be fervent in spirit to those who, as iniquity increased and the love of many grew cold, have lost their former warmth, so that all flesh may perceive that the flame of the Lord is not extinguished.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.20.46":["And the word of the Lord came to me, saying: Son of man, set your face against the way of the south, and drip toward the southwest, and prophesy to the forest of the southern field. And you shall say to the southern forest: Hear the word of the Lord: Thus says the Lord God: Behold, I will kindle a fire in you, and I will burn up in you all green wood and all dry wood; the flame of the kindling shall not be extinguished, and every face shall be burned in it from the south to the north: and all flesh shall see that I the Lord have kindled it, and it shall not be extinguished. And I said: Ah, ah, ah, Lord God; they say of me: Does not this man speak in parables? (LXX: \"And the word of the Lord came to me, saying: Son of man, make firm your face upon Theman, and look toward Darom, prophesy upon the forest, the leader Nageb. And you shall say to the forest Nageb: Hear the speech of the Lord: Thus says the Lord: Behold, I will kindle a fire in you, and it shall devour in you all green wood and all dry wood; the kindled flame shall not be extinguished, and every face shall burn in it from the midday to the north: and all flesh shall know that I the Lord have kindled it; it shall not be extinguished. And I said: By no means, Lord, Lord; they say to me: Is not this which is spoken a parable?\") What Set (or make firm) your face means, we have said more fully above; for there is need of firmness of countenance and hardness of brow, so that the prophet may speak fearlessly the things he is commanded, above all when sad things must be announced to the whole multitude of the people. Now what 'Theman' and 'Darom' and 'Nageb', and again 'Nageb', for which the Seventy translated ἀπηλιὼτην [Apeliotes, the east wind], signify in the Hebrew, our translation has expressed. And as for their saying 'leader Nageb', the Hebrew has it better, 'of the field' or 'of the southern region'; for what is written is not 'sare', as the Seventy supposed, a word that means 'leader' and 'prince', but 'sade', which properly signifies 'field' and 'region'; and the error grew up because of the likeness of the letters 'daleth' [d] and 'res' [r].\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.20.47":["The divine word, then, speaks through the μεταφορὰν [metaphor] of a forest against Jerusalem, which is a dwelling of beasts and of fierce men, saying that he will set it on fire and that all its wood will be burned up together; by no means does he call trees those that were lacking in fruit, but wood, as if made ready for burning. And first he burns in it the green wood, according to what we read in this same prophet: And begin from my holy ones, and afterward the dry, which could have nothing living in itself; that is, the saints and the sinners together, so that some by captivity may escape an evil death, while others are handed over to eternal punishments. And what he adds, From the south to the north, indicates this: from Jerusalem to Babylon, so that the whole road of those going into captivity may be filled with those falling by sword, famine and pestilence; for to those who are in Babylon, Jerusalem lies to the south, just as, conversely, the pot of Jeremiah, which signifies Jerusalem, is kindled from the face of the north, that is, of Babylon.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.20.49":["Understanding this, the prophet answered: Ah, ah, ah, Lord God (or, as the Seventy translated: By no means, Lord), and he adds: They say to me: Does not this man speak in parables? (or: Is this a parable which is spoken?), and the sense is: 'Speak more plainly; we do not understand what you are saying in a parable; open to us the meaning of God in plain speech'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","And the prophet prays that what the Lord has threatened may not come to pass, that is, that the forest may not be set on fire and all the wood burned up together, so that either they may still receive room for repentance, or the necessity of announcing sad things may not be laid upon him, especially since the people do not understand them and are provoked rather to madness by the obscurity of the sayings.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.21.2-4":["And the word of the Lord came to me, saying: Son of man, set your face toward Jerusalem, and distil toward the sanctuaries, and prophesy against the ground of Israel. And you shall say to the land of Israel: Thus says the Lord God: Behold, I am against you, and I will cast out my sword from its sheath, and I will kill in you the righteous and the wicked. But because I have killed in you the righteous and the wicked, my sword shall go forth from its sheath against all flesh, from the south to the north, and all flesh shall know that I the Lord have drawn my sword from its sheath, irrevocable. (LXX: \"And the word of the Lord came to me, saying: Therefore prophesy, son of man, and set firm your face against Jerusalem, and look toward their holy places, and prophesy over the land of Israel. And you shall say to the land of Israel: Thus says the Lord God: Behold, I am against you, and I will draw out my sword from its sheath, and I will slay out of you the lawless and the unrighteous. Because I will destroy out of you the lawless and the unrighteous, so shall my sword go forth from its sheath upon all flesh, from the south as far as the north, and all flesh shall know that I the Lord have drawn my sword from its sheath; it shall not return any more.\") Because he had said above: They say of me: Does not this man speak in parables?, and the people were demanding a plain statement, therefore the Lord now says more plainly what he had spoken through a metaphor, or 'parable', and, as others rendered it, 'proverb': that the forests of Nageb and Darom and Theman [Hebrew words for the south] are Jerusalem and its temple and the Holy of Holies and the whole land of Judea, and that the flame which was to burn up the forest is to be understood as the devouring sword, which has been drawn from its sheath to kill the righteous and the wicked — for this is the green wood and the dry; whence the Lord also says: If they do such things, he says, in the green wood, what will they do in the dry? —; in place of which the Seventy, intending I know not what, translated 'the lawless and the unrighteous', as though both did not signify the same thing.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.21.5":["And what he had said there: And all flesh shall see that I the Lord have kindled it, that is, the forest or the flame, and it shall not be quenched, here he says in other words: That all flesh may know that I the Lord have drawn my sword from its sheath, irrevocable. For truly, for Jerusalem the flame has not been lulled to sleep, nor the fire quenched, nor the sword recalled, because there was a short time in between, and Jerusalem, with its temple, was set ablaze by Babylonian fire.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.21.6-7":["And you, son of man, groan in the breaking of the loins, and in bitternesses groan before them. And when they say to you: Why do you groan?, you shall say: For the report, because it comes, and every heart shall melt, and all hands shall be loosened, and every spirit shall grow weak, and waters shall flow down all knees; behold, it comes, and it shall be done, says the Lord. (LXX: \"And you, son of man, groan in the breaking of your loins, and in griefs groan in their sight. And it shall be, if they say to you: Why do you groan?, then you shall say: Because of the tidings, because it comes, and every heart shall be seized, and all hands shall be loosened, and all flesh and every spirit shall be tested, and all thighs shall be defiled with moisture; behold, it comes, and it shall be, says the Lord God.\") First he had said: 'Prophesy, or distil, toward the south, the southwest and the noonday, and toward the southern forest'. Because this seemed obscure, and the people did not understand the prophet's words, in the second place it is set down more plainly that the southern forest is Jerusalem, and that all the unfruitful trees, at whose roots the axe has been laid, are to be understood as its inhabitants, and that the sword is to be interpreted in place of the fire. In the third place the prophet is commanded that, since they are silent and do not ask why he has prophesied these things, he should do something by which he may be questioned, and should answer what the Lord has spoken. 'Groan', he says, 'and wail, not with a light voice nor with moderate grief, but in the breaking of the loins, so that your groaning may be brought forth from your inmost bowels and from bitterness of soul; and you shall do this before them so that, when they have asked you why you are crushed with such great groaning and what evil has befallen you that you groan so, you may answer them with my word: This is why I lament and cannot hide the grief of my heart, because the report which had always sounded in my ears is being fulfilled in deed, and it comes — the menace, that is, of the raging Babylonian army; and when it has come and has hemmed Jerusalem in with a rampart, then every heart shall melt and all hands shall be loosened, so that, with panic seizing men's minds, no one dares to resist; for the spirit of all the warriors shall grow weak, and such trembling and dread shall seize them all that, under the compulsion of fear, their limbs and bowels are loosened and the bladder cannot hold its moisture, so that their knees are defiled with urine' — for it is natural that, under the compulsion of fear, the bladder is relaxed and, against a man's will, the moisture flows down; which indeed also tends to happen in the severity of illnesses, that, after the strength of the sick has failed, not only the knees but even the beds are defiled —. 'Behold', he says, 'it comes, what I have often foretold and what is being fulfilled in deed, and it shall be done, which not I but the Lord has spoken'. I know that a certain man unrolled many testimonies on the breaking of the loins and the flowing waters, so that the prophet's chastity might be made out, and the bitterness of soul and the inward pain of mind, namely that the waters of seed defile the knees; but this exposition does not belong to the present passage.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.21.9-11":["And the word of the Lord came to me, saying: Son of man, prophesy, and you shall say: Thus says the Lord God: Speak. The sword, the sword is sharpened and filed; it is sharpened to slaughter victims, and it is filed that it may gleam: you who move the scepter of my son, you have cut down every tree. And I have given it to be polished, that it may be held in the hand; this sword is sharpened, and this one is filed, that it may be in the hand of the slayer. Cry out and howl, son of man, because this one has come among my people, this one among all the leaders of Israel who had fled; they have been handed over to the sword with my people, therefore clap upon your thigh, because it has been proved, and this when it has overturned the scepter and it shall be no more, says the Lord God. You therefore, son of man, prophesy, and strike hand against hand, and let the sword be doubled, and let the sword of the slain be tripled. This is the sword of great slaughter, which makes them stand aghast, and melt in heart, and multiplies the ruins. In all their gates I have set the confusion of the sword, sharpened and filed to flash, clad for slaughter. Be sharpened, go to the right or to the left, wherever the desire of your face is. Moreover I too will clap hand against hand, and I will fulfill my indignation; I the Lord have spoken. (It is too much if I set down both editions, and the length of the books is drawn out, especially where there is either no difference or a small one; I will therefore set down some of the things that disagree. Where we said: You who move the scepter of my son, you have cut down every tree, they translated: Kill, despise, reject every tree; again, where we said: Who had fled, have been handed over to the sword, both they and others translated: My guests, or inhabitants; and where we said: Clap upon the thigh, they put: Strike the hand; and where we translated: And this when it has overturned the scepter, their edition has: The tribe has been rejected.) He speaks, moreover, to the sword of Nebuchadnezzar, that it should come against Jerusalem and be sharpened and filed, for many must be slaughtered; and he makes an apostrophe to it: 'You are the one who moves and overturns the scepter of my son, namely the rule of the people of Israel, and you cut down every tree, the whole multitude of the people. I have given', he says, 'this sword to be held in the hand of Nebuchadnezzar, and to be in the hand of a most powerful man.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.21.9-17":["According to the ἀναγωγὴν [anagogy, the higher sense] we can take the sharpened sword, prepared for slaughter, as the devil, of whom the Apostle says: To hand over such a one to Satan for the destruction of the flesh, that the spirit may be saved; and in another place: Hymenaeus and Alexander, whom I have handed over to Satan, that they may learn not to blaspheme; and in the Psalms it is written: He sent fury and wrath and distress, a sending by most evil angels. Some think that this sword was also in the hand of the angel when Jerusalem was struck under David. Others, however, suspect that it is also said of that sword of which it is written in the Gospel: 'I came not to send peace upon the earth, but a sword, and to divide among themselves every bond of kinship, so that the wicked may fall and the saints rise again'. But it is impious to believe this of the Savior's sword, and not rather of the devil, who overturns the scepters of the Lord in the Church, who slays all among the people, both the leaders and the guests of God, and is then approved by the Lord, when he has overturned Judas the traitor and those like him; into whom, after he had eaten the morsel, Satan entered. And the sword of the slain is doubled and tripled, so that the sin of Jerusalem may be loosed, because she has received from the hand of the Lord her sins twofold. The Lord, moreover, rejoices and exults, and is the exhorter of the raging sword, that it may go either to the right or to the left, and wherever the desire of its face carries it, so that, the wicked being slain, the good may remain, and that which is written may be fulfilled: What has chaff to do with wheat?, says the Lord.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.21.12-13":["Therefore, O prophet, cry out and howl, and bear witness to the impending evils with the lamentation of your voice. This sword has spared no one, nor left anyone without a share in the impending evils. It has come among the people, it has come among the leaders of Israel, it has come among my guests and the priests who dwelt in my temple. Therefore strike your thigh, or your hand, and take on the bearing of a mourner. For this sword has been proved by me, and this when it has overturned my scepter and kingdom, which shall be no more and shall come to its end in King Zedekiah.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.21.14-15":["Again I command you, prophet, that you, who had struck your thigh in astonishment and wonder, should strike your hands also, so that not only once but a second and a third time the sword may come to slay'. For Nebuchadnezzar had come the first time when he carried off Jeconiah, and the second time when Jehoiakim reigned, the third time when Zedekiah did. 'And this is the tripled sword, when a great slaughter took place, and it multiplied the ruins to such a degree that they were stunned in mind and melted in heart, and it caused confusion in all their gates'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.21.16-17":["It is commanded therefore to it, that is, to the sword sharpened and filed to flash, clad and ready and prepared for slaughter, that it go to the right if it so wishes, or to the left. 'Wherever', he says, 'it pleases your face, enter boldly, having me as your leader and helper. I will clap hand against hand, so that, as your patron and exhorter, I may spur you on as you rage against my adversaries. Do not hesitate, do not dread and fear to suffer what Sennacherib suffered; I the Lord have spoken; you are the minister of my will, carry out my sentence'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.21.18-20":["'When', he says, 'the divine word had said to me: Prophesy, son of man, and speak to the sword, and say: The sword, the sword, and the rest that the prophecy of the sword contains, a second time the word of the Lord came to me, saying: Do you wish, son of man, to know who this sword is, and to learn more openly the person of the raging sword? Listen to what I say: Set two ways, so that by them the sword of the king of Babylon may come, who will indeed go forth by the one road of the Chaldeans but, when he has come through the desert and the wilderness to the fork of the land of Arabia which is called that of the Ammonites, of which one way leads to Jerusalem on the right-hand side, but the left to Rabbath of the Ammonites' — which is a metropolis and today is named Philadelphia.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.21.19-24":["And the word of the Lord came to me, saying: And you, son of man, set for yourself two ways, and let the sword of the king of Babylon come: both shall go forth from one land; and with his hand he shall take a conjecture, at the head of the way of the city he shall cast it. You shall set a way for the sword to come to Rabbath of the Ammonites, and to Judah, into Jerusalem the most fortified. For the king of Babylon stood at the fork, at the head of the two ways, seeking divination, mixing arrows; he questioned idols, he consulted entrails. At his right hand the divination came out against Jerusalem, to set battering rams, to open the mouth in slaughter, to lift up the voice in howling, to set battering rams against the gates, to heap up a mound, to build siege works. And in their eyes he shall be as one consulting an oracle in vain, and imitating the idleness of the sabbaths; but he himself will remember the iniquity, so as to capture. Therefore thus says the Lord God: Because you have called your iniquities to remembrance, and have uncovered your transgressions, and your sins have appeared in all your thoughts; because, I say, you have called them to remembrance, you shall be taken by the hand. I know that in this place, following the Seventy translators, I have read a manifold exposition, which refers Rabbath of the Ammonites to the people of the nations, but Judah and Jerusalem to those who are gathered under the name of Christ and are given by their deeds the dignity of the name; and holds that the king of confusion, the devil, stands at every head of the ways and lies in ambush in secret, and that that little verse is fulfilled: Beside the path they have set a stumbling block for me; and that he stands at the fork, desiring either to hold those who are his own or to acquire new servitude for himself, and always goes to the right, namely to those who are placed on the right side, to storm them, to exult in slaughter, to capture wretched Jerusalem with battering rams and the heaping up of earthly works; and that at first indeed he seems to her to do nothing and to strive in vain, but, as he recalls the iniquities of those who dwell in the Church — or rather, by their evil works they bring the adverse powers back to the remembrance of their former crimes —, all the sins of the people and their thoughts are laid bare, and therefore they are captured, because they have heaped new offenses upon old ones. Another may have said these things; we must pursue the truth of the history we have begun.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.21.21-22":["'Mindful', he says, 'of the slaughter that befell the Assyrian king when a hundred and eighty-five thousand were cut down in one night, he will be afraid to turn aside to the right-hand side and go against most fortified Jerusalem, but he will stand at the very crossroads and, according to the rite of his nation, will consult the oracle, putting arrows into the quiver and mixing them, inscribed or marked with the names of each, to see whose arrow comes out and which city he ought to attack first' — this the Greeks call βελομαντίαν [belomancy, divination by arrows] or ῥαβδομαντίαν [rhabdomancy, divination by rods] —. 'He questioned the idols, therefore, he consulted the entrails. To the right came his divination, that he should go against Jerusalem and attack it, heap up mounds, set battering rams, build siege works and shut in the city, and in slaughter open his mouth and rouse the roar of an exulting and howling army.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.21.23-24":["Doing this', he says, 'he will seem to the inhabitants of Jerusalem to consult the oracle in vain, and as it were to wear away the idleness of the sabbaths, namely to play and accomplish no work. But the king of Babylon will trust not in his own strength but in the iniquity of the people, by which he knows that they have offended God and have heaped up the sins of their fathers with new offenses, and that their transgressions have been laid open to all; and therefore he will not doubt of victory, because he will trust in the iniquity of Jerusalem'. This is according to the Hebrew, from which the Seventy disagree in most places, not so much in sense as in words.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.21.25":["But you, profane, wicked leader of Israel, whose day has come, foreordained in the time of iniquity, thus says the Lord God: Take off the miter, take away the crown; is not this that which raised up the lowly and brought low the lofty? Iniquity, iniquity, iniquity I will set it; and this shall not be until he comes whose is the judgment, and I will deliver it to him. (LXX: \"And you, profane, lawless leader of Israel, whose day has come in the time of iniquity, the end, thus says the Lord God: You have taken off the miter and put on the crown; this shall not be alike; you have humbled the lofty and raised up the lowly. Iniquity, iniquity I will set it; nor shall this be such, until he comes to whom it is due, and I will give it to him.\") Symmachus translated this passage thus: But you, profane, lawless leader of Israel, whose day has come, appointed in the time of iniquity, thus says the Lord God: He has taken off the miter and taken away the crown; neither this nor that; he has raised up the lowly and brought low the lofty. Iniquity, iniquity, iniquity I will make it; and this was not he whose is the judgment, whom I am going to give. After the overthrow of the city of Jerusalem and the captivity of the people, the word is directed to the leader of Israel, whom no one doubts to signify Zedekiah, in whom the rule of the kings of Israel from the stock of David came to an end. 'There has come to you', he says, 'the day which was foreordained against you long ago, and because of you the priesthood and the kingdom of the Jewish people perished'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.21.26-27":["In the place where we translated: Is not this, Symmachus rendered beautifully: Neither this nor that; for because he had said: Take off the miter, take away the crown, he added: 'Neither this nor that is', that is: 'Both the kingdom and the priesthood shall cease'. Hence, of those who afterwards, until the coming of Christ, were kings and priests alike, one, Hyrcanus the high priest, placed a diadem on his own head and wished in vain to claim both this and that for himself, since the kingdom was not owed to him after Zedekiah, but to him for whom it was laid up and who was the expectation of the nations; of whom Malachi also speaks: You priests, who defile my name, and a little after: My will is not in you, and I will not accept victims from your hands. For from the rising of the sun to its setting my name is great among the nations, and in every place sacrifice is made and a pure offering is offered to my name; which pure offering, without the blood of goats, rams and bulls, is fulfilled at the coming of Christ, when he who is desired by the nations has come, and the sun of righteousness has risen, in whose wings is healing.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.21.26":["For the miter is the insignia of the high priest; the crown, that is, the diadem, is the mark of a king. 'Is not this the miter and this the crown, which did nothing by judgment but, in imitation of the Babylonian king: Whom he wished he exalted, and whom he wished he humbled?\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.21.27":["Therefore, not once nor — according to the Seventy — twice, but a third time I will set iniquity upon you forever, which was not reckoned to you at once, but until Christ comes, whose is the judgment, and the Father delivers to him the kingdom and the priesthood, or the Church gathered from the nations'. For the Father judges no one, but has given all judgment to the Son; and in another place: O God, he says, give your judgment to the king, and your righteousness to the king's son; this is he for whom the everlasting rule and priesthood are laid up, of whom Jacob also spoke: A prince shall not fail from Judah, nor a leader from his thighs, until he comes for whom it is laid up; and he shall be the expectation of the nations.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.21.28-31":["And you, son of man, prophesy and say: Thus says the Lord God, to the Ammonites and to their reproach; and you shall say: Blade, blade, unsheathe yourself to slay, file yourself that you may kill and flash, while vain things were seen for you and lies were divined, that you might be set upon the necks of the wounded wicked, whose day has come, foreordained in the time of iniquity; return to your sheath, to the place in which you were created; in the land of your birth I will judge you, and I will pour out my indignation upon you, in the fire of my fury I will blow upon you, and I will give you into the hands of foolish men and of those who fashion destruction. You shall be food for the fire, your blood shall be in the midst of the land; you shall be given over to oblivion, because I the Lord have spoken. That the sword of the king of Babylon stood at the head of two ways, at the crossroads of the right and the left, of which one led to Jerusalem, the other to Rabbath of the Ammonites, and that the lot came out that he should go first against Jerusalem, we have read above; and, now that it has been stormed and taken, he foretells that its kingdom also and its priesthood have perished forever. What remained concerned the Ammonites, and the very order of the division called for it: what had become of the left-hand way. Hence the prophet is commanded to speak to the Ammonites and to their reproach, that they themselves are to be captured, and he directs his speech to the same sword: 'O blade, blade, you who are prepared for slaughter, who are filed so that you may flash and kill, although the idols have given you answers, and all that is answered by demons is vain, so that you might, namely, hang over the necks of the wounded and that through you might be fulfilled what the Lord had long threatened, nevertheless, once your work is completed which you did against the Ammonites, return to your sheath, that is, into Babylon, to the place in which you were made and created, so that in the land of your birth I may judge you and pour out my indignation upon you, and you may be captured by the might of the Medes and Persians'; which is said more fully in the volume of Isaiah, in the vision against Babylon: Behold, I will stir up the Medes against them; and a little after: And that Babylon, glorious among kingdoms, renowned in the pride of the Chaldeans, shall be as God overthrew Sodom and Gomorrah. It shall not be inhabited to the end.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.21.31-32":["And because he was once speaking under the person of the sword about the king, or rather the kingdom, of Babylon, he keeps the μεταφορὰν [metaphor]. 'In the fire', he says, 'of my fury I will blow upon you, so as to have you consumed by fire; and I will hand you over to the hands of foolish and unskilled men, who have not the art of forging and sharpening swords, so that you may by no means be sharpened any more, be filed and flash to kill, but may be food for the fire, and your blood, which you shed with all looking on, may rather flow back upon you; and you shall be handed over to eternal oblivion; and may you perish forever, because I the Lord have spoken these things, for whom to have said is to have done'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.22.2-3":["And the word of the Lord came to me, saying: And you, son of man, will you not judge the city of bloods? And you shall show her all her abominations, and you shall say: Thus says the Lord God: A city shedding blood in her midst, so that her time may come; and which made idols against herself, so as to be polluted. By your blood, which has been shed by you, you have sinned; and in your idols which you made you are polluted; and you have made your days draw near, and you have brought on the time of your years. Therefore I have made you a reproach to the nations and a mockery to all lands. Those that are near and those that are far from you will triumph over you (or will mock you), filthy one, noble one, great in ruin. Behold, the princes of Israel, each in his own arm, have been in you to shed blood. Father and mother they have treated with insults in you (or they have cursed father and mother), the stranger they have falsely accused in your midst, the orphan and the widow they have grieved (or have oppressed) in you. My sanctuaries you have despised and my sabbaths you have polluted. Men who are detractors (or robbers) have been in you to shed blood, and upon the mountains they have eaten in you; wickedness (or, as the Hebrew has it more expressively, 'zemma', that is, incest) they have wrought in your midst. The more shameful parts of the father they have uncovered in you, and the uncleanness of the menstruous woman they have humbled in you; and each one has wrought abomination with his neighbor's wife, and the father-in-law has wickedly defiled his daughter-in-law; the brother has oppressed (or humbled) his sister, his father's daughter, in you. They have taken gifts in you to shed blood; usury and excess you have taken, and greedily you have falsely accused your neighbors (or and I will fill up the consummation of your malice in your oppression), and you have forgotten me, says the Lord God. Behold, I have clapped my hands over your avarice which you have practiced, and over the blood which has been shed in your midst. Will your heart endure, or will your hands prevail, in the days which I shall make for you? I the Lord have spoken and I will do it; and I will scatter you among the nations, and I will winnow you into the lands, and I will make your uncleanness fail from you; and I will possess you in the sight of the nations (or, according to the first edition of Aquila and Theodotion, and I will defile you; or, according to Symmachus, and I will wound you in the sight of the nations), and you shall know that I am the Lord. 'And you', he says, 'son of man — for I will use the word I am accustomed to use with you —, judge the city of bloods and show her all her abominations, so that she may know that she deservedly endures the imminent captivity and that by many bloods she has brought on the time of her ruin.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.22.4-5":["First of all crimes, in place of God you have worshiped idols, and you have adored the works of your hands, and you have made the time of your years draw near, which for a long time was being announced and postponed, so that you might do penance. But according to your impenitent heart you treasure up wrath for yourself against the day of wrath. Therefore I have made you a reproach to the nations and a mockery to all lands that are round about you, both near and far, which will triumph over you, or mock you, filthy, noble, great in ruin: filthy, because you have ceased to be clean; noble in evil, you who once surpassed all cities in nobility in good; great in ruin, so that the more lofty you were, the more heavily you should fall.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.22.6-12":["And meanwhile, to pass over the rest, I will enumerate a few things that took place in you: your princes, judging not by righteousness but by the strength of their might and of their arm, shed innocent blood in you; others treated father and mother with insults, or cursed their parents, though Scripture says: Whoever curses father and mother shall die the death; the stranger and the sojourner they oppressed in your midst, as though sojourning and exile from their homeland were not enough for them in place of every distress; the orphan and the widow they grieved, or oppressed, in you, so that those whose whole protection lies in the justice of the laws were not only grieved but oppressed by you; my sanctuaries, or holy things, you despised, and my sabbaths you polluted, so that you did not distinguish between holy and polluted, between the day decreed for religion and the rest of the sabbath for the worship of God, and the other days on which it is permitted by the law to work and to serve the needs of the flesh; men who were 'detractors' — or, according to Symmachus and Theodotion, 'deceitful', which in the Hebrew is called 'rachil' — and murderers were in you, so as to blaspheme God or to do everything by deceit or violence; and they ate on the mountains, minding high things and lifted up against God in pride; wickedness, or incest, they wrought in your midst, so that what is rarely done even in corners and in secret, when the awareness of men is shunned by the wicked, this you did in the open — and what the incest is, the following passage indicates: They have laid bare the more shameful parts of the father in you, coupling with a stepmother against what is right and lawful, and they have humbled the uncleanness of the menstruous woman in you, not sparing nature nor granting a truce to their foulness, and neighing after their neighbor's wife; and, what is more wicked than this, the father-in-law burned for his daughter-in-law, and the brother knew nothing of a sister's rights, so as to violate a sister born not of the same womb but of the same father —; they took gifts so as to shed innocent blood; they turned the need of the poor into gain, so as to take usury and excess; and out of avarice they falsely accused their friends. This you have done', he says, 'O Jerusalem, because you have forgotten me; for the remembrance of God shuts out all shameful deeds.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.22.13-15":["Hence I have taken on the bearing of the Lord in anger and have clapped my hands against your avarice and against the blood that has been shed in your midst, so that you may understand and recognize in your mind whether you can endure my wrath either in spirit or with your hands; for I the Lord have spoken and I will do it, so that, after you have been captured, Jerusalem, I will scatter you among the nations and winnow you into the air like useless chaff to be swept this way and that.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.22.15-16":["And I am of such great clemency that my severity is turned into mercy; for I will do everything so that your uncleanness may fail from you and you may recover your former purity; and when you have recovered it, I will possess you in the sight of all the nations, so that, when you have been possessed by me, you may then understand that I myself am the Lord'. If, however, we follow the interpretation of Symmachus and Theodotion, of whom the one says: And I will wound you (or I will pierce you through), the other: I will defile you in the sight of the nations, everything must be referred to the bad sense, although what is said above is contrary to this sense: And I will make your uncleanness fail from you; for the failing of uncleanness is the restoration of purity. I have discussed both editions together, so as to avoid enlarging the books.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.22.18-22":["What we have understood of Jerusalem, let us understand of the state of souls, which were created pure by God and received gold in understanding and wisdom, and silver in speech and eloquence, so that what they have conceived in the mind they may unfold in words. Of silver we read: The utterances of the Lord are chaste utterances, silver tried by fire, proved for the earth, purged sevenfold. Of gold and silver alike Psalm 67 (68) speaks, in which it is written: If you sleep in the midst of the lots, the wings of a dove covered with silver, and the hinder parts (or the inner parts) of her back in the greenness (or paleness) of gold. All indeed the glory of the king's daughter is within, she who speaks in the Song of Songs: The king has brought me into his chamber. Whoever has slept and rested between the two testaments at once takes up the silvered wings of the dove, and will shine with the brightness of gold in the treasury of the heart. To this sense, I think, those passages too are to be referred: that for sinners and for those who have deserved God's displeasure the heaven is bronze and the earth iron, and above all the land of Egypt, out of which the Lord leads Israel as out of an iron furnace; in Zechariah, too, iniquity sits upon a talent of lead, and in the song of Exodus it is said: They sank like lead in the most violent water; for they were pressed down by the heavy weight of their sins and could say: Like a heavy burden they have weighed heavily upon me. And just as the counterfeit likeness of bronze feigns the appearance of gold, so tin imitates the whiteness of silver, which the simple and rustic man does not easily tell apart. And it often happens that gold and silver, mixed with heretical depravity, are separated by the fire of the Holy Spirit in the judgment of the Lord, and the gold remains pure, and the silver of which the prophet says: I gave them gold and silver, but they made Baal out of them, to which this is similar: I brought them out with silver and gold, and there was not a feeble one among their tribes. And let the end of all punishments be to know that he himself is the Lord. A tin stone, or a mass of tin, we read in Zechariah too; what it signifies we have explained in its place.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.22.18":["And the word of the Lord came to me, saying: Son of man, the house of Israel has been turned into dross for me; all these are bronze and tin and iron and lead in the midst of the furnace; they have become the dross of silver. Therefore thus says the Lord God: Because you have all been turned into dross, therefore behold, I will gather you into the midst of Jerusalem, with a gathering of silver and bronze and iron and tin and lead into the midst of the furnace, so that I may kindle a fire in it for smelting: so will I gather in my fury and in my anger, and I will rest, and I will smelt you, and I will kindle you in the fire of my fury, and you shall be smelted in the midst of it. As silver is smelted in the midst of the furnace, so shall you be in the midst of it; and you shall know that I am the Lord; that I have poured out my indignation upon you. The Hebrew word 'sig' Symmachus translated 'dross', the first edition of Aquila and Theodotion στέμφυλον and γίγαρτον, both of which signify the 'pip' of the pressed grape; but the second translation of the same Aquila and the Seventy rendered it 'mixture'. Now dross denotes the filth and refuse of metals; and accordingly, with captivity near, nay, with the destruction of the city already impending, fire is applied so that, under the metaphor of smelting, pure silver may remain, which had been mixed with and corrupted by bronze, tin, iron and lead; of these, three — bronze, tin and lead — are of a fusible substance and are melted by fire, while iron is softened and thinned between anvil and hammer and takes various forms, according as the will of the craftsman may be.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.22.19-22":["Just as, then, silver, which the metals mentioned above have corrupted, is put into the furnace so that, once the filth and the counterfeit substances have been removed, it may remain pure: 'so', he says, 'I will gather you into the midst of Jerusalem and surround you with the Babylonian siege, so that I may kindle a fire in you for smelting; and what the immense heat of the flames does there, famine and pestilence do here, so that, after I have gathered you and kindled you in the fire of my fury, I may then rest and quell the pain of being despised by the vengeance of your punishments; and all this I will do so that, after I have poured out my indignation upon you, the end of your torment may be the knowledge of me, and you shall know that I am the Lord, the judge and requiter of all'. That he is said to be a smelter we read in many other places too, but above all in Malachi and Isaiah, of whom the one says: Behold, he says, the Lord enters like the fire of a smelter and like the herb of the washers, and he will sit smelting as gold and silver, and he will smelt the sons of Levi, the other: And the Lord will wash away the filth of the sons and daughters of Zion, and he will cleanse the blood from their midst by the spirit of judgment and the spirit of burning; and I think that this is also the sense of what is said in the Psalms: Prove me, O Lord, and try me, after which is added: Burn my reins and my heart, so that all the phlegm of the harmful humor in my loins may be dried up.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.22.24":["And the word of the Lord came to me, saying: Son of man, say to her: You are a land unclean, and not rained upon in the day of fury. A conspiracy of prophets in her midst, like a lion roaring and seizing prey; they have devoured souls, they have taken wealth and price, they have multiplied her widows in her midst. Her priests have despised my law and have polluted my sanctuaries; between holy and profane they made no distinction, and between polluted and clean they did not discern, and from my sabbaths they turned away their eyes, and I was defiled in their midst. Her princes in her midst like wolves seizing prey, to shed blood and to destroy souls and greedily to pursue gains. And her prophets daubed them without tempering, seeing vain things and divining her falsehood, saying: Thus says the Lord God, when the Lord has not spoken. The people of the land they wronged with false accusation and plundered violently; the needy and the poor they afflicted, and the stranger they oppressed with false accusation without judgment. And I sought among them a man who would set up a wall and stand opposed against me for the land, so that I should not destroy it, and I did not find one. And I poured out my indignation upon them, in the fire of my anger I consumed them, I rendered their way upon their head, says the Lord God. A careful hearer might have asked what the dross was that violated the purity of the silver and mixed it with bronze, lead, tin and iron. What was expressed there, then, in the person of a city and a furnace is proclaimed here under the image of a land that has not been watered nor received rains — those rains of which it is written: I will command the clouds not to rain a shower upon it, the shower we await when the early and the late rain is given us, of which it is written: A voluntary rain you will set apart, O God, for your inheritance. And the day of fury is the one which each person procures for himself by the multitude of his sins.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.22.25-26":["But in our Jerusalem too we often see this: those who, according to the Seventy, devour souls in power and take a price, so as to make many widows who have lost the Lord, their bridegroom; and these prophets form a faction and a conspiracy, so as to be one another's supporters and to do everything for the sake of shameful gain; the priests, who ought to be the guardians of the temple and from whose mouth the knowledge of the law is sought, violate the sanctuaries and make no distinction between holy and profane except that of money; they turn their eyes away from the sabbaths and do not ponder the rest of God, which lies in the knowledge of the Scriptures, nor do they say with the prophet: Unveil my eyes, and I will consider the wonders of your law, nor that word of the Apostle: But we all with unveiled face behold the glory of the Lord. Among such prophets and priests the Lord, standing in their midst, is defiled, of whom it is written: In the midst of you stands one whom you do not know.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.22.25":["We wish to know what the dross of the city is, what the squalor of a land most hard and full of brambles and thorns. A conspiracy, he says, of prophets (or, according to the Seventy, of leaders) in her midst, who bear the likeness of a lion, of which Peter too writes: Our adversary the devil goes about like a roaring lion; and this lion and all his companions seek to devour not bodies but souls, and they take rewards and decide everything by money. That this happened to the people of the Jews at that time indeed, no one doubts; for because of the priests and the princes and the prophets the Lord brought evil upon them.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.22.27":["The prophets, then, or leaders, are compared to lions; but the princes, whom we understand as men of a lower rank, imitate the rapine of wolves, so as to shed the blood not of bodies but of souls, and greedily pursue gains, by no means content with this: Those who serve the altar live by the altar, but, once they have come to the ministry of God, they amass the riches of Croesus.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.22.28-29":["Her prophets also, who foretell things to come, daubed them without tempering — which we also discussed above, that of the soothsaying prophets who, without an admixture of chaff, plastered a wall that is broken down by the rains; men of this kind see vain things, and do not so much prophesy as divine falsehood, saying to the wretched land: 'Thus says the Lord, this he promises', when the Lord has not spoken to them; and the people of the land, being an imitator not of God at all but of the earthly works of the princes and priests, did everything through false accusation and power, oppressing not the rich but the poor, of whom it is written: But the poor man does not bear threatening, and: The ransom of a man's soul is his own riches; the stranger, too, and the sojourner, who had not yet become a citizen of the Church but was only a hearer, possessing the beginnings of faith, they oppressed with false accusation, so that, after one had gone around sea and dry land to make one proselyte, they made him a son of Gehenna.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.22.30-31":["'Amid such great', he says, 'vices and a multitude of crimes, I sought among them a man who could resist my anger and, after the pattern of Moses and Aaron and Samuel, set himself against my fire and my burning, and I could not find one — whence he said to Moses: Let me alone, and I will destroy this people —; and because I could not find one to resist me and hold me back, therefore I poured out all my indignation upon them and consumed them, not without measure and judgment, but so as to render their ways upon their own heads', either assigning to their authors the very sins they had committed, or surely upon the heads of the leader of the people, the prince and the prophets, of whom there was none who dared to resist the Lord in his anger, and on whose account the land, squalid and deserted, did not deserve to receive the rain of the Lord.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.23.2-4":["And the word of the Lord came to me, saying: Son of man, there were two women, daughters of one mother, and they committed fornication in Egypt; in their youth they committed fornication: there their breasts were subdued, and the teats of their puberty were broken (or, as is contained in the Seventy, there their breasts fell, and there they were deflowered). Their names were Oholah the greater (or the elder) and Oholibah her sister; and I had them (or they became mine) and they bore sons and daughters: and their names were Samaria Oholah and Jerusalem Oholibah. Oholah therefore committed fornication over me; and she went mad (or went off) after her lovers, after the Assyrians, who drew near to her, clothed in hyacinth, princes and magistrates, young men of desire (or chosen), all horsemen, riders of horses. And she bestowed her fornications upon them, the chosen sons of the Assyrians, all of them; and in the uncleannesses (or inventions) of all those over whom she went mad she was polluted. Moreover she did not abandon her fornications either, which she had had in Egypt; for they too had slept with her in her youth, and had broken the breasts of her puberty (or and they themselves had deflowered her), and had poured out their fornication upon her. Therefore I delivered her into the hands of her lovers, into the hands of the sons of Asshur, over whose lust she went mad; they uncovered her shame, they took her sons and daughters, and they killed her herself with the sword; and they became notorious women (or she became a byword among women), and they executed judgments (or vengeances) upon her. That the twelve tribes of Israel, which were joined together in Egypt, were afterwards divided into two and ten tribes and called Judah and Israel, the scripture of Kings and of Παραλιπόμενον [Chronicles] testifies. Over Judah there reigned, from the stock of David, Rehoboam son of Solomon, in Jerusalem, where the temple was, and the priests, and a great part of the tribe of Levi; but over the ten tribes, in Samaria, Jeroboam son of Nebat, of the tribe of Ephraim and of Joseph; of which Samaria is now called 'Oolla' [Oholah], which in our language sounds 'tabernacle', but Jerusalem 'Ooliba' [Oholibah], that is, 'my tabernacle in her'; for indeed among the ten tribes too there was a tabernacle, not of God but of idols: for Jeroboam, in order to turn the people away from the worship of God, had set up golden calves in Dan and Bethel. Let us speak, then, of each. These two women, Samaria and Jerusalem, were daughters of one mother, begotten of the stock of Israel, and they committed fornication in Egypt in their youth; for they would not, when led out of Egypt, have received on Mount Sinai the law forbidding them to venerate idols, had they not venerated the Egyptian monstrosities in Egypt —but the mother of both can be the Hittite, of whom we read above: Your father was an Amorite and your mother a Hittite; and also when it is said: 'Let each one cast away the abominations of Egypt from his eyes', this signifies that Israel served idols in Egypt and that there the breasts of its virginity fell, or were broken — ; and Samaria is called the greater and the elder, either because of the multitude of the ten tribes, or because, after the death of Moses, Joshua son of Nun, of the tribe of Ephraim, presided over the people, from which tribe too, we read, Jeroboam came, who split them off from the house of David. And God had them, or they became his, when they groaned under the works of Egypt, slaving before that at clay and brick; and afterwards they bore him sons and daughters, whether in the wilderness or in the land of promise.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.23.4-10":["According to the ἀναγωγὴν [anagogy, the higher sense], we have discussed more fully in the prophet Hosea what Joseph, Ephraim, Samaria, Jezreel and Israel signify, and how they are opposed to the house of David and to Jerusalem, which signifies the Church — of whom Psalm 77 (78) also sounds: The sons of Ephraim, bending and shooting the bow, turned back in the day of battle, and again: He rejected the tabernacle of Joseph and chose the tribe of Judah; for the Lord chose the house of confession, the Church, and rejected the tabernacle of 'Joseph', which means 'increase'; and they, setting aside the law of God, added golden calves of their own worship —; and she is called 'Oolla', that is, 'tabernacle', not of God but of error and of demons; and the sister, greater and elder because of her multitude and her captivity by the Assyrians, committed fornication against God, because she abandoned the truth of the faith and went mad after the princes of the heretics, her lovers, who were clothed in hyacinth, promising themselves lofty and heavenly things, and riders, of whom it is said: Some in chariots and some on horses, chosen sons of the Assyrians, and leaders and magistrates, who promise themselves knowledge and eloquence; and she did this because the same fornications that she had had in Egypt, that is, before she believed and while she was still living in the world, she practiced in heresy too; and she was deflowered by Assyrian men, who persevere in malice, or by avengers — for our adversary the devil is himself the enemy and the avenger — ; they broke the breasts of Samaria, and destroyed the virginity which she had had in the Church; therefore she was handed over to her lovers, according to the Apostle, who writes: I delivered them to Satan, that they may learn not to blaspheme, and he delivered them for the destruction of the flesh, that the spirit may be saved; they uncovered the shame of wretched Samaria, and displayed all her foulness; and her sons and daughters, who were begotten of her, both those endowed with knowledge and the simple and unskilled, they led captive; and they killed her herself with the spiritual sword; and they are celebrated, in the bad sense, in the talk of the whole world; and they become notorious among all the doctrines of the world, so that her foulness is punished as an example to all women.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.23.5-8":["And first Oholah, that is, Samaria, committed fornication over God, when she went mad after the Assyrians, who were clothed in hyacinth, and not just anyone of the people but leaders and magistrates, not men worn out with age but all young men, chosen horsemen, whose idols she followed and from whom, against the command of God, she requested help; and they came and polluted her, so that she did not abandon whatever she had done in her youth in Egypt —for the Egyptians too had lain with her in her youth, whose images she venerated, and there she was deflowered and her breasts were broken; and so great was the abundance of fornication that they are said not to have committed fornication with her but to have poured out their fornication —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.23.9-10":["Therefore she was handed over to her lovers themselves, Pul and Tiglath-pileser and Shalmaneser, after whose lust she went mad; and they themselves uncovered her shame — τροπολογικῶς [tropologically], as of a harlot woman; but the uncovering of a city is captivity — ; and they led their sons and daughters captive and slaughtered her herself with the sword, so that she was butchered as an example to all women —and is celebrated, in the bad sense, in the talk of all of them — ; and they executed judgments and vengeances upon her —so as to leave nothing of safety in her, but the ten tribes are held in captivity to this day in the mountains and cities of the Medes to which they were transported —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.23.11-13":["And when her sister Oholibah had seen this, she went mad with lust more than she, and shamelessly offered her fornication, beyond the fornication of her sister, to the sons of the Assyrians: to leaders and magistrates coming to her, clothed in varied garments (or costly), to horsemen who rode upon horses, and to youths all of outstanding form. And I saw that she was polluted: one way for both; and she increased her fornications. And when she had seen men painted on the wall, images of the Chaldeans expressed in colors, with their loins girded with belts, and dyed tiaras on their heads, with the appearance of all leaders (or of all the tristatae), in the likeness of the sons of Babylon and of the land of the Chaldeans in which they were born, she went mad over them with the lust of her eyes, and sent messengers to them in Chaldea. And when the sons of Babylon had come to her, to the bed of breasts (or of those turning aside), they polluted her with their debaucheries; and she was polluted by them, and her soul was sated (or withdrew) from them. And she laid bare her fornications, and uncovered her shame; and my soul withdrew from her, as my soul had withdrawn from her sister. For she multiplied her fornications, remembering the days of her youth in which she committed fornication in the land of Egypt; and she went mad with lust over the bedding of those whose flesh is as the flesh of asses, and whose issue is like the issue of horses (or whose private parts are like the private parts of horses). And you revisited the wickedness of your youth, when your breasts were subdued in Egypt, and the teats of your puberty were broken. According to the letter the interpretation is easy: that Oholibah —that is, Jerusalem, in which was the tabernacle of God —, seeing the blows dealt to her sister, was not warned by the example to draw back her foot from error, but increased her sister's fornication —for that one fabricated idols once, and outside, in Dan and Bethel; but this one often, on the high places and in the temple of God, worshiped the statue of Baal and committed fornication with the Assyrians; for the idol Baal, or Bel, and, to speak more plainly, Belis, the religion of the Assyrians, was consecrated by Ninus son of Belus in honor of his father — ; and she shamelessly offered her fornication to the Assyrians, to leaders and magistrates who were clothed in varied and many-colored garments, to horsemen and youths all of outstanding form, so that the fornication of both sisters became one.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.23.11-21":["Moreover, according to tropology the understanding is difficult: how the Church can surpass heretical lust; unless perhaps we can say this, that the servant who knows the will of his master and does not do it will be beaten with many blows, and that heretics commit abominations outside and perish by shipwreck outside the ark of Noah, while churchmen, if, though following the true faith, they imitate Assyrian and Chaldean vices and follow the many-colored images of sins, deserve greater torments — or do we not send messengers to the 'Chaldeans', which means 'as it were demons', when we lay open and offer them the breasts to be broken in the bosom, where the lodging of the mind is, and, sated with pleasures, pass from some women to others; and desire not so much fornication as a number of harlots; and come to such madness that, after long years of the Lord's service, we return to Egypt and do the things we did in the world before we received the name of the faith? —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.23.14-16":["But Jerusalem increased her fornications in this, that, seeing on the walls images of the Chaldeans, she went mad with lust and, deceived by their form and dress, sent messengers to them requesting help, and they came and polluted her.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.23.17":["And because pleasure is not perpetual but quickly brings satiety, polluted and sated with them, she withdrew from their covenant.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.23.18":["'Therefore I too, seeing her foul deeds and fornications made public to all, withdrew from her, so that she who had outdone her sister's crimes might also surpass her sister in the magnitude of her punishments'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.23.19-20":["She was of such wantonness that she committed all the errors of her youth at an age already more mature, and pursued the Egyptian vices in her lust for the Chaldeans too; for she once went mad in her bedding with the Egyptians, whose flesh is in the likeness of asses, with so copious an issue of seed, or private parts so huge, that they surpass the deformity of horses.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.23.21":["'Nor did the wickedness of her youth cease; rather, she went back to it after she became mine, so that both in the wilderness and in the land of promise she sighed for the old lust in which she was deflowered and her breasts were broken and all her virginal beauty perished'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.23.22-27":["All of this is said under the metaphor of an adulteress, disfigured because of her adultery, so that she may no longer glory in her comeliness but blush in the hideousness which she has merited by the magnitude of her pleasures. Whatever is said τροπικῶς [figuratively] of Jerusalem can also be referred to the soul, which, joined by its virtues to the embrace of God, afterwards turned adulteress to vices and withdrew from him, and, all her crimes being laid open, was stripped and disfigured and shown to all, so that she may at some time desist from her wickedness; and may not glory in the dignity of her name, but, from her consciousness of evil and her remembrance of Egypt, may not dare any longer to lift her eyes to heaven; whence too her nose is cut off and her ears are amputated, because she had turned the odor of good fragrance into stench and had heard the judgment of blood; and this we understand not only of smell and hearing but of sight too, of which Samson and Zedekiah are deprived because they had abused it; God also makes deaf and mute, so that their tongue may not pass through to the earth; and all touch is taken away in those who have utterly lost life. And these things will happen so that all things may be purged by fire, and the fornication of Egypt may perish, and she may no longer be able to raise her eyes to evils.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.23.22":["Therefore, Oholibah, thus says the Lord God: Behold, I will stir up all your lovers against you, with whom your soul was sated; and I will gather them against you round about, the sons of Babylon and all the Chaldeans, nobles and tyrants and princes, all the sons of the Assyrians, youths of outstanding form, all the leaders and magistrates, princes of princes, and renowned riders of horses; and they will come upon you equipped with chariot and wheel, a multitude of peoples: with breastplate and shield and helmet they will be armed against you on every side, and I will set judgment before them, and they will judge you with their judgments. And I will set my jealousy in you, which they exercise with you in fury: they will cut off your nose and your ears, and what remains they will cut down with the sword; they themselves will take your sons and your daughters, and your last will be devoured by fire. And they will strip you of your garments, and take away the vessels of your glory; and they will make your wickedness rest from you, and your fornication from the land of Egypt, and you will not lift up your eyes to them, and you will not remember Egypt any more. (LXX: \"Therefore, Oholibah, thus says the Lord God: Behold, I will stir up your lovers against you, from whom your soul withdrew; and I will bring them against you round about, the sons of Babylon and all the Chaldeans, Facud and Sue and Cue, and all the sons of the Assyrians with them, chosen youths, all leaders and magistrates, τριστάτας [tristatae, high officers], and renowned men riding on horses; all will come upon you from the north, chariots and wheels with a multitude of peoples: shields and targes, and they will set a guard upon you round about, and I will set judgment before their face, and they will judge you with their judgments. And I will set my jealousy in you, and they will deal with you in the wrath of fury: they will take away your nose and your ears, and your remnant they will cast down with the sword; they themselves will take your sons and your daughters, and fire will devour your residue. And they will strip you of your garment, and take up the vessels of your boasting; and they will turn away your impieties from you, and your fornication from the land of Egypt, and you will not raise your eyes to them, and you will not remember Egypt any more.\") 'Because you remembered the flesh of the Egyptians and imitated the foulness of an unclean nation which venerates images of all the beasts, therefore I will bring against you your lovers, sated with whose embrace you withdrew from their covenant, so that, as great as your love had been before, into so great a hatred you might be turned.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.23.23-27":["The sons, I say, of Babylon and all the Chaldeans, nobles, tyrants and princes — in place of which the Hebrew has 'facud' and 'sue' and 'cue', which many understand as nations of the east, since we find names of this kind neither in the volume of Kings nor in Chronicles, nor even in Jeremiah, which describe the captivity of Jerusalem, nor in any other place of holy scripture; nor is it surprising if the Seventy set down the Hebrew names themselves, since Symmachus too and Theodotion agree in the same words — ; all', he says, 'the Assyrians, horsemen and youths of outstanding form, leaders and magistrates — or τριστάτας, which we have translated 'princes of princes', of whom we also read in Exodus: Chosen riders, τριστάτας, in place of which Latin simplicity translated 'ternos stratores' [grooms by threes]; and 'tristatae' is the name among the Hebrews for the second rank after the royal dignity, of whom it is written: Yet he did not attain to the first three; they were princes of the cavalry and the infantry and of the tributes, whom we call magistrates of both services and prefects of the grain office — ; all', he says, 'in chariots and on horses, armed with breastplate, helmet and shield, will besiege you, and that round about; and I will give them judgment, so that you may be judged by those whom you had left and whose pact you had made void; and they will judge you with their judgments as an adulteress; and I will set', he says, 'my jealousy in you, which they exercise with you in fury — for my jealousy over faith not kept is present even among enemies — : your nose', he says, 'and your ears they will cut off — as of an adulteress caught in debauchery — so that, disfigured, you may cease to please — for just as in the face all the beauty of the countenance consists in the comeliness of the nostrils and in the ears, from which in women pearls and gems hang down, so the dignity of the king and of the judges, which is signified in the face and the ears, will be cut off by their sword — ; they themselves will take your sons and your daughters — yours, he says, and yours, not mine and mine, because they were begotten of adultery —, and the rest will be devoured by fire; and they will strip', he says, 'you of your garments, with which I had adorned you, and of the vessels of your glory, of gold and silver, which I had granted you for the ceremonies of the temple — of which it is written: I gave them gold and silver, and they made them for Baal — ; and I will do all these things so that, your beauty lost and your bewitching face disfigured, your wickedness may at last rest, and the fornication which you learned and practiced in Egypt, so that, after you have lost your nose and ears, you may by no means dare to lift your eyes to the Chaldeans and show your hideousness to your former lovers; nor may you any longer remember the lust of the Egyptians, in the size of whose flesh you delighted'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.23.28-30":["For thus says the Lord God: Behold, I will deliver you into the hands of those whom you hated, into the hands with which your soul was sated, and they will deal with you in hatred, and take away all your labors, and leave you naked and full of disgrace. Your wickedness and your fornications have done these things to you, because you committed fornication after the nations, among whom you were polluted in their idols. You walked in the way of your sister, and I will give her cup into your hand. Thus says the Lord God: You will drink the cup of your sister, deep and wide — you will be for derision and for scorn: you who are most capacious, you will be filled with drunkenness and sorrow! —, and the cup of grief and sadness, the cup of your sister Samaria; and you will drink it and drain it to the dregs; and you will devour its fragments, and you will tear your breasts (or, as the Seventy translated, I will take away her feasts and new moons), for I have spoken, says the Lord. Therefore thus says the Lord God: Because you have forgotten me, and cast me behind your body, you too bear your wickedness and your fornications. It is profitable for Jerusalem too to have enmity against the Assyrians and Chaldeans and by no means to remember Egypt, but to be delivered into the hands of those who hated her, to be punished; and she is not delivered to them before her soul has been sated with them, so that, after she has repented of her old sins, she may then be purged by punishments, and lose all the labors which she acquired by the harlot's art, and see her nakedness and disgrace, and know that it was not the Lord who did all these things to her but her own fornications, by which she committed fornication among the nations, which he enumerated above, so as to worship their images.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.23.31-34":["'In the ways', he says, 'of your sister Samaria you walked; therefore you must drink the cup of her whose sins you followed — and that a cup is often taken for punishments and torments, there are many examples, among which is this: A cup in the hand of the Lord, full of pure wine, mixed. And he has tilted it from this; yet its dregs are not emptied: all the sinners of the earth shall drink; and in Isaiah it is called 'the cup of ruin' and 'the draught of fury'; and God speaks to Jeremiah, saying: Take the cup of pure wine from my hand, and you shall give drink to all the nations to which I shall send you; and they shall drink and vomit, and go mad; for just as certain cathartics are given so that the noxious humor that is in bodies may be purged out, so the Lord too gives a most undiluted cup of torments, to take away from sinners whatever there is of gall and bitterness and restore them to their former health — ; and the cup itself will be deep and wide: deep in the magnitude of the punishments, wide in the length of the captivity; but when', he says, 'you have been made drunk and seized by sorrow, and have drunk the cup to the dregs and drained it to such a degree that you devour even the fragments of the cup itself, then you will be weary of your former fornication and will tear the breasts which had been broken in Egypt and which were subdued and crushed by your lovers — or I will take away all your feasts and new moons, so that in place of solemnity and gladness you may have eternal lamentation and grief —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.23.34":["This that is said: And you will drink to the dregs; and you will devour its fragments, can be understood meaningfully of Jerusalem, which drank the cup of her sister Samaria last, and drained, as it were, the dregs of her torments to the bottom.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.23.35":["Since, therefore, you have done these things and forgotten me and cast me behind your body —or backward, because of the vices of the body, or behind the genitals, by which the foulness of turpitude is indicated —, therefore bear your wickedness and your impiety — that is, the punishments which you have merited by your impiety and crimes —'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.23.36-39":["And the Lord spoke to me, saying: Son of man, will you judge Oholah and Oholibah, and declare to them their crimes (or iniquities)? For they are adulteresses, and blood is on their hands, and they have committed fornication with their idols (or inventions); moreover their sons too, whom they had borne to me, they offered to them to be devoured (or they led through the fire). But this too they did to me: they polluted my sanctuary on that day, and profaned my sabbaths. And when they sacrificed their sons to their idols, and entered my sanctuary on that day to pollute it, these things too they did in the midst of my house. They sent to men coming from afar, to whom they had sent a messenger; and so, behold, they came. For them you washed yourself (or when they had come, at once you bathed), and painted round your eyes with stibium, and adorned yourself with womanly finery; you sat on a beautiful couch, and a table was adorned before you; my incense and my ointment (or oil) you set upon it. And the voice of an exulting multitude was in her (or and with a composed voice she leapt about), and among the men, indeed, multitudes of people were brought and came from the desert (or drunk from the desert); they put bracelets on their hands and most beautiful crowns on their heads. And I said to her who is worn down in adultery: Now she too will commit fornication in her fornication. And they went in to her as to a harlot woman; so they went in to Oholah and to Oholibah, wicked women (or to work iniquity). Righteous men, then, are they; these will judge them with the judgment of adulteresses and the judgment of those who shed blood, because they have committed adultery and blood is on their hands. For thus says the Lord God: Bring a multitude against them, and deliver them to tumult and to plunder; and let them be stoned with the stones of the peoples, and pierced with their swords; they will kill their sons and daughters, and burn their houses with fire. And I will remove wickedness (or impiety) from the land, and all women will learn not to act according to their wickedness (or impiety); and they will lay your wickedness (or impiety) upon you, and you will bear the sins of your idols, and you will know that I am the Lord God. As I have often pointed out, the small points in which the edition of the Seventy differs from the Hebrew I have joined on at the side, providing against the reader's distaste. Now it followed that he who had described the adulteries of Samaria and Jerusalem separately should announce their punishments together. And everything is said as to adulteresses, and, after a ἀνακεφαλαιῶσι [recapitulation] has been made, their crimes are briefly set forth, so that, rightly, according to the command of the law, the stoning of the people may follow upon the adulteresses. 'Since, therefore, they are adulteresses, pronounce a just sentence; for they have committed adultery with idols, and blood is on their hands, the blood, namely, of those whom they sacrificed to idols, so as to consecrate to demons the sons whom they had borne to me; nor did they do this outside, and on the mountains and in the groves, as they had often been accustomed to do, but at the last they polluted my temple, so as to set the statue of Baal in it; and they profaned my sabbaths, so that the religion neither of place nor of time was kept among them.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.23.36-49":["All these things were said τροπικῶς [figuratively] and under the type of adulteresses against Samaria and Jerusalem: that, delivered to the Assyrians and Chaldeans, they were led into captivity and their cities were burned up in fire, and the peoples were killed who had venerated the images of demons in place of God. These things can also be understood of the time after the coming of Christ: that righteous men from the peoples of the Gentiles, they themselves, judge the Samaritans and the Jews by comparison with themselves and deliver them to be devoured, those to whom the Lord had said: Your house will be left to you desolate, and: When you see Jerusalem surrounded by an army, know that its desolation draws near; for to this day they are adulteresses and are crushed by the stones of the whole world, because they sent messengers to those who were far off and could not say: I am the Lord who draws near, and not from afar, says the Lord; and they prepared themselves for their lovers, the demons, and all the adornment and finery which they had received from God as a natural good they turned into the worship of demons, and their works and comeliness, which we understand in the bracelets and the diadem, they offered to their adulterous lovers; and their punishment is a fear to the Gentiles, or rather to those of the Gentiles who have believed, lest they suffer the like if they do what Samaria and Jerusalem did. From them impieties will be taken away then, when they have received their crimes and have taken away from their eyes the veil that is placed before the face of Moses, so that they may know that he himself is the Lord Jesus Christ, whom they denied to be the Son of God.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.23.40-42":["But neither were they content only with present adulteries, nor did it suffice them that, after sacrificing my sons, they made them their idols' or the demons', but, wanton also in foreign pleasures, they venerated the images of the Assyrians and Chaldeans. And when these had come, Oholibah, Jerusalem, you fulfilled for them the whole bearing of an adulteress: you washed your body, smeared your eyes with stibium, put on womanly adornment, sat on a couch prepared for lust, set out feasts with which to restore the strength of your lovers, broken by lust; my incense and ointment — which I had ordered to be made specially for me, and that soul to be exterminated from the people which wished to make it for private uses — you offered to idols, so as to gather a throng of peoples, or sang with every kind of musician, so as to please your lovers not only by sight but by hearing and smell and all the senses; and they came to you drunk, not with any urbane elegance, but from the desert and the wilderness, displaying the bearing of rustics and brigands. And into such madness of pleasures did you burn, O most wicked sisters, that you placed your bracelets and hair-ornaments upon the hands and heads of your lovers, or upon yourselves, so that, full of adornment, you might be disgraced.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.23.43-44":["Wherefore I said to her, whether to Jerusalem or to Samaria or to both: So have you worn down your brow that you are by no means polluted in secret, nor do you heed the gaze either of a husband or of men, but like harlots you are prostituted publicly; they went in to you as to harlots, vying with one another in their desire to pollute you.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.23.45":["I will therefore deliver you for punishment to those to whom you delivered yourselves of your own accord for defilement; for they are righteous men in this, that at my command they torment the adulteress and parricide.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.23.46-47":["For thus says the Lord God: By the warning of your voice, O prophet, gather against them a multitude of peoples, and first let all their substance be plundered; then let them be stoned and pierced with swords; and let their sons and daughters be slaughtered, and their houses burn.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.23.48-49":["And all these things will be done so that impiety may be removed from the land, and all women and all provinces may learn from their punishments to avoid the like, so that, after you have received what you deserve and have borne the crimes of your idols, you may then know that I am the Lord'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.24.1-14":["It must also be noted how, from the beginning of the prophecy up to the present day when Jerusalem began to be besieged, the captivity comes in order. First the sword is called forth; then two ways are set out, Ammon and Jerusalem, and of these the lot falls for Nebuchadnezzar on the right-hand one; silver, bronze, tin, lead and iron are melted together in the city; the land too is not rained upon, but is overgrown with briers; afterwards, under the name of the two sisters Oholah and Oholibah, the sins of Samaria and Jerusalem are narrated, how the one has been captured and the other is to be captured; finally, in the ninth year, in the tenth month, on the tenth day of the month, it is shown to Ezekiel, placed in captivity, and to those who were with him and had surrendered themselves with King Jeconiah, on what day the city began to be besieged, and the likeness of the cauldron is set forth so that, just as the flesh and the bones, heaped into the cauldron, are burned up by the fire, so the whole city is to perish together with its citizens, and none of those who were besieged is to remain without being consumed by famine, sword, pestilence, and the remaining part is to be led captive into Babylon. This same prophecy, however, can also pertain to the time of the Lord's passion, on account of which Jerusalem was surrounded by an army and her sons were slain and, when Titus the son of Vespasian besieged it, no one escaped to safety, and the temple suddenly, and, after fifty years, under Hadrian, the city was consumed by an everlasting fire. As for the present captivity, when Ezekiel was beholding these things in Babylon, and which Jeremiah sets at the beginning of his volume: What do you see, Jeremiah? And answering he said: A pot set alight, and its face from the face of the north, there are those who, according to tropology, transfer this whole passage to the consummation of the world, and who temper the ninth number, which in the holy Scriptures is the number of punishments and sorrows and always follows righteousness, by the clemency of the tenth month and the tenth day. For punishments are applied to the sick for this reason: that health may follow pain. The world, therefore, which is set in the evil one, is set alight by divine fire on the day of judgment; and the city of bloods is set upon coals of fire, so that what is written in Isaiah may be fulfilled: You have coals of fire, sit upon them: these will be a help to you. And this is difficult: how, when the coals and the bones are consumed — the bones of which even the Pharisees are full, to whom the Lord speaks in the Gospel: Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, who are like whitewashed tombs, which within are full of filth and of the bones of the dead —, it is now said that the rust and filth of the world have not been consumed, but that even after the severity of judgment malice remains; this is easily resolved if we understand that saying of the Apostle: God has shut up all things under sin, that he may have mercy on all; for the creation was subjected to vanity. And the blood that has been poured out is set in the open sight of the whole creation, so that the crimes of all may be laid bare. But we can also take the most clear rock to be the Lord Savior, the rock which followed the people of Israel into the wilderness, of which it is written: They drank, moreover, from the rock that followed them, and the rock was Christ, because in him all things are revealed and what is written is fulfilled: Nothing is hidden that will not be revealed. And the Lord says that he will not have mercy, nor pass over the sins of the world, nor spare the impious nor be appeased, so that the ways of the impious may perish, according to what is set at the end of the first psalm: And the way of the impious will perish.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.24.1-5":["And the word of the Lord came to me in the ninth year, in the tenth month, on the tenth of the month, saying: Son of man, write down for yourself the name of this day, on which the king of Babylon has been made strong against Jerusalem today. And you shall speak by a proverb a parable to the provoking house, and you shall say to them: Thus says the Lord God: Set on the pot; set it on, I say, and put water into it. Heap its pieces into it, every good part, the thigh and the shoulder, choice pieces and full of bones; take the fattest of the flock, and arrange also piles of bones under it. Its cooking has boiled up, and its bones have been boiled through in the midst of it. Therefore thus says the Lord God: Woe to the city of bloods, to the pot whose rust is in it, and its rust has not gone out of it. Piece by piece, and by its pieces, cast it out; the lot has not fallen upon it, for its blood is in the midst of it; upon a most clear rock she poured it out; she did not pour it out upon the ground, so that it could be covered with dust: that I might bring my indignation upon her and avenge with vengeance, I have set her blood upon the most clear rock, so that it should not be covered. Therefore thus says the Lord God: Woe to the city of bloods, whose pyre I will make great. Heap up the bones, which I will set alight with fire; the flesh shall be consumed, and the whole composition shall be boiled down, and the bones shall waste away. Set it also empty upon the live coals, that it may grow hot, and its bronze may melt, and its defilement may be melted down in its midst, and its rust may be consumed. With much labor there has been sweating, and its excessive rust has not gone out of it, not even through fire. Your uncleanness is execrable, because I wished to cleanse you, and you were not cleansed of your filth; but neither will you be cleansed before I make my indignation rest upon you. I the Lord have spoken; it comes, and I will do it: I will not pass over, nor spare, nor be appeased. According to your ways and according to your devices I have judged you, says the Lord. (LXX: \"And the word of the Lord came to me in the ninth year, in the tenth month, on the tenth of the month, saying: Son of man, write down for yourself the day from this day on which the king of Babylon set himself firmly over Jerusalem from this present day. And speak a parable to the exasperating house, and you shall say to them: Thus says the Lord God: Set on the cauldron, and pour water upon it. And put into it the divisions, every good cut, the leg and the shoulder, taken from choice cattle, with the flesh separated from the bones; and burn the bones beneath them. It has boiled, it has boiled, and its bones have been boiled out in the midst of it. Therefore thus says Adonai the Lord: O city of bloods, cauldron in which there is rust in it, and the rust has not gone out of it. By its parts it has brought it out; the lot has not fallen upon it, because its blood is in the midst of it; upon a most smooth rock I have set it; I did not pour it out upon the ground, so that it should be covered with earth: that fury might go up to the avenging of vengeance, I have set her blood upon the most clear rock, so that it should not be covered. Therefore thus says Adonai the Lord: Woe to the city of bloods, and I will make the firebrand great and I will multiply the wood and I will kindle the fire, that the flesh may waste away and the broth be diminished, and the bones be burned up and it stand upon its coals. It has been kindled that it may be burned out, and its bronze be roasted, and its uncleanness be burned up in its midst, and its rust fail. The rust shall be humbled, and its abundant rust shall not go out of it; its rust shall be put to confusion. Your uncleanness is 'zemma', because you were defiled, and you have not been cleansed of your uncleanness. And what will it be if you are not cleansed any more, until I fulfil my fury upon you? I the Lord have spoken; and it will come, and I will do it: I will not delay, nor have mercy, nor be entreated. According to your ways and according to your devices I will judge you, says Adonai the Lord.\" — Most of these were added from Theodotion, and so was the Hebrew word 'zemma', for which we translated 'execrable uncleanness'; and this too was added by them, which is not found in the Hebrew and must be marked beforehand with an obelus: ÷ Therefore I judge you according to your blood, and according to your thoughts I will judge you, unclean and notorious and excessive in provoking. ⁚) Let us speak, then, about each point. In the ninth year of the captivity of King Jehoiakim — for he began to prophesy in the fifth —, and in the tenth month of the same ninth year, on the tenth day of the tenth month, the word of the Lord came to Ezekiel the prophet, who was placed in the regions of Babylon, and said to him: 'Son of man, write down for yourself this same day, and know that today the king of Babylon has begun, in the region of Judea, to besiege Jerusalem and to wall it in with an army; and describe the siege itself by a parable and a proverb — which in the present place signifies a metaphor and transference — : Set on,' he says, 'a bronze pot, that is, a cauldron, and put water into it, and cut into pieces the whole body of the fattest animals, the thigh as well as the leg and the shoulder, and carefully separate the flesh from the bones and, once the flesh has been put into the cauldron, set, or arrange, the bones beneath the cauldron, and make piles and heaps of bones, so that the cooking of the cauldron may boil up not once but again and again, and the flesh may be boiled through within, and the bones beneath may be burned up by the fire.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.24.6-8":["But do you wish, prophet, to know what this cauldron is, or what the flesh or the bones are? Speak these things, says the Lord: Woe to the city of bloods, that is, Jerusalem, to the cauldron whose rust, that is, malice, is excessive, and although fire has been put beneath it, yet its rust has not gone out of it; for even when captured and tortured they persevered in their former crime. Consume it part by part and one by one, so that no one remains to be saved. The lot has not fallen upon it, so that some should perish and others be saved, but a common destruction has come upon all; for its blood, that is, its murders and the slaughter of sons, is in the midst of it. This blood she poured out upon the most clear rock, so that it would be visible to all and not be covered with earth or with dust, so, namely, that my indignation might not pass by, but vengeance might hasten upon blood lying wholly in the open — for it is the way of nature that, if blood is poured out on the earth, the soil drinks up the moisture of the blood, or it is gradually covered with earth and dust; but when blood has been poured out upon a most clear rock which has no hollow, it slides and spreads over a wide space. And this indicates that she committed murders not hidden but public, according to what is said to Cain: The blood of your brother cries to me —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.24.9-10":["Therefore say to the city of blood — of bloods not of one but of many —: Behold, I will make a great pyre in you, or I will bring a firebrand, which the common people call a titio [the everyday word for a firebrand], and I will set alight in you not only the soft and tender flesh but the hardest bones as well, and nothing shall remain in you that is not burned up by the fire.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.24.11":["And when both the flesh and the bones have been consumed and burned, set the empty cauldron itself upon the live coals, so that its bronze too may melt and be consumed and, the bronze being consumed, its rust too may perish; that is, when the city has been burned up by the fire, its malice may perish utterly with the city.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.24.12-13":["But what does it profit to have done what was commanded? Not even by fire could the rust and malice of the cauldron and of the city be taken away, and there remains in it a wicked and execrable uncleanness — for this is what 'zemma' signifies. And he makes an apostrophe to the cauldron itself, that is, to the city itself — : I wished to cleanse you, and you were not cleansed of your filth, nor will you be able to be cleansed any more until I fulfil my indignation upon you.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.24.14":["I the Lord have spoken, and my sentence cannot pass away. The siege of the city has already come; I will do what I have threatened, and I will not pass over — as I have often done — your crimes, and I will spare no longer, nor will I be appeased toward you even if you multiply your prayers, but I will repay you your ways and your devices; indeed, I will judge you according to your works and your thoughts, so that in this too I may show my clemency' — like a physician who does not spare rotten flesh, so that the healthy limbs may be saved: he does not spare in order to spare, he is cruel in order to have mercy, and he considers not the pain of the patient but the healing of the wound, according to that saying of the Gospel, preferring that one member should perish rather than that the whole body be killed —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.24.16-17":["And the word of the Lord came to me, saying: Son of man, behold, I am taking from you the desirable thing of your eyes with a stroke, and you shall not mourn nor weep, nor shall your tears flow. Groan in silence; you shall not make the mourning for the dead; let your crown be bound about you, and your shoes shall be on your feet, and you shall not veil your face with a mantle, nor eat the food of mourners. So I spoke to the people in the morning, and my wife died in the evening; and in the morning I did as he had commanded me. And the people said to me: Why do you not show us what these things mean that you are doing? And I said to them: The word of the Lord came to me, saying: Speak to the house of Israel: Thus says the Lord God: Behold, I will profane my sanctuary, the pride of your dominion and the desirable thing of your eyes, and that over which your soul trembles. And your sons and daughters, whom you have left behind, shall fall by the sword. And you shall do as I have done: you shall not veil your faces with a mantle, and you shall not eat the food of mourners; you shall have crowns on your heads and shoes on your feet; you shall not mourn nor weep, but you shall waste away in your iniquities, and each shall groan to his brother. And Ezekiel shall be a portent to you: according to all that he has done you shall do, when this comes, and you shall know that I am the Lord God. And you, son of man, behold, on the day when I take from them their strength, and the joy of their dignity, and the desire of their eyes, on which their souls rest, their sons and their daughters; on that day, when one fleeing comes to you to announce it to you, on that day, I say, your mouth shall be opened with him who fled; and you shall speak and be silent no longer, and you shall be a portent to them, and you shall know that I am the Lord. (LXX: \"And the word of the Lord came to me, saying: Son of man, behold, I will take away from you the desirable things of your eyes in battle array (or in preparation); you shall not mourn nor weep, nor shall tears come to you. The groaning of the blood of the reins is mourning; your hair shall be bound up upon you and your shoes on your feet, nor shall you be comforted by their lips, and you shall not eat the bread of men. And I spoke to the people in the morning as he had commanded me, and my wife died in the evening; and in the morning I did as it was commanded me. And the people said to me: Will you not announce to us what these things are that you are doing? And I said to them: The word of the Lord came to me, saying: Say to the house of Israel: Thus says Adonai the Lord: Behold, I will defile my holy things, the pride of your strength, the desirable thing of your eyes, and the things which your souls spare. Your sons and your daughters, whom you have left behind, shall fall by the sword. And you shall do as I have done: you shall not be comforted by their mouth, and you shall not eat the bread of men, and your locks shall be upon your heads and your shoes on your feet; you shall not mourn nor weep, and you shall be consumed in your iniquities, and each shall comfort his brother. And Ezekiel shall be a portent to you: according to all that I have done you shall do, and when these things come, you shall know that I am Adonai the Lord. And you, son of man, will it not be on the day when I take away from them their strength, the exaltation of their glorying, the desirable things of their eyes, and the elation of their soul, their sons and their daughters; on that day he who has been saved will come to you, to announce it in your ears? On that day your mouth shall be opened to him who has fled; and you shall speak, and by no means be silent any longer, and you shall be a portent to them, and they shall know that I am the Lord.\") Above, by the kindling of the pot and the cauldron, he had shown the siege of the city; now the prophetic word testifies that it has been captured and destroyed, and that the whole people has been either slain by the sword or led into captivity. And first, according to our custom, let us lay the foundations of the history. 'Son of man,' he says, 'behold, I will take away from the sight of your eyes whatever is desirable to you, and I will take it away 'with a stroke' — or, as the Seventy rendered, 'in preparation' —, so that, namely, you may prepare yourself not to do the things that are customarily done in mourning. You shall not mourn nor weep, nor shall flowing tears show the grief of your heart, but if perhaps you are overcome by excessive sorrow, keep your groaning silent, such as does not burst out into words'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.24.16-27":["Let us come to the ἀναγωγὴν [anagogy, the higher sense], if only it does not seem forced to the prudence of a cautious reader. No one doubts that the son of man is said to be the Lord Savior, who at the last hour, that is, at the evening of this world, lost his former wife, and did not weep for her nor veil his face with a mantle nor receive any consolation from men, and who foretold in the morning the things to come which were fulfilled in the evening, and again in the morning, at his second coming, proved accomplished what he had foretold was to come: that the sanctuary of the Jews, and what they had held desirable in their eyes and that over which their souls trembled, had been profaned, and that their sons too and their daughters had fallen by the Roman sword. But what is said: You shall have a crown on your heads, and shoes shall be on your feet, we shall refer to this part: that they ought by no means to mourn nor to take up the signs of mourning, after they have received the truth in place of the shadow and certain of them have become Nazirites, and each one will be comforted over his brother, so that Ezekiel, whom God the Father strengthened, may be a portent to them, foretelling things to come; and that, after Jerusalem has been overthrown and the temple burned, and the whole world has heard this, and the dispersion of Israel, which inhabits the ends of the earth, then the words of the Lord, fulfilled in deed, may have freedom, and, his sanctuary having been profaned and the law fulfilled or destroyed according to the letter that kills, the glory of the Gospel may be laid open, and Israel may know that he himself is Lord and God, who foretold in the morning that these things would come, and proved by his coming that they were fulfilled in the evening and at the consummation of the world.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.24.17":["You shall not make the mourning for the dead (or, as the Seventy rendered: The groaning of the blood of the reins is mourning): by this he shows that his wife is going to die; for there follows immediately: And my wife died in the evening, which properly pertains to the union of the reins: nothing is more lovable to a man than a good wife, who is provided for a man by God, and to whom he is joined by the law of the Lord, and with whom he becomes one body. And what follows: Let your crown be bound about you, and your shoes shall be on your feet, signifies this: 'You shall have the hair which is shorn in mourning, and on your feet the shoes which mourners are accustomed to cast off' — whence David too, fleeing from his son Absalom and repenting over the murder of Uriah, walks with bare feet —. The Hebrews say: up to this day the Babylonian teachers, keeping the precepts of the law, bind the Decalogue, written on little parchments, around their heads, and these are the things that are commanded to hang before the eyes and on the forehead, so that they may always see what has been commanded; and because Ezekiel was a priest, he ought by no means to lay aside the crown of glorying, but to have it bound on his head. This is what they said. But let us, following the Seventy, understand by 'crown' the head of hair, although their translation too has by no means expressed the force of the Hebrew word. Nor shall you veil your face, he says, with a mantle, nor take the food of mourners: things which are customarily done in mourning, that people cover the face and receive from those who console them what the Greeks call περίδειπνα [funeral meals].\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.24.18-22":["'I spoke,' he says, 'to the people the things the Lord had commanded me, and in the evening my wife died, the desirable thing of my eyes and the mourning of the loins and of the blood. And immediately, on the next day, in the morning, I did all the things that had been commanded me; and the people asked me why I had acted against the custom of all; and to those seeking the reasons for such great endurance I replied: The word of the Lord which came to me, that I should speak to the people of Israel, commanded these things: Behold, I (that is: now already) will profane my sanctuary, so that the temple may be burned and lie open to all foreigners and profane persons, the temple in which you had your pride, that is, your confidence and hope and the desire of your salvation, and at whose siege your souls were in dread; your sons too and your daughters, whom famine and pestilence had left remaining to you, I will consume with the sword, so that whatever I have done, you too may do'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.24.22-24":["When custom is observed in mourning, the grief is moderate; but when so great a necessity of evils presses upon men that it is greater than all lamentation, each one, anxious about his own death, does not groan over the miseries of others, even though they are his nearest. 'You shall do nothing,' he says, 'of what is customarily done in mourning, but, when the city and the temple have been set on fire, you will be stunned at the magnitude of the evils; you will not cut your hair nor walk with bare feet, you will not mourn nor veil your faces with a mantle nor receive food from those who console you, but you will waste away, and you will be consumed in your iniquities, and you will scarcely have free groaning, lest you offend the pride of the victors if you are seen to weep publicly; so that Ezekiel the prophet may be a portent to you, and all that he did not do at the death of his wife, you may not do at the overthrow of the city and the temple'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.24.25-27":["And yet more plainly: 'O son of man,' he says, 'at the time when I take away from them their strength — the confidence of the temple, as we have said —, and the joy of dignity and that in which they had their only hope, and the desire in which their souls rested, and besides their sons and their daughters — understood from the above: — I take away, at that time,' he says, 'when one fleeing from Jerusalem, or one who has escaped, comes to you, saying that the city has been overthrown, then you, who before had been silent and had no free groaning, and were of such great endurance that at the death of your wife and in the pain of your loins you did not shed a tear, will open your mouth and speak, not to him who has come but with him, so that whatever you had foretold was to come, he may announce as now accomplished; and you will no longer be silent, but you will rebuke them and say that all these things have befallen them deservedly, so that you may be a portent to them', according to what is written in Zechariah, that the prophets are men who portend, and the Lord says elsewhere: In the hands of the prophets I have been likened. Let this be said according to the history.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.25.1-5":["And the word of the Lord came to me, saying: Son of man, set (or make firm) your face against the Ammonites and you shall prophesy concerning them, and you shall say to the Ammonites: Hear the word of the Lord God. Thus says the Lord God: Because you said: Well done, well done, over my sanctuary (or because you rejoiced over my sanctuary) because it was profaned, and over the land of Israel because it was made desolate, and over the house of Judah because they were led into captivity, therefore I will hand you over to the sons of the east (or Cedem) as an inheritance; they shall set up their sheepfolds in you, and they shall pitch their tents in you; they themselves shall eat your fruits, and they themselves shall drink milk (or your richness). And I will make Rabbath a dwelling for camels (or the city of Ammon pastures for camels), and the Ammonites a lair for cattle, and you shall know that I am the Lord. For thus says the Lord God: Because you clapped with the hand, and struck (or stamped) with the foot, and rejoiced with all your feeling (or mocked from your soul) over the land of Israel, therefore behold, I will stretch out my hand over you, and I will hand you over for plunder to the nations, and I will slay you from among the peoples, and destroy from the lands, and crush, and you shall know that I am the Lord. When Jerusalem had been taken or besieged, after the one who had been able to escape came to Babylon and announced that the city was at any moment to be taken, or had been overthrown together with the temple, the prophetic discourse, according to the custom of all the prophets, turns against the other nations round about, which had mocked at the ruin of Jerusalem and the burning of the temple; and first against the Ammonites, who had a capital named Rabbath, which today is called Philadelphia after the king of Egypt Ptolemy, surnamed Philadelphus, who held Arabia together with Judea: 'Son' he says 'of man, set (or make firm) your face against the Ammonites, who were begotten of the stock of Lot, and you shall say to them: Thus says the Lord God — for these are not the words of the prophet but of the Lord. Concerning the making firm of the face we have discussed more fully in this same prophet —: Because you said: Well done, well done, or, you mocked and rejoiced, because my temple and sanctuary was profaned by the entry of enemies, and over the land of Israel, because it was made desolate, and especially over the house of Judah, of the two tribes, which have now been led into captivity, therefore I too will hand you over to the sons of Cedem', whom we have translated 'eastern' — and no one doubts that the Midianites and the whole vast expanse of the desert lie next to the land of Arabia, who have herds of camels and multitudes of sheep and goats and live on this wealth —; which, as history relates in the book of Judges, also befell the land of Israel, when the Midianites came and grazed bare all their regions as far as Gaza. Μεταφορικῶς [metaphorically], then, Scripture signifies by way of the Midianites, the Ishmaelites and the Agarenes — who are now called Saracens, falsely taking to themselves the name of Sarah, so that, of course, they may seem to have been born of the free woman and mistress —; and it is said of King Nebuchadnezzar and all his army that he will come and take the city of Rabbath, which is called by this name either properly or because of its size — for 'Rabbath' means 'greatest' —; and so that we may not doubt that Nebuchadnezzar overthrew Arabia after Jerusalem was taken, Scripture testifies above: And you, son of man, set for yourself two ways, Ammon and Jerusalem, and the sword of the king of Babylon shall come, and again: At the head of the way of the city he shall cast. You shall set a way so that the sword may come to Rabbath of the Ammonites, and to Judah in most fortified Jerusalem, and again: Thus says the Lord God to the Ammonites and to their reproach. 'So the sons of Cedem will come and set up their sheepfolds like shepherds, and pitch tents; they themselves will lay waste your fruits and drink the milk and the richness of the land, once the flocks of camels and of all peoples have been let in, so that the Ammonites may be a lair for cattle', and by the compulsion of their misfortunes they may understand that he himself is the Lord, who both foretold the things to come and commanded that they should happen.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.25.2-5":["According to tropology we can understand the Ammonites — who were begotten of the seed of Lot in a cave, and begotten in drunkenness and incest — as all the heretics, of whom the apostle John also says: They went out from us, but they were not of us; for if they had been of us, they would surely have remained with us; these have sprung from 'inclination' — for this is what 'Lot' is rendered in our language —, because: All have turned aside, together they have become useless; there is none who does good, there is not even one. Against these the prophet's face is set, or made firm, so that it may crush them by its severity, and it says to them: 'Because you mocked my Church in the time of persecution, and because my sanctuary had been profaned through various vices, whether by the vice of those who served me or by the cruelty of the enemies who persecuted my own, and because the land of Israel too, the senses that see God, was desolate of the choir of virtues, and because the sons of the house of Judah — the house that confesses the Lord, in which is the true faith and right confession — were led into captivity and ceased to serve the Lord, therefore you, who are all mockery, will be handed over to the sons of the east, who have set out from the true light, and you will be handed over as an inheritance, so that you too may learn to confess the Lord'. And by metaphor it is said that the sons of the east set their sheepfolds over them and pitch tents and drink milk and eat fruits, and that the city once of pride becomes a dwelling for camels, which, having laid down the burden of sins, can enter through the eye of a needle, and a lair for cattle, which are ruled by the good shepherd, so that, when they have done this, mingled with and handed over to the shepherds of the east, they may understand that he himself is the Lord.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.25.6-7":["Nor was it enough for the Ammonites to mock and say 'Well done, well done' over the temple and sanctuary of the Lord, which had been profaned by the entry of various nations; but so great was the wantonness of their mocking that the whole people together clattered with hands and feet and shouted with all the passion of the heart, because the land of Israel was desolate. Therefore not through angels nor through other ministries, but the Lord himself, stretching out his hand to strike, will hand it over for plunder — not at all to camels and flocks of sheep but, more plainly, to the nations — so that it may be utterly uprooted from the number of the peoples, and perish, and be brought to nothing; and after it has been crushed, then it may understand that he himself is the Lord, who judges all things and has power over all lands.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","And again he reproaches them for having mocked with excessive passion of heart at the ruins of the people of God, and for having thought the land of Israel utterly deserted, for which they deserve the hand of the avenging God, so that those who had exceeded the measure of exultation and joy over the ruin of the house of Judah are not handed over to the sons of the east at all, but become a plunder for all the nations, and are slain from among the peoples, and perish, and are crushed, and are to be believed to belong not at all to the stock of Abraham but to all the nations; and then, by the greatness of their misfortunes and by their own crushing and the slaughter of their people, they may know that he himself is the Lord. What we have understood generally of the Church can be understood of each one of the saints, at whose ruin the men of the world rejoice, and the adverse powers too, if they have lost a chastity of long standing, since the wicked think it a consolation for their own crimes if they have many partners in crimes and in punishment; and no one does this unless he is a son of Ammon, that is, a son of the people, who was begotten in declination.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.25.8":["Thus says the Lord God: Because Moab and Seir said: Behold, the house of Judah is like all the nations, therefore behold, I will open the shoulder of Moab, from its cities, I say, and from its borders, the glorious places of the land, Bethiesimoth and Beelmeon and Cariathaim, to the sons of the east together with the Ammonites; and I will give it as an inheritance, so that there may be no more remembrance of the Ammonites among the nations. And upon Moab I will execute judgments, and they shall know that I am the Lord. (LXX: \"Thus says Adonai the Lord: Because Moab ※ and Seir ⁚ said: Behold, the house of Judah is like all the nations, therefore behold, I will loosen the shoulder of Moab from the cities of its promontories, the chosen land, the house of Bethiesimuth above the spring of the maritime city, the sons of the east over the Ammonites; I have given it to him as an inheritance, so that there may be no remembrance of the Ammonites among the nations. And upon Moab I will execute vengeances, and they shall know that I am the Lord.\" What we have translated 'and Seir' is not found in the Seventy but has been added from Theodotion.) In this place, however, the Hebrews tell a ridiculous tale: after the city was opened and the temple unbarred, and the sons of Ammon and Moab and Seir had entered the temple and seen the Cherubim protecting the mercy seat, they said: 'As all the nations worship images, so Judah too has idols of its religion'; and for this reason, they say, the Lord was angered and handed them over to captivity. We, however, say this: that these too mocked the house of Judah when it was taken by the Chaldeans, thinking that, as there had been no help from God among the other nations, so too the kingdom of Judah and Jerusalem, bereft of God's protection, had lain open to the power of Babylon.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","But to this day 'Moab', which means 'from the father', and 'Seir', which is rendered 'hairy' and 'shaggy', looks down on and despises the house of Judah, in which is the true confession, and thinks that it can be overthrown as easily as the fortifications of the other cities are overthrown.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.25.9-11":["Therefore, not a long time afterward but at the present moment, the Lord threatens that he will loosen, or open, the shoulder of Moab — that is, its strength and might — from its cities, so that the Babylonian conqueror may enter in to them too, and the cities in Moab may cease to be, and in its borders and boundaries the glorious cities may be destroyed whose names he sets down: 'Bethiesimoth and Beelmeon and Cariathaim', for which the Seventy, intending I know not what, translated 'the house of Bethiesimuth', although this word signifies 'a deserted village', while 'Beelmeon' too is to this day a very large village in Moab, which they rendered 'above the spring', and 'Cariathaim' 'a maritime city'; and it too is handed over, together with the Ammonites, to the sons of the east, namely the Babylonians, according to the sense which we have expounded against the Ammonites. 'And I will give,' he says, 'Moab as an inheritance, so that, as there is no more remembrance of the Ammonites among the nations, so also upon Moab I will execute vengeances and judgments, and they may know that I am the Lord, who handed Judah over for sin and stand forth as avenger against the Ammonites and against Moab'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","Hence the Lord threatens that he will open, or loosen, all the strength of the Moabites, so that they may cease to have cities and may no longer trust in the dialectical art, but that all its boundaries, which he calls promontories because of the dashing of the waves, may be destroyed, and that the glorious and chosen lands — 'Bethiesimoth', which means 'house of solitude' and 'deserted', and 'Beelmeon', which is likewise rendered 'having a spring' or 'above the spring', and 'Cariathaim', which the Seventy translated 'a maritime city' — may perish; for although they have the strength of fighters, and the glorious go forth to battle with the art of fighting, and advance hedged about and fortified on every side, yet their glorious land is not a house of habitation but a house of solitude; and though they think that they have a spring of knowledge and a maritime city that can withstand all the storms of the world, promising themselves endurance and strength, yet they will be turned to this end, that they too may be handed over to the sons of the east of the true light in Christ, just as the Ammonites were handed over, who trusted in their heretical multitude. And let us consider the benefit, and the clemency of God in the very threat: that they are handed over together with the Ammonites to the sons of the east as an inheritance, and that no heresy at all may be named among the nations, but that, judgments and vengeances having been executed upon Moab for its arrogance, they may know by the outcome of things that it is the Lord who orders all things. Now to prove that in the present place Moab is to be understood of the pride of the philosophers, of whom it is said: I will destroy the wisdom of the wise, and the understanding of the understanding I will reject, a few examples from the prophet must be unfolded: Amos says: Thus says the Lord: For three impieties of Moab and for four I will not turn away from him, because he burned the bones of the king of Edom to ashes — for truly, whatever there is in the world of perverse doctrines, whatever pertains to earthly knowledge and is thought to be robust, this is overthrown by the dialectical art and, like a fire, dissolved into ashes and embers, so that what was thought to be strongest is proved to be worth nothing —; Isaiah too rebukes the pride of Moab, saying: We have heard the insolence of Moab, he is exceedingly insolent; I have taken away his pride; Jeremiah also, prophesying against all the nations, speaks specifically to Moab: Because you trusted in your fortifications, and a little after: You had confidence in your glory, and again: How do you say: We are strong?, and then: Near is the day of Moab, that it should come, and its malice is exceedingly swift, and more plainly: Say: How has the glorious staff been broken, the rod of magnificence?, and again: The horn of Moab is broken; and these things are said so that, under the name of Moab, the foolish pride of the world that lifts itself up to heaven may be crushed.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.25.12-14":["According to tropology, this seems to me the sense: Idumaea is called 'earthly' and 'fleshly', which rises up against the spirit so that we do not do the things that are of the spirit, and hastens to draw to itself the soul placed in the middle, and wishes to repay vengeance on the sons of Judah, to whom it had formerly been subject, and remembers the old pain, that it was not in its own power, and therefore seeks vengeance on those who, withdrawing from the flesh, followed the spirit; therefore this Lord, being the avenger of the sons of Judah, stretches out his hand over Idumaea and takes away from it man and beast — whatever it seems to have either of reason or of simple faith — so as to reduce it to solitude, and of 'Theman', which means 'failing', and 'Dedan', which itself signifies 'kinship', he slew all with the sword which he placed in the hand of his people Israel, so that Edom may be overthrown, and feel the anger and fury of the Lord, and understand that his vengeance has served to this end: that it should serve the house of Judah. This is what the Apostle too argues about, writing: Jacob I loved, but Esau I hated; and he loves Jacob because he supplants and overcomes what is fleshly and earthly, and deserves to receive its blessings; for first we live according to the flesh and afterwards according to the spirit. First the vices, then the virtues by which the vices are undermined, because the heart of man is set on malice from boyhood, and mature age condemns the errors of youth; hence David too: The offenses, he says, of my youth and my ignorances do not remember. And yet it must be known that Jacob receives the first blessing, Esau the second, and at the last it is said of him: You shall be the servant of your brother, so that, after the flesh has been made subject to the spirit and joined to it, it may cease to be flesh, and Idumaea may pass over into Judea.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.25.12":["Thus says the Lord God: Because Idumaea took vengeance, to avenge itself on the sons of Judah, and sinned in transgressing and sought vengeance on them, therefore thus says the Lord God: I will stretch out my hand over the Idumaeans, and I will take away from it man and beast, and I will make it desert from the south, and those who are in Dedan shall fall by the sword. And I will lay my vengeance on Idumaea by the hand of my people Israel, and they shall do in Edom according to my anger and my fury, and they shall know my indignation, says the Lord God. (LXX: \"Thus says the Lord Adonai: Because Idumaea acted so that they avenged a vengeance on the house of Judah, and they kept the memory of evil and avenged themselves with vengeance, therefore thus says Adonai the Lord: I will stretch out my hand over Idumaea, and I will slay from it man and beast, and I will make it desert, and those fleeing from Theman shall fall by the sword. And I will lay my vengeance on Idumaea in the hand of my people Israel, and they shall do in Idumaea according to my anger and my fury, and they shall know my vengeance, says Adonai the Lord.\") Above he had set out two: Because Moab and Seir said, and afterwards, keeping silent about Seir, he spoke against Moab only; now he returns to the πρόβλημα [problem, the question set] he had begun: what Seir, that is, Idumaea, did — that Esau and Seir and Edom and Idumaea and Duma are called one nation, no one who has knowledge of the Scriptures will doubt; and, to say nothing of the other prophets — Isaiah, Jeremiah, Amos, who prophesied against Idumaea and Duma and Edom — the prophet Obadiah directs his whole prophecy against this nation; we expounded him long ago as best we could —. So Seir is rebuked, who, because he was shaggy, received the name of hairy, and bloody Edom, who for the cooking of red lentils lost his birthright and took his name from the dish; Esau too they interpret as 'made'; and this must be known, that in the Hebrew Idumaea is never written, but always Edom, which the Greek translation expressed as Idumaea. So Idumaea did not speak, as Moab did, but took vengeance on the sons of Judah, and sinned, or kept the memory of the former pain, so as to take vengeance on those by whom he had been supplanted in the womb.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.25.13-14":["Therefore not through angels nor through anyone else, but the Lord himself, stretching out his hand over Idumaea, took away from it man and beast, and reduced its cities to desert, that is, 'Theman', which we rendered 'south', from which, and from the other city, Dedan, the slain will fall by the sword; 'and when they have avenged themselves on Judah, I will lay vengeance on the Idumaeans by the hand, or in the hand, of my people Israel, so that they may be crushed by those on whom they exercised vengeance, who will do in Edom, or Idumaea, according to my anger and fury, so as to fulfil my indignation, and so that, through the hands of my people Israel, my anger may rage against the hostile nation, and they may understand that, against the unjust vengeance of Idumaea, my vengeance was just,' says Adonai the Lord.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.25.15-17":["Thus says the Lord God: Because the Philistines acted in vengeance, and avenged themselves with their whole soul, slaying, and fulfilling old enmities, therefore thus says the Lord God: Behold, I will stretch out my hand over the Philistines, and I will slay the slayers, and destroy the remnants of the maritime region; and I will execute great vengeances on them, rebuking in fury, and they shall know that I am the Lord, when I have laid my vengeance on them. (LXX: \"Therefore thus says Adonai the Lord: Because the foreigners acted in vengeance, and stirred up retribution, mocking from the soul, so as to destroy for ever, therefore thus says Adonai the Lord: Behold, I will stretch out my hand over the foreigners, and I will destroy (or slay) the Cretans, and destroy the remnants of the shore; and I will execute great vengeances on them in the retribution of fury, and they shall know that I am Adonai the Lord, when I have laid my vengeance on them.\") After the Ammonites, Moab and Seir — who are the Edomites, and who through Lot and Esau are joined by kinship to the people of Israel and the house of Judah — he comes to the Philisthiim — whom the Seventy translated 'allophylos', that is, 'foreigners', not by the general name of all the nations that are not of the race of the Jews, but by the special name of their own nation, which is now called Palaestina, the letter 'phe' having been changed into 'pi' according to the custom of the Greeks, just as among us, in place of the Hebrew 'phascha', the Greek and Latin 'pascha' is celebrated —. 'Philisthiim' they interpret as 'falling by the cup'; they too were made drunk from the golden cup of Babylon, and they rage, and mock with their whole soul, remembering their old enmities against Israel, by which they were divided from the people of God; and whereas the Lord says: Vengeance to me, I will repay, the Lord says: They themselves avenge themselves, slaying and fulfilling ancient hatreds; on account of which they will feel the hand of the Lord stretched out over them, who will slay the 'slayers' — for which the Seventy, intending I know not what, translated 'Cretans', whereas Aquila and Symmachus and Theodotion rendered either the Hebrew word itself, 'chorethim', or 'slayers' and 'slaying', not only in the present place but also in Zephaniah, where these things are said against the Philistines: Gaza shall be plundered, and Ashkelon given to destruction, and Ashdod and Ekron shall be uprooted. Woe to the inhabitants of the cord of the sea, neighbors of the Cretans! The word of the Lord is upon you, Canaan, land of the foreigners; and I will destroy you from habitation. And Crete shall be pasture for shepherds and a fold for sheep. For there too, whereas the Seventy rendered 'Crete', the other translators translated ὄλεθρον, that is, 'slaying' or 'destruction', about which we said what seemed right to us in the above-mentioned prophet, God granting it; hence too, of David's two legions, one of which was called 'Phelethi' and the other 'Cerethim', the latter, because they slew and cut throats, received the name 'Chorethim', that is, 'of the slayers' —; and the Lord destroys all the remnants of the maritime region, which is beaten by salt waves, and will execute vengeances, not small but great, in fury, so that his fury may have mercy as its end and, after he has executed vengeances, rebuking with fury, then the Philistines may know that he himself is the Lord.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.26.2-6":["And it came to pass in the eleventh year, on the first (or one) of the month, that the word of the Lord came to me, saying: Son of man, because Tyre (or Sor) has said of Jerusalem: Aha, the gates of the peoples (or nations) are broken, she is turned to me, I shall be filled, she is laid waste (or she who was full is laid waste); therefore thus says the Lord God: Behold, I am against you, O Tyre, and I will make many nations come up to you, as the surging sea comes up; and they shall break down the walls of Tyre and destroy her towers, and I will scrape (or winnow) her dust from her, and I will make her a most clear (or most gleaming) rock. There shall be a drying of dragnets in the midst of the sea, for I have spoken, says the Lord God; and she shall be for plunder to the nations. Her daughters also who are in the field (or in the plain) shall be slain by the sword, and they shall know that I am the Lord. The order of the prophecy is woven together in sequence. He had spoken against the Ammonites and Moab and Seir, who are themselves the Edomites, and the Philistines who are on the coast; and he keeps the same boundary along the cord of the sea: for after the Palestinians comes the region of Phoenicia, whose metropolis is the city 'Tyre', which in the Hebrew and Syrian tongue is called 'Sor': 'And because, when Jerusalem was taken, she too rejoiced and insulted her and said: The gates of the peoples are broken; I have a most secure harbor, and now that she is overthrown, the whole multitude that was ruled by her will has turned to me; and because she is laid waste, therefore I shall be filled; or, she who was full before will be reduced to a wilderness: you have said these things, then, and have insulted the ruins of Jerusalem, which was overthrown by the judgment of God because of her sins; therefore, not through messengers nor through any others, but I myself will be against you, O Tyre, and like the waves of the sea I will make the Babylonians come up against you with very many nations, by whose assault your walls and towers will be destroyed, so far that they fall right down to the ground and its dust is scraped away or winnowed into various parts; and she will be so humbled that she is compared to a most gleaming rock, and so great will be the desolation in the city, once most thronged, that the dragnets of fishermen will be spread out and dried in its midst; for I the Lord have spoken, whose words cannot be void, and I will hand her over for plunder to the nations; her daughters also who are in the field, or in the plain, that is, on the coast of Phoenicia, cities and towns, villages and forts, will be slain by the sword — μεταφορικῶς [metaphorically]: because he called them daughters, he speaks of a slaying by the sword —, so that by the outcome of events they may know that I am the Lord, who commanded these things to happen'. According to tropology, since the Hebrew name 'Sor' is rendered 'distress', every soul that is seized by passions can be called 'Sor'; when it sees Jerusalem, in which were the temple and the vision of peace, overthrown by the judgment of God for her vices and sins, it exults and thinks that the dominion of the once famous city is to be transferred to itself. This is understood both of contrary doctrines and of the men of the world: for when they see that a holy man has slipped through negligence, they rejoice and exult, as if the ruin of others were their own resurrection. Therefore the Lord threatens that he himself will fight against men of this kind, and will make very many nations come up against them like the waves of the sea, which destroy in them whatever seems most fortified and firm, and that he will so overthrow all their arguments that they are reduced to dust; that he will also make the dragnets and nets with which they formerly fished for the deceived multitude cease, and have them spread out and dried on the ground of the deserted city, so that in their place other fishermen may come in, whom the Lord sent to fish; and also that her daughters, who were formerly kept as if in a chamber and, being mystic and more secret doctrines, were not readily entrusted to anyone, will come forth and be dragged out into public and cut down by the Church's blade, so that then they may understand that he is the Lord, who both abandoned Jerusalem for a little while on account of her sins and destroyed the fortifications of Tyre on account of her insulting and her rejoicing.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.26.7-14":["For thus says the Lord: Behold, I will bring to Tyre (or to you, Sor) Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon from the north, king of kings, with horses and chariots and horsemen and a company and a great people. Your daughters who are in the field he shall slay with the sword, and he shall surround you with siegeworks, and heap up a mound round about, and raise the shield against you, and he shall direct mantlets and rams (or lances) against your walls, and destroy your towers with his weaponry. By the flood of his horses their dust shall cover you; at the sound of horsemen and wheels and chariots your walls shall be shaken, when he has entered your gates as through the entry of a city laid waste (or as one entering a city from the plain). With the hoofs of his horses he shall trample all your streets, your people he shall cut down with the sword, and your noble statues shall fall to the ground (or he shall bring down the substance of your strength to the ground). They shall lay waste (or he shall lay waste) your riches, they shall plunder your merchandise (or he shall despoil your substance), and they shall destroy (or he shall destroy) your walls, and your splendid (or desirable) houses they shall overthrow (or he shall overthrow), and your stones and your timber or your dust they shall put in the midst of the waters (or he shall cast into the midst of the sea). And I will make the multitude of your singers cease (or he shall destroy the multitude of your musicians), and the sound of your lyres (or the voice of your instruments) shall be heard no more, and I will make you a most clear (or a most gleaming) rock; you shall be a drying of dragnets, nor shall you be built any more, for I have spoken, says the Lord God. As is my custom, I have joined both editions, and where they differ I have coupled them side by side, looking to the brevity of the volumes. So what he had said: I will make many nations come up to you as the surging sea comes up, and Tyre shall be for plunder to the nations, he now sets out more plainly: that the Lord will bring Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon from the north, the king of kings, with horses and chariots and with a boundless multitude of troops, who will first overthrow the cities and forts on the coast that are subject to Tyre, and afterwards cause Tyre to be besieged, and wall her in with his army and mound and siegeworks, and assail her walls with mantlets and rams, and destroy them; and so great will be the multitude of his horsemen that, by the dust of the cavalry and the noise of the howling army and the rattle of wheels and the neighing of horses, the foundations of the city will be shaken; and so he will enter the city, its walls torn down, like one entering securely from the plain; and so great will be the security of the conquering army that the hoofs of horses will trample the streets of Tyre, and all the people will fall in the midst of the city, and the once noble statues, or all her substance, will fall to the ground, and nothing of her former riches will be preserved in the captured city; but along with the walls the noble houses too will be destroyed, and so great will be the savagery of the victors that the timber and stones left over from the fire will be thrown into the midst of the sea; and either Nebuchadnezzar or the Lord himself will make cease all the gladness of the city with which it once resounded in songs and psalteries, and, overthrown right down to the ground, it will be a drying of dragnets.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.26.7":["But it is doubtful to no one that, according to the ἀναγωγὴν [anagogy, the higher sense], Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon, who comes from the north, is understood as the devil, who, though he is a most harsh wind and a left-hand one, is called by the name 'right-hand', claiming for himself the dignity of the name; and he is the king of kings and of all the kingdoms which he showed to the Lord, when he said to him: All these have been handed over to me, and I will give them to you if you fall down and worship me. He has horses and chariots, of which it is written: A horse is deceitful for safety, and: All who mount horses have fallen asleep, and in another place: Horse and rider he has thrown into the sea.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.26.8-14":["He slays the daughters of Tyre, souls placed in distress and in a kind of prison of sins, in the field and the widest plain, and surrounds Tyre with his siegeworks, so as to hold her shut in, so that no one from her can escape the dominion of the Babylonian king; he destroys her walls and overthrows her towers, and whatever she may have had in the way of doctrines trembles violently at the flood of his horses and the sound of his horsemen and the clatter of wheels and chariots; and he will enter her gates with no one preventing him, and the hoofs of his horses will trample the streets of the city — for wide and spacious is the way that leads to death —, and he will cast down to the ground all her substance — or the noble statues, by which they had fashioned for themselves images of falsehood, of which it is written: Lord, in your city you will scatter their image —; and, the walls destroyed, the noble houses too will be overthrown which she had prepared for herself and in which she exulted; and she will come to such desolation that they throw into the deep her stones and timber, with which she had built her walls and constructed her masonry, and her songs and music, by which she trusted in herself in the wisdom of this world, and all the sound of lyres will be heard no more; and her fishing will cease, and she will be brought so low that she is a drying of dragnets and is not built any more, because, handed over to Nebuchadnezzar at the sentence of the Lord, she has been utterly overthrown, in accord with what the Apostle also says: Whom I have handed over to Satan, that they may learn not to blaspheme. The passages are difficult, and the prudent and diligent reader ought to pardon my labor; or if he can find something better, I too shall pass over to his opinion, provided he knows that he will receive from others the pardon he grants to me.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.26.14":["But what follows: Nor shall you be built any more, seems to raise a question: how has she not been built, when today we see her as the most noble and most beautiful city of Phoenicia? — whence some will have it that Tyre is to suffer these things in the last time, and is not to be built afterwards —; but how will they be able to keep to the person of Nebuchadnezzar — especially since we read in what follows that Nebuchadnezzar besieged Tyre and did not receive the wages of his labor, and that Egypt was therefore handed over to him, because he had served the sentence of God in the siege of Tyre —? Those who contradict this history say that they have read the Greek histories and those of the Phoenicians, and especially of Nicolaus of Damascus, and others of the barbarians, and have found nothing about a siege of the city of Tyre by the Chaldeans; whereas we can prove that many things are said in the Scriptures to have been done which are not found in Greek volumes, and that we ought not to acquiesce in the authority of those whose faithlessness and lies we detest. So what is said: Nor shall you be built any more, can be taken thus: that she will by no means any longer be queen of the peoples, nor have her own dominion as she had under Hiram and the other kings, but will be in servitude either under the Chaldeans or the Macedonians or the Ptolemies and, last of all, the Roman kings.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.26.15-18":["Thus says the Lord God to Tyre: Shall not the isles be shaken at the sound of your fall and the groaning of your slain, when they have been killed in your midst? And all the princes of the sea shall come down from their seats, and shall take off their spoils, and cast away their varied garments; and they shall be clothed with astonishment, they shall sit on the ground, and, dumbfounded at your sudden fall, they shall marvel. And taking up a lamentation over you, they shall say to you: How you have perished, you who dwell in the sea, renowned city, who were strong in the sea with your inhabitants, whom all shall dread! Now the ships shall be astonished in the day of your terror, and the isles in the sea shall be troubled, because no one goes out from you. (LXX: \"For thus says the Lord God to Sor: Shall not the isles be shaken at the voice of your fall, when your wounded groan, when the sword has been drawn in your midst? And all the princes of the sea shall come down from their seats, and shall take off their crowns, and shall be stripped of their varied garments; they shall go mad with frenzy, they shall sit on the ground, and they shall dread their own destruction, and shall groan over you. And they shall take up a lamentation over you, and shall say to you: How you have perished and been scattered in the sea, praised city, who were strong in the sea, she and her inhabitants, who gave her fear to all her inhabitants! And the isles shall dread in the day of your fall, and the isles in the sea shall be troubled at your going out.\") First let us lay open the very words of the history, disclosing the sense of what is said by a very brief μεταφράσει [paraphrase]. 'Thus says Adonai the Lord to Tyre, which is called 'Sor': In the day of your fall, when you have collapsed under Nebuchadnezzar's assault, and at the groaning of the wounded or slain who have been killed in your midst, shall not the isles be shaken? And all the kings will come down from their seats or thrones, and the princes of the sea who rule over various isles, and they will take the diadems off their heads, laying aside all the glory of reigning, and will cast from them their most precious garments, gleaming with incredible variety; and by the greatness of their astonishment they will be turned to madness and, cast down in humility, will sit on the ground; and when they see that you have collapsed, they will dread like things for themselves, and will think that no good thing on earth is lasting; rather, on the contrary, they will take up a lamentation over you and recite a tearful song, saying: How you have perished, you who dwell in the sea, renowned city (or praised)' — for we read in Greek and Latin and barbarian histories that Tyre was an island and had no approach from the land, but that afterwards mounds were thrown up by Nebuchadnezzar king of the Chaldeans or, as some affirm, by Alexander king of the Macedonians, and the place was prepared by siege and with mantlets and rams, and from an island a peninsula was made —; 'you, then, O Sor, O Tyre, who were once the strongest among all the isles, or praised by the voice of all among the cities, together with your inhabitants whom all feared, how suddenly you have collapsed! Now the isles will be astonished or terrified over you, and — as it stands better in the Hebrew — the ships, in the day of your terror and fall, and the isles in the sea will be troubled — hence it is more consistent to read 'ships' above, so that 'isles' is not set down a second time —, because no one goes out from you — or, as the Seventy translated, in the day of your going in —, when you have been led away captive'. Let these meanwhile be laid as a kind of foundation of the history; now we shall strive to set the spiritual roof upon it. Imagine someone who, having long kept his chastity, flourished in many virtues, and afterwards collapsed into some grave and mortal sin: will not all the isles be moved at the sound of his fall and of his slain virtues, those, namely, who are beaten by the salt and bitter waves of this world? And all who seemed to be princes in the brine of this world will come down from the throne of their pride and, cast down in humility, will take off from their heads the diadems they seemed to possess through good works, and will cast away the garments varied with a diversity of virtues, and will be clothed with astonishment, or through the greatness of their fear will seem in some way insane, and will sit on the ground, and, stunned at his sudden fall, will marvel, so much so that all take up wailing and lamentation over him who has collapsed, and say: How you have perished, you who were formerly safe; how you have collapsed, you who had stood so long, who in the sea of this world were thought renowned among all, who were strong in the opinion of all; and your inhabitants, that is, your most strong thoughts that resisted all the fiery darts of the devil, which formerly were a terror to all adversaries, have been turned to astonishment! And the ships too, namely all the saints, who cross the waves of this world, are stunned when they see that you have fallen, in the day of your terror and fall; and the isles will be troubled, lest they too suffer like things — for the falls of others are examples for the righteous, since we all dwell in this world in a weak and feeble state and with wavering step, and our victory is never certain and secure, when those who had stood before go out from you, or at your going out, in your end and consummation —. For we are judged not by things past but by things present; and we must beware and always fear lest the storm of a single hour overthrow old glory and solid firmness.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.26.19-21":["For thus says the Lord God: When I make you a desolate city like the cities that are not inhabited, and bring up the deep upon you, and many waters cover you, and I drag you down with those who go down into the pit (or to those who go down into the ditch) to the everlasting people, and set you in the uttermost land (or in the depth of the lands) like the ancient wastes, with those who are led down into the pit (or go down into the ditch), so that you are not inhabited, and I give glory in the land of the living (or so that you do not rise again in the land of the living), I will reduce you to nothing (or I will give you to destruction), and you shall not be, and when sought you shall not be found any more forever, says the Lord God. Join what has been said with what preceded. 'When I make you, O Tyre, a city desolate forever, as are other cities that are not inhabited at all, and bring up the deep upon you, and many waters, or boundless multitudes of enemies, cover you — or certainly, because he is speaking to an island and to a city set on an island, he τροπικῶς [figuratively] compares the throng of adversaries to the waves of the deep —, and drag you down, with those who go down into the pit or into the ditch, to those who are in hell — of whom we also read in the Psalms: They shall go into the lower parts of the earth and shall be delivered into the hands of the sword, they shall be portions for foxes —, to the everlasting people, where there is weeping of eyes and gnashing of teeth, and set you in the uttermost land, in which the ancient dragon has been placed, and like the ancient wastes and deserts, where there is no remembrance of good things but punishments are everlasting, and do not cause you to be raised up in the land of the living — of which it is written: Blessed are the meek, for they shall possess the land, and in another place: I will please the Lord in the region of the living —, then you will be reduced to nothing, or you will perish forever, and you shall be no more', according to what is written in another psalm: Spare me, that I may be refreshed before I depart and shall be no more: not that he who is in everlasting punishments ceases to be, but that he who does not live to God is said in the holy Scriptures not to subsist. Hence Esther too, speaking against idols: Do not hand over, she says, your scepter to those who are not: surely those to whom she begs that it not be handed over existed, but they were not, to God, since they had ceased to be to the virtues and to the living God.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","This we have said briefly about Tyre μεταφραστικῶς [by way of paraphrase]; it can also be referred to those who, set in the distress of this world, are led down to the underworld and are covered by the deeps and waves of punishments, and are dragged to the lower parts of the earth, and are joined to those who are in the ancient wilderness, and are led down into the pit or the everlasting ditch, so that they are no longer inhabited by the Holy Spirit; of whom it is written: The ungodly, when he has fallen into the depth of evils, despises; and henceforth they will not be in the land of the living, but will perish, and be reduced to nothing, and cease to be to God. These testimonies are made use of by those who say that the punishments of the ungodly, and not of sinners, are perpetual: of the ungodly who, sought by God, were not found, and have ceased to be forever, because by their own fault they lost him who says: I am the life.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.26.21":["And this is to be noted: that Tyre is sought by him who came to seek and to save what had perished, and who, leaving the ninety-nine sheep on the mountains, seeks the one straying sheep — the woman too who had lost one drachma seeks and finds it, and calls together her neighbors to share her joy —; but if Tyre, when sought, is not found, the fault is not the seeker's but his who has fled the hand of the good shepherd. 'And no more', he says, 'shall you be forever' — or, as is written in the Hebrew 'lolam' and in the Greek αἰών [age], it signifies 'one age', according to that passage of Isaiah, who says that after seventy years Tyre is to be restored to her whole state; and they say that 'one age', that is, the span of a human lifetime, is reckoned at the number of seventy years, as the psalmist says: The days of our years in them are seventy years; but if in the strong, eighty years; whatever is beyond is toil and pain —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.27.2":["And the word of the Lord came to me, saying: You therefore, son of man, take up a lamentation over Tyre, and you shall say to Tyre, which dwells at the entrance of the sea, the trade of the peoples to many islands (or from many islands): Thus says the Lord God. If I should wish to comprehend the whole prophecy against Tyre, or over the lamentation of Tyre, in a single discourse, I shall both make it long and obscure the reader's understanding, since he cannot hold everything in mind at once, especially the places in which the edition of the Seventy differs from the Hebrew in this passage, that is, what they have added or what they have taken away. Therefore we shall be content with our own translation, and wherever they disagree we shall attach theirs alongside, leaving the perfect knowledge of all things to God, and we shall briefly point out what seems right to us in each point. He who is lamented is still a concern to the one by whom he is lamented; hence Samuel too wept and lamented over Saul, and the Apostle Paul laments and mourns over those who have committed fornication and have not done penance, and Jeremiah writes lamentations over the overthrow of Jerusalem in four alphabets.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.27.3-4":["O Tyre, you have said: I am of perfect beauty (or you have said: I have surrounded myself with beauty), and set in the heart of the sea. The first charge against Tyre is that, if she seems to have anything good, she thinks it not God's but her own, and reckons all the beauty that came to her from various regions to be the work of her own diligence and strength; for she said: I am of perfect beauty, or: I have surrounded myself with beauty, although she is set in the heart, that is, in the middle, of the sea, and is tossed about like an island, and is shattered by the surge and the dashing of the waves. That the heart of the sea signifies its middle, that prophetic saying also shows: Therefore we will not fear when the earth is troubled, and the mountains are carried into the heart of the sea. Their waters roared and were troubled; and our Lord too is said to have descended into the heart of the earth, that is, into its middle, and to the underworld. But true and perfect beauty is in no human being except in the body of Christ, which is interpreted as the Church, and which is gathered together from the virtues of many saints; hence he himself also speaks to the bride: You are all beautiful, my nearest, and there is no blemish in you.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.27.3":["Tyre, then, or Sor, according to the letter, dwells at the entrance of the sea, either because it was formerly an island or because it receives ships coming from the deep into a most safe harbor, and it is the trade of the peoples to many islands, or from many islands, which indeed continues to this day, so that the commerce of almost all nations is carried on in it. But following the interpretation we have begun, let us refer whatever is said of Tyre to the συνοχὴν, that is, the 'straits', of this world, which is set in the evil one and is hemmed in and battered by various disturbances as if by waves — of traders of this sort it is told also in the Psalms in mystical language: Those who go down to the sea in ships, doing business in many waters, these have seen the works of the Lord, and his wonders in the deep —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.27.4":["Your neighbors, who built you, have filled up your beauty; with firs from Sanir they constructed you, with all the planking of the sea; they took a cedar from Lebanon to make a mast for you. (In place of which, intending I know not what, the Seventy translated thus: Beelim your sons surrounded you with beauty; cedar from Sanir was built for you; they took thin boards of cypress from Lebanon to make masts of fir for you; for the word 'Beelim' is not found at all in this place among the Hebrews, but in place of 'Beelim' is written 'gebulaich', which means 'your borders'; in what they said, 'your sons', too, they were deceived by the ambiguity of the word and the similarity of the writing, since the same letters, with a different pronunciation, are read as 'of masons' and 'of sons', 'bonaich' and 'benaich'.) 'O Tyre, then, you who said in pride: I am of perfect beauty (or: I have surrounded myself with beauty), although you are set in the middle of the sea, hear how much has been bestowed on you by the bounty of God: your neighbors and those who border on you, who are not from distant but from nearby regions, they themselves have filled up your beauty; and you think yours what is another's!'\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.27.5":["And he speaks as if to a ship, τροπικῶς [figuratively] signifying the beauty of the city and its abundance of all things, so that, after he has described all its equipment — mast, yards, oars, sails, prow, keel, ropes, coverings, hides, and the other things which the use of the best-fitted ships requires — he may then announce that a storm and the south wind, by which the greatest waves will be stirred up, will come upon it, and that it will be subjected to shipwreck — by which he signifies the overthrow of the Tyrian city by king Nebuchadnezzar, or, as many think, by Alexander king of the Macedonians, who is related to have besieged and taken that very city in six months, after he had defeated Darius in Lycia —. According to the mystical understandings, the planking of the Tyrian ship, with which it is fitted and joined together, is cut from firs or cedars from Sanir, and its mast is raised from a cedar or cypress from Lebanon: from firs because of their lightness and the joints of the boards, which hold and bite into one another more gently, or from cedars because it is a wood that does not rot; and they are said to be 'from Sanir', which means 'way of the lamp' or, as we judge more truly, 'tooth of the watches', because all the prosperity and illumination of the ship came from the true light; Sanir, moreover, according to the letter, is the very mountain that is also Hermon, which others call Sarion — read the history —; it means 'whiteness' or 'whitening', which itself also pertains to grace coming from elsewhere.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.27.6":["Oaks from Bashan they hewed into your oars, your benches they made for you of Indian ivory, and cabins from the islands of Italy. (LXX: \"From Bashan they made your oars, your temples they made for you of ivory, forest houses from the islands of Chetthiim.\") How great the beauty of the ship and of its equipment, that it should have oars of cedar — not from just anywhere but from Bashan —, and benches of ivory, and cabins — or storerooms in which the most precious merchandise is laid up — from the islands of 'Chetthiim' — which we have rendered as 'Italy' —, from that region which is nearer to Greece, understanding by it all the parts of the western islands! Let us say, then, according to the ἀναγωγὴν [anagogy, the higher sense], that the oars of the Tyrian ship are made from Bashan — of which it is also written in the Psalms: The Lord said: From Bashan I will turn back, I will turn back from the depth of the sea; Basan in our language means 'ignominy' —; those, then, who were in the depth of the sea and in the ignominy of sins, the Lord turns back, and turns them into oars, so that, sailing with the apostles, they may reach the land and the shores; and benches are made of ivory when they have mortified their bodies, or have devoted the use of their teeth to the praises of God; and cabins or storerooms from the islands of 'Chetthiim', which, according to the ἐτυμολογιὰν [etymology] of the Hebrew language, is rendered 'struck', so that it has been not so much killed as tested by the blows of the devil. We can speak of the 'of ivory, and forest houses' 'from the islands of Chetthiim' as those which the heretics try to construct not in the house of God but in the Tyrian ship, they themselves having their own ivory of speech, and building temples of the tongue contrary to the temple of God, and forest houses, the dwellings of beasts, whereas Scripture says that groves and woods and sacred groves are not to be planted in the house of God.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.27.7":["Embroidered byssus from Egypt was woven for you into a sail, to be set on the mast; hyacinth and purple from the islands of Elishah became your covering. (LXX: \"Byssus with embroidery from Egypt was made for you into a spread to surround you with glory, and to cover you with hyacinth and purple from the islands of Elisee, and they became your coverings.\") Because Tyre had said: I am of perfect beauty (or: I myself have surrounded myself with beauty), she is convicted as to what she has received from each region, according to that word of Scripture: What do you have that you have not received? But if you have received it, why do you glory as if you had not received it? So it is described what each province sends, according to that line of Vergil: India sends ivory, the soft Sabaeans their incense, But the hardy Chalybes iron, and Pontus the rank castoreum, and the rest. Byssus grows most of all in Egypt, and from it was woven the sail of the Tyrian ship, which is hung on the mast; and its covering, which in the heat of the sun and in calm weather affords shade to sailors and passengers, is made of hyacinth and purple, which come from the islands of Elishah, so he calls the islands of the Ionian Sea. Further, according to the Seventy, coverings of byssus are woven for the spread and for the repose of the Tyrian ship, so that, covered with these, it may be more glorious, and, surrounded with them, may have a more beautiful garment. Now we have often pointed out that byssus is referred to the earth because it springs from the earth, and hyacinth to the air, purple to the sea, from which it is made, with the twice-dyed scarlet added, of which the vestments of the high priest are woven, because it signifies the four elements: earth, fire, air and waters, of which all things consist; and these Tyre takes to herself, so as not to use God's creatures with thanks, but to say: I am of perfect beauty (or: I myself have surrounded myself with beauty).\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.27.8-9":["So much meanwhile according to the letter; but according to the mystical understandings, since 'Sidonians' means 'hunters', and 'Aradians' 'those who put down', we say that Tyre, the glorious and most proud city, which is afterwards to be shattered when the wind blows, has citizens or princes who are hunters — of whom it is written: Our soul has been snatched like a sparrow from the snare of the hunters; for where we read 'of the hunters', in the Hebrew is written 'of the Sidonians' —; these hunt: they draw unwary souls, set on high, down to earthly things, and they become rowers to lead them to shipwreck; but the wise men of Tyre, who are taken in the bad sense, in which the sons of darkness are wiser than the sons of light, they themselves steer what has been made ready for Tyre for shipwreck; the elders of Byblus and its prudent men provided sailors to Tyre for the service, or strengthened its counsel — sacred history relates that very many elders were rejected by the Lord, and younger ones chosen, as a type of the Synagogue and the Church: the elder Cain is cast off, and the younger Abel is chosen; Ishmael, the son of Abraham, is estranged from his father, and Isaac the younger received the inheritance; of the sons of Isaac too, the elder Esau is a hunter and roams in the woodlands, the younger Jacob dwells simply at home, whence it is written also in Malachi: Jacob I loved, but Esau I hated; and certainly, according to the Apostle, while set in their mother's womb they had done nothing good or bad, nor had they merit or offense, that the one should be chosen and the other cast off, except that, in a type, as we have said, of the Synagogue and the Church, the elder is rejected and the younger taken up —. All, he says, the ships of the sea and their sailors were among the people of the Tyrian trade (or toward the west of the west — just as 'Song of Songs' and 'age of ages' and 'work of works' are said, so that a song is greater than the other songs, and an age longer than the other ages, and a work more useful than the other works, so 'west of the west' is said in order to signify the greatness of the western regions —), and fittingly the ships of the sea and their sailors and rowers, who are in aid of the Tyrian ship, do not go to the east, nor to the rising of the light, where the sun of righteousness rises, but to the 'west of the west', where the light sets and whence is the beginning of darkness.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.27.8":["The inhabitants of Sidon and the Aradians were your rowers; your wise men, O Tyre, became your pilots. The elders of Byblus and its prudent men provided sailors for the service of your varied equipment. All the ships of the sea and their sailors were among the people of your trade. (LXX: \"Your princes who dwelt in Sidon and the Aradians were your rowers; your wise men, Sor, who were in you, these were your pilots. The elders of Byblus and its wise men were in you: these strengthened your counsel. And all the ships of the sea and their rowers became yours toward the west of the west.\") He had said above: Your neighbors, who built you, have filled up your beauty; before he comes to those who dwell far off, he describes the help of the nearest provinces: 'Your inhabitants,' he says, 'or the princes of Sidon and of Aradus, which is a neighboring island, are your rowers; your wise men, O Tyre, have become your pilots' — for piloting belongs properly to the wise, as Scripture says: Those who have no piloting fall like leaves —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.27.9":["'The old men or elders of Byblus and its prudent men provided sailors for the service, or strengthened the counsel of Tyre, and provided varied equipment; and all the ships of the sea and their sailors were among the people of the Tyrian trade, or toward the west of the west'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.27.10":["Persians and Lydians and Libyans were in your army; your men of war hung up shield and helmet in you for your adornment (or hung up targes and helmets in you, they gave you your glory). That the Persians were most brave, whose king Cyrus, as is foretold by the prophecy of Isaiah, having overthrown Astyages king of the Medes, captured Babylon, both sacred and secular history relate; that the Lydians too were reckoned at that time among the most robust nations, whose king Croesus was captured by the same Cyrus, Xenophon writes most fully; and that Libyans came against Jerusalem with the Troglodytes and Ethiopians, we read in the volume of Chronicles; because in those times they were most flourishing and fighters famous in battle, they are recorded to have been defenders of the Tyrian city, and to have hung up shields and helmets on the battlements of the walls to terrify enemies. But the spiritual understanding sounds thus: that the 'Persians', who mean 'tempting' or 'tempted', and the 'Lydians', whom we understand as 'begotten', and the 'Libyans', who in the Hebrew language are called 'phut' and are rendered 'os' [mouth] — from 'ore' [mouth], not from 'osse' [bone] —, strive in vain to defend Tyre, since they are overcome by temptation and serve generation and lust, and only multiply empty words, not having the helmet of salvation nor the shield of faith, but only put forward glory and the din of words for its adornment.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.27.11":["The sons of the Aradians with your army were upon your walls round about; but the Pygmies too, who were in your towers, hung their quivers on your walls all around; they completed your beauty. (LXX: \"The sons of the Aradians and your strength were upon your walls round about; guards were in your towers; they hung their quivers upon your battlements all around; they perfected your beauty.\") The word 'gamadim' the first edition of Aquila translated 'Pygmies', Symmachus 'Medes', the Seventy 'guards', Theodotion 'gomadim', the Hebrew word itself; we see to this day the island of Aradus, which is entirely a city, and has opposite it the town of Antaradus, and is near to Tyre, and lies out off the Phoenician shore of the mainland. These are in the army of the Tyrian city and are the guards of its towers, and they hang their quivers all around and complete its beauty, so as to show that they are archers; or they are pygmies, that is, warriors and most ready for wars, ἀπὸ τῆς πυγμῆς, which in the Greek language is rendered 'into combat'. But if the Aradians mean 'those who put down', everyone who puts forward the knowledge falsely so called, and has in the quiver of his breast the fiery darts of the devil, with which he wounds and sets on fire the hearts of the deceived, is to be called an Aradian: for he desires to put down those who strive to climb to the heights, and he completes the beauty of Tyre, of which it is also written in Proverbs: Let not the desire of beauty deceive you, nor be captured by your eyes, and again: Like earrings in the nostrils of a pig, so is beauty to a woman of bad character.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.27.12":["The Carthaginians were your traders, by reason of the multitude of all riches; with silver, iron, tin and lead they filled your fairs. Not only in the present place, but also in Isaiah, where it is written: Howl, ships of Carthage, the other translators rendered the Hebrew word as 'Tharsis'; and that these are a colony of the Tyrians no one doubts; they themselves, from the regions of the west, filled the fairs of Tyre with silver, iron, tin and lead. 'Tharsis' in our language sounds 'exploration of joy'; but we ought not to understand the explorers here in a good sense, such as Moses had sent to explore the land of promise, but in the contrary sense, such as the Apostle too says that he avoids: Because of the false brothers secretly brought in, who came in to explore our liberty which we have in Christ, and of whom Joseph speaks: You are explorers; you have come to examine the tracks of the land, and his brothers, understanding that it is no small crime to explore other people's things through ambush, answered: Your servants are not explorers. These, then, multiply the fairs of Tyre not with gold nor with precious stone, but with silver, iron, tin and lead, having the charm of eloquence and weapons for assault, and in the tin the likeness of eloquence, which counterfeits silver, and in the lead the heaviest impiety, according to Zechariah, in whom a woman sits upon a talent of lead, and the Egyptians were sunk into the deep like lead. The discussion of the individual metals would be long, but we must strive for brevity.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.27.13-14":["Greece, Tubal and Meshech, they were your dealers; they brought slaves and vessels of bronze to your people. From the house of Togarmah they brought horses and horsemen and mules to your market. (LXX: \"All Greece and the parts adjacent to it, they were your traders in the souls of men, and they gave vessels of bronze as your merchandise. From the house of Togarmah they gave horses and horsemen and mules as your fairs.\") 'The Ionians,' he says, '— who in Hebrew are called Iauan —, and Tubal — that is, the eastern Iberians, or the Spaniards from the regions of the west, who are called by this name from the river Iberus —, and Meshech — whom we understand as the Cappadocians, whose metropolis, which was later called Caesarea by Augustus Caesar, is to this day called Mazaca in their own language —, these made the precious commerce of Tyre, bringing slaves and bronze vessels from Corinth to Tyre, and from the house of Togarmah, that is, from Phrygia, horses and horsemen and mules, of which that province once had the greatest abundance'. The Hebrews say that 'Greece', that is, 'Iauan', means 'is and is not', which refers properly to secular 'wisdom', in which, if they find something rightly, it is called 'is', if in the contrary sense, 'is not'. For they too, by the good of nature, discourse much on duties, on continence, on despising riches — which the Stoics properly claim for themselves —, and they seem to gain the souls of the men whom they have deceived; and they have bronze vessels, with which they counterfeit the likeness of gold, which they press upon the peoples of Tyre so as to trip them up with the opinion of false doctrine; from the house too of 'Thogorma', which means 'pilgrim' and 'stranger', horses will be brought to its market and fairs, and horsemen and mules, of which it is written: A horse is a deceitful thing for safety, and in another place: All who mounted horses have slumbered, and in the Psalms: Do not become like the horse and the mule, which have no understanding; for which reason Doeg too, the accuser and slayer of the priests, was also the overseer of many; and that these are from the house of the stranger and pilgrim, who do not eat the flesh of the lamb, of whom it is written: The stranger and the hireling shall not eat of it, so that all the fairs of Tyre are filled with these wares.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.27.15-16":["Many islands were the trade of your hand: they exchanged teeth of ivory and of ebony at your price. The Syrian was your trader because of the multitude of your works: drop, purple and checkered cloths and byssus and silk and chodchod they set out in your market. (LXX: \"From the islands they multiplied your trade: elephants' teeth, and to those who were brought in you paid your wages. Men were your trade from the multitude of your market: in aphec, stacte, and many-threaded cloths from Tharsis, and ramoth and chodchod they gave as your fairs.\") The edition of the Seventy differs much from the Hebrew in the present place; therefore a few things must be said according to each, lest, occupied with the wares of Tyre, we pass too slowly to the remaining prophecies. The sons of Dadan, the traders of Tyre, multiplied its trade from many islands, so that they gave elephants' teeth to those who came to its commerce, and had men in the fairs because of the multitude of the trade. They had, moreover, 'in aphec' — which was added in the Seventy from the edition of Theodotion, for which Symmachus rendered 'polymita' [many-threaded cloths] —, 'stacte' too — for which all the others translated 'purple', which in Hebrew is called 'argaman' —, and 'varieties from Tharsis' — for which in the Hebrew 'bus' is said, which has been translated by all as 'byssus'. But Tharsis is not found at all in the present place —. And 'ramoth', they say, and 'chodchod' they gave as your fairs — which is found so in the Hebrew, except that for 'ramoth' Aquila translated 'silks' or 'fine stuffs'; but 'chodchod' all the translators rendered just as it is set down in the Hebrew —'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","But from many islands, to pass to the ἀναγωγὴν, which are beaten by the salty and bitter waves of this age, the traders of Dadan bring teeth of ivory, promising the whiteness of eloquence, of which it is written: From the ivory houses, from which the daughters of kings delighted you in your honor; but they are not white, nor do they imitate the bride of whom it is said: Who is this who comes up made white, leaning upon her kinsman?, but are of ebony, of a black color, who cannot change their blackness, as Jeremiah says: If the Ethiopian will change his skin and the leopard his mottling. 'The Syrian' too, that is, 'aram', who means 'lofty' and swells with pride, is a trader of Tyre, and in the multitude of the Tyrian works he brings in the knowledge falsely so called, promising many varieties and 'stacte' of the best odor, and purple of royal dignity, and checkered cloths from the dialectical art, and byssus, for which the Seventy translated 'Tharsis', given over to the works of the earth, and silk, for which in the Hebrew is written 'ramoth', which means 'vision of death' — for all earthly works hasten to destruction —, and 'chodchod', whatever it is that is understood by it, setting them out in the fairs of Tyre. 'Dadan', moreover, judging from those wares which are said to be brought by its traders and islands, is to be understood either as the region of India, or, as some suppose, as that of the Edomites and of the vast desert, and it sounds 'kinship', so that we may recognize in the heretics a likeness of the divine doctrines.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.27.15":["The sons of Dadan were your traders. In place of which, intending I know not what, the Seventy translated 'the sons of the Rhodians', unless perhaps by the deceptive similarity of the first letter, so that instead of 'Dadan' they read 'Radan', which itself is also the largest of the Cyclades, and was once a most powerful city in the Ionian Sea and glorious in naval combat, and, because of its very safe harbor, the resort of all merchants. And since 'Rhodians' is rendered in our language 'seeing judgment', it is now said tropologically of those who discern the truth of judgment and do not themselves do it, as the Apostle says: Therefore you are inexcusable, O man, every one who judges; for in that in which you judge another, you condemn yourself, for you do the same things which you judge. But it is better to take 'Dadan' as the name of another place, as it is found in the Hebrew and among the other translators.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","Therefore, according to the Seventy, the sons of the Rhodians, traders of Tyre, multiplied its trade from very many islands; according to the Hebrew, after the sons of Dadan, its traders, islands too of various nations increased its merchandise, bringing teeth of ivory from India and ebony wood — which, being of a black color, are most precious —, and exchanged them for other wares of Tyre.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.27.16":["'The Syrian' too was a trader of Tyre, for which in the Hebrew is set down 'aram', in whose place the Seventy translated 'men', reading 'adam' for 'aram', deceived, as above, by the similarity of the letters 'res' and 'daleth' [r and d]. And to this day there remains in the Syrians an inborn ardor for trade: they run about through the whole world out of desire for gain, and they have such a madness for trading that, now that the Roman world has been seized, they seek riches amid swords and the slaughter of the wretched, and flee poverty through dangers; men of this kind are the traders of Tyre who deal in many-threaded cloths, purple and checkered cloths, and who set out byssus too, and silk and chodchod, in the market for her. For 'purple', which all translated, the Seventy rendered 'stacte', that is, 'drop'; but what 'chodchod' signifies I have not been able to find up to the present — the Hebrews say that all the most precious wares are signified by this name, or that it is a certain kind of precious wares which the Roman tongue does not express, or that it is the common term of those who sell trinkets —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.27.17":["Judah and the land of Israel, they were your dealers in first wheat; balsam and honey and oil and resin they set out in your fairs. (LXX: \"Judah and the sons of Israel, these were your traders in the commerce of grain and in ointments; first honey and oil and resin they gave in your fairs.\") The Hebrew word 'phanag' Aquila, Symmachus and Theodotion translated just as it is set down among the Hebrews; for it the Seventy rendered 'ointments', we 'balsam'. It is told, moreover, with what supplies the land of Judea, which is now called Palestine, abounds: grain, balsam, honey and oil and resin, which are brought by Judah and Israel to the fairs of Tyre. And since the letter is plain, according to the spiritual sense we say: the land of confession and the understanding of one who sees God, which refers to the Church, ought not to bring to Tyre the first wheat, which, falling into the earth, is multiplied and is taken for the word of God: for not by bread alone does man live, but by every word of God; then balsam, which grows in the vineyards of En-gedi, or ointment, of which it is written: Like the ointment on the head, which runs down onto the beard, the beard of Aaron; and honey, of which Solomon also speaks: Finding honey, eat as much as is enough; for if you eat more, you will vomit it up, since through excessive surfeit honey turns into wormwood; oil too, from which the lamp is lit in the tabernacle of God, lest that prophetic word be applied to us: You were selling oil in Egypt; for if it is in Egypt and in Tyre, it will be turned into its contrary, and it will be said of it: But let not the oil of the sinner fatten my head; and resin too is gentle, suited to bodies, and is taken for medicine, whence it is written in Jeremiah also: Is there no resin in Gilead; or is there no physician there? Why has the healing of the daughter of my people not gone up?; this resin Jacob too had, and he sent it to his son Joseph with honey and terebinth, nuts, incense and stacte; the Ishmaelites too, who bought Joseph from the land of Israel, were carrying these things to Egypt: incense and resin from Gilead and stacte. And that we may know more clearly what it is to bring grain, balsam, honey and oil and resin to the market of Tyre, let us hear the words of the Lord, who says: Do not give what is holy to dogs, nor cast your pearls before swine; to the Canaanite woman also, who was praying for her daughter, saying: My daughter is badly troubled by a demon, the Lord answered: It is not right to take the children's bread and give it to dogs. But because she had gone out from the borders of Tyre and Sidon and was drawing near to the land of Israel, she therefore obtained what she had asked.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.27.18":["The Damascene was your trader in the multitude of your works, in the abundance of various riches, in rich wine, in wool of the best color. (LXX: \"Damascus was your trader in the multitude of your works, out of the multitude of all your strength: wine from Chelbon and wool from Miletus.\") For 'rich wine', which Symmachus translated, both in Aquila and Theodotion and in the Hebrew itself it has 'wine from Chelbon'; and again, where we have put 'wool of the best color' or 'shining', Aquila and Theodotion translated 'in wool of Soor'. It signifies, moreover, that among the other trades of Tyre, very rich wine and choice wool were brought to its fairs from Damascus, which we see to this day. But if 'Damascus' means 'drinking blood', and it is a true tradition of the Hebrews that the field in which Abel was killed by the fratricide Cain was in Damascus, whence the place too is marked with this name — rightly, too, Paul, after the killing of Stephen, the first martyr in Christ, went to Damascus to lead believers in Christ bound to Jerusalem, and, by the mercy of God, who makes the seeing and the blind, he lost the eyes of the flesh so as to receive those of the mind, and the scales of the dragon fell from his eyes, through which he had lost the light of truth, so that he went to the street which is called 'Straight', and found 'Ananias', who in our language means 'obedient' —, from this land, then, to which it was said: Cursed is the earth which opened its mouth and drank the blood of your brother, the fairs of Tyre are gathered, and rich wine or shining wool, for which 'Chelbon' means 'milky', and for 'wool of the best color', 'wool from Miletus' — or 'Soor': 'Miletus' is not found in the Hebrew, but because choice wool is brought from there, those who translated understood 'Miletus' for 'Soor' —; from which it is to be perceived that Tyre feeds on the milk of infancy, and has no garments of its own, but from elsewhere: gathered from various provinces. That 'Damascus' means 'blood of sackcloth' and 'blood of a kiss' does not suit the present place; for Hebrew names are frequently interpreted variously, according to the diversity of accents and the change of letters and vowels, especially those which have their own properties among them.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.27.19":["Dan and Greece and Mozel set out wrought iron in your fairs; stacte and calamus were in your trade. (LXX: \"※ Dan and Iauan and Mozel in your fairs ⁚\" — which in their edition were added from Theodotion —; \"iron made by workmanship and a wheel is in your mixture.\") From the name of the patriarch Dan both the tribe and the place in which the tribe dwelt received their name, where today is Paneas, which was formerly called Caesarea Philippi, whence the river Jordan too obtained its name: 'Ior', that is, stream, 'Dan', which flows from Lebanon; 'Iauan', moreover, sounds 'Greece', which means, as we have said, 'is and is not'; for 'Mozel' too Symmachus translated 'bringing', so that the sense is: 'Dan and Greece brought wrought iron into your fairs, and the rest'; but Aquila for 'Mozel' put 'from Uzal', from which regions we must believe that iron and stacte and calamus were carried to the fairs of Tyre. And let us say that Greece, that is, Iauan, has wrought iron, most ready for wars and constructed by the dialectical art, boasting that it brings forth all things by judgment and reason, and that it promises a vocal sound, which is interpreted in the calamus, and stacte of the best odor; or wrought iron, and a wheel which revolves in the composition of words and has the course of speech.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.27.20":["Dadan were your dealers in carpets for sitting. (LXX: \"Dedan were your traders with choice beasts for chariots.\") The Hebrew and the Seventy differ much in the present place, except for the name of the region, which is close: 'Dedan' and 'Dadan'. For there is an infinite distance between 'carpets for sitting' and 'choice beasts for chariots'; nor is there any doubt that four-horse teams, chariots and horses are taken in the bad sense if they are earthly; otherwise, of heavenly ones, Elijah too is carried off in a chariot, and is called by Elisha: Father, father, charioteer and chariot of Israel, and the eyes of Gehazi are opened, so that he sees chariots on the mountain and horses without riders, prepared for the Lord's seat; but in the carpets the beauty of words is shown, on which the dealers of Dedan ride and rest, in a composed sitting, coming to the fairs of Tyre.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.27.21":["Arabia and all the princes of Kedar, they were the traders of your hand; with lambs and rams and kids they came to you. For 'lambs, rams and kids' the Seventy translated 'camels and rams and lambs'. Arabia and the princes of Kedar — which today is the region of the Saracens —, as has been shown most fully in the discourse of Jeremiah which is written against Kedar, abound in these animals, namely lambs and rams and kids — and it multiplies the fairs of Tyre by this importation —, and it is also a region most fertile in camels, because of the breadth of the desert's pastures and the mildness of the air, in which this animal delights. 'Arabia', moreover, means 'evening', and 'Cedar' 'darkness', of which it is also said in the Psalms: I have dwelt with the inhabitants of Kedar; these regions bring to Tyre camels weighed down with the heaviest burdens of sins, or lambs and rams and kids, which they sacrifice on their altars, which they have feigned out of an iniquitous, rebellious heart. But in this same prophet too, in the vision of the shepherds, we read of rams troubling the purest waters and thrusting at the sheep with their flanks and fighting with their horns; of kids too, which are accustomed to stand at the left; and of lambs, which counterfeit the lamb of whom it is written: Behold the lamb of God, behold him who takes away the sins of the world. But in the good sense: Isaiah writes that camels from 'Madian' — which means 'judgment of the Lord' — come to Jerusalem, and Ephah, and the rams of Nebaioth, and Sheba, bringing gold and frankincense — the last two of which are also offered to the Lord by the Magi —; and these camels, having laid down the burden of sins, can enter the strait and narrow way which leads to life.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.27.22":["The sellers of Sheba and Rema, they were your traders, with all the first spices and with most precious stone and gold, which they set out in your market. In the Psalms, where it is written: The kings of the Arabians and of Saba will offer gifts to you, in the Hebrew it has: The kings of Saba and Saba will offer gifts to you, of which one 'Saba' is written with the letter 'sen', the other with 'samech', which is like our letter. Since, then, 'Saba' means 'conversion', this must be said: that in the present place it signifies not 'conversion' but 'aversion', from which gifts are brought to Tyre, with all spices and the best odors and precious stone and gold, all of which are set out in the fairs of Tyre; for they too counterfeit the best odor, and the precious stone with which they strive to build the churches of their perversity, and the gold which they promise in meaning, and all things are perverse — for they receive nothing freely nor give freely, but trade in everything, whereas the kings of the Arabians and of Sheba offer gifts to Christ freely —; these are they who do all things for the sake of shameful gain, and they have received these gifts from him who says in the Gospel: All these have been delivered to me, and these, if you fall down, I will give you. 'Rema', or, according to the Seventy, 'Regma', I have not been able to find in any other place of the Scriptures, nor what region it is, or what it signifies, except, what is clear, also from the fact that it is joined to Saba, that it is a region neighboring this province.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.27.23-24":["Aran and Chenne and Eden were traders, Saba, Assur, Chelmad your sellers. They were your traders in many ways, in wrappings of hyacinth and of many-threaded cloths and of precious treasures, which were wrapped and bound with ropes. (LXX: \"Charran and Channa and Edne, these were traders, Saba, Assur and Chalmad your dealers, bringing trade ※ in Machalim and in Galima ⁚ hyacinth, and ※ polymita ⁚ choice treasures bound with ropes.\") These too are nations containing the names of various places. What was added in the Seventy from Theodotion: In Machalim and in Galima, Symmachus translated: In precious wrappings; 'polymita' too, for which Theodotion translated 'varied', we added from Aquila and Symmachus — the garments of the traders were so precious that they were carried in wrappings and bound with ropes of hyacinth —; 'Aran', or, as the Seventy said, 'Charran', sounds in our language 'holes'; 'Chenne', 'preparation'; 'Eden', 'delights', for which among the Seventy is 'Edne', which is not found in the Hebrew; what it sounds we do not know, and we ought not to seek the ἐτυμολογιὰν of a fictitious name. In the trade of Tyre, then, through the fleshly senses, which are indicated in the holes, is the whole preparation of the men of the world, who think it the only delights if they increase the commerce of the Tyrian city. Of 'Saba' we have spoken above; 'Assur' means 'directing'; 'Chelmad', or, as is contained in some copies, 'Cherma', is rendered 'vineyards'; no doubt those of the Sodomites, who come into the market of Tyre so that they may direct crooked things, or rather may rebuke with punishments and correct what had been perverted, according to that which is said in the Psalms: That you may destroy the enemy and the avenger.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.27.24-25":["They also had cedars in your trades. The ships of the sea (or, as is contained in the Hebrew: Tharsis) were your princes in your trade. For 'cedars' the Seventy translated 'cypresses'. And since we have spoken of both above, it is superfluous to increase the reader's weariness.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.27.24":["And they have manifold equipment, distinguished with incredible variety, which is bound in wrappings of hyacinth; or their treasures, which they have treasured up for themselves on earth, are bound with ropes, according to what is written: Each one is bound with the ropes of his own sins, and they did not have a free trade, but bound everything with the chains of sins. The wrappings of hyacinth, because of the similarity of the color, I shall refer to the powers of the air, which lavish their treasures on the fairs of the world.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.27.25":["And she was filled and glorified (or weighed down) exceedingly in the heart of the sea. 'Nothing,' he says, 'was lacking to you that pertains to the riches of the islands; yet that glorification was a weighing down of the possessor, since you cannot bear riches with moderation, although poverty too is often a temptation' — whence Solomon too asks only for necessities, and detests riches and poverty alike, lest pride creep in in the one, and in the other impatience and lying.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.27.26":["Your rowers brought you into many waters. Above we read that the rowers of the Tyrian city were the 'Sidonians' and the 'Aradians', of whom the one mean 'hunters', the others 'those who put down': for they hunt the souls of those whom they lead by their own will amid the waves of this age, and they do not lift them up to the heights but sink them into the depths; nor do they imitate those who, sailing until the morning watch, broke the raging waves of the sea and were found worthy to receive the Lord Savior, and at once, with such a passenger and companion, reached the harbor of rest. Of which it is written more fully in the Gospel.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","The south wind has shattered you in the heart of the sea. All the riches of Tyre perish when the 'south wind' blows, which more expressively is called in Hebrew 'cadim', and is rendered in Greek καύσων, which we can translate as 'burning wind'; of which the holy man also said: By day the sun will not burn you, nor the moon by night. By this wind Jacob had been burned, and yet not shattered, when he says: I was burned by day by the heat and by the frost of night; those too who were hired from the first hour bore the heat and burning of the whole day, and yet they receive the denarius, because they were burned and not shattered; whence the bride too says in the Song: I am black and beautiful, because there has looked down on me (or, as is better contained in the Hebrew, there has discolored me) the sun.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.27.27":["Your riches and your treasures and your manifold equipment. Many read thus: In the heart of the sea are your riches and your treasures and your manifold equipment, so that all the riches of Tyre and all its substance are set in the heart of the sea and are overwhelmed by the waves of the world, and its inhabitants have nothing stable and lasting, that Gospel word being fulfilled: Fool, this night they will take your soul from you; and what you have prepared, whose will it be?, and that apostolic one: Those who wish to become rich fall into temptation and many snares, and are dragged down into the depth. The history is plain; and therefore we fit short little comments to almost each little verse. For our discourse is already hastening to the end of the book.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","Your sailors (or rowers) and your pilots, who held your equipment and were over your people; and your men of war who were in you, with all your multitude which is in your midst, will fall in the heart of the sea on the day of your ruin. The rowers, or sailors, of the Tyrian city — we read that they are the Sidonians and the Aradians, of whom we have already spoken, and, according to the edition of the Seventy, the counselors of 'Byblus', which in Hebrew is called 'gebal' —, its men of war too — Persians, Lydians and Libyans —, will all fall and be shown to be of no account, when Tyre has fallen and all its glory has had the end of ruin; but the multitude — which the Seventy translated 'mixture' —, apart from the princes, signifies the common people, who, without titles of dignity, will be crushed by a like judgment. And the prophecy is mixed between the city and the ship, so that from the one you may understand the other; and yet both pertain to the consummation of the age and to shipwreck.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.27.28-31":["At the sound of the cry of your pilots the fleets will be troubled, and they will come down from their ships, all who held the oar (or the sailors and all the pilots of the sea), and the passengers and the prow-men will stand on the land, and they will wail over you with a loud voice and cry out bitterly, and they will cast dust upon their heads and be sprinkled with ash (or will strew ash for themselves). But what follows, ※ And they will shave baldness over you, and gird themselves with sackcloth, and weep for you in bitterness of soul with most bitter weeping, and take up over you a mournful song, and lament you ⁚, is not found in the Seventy, but was added from the edition of Theodotion. When Tyre has fallen, its pilots will be troubled and will come down from the fleets, and the rowers and passengers and prow-men — or the sailors and all the pilots of the sea —, tossed by the waves, will at last stand with a firm step; they will wail with a loud voice over her whom they formerly enriched with their wares, and will mark the bitterness of their mind with a cry; dust too — or earth — they will cast upon their heads, doing penance for earthly works, and they are sprinkled with ash, either of the heifer according to the law, so that they may be purified, or at least they will strew ash under themselves — according to what is said to Jerusalem: Sprinkle earth upon your head, and strew ash under you, and make lament, and in the Gospel it is written: If these signs had been done in Tyre and Sidon, they would long ago have done penance in sackcloth and ash, and the psalmist says: I have eaten ash like bread —; and they will shave the head over Tyre and make baldness, which is customarily done in mourning, at the time when the greatness of grief has shut out all gladness — but the hairs of the apostles were numbered, because they had consecrated their hair to the Lord after the manner of the Nazirites; and Samson, as long as he had his locks, possessed strength; when his hair was taken away, he was captured by the foreigners —; and when it is said: And they will gird themselves with sackcloth, the old lust of the loins is condemned, so that those who had lived in delights may afterwards, doing penance, live in austerity and hardness — the Ninevites had sackcloth; and again it is said to Jerusalem: Lament to me like a bride girded with sackcloth over the husband of her virginity —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","Some, ridiculously — but still, what we have read must be told — interpret the 'pilots' as the bishops of the heretics, the 'counselors' as the presbyters, the 'prow-men' as the archdeacons, the 'rowers' and 'sailors' as the deacons, and they refer the 'passengers' to the whole people — if they had added the 'shipmaster' as the devil, they would have completed the tragedy —, who will all wail together and weep most bitterly and do penance after they have perceived the shipwreck of their ship and have stood on land, and have lost all the beauty of the knowledge falsely so called, and in place of joy and gladness have marked their penance with grief in bitterness of heart.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.27.31-32":["And they will weep for you, he says, in bitterness of soul with most bitter weeping — for it is better to go into the house of mourning than into the house of feasting —, so that they may lament Tyre and redouble the mournful song; which the following Scripture adds. But that we may know that there is profit for those who lament her as she falls, and who formerly dwelt in the heart of the sea and in its midst, let the example of the prophet teach us, who himself too is commanded to lament Tyre, so that, when the time of penance has been completed, she may be restored to her ancient state, and, having taken up the harp, may sound forth to the Lord: read Isaiah.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.27.32":["Who is like Tyre, which has become dumb (or has fallen silent) in the midst of the sea? — which is not found in the Seventy —. You who, in the going out of your trades (or what reward you found), from the sea filled many peoples, in the multitude of your riches and of peoples enriched many kings; now you have been crushed by the sea (or in the sea), your wealth is in the depths of the waters (or your trade); and all your multitude which was in your midst have fallen. All the inhabitants of the islands were astonished (or were crushed) over you, and all their kings, struck by the storm (or turned to madness), changed their countenances (or wept); the traders of the nations (or of the peoples) hissed over you: you have been brought to nothing (or you have become perdition), and you will be no more for ever (or in perpetuity). The pilots, rowers, prow-men and passengers will recall these things in the lament of Tyre: what other of the cities has so fallen silent that it had no confidence to speak, and heard that Gospel word: Be silent and be dumb, and that from the Psalms: But to the sinner God said: Why do you declare my righteousnesses, and take my covenant in your mouth?.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.27.33-34":["'With how great wares, O Tyre, have you been enriched? And what have you sought with so much labor, that by day and by night you should gather what was to perish by shipwreck? You did indeed fill many peoples and enriched kings with your wealth — of whom it is written: The kings of the earth stood by, and the princes gathered together against the Lord and against his Christ —; but now you have been crushed in the sea and in the depths of the waters' — according to what is written of Pharaoh: The chariots of Pharaoh and his strength he cast into the sea, who sank into the depth like a stone and could say: I came into the depth of the sea, and the storm sank me —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.27.33-36":["All these things, moreover, we can refer to the person of the heretics, who, in the crushing and shipwreck of Tyre, will perceive their error, and, set in the midst of the sea, will long to free their souls, and will lament the riches wrongly gathered, with which they had enriched many kings, namely their patriarchs, and the ignoble common people, whose wealth will collapse in the midst of the sea; the inhabitants too of all the islands — if, however, we wish to refer what has been said to the good sense — will be astonished over the crushing of Tyre, and the kings of all the islands either will themselves, as if struck by a storm, change their countenances, or at least will show the grief of their heart with weeping and tears, and will testify to the greatness of their stupor with wonder and hissing, at how she has been brought to nothing and has perished who promised salvation to many nations; and she will be no more, not at all for a fixed time — so that we should interpret αἰῶνα as 'in the age' —, but in perpetuity, because the same word signifies both.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.27.35-36":["'All the islands, or the inhabitants of the islands, which are beaten by the waves of this age, will be astonished over you, and their kings, struck by the storm, will perceive their own destruction, and, imitating the voice of the serpent, will hiss and say: You have been brought to nothing (or you have become perdition) — so that you had not the nature of perdition, but the will —, and you will not be' — if he had spoken up to this point, a great question would arise: how it should not be, when we now see it built up; but from what follows: 'for ever', he shows the time of one age, which, according to the age of man, is reckoned in a cycle of seventy years —, or 'you will not be to God' — as the Apostle says: Who called the things that were not, and Isaiah the prophet: Behold, all your adversaries will be confounded and will blush; for they will be as though they were not, and Esther, according to the Seventy translators: Do not hand over your scepter to those who are not, and in the Psalms: Spare me, that I may be refreshed before I depart and be no more; in what sense these testimonies are to be taken we have said above —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.28.2-9":["Therefore let us discuss what is written according to both understandings, that of proud kings and equally that of apostate princes: 'Though you are', he says, 'in the midst of the sea, and are held in the narrow confines of an island, and, I will say more, though you are a man and surrounded by the frailty of human flesh, lifted up by the swelling of your heart you have supposed that you dwell on the throne of God, in the heavens; and at the same time, because you boast of wisdom, so much so that the men of your kingdom challenged Solomon with riddles: are you then wiser than Daniel, who by the grace of God overcame all the magi and soothsayers and is now the wisest in Babylon?' Or it is to be understood thus: 'Even if you are wiser than Daniel, who by the confession of all is the wisest, and by the greatness of your riches and power desire to make yourself equal to the power of God, nevertheless you will be taken by your adversaries and, your city lost, you will be pierced with many wounds, and you will no longer say: I am God; taught by your destruction that you are a man, not God'. But if we wish to say that the prince of Tyre is that same power to whom the city itself, or province, was entrusted by God, let us take up that testimony: I said: You are gods, and all sons of the Most High. But you shall die like men, and fall like one of the princes; for provinces were handed over to them to rule, as to judges, by the Emperor; and they, forgetting that their honor depended on the command of another, such as Heraclianus lately was in Africa, rose up with a tyrant's mind against their king and Lord, so that, scattered through the whole world, under the names of idolatry, they took to themselves the names of gods and, puffed up with pride, fell into the judgment and the snare of the devil, of which the Savior also spoke in the Gospel: I saw Satan like lightning falling from heaven.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.28.2":["And the word of the Lord came to me, saying: Son of man, say to the prince of Tyre: Thus says the Lord God: Because your heart has been lifted up, and you have said: I am God, and I have sat in the chair of God in the heart of the sea; though you are a man and not God, and you have set your heart as the heart of God. Behold, you are wiser than Daniel, no secret is hidden from you; in your wisdom and your understanding you have made strength for yourself, and you have acquired gold and silver in your treasuries; in the multitude of your wisdom, in your trading you have multiplied strength for yourself, and your heart has been lifted up in your might. Therefore thus says the Lord God: Because your heart has been lifted up as the heart of God, therefore behold, I will bring upon you strangers, the mightiest of the nations; and they will bare their swords against the beauty of your wisdom, and they will defile your comeliness. They will slay you and drag you down, and you will die in the destruction of the slain in the heart of the sea. Will you indeed speak, saying: I am God, before those who slay you, though you are a man and not God in the hand of those who kill you? By the death of the uncircumcised you will die in the hand of strangers, for I have spoken, says the Lord God. (LXX: \"And the word of the Lord came to me, saying: And you, son of man, say to the prince of Tyre: Thus says the Lord God: Because your heart has been exalted, and you have said: I am God, I have dwelt in the dwelling of God in the heart of the sea; but you are a man and not God, and you have set your heart as the heart of God. Are you wiser than Daniel? The wise have not instructed you with their discipline: did you by your erudition or your understanding make strength for yourself, and make gold and silver in your treasuries, or by your great erudition and your trading did you multiply your strength? Your heart has been raised up in your strength. Therefore thus says the Lord God: Because you have set your heart as the heart of God, on account of this behold, I will bring upon you strangers, pestilent ones from the nations; and they will unsheathe their swords against you, upon the beauty of your learning, and they will humble your beauty in perdition. And they will bring you down and lay you low, and you will die the death of the wounded in the heart of the sea. Will you indeed, speaking, say: I am God, in the sight of those who slay you? But you are a man and not God in the multitude of those who wound you. By the deaths of the uncircumcised you will die in the hands of strangers, for I have spoken, says Adonai the Lord.\") Wherever, according to the Seventy, 'Lord, Lord' is set down twice, the first name is the tetragrammaton, which belongs properly to God and is called 'ineffable', the second is the common one, which is often found among men as well. Now just as, under the figure of a ship, first the riches of the city of Tyre and then its ruin and lament were described, so the prophetic word is addressed first to the prince of Tyre, that he was raised up through pride and did not use well the wealth that he had, then comes mourning and lamentation over the great goods from which he came to such great evils. First things, then, must be said first. It is written in Isaiah of the king of Babylon, Nebuchadnezzar, that he made himself equal to the power of God and was lifted up into such arrogance that he dared to say: I will ascend above the stars of heaven, and I will be like the Most High — and, cast headlong from his throne, he deserved to hear: How has Lucifer fallen, who rose in the morning? —, and of Pharaoh in this same prophet: The rivers are mine, and I made them, and of the prince of Tyre, that with lifted-up heart he said arrogantly: I am God and I sit in the chair of God (or I have dwelt in the dwelling-place of God), though he is a man and not God; and these words, although they seem to exceed the strength of human frailty and to be the words not so much of men as of raving demons, we must nevertheless take as ὑπερβολὴν [hyperbole, exaggeration]: that they swelled up to such a degree and did not know their own measure that, lifted up by the prosperity of the world and the power of their kingdom, while they think present goods to be everlasting, they did not know that they were men and claimed eternal rule for themselves; although under the type of princes and kings and of individual cities or provinces the adverse powers are shown — of whom the apostle Paul also writes: Our fight is not against flesh and blood, but against the princes and powers and rulers of this darkness and the spiritual things of wickedness in the heavenly places, and in another place: We speak wisdom among the perfect, yet a wisdom not of this world, nor of the princes of this age, who are being destroyed, and, again discussing wisdom, he says: Which none of the princes of this world knew: for if they had known, they would never have crucified the Lord of glory; in the prophecy of Daniel too one reads plainly: The prince of the Israelite people Michael, and the prince of Greece, and the prince of the Persians; and Moses writes more openly in the song of Deuteronomy: When the Most High was dividing the nations, was scattering the sons of Adam, he set the boundaries of the nations according to the number of the angels of God (or, as it stands better in the Hebrew, according to the number of the sons of Israel); and the Lord's portion became his people, Jacob, the cord of his inheritance Israel; nor shall we wonder that on the contrary side the worst kings go before as a type of the transgressing princes, since we teach that the best kings, David and Solomon and Josiah, and the patriarchs and prophets went before in figure of the Lord Savior —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.28.12-15":["Further, in Ezekiel we find it written thus: You are the seal of likeness and the crown of beauty; you were in the delights of the paradise of God. You were clothed with every first stone: sardius, topaz and emerald, carbuncle and sapphire and jasper, silver also, and gold and ligure, and agate and amethyst, and chrysolite and beryl and onyx; and with gold you filled your treasuries and your storehouses within you. From the day you were created, I set you with the Cherubim on my holy mountain; you were in the midst of the fiery stones, spotless in your days, from the time you were created until your iniquities were found in you (Ezek 28:12ff.). For who is so foolish and of so senseless a mind as to think that the prince of Tyre, whoever he believes him to be, set in the paradise of God, and created among the Cherubim, and dwelling among the fiery stones (which we understand without doubt as the Angels and heavenly Powers), was one adorned with earthly stones and having the likeness and the seal?\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"EZK.28.12-19":["The Hebrews, among their other fables and γενεαλογίας [genealogies] and endless questions, are accustomed to understand these things as spoken against Hiram king of Tyre, although from Solomon to Ezekiel there are a great many years, which it is evident men did not live at that time; and to pronounce it thus, as if the prophet were speaking to him in irony: 'Are you the seal of the likeness of God, full of wisdom and perfect in comeliness, you adorned with all stones, you a Cherub, or created with a Cherub, when on the contrary you have sinned and are to be dissolved into ashes?'; and they add to their fable a marvel, so that, against the authority of Scripture, or rather without the authority of Scripture, they say: Hiram lived a thousand years. But how forced this interpretation is, the prudent reader understands without our judgment.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.28.12":["And the word of the Lord came to me, saying: Son of man, raise a lament over the king of Tyre, and you shall say to him: Thus says the Lord God: You, the seal of likeness, full of wisdom and perfect in comeliness; you were in the delights of the paradise of God, every precious stone was your covering: sardius, topaz and jasper, chrysolite and onyx and beryl, sapphire and carbuncle and emerald; gold, the work of your comeliness, and your openings were prepared on the day on which you were formed. You, a Cherub stretched out and protecting; and I set you on the holy mountain of God; you walked in the midst of the fiery stones, perfect in your ways from the day of your formation, until iniquity was found in you. In the multitude of your trading your inner parts were filled with iniquity, and you sinned. And I cast you out from the mountain of God, and I destroyed you, O protecting Cherub, from the midst of the fiery stones. Your heart was lifted up in your comeliness; you lost wisdom in your beauty: I cast you down to the earth, I set you before the face of kings that they might behold you. In the multitude of your iniquities and the iniquity of your trading you have polluted your sanctification; I will therefore bring forth a fire from your midst which shall devour you, and I will make you ashes upon the earth in the sight of all who see you. All who see you among the nations will be astonished over you; you have been made nothing, and you shall not be for ever. (LXX: \"And the word of the Lord came to me, saying: Son of man, take up a lament over the prince of Tyre, and say to him: Thus says the Lord God: You are the seal of likeness full of wisdom, and a crown of comeliness; you were in the delights of the paradise of God, you were surrounded with every good stone: with sardius and topaz and emerald and carbuncle and sapphire and jasper and silver and gold and ligure and agate and amethyst and chrysolite and beryl and onyx, and with gold you filled your treasuries, and your granaries in you they prepared from the day on which you were formed, with the Cherub anointed by God and dwelling in the tabernacle, and I set you on the holy mountain of God: you were in the midst of the fiery stones, you were unblemished in your days from the day on which you were created, until iniquities were found in you. From the multitude of your trading you filled your storerooms with iniquity, and you sinned. And you were wounded from the mountain of God, and the overshadowing Cherub led you out from the midst of the fiery stones. Your heart was raised up in your comeliness; your discipline was corrupted with your comeliness: because of the multitude of your sins and iniquities I cast you down to the earth, in the sight of kings I set you, that you might be put to shame. Because of the multitude of your sins and the iniquities of your trading I have defiled your holy things; and I will bring out fire from your midst, it will devour you, and I will make you ashes upon the earth in the sight of all who see you. And all who knew you among the nations will be saddened over you; you have become perdition, and you will be no more for ever.\") Since we have learned who the prince of Tyre is and how he fell through pride, let us come to know the lamentation over him for his former glory. First he is reminded of what he was, so that he may grieve that he has lost what he had: You, he says, the seal of likeness, according to what John the evangelist says properly of the Savior: For him God the Father has sealed, and of men: He has sealed that God is true, and in the Psalms: The light of your countenance has been sealed upon us, O Lord, and in another place: Beloved, now we are sons of God, and it has not yet appeared what we shall be; we know that when he appears, we shall be like him, whence also it is said to God: Who will be like you?; for likeness is one thing, equality another. Hence that is a most savage heresy which confesses in Christ only the likeness of the Father and takes away his nature; but we say in the Son not only likeness but also equality — for this reason the Jews persecuted him: because not only did he break the sabbath, but he also made himself equal to God —; and where there is equality, there is the same nature and one substance. This is what the Apostle too says about likeness: 'My little children, with whom I am again in labor until Christ be formed in you, that is, that you may recover his likeness, which you lost by your own fault'. And because in the Latin codices 'resignaculum' [unsealing] is read instead of 'signaculo' [seal] — since the one who translated, rendering word for word κακοζήλως [with misplaced zeal], set down 'resignaculum' for ἀποσφράγισμα [seal, seal-impression] according to the translation of the Seventy —, they accordingly understand it thus: that the king of Tyre unsealed and lost the seal of God and the figure that had been impressed as if in the softest wax, so that instead of a seal he made an unsealing, by no means having the image and likeness of God, according to which the first man too was formed, when God said: Let us make man according to our image and likeness; and it is to be noted that then only the image was made, while the likeness is completed in the baptism of Christ. And consequently to him to whom it was said: You, the seal of likeness, is joined: Full of wisdom and perfect in comeliness (or a crown of comeliness); for where the likeness of God is, there also is fullness of wisdom and perfect comeliness, or a crown of virtues, adorned and composed as if of various flowers, which the diligent man increases by his own effort while by industry he nourishes the good of nature, and the negligent man diminishes, according to what is said in Proverbs under the figure of a beautiful woman of bad character: As earrings in the nostrils of a pig, so is beauty to a woman of bad character.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.28.13":["There follows: You were in the delights of the paradise of God — for which in the Hebrew there stands 'in eden' — which the history of Genesis also narrates — and 'eden' is rendered as 'delights' —, and fittingly, for distinction, he names the paradise 'of God', to show that there is also a contrary paradise, 'not of God', among those who change the truth into a lie and boast that they have paradise; by which word he shows that the one of whom it is written is by no means a man, but a contrary force that once dwelt in the paradise of God; although the Jews think that this is prophesied of Hiram king of Tyre through the τρόπον [figure of speech] which is called ὑπερβολὴ; and to him to whom it is said: You were in the delights of the paradise of God, or 'you were made', it shows what he had, and what he lost. Further, as to what is joined to the dwelling in paradise: Every precious stone was your covering (or 'your binding and encircling'): sardius, topaz and jasper, chrysolite and onyx and beryl, sapphire and carbuncle and emerald, or, as in the Seventy, twelve stones are contained in a different order and with other names, this is to be observed: not that the king of Tyre was surrounded with every precious stone — or 'covered' and, as Symmachus translated, 'bound' and 'built in' —, but that every stone which the prince of Tyre had was precious. Otherwise there are many precious stones which Scripture does not name in the present passage: chalcedony, sardonyx, chrysoprase, jacinth, crystal too, and the most precious pearl; but Aquila and Symmachus and Theodotion also differ greatly in the present passage, both among themselves and from the Seventy translators, not only in order but also in number and in names; in the Revelation of John too, where Jerusalem is recorded as built of living stones, the same stones, with the order changed a little in the last parts, are set in its foundations, so that its gates are written to have been built of the light of crystal; but on the breast of the high priest also the same stones are described, in four rows on the breastpiece, and on his shoulders two onyx stones, on which the names of the twelve patriarchs are written, which the true high priest, of whom it is written: You are a priest for ever according to the order of Melchizedek, bears on his breast, bears on his shoulders, so as to show in the twelve stones the number of the apostles, and in the two the mysteries of both testaments; of whom one, John the evangelist, reclined on his breast to drink in the streams of wisdom, and to be able to say: In the beginning was the Word, and the Word was with God, and the Word was God. This was in the beginning with God; these are the living stones of which the Church is built, and the apostle Peter writes: If you have believed that the Lord is sweet. Coming to him, a living stone, rejected indeed by men, but chosen by God, honorable, you also yourselves, as living stones, are built up as spiritual houses, into a holy priesthood, to offer spiritual victims, pleasing to God through Jesus Christ. For the scripture says: Behold, I will lay in Zion a corner stone, chosen, precious; and he who believes in it will not be confounded, on which the vessel of election also agrees with like voice, saying: Built upon the foundation of the apostles and prophets, Christ Jesus our Lord himself being the corner stone; in whom the whole building, being constructed, grows into a holy temple of God; these are the stones of which we also read in another place: 'Holy stones roll upon the earth, like wheels, barely touching the ground and by their rolling hastening to heavenly things', of which Scripture also makes mention: Behold, I prepare your stone, the carbuncle, and your foundations, sapphire; and I will set your ramparts, jasper, and your gates with crystal stone, and your wall with choice stones, and all your sons taught by God, and your sons in much peace; and in righteousness you shall be built, on which we have said what seemed right to us in our explanations of that same prophet; of stones of this kind Psalm 20 (21) also sings: Lord, in your strength the king will rejoice, and over your salvation he will exult exceedingly. You have granted him the desire of his soul, and you have not cheated him of the will of his lips. For you have gone before him with the blessing of sweetness; you have set on his head a crown of precious stone; these are the pearls of the prophets and apostles which, in comparison with Christ, are all sold in the Gospel so that the most precious pearl may be bought, and the stone of which Zechariah writes that it has seven eyes, that is, the seven graces of the Holy Spirit, read Isaiah, and it is laid by the apostle Paul in the foundation of the Church, upon which gold, silver, precious stones are to be built; whose colors and natures and the powers of each it is not for this occasion to discuss, but they require a volume of their own, so that in Ezekiel and in Exodus and in Revelation and in Isaiah all the stones and the orders of the stones, compared with one another, raise a great question both for the reader and for the expositor; on which the holy man Bishop Epiphanius also handed me in person a volume of his own, and the thirty-seventh book of the Natural History of Pliny Secundus, after a manifold knowledge of all things, discusses gems and stones; to the knowledge of these the diligent reader must be sent by us; further, the translation of Symmachus writes that this prince of Tyre was studded with stones like a most precious necklace. Indeed it calls it a tympanum of gold, in which the stones were set; whence also according to the Hebrew there follows: Gold, the work of your comeliness, and your openings were prepared on the day on which you were formed; for which the Seventy set down: With gold you filled your treasuries, and your storehouses, referring it to the sense and mind, that his thought lay open to the divine mysteries and gathered for itself spiritual riches, of which the Lord commanded: Lay up for yourselves treasures in heaven, where neither moth nor worm destroys, nor thieves dig through and steal. For where your treasure is, there is your heart also; this is the hidden treasure of which he also speaks in the Gospel: The kingdom of heaven is like a treasure hidden in a field; which he who finds hides, and for his joy goes and sells all that he has, and buys that field; and the storehouses, or granaries, are those of which it is written in another place: Blessed are your storehouses and your remnants.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.28.14":["After this it is said, according to the Hebrew: You, a Cherub stretched out and protecting — it is understood: the ark of God and the mercy seat; or, according to the Seventy, 'that he himself was anointed and created with the Cherub'; from which it is shown that this cannot apply to a man, the prince of the city of Tyre, but to a force once holy and preeminent, which was set as prince of the city of Tyre —; and I set you, he says, on the holy mountain of God — no doubt it signifies paradise, to which the apostle Paul says he was caught up after the third heaven —; now 'Cherub' is said in the masculine gender, singular number, and in the plural they are called 'Cherubim', which means 'multitude of knowledge', upon whom God rests and sits, and he uses this chariot, as the prophet says to him: You who sit upon the Cherubim, show yourself, and in another place: He ascended upon the Cherubim and flew upon the wings of the winds; this Cherub, then, or the one created with the Cherub, stretched out and protecting the mysteries, was set on the holy mountain of God, of which we have often spoken and of which the apostle Paul speaks — if anyone, that is, accepts the epistle to the Hebrews —: You have come to Mount Zion and the city of the living God, the heavenly Jerusalem, and thousands of angels; or certainly the holy mountain of God is to be understood, as we said, as paradise. He walked too in the midst of the fiery stones, of which it is written: Who makes his angels spirits, and his ministers a burning fire; and not only does God, who is called 'a consuming fire', consume hay, wood and stubble, but so do the angels, who are called 'fiery stones' and are fervent in spirit; whence the Lord too: Fire, he says, I came to send upon the earth, and how I wish that it may burn.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.28.15-16":["'Until', he says, 'iniquity was found in you — found by God —, which was held shut up in the treasury of your breast, through pride and the abuse of the power you had received'; in the multitude of his trading, too, his inner parts, or storerooms, were filled with iniquity — for while he seeks out many things, and is not content with the knowledge he had received nor with the power over which he had been set, he filled the storerooms and inner parts of his breast —; so that, sated and grown fat, he kicked against his Creator: for Jacob ate indeed and was sated, and the beloved kicked back; he grew fat and was fattened and was enlarged, and forsook God who had made him, and: Out of the heart proceed the worst thoughts, on account of which God says: You sinned. And I cast you out from the mountain of God (or you were wounded from the mountain of God); reading this, we are compelled to fear: for if a Cherub stretched out and protecting, set on the holy mountain of God and in the midst of the fiery stones, perfect and unblemished, on account of the multitude of his trading filled his inner parts with iniquity, and sinned, and was cast out from the mountain of God — that is, from the dwelling of paradise — or was wounded from the mountain of God — which is plainly understood as Christ —, or certainly, while placed and dwelling on the mountain of God, was wounded by himself, and pricked by the evil of his conscience when he understands himself to be unworthy of dwelling on the mountain, what must be thought of us? For this reason it is said to him: 'And I destroyed you, O protecting Cherub, from the midst of the fiery stones, so that you should by no means stand among the fiery stones, but perish; O Cherub yourself, or the Cherubim who protected you, I led you out from the midst of the fiery stones', according to what is also written of Adam: He cast out Adam, and set him over against the paradise of delights.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.28.15":["And what follows: Perfect in your ways from the day of your formation (for which the Seventy set down: You were unblemished in your days, from the day on which you were formed), until iniquity was found in you, shows that every creature was formed good by God and had perfect virtue, and through this that the prince of Tyre too was unblemished, so that the blemish thereafter is not of nature but of will.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.28.17":["And he gives the reasons why he was led out, or cast out, from the midst of the fiery stones: 'For lifted up indeed was your heart in your comeliness, while you think yours what is God's'; whence the Apostle too says he received a goad of the flesh and an angel of Satan to buffet him, lest he be proud through the multitude of revelations and fall into the judgment of the devil. And therefore: 'Your learning has been corrupted', he says, 'with your comeliness (or you have lost your wisdom in your comeliness), so that, while you wish to be more than you were formed and to be wiser than you had received from God, you lost even what you had, and instead of beauty and learning, deformity and folly possessed you; whence you were cast down to the earth, you who formerly dwelt on the mountain of God — of whom Isaiah also writes: How has Lucifer fallen, who rose in the morning?, and the Savior in the Gospel: I was watching, he says, Satan like lightning falling from heaven, which Jeremiah also says to Jerusalem: How the Lord in his anger has darkened the daughter of Zion, has cast down from heaven to earth the glory of Israel —, and cast down in the sight of all kings so that they might be terrified by your example, whether the good kings, whose heart is in the hand of God, or the evil ones, whose kingdoms the devil showed to the Savior, who went to meet the Babylonian king, saying: You too have been taken as we were, and are counted among us'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.28.18-19":["It is profitable too that all evil works be turned into ashes, so that the harmful fire may be utterly extinguished, and all may see and be astonished that the Tyrian king has perished and been made nothing, not for many ages, but for one, or certainly for ever, so that what is written may be fulfilled: I will not spare you, and I will not have mercy.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.28.18":["Wherefore he also polluted his sanctification, which he had had when he dwelt on the mountain and lived in the midst of the fiery stones. There follows: I will bring forth fire in your midst which may devour you; this fire would be kindled in the heart of the king of Tyre by him whose darts are fiery, and of whom it is written: All adulterers, their hearts are like an oven, of which fire Isaiah also speaks: Walk in the light of your fire and in the flame which you have kindled, so that, going forth outward, it may devour its possessor, according to what is written in the same Isaiah: It will devour the matter like hay; on that day the mountains and hills and forests will be quenched, and it will devour from the soul even to the flesh; this fire, which is called strange, Nadab and Abihu had offered at the altar of the Lord, and therefore they were consumed by divine fire; whence Moses too says: This is the word which the Lord spoke: In those who draw near to me I will be sanctified; and the sanctification of God is the punishment of sinners. After this it is said: And I will make you ashes, so that the fire may consume all that you have built upon your evil conscience; you who, though you ought in the rest and the sabbath by no means to do servile work, gathered wood on the sabbath, so that the blaze might have something to devour in your breast.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.28.21":["And the word of the Lord came to me, saying: Son of man, set (or make firm) your face against Sidon, and you shall prophesy concerning it, and you shall say: Thus says the Lord God: Behold, I am against you, Sidon, and I will be glorified in you (or in your midst), and they shall know that I am the Lord, when I have executed judgments in it (or in you) and have been sanctified in you (or in it). And I will send upon it (or into you) pestilence and blood in its streets (or your), and the slain (or the wounded) shall fall in its midst by the sword (or by swords) round about, and they shall know that I am the Lord. And there shall no longer be for the house of Israel a stumbling block of bitterness and a thorn bringing pain on every side round about, from those who oppose them (or who were doing them insult), and they shall know that I am the Lord God. Thus says the Lord God: When I have gathered the house of Israel from the peoples (or from the nations) among whom they have been scattered, I will be sanctified in them before the nations, and they shall dwell in their land which I gave to my servant Jacob; and they shall dwell in it secure (or in hope), and they shall build houses, and plant vineyards, and dwell confidently (or in hope), when I have executed judgments upon all who oppose them round about (or have done them insult), and they shall know that I am the Lord their God. (And what follows according to the Seventy: And the God of their fathers, is not found in the Hebrew.) Consequently, after Tyre, the word is addressed to Sidon; for both cities belong to one province, and in the Gospel Tyre and Sidon are named together: If in Tyre and Sidon had been done the mighty works which have been done in you, whence also the Canaanite woman, whose daughter was troubled by a demon, because she went out from the borders of Tyre and Sidon, obtained what she had asked.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","Further, according to the spiritual understanding, this seems to us the sense. 'Sidonians' means 'hunters', of whom it is also written in the Psalms: Our soul has been snatched like a sparrow from the snare of the hunters, and again: For he will rescue me from the snare of the hunters and from the harsh word, and in Proverbs the eye of a harlot is the snare of a sinner; but there are also hunters in the good sense, contrary to these, of whom Jeremiah writes: Behold, I will send fishers and hunters who will hunt you from every hill and mountain, whom the Lord also sends to fish, and makes them from fishers of fish into fishers of men; whence the little village of Peter and Andrew too is called by this name — for 'Bethsaida' means in our tongue 'house of hunters' —. Against these, then, the worst hunters, the word of God is directed, and the prophet is commanded to set, or make firm, his face against Sidon, and by no means to waver with every wind of doctrine, but, standing firm in the truth, he will slay the hunters.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.28.22-23":["What, then, are the things with which the Lord threatens Sidon?: 'Behold, I myself will come to you, and I will be glorified in you when I have slain you; and all who live will know that I am the Lord, when I have repaid you what you deserve. And I will send pestilence and blood into your streets' — and rightly are the streets said to be Sidon's: for broad indeed and spacious is the way that leads to death —; and those who stood badly will fall slain by the sword round about, that sword which the Lord came to send upon the earth to separate what was badly joined; and those who remain will know that he is the Lord.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.28.22-26":["Now the divine word foretells that it too is to be taken by the Babylonians, and that by the weight of its evils it is to know that he is the Lord, when he has judged it and been sanctified in it and consumed it by famine and pestilence and the sword, so that it falls in its streets and is no longer a stumbling block to the people of God, nor assails them frequently; and when this has been done, the people of Israel, whom he had scattered among various nations, are to return to their own land, and he is to be sanctified in them, not, as in Sidon, in the bad sense but in the good, when he has had mercy on them, and they have dwelt in the land which he gave to their father Jacob; and they have dwelt secure and trusting in the Lord, and have built houses and planted vineyards, when he has fulfilled his promises; and then they are to know that he is the Lord, whose promises are most firm. Many refer this to the time of Zerubbabel, Ezra and Nehemiah, when the people of Israel returned and dwelt in the land of Judea; but others hope that it is to be fulfilled in the last time and in the thousand years.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.28.24":["And when they have ceased, there will no longer be a stumbling block of bitterness and a thorn of pain pricking and wounding the people of God.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.28.25-26":["All these things, moreover, are said against the adverse powers: that in the last time they are to be destroyed, and there will be eternal security, when the Lord has gathered the house of Israel, those who discern God with the mind, and has been sanctified in them, and that will be fulfilled which is written: Be holy, for I am holy, and they have dwelt in their own land, of which we also read elsewhere: I believe that I shall see the good things of the Lord in the land of the living, which he had given to Jacob, who had supplanted his elder brother and had deserved to receive his birthright; and they will dwell in it secure, or in hope, and they will build houses and plant vineyards, and they will dwell a second time in hope, according to what is read in Isaiah: You who have been weaned from milk, who have been drawn from the breast, expect tribulation upon tribulation, hope upon hope; and they will build houses, such as he builds in the Gospel who laid his foundations not upon the sand but upon the rock, of which it is written: Unless the Lord builds the house, they have labored in vain who build it, and in Exodus, according to the Seventy: Because the midwives feared the Lord, they made houses for themselves, and many things of this kind; and when the Lord has executed judgment against those who oppose Israel round about, or treat it with insults, then every creature will know that he is the Lord, whose judgments are true and justified in themselves.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.29.1-2":["In the tenth year (or, according to the Seventy, the twelfth), in the eleventh month (or, according to the Hebrew, the twelfth), on the first (or one) day of the month, the word of the Lord came to me, saying: Son of man, set (or make firm) your face against Pharaoh king of Egypt, and you shall prophesy concerning him, and concerning all Egypt you shall speak, and you shall say: Thus says the Lord God. After Sidon, which itself too is situated on the coast of Phoenicia, the word comes to the prophet in the tenth year of the captivity of King Jehoiachin, and in the twelfth month, and on one day of the month, that he should set his face, or make it firm, against Pharaoh king of Egypt and speak concerning all, or to all, Egypt; and that the province of Egypt, situated on the same coast on the other side of Judea, should be described, and the things that are to come upon it be prophesied. But if something must be said about the numbers: the place that one holds among ten days — whence also at the beginning of Genesis it is not said: There was evening and morning, day 'first', but 'one', so as to teach that the same day always comes round again — that same rank the tenth number will hold among ten decades, that is, the number one hundred, the tenth on which the lamb is taken up for the victim, to be sacrificed on the fourteenth day; and in this way, up to a thousand and ten thousand and a hundred thousand and beyond, the order of numbers proceeds by its decades; and after the tenth year the 'twelfth' month is set down, so that the perfect number of the twelve apostles and of the prophets — who are contained in one volume — may be shown; further, according to the Seventy, the 'tenth' month, which in Hebrew is called 'tebeth' and among the Egyptians 'tybi', and among the Romans is called 'Ianuarius' [January] because among them it is the door [ianua] of the year, holds the first place of winter's cold, all heat having been taken away; let it suffice to have said this about the numbers.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.29.2":["But this above all must be inquired: whether it is the same Pharaoh who is named in Exodus and in Isaiah and Jeremiah and Ezekiel and in many other places, and in the Song: To my cavalry among the chariots of Pharaoh I have likened you, my near one, or one and then another; and it seems to me that he is not one, but that among the Egyptians the royal dignity is indicated by this word, just as among the Romans their kings are called 'Caesars' and 'Augusti' — from the first, Gaius 'Caesar', and the second, his adopted son Octavian, who was afterwards named 'Augustus' —, and among the Syrians 'Antiochuses', among the Persians 'Arsacids', among the Philistines 'Abimelech', and, after Alexander, in Egypt 'Ptolemies' down to Cleopatra, at whose defeat at Actium Egypt was made a Roman province; therefore at present the word of the Lord is spoken against any one whatever of the kings of Egypt, who is interpreted as διασκεδάστης, namely 'slayer' and 'mangler' and 'one who cuts down and divides all things with the sword'. This, according to the mystical senses, is to be referred to that power to which Egypt is subject; for a man would never dare to say: The rivers are mine and I made them, nor would he be called a 'dragon', 'great and sitting in the midst of his rivers'; now Egypt is called 'mesraim' in Hebrew, and into our language it is rendered ἐκθλίβουσα, that is, 'straitening' and 'afflicting' those who are subject to it, and not allowing them to lift their eyes to heaven but — according to the Gospel and the example of that woman whom the devil had bent over for eighteen years — always plunging them down toward earthly things. Let us see, then, what the threat is against Pharaoh and all Egypt.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.29.3-7":["Behold, I am against you, Pharaoh king of Egypt, great dragon who lie (or sit) in the midst of your rivers and say: The river is mine, and I made myself (or them). And I will put a bridle (or a snare) in your jaws, and I will make the fish of your rivers stick to your scales (or wings), and I will draw you out of the midst of your rivers, and all your fish shall cling to your scales. And I will cast (or set down) you swiftly (or into the desert), and all the fish of your river upon the face of the earth (or of your field); falling, you shall not be gathered up nor brought together; to the beasts of the earth and the birds of heaven I have given you to be devoured. And all the inhabitants of Egypt shall know that I am the Lord, because you have become (or have been) a staff (or a reed rod) to the house of Israel, when they took hold (or it took hold) of you with their hand and you were broken, and you tore all their shoulder, and when they leaned upon you, you were shattered, and you loosened (or broke) all their reins. For the sake of brevity we mix both editions where they do not differ much from each other; otherwise, where there is great diversity, we set out both. Now he speaks against Pharaoh, the prince of Egypt, and, under his figure, addresses a great power to which Egypt was handed over to be ruled and which nonetheless grew proud against its Creator, claiming for itself dominion over the land and offering itself to the Egyptian peoples to be worshiped; and he speaks, in keeping with the situation of the province, as to a king: that he trusts in the flooding of the Nile and does not greatly desire rains from heaven, and thinks himself his own author, or boasts that the rivers, that is, the διωρυγάς [canals], and the streams of the Nile were made by himself: 'I will not,' he says, 'send an angel, but, O great dragon who lie and settle in the midst of your rivers, I myself will come to you to punish you; for you dared to say: The river Nile is mine and I am my own founder, or: I myself made the river by which all Egypt is watered. And when I come, I will put a bridle in your jaws, or bind with snares your mouth with which you spoke so grandly, and all your allies and leaders, whom they call fish, I will make cling to your wings or scales, so that, drawn out of the river, you may be cast out or set down: for you had lifted yourself up too high, in the desert; and you will fall upon the face of the field, or of your land, because Egypt is a province of plains, and your carcass will not be gathered up nor will you be brought together, that is: you will by no means be buried, but I will give you to the beasts and the birds of heaven to be devoured, so that, when they have seen these things accomplished in you by my judgment, the inhabitants of Egypt may know that I myself am the Lord; and these things will befall you because you deceived my people Israel with your help, so that they trusted not at all in God their creator but in you: you were to them a reed staff — according to Isaiah —, or a hollow rod and a most fragile reed, leaning on which they found their protection worthless, so that, as they rested their weight on you, you were broken and tore the shoulder and the hand by which you were held, and, while they felt secure in you, all their loins were shattered as they fell to the ground and received wounds instead of help'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.29.3":["These things are said by μεταφορὰν [metaphor] as to the king of Egypt, because the province itself too was by no means hostile to Israel, but, while it promised great things, it separated them from the help of God; but according to ἀναγωγὴν [anagogy, the higher sense] we often read the 'dragon' as the opposing power — whence also Pharaoh is called διασκεδάστης, because he separates and disjoins from God, and 'Egypt', that is, μεσραὶμ, is rendered 'those who afflict' and 'those who distress', namely those whom it has been able to subjugate to itself. This dragon is the transgressor of whom Job speaks most fully, and in the Psalms it is written: You broke the heads of the dragon, you gave him as food to the Ethiopian peoples —, and the dragon is called 'great' in comparison with lesser dragons — of which it is sung: You broke the heads of the dragons in the waters, and in another place: This sea, great and spacious, there are creeping things of which there is no number, small animals with great. There the ships will pass through, this dragon which you formed to mock it, of whom it is also said in another place: He is king of all that are in the waters, and the beginning of the Lord's fashioning, who was made to be mocked by his angels —; and he sits, or lies, in the midst of his rivers, not of one river but of many, which we take to be the various heresies, through which he flowed into the Egypt of this world and watered the souls of the seduced, not with rains coming from heaven but with muddy waters from the earth — which Jeremiah does not forbid to drink, saying: What have you to do with the way of Egypt, that you should drink the water 'Geon' — for which in the Hebrew 'Sior' is written, a word which is rendered 'muddy' and 'miry' waters — ?; but that we may understand what the rivers of the dragon of Egypt are, we shall be able to know from their opposites: the Lord speaks of his own rivers: He who believes in me, rivers of living water shall flow from his belly, and to the Samaritan woman: Whoever drinks of the water that I shall give him will not thirst forever, but there will become in him a spring of water leaping up to eternal life; these are the rivers coming from heaven, of which David sings: The rush of the river gladdens 'the city of God', which no doubt signifies 'the Church' —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.29.4":["Let us consider, then, what the dragon's penalty is, or what his punishment. There follows: And I will put a bridle (or a snare) in your jaws — like which is that passage of Job: But you will bring the dragon with a hook and you will put a halter around his nostrils. He will trust that the Jordan may enter into his mouth; with his eye he will take it in. But he will pierce his nostrils, with a ring his lip —. Now the Lord puts a bridle in the jaws of this dragon and pierces his lips and binds them with the circle of a ring when, through churchmen who are learned in the holy Scriptures, he imposes silence on him and all the doctrines of perversity are dissolved; and he glues the fish of his rivers to his wings or scales — by which the heretics through pride hasten to the heights —, so that they too, joined with the dragon, may make one body with him; and they are coupled to him either in fellowship of error or in likeness of punishment, just as: He who cleaves to the Lord is one spirit. Nor indeed does the Egyptian dragon have one river, but many rivers, with which he waters Egypt, low and cast down and having no mountains in it, nor the waters of Siloam, which go with silence, but 'muddy' and 'miry' ones; whence also the Lord draws him out of the midst of his rivers, so that he may by no means lie upon them nor sit in them, and all the fish cling to his scales — clinging over the whole body of the dragon, according to the quality of their vices, either to the head or to the belly or to the tail and the extremities — so that, when the dragon is drawn out, the fish too which cling to him may be drawn out together.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.29.5-7":["'And I will cast you,' he says, 'into the desert, so that you will by no means find any to deceive, or certainly I will set you down from the summit of your pride, and I will set you down swiftly' — according to that saying of the Apostle: But may the God of peace crush Satan under your feet swiftly —, so that, when the dragon is crushed and set down, the fish of his river too may be set down, and the author of crimes may fall, who formerly thought that he stood and ran about through the whole world; and let him fall upon the face of his own earth, to which he was cast headlong from heaven, so that he may by no means any longer be gathered in the churches of the heretics, nor be brought together or buried in those who believed him, once they have been freed from him, but may be given to be devoured by the beasts of the earth and the birds of heaven: the beasts, of which it is written: Do not hand over the soul that confesses to you, and the birds of heaven, which eat the seed beside the way and are interpreted by the Savior to be devils — for not only the prince of wickedness but also his disciples are named devils, according to what is said of Judas: Did I not choose you twelve? and one of you is a devil —; and they are called birds of heaven because they promise themselves lofty things; so that, after the dragon with his fish has been set down and cast out, and handed over to be devoured by the beasts of the earth, which have nothing of gentleness in them, and by those who are carried about by every wind of doctrine, then the inhabitants of this world may understand that he himself is the Lord; and the whole cause of the punishments is that he promised help to the house of Israel in vain and was to it a rod or reed staff, hollow and empty, having nothing of fullness in it, because he could not say: But we have all received of his fullness, since Scripture has commanded: You shall not appear in the sight of the Lord your God empty (or hollow); and that we ought not to ask help from Egypt, Scripture testifies elsewhere too: Woe to those who go down into Egypt for help.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.29.6-7":["Of a reed staff of this kind Rabshakeh too vainly reproaches Hezekiah king of Judah, saying: Behold, trusting in this reed rod, and a broken one, upon Egypt; whoever leans on it, it goes into his hand: so is Pharaoh king of Egypt and all who trust in him; and he indeed was lying about a righteous king in recalling these things — he who is interpreted as 'much with a kiss': for he was drunk from the golden cup of Babylon, and therefore he was thrusting his own things upon the people, while it was confessing the Lord —; here, however, Pharaoh is reproached for having been to the house of Israel a rod or reed staff, promising them help that was vain and fragile and soon to be shattered. But that we may be able to know which is the reed staff on which the house of Israel ought not to trust, we shall be able to understand it from the contrary staff and rod of the Lord, of which it is said to the Lord: Your rod and your staff, they have comforted me; this rod Aaron also had, which devoured the Egyptian dragons, and when he struck the banks of the Nile and gnats were produced in all Egypt; Moses too, according to the Seventy: Stretched out this rod and lifted it to heaven, and the Lord brought the south wind upon the land all that day and all night, and in the morning the wind lifted and brought locusts upon all the land of Egypt; of this rod, I think, it is also written in the book of Numbers that it blossomed and budded into a nut or almond; this the Apostle had, saying: What do you wish? Shall I come to you with a rod?; and those who celebrated the Passover held staffs in their hands, without which, as they support the weakness of the human body, they could not eat the flesh of the lamb; this is the rod from the root of Jesse, upon which the seven spirits rested. But not such is Pharaoh, nor the staff of Egypt and the reed rod, which deceives those who take hold of it and tears their shoulders, that is, their strength; and whoever leans upon it, his loins are loosened, and he cannot stand nor, girded in the reins, celebrate the Passover; which befits those whose hearts and reins God searches.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.29.8-10":["Therefore thus says the Lord God: Behold, I will bring a sword upon you, and I will kill from you man and beast; and the land of Egypt shall be a desert (or destruction and solitude), and they shall know that I am the Lord. Because he said (or you said): the river is mine, and I made it (or the rivers are mine, and I made them); therefore behold, I am against you and against your rivers, and I will make the land of Egypt into solitudes, laid waste by the sword (or destroyed) from the tower of Syene (or from Migdol and Syene) to the borders of Ethiopia. The foot of man shall not pass through it, nor shall the foot of beast walk in it, and it shall not be inhabited for forty years; and I will make the land of Egypt deserted in the midst of deserted lands, and its cities in the midst of overthrown cities: they shall be desolate for forty years, and I will scatter (or sow) the Egyptians among the nations, and I will winnow them into the lands. For thus says the Lord God: After the end of forty years I will gather Egypt (or the Egyptians) from the peoples (or from the nations) among whom they had been scattered, and I will bring back the captivity of Egypt, and I will settle them in the land of Pathros, in the land of their birth (or in the land from which they were taken); and they shall be to you for a kingdom (or a lowly principality). Among the other kingdoms it shall be the lowliest, and it shall no longer be lifted up over the nations, and I will diminish them so that they do not rule over the nations (or so that they are not many among the nations). Nor shall they any longer be to the house of Israel for confidence (or for hope), teaching iniquity, so that they flee and follow them (or they recall them to the memory of iniquity, so that they follow them), and they shall know that I am the Lord God. Join what follows to what precedes: 'Because you were a reed staff to the house of Israel, and were not only broken in its hand but — according to Isaiah — pierced its hand, and now you have torn its shoulder and were yourself shattered, and loosened the loins of those leaning on you, therefore I will bring upon you the sword of enemies, and I will lay waste beasts together with men, and the land of Egypt will be reduced to solitudes, and a second time the Egyptians will know that I am the Lord. Nor will I be content with this end, but because he burst out into such great blasphemy as to say that the rivers and all the abundance of Egypt were his, therefore I will take away both him who had called himself creator and the rivers which he had boasted were created by himself, and I will reduce the land of Egypt to a very long solitude, laid waste by the sword from the tower of Syene to the borders of Ethiopia'. For 'tower', which in Hebrew is called 'magdal', the Seventy put the name of a place, so as to write 'magdolum' [Migdol]; and the tower of Syene remains to this day, a fortress subject to Roman rule, where the cataracts of the Nile are, and up to which place the Nile is navigable from our sea.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","Now the cloud of allegory must be discussed, and with the same brevity we shall try to avoid the length of this explanation too. The word is to the dragon, who had said: The rivers are mine and I made them, that the Lord himself is bringing upon him the sword of which it is written in Isaiah: My sword has become drunk in heaven: now it will descend upon the earth, to destroy from him man and beast, whatever seems to have either reason or simplicity; and to destroy not absolutely, but as to the dragon, so that they may perish to the dragon and live to God; and that the land of Egypt may become destruction, according to the sense given above, in which it perishes to the dragon, and after destruction be reduced to solitude, ceasing to have the worst of guests; and then men and beasts and the destroyed land will know that he himself is the Lord — in that sense which is written in Psalm 77 (78): When he slew them, then they sought him: For everyone who seeks will find, and it is God's mercy that the abundance of this world should perish —, and that the streams of Egypt may be dried up, nay rather that their land may be in solitudes and be laid waste by the sentence of the Lord from the tower of 'Soene' [Syene], which means 'circle', so that it has nothing straight in it, up to the land of the Ethiopians, who are called lowly, so that, namely, all pride which had raised itself against the knowledge of God may be destroyed, and be humbled unto its own salvation.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.29.10-11":["He says, therefore, that all Egypt is to be ravaged up to the borders of Ethiopia, to which the farthest region of Egypt is joined, so that the foot of man does not pass through Egypt nor are beasts found in it, and it is not inhabited for forty years — for the Egyptians are spared, and, because they were once hosts to Israel, their sentence is shorter in time: Tyre kept its sabbaths for seventy years and so was restored to its former state; the captivity of Judea too and the overthrow of the temple completed seventy years, until Cyrus king of the Persians: For the mighty shall mightily suffer torments.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.29.11-14":["Nor may the foot of man, that is, anything rational, pass through Egypt, nor the foot of beast walk in it — so that it may not hold any of the simple, whom Pharaoh, letting the men go, wished to keep in Egypt, while Moses opposed him and wished to free the beasts too from the captivity of Egypt —. And it shall not, he says, be inhabited for forty years — which number is always one of affliction and punishment: whence Moses and Elijah and the Savior himself fasted forty days and nights, and for forty years the people was in the wilderness, so that afterwards, circumcised at Gilgal, it might be freed from the reproach and disgrace of Egypt; in the mystery of this number this same prophet too slept forty days on his right side for the tribe of Judah, and the people is announced as about to serve in Egypt four hundred years, which make forty decades or four ἑκατοντάδας [hundreds]; the rains of the flood too bring shipwreck on the world for forty days: for it was just that he who, through the four elements of the world, of which all things are said to consist, had offended God while he loved and cherished them, should be punished in that very number; and that Israel, which had sinned against the sabbath, should endure a punishment of seven decades, that is, of seventy years —; and the land of Egypt is made deserted, and its cities in the midst of the overthrown lands and cities, which were not built of stones but of brick and straw, so that Egypt, which had been badly joined together, may be scattered and winnowed into the lands, namely so that the grain may be separated from the straw; and, when the end of forty years is completed, there may be a restoration of Egypt, and its captivity be brought back and settled in the land of 'Phatures' [Pathros], which means 'trampled bread', where that bread which had said: I am the bread that came down from heaven, was trampled by heretical perversity; so that, when they have come to the Church, they may dwell in the trampled bread, and by no means be lifted up into pride, but be a lowly kingdom.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.29.12-16":["And when he says: I will make the land of Egypt deserted in the midst of deserted lands, he means the Philistines, the Edomites and the Moabites and all the other regions against which the prophet's oracle stands above —; then he will scatter or sow the Egyptians among the nations and winnow them into the lands, and, because The Lord is merciful and compassionate, patient and of many mercies, after forty years there is the restoration of Egypt, and the whole captivity will be brought back to its ancient soil and settled in the metropolis, which is called Pathros, where it arose and from which it set out, only on condition that, for its own good, it loses its ancient pride and is a lowly kingdom, indeed the lowliest of all nations, and is no longer lifted up against the nations nor has rule over them, but, reduced to fewness, by no means deceives the house of Israel by confidence in itself, nor teaches them iniquity, or recalls them to the memory of iniquity, in that, having forsaken the help of God, they sought the support of Egypt; and all these things will happen so that a third time the Egyptians may know that he himself is the Lord. These things we have touched on in brief discourse, laying the foundations of the history.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.29.15-16":["And so that, even when they have been restored to their former state, they may cast themselves down through humility because they lived in Egypt and built cities of brick, and among the many kingdoms of the Church may be lowly, and may know against whom they have sinned; and that Egypt may no longer be lifted up over the churches spread through the whole world, but be reduced to fewness, and few may remain in it among the nations, according to what is written: Give them, O Lord. What will you give them? Give them a barren womb and dry breasts, so that they may by no means rejoice and exalt themselves in the multitude of the deceived, but be reduced to a few: For many are called, few chosen. And may they no longer deceive the house of Israel, that is, the Church, nor promise them a vain hope and confidence, teaching iniquity, so that they flee the discipline of the Church and follow Egyptian pleasures. And these things will happen so that a third time the Egyptians may know that he himself is the Lord; which seems to me to be said for this reason: that the first knowledge of the Egyptians may be in the flesh, the second in the soul, the third in the spirit: first upon the earth, second when the life of this world is ended, third after the resurrection.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.29.17":["And it came to pass in the twenty-seventh year, in the first and on one day of the month, the word of the Lord came to me, saying: Son of man, Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon made his army serve a great service against Tyre; every head made bald and every shoulder rubbed bare, and wages were not paid to him, nor to his army, over Tyre for the service with which he served me against it. Therefore thus says the Lord God: Behold, I will give Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon in the land of Egypt, and he will carry off its multitude, and plunder its booty, and seize its spoils, and it will be wages for his army, and for the work for which he served against it. I have given him the land of Egypt because they labored for me, says the Lord God. In that day a horn shall sprout for the house of Israel; and to you I will give an open mouth in their midst, and they shall know that I am the Lord. The question is asked how, after the tenth year of the preceding discourse, the twenty-seventh year is immediately set down, and in what follows the tenth and the twelfth, and in the final description of the temple the twenty-fifth; but the solution is easy: for since both the preceding prophecy and this one which is now spoken are woven together concerning Egypt, although it was made at different times, nevertheless, because it is prophesied concerning one province, they are joined to each other; which we also frequently read in Jeremiah, that the times are described in reversed order: for in certain places the deeds of Zedekiah are related first, and afterwards those of Jehoiakim, who was before him; in the Psalms, however, because it is lyric song, the order of history is not sought.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","And lest we seem to pass anything over, according to the spiritual understanding we inquire where we have read this number, that is, the twenty-seventh: in the book of Genesis Scripture testifies that in the six hundredth year of Noah's life, in the second month, on the twenty-seventh day of the second month, the waters of the flood were brought upon the face of the earth, and afterwards in the seventh month of the same year, which is near to the sabbath, and on the twenty-seventh day of the same seventh month, the waters of the flood ceased, and the ark of Noah rested upon the mountains of 'Ararat', which means 'of Armenia'; from which we understand that the number is a middle one and can be applied to either side: since in it both the wrath of God begins with the flood, and his clemency is shown in the seventh month on the same number of days; and because among the Hebrews the month, which among us is divided by the Kalends and Nones and Ides, is reckoned according to the course of the moon — whence also by the Greek word μήνη, that is, 'moon', it took its name from the month —, this has been said: that on the twenty-seventh day of the moon a little light remains, lest his wrath be entirely without mercy; but when the whole orb of the moon is filled, then both the Passover is celebrated and all the greatest solemnities. This we have set down briefly, so that we may know that in this number good and evil things are contained alike: good things for Nebuchadnezzar, whose labor is repaid with wages, evil things for the Egyptians, whose devastation is announced.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.29.18-20":["Let us speak first, then, according to the letter. When Nebuchadnezzar was besieging Tyre and could not bring rams, engines and mantlets up to the walls — because it was surrounded by the sea —, he ordered the countless multitude of his army to carry stones and earth for a mound and, having filled up the sea in between, or rather the very narrow strait, he made the neighboring shore continuous with the island; when the Tyrians saw this now all but completed, and the foundations of the walls were being shaken by the battering of the rams, whatever precious things the nobility had in gold, silver, clothing and various furnishings, it put on ships and carried off to the islands, so that, when the city was taken, Nebuchadnezzar found nothing worthy of his labor; and because in this respect he had obeyed the will of God, some years after the captivity of Tyre Egypt is given to him; and Tyre was much more savage than Egypt: for the former attacked Jerusalem, the latter promised vain help — and it is one thing to deceive hope through weakness, another to fight against the people of God —. This, then, is what he says: 'Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon, in the siege of Tyre, made his army serve me, to fulfill my will: Every head made bald and every shoulder rubbed bare of those carrying baskets of earth and stones, by which shoulders are rubbed and the head is made bald; and yet neither he nor his army found anything worthy in Tyre. And since he served me in this and fulfilled my will against Tyre, therefore I will give him the land of Egypt'; which some say was fulfilled under Nebuchadnezzar, others under Cambyses son of Cyrus, who laid waste Egypt as far as Ethiopia, so that he killed Apis and destroyed all their images, for which reason they think he was turned to madness and was pierced through with his own dagger in a fall from his horse — Herodotus narrates this history most fully, where all Egypt too is described by districts and villages and forts, and the source of the Nile and the peoples of that nation and the measurement of the land in circuit up to the desert of Ethiopia, and the shores of the Great Sea, and the borders of Libya and Arabia are shown —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","Let these things have been said of the prophecy according to the letter, or rather according to the truth; but from the fact that Nebuchadnezzar received the wage of a good work, we understand that even in the case of the heathen, if they have done anything good, it is not passed over without reward by the judgment of God; whence also through Jeremiah Nebuchadnezzar is called the dove of God, because he served God's will against a sinful people. And I will bring, he says, my servant Nebuchadnezzar; from which it is clear that we are condemned by comparison with the Gentiles, if they do by natural law the things which we neglect even when written; of which the apostle Paul argues most fully to the Romans.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.29.18":["For in another prophecy too, which is seen to be against the Egyptians, the same Scripture weaves in: Son of man, Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon has made his army serve a great service against Tyre: every head made bald, and every shoulder rubbed bare, and no wage has been paid to him, nor to his army, for Tyre (Ezek 29). By this he signifies that a mound was heaped up over a long time by the army of Babylon, so that he tried beforehand to do what Alexander did afterwards, joining the island to the mainland (Quintus Curtius, book 4).\n— Commentary on Isaiah","We read in the histories of the Assyrians that the besieged Tyrians, when they saw no hope of escape, embarked on ships and fled to Carthage, or to other islands of the Ionian and Aegean sea. Hence too it is said in Ezekiel: No wage has been paid to him, nor to his army, for Tyre (Ezek 29): because all the wealth of the city had been carried away, and all the nobles had left it empty.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"EZK.29.21":["The cause of the Lord's anger against Egypt is this: that it deceived the people of God by its help, so that it would not hope in the Lord and would provoke him to wrath: 'In that day,' he says, 'on which Egypt has been taken, the horn of the house of Israel will sprout' — no doubt signifying the royal line; which some refer to Zerubbabel son of Shealtiel, who was born of the stock of David through Jeconiah, others to the last time, when they think that Elijah too will come; but we, understanding the horn of the Lord as concerning Christ, touch lightly on the present history —. 'And when,' he says, 'O prophet, this has first been fulfilled, then your mouth will be opened, and your prophecy will by no means hang on a doubtful promise but will be seen accomplished in deed, so that those who have heard may know that I am the Lord, for whom to have said is to have done'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.30.1-3":["We have set down these things like elementary lessons for little children, so that through letters, syllables and nouns and the joining of words they may arrive at the reading of prose or verse. Now let us approach the tropology, and let us display the vast sea of discussion on a small panel, as it were, of a painting. The Word of the Lord, which was always in the Father, came to the prophet and calls him not by his own name but 'son of man', which in the holy Scriptures is always taken in a good sense, at least in the singular number — as in this same prophet and in Daniel and in the Gospel —; otherwise, in the plural number, it is read in the opposite sense — as in that verse: Sons of men, their teeth are weapons and arrows, and their tongue a sharp sword, and again: Sons of men, how long heavy of heart? The lion indeed will roar, and who will not fear? The Lord has spoken, and who will not prophesy? —. Howl, he says, woe, woe for the day, for the day is near, and the day of the Lord draws near, a day of cloud; it will be the time (or the end) of the nations; the consummation is twofold: either the general one of all, when the end of the world has come, or the particular one of each individual, when the time of death has pressed upon him; and it is called 'near' because, compared with eternity, all time is short — whence Jacob too, of the hundred and thirty years he had lived: Few, he says, and very evil are my days, and the psalmist, discussing the frailty of the whole human race, says: Our days pass away like a shadow; reflecting on this, we shall neither be lifted up by power nor brood over riches nor rejoice in good fortune, knowing that all things are soon to be taken away —; and fittingly it is called the 'day' 'of the Lord', when the whole way of life of this world will be destroyed and, error removed, the one truth will appear; 'and a day of cloud and mist' — for no one, fearless and uncertain of the sentence, awaits the judge without dread —, and 'it will be the time, or the end, of the nations' — not of the one Egyptian nation, but of all, so that it may become clear that the prophecy concerns all the nations —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.30.1-5":["And the word of the Lord came to me, saying: Son of man, prophesy and say: Thus says the Lord God: Howl, woe, woe for the day, for the day is near, and the day of the Lord draws near, a day of cloud; it will be the time of the nations. And a sword will come upon Egypt, and there will be terror in Ethiopia, when the wounded have fallen in Egypt and its multitude has been carried off and its foundations destroyed. Ethiopia and Libya and the Lydians, and all the rest of the common throng, and Chub, and the sons of the land of the covenant with them will fall by the sword. Thus says the Lord God: And those who prop up Egypt will collapse, and the pride of its dominion will be destroyed; from the tower of Syene they will fall in it by the sword, says the Lord of hosts. And they will be laid waste in the midst of desolate lands, and its cities will be in the midst of deserted cities; and they will know that I am the Lord, when I have set fire in Egypt, and all its helpers have been crushed. On that day messengers will go out from my face in triremes to crush the confidence of Ethiopia, and there will be terror among them on the day of Egypt, for without doubt it will come. Thus says the Lord God: And I will make the multitude of Egypt cease by the hand of Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon. He and his people with him, the mightiest of the nations, will be brought to destroy the land; and they will unsheathe their swords against Egypt, and will fill the land with the slain. And I will make the channels of the rivers dry, and I will deliver the land into the hand of the most wicked, and I will lay waste the land and its fullness by the hand of foreigners. I the Lord have spoken. Thus says the Lord God: And I will destroy the images, and I will make the idols cease from Memphis, and there will no longer be a leader in the land of Egypt, and I will put terror in the land of Egypt. And I will destroy the land of Pathros, and I will set fire in Tahpanhes, and I will execute judgments in Alexandria; and I will pour out my indignation upon Pelusium, the strength of Egypt, and I will slay the multitude of Alexandria. And I will set fire in Egypt; Pelusium will be in pain like a woman in labor, and Alexandria will be laid waste, and in Memphis there will be daily distresses. The young men of Heliopolis and of Bubastus will fall by the sword, and they themselves will be led captive. And in Tahpanhes the day will grow black, when I have broken there the scepters of Egypt and the pride of its power has failed in it; a cloud will cover her, and her daughters will be led into captivity. And I will execute judgments in Egypt, and they will know that I am the Lord. (LXX: \"And the word of the Lord came to me, saying: Son of man, prophesy and say: Thus says Adonai the Lord: O day, for the day is near, and the way of the Lord is near, a day of cloud; it will be the end of the nations. And a sword will come upon the Egyptians, and there will be confusion in Ethiopia, and the wounded will fall in Egypt, and they will carry off its multitude, and its foundations will be cut down. Persians and Cretans and Lydians and Libyans, and all the mixed peoples, and some of the sons of my testament will fall by the sword. Thus says Adonai the Lord: And the supports of Egypt will fall, and the insolence of its strength will come down; from Migdol to Syene they will fall in it by the sword, says Adonai the Lord. And it will be made desolate in the midst of desolate regions, and its cities will be in the midst of deserted cities; and they will know that I am the Lord, when I have set fire upon Egypt, and all who help it have been crushed. On that day messengers will go out from my face, 'siim', hastening to destroy the hope of Ethiopia, and there will be confusion among them on the day of Egypt, for behold, it will come. Thus says the Lord God: And I will destroy the multitude of the Egyptians by the hand of Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon, of him and of his people with him: pestilent men who have been sent from the nations to destroy the land; and they will all unsheathe their swords against Egypt, and the land will be filled with the wounded. And I will make their rivers deserted, and I will deliver the land into the hand of the most wicked, and I will destroy the land and its fullness by the hands of aliens. I the Lord have spoken. For thus says the Lord Lord: and I will make the nobles fail from Memphis and the princes from the land of Egypt, and they will be no more, and I will put terror in the land of Egypt. And I will destroy the land of Pathros, and I will set fire upon Tanis, and I will execute vengeance in Diospolis; and I will pour out my fury upon Sain, the strength of Egypt, and I will destroy the multitude of Memphis. And I will set fire upon Egypt; and Sais will be thrown into confusion with confusion, and in Diospolis there will be a breach, and waters will be poured out. The young men of Heliopolis and of Bubastus will fall by the sword, and the women will be led captive. And in Tahpanhes the day will grow dark, when I have broken there the scepters of Egypt, and the insolence of its strength will perish there; and a cloud will cover her, and her daughters will be led captive. And I will execute judgments in Egypt, and they will know that I am the Lord.\") After the twenty-seventh year of the captivity of King Jehoiachin, he will return to the present time when he had begun to prophesy against Egypt — that is, to the tenth year and the tenth month, on the eleventh day of the month —, and he will say that he was commanded by the Lord to speak to all the nations and, together with them all, specially to Egypt. What then are the things he speaks?: 'Howl, woe, woe for the day, for the day is near, and the day of the Lord draws near, not glowing with a bright sun, but covered with clouds, and bringing a Babylonian storm; and when the sword has begun to lay Egypt waste, there will be terror in Ethiopia, which is neighbor to Egypt, lest the Babylonian blade reach even to itself: for the wounded will fall in Egypt, and its multitude will be carried off, and everything will be destroyed down to the foundations, so that, if anyone is found in Egypt from Ethiopia and Libya and Lydia and, in general, from the various peoples and 'Chub' — which Symmachus renders as 'Arabia' — and from the sons of the land of the covenant, that is, from the people of the Jews, he will fall by the sword with them — for which nations the Seventy put: Persians and Cretans and Lydians and Libyans, and all the mixed peoples, and the sons of my testament will fall by the sword with it —'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.30.4":["There follows: A sword will come upon Egypt — the 'sword' turning and flaming: the divine word, which divides the good from the evil and consumes the most wicked with its fire; and 'upon Egypt': of this world —, so that there is terror in Ethiopia: those who dwell beyond Egypt in the night of error and in darkness, and whose blackness is turned to whiteness either with difficulty or not at all. And the wounded will fall in Egypt, those who had stood badly in wickedness. 'And the multitude of Egypt will be carried off' — For wide and spacious is the way that leads to death —. And its foundations will be destroyed, so that nothing of its former firmness may remain in Egypt, but, the most wicked foundations abandoned, the foundation of Christ may be laid, on which the Church may be built — every indeed planting which the heavenly Father has not planted will be rooted up; whence Jeremiah too is commanded to destroy what had been built, so that he may build better things —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.30.5":["'There will perish in Egypt also the Ethiopians, Libyans, and Lydians — or, according to the Seventy, Persians, Cretans and Lydians and Libyans — and Chub, that is, the Arabs, and all the rest of the common throng — whom the Seventy translated as hybrids and mixed peoples —'; all of whom, according to the diversity of vices and the interpretation of the names which we set out in the vision of Tyre, we can understand as different nations — whence the Apostle too: You, he says, Gentiles in the flesh, who are called the foreskin: for he would never have said Gentiles in the flesh unless there were others in the spirit; and in another place: Behold Israel according to the flesh —; whence we must take great care not to return in heart to Egypt, from which we once went out, and be found among the other nations, and perish by the sword — of which it is said in what follows: The young men of Heliopolis and of Bubastus will fall by the sword —, especially since it is added: And the sons of the land of the covenant (or of my testament) will be struck down together by the blade — of whom it is written: Woe to those who go down to Egypt for help, trusting in horses and chariots —; but if men of the other nations are slaughtered in Egypt, how much more the sons of the covenant and testament of God, who, despising the bread of angels, remembered the melons and onion and garlic and cucumbers of Egypt!\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.30.6-8":["And so that we might know that all these nations had been in aid of Egypt, the following passage shows it: 'And those who prop up Egypt will collapse — or the supports of Egypt, that is, its allies —, and all the pride of its dominion — or the insolence of its strength — will be destroyed and brought down from the tower of Syene — which we have said is situated at the farthest borders of Egypt; or 'from Migdol to Syene', as the Seventy translated —. All the cities of Egypt will be deserted, so that they may acknowledge the Lord when the fire of the Chaldeans has laid everything waste and its helpers have been crushed.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.30.6":["Then 'the supports and props of Egypt will collapse', which held it up in wickedness: the subtleties of the dialecticians and the argumentation, the tricks, of the philosophers; 'the insolent and proud dominion will also be destroyed', of those who speak all things through arrogance and count the simplicity of the Church as nothing; for 'from the tower of Syene they will fall in it' — which lies at the farthest limits of Egypt, bordering on Ethiopia and the Blemmyes, where the Nile is unnavigable, and there is the roar of the cataracts, and everything is pathless and full of serpents and venomous creatures —; but if, as we said above, 'Magdolus' means 'magnificence' and 'Soene' 'circle', it is clear that the wealth of Egypt and its insolent strength and magnificence, that is, boasting and exaltations, perish by force as far as the circle of Egypt, where nothing is stable but rolls in an uncertain fall, and comes to ruin.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.30.7-8":["'Then the Egyptian cities will be laid waste and the land will be deserted and no congregation will remain that has any firmness, so that, at the end of things, they may know that he himself is the Lord, when he has sent fire into Egypt', which the Lord desires to burn, so that the hay, wood, stubble that have been built upon the foundation of Christ may be burned up, 'and all the helpers of perverse doctrines may be crushed' — from which it is to be understood that the knowledge falsely so called, and those of whom it is written: Scatter, O Lord, the nations that want wars, are called the allies and helpers of Egypt —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.30.9":["And messengers will have come — according to Aquila and Theodotion 'siim', which Symmachus translated 'hastening', and we rendered as 'triremes': for so we received it from the Hebrews — so that all the confidence of Ethiopia may be crushed, and, when the neighboring province has been made desolate, terror may seize those nearest'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","'On that day', he says, 'messengers will go out from the face of God' — of whom it is written in the Gospel: Their angels always see the face of my Father who is in heaven; and in the parable of the sowing 'the reapers, the angels', that is, 'they are messengers', are sent: To gather all stumbling blocks and cast them into the furnace of fire: where there is weeping of eyes and gnashing of teeth —, and they will hasten to fulfill the command, 'to frighten off, or crush, the confidence of Ethiopia', those who have reached the highest peak of malice; 'and, in the overthrow of Egypt, Ethiopia may be crushed and tremble, because the day of the Lord is one of vengeance and retribution', by which the darkness of sins is put to flight and the light of virtues remains.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.30.10-11":["But so that we might know what this sword was that would lay Egypt waste and terrify Ethiopia, there follows more plainly: And I will make the multitude of Egypt cease by the hand of Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon, who will not only come, but will come accompanied by many nations, so that all Egypt will be filled with the blood of the slain.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.30.10-12":["And I will make, he says, the multitude of Egypt cease by the hand of Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon, to whom they were handed over for him to punish — for he is the enemy and avenger who also received a wage because he had served in the siege of Tyre — the multitude of Egypt: so that the many may cease to be in Egypt, who are always in it — but if anyone should object, asking how that is said in Deuteronomy: In seventy souls your fathers went down into Egypt, but now you have become like the stars of heaven in multitude, it is easily resolved: for he did not set down an example of an earthly multitude but of a heavenly one, which shines with virtues and is full of lights —; against which multitude of Egypt 'the mightiest' of the nations are brought, to destroy the land — for whom the Seventy translated 'pestilent men', which I do not know how it suits those who are brought by the Lord, unless perhaps according to that example: A sending by most wicked angels —; and they will unsheathe their swords against Egypt and fill the land with the slain, or the wounded, so that they may feel themselves slain and wounded, and that Egypt has been destroyed to such a degree and has come to nothing that all the rivers of eloquence by which the Egyptian rush and reed of errors were watered dry up, and they are delivered into the hand of the most wicked, who torment them, and the fullness of the land of Egypt, which had grown evilly, is wiped out by the hand of those alien to God — for not good but evil angels are set over the torments —. These things must be, 'so that they may know that the Lord has spoken' — which is frequently added so that those who hear may know that the words are not the prophet's but the Lord's, whose commands cannot be void —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.30.12":["And so great will be the wrath of the Lord that the channels of the rivers — that is, the διωρύγες [canals] of the Nile — will be dried down to the ground, and the fullness of Egypt will be made desolate by the hand of pestilent men — or of the most wicked men —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.30.13-15":["There follows: And I will destroy the images (which the Seventy translated abominations), and I will make the idols (or the nobles) cease from Memphis, and a leader (or princes) in the land of Egypt; they will be no more: for it belongs to the most merciful Lord to overthrow evil works, so that no likeness that falsely counterfeits the image of truth may remain in Egypt; and that the nobles too may perish from 'Memphis', which means 'from the mouth', from which they fashioned all the idols, so that, with the heads and masters of the idols cut off, there may be no prince in Egypt, and 'all Egypt may be filled with terror and destruction'; of whose cities it is said: And I will destroy the land of Pathros, and I will set fire in Tahpanhes (or in Tanis), and I will execute judgments in 'No' (which the Seventy translated 'Diospolis'); and I will pour out my indignation upon Sain, the strength of Egypt; 'Phatures' means 'trampling of bread', 'Tanis' 'a lowly command', 'Diospolis' — in place of which the Hebrew has 'No' — 'rest', 'Sain' 'temptation'; by these names are shown the various conventicles of heretics and of all lies, who trample on and despise the bread of the Church, and follow a lowly command that does not lead to heavenly things, and give themselves over to pleasures, and are at rest — such as the rich man clothed in purple of whom we read in the Gospel —, and are slaves to temptations — one of whom sought from the Lord to have power to tempt Job —. All these the Lord destroys and sets on fire; and he will pour out his indignation upon it, and he scatters the strength of Egypt, so that, reduced to nothing, they may no longer entice the people of God and make them hope in their help, so that, truth forsaken, they seek a lie and are wounded by their protection as by a staff of reed.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.30.13":["'For my words cannot be void; and my indignation will advance to this point: that the images of Egypt are destroyed and the idols cease from Memphis', which to this day is the metropolis of Egyptian superstition — or the nobles and princes from Memphis and from the land of Egypt —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.30.14":["And so great a terror will possess all Egypt that the whole land of Pathros will perish, and fire will lay waste Tahpanhes — or, as the Seventy translated, Tanis —. And I will execute, he says, judgments in 'Alexandria', which is so called today; but it has the ancient name 'No', which Aquila, Symmachus and Theodotion translated as it stands in the Hebrew; in its place the Seventy, intending I know not what, said 'Diospolis', which is a small city of Egypt; but we have put 'Alexandria' for 'No' — by 'anticipation', which in Greek is called πρόληψις, according to that line of Vergil: and he came to the Lavinian shores: not that at that time, when Aeneas came into Latium, they were called Lavinian, but because they were later named Lavinian, so that the passage might become clearer to the reader's understanding —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.30.15":["And I will pour out, he says, my indignation upon 'Sain', which we rendered as 'Pelusium'; and it is called the strength of Egypt because it has a very safe harbor and the trade of the sea is carried on there most of all — whence the poet too calls the lentil Pelusian, not because this kind of legume is grown there, or chiefly there, but because the greatest quantity of it is brought there from the Thebaid and all Egypt along the stream of the Nile —. And I will slay, he says, the multitude of 'Alexandria', which again is set down in the Hebrew as 'No', for which the Seventy translated not 'Diospolis', as above, but 'Memphis'. And, to show that the city was already populous at that time: I will destroy, he says, the multitude of 'No'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","After this it is said: And I will slay (or destroy) the multitude of 'Memphis' — in place of which the Hebrew has 'No', which above the same Seventy translated 'Diospolis', which means 'rest' —: for there are many who seek rest and want to lie on ivory beds and eat suckling lambs; or — according to the Seventy, who translated 'Memphis' —: there are many who say to others: Allow me to remove the speck from your eye, when they themselves have beams.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.30.16":["And I will set fire, that is, the king of Babylon, in Egypt, who, like fire, is to ravage everything. Like a woman in labor 'Sain' will be in pain, that is, 'Pelusium' (or will be thrown into confusion with confusion), and in 'Alexandria' (that is, in 'No') there will be a breach, and waters will be poured out (for which again the Seventy translated 'Diospolis'). Now it is the custom of the Egyptians, because of the flooding of the Nile, to build high embankments along the banks of the Nile; and if these have been broken through the negligence of the guards or by the excessive volume of the waters, the waters do not irrigate the fields lying below but overwhelm and ravage them; by these it is signified that Egypt was to be occupied by the army of the Chaldeans just as it is usually covered and ruined when the waters of the Nile flood and rise beyond measure. In place of the flooding of the waters and the bursting and breaching of the embankments, the Hebrew has: And there will be daily distresses in Memphis, so that where the Nile divides, and where the temple of Apis is and the oracles give answers to those who consult them, there daily distresses come about.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","'And fire is set in Egypt', to consume wordiness and pleasures with its burning. 'All temptation will be in pain, and in Diospolis — which again in the Hebrew is set down as 'No' — there will be a breach', so that the waters are poured out, and every most wicked congregation is scattered and perishes here and there; or, according to the Hebrew: 'In Memphis there will be daily distresses', so that they may render account for every idle word and understand that nothing of their sayings escapes the judgment of the Lord.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.30.17":["'The young men also of Heliopolis — which in Hebrew is called 'On' — and of Bubastus, another city, will fall by the sword, so that the cities themselves, that is, their inhabitants, or their women, are led into captivity — the sex that lies exposed to outrage —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","The young men also of Heliopolis and of Bubastus will fall by the sword — 'Heliopoleos' is called 'On' in Hebrew, which means 'pain'; and 'Bubastus', in the Egyptian language, means 'trial of the mouth' —; all these, who could not bear the pain of the world but pursued the pleasures of Diospolis and trusted in their fluency of speech and, disputing against the other doctrines, had had experiences of victory, will fall by the sword of the word of God; and those who are called not young men at all but, from weakness of mind, women, will be led captive; or the cities themselves, through pain and the boasting of the mouth, will be brought into captivity.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.30.18-19":["But in Tahpanhes, which is a royal city, the scepters of Egypt will be broken, that is, the whole royal line; and when its power has failed, its princes slain, then the day will grow black and everything will be filled with gloom and darkness, so that the city itself is covered with a cloud of sorrow and mourning, and its daughters, that is, the remaining towns, are led into captivity; so that, after I have executed judgments in Egypt and shown myself judge to all, then the Egyptians may know that I am the Lord'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.30.18":["And in Tahpanhes, he says, the day will grow black; 'Taphnae' means 'yielding to the mouth', 'of the devil' being understood; those who have yielded to him will lose the light of truth and exchange day for night, and will perceive that the scepters of Egypt and all its dominion have been broken in Tahpanhes, so that the insolent strength, or the pride of its power, fails in it, and the rays of the sun of righteousness are blinded by a cloud, and not women at all, but daughters, are led into captivity.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.30.19":["'And let the Lord execute not one judgment but many judgments in Egypt' — for just as there are various mansions of the good with the Father, so also there are various judgments of the punishments of Egypt — so that, when all these things have been done, the Egyptians may know that he himself is the Lord, whose judgments are true, justified in themselves.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.30.20":["And it came to pass in the eleventh year, in the first, on the seventh of the month, the word of the Lord came to me, saying: Son of man, I have broken the arm of Pharaoh king of Egypt, and behold, it has not been bound up so that health might be restored to it, that it might be tied with cloths and packed with linen bandages (or that it might receive a poultice) and, its strength recovered, might be able to hold a sword. Therefore thus says the Lord God: Behold, I am against Pharaoh king of Egypt; and I will shatter his arm, strong but broken (or arms strong and set in order), and I will cast the sword down from his hand, and I will scatter (or I will sow) Egypt among the nations, and I will winnow them into the lands. And I will strengthen the arms of the king of Babylon, and I will give my sword into his hands; and I will break the arms of Pharaoh and the slain will groan with groanings before his face (or, as is contained in the Seventy: And he will bring a sword upon Egypt and will plunder its booty and despoil its spoils. There follows:) And I will strengthen the arms of the king of Babylon, and the arms of Pharaoh will fall; and they will know that I am the Lord, when I have put my sword into the hand of the king of Babylon and he has stretched it out over the land of Egypt. And I will scatter Egypt among the nations, and I will winnow them into the lands, and they will know that I am the Lord. He returns to the order of the prophecy: for after the twenty-seventh year he now sets down the eleventh; and this question is contained in the earlier passages too: for although the word of the Lord against Tyre came in the eleventh year, which was set down first, in what follows he speaks to Pharaoh in the tenth year, and again sets down the twenty-seventh, as we said, and now the eleventh — to pass over the other things contained in the order of the psalms: how the third psalm — the psalms in which David changed his face before Abimelech, and on Doeg the Edomite, and when he was found in the cave, and Psalm 50 (51), of penitence, in whose title it is shown that he went in to Bathsheba the wife of Uriah, are earlier psalms than the third, in which he is noted as fleeing from the face of his son Absalom; but in the Psalms the answer is easy: that it is lyric poetry, and in a work of this kind one does not look for the order of history but for songs on the individual deeds —. In history, however, this must be said: that the things said about one matter at different times ought not to be divided in the telling but enclosed in the narrative of one passage; for example, so that the things said about Egypt at one time and another may be learned in one sequence of reading. Though we have said this, the question nevertheless remains why in this same place the word of the Lord came to Pharaoh first in the eleventh year, and afterward in the twenty-seventh, and then in the tenth year, when surely according to order the tenth year should have been noted first, the eleventh second, the twenty-seventh third, at the head of the individual prophecies; to which we can answer this: O the depth of the riches of the wisdom and knowledge of God! How inscrutable his judgments and untraceable his ways!, and in another place: The abyss and wisdom, who will search out?. We can, however, say this: that in the prophets too the order of history is not kept, at least not in all but in certain places, for they do not narrate past things but foretell future ones, as the will of the Holy Spirit has been; but in history, such as the five books of Moses and the volumes of Joshua and of Judges, Ruth also and Esther, Samuel and Malachim [Kings], the book of Chronicles and Ezra, with Nehemiah likewise joined to it, an inverted narrative is nowhere found. Let it suffice to have said this about the order of the years.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.30.21-26":["Now let us see what the prophecy against Pharaoh, or about Pharaoh, is. He says that he has broken his arm, and that it has not been bound up nor tied nor packed with linen bandages nor received a poultice — according to what is written: Not to apply a poultice, nor oil, nor bandages —; had this been done, surely, its strength recovered, he could hold a sword and go forth to wars. Therefore the merciful and compassionate God shatters his arm again, or his arms, so that the sword may fall utterly from his hand and Egypt be scattered among the nations and winnowed into the lands — but in the arm understand strength and might, as Scripture says: Crush the arm of the sinner and the evil one —; and this is crushed in our adversaries when they persecute us indeed but cannot overwhelm us; and for the salvation of the servants of God the Babylonian king often rises up against Pharaoh, so that a more powerful one overwhelms the powerful and the evil are handed over to the worse; and the arms of the most wicked are strengthened by God, so that he who has been set free may know that he himself is the Lord; and it is an advance in virtue to know that he himself is the Lord — on the contrary we can say: The sons of Eli, sons of pestilence, not knowing God, and in the good sense it is written of the kings who did what was right in the sight of God that they knew the Lord —. But what is said in the Seventy: And I will crush his arms, strong and 'set in order', which in Greek is said 'tetagmena', has been corrupted by the fault of the copyists; for they translated not 'tetagmena' but 'tetamena', that is, not 'laid down' but 'stretched out'; in place of which the other translators, according to their custom, put 'lofty'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.31.1-2":["Let these things be said for the present according to the history; most understand them as things that will happen at the consummation of the world, and that King Pharaoh, that is, the power to which Egypt is subject, is compared to the most mighty king of the Assyrians, that is, the Antichrist; and if he falls, surely the other rulers too of this darkness and the princes of the world will fall the more easily. Let us speak, therefore, of the particulars, touching on everything briefly, so that we may seem not so much to discuss and explain obscure things as to have given material to those who wish to explain them. And first it must be discussed why in the eleventh year, the third month, on the first day of the month, the word of the Lord came to Ezekiel, saying: Son of man, say to Pharaoh king of Egypt and to his multitude. To one who is about to speak to Pharaoh, the word of the Lord comes in the eleventh year; for he had not yet come to the twelfth, which is the number of full and consummate virtue — whence also there are twelve sons of Jacob, from whom the twelve tribes are afterwards named; and the names of twelve prophets are contained in one volume; and the apostles are twelve, in whose number, because one, Judas, was a traitor, Matthias is chosen in his place; and the daughter of the ruler of the synagogue, twelve years old, is raised up by the Lord; the αἱμορροοῦσα [the woman with the flow of blood] too received health in the twelfth year —, but because Pharaoh is rebuked and afterwards lamented, therefore the third month and one day are joined to the number of eleven years — whence in what follows, in the twelfth year, in the twelfth month, on the first of the month, the word of the Lord comes to Ezekiel, saying: Son of man, take up a lament over Pharaoh king of Egypt and you shall say to him; for he who is lamented is lamented for this reason, that he may understand how great goods he has lost; and yet, because he was Pharaoh, and his arm had not yet been bound up nor had he recovered his former strength, therefore, in the twelfth year and month, twenty-nine days still remain before the number of twelve years is completed — ; on the first day of the month, then, the word of the Lord comes, and he is commanded to speak to the king of Egypt and to his 'multitude' — in which it is remarkable that the four translators did not say 'people' but 'multitude': For many are called but few chosen, and in the Law it is written: You shall not be with the multitude in evil —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.31.2-3":["And it is said to the king of Egypt: Whom have you become like in greatness (or height) of yours? — because he too exalted himself and is subject to that sentence: Everyone who exalts himself will be humbled —, but yet he is not like the height of the one who was like a cedar, or cypress, in Lebanon: Beautiful in branches and thick with leaves, and lofty in height, and raised his head right up to the clouds — to which, according to David, the truth of God reaches, and which are commanded not to rain a shower upon Israel —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.31.2-9":["And it came to pass in the eleventh year, in the third, on the first of the month, the word of the Lord came to me, saying: Son of man, say to Pharaoh king of Egypt and to his people: Whom have you become like in your greatness? Behold, Assyria like a cedar (or cypress) in Lebanon, beautiful in branches and thick with leaves, and lofty in height, and among the dense leaves (or in the midst of the clouds) its top was raised up; the waters nourished him, the deep raised him up, its rivers flowed round about its roots, and it sent out its streams to all the trees of the region. Therefore its height was raised above all the trees of the region (or of the field), and its shrubs (or branches) were multiplied ※ and its branches (or shrubs) were raised up ⁚ because of many waters. And when it had stretched out its shade, all the birds of heaven made nests in its branches, and under its leaves all the beasts of the forests brought forth young, and under its shade dwelt the assembly of all the nations; and it was most beautiful in its greatness and in the spreading (or multitude) of its shrubs (or branches), for its roots were beside many waters. The cedars (or cypresses) were not loftier than it in the paradise of God, and the firs (or pines) did not equal its top, and the plane trees (or firs) were not equal (or like) to its leaves (or branches); no tree of the paradise of God (or in the paradise of God) was likened to it and to its beauty, since I made it beautiful, and with many and dense leaves: and all the trees of pleasure (or of delights) that were in paradise emulated it. Therefore thus says the Lord God: Because it was lifted up in height, and set its top green and dense (or in the midst of the clouds) — its heart was lifted up in its height —, I delivered it (or him) into the hand of the mightiest (or the prince) of the nations; doing he will do to it (or and he will bring about its destruction): according to its impiety I cast it out. And strangers will cut it down, and the most cruel of the nations (or the pestilent of the nations); and they will throw it down upon the mountains, and in all the valleys its branches will fall, and its shrubs will be broken on all the crags (or plains) of the earth, and all the peoples of the earth will withdraw from its shade, and will leave it. On its ruin all the birds of heaven dwelt (or rested), and in its branches were all the beasts of the region. Therefore all the trees of the waters will not be lifted up in their height, nor will they set their loftiness among the wooded and leafy places (or in the clouds), nor will all that are watered by waters stand in their loftiness, for all have been delivered to death, to the lowest land, in the midst of the sons of men, to those who go down into the pit. Thus says the Lord God: On the day when he went down to the underworld I proclaimed mourning (or it mourned), I covered him with the deep and held back its rivers and restrained many waters; Lebanon was crushed (or darkened) over him, and all the trees of the field were shaken (or failed). At the sound of its fall I shook (or were shaken) the nations, when I led him (or it) down to hell with those who were going down (or go down) into the pit; and all the trees of pleasure (or of delights), choice and splendid in Lebanon, all that were watered by waters, were consoled in the lowest land. For they too with it (or him) will go down (or went down) to hell to those slain (or wounded) by the sword, and the arm (or seed) of each of those dwelling under its shade in the midst of the nations (or perished of their life). To whom have you been likened, O renowned and lofty one among the trees of pleasure? Behold, you have been brought down with the trees of pleasure to the lowest land; in the midst of the uncircumcised you will sleep with those who were slain by the sword; so Pharaoh and all his multitude, says the Lord God. As is my custom, I have blended both editions, where, however, they differed very greatly, so that a double setting-out of the text would not increase the size of the books. And first it must be known that whatever is said about the king of the Assyrians in future time is recorded in the Seventy as already past, and he speaks thus either to the tree or to the prince of the Assyrians, so that it is understood of one person in the masculine as well as in the feminine gender, while the tree is referred to the Assyrian and the Assyrian is named in the tree; nor is any mention made of the Babylonian king in the present discourse, lest he, placed in Chaldea, should seem to provoke the wrath of the masters and rulers against the captive people; but it is said of the king of the Assyrians, who had already at that time been captured by the Chaldeans, and all the strength of the Assyrians lay subject to the Babylonian empire; whence also the Assyrians take captive the ten tribes, that is, Israel, while the Chaldeans overcome the two, that is, Judah and Benjamin, and overthrow the city of Jerusalem. The divine word, then, being about to speak against Pharaoh king of Egypt and his people, used such an opening against him: Whom have you become like in your greatness? Behold, Assyria like a cedar in Lebanon and the rest; and the sense is: 'Do not wonder if you are to be conquered by the Babylonians and to perish, and to lose your empire, and all the wealth of Egypt is to be destroyed, since Assyria, much stronger than you, has been destroyed when the same Chaldean prevailed'; and he describes the power of the Assyrian king under the figure of a tree, a 'cedar' according to the Hebrew, a 'cypress' according to the Seventy, set on Mount Lebanon, which is beautiful in branches and dense with leaves and raised up right to the clouds, which grew so great for this reason, that it is watered not by waters but by deeps, that is, by most abundant waters — for the deep is a multitude of the sound of waters —, so that all the birds made nests in its branches, and under its leaves all the beasts of the forests brought forth young; and lest we should doubt what the birds of heaven were, and what the beasts of the woods, he put it more openly: And under its shade dwelt the assembly of very many nations. The cedars, he says (and cypresses), the firs (or pines) and the plane trees (or cypresses) were not equal to its branches; and, so as not to speak of all the trees one by one: All, he says, the trees of the paradise of God were not likened to it and to its beauty, by which some understand that it is said not of the Assyrian king, but of the adverse power which in the discourse on Tyre is said to have been made among the cherubim and adorned with precious stones, and afterwards by its own fault to have fallen down to earthly things, which in Isaiah too is called 'Lucifer falling', and is marked out by the Lord in the Gospel: I saw Satan like lightning falling from heaven; but let us, in order to follow the history for the present, understand all these things as said ὑπερβολικῶς [hyperbolically], because the Assyrian was of such great power and so oppressed all the nations that he compared himself to the might of the angels; and all things are said μεταφορικῶς [metaphorically] to the Assyrian under the very lofty 'cedar' or 'cypress', and his slaying is called 'cutting down'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.31.3-4":["His beauty too is proclaimed, which was changed into ugliness by a depraved will — so that it is said of him: The Lord will break the cedars of Lebanon —, and he, lofty and set on Lebanon, the highest of mountains, would fall the more heavily the more sublime he had been; and holy Scripture, wishing to show his height, calls him a great top which the waters nourished — not the waters of Siloam: Which go with silence, but the waters of Rezin and the waters of Egypt, of which it is written: What is it to you, that you should drink the water of Geon (or Sior, which means 'turbid' waters)? —. The deep also exalted him — the deep over which darkness was borne at the beginning of the world, and into which in the Gospel the demons beg not to be sent —; the rivers of that same deep were round about his roots — of which it is said: What have you to do with the way of the Assyrians, that you should drink the waters of the rivers?, and in another place: Behold, the Lord will bring upon you the water of the river, strong and abundant, the king of the Assyrians and all his glory —; the deep also sent out its streams to all the trees of the region, so as to water not only the prince of this world but also his allies.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.31.5":["Therefore he was lifted up and grew up above all the tallest trees, and his shrubs were multiplied, and he possessed the empire of the whole world, saying: All these things have been delivered to me; and his branches were lifted up, which had been watered by the waters of the deep.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.31.6":["He stretched out, or spread, his shade, so as to subject all to his sway. In its branches all the birds of heaven made nests — For everyone who commits sin is born of the devil and is counted among the number of his branches; and those birds made nests in his branches which ate the seed beside the way in the Gospel, or, since All have sinned and are in need of the mercy of God, of whom it is said: All have turned aside, together they have become useless, for this reason they made nests in his branches —; there follows: And under its leaves all the beasts of the forests brought forth young, those which have lost the gentleness of Christ, and under its shade dwelt the assembly of very many nations, so that there was no one who was not touched by his shade — except the one who said: Behold, the prince of this world comes, and will find nothing in me. Through one indeed man sin entered into the world, and through sin death, and so death passed through to all men, in which we all sinned; and in Adam we all die; and we have all sinned and are in need of the glory of God —; and yet in that very dwelling the condition is different: some dwell in his branches like birds, promising themselves knowledge in philosophy and in the doctrines of heretics, others like beasts, wild, savage and cruel, others like a multitude of very many nations, so that he signifies those mixed up in various vices and sins.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.31.7-9":["Now Assyria was most beautiful in its greatness and in the spreading of its shrubs, because its root had been watered by many waters, so much so that in the paradise of God none of the cedars and cypresses was higher, nor did firs and pines equal its loftiness, or plane trees were not equal to its leaves; and for this reason none of the trees planted in paradise had been made equal to it, because: No tree of the paradise of God was likened to it and to its beauty — for they were by no means lifted up through pride, but knew their humility, nor did they try with a sacrilegious mind to claim likeness to God for themselves —, and what follows: And all the trees of delights that were in paradise emulated it, we expound thus: 'The trees of paradise emulated the multitude of its branches' — For many are called but few chosen, and: Wide and spacious is the way that leads to death —; for it longs to have as many for salvation as Assyria had for perdition.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.31.10-11":["Whence, because Assyria was lifted up in height, and set its loftiness green and dense (or raised its top right up to the clouds), and its heart was lifted up, so that he said: I will be like the Most High, therefore he was delivered into the hand of the mightiest of the nations — which let us understand as another detestable power, to which he is delivered for punishment: for he is the enemy and the avenger; so that Assyria is understood as the Antichrist, and the mightiest of the nations, to whom he was delivered, as Satan, according to that saying of the Apostle: Whom I have delivered to Satan, that they may learn not to blaspheme —, who: Doing will do to him whatever has been commanded him by the Lord: and this cypress was cast out of the paradise of God on account of its impiety.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.31.11":["And what follows: I delivered it into the hand of the mightiest of the nations, he calls the Babylonian king, so that he may understand that his conquering the Assyrian was not of his own might but of divine power. According to its impiety, he says, I cast it out, so as secretly to warn the Babylonian that he ought not to be cruel nor oppress the people of God which had been delivered to him, lest he suffer things like those of the Assyrian, who was cruel and impious.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.31.12-13":["And strangers and cruel men will cut it down (or the pestilent among the nations) — so that that which is written may be fulfilled: When the pestilent man is scourged, the fool will become wiser, and again: Already the axe is laid to the roots of the tree —, and they will cut it down upon the mountains, so that it falls in its pride. In all moreover the valleys its branches will fall, so that all who were in his retinue are led down to Tartarus and withdraw from its shade and leave it, so that the birds of heaven and the beasts of the earth, which before had been under it and were covered by its shade, afterwards become partakers of its punishment.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.31.12-14":["And strangers and the most cruel of the nations will cut it down (or, according to the Seventy, the pestilent), which he would never say while placed in Chaldea, lest he stir up their madness against his own nation; it is also thrown down upon the mountains, and in all the valleys its branches fall and are broken on the crags, and it is abandoned by all the peoples, that is, by the birds of heaven and the beasts of the field, and it will come to such great destruction that all the trees which were thought lofty in comparison with the Assyrian tree cease, by its example, to be lifted up any longer and raised on high, nor do they stand in their height, but, once it has been cut down, all are cut down together by fear; and what had been said by metaphor becomes clearer: All, he says, have been delivered — kings is understood — to death, to the lowest land, that is, to hell, in the midst of the sons of men, by no means among trees, to those who go down into the pit, by which there is no doubt that he means the infernal regions.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.31.14":["And when the other trees of the forests, or of paradise, see that Assyria the cypress has fallen by the sentence of the Lord, they will not be lifted up in pride, nor will they think themselves anything, nor will they consider the watering of their waters their own riches: because all the branches and the birds of heaven and the beasts of the earth which were under the rule of Assyria are led down to death, to the lowest land — of whom it is written: In his sight all who go down into the earth will fall, and in another place: They will go into the uttermost parts of the earth —, and they will be by no means with the angels, but with the sons of men: Who go down into the pit (or into the ditch) everlasting.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.31.15":["After the cutting down of the Assyrian tree, or on the day when the Assyrian went down to the underworld, the Lord proclaimed mourning, and again by metaphor speaks of him, that he was covered with the deep — according to that which is written: I came into the depth of the sea, and the tempest overwhelmed me —, and that all the rivers, namely all the nations, ceased to water him any longer; and that Lebanon was saddened, or darkened, over her, that is, the tree, or over him, that is, the Assyrian.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","But when he has gone down into the uttermost part of the earth and has become the portion of foxes, by which he is to be torn, then there will be mourning, either of all his allies or of the holy powers, who will grieve that he has gone down to the underworld; whence the Lord says: I covered him with the deep, by whose waters he had before been nourished, and I held back its rivers, that is, the deep's, and restrained many waters, so that they would not water him but rather overwhelm him; Lebanon was saddened over him, in which he had been exalted, and all the trees of the field were shaken, dreading a like cutting down.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.31.16-17":["And lest we should think the discourse is about a tree, there follows: At the sound of its fall I shook the nations — for what fall of a single tree can be so great that all the nations are shaken at its sound? —, when I led it, he says, down to hell — according to the letter, a tree cannot be led down to the underworld — with the others who were going down into the pit, that is, to the underworld; and were consoled in the lowest land, namely in the heart of the earth, all the trees of pleasure (or of delights, which in Hebrew is called 'eden', so as to signify wooded glades and forests), choice and splendid, which were in Lebanon and were watered by waters. For those trees too with it, that is, with the Assyrian, will go down to hell to those slain (or wounded) by the sword — trees are not wounded by swords but felled, nor do they go down to hell —, and the arm, he says (or seed, since among the Hebrews the word 'zara' signifies both), rests in its shade in the midst of the nations, not of the forests at all, but of the nations.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","At the sound of its fall I shook the nations, so that no one would be ignorant of his destruction and of his being led down to the underworld with his other allies; and all the trees of pleasure, that is, of paradise, received consolation, while they see evil things repaid to the evil and good things to the good — and these are the trees of Lebanon, watered by the waters of the rivers of the Lord, with which that tree too which fell had been watered; or all the trees which once were good and were watered by waters and dwelt on the height of Lebanon will receive consolation when they see their prince suffer like torments —; for they too will go down to hell to those slain by the sword of the Lord, and all the strength of the trees will dwell under its shade, that is, in punishment, in the midst of all the nations which will be held by the bars of hell.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.31.18":["At the end an apostrophe is made to the Assyrian himself or to Pharaoh: To whom have you been likened, O renowned and lofty one among the trees of pleasure? 'No one', he says, 'was your equal, but you surpassed all kings in power, and yet with the other kings who were in your retinue you have been brought down to the lowest lands, that is, to the underworld' — as Scripture says: They will go into the uttermost parts of the earth, they will be delivered into the hands of the sword, they will be portions for foxes —. In the midst, he says, of the uncircumcised you will sleep with those who were slain by the sword: he shows what the trees were, what the cedar or cypress, wounded by swords and lying with the uncircumcised; and, to join the last things to the earlier, since he had said above: Whom have you become like in your greatness? Behold, Assyria like a cedar in Lebanon, beautiful in branches and leaves and the rest, he now adds: So is Pharaoh, and all his multitude, says the Lord: 'As', he says, 'the Assyrian, the cedar and cypress, was cast down when the Babylonian cut it down, so also Pharaoh and his people, that is, the tree itself and its branches, will be cut down by the Babylonian'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","After this the discourse is turned to Pharaoh himself, to whom it had been said at the beginning: Whom have you become like in your height?, and it is said to him: To whom have you been likened, O renowned and lofty one among the trees of pleasure? — to the Assyrian, of course, who fell —: 'You too were renowned and lofty among the trees of Eden — that is, of delights and of paradise —, and behold, you have been led down like the Assyrian, with all the trees that were once in delights, to the lowest land and to the underworld; and you will sleep not in rest but in punishments, in the midst of the uncircumcised — that is, the unclean — and with those who fell by the sword of the Lord'; and so that we may know who it is to whom he said: O renowned and lofty one among the trees of pleasure, he puts it more openly: He is Pharaoh and all his multitude (or, according to the Seventy: So is Pharaoh and all his multitude), so that, as the Assyrian above was cut down and fell, so also the Egyptian may be overthrown.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.32.1":["And it came to pass in the twelfth year, in the twelfth month (or the tenth), on the first day of the month, the word of the Lord came to me, saying: Son of man, take up a lament over Pharaoh king of Egypt, and you shall say to him: You have been likened to the lion of the nations, and to the dragon that is in the sea; and you tossed with your horn in your rivers, and you troubled the waters with your feet, and you trampled their rivers (or your rivers). Therefore thus says the Lord God: I will spread my net over you in a multitude of many peoples, and I will draw you out in my dragnet (or I will draw you out on a hook); and I will cast (or I will draw) you onto the land, upon the face of the field I will throw you (or the plains will be filled with you), and I will make dwell (or sit) upon you all the birds of the sky, and I will glut the beasts of the whole earth with you; and I will put your flesh upon the mountains, and I will fill the hills (or the valleys) with your gore (or with your blood). And I will water (or the land will be watered) with the stench of your blood (or with your dung) upon the mountains, and the valleys will be filled with you; and when you have been extinguished I will cover the sky, and I will make its stars grow black: I will cover the sun with a cloud, and the moon will not give its light; all the lights of the sky I will make mourn (or they will grow dark) over you, and I will put darkness upon your land, says the Lord God. And I will provoke the heart of many peoples, when I bring your destruction (or your captivity) among the nations upon a land (or in a land) which you do not know. And I will make stand astonished (or there will be saddened) over you peoples (or many nations), and their kings will tremble over you with exceeding horror, when my sword begins to fly over their faces, and each one will be suddenly dumbfounded for his own life on the day of his ruin (or awaiting their own ruin from the day of your ruin). For thus says the Lord God: The sword of the king of Babylon will come to you; by the swords of the mighty (or of giants) I will cast down your multitude (or your strength) — all of them invincible nations (or pestilent ones from all the nations) —, and they will lay waste (or destroy) the pride (or the insolence) of Egypt, and its multitude will be scattered (or all its strength). And I will destroy all its beasts that were beside many waters (or from many waters), and the foot of man will trouble them no more, nor will the hoof of beasts trouble (or trample) them; then I will make their waters most pure (or so that their waters may at length be at rest), and I will lead their rivers like oil (or so that they flow like oil), says the Lord God, when I have made the land of Egypt desolate (or ruined): and the land will be deserted of its fullness (or with its fullness) when I strike (or scatter) all its inhabitants, and they will know that I am the Lord. It is a lament, or they shall lament him; the daughters of the nations shall lament him; over Egypt and over its multitude (or all its strength) they shall lament him, says the Lord God. We mingle both editions, only in those places in which they disagree; otherwise, where there is one sense, we follow the Hebrew. In many copies, moreover, according to the Seventy 'the twelfth year' and 'the tenth month' is set down, but according to the other translators 'the tenth year' and 'the twelfth month', so that either Jerusalem has already been taken, and Pharaoh's joy, with evils hanging over him, is taken away, or at any rate it is about to be taken, and he should grieve more over his own miseries than rejoice at the captivity of others; and meanwhile, briefly laying down certain foundations of the history, let us run through what the Hebrews understand concerning this passage.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.32.2":["Pharaoh is compared to a lion — not of one nation but of many nations — or to a dragon in the sea, so that he possesses land and waters; he was lifted up into 'pride' by the watering of the rivers — which he calls 'horn' — and, by the multitude of his army, passing through all the waters, he was able to trouble them.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","Let it suffice to have said these things in passing on the simple letter, so that we may come to the spiritual understanding. Nor do I think one must labor greatly to know who Pharaoh king of Egypt is, since we have spoken of it more fully above: namely that power to which Egypt was entrusted, whether one province or the Egypt of the whole world, which was not created a lion but by its own vice was likened to the lion of the nations — for Man, indeed, when he was in honor, did not understand; he was compared to the senseless beasts, and was made like them; of this lion Peter also speaks: Our adversary the devil goes about like a roaring lion, and in Psalm 9 (10) it is written: He lies in wait in secret like a lion in his den; he lies in wait to seize the poor, and Jeremiah says by the Holy Spirit: A lion from the forest has struck them —; but that the devil is called a dragon — for which Aquila translates Leviathan, setting out the name of the dragon — I have shown in the earlier explanation with many testimonies, he who had said: The rivers are mine and I made them. This dragon, then, tossed very many nations with his rivers as with horns; which we can understand of Marcion, Valentinus, Arius and Eunomius, and the other leaders of heresies, who imitate churchmen saying to the Lord Savior: In you we will toss our enemies with the horn, and they do not toss them unto salvation, to lift them from earth to heaven, but to cast them into the deep. Then follows: And you troubled the waters with your feet — according to that saying of the Apostle: But he who troubles you will bear the judgment —; for he does not wish to drink the waters of Siloam, which go in silence, but the 'turbid' and 'muddy' waters of Egypt, which, before they were trampled by the dragon's feet, were pure and flowed in their own order, but after they were troubled by his feet, lost the order of their course — for the heretics do not use testimonies of the Scriptures that cohere with one another, but they trouble everything —; and, because he himself is the enemy and the avenger, the Egyptian dragon not only troubles other people's waters with his feet, but tramples his own rivers, so as not to spare those whom he has once brought under his power — which the righteous man, desiring to avoid it, prays: Let not the foot of pride come against me; and in another place, having been trampled, he asks not to be trampled again: Have mercy on me, O Lord, for man has trampled me; and the enemy man is the devil, of whom another psalm also speaks: That man may no more be magnified upon the earth —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.32.3-4":["Therefore thus says the Lord God: I will spread my net over you, in a multitude of many peoples; the Lord's net, woven by the plan of the Old and New Testament, is cast over the dragon — who dwells in the multitude of peoples, and delights in crowds and multitude —, so as to draw him out in his dragnet, or on his hook — this is the dragnet that is cast into the sea of this world and draws out many fish, some to be chosen and others to be thrown away; or this is the hook of which it is written in Job: But you will draw out the dragon with a hook and put a halter around his nostrils, for which Aquila translates: Drawing out Leviathan with a hook, and with cords you will bind his tongue —; and the dragon — or Leviathan — is drawn out of the sea with the hook — or the Lord's dragnet —, so as to be cast — or stretched out — upon the land, and all his coils, in which he used to hide his snares, may be opened and brought out into public and thrown down upon the land, and he may lie prostrate who had set his mouth in heaven and boasted that he was like the Most High; hence there follows: And I will make dwell (or sit) upon you all the birds of the sky, and I will glut the beasts of the whole earth with you — according to what is written: You gave him as food to the peoples of the Ethiopians —, who are called either birds or beasts of the earth: birds, which, as we have said, snatch the seed beside the way; beasts of the whole earth, made savage by vices; which can be referred to heretics and Gentiles;\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.32.3-6":["Therefore he says that he will spread his net over his multitude and draw him out with his dragnet — or hook —, and cast — or stretch — him out upon the land, him who like a serpent had been wound in many coils, so that he may be devoured by all the birds of the sky. And when not only the birds but all the beasts of the earth have torn him, the rest of his flesh will fill the mountains and valleys and plains, so that all things swarm with worms — and he says this by way of metaphor, so that, after the birds of the sky and the beasts have been glutted with his flesh, that is, his army and the whole multitude, the remaining part may be turned into worms and gore —:\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.32.5":["and what follows: And I will put your flesh upon the mountains, and I will fill your hills with your gore (or I will fill the valleys with your blood), has this sense, that we should understand the birds of the sky and the beasts of the earth as the peoples of the deceived and the cruelty of the Gentiles, and refer the mountains to the leaders of heresies — such as Valentinus and Marcion were —, and the hills to their successors who are glutted with the dragon's gore, or the valleys which are filled with the dragon's blood to all the lower sort of believers; or certainly the mountains are the hostile powers that roam about in the air, and the valleys those which penetrate the underworld and have been consigned to eternal punishments.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.32.6":["Further, what is said: And I will water the land with the stench of your blood upon the mountains (or the land will be watered with your dung upon the mountains), and the valleys will be filled with you, signifies this, that all the pride and arrogant swelling of the heretics is filled not so much with living and pure blood as with putrid and stinking blood — according to what is written: Make them like the dung of the earth, from which the holy man is delivered by the Lord: Who raises the needy from the earth, and lifts the poor from the dung, to set him with princes, with the princes of his people —; and the valleys, sunk into the depths, as we said above, signify either the underworld or the lowly mind of the heretics, who do everything for the sake of pleasure and earthly wealth;\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.32.7-8":["'Then,' he says, 'the heavens will be wrapped in darkness: the sun and the moon and the other lights will not give their light, but over you all things will grow black, and nothing will be joyful for you.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.32.7":["and what is joined: And when you have been extinguished I will cover the sky, and I will make its stars grow black: I will cover the sun with a cloud, and the moon will not give its light; all the lights of the sky I will make mourn (or grow dark) over you, and I will put darkness upon your land, says the Lord God, if we follow the letter, cannot be understood at all: for when, after Pharaoh — or the dragon — was slain, did the sun and moon and stars not give their light, and the sky was covered with darkness — according to that poetic line: And the impious ages feared eternal night —? This, then, must be said: that when Pharaoh has been extinguished — who transformed himself into an angel of light, of whom it is written in another place: The light of the impious will be extinguished —, the sky, or the heavens, will be covered with darkness — of which Paul also speaks: And the spiritual things of wickedness in the heavenly places —; and the Lord made its stars grow black, whether the dragon's or the sky's — concerning whom Jude the apostle also writes: Wandering stars, for whom the gloom of darkness is reserved forever —; the sun too is covered in a cloud: the sun of iniquity — which is the opposite of the sun of righteousness —, and with a cloud: either by the Lord Savior himself — who descends into Egypt upon a light cloud, weighed down by no burden of sins —, or by the prophets and apostles — of whom we read: I will command the clouds that they rain no rain upon it, and in the Psalms: Your truth reaches to the clouds —; and when the sun of iniquity has been darkened, the moon — which we take to be the church of the heretics, and which was thought to receive its light from the sun of iniquity — will not give its light, nor will it deceive believers with the knowledge falsely so called;\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.32.8":["all the lights also, and whatever of doctrine seems to be among the heretics, will be blinded when the dragon has been extinguished, or they will mourn the loss of their prince, so that the Lord himself covers the land of the heretics with darkness, namely ignorance of the truth, so that the blind lead the blind into a ditch and the inhabitants of the land are sent into the outer darkness, where there will be weeping and gnashing of teeth.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.32.9-10":["And at your destruction all peoples and all their kings will tremble with exceeding horror, seeing my sword flying this way and that and sparing no one, and in your ruin they dread a like ruin.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.32.9":["After this it is said: And I will provoke the heart of many peoples, when I bring your destruction (or your captivity) among the nations upon a land which you do not know, so that those who formerly served Pharaoh are angered against him, seeing that his captivity has been led away to another land, which the dragon did not know. Nor ought we to doubt that the lands which Pharaoh does not know are good, namely when the captivity of Pharaoh is exchanged for another captivity — of which it is said to the Savior: Ascending on high, he led captivity captive; he received (or, according to the Apostle, gave) gifts among men —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.32.10":["And I will make, he says, many peoples stand astonished over you, so that those who formerly admired Pharaoh's power may afterwards marvel at him for having fallen from his summit; the kings of the peoples, too, will tremble over him with exceeding horror — whose kingdoms the devil showed to the Lord, and of whom it says in the psalm: The kings of the earth stood up, and the princes gathered together —; and this will come about when the sword of the Lord begins to fly over their faces, understood as those of the kings, or of the peoples — and the sword of the Lord is to be understood as the word of God, living and effective, and sharper than any two-edged sword, which flies and darts about and dazzles the eyes of those who behold it and frightens their faces —, so that, astounded at the ruin of Pharaoh, they may see themselves cast down in him; and indeed the sword of the Lord — of which it is written: Behold, this one is set for the fall and rising of many — raises those who lie fallen and by humility changes those wrongly lifted up.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.32.11-12":["But so that you may know what this sword is, hear it more plainly: The sword of the king of Babylon will come to you; by the swords of the mighty (or of giants) I will cast down all your multitude — which giants are the invincible nations that were held in the army of the Babylonian king —; these will lay waste (or will destroy) the pride of Egypt and all its multitude, and nothing will remain in Egypt, but together with the men the beasts and brute animals too will be carried off alike.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","But the sword of the king of Babylon will come upon the Egyptian dragon, so that by the swords of the mighty — or of giants — the Lord may cast down his multitude, which followed the king of Egypt: Invincible, he says, (or pestilent ones from the nations) are all these, so that the evil are handed over to worse ones to be punished — of whom it is written: A sending by most evil angels, and the Apostle: Whom I have handed over, he says, to Satan, that they may learn not to blaspheme, or for the destruction of the flesh, that the spirit may be saved —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.32.13-15":["And so great will be the desolation in Egypt that its waters will be troubled neither by the foot of man nor by the hoof of beast, but all things will be quiet; and the waters of its rivers will flow like oil, most pure and shining, with no one passing through them nor troubling their streams' — and this will come about because of the desolation of Egypt, which will be reduced to a desert, stripped of all its multitude, when the Lord has struck its inhabitants, so that those who remain may understand that he himself is the Lord —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.32.13":["And when by ministers of this kind the pride of Egypt has been cast down and its multitude scattered, all its beasts that were beside many waters will perish, and the foot of man will trouble them no more — for if Blessed is he who sows upon the waters, where ox and ass tread, conversely unhappy is he who will be able any longer to hold the simple by his terror, so that the foot of man does not tread in them, whereby the Egyptian waters seem not to have in them even a sign of wisdom and reason —; nor will the hoof of beasts trouble them, so as to make them 'turbid and muddy' instead of most limpid and shining.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.32.14-15":["Then the waters that had been troubled by the dragon's dominion will be rendered most pure, not by another but by the Lord himself, so that their rivers are rendered like oil and are the nourishment of the true light; and these things will come about when the Lord has made the land of Egypt desolate and has destroyed its fullness and all its inhabitants have been struck, so that by this progress they may be able to know that he himself is the Lord.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.32.16":["Therefore it is a lament for Pharaoh, and the daughters of the nations round about will lament him, and not only over him, but also over his multitude which, as the Lord laid it waste, was blotted out.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","And what is joined: It is a lament, and they shall lament him; the daughters of the nations shall lament him; over Egypt and over its multitude they shall lament him, says the Lord God, makes it plain that the souls of all the nations which had formerly been oppressed by him, or of those nations which by no means dwelt in Egypt but in the region of the living, lament the slain dragon and all his multitude: not in hope of salvation, but because from so great a power, by his own vice and pride, he has been cast down into eternal punishments.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.32.17-18":["Now, with the same brevity, so far as the difficulty of the explanation permits, let us gather the chief points of the spiritual understanding. Above, the word of the Lord came to the prophet on the first day of the month; here, on the fifteenth — now on the first day, that is, on the calends, is the beginning of the month; on the fifteenth, when the whole orb of the moon is filled; and if it was the first month, it is the first day of Unleavened Bread, but if the seventh, of Tabernacles, when the tents are pitched, which feasts are among the very greatest among the Hebrews —; and the lament is made over the strength of Egypt, so that he who is strong in evil may cease to be so and take on weakness, and when he is weaker, then he may be stronger. For the 'strength' of Egypt, the Hebrew has 'multitude' — For wide and spacious is the way that leads to death, and many enter by it, just as, on the contrary, it is said to Israel: But you are few among all the nations; for virtue is always rare, and: Narrow and strait is the way that leads to life, and few are those who enter by it —. And that Egypt is lamented, and its dead daughters — 'her' daughters according to the Seventy, or those 'of the mighty nations' — are led down to the lowest land into the deepest pit — or ditch —, signifies the souls dwelling in the Egypt of this world — which have lost him who says: I am the life, and, dead in sins, are dragged down to the underworld by their weight, as the sinner says: For my iniquities have gone over my head; like a heavy burden they weigh upon me; these are the ones of whom it is also written in another place: They go into the lower parts of the earth; they will be given over into the hands of the sword; they will be portions for foxes. He who, indeed, digs a ditch, falls into it; whence we also read: He opened a pit and dug it out, and he will fall into the ditch which he made —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.32.17-32":["And it came to pass in the twelfth year, on the fifteenth of the month, the word of the Lord came to me, saying: Son of man, sing a dirge over the multitude of Egypt, and drag it down, it and the daughters of the mighty nations, to the lowest land with those who go down into the pit. Than whom are you more beautiful? Go down, and sleep with the uncircumcised. In the midst of those slain by the sword they shall fall; the sword has been given; they have dragged her away and all her peoples. The mightiest of the strong will speak to him from the midst of hell, they who went down with his helpers; and they have slept uncircumcised and slain by the sword. There is Assyria and all her multitude, round about her are their graves, all of them slain, and they who fell by the sword; whose graves have been set in the uttermost parts of the pit, and her multitude is round about her grave; all of them slain and falling by the sword, who once had spread dread in the land of the living. There is Elam and all her multitude round about her grave; all these slain and falling by the sword, who went down uncircumcised to the lowest land, who set their terror in the land of the living, and they have borne their shame with those who went down into the pit. In the midst of the slain they have set his bed, among all his peoples; round about him is his grave, all these uncircumcised and slain by the sword; for they had spread terror in the land of the living, and they have borne their shame with those who go down into the pit; they have been set in the midst of the slain. There is Meshech and Tubal and all her multitude, round about her are her graves, all these uncircumcised and slain, falling by the sword, who set their dread in the land of the living; and they will not sleep with the mighty who fell, and the uncircumcised, who went down into hell with their weapons and set their swords under their heads, and their iniquities were in their bones, because they became the terror of the mighty in the land of the living. And you therefore will be crushed in the midst of the uncircumcised, and you will sleep with those slain by the sword. There is Edom and all her kings and her leaders, who were given up with their army with those slain by the sword, and who have slept with the uncircumcised, and with those who went down into the pit. There are all the princes of the north and all the Sidonians (or 'hunters'), who were led down with the slain, trembling, and confounded in their strength, for they have slept uncircumcised with those slain by the sword, and they have borne their confusion with those who go down into the pit. Pharaoh saw them and was comforted over all his multitude which was slain by the sword, Pharaoh and all his army, says the Lord God, for I have set his terror in the land of the living; and he has slept in the midst of the uncircumcised with those slain by the sword, Pharaoh and all his multitude, says the Lord God. (In this place the edition of the Seventy translators disagrees greatly both in order and in translation, and some things in it have been added from Theodotion; hence we have found it necessary to set it down too, by no means sparing length, and serving the diligence of the studious reader. LXX: \"And it came to pass in the twelfth year, on the fifteenth of the month, the word of the Lord came to me, saying: Son of man, lament over the multitude of Egypt, and the nations will lead her daughters down dead into the depth of the earth, to those who go down into the ditch.\" — Moreover, under asterisks there has been added: ※ From the most beautiful water go down, and sleep with the uncircumcised ⁚. Again the sequence follows: — In the midst of those wounded by the sword they shall fall with him, and all their strength will sleep. And the giants will say to you: Go down into the depth of the ditch. Than whom are you better? Go down, and sleep with the uncircumcised in the midst of those wounded by the sword. There is Assyria and all her congregation. — And what is joined: ÷ All the wounded have been set there, and his grave is in the depth of the ditch, and his congregation came to be ⁚, is not found in the Hebrew, but was added by the Seventy. Again it is said: — Round about his grave, all the wounded who had fallen by the sword. — And again from the edition of Theodotion there has been added under asterisks: ※ Who had set graves for them in the sides of the pit, and the congregation came to be round about his grave; all these wounded and falling by the sword ⁚. After which the Seventy set down: — Who set their fear in the land of life. There is Elam and all her strength round about her grave; all wounded and falling by the sword, and who go down uncircumcised into the depth of the earth, who set their terror in the land of life, and they received their torment with those who go down into the ditch, in the midst of the wounded. There have been set Meshech and Tubal and all their strength, round about his grave, all his wounded, all uncircumcised and wounded by the sword, who set their terror upon the land of life; and they did not sleep with the giants, who had fallen from of old, who went down into hell with the weapons they used for wars and set their swords under their heads, and their iniquities came to be in their bones, because they had terrified giants in their life. And you in the midst of the uncircumcised ※ will be crushed, and ⁚ will sleep with those wounded by the sword. There is Edom, ※ her kings and all ⁚ the princes of Assyria, who gave his strength to the wound of the sword; these have slept with the wounded, with those who went down into the ditch. There are all the princes of the north, all the magistrates of Assyria, who go down wounded with their terror, and confounded in their strength; they have slept, uncircumcised too, with those wounded by the sword, and they have carried off their torment with those going down into the ditch. Pharaoh will see them and will receive comfort over all their strength ※ wounded by the sword, Pharaoh and all his strength ⁚, says the Lord God, for I have set his dread upon the land of life, and he will sleep in the midst of the uncircumcised with those wounded by the sword, Pharaoh and all his multitude, says the Lord God.) I am not unaware that so great a discrepancy between the two editions will be tiresome to the fastidious reader; but what am I to do with my slanderers, who, if I take away anything from the translation of the Seventy translators, cry out that I am sacrilegious and without the fear of the Lord — especially those who, though they disagree with me in the truth of the faith and follow the errors of the Manichaeans, stir up the minds of the unlearned on this very point, if they can show that anything of the old has been changed against established custom, preferring to err rather than to learn anything true from a rival. Meanwhile, according to the history the sense is plain. For in the same twelfth year, and in the same month, as we think, but not on the first of the month, as above, but on the fifteenth day, a lament is taken up not over Pharaoh, but over the strength — or multitude — of Egypt, which is dragged down from its pride with all its daughters — or those of the mighty nations — to the lowest land, that is, into the depth of hell. And it is said to the king of Egypt: 'Than whom are you better, that you should deserve to escape death? — for since Assyria and Elam — that is, the Persians —, and Meshech — who are interpreted as the Cappadocians —, and Tubal — whom some take to be the Iberians, others the Italians —, the Edomites also and the princes of the north and the Sidonians, with all their armies by whose help they struck horror into all the nations, have died and been slain by the sword, and have set their swords under their heads — which saying is to be taken ἐμφατικώτερον [more emphatically, as an expressive figure] —, will you alone be able not to endure the same things? Rather, when you see so great a multitude of sleepers with you among the dead below, and your grave surrounded by the memorials of princes once most mighty, you will have consolation, reckoning your torments lighter through the fellowship of very many —'. Let it suffice for the present to have said these things briefly according to the simple sense.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.32.19":["And what follows: Than whom are you more beautiful? Go down, and sleep with the uncircumcised, the word is properly addressed to Pharaoh king of Egypt — or, as was added in the Seventy from Theodotion, to Egypt itself: From the most beautiful water go down, and sleep with the uncircumcised; which pertains especially to him who, reborn in the baptism of Christ, hearing with the Church: Who is she that comes up made white, leaning upon her kinsman?, afterwards, defiled either by fornication or by other vices, is cast out of the Church, and it is said to him: From the most beautiful water go down, and sleep 'with the uncircumcised', that is, 'with the unclean', according to that sense which the Apostle sets down: We are the circumcision, who serve God in the spirit, and glory in the Lord, and put no confidence in the flesh —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.32.20-21":["All these will fall with Pharaoh in the midst of the wounded — or the slain —, by that sword which is the opposite of the sword which the Lord came to send upon the earth: for just as the sword of Christ separates the good from the evil — as he says: I came not to send peace upon the earth, but a sword —, so the sword of the heretics cuts down all peoples and leads the wounded down to the underworld; these are the giants, most mighty and rebellious and setting their mouth on high, and the more they raise themselves up to the heights through pride, the more they are dragged down to the depth of the ditch and the uttermost parts of hell, all of whom have been slain by the sword.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.32.22-23":["But the following short verses, which are marked in front with an obelus, I think should be passed over, and what is found in the Hebrew should be stated: Round about her are their graves, namely of those deceived by him, who are all wounded (or slain) by the sword, and therefore have fallen; whose graves have been set in the uttermost parts of the pit — and that hell is called a pit is plainly shown by that psalm in which the penitent says: I have been likened to those who go down into the pit; which we ought by no means, according to the proper sense of the Latin word, to understand as that which in Greek is called λίμνη [lake] — such as the lake of Tiberias, and lakes Larius and Benacus, and many others —, but as what we are accustomed to call cisterns; which pits, because they chill the waters and take from them all the warmth of the spirit, sinners and all heretics dig; and, so far as my memory goes, Scripture testifies that none of the saints dug a pit — that is, a cistern — but all sinners — among whom was Uzziah, the leprous king, who also fell through his pride, of whom it is written that he was a man brooding over the works of the soil, and that he built many towers of pride in the corners, and that he dug pits —; on the contrary it is said to the righteous man: Drink waters from your own vessels and from the springs of your wells, and again: Let the fountain of your water be your own, whence the Lord also says: They have forsaken me, the fountain of living water, and have dug for themselves pits (that is, cisterns) which cannot hold water —. There follows: And her multitude is round about her grave; all these surround the Assyrian, and their multitude is very great, all of whom have been wounded and slain and are falling by the sword — for none of them can stand with Moses, or hear: You who stand in the house of the Lord; but all have been wounded and slain and are falling —: who once had spread dread in the land of the living; and dread to those who were set over the churches, lest in the land of the living they should make peoples of the dead and deceive all the simple — whence Paul also said: But I fear lest, as the serpent deceived Eve in his malice, your minds be corrupted through the simplicity that is in Christ; but this fear is softened by hope in the Lord, as the man of the Church says: The Lord is my light and my salvation; whom shall I fear? The Lord is the protector of my life; of whom shall I be afraid? —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.32.22":["And so that either Pharaoh or the whole strength of Egypt, which has been dragged down to the underworld, might know whom he had as companions in punishment, the following word shows: There is Assyria and all the multitude (or synagogue) of her; for the prince of the heretics is the devil — whose congregation is truly a synagogue, of which it is said in the Revelation: Who are the synagogue of Satan —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.32.24-25":["And they have borne, he says, their shame (or their torment) with those whom they dragged down with them into the pit and the depth of hell, whose bed is in the midst of the slain; namely of those whose throats they cut with their blade, and they are surrounded by peoples of this kind; these peoples are the peoples of the Assyrian king, and although they boast that they are circumcised, yet they are uncircumcised and slain by the sword, and a third time they have spread terror and dread in the land of the living; hence they have borne their torment and punishment in the midst of those whom they deceived by their frauds.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.32.24":["After this it is said: There is Elam, and all her multitude round about her grave, no doubt the Assyrian's. 'Elam' is rendered into our tongue as 'their ascent'; for all who, despising the humility of Christ, have ascended to the pride of the devil and have thought themselves to be something in the knowledge falsely so called, are to be named Elamites, who go round the grave of the Assyrian king and have been slain and wounded by the sword; and although they set their mouth on high, yet they went down unclean and uncircumcised to the lowest land, so that the more loftily they had been raised up, the more heavily they might fall; these set their terror not once but a second time in the land of the living — for who among the churchmen is safe from the terror of Elamites of this kind and has not lost someone of his flock? —; and beautifully the Church of Christ is called the land of the living, so that, on the contrary, the conventicles of the heretics may be believed to be the land of the dead.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.32.26":["There also is — that is, beside the Assyrian — Meshech and Tubal and all her multitude, round about her grave, all uncircumcised and slain and falling by the sword, who had set their dread in the land of the living. For 'Mosoch and Thubal', Symmachus and Theodotion translated 'their bed', to show that the beds of the heretics, or rather their everlasting ditches, are the punishments of the Assyrian king; 'Mosoch' means 'madness', 'Thubal' 'conversion' — not to good from evil but from good to evil — or 'all things', to show that all heretics are mad and with equal zeal are turned to worse things; nor is it surprising if those who are mad and heaped together for evil have a multitude, since they are companions of the Egyptian king, who rejoices in multitude; all these have been slain by the sword, who set their dread, not once nor a second time, but a third time, in the land of the living, of which we have spoken more fully above.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.32.27":["There follows: And they will not sleep with the mighty (or the giants) who fell, and the uncircumcised, who went down to hell with their weapons and set their swords under their heads, and their iniquities were in their bones, because they became the terror of the mighty in the land of the living. 'These,' he says, 'who stood out as leaders of the heretics, have come to such a summit of malice and of torment, that they do not deserve to bear like punishments even with the mighty and the giants who fell from the beginning; who, having by no means done penance for their error, went down to the underworld with their weapons, rebels against God and his Church, carrying their spirit with them' — of whom it is written: Sons of men, their teeth are weapons and arrows —, 'and they set,' he says, 'their swords under their heads, resting in a hostile spirit, and, as their supreme victory, embracing the darts of their opinions, to such a degree that their iniquities were in their bones — that is, their strongest fabrications —, and holding fast the doctrines of their error: Because they became the terror of the mighty in their life (or in the land of the living)', which is said for the fourth time, that by their perversity they terrified even the mightiest, and those who had knowledge of the Scriptures and who were of the region of the living, while they go down to hell with their weapons and set their swords under their heads and their iniquity reaches to their bones.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.32.28":["After this it is written: 'And you therefore will be crushed in the midst of the uncircumcised' — which is said either to Pharaoh or to the strength, or multitude, of Egypt, that it too is crushed, according to what is written: But may God crush Satan under your feet swiftly — 'and you will sleep,' he says, 'with those slain by the sword, in perpetual sleep'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.32.29":["There, with the Assyrian and with the Egyptian multitude, will be Edom and her kings, all who have served earthly works or delighted in blood — for 'Idumaea' [Edom] sounds both 'earthy' and 'bloody' —; all the kings and all the princes — of whom the Apostle also frequently speaks: 'brooding over earthly works and daily shedding the blood of those whom they have deceived by their fraud' — have slept with the uncircumcised and unclean and with those who went down into the pit, of whom we have spoken above.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.32.30":["In the same company will also be the princes of the north, from which evils blaze up upon the earth — and from which, whether in Jeremiah or in this same prophet, that pot full of flesh and bones is set alight —, and not only the princes of the north, but every magistrate of 'Assur' — for which in the Hebrew is more truly set down all the 'Sidonians', whom we have rendered as 'hunters', according to what is written: Our soul has been snatched like a sparrow from the snare of the hunters (for which in the Hebrew is set down of the Sidonians); which Sidonians — or hunters — will be led down to the underworld trembling, who once trusted in their own strength; and they will sleep unclean, and they will bear their confusion — or torment —, having the everlasting remorse of an evil conscience, so that their fire is not quenched and their worm does not die.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.32.31-32":["When Pharaoh has seen all these, he will be comforted, either still having his former malice and seeing that he has many companions of his punishment, or at any rate he was comforted seeing that they too are confounded in their punishments and their terrors, over all his multitude which was slain by the sword: that is, Pharaoh's, or that of all his companions, and especially of the Assyrian king and Meshech and Tubal and the Edomites, and of the princes of the north and of the Sidonians; for these had set their terror in the land of the living — which is said for the fifth time, so that we ought to beware of and flee all nations of this kind, which have deceived us in all our senses, and know that they are dreadful and cannot easily be avoided unless we keep our heart with all vigilance —. 'He has slept,' he says, 'Pharaoh himself too, with his companions slain by the sword', which companions are his multitude, going by the wide and spacious way to everlasting punishments.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.33.1-9":["And the word of the Lord came to me, saying: Son of man, speak to the sons of your people, and you shall say to them: A land, when I bring the sword upon it, and the people of the land take one man from the last of them (or one man from among them), and set him over themselves as a watchman, and he sees the sword coming upon the land, and sounds the trumpet and announces it to the people; but whoever he is who hears the sound of the trumpet does not take heed for himself, and the sword comes and takes (or seizes) him: his blood shall be upon his own head; he heard the sound (or voice) of the trumpet and did not take heed for himself, his blood shall be upon him. But if he guards himself, he has saved his soul. But if the watchman sees the sword coming, and does not sound the trumpet, and the people do not guard themselves; and the sword comes and takes a soul from among them: that one indeed has been taken in his iniquity, but his blood I will require from the hand of the watchman. And you, son of man, I have made you a watchman for the house of Israel. Hearing therefore the word from my mouth, you shall announce it to them from me. If, when I say to the wicked: Wicked one, you shall die by death; you have not spoken so that the wicked may guard himself from his way, that wicked one shall die in his iniquity, but his blood I will require from your hand. But if, when you announce to the wicked that he should turn from his ways, he has not turned from his way, he shall die in his iniquity, but you will deliver your soul. Again the word of the Lord comes to the prophet — the word which had for a time been silent, because the prophet cannot, nor can human frailty, sustain within itself an unceasing and continuous prophesying —, and it speaks the same things that are contained in the earlier passages: 'Son of man, I have made you a watchman for the house of Israel, and hearing the word from my mouth, you shall also threaten them from me; in that I shall say to the unrighteous: You shall die by death, and you did not announce it to him, nor did you speak so as to announce it to the unrighteous, that he might turn from his ways and live, that unrighteous man shall die in his iniquity and his blood I will require from your hand. And you, if you announce it to the unrighteous, and he has not turned from his iniquity and from his way, that unrighteous man shall die in his iniquity, and you will deliver your soul'; if you examine these words carefully, you will understand that they are indeed similar, but not the same, since they disagree in a great many points — and this we ought to observe in all the holy Scriptures: where there seems to be some similarity of meaning, not everything is said identically, but a great many things are either taken away or added, and the difference between the individual words has its reason —. And meanwhile, before we come to higher things, the sense must be briefly explained: 'If a watchman has been appointed among the people to announce the sword of the Lord and the wrath that is coming, and, when he announces it, the people are unwilling to hear, the watchman will be free, and he who has been overwhelmed by the sword will himself bear the guilt of his own blood; but if he has heard, he has saved his soul; if, however, the watchman has not sounded the trumpet and the people, not knowing that the sword is coming, have by no means taken heed for themselves, the people indeed will die in their iniquity; yet I will require the blood of the dying from the watchman's hand'; and so that the prophet Ezekiel might know that this general discussion pertained above all to himself: 'And you,' he says, son of man, not by the land and by the people of the land, as I said above, but by me you have been appointed watchman for the house of Israel: If therefore, when I say to the wicked: Wicked one, you shall die by death, you have not spoken to him so that he may guard himself and be delivered from destruction, he indeed will die in his iniquity, which he had committed before and from which, had you announced it, he could have been delivered: but his blood I will require from your hand. But if you declare it to the wicked, and command him to turn from his most evil ways, and he is unwilling to do this, he indeed will die in his iniquity, but you have delivered your soul from the destruction of negligence'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.33.2-6":["We can, however, discuss this passage in a threefold way: so that the land which appoints a watchman for itself is either, according to the letter, the land of Judea, or, according to the spiritual understanding, the Church, which often chooses a watchman from the last of its people — namely that one whom the Apostle too, writing to the Corinthians, takes as judge —, or certainly the soul of the believer, which sets the mind and reason over the people and crowd of its thoughts — so that it may not admit every incitement of the thoughts, but may judge and discern which things it should pursue and which it should flee —. The watchman of the land of Judea can be understood as either the king or the prophet; but the watchman of the Church as either the bishop or the presbyter, who has been chosen by the people and, knowing and foreseeing through the reading of the Scriptures what is to come, is to announce it to the people and correct the one who goes astray. Hence we must greatly fear lest we approach this office unworthy, and, having been taken up by the people, give ourselves over to negligence and sloth, and, what is worse than these, serving delicacies and the belly and leisure, think that we have received an honor, not a ministry: For the Son of man did not come to be ministered to, but to minister, and he washed the feet of the disciples, to show that all the filth and vices in the disciples ought to be washed away and cleansed by the teacher; nor let us at once reply: 'What use is it to speak, if the hearer is unwilling to do what you have taught?', for each one is judged by his own intention and office: you, if you have not spoken; he, if he has been unwilling to hear; of negligent teachers Solomon speaks: Wisdom hidden and treasure hidden, what profit is there in either?; something of the kind is signified also in the Gospel: that for one who has caused one of the least of the Church to stumble, it would be better that a donkey's millstone be tied to his neck and he be cast into the deep, than that, set in the watchtower, he should harm very many.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.33.7-9":["From these words we learn: that a man, though unrighteous and wicked, can be saved from his wickedness if he hears the words of the teacher and repents; and no less, that the teacher incurs peril if he is unwilling to teach, whether from fear of peril or from despair of the sinner, since he is guilty of the blood of one who could have been delivered and rescued from death, had he not fallen through the teacher's silence; and that in both free will is preserved, since it lies both in the teacher's will either to keep silent or to speak, and in the hearer's choice either to hear and do and be saved, or to despise and perish by his own contempt. Nor does it immediately follow that, because a prophet foretells something, what he foretold comes — for he does not foretell it so that it may come, but so that it may not come —; nor, because God speaks, must what he threatens necessarily happen; rather he threatens for this reason: that the one whom he threatens may turn to repentance, and that what is to come if the words of the Lord are despised may not happen.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.33.10-11":["You therefore, son of man, say to the house of Israel: Thus you have spoken, saying: Our iniquities and our sins are upon us, and we are wasting away in them; how then shall we be able to live? Say to them: As I live, says the Lord God; I do not want the death of the wicked, but that the wicked turn back from his way, and live; and turn from your most evil ways; and why will you die, house of Israel? You therefore, son of man, say to the sons of your people: The righteousness of the righteous shall not deliver him on whatever day he sins, and the wickedness of the wicked shall not harm him on whatever day he turns from his wickedness; and the righteous shall not be able to live in his righteousness on whatever day he sins. Even if I say to the righteous that he shall live by life, and, trusting in his righteousness, he commits iniquity; all his righteous deeds shall be consigned to oblivion, and in his iniquity which he has wrought, in it he shall die. But if I say to the wicked: You shall die by death, and he repents of his sin, and does judgment and righteousness, restores the pledge, and gives back what he has robbed, walks in the commandments of life, and does nothing unjust, he shall live by life, and shall not die: all the sins which he has sinned shall not be imputed to him; he has done judgment and righteousness, he shall live by life. And the sons of your people have said: The way of the Lord is not of equal weight; and it is their own way that is unjust. For when the righteous withdraws from his righteousness and commits iniquities, he shall die in them; and when the wicked withdraws from his wickedness and does judgment and righteousness, he shall live in them. And you say: The way of the Lord is not right. I will judge each one of you according to his ways, house of Israel. If we read carelessly, it seems to us to be the same prophecy as the one above, in which it is said: Do I willingly desire the death of the unrighteous, says the Lord, and not rather that he turn from his evil way and live?, and at the end of the same prophecy: Turn and return from all your wickednesses, and they will not be to you a torment of iniquity; for there the word is addressed to those who wish to repent and would blot out their sins with righteousness, so that they may turn with confidence and repent with a full heart; here, however, he speaks to those who, because of the greatness of their sins, or rather of their wickednesses, despair of salvation and say: Our iniquities and our sins are upon us, and we are wasting away in them; how then shall we be able to live?; and the sense is: 'Since death has once been set before us, and no medicine can restore health to our wounds, what need is there to toil and be worn out in vain, and not to pass the present life in desperation, so that at least we may enjoy this one, since we have lost the future one?'; to them God answered that he does not want the death of the wicked, but that he turn back and live, and he makes an apostrophe to the despairing wicked: Turn from your most evil ways; and so that we may know who the wicked are to whom he speaks, the following word shows it: Why will you die, house of Israel? — but the life and death signified in this place is not this one by which all of us, by the law of nature common with the beasts, either live or are dissolved by death, but that of which it is written: I will please the Lord in the land of the living, and: The soul that sins, it shall die —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.33.11":["This is really what the Prophet Amos says: 'For three crimes and for four, I will not revoke my word, says the Lord.' Moreover, he says this same thing eight times. Now, this is what he actually is saying: you have entertained sin, I have pardoned you; you have done evil, I have forgiven you; you have not repented of your sins, I have excused you: did you also have to teach evil? What the Scripture implies is this: For three sins and for four, I shall not be angered against you, says the Lord.\n— Homily 1, on Psalm 1"],"EZK.33.12-20":["And from the particular admonition with which he was speaking to the house of Israel, he passes to a general discussion: that past acts of righteousness do not save the righteous man if he has become taken up with new crimes, and old sins do not destroy the sinner or the wicked if he has amended his old errors by works of righteousness, and that God in both cases judges not past things but present ones: 'If I say,' he says, 'to the righteous: You shall live by life, and promise him the rewards of righteousness, and he, trusting in this, sins, all his former righteous deeds will be consigned to oblivion and he will die in his present iniquity; nor has my sentence changed, since I cannot render to the same man, now a sinner, what I had promised to the righteous man; on the contrary, if I threaten the sinner and the wicked — and say: Yet three days and Nineveh shall be overthrown —, and he repents and amends his old error by good works, so that he does judgment and righteousness, restores the pledge, and gives back what he has robbed, walks in the commandments of life, and does nothing unjust: ought he not to live by life, which is Christ, and by no means die, since the threat made to the sinner ought not to punish the righteous man?' — something of the kind the divine word says to Jeremiah when he goes down into the potter's house and hears that God's promises or threat aim at this: either to call men forth to salvation or to deter them from sin — 'hence those who say: The way of the Lord is not fair, are convicted of this, that it is their own judgment that is unfair, since they have a most evil eye and judge by no means the new things but the old'. By all these things it is shown that neither ought the sinner to despair of salvation if he repents, nor the righteous to trust in his righteousness if he has carelessly lost what he had acquired with great labor. We pass quickly over what is plain, in order to linger over what is more obscure; but in what points the present prophecy differs from the earlier one, and in what it says similar things, a comparison of the two will be able to show. Moreover, what it is to do judgment and righteousness, to restore the pledge, to give back what was robbed, to walk in the commandments of life, and the rest, we have said above in this same prophet.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.33.21-22":["This is according to the letter; but according to the ἀναγωγὴν [anagogy, the higher sense]: if 'Hiezechiel' [Ezekiel] means 'strength of God' — and Christ, moreover, is the power of God and the wisdom of God! —, this is to be understood: that, Jerusalem having been taken and overthrown, whoever has been able to escape the faithlessness of the Jews — such as were the apostles and the remnant that was saved —, he himself announces to Christ that all the ceremonies of the Jews have been overthrown — which certain people think must be observed even to this day, not hearing that saying of the Apostle: You have fallen from grace, you who are justified in the law. And I marvel at the obstinacy of men in wishing to defend in word what they do not dare to fulfill in deed, unless perhaps Jewish wolves are hiding under the skin of sheep, that is, of Christians. Why does a defender of the Synagogue make his voice resound in the churches of Christ? —. Jerusalem, therefore, having been taken and overthrown, the mouth of the Lord is opened through the apostles and apostolic men — who can say: My mouth is opened to you, O Corinthians, and: A great and effective door has been opened to me, and that saying: I opened my mouth and drew in the spirit —, a mouth which will never be able to keep silent, nor to hear — along with Israel: Hear, Israel, and be silent —, but will resound in the whole world and will lay open the Gospel of Christ to the nations. Hence, according to this sense, the 'twelfth year' refers to the twelve tribes, and the 'tenth month' to the time of the Jewish propitiation, which in Greek is called ἱλασμὸς, and the 'fifth day of the month' to the fleshly senses — all of which, Jerusalem having been taken and the Gospel succeeding, we have shown to have been abolished and to have passed away —, and 'in the evening', that is, at the consummation of the world, to the hand of the Lord having come upon the true Ezekiel, who had sung of the future ruins of the city through the prophets and in the morning showed that they had been fulfilled.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.33.21":["And it came to pass in the twelfth year, in the tenth month, on the fifth of the month of our exile (or captivity), there came to me one who had fled (or who had been saved) from Jerusalem, saying: The city has been laid waste (or taken). Now the hand of the Lord had come upon me in the evening, before he who had fled came; and he opened my mouth until he came to me in the morning, and, my mouth being opened, I was silent no longer. In the eleventh year of the reign of Zedekiah, in the fifth month, the city of Jerusalem was taken. But this prophecy is dated the twelfth year, the tenth month, the fifth of the month, of the captivity or exile, when he was taken captive with Jeconiah. From this it is shown that one year, four months and twenty-five days after Jerusalem was taken, one of the citizens of Jerusalem came to Babylon to report that the city had been taken and laid waste.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.33.22":["One day before the one who was to tell these things came, in the evening the hand of the Lord came upon the prophet Ezekiel, and it opened his mouth, which had long been closed; and whatever that man was going to say, this one recounted beforehand as already done, and he was silent no longer, seeing his prophecy fulfilled in deed, and that the people who were in Babylon, or those who were captives, by no means doubted the prophetic oracle; for the mouth of the prophet is opened at the moment when he has shown accomplished in deed what he had announced before; and he proclaims with all freedom who points out not future things at all, but things either present or past.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.33.24":["Jerusalem having been taken, as we have said, and the temple overthrown, the poor of the land, of whom Jeremiah writes, had alone been left in Jerusalem, to tend the vineyards and the fields and to dwell in the ruins of the burned city; and though they ought to have repented over the things on account of which the captivity had come, deceiving themselves with an empty hope, they said: 'Our father Abraham was one, and yet he possessed this land by inheritance — not that he himself possessed it, but that his seed received the land of promise; if therefore that one man was multiplied into such great peoples, we, who are many more, who have been left in the land of Judea and dwell in deserted and ruined cities, shall surely be multiplied much more, so that we, being many, may possess what that one man possessed'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","Now let us come to the tropology and, according to our custom, draw the broad discussion together rather than spread it out. Every heretic dwells among crumbling walls and in deserts, and thinks that he possesses the land of Israel: 'If Abraham, one man, by his faith came to such great blessedness that his seed was multiplied like the sand of the sea and like the stars of heaven, how much more shall we, being many, possess the land of 'Israel', that is, 'of those who see God', and 'Iudaea' [Judea], 'of the Lord's confession'?'; to them the Lord answered: 'He possessed the land by the merit of faith; but your unbelief, or rather blasphemy, will not be able to possess the land of Israel, that is, the Church.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.33.25-26":["And the word of the Lord came to me, saying: Son of man, those who dwell in these ruins upon the soil of Israel speak, saying: Abraham was one, and he possessed the land; but we are many, to us the land has been given as a possession. Therefore you shall say to them: Thus says the Lord God: You who eat in blood, and lift up your eyes to your uncleannesses, and shed blood, shall you possess the land by inheritance? You have stood upon your swords, you have done abominations, and each of you has defiled his neighbor's wife, and shall you possess the land by inheritance? These things you shall say to them: Thus says the Lord God: As I live, those who dwell in the ruins shall fall by the sword, and he who is in the field shall be given to the beasts to be devoured, but those who are in strongholds and in caves shall die of pestilence. And I will give the land into solitude and into desert, and its proud strength shall fail, and the mountains of Israel shall be made desolate, because there is no one who passes through them; and they shall know that I am the Lord, when I have made their land desolate and deserted, because of all their abominations which they have wrought. And you, son of man, the sons of your people talk about you beside the walls and in the doorways of the houses, and they say one to another, a man to his neighbor, speaking: Come, and let us hear what is the word that comes forth from the Lord. And they come to you as if a people were entering (or as a people is accustomed to gather), and my people sit before you; and they hear your words and do not do them, for they turn them into a song of their mouth, and their heart follows its avarice. (or, as is contained in the Seventy, for they make a lie in their mouth, and their heart walks after their abominations. It follows:) And you are to them like a musical song which is sung with a sweet and pleasing sound, and they will hear your words and will not do them. And when what has been foretold comes — for behold, it is coming —, then they will know that you prophesied in their midst. First it must be known that some eight lines, more or less, from the place we have set down: You who eat in blood, and lift up your eyes to your uncleannesses, up to the place where it is written: These things you shall say to them: Thus says the Lord God, are not found in the Seventy, who omitted these too along with many others; or else, having been translated by them, they were gradually removed through the fault of copyists. And our Latins, or rather envious Christians, and, to speak more openly, heirs of the Grunnian faction, bark against us: why do we expound according to the Hebrew — as though food were to be forced upon the unwilling, and the banquet were not being prepared for those who receive it with thanksgiving; surely, if they do not trust us, let them read the other editions: of Aquila, Symmachus and Theodotion; let them question Hebrews — not of one place, lest they keep boasting that they were bought by me, but of different provinces; and when they see that all agree with my error or inexperience, then let them understand that they are excessively wise, and desire to sleep rather than to learn —, and let them dwell in the seventy little cells of the Alexandrian Pharos, lest they lose the sails from their ships and sigh over the loss of their ropes. So much against the envious; now let us discuss what we have set before us.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","To them the Lord answered that Abraham, one man, had possessed the land of promise by faith — For Abraham believed, and it was reckoned to him for righteousness —, but that these, taken up with unbelief and crimes, even if they are more, cannot possess it; and at the same time he enumerates by what deeds they offend God, namely six kinds of sins: 'You who eat in blood, and lift up your eyes to uncleannesses, that is, to your idols, and shed blood, that is, commit homicide; doing these things, will you be able to keep the land by inheritance? Nor are you content with this limit of crimes, but you stand upon your swords daily, ready to kill and imitating Esau, who stood, and lived by his sword; you do incredible abominations, namely the foulnesses of lusts; and each one defiles his neighbor's wife, so that he is the more wicked in this, that he had defiled the wife of a friend and neighbor. Would you do these things,' he says, 'and suppose that you will hold the land by inheritance?\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","For first, you eat in blood, pouring out the blood of those whom you cause to stumble; next, you lift up your eyes to uncleannesses or to your abominations which you have fabricated out of your own mind — when you ought to have imitated the man of the Church and said: To you I have lifted up my eyes, you who dwell in the heavens —; third, you shed blood, not giving life to those whom you have led astray, but killing them; nor is it enough for you to have done these three things, but you stand upon your swords, that is, you persevere in the depravity of your opinion, and are ready for slaughter; and you have done abominations, doing in bedchambers things that are shameful to speak of; and you have defiled the wife of your neighbor, hastening, namely, to snatch away daily from the embrace of Christ, by deceiving them, those of the Church's way of life. And when you have done these things, do you think that you will hold the land of Israel by inheritance?'\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.33.27-29":["To them God says that whoever has dwelt in the ruined conventicles of the heretics shall fall by the sword of the Church — of which it is written: And two-edged swords in their hands, and in the Gospel: I did not come to send peace, but a sword, and: 'The servant who gives himself over to luxury and leisure shall be divided, that is, shall be struck with the blade, and his portion shall be placed with the unbelievers' —, and he who is in the field, or in the open country, shall be given to the beasts to be devoured — the beasts which the prophet, desiring to avoid them, prays against: Do not give to the beasts the soul that confesses to you —, but he who is in strongholds and in walled places — of which it is written: The righteous man went up against the strongest cities and destroyed their fortifications, in which the wicked trusted — and lingers in caves — of which it is said: It is written: The house of my Father shall be called a house of prayer; but you have made it a den of robbers —, that one shall die by famine of the word of the Lord and by pestilence; and the whole land of the heretics shall be given into solitude, so that their pride may be broken and the mountains which promised themselves the loftiness of knowledge may be reduced to solitude — mountains that are called of Israel because under the name of Christ they trip up all whom they deceive —, and no one shall pass through them — nor will anyone be able to say what Moses said: Passing over, I will see this great vision; for they are inhabitants of the perverse mountains, not pilgrims and sojourners —, so that, when they have suffered these things, they may then know that he himself is the Lord, who has given their land into solitude because of the abominations which they have wrought.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.33.27":["Answer them therefore, O prophet, and bring forth this sentence upon them from my word: I swear by myself that those who dwell in the ruins and the crumbling walls shall fall by the sword, and those who are in the fields — or in the open country — shall be devoured by beasts, and those who are in walled places — or in strongholds — and in caves shall not be able to escape the wrath of God, but shall die by famine and pestilence.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.33.28-29":["And I will give,' he says, 'the land of Judea into solitude, and its proud strength shall fail — which had once been strength: The proud, however, God resists, and gives grace to the humble —; the mountains too and everything shall be deserted, and they shall come into such solitude that no one passes through them; and then the few who had remained shall by no means trust in their multitude, but shall know, by the greatness of the solitude, that I am the Lord, who gave the land into desert because of all the abominations which they have wrought'. Let these things be said against those who, Jerusalem having been taken and the whole region of the Jews laid waste, dwelt, a few of them, in the ruined and deserted cities and little villages.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.33.30":["There follows — on the same day indeed and at the same time, that is, in the twelfth year, the tenth month, on the fifth of the month, when the one who had fled from Jerusalem came, and when the prophet spoke to those who were in the land of Judea and promised themselves the hope of an everlasting possession — and he commands the prophet to speak to the people that lives in Babylon and dwells with him, and to rebuke them for their flattering snares, those who desire to hear the words of the prophet not for the salvation of the soul but for the pleasure of the ears; these walk beside the walls and in the doorways of the houses, by no means desiring to enter into the prophet's meaning, but they urge one another on and say: Come, and let us hear what is the word that comes forth from the Lord.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.33.31-33":["'And so they come like a people entering the Church of God, and they sit before you, my people, which says that it is mine, and they are unwilling to do what they have heard; people of this sort seem to me like those who are beguiled by theatrical songs and listen either to tragedians or to comedians and are there caressed with pleasure, so that, when they have gone out from you, they repeat these things and sing them over and deceive themselves with the sweet sound: And they will hear,' he says, 'your words and will not do them'. Such, even to this day, are many in the churches, who say: 'Come, let us hear this man and that man', as he rolls out the words of his preaching with marvelous eloquence; and they stir up applause and shout and wave their hands; and the things they had neglected in deeds, once they learn that these have come — for what the prophet pronounces by the word of God must come —, then they will begin to acknowledge, and to know that all that they had heard were not the words of a man, but of the Lord, who spoke through the prophet and the man of the Church.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.34.1-2":["And the word of the Lord came to me, saying: Son of man, prophesy concerning the shepherds (or to the shepherds) of Israel, prophesy and say to the shepherds: Thus says the Lord God: Woe to the shepherds of Israel who were feeding themselves (or O shepherds of Israel, do shepherds feed themselves); are not the flocks fed by the shepherds (or do not the shepherds feed the sheep)? You ate the milk and covered yourselves with the wool, and what was fat you slaughtered, but my flock you did not feed; what was weak you did not make firm (or strengthen), and what was sick you did not heal, what was broken you did not bind up, and what was cast off (or was straying) you did not bring back, what had perished you did not seek, but you ruled over them with harshness and with power (or and what was strong, you afflicted with toil). And my sheep were scattered, because there was no shepherd (or shepherds); and they were made to be devoured by all the beasts of the field, and they were scattered. My flocks (or sheep) had wandered over all the mountains and on every high hill, and over the whole face of the earth my flocks (or sheep) were scattered; and there was none who would seek, there was none, I say, who would seek (or who would bring back). Therefore, O shepherds, hear the word of the Lord: As I live, says the Lord God, because my flocks (or sheep) have become a prey, and my sheep have been made to be devoured by all the beasts of the field, because there was no shepherd (or shepherds); for the shepherds did not seek my flock, but the shepherds were feeding themselves, and were not feeding my flocks. Therefore, shepherds, hear the word of the Lord. Thus says the Lord God: Behold, I myself, against the shepherds, will require my flock at their hand, and I will make them cease, so that they no longer feed my flock, nor shall the shepherds feed themselves any more (or the sheep); and I will deliver my flock (or sheep) from their mouth, and it shall no longer be food for them. For thus says the Lord God: Behold, I myself will seek out my sheep, and I will visit them. As a shepherd visits his flock on the day when he is in the midst of his scattered sheep (or when there is gloom and cloud in the midst of his separated sheep), so will I visit my sheep, and I will deliver them from all the places into which they had been scattered in the day of cloud and gloom. And I will lead them (or them) out from the peoples (or from the nations), and I will gather them (or them) from the lands (or regions), and I will bring them (or them) into their own land, and I will feed them (or them) on the mountains, Israel, by the streams and in all the dwelling places of the land. In the richest pastures I will feed them (or them), and on the high mountains of Israel shall be their pastures (or their folds); there they shall rest in green grass, and in fat pastures they shall feed upon the mountains of Israel. I will feed my sheep, and I will make them lie down (or rest), says the Lord God. What had perished I will seek, and what had been cast off (or had strayed) I will bring back, and what had been broken I will bind up, and what was weak I will make firm (or strengthen), and what is fat and strong I will guard, and I will feed them in judgment (or with judgment). But you, my flocks (or my sheep), thus says the Lord God: Behold, I judge between cattle and cattle, of rams and of he-goats. Was it not enough for you to graze down the good pasture? Moreover you will also trample the remnants of your pastures with your feet, and when you drank the purest water, you fouled the rest with your feet; and my sheep fed on what had been trampled by your feet, and drank what your feet had fouled. Therefore thus says the Lord God to them: Behold, I myself judge between fat cattle and lean (or between strong cattle and weak): because you thrust with your sides and shoulders and tossed all the weak cattle with your horns, until they were scattered abroad, I will save my flock and it shall no longer be a prey, and I will judge between cattle and cattle (or between ram and ram). And I will raise up over them (or them) one shepherd to feed them (or them), my servant David, he shall feed them (or them), and he shall be to them for a shepherd. But I the Lord will be to them for God, and my servant David prince in the midst of them: I the Lord have spoken. And I will make with them a covenant (or testament) of peace, and I will make the most evil beasts cease from the land, and those who dwelt in the desert shall sleep secure in the woods; and I will make them, round about my hill, a blessing, and I will bring down (or give) the rain in its season: there shall be rains of blessing. And the tree of the field shall give (or the trees of the plains shall give) its fruit, and the earth shall give its shoot (or strength), and they shall be (or dwell) in their own land without fear (or in hope of peace), and they shall know that I am the Lord, when I have broken the chains (or the collar) of their yoke, and rescued them from the hand of those who ruled over them (or who made them serve). And they shall no longer be a prey to the nations, nor shall the beasts of the earth devour them, but they shall dwell confidently (or in hope) without any terror. And I will raise up for them a renowned shoot (or a planting of peace), and they shall no longer be diminished (or perishing) by famine in the land, nor shall they bear any longer the reproaches of the nations; and they shall know that I am the Lord their God with them, and they my people, the house of Israel, says the Lord God. But you, my flocks, the flocks of my pasture (or sheep), are men, and I the Lord your God, says the Lord God. I have often pointed out that I mix the two editions so as to avoid making the books too large, at least in those places that do not differ much from each other in translation. After Jerusalem was taken, after the one who had escaped brought the news to Babylon, he spoke concerning those who dwelt in the ruins of Jerusalem, and then to those who, though placed in captivity, nonetheless persevered in wickedness, unwilling to hear the prophet's words; now he directs his discourse to the shepherds, that is, to the princes, by whose fault the sheep, that is, the peoples, were scattered. And this must be noted, that from the twelfth year, the tenth month, the fifth of the month of the exile — or of the captivity of Jeconiah and of those who had been taken captive with him —, up to the twenty-fifth year, when the temple is built upon the mountain of the built city and the incredible mysteries of the Church are laid open, no year and no time is set in between, but it is simply said: 'The word of the Lord came to me, saying: Speak to these and to those', so that we should understand that all the things read over thirteen years, though spoken at different times, nevertheless do not have fixed intervals of time between them. But the discourse is directed to the shepherds of Israel — whom we ought to take either as the kings and princes, the scribes and Pharisees and the teachers of the Jewish people, or certainly, in the people of the Gospel, as the bishops, presbyters and deacons, or, according to the mystical senses, as the angels of the individual churches, to whom John writes in his Revelation, and whose angels daily see the face of God —, and first it is said: Woe to the shepherds of Israel, who, when they ought to feed the Lord's flock and provide for its welfare, hasten after their own luxury — whence great care must be taken, and those precepts observed: Do not seek to become a judge, lest perhaps you be unable to take away iniquities, and again: The greater you are, the more humble yourself, and you will find grace in the sight of the Lord, and once more: Have they made you leader? Do not be lifted up: be among them as one of them; whence the Apostle too says that he was among the disciples as a little one and a suckling —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.34.3-4":["As for what follows: You ate the milk and covered yourselves with the wool, it is by a μεταφορὰν [metaphor] of shepherds that he speaks to the princes — of whom it is also written in another place: Who devour my people like a meal of bread —; in the milk understand all wealth, in the wool the manifold variety of garments. And what is said: And what was fat you slaughtered, speaks of the rich of the people, whom the wicked princes in the churches are said to slaughter, in that they praise them and dare not rebuke their vices — of whom the prophet also says: My people, those who call you blessed lead you astray, and trip up the path of your feet; of whom James too speaks, saying that when they have come in to them, clothed in costly garments and with a gold ring, they are honored by them, and to the holy poor it is said: But you, sit on the footstool, or on the ground, or stand there — what is weak they do not strengthen or make firm — whence Paul says: Receive the weak, and: Take up the one weak in faith, and: Many weak among you and sick, to whom is sent the divine word of which it is written in the Psalms: He sent his word and healed them, and rescued them from their corruptions —; 'and what is broken', he says, 'they do not bind up', by no means taking account of the mortal wounds among the peoples, such as the adulterer, the murderer, the sacrilegious man; 'and what is cast off, or what they see straying, they do not bring back', allowing them to be deceived by heretics; 'and what has perished they do not seek', desiring not so much to save the lost as to devour those who are in the churches, but ruling over them with harshness and with power — which properly pertains to the haughtiness of bishops, namely of those who disgrace the dignity of their name by their works and take up pride in place of humility, so that they think they have attained an honor, not a burden, and whomever they see in the church taking the lead and possessing the word of God, they strive to oppress; whence according to the Seventy it is written: And what was strong you afflicted with toil —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.34.5":["And the people of God was scattered, either by vices or by heretical error, because there was no good shepherd who would lay down his life for the sheep, but all were hirelings, who consider only the profits from the flocks and flee when they see the wolf; through whose negligence the Lord's flock is devoured by the beasts of the field — of which it is written: You set darkness, and night came: in it all the beasts of the field will pass through, the lions' whelps roaring, to seize and to seek their food from God —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.34.6":["And they are scattered, and they wander on all the mountains, who are lifted up against the knowledge of God, and on every high hill, who through heretical pride despise the simplicity of the Church, and they are scattered over the whole face of the earth, seeking earthly things, not heavenly: And there was none who would seek, nor who would bring back, because, occupied with their pleasures, they do not care about the losses of the Lord's flock.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.34.7-10":["Hence the word of the Lord comes to the wicked shepherds, 'because you have done this and that', the things which I expounded above and which he enumerates a second time. 'Behold, I myself will come to the shepherds and will require my flock at their hand, for whom it is better that a donkey's millstone be tied to their neck than that they scandalize the least of my people; and this will be their punishment, even the greatest: That they no longer feed my flock, lest, under the pretext of the sheep, they feed themselves and amass wealth; and I will deliver my people from their mouth'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.34.10-12":["'I will require', he says, 'from the hand', and 'I will deliver from the mouth', by which they are devoured with greedy jaws; and when he has sought out the sheep, he visits them as sick and lean and scattered through the negligence of the shepherds; and he will deliver them from all places in the day of cloud and gloom — of which Joel too speaks: The day of the Lord is at hand, and it is near; a day of darkness and gloom, a day of cloud and mist —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.34.13-14":["Then they are led out from the lands, so as to be brought into their own land, which is the land of the living, and so that he himself may feed them on the mountains of Israel — of which David speaks: I lifted up my eyes to the mountains, whence help will come to me —, and not only on the mountains, but by the streams and in all the dwelling places of the land, in the richest pastures and on the high mountain — of which Isaiah and Micah prophesy more fully —, or on the high mountains, Israel; there they will rest in green grass — and they will say: The Lord feeds me, and nothing will be lacking to me: in a place of pasture, there he has set me; beside the water of refreshment he has brought me up —, and they will feed in the fattest pastures upon the mountains of Israel.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.34.15":["And it is an infinite promise and a hope of blessedness, when the Lord himself promises, saying: 'I will feed my sheep, and I will by no means entrust them to wicked shepherds, and I will make them lie down, says the Lord God, so that they may rest in the bosom of Abraham, Isaac and Jacob'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.34.16":["Then what had perished among the peoples of the nations will be sought, and what had strayed through the persuasion of heretics will be brought back, and what had been broken will be bound up, and what is crushed and weak will be made firm — that what is written may be fulfilled: Who heals their infirmities, and binds up their bruises. A heart, indeed, crushed and humbled, God does not spurn; whence the holy man too says: Heal me, for my bones are troubled, and in Psalm 37 (38), repenting: There is no peace, he says, for my bones before the face of my sins —. And what will be fat, he says, and strong I will guard, lest it be devoured by beasts; and he will feed his sheep in judgment, knowing what pastures are owed to which sheep — for Diverse indeed are the dwellings with the Father. And the Father judges no one, but has given all judgment to the Son —. These things, then, he spoke against the shepherds.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.34.17-19":["Now he speaks to the sheep, that is, to the people, and to the flock of both kinds of cattle, that is, of sheep and of goats, and not only to the sheep and goats, but to the rams and he-goats, who are the princes in the flocks; to whom he says: 'Was it not enough for you that you fed on the good pastures of the Scriptures? But on top of that you will trample the remnants of your pastures with your feet, and when you drank the purest water of God's utterances, you fouled the rest of the waters with your feet, so that my people ate the pastures trampled by you and drank the fouled waters, and, through your fault, received corrupted and violated what was good in itself'. This indeed all heretics do, picking at the utterances of the Scriptures and, as far as in them lies, staining them; but churchmen who do not guard the truth of doctrines, but fabricate them from their own heart and have their own presumption for their teacher, are held fast in a like error; who, when they have persuaded the people that what they fabricate is true, and have stirred up applause and shouts in the manner of the theater, become forgetful of their own lack of skill, and, with brow drawn up and with words weighed and balanced, assume for themselves the authority of teachers.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.34.20-22":["Let us see, then, what the judgment between cattle and cattle is, and with what judgment the good and true shepherd judges them, he who received all judgment from the Father: Behold, he says, I myself judge between fat cattle and lean (or between strong and weak): for the strong thrust with their sides and shoulders and dash against the weak cattle and toss them with their horns, not knowing that in the law the goring bull must be punished; and they thrust with their sides and tossed with their horns until they scattered the sheep and drove them out: for through the pride of the greater and the iniquity of those set over them, they are frequently driven from the church, so that those whom the Lord himself saved are scattered away from him; but the Lord will no longer abandon them to be a prey, and he will judge between cattle and cattle: not between the names of dignities with which those set over others swell, but between man and man, according as each is a servant of the Lord.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.34.23-24":["Hence also in the same Ezekiel it is written: I will raise up over them one shepherd, and he shall feed them, my servant David; and he shall be their shepherd, and I the Lord will be their God, and David a prince in their midst. I the Lord have spoken, and I will make with David a covenant of peace (Ezek 34:23-25). In this it is to be considered that, long after the time of David, the gluttonous shepherds being rejected, he says he will raise up our Lord, who is of the stock of David, to gather the lambs and cherish them in his bosom, and himself to carry on his shoulder the young of the sheep, or the ewes with young; as we read in the Gospel that he carried back to the fold on his shoulders the sheep that had strayed and stayed behind from its accustomed flock (Luke 15).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"EZK.34.23":["Then he will raise up one shepherd — who says in the Gospel: I am the good shepherd —, his servant David — inasmuch as he deigned to take on the form of a servant; and 'David' means 'strong of hand' —; he himself will feed them.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.34.24":["And the Lord will be to them for God — either the Son and the Father, or certainly the shepherd according to the flesh he assumed. But 'Lord' and 'God' according to: The 'Word' that was in the beginning with 'God' —. This David, when the cattle have advanced to being a rational animal, will no longer be called shepherd but prince in the midst of them — of whom it is written: In the midst of you stands he whom you do not know —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.34.25-26":["And when the beasts have been driven out and the land reduced to a solitude, they will sleep secure from animals of this sort and will say: The Lord is my light and my salvation, whom shall I fear? —; and he will set them, those who will sleep secure, round about his mountain or hill, and they will be a blessing; then he will give rain in its season, and there will be rains of blessing — which he promises in the blessings of Deuteronomy —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.34.25":["Then he will make with them a covenant of peace — by no means of wars and discord, for: His place was made in peace, but of the peace of Christ: Which surpasses all understanding —, who says: My peace I give to you, my peace I leave to you; and he will make the most evil beasts cease from the land — more mildly: either the passions by which human souls are troubled, or the adverse powers by which they are assailed —, and those who dwell in the desert will sleep secure in the woods — for the Lord will lay bare the thickets of the forests, and in his temple all will speak glory.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.34.27":["And the tree of life, he says, will give — of which it is written in Genesis —, and the tree of wisdom — of which it is said: It is a tree of life to all who take it up —: it will give its fruit when the earth too has given its fruit, or the trees of the regions, the very great multitude of the saints; and the earth will give its shoot: for Truth indeed has sprung from the earth; and they will be in their own land without fear, or in hope of peace, in the land of the living — the land of the meek, of which it is written: Blessed are the gentle, for they shall possess the land —, so that through the felicity of all things they may know that he himself is the Lord, when he has broken the chains or the iron ring of their yoke, by which they were pressed down as by a most heavy rule, and has rescued them from the hand of those ruling over them, or of those afflicting them with servitude: For each one is the slave of him by whom he is overcome.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.34.28":["And they will by no means be a prey any longer to the demonic nations, nor will the beasts of the earth, of which we have spoken, devour them, but they will dwell confidently without any terror, according to the exposition above.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.34.29-30":["And I will raise up for them, he says, a renowned shoot (or a planting of peace) — which speaks in the Gospel: I am the true vine —, and they will no longer be diminished by famine in the land — so the famine is in earthly things; but the famine of hearing the word of God, which, under the good shepherd, and the renowned shoot, celebrated in the speech of all, and the planting of peace, will by no means be in the land —, nor will they be subject any longer to the reproaches of the nations — so that they say: Where is the Lord their God? —; and after all these things they will know, not only that he himself is the Lord — which he had also said above —, but, with an addition, that the Lord of all is properly their God, with them — who says in the Gospel to the apostles: Behold, I am with you all days, even to the consummation of the age —; and when he himself is the Lord their God, then they too will be his people: not just anyone, but those who have deserved to be called the house of Israel.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.34.31":["And lest we should think that all that has been said pertains to shepherds and sheep and he-goats and rams, he solves the riddle, or rather the μεταφορὰν, and sets it down more plainly: But you, my sheep, and the sheep of my pasture, are men; the whole discourse, therefore, is about men, to whom he says: I the Lord your God, says the Lord God.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.35.1":["And the word of the Lord came to me, saying: Son of man, set (or turn) your face against Mount Seir, and you shall prophesy concerning it (or to it), and you shall say to it: Thus says the Lord God: Behold, I am against you, Mount Seir; and I will stretch out my hand over you, and I will make you desolate and deserted. I will demolish your cities (or in your cities I will make a waste), and you shall be deserted, and you shall know that I am the Lord. Because you have been a perpetual enemy, and have shut up the sons of Israel into the hands of the sword (or and have laid siege to the house of Israel deceitfully in the hand of the sword) in the time of their affliction, in the time of the final iniquity; therefore, as I live, says the Lord God, I will hand you over to blood (or since you have sinned in blood), and blood pursues you, and since you have hated blood, blood pursues you. And I will make Mount Seir desolate and deserted, and I will take away from it him who goes and him who returns (or men and beasts), and I will fill its mountains with its slain (or wounded) in your hills, and in your valleys and torrents those slain by the sword shall fall. I will hand you over to everlasting wastes, and your cities shall not be inhabited, and you all shall know (or you shall know) that I am the Lord. Because you have said: Two nations and two lands (or regions) shall be mine, and I will possess them as an inheritance, though the Lord was there; therefore, as I live, says the Lord God, I will act according to your anger (or enmity), and according to your zeal which you showed, holding them in hatred, and I will be made known to you when I have judged you; and you shall know that I am the Lord. I have heard all your reproaches (or the voice of your blasphemies) which you have spoken (or that you have spoken) concerning the mountains of Israel: Deserted, they have been given to us to devour. And you rose up against me with your mouth (or you spoke boastfully against me with your mouth), and you have prayed against me (or you have shouted against me) your words; I have heard. Thus says the Lord God: While the whole earth rejoices, I will reduce you to a waste; as you rejoiced over the inheritance of the house of Israel because it was laid waste, so will I do to you: you shall be laid waste (or deserted), Mount Seir, and all Edom, and they shall know that I am the Lord. (But what is added in the Seventy, And they shall be consumed, is not found in the Hebrew.) If the word of God were always in the prophets and had a constant lodging in their breast, Ezekiel would never so often set down: 'And the word of the Lord came to me, saying'; but it was because, on account of human frailty and the necessities of this life, it at times withdrew from them; therefore John the Baptist says: He who sent me to baptize, he said to me: He on whom you see the Holy Spirit descending in the form of a dove and remaining in him, he it is — for what is added, And remaining in him, would never be something proper to Christ, unless it sometimes withdrew from others —; besides, God also says to Moses: I will take away from the spirit that is in you — which he certainly would not have had unless it had descended to him from the Lord —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.35.2-15":["Now the whole prophecy of this chapter is against Mount Seir — namely of the sons of Esau and Edom, who in Greek and in our language are called Idumaeans [Edomites], because, in the time of need and distress, when Judah was being captured by the Babylonians, they insulted them, and, the people of the Jews having been cast out, they thought the land had been handed over to them to possess, and not only did they not take in his own sons, namely those of Jacob his brother, but they persecuted them and shut them up into the hands of the sword; whence Symmachus translated it more plainly: You have hated your blood, and blood pursues you —. It is prophesied, moreover, that he too is to be captured and reduced to everlasting wastes, and is to learn by the outcome of events that God is judge, because he said: Two nations and two regions are mine — either of the Edomites and the Jews, or surely of Judah and Israel, that is, of the two and the ten tribes —. And you spoke concerning the mountains of Israel, saying: Deserted, they have been given to us to devour, not considering that this blasphemy recoiled against God; hence there follows: While the whole earth rejoices, I will reduce you to a waste; and the sense is: 'When the whole land of Judea has recovered its former state, you will remain in an everlasting waste' — and what is added next: As you rejoiced over the inheritance of the house of Israel because it was laid waste, so will I do to you, is not found in the Seventy, but has been added under asterisks from the edition of Theodotion —. But the whole wrath of God against Mount Seir tends to this end: that when it has been laid waste, both it and all Edom may know that he is the Lord. These things I would say according to the history, and hastily, the sense rather than the words; I shall pass to the ἀναγωγὴν [anagogy, the higher sense], and, for the sake of brevity, touch briefly on a few points.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.35.2":["Set, he says, (or turn) your face against Mount Seir; and he says 'turn' significantly: from one prophecy to another prophecy; and 'face': not of the body but of the mind — of which the Apostle also speaks: But we all, with unveiled face beholding the glory of the Lord —; and Mount 'Seir', which means 'shaggy' and 'hairy', is to be taken as the opposing power that presided over the nation of the sons of Esau and had raised itself up against the people of 'Iuda' [Judah], that is, of 'confession' and of the true faith — but if a prudent reader objects, how in this place 'Seir', that is, 'shaggy' and 'hairy', is understood in a bad sense when Elijah too was called a hairy man, I shall answer briefly: it is written of Esau that he was red and shaggy like a hide, but of Elijah only that he was a hairy man; in Esau, who, bloodthirsty and bloodstained, is compared to a shaggy hide, it is a sign of mortal works and of death itself; in Elijah, an increase of manliness, for which reason both his dwelling in the wilderness and the austerity of his way of life showed him to be not only a man but a most valiant man; moreover Jacob, who had supplanted Esau and received his birthright, because he dwelt simply at home and was not a hunter like the giant Nimrod, is for that reason called smooth and sleek —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.35.3-4":["Let us see, then, what God commands to be said to the mountain, or against Mount Seir: 'Behold, I am against you, Mount Seir: I will not send angels, nor will I use other ministers, but I myself will come to you; and I will stretch out my hand over you, so as to take on the bearing of one who strikes, and I will make you desolate and deserted, so that you, who were wickedly inhabited and had very many companions in your impiety, to such a degree that you even built cities and claimed for yourself a dominion of your own, now, made desolate with your cities — which Jeremiah too is commanded to destroy and to dig up, so that he may build good cities in their place —, may profit in this, that you may know that I am the Lord, whom before you were laid waste you could not know'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.35.5":["Now the whole cause of the sin is this: that he was a perpetual enemy; hence the psalm also says: Be angry and do not sin, so that we may quickly be reconciled with our enemies and exchange hatreds for love; but Mount Seir is not such, which, on top of its perpetual enmities, shut up, or besieged, the sons of Israel by guile in the hand of the sword; from which we understand that whoever has seized the sword against the sons of God and has done anything deceitfully and has besieged them has Mount Seir in the hand of the sword in the time of the final iniquity: when the days are most evil, and: The world is set in the evil one, and: Because iniquity is multiplied, the love of many grows cold.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.35.6":["There is, moreover, another sin of Mount Seir, so that, since it hated the blood of the righteous, which it longed to see poured out one by one in persecution after persecution, it suffers persecution from that very blood — whence also under the altar the souls of the saints keep crying out and beg the Lord for vengeance for their blood —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.35.7":["And those who go and those who return are taken away from Mount Seir, that is, every inhabitant, or the men and beasts that are saved in the Church — of whom it is written: Men and beasts you will save, O Lord —; both of which are destroyed in Mount Seir, so that both those who seem to have some reason and those who are content with simple faith perish at God's command.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.35.8-9":["And its mountains are filled with the wounded and the slain; and not only the mountains, which have reached the summit of impiety, but the hills too, that is, the lesser disciples of Mount Seir, and the valleys, which are sunk into the depths, or the torrents, which have muddy waters, gathered from here and there and coming from pride, or, according to the Seventy, the plains, which lie midway between hills and valleys: all these, slain, shall fall by the sword of the Lord and of his outstretched and striking hand, so that they may be reduced to everlasting wastes; and if there were any cities, that is, gatherings of an evil habitation, they too are to perish and cease to hold agreement in the bad sense, and their tongues are to be divided, so that they cannot build a tower of blasphemy against God; and, reduced to a waste and a desert, let them know that he is the Lord.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.35.10":["Nor was it enough for Mount Seir to have said so much and done so much, as the preceding discourse has related, but this too it spoke in its pride: 'Two nations and two lands (or regions) are mine: namely, both Edom and Judea, both heretics and churchmen, I shall possess alike'. 'And this', he says, 'you said while the Lord was there, who presided over his people'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.35.11-12":["Therefore the Lord swears and says: 'As I live, says the Lord God, according to your anger with which you raged against the people of God, and your zeal with which you persecuted the household of Christ, holding in hatred those whom you longed to possess, when I have struck you and judged you, then I will be made known to you. And you shall know that I learned of your reproaches and blasphemies not through another's report, but that I myself heard what you spoke against the mountains of Israel, saying: Deserted, they have been given to us to devour, the mountains of Israel' — we ought to understand Moses, the prophets, the apostles, whom the heretics and the prince of heretics, the devil, think have been handed over to them to devour, when either rarely anyone or no one is found in the Church who can fight for the mountains of the Lord and ward off their mangling with his voice —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.35.13":["'And you rose up', he says, 'against me with your mouth, or you spoke boastfully, not, as you think, against the mountains, but through them against me; and you prayed, or cried out, against me; for all your prayers, O Mount Seir and companions of Mount Seir — of whom it was said above: I will fill its mountains and hills and valleys and plains (or torrents) —, were against me: not at all prayers and petitions, as you think, to God, but a clamor of shouting and blasphemy'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.35.14-15":["Hence the Lord also threatens and says: 'As you rejoiced over the inheritance of the house of Israel when by your persecutions it was laid waste and torn and scattered through the whole world, so will I do to you: for while the whole land of the living and the land of the saints rejoices, and all are turned to the service of God, you shall be laid waste, shaggy mountain, hairy mountain, and all Edom — namely, earthly works given over to gore and blood —, so that then you may know that I am the Lord, who have said elsewhere: I am the Lord your God'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.36.1-15":["These things we have said παραφραστικῶς [by way of paraphrase], in the Jewish manner: things which they expect in the reign of a thousand years, when they assert that the city of Jerusalem is to be built, and the temple which is described at the end of this volume, and the happiness of all things; and some of them think that these things were fulfilled under Zerubbabel — but how can what is said stand: And I will make you to be inhabited as from the beginning, and I will endow you with greater goods than you had from the beginning? For under Zerubbabel, Ezra and Nehemiah few of the people were brought back, and they themselves, obeying the Medes and Persians and thereafter the Macedonians and Egyptians and Romans, served the kings of various nations up to Titus and Vespasian, and serve them to this day, so that what is said at the end of the prophecy cannot stand at all: And the reproach of the peoples thou shalt by no means bear, and thou shalt no more lose thy nation —; and since it would be a long matter to speak now, in a brief explanation, against the Jewish dogma and against a blessedness that serves the belly and gullet of the Jews, who desire all earthly things and say: Let us eat and drink — of whom the Apostle also speaks: Food is for the belly and the belly for foods; but God will destroy both it and them —, let us now pass to the spiritual understanding, according to which we also interpreted the last parts of Isaiah. For we do not, according to the Jewish fables which they call δευτερώσεις [deuteroses, secondary traditions], expect a jeweled and golden Jerusalem from heaven, nor are we going to suffer again the injury of circumcision, nor to offer victims of bulls and rams, nor shall we sleep in the idleness of the sabbath — which many of our own also promise, and especially the book of Tertullian entitled 'On the Hope of the Faithful' and the seventh volume of Lactantius' 'Institutes', and the frequent 'Expositions' of Victorinus, bishop of Poetovio, and recently our Severus in the dialogue to which he gave the name 'Gallus', and, to name the Greeks and join the first and the last, Irenaeus and Apollinaris —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.36.1-3":["We, therefore, say that the mountains of Israel are the prophets and apostles, who hear the word of God and whom the devil, the enemy, insulted, saying: 'Aha, the mountains once lofty — of which it is written: Mountains are round about it, and the Lord is round about his people — have been given to me as a possession, when through the straits of persecution they were made desolate and trampled down round about, and made an inheritance for the rest of the nations, and blasphemed by all'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.36.1-5":["But you, son of man, prophesy over the mountains of Israel, and you shall say: Mountains of Israel, hear the word of the Lord. Thus says the Lord God: Because the enemy has said of you: Aha, the everlasting heights (or solitudes) have been given to us as a possession; therefore prophesy and say: Thus says the Lord God: Because you have been made desolate and trampled down round about (or Because you have been scattered by those who are round about you), and made an inheritance (or a possession) for the rest of the nations, and you have come up upon the lip of the tongue and become a reproach to the peoples (or nations); therefore, mountains of Israel, hear the word of the Lord God: Thus says the Lord God to the mountains and the hills, to the torrents and the valleys and the deserts, to the ruined walls (or the things scattered) and the forsaken cities, which have been depopulated and derided (or trampled down) by the rest of the nations round about. Therefore thus says the Lord God: Since in the fire of my zeal I have spoken concerning the rest of the nations, and concerning all Edom, who have given my land to themselves as an inheritance with joy and with the whole heart and from the soul, and have cast it out in order to lay it waste (or with joy, despising souls, in order to overthrow them in devastation), therefore prophesy over the land of Israel, and you shall say to the mountains and the hills, to the ridges and the valleys: Thus says the Lord God: Behold, I have spoken in my zeal and in my fury, because you have borne the confusion (or the reproaches) of the nations; therefore thus says the Lord God (or Adonai the Lord): I have lifted up (or I will lift up) my hand, so that the nations which are round about you shall themselves bear their confusion (or ignominy); but you, mountains of Israel, shall put forth your branches (or your grapes), and bear your fruit for my people Israel, for it is near that it should come. For behold, I am toward you, and I will turn to you (or I will look upon you), and you shall be plowed and shall receive seed; and I will multiply men upon you, and the whole house of Israel, and the cities shall be inhabited and the ruined places shall be restored (or what is deserted shall be built). And I will fill you with men and with beasts, and they shall be multiplied and shall increase; and I will make you to be inhabited as from the beginning, and I will endow you with greater goods than you had from the beginning, and you shall know that I am the Lord. And I will bring (or I will beget) upon you men, my people Israel, and they shall possess thee by inheritance (or you), and thou shalt be (or you shall be) a possession for them, and thou shalt not add any more to be without them (or and you shall not add any more that they should make you without children). Thus says the Lord God: Because they have said of you (or to thee): Thou art a devourer of men, and one who strangles thy nation; therefore thou shalt no more eat men, and thou shalt no longer kill thy nation, says the Lord God. Nor will I any more cause the confusion (or ignominy) of the nations to be heard in thee (or in you), and the reproaches of the peoples thou shalt by no means bear (or you shall bear), and thou shalt no more lose thy nation (or thy nation shall no longer be without children), says the Lord God. Above, the prophet's face is set, or turned, against Mount Seir; here the son of man speaks to the mountains, or over the mountains, of Israel, which are commanded to hear the word of God; and, to put off the tropology for a little, this in brief is the sense of the whole prophecy to the mountains of Israel: 'Because the enemy, that is, Mount Seir, insulted you, and thought that he himself would possess you forever, and that the heights, once the Lord's and now deserted, had passed to him as an inheritance, and not only said so but also possessed you for some time, to such a degree that he spoke proudly against you and you were a reproach to all the nations, therefore thus says the Lord God, not only to Mount Seir, but also to its mountains and hills, torrents, valleys and deserts and wasted places and forsaken cities: that in the fire of his zeal he has spoken, against all the nations indeed, but especially against Edom, which with the other' he says 'nations held my land as a possession, and possessed it with joy, and possessed it with the whole heart, so as to cast the inhabitants out of it and lay the land waste.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.36.4-5":["Therefore the Lord speaks to the mountains trampled down and made desolate, and not only to the mountains but also to those lower down, set at their several degrees in the land of Judea, that is, on the mountain of the Church — of which it is written: A city set upon a mountain cannot be hidden —, and to the hills and torrents, whose faith is increased by the winter of persecutions and the storm of affliction, to the valleys also, those who have been brought low in humility, and to the deserted churches, and to the ruined walls, and to the meeting places of the churches forsaken throughout the whole world — such was the persecution throughout the whole world under Diocletian and Maximian, through which the churches were proscribed and laid waste, when all the nations of the Gentiles and of the heretics round about derided the Church —: that he has spoken in the fire of the zeal of his fury; and particularly against Edom, which by earthly works wanted everyone to lay aside the image of the heavenly and bear the image of the earthly: for they gave the land of God to themselves as an inheritance, and with the whole heart and from the soul they persecuted the Church of the Lord, and cast it out of its seats, and laid it waste.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.36.6-8":["Therefore speak,' he says, 'O prophet, over the land of Israel, and you shall say to the mountains and hills and ridges — for which the Seventy translated νάπας [glens], which we understand as 'places' that are 'wooded' —, and to the valleys: Thus says the Lord God: In my zeal and in my fury I will stand forth as the avenger of your reproach; and I will lift up my hand, so that the nations which are round about you may bear their ignominy and confusion; but you, O mountains of Israel, shall sprout according to your former state, and put forth shoots and branches — or, according to the Seventy, bear grapes —, with which my people Israel may be filled; and do not think that this will come after a long time: it is near, the time is at hand and will come.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","But what did it profit the nations, what did it help the nations of heretics to have insulted the land of the Lord, when the Lord speaks to it, and has spoken in his zeal and in his fury to its mountains and hills and ridges, or wooded places, which were green with the loveliness of paradise, and to the hills, of which we spoke above, and lifts up his hand, on account of the reproach of the nations, against the adversaries who persecuted the Church, so that they bear their confusion and ignominy?: then, when the persecutors have been slain and peace has been restored to the Church, the mountains of Israel, namely the apostles and apostolic men, will make their trees sprout and stretch out their branches, and will bring grapes to be trodden in the winepress of the Lord, from which the new wine flows that inebriates the peoples of believers, and the wine is pressed out that gladdens the heart of man, so that all the fruit of the mountains, from which is made the bread descending from heaven, may be eaten by the people of God, Israel.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.36.8-10":["And lest perhaps we despair in persecutions, it is added: It is near — the time — that it should come — for coming indeed he will come and will not delay —; hence he promises his coming to the mountains and to those who have endured in tribulations, and promises that, after the heat of persecution, the land formerly stripped may be worked by the plowshare of Christ, and receive every seed of the virtues, from which men may be born and multiplied, and the whole house of Israel.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.36.9-11":["And lest this seem difficult to you, I myself will come to you, and I who had withdrawn from you will turn to you, so that you may recover your former cultivation and all things may be filled with seed, so that there may be many men in you, and the cities which had formerly been destroyed may be inhabited. And not only will I multiply men, but the beasts too shall likewise be multiplied and shall increase; and I will make you to be inhabited as from the beginning, when, under the leadership of Moses, two and a half tribes received their possession beyond the Jordan, and, under Joshua the son of Nun, the other nine and a half tribes possessed the land of Judea; and I will endow you with goods much greater than you were from the beginning, before you experienced the various captivities; and when I have done this for you, then you will know that I am the Lord.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.36.10-12":["And when the cities, that is, the churches of believers, have been inhabited by the returning crowds, then not only the men who will be strong in the knowledge of the Scriptures, but also the beasts, all the simple among the believers, will be multiplied and grow up; and the churches will be inhabited as from the beginning, that is, before there was persecution, and will flourish with greater goods than they had from the beginning, crowned with the victories of the martyrs. And then they will know that he is the Lord who restores his people to its former state, so that it may possess the churches and itself be the inheritance of the churches, and no longer be without the children whom it had lost in persecution.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.36.12":["And you shall be filled,' he says, 'O land of Israel, with my people, who will hold you as an inheritance, and you shall be a possession for them, and you shall not add any more to be without sons.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.36.13-15":["But when your inhabitant has been multiplied, O mountains of Israel, then your enemies will by no means say against you that you devour men and that your possession is without children; but on the contrary you will multiply men, and your posterity will bring forth a most abundant offspring. Nor will you hear,' he says, 'any more, nor will you bear, the confusion of the nations; nor yet will you lose your nation so as ever to undergo captivity, since the Lord has spoken'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","Hence the prophet is commanded to speak to the land of the meek and the land of the living, or to the mountains of Israel, and to say: 'You shall no longer suffer the reproach of the persecutors, nor will they insult your slaughtered people as sheep for the slaughter; for when peace has been restored, all reproaches will cease, nor will you bear confusion and ignominy among the various nations round about, but you will have your nation and your people, because the truth of the promise has been confirmed, since the Lord has spoken'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.36.16-38":["And the word of the Lord came to me, saying: Son of man, the house of Israel have dwelt (or has dwelt) in their land, and they defiled it in their ways and in their pursuits (or idols); like the uncleanness of a menstruous woman their way has become before me. And I poured out my indignation (or my fury) upon them, for the blood which they shed upon the land; with their idols they defiled it. And I scattered them among the nations, and they were winnowed (or I winnowed them) into the lands (or into the regions); according to their ways and their devices (or sins) I judged them. And they went in to the nations to which they entered, and they defiled my holy name, when it was said of them: This is the people of the Lord, and they have gone out of his land. And I spared my holy name (or I spared them for the sake of my holy name), which the house of Israel had defiled among the nations to which they went in. Therefore you shall say to the house of Israel: Thus says the Lord God: Not for your sake will I act, house of Israel, but for the sake of my holy name, which you defiled among the nations to which you entered; and I will sanctify my great name, which has been defiled among the nations, which you defiled in the midst of them, so that they may know that I am the Lord, says the Lord of hosts, when I shall have been sanctified in you before them. For I will take you from the nations, and gather you from all the lands, and bring you into your own land; and I will pour out (or sprinkle) clean water upon you, and you shall be cleansed from all your defilements, and from all your idols I will cleanse you. And I will give you a new heart, and a new spirit I will put in your midst (or I will give you), and I will take away the heart of stone out of your flesh, and give you a heart of flesh; and my spirit I will put in your midst (or I will give you), and I will cause you to walk in my precepts (or in my justifications), and to keep my judgments and to work (or do) them. And you shall dwell in the land which I gave to your fathers, and you shall be to me for a people and I will be to you for a God. And I will save you from all your defilements (or uncleannesses), and I will call the fruit and multiply it, and I will not lay (or give) famine upon you. And I will multiply the fruit of the tree and the produce of the field, so that you may no longer bear the reproach of famine among the nations. And you shall remember your most evil ways, and your pursuits that were not good, and your iniquities and your crimes shall displease you. Not for your sake will I act, says the Lord God; let it be known to you: be confounded and blush for your ways, house of Israel. Thus says the Lord God: On the day when I shall have cleansed you from all your iniquities, and caused the cities to be inhabited and restored the ruined places, and the deserted land shall have been tilled which once was desolate in the eyes of every traveler, they will say: That untilled land (or deserted) has become like a garden of pleasure, and the cities deserted and forsaken and undermined have sat fortified. And the nations which shall have been left round about you will know that I the Lord have built what was scattered and planted what was untilled; that I the Lord have spoken and have done it. Thus says the Lord God: Yet in this will the house of Israel find me (or I will be sought by the house of Israel), that I may do it for them: I will multiply them like a flock of men (or like sheep, men), like a holy flock (or like holy sheep), like the flock (or sheep) of Jerusalem in her solemn feasts: so shall the deserted cities also be full of flocks (or sheep) of men, and they shall know that I am the Lord. What is said is clear, and the whole sense concerning the mountains of Israel, which had formerly been deserted and were afterwards restored, is now set out more plainly; and God gives the reasons why he handed the people of Israel over to captivity, and on account of his clemency promises that he will lead them back into the land of Judea, and bestow far more than he had taken away — which some of the Jews refer to the times of Zerubbabel, when, as Cyrus king of the Persians relaxed their captivity, very many from the tribe of Judah and Benjamin were brought back into Judea; but others refer it to the reign of a thousand years, when under the Christ, who they think is yet to come, both the city of Jerusalem is to be built up and the temple to be built, of which we shall speak in the last part of this prophecy —. And meanwhile, to leave behind Jewish fables and endless genealogies, let us, according to our custom, briefly run through the περικοπὴν [section] of this chapter, and set out what seems right to us according to the ecclesiastical understanding.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.36.17-18":["Whoever is of the house of Israel and sees the peace of Christ, which surpasses all understanding, dwells in his own land, the Church, or in the lodging of the flesh which has been handed over to him by God; but if he defiles either land in his most evil ways and is compared to the uncleanness of a menstruous woman, God pours out his indignation upon him, for the blood, whether his own or that of those whom he has scandalized and has shed upon the land, and for the perverse thoughts with which he defiled it.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.36.17":["The house of Israel, that is, the people of the Jews, once dwelt in its own land, that is, in the land of Judea, when it was led out of Egypt; but they defiled it in their ways and in their pursuits, or idols; and they became so polluted that they were compared to the uncleanness of a menstruous woman.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.36.18-20":["On which account God pours out his indignation upon them for the blood of the prophets and the righteous which they shed upon the land — as the Savior says in the Gospel: Jerusalem, Jerusalem, you who kill the prophets and stone those who have been sent to you, and again: Amen, amen I say to you: the blood which has been shed shall be required of this generation, from the blood of Abel the righteous up to the blood of Zechariah the son of Barachiah, which they shed between the altar and the temple —; and not only did they shed blood, but they also defiled the land with their crimes; for which reason he scattered them, whether under the Babylonian captivity or under the Roman, when they truly shed the blood of Christ; and they were winnowed into the lands as chaff is winnowed, and judged according to their ways and their most evil inventions, so that among the nations too, to which they had been led captive, they defiled the holy name of God, because all the nations said in mockery: 'Behold, this is the people of God, which boasted that it properly knew the worship of God'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.36.19-20":["Then he will be scattered among the nations so as to be made equal to the unbelieving nations, and he will be winnowed on the earth like chaff so as to be separated from the grain of the Church, and he will be judged according to his ways; and if not even so does he perceive his sin, but defiles his holy name, all among whom he dwells will say: 'Behold the people of God, behold one who went out of his land, or rather was cast out'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.36.21-26":["'When I had seen this,' he says, 'not for their sake but for the sake of my holy name — for I am the Creator of all — I spared them; and I sanctified them and restored them to their former glory, so that upon those who believed and were converted from error I poured out the clean water of saving baptism, and cleansed them from their abominations and from all the errors with which they had been taken up; and I gave them a new heart, that they might believe in the Son of God, and a new spirit' — of which David speaks: Create a clean heart in me, O God, and renew a right spirit in my inward parts —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.36.23-25":["'But if they repent and I am sanctified in their midst, then the whole crowd of the nations will know that I will take them from the lands into which they had been scattered, and lead them back into the land of the Church; and I will pour out upon them not the waters of saving baptism but the waters of the teaching and the word of God, and I will cleanse them from all their defilements and from all the idols and errors which they have feigned in their heart.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.36.25-27":["And it must be considered that the new heart and the new spirit are given after the pouring out and sprinkling of the water; and when the new heart and the new spirit have been given, then all the hardness which is compared to stone will be taken away from the Jewish heart, and in place of the heart of stone there comes to be a heart of flesh, soft and tender, which can receive the spirit of God into itself and be inscribed with saving letters; then they will walk in the precepts of the Lord and keep his judgments.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.36.26-27":["And I will give them,' he says, 'a new heart, which they had lost through sin, and I will renew a right spirit in their hearts, and I will take away the heart of stone, namely the unbelieving heart, and give a heart of flesh, soft and tender, which may receive the precepts of God, so that they walk in my precepts and do the judgments which they had formerly neglected.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.36.28":["And they will dwell in the land of confession, which he had given to their fathers Abraham, Isaac and Jacob and to all the saints and prophets, and they will be for a people of God and the Lord will be to them for a God, which is borne out at the present time.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","And let them dwell in the land which I had given to their fathers, namely the masters and teachers, and let them again be the people of God, and let the Lord, whom they had formerly offended, become God to them.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.36.29":["And when they have been saved, the Lord will call the grain and multiply it — for unless indeed a grain of wheat falling into the earth has died, it remains alone —, and they will by no means suffer famine — not a famine of bread and a thirst for water, but a famine of hearing the word of God — the famine which he suffered who, setting out from his father, squandered all the substance he had received, when, compelled by the necessity of hunger, he fed on the pods of the swine.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","Then they will be saved from all their defilements, and the grain from which the heavenly bread is made will be multiplied for them, and they will no longer suffer the famine of the word of God.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.36.30":["Then the fruit of the tree will be multiplied — that is, of wisdom, of which it is written: She is a tree of life to all who seek her — and the produce of the field — in the likeness of Jacob, whose smell was: Like the smell of a full field which the Lord has blessed —, and they will no longer bear the reproach of famine among the nations, which the unbelief of the Jews suffers even today; but, after they have attained blessedness, they will imitate the Apostle, who says: I am not worthy to be called an apostle, because I persecuted the Church of God.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","And the tree of wisdom will be multiplied in them, and the produce will be brought to fullness in their breast, nor will they any longer be a reproach among the nations.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.36.31-32":["But, after God has had mercy on them, they will remember their most evil ways, and they will know where they have been, and they will be displeasing to themselves in their iniquities'; which the Lord says he does, not for their sake, but for the sake of his own clemency.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.36.31":["And they will remember their most evil ways and their pursuits that were not good, by which they had offended God, and their iniquities and their crimes, by which they had formerly gone astray, will displease them.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.36.32-36":["Hence he also exhorts those who stray to be confounded and to blush for their ways, and to be restored to the cities of the Church, so that these may again be inhabited by them, and everything that had collapsed in them may be built up, and they may return to their former ceremonies, and the land once deserted, which had seemed desolate to the neighbors and strangers and to those passing through the conduct of this world, may return to its former state, and all may marvel and say: 'That untilled land, that deserted land in which all the virtues once perished, has now become like a garden of pleasure and the paradise of God'; and the cities once deserted, which did not have God as their guest and were forsaken by the Holy Spirit and undermined by unbelief, will be fortified by the faith of Christ, so that all round about may know that the Lord has built up the scattered souls, and planted in them and sown together the trees of all the virtues, and that he who promised by word has himself made it good in deed.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.36.32":["Now the Lord will bestow all these things, not for the sake of those who perished by their own error, but for the sake of his holy name; hence he challenges the house of Israel to be confounded and to blush for its former vices, and to understand its Creator.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.36.33-36":["But after he has cleansed the people of believers from among the Jews from their iniquities, and caused the cities of the Church to be inhabited, and restored the ruined places which had fallen in the synagogues, and the land of confession has been tilled with the ceremonies of God, which once seemed desolate to all the saints who pass by the works of earthly conduct, then all will say: 'How is it that the land of Judea, which had once been untilled, now has the likeness of the paradise of God, and the cities of Judea, which had been deserted and forsaken and undermined by their own unbelief, are now raised up and fortified in the confession and the name of the Lord Savior?'; so that every creature, and the multitudes of angels that encircle the land of Israel, may know that the Lord has built the scattered cities and planted the untilled regions, and has fulfilled in deed what he promised through the prophets.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.36.37-38":["Nor will he be content with this gift of his liberality toward the people he has saved, but he will bestow far greater things: for he will be found by the house of Israel, he who had been sought for a long time and not found; and, after it has found him, he will multiply them like sheep, not sheep of brute animals but sheep of men — who are full of reason and confession —, like holy sheep and the flock of Jerusalem — in which properly is the worship of God and the vision of peace —, in her solemn feasts — when we eat the unleavened bread of sincerity and truth, and feed on the flesh of the Lamb and are given his blood to drink, and in the seven weeks and in the tabernacles of this age, of the Lord, of the Lord, celebrate the feasts —, so that the cities, once deserted, may become full of flocks of men, and by this means they may know that he is the Lord who has bestowed all things. Let it suffice to have said these things briefly according to the truth of the history, or rather according to the faith of the prophecy: how the people of Israel is restored to its unimpaired state, and the remnant of the people of the Jews, as the Apostle Paul teaches, is saved in the apostles and in all who from the Jews believed in the beginning and to this day return to the Church; now the sense of the tropology must be opened with the same brevity.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","And again the prophetic word exhorts the penitent that, even when they have returned into the churches, they should always seek the Lord and find him, so that the flocks of the Lord may be multiplied by the crowds of those returning, not flocks of beasts and brute animals, but flocks of men who are full of faith and reason, holy flocks, flocks of the city of Jerusalem, in all the solemn feasts which they have received by the clemency of God, so that, after a multitude of such flocks has filled the cities of the Lord, then all may know that he is the Lord.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.37.1-14":["But those who interpret otherwise will put forward the following: it ought not to bring odium upon us that, by expounding this passage otherwise, we seem to deny the resurrection: for we know that we find in the holy Scriptures much stronger testimonies, and ones in which there is no doubt — such as that of Job: You will raise up my skin, which endures these things, and in Daniel: Many who sleep in the dust of the earth shall rise again, these to life eternal, and these to reproach and to eternal confusion, and in the Gospel: Do not fear those who kill the body and cannot kill the soul; but rather fear him who can destroy both soul and body in gehenna, and in the apostle Paul: Who will give life to your mortal bodies because of his spirit dwelling in you, and many others —; from which it is clear that we do not deny the resurrection, but contend that these things were not written of the resurrection and that, through the parable of the resurrection, it is the restoration of Israel that is prophesied, which at that time was captive in Babylon. Nor shall we at once give an occasion to heretics if we deny that these things are to be understood of the common resurrection: for the likeness of the resurrection would never be set forth to signify the restoration of the Israelite people, unless the resurrection itself stood firm and were believed to be in the future — since no one confirms uncertain things from things that do not exist —; and our whole assertion tends to this: that, just as it seems unbelievable that a future resurrection should be promised to dry bones worn away by great age, and yet what is promised will be, so also the restoration of the people of Israel, which was led into captivity and scattered over the whole world, seems indeed incredible to those who do not know the power of God, but yet it will be: 'For I' he says 'the Lord have spoken and will do it, as I have promised'. Finally, the earlier prophecies too, in which their ancient state is promised again to the mountains of Israel, and the Lord says to them: And I will turn to you, and you shall be plowed and shall receive seed; and I will multiply men upon you, and all the house of Israel, and again that the house of Israel will dwell in its own land, which, once uncultivated, will afterwards be like a garden of delight, and that the deserted and abandoned cities are fortified, and the house of Israel is multiplied in them like a flock of sheep, and the rest that follows — these pertain to the same sense which is now spoken under the tropology and parable of the dry bones that have no moisture of life at all — that being fulfilled which is written in the Gospel: What seems impossible with men is possible with God —; and they were cast forth from their graves, from the graves of captivity and from the chains by which they were held bound in Babylonian servitude. But the Jews affirm that all these things were fulfilled either under Zerubbabel, as I said before, when a great commotion took place and the kingdom of the Chaldeans was transferred to the Medes and Persians, or at the presence of their Christ, who they think is yet to come. We, however, declare that they were fulfilled spiritually after the cross of the Lord Savior and are fulfilled daily, above all in those who, like Lazarus bound in the grave-bands of their sins, are raised at the voice of the Lord, and are truly the house of Israel, once dry and having no hope of salvation, but who, as the spirit of grace enters into them and the Lord stretches out his hand, are set free from the depth of the underworld; and those who had earlier said: Lord God, you know, may afterwards, once set free, hear this: O you of little faith, why did you doubt?\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.37.1-2":["The hand of the Lord came upon me, and he led me out in the spirit of the Lord, and set me down in the midst of a plain which was full of bones; and he led me around through them in a circle: and they were very many upon the face of the plain, and exceedingly dry. And he said to me: Son of man, do you think these bones will live? And I said: Lord God, you know. And he said to me: Prophesy concerning these bones, and you shall say to them: Dry bones, hear the word of the Lord; thus says the Lord God to these bones: Behold, I will send spirit into you and you shall live, and I will put sinews upon you and make flesh grow up upon you, and I will stretch skin over you, and I will give you spirit, and you shall live, and you shall know that I am the Lord. And I prophesied as he had commanded me. And there was a sound, as I prophesied, and behold, a commotion; and the bones drew near, bones to bones, each one to its joint. And I looked, and behold, sinews and flesh came up upon them, and skin was stretched over them from above, and they did not have spirit. And he said to me: Prophesy to the spirit; prophesy, son of man, and you shall say to the spirit: Thus says the Lord God: Come from the four winds, O spirit, and breathe upon these slain, and let them come to life again. And I prophesied as he had commanded me, and the spirit entered into them; and they lived, and they stood upon their feet, an army great beyond measure, exceedingly. And he said to me: Son of man, these bones are the whole house of Israel; they say: Our bones have dried up and our hope has perished, and we are cut off. Therefore prophesy and you shall say to them: Thus says the Lord God: Behold, I will open your tombs, and I will lead you out of your graves, my people, and I will bring you into the land of Israel; and you shall know that I am the Lord, when I have opened your graves and have led you out of your tombs, my people, and have put my spirit in you and you have lived, and I will make you rest upon your own soil, and you shall know that I am the Lord: I have spoken and I have done it, says the Lord God. (LXX: \"And the hand of the Lord came upon me, and he led me out in the spirit of the Lord, and placed me in the midst of a plain, and this was full of the bones of men; and he led me around over them by a circuit round about: and behold, very many upon the face of the plain, and dry exceedingly. And he said to me: Son of man, will these bones live? And I said: Lord, Lord, you know these things. And he said to me: Son of man, prophesy over these bones, and you shall say to them: Dry bones, hear the word of the Lord; thus says the Lord God to these bones: Behold, I will bring upon you the spirit of life, and I will put sinews upon you and bring flesh upon you, and I will stretch hide over you, and I will give you my spirit, and you shall live, and you shall know that I am the Lord. And I prophesied as he commanded me. And there was a voice, when I was prophesying, and behold, a commotion; and the bones were drawing near, bones to bones, to its joint. And I looked, and behold, sinews and flesh were springing up upon them, and hides were coming up over them from above, and there was no spirit in them. And he said to me: Prophesy over the spirit, prophesy, son of man, and say to the spirit: Thus says the Lord, the Lord: Come from the four spirits, O spirit, and breathe upon these dead, and let them live. And I prophesied as he had commanded me, and the spirit entered into them; and they lived, and stood on their feet, a congregation large, exceedingly, beyond measure. And the Lord spoke to me, saying: Son of man, these bones are all the house of Israel; they say: Our bones have become dry, our hope has perished, we have despaired. Therefore prophesy, and say to them: Thus says the Lord, the Lord: Behold, I will open your memorials, and I will bring you back from your graves, my people, and I will bring you into the land of Israel; and you shall know that I am the Lord, when I have opened your graves so as to lead you out of your graves, my people, and I will give my spirit in you and you shall live, and I will place you upon your own land, and you shall know that I am the Lord: I have spoken and I will do it, says the Lord, the Lord.\") The vision is a famous one, and celebrated in the reading of all the churches of Christ; and for this reason above all I have set down both editions in full, so that no calumny might be raised against us by the Hebrews if they differed even in some words — for they are accustomed to laugh at us and to raise their eyebrows, and with puffed-out cheeks to belch forth their knowledge of the Scriptures, if they have been able to point out in our codices, I will not say a discrepancy of sense — which, if there were one, is rightly censured — but a dissonance of words —. Those, then, who think that the word of God is about the resurrection — which is believed by all, both Jews and Christians — are accustomed to say this: The hand of the Lord came upon the prophet, that is, the Lord and Savior, through whom the Father wrought all things — All things indeed were made by him, and without him nothing was made that was made —. And he led me out, he says, in the spirit of the Lord: the hand of the Lord is understood; and he led him out in the spirit: not in the body but outside the body, and placed him, or set him down, in the midst of a plain which was full of human bones; nor did it allow him to rest, but made him go around all the bones, which were not covered with earth but lying upon the plain: and not only many, but very many and, because of the length of time, dry and exceedingly parched and having no moisture in them.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.37.3":["And when the divine word had asked him whether he thought these bones could live, he replied: 'Lord God, you know, you who have full knowledge of things to come'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.37.4-6":["And the Lord said to him: Prophesy concerning the bones (or over these bones), and you shall say to them: Dry bones, hear the word of the Lord. In this it is to be wondered at how he spoke to dry bones, which, before the sinews, flesh and skin and the life-giving spirit, could hear the word of God; and first bones are bound to other bones by the bonds of sinews, then they are filled with flesh, and over them, for beauty, skin is stretched to cover the unsightliness of the bare flesh, and then they receive the spirit which makes them live, so that, after they have lived, they may then know that he is the Lord.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.37.7-8":["So when the prophet spoke what he had been commanded, at once a commotion arose; and the bones were fitted into their framework, bound with sinews, full of flesh, covered with hide; and there lay human bodies that did not have spirit.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.37.9-10":["Therefore the prophet prophesies to the spirit, and says: Thus says the Lord God: Come from the four winds, O spirit — namely from the four quarters of the world, so that, just as in the first creation of man: God breathed into his face and he was made into a living soul, so also the second creation, that is, the resurrection of the dead, may be given life by the breathing of the spirit —, and the spirit entered the human bodies, and at once they lived and stood upon their feet — whence also the resurrection of the dead is called a 'congregation', or church, 'large' and, as it is in the Hebrew, 'a great army'; and at that time is fulfilled: Send forth your spirit and they shall be created, and you shall renew the face of the earth —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.37.11-14":["But what follows: And he said to me: Son of man, these bones are the whole house of Israel, seems to raise a question, in that it is said not of the general resurrection but properly of the resurrection of the house of Israel, which says: 'Our bones have dried up and our hope has perished, and we are cut off from our land' or 'we have utterly despaired'; and as they say these things, Ezekiel is compelled to prophesy a third time and to say to the dry bones: Thus says the Lord God: Behold, I will open your tombs, and here it is asked, if he opens tombs, how he said above: And they were very many upon the face of the plain, and exceedingly dry. And I will lead, he says, you out of your graves, my people — according to what is written in the Gospel: The hour comes when those who are in the graves shall hear the voice of the Son of God; and those who have done good shall come forth to the resurrection of life, and again: Those who hear shall live —. And if, as some think, the divine word speaks of the general resurrection, what need was there to say specifically: And I will bring you into the land of Israel, when throughout the whole world the dead must rise from those places in which they were buried? And when, he says, I have led you out of your tombs and have put my spirit in you and you have lived, then: 'I will make you rest upon your own soil, so that, after you have rested in the land of Israel, you may then know that I am the Lord, who have fulfilled my promises in deed'. Those, then, who understand these words of the general resurrection refer what seems to raise a question: These bones are the whole house of Israel, to the resurrection of the saints — of which the apostle John also speaks in the Revelation: Blessed and holy is he who has part in the first resurrection; over him the second death has no power —, namely that the resurrection of the saints is one thing, that of sinners another — whence also it is said in the first psalm: The ungodly shall not rise again in judgment, nor sinners in the council of the righteous —; and the land of Israel, which the Lord promises to those who rise again, they affirm to be that of which it is written: Blessed are the meek, for they shall possess the land, and: I will please the Lord in the region of the living. These things are said by those who hold that Ezekiel wrote, as we have said, of the general resurrection.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.37.15-28":["But if the Jews and judaizing Christians wish to refer these things to the reign of a thousand years, they will be compelled by necessity to accept that all who have been saved will dwell in the land of Israel, that Jerusalem is to be built, the temple to be erected, all the ceremonies of the law to be practiced, the sabbath to be observed, the injury of circumcision to be received, that there is to be eating and drinking, and that an abundance of riches is to be esteemed as the highest blessedness and all wealth, when the Apostle says: Food for the belly and the belly for foods: but God will destroy both it and them; but just as, in the type of the resurrection, the preceding prophetic word showed the restoration of the Jewish people, not carnal but spiritual, so also this prophecy pertains not at all to the happiness of the flesh but to that of the soul, and to the faith of Christ, by which we are cleansed in baptism, whose kingdom is for ever, so that we may not await as things to come what has already been accomplished and what we know is accomplished daily.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.37.16-19":["And the word of the Lord came to me, saying: And you, son of man, take for yourself one stick and write upon it: Of Judah and of the sons of Israel his companions. And take another stick and write upon it: Of Joseph — the stick of Ephraim — and of the house of Israel and of his companions. And join them one to the other for yourself into one stick; and they shall be in union in your hand. And when the sons of your people say to you, speaking: Will you not show us what you mean by these things? you shall speak to them: Thus says the Lord God: Behold, I will take the stick of Joseph — which is in the hand of Ephraim — and the tribes of Israel which are joined to him, and I will put them together with the stick of Judah and make them one stick, and they shall be one in his hand. And the sticks on which you have written shall be in your hand before their eyes, and you shall say to them: Thus says the Lord God: Behold, I will take the sons of Israel from the midst of the nations to which they have gone, and I will gather them from every side, and I will bring them to their own soil, and I will make them one nation in the land on the mountains of Israel; and one king shall be ruling over all, and they shall no longer be two nations, nor shall they be divided any more into two kingdoms. Nor shall they be defiled any more with their idols and abominations and with all their iniquities, and I will save them out of all their dwelling places in which they sinned, and I will cleanse them, and they shall be my people, and I will be their God. And my servant David shall be king over them, and there shall be one shepherd of them all; they shall walk in my judgments, and they shall keep my commandments and do them. And they shall dwell upon the land which I gave to my servant Jacob, in which your fathers dwelt; and they shall dwell upon it, they and their sons and their sons' sons for ever, and David my servant shall be their prince for ever. And I will strike with them a covenant of peace, it shall be an everlasting pact for them, and I will establish them and multiply them; and I will put my sanctification in the midst of them for ever, and my tabernacle shall be among them; and the nations shall know that I am the Lord, the sanctifier of Israel, when my sanctification shall be in the midst of them for ever. (LXX: \"And the word of the Lord came to me, saying: And you, son of man, take to yourself one rod and write upon it: Judah and the sons of Israel who are added to him. And you shall take to yourself a second rod and you shall inscribe it: Joseph — the rod of Ephraim — and all the sons of Israel who are added to him. And you shall join them to each other for yourself into one rod, so as to couple them; and they shall be in your hand. And it shall be, when the sons of Israel say to you, speaking: Will you not show us what these things are to you? you shall say to them: Thus says the Lord, the Lord: Behold, I will take into the tribe of Joseph — through the hand of Ephraim — the tribes of Israel which are joined to him, and I will put them all upon the tribe of Judah and they shall be into one rod, and they shall be one in the hand of Judah. And the rods which you inscribed shall be thus, in your hand in their presence, and you shall say to them: Thus says the Lord, the Lord: Behold, I will take the whole house of Israel from the midst of the nations into which they have entered, and I will gather them from all who are round about it, and I will bring them into the land of Israel, and I will make them into one nation in my land on the mountains of Israel; and one prince shall be over all these as king, and they shall no longer be into two nations, nor shall they be divided any more into two kingdoms. And let them no longer be defiled with their idols and with their stumbling blocks and with all their impieties, and I will rescue them from all their iniquities in which they sinned, and I will cleanse them, and they shall be to me for a people: I will be to them for God. And my servant David shall be prince in their midst, and there shall be one shepherd of all these, for they shall walk in my precepts and keep my judgments and do them. And they shall dwell in their own land which I gave to my servant Jacob, where their fathers dwelt; and they shall dwell in it, they and their sons and their sons' sons for ever, and David my servant shall be their prince for ever. And I will appoint for them a testament of peace, it shall be an everlasting testament with them, and I will give them and multiply them; and I will set my holy things in their midst for ever, and my tabernacle shall be among them, and I will be their God, and they shall be my people; and the nations shall know that I am the Lord who sanctifies them, when my holy things shall be in their midst for ever.\") The history of the Kings relates that under Rehoboam the son of Solomon the twelve tribes were divided, and that two, that is, Judah and Benjamin, with the Levites and priests, followed Rehoboam, who reigned in Jerusalem: and his kingdom was called Judah; but that the other ten tribes — who said: We have no part in David, nor inheritance in the son of Jesse — submitted their necks to Jeroboam the son of Nebat, of the tribe of Ephraim, who was the son of Joseph, and served him: and that they were called, from the greater part of the people, by the ancient name Israel; and that for a long time the kingdom of Judah and Israel waged conflicts against each other and obeyed their own kings, and that first the ten tribes were taken captive by the Assyrians, and, after some time, those which were called Judah were led captive by the Chaldeans into Babylon; and that the tribe of Judah was restored to its ancient land after seventy years, but the ten tribes, which were called Israel, serve as captives to this day in the mountains and cities of the Medes. The present prophecy, therefore, promises these things from the mouth of the Lord: that both realms are to be joined together, that is, the kingdom of Judah and the kingdom of Israel, and that the rod of Ephraim, who was begotten of the stock of Joseph, is to be taken up with the remaining tribes which are associated with him, and joined to the rod of Judah, so that they are no longer called Judah and Israel but are called by one name, Judah, and, under the figure of the prophet, who goes before as a type of the Lord Savior, are held not by two hands but by the one hand of Christ.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.37.16-23":["But if we wish, following the prophecy of Hosea, which is almost wholly directed to the ten tribes, that is, to Joseph and Ephraim and Samaria and Israel, to understand the knowledge falsely so called and the various crowds of heretics, we shall say this: that in the last time, when the fullness of the Gentiles has come in and all Israel has been saved, then the hostile peoples also, who fought against the house of Judah and the confession of the Church, will give themselves up to the faith of the Church, and, having abandoned all their errors and the princes of this world who are being destroyed and their own patriarchs who led them down into the abyss of blasphemies, they will rise up, and leave their idols and their abominations which they had fashioned out of their own heart, and from all their dwelling places in which they sinned will pass over to the faith of the Church, and be cleansed, and be the people of Christ, and he will be their God; which I know I have dictated more fully on the prophet Hosea.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.37.16-25":["And that the Lord rejected the tribe of Ephraim and chose the tribe of Judah, we read also in the Psalms, where it is written: And he rejected the tabernacle of Joseph, and did not choose the tribe of Ephraim; but he chose the tribe of Judah, of which it is written: A prince shall not fail from Judah, nor a leader from his thighs, until he comes for whom it is laid up; and he shall be the expectation of the nations: for truly at the coming of the Lord Savior the 'two rods' and, as it is set down in the Hebrew, the 'two sticks', were joined into one scepter, and in the baptism of Christ those long separated are united, so that they become one new man and one nation, and are no longer defiled with idols and abominations but, made clean by the washing, are the people of God, and Christ rules over them, and they dwell upon the land of the meek and the land of the living which he had given to his servant Jacob, who supplanted the people of Israel in his mother's womb.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.37.21-23":["For he says that he takes the sons of Israel from the midst of the nations to which they were led captive, and will lead them back into the land and into the mountains of Israel — of which we read above —, and that they are to be called one nation and ruled by the authority of one king, so that they are no longer defiled with their idols and abominations; but that, when they have been freed from the dwelling places of captivity in which they had sinned, they are to be cleansed of all vices and will be the people of God, so that the Lord is their God.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.37.24-25":["And my servant, he says, David shall be king over them — of whom the angel also speaks in the Gospel, saying that he will reign over the house of Jacob and of his kingdom there will be no end —; and he will be of such clemency that he is called not only king but also shepherd, so that the name of shepherd may soften the proud name of power; and they, after they have become one nation and have dwelt in the land and on the mountains of Israel, will walk in all the judgments of the Lord and keep his precepts; and they will dwell not in another land but in that which he gave to his servant Jacob, in which their fathers Abraham, Isaac and Jacob and the other saints dwelt; and not only will they themselves dwell there, but also their sons and grandsons — according to that line of Vergil: And the sons of sons and those who will be born of them —; and they will dwell there not for a short time, but for ever.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.37.24-26":["'But do you wish' he says 'to know who this king and shepherd is? He is the one of whom I spoke above: My servant David: Who, though he was in the form of God, did not think it robbery to be equal with God, but emptied himself, taking the form of a servant, and became obedient to the Father unto death, even the death of the cross. And when they are under such a king, I will strike with them a covenant of peace': not, as in the old testament, a covenant of contests and wars, but a pact of peace: Which surpasses all understanding — of which the Savior speaks to the apostles: My peace I give to you, my peace I leave to you, and the prophet says: His place was made in peace —. And when he has founded and established them in the Church — so that they can say: He set my feet upon a rock —, then they will be multiplied, either in the number of believers or in the multiplication of virtues.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.37.26-28":["And I will put, he says, my sanctification (or sanctuary) in the midst of them for ever, which the Jews interpret of the temple that was built under Zerubbabel — but how will what is said, 'for ever', be able to stand, when that temple, which was built by Zerubbabel and afterwards restored by many, was burned by Roman fire? —. All these things are to be referred to the Church and to the times of the Savior, when his tabernacle was set in the Church, where he has become our God and we his people. And it is the advancement of all, that they may know that he is the Lord; and that he himself sanctifies Israel, not according to the flesh but according to the spirit, when his sanctification has been set in the midst of believers for ever.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.38.1":["And the word of the Lord came to me, saying: Son of man, set your face against Gog and the land of Magog, the prince of the head (or Ros) of Meshech and Tubal, and prophesy concerning him, and you shall say to them: Thus says the Lord God: Behold, I am against you, Gog, the prince of the head (or Ros) of Meshech and Tubal, and I will turn you about, and I will put a bridle in your jaws, and I will lead out (or I will gather) both you and all your army, horses and horsemen, all of them clothed with breastplates, a great multitude seizing spear and shield and sword (or bucklers and helmets and swords). Persians and Ethiopians and Libyans with them, all with shields and helmets; Gomer and all its troops, the house of Togarmah, the sides of the north (or from the farthest parts of the north) and all its strength, and many peoples (or nations) with you. Prepare and equip yourself and all your multitude that is heaped together to you, and be for them a command (or and you shall be to me for a guard). After many days you will be visited; in the last of the years you will come to the land which has turned back (or been perverted) from the sword and has been gathered out of many peoples to the mountain (or land) of Israel, which had been continually deserted: this one has been led out from the peoples, and all of them will dwell confidently. And going up you will come like a storm and like a cloud, to cover the land, you and all your troops and many peoples (or nations) with you. Thus says the Lord God: On that day words will go up according to your heart, and you will think a most wicked thought, and you will say: I will go up to the land (or upon the land) without a wall (or cast off), I will come to those who are at rest and dwell securely (or in peace); all of them dwell without a wall, they have no bars and gates; to seize spoils and fall upon plunder, and to lay your hand upon those who had been deserted and were afterwards restored, and upon the people which has been gathered out of the nations, which has begun to possess and to be the inhabitant of the navel of the earth. Sheba and Dedan and the merchants of Tarshish (or the Carthaginians) and all its lions (or villages) will say to you: Is it to take spoils that you come? Behold, to seize plunder you have gathered your multitude, to carry off silver and gold, to take away household goods and property, and to seize boundless booty. Therefore prophesy, son of man, and you shall say to Gog: Thus says the Lord God: Will you not, on that day when the people Israel dwells confidently (or in peace), know (or rise up)? And you will come from your place from the sides (or from the farthest parts) of the north, you and many peoples with you, all of them riders of horses, a great company and a mighty army. And you will go up against my people Israel like a cloud to cover the land. In the last days you will be (or it will be), and I will bring you upon my land, so that all the nations may know me, when I am sanctified in you before their eyes, O Gog. Thus says the Lord God: You then are he of whom I spoke in ancient days, by the hand of my servants the prophets of Israel, who prophesied in those days and times (or years), that I would bring you upon them. And it shall be on that day, on the day of the coming of Gog upon the land of Israel, says the Lord God, that my indignation will go up in my fury. And in my zeal, in the fire of my wrath I have spoken, that on that day there shall be a great commotion upon the land of Israel, and the fish of the sea and the birds of the sky and the beasts of the field (or of the plain), and every creeping thing that moves upon the ground, and all the men who are upon the face of the earth shall be moved before my face; and the mountains shall be overturned (or split) and the enclosure walls shall fall (or the valleys), and every wall shall collapse to the ground. And I will call together against him on all my mountains a sword (or every terror of the sword), says the Lord God; the sword of each one (or of each man) shall be directed against his brother. And I will judge him with pestilence (or death) and blood and violent rain and immense stones (or of hail); fire and sulphur I will rain upon him, and upon his army (or upon all who are with him), and upon the many peoples who are with him, and I will be magnified and sanctified and I will be known in the sight of many nations, and they shall know that I am the Lord. First the foundations of the history must be laid, and it must be known that this is the last word of the Lord to the prophet Ezekiel; for after it we cannot in any way find anything similar, except what is written in the twenty-fifth year of the exile of Jeconiah: The hand of the Lord came upon me, and brought me there, that is, into the land of Israel, when the building of the temple is described and the order of its ceremonies is related.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.38.2":["The eleventh book of the explanations on Ezekiel will contain, in its last part, the prophecy against Gog and Magog, and it will come as far as the beginning of the city set on the mountain, Jerusalem, and of the temple situated in it. If, with the Lord helping and expounding his own mysteries, I am able to bring it all the way to the end, I shall have to hesitate long and much whether I ought to set my hands to the spiritual temple or openly confess my ignorance, especially since, both among the Jews and among us, there is a great silence over its interpretation: some judge that it is to be built in time to come; others, with a good intention indeed but an unskilled one, know that the things said are spiritual, and yet do not know how those spiritual things themselves ought to be expounded. And in the meantime, so that the care of the present work may hold us, know also, O virgin of Christ Eustochium, that you must pray for us. In a most difficult prophecy I will briefly point out this: that a man of our own age, by no means undistinguished, writing to the emperor, said concerning this nation: This Gog is the Goth; by what reasoning everything that is written in it can be fitted to him is not mine to expound, but theirs who think so.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","Next it must be observed that the face of the prophet is set, or made firm, against Gog the land, or of the land, of Magog; for there is need of firmness and of much consideration of the countenance, so that we may be able to know the things that are said, according to that apostolic saying: But we all, contemplating the glory of the Lord with unveiled face, are re-formed into the same image, from glory to glory, as by the Spirit of the Lord. Accordingly the Jews and our own Judaizers think that Gog is the Scythian nations, savage and innumerable, which stretch beyond the Caucasus mountain and the Maeotic marsh and near the Caspian Sea as far as India — and that these are to be stirred up by the devil after the reign of a thousand years —, and that they are to come into the land of Israel to fight against the saints, with many nations gathered together with them: first Meshech, whom Josephus interprets as the Cappadocians; then Tubal, whom the same man supposes to be the Iberians or Spaniards, and the Hebrews the Italians, having with them in their army Persians, Ethiopians and Libyans; also Gomer and Togarmah, whom they interpret as the Galatians and Phrygians; also the Sabaeans and Dedan and the Carthaginians, or Tarshish; and that this is what John too sets down in his Revelation: And when the thousand years are finished, Satan will be loosed from his prison, and he will go out to seduce the nations in the four corners of the earth, Gog and Magog, to gather them for war: whose number is as the sand of the sea. And they went up over the breadth of the earth, and surrounded the camp of the saints and the beloved city. And fire came down from God out of heaven and devoured them; and the devil who seduced them was cast into the lake of fire and sulphur, where the beast and the false prophet were, and they will be tormented day and night for ages of ages; not understanding that the whole volume of John, which is headed with the title of Revelation, is mystical, and that we need revelation so that we may be able to say with the prophet: Unveil my eyes, and I will consider the wonders of your law. Others, however, abandoning the earthly sense and the Jewish and old-wives' fables, which are harmful and drag down into the depths those who acquiesce in them, climb up too far to the heights and invent much worse trifles, so that they describe the wars of the devil and of all his army in the heavenly Jerusalem, and under the ἐτυμολογίαις [etymologies] of the individual nations interpret the spiritual forces of wickedness in the heavenly places. Leaving all these things to the reader's judgment, we shall try not so much to condemn the views of others as to affirm the Church's explanation. 'Gog' is called in the Greek tongue δῶμα, in the Latin 'roof'; 'Magog', moreover, means 'from the roof' — so all pride, and the knowledge falsely so called which raises itself against the knowledge of the truth, is shown by these names —; these are the roofs of which Isaiah too speaks in the vision against the valley of Zion: What has happened to you now, that you all go up onto the empty roofs?; and by 'roof' we shall interpret the princes of the heretics, and by 'from the roof' those who have taken up their doctrines; and fittingly, after the many mystical prophecies in this volume, the last prophecy is against Gog and Magog — for if It is the time of judgment, according to Peter, that it should begin from the house of God, and according to this same Ezekiel, who says: And begin from my saints, and: The last enemy, death, is destroyed, in Isaiah too the first word is directed against Judea, in which is the confession of the Lord, and the last against the four-footed beasts which are in the desert; rightly here too the last word is directed against Gog and Magog, who assail the city of God, which the rush of the river makes glad, and which says in Isaiah: I am a strong city which is besieged, and of which it is written in the Gospel: A city set on a mountain cannot be hidden, and more fully in the psalm: Jerusalem, which is built as a city whose sharing of it is in the selfsame. Mountains are round about it, and the Lord is round about his people —. Moreover, as to the fact that in the army of Gog or Magog, who according to the Seventy, Symmachus and Theodotion is prince of Ros, Meshech and Tubal, Aquila translates the first nation, 'Ros', as 'head', and we too have followed him — so that the sense is: The prince of the head of Meshech and Tubal; and in truth neither in Genesis, nor in any other place of Scripture, nor even in Josephus, who in the first book of the Antiquities sets out the names of all the Hebrew nations, have we been able to find this nation, from which it is clear that 'Ros' signifies not 'a nation' but 'head'; this too must be briefly noted, that in Ezekiel Gog is said to be the prince of the land of Magog, whereas Revelation records that both Gog and Magog are nations which go out from the four corners of the earth; and that just as from Jacob, who was afterwards called Israel, the whole people of the Hebrews received the name Israel, and from Aram Syria, from Mesraim Egypt, whose names are written in Genesis, so too from the prince Gog all who are subject to him are called Magog —; 'Mosoch', however, means 'madness', and 'Thubal' 'whole' or 'all things': so the 'prince' and 'head of proud madness and of all evils' — according to what is written: The world is set in the evil one — are called 'Gog' and 'Magog' — nations which, hostile and adverse to the saints, go out from the corners of the earth, abandoning the straight line and the arrangement of the camps of God which is related in Numbers, and not knowing that testimony: Many will come from the east and the west and the north and the south, and will recline with Abraham, Isaac and Jacob in the kingdom of God, and in another place: I will say to the north: Bring; and to the south-west wind: Do not hinder, bring my sons from a distant land and my daughters from the end of the earth, all who are called by my name; but not such are the corners of the earth from which Gog and Magog go out: beside such corners the harlot stands in Proverbs, who hastens to deceive the foolish young men passing by through the streets, that is, through the broad and spacious way which leads to death: the scribes and Pharisees too pray at the corners of the streets so as to be seen by men, who have received their reward —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.38.4-6":["Of his horses too, and horsemen clothed with breastplates — who imitate the armor of the Apostle and boast that they have the breastplates of righteousness — there is an excessive multitude, and all of them seize shield and sword against the Church, and they are most savage nations, hostile to Israel: Persians, Ethiopians and Libyans — whose wars against the people of God are related in the holy Scriptures —; Gomer too and the house of Togarmah, who come from the sides of the 'north' — which is a most cold wind and is called by the name 'dexter' [right-hand] —, and all the strength of Magog and all the peoples that are subject to him, will come with him to battle.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.38.4":["What, then, is the threat against Gog? 'I will turn you about (or I will convert), and I will put a bridle in your jaws: first I will make you waver from your opinion and be converted, and I will put a bridle in your jaws — of which it is written: With bridle and bit bind fast the jaws of those who do not draw near to you —, so that the untamed horse, wanton and rushing headlong over precipices, may be subjected to the bridle of the Lord' — to whom the prophet says: You will mount upon your horses, and your riding is salvation, and to Job it is said through clouds and whirlwind: Did you surround the horse with strength? Such were the horses and horsemen whose multitude John saw in heaven —. So he says to Gog: I will lead out (or I will gather) you and all the army (or strength) of yours; he who is led out and gathered from dispersion is drawn back to salvation — according to what is promised in the law: If your dispersion be from the height of heaven to its height, from there I will gather you —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.38.7-8":["To him it is said by way of εἰρωνείαν [irony]: 'Prepare and equip yourself, and heap together to yourself all your multitude, which obeys your commands; but know that after many days you will be visited' — according to what is written: I will visit their iniquities with the rod and their sins with stripes —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.38.8":["'In the last', he says, 'of the years you will come'; whence John too says: My little children, it is the last hour, and the people of the Gentiles is hired at the eleventh hour to work in the vineyard; to whose land — that is, the land of the meek and the land of the saints, which brings forth fruit a hundredfold and sixtyfold and thirtyfold — Gog comes to storm it, the land which has turned back, or turned away, from the sword — for it knows that it is written: Scatter, O Lord, the nations that desire wars —, and abides in peace, and has been gathered from many peoples and nations so that, having despised the error of the nations, it might come to the mountains of Israel, that is, the patriarchs and prophets, who were once continually deserted, without the law and the precepts of God, to which it is afterwards said: More are the children of the deserted one than of her who has a husband; these are the deserted and waterless places in which the devil could find no place, because they had been called to salvation, and afterwards, taking with him seven other demons more wicked than himself, he returned to his house, that is, to the nation of Israel. This, he says, has been led out from the peoples, land being understood, or: This one has been led out from the nations, so that the people of believers may be understood, which has dwelt in peace; and it trusts in the Lord.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.38.9":["And Gog, going up with all his army, will come like a storm and like a cloud, to cover the land of the believers — for which of the heretics, whose prince is the devil, does not come like a storm against the Church, and hasten to overwhelm and cover with the cloud of his words all the simple among the believers? —; whence it is said to him: 'As far as lies in you, you will cover all things, you and all your troops and many peoples with you'; and fittingly Gog, going up, has no willing rain, no early and late shower to gladden the parched fields, but storm and darkness, so as to confound all things with gloom and error.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.38.10-11":["Therefore the Lord God speaks these things to him: At that time, that is, in the last days, words will go up upon your heart, and you will think a most wicked thought — as Scripture says: If the spirit of one who has power should wish to go up upon you, do not abandon your place; but on the contrary God goes up into the heart of the holy man, of whom it is written: He has set ascents in his heart —; and Gog says: I will go up to the land without a wall (or cast forth) — that is, the land which is destitute of the help of God and not fortified by the argumentation of the dialecticians, I will come to those who are at rest, dwelling securely (or in peace) — for our king is peaceable, and his place is in peace —; all of them, he says, dwell without a wall, they have no bars and gates.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.38.11-12":["This the devil, the prince of the heretics, says: that the Church has no fortifications, nor the worldly wisdom which is foolishness with God, so that he may seize the spoils and fall upon the plunder of the Church, and lay his hand upon those who had been deserted when they did not have the knowledge of God and afterwards, restored through Christ, come to the Father — he says in the Gospel: Father, I have revealed your name to men —; and so that we may know that the people which had been deserted and afterwards restored signifies the Christian people, there follows: And upon the people which has been gathered out of the nations, which people has begun to possess the inheritance of Christ and to be the inhabitant of the navel of the earth — of which it is written: He has worked salvation in the midst of the earth. Truth, indeed, has sprung from the earth; which says in the Gospel: I am the way and the truth and the life —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.38.13":["While he thinks and says these things and hastens to snatch the possession of the Church, the nations which had been converted from their former error to salvation: Sheba and Dedan and the merchants of Tarshish — of Carthage or of the sea — who amid the waves of this world seek merchandise by good works, and all their villages or lions — or lions' whelps, as is contained in the Hebrew —, will say to him what follows — that the Seventy and Theodotion, however, put 'villages' in place of 'lions' whelps', which in Hebrew are called 'chaphir', is an evident error: for if you write it with the letter 'chi', it is called 'lion's whelp', but if with 'ce', which in Hebrew is called 'coph', it is called 'field' and 'village'; whence Capharnaum too is called 'most beautiful field' —. So Sheba and Dedan and the merchants of Tarshish will say, and all the saints who are called lions' whelps, or at any rate the villages and dwellings of the believers: 'O Gog, is it for this that you come: to snatch the spoils of the Church? and is it for this that you have gathered a multitude: that the possession of Christ may become your inheritance? or do you think that you will possess the silver and gold of the Church — which is understood of eloquence and meaning —, so as to take away its household goods and all its property, and seize the boundless booty gathered by Christ the victor?'\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.38.14-15":["'Therefore, O prophet Ezekiel, who in the type of Christ are called son of man, speak to Gog and say to him: Thus says the Lord God: When, the error of the whole world having been taken away, my people Israel, which discerns God with the mind, dwells in the Church confidently or in peace, then you will know, or rise up and come from your place' — and what the place of heretical depravity is, the following word shows: From the sides of the north, which tries to chill all the warmth of the believers —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.38.15-16":["And many peoples will come with him, all riders of horses — of whom it is written: The horse and the rider he has cast into the sea —, a great company and a mighty army; with whom: 'You will go up', he says, 'against my people Israel like a cloud and darkness, to cover my land — of which it was spoken above —. In the last days you will be, in the time of the preaching of the Gospel, and I will bring you upon my land — For there must also be heresies, so that those who are approved may be made manifest, and by the will of God the devil, the ἀνταγωνιστής [adversary], and all the dogmas of perversity have been left —, so that all the nations may know (and understand) me, when I am sanctified in you before their eyes, O Gog, that is, when through your punishments they have understood me as judge'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.38.17":["And he makes an apostrophe to Gog himself: 'Are you not he of whom I spoke in ancient days, by the hands of my servants the prophets of Israel?': that is, of Moses — who says in the book of Numbers, at least according to the Seventy: A man shall come forth from his seed and shall rule over very many nations, and Gog his kingdom shall be lifted up, and his kingdom shall increase, but according to the Hebrew I found it written thus: His king shall be taken away because of Agag, and his kingdom shall be removed, because the kingdom of Israel was destroyed in Saul on account of Agag king of Amalek; and in another place: He will cleanse the land of his people —; Joel too describes the peoples to be gathered in the valley of 'Iosaphat', that is, 'of the judgment of God'; and Isaiah, and all the prophets, in whose hands and good works the word of the Lord comes about.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.38.18-20":["'When therefore you come upon the land of Israel, my indignation will go up in my fury against you. And in my zeal for my people, in the fire of my wrath I have spoken, which is to consume all the timbers of your vices: At that', he says, 'time there will be a great commotion upon the land of Israel': for at the instigation of the devil frequent persecution will arise against the Church, and when it has been lulled to rest and overcome by the protection of God, then the fish of the sea and the birds of the sky and the beasts of the field, and every creeping thing that moves upon the earth, and all the men who are upon the face of the earth, will be moved before his face; which is clearly shown of the inhabitants of the Church, of whom some like the fish of the sea, some like the birds of the sky, some like the beasts of the field and all the creeping things that move upon the earth, others as retaining the dignity of man, dwell upon the face of the earth — and that diversity of conduct is signified by various names the Apostle also writes to the Corinthians, since he says that after the resurrection some bodies are heavenly, others earthly, one the body of a fish, another of a bird, another of beasts, another of creeping things, and another of men, who have kept their original name; in the Acts of the Apostles too, that sheet which is shown three times to the apostle Peter by its four corners signifies the variety of believers which was also contained in the ark of the flood —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.38.20":["Then, from the face of the Lord and from the sight of his majesty, the mountains which raised themselves against the knowledge of God will be overturned, and the hedges or valleys will fall, which either have sunk to the depths through lowness of understanding or at any rate promise some fortification so as to imitate the Church of God — of whose hedges it is said: Whoever breaks down a hedge, a serpent will bite him —, and every wall will collapse to the ground: for when the strength of the Church's word appears, all the fortifications of the heretics will collapse.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.38.21":["'And I will call together against him, that is, against Gog, the prince of the heretics, a sword on all my mountains, so that the princes of his army may be destroyed by the sword for the Lord'; then heresy will fight against heresy, and their fighting among themselves is our victory.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.38.22":["And I will judge, he says, him with death (or pestilence) and blood and violent rain and immense stones (or of hail): and Gog is judged by his own death, and by the blood which he shed, and by violent rain, the words of a learned and perfect man, and by immense stones which overwhelm him with the testimonies of the Scriptures, or of hail, which make his heat grow cold — All, indeed, who commit adultery, their hearts are like an oven —. Fire, he says, and sulphur I will rain upon him, by which the punishment of eternal judgment is shown; and not only upon him who was the author of perversity, but also upon all his army and multitude and upon the many peoples who are with him — for the heretics have many companions, or rather the devil, their prince, is surrounded by an infinite multitude —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.38.23":["And when all error has been taken away and the princes of the heretics have been punished and destroyed, the Lord is magnified, and sanctified in the believers, and becomes known in the eyes of the many nations which have believed in his faith, and both from their own blessedness and from the punishments of the adversaries they will know and recognize that he himself is the Lord. These things we have interpreted as we were able, obeying that precept: You shall turn aside neither to the right nor to the left, but: You shall walk on the royal road; but if anyone finds fault with what is ours, let him either bring forward better things for us to follow, or, if he wishes to say nothing, let him leave perfect knowledge to God, provided only that he knows that it is by no means our powers, but our intention, that is to be judged.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.39.1-16":["But you, son of man, prophesy against Gog and say: Thus says the Lord God: Behold, I am against you, Gog, prince of the head (or Ros) of Meshech and Tubal; and I will turn you about (or gather you) and lead you astray (or lead you down), and make you go up from the sides (or from the uttermost parts) of the north, and bring you upon the mountains of Israel. And I will strike (or destroy) your bow in your left hand (or from your left hand), and your arrows from your right hand, and I will cast you down upon the mountains of Israel. And you will fall, you and all your troops, and the peoples (or nations) that are with you; to the wild beasts, to the birds and to every winged thing and to the beasts of the earth I have given you to be devoured: on the face of the field (or plain) you will fall, for I have spoken, says the Lord God. And I will send fire on Magog, and on those who dwell in the islands confidently (or and the islands will be inhabited in peace), and they will know that I am the Lord. And I will make my holy name known in the midst of my people Israel, and I will not pollute (or it will not be polluted) my holy name any more, and the nations will know that I am the Lord God, the holy one of Israel. Behold, it comes, and it has been done (or you will know that it will be), says the Lord; this is the day of which I have spoken. And the inhabitants of the cities of Israel will go forth, and will kindle and burn the weapons, the shield and the spears (or the targes and the pikes), the bow and the arrows, the hand staves and the pikes (or lances), and they will kindle them with fire seven years. And they will not carry (or will not take up) wood from the regions (or from the fields), nor cut it down from the forests, since they will kindle the weapons with fire; and they will plunder those whose prey they had been, and despoil their despoilers, says the Lord God. And it shall be, on that day, I will give Gog a renowned place, a sepulcher in Israel, the valley of the travelers east of the sea, which makes those who pass by stand aghast (or the πολυάνδριον [common burial ground, literally \"place of many men\"] of those who will come to the sea, and they will build round about the mouth of the valley); and there they will bury Gog and all his multitude, and it will be called the valley (or 'ge') of the multitude of Gog. And the house of Israel will bury them, to cleanse the land (or that the land may be cleansed) for seven months; and all the people of the land will bury them, and it will be for them a renowned day on which I was glorified, says the Lord God. And they will appoint men continually going through (or going round) the land, to bury and to search out those who remain upon the face of the land, that they may cleanse it; and after seven months they will begin to search, and they will go round traversing the land; and when they see a man's bone, they will set up a marker beside it, until the undertakers bury it in the valley (or 'ge') of the multitude of Gog. And the name of the city is Amona (or Πολυάνδριον), and they will cleanse the land (or the land will be cleansed). These things the heirs of the Jewish tradition and the disciples of endless fables contend will come to pass after the reign of a thousand years: that Gog, prince of Ros, Meshech and Tubal, is to be slain in the borders of Israel and to be devoured by all the winged creatures and the beasts; and that the inhabitants of the cities of Israel will for seven years by no means have the use of wood from the cutting of forests, but from the weapons of Gog, namely shields and spears and arrows and staves or pikes; and that Gog himself is to be buried in the valley which in Hebrew is called 'ge', and the name of his sepulcher is to be called πολυάνδριον, namely where a multitude of men has been buried; and that he is to be buried by the house of Israel for seven months so that the land may be cleansed, and that the day when Gog was slain will be renowned, and that men are to be appointed to search diligently for the bones and bury them, so that, namely, nothing remains unburied in the land; and that after the seven months, or within the seven months, the land is to be traversed, and wherever they observe a man's bone lying, it is to be pointed out by a marker set beside it, so that afterward those who have been put in charge of this work may bury it; and that the name of the city is to be called 'Amona', which in Greek is called Πολυάνδριον, that is, 'a multitude of buried men', and that in this way the land is to be cleansed. This is what they have said; but let us, holding to the sense of the explanation we have begun, discuss one by one the things we have set forth.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.39.1":["Gog too has his own trinity, 'Ros' and 'Mosoch' and 'Thubal', that is, 'head' and 'madness' and 'all things', so that there is no vice that does not stand in Gog's possession.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.39.2-3":["He will be led out, or turned about, and he will be stroked and coaxed so that, hoping for victory, he may be led to battle to be slain; and he will go up from the sides, or from the uttermost parts, of the north, from which evils blaze up over all the earth, and by that very one he will be brought upon the mountains of Israel, which we ought to understand as the apostles and apostolic men and men of the Church, so that, after he has been brought to the mountains of Israel, then the bow that is in his left hand may be struck, and the arrows that are held in the right hand: for he too imitates, in order to kill those whom he has deceived, the having of weapons on the left and on the right, through good report and bad — these are the arrows and these the darts of which the psalmist too speaks: For behold, sinners have bent the bow, they have made ready arrows in the quiver, to shoot in the dark at the upright of heart, which fiery darts of the devil are to be quenched with the shield of faith —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.39.4-5":["And Gog, who had gone up upon the mountains of Israel, is cast down, or will fall, on those very mountains with all his army and all his troops; and he will be food for the wild beasts, the birds and every winged thing and the beasts of the earth, namely the adverse powers that ate the seed by the wayside, and the bloodthirsty beasts — for as it is written of the dragon: You gave him as food to the Ethiopian peoples, so all who are deceived by the heretics are the food of demons —: and Gog will fall either in the breadth of the plain or in that of the field which has been tilled by God's husbandmen; for it cannot be that the Lord's words should be void.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.39.6":["Then fire will be sent on Magog, namely on those who received Gog's teaching, and on those who, like islands, are beaten by the waves of the world and think that they ought to be secure — that fire of which the Lord speaks: I came to send fire upon the earth, and how I will that it burn —: 'that all may know and understand that I am the Lord'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.39.7":["'And let my holy name be made known in the midst of my people, which was by no means led astray by the authority of Gog, and let my name no longer be polluted among the heretics on the pretext of the knowledge falsely so called, and let the nations that are round about know that I am the Lord'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.39.8":["But what follows, Behold, it comes, and it has been done, is said of the judgment of Christ, who coming will come and will not delay; and this is the day of punishments and vengeance of which the Lord has spoken through all the prophets.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.39.9-10":["And the inhabitants of the cities of Israel will go forth: the peoples of believers; and by the cities of Israel we understand the Church of right faith. And they will kindle and burn the weapons — of which it is written in another place too: He will crush the bow, he will shatter the weapons, and he will burn the shields with fire —, shields and spears, bow and arrows, hand staves — with which they celebrated a perverse Passover —, lances or pikes — with which they inflicted the broad and incurable wounds of their doctrines —, and shields, bows and arrows, of which it was said above. And what follows, And they will kindle them with fire seven years, is to be taken from Exodus and Leviticus, in which it is commanded by the law that in the seventh year, of release, when liberty is restored to Hebrew slaves and all debts are cancelled and the ancient possession returns to its owners, rest too is granted to the land and all the crops are given over to the poor; so that, namely, in seven years, a sacred and perfect number, the armor of the heretics may perish utterly, and the men of the Church may by no means cut wood from the fields and regions and woods and forests of the nations, whom they desire rather to save than to destroy, but from those of the heretics whom they have conquered, from shields, spears, arrows, staves and pikes and lances: for they too have various weapons for attacking the Church of Christ, which are to be set on fire by learned men with the fire of the Holy Spirit — namely by the Church's word, and whoever has it will be able to say: Was not our heart burning on the way, when Jesus opened the Scriptures to us? —; nor can we have full peace and confidence in our dwelling unless we plunder all the gear of our adversaries, so that everything may perish and be burned to ashes, and we plunder those who had earlier plundered us, and lay waste those who had first laid waste the Church.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.39.11":["On that day — in the clear light of preaching — I will give, he says, Gog a renowned place, a sepulcher in Israel, according to the Hebrew: the valley of the travelers east of the sea, which makes those who pass by stand aghast; the sense of this saying is this: The sepulcher of Gog will not be on the mountains but in low valleys and rugged places — which in Hebrew are called 'ge'; and the heretics, though they are situated in the west, pretend that these are places of the east —, in order to deceive the travelers, namely those who pass through this world and are not inhabitants but sojourners — saying that prophetic word: I am a stranger and a sojourner, as all my fathers were —. For which of those passing by — of whom it is written: And those who passed by did not say: The blessing of the Lord be upon you! — does not marvel and stand aghast when he sees the valley of the travelers, which to the travelers seems a valley, to the inhabitants a mountain? This is according to the Hebrew. But the Seventy translated: the πολυάνδριον of those who will come to the sea, and they will build round about the entrance of the valley: for it is the labor of those who go out from the cities of Israel to shut and build round every entrance and exit of the heretical depravity, of those who had come to the sea and delighted in its bitterness, in the masses of its waves and in the cruelty of shipwreck, and to bury them in the chasms of the earth, so that they may no longer be able to go out and deceive anyone by their fraud. There, therefore, they will bury Gog and all his multitude, in which the heretics always delight; and the name of that valley in which Gog was buried will be called 'ge of the multitude', or πολυάνδριον, that is, 'the sepulcher of a very great multitude'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.39.12-13":["And as we said above that the weapons of the adversaries are kindled through seven years, so through seven months the land will be cleansed of the filth of the heretics; for from the first month — when we celebrate the Lord's Passover and escape the destroyer of Egypt by the blood of the lamb on our doorposts —, up to the end of the year — that is, up to the seventh month, when we pitch tabernacles and, among the other foliage, are sheltered also by branches of palms, so that a perfect victory over the enemies may be shown —, we fulfil all the festivals among the peoples; and not only the teachers but the whole people too will do this eagerly, to bury Gog, and cover him with earth, and by no means allow him to take in the free air.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.39.14-15":["After the peace, however, and the slaying, or the burial, of Gog, men of the Church will be chosen who have this as their concern: that nothing of the former filth remain in the land of Israel, nothing of carrion; men to go through the land and search out the dead and bury them, so that, namely, the land of the Church may be cleansed. But if after seven months, when all things ought to have been purged, those who traverse and go round the land see that in anyone a man's bone — that is, the hardness of heretical depravity — or something of the carrion of the former doctrine has remained, they will set up, or build, a marker beside it, so that, after those who are of this kind have been marked, they may then either be corrected or be buried with Gog and thrust away into the throng of his burial.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.39.16":["And the name of the city where the victory of the servants of the Lord is, and where the adversaries lie and the whole multitude of his enemies has been laid low, will be called 'Amona' or Πολυάνδριον, so that the end of all things may be the restoration of purity. Finally there follows: And they will cleanse the land, doubtless those of whom it is written above: They will begin to search, and will go round traversing the land.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.39.17-29":["Therefore you, son of man, thus says the Lord God: Say to every bird and to all the fowl and to all the beasts of the field (or plain): Assemble and hasten, run together from every side (or round about) to my victim, which I have sacrificed for you, a great victim upon the mountains of Israel, that you may eat flesh and drink blood. You will eat the flesh of the mighty (or of giants), and you will drink the blood of the princes of the earth, of rams, of lambs and of he-goats, and of fatted bulls and of all fat things; and you will eat fat to the full, and drink blood to drunkenness from the victim which I have sacrificed for you. And you will be filled at my table with horse and with mighty horseman (or giant), and with all men of war, says the Lord God. And I will set (or give) my glory among the nations (or among you), and all the nations will see my judgment which I have done, and my hand which I have laid (or brought) upon them; and the house of Israel will know that I am the Lord their God from that day and thereafter, and all the nations will know that the house of Israel was taken captive for their iniquities, because they forsook me, and I hid (or turned away) my face from them, and delivered them into the hand of the foes (or of their enemies), and they all fell by the sword. According to their uncleannesses and their crimes (or iniquities) I dealt with them, and I hid (or turned away) my face from them. Therefore thus says the Lord God: Now I will bring back the captivity of Jacob, and I will have mercy on the whole house of Israel, and I will take up zeal for my holy name. And they will bear their confusion (or ignominy), and all the transgression by which they transgressed against me, when they dwell in their own land confidently (or in peace), fearing no one. And when I have brought them back from the peoples (or nations), and have gathered them from the lands of their enemies (or from the regions of the nations), and have been sanctified in them in the eyes (or in the sight) of very many nations, they will also know that I am the Lord their God, since I have carried them over into the nations (or since I have appeared to them among the nations), and have gathered them upon their own land, and have not left any of them there. And I will no longer hide (or turn away) my face from them, because I have poured out my spirit (or fury) upon the whole house of Israel, says the Lord God. (What we have translated according to the Hebrews: I will gather them upon their own land, and I will not leave any of them there, is not found in the Seventy; and again, where according to the Hebrews a blessing is set at the end: Because I have poured out my 'spirit', the Seventy translated 'fury', which pertains to anger, especially since in the Hebrew 'ruhi' is written, which properly means 'my spirit' and by no means 'my fury'.) But everything up to the place where the building of the temple follows, those we spoke of above, the Jews and our own Judaizers, refer to the last time: that the birds and the beasts are to be sated with the flesh of Gog and of all his army as with the fattest offerings, and that Israel is to be restored to its former state and by no means be assailed any longer by the nations, but that God will pour out his spirit upon them so that they may dwell in their own land: not all the nations, but properly the house of Israel.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.39.17":["But we, following the tropology we have begun, say this: that the Lord calls together all the birds and all the beasts, to hand over to them the princes of the heretics to be devoured as the fattest offerings — and those to whom the adversaries are handed over are called birds and beasts either for their swiftness in running about into all things or for their savagery and cruelty: For the destruction of the flesh, that the spirit may be saved, and that they may learn not to blaspheme —; and they will eat a great and very fat victim: not elsewhere, but upon the mountains of Israel, which we ought to understand as the prophets and apostles and holy men — for upon them the teachers of contrary dogmas fall in ruin, and by them they perish wounded —, upon whom the Church is built, and, to speak more truly, upon the mountain of mountains — of which Isaiah speaks, and Micah: Come, let us go up to the mountain of the Lord and to the house of the God of Jacob, and he will announce to us his way —. And these birds and these swift and cruel beasts will eat flesh and drink blood — which cannot possess the kingdom of God —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.39.18-19":["The flesh of the mighty, or of giants, who rebelled against the knowledge of God, and the blood of the princes, not of heaven but of the earth, who savored all earthly things, they will drink: the blood of rams, of lambs and of he-goats, or, according to the Seventy, of rams, of calves and of he-goats, so that it signifies three living creatures that are sacrificed among the victims of God: for the heretics too imitate the gentleness of the Church, but their oblation serves not for the worship of God but for the food of demons, and this is their fattest offering; and they are sated as if with fat, and they drink the blood of the deceived to drunkenness; but God sacrifices this victim through the men of the Church, so that the most wicked guests may be sated with the multitude of the deceived, and drink to vomiting and drunkenness.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.39.20-21":["When you see holy men, learned in the divine Scriptures, cutting down the horses of the heretics and their riders — of which it is written: The horse and the rider he has cast into the sea —, and hewing down all the rebels and giants with their own blade, and shedding the blood of all the warriors of the knowledge falsely so called, then know that the table of the Lord has been prepared, that he may set his glory among all the nations that believe in his name.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.39.21-24":["And the churches of the saints will understand his judgment which he has executed upon the adversaries, and the strong hand with which he struck them; and let the house of Israel know and recognize that he is the Lord their God — who said: I am the Lord your God — from the day of the Lord's victory for ever, and let them reflect that the house of Israel was once taken captive by the heretics and scattered through the whole world of perverse dogmas for this reason: that they forsook him who for a little while hid, or turned away, his face from them and delivered them into the hand of the heretics, and they fell, pierced through by their blade, because of their uncleannesses and iniquities; and this was the cause of his hiding and turning away his face from them.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.39.25-26":["And having set forth the reasons — according to that which is written: There must also be heresies, so that those who are approved may be made manifest — for the captivity of Israel, that is, of the Church, in which those dwell who see God, he now promises that he will bring back into the Church the captivity of Jacob, who had supplanted the Jewish people and afterward was himself supplanted by the frauds of the heretics; and that he will have mercy on the whole house of Israel, not according to the flesh but according to the spirit. And I will take up, he says, my zeal for my holy name, which was blasphemed among the nations because of the heretics, so that, after he has set them free, they may blush and be confounded because, having abandoned the faith of the Church, they transgressed against me. Let them be confounded, then, and blush very swiftly, when they dwell in their own land, the land of the meek and the land of the saints, and dwell confidently, or in peace, by no means fearing the snares of the heretics.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.39.27-28":["Then they will be brought back from the peoples, and gathered from the lands of their enemies into their own land, and the Lord will be sanctified in them in the sight of very many nations, which themselves too are going to believe in the Lord; and let this be the end of blessedness: to know and recognize that he is the Lord their God, because he appeared to them among the nations, or carried them over from the nations, and gathered them upon their own land, the land of Judea, the land of confession, the land of the meek and the land of the living, and left not even one to heretical depravity.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.39.29":["And let him no longer hide his face from them nor turn away from them, because he has poured out the spirit of his grace — of which the prophet Joel too speaks: In the last days I will pour out of my spirit upon all flesh —; and he has poured it out upon the whole house of Israel. But if instead of 'spirit' we read 'fury' according to the Seventy, which is not found in the Hebrew, it is to be understood thus: that he no longer hides his face from those upon whom he had earlier poured out his fury.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.40.1":["In the twenty-fifth year of the exile (or of the captivity) of Jeconiah, at the beginning of the year (or in the first month), on the tenth of the month, in the fourteenth year after the city was struck (or taken), on this very day (or on that day), the hand of the Lord came upon me, and he brought me there, in visions (or in a vision) of God, and he brought me into the land of Israel, and set (or placed) me upon an exceedingly high mountain, upon which there was as it were the building of a city facing toward the south (or opposite). And he brought me in there, and behold, a man whose appearance was like the appearance of bronze (or of shining bronze), and a cord of linen (or of the masons) in his hand, and a measuring reed in his hand; and he was standing in the gate. And the same man spoke to me: Son of man, see with your eyes, and hear with your ears, and set your heart upon all that I shall show you (or set in your heart all that I shall have shown you), for you have been brought (or have entered) here that they may be shown to you; and declare (or you shall show) all that you see to the house of Israel. If in the fifth year of the captivity or exile of King Jehoiachin, in the fourth month, on the fifth of the month, the heavens were opened to the prophet Ezekiel beside the river Chebar and he saw visions of God, but now it is said that in the twenty-fifth year of the exile of the same Jeconiah, at the beginning of the year, on the tenth day of the month, the hand of the Lord came upon him, and he was brought into the land of Israel and placed upon an exceedingly high mountain, so that he might see the building of a city facing toward the south, no one doubts that his whole prophecy was woven together when nineteen years, and nine months and five days of the twentieth year, had been completed. But if, according to Theodotion, who in the place where we have put 'at the beginning of the year' translated 'in the new year', on the tenth day of the month — now among the Hebrews the new year is the name of the seventh month, which among them bears the name 'thesri' [Tishri], so that on the first day of the seventh month there is the blast of trumpets, and on the tenth day of the same month the day of fasting and propitiation, and on the fifteenth, when the whole orb of the moon is filled, the day of Tabernacles — it is given to be understood that the building of the city had been shown to Ezekiel on the tenth day, the day of the Lord's propitiation. And just as he showed the restoration of the people, or rather their bringing back to life, in the bones of the plain under the image of the resurrection, so now the Lord promises, under its description, the renewal of the city which fourteen years before had been destroyed by fire by the Babylonian, so that, as the type of the pot kindled from the face of the north had shown the captivity and the overthrow, and the truth of the prophecy had been proved by the event, so, from trust in things past, the truth of the building to come might be proved by the prophecy. Nor is this said of that time, as some unlearned among the Jews would have it, when under Zerubbabel and under Joshua son of Jehozadak, the great priest, the temple was built, while Haggai and Zechariah prophesied: for this temple which is now described, and the order of the priesthood, and the division and fertility of the land, is far more majestic than the one which Solomon had built; but the one which was built under Zerubbabel was so small and, in comparison with the former, of so little worth, that those who had seen the first temple and afterward looked upon this one wailed and bore witness to their grief with tears, and the cry of those lamenting was far greater than the blast of the trumpets; read the book of Ezra. Further, what is added: In the fourteenth year after the city was struck (or taken), according to the mystical senses signifies the fourteen generations from David to the nativity of Christ; when they were completed, the restoration of the city is promised by him of whom it is written: He himself will build my city and will bring back the captivity of my people, and again, he came: To proclaim release to the captives and sight to the blind, saying to those who were in chains: Come out, and to those who were in darkness: Be uncovered.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.40.2":["Now the hand of the Lord comes upon him so that, though placed in Babylon according to the flesh, he might come in the spirit to the land of Israel, and might be set, not at all in a vision, but in the visions of God, upon an exceedingly high mountain — of which Isaiah and Micah prophesy: Come, let us go up to the mountain of the Lord and to the house of the God of Jacob —, which mountain is exceedingly high in comparison with the other mountains — of which the prophet testifies, saying: I have lifted up my eyes to the mountains, from where my help shall come, and in another place: Mountains are round about it, and the Lord is round about his people, of which it is also said in Isaiah: Go up onto a high mountain, you who bring good tidings to Zion —; upon this mountain is shown the building of a city, according to the Seventy and Aquila 'opposite' and 'over against the north', from where the prophet had come to the land of Israel, but according to Theodotion and Symmachus 'facing toward the south', where the light is full and the sun of righteousness is set at the highest summit of heaven — whence it is also said in the Song of Songs: Arise, north wind, and come, south wind; blow through my garden, and let its spices flow; for the north wind, the harshest of winds, is driven away from the Lord's garden, so that the warmth of love may not grow cold and the flowers may not wither —; and when it is said: As it were the building of a city, not truly a city but the likeness of a city is shown — of which it is written: Glorious things are spoken of you, city of God, which is: Jerusalem, built as a city, whose sharing of it is in the selfsame, and in which: Great is the Lord and exceedingly to be praised, in the city of our God, on his holy mountain, and: In the towers of the city God is known to the righteous, when he upholds it, of which it is said elsewhere too: The rush of the river makes glad the city of God, and: A city set upon a mountain cannot be hidden, which speaks in Isaiah: I am a strong city, a city which is besieged — he did not say: which 'is stormed', but 'is besieged' —, which 'is built upon the rock and is shaken by no storm' —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.40.3":["There follows: 'And he brought me in there — the hand of the Lord is understood; and 'there', that is, 'as it were the building of a city' —, so that he might show me all the things that were within'. Behold, he says, a man whose appearance (or vision) was like the appearance of gleaming bronze — he, namely, of whom it is written: Behold a man, the Orient is his name —; now he had an appearance by no means of electrum, as is said at the beginning of this volume, nor was he girded with a golden belt, as is contained in the Revelation of John, but he had an appearance, according to the Hebrew, as of bronze — for this material is more resonant than all the metals and rings out far with its clang; hence also in Daniel, in the image which was put together from gold, silver, bronze and iron, the kingdom of Alexander and of the Greeks is shown in the likeness of bronze, so that the eloquence of the Greek language might be signified; by which it is plain that those who do not yet know the full mysteries of the temple that is to be built spiritually are still in need of teaching —; there was also a cord of linen, or of the masons, in his hand — of which it is also written in Zechariah that he had a surveyor's cord, to measure the breadth and the length of the city. And of the masons: either of the angels who ministered at God's command, or of Moses and of all the prophets and apostles who build the city of God and are helpers or ministers of the Lord's will; hence the apostle Paul also said: We are God's husbandry, God's building —. And what follows: And a measuring reed in his hand, designates the prophetic grace — of which it is written in Psalm 44 (45): My tongue is the reed of a scribe writing swiftly, and of John, prophet and baptist: What did you go out into the desert to see? A reed shaken by the wind?, which reed those desire to imitate who writing, write iniquity, and upon whom the prophet calls down a curse, saying: Rebuke the beasts of the reed —. And he was standing in the gate, because through him we go in to the Father, and without him we cannot enter the city of God, so that he may receive the worthy and cast out the unworthy; in the gate too there is judgment — whence the prophet also says: They hated those who reprove in the gate, and in another place: They shall not be put to shame when they speak with their enemies in the gate —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.40.4":["This man spoke to the prophet, he in whose hand was the cord, and whose appearance was as of bronze, and who held the reed in his hand: the true architect spoke to Ezekiel — he whom the apostle Paul imitated, saying: As a wise architect I have laid the foundation; and he calls the architect wise to distinguish him from the one who is foolish and in Zechariah is called the foolish shepherd —; and he spoke the words that follow: Son of man, see with your eyes, and hear with your ears, not with the eyes of the flesh but of the spirit, nor with the ears of the body but of the soul —. Lift up, he says, your eyes and see, for the harvests are already white for reaping, and: He who has ears to hear, let him hear —; and the building of the city is seen, and the order of the ceremonies and of the priests and the description of the land are heard; nor was it enough to have commanded him to see with his eyes and hear with his ears, but he added: And set your heart upon all (or and set in your heart all) that I shall show you; for it profits nothing to have seen and to have heard, unless you have laid up in the treasury of memory the things you have seen and heard; and when he says: All that I shall show you, he makes him an attentive hearer, makes him ready too with the eyes of the heart, so that he may hold in memory the things that are to be shown to him: For you have been brought here that all things may be shown to you — by which saying he shows in a special way that nothing is sweeter than contemplation and knowledge, which the prophet desires, saying: One thing I have asked of the Lord, this will I seek: that I may dwell in the house of the Lord all the days of my life, that I may see the will of the Lord and visit his temple; whence he adds and says: Lord, I have loved the beauty of your house, and the place of the dwelling of your glory —. 'Declare', he says, 'all that you see to the house of Israel, so that those who cannot see by themselves may learn through you the things that are shown to you by the Lord'; and to the house of Israel: to those who behold God with the mind — among whom was Nathanael too, who sought Christ most zealously and was found worthy to hear: Behold a true Israelite, in whom there is no guile —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.40.5":["And behold, a wall outside, round about the house on every side, and in the man's hand a measuring reed of six cubits and a handbreadth; and he measured the breadth of the building, one reed, the height also, one reed. And he came to the gate which looked toward the eastern way, and he went up by its steps, and he measured the threshold of the gate, one reed in breadth, that is, one threshold one reed in breadth, and the cell one reed in length and one reed in breadth, and between the cells five cubits, and the threshold of the gate beside the vestibule of the gate within, one reed. And he measured the vestibule of the gate, of eight cubits, and its front, two cubits; and the vestibule of the gate was within. Further, the cells of the gate toward the eastern way, three on this side and three on that, one measure of the three, and one measure of the fronts on either side. And he measured the breadth of the threshold of the gate, ten cubits, and the length of the gate, thirteen cubits, and the margin before the cells, of one cubit, and one cubit the boundary on both sides; and the cells were of six cubits on this side and on that. And he measured the gate from the roof of a cell to its roof, a breadth of twenty-five cubits, door opposite door. (LXX: \"And behold, a wall outside the house round about, and in the man's hand a measuring reed of six cubits and a palaestes; he measured the wall, the breadth equal to the reed, and the height of the same measure. And he went into the gate which looked toward the east, by seven steps, and he measured ÷ the 'thee', six on this side and six on that: the aelam of the gate equal to the reed in length and equal to the reed in breadth, and the 'aelam' in the middle of the 'theelatha', of five cubits, and the second 'thee' equal to the reed in breadth and equal to the reed in length, and the 'aelam' of five cubits, and the third 'thee' equal to the reed in breadth and equal to the reed in length, of the three, and the 'aelam' of the gate which is beside the 'aelam' of the gate, of eight cubits, and the 'aeleu' of two cubits, and the 'aelam' of the gate within. And the 'thee' of the gate opposite, three on this side and three on that, and one measure of the three, one measure of the 'aelam' on this side and on that. And he measured the breadth of the entrance of the gate, ten cubits, and the breadth of the gate, thirteen cubits, and one cubit was drawn in before the 'theeri', of one cubit, and a boundary of one cubit on this side and on that; and the 'thee' of six cubits on this side and of six cubits on that. And he measured the gate from the wall of the 'thee' to the wall of the 'thee', a breadth of twenty-five cubits; this gate opposite gate.\") When I was a boy at Rome and was being educated in liberal studies, I used, with others of the same age and purpose, to go round the tombs of the apostles and martyrs on the Lord's days, and often to enter the crypts which, dug out in the depths of the earth, have along the walls, on either side of those who enter, the bodies of the buried; and everything is so dark that that prophetic word is almost fulfilled: Let them go down alive into hell, and light let in rarely from above tempers the horror of the darkness, so that you would think it not so much a window as a hole for the light let down; and again one moves forward step by step and, to those surrounded by blind night, that line of Vergil presents itself: Horror everywhere for the mind, and at the same time the very silences terrify. Let this have been said by me so that the prudent reader may understand what view I hold about the explanation of the temple of the Lord in Ezekiel, of which it is written: Cloud and darkness under his feet, and again: Darkness is his hiding place; whence Moses too entered into the cloud and the darkness, so that he could contemplate the mysteries of the Lord, which the people, placed far off and remaining below, could not see; and then, after forty days, the ignoble crowd with darkened eyes could not look upon the face of Moses, because the face of Moses 'had been glorified', or, as is contained in the Hebrew, 'was horned'; so it happens to me too as I read the description of the mystical temple — which the Jews think is to be built according to the letter at the coming of their Christ, whom we prove to be Antichrist, and which we refer to the Church of Christ, and which I see being built daily in the saints —: for wherever the eye of the heart is opened, and I have thought that I see something and hold the bridegroom — and rejoicing have said: I have found him whom my soul sought; I will hold him and will not let him go —, again the divine word deserts me, and the bridegroom flees from my hands, and my eyes are closed in blindness — so that I am compelled to say: O the depth of the riches of the wisdom and knowledge of God! How unsearchable are his judgments, and his ways past tracing out!, and what is written elsewhere: The judgments of the Lord are a great deep, and: Out of the depths I have cried to you, O Lord; Lord, hear my voice, and that word of Elisha, who followed with the eyes of the heart the master snatched away from him, saying: Father, father, the chariot of Israel and its charioteer —. It must therefore be simply confessed that I do not wish to discuss the temple of blessed Ezekiel, about which all the ages have been silent, out of rashness, but desire, in faith and the fear of God, to show readers the conjecture of my mind, so that, if I offer to the tabernacle of God goats' skins and hair, by which the thorns and stings of sins may be covered and the rains of storms kept off, another, who is rich, may offer, according to the quality of his merits, either iron or bronze or silver and gold and most precious stones, and may not despise our gifts because they are lesser, but rejoice in his own wealth: for unless there are lowest things, there cannot be highest ones, and so that the greater may be praised, they grow by comparison with the lesser. Let us see, then, what Ezekiel, having entered the building of the city facing toward the south, first saw, then heard: And behold, he says, a wall outside, round about the house on every side. In place of the 'wall' which Aquila and Theodotion translated, Symmachus and the Seventy put περίβολος [enclosure]: on every side it surrounded the house, that is, the temple of the Lord; and the man whose appearance was as of bronze had not only the surveyor's cord, the cord of the masons, but he also held in his hand the reed, whose measure was passed over in silence above and is now given: of six cubits and a handbreadth — which in Greek is more correctly called palaestes, and is the sixth part of a cubit; otherwise 'palmus' means σπιθαμὴν [a span], which some have been accustomed to call 'palma' [palm] for the sake of distinction, and further to call the 'palaesten' 'palmum' [handbreadth] —; with this reed, then, he measured the breadth and the height of the wall, and both, that is, height and breadth, were of one reed, that is, of six cubits and one handbreadth.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","The apostle Paul, wishing the Ephesians to know the more sacred things, prays for them 'that they may be filled with the wisdom and love of the Lord, and, being rooted, may be able to know and comprehend what is the breadth and length and height and depth of his riches'; and to the same he says: So then you are by no means strangers and sojourners, but fellow citizens of the saints and members of the household of God, built upon the foundation of the apostles and prophets, Jesus Christ himself being the cornerstone; in whom the whole building, fitted together, grows into a holy temple for the Lord, in whom you too are built up into a dwelling place of God in the Holy Spirit. Peter too, in almost the same words, because with the same spirit, says in his epistle: If indeed you have believed that the Lord is good. Coming to him, a living stone, rejected indeed by men, but chosen and honored by God, you yourselves too, as living stones, are built up as a spiritual house, into a holy priesthood, offering spiritual victims acceptable to God through Jesus Christ. From this it is clear that the Lord Savior, who is the true architect, tests the stones: which can be called together into the foundation of the temple, which into the middle, which into the higher places, and which, being of lesser measure or of not so solid firmness, into the wall and outside the temple, into the περιβολῇ round about — for it is a thing of no small strength nor of the least firmness to bear the stones laid on from above; whence the apostle writes to believers: Bearing one another's burdens, and in another place it is written that we ought not to take up a weight which we cannot carry —. And the measuring reed, which had six cubits and one handbreadth, signifies this: that our way of life is known to God, which is contained in the six days in which the world was made, and that the reason of each of our works is known too, so that one thing pertains to works, another to the mind; whence also the breadth is equal to the height, and of these the breadth refers, as we have said, to works, the height to the mind, which hastens toward high things. Let it suffice to have given this warning once: that I know that cubitum and cubita [cubit, cubits] are used in the neuter gender, but that, for simplicity of understanding and by the custom of the common people, I also put them in the masculine gender — for it is not our concern to avoid faults of speech, but to explain the obscurity of holy Scripture in whatever words we can —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.40.6-7":["The man, then, whose name is Orient, goes in by the eastern gate, to enlighten those whom he found at the first front of the vestibule, or at the very entrance of the gate: not by one step but by several, whose number is passed over in silence so that the difficulty of the ascent may appear, and so that, however many steps you propose to yourself, you may know that it is less than what is left in doubt — I think this is the gate of which it is also sung in the Psalms: This is the gate of the Lord; the righteous shall enter by it —; and he measured the threshold of the gate, so that we may be able to know that all things lie open to the knowledge of God; this threshold is said to be of one reed in breadth, and nothing is said of its height and length: for it is a way and an entrance, and through it one must proceed to the inner parts — although in this place the Seventy superfluously said 'length' or, as is contained in most manuscripts, 'height' —; and the rooms, that is, the cells and παραστάδες, have one reed each, as much in length as in breadth, but between cell and cell there are only six cubits, so that the inner parts of the rooms may have the full measure of the reed, that is, of six cubits and a handbreadth, while works and reason agree alike, but those things which are outside, that is, between cell and cell, five cubits; for they have not yet penetrated the secrets of Christ, nor can they say: The king has brought me into his room, and elsewhere: All the glory of the king's daughter is within, but, while the disciples stay at home with Christ, they cannot hear his mysteries, and they desire the word of God to come out to them outside; and therefore the measure of five cubits is set in them, so that they may be taught to refer all things to the five senses; the threshold of the other gate too, or, as is contained in the Seventy, of the second and the third vestibule, is of the same measure of one reed.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.40.6-9":["The threshold of the gate, then — or 'thee', or rather 'seph', which Symmachus translated οὐδὸν [threshold], Aquila and Theodotion πρόθυρον [front doorway] —, had one reed, that is, six cubits and a handbreadth, in breadth; further, meaning I know not what, the Seventy added of their own 'six on this side and six on that'; and after the threshold of the gate, according to the Hebrew, he measured the cell — or room, and, as Symmachus translated, παραστάδας, that is, 'posts' — on either side, at one reed, as much in length as in breadth — which the Seventy also translated 'aelam', and which in what follows Symmachus translated 'fore-threshold' —; and between the two cells — or rooms and παραστάδας — there were five cubits, and another near-threshold of the gate beside the vestibule within, of one reed; a third, the vestibule of the gate, also had eight cubits, and in its front, or in its margins, two cubits; and lest anyone think this vestibule to be the same as the one above, he added: the vestibule of the inner gate; in place of which the Seventy translated a first and a second and a third vestibule.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.40.6":["Though there were very many gates, he would not enter by the other gates, but by the one which looked toward the eastern quarter; and because the place was not level — for the wall round about, and the house of God itself, had been built on higher ground —, he therefore goes in, or rather climbs up, by steps — which the Seventy alone name as 'seven', whereas both in the Hebrew and in the other translators we read only 'steps', without a number —; and having entered the gate, he at once measured the threshold of the gate — which the Seventy name 'thee', in place of which in the Hebrew is written 'seph' [threshold]; and I think the diligent and studious reader should be reminded of this, if indeed he is led by knowledge of the Scriptures and not by the empty declamations of orators: that he should know that almost all the Hebrew words and names which have been set down in the Greek and Latin translation have been corrupted by excessive age and depraved by the fault of the scribes, and that, as ever more uncorrected copies are written from uncorrected ones, out of Hebrew words Sarmatian words have been made, or rather words of no nation, since they have ceased to be Hebrew and have not begun to be foreign —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.40.9":["After this, for its vestibule, that is, for the threshold of the gate, the entrance, eight cubits are set, so that the inner parts of the vestibule may hold not the number seven, and six cubits and one handbreadth, but the number eight, which pertain to the mystery of the resurrection and of the Lord's day. 'And on the front', he says, 'of the same vestibule there were two cubits', which pertain either to both Testaments, or to the letter and the spirit, or to the mystery of the tongs with which, in Isaiah, the coal is taken from the altar and carried to cleanse the prophet's lips; and so that we may know what this vestibule is which is bounded by eight and two cubits, he puts it more plainly: And the vestibule of the gate was within, through which we come to the temple of God.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.40.10":["And the cells, that is, the rooms which had been built in the vestibules after the eastern gate, looked toward the eastern way; and so that we may know how many cells there were, it is added: three on this side and three on that, looking, namely, toward the north and toward the south and toward the eastern way; and there was one measure of the fronts on either side.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","The cells too, and rooms, which were toward the way of the eastern gate, in place of which the Seventy write 'aeleu', were three on this side and three on that, of one measure, that is, of one reed which had six cubits and one handbreadth, of which it was spoken above, and the 'aelam' one reed in length and one reed in breadth, and between the cells five cubits; so, lest we should think that there were only two cells, he set three on either side, to show the number six, which is shown also in the water jars of the Gospel, in which the waters were changed into wines; and daily the Jewish waters are turned into wine, which makes glad the heart of man, and becomes sweeter with the blood of Christ.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.40.11-12":["He also measured the measure of the threshold of the gate in breadth and found ten cubits, and in length thirteen cubits, and the ends of the cells — or, as Symmachus translated, παραστάδες — were narrowed into one cubit, and each front had one cubit; and the cells, or παραστάδες and, as the Seventy put of their own, 'theelatha' —, had six cubits each on either side.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","He also measured the breadth of the threshold of the gate, of ten cubits, which is a perfect number and is contained either in the Decalogue or in the mystery of the four Gospels, which, beginning from one, so proceeds to I.II.III.IIII that it fills up the number ten; its length had thirteen cubits; in place of which, in the Seventy, breadth is put again — which seems to me to have been corrupted by the negligence of the scribes: for Scripture would not have said in one place breadth and again breadth, of ten or of thirteen cubits —; and the gate of the threshold, in which the number of the old and the new Testament is consecrated, can be understood either as the Lord himself, who says: I am the door, or certainly as all the saints through whom we enter into the knowledge of God, of whom the apostle Paul spoke: My mouth is open to you, O Corinthians; be enlarged, you too; the thirteen cubits, after the number eight, contain the mystery of the books of Moses, which are shown both in the five loaves and in the Samaritan woman of the Gospel, who is reproved because she had had five husbands, and the sixth, whom she thought she had, she did not have; and yet the number eight and the number five are completed by one cubit, that is, the fourteenth, because in Christ all things are summed up; whence it is said: And one cubit the boundary on both sides, since one measure of Christ embraces both the old and the new testament, and one cubit encloses the height of thirty cubits of Noah's ark.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.40.12":["And the cells, he says (or, as the Seventy put it, 'thau' or 'thee' — turning and changing the Hebrew names in each place as they pleased —), were of six cubits 'on either side', which is said as on this side and on that, and they by no means had the handbreadth, nor the reason added above, which might call our present way of life to the future blessedness, but only six cubits.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.40.13":["And he measured the gate from the roof of a cell — or παραστάδος — to its roof or wall, and found, between roof and roof, twenty-five cubits of breadth, for so much was the space between door and door, or between gate and gate. Let us have said these things briefly, in the spirit of one divining rather than explaining, wishing to sketch, according to the letter, a picture that is obscure and almost invisible; for the rest, we shall set down, briefly and with hesitation, the few things we can conjecture.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","Whence there follows: And he measured the gate from the roof of a cell to the roof (or wall) of it, and he found twenty-five cubits of breadth; and the number itself, though it is square, is nevertheless referred to the senses: for if you set five cubits against each other five times, you will produce the number twenty-five, which is between the roof of the cells and the roof; and yet both, set opposite each other, have from their highest parts door opposite door. Let these things not seem trivial to the reader — although they displease even me myself who say them, feeling that I am knocking at a closed door —, but they are to be read with indulgence; otherwise I could simply have confessed my ignorance and cut off every desire of the studious: for as we are far from perfect knowledge, so we judge it a lighter fault to say at least a little than nothing at all.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.40.14-16":["And he made fronts of sixty cubits, and at the front a court of the gate on every side round about; and before the face of the gate, which reached as far as the face of the vestibule of the inner gate, fifty cubits; and slanting windows in the cells and in their fronts, which were within the gate on every side round about; and likewise there were also windows in the vestibules round about within, and before the fronts a painting of palm trees. (LXX: \"And under the open sky the 'aelam' of the gate, sixty cubits, and the 'theïm' of the court of the gate round about; and under the open sky of the court outside, the 'aelam' of the inner gate, fifty cubits; and hidden windows over the 'theïm' and over the 'aelam', within the gate of the court round about; and likewise the 'aelamoth' and windows round about within, and on the 'aelam' palms on this side and on that.\") Symmachus translated this place thus: And he made περιστάσεις, which we can call 'circumstantias' [things standing round], in place of which the Hebrew also has 'ulium', which Aquila translated κρίωμα [ram-work, a pilaster] and we have turned into 'front'; again, where the Seventy put 'theïm of the court', the Hebrew has 'ulim' in the singular number, which Symmachus translated 'beside the court', Aquila κρίωμα, that is, 'the front of the court'; 'under the open sky' too, 'the aelam of the gate', Aquila translated 'before the face of the posts of the gate', Symmachus 'before the face of the vestibule of the gate'; and the 'hidden' windows, which in Hebrew are called 'aetemoth', Symmachus translated τοξικὰς [arrow-like]; and where the Seventy said 'over the theïm' and 'over the aelam', the same Symmachus put 'posts' and 'circumstantias', Aquila 'cells' and 'their fronts', in place of which in the Hebrew is written 'tham' and 'aele'; further, 'aelamoth', which the Seventy and Theodotion translated alike, Symmachus translated 'circumstantias', Aquila 'fronts'; and 'aelam', in place of which in the Hebrew is written 'ul', is called either 'door' or 'portico'; 'palm' too is written in the Hebrew 'thamarim', in the plural number, because the singular number calls a 'palm' 'thamar'. I do not doubt that these things will be tiresome to a fastidious reader, but I wished to show briefly how far, through length of time, or rather through the fault of the scribes and, to speak more openly, through their fault and unskillfulness, the old translation differs from the Hebrew truth, and especially in the Hebrew words which we have rendered into the Latin tongue from the editions of others, simply indicating not so much an explanation of the words as our conjecture. And I think that between the wall of the temple, outside round about, and the house itself, in the court, that is, in the middle, certain things were set for ornament, which Symmachus translates 'circumstantias', that is, 'certain standing things', raised up on high from the ground of the earth, and that these occupied a space of sixty cubits; and again, for those going out from the inner gate to the face of the vestibule which looked toward the outer gate, that the place occupied fifty cubits, in which there were slanting windows, which the Seventy call 'hidden', Symmachus τοξικὰς; and these windows were in the cells, that is, in each of the rooms, and in the porticoes which extended before the rooms, occupying fifty cubits — these slanting windows, or τοξικαὶ, received their name from arrows for this reason, that, like arrows, a narrow light is let into the building; and within they widen —; and everything round about was full of windows of this kind. 'And before the fronts', he says, 'of the porticoes, a painting or carving of palm trees'; by which it is shown that, for those at the entrance of the gate and as soon as they enter the wall, sixty cubits meet them with various ornaments, which are referred to the creation of the world, so that the Creator may be understood from the creatures, and so that the variety of the world may show all things running in order and reason — the world which among the Greeks receives the name κόσμος from 'adornment', and was made in six days, so that for each day one decade may be reckoned, which we said above is a perfect number —; after this, as we enter the inner court, there meet us, before the very vestibule of the inner gate, cells with slanting windows, which occupy a space of fifty cubits, which itself too is a consecrated number: and after seven full weeks of festivity and joy, the first day of the ogdoad begins, which is the day of the resurrection, and brings us into the neighborhood of the temple — for when we have done all things, doing penance for our former error, then we are made neighbors and nearest to God, so that, in the outer court, the order and constancy of the creatures may teach us the knowledge of the Creator, and, in the inner, the true jubilee, in which all debts are forgiven us, may instruct us in θεολογίαν [theology, the knowledge of God].\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.40.16":["It must also be noted that the inner court has many windows, not straight and even but slanting, narrow on the outside and widening within, so that through certain small openings we may be able to penetrate to the inner things and to come to the fullness of the brightest light which dwells in the temple —; finally, after the sixty and the fifty cubits, and the cells and the porticoes, the fronts of the porticoes and the very many windows round about, the carving or painting of palm trees is shown to us, so that, possessing the victory over the world, we may be made worthy to see the palms of the virtues.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.40.17-18":["And those are called 'gazophylacia' [chambers, literally treasuries] which are full of spiritual riches — of which it is written: The redemption of a man's soul is his own riches, and over which the apostle Paul too congratulates his disciples, that they are full in every word and in all knowledge; and in the language of the Persians 'riches' are called 'gaza' —, or certainly cells, which in Greek are called παστοφόρια [bridal chambers]: they show rooms prepared for the coming of the bridegroom; and they were thirty in number, to show the perfect age of those who dwell in them. And the 'pavement' was laid with stone, so that the footsteps of the inhabitants might not be defiled by the mud and earth and dust of sins, but that they might walk upon the living stones with which the temple of God is built; or there were 'intercolumniations' before the doors of the cells, which sustained the building laid upon them from above — these are the columns of which the apostle Paul also writes: They gave the right hand to me and Barnabas, Peter and John, who seemed to be columns, and in another place: The column and foundation of the truth; and in the Revelation of John we read: He who conquers, I will make him a column in the temple of my God, and he shall go outside no more —; so the 'pavement' which was at the front of the gates, of which we have already spoken, or the 'porticoes' behind the back of the gates, which protected the inhabitants of the cells from the rains, was lower: for the things which are lower are always placed in the higher ones, so that we may be able to come by ascent and by steps to the things that are mystical and secret and full of spiritual riches.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.40.17-19":["And he brought me out to the outer court, and behold, chambers, and a pavement laid with stone in the court round about, thirty chambers round about the pavement; and the pavement at the front of the gates, according to the length of the gates, was lower. And he measured the breadth from the face of the lower gate to the front of the inner court outside, a hundred cubits toward the east and toward the north. (LXX: \"And he brought me into the inner court, and behold, cells and intercolumniations of the court round about, thirty cells between the columns; and porticoes behind the doors, along the length of the doors, before the columns, lower. And he measured the breadth of the court from that place which was under the open sky of the outer gate to the inner place under the open sky of the inner gate, which looked toward the outer parts, a hundred cubits, and it faced toward the east.\") In place of the thirty 'cells' which the Seventy rendered, or 'chambers', and 'storerooms', as Aquila translated, Symmachus put 'exedrae'; and in place of the hundred cubits of the outer part which extended as far as the eastern gate, in the Hebrew the same number of cubits is said to extend toward the north too, but in the Seventy the name of the north has been removed. That man, then, who had the masons' cord in his hand and the measuring reed, and stood in the gate, among the other things which he showed to his sight, after the court of sixty cubits and the other vestibule of the inner gate which occupied fifty cubits, brought the prophet out to the outer court or, as is contained in the Seventy, brought him into the inner court — and I wonder greatly, according to the letter and the Seventy, how, after the outer courts occupying sixty and fifty cubits, there is an inner court which extends over spaces of a hundred cubits in breadth, unless perhaps, according to the mystical senses and the progress of those who enter, the things which were more inward in teachings are broader —; and having been brought into the inner court, or led to the outer court, as is contained in the Hebrew, he at once beheld thirty 'cells' or 'chambers' and, as Symmachus translated, 'exedrae', which had been prepared for the dwelling of the Levites and priests, and the pavement laid with stones, or intercolumniations, so that it may be shown that the inner or outer court has before the doors a row of columns and a pavement at the front of the gates, or porticoes behind the back of the gates, in which porticoes I think the columns were arranged in order, so that before the chambers there were porticoes, and at the fronts of the porticoes columns sustaining the porticoes; and the pavement which was laid with stones and extended before the porticoes of the court was lower at the ascent of the porticoes, and had the same length which the building of the cells possessed. And the same man, in whose hand were the cord and the reed, measured from the face of the lower gate or, as the Seventy would have it, of the outer gate, to the front of the inner court or gate, a hundred cubits toward the east and, as is said in the Hebrew, toward the north too. From which we understand that the saying of the Gospel which the Savior relates, of the seed sent into the field onto good ground, which multiplies its fruits according to the variety of the virtues into the number thirty and sixty and a hundred, agrees with the prophecy of this place: here too the number thirty and sixty and a hundred is set, so that by these, as by steps, we may be able to come to perfect knowledge, yet only in such a way that the number fifty leads us to the inner house, which, when the sabbath of the seven weeks is finished, breaks forth into the day of the resurrection.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.40.19":["And the full and perfect number is in the number of ten decades, which also multiplies the sowing of Isaac with a hundredfold crop; but because he was on earth and could still say: I am a sojourner and a stranger, as all my fathers were, he is related to have sown barley and reaped a hundredfold crop: for the grain of wheat had not yet died in the earth, to bear many fruits and to satisfy the Jewish people's hunger for hearing the word of God with that bread which came down from heaven; but how the hundred cubits were not only toward the eastern gate but also toward the gate of the north, the following testimony will show.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.40.20":["He also measured the gate which looked toward the way of the north of the outer court, as much in length as in breadth; and its cells, three on this side and three on that, and its front and its vestibule according to the measure of the former gate, its length fifty cubits, and the breadth twenty-five cubits. And its windows and its vestibule and carvings according to the measures of the gate which looked toward the east; and its ascent was of seven steps, and a vestibule before it. And the gate of the inner court opposite the gate of the north and the eastern one; and he measured from gate to gate a hundred cubits. (LXX: \"And he brought me toward the north, and behold, a gate looking toward the outer court, and he measured it in length and in breadth; and its 'thee', three on this side and three on that, and the 'aeleu' and the 'aelamoth' — and what is not found in the Hebrew: — and its palms, and it was made according to the measure of the gate which looked toward the east, fifty cubits the length and the breadth twenty-five cubits. And its windows and the 'aelamoth' and its palms as in the gate which looked toward the east; and by steps they went up to it, and the 'aelamoth' within. And the gate of the inner court looking toward the gate of the north — and what is not found in the Hebrew: — as the gate which looks toward the east; and he measured the court from gate to gate, of a hundred cubits.\") What the Hebrew words 'thee' and 'aeleu' and 'aelamoth' signify we have said more fully above, but in the present passage too our translation will teach it by their placing. And the man in whose hand was the cord measured the gate of the north too, of the outer court, or brought the prophet in through the gate of the north to the outer court — in which it must be considered that he is brought in first through the gate of the north, second through the gate of the south, third through the eastern gate — From the north, for, evils blaze up upon the inhabitants of the earth, and fittingly he who is an inhabitant of the earth, and not a sojourner and a stranger, lies open to the darts of him who comes from the north, whose darts are fiery, which, according to the Apostle, are quenched by the shield of faith —; and toward the north the court is called outer, but toward the midday, that is, toward the south, not outer but inner — for Scripture says: And he brought me into the inner court, to the southern gate, which is found written likewise of the eastern court too —: for the first entrance to virtue is to tread the north underfoot and, through the number fifty and twenty-five, to come to the summit of the hundred; then to be brought into the southern quarter and to drive out the cold of the north; then to come to perfect virtue, that is, to the eastern gate, in which the sun of righteousness rises and through which the high priest alone enters — and this is to be observed, that, though there are four quarters, the western gate is passed over in silence, of which it is written at the beginning of that psalm: Make a way for him who ascends over the setting: the Lord is his name, so that, after we have prepared the way for the Lord and have fulfilled what John the Baptist cries in the desert: Prepare the way of the Lord, make his paths straight, we may be able to hear what is said in the last part of the same psalm: who ascends above the heaven of heaven toward the east —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.40.21":["And that the cells are said to be three on this side and three on that teaches this, that both the letter and the spirit, and each understanding, that of history and that of tropology, pertain to the mysteries of the Trinity. And what is said: And its front and vestibule according to the measure of the former gate, signifies the eastern gate, which is called former not in order but in merit. 'And they had', he says, 'fifty cubits in length and twenty-five in breadth', so that in the fifty, after seven weeks, the eternal rest may be shown, extending through the day of the resurrection to the kingdoms of heaven, in which there is true rest, and in the twenty-five, of which number we have spoken before, the five senses may be kept, through five rows, in the measure of a square, one of which is fitted to the length, the other to the breadth, so that in the length may be shown the rest and delight of a more sacred knowledge, in the breadth the reason of present things which pertains to the senses.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.40.22":["And what is added: And the windows and the vestibules and the carvings (or, as the Seventy have handed down, the palms) were according to the measure of the gate which looked toward the east, indicates this spiritually: that whoever, placed at the entrance of the virtues, has entered the three and three cells and has recognized the front and the vestibule of the measure of the former gate, may also come to those things, and behold those, which he is going to receive most fully in the eastern gate. Further, what follows: And its ascent was of seven steps, and a vestibule before it (or within), has this sense: that through the seven steps of the week, and of the pot which is kindled from the face of the north, and of the ceremonies of the Jews, to whom God gave precepts that were not good and ordinances in which they should not live in them, we climb to higher things and to its vestibule, that is, the vestibule of the gate, in place of which the Seventy translated 'aelamoth', which vestibule is before the gate, or within.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.40.23":["Nor is there only one gate of the north, which above is called the outer gate, but another which has the likeness of the eastern gate, as the Seventy said more plainly: And the inner gate of the court looking toward the gate of the north, as was the gate which looked toward the east; and he measured from the outer gate of the north to the inner gate of the same north a hundred cubits, so that by these as by steps, withdrawing from the outer gate, we may be able to come to the inner one, concerning the mystery of which number it has been spoken.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.40.24-26":["And he led me to the southern way, and behold, a gate which looked toward the south; and he measured its front and its vestibule according to the measures above, and its windows, and the vestibules round about it, like the other windows, in which there was a length of fifty cubits and a breadth of twenty-five. And by seven steps one went up to it, and a vestibule before its front, and there were carved palms, one on this side and another on that, on its front. And the gate of the inner court on the southern way; and he measured from gate to gate on the southern way a hundred cubits. And he brought me into the inner court at the southern gate, and he measured the gate according to the measures above, its cell and its front, and its vestibule with the same measures, and the windows of the vestibules round about: fifty cubits of length, and of breadth twenty-five cubits, and the vestibule round about, in length twenty-five cubits and in breadth five cubits, and its vestibule toward the outer court, and palms on the front, and there were eight steps by which one went up through it. (The Seventy agree in the same words, except for 'thee' and 'aeleu' and 'aelamoth' and 'aelam', in place of which in the Hebrew is put 'ulam'; and 'thee' means 'threshold' or 'front', 'aeleu' 'around it' or 'supports', 'aelamoth' 'propyla' [gateways] or 'vestibules', 'ulam tritilon' 'round about', that is, 'before the doors'. But what we have put according to the Hebrew: And the vestibule (or πρόπυλον [gateway]) round about, of a length of twenty-five cubits and of a breadth of five cubits, is not found in the Seventy. But this too is to be noted, that at the southern gate, according to the Seventy, 'way' is not said, but simply: And he led me toward the south, and behold, a gate which looked toward the south.) Through each of the entrances, then, Ezekiel, whom God strengthened — for this is what his name means —, is led, and he passes from the gate of the north to the southern gate, whose fronts and thresholds, πρόπυλα or vestibules, and windows round about had similar measures: a length of fifty cubits and a breadth of twenty-five cubits. And by seven steps one went up to it, that is, to the southern gate, and its vestibule, that is, the 'aelamoth', was before its doors; there were also two carved palms, of which we spoke at the northern quarter of the gate. And let it suffice to have advised only this, that what is set there according to a lowlier understanding, that is, according to the letter, we understand at the southern gate according to the spiritual sense: for history and tropology run along the same lines, but the former is humbler, the latter more sublime; the former clings to the earth, the latter flies up to heavenly things.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.40.25-26":["And round about, through the windows, all things were full of light — for the several προβλήματα [questions] have their own limits —, and through them its own light enters the soul of the one who sees, especially if the length of his life has been prolonged by the mystery of the fiftieth year, that is, of the jubilee, and he has been able to come to the breadth of the more divine sense; in which breadth and streets wisdom acts confidently and cries aloud with a lofty voice. He enters by seven steps so as to come to the vestibule, and through the palms of his purpose and effort to hold the everlasting victory.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.40.27":["And lest we should think there is one court toward the southern part, it is said of a second court: And the gate of the inner court on the southern way; and he measured from gate to gate on the southern way a hundred cubits, so that after the mysteries of the week and of the old Testament he might come to the good land and to the grace of the Gospel.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.40.28-29":["Nor is there only a second court at the southern gate, but a third, of which it is now said: And he brought me into the inner court at the southern gate, and he measured the gate according to the measures above, and the rest: for each court has its own measures and orders, and in the same measures there is a different grace, since we know the same things in one way at the beginning, in another in our progress, in another at the end.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.40.30-31":["But this, which is not found in the Seventy: And the vestibule (or πρόπυλον) round about, in length twenty-five cubits and in breadth five cubits, seems to raise a question, on account of which it was also not set down in the translation: how, after the progress of the third step, twenty-five cubits and five are set; but what follows removes the ambiguity: And its vestibule toward the outer court, and the palms on its front, namely, that this court, which has twenty-five cubits in length and five in breadth, belongs not to the inner but to the outer court, so that the length of the outer court possesses the breadth of the former court, that is, twenty-five cubits, and holds five cubits in breadth; which measures, that is, twenty-five and five, we refer to those senses, namely sight, hearing, taste and smell and touch, which, if we climb to higher things, we double five times, and out of carnal senses we shall make spiritual ones — as in that saying: Lift up your eyes and see, for the harvests are white for reaping, and: He who has ears to hear, let him hear, and: Taste and see that the Lord is sweet, and: We are the good odor of Christ among those who believe; and in the epistle of John it is said: What we have heard and seen, and our hands have handled, concerning the word of life —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.40.31":["And at the last they make not, as at the northern quarter, only seven steps, but after the seven the number eight is set, so that we may pass from the Synagogue to the Church, from the old testament to the new, from earthly things to heavenly ones.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.40.32-34":["And he brought me into the inner court by the eastern way, and he measured the gate according to the measures above; the cell and its front and the vestibules as above, and its windows and its vestibules round about, the length fifty cubits and the breadth twenty-five cubits; and its vestibule, that is, of the outer court (in place of which the Seventy and Symmachus put inner), and carved palms on its front, on this side and on that, and its ascent by eight steps. From the northern quarter, into whose court one went up by only seven steps, he passes to the southern gate and to the court of the same quarter, to which one ascends not only by seven steps, as we said above, but by eight: namely, so that through the old things we may pass to the new and, to speak more plainly, through the patriarchs and prophets we may come to the apostles: so that in the one there is the hebdomad, in the other the ogdoad. But now he comes to the last, or rather to the highest, and brings the prophet in by the way of the inner gate, or through the court to which one goes by the eastern way — 'way', according to the Seventy, is found in this place only: at the northern and the southern quarter it is wholly passed over in silence, so that we may understand that at the eastern gate alone is that way to be understood which says: I am the way and the truth and the life; for through him we come to the Father, and of him it is written: Blessed are the undefiled in the way, who think nothing sinister about Christ, but whose faith is pure and in no way polluted with the filth of heretics —; and there is the same number of cells, of fronts and vestibules and windows and of their light, and the same measure of length and breadth, preserving, namely, the mysteries of fifty and of twenty-five cubits; and the vestibule of its gate, or of the inner court, which according to the Hebrew is set down as outer, so that all things may be held enclosed by the eastern light — according to that which is written: Nor is there anyone who hides himself from its heat —; but if we take the court as both outer and inner, it is to be understood thus: that God is both poured around and poured in, saying through the prophet: Who holds the heaven in his palm and the earth in his fist, so that all things may be seen to be enclosed by him, and again: Heaven is my throne, and the earth the footstool of my feet, so that he is to be believed to be within all things — according to that passage of Vergil: In the beginning the heaven and the lands and the liquid plains, the shining globe of the moon and the Titanian stars, a spirit within nourishes, and mind, poured through the limbs, moves the whole mass and mingles itself with the great body —; and when, through the weapons on the right and on the left, through glory and dishonor, through good report and bad, through praises and reproaches, we have won the victory over this world, then palms are given to us on both sides, whom neither joys have changed nor grief, neither prosperity nor adversity has overcome; and by no means through seven steps, as in the quarter of the north, nor through seven and eight, as is contained also at the midday, but through eight steps only we climb to the summit, because the number eight, that is, the mystery of the Gospel, suffices for us, and we may say, according to the Apostle: Even if we had known Christ according to the flesh, yet now we know him no longer, understood: according to the flesh.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.40.35-38":["He is brought, then, again to the gate which looked toward the north, but by no means, as above, to the outer court, but to the inner — for in what is said in what follows: Its vestibule looked toward the outer court, it is shown that this court was within —; by which the progress of the one entering is shown, since in each place the names and measures are its own, and the windows round about show all things full of the light of knowledge; and through the fifty and the twenty-five cubits the mysteries of remission and of the divine senses are set, so that, placed in the vestibule of the inner court, we may see the outer things, and, contemplating the knowledge of things past and present, through which we may come to the double palms and victories, may know the mysteries of the eight steps, having gone into the chambers, in which the riches of the temple are contained and our prayers are washed from every stain of sin.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.40.38-43":["And he brought me to the gate which looked toward the north, and he measured according to the measures above its cell, its front and its vestibule and its windows round about, in length fifty cubits, in breadth twenty-five cubits. Its vestibule looked into the outer court, and a carving of palms on its front on this side and on that; its ascent was by eight steps. And by each chamber a door at the entrances of the gates; there they washed the holocaust. And in the vestibule of the gate two tables on this side and two tables on that, that the holocaust may be sacrificed upon them, and for sin and for trespass. And at the outer side which goes up to the door of the gate that leads toward the north, two tables; and at the other side, before the vestibule of the gate, two tables. Four tables on this side and four tables on that: along the sides of the gate there were eight tables, upon which they sacrificed. And four tables for the holocaust, built of squared stones, of a length of one cubit and a half, and a breadth of one cubit and a half, and a height of one cubit, upon which they are to put the vessels in which the holocaust and the victims are sacrificed; and their lips of one handbreadth, bent back inward round about, and upon the tables the flesh of the oblation. In this testimony the Hebrew truth disagrees greatly with the edition of the Seventy translators: for what we have said: There they washed the holocaust. And in the vestibule of the gate two tables on this side, and again: That the holocaust may be sacrificed upon them, and for sin, is not found in the Seventy, but in most manuscripts it has been added from Theodotion; again, where we said: And their lips of one handbreadth, bent back inward round about, the Seventy translated: And having a 'gisum' of a handbreadth, hewn inward round about — which word, whether it is Hebrew or Greek, we cannot know, except only this, that in place of 'gisum' Symmachus and Theodotion translated 'lips', the first edition of Aquila ἐπιστάσεις [ledges] in the plural number, the second 'lips', in place of which in the Hebrew there is 'asaphanaim' —; and where we said: And upon the tables the flesh of the oblation, the Seventy put of their own: And on the tables, coverings above, that they might be covered from rain and from drought; and as for what they rendered with Hebrew names, 'thee' and 'aeleu' and 'aelamoth' and 'aelam', what it signifies we have both advised above, and our translation, set over against it, can show, nor is there any need to advise the reader often about this and to distrust his prudence and diligence.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.40.39":["And two tables were set on each side, so that first the holocaust may be sacrificed, which the sacred fire consumes wholly, then the offering for sin, and lastly that for ignorance: for what we have committed either knowingly or in ignorance.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.40.40":["At the outer side too, according to the Seventy, there was a skillfully made channel which carried outside the ashes of the holocausts and the putrid matter of the flesh, or: there were two tables; and at the other side of the vestibule of the same gate two tables, that is, four on each side.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.40.41":["Four on this side and four on that, and behind them or at their side another eight tables, so that together they make sixteen tables; by which number the mysteries of the prophets are shown, so that whatever we offer through the condescension of the Gospel we may prove to have been foretold by their prophecies.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.40.42-43":["And as for the four tables that are brought in, built of squared stones, and these too for the sacrifice of the holocaust: these are the living stones which roll upon the earth and have with them the cornerstone by which the walls of the old and the new Testament are held together; and the number four shows the mysteries of the Gospel; having in length and breadth one cubit and a half, that is, in a square, which joined together make three cubits, and these three cubits have one cubit in height, so that the measure of one cubit, that is, of the divine majesty, may preserve the mysteries of the Trinity, as the Lord says to his disciples: Go, baptize all nations in the name of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Spirit: and the name of God is, as it were, the one cubit of height, which embraces three properties and always takes up the holocausts of our offerings; and upon these four tables of the holocausts, built of living stones, whose length and breadth and height have been described, are put the vessels in which is sacrificed the holocaust of the martyrs, of whom it is said: Precious in the sight of the Lord is the death of his saints, and the victims of those who by continence and holiness of body sacrifice their souls to the Lord; and I think the names of these tables are contained in the apostles Matthew and Luke and Mark and John; their rim, bent back inward from above, went round the edges round about, so as to hold the holocausts within and not allow them to flow everywhere, and to hold the flesh of the oblation; or, as the Seventy translated: And the tables themselves were covered above, because of the violence of the rains and the excessive drought of the summer sun, so that the flesh placed upon them may say with the prophet: By day the sun shall not burn us, nor the moon by night, of which the one dissolves the offerings with excessive rains and storms of tribulations, the other makes whatever has been offered dry up in the fierce heat of persecutions. Pardon, reader, the difficulty of the passages, or, if you can find something better, teach me: we gladly learn what we do not know.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.40.44-48":["And outside the inner gate the chambers of the singers in the inner court, which was at the side of the gate looking toward the north, and their faces toward the southern way, one at the side of the gate which looked toward the way of the north. And he said to me: This is the chamber which looks toward the way of midday, for the priests who keep watch in the charges of the temple. Further, the chamber which looks toward the way of the north shall be for the priests who keep watch for the ministry of the altar: these are the sons of Zadok, who from the sons of Levi come near to the Lord to minister to him. And he measured the court, in length a hundred cubits and in breadth a hundred cubits, in a square, and the altar before the face of the temple. And he brought me into the vestibule of the temple; and he measured the vestibule, five cubits on this side and five cubits on that, and the breadth of the gate, three cubits on this side and three cubits on that; and the length of the vestibule twenty cubits and the breadth eleven cubits, and by eight steps one went up to it, and their columns on the fronts, one on this side and another on that. In place of 'chambers' the Seventy translated 'exedrae'; and what we said, exedrae or chambers 'of the singers', was passed over by them; and again, after the measure of the vestibule of five cubits on this side and five cubits on that, they added of their own: And the breadth of the gate of fourteen cubits, whereas in the Hebrew there is set only: And the breadth of the gate of three cubits on this side and three cubits on that. I have advised the reader of these things so that he may not be disturbed by the diversity of the translations, but that, in the present place and especially in the measures of the temple, he may be content with the Hebrew truth.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.40.44":["So, after the wall, which the Seventy translated περίβολον, which went round the whole temple in a square round about, we read that the prophet was brought into the inner court, and then to the north and to the south and to the eastern gate and again to the north, of which we have spoken as best we could, and in whose diversities, whether of names or of measures, our past discourse has shown what seemed right to us — whose sense the testimony of the Savior briefly shows, saying: In my Father's house are many mansions —. These things completed, he enters the temple, that is, the Holy of Holies, of which it is now spoken, and within the inner gate are the chambers or exedrae of the singers — to whom that command is given: Sing to the Lord a new song, who cry aloud with the angels: Glory to God in the highest, and on earth peace among men of good will —, which inner court was situated at the side of the gate which looked toward the north, and toward the south and toward the east, so that the singers of the Lord, and those who are held in the office of the angels, may always remember by what steps they have come to the summit, and may diligently recognize the mysteries of each place.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.40.45-46":["Then that man, in whose hand were the cord and the reed, brings the prophet into the inner court which looked toward the way of midday, and spoke these words: This is the chamber (or exedra, and, as Theodotion put it, cell) which has been set apart for the dwelling of the priests who keep watch in the vigils of the temple. Further, the chamber which looks toward the way of the north shall be for the priests who keep watch for the ministry of the altar. In which it is to be noted that the priests to whom the guarding of the temple has been delegated dwell in the chamber which looked toward the way of midday, in which the light is fullest, but those who keep watch for the ministry of the altar, on which the victims for sin are offered, are in the chamber which looks toward the way of the north, desiring to receive and save those who come from the north and offer victims for sin: and both of these, serving the Lord according to their offices, are called sons of 'Sadoc' [Zadok], which means 'righteous' or 'justifying', namely the almighty God — of whom it is written: The Lord is righteous and has loved righteous deeds; his countenance has beheld equity —; and these sons of Zadok are from the sons of 'Leui' [Levi], which means 'taken up', and they too are taken up by the Lord, so that they may come near to him — and it is by no means said of Moses alone: Moses came near to the Lord, while the others did not come near, but all who serve him and minister to him are said to draw near to the Lord —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.40.47":["And this man, who brought Ezekiel into the outer court and, so to speak, to the Holy of Holies, measured the court itself, in which were the two exedrae or chambers assigned as dwellings for the guardians of the temple or of the altar, and found a hundred cubits each of length and of breadth, in a square, so that the priests serving in the ministry of the Lord, who had entered by the strait and narrow way, may have very broad walks and may abide in the perfect and complete number of the virtues; and in the court itself was the altar before the face of the temple, so that the prayer of the saints to the Lord may never cease.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.40.48":["The aforesaid man also brought Ezekiel into the vestibule, or πρόπυλον, of the temple, which he measured at five cubits on this side and five on that, since he who was brought in had been instructed in the divine senses, and knew neither the right hand nor the left, but walked by the royal road; and the breadth of the gate itself, through which was the entrance to the vestibule of the temple, had three cubits 'on this side and on that' — in which the elegance of holy Scripture is to be observed, that it did not say 'on the right and on the left', lest it should seem to name anything sinister in the Holy of Holies, but 'on this side and on that', that is, on either side —, and at once the prudent reader understands that the number three pertains to the mystery of the Trinity, which is the gate of those who enter to God, since the Father is in the Son and the Son in the Father, and the Holy Spirit in both; and this threefold number is the one gate of those who, after the baptism of the Trinity, come to salvation.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.40.49":["But what follows: The length of the vestibule twenty cubits and the breadth eleven cubits, seems to raise a question: how the dual number, which is referred to two decades, and the eleventh, which lacks one to fill up the sacred number twelve, can be said to be in the vestibule of the temple; which most people solve thus: although the priests are holy and placed in the ministries of the temple and daily offer victims to Christ, yet, because they dwell in this mortal and frail little body — and one which is subject to sicknesses and vices —, they have the number twenty and the eleventh — whence the apostle too said: Wretched man that I am! Who will deliver me from the body of this death?, not that we should believe, according to a most savage heresy, that bodies are to be abolished, but that it is for us to be clothed over, not stripped, and for this mortal to receive immortality and for the corruptible to put on incorruption —. But what follows: And by eight steps one went up to it, that is, to the inner gate, is a great difficulty for those who know the Hebrew language: in fact Symmachus, whom we have followed in this place, put 'by eight steps', the Seventy 'ten', Theodotion and the second edition of Aquila 'eleven', but the first put no number, but says absolutely: And by steps by which one went up to it, so that we should understand either, according to the custom of the earlier gates, 'eight' 'steps', as Symmachus also understood, or certainly those steps which the inner gate had, whose number Scripture did not state: because in the Hebrew there is set 'urob' — that is, 'and the breadth' — 'ast-hesre' — which, if it is read so, signifies 'eleven', but if instead of 'hesre', that is, 'ten', we read 'heser', it does not signify 'ten' but a pronoun —; which may seem ravings to those who have no experience of the Hebrew language. But now we are not writing disputations or orations, in which one is accustomed to play with the arrangement of words, but explanations and commentaries on the Hebrew volumes; but let us return to our undertaking. By eight or ten steps, which is a perfect number, or eleven, of which we have already spoken, one went up to the gate of the holy places, on whose fronts were columns, one on this side and another on that: he did not say 'two', lest he should run into the dual number, but 'one and one', which is a perfect number and is taken from the oneness of God — and these were its columns, of which it is also said in the Revelation: He who conquers, I will make him a column in the temple of my God, and he shall not go outside; and Peter and John are called columns of the Church, through whom we enter into the Holy of Holies, and by whose teaching is our entrance to Christ —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.41.1":["And he brought me into the temple, and measured the fronts: six cubits of breadth on this side and six cubits of breadth on that side, the breadth of the tabernacle. And the breadth of the gate was of ten cubits, and the sides (or shoulders) of the gate of five cubits on this side and five cubits on that side; and he measured a length of forty cubits and a breadth of twenty cubits. After many mysteries and various entrances and the particular features of each place, at long last the man in whose hand were the masons' line and the measuring reed brings the prophet Ezekiel into the temple, and he measured the front of the temple — for which the Seventy translated 'aelam', and Theodotion 'aelim', and Symmachus 'round about' —, and each breadth of the tabernacle was of six cubits, containing the mystery of all creatures — for in six days the world was founded —, through the knowledge of which, and of the causes and the principle of their founding, we enter into the temple of God, and from the order and constancy of creatures we know the Creator.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.41.2":["The breadth of the gate of the temple, moreover, was of ten cubits — which is a sacred and perfect number, and the one on which, in the seventh month, on the tenth day of the month, there is the fast and the propitiation; and on the tenth day of the first month, which is called Nisan, the lamb is taken for the sacrifice and preparation of the Passover. In the seventh month, therefore, tithes are offered into the granaries of God, or, as Scripture is accustomed to call them, the winepresses; whence the Apostle also says: Be enlarged, you also —, so that, after someone has entered the temple through the ten cubits of the gate, its sides on either part, or, as it is properly said in the Hebrew, its shoulders, are measured, which always refer to works — whence also: Issachar desired the good, resting among the middle lots; and he saw rest, that it is good, and the land, that it is fat: he put his shoulder to labor and became a husbandman; and in the garment of the high priest the shoulder-piece is bound together with the rational, so that works may be completed by reason, and the churchman may say: From your commandments I have understood —, which shoulders of the gate had five cubits each on either side, so that through the earthly senses and the divine, about whose difference we often write, we may enter the innermost recesses of the temple; of which that man, who was the prophet's guide, measured the length of forty cubits and the breadth of twenty cubits, or, as is better, the length and breadth of the gate, because above he had spoken only of its breadth and its shoulders. Now that twenty times forty makes the number eight hundred, which is akin to the number eight, is doubtful to no one — whence also in Ecclesiastes it is commanded: Give a portion to seven, give also to eight —, and I know that I have often discussed the mystery of that number; or else, because it is through the tribulations and straits of this world that we enter into the sanctuary of the Lord, therefore the length of the gate had forty cubits, a number that is always set in labor — whence both Moses and Elijah for forty days do not eat bread and do not drink water, and for forty years the people of Israel is harassed and tested in the wilderness and after many labors enters the land of promise, and the Lord and Savior, according to the frailty of the body he assumed, is tempted for forty days in the wilderness, so that after the victory angels might come and minister to him —. But when, after tribulations and straits, we are able to say that apostolic word: In all things troubled, but not straitened, and the prophetic one: In tribulation you have enlarged me, then a most wide entrance is opened to us, which has a breadth of twenty cubits, so that, having overcome the contests of the world and of earthly things, which itself also is a dual number, we may enter the inner places of the temple, and hear the Lord commanding the disciples: Take courage, I have conquered the world.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.41.3":["And having entered within (or into the inner court) he measured in the front (or 'aelam') of the gate two cubits, and the gate of six cubits, and the breadth of the gate of seven cubits (or and the shoulders of the gate seven cubits on this side and seven cubits on that side). And he measured its length (or of the doors) of twenty cubits and the breadth of twenty cubits before the face of the temple, and he said to me: This is the Holy of Holies. However much we advance, a greater advance always opens before us. Hence, after the entrance of the temple, we again enter within, or, as the Seventy translated, the inner court, in whose 'front' or 'ael' — for which Symmachus translated 'around the gate', Theodotion 'aelam', the Hebrew 'ul', Aquila 'on this side of the door', we 'front' —, there were two cubits, so that, according to the sense given above, through the dual number we may enter that place where the Holy of Holies is; whence also the gate itself was of six cubits, through which, having entered, we possess eternal rest, which is shown in the number seven: for which the Seventy translated: And the breadth of the gate of seven cubits on this side and of seven cubits on that side, whereas in the Hebrew and the other editions the cubits in the breadth of the gate are set down only once.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.41.4":["What follows, however: And he measured its length of twenty cubits (or, as is contained in the Seventy, forty), the breadth also of twenty cubits before the face of the temple, signifies this: that before the face of the temple and at its entrance the measure of past labor and contest always meets us; for the same meaning that the double number holds among the single numbers, the two decades have. And this must be considered: that, having entered within through the gate of two cubits in front, which has six cubits and seven in breadth on either side and twenty cubits in length and as many in the very doors of the temple, the prophet is taught what that place is, or by what name it is called: for he said, he says, to me: This is the Holy of Holies. But if before the temple, or, as Scripture names it, before the face of the temple, there is the Holy of Holies, how great a blessedness ought we to reckon there is in the innermost recesses of the temple and in its interior — of which the prophet speaks: This is labor in my sight, until I enter into the sanctuary of God; I shall understand in their last things —! But this too must be noted, that in the description of the tabernacle Sancta sanctorum [holies of holies] is said in the plural number, but here Sanctum sanctorum [holy of holies] in the singular number, so that after the holy things we may come to the Holy — just as after many songs we arrive at the Song of Songs, which is the song of all songs and singing which we are joined in the embraces of the Bridegroom —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.41.5":["And he measured the wall of the house, six cubits, and the breadth of the side, four cubits, round about the house on every side; and the sides, side to side, twice thirty-three. And there were projections which entered through the wall of the house in the sides round about, to hold and not to touch the wall of the temple. And there was a broad way going upward in a round by a spiral, and it led into the upper room of the temple by a circuit. Therefore the temple was wider in the upper parts, and so from the lower parts one went up to the upper into the middle. (But what is set down in the Seventy, and from the middle parts to the tristega — that is, 'to the third upper rooms' and 'thrael', is not found in the Hebrew; and for 'thrael' too, which I do not know what it signifies, for it is not found in the Hebrew, there is read among them 'uraithi', which both we and the others translated 'and I saw', so that the order of the testimony may follow:) And I saw in the house a height round about, the sides founded to the measure of a reed with a space of six cubits; and the breadth through the wall of the side outside of five cubits, and the inner house in the sides of the house, and between the chambers a breadth of twenty cubits round the house on every side. And the door of the side for prayer (which is not found in the Seventy), one door toward the way of the north, and one door toward the southern way; and the breadth of the place for prayer (which the Seventy likewise did not translate) of five cubits round about. And the building which was separate and turned toward the way looking to the sea, of a breadth of seventy cubits, but the wall of the building five cubits of breadth round about, and its length ninety cubits. After he came to the Holy of Holies, of which we spoke above, that venerable man measured the wall of the Holy of Holies, which, on account of the fabric of the world and all the visible creation that was founded in six days, had six cubits, and the breadth of one side, four cubits: not on one side only, but round about the house was the breadth; and the breadth held four cubits round about the house, so as to show the four elements from which all things are compacted, and especially human bodies, against which the saints, fighting and subjecting them to the power of the soul, merit to enter the inner places and to know the secrets of the Lord.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.41.6":["The sides themselves, which were round about the house and were separated from the wall of the temple by a space of four cubits, were joined to one another, so that one side touched another side and had in length not thirty-three cubits but twice thirty-three, that is, sixty-six. The Old Testament speaks in Leviticus about the birth of a male, that after one week both she who bore and he who was born fulfill thirty-three days so that they may come to purification; but if she bears a female, she is to observe a double number, that is, sixty-six; and because, in order to come to the Holy of Holies, we need not only the first birth but also the second, so that, born in the flesh, we may be reborn in the spirit, therefore not the number sixty-six but twice thirty-three is set down, so that each birth may be owed to God as author and to his clemency, and that the sides of the house which support the building of the temple may go round it with a double wall. And what follows according to the Hebrew: And there were certain projections which entered through the walls of the house through the sides round about, to hold and not to touch the wall of the temple, signifies this: that holy men in the multitude of believers break forth through the wall of the temple, that is, through all the sides round about, and hold the foundations of the Church, and yet do not touch the wall of the temple, content only to have seen and from afar to have adored the unspeakable mysteries: for now we see through a mirror in an enigma, and we cannot touch and comprehend the fullest truth.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.41.7":["But as for: There was a broad way going upward in a round by a spiral, and it led into the upper room of the temple by a circuit, and: Therefore the temple was wider in the upper parts, and so from the lower parts one went up to the upper and the middle, I think it is clear to the reader that the things below are always narrower, in fasts, in sleeping on the ground, in restraint of diet, and that little by little, as we climb to the heights, a wider way is opened to us and that is fulfilled which is written: In tribulation you have enlarged me; and by the round and the spiral we climb to the upper room of the temple, a figure which among all σχήματα [shapes, figures] is approved as the more beautiful even by the philosophers of this world, since the sky and the sun and the moon and the other stars and the point of the earth, and in human bodies too the eyes, like other stars, and the shape of the head, which is the receptacle of all the senses, and the rounded fingers and thighs and arms display this roundness; moreover, the upper room of the temple, to which one climbs from narrower things to higher, I think is that which Elijah and Elisha had in the volume of Kings, and which our Tabitha — that is, δορκάς and doe — possessed in the Acts of the Apostles, she who by good works had climbed to the heights; but the apostle Peter, upon whom the Lord made firm the foundations of the Church, went beyond the upper room and came to the roof — which is more expressively called δῶμα in Greek — that is, the sun-terrace of the roof —, and came to know the mysteries of the Church previously unknown to the world; the Savior of the human race also made the Passover in an upper room, and in a large and broad upper room, purged of all filth and spread and prepared for the spiritual banquet, where he handed on to his disciples the mystery of his body and blood and left us the eternal festival of the spotless lamb. And what he adds: And from the middle parts to the tristega (that is, third upper rooms) and 'thrael', seems to me fit to be marked beforehand with an obelus: for what need is there to discuss doubtful things that are not written, when we ought to apply ourselves to the things contained in the books of the Hebrews?\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.41.8-10":["There follows: And I saw in the house a height round about, the sides founded to the measure of a reed with a space of six cubits; and the breadth through the wall of the side outside of five cubits; and between the chambers (which Symmachus calls ἐξέδρας [exedras]) a breadth of twenty cubits round the house, by which numbers it is shown that from this world and from the earthly senses and from the dual number of two decades, which is referred to twenty, we may merit to ascend not only into the Holy of Holies but also into its upper rooms, and may always keep in memory that through the six days of our creation and through the five senses and through the twenty cubits of breadth we have ascended to the upper room of the temple and, leaving behind earthly lowliness and the sense of the letter, have crossed over to the summit of the Church, and rejoice in the fellowship of the Holy Spirit.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.41.11":["And the door, he says, of the side for prayer facing the way of the north: From which evils blaze forth upon all the earth, and which the Lord promises he will drive away from us, saying: And him who is from the north I will drive away from you; and beautifully at the door facing the north is the place of prayer, so that according to the Apostle we may pray without ceasing, and say with Jeremiah: Let not the pupil of my eye be silent, either desiring to avoid present evils or giving thanks for past ones — for as long as we are in the tabernacle of this body, we groan and say: Wretched man that I am! Who will deliver me from the body of this death? —; but toward the southern way too there was a door, and it also, according to the Hebrew, had a place of prayer and extended five cubits round about — for after the cold of the north let us cross over to the warmth of the south —, and yet once more we seek a place of prayer: at the door of the north, that we may escape dangers; at the southern one, that we may give thanks for past things and that the possession of victory may be secure for us; and the five cubits in this place too indicate the divine senses, through which we climb from earthly things to higher.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.41.12":["And the building, he says, which was separate and turned toward the way looking to the sea, of a breadth of seventy cubits, so that, after labors and dangers and the waves and shipwrecks of this world, and the seventy years of which Jeremiah writes, and Daniel and Zechariah, we may attain the true and lawful sabbath of seven decades, and there may be eternal rest for us; and that through the breadth of five cubits we may come to the length of ninety cubits, the age at which Sarah bore a son by the promise, she who at ninety years, that is, of nine decades, gave birth to Isaac, joined to Abraham the hundred-year-old, who held the mystery of ten decades — what this number signifies, the Scripture that follows will show.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.41.13-15":["And he measured the length of the house, a hundred cubits, and the building that was separate and its walls, of a length of a hundred cubits; but the breadth before the face of the house and of that which was separate toward the east, a hundred cubits. And he measured the length of the building facing that which was separate at the back, ἔκθετας (that is, 'projections') on either side, a hundred cubits; and the inner temple and the vestibules of the court, the thresholds and slanting windows, and ἔκθετας round about on three sides facing each threshold, and it was laid with wood in a ring round about; and earth up to the windows, and the windows closed above the doors. And up to the inner house and outside, over the whole wall round about, within and without, by measure. And Cherubim and palms were wrought, and a palm between Cherub and Cherub; and a Cherub had two faces: the face of a man next to a palm on this side, and the face of a lion next to a palm on the other side, fashioned through all the house round about. From the ground up to the upper parts of the gate Cherubim and palms were carved on the wall of the temple. A four-cornered threshold, and the face of the sanctuary, aspect facing aspect. Of the wooden altar the height three cubits, and its length two cubits, and its corners and its length and its walls of wood. (LXX: \"And he measured ÷ facing ⁚ the house, a length of a hundred cubits, and the remaining parts and the separating parts and its walls in length a hundred cubits; and the breadth facing the face of the temple and the remaining parts opposite, a hundred cubits. And he measured the length of the separating part facing the face of the remaining parts which were behind the back of that house, and the remaining parts on this side and on that, a length of a hundred cubits; and the temple and the corners and the 'aelam' outside laid with wood, and windows made in the manner of a net, three, for giving light, so that one might see through them; and the house and the things that were next to it laid with wood round about, and the pavement, and from the pavement up to the windows, and the windows opened in threefold manner so that one might look out through them. And up to the inner and the outer house and over every wall round about, within and without, measure; and carved Cherubim and palms between Cherub and Cherub: the face of a man toward the palm on this side, and the face of a lion toward the palm on that side, every wall carved round about: from the pavement up to the ceiling Cherubim and palms carved. And the sanctuary and the temple opened by four corners. Facing the face of the holy things a vision like the appearance of a wooden altar, its height three cubits and its length two cubits and its breadth two cubits, and it had horns, its base also and its walls of wood.\") The word 'facing', which the Seventy translated at the beginning of the testimony, where it is written: And he measured 'facing' the house, is not found in the Hebrew, and therefore it must be marked beforehand with an obelus, so that it may read more rightly and more truly: And he measured the house, a length of a hundred cubits, and the rest. There are described, moreover, the Holy of Holies and the inner temple, which after ninety cubits is said to have a hundred cubits on three sides: the perfect number, namely, of ten decades, so that we may serve God with mind and speech and works, remembering that apostolic example in which he says: That we may be able to know what is the breadth, and length and height and depth. But what the Hebrew calls 'the separate building and its walls', the Seventy render 'the remaining parts' 'and the separating parts', which do not cling to the walls of the temple but have walls near at hand and their own.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.41.15-16":["The temple itself also and its corners and the 'aelam' (that is, πρόπυλον or 'vestibule') were laid with wood outside, namely with wisdom, which Scripture records as the tree of life set in paradise. The windows also were made in the manner of a net, like lattices, so that they were closed not with transparent stone nor with glass, but with wood cut in openwork and worked in wavy patterns — for which the Hebrew has 'slanting windows' —; and they were on three sides of the temple only, namely to the right and the left and behind the back, that is, toward the south and the north and toward the west; but the eastern side had no windows, because the entrance itself let clear light in within, and all the interior of the temple was filled with the light of the doors, so that through the individual windows and the openings of the lattices one could look inside. And not only were the outer parts laid with wood, but so great was the abundance of wood round about that both the pavement and, from the pavement up to the windows, everything was joined with wood; and these windows, according to the Seventy, opened in threefold manner, so that, when they were opened, a threefold understanding might be shown in the temple of God — according to what is said elsewhere: But you, write them threefold —; and one might look out through them not only inward but outward, so that we may recognize the inner things as invisible and the outer things as visible; so that one shows the mind, another the flesh, one the spiritual understanding, another the simple history.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","Forgive, reader, the difficulty, and grant pardon to a poor understanding: for through windows that are slanting and made in the manner of a net and always closed, we can scarcely send the eye of our heart in as far as the inner house, so that all that we discern we see in shadow and image — and let us cry out with the Apostle: O the depth of the riches of the wisdom and knowledge of God! How inscrutable are his judgments, and unsearchable his ways! And who has known the mind of the Lord? except him who is the angel of great counsel and can open his mysteries to the worthy —. Now at Rome they call Ἔκθετας 'solaria' [sun-balconies], jutting out from the walls of upper rooms, or 'maeniana' [balconies], after him who first invented them; some of the Greeks call them ἐξώστρας [projecting galleries].\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.41.17":["And over all the walls round about, both within and without, there were measures of their own: for nothing was made by God without reason and measure in the temple of God, and especially in the Holy of Holies.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.41.18-19":["Carved also were Cherubim, no doubt on the wood reaching from the pavement up to the windows, and the carvings were so skillfully wrought that they seemed not carved but applied. 'Cherubim' means 'multitude of knowledge'; therefore within, in the interior of the temple, after the multitude of knowledge, palms also are carved — in which is the sign of victory, as the Apostle says: But I press on toward the appointed goal, toward the prize of the upward calling of God in Christ Jesus —, which palms: Palms were between Cherub and Cherub, so that each Cherub was fenced in by the flanks of two palms; and this Cherub by no means had four faces, as we read at the beginning of this prophet, namely of a man and of a lion and of a calf and of an eagle, but only two, that is, of a man and of a lion, of which the first pertains to reason, the next to fury — which is more expressively called θυμός in Greek —: but by fury we mean not that which is next to perturbation and vice, but that which firms up the softness of the mind and makes the spirit of the one fighting stronger; for at the beginning we are subject also to desire, which is referred to the calf because of the works of the earth, and some things are carnal and others spiritual, the last of which is assigned to the eagle; but when we have entered the Holy of Holies and come into possession of the interior of the temple, we need nothing else but reason and fortitude, of which the one is referred to sense and prudence, the other to perseverance of spirit.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.41.20-21":["Every wall, therefore, round about the temple, from the pavement up to the ceiling, had Cherubim and palms carved with wonderful art; after these the sanctuary was opened by four corners facing the face of the holy things: for first we have the multitude of knowledge, through which we gain the victory over the enemy, and afterward the sanctuary is opened to us, and the temple, which has four corners, a most firm station, and one that leans toward no side; for this is the nature of the measure of a quadrangle, that it stands on a stable base and, in a mystical number which is clear to the prudent reader, possesses the four elements, of which all things consist, solid and perpetual.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.41.22":["And all things looked toward the face of the sanctuary; before which sanctuary, or in which sanctuary, there was: The appearance of a wooden altar three cubits in height and two cubits in length and two cubits in breadth, which joined together make seven cubits. This altar had horns, and both its base and its walls, that is, its sides, were wooden: in which the human tongue cannot unfold the mysteries of Holy Scripture: how an altar on which fire was to be kindled, namely the table of incense, suffers nothing from the fire but, so to speak, becomes purer than fire; for just as the works of the saints — of which the Apostle writes: The fire will test what sort each one's work is — do not perish but are shown purer through fire, so too the wood of the altar, which is of the trees of paradise, is not burned by the neighboring fire but is rendered purer — nor is it a wonder to believe this of the sanctuary and the interior of the temple and the altar of incense, since even ἀμίαντον [amiantus, asbestos] — it is a kind of wood, or one having the likeness of flax — is found the cleaner the more it has burned —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","And he spoke to me: This is the table before the Lord. And there were two doors in the temple and in the sanctuary; and in the two doors on either side there were two little doors each, which folded into one another; for there were two doors each on either side of the doors. And there were carved on the very doors of the temple Cherubim and sculptures of palms, just as they were also fashioned on the walls; wherefore the timbers also were thicker on the front of the vestibule outside. Above which were slanting windows, and the likeness of palms on this side and on that in the little shoulders of the vestibule, along the sides of the house and the breadth of the walls. (In this place the edition of the Seventy disagrees much with the Hebrew truth. Hence I will point out only this: that for 'thicker timbers' they translated σπουδαῖα, that is, 'necessary' or 'worthy of care', and for 'slanting' windows, 'hidden', and what is placed at the end of the testimony: And he measured on this side and on that the ceilings of the lintel — or of the vestibule — along the sides of the house, compacted — or joined, which they called ἐζυγωμένα, for which we can render 'of equal weight' —.) That man, therefore, who had brought the prophet into the innermost recesses of the temple, and among other things had shown him the wooden altar, which had corners, and horns according to the Seventy, and wooden walls, said to him: 'This altar which you behold: This very one is the table before the Lord', which in the likeness of the bush burns and is not consumed — of which the holy man speaks to the Lord: You have prepared a table in my sight against those who afflict me —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.41.23":["Two also doors were in the temple and in the sanctuary itself, through which the mysteries of both Testaments are shown.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.41.24":["And in the two doors on either side there were two little doors each, which folded into one another, so that we may have both the spiritual understanding in the history and the truth of the history in the tropology; each of these needs the other, and if one is lacking, perfect knowledge is wanting. What follows, however: There were two doors each on either side of the doors, is clear according to the letter: for in larger dining halls two doors are wont to adjoin each other and be joined together, so that the greater entrance is closed or opened not by two huge leaves but by four smaller ones.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.41.25":["On which doors of the temple (or of the Holy of Holies) were carved Cherubim — of which we spoke above — and sculptures of palms, so that after the multitude of knowledge victory might be granted to those who enter; and this sculpture of palms was also on all the walls of the temple. And he gives the reasons why Cherubim were carved on the doors themselves and likenesses of palms fashioned: Whence also the timbers were thicker in the vestibule of the gate outside, so that they might have firmness and also be able to receive carving; and beautifully, according to the spiritual understanding, the doors are firmer and carved, so that they may have both firmness and beauty, lest anyone like Uzziah should be able to burst into the Holy of Holies and claim the priesthood for himself.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.41.26":["Above which doors there were slanting windows (or hidden), so that the very light that was given within might not have perfect knowledge, nor a clear light open to all, but for most might be hidden in part — for now we see in an enigma, and we do not yet know as we ought to know; but when that which is perfect has come, then what had been in part will be destroyed —. And there was a likeness of palms on this side and on that: elegantly, in the interior of the temple and in the Holy of Holies, he did not put 'right and left', lest he seem to say anything sinister in things that are great and secret, but 'on this side and on that' — according to what is written in the Gospel: Whoever strikes you on the right cheek, offer him the other also. Could he not have said 'and the left'? But when the right is struck, another right is offered, because in a holy man both are right, both are perfect —. And: In the little shoulders of the vestibule, along the sides of the house and the breadth of the walls (for which the Seventy translated: In the ceilings of the 'aelam' — that is, πρόπυλον [gateway] — and the sides of the house of equal weight — or measure —), by which it is shown in a hidden way that the posts of the lintel or of the vestibule — for this is what the little shoulders seem to signify —, and the sides of the house, and the breadth of the walls are all full of reason and measure, and that nothing is found in the temple of the Lord which has been established without measure and wisdom.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.42.1":["And he brought me out into the outer court by the way leading to the north; and he brought me into the chamber that was opposite the separate building, and opposite the house facing toward the north. In front of the length, a hundred cubits, of the door of the north, and of the breadth fifty cubits, opposite the twenty cubits of the inner court, and opposite the pavement laid with stone of the outer court, where there was a portico joined to the threefold portico. And before the chambers a walk of ten cubits in breadth, and looking toward the inner parts of the way of one cubit; and their doors toward the north, where the chambers were lower in the upper parts, because they supported the porticoes which projected from them, from the lower and from the middle seats of the building. For they were three-storied, and they did not have columns as were the columns of the courts; therefore they projected from the lower and from the middle, from the ground, by fifty cubits. And the outer peribolus alongside the chambers which were in the way of the outer court before the chambers; its length of fifty cubits, because the length of the chambers of the outer court was fifty cubits, and the length before the face of the temple a hundred cubits. And beneath these chambers there was an entrance from the east for those entering into them from the outer court. In the breadth of the peribolus of the court which was opposite the eastern way, in front of the separate building, and there were chambers before the building. And a way before their face according to the likeness of the chambers which were in the way of the north; according to their length so also their breadth, and all their entrance and likenesses and their doors according to the doors of the chambers which were in the way looking toward the south; a door at the head of the way which was before the separate vestibule, for those entering by the eastern way. (LXX: \"And he brought me out into the outer court ÷ toward the east ⁚ opposite the gate of the north; and he brought me in, and behold, five exedrae next to the wall of the building and beside the separate building toward the north, and opposite, a hundred cubits of length toward the north and of breadth fifty cubits, laid out like the gates of the inner court and like the columns of the outer court, in order opposite the face of the threefold portico. And opposite the exedrae a walk of ten cubits of breadth, for a hundred cubits of length — into the inner way of one cubit —; and their doors toward the north. And the walks of the upper rooms likewise, since columns projected from the lower columns, and the space was similar. And the porticoes were threefold, and they did not have columns like the columns in the outer parts; therefore they projected from those which were below and in the middle, from the ground — of fifty cubits —. And the light outside, like that of the exedrae of the outer court which looked opposite the exedrae of the north, in length fifty cubits; for the length of the exedrae looking onto the outer court was fifty cubits, and these are opposite their own face: all of a hundred cubits. And the doors of these exedrae toward the eastern entrance, so that one may go in by them from the outer court according to the light which was at the beginning of the walk — toward the south, opposite the midday and the face of the remaining and separate building. And the exedrae and the walk opposite their own face according to the measures of the exedrae and the way toward the north, and their length and their breadth and all their exits and entrances and lights and the doors of the exedrae opposite the midday, and the entrance from the beginning of the walkway to the light at the space of a reed, and so that one might go to the east by them —.\") This which the Seventy translated, 'toward the east', is not found in the Hebrew, and it is plain that it is superfluous — for how is he led out to the outer gate 'toward the east', when there follows 'opposite the gate of the north'? —; and that which we set down both above and in this place, And he brought me into the chamber that was opposite the separate building, and opposite the house facing toward the north, for which there stands in the Seventy, And he brought me out, and behold, five exedrae joined to the remaining building and beside the separate one toward the north: for 'separate', which Aquila and Symmachus translated, the second edition of Aquila and Theodotion set down the very word 'gazera', rendering it in the same way in all places; and many other things which are set forth in the present place disagree between the Hebrew and the Seventy not only in order but also in number and in the rendering of the words, so that, if we wished to linger over each one and to seek out and explain the reason for their difference, we would stray far from our purpose; and I had wished, out of despair and because of the magnitude of the matter, to pass over the present passage in silence, but I judged it better to say something, whatever it might be, than to say nothing at all, taking up that Socratic saying: 'I know what I do not know'; for it is a part of knowledge to know what you do not know. After the prophet, then, had contemplated more carefully the things that were within, the man in whose hand were the cord and the reed led him out into the outer court by the way leading to the north, which he had already seen above as well, before he penetrated the inner parts — but we see in one way when we have perfect knowledge, in another at the beginning of instruction; and it is necessary that one who has beheld the inner parts according to the same form and measures and hidden mysteries should also see the things that are outer —; and he was led out into the chamber — or, as Symmachus says and the Seventy translated, 'exedra', and Theodotion παστοφόριον, which is rendered 'cell' — that was opposite the separate building; but what the Seventy added in place of the chamber, 'five exedrae', is not found in the Hebrew —, which chamber was opposite the separate building — concerning which we said above, 'gazera' —: and it was separate, receiving those who came from the north, and it was opposite the house facing toward the north, that is, looking indeed toward the parts of the north but not situated in the north, so that the passage might be easier for those who were leaving behind the cold of the north, from which evils blaze up over the earth — we read in the book of Numbers that the tribes of Dan and Naphtali and Asher, the sons of Bilhah and Zilpah, the handmaids of Rachel and Leah, held their camp on the northern quarter —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.42.2-3":["Now too, in front of the length of the north door there were a hundred cubits, and of breadth fifty, so that, by the square and perfect number made of ten decades, and by the number made of seven hebdomads, which is the number of remission and breaks out into the beginning of unity, that is, of the ogdoad, the priestly spaces in the temple of God might be shown; and that which is joined according to the Hebrew, Opposite the twenty cubits of the inner court, for which the Seventy translated, They were laid out like the gates of the inner court, has this sense: that the number one hundred and the number fifty of the outer court have the same force as the number twenty of the inner court: for if you reckon four times five in the number twenty, you will find both testaments, so that the law is held within the Gospel and the Gospel is born from the root of the law; in place of this number the Seventy translated 'the likeness of the inner court', perhaps fearing to place in the inner court the number twenty — in which gifts are offered to Esau. There follows: And opposite the pavement laid with stone of the outer court, where there was a portico joined to the threefold portico, for which the Seventy translated: And like the columns of the outer court, set in order opposite the face of the threefold porticoes: and it signifies that the pavement of the outer court was laid with living stone, so that the footsteps of the priests might be defiled neither by the dust of summer nor by the mud of winter — whence also the Lord, about to ascend to the Father, washes the feet of the apostles, so that with clean and purified footsteps they may climb to the kingdoms of heaven, and he commands the apostles that, whatever city they have entered and they have not received them, they should shake off the dust of their feet, so that, namely, they may suffer nothing of earthly gifts from them to remain with them —; and not only was the pavement laid with stone, but there was also a portico joined to the threefold portico: one portico protecting the priests from rain and heat and joined to the threefold portico, and it shows the mystery of the Trinity, which, though it is divided into the names of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit, is nevertheless joined to itself in one divinity.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.42.4":["There was also before the chambers — or exedrae, or cells — a walk ten cubits in breadth, looking toward the inner parts of the way of one cubit — and what was added by the Seventy, For a hundred cubits of length, is superfluous, because it is not found in the Hebrew; and in setting it down they removed what is found in the Hebrew, Looking toward the inner parts of the way of one cubit, which we translated from the Hebrew truth —: and it signifies that before the doors of all the chambers, or exedrae and cells, there was a walk having a breadth of ten cubits, which is itself also a perfect number, either because of the Decalogue of the law or because of the mysteries of the Gospel: for if you go from one through two and three to the fourth number, the number ten is produced, and the number of the Gospels —, in whose breadth the priests walk about, stepping before each of the chambers, only in such a way that they always look toward the inner parts of that way which leads to one cubit, namely to the worship of one divinity — as the Son says to the Father: I have revealed your name to men. For this is the way looking toward the inner parts which will be in the Gospel: I am the way and the life and the truth, for no one comes to the knowledge of the Father except through the Son —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.42.5":["There follows: And their doors toward the north, where the chambers were lower in the upper parts, because they supported the porticoes which projected from them, from the lower and from the middle of the building. For they were three-storied, and they did not have columns as were the columns of the courts; therefore they projected from the lower, and from the middle, from the ground, by fifty cubits. In what is joined, 'their doors', there is understood 'of the exedrae' or 'of the chambers', of which there was talk above; which chambers were lower in the upper parts, that is, in the upper rooms — the upper rooms which Elijah and Elisha climbed, and the Lord with his disciples when he was about to keep the Passover, and Tabitha, who was raised up both by the faith of the apostle and by the merit of her own virtues; these are the upper rooms of which it is also written in the psalm: He who waters the mountains from his upper rooms: for unless someone has been made a mountain and has risen to higher things, he will not be watered by the rains of the Lord, he who according to the diversity of merits is watered from the first and the second and the third upper room; these very chambers too, that is, the treasuries of the riches of the Lord, are lower in the upper parts: for the more exalted someone has been, the more is he cast down in humility with the apostle, who says: I who am not worthy to be called an apostle, because I persecuted the Church of God —; and these chambers were lower in the upper rooms because they supported the porticoes which projected from them, from the lower and from the middle of the building: he gives the reasons why the chambers placed in the upper rooms were lower: 'Because they supported' he says 'the porticoes which enjoyed the freedom of the air, and projected from the lower and from the middle of the building', so that the second upper room stood out above the pavement of the inner house and the third upper room was higher than the second, and the higher someone climbed, the loftier the upper room he would use, which grew by humility — as the Lord says: Whoever wishes to be greater among you, let him become the least of all —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.42.6":["For they were not carried by other columns, as we read of the columns of the courts, but they projected from the lower, and from the middle, from the ground, by fifty cubits, the number of the remission of all debts — and these are the three-storied things of which it is commanded: Describe it threefold with counsel and knowledge, so that you may answer words of truth to those who are set before you —. The lower and outer things, then, need columns, but those which are higher and inner have no use for the help of columns, that is, of another.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.42.7-8":["After this it is said: And the outer peribolus alongside the chambers which were in the way of the outer court before the chambers; its length of fifty cubits, because the length of the chambers of the outer court was fifty cubits, and the length before the face of the temple a hundred cubits. 'Peribolus' signifies 'a wall', which was outer and surrounded the chambers in the way of the outer court, having in length fifty cubits, a number of whose mystery we have often spoken; moreover, the length before the face of the temple was by no means of fifty cubits, as before the chambers, but of a hundred cubits, so that after the remission of all sins we may await perfect rewards in the number one hundred; for 'peribolus' or 'wall', which in Hebrew is called 'gader', the Seventy translated 'light', which comes from outside and is to illumine the eyes of our heart, since our nature is not sufficient for the light that dwells in the understanding to have the perfect brightness of knowledge, unless it enters from outside through the grace of God: which first illumines the eyes of our heart and makes the whole outer court, which extends with a breadth of fifty cubits, grow bright; but afterwards, when we have come before the face of the temple, we are to receive the perfect rewards in the number one hundred.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.42.9-10":["And there was beneath the chambers an entrance from the east for those entering into them from the outer court. For we must possess the riches of God, and from the outer court find the entrance of the east beneath the chambers, and through the height of the peribolus which looks toward the way of the east come to the building which is set apart for the saints; before which are the chambers.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.42.11-12":["And in that very way there is the likeness of the chambers which were in the way of the north: for although we arrive at the entrance of the east, yet the likeness of the greater things is in the lesser, and we cannot arrive at the light of the east except by the way of the north, that is, unless through lesser and humbler things we strive toward greater and higher ones. And in the entrance itself the length was like the breadth, that is, the same measure in a square, and every entrance and the likenesses and the doors had the same measure and likeness as the chambers which were in the way looking toward the south: for, leaving behind the cold of the north, we arrive through the east at the south, in which the bridegroom reclines at midday and dwells in full light; and at the head of that same way, that is, the eastern one, which lies open to those who enter, there is a door — which is not opened unless it has been opened by him who says, I am the door, and who has the key of David —, to the vestibule set apart for the virtues of the saints; and that which receives those coming from the north we cannot reach. It is superfluous to discuss each word of this passage according to the Seventy, since both the things that have been said are not free of obscurity, and those about which we keep silent are nearly similar to those that we have said.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.42.13-14":["And he said to me: The chambers of the north and the chambers of the south which are before the separate building, these are the holy chambers in which the priests eat who draw near to the Lord in the Holy of Holies: there they shall place the Holy of Holies and the offering for sin and for trespass, for the place is holy. And when the priests have entered, they shall not go out from the holy places into the outer court, and there they shall lay up their garments in which they minister, because they are holy; and they shall be clothed with other garments, and so they shall go forth to the people. That man who was the prophet's guide, after he had led him into the outer court and shown him all the things which the preceding discourse has narrated, the chambers too, or exedrae, in which he lingered a very long time, said to him: 'These are the chambers, or exedrae and cells, toward the north and toward the south which are before the separate building, and they are called the holy chambers in which the priests eat who draw near to the Lord in the Holy of Holies' — from which we learn that there are many differences of foods which the priests are or are not permitted to eat, and that of those very ones which it is permitted to take as food, they are not taken in all places, nor by all, nor at every time —; I think the chambers of the north and of the south are those which contain either the simplicity of the history or the mysteries of spiritual understanding, so that through the north we may come to the midday: for neither is the letter to be read and the foundations of the history to be laid in such a way that we do not come to the summits, nor are roofs to be set on top of a most beautiful building in such a way that the foundations are by no means solid; and the priests who eat in the storerooms in which many riches are contained are those who draw near to the Lord — of whom Scripture testifies that Moses drew near to the Lord and the rest could not approach —: in the building of the mystical temple and in the mystery of the Church there are many priests and apostolic men who approach the Lord, and not in just any place, but in the Holy of Holies — and what is said according to the Seventy, The sons of 'Sadoc' [Zadok], which means 'righteous', is not found in the Hebrew —. 'There' he says 'the priests shall place the Holy of Holies and the offering, that is, the victim of the holocaust, and for sin and for ignorance', so that they may not only offer the perpetual sacrifice, but may know how to appease the Lord according to the diversity of times and the quality of the sin and of the ignorance: 'Because the place itself is holy', and the priestly dignity dwells in it, which is able to make petition for the rest: 'But when one must go forth to those who cannot penetrate the inner sanctuaries of the temple nor know the secrets of divine knowledge, let the priests go out' he says 'outside to them into the outer court, by no means with those garments with which they had been clothed within' — namely, the bowels of mercy and the Lord Savior, of whom it is written: Put on Christ Jesus; nor do they use those words of which God speaks: My mystery is for me and for mine; nor should they incur what the Savior forbids, saying: Do not give what is holy to dogs, nor cast your pearls before swine —, 'but they shall lay down their garments within, with which they are clothed when they minister in the inner sanctuaries of the temple: for they are holy, and they ought not to be brought out to those who do not have perfect holiness; and they shall receive other garments, and so they go forth to the people': 'So' he says 'they shall speak to the people, as the people is able to hear' — whence also the apostle spoke to the Corinthians — among whom fornication was heard of, and such fornication as is not even among the nations —: I gave you milk to drink, not food — that is, solid food —: you were not yet able to take it; to whom again he says: My little children, with whom I am in travail again until Christ be formed in you: for to little ones and sucklings solid food, which befits men of mature age, not only does no good but kills them —. But that which was added by the Seventy, Therefore the priests shall not go out into the outer court, so that those who offer may always be holy, let us mark beforehand with an obelus, because it is not found in the Hebrew.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.42.15":["And when he had completed the measures of the inner house, he brought me out by the way of the gate which looked toward the eastern way, and he measured it on every side round about (or the likeness of the house round about in order). And he measured toward the east wind (or and he stood behind the back of the gate looking toward the east) with the reed of measure round about five hundred reeds, by the reed of measure all around. And he measured toward the north wind five hundred reeds by the reed of measure all around. And toward the south wind he measured five hundred reeds by the reed of measure round about. And toward the west wind he measured five hundred reeds by the reed of measure. By the four winds he measured its wall on every side round about, the length of five hundred cubits, and the breadth of five hundred cubits, dividing between the sanctuary and the place of the common people. It should be known that, after the east wind and the north, in the south wind and the west the order in the Seventy is reversed: for they put first the west — that is, the sea — and afterwards the south, whereas among the Hebrews the south wind is put first and afterwards the west. And what Scripture now says, And when he had completed the measures of the inner house, he brought me out by the way of the gate which looks toward the eastern way, shows that all the things which were said above, both outside and inside and in the inner parts of the temple — that is, the Holy of Holies —, belong properly to the building of the temple, and of the inner temple. Hence there now follows: He brought me out by the way of the gate which looked toward the eastern way, and he measured it on every side round about (or the likeness of the house), by which it is shown that it is not the house itself but the likeness of the house that is seen — because: Now we see through a mirror in an enigma; but when that which is perfect has come, then that which is in part will be destroyed; whence also Moses in the tabernacle, and Solomon in the building, made not the truth of the tent and of the temple but a likeness and figure —, so that through these things which are lesser and earthly we might be able to understand those which are in the heavenly places and in the spiritual building.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.42.16-20":["Now the man who had led the prophet out, by no means into the outer court but by the way of the gate which looked toward the eastern way, measured first toward the east wind — that is, toward the eastern quarter — five hundred reeds all around, second toward the north, third toward the south, fourth toward the sea — that is, toward the west —: by the four winds, namely, the wall round about, as much in length as in breadth, that is, in a square: which wall had in all two thousand reeds: but if the reed was of six cubits and one παλαιστῇ [handbreadth] — which παλαιστής is the sixth part of a cubit —, it is clear that the outer wall had round about two thousand reeds: which make twelve thousand three hundred and thirty-three cubits and a third, out of the two thousand — let the prudent and diligent reader read the book of Joshua son of Nun, and he will find how this measure is kept in the suburbs; whence also the Legion of demons chose this number in the choking of the swine, so that what by the Lord's command was set apart for those who serve God might be turned, in the opposite direction, to the perdition of those who pursue a life worthy of mud and filth. And the whole wall, which is outer and has a most spacious length and breadth in a square, contains the number of the apostles, that is, of twelve thousand — so that a thousand may be assigned to each apostle —, and yet it would not come to the measure of the fullness of Christ and of the perfect man unless the παλαιστής too is joined to it: which makes three hundred and thirty-three cubits and a third part of a cubit: by which things the mystery of the holy and venerable Trinity is shown, which girds and walls in all things and renders the inhabitants of its temple most secure; whence also Noah's ark has a length of three hundred cubits and a height of thirty cubits, which is finished off in one cubit — and that fifty cubits in breadth are added there signifies, as we have often said, the mystery of remission —; whence also the Lord came to baptism at thirty years of age; and this very prophet at the beginning of his volume sets down the thirtieth year; and this thirtieth and three-hundredth number is completed by the addition of three and a third part —. But what, through simplicity of rendering, while we paid too little attention in the speed of dictation, both the Seventy have and our translation, Its wall on every side round about, the length of five hundred cubits, and the breadth of five hundred cubits, the Hebrew language does not contain, but simply 'the length of five hundred and the breadth of five hundred', so that 'of reeds' is understood — just as was said four times above: toward the east wind and toward the north and toward the south and toward the sea, five hundred 'reeds' each —, and that the wall of the individual sides was measured by him who held the reed — and that the number five hundred, because it is close to fifty, pertains to the remission of all sins, not only the old Scripture but also the words of the Savior in the Gospel show, when he says: There were two debtors to a certain moneylender; one owed five hundred denarii, and the other fifty —; and the wall divides between the sanctuary and the place of the common people, from which we understand that the whole description of the temple above was assigned to the ministries of the priests — who are the sons of Zadok —, but that this wall — which is extended over so great a space and goes around everything — separates the sanctuary and the place of the common people.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.43.1-3":["And he brought me to the gate that looked toward the eastern way, and behold, the glory of the God of Israel was entering by the eastern way, and its voice was like the voice of many waters, and the earth shone from his majesty. And I saw a vision according to the appearance that I had seen when he came to destroy the city, and the appearance according to the aspect that I had seen beside the river Chebar, and I fell upon my face. And the majesty of the Lord entered the temple by the way of the gate that looked toward the east. And the spirit lifted me up and brought me into the inner court; and behold, the house was filled with the glory of the Lord. And I heard one speaking to me from the house, and the man who was standing beside me said to me: Son of man, the place of my throne and the place of the footprints of my feet, where I dwell in the midst of the sons of Israel forever; and the house of Israel shall no more pollute my holy name, they and their kings, by their fornications and by the ruins of their kings and in the high places: who built their threshold beside my threshold, and their posts beside my posts, and there was a wall between me and them, and they polluted my holy name by the abominations that they committed; for which reason I consumed them in my wrath. Now therefore let them put far away their fornication and the ruins of their kings from me, and I will dwell in the midst of them always. (LXX: \"And he brought me to the gate that looked toward the east ÷ and he led me out ⁚, and behold, the glory of the God of Israel was coming by the way of the east, and the voice of the camp like the voice of many redoubling, and the earth shone out like lightning from the glory round about. And the vision which I saw, according to the vision that I had seen when I was entering to anoint the city, ÷ and the vision of the chariot that I saw ⁚ according to the vision that I had seen over the river Chebar; and I fell on my face. And the glory of the Lord entered the house by the way of the gate that looked toward the east. And the spirit took me up and brought me into the inner court, and behold, the house full of the glory of the Lord. And I stood, and behold, a voice from the house of one speaking to me, and the man was standing beside me and said to me: Son of man, you have seen the place of my throne and the place of the footprint of my feet, in which my name shall dwell in the midst of the house of Israel forever; and the house of Israel shall no more defile my holy name, they and their leaders, in their fornication and in the murders of the leaders in their midst, when they set my threshold in their thresholds, and my posts beside their posts, and they made my wall as if joined between me and themselves, and they defiled my holy name by their iniquities which they were doing; and I crushed them in my fury and in slaughter. And now let them cast away their fornication and the murders of their leaders from me, and I will dwell in the midst of them forever.\") First we must speak about the variety of the translation. What the Seventy put, And he led me out, is not found in the Hebrew — for if he had been brought to the gate that looked toward the east, what need was there for him to be led out, since he had been brought in for this very purpose, to see what was happening at the eastern gate? —; next, where it is said, There was a voice (doubtless God's) like the voice of many waters — which John too confirms in his Revelation —, the Seventy put: And the voice of the camp like many redoubling; third, in the Hebrew we have: A vision according to the appearance that I had seen when he came to 'destroy' the city: and there came that one who at the beginning of this prophet was clothed in linen and had an inkhorn at his loins, together with six others, to 'destroy' the city, not to 'anoint' it, as the Seventy translated; and they, fourth, said: And the vision of the chariot that I had seen, which is not found in the Hebrew. Let us speak briefly on each point, as far as the difficulty of the explanation allows, lest, while we strive for brevity, the veil not only of Moses but also of the prophet Ezekiel remain upon us who desire to contemplate the truth of the Lord with unveiled face. The glory, therefore, of the God of Israel enters by the eastern way, by which it had also gone out when the city of the Lord was struck by fury; and it enters, or rather returns, to it because he had shown the temple of the Lord built upon the mountain. And yet what is said in what follows is much more: The spirit lifted me up and brought me into the inner court; and behold, the house was filled with the glory of the Lord: for here only the glory of the God of Israel enters, but there it is said that the fullness of the glory of the Lord was in the temple — of which Isaiah too writes: I saw the Lord upon a throne high and lifted up, and the house full of his glory — by which, when with unveiled face we contemplate the glory of the Lord, we are re-formed into the image of the Creator. The voice of God also was like the voice of 'many' waters: that is, of all the peoples in the whole world, as John the evangelist interprets; or: Like the voice of a 'camp' and as the voice of many redoubling: so that he might know the mysteries of the army of God — understanding which, Jacob too called the name of that place Camps; of which it is also written elsewhere: The chariot of God is ten thousandfold, thousands of those rejoicing —; and one 'voice' is spoken of, of the camp and of the multitude, because of the one accord in the praise of God; and the voice 'is redoubled' of those singing to the Father and the Son and the Holy Spirit: Holy, holy, holy, Lord God Sabaoth; the earth is full of his glory. There follows: And the earth shone from his majesty, which was properly fulfilled at the coming of Christ, when the sound of the apostles went out into all the earth and their words to the ends of the world; and it is fulfilled daily in believers and will be completed to perfection when this corruptible has put on incorruption and this mortal has been clothed with immortality. But what is added, And I saw a vision according to the appearance that I had seen when he came to destroy the city, for which the Seventy translated, When I entered to anoint the city, though it is clear according to the Hebrew, according to the Seventy it holds very great obscurity: how Ezekiel entered to 'anoint the city' for his prophecy — unless perhaps we say this, that the rebuke of the prophet is the anointing of the city, and the anointing with the oil of gladness, priestly and royal, and those who have willingly received it and been willing to hear become anointed ones of the Lord — of whom it is written: Do not touch my anointed ones, and do no evil to my prophets —; but that one can, according to the ἀναγωγὴν [anagogy, the higher sense], anoint the city — of which it is written: Glorious things are spoken of you, city of God — who has seen the vision of the chariot and of the mysteries of God which Ezekiel beheld over the river Chebar — which signifies 'burden and weight': for what is 'heavier' than the Babylonian rivers, by which David sat and wept when he remembered Zion — of which it is written: For the form of this world is passing away —? and I think that nothing in the confusion of this age is lasting, but that all things pass away and flow —; and whoever has considered these things will fall upon his face, understanding how far he is from the majesty of God, and will bend his knees to the Father in the name of Jesus Christ.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.43.4-6":["'And when', he says, 'I had fallen down, the majesty of the Lord entered the temple by the way of the gate that looked toward the east; and at once the spirit lifted me up — for lying down I was not able to go on — and brought me into the inner court — for I had fallen outside —; and behold, I who had earlier beheld the glory of the God of Israel coming by the eastern way saw his house filled with the glory of the Lord, and I heard the voice of one speaking to me from the inner house' — what it said, Scripture does not relate, unless perhaps that saying of the apostle: And I heard unspeakable words which it is not lawful for men to speak —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.43.6-8":["But that man, he says, who was standing beside the prophet spoke to him; and we plainly understand him to be the Lord: for to whom could what follows apply, Son of man, the place of my throne and the place of the footprints of my feet, where I dwell in the midst of the sons of Israel forever, except to him who dwells in the Church in the midst of the sons of Israel who see God? — and 'he dwells forever', not as in the temple of Solomon, for a time; and 'his place' is that of which it is written: And his place was made in peace, which surpasses all understanding; and 'the place of the footprints of his feet', as the apostles say: Let us worship in the place where his feet stood, and he said 'stood' beautifully: for in the Church the feet of the Lord stand, in the Synagogue they walk and pass by —; and so that we may know that this is said of the Church, there is joined: And the house of Israel shall no more pollute my holy name, which properly pertains to those who, being of holy life, abide in the Church; but who those are who earlier polluted the holy name of God, he sets out more plainly: They and their kings, the people as much as the priests, by their fornications, by which they committed fornication away from God, and by the ruins of their kings, who vainly take to themselves the royal name through pride; finally there follows: And in the high places — for an arrogant mind offends God, a humble one calls forth mercy —: who, after pride, or rather through pride, built their threshold beside the threshold of God, and their posts beside his posts.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.43.8":["Let the silly women laden with sins, who are carried about by every wind of doctrine, always learning and never arriving at the knowledge of the truth, hear these things according to the letter: that it is a mind joined to God, not nearness to the churches and a dwelling close to God, that calls forth clemency upon itself, and that rather the indignation of the Lord is stirred up when there is an unworthy inhabitant in consecrated places. They built, he says, their threshold beside my threshold, so that there was no difference between sacred and profane, and their posts beside my posts, so that there was one entrance for laity and priests — whence the apostle too says: But let a man prove himself, and so approach the body and blood of the Lord —; and to make what he was saying weightier, he joins in the following verse: And there was a wall between me and them, so that a very short wall divided the priestly sacrifices and the places of the sacraments of the body and blood of Christ. And they polluted, he says, my holy name by the abominations that they committed. What does nearness of dwelling profit, and a wall standing between our cell and the altar of the Lord, when in the things we do in secret, which it is shameful even to speak of, the name of the Lord is defiled and polluted? I think this: that no one pollutes the name of the Lord except him who has seemed to believe in his name and to be reckoned by his title; and as he who earlier believed in Christ takes the members of Christ and makes them members of a harlot, so he pollutes the name of the Lord who earlier received faith in his name — otherwise the heathen and the Jew, since they are polluted and defiled, or rather defilement itself and pollution, cannot pollute and defile the name of the Lord; but those pollute it to whom it is said: Through you my name is blasphemed among the nations —; for these causes, therefore, I consumed them in my wrath, because they did these things that we have described.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.43.9":["And yet the merciful Lord again commands the prophet to bid them remove their former fornications far from themselves, and to abandon the ruins of their kings and princes and drive them away, not so much from themselves as from God; and he at once promises the rewards of good work, saying: And I will dwell in the midst of them — of whom it is said in the Gospel: There stands in the midst of you one whom you do not know —; and he will dwell not for a short time, as in the Synagogue, but forever, which is borne out in the Church of Christ.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.43.10":["But you, son of man, show the house of Israel the temple, and let them be confounded by their iniquities, and let them measure the structure, and let them blush for all that they have done. The figure of the house and of its structure, its exits and entrances, and its whole design, and all its precepts, and its whole order and all its laws, show them; and you shall write it in their sight, that they may keep all its distributions and its precepts and do them. This is the law of the house on the summit of the mountain: all its bounds round about are the Holy of Holies; this therefore is the law of the house. (LXX: \"And you, son of man, show the house of Israel the house, and they shall cease from their sins, and its vision and its arrangement, and they themselves shall bear their torment for all that they have done. And you shall describe the house and its preparation, and its exits and its entrances, and its substance, and all its precepts, and all its ordinances and all its laws you shall show them; and you shall describe it before them, and they shall keep all my justifications and all my precepts and do them. And the description of the house on the top of the mountain: all its boundaries round about the Holy of Holies; this is the law of the house.\") It is no small task to demonstrate to hearers what you have beheld with the eyes of the flesh or with the mind, so that they too may seem to have seen it with you — for which reason Josephus writes that those who were sent by Joshua son of Nun to describe the land were γεωμέτρας [geometers, land-surveyors], and possessed in the proper sense the art of geometry, which belongs to philosophers —. To the house of Israel, therefore — of those who behold God with the mind — the house is shown, that is, the temple which Ezekiel beheld situated on a mountain, and not only on a mountain but, as is now said, on the summit of the mountain; and let us by no means think it to be that one which is related in the books of Kings and Chronicles to have been built by Solomon: for that one is of another order and measure, and has great diversity in every detail, and is so far inferior to the temple which is now shown to Ezekiel that not only its worshipers and sacristans, but even its builder Solomon himself sinned and offended the Lord — although afterward he did penance, writing the Proverbs, in which he says: At last I did penance, and I looked back so as to choose discipline —; but this one, which is shown to Ezekiel and through Ezekiel to the house of Israel, is such that whoever has beheld it with the mind ceases from his iniquities, not from some but from all — or, as is contained in the Hebrew: is confounded —, and bears his torment for all that he has done — and he bears the torment of his iniquities who has ceased to commit his past sins —. And it greatly profits him who has sinned before to see the house of God and to know the principles of its whole structure — let him both desist from sinning and, having a longing for it, say to the Lord: 'Lord, I have loved the beauty of your house, and the place of the dwelling of your glory', and again: 'One thing I have asked of the Lord, this will I seek: that I may dwell in the house of the Lord all the days of my life, when I have begun to live and to be with Christ, who is the life of believers' —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.43.11":["There follows: Its exits and entrances, 'of the house' being understood — the exits, that is, of those who go out to those who are outside, and the entrances of those who through the discipline of teachers penetrate to the inner parts —. 'And all', he says, 'its design (or substance, as the Seventy translated: which pertains not so much to the nature of the house as to its furnishings and riches), and all the precepts, and the whole order and all the laws of the temple you shall show them, who have borne the torment of their iniquities and have ceased, or been confounded, over the things they had done before; and for this reason you shall show them, that they may keep all that has been commanded: for it profits nothing to know the design of the house and all its distributions — of which it is written: Many mansions with the Father, and again: He set the bounds of the nations according to the number of the sons of Israel (or of the angels of God) — unless you do what has been commanded'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.43.12":["But the house, and the law of all the precepts of God, and the city which is established on the summit of the mountain, must be believed to be that one of which it is written: A city set upon a mountain cannot be hidden, and: The rush of the river gladdens the city of God: which plainly refers to the Church of God, and it is situated on that mountain which is at the peak of all mountains and by which the prince of Tyre was wounded; and all the bounds and boundaries of this house are the Holy of Holies — in that house, that is, in the tabernacle that was made by Moses and in the temple that was built by Solomon, only the inner parts, where the Cherubim were and the mercy seat and the ark of the covenant and the table of incense, were called the Holy of Holies; but in this house, which is shown to Ezekiel and which is established on the summit of the mountain, all its boundaries are reckoned among the Holy of Holies —; and what is added, This is the law of the house, pertains either to past matters, of which we have already spoken, or to those which are to be spoken of afterward.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.43.13":["And these are the measures of the altar in the truest cubit, which had a cubit and a handbreadth (or palaesten [handbreadth]): in its bosom was a cubit, and a cubit in breadth, and the delimitation up to its lip, and round about one handbreadth: this also was the trench of the altar. And from the bosom of the earth to the lowest ledge two cubits, and the breadth of one cubit; and from the lesser ledge to the greater ledge four cubits, and the breadth of one cubit. And the 'ariel' itself of four cubits, and from the 'ariel' upward four horns. And the 'ariel' twelve cubits in length by twelve cubits of breadth, squared with equal sides. And the ledge fourteen cubits of length by fourteen of breadth in its four corners; and the crown round about it of half a cubit, and its bosom of one cubit round about; and its steps turned toward the east. (LXX: \"And this is the measure of the altar in the perfect cubit — a cubit and a παλαιστῆς [handbreadth] —: its bosom in height by a cubit, and a cubit of breadth, and a 'gisus' along its lip round about of one handbreadth. And this is the height of the altar: from the depth of the beginning in its bosom up to the great mercy seat which is beneath, of two cubits, and the breadth of a cubit; and from the lesser mercy seat up to the greater mercy seat four cubits, and the breadth of a cubit. The 'ariel' of four cubits, and from the 'ariel' up to the upper parts of the horns one cubit. And the 'ariel' itself had twelve cubits in length, and twelve cubits in breadth, of four corners on each of its sides. And the mercy seat of fourteen cubits in length by fourteen cubits in breadth on its four sides; and its 'gisus' round about, encircling it by half a cubit, and its circuit by a cubit round about; and its steps looking toward the east.\") It is written: The abyss and wisdom, who will search them out? and: O the depth of the wisdom and knowledge of God!: whose depth is profound, and who will find it? It is a great matter if we wish to compare the temple of Solomon with this temple, and all its parts and those of the tabernacle; and since that is most difficult, for the present let us compare, in a small part, the altar which was shown to Ezekiel with the altar which we read of in Exodus: there an altar of five cubits in length and five cubits in breadth, that is, square, and of three cubits in height is described; here, departing from the five senses, it tends toward unity, in which a perfect or truest cubit is set down, to which a handbreadth is joined, that is, a παλαιστής, having, as we said above, the sixth part of a cubit; and in its bosom, that is, the altar's, there was a cubit, namely the one that received the things which were consumed by fire, and in its breadth another cubit, so that the inner parts too, that is, the secret places and spaces of the altar which are shown in breadth, might be bounded within one cubit. And as for what follows, And the 'delimitation' up to its lip — for which Aquila, Symmachus and Theodotion put 'boundary' —, the Seventy again translated 'gisum' — of which word, as I already said before, I cannot know the reason, nor say confidently to what language it belongs, except to advise the reader of this, that where I have now translated 'delimitation', above I rendered 'crown'; and this is briefly shown, that round about the altar itself, that is, at its margin and circle, there was a lip bent back and, as it were, a crown in the manner of a lily, having very great beauty in appearance —; which 'delimitation', or 'gisus', extended round about in the breadth of one handbreadth, or παλαιστοῦ.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.43.14":["But the 'pit' of the altar, or 'depth' and 'height', for which in the Hebrew 'gab' is set down, was of two cubits from the bosom of the earth up to the lowest ledge, or up to the greatest mercy seat, and its breadth of one cubit: for the lower parts, deep and clinging to the earth, are contained in the dual number — which was also set down for the unclean animals —, but the upper parts, reaching to the ledge or mercy seat — which Theodotion called by the Hebrew name itself, 'azara' [ledge, enclosure] — have a breadth of one cubit, so that the dual number passes over to the single, and the good of marriage, which is lower, attains to the blessedness of unity. And as for what follows, And from the lesser ledge up to the greater ledge four cubits, and the breadth of one cubit — for which in the Seventy is set down: And from the lesser mercy seat up to the greater mercy seat, and Theodotion in both places translated 'azara' —, this is to be understood: that the lesser mercy seat is the stone cut from the mountain without hands, and the great mercy seat is the stone itself which grew into a great mountain and filled the whole world; or, according to another sense, the lesser mercy seat is when he emptied himself, taking the form of a servant, and the great mercy seat when he received back the glory which he had with the Father before the world was made: for first we come to know the humbler things of Christ, and so we arrive at the height of his divinity; and yet the mercy seat of both natures, that is, of the lesser and the greater, pertains to the mystery of one altar. And between the two mercy seats, the lesser and the greater, there were four cubits, signifying the four elements of the world, which themselves too arrive at the breadth of one cubit — and this is to be noted, that in the measure of the altar the first cubit in breadth is coupled to none but is perfect in its own unity, while the second cubit of breadth grows to the top after two cubits, and the third cubit, after the lesser and greater mercy seat, tends through the four elements to the mystery of one cubit: so that the one and the second and the third cubit in the altar of the Lord are by no means said to be first, second and third, but one and one and one —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.43.15":["And the 'ariel' itself was of four cubits, and from the 'ariel' upward four horns (or, as is said in the Seventy, and from the 'ariel' up to the upper parts of the horns one cubit): 'ariel', as most suppose, means 'my light is God' — about which we have spoken more fully in the prophet Isaiah too, where it is written: Woe to you, city 'ariel', which David stormed (or surrounded) —, but, as I think, 'lion' or 'strong one of God' — a name which refers properly to the altar, in which there is either the illumination of God, or the lion and his strength, as Jacob says to Judah: A lion's whelp, my son, Judah; to the prey, son, you have gone up. Resting you lay down as a lion, and as a lioness: who will rouse him? —; and it is of four cubits and has four horns, or the upper parts of the four horns are bounded by one cubit: so that the measure of the Gospels and the strength running out into the whole world may be shown under the illumination of God and the strength of the lion, and may pertain to the one cubit of the divine confession.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.43.16":["And so that the obscurity of the present passage may little by little become clearer, there follows: And the 'ariel' twelve cubits in length by twelve cubits of breadth, square with equal sides, which no one doubts pertains to the twelve tribes which are written in the Revelation of John, and to the number of the apostles, about whose mystery I remember having spoken above; along the four sides — the sides of the world — twelve cubits each together make forty-eight cubits — of the priestly cities —, so that, with these as it were foundations distributed through the whole world, the strength of the Church may be made firm.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.43.17":["Furthermore: The ledge (for which the Seventy translated mercy seat, Theodotion, as above, 'azara', Symmachus περίβολον, that is, 'circuit') was of fourteen cubits of length by fourteen of breadth, in its four corners; and the 'crown' round about it of half a cubit (for which again the Seventy translated 'gison'), and the bosom (or, according to Symmachus, 'consummation') of it (and 'perfection') of one cubit round about: this covertly shows that our Lord, who is truly called the mercy seat, not only for our sins but for the whole world, came through fourteen generations from Abraham to David, and again through another fourteen to the captivity of Jeconiah, and by the mystery of the same number descended to earthly things, so that in the third tesseriscedecade [set of fourteen], and by the mystery of the divine number, he might save the four corners of the world — of which it is written: Many will come from the east and from the west and from the north and the south, and will recline with Abraham, Isaac and Jacob in the kingdom of heaven —. And what follows, And the crown (or gisus) round about it — 'of the mercy seat' being understood — had half a cubit, and the 'bosom' of the mercy seat itself (or consummation and perfection, which Symmachus translated περιγραφήν [outline, circumscription]) had one cubit, signifies this: that both sinners and the righteous are saved by the propitiation of the Lord — as the apostle Paul says: We have been reconciled to God in the blood of his Son —; and of sinners it is said that they have the measure of half a cubit round about: who nevertheless are saved by the mercy of the Creator — according to what is written in the psalm: For nothing you will save them —; of the righteous: that they are saved in the one number, single and perfect and imitating the one divinity — as the apostle says: God was in Christ reconciling the world to himself —. But as for what is set down at the end of this testimony, And its steps turned toward the east, by the steps of this mercy seat we ought to understand either the twenty-four books of the Old Testament, which had harps in the Revelation of John and crowns on their heads, or the mystery of the Father and the Son and the Holy Spirit, in which true propitiation is given to us; and to speak more openly: the number of the steps is left uncertain for this reason, that with however much zeal we may have been able to climb to higher things, we may think that we are still to be placed in the inner things, and may ponder that saying of the psalm: They will go from strength to strength.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.43.18":["And he said to me: Son of man, thus says the Lord God: These are the rites of the altar on whatever day it shall have been built, that a holocaust may be offered upon it and blood poured out. And you shall give to the priests and Levites who are of the seed of Zadok, who approach me, says the Lord God, that they may offer me a calf of the herd for sin. And taking some of its blood, you shall put it upon the four horns, and upon the four corners of the ledge, and upon the crown round about, and you shall cleanse it and expiate it. And you shall take the calf that has been offered for sin, and you shall burn it in a separate place of the house outside the sanctuary. And on the second day you shall offer a he-goat of the goats without blemish for sin, and they shall expiate the altar as they expiated it with the calf. (LXX: \"And he said to me: Son of man, thus says the Lord God: These are the precepts of the altar on the day on which it shall have been made, that holocausts may be offered upon it and blood poured out upon it. And you shall give to the priests and Levites who are of the seed of Saddoch, and who draw near to me, says the Lord God, that they may minister to me a calf of the oxen for sin. And they shall take of its blood, and shall put it upon the four horns of the altar, and upon its base round about, and they shall cleanse and expiate it. And they shall take the calf for sin, and it shall be burned in a separate place of the house outside the Holy Place. And on the second day they shall take two kids of the goats without blemish for sin, and they shall expiate the altar as they expiated it with the calf.\") After he had shown the prophet the altar and its cubit and two cubits, and again a cubit and four cubits and, thirdly, a cubit, and also twelve cubits by another twelve, and the four horns of the altar, and the 'ariel' of twelve cubits by twelve cubits, and the ledge, that is, the mercy seat, of fourteen cubits by fourteen cubits, and also its crown and bosom of half a cubit and one cubit round about, and the steps toward the east whose number is uncertain, the man in whose hand were the reed and the cord spoke, and he teaches him how, once the work of the altar is completed, the altar ought to be expiated and consecrated.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.43.19-22":["And first a victim is offered, and it is given to the priests of the race of Levi who are of the seed of 'Sadoc' — why the Seventy name him 'Saddoch' I am not able to know. And 'Sadoc' means 'righteous' —. And there is offered, according to the Seventy and Theodotion, a 'calf', according to Symmachus a 'bull', which in Hebrew is called 'phar' — and many passages of the Scriptures, and especially the epistle of Barnabas, which is held among the apocryphal scriptures, name him who was sacrificed for us a 'calf' —; and a he-goat of the goats is offered on the second day: whence also those who are to keep the Passover take from the flock a lamb and a kid, and those who could not keep the first Passover keep it in the second month: of which the one pertains to the righteous, the other to the penitent. But if the translation of the Seventy pleases us — who said: And on the second day they shall take two kids without blemish for sin —, what we are about to say is bold indeed, but nevertheless the opinion of others must be set down plainly, who say that two kids without blemish were offered at the altar of the Lord after the passion of the Lord: James and Stephen, of whom the one was of the number of the apostles, the other was the chief of the seven chosen for the ministry of the Lord — this is the James who saw the Savior transfigured on the mountain, who with the Lord and Peter and John beheld the daughter of the ruler of the synagogue rising, who in the κατάλογῳ [catalogue, list] of the apostles — in which the names are joined in pairs — is placed before his brother: him Herod killed, and as it were sacrificed as a kid without blemish on the second day after the passion of the Savior; Stephen too, whose wisdom and teaching no one could resist, and who saw the Son of man standing at the right hand of the Father, and said, Lord Jesus, receive my spirit, the Jews overwhelmed with stones; and as the apostle names certain of the believers the firstfruits of Achaia and of Asia, so these too were the firstfruits of the martyrs, whom the confession of Christ afterward crowned —. And this is to be noted, that the calf is offered whole as a holocaust, and the fatness of its body is consumed by divine fire — for even if we had known Christ according to the flesh, yet now we no longer know him according to the flesh —, but James and Stephen, who were offered on the second day, or the he-goat, which according to the Hebrew is related to have been offered, is indeed offered as a victim, but the holocaust is reserved to the preeminence of the Lord. And that the blood is taken — of which Peter speaks: We have been redeemed from our vain manner of life of paternal tradition by the precious blood of Christ; and the apostle Paul teaches: You have been redeemed with a price, and in another place: Making peace through the blood of his cross, whether on earth or above the heavens — signifies this: that by the blood of the Savior the four horns of the altar are purified — that is, the four regions of the world —, and the crown of the mercy seat, or the bases round about, is sprinkled, so that all things may be cleansed and the propitiation may be firm. And for this reason, on the second day, either two kids — of which we spoke above — or a he-goat is taken, because it is an animal always hastening to the heights and suffering no danger on precipices, and it finds a way there where for the other animals there is destruction — whence also in the Greek language 'a flock of she-goats and he-goats' is called 'the dwelling of the lofty', that is, 'αἰπόλιον', as if 'αἰπυ-πόλιον': since αἰπύ signifies, as it were, 'lofty', and πολεῖν 'dwelling' —. Furthermore, the altar of Christ is expiated with blood, so that the prayers of the saints may reach God clean. This too is to be noted, that according to the Hebrew Ezekiel himself, as a priest, is ordered to take the calf and to make the holocaust and to sprinkle its blood round about, on the altar as well as on the corners and the crown, or base; but the Seventy translators show that other priests do this, of whom it was said: And you shall give to the priests and Levites who are of the seed of Zadok, who approach me.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.43.23-24":["And when you have finished expiating it, you shall offer a calf of the herd without blemish, and a ram of the flock without blemish; and you shall offer them in the sight of the Lord, and the priests shall cast salt upon them, and shall offer them as a holocaust to the Lord. For seven days you shall make a he-goat for sin daily, and a calf of the herd and a ram of the flocks without blemish they shall offer. For seven days they shall expiate the altar and cleanse it, and they shall fill its hand; and when the days are completed, on the eighth day and onward the priests shall make your holocausts upon the altar, and those which they offer for peace; and I will be appeased toward you, says the Lord God. (LXX: \"And when you have completed the expiation, they shall offer a calf of the oxen without blemish, and a ram of the sheep without blemish; and you shall offer them before the Lord, and the priests shall sprinkle salt upon them, and shall offer them as holocausts to the Lord. For seven days you shall make a kid for sin daily, and a calf of the oxen and a ram of the sheep, without blemish, they shall make for seven days; and they shall expiate the altar and cleanse it, and they shall fill their hands; and they shall complete the days; and it shall be, from the eighth day and onward the priests shall make your holocausts upon the altar, and for your salvation; and I will receive you, says the Lord God.\") After the altar and its measures on the top of the mountain had been shown, and again its expiation and consecration had been shown to the prophet through one calf without blemish and a he-goat or two kids, of which we have referred the first to the Lord Savior and the two following to the apostles and ministers, then, lest in the consecration of the spiritual altar, which properly pertains to the Church, the Law and the Prophets should seem excluded, for this reason, once the altar has been consecrated, a calf without blemish is taken, and a ram; and they are offered in the sight of the Lord, and the sons of Zadok, that is, the priests of the righteous, sprinkle salt upon their heads: so that both the Law and the Prophets may be seasoned with the savor of the Gospel; nor is there any sacrifice — according to the command of the Law and the interpretation of the apostle, who says: Let your speech be seasoned with salt — which lacks salt. And both are offered as a holocaust to the Lord, so that the fatter body — the letter, which is signified in the Law — and the cloud of prophecy may, by the fire of the Lord — that is, by the Holy Spirit, of whom Paul says: Fervent in spirit — be converted into a spiritual and fine substance. Do we wish to know more openly who is the calf of the herd without blemish, and the ram of the sheep with the purest fleece? Let us understand Moses and Elijah — of whom the first was the meekest among all men who dwelt on the earth, the other in ardor of faith was like Moses, whence he also dared to say: I alone am left —. But that in the Hebrew it is written, 'You shall offer' a calf, and in the Seventy, 'They shall offer', the priests, raises no question — for Ezekiel himself too, to whom these things are said, is of the number of the priests, of full and perfect age: and the grace of prophecy increased his priestly rank; and Moses and Elijah, that is, the Law and the Prophets, are seen on the mountain with the Lord, announcing to him what he was going to suffer in Jerusalem —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.43.25-26":["And when the altar has been expiated, for seven days a he-goat or kid is offered for sin daily, and a calf of the herd and a ram of the flocks without blemish, so that through these sacrifices over seven days the altar may be expiated to perfection: in the seven days is shown the sabbath rest which, according to the apostle, is reserved for the people of the Lord, in which we hope for eternal and true rest and may by no means do the servile work of sins; and in the he-goat and the calf and the ram are shown three general offenses to which the whole race of mortals is subject: for we sin either in thoughts or in speech or in deed — thought is referred to the ram, which is the first of all sins and from which the other two sins are born; and the kid, or he-goat, to utterance and speech, which always disputes about loftier things; but deeds are properly assigned to the calf, which is bound over to the plowshare and to labor and to earthly works —. These, therefore, without blemish, we ought to offer to God through the seven days of the true and perfect sabbath, and to expiate the altar, so that our prayer may reach God clean.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.43.26-27":["And what is added, And they shall cleanse it, and shall fill its hand, as both the Hebrew and the other translators rendered it, signifies this: that the gifts of the altar itself too, for whose expiation the sacrifice is offered, must be filled up, just as it is offered for the priests and the people and the high priest, lest anything should seem to have stood empty in the sight of the Lord; for which the Seventy put: And they shall cleanse it, and shall fill their own hands, so that 'the priests' is understood, who, when they have been full of good works — for this is what full hands signify —, once the sabbath is past, may come to the eighth day of the resurrection — and say with the apostle: We have risen with Christ —, and beyond the eighth may tend toward heavenly things, and offer for us holocausts, or those offerings which are for the peace of our sins and for our salvation, so that, through the fire of the Holy Spirit, all that we think, speak and do may be converted into spiritual substance, and the Lord, delighted with sacrifices of this kind, may become placable toward us.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.44.1-3":["And he turned me back to the way of the gate of the outer sanctuary which looked toward the east, and it was shut; and the Lord said to me: This gate shall be shut, and it shall not be opened, and no man shall pass through it, because the Lord God of Israel has entered (or will enter) by it, and it shall be shut for the prince: the prince himself shall sit in it to eat bread before the Lord; he shall enter by the way of the vestibule (that is, 'aelam') of the gate, and by its way he shall go out. (Where it is written in the Hebrew: It shall be shut for the prince, the Seventy translated: And it shall be shut, because the leader himself shall sit in it.) There are many gates which Scripture mentions in the description of Ezekiel's temple, both within and without; the preceding discourse also ran through the shape of the altar, its consecration and its sacrifices. That being finished, he comes to the gate of the outer sanctuary which looked toward the east, and it was shut; and at once that man who was the prophet's guide and was showing him everything spoke to him: 'This gate, which you see shut, will always be shut, and it will not be opened, and none of men will pass through it'; and he gives the reasons why it is always shut: Because the Lord of Israel has entered (or will enter) by it, and it shall be shut — according to the Hebrew — for the prince (whom the Seventy translated 'leader'): who, the prince (and leader, that is, 'nasi') shall sit in it to eat bread before the Lord, and: By the way of the vestibule of the gate he shall enter, by the same he shall go out.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.44.2-3":["— That the eastern gate beyond the bounds of the world is always shut and by no means lies open to human sight, the Gospel of John proves — when it says: No one has ever seen God except the only-begotten Son, who is in the bosom of the Father; he has made him known, as if he had said in other words: And it shall be shut for the prince: the prince alone shall sit in it to eat the bread 'of perfect and consummate knowledge': for No one knows the Son except the Father, and no one knows the Father except the Son, and he to whom the Son wills to reveal him —. Some fittingly understand the shut gate, through which the Lord God of Israel alone enters, and the leader for whom the gate is shut, as the Virgin Mary, who remained a virgin both before giving birth and after giving birth — for both at the time when the angel was saying: The Holy Spirit will come upon you, and the power of the Most High will overshadow you; and that which will be born of you will be called Holy, the Son of God, and when he was born, she remained a virgin forever —, to confound those who think that after the Savior's birth she had sons by Joseph, taking occasion from his 'brothers' who are so called in the Gospel: on which little question I know that in my youth, at Rome, I wrote a book of no great size against Helvidius, a heretic of that time —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.44.2":["What, then, is this gate which is always shut, and by which the Lord God of Israel alone will enter? Surely it is the one of which the Savior speaks in the Gospel: Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites, and: Woe to you, doctors of the law, woe, who take away the key of knowledge: you yourselves do not enter, and those who are entering you hinder — of this, under the name of a book, Isaiah writes: The words of this book shall be like sealed words; when you give it to a man who does not know letters, and say to him: Read; he will answer you: I do not know letters. And they will give it to a man who knows letters, saying: Read; and he will say: I cannot read, because it is sealed: and this is the book whose seals no one can loose and open, neither in heaven nor on earth nor under the earth, except him of whom it is said in the Revelation of John: Behold, the lion of the tribe of Judah has conquered, the root and offspring of David, to open the book and loose its seals: for before the Savior assumed a human body and humbled himself, taking the form of a servant, the law and the prophets and all the knowledge of the Scriptures were shut, paradise was shut; but after he hung on the cross and said to the thief: Today you will be with me in paradise, at once the veil of the temple was torn and all things were opened; and with the veil taken away we say: But we all, beholding the glory of the Lord with unveiled face, are transformed into the same image from glory to glory. But if all things have been unveiled — for in Christ, according to the word of Paul, all things are unveiled — how is it: The gate shall be shut, and it shall not be opened, and no man shall pass through it? From this we learn — even though we may have come to the highest knowledge, by comparison with the divine knowledge — that now we know in part and understand in part: But when that which is perfect has come, then that which is in part will be done away. Hence in another place too the Apostle himself says that he is imperfect and again perfect, which, if it lacks interpretation, seems to be a contradiction; for he says: Not that I have already received, or am already perfect. Brethren, I do not reckon that I have laid hold; but one thing: forgetting what lies behind, stretching forward to what lies ahead, I pursue the appointed goal, toward the prize of the upward calling of God; and when we would think that by his own profession he is not perfect, and that he is seeking rather than has found what is true, he says that not only he himself but others with him are perfect: As many then as are perfect, let us be of this mind; and the sense of this place is: 'In comparison with the rest of men, who have no care for the knowledge of the Scriptures nor of the mysteries of God, I confess that I am perfect; but as regards the knowledge of the divine majesty, I now see in an enigma, and through mist and darkness, and I say with the prophet: Your knowledge has become wonderful to me; it is too strong, and I shall not be able to reach it' —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.44.3":["This gate, then, which is shut to all — for No man shall pass through it —: It shall be shut for the prince (or for the leader), and will be reserved for his coming: who shall sit in it to eat bread before the Lord — of which he himself declares in the Gospel, saying: My food is to do the will of him who sent me, and to accomplish his work —; he himself is prince and high priest according to the order of Melchizedek, both victim and priest, who in the sight of the Father eats the heavenly bread with us and drinks the wine — of which he speaks in the Gospel: I will not drink of this fruit of the vine until I drink it new in the kingdom of my Father: namely in that kingdom of which the Apostle also writes: The kingdom of God is within you —; and the gate will be shut: for no one can know the mysteries of the Lord's passion and of his body and blood as the majesty of the thing deserves; and our prince is of such goodness and mercy that, although he alone sits in the gate which is shut and eats bread before the Lord, he wills to have many sharers of his table and banquet — and he says: Behold, I stand and knock; if anyone opens to me, I will come in to him, and I will sup with him, and he with me —; but he alone eats bread before the Lord, because his substance and divine nature is set apart from all the substances of created things. He himself enters and goes out through the same gate of the vestibule, because he is both within and without — that is, poured into all things and poured around all things —: entering through the gate to bring in with him those who cannot enter without his teaching and help, and going out to bring in others again, and to speak to those who do not grasp the more difficult things.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.44.4-5":["Therefore, because he had been cast down in humility, it is no longer the man but the Lord who says to him: Son of man, set your heart, and the rest: at the completion of the tabernacle and at the building of the temple which was erected by Solomon the glory of the Lord appeared, which afterward was done away by comparison with the glory of the Gospel — as the Apostle says: For what was glorified has not been glorified in this respect, because of the surpassing glory. For if what is done away was through glory, much more what remains is in glory —; and we must beware of thinking the doing away of the former glory to be its abolition, but must understand it thus: After that which is perfect has come, that which was in part will be done away — as if you were to compare a torch to the rays of the sun, or the light of a torch to a little lamp; for which reason it is also said of John the Baptist: He was a lamp shining in the house; but after the sun of righteousness came, the light of the lamp was hidden, as the prophet and Baptist himself says: He must increase, but I must decrease —; and the prophet fell on his face lest, while he longed to discern more than human frailty can behold, he should lose even the very light of his eyes; hence the Lord calls him more familiarly son of man, and commanded him to set his heart, and see with his eyes, and hear with his ears — for first the mind must be opened to understand what is said, second the eyes of the heart must be directed, of which it is said to Abraham: Lift up your eyes and see the stars of heaven, third one must hear with those ears of which the Savior says: He who has ears to hear, let him hear —, so that he may be able to understand all the ceremonies of the temple and its ordinances, and finally to set his heart on the 'ways' of the temple — because there are various entrances to it —, or the 'way' of the temple — it signifies the order of the ceremonies —, and the exits of the sanctuary. It is, then, a preface and a preparation of the prophet for understanding what he is afterward to learn concerning the order of the temple. And this too must be noted: that in the region of this world, which lies in the evil one and is set in the cold of the north, the order of the heavenly ceremonies is shown to us.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.44.4":["And he brought me by the way of the north gate in front of the house; and I looked, and behold, the glory of the Lord had filled the house of the Lord, and I fell on my face. And the Lord said to me: Son of man, set your heart, and see with your eyes, and hear with your ears all that I say to you concerning all the ceremonies of the house of the Lord, and concerning all its laws; and you shall set your heart on the ways of the temple, through all the exits of the sanctuary. The man who is the prophet's guide, and through whose showing he learns everything in the temple, after he had shown him the gate shut, never to be unbarred and yet open to him who entered through shut doors, leads the prophet to the way of the north gate, which itself too was in front of the house: no doubt this signifies the temple; and when the prophet, from directly opposite, had seen the house of the Lord full of the glory of the Lord — the same, namely, which he had beheld facing him when he was placed in the region of the north —, at once he fell on his face, not enduring to bear the majesty of the glory of the Lord.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.44.6":["And you shall say to the house of Israel that provokes me: Thus says the Lord God: Let all your crimes suffice you, house of Israel, in that you bring in alien sons (or foreigners) uncircumcised in heart and uncircumcised in flesh, to be in my sanctuary and to pollute my house; and you offer my loaves, the fat and the blood, and you dissolve my covenant (or you pass over my precept) in all your crimes. And you have not kept the precepts of my sanctuary, and you have set keepers of my observances in my sanctuary for yourselves. When he says: Let all your crimes suffice you (or your iniquities), he exhorts to repentance for former sins, lest we add sins to sins and prepare fuel for the fire to come — but let us hear what Isaiah says: Woe to those who draw sins as with a long cord, and iniquities as with the yoke-strap of a calf —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.44.7-8":["And it must be read thus, and the order of the reading rendered so: 'Since you bring alien sons, uncircumcised in heart and in body, into my sanctuary and pollute my house, you dare to offer loaves and fat and blood — the mystic sacraments — and you do not understand what you have dissolved, and that you have transgressed my covenant in all your crimes and impieties; and that you have not kept the precepts of my sanctuary nor set keepers of my ceremonies in my sanctuary' — and the whole threat is against those who have not heeded the Apostle warning beforehand: Lay hands hastily on no one, and do not share in other men's sins —; or certainly it must be understood thus: 'You have dissolved my covenant in all your crimes and have not kept the precepts of my sanctuary, and you dare to set unworthy and alien keepers in the observance of my sanctuary — so that 'for yourselves' is understood, not 'for me', and the sense is —: who serve you and minister to you in carnal things, and for the sake of your comforts pollute my sanctuary'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.44.7":["And the first sin is that they bring in alien sons — or foreigners — uncircumcised in heart and uncircumcised in flesh, to be in the sanctuary of God and to pollute the house of the Lord — for A little leaven corrupts the whole lump, and in some copies of Proverbs there is found: Do not bring the wicked man into the tents of the righteous —. At this place the Jews and the Ebionites — who accept circumcision of the flesh — press us as to how we can explain the present passage: uncircumcised in heart and uncircumcised in flesh, meaning, that is, that after the spiritual understanding we ought also to undergo circumcision of the flesh; but — we who read Paul saying: And I testify to every man who is circumcised that, if you are circumcised, Christ will profit you nothing, and that word of Jeremiah: Behold, your ears are uncircumcised, and you would not hear, and in Exodus: Moses spoke before the Lord, saying: Behold, the sons of Israel have not listened, and how will Pharaoh listen to me? But I am slow of speech (for which the Seventy said: Ἐγὼ δὲ ἄλογός εἰμι [But I am without speech], which is better rendered in the Hebrew: But I am uncircumcised of lips) —, let us question them — nay, let us compel them to circumcise their ears and cut off their lips, that they may be seen to fulfill the Scripture —: but if they begin to interpret circumcision of the 'ears' as when we allow ourselves to hear nothing base and unseemly, and circumcision of the 'lips' as when we speak nothing indecent, let us say to them: you ought to keep the same exposition also for 'heart' and for 'flesh': we circumcise the 'heart' with the knife of God, and the foreskin is removed from our heart, when base thoughts in no way go forth from our heart — and it is not said of us: The heart of this people has grown fat, and with their ears they have heard heavily —; so the 'flesh' too is likewise circumcised, so that we by no means do the earthly works which we are compelled to do for the necessity of the body — to take food and drink and sleep and to use clothing —, which we circumcise then, if we do all these things not for pleasure and for luxury and for idleness, but for the necessity of nature and the sustenance of this body — he who drinks a little wine for his stomach and his frequent infirmities and hates drunkenness circumcises his flesh; he who sleeps as much as nature allows will hear from Solomon: If you sit, you will be without fear; if you sleep, you will sleep sweetly: and you will not fear the terror coming upon you, nor the assaults of the wicked coming upon you; and he who flees fornication and renders to his wife her due, coming together again lest Satan tempt him, will hear with the people of Israel: On this day I have taken away from you the reproach of Egypt; he will also use clothes that keep off the cold, not those that by their thinness show bodies naked —: the flesh worn down by fasts and circumcised by continence escapes the reproach of the Egyptians: Who are of great flesh — and it can say that which is read in most copies according to the edition of the Seventy: As my flesh has wasted away, in a land desert and pathless and waterless, so in the holy place I have appeared to you —. If ever, then, we wish to bring foreigners into the temple of God, let us circumcise their ears and lips and heart and all their flesh, and their eyes, taste and smell: so that we may do all things with the fear of God and with reason; let the bishops and presbyters and the whole ecclesiastical order hear this, that they may not bring in alien sons uncircumcised in heart and uncircumcised in flesh, lest they be in his sanctuary and pollute his house; for if they do this, what follows will be fitted to them: And you offer my loaves — the loaves, namely, of proposition, in all the churches and throughout the world, springing up from the one bread —, and not only the loaves but also the fat — of which it is written: He fed them with the fat of the wheat —, and the blood — which was poured out in the passion of Christ —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.44.9-16":["Thus says the Lord God: No foreigner uncircumcised in heart and uncircumcised in flesh shall enter my sanctuary, no alien son who is in the midst of the sons of Israel. But also the Levites who went far away from me in the straying of the sons of Israel, and strayed from me after their idols, shall bear their iniquity, who were in my sanctuary temple-keepers and doorkeepers of the gates of the house and ministers of the house: and they slaughtered the holocausts and the victims of the people, and they stood before them to minister to them. Because, then, they ministered to them in the sight of their idols and became to the house of Israel a stumbling block of iniquity, therefore I have lifted up my hand against them, says the Lord God, and they have borne their iniquity. And they shall not draw near to me to exercise the priesthood for me, nor shall they approach any of my sanctuary next to the Holy of Holies, but they shall bear their shame and their crimes which they committed. And I will make them doorkeepers of the house in all its service and in all that is done in it. But the priests, the Levites, the sons of Zadok, who kept the ceremonies of my sanctuary when the sons of Israel strayed from me, they shall approach me to minister to me, and they shall stand in my sight to offer me the fat and the blood, says the Lord God. They shall enter my sanctuary, and they shall approach my table, to minister to me and to keep my ceremonies. (LXX: \"Therefore thus says the Lord: No foreign son uncircumcised in heart and uncircumcised in flesh shall enter my holy places, among all the sons of foreigners who are in the midst of the house of Israel. But also the Levites who went away from me when Israel strayed from me after their own thoughts, and they shall receive their iniquities, and they shall be in my holy places ministering, porters at the gates of the house and ministers of the house: they shall sacrifice the holocausts and the victims for the people, and they shall stand before the people to minister to them, because they ministered to them before the face of their idols, and it became to the house of Israel a torment of iniquity. Therefore I have lifted up my hand against them, says the Lord God, and they shall receive their iniquity: that they may not approach me to exercise the priesthood for me, nor to offer at all the holy things of the sons of Israel nor at my Holy of Holies, and they shall bear their disgrace in the error in which they strayed. And they shall appoint them to keep the charges of the house in all its works and in all that they have done in it. The priests, the Levites, the sons of Zadok, who kept the charges of my holy places when the house of Israel strayed from me, they shall approach me to minister to me, and they shall stand before my face to offer me the sacrifice, the fat and the blood, says the Lord God. They shall enter into my holy places, and they shall approach my table, to minister to me and to observe my charges.\") I have set down the testimony according to both editions, so that by comparing them we may be able to know what the Seventy meant and what the Hebrew contains. The Seventy signify this: that foreigners uncircumcised in heart and flesh, who dwell in the midst of the sons of Israel, ought not to enter the sanctuary of God; but that the Levites, that is, the priestly rank, who went far away from the Lord in the straying of the sons of Israel and strayed after their idols, after they have received their iniquity, are to stand in the sanctuary of the Lord as temple-keepers and doorkeepers and ministers of the house, and slaughter the holocausts and victims of the people, and be in their sight to minister to them, because they ministered to them in the sight of their idols and became to the house of Israel a torment of iniquity; and that the Lord God therefore says he has lifted up his hand against them, so that they may not approach him and exercise the priesthood for him nor offer for him in the Holy of Holies, and may bear their disgrace in the error in which they strayed: so that, namely, they are appointed to the observance of the charges of the house in all its works and in all that is done in the house — but if this is so, how can it stand that the Levites — who went far away from God in the straying of the sons of Israel and strayed from him after their idols — should themselves minister in the house and slaughter the holocausts and victims of the people, and themselves stand in their sight to minister to them — for what more are the priests, the sons of Zadok, to do, who kept the ceremonies of the sanctuary of God, of whom he says: They shall approach me to minister to me, and they shall stand in my sight to offer me the fat, and the rest —? —. But the Hebrew has this sense: 'No foreigner uncircumcised in heart and flesh shall enter my sanctuary, even though he seems to be with the people of Israel. But the Levites, that is, the priestly rank, who went far away from me in the straying of the sons of Israel and followed idols, shall bear their iniquity, because they were in my sanctuary ministering in the office of the gates of the house and its ministers: for they offered the holocaust and the victims of the people, and they stood in their sight to minister for them. Because, then, they ministered for them in the sight of their idols and were to the house of Israel a stumbling block of iniquity, therefore I have lifted up my hand against them, says the Lord God, and they have borne their iniquity. And they shall not draw near to me to exercise the priesthood for me, nor shall they approach any of my sanctuary next to the Holy of Holies, but they shall bear their shame and their crimes which they committed — for instead of the priestly office, they who had been accustomed to offer the holocaust and the victims and every sacrifice will be reduced to the lowest rank —. And they will be doorkeepers of the house, to everlasting disgrace — so that they may be seen by the whole people, entering and going out, from what lofty dignity they have come to what lowest rank —. But the priests,' he says, 'that is, the Levites who are the sons of Zadok — that is, of the righteous, or of the righteous one of God — and who in the time of the straying of all Israel kept the ceremonies of my sanctuary, they shall approach me to minister to me, and shall stand in my sight and offer me the fat — that is, the richest sacrifice —, and the blood — that is, a living sacrifice and pleasing to God —. And they shall enter my sanctuary and approach my table, to burn incense to me and to keep the whole rite of sacrifice' — but if this is so, that those who in the time of error or of persecution followed idols which they had fashioned from their own heart, and asserted not only by voice but also by the subscription of their hand that the Son of God is a creature: And they served the creature rather than the Creator, who is blessed forever: how do they assume for themselves the priestly and pontifical eminence, and dare to offer victims to God, they who were once worshipers of idols? — but, as the most ardent poet testifies: Whatever is sinned by many goes unpunished; the multitude of sinners has made pardon easier for the impious to obtain: so that those who, reduced to the laity, ought to have bewailed the former crimes of their sacrilege, now sit leaning back on the pontifical throne, and belch at us the nauseas of a feigned faith, nay rather the open epitomes of a misborn perfidy —; let them hear, then, at least late, and observe the precepts of almighty God: No foreigner uncircumcised in heart and uncircumcised in flesh shall enter my sanctuary, though he be a son, though joined by kinship, though he cling to us by an old bond: if he is uncircumcised in heart or flesh, let him not be brought into the sanctuary of God, lest we make the ministers of Christ our own ministers, and pollute the altars of the Lord for the sake of carnal comforts —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.44.14":["Where we have put: And I will make them doorkeepers of the house in all its service and in all that is done in it, them, namely, of the Levites who went far away from God in the straying of the sons of Israel and followed idols to which they sacrificed victims, Symmachus, considering the order of the reading and its sense, translated more correctly, saying: For I had made them keepers of the doors of the house, of all its service and of all that is done in it, so that it does not refer to those who are to be in the temple, but to those who were.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.44.17-18":["And when they enter the gates of the inner court, they shall be clothed with garments (or robes) of linen, and nothing woollen shall come upon them (or and they shall not be clothed with woollens) while they minister in the gates (or from the gates) of the inner court and within. Linen fillets shall be on their heads (or they shall have linen turbans upon their heads), and linen breeches shall be on their loins, and they shall not be girded in sweat (or violently). And when they go out to the outer court to the people, they shall put off their garments (or robes) in which they had ministered, and they shall lay them (or them) up in the chambers (or exedras) of the sanctuary (or of the holy places), and they shall clothe themselves in other garments (or robes), and they shall not sanctify the people in their garments (or robes). But they shall not shave their head, nor let their hair grow long, but clipping they shall clip their heads (or: They shall not shave their heads, and they shall not crop their hair close; covering they shall cover their heads). And no priest shall drink wine when he has entered into the inner court. First the words of the history must be laid open. Among the other things which the word of the Lord commanded the priests, it orders this too to be observed: that in the very gates of the inner court they be clothed with garments — that is, robes — of linen, and that they not use woollen clothing, whether in the gates of the inner court or within — that is, in the inner shrines and the Holy of Holies —; that linen fillets too — or turbans — be on their heads, linen breeches on their loins; and what follows according to the Seventy: And they shall not be girded violently (for which Aquila and Symmachus translated in sweat, Theodotion expressing the Hebrew word itself, 'in ieze', Aquila's second edition 'buza'), by which he signifies that they are not to be girded violently, tightly and constrictedly like men in bonds, lest in the priestly and Levitical ministries they become unfit both to hold and to slay the victims, and be unable also to drag them and to run about.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.44.17-19":["What all these things indicate according to the spiritual sense, what follows will show. That there are holy and spiritual garments the Apostle teaches, saying: Put on the Lord Jesus Christ, and in another place: Put on the bowels of mercy, goodness, humility, meekness and patience, and again: Strip off the old man with his works, and put on the new, which is renewed in knowledge after the image of the Creator; and I think this too signifies the same: For this corruptible must put on incorruption, and this mortal must put on immortality. And when this corruptible has put on incorruption — and this mortal immortality —, then shall come to pass the word that is written, and the rest — concerning the priestly garments it is written more fully in Exodus, and we once published a book to whose interpretation the diligent reader must be sent: for we cannot comprehend a broad subject in a brief discourse —; these garments we make for ourselves by our own labor, woven from the top — such as the tunic which the Lord also had, which cannot be torn —, and we are clothed with them when we know the secrets and hidden things of the Lord and have the spirit which searches even the high and deep things, which are not to be shown to the crowd nor brought out to the people who are not sanctified nor prepared for the holiness of the Lord — lest, if they hear things greater than themselves, they be unable to bear the majesty of knowledge and be choked, as it were, by solid food, they who are still to be nourished with the milk of infancy —; but that there are, on the contrary, very evil garments the divine Scripture teaches, as is that which is sung in the Psalms of Judas the traitor: And he put on cursing like a garment, and a little after: Let it be to him like a garment which is wrapped about him, and like a girdle with which he is always girded: hence the Lord too, who suffered for us and bore our sins, is said in Zechariah to have filthy garments — of which it is written: And Jesus was clothed in filthy garments —, which he afterward lays aside — as Scripture says: Take the filthy garments from him and clothe him with a full-length robe, and the rest —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.44.17-21":["The priests of Egypt use linen garments not only within but without — but the divine religion has one dress for the ministry, another for common use —; and the fillets and breeches are rightly put on, so that seemliness and decorum may be preserved — namely, lest in climbing the steps of the altar and running about at the ministries they uncover their shame —; shaven heads belong to the superstition of the Gentiles; but from wine, as far as my knowledge goes, I do not think any of the Gentiles abstains.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.44.18":["As for what follows: Linen fillets (or turbans) shall be on their heads, I think it shows the crown of grace of which it is written: For your head shall receive a crown of graces; nor let that which the Apostle says about the uncovered and bared head raise a question for us: A woman ought to have authority over her head because of the angels. For if a woman is not veiled, let her be shorn; but if it is shameful for a woman to be shorn or shaven, let her be covered. For a man ought not to cover his head, since he is the image and glory of God; but the woman is the glory of the man: for if it belongs to men not to cover the head, it seems contradictory how the priests are now ordered to cover their heads with linen fillets or a turban; but let us read more carefully: the present words will be resolved from the preceding ones: for it is said above: When all minister in the gates of the inner court and within — for if we enter into the holy places and stand in the sight of the Lord, we ought to cover our heads, because: No living man shall be justified in the sight of the Lord, and from infancy the heart of man is set on malice —; and further, we use breeches within, lest any shame of a polluted conscience and of the marriage act appear in the sight of the Lord: with which breeches the Savior also girds his apostles, saying: Let your loins be girded, and lamps burning in your hands, and the Apostle says to believers: Stand therefore, having your loins girded in truth, and let the imitators of Christ hear what is written of him: He shall be girded with righteousness about the loins and encompassed with truth; and with this very holy Levitical girdle he who is holy and has climbed to the summit of the virtues is by no means bound violently, lest he seem to keep the good of continence unwillingly and to eat bread in the sweat of his face, rather than to do the Lord's precept with his will.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.44.19":["And because he had once commanded what garments the priests ought to use when they are within at their ministries, he orders again that, going out into the chambers — or exedras — of the holy places, they put off their former garments and put on others, lest, if they have the holy garments, they sanctify the people placed outside, who have not yet been sanctified nor prepared themselves for the sanctification of the temple, so as to be a Nazirite of the Lord; by which we learn that we ought not to enter the Holy of Holies in everyday garments, any whatever, polluted by the use of common life, but to hold the sacraments of the Lord with a clean conscience and clean garments.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","Further, when he goes outside and mingles with the people, he lays aside the priestly garments in the chamber in which the riches of the Lord are kept, and the priests use other garments and other teaching for the common crowd, lest the people be sanctified in their robes: for it is a grave thing to take up the burden of the multitude beyond one's strength, as Solomon says: It is a snare to a man to sanctify hastily anything of his own: for when he has made a vow, repentance steals upon him. — Further, the Nazirites too offer themselves of their own accord; and whoever has vowed anything and has not fulfilled it is guilty of the vow; hence it is also said of widows: When they have grown wanton in Christ, they wish to marry, having condemnation because they have made void their first faith — for It is better not to promise than to promise and not to perform —: Hannah offered Samuel, who remained in perpetual holiness; and Manoah offered his son Samson, but the lust of Delilah defiled the consecration of his hair: then the Lord departed from him and he was condemned to blindness, except that afterward, as his hair grew, he received his former strength, and, in a type of Christ, by far more of the foreigners did he kill in death than in life —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.44.20":["As for what follows: But they shall not shave their head, nor let their hair grow long, but clipping they shall clip their heads, it is clearly shown that we ought neither to have shaven heads like the priests and worshipers of Isis and Serapis, nor again to let our hair hang down, which is properly the mark of the luxurious and of barbarians and soldiers, but that the seemly bearing of priests should be shown at first sight; as for what the Seventy said: They shall not shave their heads, and they shall not crop their hair close, but covering they shall cover their heads, according to them we learn that baldness is not to be made with the razor, nor is the head to be cropped so close that it seems like those of the shaven, but the hair is to be let grow only so far that the skin is covered — or certainly that the priests are always to cover their heads, according to that line of Vergil: Covered, to veil his locks with a purple mantle; but this is a forced interpretation —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","And the Lord wills his priests to have perpetual hair of consecration and to cover their heads, not with any veil from outside but with their own natural hair, not for ornament and luxury but for seemliness — hence the hairs of the head of the apostles too are numbered, as the Savior says: But even the hairs of your head are all numbered: of that head of which it is also written in Ecclesiastes: The eyes of the wise man are in his head. And the head of the man is Christ, which the sinner tramples and despises and, so to speak, shaves with a razor and takes away its beauty: which the Ammonites also did to those whom David had sent as envoys —; but if transgression of the law dishonors God, it follows that he who keeps the precepts of the law honors him — that what is written may be fulfilled: That all may honor the Son, as they honor the Father —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.44.21":["And wine the priests and Levites shall not drink, not only for their ministry, but indeed when they are about to enter the Holy of Holies, lest the mind be weighed down and the understanding become duller — hence the Apostle too says: It is good, he says, not to drink wine and not to eat meat, and in another place: And wine, in which is debauchery — for The people ate and drank, and rose up to play —; and what he grants Timothy, to drink a little wine, he clearly shows why he granted it: For your stomach, he says, and your frequent infirmities —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","Further, as for what follows, that: No priest, when he is about to enter the inner court, shall drink wine, it is clear that by drunkenness and strong drink the state of the mind is overturned, and that we are not fully wise when the mind is taken up with wine and satiety; but drunkenness is shown not only in the drinking of wine but in all things by which, both in contracts and in the business of the world, in losses and gains, in love and hatred, the mind is made drunk and wavers and cannot keep its state; and because we are encompassed by frail flesh and are compelled — for food and clothing and the necessities which pertain to the body — to do some things which we ought not to do, at least this is required of us: that — when we return into ourselves and know why we were born and enter the inner parts and shrines of the temple — we withdraw from the vices of the world and commit not even the least sins; by which it is shown both that we sin — when it is for the necessities of the flesh — light sins, and again that there is a time when — entering the inner court — we ought to be clean and estranged from all drunkenness.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.44.22":["A widow and a divorced woman (or a woman cast out) they shall not take as wives, but a virgin of the seed of the house of Israel; but also a widow who has been the widow of a priest they may take. And they shall teach my people; what the difference is between holy and polluted, between clean and unclean, they shall show them. And when there is a controversy (as is added in the Seventy, of blood), they shall stand in my judgments, and they shall judge; my laws and my precepts they shall keep in all my solemn feasts, and they shall sanctify my sabbaths. And they shall not go in to a dead man, lest they be polluted, except to father and mother, son and daughter, brother and sister who has not had another man: in these they shall be defiled. And after he has been cleansed, seven days shall be counted to him, and on the day of his entry into the sanctuary, into the inner court, to minister to me in the sanctuary, he shall offer for his sin, says the Lord God. But they shall have no inheritance; I am their inheritance, and you shall give them no possession in Israel, for I am their possession. The victim both for sin and for trespass they shall eat, and every vow of Israel shall be theirs; and the first portions of all the firstborn, and all the libations of all that are offered, shall be the priests'; and the first portions of your foods you shall give to the priest, that he may lay up a blessing for his house. Anything that has died of itself or been caught by a beast, of birds or of cattle, the priests shall not eat. This whole passage contains precepts for the priests, who, after the many things which we have expounded above, are ordered not to take as wives a widow and a divorced woman, or one cast out — the widow and the divorced woman is the Synagogue, that is, the congregation of the Jews, which did not receive the Lord; hence the Synagogue is also rightly called 'cast out' or 'widow': but the one cast out is the throng of heretics, who went out from us because they were not of us —, but: A virgin, he says, of the seed of the house of Israel, who has been nurtured in the house of God, in the law and the prophets, of whom the Apostle also speaks: I wish to present you all as a holy virgin to Christ; this is the virgin of whom the divine word also says elsewhere: Love her and she will keep you, encircle her and she will exalt you, honor her that she may embrace you; and who the virgin is who is to be loved and guards her lover, he will know who has read this: Her I loved and sought from my youth, and I sought to take her as my bride, and I became a lover of her beauty; and the Lord of all loved her. And not only a virgin, but also a widow the priest ought to marry, one, however, who has been the wife of another priest: knowledge, namely, which another worshiper of God has found; for we ought not to be content with new teaching only, but also to cultivate the old and join it to our company, provided it has been trained in sacred worship.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.44.23":["It is the office of the priest too to teach the people: What the difference is between holy and polluted, between clean and unclean, so that we refer the first to doctrines, the second to the works which are performed through the flesh — for who understands transgressions? —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.44.24":["And when there is a controversy 'about any matter' or, as the Seventy translated, 'of blood', that is, what pertains to a mortal crime: The priests shall stand in my judgments, so that they do not judge according to rank nor accept the person of poor or rich in judgment, but stand in the judgments of God — and remember that psalm: God has stood in the synagogue of gods, and in the midst he judges gods, calling 'gods' those who have the power of judging men —: for with what judgment they have judged, they themselves will be judged. My laws, he says, and my precepts they shall keep in all my solemn feasts, so that they may know how Christ our Passover was sacrificed, how we ought to fulfill seven weeks of joy and gladness, and humble our souls in fasting and in the sound of trumpets, and understand also spiritual feasts of Tabernacles — in which we say: I am a sojourner and a stranger, as all my fathers were —; these are the true solemn feasts of God, which one discussing the Pentateuch will interpret more fittingly: the first virtue of a priest is not only to teach what he knows, but to keep all the festivals of God, so that he may be able to command others to keep what he himself has observed — that it is the office of priests to have knowledge of the law we learn also in Malachi, who says: The lips of the priest shall guard knowledge, and they shall seek the law from his mouth, because he is the angel of the Lord of hosts —. And they shall sanctify my sabbaths. And they shall not go in to a dead man, lest they be polluted (for which the Seventy translated: And they shall not go in upon the soul of a man, lest they be polluted), except to father, he says, and mother, son and daughter, brother and sister who has not had a man: they shall be defiled. And after he has been cleansed, seven days shall be counted to him, and on the day of his entry into the sanctuary and into the inner court, to minister to me in the sanctuary, he shall offer for his sin to the Lord God (or he shall offer a propitiation). And there shall be to them their inheritance, and you shall give them no possession in Israel, I am their possession, the priests are still being commanded; in which it must be observed that he did not say absolutely: and they shall sanctify 'sabbaths' — or, according to Isaiah: Your new moons and sabbaths my soul hates —, but with a distinction: 'my' sabbaths — hence it is written in the Gospel too: The priests in the temple violate the sabbath and are without blame: not the sabbath of God, but the sabbath of the letter, the sabbath of the Jews, which they rightly violate who are: A chosen race, a royal priesthood —; but the sabbath that has been sanctified let us understand as that which the Apostle too teaches has been left for the people of God — of which it is written: If they shall enter into my 'rest', which in Hebrew is called 'sabbath' —; and he sanctifies the sabbath of God who does not carry the burden of sin on the sabbath nor say: Like a heavy burden they have weighed heavy upon me: he who is such does not gather wood on the sabbath, nor build upon the foundation of Jesus Christ wood, hay, stubble, nor kindle a fire to consume useless material, and on the sabbath day he remains in one place and does not go outside, but remains set like a pillar in the temple of God — of which John writes in the Revelation: He who conquers, I will make him a pillar in the temple of my God, and he shall go out no more —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.44.25":["As for what follows: And they shall not go in to a dead man, lest they be polluted: it belongs properly to the priestly office not to touch dead things, so that he who prays for the vices of others may himself approach the altar clean — of which dead the Savior too speaks in the Gospel: Leave the dead to bury their own dead —; these are defiled in a dead father when they forsake their Creator, and in a dead mother, departing from the Church, in a son also and a daughter, in that the one pertains to thoughts, the other to works — which is said more expressively in Greek, νοητὰ καὶ αἰσθητὰ [things of the mind and things of the senses] —; in brother also and sister: he who, reborn of the same spirit, if he has reckoned him dead, is defiled in him, only on this condition, if the sister has remained a virgin and not been stained by the embrace of any man — from which we understand the virginal privilege, that the contact of any man whatever takes away perfect purity; whether these things have been said rightly or wrongly, let us leave to the judgment of the reader —; the affection of nature too is approved herein, so that in the order of charity — according to what is written: Set charity in order in me —, after God the parent of all things, the father of the flesh too is loved, and the mother, son and daughter, brother and sister, only on this condition, if she has not gone out of the house nor passed into the power of another.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.44.26-27":["And after the priest has been cleansed by the order of purification which is written in the Mosaic law: Seven days shall be counted to him — either the perfect number of penitence, or after the consummation of the world, when we have passed over to the true ogdoad —, that he may enter the Holy of Holies — for this is the inner court — and be able to minister in the sanctuary and always to offer a victim for the sin of conscience; and let there be no time in which he does not remember that he was stained over father and mother, son and daughter, brother and sister: for we are compelled to do many things, set as we are in the straits of this world, either by the condition of the flesh or by the frailty of nature.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.44.28":["But he who shows himself such as to minister in the sanctuary and enter the inner court and always offer sacrifice to the Lord — so that, a true priest, he may be true, or rather an imitator of him of whom it is written: You are a priest forever according to the order of Melchizedek —, he shall have no inheritance except God, who is his inheritance, nor shall he receive a possession in Israel — that is, among the common crowd —, but a priestly one, so that the Lord may say of him: I am the inheritance and: I am their possession — whom, when they have found him, they will speak and say: I will hold him and will not let him go, and they will sing with the prophet: The Lord is my portion —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.44.29":["The victim both for sin and for trespass (or for ignorance) they shall eat, and every vow (or separation) in Israel shall be theirs; and the first portions of all the firstborn, and all the libations of all that are offered, shall be the priests'; and the first portions of your foods you shall give to the priest, that he may lay up a blessing for his house (or that he may put blessings upon your houses). For 'victim' and sacrifice, which in Hebrew is called 'manaa', Aquila and Symmachus translated 'gift'; again, for 'vow', which the Seventy translated ἀφόρισμα, that is, 'separation', Aquila and Symmachus and Theodotion translated ἀνάθημα [a thing devoted], which in Hebrew is called 'herem'; and it must be considered that all the things which the Lord now speaks through the prophet he had already said before through Moses; and it must be asked why he repeats the same things that have been said: namely, that the things which are in the minds of men, blotted out either by negligence in reading or by contempt and forgetfulness in hearing, may be renewed by the living voice: things which are not written with pen and ink but by the Spirit and the Word of God — hence the Savior too left no volume of his teaching of his own, as the ravings of the apocrypha invent in many places, but by the Spirit of the Father and his own he speaks daily in the heart of believers —; and this will be the answer against those who slander the prophets: why they repeat in their own volumes what is contained in the Pentateuch. The victim, then, both for sin and for trespass (or ignorance) the priests are to eat — of whom it is written: They shall eat the sins of my people —; and we should not so much rejoice at the offering of gifts as fear the condemnation of our honor if we abuse it unworthily.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.44.30":["The first portions, according to the letter, are those of living and lifeless things which are born first and which the earth first brings forth; the firstfruits of our foods too are offered to the priests, so that we taste nothing of the new crops unless the priest has tasted it first; and we do this so that the priest may lay up our blessing and offering in his house, or so that at his prayer the Lord may bless our houses: great is the dignity of priests, but great the ruin if they sin; we rejoice at the ascent, but let us fear the fall: it is not so great a joy to have held the heights as it is a grief to have fallen from the loftier places — for we shall render account not only for our own transgressions but for those of all whose gifts we abuse; and we are by no means anxious for their salvation —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.44.31":["Anything that has died of itself or been caught by a beast, of birds or of cattle, the priests shall not eat; and according to the letter these precepts befit every chosen race, royal and priestly — which refers properly to Christians, who have been anointed with the spiritual oil of which it is written: God, your God, has anointed you with the oil of gladness above your fellows —: that he not eat 'what has died of itself', whether of birds or of cattle, whose blood has not been shed — which in the Acts of the Apostles is called 'strangled', and that these things must necessarily be observed the letter of the apostles from Jerusalem warns — and what has been caught by a beast, because that too is likewise strangled: and it condemns priests who, out of the greed of the gullet, do not keep these things in the case of thrushes, figpeckers, dormice and other things of this kind. But according to ἀναγωγὴν [anagogy, the higher sense] we can call that dead of itself in which the blood of the soul has died, and which imitates 'Nabal' the Carmelite, whose name means 'fool', and who, when he heard of the wrath of David, trembled with fear: And his heart died within him; and caught by beasts — the beasts by whose bites the prophet sighs not to be torn, saying: Do not deliver to the beasts the soul that confesses you — must be said of him who is devoured by the bites of the adversary lion, and of the leopard which does not change its spots, and of the she-bear raging for her stolen cubs, and by the fury of wolves, and of the other beasts, which we take as the variety of demons; and the birds we say are those who set their mouth in heaven; and those cattle, the ones that, bent down and turned toward the earth, serve only the belly and the things that are below the belly.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.45.1-2":["And when you begin to divide the land as a possession, set apart firstfruits to the Lord, a sanctified portion of the land, twenty-five thousand in length and ten thousand in breadth; it shall be sanctified in all its border round about. And of this there shall be a sanctified portion five hundred by five hundred, four-square round about, and fifty cubits for its suburbs all around. And from this measure you shall measure a length of twenty-five thousand and a breadth of ten thousand, and in it shall be the temple (or the sanctification) and the Holy of Holies. The sanctified portion of the land shall be for the priests, the ministers of the sanctuary, who approach to the ministry of the Lord; and it shall be a place for them for houses and for a sanctuary of holiness: twenty-five thousand of length and ten thousand of breadth. But the Levites who minister to the house, they shall possess twenty chambers (for which the Seventy translated: they shall hold a city to dwell in). And you shall give the possession of the city five thousand in breadth and twenty-five thousand in length, according to the separation of the sanctuary, to the whole house of Israel. To the prince also, on this side and on that, by the separation (or the firstfruits) of the sanctuary in the possession of the city, over against the face of the separation of the sanctuary and over against the face of the possession of the city, from the side of the sea to the sea and from the side of the east to the east, and of length along each part, from the western border to the eastern border, of the land: it shall be his possession in Israel, and the princes shall no more ravage (or lay waste) my people; and they shall give the land to the house of Israel according to their tribes. After the ceremonies of the priests, and their worship and their food — what they ought to take and what to avoid —, he now gives a description of the holy land; and before he divides the whole land among the tribes, he commands that out of all the tribes a place be chosen which should have twenty-five thousand in length and ten thousand in breadth — and since 'of cubits' or 'of feet' or 'of ells' is not set down, we are given to understand that it signifies 'the reed', which was in the man's hand and had a measure of six cubits and a sixth part of one cubit, that is, a παλαιστήν [palm, a handbreadth]: let the diligent reader reckon how many reeds make a thousand paces and how many thousands there were in length and breadth —; after this description he again commanded that, from the sanctified land which was chosen out of all the tribes of Israel, that is, of twenty-five thousand reeds in length and ten thousand in breadth, another land within, of five hundred reeds round about, that is, of two thousand, be chosen for building the sanctuary, that is, the temple of the Lord. And lest perhaps other buildings should be joined to the building of the temple, he commanded that for fifty cubits round about the land be empty 'for suburbs', or, as Symmachus, Theodotion and the Seventy translated, 'for a boundary' and 'a separation' and 'a space'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.45.1-8":["But if we wish to compare spiritual things with spiritual and, following certain interpreters, to climb to higher things — they assert that the present things are a figure of things to come, and that all that is said pertains to the heavenly Jerusalem, which is the mother of us all, and to the Church of the firstborn —, it will not indeed be difficult to say what has been said by others, but we fear that the prudent reader may by no means accept an exposition of this kind; hence all things are to be referred both to the present time and to the Church, which now labors in the world and hastens to go to heavenly things: so that out of the whole world some are chosen as a possession of God to possess the holy place, and others, who have advanced to greater things, to hold the Holy of Holies; in the ministries too the order of priests and Levites is different: some of them daily offer sacrifice to God by their prayers and virtues, others are in a second and lesser rank, so as to minister to these, and through those who have climbed to the summits of the virtues; and last of all there is a leader or prince who has such power that he receives a possession over against one tribe and by the merit of one balances the merits of very many, and first, while he lives in the world, possesses the breadth from sea to sea, then from the west, or from the sea, as far as the east, so that, leaving the present things behind, he may hasten to the things to come, and may have his own fixed possession, and the prince may by no means desire what belongs to others, and each tribe may hold its own part: which can properly be referred to bishops and presbyters, to whom it is said that they should no more lay waste and ravage the people of God, nor possess the land of Israel as an inheritance, but that each should have his own possession, the one appointed to his rank. Now the twenty-five thousand they refer to the senses, which are set down four times; and the ten thousand to perfect knowledge, so that in the one only the plain history is held, while in the other there is discussion also of θεολογίᾳ [theology] and of the heavenly powers, because ten is a perfect number. This too must be considered: that the priests, who are set in the higher rank, possess twenty-five thousand in length and ten thousand in breadth; but the rank of the Levites, that is, the lower one, has indeed the same number in length, that is, twenty-five thousand, but its breadth is bounded by the number five: hence too they hold only twenty chambers — the number in which the gifts for Esau are offered —; but the prince, who is truly a prince among the people and deserves to receive the name of leader, receives a possession of the sanctuary on this side and on that, namely from all who are subject to him of their own will, and he is a dweller in the city, and makes the salvation of many his own reward. In the first temple, which is called the sanctuary, five hundred reeds are described on each side, and again fifty cubits by which the whole possession of the priests is separated; but in the second sanctuary, where the Holy of Holies is, the breadth of the temple and its space are not described, but the number is uncertain: and this we find also in the possession of the prince, who has a possession among the people of Israel without any number, since the progress of all overflows to the prince: and the salvation of the disciples is the reward of the teachers.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.45.3":["When this description is finished, another measure, of a sanctification to be set apart, is prescribed afresh: that after the former measure another land be measured, of twenty-five thousand reeds in length and ten thousand in breadth, in which there is by no means the temple, that is, the holy place, but the Holy of Holies.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.45.4":["And in it let the priests dwell who approach to the ministry of the Lord; and let them dwell there at the time when they discharge the priestly office: and let this possession be, as we have said, of twenty-five thousand reeds in length and ten thousand in breadth.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.45.5-6":["Further, the Levites, who serve the priests and have ministries of their own, will receive a place for building twenty 'chambers' — according to Aquila; 'exedras' according to Symmachus; 'cells' according to Theodotion; or, as the Seventy translated, 'a city', by which we understand that a certain separate place for the dwelling of the Levites is called by the name of a city —, a place which is to have five thousand reeds in breadth and twenty-five thousand in length. All these things, that is, the sanctuary and the second sanctuary, and the Holy of Holies, and the dwellings of the priests and the houses of the Levites who minister to the priests, are called places separated and consecrated for ministry and for sanctification for the house of Israel.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.45.7-8":["After the fourfold description of the holy land a fifth division is set down: that the prince or leader of the people should receive on this side and on that, that is, on either side of the sanctuary, for his possession, and should dwell in the city, and that his possession should face the place set apart for the temple and the face of the city, and should be from the side of the sea, that is, from the west, as far as the other side of the sea, having the breadth which the prophet describes below in the description of the individual tribes. Then follows: But in length along each part — which the individual tribes receive — from the western border — that is, of the sea — as far as the eastern border — where this ends we learn in the description of the tribes —; and this will be the possession of the leader or prince in the land of Israel. These things have been said for the present so that the plain account of the history may be known.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.45.9":["Thus says the Lord God: Let it suffice you, princes in Israel; leave off iniquity and plunder, and do judgment and righteousness: separate your borders from my people, says the Lord God. For 'plunder' the Seventy translated 'misery'; for 'borders', 'oppression'. And the sense is: 'Since you have received your portion, O princes — as Scripture says: To the prince also on this side and on that, in the separation of the sanctuary, in the possession of the city (understand: you shall give) —, and your power is so great that your possession has received the portion of one tribe: therefore I command and admonish you that it suffice to have committed iniquity and plunder up to now, while you invade and snatch away what belongs to others; and — since it is written: Put away evil and do good, and conversely: Do judgment and righteousness, judging for the orphan and justifying the widow —: Separate your borders from my people, namely so that you do not move the boundaries, and because the very neighborhood harms the humbler man, who cannot bear the haughtiness of one greater and more powerful. Let this be said for the present to the people of that time; but the command can also be fitted to our own princes — who after the manner of Pharaoh and the Egyptians oppress the children of Israel through their power, and do not remember that it is written: Have they made you leader? Do not be lifted up, but be among them as one of them —, and also that which the Lord says in the Gospel κατὰ [according to] Luke: But if that servant says in his heart: My master delays to come; and begins to beat his men-servants and maid-servants, and to eat and drink and be drunk: the master of that servant will come on a day when he does not expect and at an hour that he does not know, and will divide him, and will put his portion with the unbelievers; his disciples too he instructs with these precepts in the Gospel according to Matthew: You know that the princes of the nations lord it over them, and those who are greater exercise power over them; it shall not be so among you, but whoever wishes to be great among you shall become your minister, and whoever wishes to be first, let him be the servant of all. Just as the Son of man came not to be ministered to, but to minister, and to give his life as a redemption for many: there are many passages, if I wished to unroll them from the sacred Scriptures, where the pride of the greater is restrained and all are called to the humility of the Lord, who says: Learn from me, for I am meek and humble of heart.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.45.10-12":["A just balance and a just 'oephi' and a just batus you shall have; the 'oephi' and the batus shall be equal and of one measure, so that the batus holds a tenth part, and the 'oephi' a tenth part: according to the measure of the 'corus' shall be their equal balancing. And the shekel has twenty obols; moreover, twenty shekels and twenty-five shekels and fifteen shekels make a mina. (LXX: \"A just balance and a just measure and a just choenix you shall have for measure, and likewise there shall be one choenix for receiving: the tenth part of the gomer the choenix, and the tenth part of the gomer the 'oephi'; in relation to the gomer it shall be equal, and for weight twenty obols: twenty shekels, five and twenty shekels and fifteen shekels shall be your mina.\") This passage, according to the Seventy, does not hang together and cannot be understood at all, since they changed not only the numbers of the measures but also the names: for in place of 'oephi' they translated 'measure', a general name for a specific one, and in place of 'batus' 'choenix'; in place of the Hebrew 'omer' too, which in Greek is called 'corus', they translated 'gomor', and again in place of 'batus' 'choenix'. Let us therefore speak according to the Hebrew and the letter, and let us explain more plainly what seems right to us in each particular. The 'balance', which in Hebrew is called 'mozene', is understood of the things that are weighed; but the 'epha', which in the Greek language is corruptly called 'oephi', pertains to the measure of various crops, for example wheat, barley, legumes; moreover the 'batus', which in Hebrew is called 'bath', is the same measure as the 'oephi', and only in liquid kinds, wine and oil and other things of this sort; the 'corus' holds thirty modii, and in both measures, that is, of dry things as well as of liquids, it holds the first place; the tenth part of the 'corus', in things that are measured by the modius, is called 'epha' — that is, three modii — and the tenth part of the 'corus' in liquid kinds is called 'batus' or 'badus': so that the 'epha' and the 'batus' are of the same measure; moreover the 'siclus' [shekel], which in Greek is called 'stater', has twenty obols; but the 'mina', which in Greek is called 'mna', has sixty shekels, which make one thousand two hundred obols; but what he says: Twenty shekels and twenty-five shekels and fifteen shekels make a mina, ought not to disturb the reader: for the difficulty is placed in the numbers for this reason, to make the hearer's mind attentive — whence also many things are spoken in parables and riddles, as: He who has ears to hear, let him hear; and all prophecy contains the truth in obscurity, so that the disciples may hear within, while the ignoble crowd placed outside may not know what is being said —. The divine Scripture therefore commanded that weights be just — saying: There shall not be in your purse a large measure and a smaller. A true and just balance you shall have, and a true and just measure you shall have; and in Proverbs it is said: A large balance and a smaller, both are abominable in the sight of God —, which indeed is to be observed by us not only in commerce and in the use of daily life, but righteousness must be guarded in all things — as Scripture says: By your words you shall make a balance and a weight —, so that, in words and in deed and in thoughts, we may do all things by weight and reason. That the obol, that is, the twentieth part of the 'siclus' or stater, is the smallest little portion, we read in another place: To the one who is faithful, the whole world of riches; but to the one who is unfaithful, not even an obol — as if he had said in other words: 'The unfaithful man possesses nothing in the world, but lacks even the smallest portion', of which it is said in another word in the Gospel: He shall not come out from there until he pays the last quadrans, that is, even the very last and smallest coin —; we read of ten minas handed over to individual servants for trading, and in another parable of talents divided among servants, and of the debtor of five hundred denarii, and of fifty, and of the laborers of the vineyard who each receive a denarius, and of the widow woman who lost a drachma and scarcely found it again by lighting a lamp: what sense all these things have, this is not the time to discuss.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.45.13-14":["And these are the firstfruits which you shall take: the sixth part of an 'oephi' from a 'corus' of grain, and the sixth part of an 'oephi' from a 'corus' of barley. The measure of oil also, a batus of oil, is the tenth part of a 'corus'; and ten bati make a 'corus', because ten bati fill a 'corus'. (LXX: \"And these are the firstfruits which you shall set apart: the sixth part of a measure from a gomor of wheat and the sixth part of an 'oephi' from a 'corus' of barley. And the ordinance of the oil: a cotyle — that is, a cyathus — of oil from ten cotylae, because ten cotylae are a gomor.\") Here too, the names of the measures having been changed, everything in the edition of the Seventy is confused, so that what is said cannot be understood: for they who above had said 'measure' for 'oephi', and 'choenix' for 'batus', and 'gomor' for 'corus', 'weight' for 'siclus', here too say 'measure' for 'oephi', for 'corus' now 'gomer', now 'corus' itself, and in the measure of oil 'cotyle', that is, 'cyathus', for 'batus'. Let us therefore speak first according to the letter. Δεκάτας — that is, the tenth part — of all crops the people owed by the law to the Levitical tribe; again, from these very tithes the rank of the Levites — that is, of the lower ministers — gave tithes to the priests, and this is what is called δεύτερον δεκάτη [the second tithe] — there were also other tithes, which each one of the people of Israel set apart in his own granaries, so that he might eat them when he went to the temple in the city of Jerusalem, and in the vestibule of the temple they would invite both priests and Levites to banquets; and there were also other tithes, which they stored up for the poor, which in the Greek language are called πτωχοδεκάται [poor-tithes] —; but the firstfruits which they offered from their crops were not defined by a specific number but left to the discretion of those offering, and we have received a tradition of the Hebrews, not commanded by the law but grown into custom by the discretion of the teachers: he who gave the most gave the fortieth part to the priests, he who gave the least the sixtieth; between the fortieth and the sixtieth it was permitted to offer whatever they wished; what therefore was left doubtful in the Pentateuch is here specifically defined on account of the avarice of the priests, so that they may not exact more from the people in the bringing of firstfruits, that is: that they offer the sixtieth part of the things that grow from the earth — for if the 'corus', which in Hebrew is called 'omer' and by the Seventy is called 'gomer', holds thirty modii in dry kinds as well as in liquids, and the 'epha' and the 'batus' too, according to what we said above, are the tenth part of the 'corus', and it is commanded that the sixth part both of the 'oephi' and of the 'batus' be given to the priests in offering the firstfruits, which out of three modii makes half a modius, it is clearly reckoned that the priests ought to receive the sixtieth part as firstfruits —. Let it suffice for the meantime to have perceived and heard these things according to the letter and according to the Hebrew truth. Now let us return to the spiritual understanding, in which it must first be asked how it is said: 'Honor the Lord from your righteous labors, and give him the firstfruits of the fruits of your righteousness, so that your storerooms may be filled with grain, and your winepresses may overflow with wine, so that, after you have offered the firstfruits of your labors and virtues and have said: Behold, now I have brought you the firstfruits of the fruits of the earth, which you gave me, O Lord, you may deserve to hear: Blessed are you in the city and blessed in the field; blessed the children of your womb and the produce of your land, and the fruit of your beasts and so on, and you may possess — according to the Apostle — every blessing of spiritual things in the heavenly places in Christ, while you hold that whatever you have is not of your own virtue but of the mercy of him who gave the crops'. And if indeed we have so much knowledge of the Scriptures that we lay open all the mystical things and teach men, that is, the rational animal, we give the firstfruits of our grain; but if, after ἀναγωγὴν [anagogy, the higher sense], we follow only the pure history, which can nourish the simple — according to what is written: Men and beasts you will save, O Lord —, we give the firstfruits of our barley — which barley: Isaac also served in the land of the foreigners, and it rose for him a hundredfold —. He who gives the firstfruits of barley can say: The Lord feeds me, and I shall lack nothing: in a place of pasture, there he has placed me, beside the waters of refreshment he has brought me up, but he who feeds rational living creatures with grain turns the sentence and says: You have prepared a table in my sight against those who trouble me, and after he has offered the firstfruits of oil and wine he joins on and says: You have anointed my head with oil, and your cup that inebriates, how splendid it is: with that oil which is prepared, most pure, without dregs, for the lamp of the Lord — and whoever has prepared it: His face is gladdened with oil, with the oil of exultation with which Christ was anointed above his fellows, and, made an olive tree, he will sing with the prophet: But I, like a fruitful olive tree in the house of God —, and with that wine which overflows from the true vine in the winepresses of the Savior — as the Lord says: I am the vine, you the branches, my Father the vinedresser —; of these winepresses we read three psalms, Psalm 8, Psalm 80 (81) and Psalm 83 (84), all of which refer to the mysteries of the Church. And indeed in the old law the firstfruits were without measure and number: For God did not give the Spirit by measure; but in the second building of the temple, which the prophecy of Ezekiel contains, there is a fixed measure, that is, the number sixty: by which the world is filled up, and it is said to us: With whatever measure you have measured, it will be measured back to you.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.45.15-17":["And as in the preceding we read that the sixtieth part of the things that are weighed and that are subject to measure ought to be offered to the prince, so now the two-hundredth part of the living creatures is commanded to be offered, and the whole people is subject to the precept of this law and of this command: that it offer to the leader or prince, because, of course, through him holocausts and sacrifices and libations are offered for the people at each solemnity, and the house of God is expiated; from which it is to be noticed that, as the people is debtor to offer firstfruits to the prince, so the prince is debtor to the people to offer victims for it; and him, according to tropology, we can understand as none other than God the Savior. He receives a ram out of two hundred living creatures — or, as the Hebrew word shows more significantly, βόσκημα [a grazing beast], which can refer to all living creatures and not properly to a ram —, and he receives the animal or ram which has been nourished in irrigated places, namely those of the holy Scriptures, and can say: The Lord feeds me, and I shall lack nothing: into a place of pasture, there he has placed me, beside the waters of refreshment he has brought me up; these were the irrigated places, nay rather these the channels, to which Jacob too led his flocks, so that in them they might drink and conceive and, according to the color of the rods, bring forth offspring for the Lord. In place of 'sacrifice', that is, θυσίᾳ, Aquila and Symmachus translate 'gift', which in the Hebrew is called 'manaa', and it properly pertains not to a victim but to the things which are offered in sacrifice from fine flour and parched grain and oil; but the 'holocaust' is what is offered whole to God and consumed by the sacred fire; the 'peace offerings', when we are reconciled to God: and on our behalf the Apostle says: We pray on behalf of Christ: be reconciled to God, and in another place: God was in Christ reconciling the world to himself. But this prince, to whom the firstfruits of living creatures are offered, is himself the king, as is shown also in the title of the cross in Hebrew and Greek and Latin letters — and when the Jews strove to have the triumphal title taken down, the throng of the Gentiles, in Pilate, answered: What I have written, I have written —. Upon the prince, he says, shall be: finely is the priestly dignity reminded to what office it is subject, so that it may know that it ought to offer for the people the sacrifice and the libations which are offered from wine at all the solemnities: namely at the calends, that is, the new moons, as well as at the sabbaths, when, with darkness covering all things, there is the beginning of the moon's light, in the rest of the sabbaths: so that he may make, namely, for sin, to make expiation for the house of Israel, which is the Church of the living God, the pillar and bulwark of the truth: for this is the house of the living God and the house of Israel, which needs the expiation of its sins.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.45.15":["And one ram from a flock of two hundred, from those which they nourish in Israel, for a sacrifice and for a holocaust and for peace offerings, to make expiation for them, says the Lord God. All the people of the land shall be bound to these firstfruits for the prince of Israel; and upon the prince shall be the holocausts and the sacrifice and the libations, at the solemnities and at the calends and at the sabbaths: at all the solemnities of the house of Israel: he shall make the sacrifice for sin and the holocaust and the peace offerings, to make expiation for the house of Israel. Above he said what portion of the things that are weighed, and of the measures too, both of dry kinds and of liquids, ought to be offered to the prince; now he keeps the same order of firstfruits in living creatures, and commands Israel to offer one out of a flock of two hundred rams: Which Israel nourishes, for a sacrifice and for a holocaust and for peace offerings, to make expiation for the people of Israel, says the Lord God. What we translated: From those which Israel nourishes, is written in the Hebrew 'memmasce', which Aquila and Symmachus translate ἀπὸ τῶν ὑδάτων, the Seventy and Theodotion ἀπὸ τῶν ποτιστηρίων: of which the one means from irrigated places, the other from channels or pools, so that, evidently, the firstfruits of the prince are nourished nowhere else but in irrigated places.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.45.18-25":["Thus says the Lord God: In the first month, on the first of the month, you shall take a calf from the herd without blemish, and you shall expiate the sanctuary. And the priest shall take of the blood of what will be for sin, and shall put it on the doorposts of the house and on the four corners of the ledge of the altar and on the doorposts of the gate of the inner court. And thus you shall do on the seventh of the month for each one who was ignorant and was deceived by error, and you shall make expiation for the house. In the first month, on the fourteenth day of the month, you shall have the Passover, a solemnity; for seven days unleavened bread shall be eaten. And the prince shall make on that day, for himself and for all the people of the land, a calf for sin; and in the solemnity of seven days he shall make as a holocaust to the Lord seven calves and seven rams without blemish daily for seven days, and for sin a he-goat of the goats daily; and the sacrifice he shall make of an 'oephi' for each calf and an 'oephi' for each ram, and a 'hin' of oil for each 'oephi'. In the seventh month, on the fifteenth day of the month, at the solemnity he shall do as has been said above, for seven days, both for sin and for the holocaust, and in sacrifice and in oil. (I have set down the whole chapter so that, divided into its single parts, it should not confuse the reader's mind; and first we must speak about the variety of the translation; where we translated: On the four corners of the ledge of the altar, the Seventy translated: And upon the four corners of the temple and upon the altar; and where we said: For each one who was ignorant and was deceived by error, they put: For the ignorant and the little one; also in the place where we said: And the sacrifice he shall make of an 'oephi' for each calf, an 'oephi', and an 'oephi' for each ram, and a 'hin' of oil for each 'oephi', their edition has: And the sacrifice and a cooking for the calf and a cooking for the ram you shall give, and a 'hin' of oil for the cooking; in the last little verse too, where we said: In sacrifice and in oil, they said: As in the manaa, as in the oil.) In the first, he says, month and on the first day of the month — no doubt he means Nisan —, and: You shall take a calf from the herd, not one which has been nourished at home but one in the herd, that is, from the flock and number of many; on the seventh day of the same month too: these two solemnities, that is, the first day of the first month and the seventh of the same, are not found in the Mosaic law; but the fourteenth day of the month, on which the Passover is celebrated, Moses too commanded to be observed: when we eat unleavened bread for seven days; but the fourth solemnity, of which he says: In the seventh month, on the fifteenth day of the month, at the solemnity and so on, seems to me to signify the Scenopegia [the feast of Tabernacles], which he has set down here without a name. Let us run back, then, to the particulars, and briefly discuss what seems right to us. That there are spiritual solemnities too the Apostle teaches: Let no one therefore judge you in food or in drink or in solemnities or of the new moon or of sabbaths, which are a shadow of things to come; and if God says through Isaiah: Your new moons and your sabbaths and the great day I do not accept: the fast and the holidays and your new moons and solemnities my soul hates, it is manifest that he who hates the solemnities of the Jews loves his own, namely the sabbath which is left for the people of God: That we may see the things that have been given to us by God: and may speak not in the learned words of human wisdom, but in the teaching of the Spirit, comparing spiritual things with spiritual: for in the present age, which is set in the evil one, we cannot see good days, but in the new, of which days the Holy Spirit sings in the Mosaic song: Remember the days of antiquity, understand the years of the generation of generations, of which the holy man also spoke in the psalm: In the night I meditated in my heart, and again: And I had the eternal years in mind and I meditated; let him who has not accepted this understanding answer how he can expound what Isaiah prophesies about the future and new age: There shall be month from month and sabbath from sabbath, and all flesh shall come to worship the Lord, when the true worshipers will by no means worship on Mount Gerizim nor in Jerusalem, but in spirit and truth, when there will be a new heaven and a new earth and: The whole creation shall be freed from the servitude of corruption into the liberty of the children of God and the sun will receive seven times as much light and the moon will be compared to the sun: We have come, indeed, to Mount 'Sion', which means 'watchtower', and to the city of the living God, the heavenly Jerusalem, and to thousands of angels' solemnities, of which solemnity another prophet also speaks: What will you do in the days of assembly and in the days of the solemnity of the Lord? this is what is also said elsewhere: Celebrate, Judah, your festivals, pay your vows.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.45.18":["If then we have learned spiritual solemnities, we shall consequently be taught spiritual victims too: a calf is taken from the herd — or a bull, as Symmachus translated —, free and pressed by no yoke, that is, by the burden of sins, and without blemish: Who did no sin, nor was guile found in his mouth, a young calf putting forth horns and hoofs: so that in him the sanctuary may be cleansed and expiated.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.45.19-20":["And the priest, he says, shall take of its blood which will be for the sin of all, who in other words is called a lamb in Exodus, and in the Gospel, as John the Baptist says: Behold the Lamb of God who takes away the sins of the world; and the blood itself is precious, in which we are redeemed in the passion of the Lord Savior, by whose flesh we are fed and with whose blood we are given drink, by which are touched the four corners of the ledge of the 'altar' — for which Theodotion put the Hebrew word itself, 'azara' [ledge, court], or of the 'temple', as the Seventy translated —, so that the house and the altar may be sanctified — for all the elements of the world are sanctified by this blood — so that, when someone has been expiated and cleansed, he may enter the gate of the inner court and be able to know the secrets of the Lord, and consequently come to the seventh day of the first month and attain rest and offer sacrifice to the Lord: both for ignorance — of which David speaks: The sins of my youth and of my ignorance remember not —, and for the little one — who, when he has come to the measure of the perfect man, destroys the things that belong to a little one and speaks confidently: When I was a little one, I spoke as a little one, I thought as a little one —, or for him who was deceived by error and sinned not so much by will as by the supposition of good.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.45.21-22":["And when the two solemnities of the first month have been completed, that is, of the first day and of its seventh, he comes to the fourteenth day of the month, on which is the solemnity of the Passover, of which it is written: For our Passover has been sacrificed, Christ; then we eat unleavened bread for seven days in rest and security of all things, when, destroying the leaven of malice and wickedness, we eat the unleavened bread of sincerity and truth, while our prince offers all these things for us: and first 'for himself' — for he took up a human body and for sin destroyed sin, he who grieves for us and bears our infirmities —, then 'for his house' — which is written in the Seventy but is not found in the Hebrew — that is, for the Church, and for all the people of the land, namely the whole human race: He is the Savior, indeed, of all men and especially of the faithful; and he himself is the propitiation for our sins, and not only for us, but for the whole world.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.45.23":["And when the calf has been offered for all the people of the land, on the seven remaining days of the Lord's passion seven calves and seven rams without blemish are offered daily, so that they may be burned up as a holocaust and by the Lord's fire: and the seven calves and seven rams are those who are conformed to the Lord's passion, so that they may imitate the true calf and the true ram, and by the blood of the martyrs through seven days the sins of the whole world may be cleansed; a he-goat of the goats too is offered for sin daily, namely for seven days.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.45.24":["And it must be noted more carefully that in the sacrifice an 'oephi' for each calf and an 'oephi' for each ram and a 'hin' of oil are offered, which are commanded by the law, but in the offering of the he-goat neither an 'oephi' nor, as the Seventy translated, a 'cooking' is set down — they who for 'oephi' translated πέμμα, that is, 'cooking' —; but the 'hin', which is a fixed measure of oil, of which we have already spoken before, is taken up in the sacrifice of the calf and of the ram, so that we may receive the fuel of everlasting light, and rest from labor, and healing from weariness after the appeasement of sins.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.45.25":["In the seventh month too, on the fifteenth day of the month, that is, of the Scenopegia, the same order of oblation and sacrifices is to be kept: Both for sin and for the holocaust and in sacrifice and in oil, so that, darkness having been put to flight and the light of the oil having risen, we may attain the festival of the Lord, and may have brighter solemnities, in which all our sins are forgiven us.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.46.1-3":["Let these things be set down for the sake of clarity; now, the Lord granting, let us unfold the spiritual sense. During the six days on which we work in the world, the eastern gate of the inner court is shut to us; but after we have come to the seventh day, that is, to the sabbath day on which there is eternal rest, or to the day of the kalends, when after blind night and dreadful darkness there is the beginning of light, the eastern gate is opened to us; and if any of us, through the merits of the virtues, has attained the name and dignity of a leader, he will enter by the way of the vestibule of the gate from outside, that is, through the labors of this world, and through the gate of the Church, which is still set within earthly bounds, not into the inner court — for this is what is written in the present passage — but to the threshold of the inner gate: and he will stand in it; but if the prince in the present scripture does not enter the inner court, but stands on its threshold, no one doubts that we now see in part and know in part, and do not yet contemplate the glory of the Lord with unveiled face. Then, while the prince stands on the threshold of the gate of the inner court, the other priests, to whom this office has been delegated — whom we can understand as the angelic powers —, will make for him the holocaust and peace offerings — for we need the mercy of God, and every creature, in comparison with the Creator, is unclean and must be purged by the divine fire, by which the lips of the prophet Isaiah too were purged —, so that, after he has received peace through the sacrifice, then, standing on the threshold, he may worship the omnipotence of God and confess himself unworthy of entering the inner court, and at once go out; but after he who is first has gone out, that very gate of the inner court will not be shut until the evening of this age, but will be open, so that the people of the land too may be at the entrance and worship toward him: by no means at every time, but when they have rested from sins and, the darkness of faith having been put to flight, have received the light of knowledge.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.46.1":["And meanwhile, before we discuss what these things signify, the very words of Scripture must be laid open παραφραστικῶς [by way of paraphrase]. The gate, he says, of the inner court on six days — of which it is written: Six days you shall labor and do all your works — shall be shut, and not just any gate, but the one that faces toward the east, whence the sun of righteousness rises; further, on the sabbath day, that is, the seventh, on which there is rest, it will be opened every week, and again, when thirty days are completed, when there is the end of one month and the beginning of another, on the new moon, namely the day of the kalends, it will be opened, so that the privilege the seventh day has in the week, the beginning of the month may have as its privilege too.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.46.2-3":["But why the gate of the inner court that faces toward the east is opened on the seventh day and on the day of the kalends, the following discourse will show: And the prince shall enter, he says, by the way of the gate from outside — namely of the outer court —, and shall stand on the threshold of the gate — of the inner court, which faces the east —, and he shall not enter the inner court, but, while he stands, other priests are to make for him the 'holocaust' — which Symmachus translated 'offering' —, and the peace offerings — or those which pertain to salvation —, so that, after the prince's victim has been burned up by the sacred fire and the leader has been reconciled to God, he may then worship upon the threshold of the gate of the inner court on which he has stood, and go out. What then? After the prince's going out will the gate be shut at once? By no means, but it will be open until evening, so that after the prince: All the people of the land may worship before the entrance of that gate: and they are to worship on the sabbaths and on the kalends, when the gate is opened.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.46.4-7":["And since he had said above: The priests shall make his holocaust and his peace offerings, and had not indicated the number of the holocaust, he now adds: And this holocaust the prince shall offer to the Lord on the sabbath day — he will offer it, however, not by himself, but through the priests, of whom it is written: And the priests shall make his holocaust and his peace offerings —: six, he says, lambs without blemish and a ram without blemish, so that the sacrifice of the ram is the measure of an 'oephi' — which in Hebrew is called 'epha' —, while for the six lambs the sacrifice is what the hand of the one who offers was able to offer, and for each 'oephi' there will be the measure of oil that is called 'hin'; but the kalends, that is, the day of the new moons, had this privilege beyond the sabbath day, that: A calf from the herd (or a bull) without blemish be sacrificed, while the rest, that is, the six lambs without blemish and the ram without blemish and their sacrifices, will keep the rite of the sabbath, only in such a way that, just as with the ram an 'oephi' of sacrifice is offered, that is, of fine flour, and a 'hin' of oil, so it is offered with the calf too.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","And this very holocaust, which the prince offers through the priests on the sabbath day, is bounded by the number of six lambs without blemish — namely after the six days on which we work in the world — and a ram without blemish, which is the prince of the flock, so that for each ram an 'oephi' is offered in sacrifice, that is, the tenth part of a 'corus'; but in the sacrifice of the six lambs no fixed measure is laid down, but as much as the hand of the one who offers shall give, that is, according to the quality and measure of his works; and he will offer a 'hin' of oil for each 'oephi' — that is, the oil which the five virgins prepared for themselves —, to anoint the fine flour or mix it, so that every offering to God may be full of the light of truth, and the face of the one who offers may be made glad with oil; but on the day of the kalends, that is, the thirtieth day, when the firstborn and the Levites are numbered according to the law apart from all Israel, of whom we spoke above, a calf from the herd without blemish is offered — about which we discoursed more fully in the previous chapter — and six lambs and a ram without blemish, and the rest that are offered on the sabbaths, so that we may attain eternal rest and may deserve to hear: Well done, good servant, because you have been faithful in a few things, I will set you over many: enter into the rest of your Lord, and may be able not only to come to the gate of the inner court but also to enter its innermost parts, and to possess in deed that saying: Planted in the house of the Lord, they will flourish in the courts of our God.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.46.5-7":["Thus says the Lord God: The gate of the inner court that faces toward the east shall be shut on the six days on which work is done; but on the sabbath day it shall be opened, and on the day of the kalends also it shall be opened, and the prince shall enter by the way of the vestibule of the gate from outside, and shall stand on the threshold of the gate, and the priests shall make his holocaust and his peace offerings, and he shall worship upon the threshold of the gate, and shall go out; but the gate shall not be shut until evening. And the people of the land shall worship at the entrance of that gate on the sabbaths and kalends before the Lord. And this holocaust the prince shall offer to the Lord on the sabbath day: six lambs without blemish and a ram without blemish, and a sacrifice of an 'oephi' for the ram, but for the lambs a sacrifice such as his hand shall give, and a 'hin' of oil for each 'oephi'; but on the day of the kalends: a calf from the herd without blemish, and six lambs and a ram shall be without blemish, and an 'oephi' for the calf, an 'oephi' also for the ram he shall make as a sacrifice, but for the lambs as his hand shall find, and a 'hin' of oil for each 'oephi'. Where we have translated 'oephi', which is the tenth part of a 'corus', the Seventy translated πέμμα, which in our language means 'cooking'; and where we said 'sacrifice', they set down the Hebrew word itself, 'manaa', which Aquila translated 'gift' or 'sacrifice', Theodotion 'sacrifice'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.46.8":["And when the prince enters, let him enter by the way of the vestibule of the gate, and let him go out by the same way. And when the people of the land enter in the sight of the Lord on the solemn feasts, he who enters by the gate of the north to worship, let him go out by the way of the southern gate; and he who enters by the way of the southern gate, let him go out by the way of the gate of the north: he shall not return by the way of the gate by which he entered, but shall go out opposite it. But the prince in their midst shall enter with those who enter and shall go out with those who go out. And on market days and on solemn feasts the sacrifice shall be an 'oephi' for the calf and an 'oephi' for the ram, but for the lambs the sacrifice shall be as his hand shall find, and a 'hin' of oil for each 'oephi'. That prince, of whom it was said above: And the prince shall enter by the way of the vestibule of the gate from outside, is both to enter by the same way and to go out by the same way: for he is of free will and has power to lay down his life and to take it up again, and, as much in coming to us as in ascending to the heavenly places, he uses the same freedom of his majesty.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.46.9":["Then too, he does not seek market days or solemn feasts, nor does he observe any difference of days, because every time is a festival as well; but the people enter and go out only on market days and on solemn feasts, and not by the same way, but by one way and then another: for it is written: And when the people of the land enter in the sight of the Lord on the solemn feasts, he who enters by the gate of the north to worship, let him go out by the way of the southern gate; and he who enters by the way of the southern gate, let him go out by the way of the gate of the north: he shall not return by the way of the gate by which he entered, but shall go out opposite it — which is commanded not only to those who go out of Sodom, and to whom it is said: Do not look back, nor only to those who put their hand to the plow, that they not look back at the things that are behind their back, but those too who are set in the house of the Lord are commanded not to look behind their backs and return to the weak and beggarly elements, and, having begun in the spirit, be finished in the flesh, but to advance from spiritual things also to greater ones and to say with the Apostle: Forgetting the things past, and stretching myself out to the things before, so that they may by no means know in part and prophesy in part, but come to the perfect: yet according to that perfection which human nature can contain, so that we may understand that saying of the Gospel: And you, when you have done all things, say: We are unprofitable servants; what we ought to do, we have done; whence the Apostle too, in the same epistle, speaks of two perfections: for as one perfect he writes: Not that I have already received, and that I am already perfect; but I pursue, if I may lay hold of that in which I also have been laid hold of by Christ, and at once he speaks as one perfect: But one thing: forgetting the things behind, stretching myself out to the things that are before, I pursue according to the purpose toward the prize of the upward calling. As many therefore as are perfect, we are of this mind — for how can the statement stand, that he who had said: Not that I have already received or that I am already perfect, should dare to say: As many therefore as are perfect, we are of this mind? —, from which it is plain that every human being and the whole creation, although it has come to perfection, nevertheless needs the mercy of God and possesses full perfection from grace, not from merit —. And so the people of the land are commanded that, if they enter by the gate of the north to worship, they go out by the way of the southern gate. Who is this people to whom this is commanded? Surely that of the Gentiles: so that he who, leaving the gate of the north — from which: Evils blaze up upon the earth —, has entered the temple, should not go out except by the way of the southern gate, to the gate of light, to the gate of warmth, to the gate at which the bridegroom lies at noon; and indeed it is said in the same Song of Songs: Arise, north wind, and come, south wind. But he who enters by the way of the southern gate also goes out by the way of the gate of the north. Which people is that? Clearly that of the Jews, which, leaving the way of the gate of light, goes to the gate of the north — of which it is said: The north wind is a most harsh wind; for Jeremiah's pot too is kindled from the face of the north —. Let us say it another way too: he enters by the gate of the north who lets go of sins, and he goes out by the southern gate who follows the virtues; and conversely, if a righteous man has fallen into sins, he enters indeed by the southern gate, but goes out by the way of the gate of the north.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.46.10":["But the prince, he says, in their midst will be, namely in the midst of those entering and going out, according to what is written: In the midst among you stands one whom you do not know, and the Apostle: Or do you not know that Christ is in you? — but if it is said to those who do not know that Christ stands in their midst, how much more to those who know; but when Christ has stood among those who know, then the body will become dead because of sin and the spirit living because of righteousness! —; and the prince enters with those who enter, and goes out with those who go out, standing by as witness of both, of virtue and of sin.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.46.11":["Therefore on market days and on solemn feasts — namely of the sabbaths and kalends and of every festival, of which we have spoken earlier — both with the calf and with the ram a single 'oephi' will be offered in sacrifice; but with the lambs there will be no fixed number, nor is the choice left to the will, but one will use his strength and the good of his conscience, so as to offer as much as he can, not as much as he wishes; and over all, a 'hin' of oil for each 'oephi', so that the fine flour may be anointed and the lamp lit in the sight of the Lord, and those who use it may be able to say: But I, like a fruitful olive tree in the house of the Lord.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.46.12":["But when the prince makes a spontaneous holocaust or voluntary peace offerings to the Lord, the gate that faces toward the east shall be opened for him, and he shall make his holocaust and his peace offerings as is usually done on the sabbath day, and he shall go out, and the gate shall be shut after he has gone out. And a yearling lamb without blemish he shall make as a holocaust daily to the Lord; morning by morning he shall make it. And a sacrifice (or manaa) he shall make with it morning by morning, a sixth part of an 'oephi' (or of a measure), and of oil a third part of a 'hin', to be mixed (or according to Symmachus sprinkled) with the fine flour; a sacrifice (or manaa) to the Lord, lawful, continual and perpetual. You shall make the lamb and the sacrifice and the oil morning by morning, an everlasting holocaust. Where we read 'without blemish' and in the Hebrew is written 'thamim', Aquila translates 'perfect'; and where the Seventy have 'manaa', Theodotion translated 'sacrifice', Aquila and Symmachus 'gift'; and for what we said, 'spontaneous', the Seventy rendered ὁμολογίαν, that is, 'confession'; and for 'prince', 'leader', for this is what 'basi' means. Moreover, the discourse is still about the prince — of whom Isaiah speaks: Behold, I have given him as a testimony among the nations, a prince and instructor among the nations, and Ezekiel: And I the Lord will be their God, and David a prince in their midst —. When therefore this prince makes a spontaneous — and says: The voluntary (or spontaneous) things of my mouth make well pleasing, O Lord —: and a 'spontaneous' holocaust, so that he offers the whole to God, and 'voluntary' peace offerings — which is not found in the earlier passages, for it is written: And the priests shall make his holocaust and his peace offerings, and about the spontaneous and voluntary ones, of which it now speaks, there is complete silence —, so that he does it not by necessity but by will; after the holocaust let him offer peace offerings, and be reconciled to God — whence virginity too is greater than marriage, because it is neither exacted nor rendered, but offered —; for 'peace offerings', which in Hebrew are called 'selamim', the Seventy translate 'saving offerings': so that the very reconciliation of the peace offerings may be the salvation of the one who offers. When therefore the prince has done this: The gate will be opened for him (or, according to the Seventy, he will open the gate for himself) that faces toward the east — that gate, namely, of which it is written: This is the gate of the Lord, the righteous shall enter by it, and whence the sun of righteousness rises —. And he shall make, he says, his holocaust and his peace offerings — of which we have just spoken —; and 'he shall make': as being a spontaneous sacrifice, and not other priests 'shall make' it for him: namely as is usually done on the sabbath day; therefore at every time the holocaust and peace offering that is offered voluntarily has the gate of the Lord unbarred, and it does not observe the sabbath, but all days are reckoned to it as rest. Then, after he has offered the holocaust and peace offerings, at once: He goes out, and the door is shut after him (or he shuts the door), so that the prince's privilege may be kept for him and his sacrifices not be mingled with the people.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.46.13":["And since he had said in general: A spontaneous holocaust or voluntary peace offerings to the Lord, and had not added bull or calf, ram or sheep, and a he-goat for sin, he teaches what this holocaust is: A yearling lamb, he says, without blemish he shall make as a holocaust; and not on fixed days, but: daily; nor at any other hour, but: morning by morning he shall make it: namely each 'morning', when, the sun having risen, there is the beginning of light — of which 'morning' David speaks in a psalm: In the morning you will hear my voice. In the morning I will stand before you, and will see, and in another place: In the evening weeping will linger, and in the morning gladness, and again: O God, my God, to you I keep watch from the morning. But who this lamb without blemish, or perfect, and yearling is, Isaiah teaches more fully, saying: As a sheep he was led to the slaughter, and as a lamb before its shearer, without voice and not opening his mouth, and Jeremiah: But I, like an innocent lamb led to the slaughter, did not know, and John the evangelist: Behold the lamb of God, who takes away the sin of the world; this is the lamb that is sacrificed in Egypt and with whose blood the doorposts of faith are armed, and the destroyer is shut out; and a yearling, because: He proclaimed the acceptable year of the Lord, the day of retribution. Nor let the reader wonder if the same one is both prince and priest and calf and ram and lamb, since in the holy Scriptures, according to the variety of circumstances, we read of him as Lord and God and man and prophet and rod and flower and root and prince and judge and righteous king and righteousness, apostle and bishop, arm, servant, ointment, shepherd, son and child, only-begotten and firstborn, door, way, angel, arrow and wisdom, and many other things, for all of which, if I wished to cite the testimonies, they would need a book of their own —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.46.14-15":["And so in the holocaust of this lamb, without blemish and a yearling, which is always offered in the morning: the prince himself shall make a sacrifice (or 'manaa', and fine flour or gift) morning by morning, a sixth part of an 'oephi', of fine flour being understood — and a sixth part of an 'oephi' is half a modius; for if an 'oephi' is the tenth part of a 'corus', that is, three modii, no one doubts that the sixth part of an 'oephi' makes half a modius —. Of the oil also, a third part of the measure 'hin' is offered, to be mixed (or sprinkled) with the fine flour; a sacrifice to the Lord, lawful, continual and perpetual, which is interrupted on no day but at every time, when the sun has risen, is always offered, so that what is set at the end of this chapter may be fulfilled: He shall make the lamb for a sacrifice, and the oil, morning by morning, an everlasting holocaust.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.46.16-17":["Thus says the Lord God: If the prince (or leader) gives a gift to any of his sons, his inheritance shall belong to his sons; they shall possess it by inheritance. But if he gives a legacy from his inheritance to one of his servants, it shall be his until the year of remission (for which Theodotion set down the Hebrew word itself, deror), but it shall return to the prince; but his inheritance shall belong to his sons. And the prince shall not take from the inheritance of the people by violence and from their possession, but from his own possession he shall give an inheritance to his sons, so that my people be not scattered, each one from his possession. Thus far the discourse about the prince; now precepts are given about his heirs, or rather about gifts, inheritance and legacies: to whom he ought to give, and whose the possession is, whether for a time or everlasting. 'If', he says, 'he gives either a gift or an inheritance to his sons, they will possess it, because it is owed to them by right of succession, and the possession cannot pass from one to another; but if he gives it to one of his servants under the name of a legacy, he will hold the possession only so long as until either the seventh year, of remission, which is called 'deror', or the jubilee, that is, the fiftieth, arrives, when every purchase and gift will return to its former owners: so that the servant has the use for a time, and the sons of the prince the ownership of the possession'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.46.18":["But what follows: And the prince shall not take from the inheritance of the people by violence (or, as the Seventy translated, so as to oppress them, or, as Aquila and Symmachus, afflict them and sadden them), but from his own possession he shall give an inheritance to his sons, censures not only the priests and princes of that time but also those of ours, who grow richer through the dignity of the priesthood and, beyond what is owed them by the Lord's ordinance, take from the poor by violence, or despoil the rich under the name of honor, so that even to their own sons, to whom the paternal inheritance is owed, they ought to leave nothing except what was left to them by their parents; therefore the priest who is richer than when he came to the priesthood ought to give whatever more he has, not to his sons, but to the poor and to the holy brothers and to those of the household of faith, who surpass the merits of his children, so that he may render to his Lord the things that are the Lord's — who speaks in the Gospel: Whatever you have done to one of these, you have done to me; for he himself, in the poor, is received in hospitality, visited in prison, clothed when naked, drinks when thirsty, is filled when hungry —: So that my people be not scattered from its possession: for if the prince has license either to take or to seize or to hold under the guise of honor what is not his and to leave it to his children, the people that had been gathered in the name of God will be scattered into parts and torn apart, or, according to the law, the inheritance of one may gradually pass to another and the cord of division perish, through which, as it happened, the inheritance came to each one; therefore he scatters the people of God and takes from it the everlasting possession of faith who leaves to his sons and brothers and kinsmen the goods of others, whether by plunder or by flattery or by blandishments or under the pretext of religion.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.46.19-20":["And he brought me in by the entrance that was at the side (or behind the back) of the gate into the chambers of the sanctuary for the priests (or exedras, and cells) that faced toward the north, and behold, there was a place sloping toward the west (or toward the sea, and, as the Seventy translated, separated); and he said to me: This is the place where the priests will cook for trespass and for sin (or for ignorance and for sin), where they will cook the sacrifice (or manaa, and gift), so that they do not carry it out into the outer court and the people be sanctified. And he brought me out into the outer court and led me around through the four corners of the court, and behold, there was a little court in a corner of the court, a little court in each corner of the court. In the four corners of the court little courts were laid out, of forty cubits in length and thirty in breadth: the four were of one measure. And a wall round about encircled the four little courts, and kitchens were built beneath the porticoes all around (or beside the couches, which Aquila translated στιβάδας [pallets], Symmachus περιφράγματα [enclosures], while Theodotion set down the Hebrew word itself, turoth). And he said, he says, to me: This is the house of the kitchens, in which the ministers of the house cook the victims of the people (or victim, for this is what zeba means in Hebrew). The man who held a reed in his hand and a mason's cord with which to measure the building: 'He brought me in', he says, 'by the entrance that was at the side of the gate (or behind the back of the gate) — and he did not state which gate, the eastern, the southern or that of another quarter, but left it uncertain —, so that I went to the place of the sanctuary and into the chambers, or rooms, of the priests, which faced toward the north and toward the sea': namely toward the northern quarter and toward the west, Holy Scripture having this custom, that by reason of the position of the land of Judah it always calls the west the sea — and it must be considered that the place of the priests where for trespass (or for ignorance) and for sin the sacrifices are cooked, lest they be carried out raw into the outer court, is set toward the north and the west, whence the coldest wind arises and where the light of the sun goes down; from which it is shown that whatever pertains to food is either for error or for sin: but if a sacrifice is offered even for ignorance, how much more for the consciousness of trespass!: whence Job too offered a victim every day in the morning for his sons, fearing lest they had transgressed through ignorance —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.46.20":["But what follows: 'So that they do not carry it out into the outer court and the people be sanctified, the people who had not been prepared for sanctification', signifies this: that we should not readily give holy things to the unsanctified; that we should not, according to the Gospel, cast pearls before swine nor give what is holy to dogs — indeed Moses wishes the people to be sanctified for three days from all uncleanness and from the embrace of wives, so that they may approach Mount Sinai and, sanctified, receive holy things. But if those about to approach to hear and receive the words of God are commanded to be sanctified from their wives, it is manifest that that apostolic saying too is contained in the law: Do not defraud one another, except perhaps by consent for a time, that you may be free for prayer; and that by consent one must abstain from wives, that we may be free for prayer —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.46.21-22":["After this it is written: And he brought me out into the outer court and led me around through the four corners of the court, and behold, there was a little court in a corner of the court, a little court in each corner of the court; in the four little courts of the corner, little courts laid out; that man whom he often mentions brought him out of the place of the priests which sloped toward the north, or which was separated, where the priests cook for trespass and for sin or ignorance; and he brought him out into the outer court: from which we understand that the court from which he went out was the inner one, and that there are many courts in the holy Scriptures — of which we read: You who stand in the house of the Lord, in the courts of the house of our God, and in John: And other sheep I have, which are not 'of this court'; and them too I must bring: and they will hear my voice, and there will be one 'court' and one shepherd — for this is what the Greek αὐλή means, which Latin simplicity translated as 'fold' —: for when he says 'of this court', he shows that there is another as well, which is pointed out either in the throng of the nations, as distinct from Israel, or in the heavens, as separate from the earthly court, and that to each office a court has been assigned according to the quality of merits, a subject that was argued more fully earlier —; and this court, of which the discourse now is, had four little courts, one in each corner — which the Seventy call 'lesser ones' and we, for ease of sense, have called ὑποκοριστικῶς [in diminutive form] 'little courts' —: they were of forty cubits in length and thirty in breadth, numbers which I remember having discussed in this same prophet: that the one refers to tribulations and straits, the other to the perfect age — whence Moses and Elijah and the Lord and Savior fasted forty days in the desert; and they come to the priestly office not, as the Seventy have it, after the twenty-fifth year of age, but after the thirtieth, for which reason the Lord too was thirty years old when he came to baptism, and in this same prophet the thirtieth year is set at the beginning. Where, therefore, there is food, there are also the straits and the temptation through which food is procured; and when we have come to the age of the perfect man, there everything must be trodden underfoot, and one must say: Having food and clothing, with these we are content, and: If the Lord gives me food to eat and clothing to cover me, or surely that saying: Food for the belly and the belly for foods: but God will destroy both it and them; for in the future we shall not eat and drink, but shall feed on that bread: Which came down from heaven, of which it is sung in the psalter: Man will eat the bread of angels, and: My food is to do the will of him who sent me —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.46.22":["And that the four little courts which occupied the corners of the greater court were of one measure signifies the four quarters of the world: that we eat bread in the sweat of our face and all our labor profits the belly and the dung.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.46.23":["And these four little courts one wall also encircled: And kitchens were built beneath the porticoes all around, or they had couches nearby, so that where the food was, there was also the preparing of the tables.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.46.24":["And lest the prophet perhaps not know what he was seeing, he said to him: This is the house of the kitchens, in which the ministers of the Lord will cook the victims of the people: victims of the people indeed, which are offered for trespass or for sin and ignorance, but nourishment and refreshment for the priests, so that they may remember that they ought to desire nothing else, since even in the temple refreshments for their flesh have been prepared.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.47.1":["And he turned me to the gate of the house, and behold, waters were going out beneath the threshold of the house toward the east; for the face of the house looked toward the east, and the waters were going down into the right side of the temple, to the south of the altar. And he led me out by the way of the gate of the north, and he turned me to the way outside, to the outer gate, the way that looked toward the east; and behold, waters overflowing from the right side. When the man who had a cord in his hand went out toward the east, he both measured a thousand cubits and led me across through the water up to the ankles (or he passed over in water, water of remission). And again he measured a thousand, and led me across through the water up to the knees (or and he passed over in water, water up to the thighs). And he measured a thousand, and led me across through the water up to the kidneys (or and he passed over the water up to the loins). And he measured a thousand, a torrent which I could not pass across, for the deep waters of the torrent had swelled, which could not be forded (for which the Seventy translated: And he measured a thousand, and he could not pass across, for the water was borne along like a headlong torrent which cannot be crossed). These waters which were going out beneath the threshold of the house, that is, of the temple, were not borne toward the north and toward the west but toward the east and on the right side of the temple, that is, toward the south, and that south not of just any place, but of the altar — from which it becomes clear that the waters are sacred and signify the teaching of our Savior, according to that which is written: Out of Zion shall go forth the law, and the word of the Lord from Jerusalem, and in another place: All things have been filled with the knowledge of the Lord, as much water covering the sea; of these waters the prophet Zechariah too prophesied, saying: On that day living water shall go out from Jerusalem, and the middle of it into the first sea, and the middle of it into the last sea; of these waters the Lord too spoke to the Samaritan woman: If you knew who it is who says to you: Give me to drink, you perhaps would have asked him, and he would have given you living water, and again: Everyone who drinks of this water will thirst again; but whoever drinks of the water that I shall give him will not thirst forever, and in the temple he cried out and said: If anyone thirsts, let him come to me and drink. He who believes in me, as the scripture says, rivers of living water will flow from his belly. But this he said of the Spirit whom those who believed in him were to receive; these are the waters of which the prophet spoke in the psalm: Beside the water of refreshment he has brought me up, and in Ezekiel: I will take you from the nations and from the lands, and I will sprinkle clean water upon you, and you shall be cleansed from all your uncleannesses. For there appeared the springs of waters, and in Psalm 92 (93) it is sung: The rivers have lifted up, O Lord, the rivers have lifted up their voices. From the voices of many waters, wonderful are the swellings of the sea; these are: The waters of Shiloah that go with silence, of which Isaiah speaks: You shall draw waters from the springs of the Savior, and the psalmist: Bless the Lord from the springs of Israel, and the same Isaiah of the Lord Savior: He, he says, shall dwell in a lofty cave of the strongest rock; bread shall be given him, and his water is faithful. For water has broken forth in the desert, and a valley in a thirsty land, and it is said to believers: Fear not, for I have redeemed you; you are mine. And if you pass through water, I am with you, and again: You who thirst, go to the water; and through Jeremiah God speaks: For my people has done evil things: they have forsaken me, the fountain of living water, and have dug for themselves cisterns, which cannot hold water. Just as, then, we read in a good sense the waters which go out from the threshold of the temple and are referred to the teaching of the Church, so there are bitter waters and most evil waters which the prophet Jeremiah forbids us to approach, saying: What have you to do with the way of Egypt, that you should drink of the waters of 'Geon'? — which in Hebrew is called 'Sior', that is, 'turbid' and 'muddy' —, of which God, angered over the heretics, speaks: Behold, I will feed them with distresses, and I will give them water of gall to drink; in which the Egyptian dragon reigns, who says: The rivers are mine, and I made them, who: Sees every high thing, and he himself is king of those who are in the waters; whence the man of the Church entreats the Lord and says: Save me, O Lord, for the waters have come in even to my soul. I am stuck fast in the mire of the deep, and there is no substance; I have come into the depth of the sea, and the tempest has overwhelmed me, and again: May I be delivered from those who hate me and from the depth of the waters. Let not the tempest of water cast me down, nor the deep swallow me up; nor let the pit press its mouth upon me, and in another psalm: Unless the Lord had been among us, let Israel now say, unless the Lord had been among us, when men rose up against us, perhaps they would have swallowed us alive; when their fury was angered against us, perhaps the water would have swallowed us up, and the bride in the Song of Songs: Much water will not be able to quench love, and rivers will not overwhelm it; this is the water of which Hosea says: Gilead has despised me, the city that works vanities, troubling the water, and its strength pirate men; which water is called by another name 'merra', that is, 'bitterness' — into which the wood of the Cross is cast, and bitter things are turned into sweet —; consider the law of Moses, if it is understood according to the carnal sense of the Jews, how bitter it is — digging out: An eye for an eye, a tooth for a tooth —: the fornication of Judah with Tamar, and Hosea joined to a harlot, Jeremiah's περίζωμα [loincloth], Moses' Ethiopian wife, the killing of the Egyptian, and a thousand other things which, if we understand them according to the letter that kills and not according to the spirit that gives life, are bitter and do not so much edify their reader as destroy him —. These waters, then, which go out from the temple of the Lord, proceed toward the east and go down into the right side of the temple, to the south of the altar, where the bridegroom feeds his guests and reclines.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.47.2":["But what follows: And he led me out by the way of the gate of the north, and turned to the way outside, to the outer gate, the way that looked toward the east, secretly shows that we cannot come at once to the eastern gate unless we come to it by the way of the gate of the north, by a long circuit: for unless we overcome the coldest wind by the heat of faith and trample its regions — so that that apostolic saying may be fulfilled in us: But God will crush Satan under your feet swiftly —, we are not able to enter the gate through which the waters enter and are on the right side; and note that these waters, which go out through the gate of the east, flee the left-hand parts to such a degree that they are described as proceeding from the right of the altar toward the south.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.47.3":["'But that man who had the cord in his hand, when he had led me through the gate of the north, outside, to the way that looked toward the east, and he himself too was in the same place, measured a thousand cubits of the same water'. And he led me across through the water up to the 'ankles' — which Aquila and Symmachus and Theodotion translated ἀστραγάλους [anklebones] — (for which the Seventy translated: But he passed over in water, water of remission), which we can understand to signify the first sins of men, which are remitted to us as we enter the waters of the Lord, and they show the saving grace of baptism, and are the beginnings of progress; finally, they reach as far as the ankles, which are neighbors to the sole and the heel — which lies open to the bites of the snake, as the Lord says: You shall watch for his head, and he shall watch for your heel; and in the Psalter it is said of Judas the betrayer: He who ate my bread has magnified tripping-up against me (or the heel: for this is what the Greek πτέρνα means).\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.47.4":["And after the thousand cubits which reach as far as the ankle: He measured another thousand cubits in the water and led me across up to the knees: since after the remission of sins and the beginnings of progress, when we strive to climb a little from earthly things to higher ones, we bend the knees to the Lord — as the Apostle says: That every knee should bend, of things in heaven and on earth and under the earth, and every tongue confess that the Lord Jesus Christ is in the glory of God the Father —. Thirdly: He measured a thousand cubits, and led me, he says, across through the water up to the kidneys: for by these steps we come to the heights, which heights themselves, however, reach up to the loins and kidneys, so that all lust in us may be cut off, and we may possess the sanctification of the body without which no one will see God — whence also in this same prophet God is seen fiery from the feet up to the loins: for the works of the loins need fire and purgation, as the righteous man says: For my loins have been filled with illusions, through which the enemy mocks us both by day and in the phantasm of the nights; but from the loins up to the head and crown he shines with the brilliance of electrum, the most precious metal, so that he has in himself nothing of filth, nothing of baseness —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.47.5":["Whence now fourthly it is said: And he measured a thousand, 'cubits' being understood, a torrent — which the Seventy did not translate —, which I could not pass across (much better than the Seventy, who said: which he could not pass across): for the prophet, and all human nature, cannot after the loins pass across the torrent of thoughts and the incitements of vices; but that man: Who was clothed in 'baddi' [linen], and the guide of the prophet, clearly passes across: Who did no sin, nor was guile found in his mouth. And he gives the reasons why the prophet could not pass across the fourth thousand cubits: For, he says, the deep waters of the torrent had swelled, which cannot be forded — and how is it written, with the prophet glorying: Our soul has passed across the torrent? But it is easily resolved if we know that in place of this it is written in the Hebrew: The torrent has passed across my soul —. Of this torrent Isaiah too speaks: Behold, I incline upon them like a river of peace, and like an overflowing torrent glory, and in Psalm 35 (36) it is said of the saints: In the protection of your wings they will hope, they will be inebriated with the abundance of your house, and with the torrent of your delights you will give them drink; for with you is the fountain of life, and in Psalm 124 (125): Turn back, O Lord, our captivity, like a torrent in the south, and of the Savior: He will drink of the torrent on the way — for who of men can boast that he has a chaste heart?, or: Into whose mind has the death of concupiscence not entered through the windows of the eyes and, to say little, a tickling of the soul? For the world is set in the evil one, and: From childhood the heart of man is set on evil, so that from the very beginning of birth the human condition is not without sin for even a single day; whence David too confesses in the psalm: 'For behold, I was conceived in iniquities, and in sins my mother conceived me, not by the iniquities of my mother or indeed my own, but in the iniquities of the human condition'; whence the Apostle too says: Death reigned from Adam to Moses, even over those who did not sin in the likeness of the transgression of Adam —. That we say 'cubitos' [cubits] in the masculine gender and not, according to the rule of the grammarians, in the neuter 'cubita', I have also taught above: that we do this not from ignorance but from custom, for the sake also of the simple and unlearned, whose number in the congregation of the Church is the greater.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.47.6-8":["And he said to me: Surely you have seen, son of man; and he led me out, and turned me to the bank of the torrent (or of the river). And when I had turned (or he had turned me), behold, on the bank of the torrent (or of the river) a great many trees (or very many trees) on either side; and he said to me: These waters, which go out to the mounds of sand of the east and go down to the plains of the desert (or this water which goes out into Galilee, which slopes toward the east, and goes down to Arabia), will enter the sea; and they will go out, and the waters will be healed: and every living soul (or of animals) that creeps (or of creeping things), wherever the torrent (or the river) comes, will live, and there will be very many fish after these waters have come there, and all things will be healed and will live to which the torrent (or the river) comes. And fishermen will stand over them from En-gedi to En-eglaim; there will be a drying of nets; very many kinds of its fish there will be, like the fish of the great sea, of exceeding multitude. But in the places that go out beyond the banks and are marshy they will not be healed, but will be given over to salt-pits. And over the torrent (or river) there will rise on its banks on either side every fruit tree (or fruit-bearing tree); its leaf will not fall from it (or nothing will grow old from it), and its fruit will not fail: each month it will bring forth early fruits (or all things will be renewed), because its waters will go out from the sanctuary, and its fruit will be for food, and its leaves for medicine (or and its ascent for health). I wished to set down the whole passage together, although it is lengthy, so as not to break in upon the sense of the reader, and so that the order of the reading, divided into parts, might not confuse the understanding of the hearer. And first it must be said that for 'torrent', which in Hebrew is called 'nehel' and in the Greek tongue is named χειμάρρος, the Seventy translated 'river'; next, that for 'Galilee', which in Hebrew is called 'galila', Aquila translated θινάς, which means 'mounds' 'of sand', Symmachus μεθόριον, which we can translate 'border-region'; for 'Arabia' too Symmachus translated 'uninhabitable', Aquila 'low places' or 'plains', Theodotion 'arabas' [a transliteration of the Hebrew word]. Let us say, then, what seems right to us in each point.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.47.6":["That man, who was Ezekiel's guide, admonishes the prophet to look more carefully and see, and to attend with the eyes of the mind to hidden mysteries; and he calls him 'son of man' either as a figure of the Lord Savior — for 'Hiezechiel' means 'strength' or 'dominion of God' —, or certainly as a reminder of human frailty — lest he forget his condition while great things are shown to him —; and having admonished him he leads him and turns him to the bank of the torrent so that, since he could not cross the middle depth, he might at least recognize the things that are on the banks.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.47.7":["And when, he says, I had turned (or he had turned me, who was guide and admonisher), behold, on the bank of the torrent (or of the river) many trees (or a great many trees) exceedingly, on either side: this 'torrent', which on account of the abundance of its waters is borne along in the manner of a torrent and receives rains from heaven, of which we spoke in the preceding reading, is itself called by the Seventy 'river', because it has perpetual waters, not collected from rains here and there but abiding from a living and perpetual spring — of which river we read many things in the holy scriptures, but for the present a few things must be said, and first that: The rush of the river makes glad the city of God, and in another place: The river of God is filled with waters; you have prepared their food, for so is your preparation, and again in the plural: The rivers have lifted up, O Lord, the rivers have lifted up their voices. From the voices of many waters, wonderful are the swellings of the sea, and in Isaiah: I will make a way in the desert, and rivers in the pathless place, and in another place of the same it will appear more plainly that in Zion a river runs down, glorious in a thirsty land —; above this river there were very many trees on either side of the bank, so that, namely, the river ran enclosed between the two instruments, the old and the new; and the many trees, or the very many and exceedingly numerous trees, I take to be those which scripture records in paradise as abounding with various fruits.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.47.8":["And the guide who goes before, the teacher, teaches the prophet and says: These waters, which go out (or into Galilee of the nations according to the Seventy, or, as is more truly contained in the Hebrew:) to the mounds of sand of the east and go down to the plains of the desert (or to Arabia), will enter the sea (or to the outlets of the sea), and the waters will be healed; we said long ago that the waters signify either the grace of baptism or the teaching of the Gospel, which, if they go out from the threshold of the temple of the Lord and hold to the apostolic discipline, make the mounds of sand, previously sterile and unfruitful, to be fruit-bearing, and so water all the plains and deserts as to express among themselves too the mystery of the spring of Jericho — which Elisha healed with the evangelic and apostolic salt, to such a degree that where there had previously been sterility and death, there was fertility and life —; nor did they heal the deserts only, but they enter the eastern sea — namely the 'Dead' sea, in which nothing living could exist, and the 'most bitter' sea, which in Greek they call λίμνην ἀσφαλτῖτιν, that is, 'lake of bitumen' —; and in a wonderful manner the dead waters are healed by the waters of the Gospel — which, from the fact that they have nothing living in them, have obtained the name of death: for they did not know him who says: I am the life; and in truth, according to the letter, up to now nothing that breathes and can move about can be found in this sea because of its excessive bitterness: not even little snails and small worms and eels and the other kinds of animals or creeping things whose little bodies we can know better than their names; finally, if the Jordan, swollen by rains, flowing in there, has carried fish along, they die at once and float on the fat waters —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.47.9-10":["Since, then, these waters have nothing of usefulness, as the plain account testifies, even if it should come about, as the foolish superstition of the Jews believes, according to the spiritual understanding, healed by the torrent of the Lord, they will have very many fish, and all things to which this torrent comes will live, so that fishermen stand over the banks from 'Engaddi' to 'Engallim' — of which the first means 'spring' or 'eye' 'of the kid', the next 'spring, or eye' 'of calves': En-eglaim is at the beginning of the Dead Sea, where the Jordan enters, and En-gedi where it ends and is consumed; and I think the Dead Sea is the one of which Zechariah speaks: On that day living water will go out from Jerusalem, and the middle of it into the first sea; Daniel too agrees with these words: I was seeing, and behold, the four winds of heaven were rushing into the great sea, and David: Wonderful, he says, are the upliftings of the sea, and in the person of the Savior in the psalm: I have come into the depth of the sea, and the tempest has overwhelmed me; the king of Egypt too, according to this same Ezekiel, is called a dragon who dwells in the sea and fans the rivers as with his horns, and again: This great and spacious sea; which, as long as it has not received into itself the water of the river or torrent, kills everything it has in itself: but the Lord — of whom it was said: He will strike: and he will heal after two days, and on the third day we shall rise and live in his sight — himself speaks in the same prophet Hosea: I bound Ephraim, I took him up in my arm, and they did not know that I healed them in the corruption of men — he who: Was wounded for our sins and made weak for our iniquities; the discipline of his peace is upon him, and by his bruise we have been healed —; he himself healed by his death this sea, which is most salty and dead from excessive bitterness, he who says through Isaiah: The Spirit of the Lord is upon me, because he has anointed me; he has sent me to preach the gospel to the poor, to heal those who are contrite of heart, whence through Jeremiah too he cries out: Turn back, you turning sons, and I will heal your contritions — for the people, impatient and not enduring to bear the pain of its wounds any longer, had said: The summer has passed, the harvest has gone by, and we are not saved, whence the Lord also answered it: Is there no resin in Gilead, or are there no physicians there? Why has the healing of my people not gone up? —, and Jeremiah himself cries aloud and says: Heal me, O Lord, and I shall be healed; save me and I shall be saved; finally the angels — who were the guardians of the Jews at that time when the foolish multitude cried out and said: His blood be upon us and upon our children, and the veil of the temple was torn and all the mysteries of the Hebrews were laid open — answered the Lord who commanded them, and said: We have treated Babylon and she is not healed; let us leave her, namely the city of confusion and of vices — whence Josephus too relates in his History that, after the Lord was crucified and the veil of the temple was torn, or the lintel of the temple broke and fell, a voice of the heavenly powers was heard in the inner shrines of the temple: Let us pass over from these seats —. Let all this have been said not superfluously but necessarily, because the Dead Sea is said to have been healed when the river of the Lord flowed into it —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.47.10":["Over this sea — from 'Engaddi', 'the eye and spring of the kid', which is always offered for sins, up to 'Engallim', 'the spring of calves', which are slaughtered to the Lord and imitate the calf that puts forth horns and hoofs, which are slaughtered at the altar in type of the Savior — there will be fishermen, to whom Jesus speaks: Come to me and I will make you fishermen, of whom Jeremiah too: Behold, I, he says, will send fishermen; and very many species, or rather kinds, of fish will be in the sea once dead, which fish Peter drew out on the right-hand side at the Lord's command, and they were one hundred and fifty-three, so that the nets were breaking because of their multitude — and those who have written on the natures of animals, and especially those who have published ἁλιευτικά [works on fishing] in the Latin as well as the Greek tongue — of whom Opianicus the Cilician is a most learned poet —, say that there are one hundred and fifty-three kinds of fish; all of which were caught by the apostles, and nothing remained uncaught, while both noble and ignoble, both rich and poor, and every kind of men is drawn out of the sea of this world to salvation —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.47.11":["But what follows: On its shores and in the marshy places (or in those which go out from the shore) the waters will not be healed, secretly shows that whoever was not in Noah's ark perishes while the flood reigns, and that those whom this river has not touched do not receive healing: But they will be given, he says, to salt-pits, according to that which is written: When the pestilent man is scourged, the fool will be wiser — for the examples of the worse instruct the good —, or: They will be given to salt-pits, according to that which is written in the Gospel: Salt is good; but if the salt has become foolish, it is useful for nothing, so that they may lack crops and greenness forever — which a city too, sprinkled with salt after its ruin, demonstrates —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.47.12":["But over the torrent (or the river) there will rise on its banks on either side every fruit tree (or, as all have translated with one voice, βρώσιμον, 'that which provides food and nourishment' and 'that which can be eaten'; and in the Hebrew tongue it is called 'machal'). And: (nothing, according to the Seventy, will grow old in it, but its produce will be renewed daily, or:) its leaf will not fall from it, and its fruit will not fail, according to that which is written in Psalm 1: And he will be like the tree that is planted beside the runnings of waters, which will give its fruit in its season; and its leaf will not fall. Each month, he says, it will bring forth early fruits (or, as is written in the Seventy: its fruit in its renewal will give early fruits), so that all the fruits of believers may be firstfruits and the single months may be assigned to the single apostles; and this will come about: Because its waters will go out from the sanctuary: lest we think that so great an abundance of produce belongs to the banks or the trees or the months, he gives the causes of the fertility and adds: Because its waters will go out from the sanctuary — So then it is not of him who wills nor of him who runs, but of God who shows mercy —. The primacy among all these trees is held by the tree of life, which is understood as wisdom, of which it is written: She is a tree of life to all who believe in her. That which is said: And its fruit will be for food, and its leaf for medicine, shows the mysteries of the divine books, of which the one pertains to the letter, the other to the spirit, so that we understand the simple words in the leaves, but in the fruits the hidden sense: for just as the knowledge of the scriptures leads to the kingdoms of heaven and offers us the bread who says: I am the bread who came down from heaven, so its leaves, which contain moral teaching, bestow health, so as to heal the wounds of those who sin. For 'leaves', which in Hebrew are called 'ale', the Seventy, on account of the likeness of the word and of the sound, translated 'ascent', which itself too can be explained thus: that, after the food of the fruits, we may climb by the admonitions of the words to heavenly things.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.47.13":["Thus says the Lord God. This is the boundary by which you shall possess the land in the twelve tribes of Israel, because Joseph has a double cord; and you shall possess it — each one equally as his brother — the land which I lifted up my hand to give to your fathers; and this land shall fall to you as a possession. In place of what we said: Because Joseph has a double cord, the Seventy translated: Increase of the cord — for 'Ioseph' means 'increase —, understanding a thing in place of a name and confusing the mind of the reader: for when the Levitical tribe had been subtracted and assigned to the sacrifices of the temple, there remained eleven tribes, of which Joseph was divided into two, Ephraim and Manasseh, who said to Joshua son of Nun: Why have you given me a possession of one lot and one cord, though I am of so great a multitude and the Lord has blessed me?, to whom Joshua answered: If you are a numerous people, go up into the forest and cut down spaces for yourself in the land of the Perizzite and of Rephaim, since the possession of mount Ephraim is narrow for you, and again: And Joshua said to the house of Ephraim and Manasseh: You are a numerous people and of great strength; you shall not have one lot, but you shall pass over to the mountain and cut down and clear for yourself spaces for dwelling; and you will be able to proceed further when you have overthrown the Canaanite, who you say has iron chariots and is very strong; I have said this for this reason, because scripture now records that Joseph, that is, Ephraim and Manasseh, has a double cord.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.47.14":["And the individual tribes receive equal spaces of land according to their multitude, by no means at the discretion of the one dividing but by lot, which rests in the power of the Lord. Finally Joshua himself too, who was the divider of the land, did not set apart his own land for himself, lest he should seem to have chosen the best, but received from the princes of all the tribes: Timnath-serah in mount Ephraim.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.47.15-17":["And this is the boundary of the land: toward the northern quarter, from the Great Sea, by the way of Ethalon, for those coming to Sadada, Hamath, Berotha, Sabarim, which is between the middle of Damascus and the border of Hamath, the court of Thicon, which is beside Hauran (or of Auranitis); and the boundary shall be from the sea up to 'the court of Enom' (or 'Aser-Enan'), the boundary of Damascus; and from north to north the boundary of Hamath, the northern quarter. Between the description of the holy land of the twelve tribes by Joshua son of Nun and the one which is now commanded to be made through Ezekiel there is this difference: that there the land is divided among the tribes by lot, here it is assigned by the discretion of the Lord who commands. In the book of Numbers, then, in which the whole land of promise is divided by four quarters in brief speech, this is the description from the north, that is, from the northern quarter: Moreover, on the northern side the boundaries will begin from the Great Sea, reaching as far as the Highest Mountain, from which you will come into Hamath, up to the borders of Sedada. And the borders will go as far as Zephrona and 'the village of Enan' (or 'Aser-Enan'). And the Hebrews say that the northern quarter begins from the Great Sea, which stretches along the shores of Palestine, of Phoenicia, and of the Syria which is called Coele, and of Cilicia, and extends through Egypt to Libya. But as to what he says: Reaching — the boundaries — as far as the Highest Mountain, the same Hebrews assert that it signifies either Mount Amanus or the Taurus — which seems truer to us —. From which, he says, you will come into 'Emath', which is now named 'Epiphania', its name having been changed by Antiochus, the cruelest of tyrants, for he had the surname Ἐπιφανής [Epiphanes, the Manifest]. Up to the borders, he says, of Sedada, which place is also set down in the present reading of Ezekiel. And the borders will go as far as Zephrona, which city they today call Zephyrium, a town of Cilicia. And what follows: And 'the village of Enan' (for which in the Hebrew is written 'Aser-Enon' or '-Enan'), the present passage also contains. And meanwhile let it suffice to have touched on it briefly according to the history and to have joined side by side the description both of Numbers and of Ezekiel, so that the prudent reader may understand in which points they record either the same or neighboring or different things; now, having interpreted the names of each, let us follow the ἀναγωγήν [anagogy, the higher sense], and let us set out, the Lord granting it, what seems right to us in each point. 'Ethalon' means 'cradle of grief'; 'Sedada', 'his side' — understand it from 'side', not from 'breadth'; 'Emath', 'fury', which in Greek is called χόλος; 'Berotha', 'wells'; 'Sabarim', 'circuit of mountains'; 'Damascus', 'blood of sackcloth' and 'of a kiss' or 'of a cup'; but the 'house' or 'court of Thicon' Symmachus translates 'the middle court', which goes on to the borders of 'Auran'; therefore 'Thicon' means 'middle'; 'Auran' — which the Seventy turned into the Greek tongue as 'Auranitis' —, 'wrath'; but 'Asar' '-Enon' — which is the boundary of Damascus — is called in our language 'court of the spring'. The northern quarter, then, begins from the Great Sea and goes by the way of 'Ethalon', which means 'cradle of grief'; through penitence it comes to 'Sadada', where there is a turning aside 'from the side': for we understand 'Emath', that is, the 'indignation' of God, impending over us, or, 'Emath' having been found to be 'truth', the 'wells' of 'Berotha' are opened to us, which the princes Abraham, Isaac and Jacob dug, and by a long 'circuit' we come to the 'mountains' of 'Sabarim', which are set between 'Damascus', that is, 'the blood of haircloth', and the border of 'Emath', that is, as we said above, 'of truth' — for just as through grief we turn aside impending evils and avoid them from the side, so after the blood and haircloth of a bloody life, before we come to the borders of Emath, we go around and come to the mountains of which it is written: Mountains round about it, and the Lord round about his people —, which place is called 'the court of Thicon', that is, 'the middle court', as Symmachus translated, beside the borders of 'Auran', which means 'wrath' — so that we may say with the prophet: I will bear the wrath of the Lord, because I have sinned against him —, and from the sea up to the borders of Damascus: in which the apostle Paul too, from Jewish fury and the shedding of the Church's blood, beheld the light through blindness and, having taken up the haircloth for his old persecution, preached the Gospel, so that he might come to the eternal 'court of the spring' and, once set in the north, might lay hold of the boundary of truth.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.47.18":["But the eastern quarter, from the midst of Hauran and from the midst of Damascus and from the midst of Gilead and from the midst of the land of Israel, the Jordan dividing, to the eastern sea; you shall measure the eastern quarter. For which in the book of Numbers I have found it written thus: Thence they shall measure the boundaries toward the eastern quarter from 'the village of Enan' (that is, 'Aser-Enon') as far as Sephama, and from Sephama the boundaries will go down to Rebla, opposite the spring; thence they will come toward the east to the sea of Chinnereth, and they will extend as far as the Jordan, and at the last they will be closed by the sea. Above he had said: And the boundary shall be from the sea up to Eser-Enon, the boundary of Damascus: from the end of the northern quarter, then, that is, from the court of Enan, the boundaries extend according to the book of Numbers as far as Sephama, which the Hebrews name Apamea, and from Apamea the boundaries will go down to Rebla, which is now called Antioch of Syria; and so that you may know that this Rebla signifies the city which is now the most renowned in Coele-Syria, there follows: Opposite the spring, which it is evident signifies Daphne, from which spring the aforesaid city enjoys most abundant waters — this place was planted by the military hand of Gnaeus Pompey, who first subjected Judea to Roman rule, and from him — following the fable of the poets — it received the name of Daphne, on account of the laurels and cypresses, trees which are very frequent in that very place too —. Thence, he says, the boundaries will come toward the eastern quarter to the sea of Chinnereth: as far as the lake of Tiberias; and it is called a sea although it has sweet waters, according to the idiom of the scriptures, which call gatherings of waters seas. And the boundaries, he says, will extend as far as the Jordan, and at the last will be closed by the sea: either by the Dead Sea or, as others think, by the tongue of the Red Sea, on whose shore Aila is situated, where a legion and garrison of the Romans is now stationed. Let this have been said according to the book of Numbers. But according to Ezekiel: from the midst of Hauran, which is a town of Damascus in the wilderness, and from the midst of Gilead, which, a mountain joined to the hills of Lebanon, fell into the lot of Reuben and Gad and of the half tribe of Manasseh, and is behind Phoenicia and Arabia — to which mountain Jacob came, having set out from Carrhae, and was overtaken by Laban; of which Jeremiah too speaks: Gilead, you are to me the beginning of Lebanon; which Gilead, son of Machir, son of Manasseh, took from the region of the Amorites —; and the end of this quarter: the river Jordan bounding it, and the eastern sea, which in this place they understand as the Dead Sea, not the tongue of the Red Sea. Let us speak according to tropology. After the boundaries of the north, by a long circuit through Ethalon and Sedada and Berotha, Sabarim too and Thicon and Auran and Damascus and the court of Enom, the eastern quarter reaches as far as Emath: from the midst of the 'wrath' of 'Auran', and from the midst of 'Damascus', namely of the 'blood' 'of penitence', and from the midst of 'Galaad', which means 'revelation' or 'mound of testimony': so that, after wrath and penitence, it may show the hope of salvation; and from the midst of the land of 'Israel', which contains the 'vision' 'of peace', it reaches to the most sweet streams of the Jordan, which, with the sea set far apart, attains the light of the east, so that it is ended by the 'river' 'Iordanis', which means 'stream of judgment', and by the boundary of the 'torrent'; but what is set down in the Seventy, 'phoeniconos', that is, 'of the palm grove', is not found in the Hebrew, for which we translated 'you shall measure': and the error is manifest, because for 'thamoddu' [you shall measure], deceived by the likeness of the letters 'daleth' and 'res' [d and r], they read 'thamorru' [palm].\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.47.19":["But the southern quarter, the meridional, from Tamar up to the waters of contradiction (or to the waters of 'Mariboth' — for these mean 'contradiction' or διαδίκασμοι, that is, 'disputes' —) of Kadesh too, and the torrent up to the Great Sea; this is the southern quarter toward the south. For which in the book of Numbers it is written thus: It will begin from the wilderness of Sin, which is beside Edom, and will have as boundaries toward the east the sea. And they will go around the southern quarter by the Ascent of the Scorpion, so that they pass Senna and come into the south as far as Kadesh-barnea, whence the borders will go out to a village named Addar, and will extend as far as Asemona. And the boundary will go around from Asemona as far as the torrent of Egypt, and will be ended by the shore of the Great Sea. Which briefly signifies this — lest on the occasion of Ezekiel we seem to pass over to a very broad exposition of Numbers —: that the boundary goes around from the wilderness of Sin, which is beside Edom, and from the Red Sea, and, through the Ascent of the Scorpion and through Senna and Kadesh-barnea and the court of Addar and Asemona, reaches as far as the torrent of Egypt, which flows into the sea beside the city of Rhinocorura; but this boundary of the southern quarter, that is, the meridional, begins from Tamar — which is a city in the wilderness, which Solomon too built with wondrous works, and today it is named Palmyra, and in the Hebrew tongue it is called 'thamar', which in our language means 'palm' — up to the waters of contradiction of Kadesh, which beyond doubt is in the desert, and the torrent entering the Great Sea, this sea which stretches along the shores of Egypt and Palestine, and this is the southern quarter toward the south. After the quarter of the north, then, and of the east, when the sun of righteousness has risen for us, we receive the meridional quarter, when the whole light is poured upon us from above, and with Abraham we enter the banquet and are inebriated with wine — which makes glad the heart of man — with Joseph and his brothers; and this possession and these boundaries begin from 'Thamar', namely from the 'palm' and the victory over vices, up to the waters of contradiction: for virtues are always contradicted — whence the Jews too, disputing against Paul, assert that the preaching of the Gospel is contradicted in the whole world; and in the Gospel we read, as Simeon says: This one will be for the ruin and resurrection of many, and for a sign that will be contradicted —, but all contradiction is of 'holiness', which 'Cades' means — as the psalmist says: The Lord will shake the desert of Cades: which, namely, at first did not have the holiness of the Lord, but was shaken and moved, so as to receive God as its guest, who says: Upon whom will my spirit rest, if not upon the humble and quiet and the one who trembles at my words? —; after the desert of Cades, up to the Great Sea, in the south of the southern quarter, is the torrent of which we spoke above, which has rains not from the earth but from heaven, collected from the variety of diverse virtues.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.47.20":["And the quarter of the sea, the Great Sea, from the border straight on until you come to Hamath; this is the quarter of the sea. Of which the book of Numbers writes more plainly and more briefly: But the western quarter will begin from the Great Sea, and will be closed by that same end, that is, from sea to sea: namely from the torrent of Rhinocorura, which flows into the sea, up to that place which is opposite Hamath, the city of Syria, of which we spoke above. But the west, according to the laws of tropology, is always in the sea, always in the brine and the waves, where every day shipwrecks are stirred up, and the deaths of the wretched, and the loss of riches and merchandise; and yet, when we have broken all these things by patience, we come straight on to 'Emath', that is, to the 'truth' of the Lord, who, once persecutions are overcome, has promised us eternal rewards.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.47.21-23":["And you shall divide this land for yourselves by the tribes of Israel; and you shall cast it as an inheritance, for yourselves and for the strangers who have come to you, who will have begotten sons in your midst, and they shall be to you as natives among the sons of Israel: with you they shall divide the possession in the midst of the tribes of Israel; and in whatever tribe the stranger shall be, there you shall give him a possession, says the Lord God. Through this passage we learn — and the haughtiness of the Jews is struck down — that nothing is divided between Israel and the people of the Gentiles: for if the land is divided to strangers and foreigners together with those who are of the people of Israel, that is, the natives, no one doubts that the inheritance is the same for the people of the Gentiles and for the people of the Jews, if only they have been converted to the worship of the God of Israel — which properly possesses the name Christian —, the Jews having the letter of the law and we the spirit, they holding the parchments and we him who is written in the parchments; and with Moses the land is divided by lot, but here, as we said, it is assigned by the judgment of the Lord, and there is one possession for the stranger and for Israel, and in the individual tribes the inheritance is given, at the command of the Lord God.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.48.1-7":["And these are the names of the tribes: from the borders of the north, beside the way of Ethalon, for those going to Hamath, Court Enon, the border of Damascus toward the north beside Hamath; and its eastern side shall be the sea, Dan one. And from the border of Dan, from the eastern side to the side of the sea, Asher one. And above the border of Asher, from the eastern side to the side of the sea, Naphtali one. And above the border of Naphtali, from the eastern side to the side of the sea, Manasseh one. And above the border of Manasseh, from the eastern side to the side of the sea, Ephraim one. And above the border of Ephraim, from the eastern side to the side of the sea, Reuben one. And above the border of Reuben, from the eastern side to the side of the sea, Judah one. Now that the possession of the holy land has been described, both according to the present prophecy of Ezekiel and according to the book of Numbers, the possession of the individual tribes is now described, from the eastern side to the side of the west, that is, of the sea. And first they receive their boundaries from the north, beside the way of Ethalon, for those going to Hamath, 'Court' — or 'Aser' — Enon, the border of Damascus toward the north beside Hamath, of which we have already spoken before; and the tribe of Dan begins first, Asher second, Naphtali third, Manasseh fourth, Ephraim fifth, Reuben sixth, Judah seventh: that is, on the left side, and the number seven is completed. — Then the firstfruits of the priests and of the Levites, and the sanctuary of the Lord in the midst, and the Holy of Holies; the city itself also is described, foursquare, having four thousand and five hundred reeds on each side; the suburbs of the priests and of the Levites are also described in order, and the possession of the prince; and again on the right side follow five tribes, likewise from the side of the east to the side of the west, of which the first is Benjamin, the second Simeon, having its own possession specially and by no means mixed with Judah as before, the third Issachar, the fourth Zebulun, the fifth Gad; and the gates of the city, three on each side, and the name of the city itself, which is contained on the plate of gold on the forehead of the high priest — about all of which we shall discourse in their places —. This too is to be noted: that the names of the forty-eight cities which in the volume of Numbers are given to the Levitical tribe to dwell in, with their suburbs, are passed over in silence here, and so are the cities of the fugitives, of which three beyond the Jordan, namely Bezer and Ramoth and Golan, were given in the two and a half tribes, and three on this side of the Jordan, namely Kedesh and Shechem and Kiriath-arba: for there was no need, in a mystical description and in the dwelling of the saints and a perfect way of life, to describe the refuges of fugitives, which Israel needed while still set upon the earth and hastening through many labors and dangers to come to this city, and saying in each of the saints: I am a stranger and a sojourner, as all my fathers were, and in another place: I have dwelt with those who dwell in Kedar; much has my soul sojourned.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.48.8":["And above the border of Judah, from the eastern side to the side of the sea, shall be the firstfruits which you shall set apart, twenty-five thousand in breadth, and in length as each of the portions from the eastern side to the side of the sea; and the sanctuary shall be in the midst of it. The firstfruits which you shall set apart for the Lord: the length twenty-five thousand, and the breadth ten thousand. It is superfluous to discuss again what has been discussed, and to labor once more, as though they had not been said, over things on which we labored above. After the boundaries of the seven tribes, the firstfruits are described from the east to the west, whose breadth is to hold twenty-five thousand reeds, but the length as much as the individual tribes hold from the east to the sea; the number of this length is uncertain, for no measure was set down in the description of the individual tribes; but if the breadth has twenty-five thousand reeds, one may infer that the number of the length, which is always greater than the breadth, is much greater.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.48.9":["In the midst, moreover, the sanctuary is placed, beside the firstfruits which are set apart for the Lord, of a length of twenty-five thousand reeds and a breadth of ten thousand — reading which, we can say nothing else but that saying of the Apostle: O the depth of the riches and of the wisdom and of the knowledge of God! How inscrutable are his judgments, and untraceable his ways!, and in another place: That you may be able to comprehend with all the saints what is the breadth and length and height and depth, to know also the love of Christ which surpasses knowledge, that you may be filled unto all the fullness of God —. The number twenty-five, moreover, is referred to the senses — of which it was said: You shall possess a divine sense —, and that of ten thousand to perfect and consummate virtue — which by the decalogue of the old instrument reaches to the mystery of the Gospel; we touched on this briefly before, and here we shall recall it in part: for if, adding from one, you reach four, you will fill up the number ten, and so it will come about that the Old Testament is bound together in the New, and the New is expanded in the Old —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.48.10-11":["The sanctuary, moreover (that is, the temple) of the Lord, shall be in the midst of the priestly firstfruits, and it will be walled about on every part with equal sides; and because he had said: These moreover shall be the firstfruits of the sanctuary of the priests, lest under the common term all priests should claim for themselves the possession of this place and the ceremonies to be observed, he sets it down specifically, adding: The sanctuary shall be for the priests, of the sons of 'Sadoc' — which means 'righteous', of whom it is said: The Lord is righteous and has loved righteousnesses; his face has seen equity; for 'Sadoc' the Seventy write 'Saduc'. This Sadoc speaks in the Gospel: For the Father judges no one, but has given all judgment to the Son, and of him, under the name of Solomon, the prophet mystically sings: O God, give your judgment to the king, and your righteousness to the king's son —. Those priests, however, shall possess the sanctuary of the Lord and keep his ceremonies: Who did not go astray when the children of Israel and the Levites went astray, about whom there was discussion in the earlier parts: they do not offer victims but, content with the privilege of the name and with the humility of their error, always say in their heart: I know my iniquity, and my sin is ever against me — let the priestly rank hear this, and he who was overcome by the heat of the Arian persecution and afterwards bowed his neck to the true faith lest he lack wealth and power: let it be enough for him if he is not expelled from the temple, if he holds some shadow and image of his former name, and let him not, lolling back and belching up delicacies, as though spotless and pure, belch upon us from a most lofty throne the nausea of his ignorance and barbarous garrulity —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.48.10":["These moreover shall be the firstfruits of the sanctuary of the priests: toward the north twenty-five thousand in length, and toward the sea ten thousand in breadth, and also toward the east ten thousand in breadth, and toward the south twenty-five thousand in length; and the sanctuary of the Lord shall be in the midst of it: the sanctuary for the priests, of the sons of Zadok, who kept my ceremonies and did not go astray when the children of Israel went astray, as the Levites went astray. And there shall be firstfruits from the firstfruits of the land, the Holy of Holies, beside the border of the Levites. The four sides of the firstfruits of the sanctuary are described — a portion which belongs not to the people or to the common crowd, nor to the Levites, a lower rank, but properly to the worship of the priests —: namely of the north and of the west and of the east and of the south; and part by part the order of progress is told: so that, leaving the cold of the north, we may come to the west, where, that is, our vices may set and perish, and thence pass over to the east, and, when the sun of righteousness has risen for us, come to the south, in which the light is brightest and perfect; and both from the north to the sea and from the east to the south there are to be twenty-five thousand in length and ten thousand in breadth, so that everywhere the same measure of length and breadth may be kept.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.48.12":["For there shall be, he says, for 'them' firstfruits from the firstfruits of the land — that is, for the priests, and for those Who did not go astray when the children of Israel and the Levites went astray; and firstfruits from firstfruits, as tithes of tithes —, and the Holy of Holies beside the border of the Levites, so that the Levites indeed may have the borders adjoining the priests, but may not be able to hold the navel, that is, its middle, and the possession itself.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.48.13":["The possession of the Levites is also described, which is indeed neighboring to the borders of the priests, and has, for the multitude of the Levites, the same spaces in length and in breadth that the fewness of the priests has, but nevertheless uses its own boundaries and understands that it is separated from the priestly rank, to strike down the pride of the ministers who, ignorant of the humility of their station, swell up beyond the priests, that is, the presbyters: they reckon their dignity not by merit but by riches; certainly, whoever is first of the ministers, because he makes proclamation item by item among the people and yet does not depart from the side of the high priest, thinks it an injury if he is ordained a presbyter, and he does not remember, minister of tables and of widows, that he was appointed to the service not only of the priests but also of the widows and of the poor.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.48.14-15":["And also for the Levites likewise, beside the borders of the priests, twenty-five thousand in length and ten thousand in breadth; the whole length twenty-five thousand and the breadth ten thousand; and they shall not sell of it, nor exchange (or measure), nor shall the firstfruits of the land be transferred (or taken away), because they are sanctified to the Lord. But the five thousand that remain in breadth by twenty-five thousand shall be profane, of the city, for dwelling and for suburbs; and the city shall be in the midst of it. What we have rendered as 'profane', for which Aquila translated βέβηλον, Symmachus and Theodotion λαικόν, the Seventy said προτείχισμα, which we can call 'outer wall'; but 'profane' and 'lay', that is, 'common', is what all the people may dwell in anywhere; and what they said: Nor shall it be measured nor taken away, for what we said: It shall not be exchanged nor transferred, plainly has no sense.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.48.14":["And they shall not sell, he says, of it, nor exchange, so that the possession of the Levitical allotment may remain eternal, and the merit of the dignity may not be overcome by price, nor be transferred from one to another: because the things that are sanctified to the Lord do not belong to those to whom they are given, but to him in whose name they are possessed.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.48.15":["But the five thousand that remain in breadth by twenty-five thousand — 'of length' is understood —, shall be profane, of the city, so that all the Israelites and anyone of the common people may dwell there; not that they are unclean, or that anything in the dwelling of the holy land is thought defiled and filthy, but that, besides the priests, all are permitted to dwell in them; by which we understand that all the things we refer to the senses, that is, to the desires of the body — what we take in by sight and hearing, by touch, smell and taste — are profane and do not have the sanctity of the temple, and are not of priestly privilege but of lay cheapness. And the city shall be in the midst — of which it is written: The rush of the river gladdens the city of God, and: A city set upon a mountain cannot be hidden —; of it we shall speak in what follows. Προτείχισμα, that is, 'outer wall', and διάστημα, that is, 'space', which the Seventy translated for 'suburbs': what sense it has in this place we do not know.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.48.16":["And these the measures of the same: toward the northern side four thousand five hundred, and toward the southern side four thousand five hundred, and toward the eastern side four thousand five hundred, and toward the western side four thousand five hundred. As to his saying: And these are its measures, 'of the city' is to be understood; for at the end of the preceding chapter it was set down: And the city shall be in the midst of it, that is, of the land. And first he begins from the northern side and sets the southern side opposite it, and again he matches like with like, so as to set over against the eastern side the western, that is, the sea; and each side of the city has four thousand and five hundred reeds, which, when reckoned, make eleven thousand and eighty-five paces, so that the whole city together, as is written at the end of this volume, has eighteen thousand reeds round about, which make forty-four thousand paces and three hundred and forty — whence also Psalm 44 (45), which is entitled: To the end, for those who shall be changed, and pertains 'to Ididia', that is, 'to the beloved of the Lord', or, as Aquila translated, 'for the lilies', and Symmachus 'for the flowers', contains the mysteries of the Church, which is the city of the Lord, of which it is written in the same psalm: The queen stood at your right hand in gilded clothing, surrounded with variety, and again: All the glory of the king's daughter is within, so that she is not like tombs whitewashed outside, but washes all things of the dish both within and without —. And when we have withdrawn from the north, the coldest wind, we pass over to the south, and, after the light of knowledge has risen in us, we dread the setting of our strengths, considering by no means the things past but the things to come, and not holding a sure possession of virtue, but saying daily in prayer: Lead us not into temptation, which we cannot bear.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.48.17":["And the suburbs of the city shall be toward the north two hundred fifty, and toward the south two hundred fifty, and toward the east two hundred fifty, and toward the sea two hundred fifty. For 'suburbs', which in Hebrew are called 'magras', the Seventy again translated διάστημα, that is, 'space'. As to its saying 'two hundred fifty' on each side, 'reeds' is understood, which have six cubits each and one handbreadth, and on each side they make a little less than six hundred and seventeen paces, and all together a thousand reeds, amounting to two thousand four hundred and sixty-eight paces: these spaces surround the walls of the city round about, so as to divide between the city and the things that remain. From this it is shown that no one, whether of those who carry out the works of the city or of those gathering produce, has license over these spaces, but that they are free from the use and work of men, so that the surroundings of the city walls may have the pleasantness of shrubs and herbs springing up of their own accord, and of the other things that the earth brings forth.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.48.18":["And what remains in length alongside the firstfruits of the sanctuary, ten thousand to the east and ten thousand to the west, shall be as the firstfruits of the sanctuary, and its produce shall be for food (or for loaves) for those who serve (or work) in the works of the city. And those serving (or working) in the works of the city shall serve the city (or shall work) from all the tribes of Israel. All the firstfruits of twenty-five thousand, by twenty-five thousand foursquare, shall be set apart for the firstfruits of the sanctuary and the possession of the city. After the description of the holy city and its suburbs, he wishes the remaining part of the firstfruits of the sanctuary, ten thousand reeds stretching to the east and another ten thousand to the west, to be assigned to those who carry out the works of the city for various uses and for whatever the city needs: namely, that they be sown, and that the workers feed on the produce that grows in this space of land, those who either build the walls of the city or restore what is broken and ruinous, so that the city may have its adornment, and its roofs be kept in repair and restored when necessity demands — and this we find done not only in Israel or in the possession of the holy land, which spiritually contain the mysteries of the Church, but in the Roman empire too, so that certain estates belong to the jurisdiction of cities, either by royal munificence or by the inheritances and donation of many, lest, namely, the buildings gradually collapse and the public edifices, which are an ornament to the city, be harmed by long neglect —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.48.19":["And the workers, he says (or those who serve in the works of the city), shall be from all the tribes of Israel, so that no one may think himself exempt from the works of the city, but all may vie with one another in building up the Church — which we read was done in the tabernacle too, so that, according to the variety of their means, one offered gold and silver, scarlet, fine linen and purple, and various and diverse woofs, others skins and goats' hair; and, though the gifts were diverse according to the measure of their substance, the reward of the givers was one, nay rather greater for those who offered small things indeed but greater in proportion to their means, after the example of the poor widow who put two mites into the gifts of the treasury and was praised by the judgment of the Lord, who said: Truly I say to you that this poor little widow has put in more than all; for all these out of their abundance have put into the gifts of God, but she out of that which she lacks has put in all the living that she had —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.48.20":["As therefore we have spoken about the works of the city, so this must be touched on briefly: that all the firstfruits of the sanctuary and the possession of the city, reckoned together foursquare, contain two hundred and forty-six thousand and a third part — for if twenty-five thousand reeds — which have six cubits each and one handbreadth — make of our paces, that is, Roman paces, sixty-one thousand six hundred and sixty-seven, no one doubts that these, reckoned fourfold, produce the number above —; from which we perceive that all things are to be understood spiritually, and that the possession of the Church is much broader than it once was in Judea, where they did not even receive portions of the holy land, but from the individual tribes received, in forty-eight cities, not a full possession but a dwelling and lodgings; so that those too who seem to be lay and not to have attained to the priestly and Levitical rank may nevertheless, if they work for the city of the Lord, feed on the holy land of the meek, and on the land of believers, one of whom said: I believe I shall see the good things of the Lord in the land of the living.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.48.21":["And what remains shall be the prince's, on every side (or on this side and that) of the firstfruits of the sanctuary and of the possession of the city, over against (or before the face of) the twenty-five thousand of the firstfruits as far as the eastern border, and also from the sea over against (or before the face of) the twenty-five thousand as far as the border of the sea, likewise in the portions of the prince it shall be; and the firstfruits of the sanctuary and the sanctuary of the temple shall be in the midst of it. And of the possession of the Levites and of the possession of the city, in the midst of the portions of the prince, it shall be toward the border of Judah and toward the border of Benjamin, and shall belong to the prince. About this prince it was argued more fully above, and that he alone was to receive as much as one tribe; but now we learn something else: that whatever remains from all the tribes, he alone receives, so that there is no tribe that does not offer gifts to the prince, not from elsewhere but from the firstfruits which serve the temple and the sanctuary and the parts assigned to the city — for this is what he says: Over against (or before the face of) the twenty-five thousand of the firstfruits as far as the eastern border (or as far as the eastern border of the sea), so that the firstfruits are between the seven and the five, and in the firstfruits themselves the city and its suburbs, and in the midst of the city the sanctuary of the temple —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.48.22":["But also whatever remains of the possession of the Levites and of the possession of the city will be allotted to the prince, and the furthest part of the seven tribes, neighboring the firstfruits and the city and the temple, will be in the tribe of Judah, and the furthest part of the five tribes, by the same right as above, in the tribe of Benjamin; and that furthest part itself will belong to the prince. Hence the mysteries of holy Scripture are to be marveled at, and pondered rather than set forth in speech. For in the earlier division of the lands beyond the Jordan, through Moses, the land was divided to the two tribes, Reuben and Gad, and to the half tribe of Manasseh; but within the Jordan, through Joshua son of Nun and Eleazar son of Aaron, Judah took possession on the south, and Ephraim and the half tribe of Manasseh on the north; afterwards, however, when spies had been sent out from Shiloh through the individual tribes and the description of the land had been brought to Joshua and Eleazar, Benjamin received a possession beside Judah on the south and beside Ephraim and the half tribe of Manasseh; the second tribe, Simeon, received its inheritance within the tribe of Judah — that what is written of Levi and Simeon might be fulfilled: I will divide them in Jacob, and scatter them in Israel —; the third, Zebulun, received Galilee, in which is Mount Tabor; the fourth, Issachar, where Jezreel is, as far as the Jordan; the fifth, Asher, as far as Mount Carmel, which overhangs the Great Sea, and Tyre and Sidon; the sixth, Naphtali, in Galilee as far as the Jordan, where Tiberias is, which was once called Chinnereth; the seventh, Dan, as far as Joppa, where are the cities of Aijalon and Selebi and Emmaus, which is now called Nicopolis — although we have read that afterwards, passing over other tribes, they took for themselves the city of Leshem in the tribe of Dan, which today is called Paneas. Since these things are so, and there is so great a difference between the earlier description of the tribes and the one now given, let us consider this: how, both in the present description and in the past one, the city and the temple are placed between the tribe of Judah and Benjamin; and in the earlier description Judah was on the south and Benjamin on the north, but here, on the contrary, it comes about that Judah is on the north and Benjamin on the south; after whom the second is Simeon, the third Issachar, the fourth Zebulun, the fifth Gad: namely five tribes, as is said in what follows; for it is written:\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.48.23-27":["And for the remaining tribes: from the eastern side to the western side, Benjamin one. And over against the border of Benjamin, from the eastern side to the western side, Simeon one. And above the border of Simeon, from the eastern side to the side of the west, Issachar one. And above the border of Issachar, from the eastern side to the western side, Zebulun one. And above the border of Zebulun, from the eastern side to the side of the sea, Gad one. And above the border of Gad, toward the side of the south, toward the midday; and the boundary shall be from Tamar as far as the waters of Contradiction, Kadesh, and the inheritance over against the Great Sea. This is the land which you shall allot to the children of Israel, and these are their divisions, says the Lord God. As to the fact that with the five and the seven tribes 'una' [one, in the feminine] is always placed at the end, either 'part' or 'possession' or 'inheritance' is understood, although Aquila and Symmachus translate in the neuter gender τό ὅριον, which signifies 'border'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.48.28":["And this is briefly to be noted: that the tribe of Gad, the last of the five tribes — which in the earlier description was beyond the Jordan — is placed in that part which Judah held before, namely toward the side of the south, toward the midday, and that the boundary is from Thamar as far as the waters of Contradiction, Kadesh, the inheritance over against the Great Sea; Thamar is the very place of which we have already spoken, which they now call 'Palmyra', and which was once built by Solomon: for 'palm' in the Hebrew tongue is called 'Thamar', and some think it obtained this name from the fact that there are very many palm groves there. And as to what follows: As far as the waters of Contradiction, Kadesh, the inheritance over against the Great Sea (for which the Seventy translated from Theman and the water of Mariboth, Kadesh, the inheritance as far as the Great Sea), this is to be observed: that in the Hebrew the same word 'nehela', because it is ambiguous, means both 'inheritance' and 'torrent', and that here it ought to be taken as 'torrent' rather than 'inheritance': for this is the torrent which enters the Great Sea at Rhinocorura, as we have already said before; Kadesh, moreover, which in the book of Joshua is called Cadesbarne, is in the desert which extends as far as the city of Petra; 'Mariboth', however, which means 'of Contradiction', is not the name of a place, as most suppose, but of the waters at which the people contradicted the Lord and Moses offended God, as the psalm says: They tempted him at the water of Contradiction, and Moses was afflicted on their account. And the whole inheritance of the holy land on the southern side, beside the borders of Egypt, is bounded by the torrent of Rhinocorura. And because 'Gad' means 'temptation', in his possession we pass from the palm groves to the waters of Contradiction, as far as 'Cades', which means 'holy', so that we may understand that even after the victory over the world we ought to be anxious and always in fear, and to remember that little verse: The life of men upon earth is temptation, and to arrive at holiness while adversaries resist, and so to obtain the victory; and let us contemplate close at hand the torrent full of rains.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.48.30-34":["And these are the outgoings of the city: from the northern side you shall measure four thousand five hundred. And the gates of the city after the names of the tribes of Israel: three gates from the north, the gate of Reuben one, the gate of Judah one, the gate of Levi one. And toward the eastern side four thousand five hundred; and three gates: the gate of Joseph one, the gate of Benjamin one, the gate of Dan one. And toward the southern side you shall measure four thousand five hundred: the gate of Simeon one, the gate of Issachar one, the gate of Zebulun one. And toward the western side four thousand five hundred; their gates three: the gate of Gad one, the gate of Asher one, the gate of Naphtali one. Round about eighteen thousand; and the name of the city from that day: The Lord is there. Concerning the circuit of the city, that on each side, foursquare, it had four thousand five hundred reeds, the preceding discourse has told; these make eighteen thousand reeds, as is also set down in the present chapter, that is, forty-four thousand paces and three hundred and forty paces. Now it is written that on each side, that is, along eleven thousand paces and eighty-five paces, there were three gates, which Aquila, Symmachus and Theodotion call διεξόδους, the Seventy διεκβολάς, and which we have translated 'outgoings' and 'exits' of the city. And first this is to be briefly pointed out: that the same tribes encamped around the tabernacle in such a way that on the east were Judah, Issachar and Zebulun, sons of Leah; and on the south Reuben, Simeon, Gad, two sons of Leah and the third a son of Zilpah, Leah's handmaid; but on the west Ephraim, Manasseh and Benjamin, namely two sons of Rachel — because Joseph, in place of the tribe of Levi, which was assigned to the sacrifices, was divided into two tribes, Ephraim and Manasseh —; and on the north Dan and Asher and Naphtali, of whom the first and the third are sons of Bilhah, Rachel's handmaid, the second a son of Zilpah, Leah's handmaid: ignoble, and placed toward the north, inasmuch as they were begotten of the stock of handmaids at odds with one another. This is according to the book of Numbers and the description of the tabernacle which, at God's command, was dictated by the mouth of Moses. But our city — which is The city of the great king: Whose artificer and founder is God, of which the psalmist also sings: Will Zion say: A man, and a man was born in her, and the Most High himself founded her? — has another description of the tribes in the holy land, and another measure of the city, order of possession and sides of the individual tribes. For first, three gates or exits of the city are set over against the northern side, of which are Reuben the firstborn, and Judah, the founder of the royal stock, and Levi, about whose name nothing was said in the description of the tabernacle and of the camp: so that, possessing the birthright, as a royal and priestly race, we may despise the cold of the north; secondly, toward the east are Joseph, Benjamin and Dan, both sons of Rachel and one of Bilhah her handmaid; toward the south, Simeon, Issachar and Zebulun, of whom three are sons of Leah; further, toward the west Gad, Asher and Naphtali, of whom the first two are sons of Zilpah, Leah's handmaid, the third a son of Bilhah, Rachel's handmaid — Who is wise, and will understand these things? understanding, will know them? For the ways of the Lord are right, and the righteous will walk in them; but the transgressors will fall in them —. These twelve gates or exits, I think, were joined to the individual tribes, according to the quality of their merits and virtues, under the names of the apostles and of the patriarchs; of them it is written more openly in the Revelation of John too, and the wonderful mysteries of the divine Scriptures bear witness.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EZK.48.35":["And it is necessary that such a city hold eighteen thousand reeds round about, under which number, in Psalm 18 (19) also, the natural law is set down, and the grace of the Gospel is described, by which the Church, that is, the city of the Savior, has been built. The name of the city itself, too, will by no means be, as before, 'Hierosolyma', which means 'vision of peace', but 'Adonai sam', which is rendered into the Latin tongue 'The Lord is there': who will never withdraw from her, as he departed before from the former people, saying to the disciples: Arise, let us go hence, and to the Jews: Your house shall be left to you desolate, but will have an eternal possession and himself be her possession, promising to the same disciples: Behold, I am with you even to the consummation of the age.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"DAN.9.27":["So when you see the abomination of desolation which was spoken of by Daniel the prophet standing in the holy place; let him who reads understand. When we are summoned to understanding, what was said is shown to be mystical. And we read in Daniel in this way: And in the middle of the week my sacrifice and libations will be taken away, and in the temple there will be the abomination of desolations until the consummation of time, and consummation will be given upon the desolation. Of this the Apostle also speaks, saying that the man of iniquity and the adversary is to be lifted up against everything that is called God or that is worshiped, so that he dares to stand in the temple of God and to show that he himself is God; whose coming, according to the working of Satan, is to destroy those who have received him and reduce them to a desolation of God. But it can also be taken simply, either of antichrist, or of the image of Caesar which Pilate set up in the temple, or of the equestrian statue of Hadrian which has stood in the very place of the holy of holies up to the present day. Also, according to the old Scripture, an idol is called an abomination, and for that reason of desolation is added, because the idol was set up in the desolated and destroyed temple.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.1.1":["Matthew, also called Levi, apostle and aforetimes publican, composed a gospel of Christ at first published in Judea in Hebrew for the sake of those of the circumcision who believed, but this was afterwards translated into Greek, though by what author is uncertain. The Hebrew itself has been preserved until the present day in the library at Cæsarea which Pamphilus so diligently gathered. I have also had the opportunity of having the volume described to me by the Nazarenes of Berœa, a city of Syria, who use it. In this it is to be noted that wherever the Evangelist, whether on his own account or in the person of our Lord the Saviour quotes the testimony of the Old Testament he does not follow the authority of the translators of the Septuagint but the Hebrew. Wherefore these two forms exist \"Out of Egypt have I called my son,\" and \"for he shall be called a Nazarene.\"\n— Lives of Illustrious Men","That there were many who wrote gospels, the evangelist Luke testifies, saying: Since indeed many have attempted to set in order a narrative of the things that have been fulfilled among us, as those handed them down to us who from the beginning themselves saw the word and ministered to it; and the records that have lasted up to the present time declare it too, records which, published by various authors, were the beginnings of various heresies, such as the one according to the Egyptians, and those of Thomas and of Matthias and of Bartholomew, of the Twelve Apostles also, and of Basilides and of Apelles and of the rest, whom it would take very long to enumerate; since for the present it is necessary to say only this, that there have been certain men who, without the Spirit and grace of God, attempted rather to set in order a narrative than to weave together the truth of history. To them that prophetic word can rightly be fitted: Woe to those who prophesy from their own heart, who walk after their own spirit, who say: The Lord says, and the Lord did not send them; of them the Savior too speaks in the Gospel of John: All who came before me were thieves and robbers. Who came, not who were sent; for he himself says: They came, and I did not send them; in those who come there is the presumption of rashness, in those who are sent the obedience of service. But the Church, which was founded upon the rock by the voice of the Lord, which the king brought into his chamber, and to which, like a young roe and a fawn of the harts, he put forth his hand through the opening of a hidden descent, pouring forth four rivers in the likeness of paradise, has four corners and four rings as well, by which, like the ark of the covenant and the guardian of the law of the Lord, she is carried on immovable poles. First of all is Matthew, the tax collector, surnamed Levi, who published a Gospel in Judea in the Hebrew language, chiefly for the sake of those from among the Jews who had believed in Jesus and, now that the truth of the Gospel had succeeded it, were by no means keeping the shadow of the law; second Mark, the interpreter of the apostle Peter and first bishop of the church of Alexandria, who did not himself see the Lord Savior, but narrated the things he had heard his master preaching, according to the truth of the events rather than their order; third Luke the physician, by nation a Syrian of Antioch, whose praise is in the Gospel, who, himself also a disciple of the apostle Paul, composed his volume in the regions of Achaia and Boeotia, going further back on certain things and, as he himself confesses in his prologue, describing things heard rather than seen; last John, apostle and evangelist, whom Jesus loved most, who, reclining upon the Lord's breast, drank the purest streams of doctrine, and who alone was found worthy to hear from the cross: Behold your mother. He, when he was in Asia and already then the seeds of the heretics were sprouting, of Cerinthus, of Ebion and of the rest who deny that Christ has come in the flesh, whom he himself also calls antichrists in his letter and whom the apostle Paul frequently strikes, was compelled by almost all the bishops of Asia at that time and by deputations from many churches to write more profoundly about the divinity of the Savior, and to break through, so to speak, to the very Word of God with a rashness not so much bold as blessed. Hence the history of the Church also relates that, when he was being pressed by the brethren to write, he answered that he would do so if a fast were proclaimed and all prayed to the Lord in common; and when it had been fulfilled, filled to the full with revelation, he burst forth into that prologue which came from heaven: In the beginning was the Word, and the Word was with God, and the Word was God; this was in the beginning with God. That these four Gospels, then, were foretold long before, the book of Ezekiel too proves, in which the first vision is woven together thus: And in the midst as it were the likeness of four living creatures, and their countenance the face of a man and the face of a lion and the face of a calf and the face of an eagle. The first, the face of a man, signifies Matthew, who began to write as if about a man: The book of the generation of Jesus Christ, the son of David, the son of Abraham; the second signifies Mark, in whom is heard the voice of a lion roaring in the desert: The voice of one crying in the desert: Prepare the way of the Lord, make his paths straight; the third, that of the calf, prefigures that the evangelist Luke took his beginning from Zechariah the priest; the fourth signifies John the evangelist, who, taking up the wings of an eagle and hastening to higher things, discourses on the Word of God. The rest that follows tends to the same sense: their legs straight, and their feet winged, and wherever the spirit went, they went and did not turn back, and their backs full of eyes, and sparks and lamps running to and fro in the midst, and a wheel within a wheel, and in each one four faces. Hence the Apocalypse of John too, after its account of the twenty-four elders, who, holding harps and bowls, worship the Lamb of God, brings in lightnings and thunders and seven spirits running to and fro and a sea of glass and four living creatures full of eyes, saying: The first living creature like a lion, and the second like a calf, and the third like a man, and the fourth like a flying eagle; and a little after: They were full, it says, of eyes, and they had no rest day and night, saying: Holy, holy, holy, Lord God almighty, who was and who is and who is to come. By all these things it is plainly shown that only four Gospels ought to be received, and that all the dirges of the apocrypha are to be sung by dead heretics rather than by living churchmen. And I wonder greatly, dearest Eusebius, why, being about to sail suddenly for Rome, you wished this to be given you by me as a sort of provision for the journey, that in expounding Matthew briefly I should be compressed in words and expansive in sense. If you had remembered my answer, you would never be asking in a few days for a work of years. For first, it is difficult to read all who have written on the Gospels; then it is far more difficult to apply judgment and pick out what is best. I confess that very many years ago I read Origen's twenty-five volumes on Matthew and as many of his homilies and his brief, verse-by-verse (commatic) kind of interpretation, and the commentaries of Theophilus, bishop of the city of Antioch, and the short works also of Hippolytus the martyr and of Theodore of Heraclea and of Apollinaris of Laodicea and of Didymus of Alexandria, and among the Latins of Hilary, Victorinus and Fortunatianus; from these, even if I plucked only small things, something worthy of remembrance would be written. But you compel me to dictate in two weeks, with Easter already at hand and the winds blowing, so that there is no telling when the secretaries are to take it down, when the sheets are to be written, when corrected, in what space of time they are to be set out in a clean copy; and this above all since you know that I have been so ill for three months that I am only now barely beginning to walk, and cannot make up for the greatness of the labor by the shortness of the time. Therefore, setting aside the authority of the ancients, whom I have been given the opportunity neither to read nor to follow, I have briefly set out the historical interpretation, which you especially asked for, and now and then I have mixed in the flowers of spiritual understanding, reserving the finished work for the future. But if my life is longer, or if you on your return fulfill your promises, then I shall strive to complete what remains; nay rather, with the foundations laid and the walls partly built, I shall set upon them a most beautiful roof, so that you may know what difference there is between the sudden boldness of dictating and the lamplit diligence of writing. Certainly you know, and I should blush to call you as witness to a lie of mine, that I dictated the present little work with such speed that you would have thought I was reading the words of others rather than composing my own. And do not think this was said out of arrogance and confidence in my talent, but because I wish to show you how much you count with me, since I have chosen to run a risk among the learned rather than refuse you anything when you asked so earnestly. So I beg you that, if the style is rather unpolished and the speech does not run on with its usual glide, you ascribe this to haste, not to lack of skill; and that, when you come to Rome, you give copies to Principia, the virgin of Christ, who asked me to write on the Song of Songs, a work from which I was shut out by a long illness and have put off my hope into the future; binding you by this condition, that if you withhold from her what was written for you, she too may shut up in her little book-chest the things that are later to be written for her.\n— Commentary on Matthew","The book of the generation of Jesus Christ. In Isaiah we read: His generation, who shall declare it? Let us not think, then, that the Gospel is contrary to the prophet, so that what he said was impossible to utter, this one begins to narrate; for there it is said of the generation of the divinity, here of the incarnation. And he began from the things of the flesh, so that through the man we might begin to learn of God.\n— Commentary on Matthew","The son of David, the son of Abraham. The order is reversed, but changed of necessity. For if he had put Abraham first and David afterwards, he would have had to repeat Abraham again in order to weave together the series of the generation. And he named him the son of these two, passing over the rest, for this reason: that to these alone the promise concerning Christ was made; to Abraham: In your seed, he says, shall all nations be blessed, which is Christ; to David: Of the fruit of your womb I will set upon your throne.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.1.3":["And Judah begot Perez and Zerah of Tamar. It is to be noted that in the genealogy of the Savior none of the holy women is taken up, but those whom Scripture rebukes, so that he who had come for the sake of sinners, being born of sinful women, might blot out the sins of all. Hence in what follows too Ruth the Moabitess is set down, and Bathsheba the wife of Uriah.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.1.4":["And Nahshon begot Salmon. This is Salmon, prince of the tribe of Judah, as we read in Numbers.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.1.8":["And Joram begot Uzziah, and Uzziah begot Jotham. In the fourth book of Kings we read that Ahaziah was begotten of Joram; and when he died, Jehosheba, the daughter of King Joram, the sister of Ahaziah, took Joash, her brother's son, and rescued him from the slaughter that was being carried out by Athaliah; to him succeeded in the kingdom his son Amaziah, after whom reigned his son Azariah, who is also called Uzziah, and to him succeeded Jotham his son. You see, therefore, that according to the faith of history there were three kings in between whom the Gospel has passed over here; for Joram did not beget Uzziah but Ahaziah and the rest whom we have enumerated. But because it was the evangelist's purpose to set three sets of fourteen in different states of the times, and because Joram had mingled himself with the family of the most impious Jezebel, therefore his memory is taken away down to the third generation, lest it be set in the order of the holy nativity.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.1.11-12":["And after the transmigration of Babylon Jeconiah begot Shealtiel. If we wish to set Jeconiah at the end of the first set of fourteen, in the following one there will be not fourteen but thirteen. Let us know, therefore, that the first Jeconiah is the same as Jehoiakim, and the second is the son, not the father; of these the former is written with K and M, and the latter with X and N, which through the fault of copyists and the length of time has been confused among the Greeks and the Latins.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.1.16":["And Jacob begot Joseph. This passage Julian the Augustus throws at us as a discrepancy of the evangelists: why the evangelist Matthew called Joseph the son of Jacob, and Luke called him the son of Heli; not understanding the custom of the Scriptures, that one is his father according to nature, the other according to the law. For we know that this was commanded through Moses at God's bidding: that if a brother or a kinsman has died without children, another should take his wife to raise up seed for his brother or kinsman. On this matter both Africanus, the writer of chronicles, and Eusebius of Caesarea have argued more fully in the books on the discrepancies of the Gospels.\n— Commentary on Matthew","He begot Joseph, the husband of Mary. When you hear husband, let no suspicion of marriage steal into your mind; but remember the custom of the Scriptures, that betrothed women are called wives.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.1.17":["And from the transmigration of Babylon to Christ, fourteen generations. Count from Jeconiah to Joseph and you will find thirteen generations. The fourteenth generation, however, will be reckoned to Christ himself.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.1.18":["Notice, he [Helvidius] says, that the word used is betrothed, not entrusted as you say, and of course the only reason why she was betrothed was that she might one day be married. And the Evangelist would not have said before they came together if they were not to come together, for no one would use the phrase before he dined of a man who was not going to dine. Then, again, the angel calls her wife and speaks of her as united to Joseph...\n\nLet us take the points one by one, and follow the tracks of this impiety that we may show that he has contradicted himself. He admits that she was betrothed, and in the next breath will have her to be a man's wife whom he has admitted to be his betrothed. Again, he calls her wife, and then says the only reason why she was betrothed was that she might one day be married. And, for fear we might not think that enough, \"the word used,\" he says, \"is betrothed and not entrusted, that is to say, not yet a wife, not yet united by the bond of wedlock.\" But when he continues, \"the Evangelist would never have applied the words, before they came together to persons who were not to come together, any more than one says, before he dined, when the man is not going to dine,\" I know not whether to grieve or laugh. Shall I convict him of ignorance, or accuse him of rashness? Just as if, supposing a person to say, \"Before dining in harbour I sailed to Africa,\" his words could not hold good unless he were compelled some day to dine in harbour. If I choose to say, \"the apostle Paul before he went to Spain was put in fetters at Rome,\" or (as I certainly might) \"Helvidius, before he repented, was cut off by death,\" must Paul on being released at once go to Spain, or must Helvidius repent after death, although the Scripture says \"In sheol who shall give you thanks?\" Must we not rather understand that the preposition before, although it frequently denotes order in time, yet sometimes refers only to order in thought? So that there is no necessity, if sufficient cause intervened to prevent it, for our thoughts to be realized. When, then, the Evangelist says before they came together, he indicates the time immediately preceding marriage, and shows that matters were so far advanced that she who had been betrothed was on the point of becoming a wife. As though he said, before they kissed and embraced, before the consummation of marriage, she was found to be with child. And she was found to be so by none other than Joseph, who watched the swelling womb of his betrothed with the anxious glances, and, at this time, almost the privilege, of a husband. Yet it does not follow, as the previous examples showed, that he had intercourse with Mary after her delivery, when his desires had been quenched by the fact that she had already conceived. And although we find it said to Joseph in a dream, \"Fear not to take Mary your wife\"; and again, \"Joseph arose from his sleep, and did as the angel of the Lord commanded him, and took unto him his wife,\" no one ought to be disturbed by this, as though, inasmuch as she is called wife, she ceases to be betrothed, for we know it is usual in Scripture to give the title to those who are betrothed. The following evidence from Deuteronomy establishes the point. [Deuteronomy 22:24-25] \"If the man,\" says the writer, \"find the damsel that is betrothed in the field, and the man force her, and lie with her, he shall surely die, because he has humbled his neighbour's wife.\" And in another place, [Deuteronomy 22:23-24] \"If there be a damsel that is a virgin betrothed unto an husband, and a man find her in the city, and lie with her; then you shall bring them both out unto the gate of that city, and you shall stone them with stones that they die; the damsel, because she cried not, being in the city; and the man, because he has humbled his neighbour's wife: so you shall put away the evil from the midst of you.\" Elsewhere also, [Deuteronomy 20:7] \"And what man is there that has betrothed a wife, and has not taken her? Let him go and return unto his house, lest he die in the battle, and another man take her.\"\n— The Perpetual Virginity of Blessed Mary","Now the generation of Christ was thus. The diligent reader may ask and say: Since Joseph is not the father of the Lord Savior, what has the order of the generation, brought down to Joseph, to do with the Lord? To him we shall answer, first, that it is not the custom of the Scriptures for the order of women to be woven into the generations; then, that Joseph and Mary were of one tribe, which is why by the law he was obliged to take her as a kinswoman; and that they are registered together in Bethlehem, as being, clearly, begotten of one stock.\n— Commentary on Matthew","When his mother Mary had been betrothed. Why is he conceived not of a simple virgin but of one betrothed? First, so that through the generation of Joseph the origin of Mary might be shown; second, so that she might not be stoned by the Jews as an adulteress; third, so that in fleeing to Egypt she might have a comfort. The martyr Ignatius also added a fourth reason why he was conceived of a betrothed woman: so that, he says, his birth might be hidden from the devil, while he supposes him begotten not of a virgin but of a wife.\n— Commentary on Matthew","Before they came together she was found to be with child of the Holy Spirit. She was found by no other than by Joseph, who, by an almost husbandly freedom, knew everything about his future wife. And as for the saying: Before they came together, it does not follow that they came together afterwards, but Scripture shows what was not done.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.1.19":["But Joseph her husband, since he was just and did not wish to expose her, wished to put her away secretly. If anyone is joined to a harlot, he becomes one body. And it is commanded in the law that not only the guilty but also those privy to crimes are liable for the sin. How is Joseph, when he conceals the crime of his wife, described as just? But this is a testimony for Mary: that Joseph, knowing her chastity and wondering at what had happened, hides in silence the thing whose mystery he did not know.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.1.20":["Joseph, son of David, do not fear to take Mary your wife. We have already said above too that betrothed women are called wives, which the book Against Helvidius teaches more fully. And the angel speaks to him in a dream with the affection of one who soothes, to approve the justice of his silence. At the same time it is to be noted that Joseph is said to be the son of David, so that Mary also might be shown to be of the stock of David.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.1.21":["And you shall call his name Jesus; for he himself will save his people. Jesus in the Hebrew language means savior. The evangelist, therefore, has marked the etymology of his name, saying: You shall call his name: Savior, because he himself will save his people.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.1.23":["Therefore the Lord himself will give you a sign. Behold, a Virgin shall conceive and bear, and you shall call his name Emmanuel. No longer at sundry times, according to the apostle Paul, and in many ways will God speak (Heb 1); nor, according to another prophet (Hos 12), will he be likened in the hands of the prophets; but he who formerly spoke through others will himself say, I am here (below, 58). Of whom the Bride too was asking in the Song of Songs: Let him kiss me with the kiss of his mouth (Song 1:2). For the Lord of powers is himself the king of glory (Ps 23 [24]:10): he himself will descend into the virginal womb, and will enter and go out by the Eastern gate which is always shut (Ezek 44); of which Gabriel says to the Virgin: The Holy Spirit will come upon you, and the power of the Most High will overshadow you: therefore the holy thing that will be born in you will be called the Son of God (Luke 1:35). And in Proverbs: Wisdom has built herself a house (Prov 9:1). And when it is said: The Lord himself will give you a sign, it ought to be new and marvelous. But if a young woman or a girl, as the Jews would have it, and not a virgin, gives birth, what sort of sign can it be called, since this is a name of age, not of integrity? And in truth, to set foot to foot with the Jews and not at all, with a contentious rope, give them occasion to laugh at our lack of skill: a virgin is called in Hebrew BETHULA (בתולה), which is not written in the present place; but in place of this word is set ALMA (עלמה), which all except the Seventy translated young girl. Now ALMA is an ambiguous word among them: for it is said both of a young girl and of one hidden, that is, ἀπόκρυφος..... Hence also in the title of the ninth psalm, where in the Hebrew is set ALAMOTH (עלמות), the other Translators translated for youth, what the Seventy rendered for the hidden things. And in Genesis we read, where Rebecca is called alma (Gen 24), that Aquila translated neither young girl nor girl but hidden. The Shunammite woman too, having lost her son, when she had thrown herself at the feet of Elisha and Gehazi was keeping her back, hears from the Prophet: Let her be, for she is in grief, and the Lord has hidden it from me (2 Kgs 4:27). In place of what is said in Latin, has hidden it from me, in the Hebrew is written EELIM MEMMENNI (העלים ממני). So alma is said not only of a girl or a virgin, but, with ἐπιτάσει, of a hidden virgin and a secluded one, who has never been open to the gaze of men, but has been guarded with great diligence by her parents. In the Punic tongue too, which is said to flow from the fountains of the Hebrews, a virgin is properly called alma. And, though we may give the Jews something to laugh at, in our own speech too alma means holy. And the Hebrews use words of almost all languages: as in the Song of Songs (Song 3:9), from the Greek φορεῖον, that is, litter, Solomon made himself one, which we also read thus in the Hebrew. The words nugas (trifles) and mensuram (measure) too the Hebrews call in the same way and with the same senses. And as far as I can contend with my memory, I think I have never read alma of a married woman, but of one who is a virgin: so that she is not only a virgin, but a virgin of younger age, and in the years of girlhood. For it can happen that a virgin is an old woman, but this virgin was in the years of girlhood. Or certainly a virgin, not a little girl and one who could not yet know a man, but now marriageable. Indeed, in Deuteronomy (Deut 22:25ff.) under the name of a girl and of a young woman a virgin is understood. If, he says, a man finds in the field a betrothed girl, and, overpowering her by force, lies with her, you shall kill only the man who lay with her, and to the girl you shall do nothing: the young woman has no sin worthy of death. For as when someone rises up in ambush against his neighbor and kills his life, so this matter happened. He found her in the field: the betrothed girl cried out, and there was none found to help her. And in the volume of Kings we read (1 Kgs 1) that they sought a girl, a virgin, named Abishag, and brought her in to the king, to sleep with him and to warm him; and the girl was exceedingly beautiful, and she ministered to him, and the king did not know her. And what follows: And you shall call his name Emmanuel, both the Seventy and the three others translated likewise, in place of which in Matthew is written they shall call: which is not found in the Hebrew. So this child who will be born of the Virgin, O house of David, let him now be called by you Emmanuel, that is, God with us, because by the very facts, being freed from two enemy kings, you will prove that you have God present; and he who will afterward be called Jesus, that is, Savior, because he is to save the whole race of men, let him now be called by you by the name of Emmanuel. The word CARATHI (קראת), which all have interpreted you shall call, can also be understood as she shall call: namely, that the Virgin herself, who will conceive and bear, is going to call Christ by this name. In many testimonies which the Evangelists or Apostles have taken from the old books, we must attend more carefully to the fact that they followed not the order of the words but the sense. Hence also in the present place, for shall conceive in the womb, Matthew set shall have in the womb (Matt 1:23); and for you shall call, they shall call. The Hebrews think that this is prophesied of Hezekiah the son of Ahaz, because in his reign Samaria was taken: which cannot be proved at all. Since Ahaz the son of Jotham reigned over Judah and Jerusalem sixteen years (2 Kgs 16), and his son Hezekiah succeeded him in the kingdom at twenty-five years of age, and reigned over Judah and Jerusalem twenty-nine years. How then, granting that this prophecy was made to Ahaz in his first year, is it said of the conception and birth of Hezekiah, when at the time Ahaz began to reign Hezekiah was already nine years old? Unless perhaps they say that the sixth year of Hezekiah's reign, in which Samaria was taken, is called his infancy, not of age but of rule. That this is forced and violent is plain even to fools. A certain one of our people contends that the prophet Isaiah had two sons, Jasub and Emmanuel, and that Emmanuel was begotten of the prophetess his wife, as a type of the Lord and Savior: so that the first son, Jasub, which is interpreted left or returning, signifies the Jewish people, which has been left and will afterward return; but the second, that is, Emmanuel, and God with us, the calling of the nations, after the Word was made flesh and dwelt among us.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","Now all this was done that what was spoken by the Lord through the prophet might be fulfilled, saying: Behold, a virgin shall have in the womb and shall bear. Where the evangelist Matthew says: shall have in the womb, in the prophet it is written: shall receive in the womb. But the prophet, because he foretells things to come, signifies what is to be and writes shall receive; the evangelist, because he narrates history not of the future but of the past, changed shall receive and put shall have. For one who has is by no means going to receive. Something of the kind we read in the psalms too: Ascending on high he led captivity captive, he received gifts among men. The Apostle, setting down this testimony, did not say received, but gave, because there it was signified of the future that he would receive, here it is told of him who had already given what he had received.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.1.25":["And Joseph, rising from sleep, did as the angel of the Lord commanded him. He took his wife and did not know her until she bore her firstborn son. From this passage certain people most perversely suspect that Mary had other sons as well, saying that no one is called firstborn unless he also has brothers; whereas this is the usage of the divine Scriptures, that they call firstborn not him whom brothers follow but him who is born first. Read the little book Against Helvidius mentioned above.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.2.1-2":["And in place of what we have translated, following Symmachus and Theodotion: Let the augurs of the heaven stand and save you, the Seventy translated more plainly, Let the astrologers of the heaven stand and save you; who are commonly called mathematicians, and think that human affairs are ruled by the course of the stars and the motion of the heavenly bodies. Hence the Magi too came from the East, saying they had seen the Lord's star, either from the knowledge of their art, or from the prophecy of Balaam, their own Prophet, who had said in Numbers: A star shall rise out of Jacob, and a man from Israel (Num 24:17).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"MAT.2.2":["For we have seen his star in the east. To the confusion of the Jews, so that they might learn of Christ's nativity from the Gentiles, a star rises in the east, which they knew was to come from the prophecy of Balaam (whose successors these men were). Read the book of Numbers. And the magi are brought by the guidance of the star into Judea, so that the priests, asked by the magi where Christ was to be born, might be left without excuse concerning his coming.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.2.5":["And they said to him: In Bethlehem of Judea. This is an error of the copyists; for we think that it was first published by the evangelist as we read in the Hebrew itself: of Judah, not of Judea. For what Bethlehem is there of other nations, that Judea should be put here to distinguish it from that? But of Judah is written for this reason, that there is also another Bethlehem in Galilee. Read the book of Joshua son of Nun. Finally, in the very testimony that is taken from the prophecy of Micah, it reads thus: And you, Bethlehem, land of Judah.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.2.6":["In the Gospel according to Matthew, when the Magi had come from the east and Herod asked the scribes where Christ the Lord was to be born, they are related to have answered: In Bethlehem in the land of Judah, and, adding the prophet's testimony, they said: And you, Bethlehem, land of Judah, are by no means least among the princes of Judah; for out of you shall come forth a leader who will rule my people Israel. That this testimony agrees neither with the Hebrew nor with the Seventy translators is plain even if I say nothing; and I think that Matthew, wishing to reprove the negligence of the scribes and priests in the reading of divine Scripture, set it down in this way too, just as it had been said by them. But there are some who assert that in almost all the testimonies taken from the Old Testament there is an error of this kind, that either the order is changed or the words, and sometimes even the sense itself is different, since the apostles or the evangelists did not pick the testimonies out of a book but trusted to memory, which is sometimes deceived.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MAT.2.11":["And opening their treasures they offered him gifts, gold, frankincense and myrrh. Most beautifully the presbyter Juvencus has comprised the mysteries of the gifts in one little verse: Incense, gold, myrrh to the king, the man and the God they bring as gifts.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.2.12":["And having received an answer in sleep that they should not return to Herod, they went back by another way into their own country. Those who offered gifts to the Lord accordingly receive an answer. And the answer (which in Greek is called χρηματισθέντες) is given not through an angel but through the Lord himself, so that the privilege of Joseph's merits might be shown. And they return by another way, because they were not to be mingled with the unbelief of the Jews.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.2.13-14":["Behold, an angel of the Lord appeared in sleep to Joseph, saying: Arise, and take the child and his mother, and flee into Egypt. When he took the child and his mother to cross into Egypt, he takes them by night and in darkness; but when he returns into Judea, neither night nor darkness is set down in the Gospel.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.2.15":["Matthew, also called Levi, apostle and aforetimes publican, composed a gospel of Christ at first published in Judea in Hebrew for the sake of those of the circumcision who believed, but this was afterwards translated into Greek though by what author is uncertain. The Hebrew itself has been preserved until the present day in the library at Caesarea which Pamphilus so diligently gathered. I have also had the opportunity of having the volume described to me by the Nazarenes of Beroea, a city of Syria, who use it. In this it is to be noted that wherever the Evangelist, whether on his own account or in the person of our Lord the Saviour quotes the testimony of the Old Testament he does not follow the authority of the translators of the Septuagint but the Hebrew. Wherefore these two forms exist \"Out of Egypt have I called my son,\" and \"for he shall be called a Nazarene.\"\n— Lives of Illustrious Men, Chapter 3","That what was spoken by the Lord through the prophet might be fulfilled, saying: Out of Egypt I have called my son. Let those who deny the truth of the Hebrew volumes answer where this is read in the LXX translators. And when they have not found it, we shall tell them that it is written in the prophet Hosea, as the copies too can prove which we have lately published. But we can also relieve this passage in another way, for the sake of the contentious, whose custom the apostle Paul denies that he or the Church of Christ has; and we bring forward a testimony from Numbers, where Balaam says: God has called him out of Egypt, his glory is like that of the unicorn.\n— Commentary on Matthew","Where we have said: Out of Egypt I called my son, the Seventy translated: Out of Egypt I called his sons, which is not found in the Hebrew; and no one doubts that Matthew took his testimony from this place according to the Hebrew truth. Therefore let those who disparage our translation produce the scripture from which the evangelist took this testimony and interpreted it of the Lord Savior, when he was brought back from Egypt into the land of Israel. And when they cannot find it, let them stop wrinkling the forehead, knitting the brow, curling the nose and snapping their fingers. The emperor Julian, in the seventh volume of what he vomited forth against us, that is, against the Christians, slanders this place, and says that what was written of Israel the evangelist Matthew transferred to Christ in order to mock the simplicity of those of the Gentiles who had believed. To him we will answer briefly: first, that Matthew published his Gospel in Hebrew letters, which none could read except those who were of the Hebrews. Therefore he did not do it in order to mock the heathen. But if he wished to mock the Hebrews, he was either foolish or unskilled: foolish, if he invented an open lie; unskilled, if he did not understand of whom these things were said. The volume itself clears him of folly, since it is composed prudently and in order; unskilled we cannot call him, whom we know from other testimonies of the Scriptures to have had knowledge of the law. It remains for us to say that the things which go before τυπικῶς (typologically) in others are referred to Christ according to truth and fulfillment; which we know the Apostle did with the two mountains, Sinai and Zion, and with Sarah and Hagar. For it is not that Sinai is not a mountain and Zion is not; that Sarah did not exist and Hagar did not exist, because the apostle Paul referred these to the two covenants. So, therefore, this which is written: Israel was a little child, and I loved him, and out of Egypt I called my son, is indeed said of the people of Israel, which is called out of Egypt, which is loved, which at that time, after the error of idolatry, was called as an infant and a little child; but it is perfectly referred to Christ. For Isaac too was a type of Christ, in that he himself carried the wood of his coming death; and Jacob, in that he had as wives Leah, sore of eye, and Rachel, the beautiful. In Leah, who was the elder, we understand the blindness of the Synagogue, in Rachel the beauty of the Church; and yet those who were in part types of the Lord Savior are not to be believed to have done in his type all the things they are related to have done. For a type indicates a part; if it went before as a whole in the type, it would no longer be a type, but ought to be called the truth of history. These things we have said briefly, as is fitting in commentaries; now let us return to the rest.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"MAT.2.18":["Then was fulfilled what was spoken through the prophet Jeremiah, saying: A voice was heard in Ramah, weeping and great wailing, Rachel weeping for her sons, and she would not be consoled, because they are not. Of Rachel was born Benjamin, in whose tribe Bethlehem does not lie. It is asked, therefore, how Rachel weeps for the sons of Judah, that is, of Bethlehem, as if they were her own. We shall answer briefly: because she was buried near Bethlehem in Ephrathah (from the inner lodging of her little body she received the name of mother); or else because Judah and Benjamin were two joined tribes, and Herod had commanded that the boys be killed not only in Bethlehem but in all its borders round about; by occasion of Bethlehem we understand that many of Benjamin also were slain. And she weeps for her sons and does not receive consolation according to a twofold understanding: either because she thought them dead for ever, or because she would not be consoled for those whom she knew would live. And as for the saying: in Ramah, let us not think it is the name of the place which is near Gibeah; but Ramah is interpreted height, so that the sense is: A voice was heard on high, that is, spread far and wide.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.2.20":["Those who sought the child's life are dead. From this passage we understand that not only Herod but also the priests and scribes at that time had been plotting the death of the Lord.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.2.21":["He, rising, took the child and his mother. He did not say: he took his son and his wife, but the child and his mother, as a foster father, not a husband.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.2.22":["But hearing that Archelaus was reigning in Judea in place of Herod his father. Many, through ignorance of history, slip into error, thinking that the Herod by whom the Lord is mocked in his passion is the same as the one who is now reported to have died. So that Herod who afterwards made friends with Pilate is the son of this Herod, the brother of Archelaus, whom Tiberius Caesar banished to Lyons, which is a city of the Gauls, and made his brother Herod successor to the kingdom. Read the history of Josephus.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.2.23":["Matthew, also called Levi, apostle and aforetimes publican, composed a gospel of Christ at first published in Judea in Hebrew for the sake of those of the circumcision who believed, but this was afterwards translated into Greek though by what author is uncertain. The Hebrew itself has been preserved until the present day in the library at Caesarea which Pamphilus so diligently gathered. I have also had the opportunity of having the volume described to me by the Nazarenes of Beroea, a city of Syria, who use it. In this it is to be noted that wherever the Evangelist, whether on his own account or in the person of our Lord the Saviour quotes the testimony of the Old Testament he does not follow the authority of the translators of the Septuagint but the Hebrew. Wherefore these two forms exist \"Out of Egypt have I called my son,\" and \"for he shall be called a Nazarene.\"\n— Lives of Illustrious Men, Chapter 3","For root, which the Seventy alone translated, in the Hebrew there is written (גזע) GEZA, which Aquila and Symmachus and Theodotion interpreted as κορμὸν, that is, trunk. And for flower, which in Hebrew is called (נצר) NESER, they translated sprout, to show that long after the time of the Babylonian captivity, when no one of David's stock possessed the glory of the ancient kingdom, Mary arose as if from a trunk, and Christ from Mary. That saying in the Gospel of Matthew of which all churchmen seek, and do not find, where it is written, For he shall be called a Nazarene (Matt 2:23), the learned among the Hebrews think was taken from this place. But it must be known that this NESER is written with the letter SADE, whose peculiar quality and sound, between z and s, the Latin tongue does not express. For it is a hissing sound, and is uttered with clenched teeth, barely, by the pressure of the tongue: and with it the city Zion too is written. Further, Nazirites, whom the Seventy translated sanctified, Symmachus separated, are always written with the letter ZAIN (ז).\n— Commentary on Isaiah","And coming he dwelt in a city that is called Nazareth, that what was spoken through the prophets might be fulfilled: For he shall be called a nazarene. If he had set down a fixed example from the Scriptures, he would never say: what was spoken through the prophets, but simply: what was spoken through the prophet; but as it is, by naming the prophets in the plural, he shows that he took from the Scriptures not the words but the sense. Nazarene is interpreted holy. And that the Lord would be holy, all Scripture records. We can also say otherwise, that it is written in Isaiah even in the same words according to the Hebrew truth: A rod shall go forth from the root of Jesse, and a nazarene shall rise up from the root.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.3.2":["Repent, for the kingdom of heaven has drawn near. John the Baptist is the first to preach the kingdom of heaven, so that the forerunner of the Lord might be honored with this privilege.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.3.3":["For this is he who was spoken of through the prophet Isaiah, saying: The voice of one crying in the desert: Prepare the way of the Lord, make his paths straight. He was preparing the souls of believers, in which the Lord was going to walk, so that he, the pure one, might walk in most pure ways, saying: I will dwell in them and walk among them, and I will be their God, and they shall be my people. Porphyry compares this passage with the beginning of the evangelist Mark, in which it is written: The beginning of the Gospel of Jesus Christ, the Son of God; as it is written in the prophet Isaiah: Behold, I send my angel before your face, who shall prepare your way; the voice of one crying in the desert: Prepare the way of the Lord, make his paths straight. For since the testimony is woven together from Malachi and Isaiah, he asks how we can think the example was taken as from Isaiah alone. To him churchmen have answered most fully. We, however, think either that the name of Isaiah was added through the fault of copyists, which we can prove in other places as well, or at any rate that one body has been made out of different testimonies of the Scriptures. Read Psalm 13 (14) and you will find this same thing.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.3.4":["Now John himself had a garment of the hair of camels and a leather girdle about his loins. Of hair, he says, he had, not of wool. One thing is the mark of austere clothing, another of softer luxury. And the leather girdle with which Elijah too was girded is a σύμβολον (symbol) of mortification. Moreover, what follows,\n\nHis food was locusts and wild honey, is fitting for a dweller in the wilderness, so that he might satisfy not the delights of food but the necessity of human flesh.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.3.9":["God is able from these stones to raise up sons to Abraham. He calls the heathen stones because of the hardness of their heart. Read Ezekiel: I will take away, he says, from you the heart of stone, and I will give a heart of flesh. In stone hardness is shown, in flesh softness; or else he simply indicates the power of God, that he who made all things out of nothing can also bring forth a people from the hardest rocks.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.3.10":["Behold, the axe is laid to the root of the trees. The preaching of the word of the Gospel, which is a sword sharp on both sides, is called an axe, according to the prophet Jeremiah, who compares the word of the Lord to an axe cutting the rock.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.3.11":["And note that he washed the filth of the daughters of Zion by the spirit of judgment, but the blood of Jerusalem by the spirit of burning. For what is light is washed; what is heavier is burned out. Of this spirit of judgment and spirit of burning John the Baptist spoke in the Gospel: I baptize you in water, but he who comes after me will baptize you in the Holy Spirit and in fire (Matt 3:11). From which we learn that man gives only water, but God gives the Holy Spirit, by whom both filth is washed away and the sins of blood are purged.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","Whose shoes I am not worthy to carry. In another Gospel: Whose, he says, shoe's strap I am not worthy to loose. Here humility is shown, there a mystery: that Christ is the bridegroom and John does not deserve to loose the bridegroom's strap, lest his house be called, according to the law of Moses and the example of Ruth, the house of the unshod.\n— Commentary on Matthew","He will baptize you in the Holy Spirit and fire. Either because the Holy Spirit is fire, as the Acts of the Apostles teach, at whose descent there sat as it were fire upon the tongues of the believers, and the word of the Lord was fulfilled who said: I came to send fire upon the earth, and how I wish that it may burn; or because in the present we are baptized with the spirit and in the future with fire, the Apostle too agreeing with this sense: The fire will test what sort each one's work is.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.3.13":["Then Jesus came from Galilee to the Jordan to John, to be baptized by him. For a threefold reason the Savior received baptism from John: first, so that, since he had been born a man, he might fulfill all the justice and humility of the law; second, so that by his own baptism he might approve the baptism of John; third, so that, sanctifying the waters of the Jordan by the descent of the dove, he might show the coming of the Holy Spirit in the washing of believers.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.3.14-15":["For this is also why he received baptism in the stream of the Jordan as if he were a penitent, though he was free from sins: to teach the rest that they must be cleansed through baptism and be begotten as sons by the new adoption of the Spirit. John the Baptist, by no means understanding this, tried to keep him from coming to the washing, saying: \"I ought to be baptized by you\" (Matt. 3:14). And at once the mystery is taught: \"Permit it now; for thus it befits us to fulfill all righteousness\", so that he who had come for the salvation of men should not pass over anything of the manner of life of men.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"MAT.3.15":["Allow it now. He said now beautifully, to show that Christ was to be baptized in water, John by Christ in the Spirit; or else, otherwise, allow it now, so that I who have taken up the form of a servant may fulfill its humility too. Otherwise know that on the day of judgment you are to be baptized with my baptism. Allow it now, says the Lord; I have also another baptism with which I am to be baptized; you baptize me in water so that I may baptize you, for my sake, in your own blood.\n— Commentary on Matthew","For thus it befits us to fulfill all justice. He did not add the justice of the law or of nature, so that we may understand both; and if God received baptism from a man, let no one disdain to receive it from a fellow servant.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.3.16-17":["The heavens were opened to him, and he saw the Spirit descending like a dove, coming upon him, and the rest. The mystery of the Trinity is shown in the baptism. The Lord is baptized, the Spirit descends in the form of a dove, the voice of the Father is heard bearing witness to the Son. And the heavens are opened, not by an unbarring of the elements, but to spiritual eyes, with which Ezekiel too, at the beginning of his volume, records that they were opened. The dove sat, moreover, upon the head of Jesus, lest anyone should think that the voice of the Father was addressed to John and not to the Lord.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.4.1":["Then he was led in the desert by the Spirit. No doubt, by the Holy Spirit. For there follows:\n\nTo be tempted by the devil. But he is led not unwillingly, nor as a captive, but by the will to fight.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.4.2":["And when he had fasted forty days and forty nights, afterward he was hungry. In this number the mystery of the forty days is shown to us, in which Moses too fasted on Mount Sinai and Elijah beside Mount Horeb. But the body is allowed to be hungry so that the devil may be given the occasion to tempt.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.4.3":["Say that these stones become loaves. It is fittingly said to one who is hungry: Say that these stones become loaves. But you are held fast by two contraries, devil: if at his command stones can become loaves, then you tempt in vain one who is of such great power; but if he cannot do it, in vain do you suspect him of being the Son of God: If you are the Son of God, say that these stones become loaves.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.4.4":["Who answering said: It is written: Not by bread alone does man live, but by every word that proceeds from the mouth of God. The testimony is taken from Deuteronomy. And the Lord answered thus because his purpose was to overcome the devil by humility, not by power. At the same time it must be noted that, had the Lord not begun to fast, the devil would have had no occasion to tempt, according to that saying: Son, as you come to the service of God, prepare your soul for temptation. But the Savior's answer itself also shows that it was a man who was tempted: Not by bread alone does man live, but by every word that proceeds from the mouth of God. So if anyone does not feed on the word of God, he does not live.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.4.5":["Then the devil took him up into the holy city. This taking up that is spoken of does not come from any weakness of the Lord, but from the pride of the enemy, who thinks the Savior's will is necessity. From this passage, moreover, it is understood what that is which is written in another place: They went into the holy city and appeared to many.\n— Commentary on Matthew","He set him on the pinnacle of the temple, so that the one he had tempted by hunger he might tempt also by vainglory.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.4.6":["If you are the Son of God, throw yourself down. In all the temptations the devil works at this: to find out whether he is the Son of God. But the Lord so tempers his answer as to leave him in doubt.\n— Commentary on Matthew","Throw yourself down. The voice of the devil, by which he always desires that all should fall down; Throw yourself, he says; he can persuade, he cannot cast down.\n— Commentary on Matthew","He has commanded his angels concerning you, that they bear you up in their hands, lest perhaps you strike your foot against a stone. This we read in Psalm 90 (91). But there the prophecy is not about Christ but about a holy man. So the devil interprets the Scriptures badly. Surely, if he really knew it was written of the Savior, he ought also to have quoted what follows in the same psalm against himself: You shall walk upon the asp and the basilisk, and you shall trample the lion and the dragon. Of the help of the angels he speaks as though to a weak man; of his own trampling, like a shifty evader, he is silent.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.4.7":["Jesus said to him: Again it is written: You shall not tempt the Lord your God. The false arrows of the devil, drawn from the Scriptures, he breaks with the true shields of the Scriptures. And it should be noted that he brought forward the necessary testimonies from Deuteronomy only, to show the mysteries of the second law.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.4.8":["Again the devil takes him up onto a very high mountain and shows him all the kingdoms of the world and their glory. The glory of the world, which is to pass away with the world, is displayed on a mountain and on a height. But the Lord comes down to low places and plains, so as to overcome the devil by humility. The devil, moreover, hurries to lead him to the mountains, so that through the mountains by which he himself had fallen the rest too may fall, according to that saying of the Apostle: Lest, puffed up, he fall into the judgment of the devil.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.4.9":["All these I will give you, if falling down you worship me. Arrogant and proud, he says this too out of boastfulness, not because the devil has power in the whole world so as to be able to give all kingdoms, since we know that very many holy men were made kings by God. If falling down, he says, you worship me. So whoever is going to worship the devil has fallen beforehand.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.4.10":["Then Jesus said to him: Go, Satan; it is written: You shall worship the Lord your God and him alone shall you serve. Satan and the apostle Peter are not, as most think, condemned by the same sentence. For to Peter it is said: Go behind me, Satan, that is, follow me, you who are contrary to my will; but here he hears: go, Satan, and behind is not said to him, so that it is understood: go into the eternal fire that has been prepared for you and your angels.\n— Commentary on Matthew","You shall worship the Lord your God. The devil, saying to the Savior: If falling down you worship me, hears on the contrary that it is rather he himself who ought to worship him as his Lord and God.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.4.11":["Then the devil left him, and angels came and were ministering to him. The temptation comes first so that victory may follow. Angels minister so that the dignity of the victor may be proven.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.4.13-16":["We have set down the double edition because the testimony is well known and was used by the Evangelist Matthew: so that either the difference of the translation or the likeness may be recognized. And first it must be noted that the Evangelist Matthew set down this testimony not according to the Seventy but according to the Hebrews. For the Gospel word says: Now when Jesus heard that John had been delivered up, he withdrew into Galilee, and leaving Nazareth he came and dwelt in Capernaum, which is by the sea in the borders of Zebulun and Naphtali: that what was spoken through Isaiah the prophet might be fulfilled, saying: The land of Zebulun and the land of Naphtali, the way of the sea beyond the Jordan, Galilee of the nations: the people that sat in darkness has seen a great light; to those sitting in the region of the shadow of death, light has risen upon them. From that time Jesus began to preach and to say: The kingdom of heaven has drawn near (Matt 4:13ff.). And John the Evangelist relates that Jesus, invited with his disciples to a wedding at Cana of Galilee, did his first sign there, turning water into wine: This beginning of signs Jesus did in Cana of Galilee: and he manifested his glory: and his disciples believed in him (John 2:11). Hence too it is said in the Seventy: Drink this first, act quickly: because the land of Zebulun and the land of Naphtali first saw the miracles of Christ: so that the land which had first seen the Lord doing signs might first drink the draught of faith. And according to the Hebrew it is said to have been lightened at the first time of the burden of sins: because the Savior first preached the Gospel in the regions of the two tribes. Hence too it is said in the sixty-seventh Psalm: Bless the Lord from the fountains of Israel. There is Benjamin the younger (Ps 67 [68]:27-28; Acts 9); Paul the apostle, in ecstasy of mind, who also said elsewhere: whether we are beside ourselves, it is for God (2 Cor 5:13): The princes of Zebulun, the princes of Naphtali, their leaders; because in these tribes were the little villages from which our leaders the Apostles believed. And they believed, according to Symmachus, swiftly, so that, hearing: Come, and I will make you fishers of men (Matt 4:19), they at once left both their father and their boat. But at the last time their faith was made heavy, since very many of the Jews remained in error. And here he calls the sea the lake of Gennesaret, which is formed by the inflowing Jordan; on whose shore Capernaum and Tiberias and Bethsaida and Chorazin are situated: in which region above all the Lord stayed; so that the people which either sat or walked in darkness saw a light, by no means a small one, like that of the other prophets, but a great one, like that of him who says in the Gospel: I am the light of the world (John 8:12). And those who dwelt in the region of the shadow of death, light has risen upon them. Between death and the shadow of death I think the difference is this: that death belongs to those who have gone down to the underworld with dead works: For the soul that sins, it shall die (Ezek 18:20); but the shadow of death belongs to those who, while they sin, have not yet departed from this life: for they can, if they will, do penance. For Galilee of the nations Aquila translated θῖνας of the nations, Symmachus borders of the nations; and by θῖνας we understand mounds of sand, which are either on the shores or on the banks. The Hebrews who believe in Christ set out this place thus: At the first time these two tribes, Zebulun and Naphtali, were captured by the Assyrians and led into the enemy's land; and Galilee was deserted (2 Kgs 15), which the prophet now says was lightened, because it bore the sins of the people. But afterward not only the two tribes, but also the rest, who dwelt beyond the Jordan and in Samaria, were led into captivity (2 Kgs 17 and 18). And this, they say, is what the Scripture now says: that the region whose people was the first to be led into captivity and began to serve the Babylonians, and which before was moving in the darkness of error, itself first saw the light of Christ preaching, and from it the Gospel was sown into all the nations. The Nazaraeans, whose opinion I set down above, try to explain this place thus: When Christ came and his preaching flashed forth, the land of Zebulun and the land of Naphtali was the first to be freed from the errors of the Scribes and Pharisees, and shook off from its neck the most heavy yoke of the Jewish traditions. But afterward, through the Gospel of the Apostle Paul, who was the last of all the Apostles, the preaching was made heavy, that is, multiplied; and the Gospel of Christ shone into the borders of the nations and the way of the whole sea. Finally, the whole world, which before walked or sat in darkness and was held by the chains of idolatry and of death, beheld the clear light of the Gospel.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"MAT.4.15":["Land of Zebulun and land of Naphtali, and the rest. These were the first to hear the Lord preaching, so that where Israel's first captivity at the hands of the Assyrians had been, there the proclamation of the Redeemer should arise.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.4.17":["From then on Jesus began to preach and to say: Repent, for the kingdom of heaven will draw near. With John handed over, he himself rightly begins to preach. As the law ceases, the Gospel accordingly arises. And if the Savior preaches the same things that John the Baptist had said before, he shows that he is the Son of the same God whose prophet the latter is.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.4.19":["Come after me, and I will make you become fishers of men. These were the first called to follow the Lord; fishermen and unlettered men are sent to preach, lest the faith of believers be thought to come about not by the power of God but by eloquence and learning.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.4.24":["And the lunatics and the paralytics, and he cured them. Not truly lunatics, but those who were thought to be lunatics because of the deceit of demons, who, observing the lunar seasons, wished to defame the creature so that blasphemies might overflow onto the Creator.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.5.1":["But seeing the crowds he went up onto the mountain, and when he had sat down his disciples came to him. The Lord climbs up to the mountains so as to draw the crowds with him to higher things; but the crowds are not able to go up, and the disciples follow, to whom also he speaks not standing but seated and drawn in. For they could not have understood him shining in his majesty. According to the letter, some of the simpler brothers think that he taught the beatitudes and the rest that follows on the Mount of Olives, which is by no means so. For from what precedes and what follows the place is shown to be in Galilee, and we think it is either Tabor or some other high mountain. In short, after he finished his discourses there follows at once: But when he had entered Capernaum.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.5.3":["Blessed are the poor in spirit. This is what we read elsewhere: And he will save the humble in spirit. But lest anyone should think that poverty, which is sometimes borne of necessity, is being proclaimed by the Lord, he added in spirit, so that you would understand humility, not want. Blessed are the poor in spirit, who for the sake of the Holy Spirit are poor by their own will. Hence the Savior too speaks through Isaiah about poor men of this kind: The Lord has anointed me; for this reason he has sent me to preach the Gospel to the poor.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.5.4":["Blessed are those who mourn, for they shall be comforted. The mourning set down here is not for those dead by the common law of nature, but for those dead in sins and vices. Thus Samuel also wept for Saul, because the Lord had repented that he had anointed him king over Israel; thus the apostle Paul too says that he weeps and mourns for those who after fornication and impurity have not repented.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.5.5":["Blessed are the meek, for they shall possess the land. Not the land of Judea, nor the land of this world, not the cursed land bringing forth thorns and thistles, which every most cruel man and warrior possesses more than others, but the land that the psalmist longs for: I believe that I shall see the good things of the Lord in the land of the living. A possessor of this kind, and after his victory a triumphant one, is also described in Psalm 44 (45): And press on, and proceed prosperously, and reign, for the sake of truth and meekness and justice. For no one possesses this earthly land through meekness, but through pride.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.5.6":["Blessed are those who hunger and thirst for justice. It is not enough for us to want justice unless we suffer hunger for justice, so that under this example we may understand that we are never just enough, but always hunger for the works of justice.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.5.7":["Blessed are the merciful. Mercy is understood not only in almsgiving but in every sin of a brother, if we bear one another's burdens.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.5.8":["Blessed are the pure in heart, for they shall see God. Those whom no consciousness of sin accuses. The Pure One is seen by the pure heart; the temple of God cannot be polluted.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.5.9":["Blessed are the peacemakers. Those who make peace first in their own heart, then among brothers at variance. For what does it profit that others are brought to peace through you, when in your own mind there are wars of vices?\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.5.10":["Blessed are those who suffer persecution for the sake of justice. He added pointedly: for the sake of justice. For many suffer persecution for their own sins and are not just. And at the same time consider that the eighth beatitude, that of the true circumcision, ends in martyrdom.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.5.11":["Blessed are you when they curse you and persecute you and say every evil against you, lying. That curse is to be despised, and creates blessedness, which is uttered by the mouth of one who curses falsely. Hence he also specifically defined what the blessed curse is, saying: every curse against you, lying, for my sake. So where Christ is the cause, there a curse is to be desired.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.5.12":["Rejoice and exult. I do not know which of us could fulfill this: that our reputation be torn with reproaches and we exult in the Lord. He who pursues vainglory cannot fulfill it. We ought therefore to rejoice and exult, so that a reward may be prepared for us in the heavens. We read it elegantly written in a certain book: Do not seek glory, and you will not grieve when you are without glory.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.5.13":["You are the salt of the earth. The apostles are called salt because through them the whole race of men is seasoned.\n— Commentary on Matthew","But if the salt has vanished, with what will it be salted? If the teacher has gone astray, by what other teacher will he be corrected?\n— Commentary on Matthew","It is good for nothing any more except to be cast out and trodden underfoot by men. The example is taken from agriculture. For salt, as it is necessary for seasoning foods and for drying meats, so it has no other use. Indeed, we read in the Scriptures that certain cities were sown with salt by the anger of their conquerors, so that no shoot might spring up in them. Let teachers and bishops therefore beware, and see that the mighty mightily endure torments and that there is no remedy, but that the falls of the greater lead down to Tartarus.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.5.14-15":["You are the light of the world. A city set upon a mountain cannot be hidden, nor do they light a lamp and put it under a bushel, and the rest. He teaches boldness in preaching, so that the apostles may not hide themselves out of fear and be like a lamp under a bushel, but may come forward with all freedom, so as to preach on the housetops what they have heard in the chambers.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.5.14":["Just as the Lord, the Light, calls his apostles light, and says: You are the light of the world; and the Rock himself granted to Peter that he should be a rock; and the good Shepherd also bestowed on them the name of shepherds, and whatever he himself is called he granted to his servants that they too should be called; so the Savior himself willed his apostles to be saviors of the world, who went up to the watchtower of the mountain of the Church and, bringing down the pride of the Jews and of all the mountains that were raised up against the knowledge of God, prepared the kingdom for the Lord.\n— Commentary on Obadiah"],"MAT.5.15":["The lamp they too understand as Christ himself, because he shines in the Church, he who said of himself: No one lights a lamp and puts it under a bushel, that is, under the measure of the law, but upon a lampstand, that is, the freedom of the Gospel, so that it may give light to all who are in the house.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"MAT.5.17":["Do not think that I have come to destroy the law or the prophets; I have not come to destroy but to fulfill. Either because he fulfilled what had been prophesied about himself through others; or because the things which before, on account of the weakness of the hearers, had been rough and imperfect, he completed by his preaching, taking away anger and the requital of retaliation, and shutting out even the lust hidden in the mind.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.5.18":["Until heaven and earth pass away. New heavens and a new earth are promised to us, which the Lord God will make. If, then, new things are to be created, it follows that the old will pass away. But as for what follows:\n\nOne iota or one apex shall not pass from the law until all things come to pass, it is shown by the figure of the letter that even the things thought least in the law are full of spiritual mysteries, and that all things are summed up in the Gospel. Of what learning, then, and of what doctrine is it to show that even the various sacrifices, and the things in the victims that seem superstitious, are fulfilled daily?\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.5.19":["But you, speak the things that befit sound doctrine. It is one thing to speak sound doctrine, another to teach the things that suit sound doctrine: for in the one there is simple instruction only; in the other, together with the teaching, there is also the correction of life. \"For whoever breaks one of these least commandments, and teaches men so, shall be called least in the kingdom of heaven\" (Matt. 5:19): the Lord commanding the disciples that they ought not only to labor in meditation on the Scriptures, so as to go over what is written and store it up in the treasury of memory, but first to do what has been commanded. \"But whoever does and teaches, he shall be called great in the kingdom of heaven.\" For unless our righteousness surpasses the righteousness of the scribes and of the Pharisees, we cannot enter the kingdoms of heaven (Matt. 5). They, sitting upon the chair of Moses, say and do not do, and bind unbearable burdens which they lay upon men's necks, and will not themselves touch them with their least finger. This, then, is what the Apostle now teaches Titus, his son in Christ and his disciple: to speak the things that suit sound doctrine; because doctrine has soundness then, when the teacher's doctrine and his life agree alike.\n— Commentary on Titus","Whoever destroys one of these least commandments and teaches men so shall be called least in the kingdom of heaven; but whoever does and teaches them shall be called great in the kingdom of heaven. This passage hangs together with the testimony above, in which he had said: One iota or one apex shall not pass from the law until all things come to pass. He therefore censures the Pharisees, who, despising the commandments of God, set up their own traditions: their teaching among the peoples does them no good if they destroy even a little of what is commanded in the law. But we can also understand it otherwise: that the learning of a master, if he is liable even to a small sin, brings him down from the highest rank, and that it does no good to teach justice which the least fault destroys, and that perfect blessedness is to fulfill in deed what you have taught in word.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.5.22":["Everyone who is angry with his brother. In certain codices there is added: without cause. But in the true ones the sentence is unqualified and anger is wholly taken away, as Scripture says: Whoever is angry with his brother. For if we are commanded to offer the other cheek to one who strikes us, and to love our enemies and pray for those who persecute us, every occasion of anger is taken away. Therefore there must be erased: without cause, for the anger of man does not work the justice of God.\n— Commentary on Matthew","But whoever says to his brother: Racha. This word is properly a word of the Hebrews. For Racha means κενός, that is, empty or void, one whom we can call, by a common insult, 'brainless'. If we are to render an account for an idle word, how much more for an insult? But it is also added pointedly: Whoever says to his brother: Racha. For no one is our brother except one who has the same Father with us. Since, then, he believes in God just as we do and knows Christ, the wisdom of God, by what reasoning can he be branded with the charge of folly?\n— Commentary on Matthew","But whoever says: Fool, shall be liable to Gehenna. Ἀπὸ κοινοῦ (from the shared clause) there is understood from the words above: Whoever says to his brother: Fool, shall be liable to Gehenna. For whoever says: Fool, to one who believes in God equally with him is impious in religion.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.5.23-24":["If, therefore, you are offering your gift at the altar and there remember that your brother has something against you. He did not say: if you have something against your brother, but if your brother has something against you, so that a harder necessity of reconciliation may be laid upon you. So long, then, as we cannot appease him, I do not know whether we may fittingly offer our gifts to God.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.5.25-26":["Be consenting to your adversary quickly, while you are with him on the way, lest perhaps the adversary hand you over to the judge, and the judge hand you over to the officer, and you be put into prison. Amen I say to you: You will not go out from there until you pay the last quadrans. Where we have consenting in the Latin codices, in the Greek is written εὐνοῶν, which is interpreted well-disposed or kind. Now from what precedes and what follows the sense is plain: that our Lord and Savior, while we run along the way of this world, exhorts us to peace and concord, according to the Apostle, who says: If it can be done, as far as lies in you, having peace with all men. For in the preceding passage too he had said: If you are offering your gift at the altar and there remember that your brother has something against you, and having finished this he immediately adds: Be well-disposed or kind to your adversary, and the rest. And in what follows he commands: Love your enemies, do good to those who hate you, and pray for your persecutors. Although this understanding is plain and follows from the context, very many think it was said of the flesh and the soul, or of the soul and the spirit, which does not stand at all. For how will the flesh be put into prison if it has not consented to the soul, since both soul and flesh are to be shut up alike, and the flesh can do nothing except what the mind commanded? Or how could the Holy Spirit dwelling in us hand over to the judge either the flesh or the soul resisting him, since he himself is the judge? Others, following the letter of Peter, who says: Our adversary the devil goes about like a roaring lion, interpret the adversary as the devil, and hold that the Savior commands that, while it is in our power, we should be well-disposed toward the devil, who is our enemy and avenger, and not make him undergo punishments on our account; for since he himself supplies the incitements to the vices, and we too sin by our own will, if we consent to him when he suggests the vices, he too must be tormented on our account; and they say that each of the saints is well-disposed to his adversary if he does not make him undergo torments on his account. Some argue in a more forced way that in baptism each one enters into a pact with the devil and says: I renounce you, devil, and your pomp and your vices and your world, which lies in the evil one. If, then, we keep the pact, we are well-disposed and consenting to our adversary and are by no means to be shut up in prison. But if we transgress in anything of what we had pledged to the devil, we shall be handed over to the judge and the officer and put into prison, and we shall not go out of it until we pay the last quadrans.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.5.26":["The quadrans is a kind of coin that contains two mites. Hence in one Gospel that poor widow woman is said to have put a quadrans into the corban, in another two mites; not that the Gospels are at variance, but because one quadrans contains two mites. This, then, is what he says: You will not come out of prison until you have paid even for the least sins.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.5.28":["Whoever looks at a woman to lust after her has already committed adultery with her in his heart. Between πάθος and προπάθειαν, that is, between passion and propassion, the difference is this: passion is reckoned as a vice; propassion, though it carries the fault of a beginning, is nevertheless not held as a crime. So whoever looks at a woman and his soul is tickled, he has been struck by propassion; but if he consents and turns the thought into an affection, as it is written in David: They have passed into the affection of the heart, he has passed from propassion to passion, and what he lacks is not the will to sin but the opportunity. Whoever, then, looks at a woman to lust after her, that is, if he so looks at her as to lust, as to resolve to do it, he is rightly said to have committed adultery with her in his heart.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.5.29-30":["But if your right eye causes you to stumble, and the rest. Because he had spoken above of lust for a woman, he now rightly called the thought and the mind flitting in different directions the eye. And by the right hand and the other parts of the body the beginnings of will and affection are shown, so that what we conceive in the mind we may accomplish in deed. We must beware, therefore, lest what is best in us quickly slip into vice. For if the right eye and the right hand cause us to stumble, how much more the things in us that are on the left! If the soul slips, how much more the body, which is more prone to sins! Alternatively: in the right eye and the right hand is shown our affection for brothers and wife and children and kinsmen and relatives; if we see that these are a hindrance to us in contemplating the true light, we ought to cut off such portions, lest, while we wish to gain the others, we ourselves perish forever. Hence it is said also of the high priest, whose soul is dedicated to the worship of God: Over mother and father and children he shall not be defiled, that is, he shall know no affection except for him to whose worship he is dedicated.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.5.31-32":["It was said: Whoever puts away his wife, let him give her a bill of divorce; but I say to you that everyone who puts away his wife, and the rest. In a later part the Savior explains this passage more fully: that Moses ordered a bill of divorce to be given on account of the hardness of heart of the husbands, not granting separation but taking away murder. For it is much better that discord, however grievous, should come about than that blood should be shed through hatred.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.5.34-36":["But I say to you not to swear at all, neither by heaven, and the rest. The Jews always had this most evil custom of swearing by the elements, as the word of the prophets frequently rebukes them. He who swears either reveres or loves the one by whom he swears. In the law it is commanded that we swear only by the Lord our God. The Jews, swearing by the angels and by the city of Jerusalem and by the temple and by the elements, venerated creatures and carnal things with the honor and homage due to God. In short, consider that here the Savior did not forbid swearing by God, but by heaven and earth and Jerusalem and by your head. And this had been granted by the law as if to little children: that just as they sacrificed victims to God so as not to sacrifice them to idols, so they were also permitted to swear by God, not that they did this rightly, but that it was better to offer it to God than to demons. But the truth of the Gospel does not admit an oath, since every faithful word stands in place of an oath.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.5.38-39":["You have heard that it was said: An eye for an eye, a tooth for a tooth; but I say to you not to resist evil. He who says: An eye for an eye, does not wish to take away the other eye too, but to preserve both. Our Lord, taking away requital, cuts off the beginnings of sins. And in the law there is retribution, in the Gospel grace; there the fault is corrected, here the beginnings of sins are taken away.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.5.39":["And there was a likeness of palms on this side and on that: elegantly, in the interior of the temple and in the Holy of Holies, he did not put 'right and left', lest he seem to say anything sinister in things that are great and secret, but 'on this side and on that' — according to what is written in the Gospel: Whoever strikes you on the right cheek, offer him the other also. Could he not have said 'and the left'? But when the right is struck, another right is offered, because in a holy man both are right, both are perfect —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","If anyone strikes you on your right cheek, offer him the other too; and to him who wishes to contend with you in judgment and take your tunic, let him have your cloak as well. The man of the Church is described, the imitator of him who says: Learn from me, for I am meek and humble of heart; and who, struck with a slap, proves his promise: If I have spoken evil, rebuke me; but if well, why do you strike me? David too said something of the kind in the psalm: If I have repaid with evils those who repaid me; and Jeremiah in the Lamentations: It is good for a man when he has sat from his youth; he will give his cheek to the one who strikes him, he will be filled with reproaches. This is against those who think there is one God of the law and another of the Gospel, since meekness is taught both there and here. According to the mystical understanding, when our right cheek is struck we are commanded to offer not the left but the other, that is, the other right one. For the just man has no left. If a heretic strikes us in disputation and wishes to wound a right doctrine, let another testimony from the Scriptures be set against him, and let us offer to the one who strikes us right cheeks succeeding one another, until the anger of the enemy grows weary.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.5.42":["Give to him who asks of you, and do not turn away from him who wishes to borrow from you. If we understand it as said only of almsgiving, in the case of most poor people this cannot stand. And even the rich, if they always give, will not be able always to give. After the good of almsgiving, then, precepts are given to the apostles, that is, to teachers, so that those who have received freely may bestow freely. Money of this kind never runs out, but the more of it is given, the more it is doubled; and though it waters the fields below it, the water of the spring never dries up.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.5.44":["But I say to you: Love your enemies, do good to those who hate you. Many, judging the precepts of God by their own weakness and not by the strength of the saints, think that the things commanded are impossible, and say that it is enough for the virtues not to hate one's enemies; but that to love them is to command more than human nature can bear. It must be known, then, that Christ commands not impossible things but perfect ones, which David did toward Saul and Absalom. Stephen the martyr too prayed for the enemies who were stoning him, and Paul longs to be anathema for his persecutors. And these things Jesus both taught and did, saying: Father, forgive them; for they do not know what they are doing.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.5.45":["That you may be sons of your Father who is in heaven. If by keeping the precepts of God someone is made a son of God, then he is a son not by nature but by his own choice.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.6.2":["When therefore you give alms, do not sound a trumpet before you, as the hypocrites do in the synagogues and in the streets, that they may be honored by men. He who sounds a trumpet when giving alms is a hypocrite; he who prays in the synagogues and at the corners of the squares so as to be seen by men is a hypocrite; he who when fasting demolishes his face so as to show the emptiness of his belly in his countenance, he too is a hypocrite. From all of which it is gathered that hypocrites are those who do anything at all in order to be glorified by men. To me it seems that he too who says to his brother: Let me take the mote out of your eye, does this for the sake of glory, so that he himself may seem to be just. Hence it is said to him by the Lord: Hypocrite, first cast the beam out of your own eye. Only then is the virtue of an observance acceptable to God, if it is done for God's sake. So it is not the virtue but the cause of the virtue that has a reward with God. And if you turn aside a little from the right path, it makes no difference whether you go to the right or to the left, since you have lost the true way.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.6.3":["But when you give alms, let your left hand not know what your right hand is doing. Not only when we give alms but whenever we do any good work, the left hand ought not to know; for if it knows, the works of the right hand are at once defiled.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.6.5":["Amen I say to you: They have received their reward, not God's reward but their own; for they were praised by men, for whose sake they practiced the virtues.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.6.6":["But you, when you pray, go into your chamber and, having shut your door, pray to your Father in secret. Understood simply, too, it instructs the hearer to flee vain glory in praying. But to me this seems rather to be the command, that with the thought of the breast shut in and with lips pressed together we should pray to the Lord, as we read that Hannah also did in the book of Kings: Only her lips, he says, were moving, and her voice was not heard.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.6.7":["But in praying do not speak much, as the heathen do. If the heathen speaks much in prayer, then he who is a Christian ought to speak little. God indeed is the hearer not of words but of the heart.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.6.8":["For your Father knows what you need before you ask. At this place there rises up a certain heresy, the perverse doctrine also of philosophers, who say: If God knows what we are going to pray for, and knows before we ask what we need, we speak in vain to one who knows. To them we must answer briefly that we are not narrators but petitioners. For it is one thing to narrate to one who does not know, another to ask of one who knows. In the former there is information, in the latter homage; there we faithfully inform, here we piteously beseech.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.6.9":["Our Father, who are in heaven. By saying Father they confess themselves sons.\n— Commentary on Matthew","Hallowed be your name, not in you but in us. For if because of sinners the name of God is blasphemed among the Gentiles, conversely because of the just it is hallowed.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.6.10":["Hearing this, the heretics of old slander the Creator as cruel and bloodthirsty, and do not consider that the apostle Paul, who is surely the apostle of the good God — as they themselves would have it —, writes to the Corinthians: If I come again I will not spare, so as to chastise offenders with the rod and draw back the erring to salvation; for those who do not understand what is profitable for them, and often pray for contrary things, it is expedient that they not be heard by the Lord. Hence in the prayer too we say: Your will be done; not our will, which is accustomed to err, but your will, which knows the things to come; and it is sometimes a great happiness not to merit mercy for the present.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","Your kingdom come. He asks either generally for the kingdom of the whole world, that the devil may cease to reign in the world, or that God may reign in each one, and that sin may not reign in the mortal body of men. At the same time this too must be noted, that it belongs to great boldness and a pure conscience to ask for the kingdom of God and not to fear the judgment.\n— Commentary on Matthew","Your will be done, as in heaven, also on earth, that as the angels serve you blamelessly in the heavens, so men may serve you on earth. Let those blush at this sentence who falsely claim that falls take place daily in heaven. For what does likeness to the heavens profit us, if there is sin in heaven too?\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.6.11":["Symmachus, then, for what is περιούσιον in the Greek, SGOLLA (סגלה) in the Hebrew, expressed ἐξαίρετον, that is, \"choice\" (egregium), or \"special\" (præcipuum); for which word, using the Latin language in another volume, he rendered \"peculiar\" (peculiarem). Rightly, then, did Christ Jesus, our great God and Savior, redeem us with his blood, to make the Christian people peculiar to himself, a people that could then be peculiar if it proved an emulator of good works. Hence also what is written in the Gospel according to the Latin translators, \"Give us this day our daily (quotidianum) bread\" (Matt. 6:11), is better rendered in the Greek \"our bread ἐπιούσιον\", that is, \"special\", \"choice\", \"peculiar\": namely him who, coming down from heaven, says, \"I am the bread that came down from heaven\" (John 6:51). Far be it, indeed, that we, who are forbidden to take thought for the morrow, should be commanded in the Lord's prayer to ask for that bread which in a little while is to be digested and cast out into the drain. Nor is there much difference between ἐπιούσιον and περιούσιον: for only the preposition has been changed, not the word. Some think that the bread in the Lord's prayer is called ἐπιούσιον because it is above all οὐσίας, that is, above all substances. If this is accepted, it does not differ much from the sense we have set out. For whatever is choice and special is outside all things and above all things.\n— Commentary on Titus","But it is better for us to understand that the bread of righteousness is he who said, I am the bread who came down from heaven, and whom we pray in prayer to be granted to us: Our substantial bread (or that is to come) give us, so that him whom we shall afterwards receive always, we may deserve to receive daily in the present age.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","Our supersubstantial bread give us today. What we have expressed as supersubstantial is in the Greek ἐπιούσιον, the word which the LXX translators very often rendered περιούσιον (special). We looked, therefore, in the Hebrew, and wherever they had expressed περιούσιον (special), we found sogolla, which Symmachus translated ἐξαίρετον, that is, choice or excellent, although in one place he rendered it peculiar. When therefore we ask God to grant us the peculiar or choice bread, we ask for that bread which says: I am the bread that came down from heaven. In the Gospel that is called according to the Hebrews, for supersubstantial bread I found maar, which means of tomorrow, so that the sense is: Our bread of tomorrow, that is, of the future, give us today. We can also understand the supersubstantial bread in another way, as that which is above all substances and surpasses all creatures. Others think simply, following the word of the Apostle who says: Having food and clothing, with these we are content, that the saints take care only for present food; whence it is also commanded later on: Do not think about tomorrow.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.6.13":["Amen. The seal of the Lord's Prayer, which Aquila translates as faithfully, and which we can render as truly.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.6.14":["For if you forgive men their sins. This which is written: I said, you are gods, and all of you sons of the Most High; but you shall die like men and fall like one of the princes, is said to those who because of their sins deserved to become men instead of gods. Rightly, then, those too whose sins are forgiven have been called men.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.6.16":["For they exterminate their faces, that they may appear to men as fasting. The word exterminate, which has become worn in the Church's Scriptures through the fault of translators, means something far different from what is commonly understood. For it is exiles, who are sent outside the boundaries, that are exterminated. In place of this word, then, we must always understand demolish. And the hypocrite demolishes his face so as to feign sadness and, while his mind is perhaps rejoicing, to wear mourning on his countenance.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.6.17":["But you, when you fast, anoint your head and wash your face. He speaks according to the custom of the province of Palestine, where on feast days they are accustomed to anoint their heads. He commands, therefore, that when we fast we should show ourselves glad and festive. Many, reading that saying of the psalmist: Let not the oil of the sinner fatten my head, hold by contrast that the oil is good of which it is said elsewhere: God, your God, has anointed you with the oil of gladness above your fellows. And he commands this, that while practicing the virtues we should anoint the ruling part of our heart with spiritual oil.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.6.21":["Where your treasure is, there is your heart also. This is to be understood not only of money but of all the passions. The glutton's god is his belly; so he has his heart there where he has his treasure too. The treasure of the luxurious is feasting, of the wanton amusements, of the lover lust. Each serves that by which he is overcome.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.6.22":["If your eye is single, your whole body will be full of light. Those with bleary eyes commonly see many lamps. The single and pure eye looks upon things single and pure. He transfers all this to the understanding. For just as the whole body is in darkness if the eye is lacking, so if the soul has lost its principal brightness, the whole understanding will linger in darkness.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.6.23":["If then the light that is in you is darkness, how great will the darkness itself be? If the understanding, which is the light of the soul, is darkened by vice, with what darkness, do you think, is the gloom itself enveloped?\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.6.24":["You cannot serve God and mammon. In the Syriac language riches are called mammona. You cannot serve God and mammon. Let the avaricious man hear this, let him hear who is enrolled under the Christian name, that one cannot serve riches and Christ at the same time. And yet he did not say: he who has riches, but: he who serves riches. For he who is the slave of riches guards his riches as a slave; but he who has shaken off the yoke of slavery distributes them as a master.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.6.25":["Do not be anxious for your soul, what you shall eat, nor for your body, what you shall put on. In some manuscripts there is added: nor what you shall drink. So from care for that which nature has granted to all, and which is common to beasts of burden and wild beasts and men, we are wholly set free. But we are commanded not to be anxious about what we shall eat, because in the sweat of our face we prepare bread for ourselves. Labor must be exercised, anxiety removed. This which is said: Do not be anxious for your soul, what you shall eat, nor for your body, what you shall put on, let us take of fleshly food and clothing. But for spiritual food and clothing we ought always to be anxious.\n— Commentary on Matthew","Is not the soul more than food, and the body more than clothing? What he says is of this kind: He who has granted the greater will surely grant the lesser too.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.6.26":["Look at the birds of the air, that they neither sow nor reap nor gather into their barns, and your heavenly Father feeds them. Are you not worth more than they? The Apostle commands that we not be wiser than it behooves us to be wise. This testimony must be kept in the present passage too. For there are some who, while they wish to overstep the boundaries of the fathers and to fly up to the heights, are plunged into the depths, saying that the birds of the air are the angels and the other powers in the ministry of God, which are fed without care of their own by God's providence. If this is so, as they wish it to be understood, how does what is said to men follow: Are you not worth more than they? It is to be taken simply, therefore: if the birds, which are today and tomorrow will not be, whose soul is mortal and which, once they have ceased to be, will never be again, are fed by God's providence without care and toil, how much more will men, to whom eternity is promised, be ruled by God's governance.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.6.28-29":["Which of you can add one cubit to his stature? And why are you anxious about clothing? As he showed by the comparison of the birds that the soul is more than food, so from the things that follow he shows that the body is more than clothing, saying:\n\nConsider the lilies of the field, how they grow, and the rest. And truly, what silk, what purple of kings, what picture of weavers can be compared with the flowers? What is as red as the rose? What is as white as the lily? But that the purple of the violet is surpassed by no murex dye is a matter for the judgment of the eyes rather than of words.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.6.34":["Do not be anxious about tomorrow. So he who forbids us to think of the future has granted that we ought to be anxious about the present. Hence the Apostle too: Night, he says, and day laboring with our hands, so as not to burden any of you. In the Scriptures tomorrow is understood as the time to come, as Jacob says: And my justice will hear me tomorrow; and in the apparition of Samuel the pythoness says to Saul: Tomorrow you will be with me.\n— Commentary on Matthew","Sufficient for the day is its own evil. Here he has set down evil not as the contrary of virtue, but as the toil and affliction and straits of the world, just as Sarah too afflicted Hagar her maidservant; which is expressively said in Greek ἐκάκωσεν αὐτήν (she did her evil, i.e. ill-treated her). So the thought of the present time is enough for us; let us leave aside the care of things to come, which is uncertain.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.7.1-2":["Do not judge, that you may not be judged. If he forbids judging, by what consistency does Paul in Corinth judge the fornicator, and Peter convict Ananias and Sapphira of lying? But from what follows he shows what he forbade, saying: for as you judge, so will it be judged of you. Thus he did not forbid judging but taught it.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.7.3-5":["But why do you see the mote in your brother's eye and do not see the beam in your own eye, and the rest. He speaks of those who, though they are themselves held liable for a mortal crime, do not pardon lesser sins in their brothers, straining out the gnat and swallowing the camel. Rightly, then, these too, as we said above, are called hypocrites for their pretense of justice, who through the beam in their own eye look at the mote in their brother's eye.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.7.6":["Do not give what is holy to the dogs. What is holy is the children's bread. We ought not, then, to take the children's bread and give it to the dogs.\n— Commentary on Matthew","Nor cast your pearls before swine. The pig, which wallows in mud-holes, does not receive adornment, and according to the proverbs of Solomon: If it has a golden ring, it is found the more foul. Some would have the dogs understood as those who after faith in Christ return to the vomit of their sins, and the swine as those who have not yet believed the Gospel and wallow in the mud of unbelief and vices. It is not fitting, therefore, to entrust the pearl of the Gospel hastily to men of this kind, lest they trample it underfoot and, turning, begin to tear you apart.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.7.7-8":["Ask and it will be given to you, seek and you will find, knock and it will be opened to you. He who above had forbidden fleshly things to be asked for shows what we ought to seek. If it is given to him who asks, and he who seeks finds, and to him who knocks it will be opened, then of him to whom it is not given, and who does not find, and to whom it will not be opened, it is plain that he has not asked, sought or knocked well. Let us knock, therefore, at the door of Christ, of which it is said: This is the gate of the Lord, the just shall enter by it, so that when we have entered there may be opened to us the hidden and dark treasures in Christ Jesus, in whom is all knowledge.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.7.11":["Nor is it surprising if Paul designated the things that were necessary to the body by the name of good, since even our Savior, to those who had not yet ascended to the summit of the virtues but were still walking on a lower level, and who were asking that faith be added to them, said: \"If therefore you, though you are evil, know how to give good gifts to your children, how much more will your heavenly Father give good things to those who ask him\" (Matt. 7:11)!\n— Commentary on Galatians","If you then, though you are evil, know how to give good things to your children. It must be noted that he called the apostles evil, unless perhaps under the person of the apostles the whole human race is condemned, whose heart, in comparison with the divine clemency, is set on evil from infancy. Read Genesis. Nor is it strange that the men of this world are called evil, since the Apostle Paul too recalls: Redeeming the time, because the days are evil.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.7.12":["The same thing is said in other words: \"What you do not wish to be done to you, do not do to others; and whatever you wish that men should do to you, do you also likewise the same to them\" (ibid. 7:12). I do not wish my wife to be defiled by adultery, I do not wish my property to be plundered, I do not wish to be crushed by false testimony, and, to comprise everything in a brief word, I take it ill that anything unjust should be done to me. If, through love working in me, I either do or wish these same things for another, the whole law is fulfilled.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"MAT.7.13-14":["Enter by the narrow gate; how wide is the gate and broad the way that leads to destruction, and many are those who enter by it; how narrow is the gate and strait the way that leads to life, and few are those who find it. The wide way is the pleasures of the world, which men seek after; the narrow one is that which is opened up through labors and fasts, which the Apostle too entered upon, and he urges Timothy to enter by it. At the same time consider how expressively he has spoken of both ways. Many walk by the wide one; few find the narrow. The wide one we do not seek, nor is there need of finding it: it offers itself of its own accord and is the way of those who go astray; but the narrow one neither do all find, nor do those who have found it enter by it at once. Indeed many, having found the way of truth, are taken captive by the pleasures of the world and turn back from the middle of the journey.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.7.15":["Beware of false prophets, who come to you in sheep's clothing, but inwardly are ravening wolves. This can indeed be understood of all who promise one thing by their dress and speech and show another by their deeds. But it is to be understood especially of the heretics, who seem to wrap themselves in continence, chastity and fasting as in a kind of garment of piety, but, having inwardly a poisoned mind, deceive the hearts of the simpler brothers. So from the fruits of the soul by which they drag innocence to ruin they are compared to ravening wolves.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.7.18":["That saying too which is spoken by the Savior, \"A good tree cannot bring forth evil fruits, nor an evil tree bring forth good fruits\" (Matt. 7:18), I think was pronounced not so much about men as about the fruits of the flesh and of the spirit: because neither can the spirit ever commit those vices which have been enumerated among the works of the flesh, nor can the flesh abound in these fruits which arise from the spirit. It can happen, however, through the negligence of the one who possesses it, that the spirit which dwells in a man does not have its fruits; and, on the contrary, that the flesh, its works having been put to death, ceases to sin. Yet they do not go so far that the tree of the spirit, when neglected, bears the works of the flesh, and the tree of the flesh, when cultivated, puts forth spiritual fruits.\n— Commentary on Galatians","However the proper sense of the names stands, this is to be said: that evil things are turned into good, and virtues spring up in place of vices, that is, righteousness in place of iniquity, fortitude in place of rashness, temperance in place of luxury, prudence in place of folly. Let us give examples of our forebears: Matthew and Zacchaeus and the publicans were saliunca, and στοιβή, and useless stalks, and κονύζη of bitter taste and most loathsome smell, saying: My scars have rotted and are corrupted, because of my folly (Ps 37 [38]:6). These, suddenly changed into Apostles, became cypress and fir and myrtle, of the best fragrance and needful for various works. Paul too, the persecutor of the Church, when he heard from the Lord, Saul, Saul, why do you persecute me? It is hard to kick against the goad (Acts 9:4, 5), was a nettle, having the stings of persecution. But when he preached the Gospel in the whole world and could say, We are the good odor of Christ (2 Cor 2:15), he is rightly called a cypress and a myrtle. Harlots and publicans go before the Pharisees into the kingdom of God, and the thief passes from the cross into paradise (Matt 21). Therefore what is said in the Gospel, A good tree cannot bear evil fruits (Luke 6:43; Matt 7:18), by no means refers to a property of nature, as the heretics would have it, but to the choice of the mind. Finally it is added: Either make the tree good, and its fruits good. From this it is clear that each one by his own will makes his soul a good or an evil tree, whose fruits vary.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","A good tree cannot bear bad fruit, nor a bad tree bear good fruit. Let us ask the heretics who say that there are two natures contrary to each other: if, according to their understanding, a good tree can never bear bad fruit, how did Moses, a good tree, sin at the water of contradiction, and David, after Uriah had been killed, lie with Bathsheba, and Peter too at the Lord's passion deny him, saying: I do not know the man? Or by what consistency did Jethro, Moses' father-in-law, a bad tree, who certainly did not believe in the God of Israel, give Moses good counsel; and Achior say something useful to Holofernes; and the comic poet say what the Apostle approved as well said: Very evil conversations corrupt good morals; and when they find nothing to answer, we add that Judas too, once a good tree, bore bad fruit after he betrayed the Savior, and that Paul, a bad tree at the time when he was persecuting the Church of Christ, afterwards bore good fruit, when from a persecutor he was changed into a vessel of election. So long, then, does a good tree not bear bad fruit as its zeal for goodness persists, and a bad tree remains in the fruits of sins as long as it is not converted to repentance. For no one, while remaining in what he was, begins to be what he has not yet begun to be.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.7.21-23":["But what he commands: Say to your brothers (Matt 28:10), is not to be taken simply; otherwise many call others brothers and do not keep love in their heart, of whom the Apostle writes: They profess that they know God, but in their works they deny him (Titus 1:16). Of these the Lord too was speaking: Not everyone who says to me: Lord, Lord, shall enter the kingdom of heaven: but he who does the will of my Father who is in heaven (Matt 7:21). And so that sentence can stand: No one says, Lord Jesus, except in the Holy Spirit (1 Cor 12:3). For though the heretics say Lord Jesus, and many in the resurrection will say: Lord, did we not prophesy in your name, and do many mighty works (Matt 7:22)? the Lord will answer them: Depart from me, I do not know you, workers of iniquity. Therefore saying is proved not by speech, which is easy, but by disposition and works.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"MAT.7.21":["Not everyone who says to me: Lord, Lord, will enter into the kingdom of heaven, but he who does the will of my Father who is in heaven. As above he had said that those who had the garment of a good life were not to be received because of the wickedness of their doctrines, so now, conversely, he asserts that credence is not to be given even to those who, though strong in the integrity of the faith, live shamefully and destroy the integrity of their teaching by evil works. For both are necessary for the servants of God, that the work be approved by the word and the word by works. That saying may seem contrary to this sentence: No one can say: Lord Jesus, except in the Holy Spirit. But it is the custom of the Scriptures to take sayings for deeds, as is proved at once in what follows, where those are refuted who boast of knowledge of the Lord without works, and they hear from the Savior: Depart from me, workers of iniquity, I do not know you; and the Apostle speaks in this sense: They profess that they know God, but in deeds they deny him.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.7.22":["Many will say to me on that day: Lord, Lord, did we not prophesy in your name, and in your name cast out demons, and in your name do many mighty works? And then I will confess to them: I never knew you; depart from me, you who work iniquity. To prophesy and to do mighty works and to cast out demons is sometimes not a matter of the merit of him who performs them, but either the invocation of the name of Christ does this, or it is granted for the condemnation of those who invoke it, or for the benefit of those who see and hear, so that, though men may despise those who do the signs, they may yet honor God, at whose invocation such great miracles are done. For Saul and Balaam and Caiaphas prophesied not knowing what they said, and Pharaoh and Nebuchadnezzar learn the future through dreams, and in the Acts of the Apostles the sons of Sceva seemed to cast out demons, and Judas the apostle, with the mind of a traitor, is related to have done many signs among the other apostles.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.7.23":["Then I will confess to them. He said expressively: I will confess, because for a long time before he had refrained from saying: I do not know you. The Lord does not know those who perish. But note why he added: I never knew you, if, as some hold, all men have always dwelt among the rational creatures.\n— Commentary on Matthew","Withdraw from me, you who work iniquity. He did not say: you who have worked iniquity, lest he seem to take away repentance, but you who work, that is, you who up to the present hour, when the time of judgment has come, although you no longer have the opportunity of sinning, still have the inclination.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.7.25":["And the rain came down, and the floods came, and the winds blew. That rain which strives to overturn the house is the devil; the floods are all the antichrists who think against Christ; the winds are the spiritual wickednesses in the heavenly places.\n— Commentary on Matthew","And it did not fall; for it was founded upon the rock. Upon this rock the Lord founded the Church; from this rock the apostle Peter too received his name; upon a rock of this kind the traces of the serpent are not found; of this the prophet too speaks with confidence: He has set my feet upon the rock, and in another place: The rock is a refuge for the hares, or for the hedgehogs. For the timid animal takes refuge in the hollows of the rock, and the one whose skin is rough and armed all over with darts keeps itself safe by such protection. Hence it is said to Moses too, at the time when he had fled from Egypt and was the Lord's little hare: Stand in the cleft of the rock, and you will see my back parts.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.7.26":["Who built his house upon the sand. The foundation which the Apostle as architect laid is one, our Lord Jesus Christ. Upon this foundation, stable and firm and grounded on its own strong mass, the Church of Christ is built; but upon the sand, which is fluid and cannot be compacted or brought together into one bond, every discourse of the heretics is built, to this end, that it may fall.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.7.29":["And he was teaching them as one having authority, not as the scribes. For they taught the peoples the things that are written in Moses and the prophets, but Jesus, as God and Lord of Moses himself, by the freedom of his own will either added in the law the things that seemed lacking or, changing them, preached to the peoples, as we also read above: It was said to the ancients; but I say to you.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.8.1-2":["And when he had come down from the mountain, great crowds followed him. And behold, a leper came and worshiped him. As the Lord comes down from the mountain the crowds meet him, because they had not been able to ascend to the higher places. And the first to meet him is the leper. For with his leprosy he could not yet hear the Savior's so manifold discourse on the mountain. And it must be noted that this one was the first to be healed individually; second, the centurion's servant; third, Peter's mother-in-law, sick with fever, in Capernaum; in the fourth place, those vexed by a demon who were brought to him, whose spirits he cast out with a word, when he also healed all who were ill.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.8.2":["And behold, a leper came and worshiped him, saying. Rightly, after the preaching and teaching, an occasion for a sign is offered, so that through the miracles of mighty works the discourse just past may be confirmed among the hearers.\n— Commentary on Matthew","Lord, if you will, you can cleanse me. He who asks about the will does not doubt the power.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.8.3":["And Jesus, stretching out his hand, touched him, saying: I will, be cleansed. As the Lord stretches out his hand, at once the leprosy flees. At the same time consider how humble and free of boasting the answer is. The one had said: If you will; the Lord answered: I will; the one had put first: you can cleanse me; the Lord joins it and says: be cleansed. So it is not to be joined and read, as most of the Latins think: I will to cleanse, but separately, so that first he says: I will, and then commands: be cleansed.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.8.4":["And Jesus said to him: See that you tell no one. And truly, what need was there for him to boast in words of what he displayed in his body?\n— Commentary on Matthew","But go, show yourself to the priest and offer the gift that Moses commanded, as a testimony to them. He sends him to the priest for various reasons. First, for humility's sake, that he may be seen to defer to the priests. For it was commanded in the law that those who had been cleansed of leprosy should offer gifts to the priests. Next, so that, seeing the leper cleansed, they might either believe the Savior or not believe; if they believed, they would be saved; if they did not believe, they would be made inexcusable; and at the same time, so that he might not seem — a thing they very often charged against him — to break the law.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.8.7-8":["A centurion came to him, entreating him and saying: Lord, my servant lies at home paralyzed and is grievously tormented. And Jesus said to him: I will come and heal him and the rest. We ought not to accuse the Lord of boasting because he promises at once that he will go and heal, for he saw the centurion's faith, humility and prudence: his faith, in that, being of the Gentiles, he believed that the leper could be healed by the Savior; his humility, in that he judged himself unworthy that the Lord should enter under his roof; his prudence, in that beyond the covering of the body he saw the hidden divinity, knowing that what would profit him was not what was seen even by unbelievers, but what lay hidden within. Of this prudence he also says this:\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.8.9":["Behold, I, though I am a man, say to this one: Go, and he goes, and the rest. He wishes to show that the Lord too could accomplish what he wished not only through the coming of his body but through the ministries of angels.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.8.10":["And Jesus, hearing it, marveled. He marveled because he saw that the centurion understood his majesty. For both the infirmities of bodies and the opposing powers, to which a man is often given over to be disabled, had to be driven out, both by the word of the Lord and by the ministries of angels.\n— Commentary on Matthew","Amen I say to you: I have not found such faith in Israel. He speaks of those present, not of all the patriarchs and prophets of the past, unless perhaps in the centurion the faith of the Gentiles is set above Israel.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.8.11":["Many will come from the east and the west and will recline with Abraham and Isaac and Jacob in the kingdom of heaven. Because the God of Abraham, the maker of heaven, is the Father of Christ, therefore Abraham too is in the kingdom of heaven, and with him the nations that have believed in Christ, the Son of the Creator, are to recline. And that sense too is likewise fulfilled of which we spoke above, that in the centurion's faith the precedence of the Gentiles is established, since the peoples who will believe from east and west are mentioned with reference to his belief.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.8.12":["But the sons of the kingdom. By the sons of the kingdom he means the Jews, over whom God reigned before.\n— Commentary on Matthew","Will be cast out into the outer darkness. Darkness is always inner, not outer. But since he who is driven out of doors by the Lord leaves the light behind, therefore it has been named the outer darkness.\n— Commentary on Matthew","There will be weeping and gnashing of teeth. If weeping belongs to the eyes, and gnashing of teeth points to bones, then the resurrection of bodies, and of the same members that had fallen, is real.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.8.15":["When Jesus had come into Peter's house, he saw his mother-in-law lying ill with fever, and he touched her hand, and the fever left her, and she rose and served them. The woman's hand is touched, and as her works are healed the infirmity of sins flees. Human nature is such that after a fever bodies are the more wearied, and as health begins they feel the ills of the sickness. But the health that is conferred by the Lord restores everything at once; nor is it enough that she was healed, but so that an ἐπίτασις (intensification) of strength might be shown, it is added: and she rose, served them. That hand served which had been both touched and healed.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.8.16":["When evening had come, they brought to him many who had demons, and he cast out the spirits with a word and healed all who were ill. All are healed not in the morning, not at midday, but towards evening, when the sun is about to set, when the grain of wheat dies in the earth that it may bear much fruit.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.8.19-20":["And a scribe came up and said to him: Master, I will follow you wherever you go. And Jesus says to him: Foxes have holes and the birds of the air nests, but the Son of Man has nowhere to lay his head. This scribe of the law, who knew only the letter that kills, if he had said: Lord, I will follow you wherever you go, would not have been rejected by the Lord; but because he reckoned him one master among many, and was a man of letters (which is more expressively said in Greek γραμματεύς, and not a spiritual hearer), therefore Jesus has no place where he can lay his head. It is shown to us, moreover, that the scribe was rejected for this reason too, that, seeing the greatness of the signs, he wished to follow the Savior in order to seek gain from the miracles of his works, desiring the very thing that Simon Magus too had wished to buy from Peter. Such faith, then, is justly condemned by the Lord's sentence, and it is said to him: Why do you desire to follow me for riches and the gains of the world, when I am of such poverty that I do not even have a little lodging and do not use a roof of my own?\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.8.21":["And another of his disciples said to him: Lord, let me first go and bury my father. What likeness is there between the scribe and the disciple? The one calls him master, the other confesses him Lord; the one, on the occasion of piety, desires to go to bury his father, the other promises that he will follow wherever he goes, seeking not the master but gain from the master.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.8.22":["Jesus said to him: Follow me, and leave the dead to bury their own dead. Whoever does not believe is dead. But if the dead buries the dead, we ought to have care not for the dead but for the living, lest, while we are anxious about the dead, we too be called dead.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.8.23":["And as he went up into the boat his disciples followed him, and the rest. The fifth sign he did when, going up into the ship from Capernaum, he commanded the winds and the sea; the sixth, when in the country of the Gerasenes he gave the demons power over the swine; the seventh, when, entering his own city, he healed the second paralytic on his bed; for the first paralytic is the centurion's servant.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.8.24-25":["But he was sleeping. And they came and woke him, saying: Lord, save us. The type of this sign we read in Jonah, when, while the others are in peril, he himself is untroubled and sleeps, and is woken, and by the command and mystery of his passion frees those who woke him.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.8.26":["Then rising he rebuked the winds and the sea. And from this place we understand that all creatures perceive the Creator. For the things that are rebuked and commanded perceive the one who commands, not by the error of the heretics, who think all things are animate, but by the majesty of the Maker, to whom things insensible to us are sensible.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.8.27":["Moreover the men marveled, saying: What sort of man is this, that the winds and the sea obey him. It was not the disciples but the sailors and the others who were in the ship who marveled. But if anyone should contentiously maintain that those who marveled were the disciples, we shall answer that they were rightly called men, because they did not yet know the power of the Savior.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.8.29":["What have we to do with you, Son of God? Have you come here before the time to torment us? This confession is not one of will, on which the reward of confessing follows, but an extortion of necessity, which compels the unwilling; just as runaway slaves, if after a long time they see their master, beg for nothing else but to be spared the lash, so too the demons, seeing the Lord suddenly dwelling on earth, believed that he had come to judge them. The presence of the Savior is the torment of the demons. And some ridiculously think that the demons know the Son of God and the devil does not, because these are of less malice than he whose henchmen they are. Whereas all the knowledge of the disciples is to be referred to the master, and both the demons and the devil are to be understood rather to suspect the Son of God than to know him. For no one knows the Father except the Son and him to whom the Son wills to reveal him.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.8.32-33":["If you cast us out, send us into the herd of swine. And he said to them: Go. The Savior did not say: Go, as granting the demons what they asked, but so that through the killing of the swine an occasion of salvation might be offered to men. For the herdsmen, seeing these things, at once report them to the city. Let the Manichee blush. If the souls of men and of beasts are of the same substance and from the same author, how are two thousand swine drowned for the salvation of one man?\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.8.34":["And behold, the whole city went out to meet Jesus, and when they saw him they asked him to pass from their borders. Those who ask him to pass beyond their borders do this not from pride (as some suppose) but from humility, by which they judged themselves unworthy of the Lord's presence, just as Peter too, at the catch of fish, falling at the Savior's knees, says: Go out from me, Lord, for I am a sinful man.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.9.1":["And he came into his own city. And behold, they were bringing to him a paralytic lying on a bed. And Jesus, seeing their faith, said to the paralytic: Take heart, son, your sins are remitted to you, and the rest. Let us understand his city to be none other than Nazareth; whence he was also called a nazarene.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.9.2":["And, as we said above, they brought to him a second paralytic, lying on a little bed, because he himself was not able to walk in. And Jesus, seeing the faith, not of the one who was being brought but of those who were bringing him, said to the paralytic: Take heart, son, your sins are forgiven you. O wonderful humility! He calls son one who was despised and infirm and loosened in all the joints of his limbs, one whom the priests did not deign to touch. Or else he calls him son for this reason, that his sins are forgiven him. According to tropology, sometimes a soul lying in its own body, with all the powers of its limbs loosened, is brought by a perfect teacher to the Lord to be healed; and if it has been healed by his mercy, it receives so much strength that it at once carries its own little bed.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.9.3-4":["And behold, some of the scribes said within themselves: This man blasphemes. We read in the prophet, God saying: I am he who blots out your iniquities. Consistently, therefore, the scribes, because they thought him a man and understood the words of God, accuse him of blasphemy. But the Lord, seeing their thoughts, shows himself to be God, who can know the hidden things of the heart, and, as it were keeping silent, he speaks: By the same majesty and power by which I look upon your thoughts, I can also forgive men their offenses; understand from yourselves what the paralytic obtains.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.9.5":["Which is easier, to say: Your sins are forgiven you; or to say: Rise and walk? Between saying and doing there is a great distance. Whether the sins had been forgiven the paralytic, he alone knew who was forgiving them. Rise, however, and walk could be verified as much by him who was rising as by those who saw him rise. A bodily sign therefore is wrought so that the spiritual one may be proved, although it belongs to the same power to remit the faults both of body and of soul. And we are given to understand that many infirmities of bodies come about on account of sins, and that perhaps for this reason the sins are forgiven first, so that, once the causes of the infirmity have been taken away, health may be restored.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.9.6":["Take up your bed and go into your house. And the paralytic soul too, if it rises, if it recovers its former strength, carries its bed, on which it previously lay loosened, and carries it into the house of its own virtues.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.9.9":["And as Jesus was passing on from there, he saw a man sitting at the tax office, Matthew by name, and he said to him: Follow me. And he rose and followed him. The other evangelists, out of modesty and regard for Matthew, did not wish to call him by his common name, but said Levi; for he had a twofold name. But Matthew himself, according to what is enjoined by Solomon: The just man is his own accuser at the beginning of his speech; and in another place: Tell your sins, that you may be justified, names himself Matthew and a tax collector, to show his readers that no one ought to despair of salvation if he has turned to better things, since he himself was suddenly changed from a tax collector into an apostle. In this place Porphyry and Julian the Augustus charge either the ignorance of a lying historian or the folly of those who at once followed the Savior, as though they had irrationally followed any man at all who called them, when so many mighty works and so many signs had gone before, which there is no doubt the apostles saw before they believed. Certainly the very brightness and majesty of the hidden divinity, which shone forth even in his human face, could draw to himself at first sight those who saw him. For if the magnet stone and amber are said to have this power, that they join rings and straw and bits of chaff to themselves, how much more could the Lord of all creatures draw to himself those whom he willed.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.9.10":["And it came to pass, as he reclined at table in the house, behold, many tax collectors and sinners came and reclined with Jesus, and the rest. They had seen that a tax collector converted from sins to better things had found a place of repentance, and on that account they too do not despair of salvation; nor indeed do they come to Jesus remaining in their former vices, as the Pharisees and scribes murmur, but repenting, as the Lord's following word signifies, saying:\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.9.13":["I desire mercy and not sacrifice. I came not to call the just, but sinners. Now the Lord used to go to the banquets of sinners in order to have an occasion of teaching and to offer spiritual food to those who invited him. In short, though he is frequently described as going to banquets, nothing else is reported except what he did there, what he taught, so that both the humility of the Lord in going to sinners and the power of his teaching in the conversion of the penitent might be shown. And as for what follows: I desire mercy and not sacrifice; and: I came not to call the just, but sinners, bringing forward testimony from the prophet he rebukes the scribes and Pharisees, who, esteeming themselves just, shunned the company of sinners and tax collectors.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.9.14":["Then the disciples of John came to him, saying: Why do we and the Pharisees fast often, but your disciples do not fast? A proud question and full of the haughtiness of the Pharisees. Certainly (not to say anything else) the boasting of their fasting is to be reproved. Nor could the disciples of John but be at fault, since they were slandering him whom they knew to have been proclaimed by their master's voice, and were joining themselves to the Pharisees, whom they knew had been condemned by John: Brood of vipers, who showed you to flee from the wrath to come?\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.9.15":["Jesus said to them: Can the sons of the bridegroom mourn as long as the bridegroom is with them? But the days will come when the bridegroom will be taken away from them, and then they will fast. The bridegroom is Christ, the bride is the Church. From this holy and spiritual marriage the apostles were begotten, who cannot mourn as long as they see the bride in the bridal chamber and know that the bridegroom is with the bride. But when the wedding has passed and the time of the passion and resurrection has come, then the sons of the bridegroom will fast. Some think that for this reason fasts ought to be entered upon forty days after the passion, although the day of Pentecost at once, and the Holy Spirit's coming, indicate festivity to us; and taking occasion from this testimony, Montanus, Prisca and Maximilla keep a forty-day fast even after Pentecost, on the ground that, when the bridegroom has been taken away, the sons of the bridegroom ought to fast. But the custom of the Church comes to the passion and resurrection of the Lord through humility of the flesh, so that by the fasting of the body we may be prepared for the spiritual banquet. By the laws of tropology, however, we must know that as long as the bridegroom is with us and we are in gladness, we can neither fast nor mourn; but when on account of our sins he has withdrawn from us and flown away, then a fast must be proclaimed, then mourning must be taken up.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.9.16-17":["But our Jerusalem, which means 'vision of peace', is torn by the heretics, when, plucking one testimony of the Scriptures or another from their proper places, they try to sew them onto places to which they cannot be fitted; to whom, as we read, it was also said above in this same prophet: Woe to those who sew pillows under every elbow of the hand, and make a covering over the head of every age, and the Lord, forbidding this to be done, says in the Gospel: No one puts a patch of rough cloth on an old garment; otherwise it takes away from its fullness, and a worse tear is made. Nor do they put new wine into old wineskins; such are the Nazarenes, who try to fit the observance of the old Law to the grace of the Gospel, and all the heretics who strive to sew a testimony that fits one sense in its own place onto another, whereas the Lord's tunic, woven from the top throughout, cannot be torn, but, woven by the Holy Spirit, admits no tearing.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","No one puts a patch of unworked cloth on an old garment; for it takes its fullness away from the garment, and a worse tear is made. Nor do they put new wine into old wineskins; otherwise the wineskins will burst, and the wine will be poured out, and the wineskins will perish; but they put new wine into new wineskins, and both are preserved. What he says is this: until someone has been reborn and, having put off the old man, has put on the new man through my passion, he cannot bear the stricter fasts and the precepts of continence, lest through excessive severity he lose even the belief that he now seems to have. He set down two examples, then, of the garment and of the wineskins, old and new. By the old we ought to understand the scribes and Pharisees. By the patch of the new garment and the new wine are to be understood the precepts of the Gospel, which the Jews cannot bear, lest a greater tear be made. Some such thing the Galatians too were eager to do, to mix the precepts of the law with the Gospel and to put new wine into old wineskins. But the Apostle says to them: O foolish Galatians, who has bewitched you not to obey the truth? The word of the Gospel, therefore, is to be poured into the apostles rather than into the scribes and Pharisees, who, corrupted by the traditions of their elders, could not keep the purity of the precepts of Christ. For the purity of a virginal soul, polluted by no contagion of former vice, is one thing, and the filth of one who has lain subject to the lust of many is another.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.9.18":["Behold, a certain ruler came and worshiped him, saying: My daughter has just now died; but come, lay your hand upon her, and she will live. And Jesus rose and followed him, and his disciples. This is the eighth sign, in which the ruler asks that his daughter be raised, not wishing to be shut out from the mystery of the true circumcision; but the woman with a flow of blood slips in and is healed in the eighth place, so that the ruler's daughter, shut out from this number, comes to the ninth, according to what is said in the psalms: Ethiopia shall go before with her hands to God; and: When the fullness of the Gentiles has come in, then all Israel will be saved.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.9.20":["Behold, a woman who had suffered a flow of blood for twelve years came up behind and touched the fringe of his garment. In the Gospel according to Luke it is written that the ruler's daughter was twelve years of age. Note, therefore, that this woman, that is, the people of the Gentiles, began to be sick at the time when the nation of the Jews believed. For a vice is not shown except by comparison with virtues. Now this woman with a flow of blood approaches the Lord not in a house, not in a city, because according to the law she was excluded from cities, but on the way, as the Lord was walking, so that while he was going to one woman, another might be healed. Whence the apostles too say: It was necessary that the word be preached to you, but since you have judged yourselves unworthy of salvation, we turn to the Gentiles.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.9.21":["For she said within herself: If I only touch the fringe of his garment, I shall be well. According to the law, whoever touches a menstruous woman or one with a flow of blood is unclean. She touches the Lord for this reason, that she too may be healed of the fault of her blood.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.9.22":["Take heart, daughter, your faith has made you well. Daughter, for this reason, that your faith has made you well. Nor did he say: Your faith will make you well, but has made you well; for in that you believed, you have already been made well.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.9.23":["And when Jesus had come into the ruler's house and had seen the flute players and the crowd in an uproar. To this day the girl lies dead in the ruler's house, and those who seem to be teachers are flute players singing a mournful song. The crowd of the Jews, too, is not a crowd of believers but a crowd of people in an uproar.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.9.24":["The girl is not dead, but sleeps, because all things live to God.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.9.25":["And when the crowd had been put out, he went in and took her by the hand. For they were not worthy to see the mystery of her rising, who mocked him that was raising her with unworthy insults.\n— Commentary on Matthew","And he took her by the hand, and the girl arose. Unless the hands of the Jews, which are full of blood, are first cleansed, their dead synagogue does not rise.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.9.27-28":["And as Jesus passed on from there, two blind men followed him, crying out and saying: Have mercy on us, son of David. And when he had come into the house, the blind men came to him. As the Lord Jesus was passing on through the ruler's house and going to his own house, as we read above: Getting into a boat he crossed over and came into his own city, there were crying out two blind men, saying: Have mercy on us, son of David, and yet they are not healed on the way, not in passing as they supposed, but after he has come into his own house they approach him and enter, and first their faith is examined, so that in this way they may receive the light of true faith. To the previous sign that we have expounded, concerning the ruler's daughter and the woman with the hemorrhage, this one is joined in sequence, so that what death and infirmity showed there, blindness might show here. For each people was blind while the Lord was passing through this world and longing to return to his own house. Unless they confess and say: Have mercy on us, son of David, and, when Jesus asks: Do you believe that I can do this, answer him: Yes, Lord, they will not receive their former light. In another evangelist one blind man is written of, with torn garments and sitting in Jericho, who is forbidden by the apostles to cry out, but by his importunity receives healing. That passage pertains properly to the people of the Gentiles and is to be expounded in its own book. Son of David, have mercy on us. Let Marcion and the Manichee and the other heretics who tear apart the Old Testament hear, and learn that the Savior is called son of David. For if he was not born in the flesh, how is he called son of David?\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.9.30-31":["And Jesus sternly charged them, saying: See that no one knows. But they went out and spread his fame through all that land. Both the Lord, out of humility, fleeing the glory of boasting, had given this command, and they, out of remembrance of the favor, cannot keep silent about the benefit. Note, therefore, that things that are just can be contrary to one another. These blind men are healed in the tenth place.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.9.32-33":["And when they had gone out, behold, they brought to him a mute man who had a demon, and when the demon had been cast out the mute man spoke. In the eleventh place a mute man receives his tongue for speaking. Now what in Greek is called κωφός is more worn in common speech in such a way that a deaf man rather than a mute is understood. But it is the custom of the Scriptures to call κωφόν either mute or deaf without distinction. Spiritually, however, just as the blind receive light, so also the tongue of the mute is loosed for speaking, so that he may confess him whom before he denied.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.9.33-34":["And the crowds marveled, saying: Never has the like appeared in Israel. But the Pharisees said: By the prince of demons he casts out demons. The crowd acknowledges the works of God and says: Never has the like appeared in Israel. In the crowd is the confession of the nations. But the Pharisees, because they could not deny the mighty work, slander the deeds and say: By the prince of demons he casts out demons, showing by their slander the unbelief of the Jews down to this day.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.9.35":["And Jesus went about all the cities and villages, teaching in their synagogues and preaching the Gospel of the kingdom and healing every sickness and every infirmity. You see that he preached the Gospel equally to country places and cities and villages, that is, both to great and to small, so that he considered not the power of the noble but the salvation of believers. He went about the cities: having this as his work, which the Father had commanded, and this as his hunger, to save the unbelieving by his teaching. And he taught the Gospel of the kingdom in synagogues and in country places, and after the preaching and the teaching he healed every sickness and every infirmity, so that those whom the word had not convinced the works might persuade. It is said properly of the Lord: healing every sickness and every infirmity; for nothing is impossible to him.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.9.36":["But seeing the crowds, he had compassion on them, who were harassed and lying like sheep that have no shepherd. The harassment of the flock, of the sheep and of the crowds, is the fault of the shepherds, the failing of the teachers. Whence there follows:\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.9.37-38":["The harvest indeed is great, but the laborers are few. The great harvest signifies the multitude of the peoples, the few laborers the scarcity of teachers. And he commands them to ask the Lord of the harvest to send out laborers into his harvest. These are the laborers of whom the psalmist speaks: Those who sow in tears shall reap in joy; going they went and wept, carrying their seeds, but coming they shall come with exultation, carrying their sheaves. And to speak more plainly: the great harvest is the whole crowd of believers; the few laborers are the apostles and their imitators, who are sent to the harvest.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.10.1":["And having called together his twelve disciples, he gave them power over unclean spirits, to cast them out and to heal every sickness and every infirmity. The kind and merciful Lord and Master does not begrudge his servants and disciples his own mighty works, and just as he himself had healed every sickness and every infirmity, so he grants to his apostles too the power to heal every sickness and every infirmity. But there is a great difference between having and granting, between giving and receiving. Whatever he does, he does by the power of the Lord; they, if they do anything, confess their own weakness and the power of the Lord, saying: In the name of Jesus, rise and walk. Note, moreover, that it is in the twelfth place that the power of signs is granted to the apostles.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.10.2-3":["First, Simon, who is called Peter, and Andrew his brother. To set out the order of the apostles and the merit of each belonged to him who searches the secrets of the heart. Simon, surnamed Peter, is written first, to distinguish him from the other Simon, who is called the Cananaean, from Cana, the village of Galilee where the Lord turned water into wine. He also calls James the son of Zebedee, because another James follows as well, the son of Alphaeus. And he joins the apostles together in pairs and yokes. He joins Peter and Andrew, brothers not so much in the flesh as in the spirit; James and John, who, leaving the father of their body, followed the true Father; Philip and Bartholomew; Thomas also and Matthew the tax collector. The other evangelists, in pairing the names, put Matthew first and Thomas after, and do not add the name of tax collector, lest by recalling his former way of life they should seem to disparage the evangelist. But this one, as we said above, both places himself after Thomas and calls himself a tax collector, so that where sin abounded there might superabound grace.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.10.2":["Now the names of the twelve apostles are these. A catalogue of the apostles is set down so that those who were going to be false apostles, outside these, might be shut out.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.10.3-4":["Simon the Cananaean. He is the one who in another evangelist is written as zelotes; for Cana is interpreted 'zeal'. The history of the Church relates that the apostle Thaddaeus was sent to Edessa, to Abgar king of Osrhoene; he is called Judas of James by the evangelist Luke, and elsewhere he is named Lebbaeus, which is interpreted 'little heart'; and we must believe that he had three names, just as Simon Peter and the sons of Zebedee, boanerges, were named from the firmness and greatness of their faith. But Judas Scariothes took his name either from the village in which he was born or from the tribe of Issachar, so that by a kind of prophecy he was born to his own condemnation. For Issachar is interpreted 'reward', so that the price of the traitor may be signified.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.10.5-6":["Do not walk into the way of the Gentiles, and do not enter into the cities of the Samaritans, but go rather to the sheep of the house of Israel that have perished. This passage is not contrary to that command which is given later: Going, teach all nations, baptizing them in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit, because this was commanded before the resurrection, that after the resurrection. And it was necessary that the first coming of Christ be announced to the Jews, lest they should have a just excuse, saying that they had rejected the Lord because he sent his apostles to the Gentiles and the Samaritans. But according to tropology we, who are reckoned by the name of Christ, are commanded not to walk in the way of the Gentiles and in the error of the heretics, so that those whose religion is separated may be separated in life as well.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.10.7-8":["And going, preach, saying that the kingdom of heaven has drawn near; heal the sick, raise the dead, cleanse the lepers, cast out demons; freely you have received, freely give. So that rustic men, without the charm of eloquence, unlearned and unlettered, should not go unbelieved when they promised the kingdoms of heaven, he gives them power: heal the sick, cleanse the lepers, cast out demons, so that the greatness of the signs might prove the greatness of the promises.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.10.8":["And because spiritual gifts always become cheaper if payment comes between, a condemnation of avarice is added: Freely you have received, freely give; I, your Master and Lord, have granted this to you without price, and you too give without price, lest the grace of the Gospel be corrupted.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.10.9-10":["These things we have said according to history. But according to anagogy, teachers are not permitted to possess the gold and silver and money that is in the belts. We often read gold for the understanding, silver for speech, bronze for the voice. These we may not receive from others, but must possess them as given by the Lord. Nor may we take up the disciplines of heretics and philosophers and of perverse doctrine, nor be weighed down by the weight of the world, nor be double-minded, nor have our feet bound with deadly bonds, but, entering the holy ground, we must go barefoot, and not have a staff that is turned into a serpent, nor lean on any protection of the flesh, because a staff and stick of this kind is of reed, which, if you press on it a little, breaks and pierces the hand of the one leaning on it.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.10.9":["Do not possess gold or silver or money in your belts, no bag for the road, not two tunics, no shoes, nor a staff in the hand; for the laborer is worthy of his food. Consistently he gave these commands to the preachers of the truth, to whom he had said before: Freely you have received, freely give. For if they preach in such a way that they accept no price, the possession of gold and silver and coins is superfluous. For if they had had gold and silver, they would have seemed to preach not for the sake of men's salvation but for the sake of gain. Nor bronze in purses. He who had cut off riches cuts away almost even the necessities of life, so that the apostles, teachers of the true religion, who taught that all things are governed by providence, might show that they themselves took no thought for the morrow.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.10.10":["No bag for the road. By this precept he refutes the philosophers who are commonly called bactroperites, because, though despisers of the world who count all things as nothing, they carried their storeroom about with them. Nor two tunics. By the two tunics he seems to me to indicate a double garment; not that in the regions of Scythia, stiff with glacial snow, anyone ought to be content with a single tunic, but that by the tunic we should understand clothing, lest, being clothed in one, we keep another for ourselves out of fear of what is to come. Nor of shoes. Plato too prescribes that the two extremities of the body should not be covered, and that one ought not to grow used to softness of the head and the feet; for when these have firmness, the rest are sturdier. Nor a staff. We who have the help of the Lord, why should we seek the protection of a stick? And because he had sent the apostles to preach as it were naked and unencumbered, and the condition of the teachers seemed to be hard, he tempered the severity of the precept with the sentence that follows, saying: The laborer is worthy of his food. Take only so much, he says, as is necessary for you in food and clothing. Hence the Apostle too repeats: Having food and clothing, with these we are content, and in another place: Let him who is catechized share with him who catechizes him in every good thing, so that disciples may make those whose spiritual things they reap sharers in their own carnal things, not for avarice but for necessity.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.10.11":["Into whatever city or village you enter, inquire who in it is worthy, and stay there until you leave. Concerning the ordination of a bishop and a deacon Paul says: Moreover they must also have a good testimony from those who are outside. The apostles, entering a new city, could not know who was of what sort. Therefore the host is to be chosen by the report of the people and the judgment of the neighbors, lest the dignity of the preaching be disfigured by the ill repute of the one who receives them. Though they ought to preach to all, one host is chosen, not conferring a benefit on him who is going to stay with him, but receiving one. For the words who in it is worthy are said so that he may know that he is receiving grace rather than giving it.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.10.12-13":["And entering into the house, greet it; and if the house is worthy, your peace will come upon it; but if it is not worthy, your peace will return to you. He has expressed, in a hidden way, the greeting of the Hebrew and Syriac language. For what in Greek is said χαῖρε and in Latin haue, this in the Hebrew and Syriac language is called salom lach or salama lach, that is, peace be with you. What he commanded, then, is this: entering a house, call down peace upon your host, and, as far as lies in you, calm the wars of discord; but if contradiction arises, you will have the reward of the peace you offered, and they will possess the war they wished to have.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.10.14":["And whoever does not receive you nor hear your words, going outside, out of the house or the city, shake off the dust from your feet. The dust is shaken from the feet as a testimony of their labor, that they entered the city and the apostolic preaching reached even to them; or else the dust is shaken off so that they may accept nothing, not even what is necessary for food, from those who have spurned the Gospel.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.10.15":["Amen I say to you: It will be more tolerable for the land of Sodom and Gomorrah in the day of judgment than for that city. If it will be more tolerable for the land of Sodom and Gomorrah than for that city which has not received the Gospel, and more tolerable for this reason, that it was not preached to Sodom and Gomorrah, but it has been preached to this city and yet it has not received the Gospel, then there are different punishments even among sinners.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.10.16":["Behold, I send you as sheep in the midst of wolves. He calls the scribes and Pharisees, who are the clergy of the Jews, wolves.\n— Commentary on Matthew","Be therefore prudent as serpents and simple as doves. So that through prudence they may avoid snares, and through simplicity may do no evil. The cunning of the serpent is set forth as an example because it hides its head with its whole body and protects that in which its life is. So we too, at the risk of our whole body, should guard our head, which is Christ. The simplicity of doves is shown from the appearance of the Holy Spirit. Hence the Apostle too says: In malice be little children.\n— Commentary on Matthew","The Lord commands in the Gospel that we be simple as doves and shrewd as serpents, so that, imitating the simplicity of doves and the shrewdness of the serpent, we may neither be able to harm others nor suffer snares from others; but by simplicity and prudence may show ourselves balanced men, because prudence without goodness is malice, and simplicity without reason is called folly.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"MAT.10.19-20":["But when they hand you over, do not think how or what you are to speak. He had said above: For they will hand you over in councils, and in their synagogues they will scourge you, and you will be led before governors and kings for my sake. Since, then, we are led before judges for Christ's sake, we ought to offer only our will for Christ. For the rest, Christ himself, who dwells in us, will speak for himself, and the grace of the Holy Spirit will be supplied in answering.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.10.21":["And brother will hand over brother to death, and father son, and sons will rise up against parents. This we see happen frequently in persecutions, and there is no faithful affection among those whose faith is different.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.10.22":["Whoever has persevered to the end, he will be saved. For it belongs to virtue not to have begun but to have finished.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.10.23":["But when they persecute you in this city, flee into another. Amen I say to you: You will not finish the cities of Israel until the Son of Man comes. This is to be referred to that time when the apostles were being sent out to preach, to whom it was also said in particular: Do not go off into the way of the Gentiles, and do not enter into the cities of the Samaritans, that they ought not to fear persecution, and yet ought to avoid it. This indeed we see that the believers did at the beginning, when, a persecution having arisen at Jerusalem, they were scattered through all Judea, so that the occasion of tribulation became the seedbed of the Gospel. But spiritually we can say: when they have persecuted us in one city, that is, in one book or testimony of the Scriptures, let us flee to other cities, that is, to other volumes. However contentious the persecutor may be, the protection of the Savior will come before victory is granted to the adversaries.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.10.25":["If they have called the householder Beelzebub, how much more those of his household. Beelzebub is the idol of Ekron, which in the book of Kings is called the idol of the fly. Beel is the same as Bel or Baal; zebub means fly. Therefore they called the prince of demons by the name of that most filthy idol, who is called fly because of the uncleanness that destroys the sweetness of the oil.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.10.26":["For nothing is covered that will not be revealed, and hidden that will not be known. And how is it that in the present age the vices of many are not known? But this is written of the time to come, when God will judge the hidden things of men and will illumine the hiding places of darkness and will make manifest the counsels of hearts; and the sense is: do not fear the savagery of the persecutors and the rage of the blasphemers, because the day of judgment will come in which both your virtue and their wickedness will be shown.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.10.27":["What I say to you in darkness, speak in the light, and what you hear in the ear, preach upon the housetops. What you have heard in mystery, preach more openly; what you have learned in secret, speak publicly; what I have taught you in a little place of Judea, say boldly in all the cities and in the whole world.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.10.28":["Do not fear those who kill the body but cannot kill the soul. If those who kill the body cannot kill the soul, then the soul is invisible and incorporeal; I mean, in comparison with the grosser substance of our body. Or at any rate it will be punished and will feel torments at that time when it has received its former body, so that it may be punished together with that with which it sinned.\n— Commentary on Matthew","But fear him who can destroy both soul and body in Gehenna. The name of Gehenna is not found in the old books, but is first used by the Savior. Let us ask, then, what the occasion of this word is. We read more than once that there was an idol of Baal near Jerusalem, at the foot of Mount Moriah, where Siloam flows. This valley and small level stretch of plain was well watered and wooded and full of delights, and in it was a grove consecrated to the idol. But the people of Israel had come to such madness that, forsaking the neighborhood of the temple, they offered victims there, and delights overcame the rigor of religion, and they burned their sons to the demon or initiated them; and the place itself was called gehennon, that is, the valley of the sons of Ennon. The book of Kings and Chronicles and Jeremiah write of this most fully. And God threatens that he will fill that very place with the corpses of the dead, so that it will no longer be called Topeth and Baal but will be called Polyandrum, that is, the burial mound of the dead. Therefore the future torments and everlasting punishments with which sinners are to be tormented are denoted by the name of this place. That there is a twofold Gehenna, of excessive fire and of cold, we read most fully in Job.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.10.29-31":["Are not two sparrows sold for an as? And one of them will not fall upon the ground without your Father. But even the hairs of your head are numbered. Do not fear, therefore; you are worth more than many sparrows. The Lord's discourse holds together, and what follows hangs upon what went before. Prudent reader, always beware of a superstitious understanding, so that you do not adjust the Scriptures to your own sense, but join your sense to the Scriptures and understand what follows. He had said above: Do not fear those who kill the body but cannot kill the soul, now he says, in consequence: Are not two sparrows sold for an as, and one of them will not fall upon the ground without your Father; and the sense is: if small and cheap animals do not die without God as their author, and there is providence in all things, and those among them that are to perish do not perish without the will of God, you who are eternal ought not to fear that you live without the providence of God. This sense was also stated above: Look at the birds of the sky, for they neither sow nor reap nor gather into their barns, and your heavenly Father feeds them; are you not worth more than they? and then: Consider the lilies of the field, how they grow, and the rest. But if God so clothes the grass of the field, which is today and tomorrow is thrown into the oven, how much more you, O you of very little faith? Some, in a forced way, interpret the two sparrows as the soul and the body; and the five sparrows according to Luke, which are sold for two asses, they refer to the senses. But how that understanding can be fitted to the whole body of the Gospel discourse is a matter of no small difficulty: But even the hairs of your head are all numbered; do not fear, therefore; you are better than many sparrows. More plainly is expressed the earlier sense of our exposition, that they ought not to fear those who can kill the body and cannot kill the soul, since, if even small animals do not die without God's knowledge, how much more a man who is supported by apostolic dignity.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.10.30":["And when he says: But even the hairs of your head are all numbered, he shows the immense providence of God toward men and signifies his ineffable affection, in that nothing of ours is hidden from God, and even small and idle words do not escape his knowledge. Those who deny the resurrection of the flesh mock the Church's understanding of this place, as though we said that even the hairs that have been numbered and cut off by the barber all rise again, when the Savior did not say: But even all your hairs are to be saved, but are numbered. Where there is number, knowledge of the number is shown, not the preservation of that same number.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.10.34":["Do not think that I have come to send peace upon the earth; I have not come to send peace but a sword. He had said above: What I say to you in darkness, speak in the light, and what you hear in the ear, preach upon the housetops. Now he adds what follows after the preaching. Over the faith of Christ the whole world was divided against itself; every house had both unbelievers and believers, and therefore a good war was sent so that an evil peace might be broken. Something of the kind God is written in Genesis to have done against the rebellious men who had moved from the east and were hastening to build a tower by which they might penetrate the heights of heaven: he divided their tongues. Hence also in the psalm David prays: Scatter, O Lord, the nations that want wars.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.10.35-36":["For I have come to separate a man against his father, and a daughter against her mother, and a daughter-in-law against her mother-in-law; and a man's enemies are those of his household. This passage is written in nearly the same words in the prophet Micah. And note, wherever a testimony is set down from the Old Testament, whether the sense alone agrees or the wording as well.\n— Commentary on Matthew","We ought to know, moreover, that in the Gospel there are nearly the same words that we now read in the prophet, and that according to the context of that place they have another sense; whether they were taken from the prophet or commanded on his own authority, it belongs to the Lord to know, who spoke both in the prophets and in the Gospels. Now there the Lord says: I came to divide a man against his father, and a daughter against her mother, and a daughter-in-law against her mother-in-law, and a man's enemies are his own household.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MAT.10.37":["He who loves father or mother more than me is not worthy of me, and the rest. Because he had said before: I have not come to send peace but a sword, and to divide men against father and mother and mother-in-law, lest anyone should put filial devotion before religion, he went on, saying: He who loves father or mother more than me; and in the Song of Songs we read: Set charity in order in me. This order is necessary in every affection. After God love your father, love your mother, love your children; but if a necessity comes such that the love of parents and children is weighed against the love of God, and both cannot be kept, hatred of one's own is devotion toward God. So he did not forbid loving father or mother, but pointedly added: He who loves father or mother more than me.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.10.38":["And he who does not take up his cross and follow me is not worthy of me. In another Gospel it is written: He who has not taken up his cross daily. Lest we think that ardor of faith shown once can suffice, the cross must always be carried, so that we may show that we always love Christ.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.10.40":["Whoever receives you receives me, and whoever receives me receives him who sent me. A most beautiful order. He sends them to preach, teaches that dangers are not to be feared, subordinates affection to religion; he had taken away gold above, he had shaken bronze out of the belt. Hard is the condition of the evangelists. Whence, then, their expenses, whence the necessities of food? He tempers the austerity of the commands with the hope of the promises: Whoever receives, he says, you receives me, and whoever receives me receives him who sent me, so that in receiving the apostles each of the believers might think that he had received Christ.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.10.41-42":["And whoever gives one of these least ones a cup of cold water to drink, only in the name of a disciple, amen I say to you, he will not lose his reward. We read in the prophet David: To make excuses in sins, because many put forward, as it were, just pretexts for their sins, so that what they commit by will they may seem to sin by necessity. The Lord, the searcher of heart and reins, beholds the future thoughts in each one. He had said: Whoever receives you receives me. But many false prophets and false preachers could hinder this command. He applied a remedy to this stumbling block too, saying: Whoever receives a just man in the name of a just man will receive a just man's reward. Again, another could plead an excuse and say: I am prevented by poverty; the slenderness of my means holds me back, so that I cannot be hospitable. This excuse too he washed away with a very light command, that we should offer a cup of cold water with our whole heart: cold, he says, water not hot, lest in the case of hot water too poverty and a shortage of firewood should be sought as a pretext. Something of the kind the Apostle too, as we have said already, commands the Galatians: Let him who is catechized share with him who catechizes him in all good things, and he exhorts disciples to the refreshment of their teachers; and since anyone could plead poverty and evade the command, before such a one could put it forward he resolves the impending question, saying: Do not be deceived; God is not mocked; for what a man sows, that he will also reap; and the sense is: in vain do you plead want when your conscience holds something else; you can deceive me as I exhort you, but know that however much you sow, so much you will also reap.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.10.41":["Whoever receives a prophet in the name of a prophet will receive a prophet's reward, and the rest. He who receives a prophet as a prophet and understands him as speaking of things to come, he will receive a prophet's reward. Therefore the Jews, understanding the prophets carnally, will not receive the reward of the prophets. Alternatively: in every profession darnel is mixed with the wheat. He had said before: Whoever receives you receives me, and whoever receives me receives him who sent me. He had urged the disciples to receive their teachers. The believers could have an unspoken answer: then we must also receive false prophets and Judas the traitor, and supply them with sustenance. Providing against this beforehand, the Lord says that it is not persons who are to be received but names, and that those who receive do not lose the reward, even though the one who was received was unworthy.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.11.2-3":["Now John, when he had heard in chains of the works of Christ, sending two of his disciples, said to him: Are you he who is to come, or do we look for another? He does not ask as one who does not know, for he himself pointed him out to the others who did not know, saying: Behold the Lamb of God, behold him who takes away the sins of the world, and he had heard the voice of the Father thundering: This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased, but just as the Savior asks where Lazarus has been laid, so that those who were showing the place of the tomb might at least in this way be prepared for faith and see the dead man rising, so John too, about to be killed by Herod, sends his disciples to Christ, so that through this occasion, seeing the signs and mighty works, they might believe in him and, while their master asked, learn for themselves. That the disciples of John were proud against the Lord and had something of a biting spirit from spite and envy, the earlier question also showed, as the evangelist relates: Then the disciples of John came to him, saying: Why do we and the Pharisees fast often, but your disciples do not fast? and elsewhere: Master, he to whom you bore witness at the Jordan, behold, his disciples baptize, and more come to him, as if they said: We are deserted, here there is scarcity, to him the crowd runs together.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.11.3":["Are you he who is to come, or do we look for another? He does not say: Are you he who has come, but Are you he who is to come; and the sense is: Send me word, since I am about to go down to the lower world, whether I ought to announce you to those below as well, I who have announced you to those above, or whether it is not fitting for the Son of God to taste death, and you will send another for these mysteries.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.11.4-5":["Jesus answered and said to them: Go and report to John what you have heard and seen: the blind see, the lame walk, lepers are cleansed, the deaf hear, the dead rise. John had asked through his disciples: Are you he who is to come, or do we look for another? Christ points to signs, answering not what he had been asked but the stumbling block of the messengers: Go, he says, and tell John the signs that you see, the blind seeing, the lame walking, and the rest, and what is no less than these: the poor have the Gospel preached to them; either the poor in spirit or at any rate the poor in wealth, so that in the preaching there is no difference between the noble and the lowborn, between the rich and the needy. These things prove the rigor of the master, these the truth of the teacher, since with him everyone who can be saved is equal. But as for what he says:\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.11.6":["And blessed is he who has not been scandalized in me, he strikes at the go-betweens, as will be shown in what follows.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.11.7-8":["When they had gone away, Jesus began to say to the crowds about John: What did you go out into the desert for? To see a reed shaken by the wind? But what did you go out for? To see a man clothed in soft garments? and the rest. If the previous sentence had been uttered against John, as most people think, in his saying: Blessed is he who has not been scandalized in me, how is John now proclaimed with such praises? But it is because the crowd standing around did not know the mystery of the question and thought that John was in doubt about Christ, whom he himself had pointed out: so that they might understand that John had asked not for himself but for his disciples. What, he says, did you go out into the desert for? Was it for this, that you might see a man like a reed, who is carried about by every wind and, from lightness of mind, would be in doubt about the one whom he had earlier proclaimed? Or perhaps he is driven by the goads of envy against me, and his preaching pursues vain glory, so that he may seek gain from it? Why should he desire riches so as to abound in feasts? He feeds on locusts and wild honey. Or was it to be clothed in soft garments? Camels' hair is his covering. Food and clothing of this kind are received into the lodging of a prison, and the preaching of truth has such a dwelling. But those who are flatterers and pursue gain and seek riches and abound in delights and are clothed in soft garments, they are in the houses of kings. From this it is shown that a rigorous life and austere preaching ought to avoid the courts of kings and shun the palaces of soft men.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.11.9-10":["But what did you go out for? To see a prophet? Yes, I tell you, and more than a prophet. John is greater than the other prophets in this, that the one whom they had proclaimed was to come, he pointed out with his finger as having come, saying: Behold the Lamb of God, behold him who takes away the sins of the world. And because to the prophetic privilege there was added also the Baptist's reward, that he should baptize his own Lord, he therefore brings in an αὔξησιν (increase) of his merits, producing the testimony from Malachi in which he is even proclaimed an angel. But let us not think that John is called an angel here by a sharing of nature, but by the dignity of his office, that is, a messenger, because he announced the Lord who was to come.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.11.11":["Amen I say to you: There has not arisen among those born of women one greater than John the Baptist. Among those born, he says, of women. He is therefore preferred to those men who were born of women and of union with a man, and not to him who was born of a virgin and the Holy Spirit. And yet in saying: There has not arisen among those born of women one greater than John the Baptist, he did not prefer John to the other prophets and patriarchs and to all men, but made the others equal to John. For it does not straightway follow that, if others are not greater than he, he is greater than the others, but rather that he has equality with the other saints.\n— Commentary on Matthew","But he who is less in the kingdom of heaven is greater than he. Many want this to be understood of the Savior: that he who is less in time is greater in dignity. But let us understand simply that every saint who is already with God is greater than he who still stands in the battle. For it is one thing to possess the crown of victory, another still to fight in the battle line. Some want to take the lowest angel ministering to the Lord in heaven as better than any foremost man who lives on earth.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.11.12":["From the days of John the Baptist until now the kingdom of heaven suffers violence. If John, as we said above, was the first to announce repentance to the peoples, saying: Repent, for the kingdom of heaven has drawn near, it follows that from his days the kingdom of heaven suffers violence, and the violent plunder it. For it is great violence that we, born on earth, should seek the seat of heaven and possess by virtue what we did not hold by nature.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.11.13":["For all the prophets and the law prophesied until John. Not that he excludes prophets after John (for we read in the Acts of the Apostles that both Agabus prophesied and the four virgin daughters of Philip), but that the law and the prophets whom we read in writing, whatever they prophesied, foretold of the Lord. When therefore it is said: All the prophets and the law prophesied until John, the time of Christ is shown, in that the one whom they said was to come, John showed had come.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.11.14-15":["If you are willing to receive it, he is Elijah who is to come. He who has ears to hear, let him hear. That this saying: If you are willing to receive it, he is Elijah, is mystical and needs understanding, the Lord's following word shows, saying: He who has ears to hear, let him hear. For if the sense were plain and the meaning manifest, what need was there for us to be prepared for understanding it? John, then, is called Elijah not according to the foolish philosophers and certain heretics who introduce a μετεμψύχωσιν (transmigration of souls), but because, according to another testimony of the Gospel, he came in the spirit and power of Elijah and had either the same grace or the same measure of the Holy Spirit. Moreover, the austerity of life and the rigor of mind of Elijah and John are equal. The one was in the desert, the other in the desert; the one was girded with a leather belt, and the other had a similar girdle; the one, because he rebuked King Ahab and Jezebel for impiety, was forced to flee, the other, because he rebuked the unlawful marriage of Herod and Herodias, is beheaded. There are those who think that John is called Elijah for this reason: that, as at the second coming of the Savior, according to Malachi, Elijah is to go before and announce the judge who is to come, so John did at the first coming, and each is a messenger, either of the first coming of the Lord or of the second.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.11.16-17":["But to what shall I liken this generation? It is like children sitting in the forum, who call out to their companions and say: We sang to you, and you did not dance; we lamented to you, and you did not mourn. For John came neither eating nor drinking, and they say: He has a demon. The Son of Man came eating and drinking, and they say: Look, a gluttonous man and a drinker of wine, a friend of tax collectors and sinners. And wisdom has been justified by her children. To children sitting in the forum and calling out and saying to their companions: We sang to you, and you did not dance; we lamented, and you did not mourn, the generation of the Jews is compared, as Scripture says: What shall I reckon this generation to be like? It is like children sitting in the forum, and the rest. We are not, therefore, granted a free understanding and an allegorical interpretation that ranges at will, but whatever we are going to say about the children must be referred to the likeness of the generation. These children who sit in the forum are those of whom Isaiah speaks: Behold, I and my children whom God has given me; and in Psalm 18 (19): The testimony of God is faithful, giving wisdom to little ones; and elsewhere: Out of the mouth of infants and sucklings you have perfected praise. These children, then, sat in the forum, or ἐν ἀγορᾷ, as it is said more expressively in Greek, where many things are for sale. And because the people of the Jews would not listen, they did not merely speak to it but cried out with full throats: We sang to you, and you did not dance: we urged you to do good works to our song and to dance to our pipe, as David too danced before the ark of the Lord, and you would not; we have lamented, and we urged you to repentance, and you would not do even this, spurning both kinds of preaching, both the exhortation to virtues and the repentance after sins.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.11.18-19":["Nor is it surprising if you have despised the twofold way of salvation, when you have spurned fasting and fullness alike. If fasting pleases you, why did John displease you? If fullness, why the Son of Man? Of these you called the one a man with a demon, the other a glutton and a drunkard. Therefore, because you would not receive either discipline, wisdom has been justified by her children, that is, the dispensation and the doctrine of God; and I, who am the power of God and the wisdom of God, have been proved by my apostles, my children, to have acted justly, to whom the Father has revealed what he had hidden from the wise and from those who are prudent in their own eyes. In certain Gospels it reads: Wisdom has been justified by her works. For wisdom does not seek the testimony of the voice but of works.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.11.20":["Then he began to reproach the cities in which very many of his mighty works had been done, because they had not repented. The reproach of the cities of Chorazin and Bethsaida and Capernaum is set out by the title of this chapter: namely, that he reproached them for this reason, because after very many mighty works and signs had been done they did not repent.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.11.21-22":["Woe to you, Chorazin; woe to you, Bethsaida; for if the mighty works that have been done in you had been done in Tyre and Sidon, they would long ago have repented in sackcloth and ashes. Nevertheless I say to you: it will be more tolerable for Tyre and Sidon on the day of judgment than for you. Chorazin and Bethsaida, cities of Galilee, are lamented by the Savior because after so many signs and mighty works they did not repent, and Tyre and Sidon, cities given over to idolatry and vices, are preferred to them. And they are preferred for this reason, that Tyre and Sidon had trampled only the natural law, whereas these, after transgressing the natural and the written law, also made light of the signs that had been done among them. We ask where it is written that the Lord did signs in Chorazin and Bethsaida. Above we read: And he went about all the cities and villages, healing every infirmity, and the rest. Among the other cities and little villages, therefore, we must suppose that the Lord did signs in Chorazin and Bethsaida too.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.11.23":["And you, Capernaum, will you be exalted even to heaven? You will go down even to hell. In another copy we have found: And you, Capernaum, who have been exalted even to heaven, you will go down even to the lower regions, and the understanding is twofold: either you will go down to the lower regions because you resisted my preaching most proudly, or because, having been exalted even to heaven by my lodging with you and by my signs and mighty works, possessing so great a privilege, you are struck with greater punishments, since you would not believe even these.\n— Commentary on Matthew","For if the mighty works that have been done in you had been done in Sodom, perhaps it would have remained until this day. The prudent reader may ask and say: If Tyre and Sidon and Sodom could have repented at the preaching of the Savior and the miracles of his signs, they are not at fault for not having believed; rather, the fault of silence lies in him who would not preach to those who would have repented. To this the answer is easy and plain: we do not know the judgments of God and are ignorant of the mysteries of each of his dispensations. The Lord had purposed not to go beyond the borders of Judea, so as not to give the Pharisees and the priests a just occasion for persecution. Hence he also commanded the apostles before the passion: Do not go off into the way of the Gentiles, and do not enter the cities of the Samaritans. Chorazin and Bethsaida, therefore, are condemned because they would not believe the Lord when he was present; Tyre and Sidon are justified because they believed his apostles. Do not ask about the times when you behold the salvation of believers. But in Capernaum, whose name is interpreted most beautiful village, unbelieving Jerusalem is condemned, to which it is said through Ezekiel: Sodom has been justified by you.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.11.25":["At that time Jesus answered and said: I confess to you, Father, Lord of heaven and earth. Confession does not always signify repentance but also thanksgiving, as we very often read in the psalms. Let those hear who slanderously claim that the Savior was not born but created, that he calls him his Father, but Lord of heaven and earth. For if he too is a creature, and a creature can call its maker Father, it was foolish not to call him likewise Lord or Father both of himself and of heaven and earth.\n— Commentary on Matthew","Because you have hidden these things from the wise and prudent and have revealed them to little ones. He gives thanks and exults in the Father because he has opened to the apostles the mysteries of his coming, of which the scribes and Pharisees were ignorant, who seem wise to themselves and prudent in their own sight. Wisdom has been justified by her children.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.11.26":["Yes, Father, for so it was pleasing before you. He speaks to the Father with the feeling of one who coaxes, that the kindness begun toward the apostles may be completed.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.11.27":["All things have been handed over to me by my Father. Understand mystically both the Father who hands over and the Son who receives. Otherwise, if we choose to think according to our frailty: when he who received begins to have, he who gave will begin not to have. And by the all things handed over to him are to be understood not heaven and earth and the elements and the other things that he himself made and founded, but those who through the Son have access to the Father and, rebels before, afterwards began to perceive God.\n— Commentary on Matthew","And no one knows the Son except the Father, nor does anyone know the Father except the Son and he to whom the Son wishes to reveal him. Let Eunomius blush, who boasts that he has as much knowledge of the Father and the Son as they have of each other. But suppose he argues from this and consoles his madness because there follows: and he to whom the Son wishes to reveal him. It is one thing to know what one knows by equality of nature, another by the condescension of one who reveals.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.11.28":["Come to me, all you who labor and are burdened, and I will refresh you. That the burdens of sin are heavy, both the prophet Zechariah testifies, saying that iniquity sits upon a talent of lead, and the psalmist laments: My iniquities have grown heavy upon me. Or else he invites to the grace of the Gospel those who were weighed down by the most heavy yoke of the law.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.11.30":["For my yoke is sweet and my burden light. How is the Gospel lighter than the law, when in the law murder, in the Gospel anger is condemned? On what reckoning is the grace of the Gospel easier, when in the law adultery, in the Gospel desire is punished? In the law there are many precepts which the Apostle teaches most fully cannot be fulfilled. In the law works are required, and whoever has done them shall live in them; in the Gospel the will is sought, which, even if it has not had its effect, still does not lose its reward. The Gospel commands what we are able to do, namely that we should not desire: this is within our choice; the law, while it does not punish the will, punishes the effect, that you should not commit adultery. Imagine some virgin prostituted in a persecution. Under the Gospel, because she does not sin in will, she is received as a virgin; in the law she is rejected as defiled.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.12.1":["At that time Jesus went through the sown fields on the Sabbath, and his disciples, being hungry, began to pluck ears of grain and to eat. As we read also in another evangelist, because of the excessive importunity they did not even have room to eat, and so, being men, they were hungry. But that they rub the ears of the crops in their hands and so relieve their want of food is a sign of a more austere life, of men who seek not prepared banquets but simple foods.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.12.2":["But the Pharisees, seeing it, said to him: Behold, your disciples are doing what it is not lawful for them to do on the Sabbaths. Note that the first apostles of the Savior destroy the letter of the Sabbath, against the Ebionites, who, though they receive the other apostles, reject Paul as a transgressor of the law.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.12.3-4":["But he said to them: Have you not read what David did when he was hungry, and those who were with him, how he entered the house of God and ate the loaves of the presentation, which it was not lawful for him to eat, nor for those who were with him, but for the priests alone? To refute the false charge of the Pharisees he recalls the old history, when David, fleeing Saul, came to Nob and, received by Ahimelech the priest, asked for food; and he, since he had no common loaves, gave him consecrated ones, which it was not lawful to eat except for the priests and Levites alone, and he asked only this, whether the young men were clean from women; and when David answered, since yesterday and the day before, he did not hesitate to give the loaves, judging it better (as the prophet says: I desire mercy and not sacrifice) to free men from the danger of hunger than to offer sacrifice to God. For the salvation of men is a victim pleasing to God. So the Lord sets this against them and says: If David too is holy, and Ahimelech the high priest is not blamed by you, but both transgressed the command of the law with an acceptable excuse, and hunger is the cause, why do you not approve in the apostles the same hunger that you approve in the others? Although even in this there is a great difference: these rub ears of grain in their hands on the Sabbath; those ate the levitical loaves, and the days of the new moons were added to the solemnity of the Sabbath, on which, being sought at the banquet, he fled from the royal court. Note that neither David nor his young men received the loaves of the presentation before they had answered that they were clean from women.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.12.5":["Or have you not read in the law that on the Sabbaths the priests in the temple violate the Sabbath and are without guilt? You slander my disciples, he says, because they rubbed ears of grain as they passed through the crops, and did this under the compulsion of hunger, when you yourselves violate the Sabbath in the temple, slaughtering victims, killing bulls, burning up holocausts with fire upon a pile of wood; and, on the testimony of another Gospel, circumcising little children on the Sabbath, so that, while you desire to keep another law, you destroy the Sabbath. But the laws of God are never contrary to one another. And prudently, where his disciples could be charged with transgression, he says that they followed the examples of David and Ahimelech; but the real violation of the Sabbath, one without the pretext of necessity, he turns back upon the very men who had made the false charge.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.12.6":["But I say to you that one greater than the temple is here. The word is to be read not as a pronoun but as an adverb of place, meaning that the place which holds the Lord of the temple is greater than the temple.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.12.7":["But if you knew what this is: I desire mercy and not sacrifice, you would never have condemned the innocent. What I desire mercy and not sacrifice, means, we have said above; but what follows, you would never have condemned the innocent, is to be understood of the apostles, and the sense is: If you approve the mercy of Ahimelech, because he refreshed David, who was in peril from hunger, and his young men, why do you condemn my disciples, who have done nothing of the kind?\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.12.9-10":["And when he had passed on from there, he came into their synagogue. And behold, a man having a withered hand. This man, who is healed in the synagogue, is the thirteenth. And it is to be noted that the hand was withered not on the road and out of doors, but in the assembly of the Jews.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.12.10":["And they questioned him, saying: Is it lawful to heal on the Sabbaths? so that they might accuse him. Since he had excused by an acceptable example the destruction of the Sabbath which the Pharisees charged against the disciples, they wish to slander him himself, and they ask whether it is lawful to heal on the Sabbaths, so that, if he did not heal, they might accuse him of cruelty or of weakness, and if he healed, of transgression.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.12.11-12":["But he said to them: What man of you will there be who has one sheep, and if it falls into a pit on the Sabbaths, will he not take hold of it and lift it out? How much better, then, is a man than a sheep! So it is lawful to do good on the Sabbaths. He so solved the question put to him as to condemn the questioners of avarice. If you, he says, hasten on the Sabbath to pull out a sheep or any other animal that falls into a pit, looking out not for the animal but for your own avarice, how much more ought I to set free a man, who is much better than a sheep!\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.12.13":["Then he said to the man: Stretch out your hand. And he stretched it out, and it was restored to health like the other. In the Gospel which the Nazarenes and the Ebionites use, which we recently translated from the Hebrew language into Greek, and which is called by most the authentic Matthew, this man who has the withered hand is described as a mason, begging for help in words of this kind: I was a mason, seeking my living with my hands; I beg you, Jesus, to restore my health to me, so that I may not shamefully beg for food. Until the coming of the Savior the withered hand was in the synagogue of the Jews, and the works of God were not done in it; after he came upon earth, the right hand was given back in the apostles who believed and was restored to its former work.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.12.14":["But the Pharisees went out and took counsel against him, how they might destroy him. That they lay plots against the Lord, envy is the cause. For what had he done to provoke the Pharisees to kill him? Why, that a man had stretched out his hand. For which of the Pharisees does not stretch out his hand on the Sabbath day, carrying food, holding out a cup, and the other things that are necessary for living? If, then, to stretch out the hand and to lift up food or drink on the Sabbath is no crime, why do they charge in another what they themselves are convicted of doing, especially since this mason carried nothing of the kind, but at the Lord's command only stretched out his hand?\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.12.15":["But Jesus, knowing it, withdrew from there. Knowing their plots, that they wished to destroy their Savior, he withdrew from there, so as to take away from the Pharisees an occasion for impiety against himself.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.12.18-21":["Behold my servant, I will uphold him; my chosen one, in him my soul is well pleased. I have put my spirit upon him; he will bring forth judgment to the nations. He will not cry out, nor will he accept a person, nor will his voice be heard outside. A bruised reed he will not break, and smoking flax he will not quench; in truth he will bring forth judgment. He will not be sad nor troubled, until he sets judgment in the earth; and the islands will wait for his law. LXX: Jacob my child, I will take him up; Israel my chosen one, my soul has received him. I have put my spirit upon him; he will bring forth judgment to the nations; he will not cry out, nor will he let go, nor will his voice be heard outside. A broken reed he will not break, and smoking flax he will not quench; but in truth he will bring forth judgment. He will shine and will not be broken, until he sets judgment upon the earth; and in his name the nations will hope. Jacob and Israel are not found in the present passage, which the Evangelist Matthew too did not set down, following the Hebrew truth. We say this to the shame of those who despise our work. This has been discussed more fully in the little Commentaries on Matthew, and in the book which we recently wrote to Algasia. Meanwhile it must be noted that where it is written of the choir of the Apostles, they are called both Jacob and Israel and the seed of Abraham, to whom, as to men and servants, it is said accordingly: Do not fear, worm Jacob, and Israel small in number. But where the prophecy is of Christ, it is read without Jacob and Israel: Behold my servant, I will uphold him; my chosen one, in him my soul is well pleased. And in what follows: I have given you as a covenant of the race, as a light of the nations, to open the eyes of the blind, and to bring the bound out of their chains, and those who dwell in darkness out of the prison house. Nor is it to be wondered at if he is called a servant, made of a woman, made under the law (Gal 4); he who, though he was in the form of God, humbled himself, taking the form of a servant, and was found in fashion as a man (Phil 2). In him the soul of God was well pleased, seeing that all virtues were in him; nay rather, that he himself was the power and wisdom of God. I have put, he says, my spirit upon him: the Spirit who descended in the form of a dove. He will bring forth judgment to the nations, which before they did not know. He will not cry out: for he will be gentle and meek; and he will not accept a person in judgment, or he will not lift up (it is understood) his voice on high. Or, according to Symmachus, he will not be deceived, understanding all the snares of the devil; or, according to the Seventy, he will not let go, namely the people of the Jews, calling them to repentance. Nor will his voice be heard outside: for he did not preach the Gospel outside Galilee and Judea among the other nations. And if we read that he was in the borders of Tyre and Sidon (Matt 15), or in the region of Caesarea Philippi, which is now called Paneas, still it must be known that it is not written that he entered those cities themselves. A bruised reed, or a broken one, he will not break. For he will be easy to appease toward all, and will give pardon to sinners, saying to the woman: Take heart, daughter, your sins are forgiven you (Luke 7:48). And smoking flax, or, as the others translated, dim and dark, he will not quench: those who were near to being quenched were preserved by the clemency of the Lord (which we have discussed in the works mentioned above concerning the Jews and the nations); but he will judge all things with truth, in no way fearing the scribes and Pharisees, whom he boldly called hypocrites (Matt 7, 15, 22, 23 throughout, and Luke 6, 12). But what follows, He will shine and will not be broken, until he sets judgment upon the earth, the Evangelist Matthew did not set down (Matt 12); or else the words in the middle, between judgment and judgment, were removed by a scribe's error. And it signifies that, rising from the dead, he enlightened all, and was not broken by death, until he set judgment upon the earth, he who said in the Gospel: For judgment I came into this world, that those who do not see may see, and those who see may become blind (John 9:39). For this we translated: He will not be sad, nor troubled, but he will keep an evenness of countenance at all times: what the philosophers falsely boast of Socrates, that he was never sadder or happier than usual. For this Aquila and Theodotion translated: He will not darken, and he will not run, until he sets judgment in the earth. And the sense is: he will deter no one by sadness of countenance, nor will he hasten to punishment, he who has reserved the truth of judgment for the last time. And what follows, and the islands will wait for his law, the Seventy set down more plainly: in his name the nations will hope; these are called islands in the Scriptures because they lie open on every side to the inroads of persecutors. And by his law let us understand not that which was given through Moses, but the Gospel: For out of Zion shall go forth the law, and the word of the Lord from Jerusalem (above, Isa 2). Of this law Jeremiah too prophesies: Behold, the days are coming, says the Lord, and I will make a new covenant, not according to the covenant which I once made with their fathers (Jer 31:31-32).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"MAT.12.18":["Behold my servant whom I have chosen, my beloved, and the rest. This is said through the prophet Isaiah in the person of the Father: I will put my Spirit upon him. The Spirit is put not upon the Word of God and upon the Only-begotten who came forth from the bosom of the Father, but upon him of whom it was said: Behold my servant.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.12.19":["Nor will anyone hear his voice in the streets. Wide indeed and spacious is the way that leads to perdition, and many enter by it. Who are the many? Those who do not hear the voice of the Savior, because they are not on the narrow way but on the spacious one.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.12.20":["A battered reed he will not break, and smoldering flax he will not put out. He who does not hold out a hand to a sinner and does not carry the burden of his brother, he breaks the battered reed. And he who despises the small spark of faith in little ones, he puts out the smoldering flax. Christ did neither of these things; for he had come for this, to save what had perished.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.12.22":["Then there was brought to him one who had a demon, blind and mute, and he healed him, so that he spoke and saw. Three signs were performed at once in one man: the blind man sees, the mute man speaks, the man possessed by a demon is set free. This was then indeed done in the flesh, but it is also fulfilled daily in the lives of believers, so that, once the demon has been driven out, they first behold the light of faith, and then mouths that were formerly silent are loosed in the praises of God.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.12.23-25":["But Jesus, knowing their thoughts, said to them: Every kingdom divided within itself will be laid waste. The crowds were amazed and confessed that he who did such great signs was the son of David; but the Pharisees ascribed the works of God to the prince of the demons; and the Lord answered them not according to what they said but according to what they thought, so that even thus they might be compelled to believe in the power of him who saw the hidden things of the heart.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.12.25-26":["If Satan casts out Satan, he is divided against himself; how then will his kingdom stand? A kingdom and a city divided against itself cannot endure; but as small things grow by concord, so the greatest fall apart by discord. If, then, Satan fights against himself, and demon is the enemy of demon, the consummation of the world ought already to have come, and the adverse powers would have no place in it, whose war among themselves is the peace of men. But if you think, O scribes and Pharisees, that the withdrawal of the demons is obedience to their prince, so that by a fraudulent pretense they may delude ignorant men, what can you say of the healings of bodies which the Lord performed? It is another matter if you ascribe to the demons the infirmities of the limbs too, and the signal marks of spiritual mighty works.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.12.27":["If I cast out demons by Beelzebub, by whom do your sons cast them out? Therefore they shall be your judges. By the sons of the Jews he means either, after the custom, the exorcists of that nation, or the apostles, born of their stock. If the exorcists, who used to cast out demons at the invocation of God, he constrains them with a prudent question, so that they confess it to be the work of the Holy Spirit. If, he says, the expulsion of demons by your sons is ascribed to God and not to demons, why should the same work in me not also have the same cause? Therefore they shall be your judges, not by power but by comparison, since they ascribe the expulsion of demons to God, and you to Beelzebub, the prince of the demons. But if it was said of the apostles, which is rather what we ought to understand, they will be their judges because they will sit on twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Israel.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.12.28":["But if I cast out demons by the Spirit of God, then the kingdom of God has come upon you. In Luke we read this passage written thus: But if I cast out demons by the finger of God. This is the finger which even the magicians, who performed signs against Moses and Aaron, confess, saying: This is the finger of God, the finger by which the tablets of stone were written on Mount Sinai. If, then, the hand and the arm of God is the Son, and his finger the Holy Spirit, the substance of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit is one; let not the inequality of the members cause you to stumble, since the unity of the body edifies. The kingdom of God has come upon you: he means either himself, of whom it is written in another place: The kingdom of God is within you, and: In your midst stands one whom you do not know, or else that kingdom which both John and the Lord himself had preached: Repent, for the kingdom of heaven has drawn near. There is also a third kingdom, that of holy Scripture, which is taken away from the Jews and given to a nation that produces its fruits.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.12.29":["Or how can anyone enter the house of the strong man and plunder his vessels, unless he first binds the strong man, and then he will plunder his house? We must not be complacent: our adversary is shown to be strong even by the words of the victor; his house is the world, which is set in the evil one, not by the dignity of the Creator but by the greatness of the offender; his vessels we once were. The strong man has been bound and fastened down in Tartarus and crushed by the foot of the Lord, and, the seats of the tyrant having been plundered, captivity has been led captive.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.12.30":["He who is not with me is against me, and he who does not gather with me scatters. Let no one think that this was said of heretics and schismatics, although it can also be understood so, as something over and above; but from what follows and from the texture of the discourse it is referred to the devil, in that the works of the Savior cannot be compared with the works of Beelzebub: he desires to hold the souls of men captive, the Lord to set them free; he preaches idols, this one the knowledge of the one God; he draws men to vices, this one calls them back to virtues. How then can those whose works are opposite have concord between them?\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.12.31-32":["Whoever speaks a word against the Son of Man, it will be forgiven him; but whoever speaks against the Holy Spirit, it will not be forgiven him, neither in this age nor in the one to come. And how do some of our people receive bishops and presbyters back into their rank after blasphemy of the Holy Spirit, when the Savior says that every sin and blasphemy is forgiven to men, but that those who have blasphemed against the Holy Spirit are not forgiven, neither in the present time nor in the future; unless perhaps we take that example from Mark the evangelist, who expressed more plainly the causes of such great anger, saying: Because they were saying: He has an unclean spirit. Therefore whoever ascribes the works of the Savior to Beelzebub, the prince of the demons, and says that the Son of God has an unclean spirit, to him the blasphemy will at no time be forgiven. Or else this passage is to be understood thus: He who speaks a word against the Son of Man, scandalized by my flesh and thinking me only a man, because I am the son of a carpenter and have brothers James and Joseph and Jude, and because I am a man who is a glutton and a drinker of wine, such an opinion and blasphemy, although it is not without fault, still has the pardon due to error because of the lowliness of the body; but he who, plainly understanding the works of God, since he cannot deny the mighty work, is goaded by the same envy and slanders them, and says that Christ, the Word of God, and the works of the Holy Spirit are Beelzebub, to him it will not be forgiven, neither in the present age nor in the one to come.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.12.33":["Either make the tree good and its fruit good, or make the tree bad and its fruit bad. For the tree is known by its fruits. He binds them with a syllogism which the Greeks call ἄφυκτον, and which we can call inescapable, which, when men are questioned, shuts them in on this side and on that and presses them with either horn. If, he says, the devil is evil, he cannot do good works; but if the things you see done are good, it follows that it is not the devil who does them. For it cannot be that good should arise from evil, or evil from good. But what follows,\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.12.34":["Brood of vipers, how can you speak good things, since you are evil, shows that they are a bad tree and bear such fruits, overflowing with blasphemy, as are the seeds of the devil which they have.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.12.35":["The good man out of the good treasure brings forth good things, and the evil man out of the evil treasure brings forth evil things. Either he shows from what kind of treasure the Jews themselves, blaspheming the Lord, bring forth their blasphemies, or else the saying clings to the earlier question: that as a good man cannot bring forth evil things, nor an evil man good things, so Christ cannot do evil works, nor the devil good ones.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.12.36":["But I say to you that every idle word that men speak, they will render account for it on the day of judgment. This too clings to what precedes, and the sense is: If an idle word, which in no way builds up the hearers, is not without danger to the one who speaks it, and on the day of judgment each one will have to render account of his speech, how much more will you, who slander the works of the Holy Spirit and say that I cast out demons by Beelzebub, the prince of the demons, have to render account of your slander! An idle word is one that is spoken without profit both to the speaker and to the hearer: if, leaving serious matters aside, we speak of frivolous things and tell old tales. But he who rehearses buffooneries, and loosens his mouth in guffaws, and utters anything shameful, he will be held guilty not of an idle word but of a criminal one.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.12.38":["Then some of the scribes and Pharisees answered him, saying: We want to see a sign from you. They ask for a sign as though the signs they had seen were not signs. But in another evangelist what they ask for is set out more fully: We want to see a sign from you from heaven, they desired either that fire should come down from on high in the manner of Elijah, or that, after the likeness of Samuel, in the summertime thunder should roar, lightning flash and rains pour down; as if they could not slander those things too, and say that they had happened from hidden and various affections of the air. For you who slander the things you see with your eyes, hold in your hand and feel in their benefit, what will you do with things that come from heaven? Surely you will answer that the magicians in Egypt too did many signs from heaven.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.12.39":["He said to them: A perverse and adulterous generation. He did well to say adulterous, because she had left her husband and, according to Ezekiel, had joined herself to many lovers.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.12.40":["Seeks a sign, and a sign will not be given it except the sign of the prophet Jonah. For as Jonah was in the belly of the whale three days and three nights, so will the Son of Man be in the heart of the earth three days and nights. We have discussed this passage more fully in the commentary on the prophet Jonah; to that place, therefore, we refer the diligence of the reader, content to have said this briefly now: that συνεκδοχικῶς (by synecdoche) the whole is understood from the part; not that the Lord spent all three days and three nights in hell, but that in part of the Preparation and of the Lord's day, and the whole day of the Sabbath, three days and as many nights are understood.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.12.41":["The men of Nineveh will rise in the judgment with this generation and will condemn it, not by the power of a sentence but by the example of comparison.\n— Commentary on Matthew","And behold, here is more than Jonah. Here you must take as an adverb of place, not a pronoun. Jonah, according to the LXX translators, preached for three days, I for so long a time; he to the Assyrians, an unbelieving nation, I to the Jews, the people of God; he to foreigners, I to fellow citizens; he spoke with a simple voice, doing no signs, I, doing such great signs, endure the slander of Beelzebub. So more than Jonah is here, that is, at present among you.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.12.42":["The queen of the south will rise in the judgment with this generation and will condemn it, because she came from the ends of the earth to hear the wisdom of Solomon. The queen of the south will condemn the people of the Jews in the same way as the men of Nineveh will condemn unbelieving Israel. Now this is the queen of Sheba, of whom we read in the book of Kings and in Chronicles, who through such great difficulties, leaving her nation and her empire behind, came into Judea to hear the wisdom of Solomon, and brought him many gifts. And in Nineveh and in the queen of Sheba the hidden faith of the nations is preferred to Israel.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.12.43-45":["But when the unclean spirit has gone out of a man, it walks through dry places seeking rest and does not find it, and the rest. Some think this passage was said of heretics: that the unclean spirit which had formerly dwelt in them when they were Gentiles is cast out at the confession of the true faith, but that afterwards, when they have gone over to heresy and adorned their house with feigned virtues, then the devil, having joined to himself seven other wicked spirits, returns to them and dwells in them, and their last state becomes worse than the first; for heretics are in a much worse condition than Gentiles, because in the latter there is hope of faith, in the former the fight of discord. Although this interpretation displays a certain appeal and color of learning, I do not know whether it has the truth. For from the fact that, once the parable or example is finished, there follows: So shall it be also with this most wicked generation, we are compelled to refer the parable not to heretics and to any men whatever but to the people of the Jews, so that the context of the passage may not drift loosely and vaguely in different directions and be thrown into confusion in the manner of the foolish, but, cohering with itself, may answer either to what precedes or to what follows. The unclean spirit went out from the Jews when they received the law, and it walked through dry places seeking rest for itself. Driven out, that is, from the Jews, it walked through the solitudes of the Gentiles; and when these afterwards believed in the Lord, it, finding no place among the nations, said:\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.12.44-45":["And coming, it finds the house vacant, swept with brooms. For the temple of the Jews was vacant and did not have Christ as its guest, who says: Rise, let us go from here; and in another place: Your house will be left to you deserted. Since, therefore, they had neither the protection of God nor that of the angels, and were adorned with the superfluous observances of the law and the traditions of the Pharisees, the devil returns to his former seat and, adding to himself the sevenfold number of demons, inhabits his former house, and the later things of that people become worse than the first. For now they are possessed by a much greater number of demons, blaspheming Christ Jesus in their synagogues, than they had been possessed in Egypt before the knowledge of the law; for it is one thing not to believe in one who is to come, and another not to have received him who has come. But understand the sevenfold number joined to the devil either on account of the Sabbath or on account of the number of the Holy Spirit, so that, as in Isaiah the seven spirits of the virtues are told to have descended upon the rod from the root of Jesse and upon the flower that rises up from the root, so, on the contrary, the number of the vices is consecrated in the devil.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.12.44":["I will return to my former house from which I went out, I go off to the Jews whom I had formerly left.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.12.46-47":["While he was still speaking to the crowds, behold, his mother and his brothers were standing outside, seeking to speak to him. And someone said to him: Behold, your mother and your brothers are standing outside seeking you. The Lord was occupied in the work of the word, in the teaching of the peoples, in the duty of preaching; his mother and brothers come and stand outside and desire to speak to him. Then someone announces to the Savior that his mother and brothers are standing outside seeking him. It seems to me that this man who brings the news does not announce it by chance and in simplicity, but is laying a trap, to see whether he would prefer flesh and blood to the spiritual work. Hence the Lord too scorned to go out, not as though he were denying his mother and brothers, but so as to answer the one laying the trap:\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.12.46-50":["Stretching out his hand toward his disciples he said: Behold my mother and my brothers. These are my mother, who daily bring me to birth in the souls of believers; these are my brothers, who do the works of my Father. So he did not, as Marcion and Mani hold, deny his mother, so as to be thought born of a phantasm, but he preferred the apostles to kinship, so that we too, in weighing our love, may prefer the spirit to the flesh. Behold, your mother and your brothers are standing outside seeking you. Some suspect that the brothers of the Lord are sons of Joseph by another wife, following the ravings of the apocrypha and inventing a certain little woman Escha. But we, as is contained in the book which we wrote Against Helvidius, understand the brothers of the Lord to be not sons of Joseph but cousins of the Savior, the children of Mary, the Lord's maternal aunt, who is said to be the mother of James the less, and of Joses and Jude, whom in another place of the Gospel we read called the brothers of the Lord. And all Scripture shows that cousins are called brothers. Let us also speak in another way. The Savior speaks to the crowds; inside he instructs the nations. His mother and brothers, that is, the synagogue and the people of the Jews, stand outside and desire to enter, and become unworthy of his discourse; and when they have asked and sought and sent a messenger, they receive the answer that they are of free will and can enter, if they too were willing to believe; yet they will not be able to enter unless they have asked others.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.12.50":["It is no concern of ours that Christ be formed in the people, that each one, returning to his own home, may have the seed of the word of God, so that when he has conceived it he may say with the prophet: \"From fear of you, O Lord, we have conceived and brought forth; we have made children of your salvation upon the earth\" (Isa. 26:17, 18). Such people pass over into the apostles, and deserve to hear from the Savior: \"Whoever shall do the will of my Father, he is my brother, and sister, and mother\" (Matt. 12:50): the diversity of their progress being shown in the different names.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"MAT.13.1":["On that day Jesus, going out of the house, sat beside the sea, and great crowds were gathered to him. The people could not enter the house of Jesus, nor be where the apostles were hearing the mysteries; therefore the compassionate and merciful Lord goes out of his house and sat beside the sea of this world, so that the crowds might be gathered to him and hear on the shore what they did not deserve to hear within.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.13.2":["So that, going up into a little boat, he sat, and the whole crowd stood on the shore. Jesus is in the midst of the waves; he is beaten by the sea on this side and on that, and, secure in his majesty, he makes his little boat draw near to the land; but the people, in no way enduring danger nor surrounded by temptations which they could not bear, stand on the shore with a fixed step to hear what is said.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.13.3":["And he spoke many things to them in parables, saying. The crowd is not of one mind but of wills differing in each person. Hence he speaks to it in many parables, so that according to their various wills they might receive different teachings. And it should be noted that he did not speak all things to them in parables, but many. For if he had said everything in parables, the people would have gone away without profit. He mixes the plain with the obscure, so that through the things they understand they may be roused to a knowledge of the things they do not understand.\n— Commentary on Matthew","Behold, he who sows went out to sow. He was within, he was staying at home, he was speaking mysteries to the disciples. So he who sows the word of God went out of his house to sow among the crowds. Now it is signified that this sower who sows is the Son of God and of the Father, sowing the word among the peoples. And at the same time note that this is the first parable that has been set down with its own interpretation; and we must beware, wherever the Lord expounds his own sayings and, when asked by the disciples, discourses within, lest we wish to understand anything else, or anything more or less, than has been expounded by him.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.13.4-8":["Some fell beside the way, and the birds came and ate them; and others fell on rocky places, where they did not have much earth, and the rest. Valentinus takes up this parable to prove his heresy, bringing in the notion that there are three natures, the spiritual, the animal and the earthly, whereas here there are four: one beside the way, another rocky, the third full of thorns, the fourth good ground. We put off its interpretation for a little, wishing with the disciples to hear in secret what is said:\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.13.9":["He who has ears to hear, let him hear. We are roused to the understanding of what is said whenever we are admonished by these words.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.13.10":["And the disciples came and said to him. It must be asked how the disciples come to him when Jesus is sitting in the ship, unless perhaps we are given to understand that some time before they had gone up into the ship with him and, standing there, had inquired about the interpretation of the parable.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.13.12":["He who has, it will be given to him, and he will abound; but he who does not have, even what he has will be taken from him. It is not by an equality of judgment that something is added to those who have, and that from those who do not have even what they seem to have is taken away; rather, to the apostles, who have faith in Christ, something is granted, even if they have somewhat fewer virtues, while from the Jews, who did not believe in the Son of God, it is taken away, even if they possess something through the good of nature. For those who do not have the head of wisdom cannot understand anything wisely.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.13.13-14":["Therefore I speak to them in parables, because seeing they do not see and hearing they do not hear. He says these things of those who stand on the shore and are separated from Jesus, and, with the sound of the waves roaring, do not hear clearly what is said, and in them is fulfilled the prophecy of Isaiah: By hearing you shall hear and shall not understand, and seeing you shall see and shall not see. These things were prophesied of the crowds that stand on the shore and do not deserve to hear the word of God. Let us therefore also come to Jesus with the disciples and ask him for the explanation of the parable, lest with the crowds we seem to have ears and eyes in vain.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.13.15":["For the heart of this people has grown fat, and with their ears they have heard heavily, and they have closed their eyes, and the rest. He gives the reasons why seeing they do not see and hearing they do not hear: because it has grown fat, he says, the heart of this people, and with their ears they have heard heavily; and lest perhaps we should think that fatness of heart and heaviness of ears belong to nature, not to the will, he adds the fault of free choice and says: They have closed their eyes, lest at any time they should see with their eyes and hear with their ears and understand with their heart and be converted, and I should heal them. In parables, therefore, and in an enigma do they hear who, with their eyes closed, do not wish to see the truth.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.13.16":["But blessed are your eyes, because they see, and your ears, because they hear. Had we not read above that the hearers were roused to understanding when the Savior said, He who has ears to hear, let him hear, we would think that the eyes and ears which now receive the beatitude are to be understood as those of the flesh. But to me those eyes seem blessed which can know the mysteries of Christ, and which Jesus commanded to be lifted up on high so that they might behold the white harvest fields; and those ears blessed of which Isaiah speaks: The Lord has given me an ear.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.13.17":["Amen indeed I say to you that many prophets and just men desired to see what you see, and did not see it, and to hear what you hear, and did not hear it. There seems to be, contrary to this passage, what is said elsewhere: Abraham longed to see my day, and he saw it and rejoiced. But he did not say: all prophets and just men desired to see what you see, but many. Among many it may be that some saw and others did not see, although in this too the interpretation is dangerous, since we may seem to make some distinction or other among the merits of the saints. Therefore Abraham saw in an enigma, saw in a form; but you hold and have your Lord in the present, and you question him at your will, and you eat with him.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.13.19":["Everyone who hears the word of the kingdom and does not understand. Putting this first, he urges us to listen more diligently to what is said.\n— Commentary on Matthew","The evil one comes and snatches what has been sown in his heart. The evil one snatches the good seed. And at the same time understand that it was sown in the heart, and that the different kinds of ground are the souls of believers.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.13.21":["But when tribulation and persecution have come because of the word, he is at once scandalized. Pay attention to what is said: he is at once scandalized. There is therefore some difference between the one who is compelled by many tribulations and punishments to deny Christ and the one who at the first persecution is immediately scandalized and falls.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.13.22":["But he who sows among thorns, this is he who hears the word, and the care of this world and the deceitfulness of riches chokes the word, and it is made fruitless. It seems to me that what is said to Adam according to the letter, Among thorns and thistles you shall eat your bread, also signifies mystically that whoever gives himself to the pleasures of the world and the cares of this world eats the heavenly bread and the true food among thorns. And he aptly added: the deceitfulness of riches chokes the word. For riches are alluring, doing one thing and promising another. Their possession is slippery, since they are carried about hither and thither, and with unsteady step they either desert those who have them or stuff those who do not have them. Hence the Lord also asserts that the rich enter the kingdom of heaven with difficulty, since riches choke the word of God and soften the rigor of the virtues.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.13.23":["But he who was sown in good ground, this is he who hears the word and understands and bears fruit. Just as in the bad ground there were three different kinds, the wayside and the rocky and the thorny places, so in the good ground there is a threefold diversity: of the hundredfold, the sixtyfold and the thirtyfold fruit. And in the one as in the other it is not the substance that is changed but the will, and it is the hearts of unbelievers as well as of believers that receive the seed. The evil one comes, he says, and snatches what has been sown in his heart, and in the second and third place: this is he, he says, who hears the word. In the exposition of the good ground too, this is he who hears the word. First, therefore, we must hear, then understand, and after understanding render the fruits of the teachings and produce either the hundredfold fruit or the sixtyfold and thirtyfold; of these we spoke more fully in the book Against Jovinian, and now we touch on them briefly, assigning the hundredfold fruit to virgins, the sixtyfold to widows and the continent, the thirtyfold to holy matrimony. Honorable indeed is marriage, and the bed undefiled. Some of our writers refer the hundredfold fruit to the martyrs; but if that is so, the holy partnerships of marriage are shut out from the good fruit.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.13.24":["Another parable he set before them, saying: The kingdom of heaven has become like a man who sowed good seed in his field; but while men were sleeping, his enemy came and sowed darnel over it in the midst of the wheat, and the rest. This is the second parable set down with its interpretation, not immediately, but explained after other parables have been placed in between. For here it is set forth, and afterwards, the crowds having been dismissed, he comes home, and there come to him his disciples, asking: Explain to us the parable of the darnel of the field, and the rest. For we ought not, through an overhasty desire to understand, to seek the knowledge of it before it is explained by the Lord.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.13.25-30":["Therefore, as I said above, to the things that have been expounded by the Lord we must give our faith, while those that are passed over in silence and left to our understanding are to be touched on briefly. By the men who sleep understand the teachers of the churches; by the servants of the householder, do not take any others than the angels who daily see the face of the Father; the devil, moreover, is called an enemy man because he ceased to be a god, and in Psalm 9 it is written of him: Arise, O Lord, let not man prevail. Therefore let him who is set over the Church not sleep, lest through his negligence the enemy man oversow darnel, that is, the doctrines of heretics. As for what is said, lest perhaps, in gathering the darnel, you root up the wheat along with it, room is given for repentance, and we are warned not to cut off a brother quickly, because it may happen that he who today has been corrupted by a harmful doctrine comes to his senses tomorrow and begins to defend the truth. That too which follows, Let both grow until the harvest, seems to be contrary to that precept, Remove the evil one from your midst, and to the rule that no fellowship is to be had with those who are called brothers and are adulterers and fornicators. For if uprooting is forbidden and patience is to be kept until the harvest, how are some to be cast out from our midst? Between the wheat and the darnel, which we call lolium, as long as it is still in the blade and the stalk has not yet come to the ear, there is great similarity, and in telling them apart the difference is either none or very hard to see. Therefore the Lord warns us beforehand not to pronounce sentence quickly where anything is doubtful, but to reserve the end for God the judge, so that, when the day of judgment has come, he may cast out of the company of the saints not a suspicion of crime but manifest guilt. As for his saying that the bundles of darnel are given over to the fires and the wheat gathered into barns, it is plain that all heretics and hypocrites of the faith are to be burned in the fires of Gehenna, while the saints, who are called wheat, are received into barns, that is, into heavenly mansions.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.13.31":["Another parable he set before them, saying. The Lord was sitting in the ship, the crowd was standing on the shore; they heard from afar, the disciples from nearer at hand. He sets before them yet another parable, like a rich householder refreshing his guests with various foods, so that each might take different nourishment according to the nature of his own stomach. Hence also in the earlier parable he did not say a second, but another. For if he had put forward a second, we could not have expected a third; he put forward another so that more might follow.\n— Commentary on Matthew","The kingdom of heaven is like a grain of mustard seed, which a man took and sowed in his field; which indeed is the least of all seeds, but when it has grown it is greater than all garden herbs and becomes a tree, so that the birds of the sky come and dwell in its branches. Let it not be irksome to the reader if we set out whole parables. For things that are obscure must be discussed more fully, lest by excessive brevity the sense be wrapped up rather than explained. The kingdom of heaven is the preaching of the Gospel and the knowledge of the Scriptures, which leads to life, and of which it is said to the Jews: The kingdom of God shall be taken from you and given to a nation producing its fruits. Like, therefore, in this manner, is the kingdom to a grain of mustard seed, which a man took and sowed in his field. The man who sows in his field is understood by most to be the Savior, because he sows in the souls of believers; by others, a man himself sowing in his own field, that is, in himself and in his heart. Who is this who sows but our mind and our soul, which, receiving the grain of preaching and fostering the seed with the moisture of faith, makes it sprout in the field of its own breast?\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.13.32":["The preaching of the Gospel is the least of all teachings. For at the first hearing of its doctrine it has no credit of truth, since it preaches a man who is God, Christ dead, and the stumbling block of the cross. Compare a teaching of this kind with the doctrines of the philosophers and with their books, and with the splendor of their eloquence and the composition of their discourses, and you will see how much smaller than the other seeds is the sowing of the Gospel. But those, when they have grown, show nothing pungent, nothing vigorous, nothing vital; the whole of it bubbles up, flabby and withered, into garden herbs and grasses which quickly dry up and collapse. This preaching, however, which seemed small in the beginning, when it has been sown either in the soul of the believer or in the whole world, does not rise up into garden herbs but grows into a tree, so that the birds of the sky, by which we ought to understand either the souls of believers or powers given over to the service of God, come and dwell in its branches. The branches of the Gospel tree that has grown from the grain of mustard seed I take to be the diversities of doctrines, on which each of the birds mentioned above rests. Let us too take up the wings of a dove, so that, flying to higher places, we may dwell in the branches of this tree and make ourselves little nests of teachings, and, fleeing earthly things, hasten toward heavenly ones. Many, reading that the grain of mustard seed is the least of all seeds, and what is said in the Gospel by the disciples, Lord, increase our faith, and the answer given them by the Savior, Amen I say to you: If you had faith like a grain of mustard seed, you would say to this mountain: Move from this place, and it would move, think either that the apostles are asking for a small faith or that the Lord is in doubt about a small faith, whereas the apostle Paul judges faith compared to a grain of mustard seed to be the greatest. For what does he say? If I have such faith that I move mountains, but do not have love, it profits me nothing. Therefore what the Lord said is done by a faith that is compared to a grain of mustard seed, this the Apostle teaches can be done by faith entire.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.13.33":["Another parable he spoke to them: The kingdom of heaven is like leaven, which a woman took and hid in three measures of meal until the whole was leavened. The stomach of men varies: some delight in bitter foods, others in sweet, these in harsher, those in milder. So the Lord, as we have already said above, sets forth different parables, so that according to the variety of the wounds the medicine too may vary. This woman who took the leaven and hid it in three measures of meal until the whole is leavened seems to me to be either the apostolic preaching or the Church, which has been gathered from different nations. She takes the leaven, namely the knowledge and understanding of the Scriptures, and hides it in three measures of meal, so that spirit, soul and body, brought into one, may not be at variance with one another, but, when they have agreed with two and three, may obtain from the Father whatever they have asked. This passage is also explained in another way. We read in Plato, and it is a widespread doctrine of the philosophers, that there are three passions in the human soul: τὸ λογιστικόν, which we can render as the rational; τὸ θυμικόν, which we call full of anger, or irascible; τὸ ἐπιθυμητικόν, which we name concupiscible; and that philosopher thinks that our rational part dwells in the brain, anger in the gall, desire in the liver. And so we too, if we have received the Gospel leaven of the holy Scriptures of which we spoke above, shall have the three passions of the human soul brought into one, so that in reason we possess prudence, in anger hatred against vices, in desire a longing for virtues; and all this will come about through the Gospel teaching which mother Church has furnished to us. I will also give a third understanding, that of certain people, so that the curious reader may choose from several whichever pleases him. They too interpret this woman as the Church, which has mixed the faith of man in three measures of meal, namely belief in the Father and the Son and the Holy Spirit, and, when it has been leavened into one, leads us not to a threefold God but to the knowledge of one divinity. The three measures of meal also, since there is no different nature in each, draw toward the unity of the substance. A pious sense indeed; but parables and the doubtful understanding of enigmas can never avail for the authority of doctrines. Now the satum is a kind of measure, according to the custom of the province of Palestine, holding one and a half modii. Other things too are said about this parable, but it is not for the present matter to say everything about everything.\n— Commentary on Matthew","Into three measures of this flour the woman of the Gospel puts leaven, so that the spirit by which we perceive, and the soul by which we live, and the body by which we walk, may be brought together into one Holy Spirit, according to the Apostle: In him we live, move and are.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"MAT.13.34":["All these things Jesus spoke in parables to the crowds, and without parables he did not speak to them. He spoke through parables not to the disciples but to the crowds; and to this day the crowds hear in parables, while the disciples question the Savior at home.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.13.35":["That there might be fulfilled what was spoken through the prophet, saying: I will open my mouth in parables; I will pour forth things hidden from the foundation of the world. This testimony is taken from Psalm 77 (78). I have read in some codices, and the studious reader may perhaps find the same thing, that in the place where we have set down, and where the common edition has, That there might be fulfilled what was spoken through the prophet, saying, there is written: through Isaiah the prophet, saying. Because this was nowhere found in Isaiah, I think it was afterwards removed by prudent men. But it seems to me that originally it was published thus, namely that it was written: through Asaph the prophet, saying (for Psalm 77 (78), from which this testimony is taken, is inscribed with the title of Asaph the prophet), and that the first copyist did not understand Asaph, thought it a copyist's fault, and corrected it to the name of Isaiah, whose name was better known. It should be known, therefore, that in the psalms and hymns and songs of God not only David but also the others whose names stand at their head are to be called prophets, namely Asaph and Jeduthun and Heman the Ezrahite and Ethan and the sons of Korah and the rest whom Scripture mentions. And as for what is said in the person of the Lord, I will open my mouth in parables; I will pour forth things hidden from the foundation of the world, one must consider it more attentively, and it will be found that the going out of Israel from Egypt is described there, and all the signs are narrated that are contained in the history of Exodus. From this we understand that all those things that are written are to be taken parabolically, and that it is not so great a letter that sounds out plainly, but hidden mysteries too. For this is what the Savior promises he will speak, opening his mouth in parables and pouring forth things hidden from the foundation of the world.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.13.36":["Then, having dismissed the crowds, he came into the house. And his disciples came to him, saying: Explain to us the parable of the darnel of the field. Jesus dismisses the crowds and returns home, so that the disciples may come to him and ask in secret what the people neither deserved to hear nor were able to. Explain to us the parable of the darnel.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.13.37-41":["He answered and said: He who sows the good seed is the Son of Man. He has plainly explained that the field is the world, the sower the Son of Man, the good seed the sons of the kingdom, the darnel the sons of the most wicked one, the sower of the darnel the devil, the harvest the consummation of the world, the reapers the angels; all stumbling blocks are referred to the darnel, the just are reckoned among the sons of the kingdom.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.13.43":["Then the just will shine like the sun in the kingdom of their Father. In the present world the light of the saints shines before men, but after the consummation of the world they themselves, the just, will shine like the sun in the kingdom of their Father.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.13.44":["The kingdom of heaven is like a treasure hidden in a field, which the man who finds it hides, and for joy over it he goes and sells all that he has and buys that field. Held back by the frequent obscurities of the parables, we go beyond the brief, verse-by-verse (commatic) interpretation, so that we seem almost to have passed from one kind of interpretation to another. This treasure, in which are hidden all the treasures of wisdom and knowledge, is either the Word of God, who is seen hidden in the flesh of Christ, or the holy Scriptures, in which is stored the knowledge of the Savior; and when anyone finds him in them, he ought to despise all the gains of this world, so that he may possess him whom he has found. As for what follows, which, when a man has found it, he hides, it is said not because he does this out of envy, but because, with the fear of one who keeps it and does not want to lose it, he hides in his heart the one he has preferred to his former possessions.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.13.45-46":["We must not pass over without scruple that the Apostle prayed for some that grace and peace might be multiplied to them, whereas now, to Titus, peace and grace are set down without multiplication. Noah, a just man, alone preserved when the world was shipwrecked, is said to have found not many graces but one grace in the sight of God. And Moses to the Lord: \"If I have found,\" he says, \"grace with you\" (Exod. 33:13). And wherever else grace is set down in the person of the saints, seek and you will find that they found not graces, but grace. That merchant in the Gospel who had many pearls at last found one precious one, which alone he bought with the many pearls (Matt. 13). For it belongs to the perfect to buy one pearl and one treasure in exchange for all their pearls and their whole substance; but to beginners, and those still set upon the road, to have not just one and only one, but many pearls.\n— Commentary on Titus","Again, the kingdom of heaven is like a merchant man seeking good pearls; and having found one most precious pearl, he went away and sold all that he had and bought it. The same thing as is said above, in other words. The good pearls that the instructor seeks are the Law and the Prophets. Hear, Marcion; hear, Manichee: the good pearls are the Law and the Prophets and the knowledge of the Old Testament, but the one most precious pearl is the knowledge of the Savior and the mystery of his passion and the secret of his resurrection. When the merchant man, like the apostle Paul, has found it, he despises all the mysteries of the Law and the Prophets and the former observances in which he had lived blamelessly, as refuse and rubbish, so that he may gain Christ. Not that the finding of a new pearl is the condemnation of the old pearls, but that in comparison with it every other gem is of less worth.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.13.47-50":["Again, the kingdom of heaven is like a net cast into the sea and gathering of every kind of fish; and when it was filled, drawing it out and sitting beside the shore, they chose the good into their vessels, but threw the bad out; so it will be at the consummation of the world, and the rest. When the prophecy of Jeremiah was fulfilled, who says, Behold, I send to you many fishers, after Peter and Andrew, James and John the sons of Zebedee had heard, Follow me, and I will make you fishers of men, they wove for themselves out of the Old and New Testament a net of Gospel doctrines and cast it into the sea of this world; and to this day it is spread out in the midst of the waves, catching from the salt and bitter whirlpools whatever falls into it, that is, both good men and bad, both the best fish and the worst. But when the consummation and end of the world has come, as he himself explains more plainly below, then the net will be drawn out to the shore, then the true judgment of the fish to be sorted will be shown, and, as in a most quiet harbor, the good will be put into the vessels of the heavenly mansions, while the flame of Gehenna will receive the bad, to be roasted and dried up.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.13.51":["Have you understood all these things? They say to him: Yes. The word is addressed properly to the apostles, and to them it is said, Have you understood these things, since he wishes them not merely to hear, like the people, but to understand, as future teachers.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.13.52":["Therefore every scribe learned in the kingdom of heaven is like a man who is a householder, who brings out of his treasure things new and old. The apostles, the scribes and notaries of the Savior, who marked down his words and precepts on the fleshly tablets of the heart, were instructed in the mysteries of the heavenly kingdoms; and they were strong in the wealth of the householder, bringing out of the treasure of their teachings things new and old, so that whatever they preached in the Gospel they might prove by the voices of the Law and the Prophets. Hence the bride too says in the Song of Songs: New things with old, my kinsman, I have kept for you.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.13.53-54":["And it came to pass, when Jesus had finished these parables, he passed over from there, and coming into his own land he taught them in their synagogue. After the parables which he spoke to the people, and which the apostles alone understand, he passed over into his own country, so as to teach more openly there.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.13.54":["Whence has this man this wisdom and these mighty works? The amazing folly of the Nazarenes! They wonder whence Wisdom has wisdom and Power mighty works; but their error lies plain to see, for they supposed him to be the son of a carpenter.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.13.55-56":["Is not this the carpenter's son? Is not his mother called Mary, and his brothers James and Joseph, Simon and Judas, and his sisters, all of them with us? The error of the Jews is our salvation and the condemnation of the heretics. For they so far saw Jesus Christ as a man that they thought him the son of a carpenter: Is not this the carpenter's son? Do you wonder that they err about the brothers when they err about the father? This passage is set out more fully in the little book Against Helvidius mentioned above.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.13.57":["But Jesus said to them: A prophet is not without honor except in his own country and in his own house. It is almost natural for citizens always to envy their fellow citizens; for they do not consider the man's present works but remember his frail infancy, as though they themselves had not come to mature age through the same stages of life.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.13.58":["And therefore he did not do many mighty works there, because of their unbelief. Not that he could not have done many mighty works even among them in their unbelief, but lest, by doing many, he should condemn his unbelieving fellow citizens. It can also be understood in another way: that Jesus is despised in his house and in his own country, that is, among the people of the Jews, and therefore did few signs there, lest they be made wholly without excuse. But greater signs he does daily among the Gentiles through the apostles, not so much in the healing of bodies as in the salvation of souls.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.14.2":["At that time Herod the tetrarch heard the report of Jesus and said to his servants: This is John the Baptist; he has risen from the dead, and therefore mighty works are at work in him. A certain one of the Church's interpreters asks the reasons why Herod suspected this, so as to think that John had risen from the dead and that therefore mighty works were at work in him; as though we had to render an account of another's error, or as though the sect of μετεμψυχώσεως (transmigration of souls) could take occasion from these words, when in any case, at the time when John was beheaded, the Lord was thirty years old, whereas μετεμψύχωσις (transmigration of souls) says that souls are introduced into different bodies after many cycles of years.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.14.3-4":["But Herod had seized John and bound him and put him in prison because of Herodias, his brother's wife. For John said to him: It is not lawful for you to have her. An old history relates that Philip, the son of Herod the elder, under whom the Lord fled into Egypt, and the brother of this Herod, under whom Christ suffered, married Herodias, the daughter of King Phetras; but that afterwards her father, his father-in-law, when certain quarrels had arisen against his son-in-law, took back his daughter and, to the grief of her former husband, joined her in marriage to Herod, his enemy. Who this Philip is, the evangelist Luke teaches more fully: In the fifteenth year of the reign of Tiberius Caesar, when Pontius Pilate was governing Judea, and Herod was tetrarch of Galilee, and Philip his brother was tetrarch of Iturea and the region of Trachonitis. So John the Baptist, who had come in the spirit and power of Elijah, with the same authority with which Elijah had rebuked Ahab and Jezebel, reproves Herod and Herodias because they had made an unlawful marriage, and because it is not lawful, while one's own brother is living, to take his wife; for he preferred to be in danger with the king rather than to be forgetful of the commandments of God for the sake of flattery.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.14.5":["And wishing to kill him, he feared the people, because they held him as a prophet. He dreaded indeed an uprising of the people on account of John, by whom he knew that very great crowds had been baptized in the Jordan; but he was overcome by love of his wife, out of burning desire for whom he had neglected even the commandments of God.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.14.6":["But on Herod's birthday the daughter of Herodias danced in the middle of the dining hall and pleased Herod. We find no one else who kept his birthday except Herod and Pharaoh, so that those whose impiety was equal might have a single festival.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.14.7":["With an oath he promised to give her whatever she should ask of him. I do not excuse Herod on the ground that he committed murder against his will and unwillingly because of the oath; he perhaps swore for this very purpose, to prepare his engines for an occasion to come. Otherwise, if he says that he did it because of the oath, had she asked for the death of his father, or of his mother, would he have done it or not? What, therefore, he would have refused in his own case, he ought to have disregarded in the case of the prophet as well.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.14.8":["Give me, she says, here on a dish the head of John the Baptist. Herodias, fearing that Herod might at some time come to his senses, or become a friend to his brother Philip, and that the unlawful marriage might be dissolved by divorce, advises her daughter to ask at once, at the very banquet, for the head of John: a fitting reward of blood for a fitting work of dancing.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.14.9":["And the king was saddened. It is the custom of the Scriptures that the historian narrates the opinion of many in the way it was believed by all at that time. Just as Joseph is called the father of Jesus even by Mary herself, so now too Herod is said to have been saddened, because those reclining at table thought so. For this dissembler of his own mind and skilled murderer displayed sadness on his face while he had joy in his mind.\n— Commentary on Matthew","But because of the oath and of those who were reclining with him, he ordered it to be given. He excuses the crime by the oath, so that under the pretext of piety he might become impious. And as for what he added, and because of those who were reclining with him, he wants all to be partners in his crime, so that bloody dishes might be served at a luxurious and impure banquet.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.14.11":["And his head was brought on a dish and given to the girl, and she brought it to her mother. We read in Roman history that Flaminius, a Roman general, was expelled from the senate by the censors because, when a little harlot reclining beside him said that she had never seen a man beheaded, he had agreed that a certain man guilty of a capital crime should be beheaded at the banquet: expelled because he had mixed feasting with blood and had offered up the death of a man, guilty though he was, for the delight of another, so that lust and murder were mingled together. How much more wicked are Herod and Herodias, and the girl who danced and asks for the head of the prophet as the price of blood, so as to have in her power the tongue that rebuked the unlawful marriage. Let this have been done according to the letter. But we see to this day, in the head of John the prophet, that the Jews have lost Christ, who is the head of the prophets.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.14.12":["And his disciples came up and took his body and buried it, and they came and told Jesus. Josephus reports that John was beheaded in a certain town of Arabia. And what follows, His disciples came up and took the body, we can understand of the disciples both of John himself and of the Savior. And they came and told Jesus.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.14.13":["When Jesus had heard this, he withdrew from there in a boat to a desert place apart. They announce the Baptist's death to the Savior; having heard of it, he withdraws to a desert place; not, as some think, out of fear of death, but sparing his enemies, lest they add murder to murder; or else putting off his own death to the day of the Passover, on which, because of the mystery, the lamb is to be sacrificed and the doorposts of the believers are to be sprinkled with blood. Or else he withdrew for this reason, to give us an example of avoiding the rashness of those who hand themselves over of their own accord, because not all persevere in torments with the same constancy with which they offer themselves to be tortured. For this reason he also commands in another place: When they persecute you in this city, flee into another. Elegantly too the evangelist does not say, he fled to a desert place, but he withdrew, so that he avoided his persecutors rather than feared them. Alternatively. After the head of the prophet had been cut off by the Jews and by the king of the Jews, and prophecy had lost its tongue and voice among them, Jesus passed over to the desert place of the Church, which before had not had a husband.\n— Commentary on Matthew","And when the crowds had heard, they followed him on foot from the cities. He may also, for another reason, having heard of John's death, have withdrawn into a desert place: to test the faith of the believers. In short, the crowds followed him on foot, not on beasts of burden, not in various vehicles, but by the labor of their own feet, to show the ardor of their minds. If we wish to lay open the reasons of every single word, we exceed the brevity of the work we have undertaken. Yet it must be said in passing that after the Lord had come into the desert, very great crowds followed him; for before he came into the solitudes of the Gentiles, he was worshiped by one people only.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.14.14":["And going out he saw a great crowd. In the words of the Gospel the spirit is always joined to the letter, and whatever seems cold at first sight is warm if you touch it. The Lord was in a desert place; the crowds followed him, leaving their cities, that is, their former ways of life and the variety of doctrines. But Jesus' coming out signifies that the crowds had indeed the will to go, but did not have the strength to arrive; therefore the Savior comes out from his place and goes to meet them, as also in another parable the penitent son; and seeing the crowd he has pity and heals their sicknesses, so that full faith may at once obtain its reward.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.14.15":["But when evening had come, his disciples came to him, saying, and the rest. All things are full of mysteries. He withdraws from Judea, he comes into a desert place, the crowds follow him, having left their cities, Jesus comes out to them, he has pity on the crowd, he heals their sick; and he does this not in the morning, not as the day grows, not at midday, but in the evening, when the sun of justice had set.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.14.16":["But Jesus said to them: They have no need to go; you give them to eat. They have no need to seek various foods and to buy themselves unknown loaves, when they have the heavenly bread with them. You give them to eat. He challenges the apostles to the breaking of bread, so that, when they testify that they have none, the greatness of the sign may become better known.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.14.17":["They answered him: We have nothing here but five loaves and two fishes. In another evangelist we read: There is a certain boy here who has five loaves, and he seems to me to signify Moses; the two fishes we understand either as both Testaments, or because an even number refers to the law. So the apostles, before the passion of the Savior and the flashing forth of the lightning-bright Gospel, had nothing but five loaves and two little fishes, which moved about in salt waters and in bitter waves.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.14.18":["He said to them: Bring them here to me. Hear, Marcion; hear, Manichee: Jesus orders the five loaves and the two little fishes to be brought to him, so that he may sanctify and multiply them.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.14.19":["And when he had ordered the crowd to recline on the grass. According to the letter the sense is plain; let us lay open the mysteries of the spiritual interpretation. They are ordered to recline on the grass, and according to another evangelist on the ground, by fifties or by hundreds, so that, after they have trodden down their flesh and have put under themselves all its flowers and the pleasures of the world like withered grass, they may then, through the repentance of the number fifty, ascend to the perfect summit of the number one hundred.\n— Commentary on Matthew","Taking the five loaves and the two fishes, looking up to heaven, he blessed and broke and gave the loaves to the disciples. He looks up to heaven to teach that our eyes must be directed there. He took the five loaves and the two little fishes into his hands, and broke them, and handed them to the disciples. As the Lord breaks them, a seedbed of food comes into being. For if they had stayed whole, and had not been torn into pieces nor divided into a manifold harvest, they could not have fed the crowds, and the children, and the women, and so great a multitude. So the law together with the prophets is broken and torn into pieces, and its mysteries are brought out into the open, so that what did not nourish while it was whole and remained in its former state might, divided into parts, nourish the multitude of the Gentiles.\n— Commentary on Matthew","And he gave the loaves to the disciples, and the disciples to the crowds. The crowds receive nourishment from the Lord through the apostles.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.14.20":["And they took up of the remnants twelve baskets full of fragments. Each of the apostles fills his basket from the Savior's remnants, either so that he may have from it something with which afterwards to offer food to the Gentiles, or so that he may teach from the remnants that those were real loaves which were afterwards multiplied. And at the same time ask how, in the desert and in so vast a solitude, no loaves are found but five only, and two little fishes, and yet twelve baskets are found so easily.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.14.21":["Now the number of those who ate was five thousand men, besides women and little children. In keeping with the number of the five loaves, the multitude of the men who ate is also five thousand. For it had not yet come, as in the narrative of another passage, to the number seven, of which those who eat are four thousand, a number close to that of the Gospels. Now five thousand men eat, men who had grown to the perfect man and were following him of whom Zechariah says: Behold a man, the Rising is his name. But women and little children, the frail sex and the lesser age, are unworthy of being numbered. Hence too in the book of Numbers, whenever the priests and the Levites and the army or the bands of fighting men are registered, slaves and women and little children and the undistinguished common people are passed over without number.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.14.22":["And he compelled his disciples to get into the boat and to go ahead of him across the water, until he dismissed the crowds. He commanded the disciples to cross the water, and compels them to get into the boat. By this word it is shown that they withdrew from the Lord unwillingly, since out of love for their teacher they do not want to be separated from him even for a moment of time.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.14.23":["And having dismissed the crowd, he went up the mountain alone to pray. If the disciples Peter and James and John, who had seen the glory of him transfigured, had been with him, perhaps they would have gone up the mountain with him; but the crowd cannot follow to the heights unless he has taught it by the sea on the shore and fed it in the desert. But that he went up alone to pray, do not refer to him who with five loaves satisfied five thousand men, besides little children and women, but to him who, having heard of John's death, withdrew into solitude; not that we separate the person of the Lord, but that his works are divided between God and man.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.14.24":["But the boat in the middle of the sea was tossed by the waves. Rightly had the apostles withdrawn from the Lord as though unwilling and reluctant, lest in his absence they should suffer shipwreck. In short, while the Lord was lingering on the summit of the mountain, at once a contrary wind arises and troubles the sea, and the apostles are in danger, and the impending shipwreck lasts until Jesus comes.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.14.25":["But in the fourth watch of the night he came to them walking upon the sea. Military guard-posts and watches are divided into spaces of three hours each. So when he says that the Lord came to them in the fourth watch of the night, he shows that they were in danger the whole night, and that at the very end of the night and at the consummation of the world he will bring them help.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.14.26":["And seeing him walking upon the sea, they were troubled, saying: It is a phantom. If, according to Marcion and Mani, our Lord was not born of a virgin but was seen as a phantom, how is it that the apostles now fear that they are seeing a phantom?\n— Commentary on Matthew","And for fear they cried out. A confused cry and an uncertain voice are the mark of great fear.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.14.27":["And at once Jesus spoke to them, saying: Have confidence, it is I. What was at issue first, that he heals first, and to those who are afraid he gives a command, saying: Have confidence, do not be afraid. And as to what follows, it is I, he does not add who he is: either they could recognize him from a voice known to them, as he spoke through the dark shadows of the night, or they recalled that it was he himself whom they knew to have spoken to Moses: This is what you say to the sons of Israel: He who is has sent me to you.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.14.28":["But Peter answered and said: Lord, if it is you, bid me come to you over the waters. In every place Peter is found to be of the most ardent faith. When the disciples are asked whom men said Jesus was, he confesses him the Son of God; when Jesus wishes to go to his passion, though he errs in the matter, yet he does not err in his affection, not wishing him to die whom he had shortly before confessed to be the Son of God; on the mountain he goes up with the Savior, first among the first; and in the passion he alone follows; the sin of denial, which had come upon him from sudden fear, he at once washes away with bitter tears; after the passion, when they were on the lake of Gennesaret and were fishing, and the Lord was standing on the shore, while the others were sailing in little by little, he does not endure delay, but, girding himself with his outer garment, at once throws himself headlong into the waves. So with the same ardor of faith as always, now too, while the others are silent, he believes that he can do by the will of the master what the master could do by nature. Bid me come to you over the waters. You command, and at once the waves will become solid, and the body that is heavy of itself will become light.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.14.29":["And Peter, getting down from the boat, walked upon the water. Let those who think that the Lord's body was not real because, soft and airy, it walked over the soft waters, answer how Peter walked, whom they will certainly not deny to be a real man.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.14.30":["But seeing the strong wind he was afraid, and when he began to sink he cried out, saying: Lord, save me. The faith of his spirit was burning, but human frailty was dragging him into the deep. So he is left to temptation for a little, so that his faith may be increased and he may understand that he was preserved not by the readiness of his request but by the power of the Lord.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.14.31":["And at once Jesus, stretching out his hand, took hold of him and said to him: You of little faith, why did you doubt? If to the apostle Peter, of whose faith and ardor of mind we spoke above, who had confidently asked the Savior, saying: Lord, if it is you, bid me come to you over the waters, it is said, because he was a little afraid: You of little faith, why did you doubt, what must be said to us, who do not have even the smallest little portion of this little faith?\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.14.33":["Those who were in the boat came and worshiped him, saying: Truly you are the Son of God. At a single sign, when calm was restored to the sea, which after excessive storms sometimes also happens by chance, the sailors and passengers confess him truly the Son of God; and Arius preaches in the Church that he is a creature.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.14.34":["And when they had crossed the water, they came into the land of Gennesaret. If we knew what Gennesaret sounds like in our language, we would understand how Jesus, through the type of the apostles and of the ship, brings the Church, delivered from the shipwreck of persecution, across to the shore, and makes it rest in a most tranquil harbor.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.14.35":["And when the men of that place had recognized him, they sent into all that region. They recognized him by report, not by face; or at any rate, because of the greatness of the signs that he was performing among the peoples, he was also known by face to very many. And see how great is the faith of the men of the land of Gennesaret, that they are not content with the health of those present only, but send to other cities round about, so that all may run to the physician.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.14.36":["And they brought to him all who were ill, and they begged him that they might touch even the fringe of his garment; and whoever touched it were made whole. Let those who are ill touch, not the body of Jesus, nor his whole garment, but the outermost fringe; and whoever touch it will be healed. Understand the fringe of his garment as the least commandment, for whoever transgresses it shall be called least in the kingdom of heaven; or as the assumption of the body, through which we come to the Word of God and afterwards enjoy his majesty.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.15.2":["Why do your disciples transgress the tradition of the elders? Astonishing is the folly of the Pharisees and scribes. They accuse the Son of God of not keeping the traditions and precepts of men.\n— Commentary on Matthew","For they do not wash their hands when they eat bread. The hands, that is, the works, not of the body but of the soul, are what must be washed, so that the word of God may come to be in them.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.15.3":["But he answered and said to them: Why do you also transgress the commandment of God for the sake of your tradition? He refutes a false charge with a true answer. Since, he says, you neglect the precepts of the Lord for the sake of the tradition of men, why do you think my disciples are to be reproved for making light of the commands of the elders in order to keep the decrees of God?\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.15.4-6":["For God said: Honor father and mother, and whoever curses father or mother, let him die the death. But you say: Whoever says to father or mother: Whatever gift is from me will profit you; and he has not honored his father or his mother. Honor in the Scriptures is understood not so much in greetings and the paying of courtesies as in alms and the offering of gifts. Honor, says the Apostle, widows who are truly widows, and here honor is understood as a gift; and in another place: Presbyters are to be honored with double honor, especially those who labor in the word and teaching of God. And by this commandment we are bidden not to close the mouth of the ox that treads out the grain, and that the laborer be worthy of his wages. The Lord, considering either the infirmities or the ages or the poverty of parents, had commanded that sons should honor their parents also by supplying them with the necessities of life. Wishing to overturn this most provident law of God, so as to bring in impiety under the name of piety, the scribes and Pharisees taught the worst of sons that if anyone wished to vow to God, who is the true Father, the things that were to be offered to his parents, the offering to the Lord should take precedence over gifts to parents. Or else the parents themselves, shunning what they saw consecrated to God so as not to incur the charge of sacrilege, were worn down by want; and so it came about that the offering of the children, under pretext of the temple and of God, went to the profit of the priests. This most wicked tradition of the Pharisees came from another occasion. Many who held men bound to them by debt, men unwilling to repay what had been lent them, assigned these debts to the priests, so that the money, once exacted, might serve the ministries of the temple and the priests' own uses. But it can also, briefly, have this sense: The gift that is from me will profit you. You compel sons, he says, to say to their parents: Whatever gift I was going to offer to God I spend on your food, and it profits you, O father and mother; so that they, afraid to accept what they see made over to God, would rather lead a life of want than eat of consecrated things.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.15.7-9":["The Lord used this testimony against the Pharisees in the Gospel of Matthew, saying: Why do you also transgress the commandment of God for the sake of your traditions (Matt 15:3). And again: Hypocrites, well did Isaiah prophesy of you, saying: This people honors me with its lips, but its heart is far from me. And in vain do they worship me, teaching the doctrines and precepts of men (ibid., 6-9). In this we ought to note what we have often pointed out: that the Evangelists and Apostles did not translate word for word, nor follow the authority of the Seventy translators, whose edition was already being read at that time, but, as Hebrews and men instructed in the Law, used their own words without loss to the sense. The people of the Jews draws near to God with its mouth and lips, because it boasts that it holds to the worship of the one God and rejects idols; but its heart is far from him, because it does not receive the Lord Jesus Christ. For he who does not receive the Son does not receive the Father. And at the same time let us learn how a person draws near to God, or becomes far from him. He speaks through Jeremiah too: I am a God who draws near, and not from afar, says the Lord (Jer 23:23 LXX). We draw near to God with the mind, not with the body; as we read of Moses too: Moses alone drew near to God; but the others did not draw near (Exod 24:2). For he prayed to God in spirit and in truth, and approached him with understanding and spirit. But these, of whom it is written that they are taken from the womb and instructed from boyhood to old age, and meditate day and night on the Law of God, do not draw near to God, because they accept the traditions of the Pharisees and scribes, which make them far from God; and they are whitened sepulchres, which outwardly appear beautiful but within are full of dead men's bones: they chose Barabbas and rejected the Lord (Matt 23:27).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"MAT.15.11":["Not what enters the mouth makes a man common, but what comes out of the mouth, this makes a man common. The word makes common is proper to the Scriptures and is not worn in public speech. The Jewish people, boasting that they are God's portion, call common the foods that all men use, for example pork, oysters, hares and animals of that kind that do not divide the hoof nor chew the cud, nor, among fish, have scales. Hence it is also written in the Acts of the Apostles: What God has sanctified, do not call common. Common, then, that which lies open to other men and is, as it were, not of God's portion, is used for unclean. Not what enters the mouth defiles a man, but what comes out of the mouth, this defiles a man. The prudent reader may object and say: If what enters the mouth does not defile a man, why do we not eat things sacrificed to idols? And the Apostle writes: You cannot drink the cup of the Lord and the cup of demons. It must be understood, therefore, that the foods themselves, and every creature of God, are clean in themselves, but the invocation of idols and demons makes them unclean.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.15.12":["Then his disciples came and said to him: Do you know that the Pharisees were scandalized when they heard this word? By a single saying the whole superstition of the Jewish observances had been struck down, for they think their religion lies in the eating and abhorring of foods. And since the word scandalum is often used in the writings of the Church, we must say briefly what it signifies. Scolon and scandalum we can call an obstacle, or a fall and a striking of the foot. So when we read: Whoever causes any one of these least ones to stumble, we understand this: he who by word or deed has given anyone an occasion of falling.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.15.13":["But he answered and said: Every planting that my heavenly Father has not planted will be uprooted. Even the things that seem plain in the Scriptures are full of questions. Every planting, he says, that my heavenly Father has not planted will be uprooted. Then that planting too will be uprooted of which the Apostle says: I planted, Apollos watered. But the question is solved by what follows: But God gave the increase. He himself also says: You are God's tillage, God's building; and in another place: We are God's fellow workers. But if fellow workers, then, when Paul plants and Apollos waters, God plants and waters together with his workers. Those who bring in different natures misuse this passage, saying: If the planting that the Father has not planted will be uprooted, then the one he has planted cannot be uprooted. But let them hear that word of Jeremiah: I planted you a true vine; how you have turned into the bitterness of a strange vine. God did indeed plant, and no one can uproot his planting. But since this planting rests in the will of its own free choice, no one else will be able to uproot it unless it gives its own assent.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.15.14":["Let them alone; they are blind, leaders of the blind. This is what the Apostle had commanded: Avoid a heretical man after a first and a second correction, knowing that such a one is perverted and condemned by himself. In this sense the Savior too commands that the worst teachers be left to their own choice, knowing that they can hardly be drawn to the truth, that they are blind and drag a blind people into error.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.15.15-16":["And Peter answered and said to him: Explain this parable to us. And he said: Are you also still without understanding? What had been said openly and lay plain to the hearing, the apostle Peter thinks was spoken in a parable, and in a plain matter he seeks a mystical understanding; and he is rebuked by the Lord for thinking that something was said in parable which he had spoken plainly. From this we observe that a hearer is at fault who wishes to understand either obscure things plainly or things plainly said obscurely.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.15.17":["Do you not understand that everything that enters the mouth goes into the belly and is cast out into the privy? All the passages of the Gospels are full of stumbling blocks for heretics and the perverse. And from this little saying some slander the Lord as ignorant of natural science, as though he thought that all foods go into the belly and are passed into the privy, when in fact food, as soon as it is taken in, is diffused through the limbs and veins and marrow and sinews; whence too, they say, many who through a disorder of the stomach suffer constant vomiting throw up what they have taken in immediately after dinners and luncheons, and yet are fleshy, because at the first touch the more liquid food and drink are diffused through the members. But men of this kind, while they wish to censure another's ignorance, display their own. For however thin the moisture and however liquid the food, once it has been concocted and digested in the veins and limbs, it slips down through the hidden passages of the body, which the Greeks call πόρους, to the lower parts, and goes into the privy.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.15.19":["For out of the heart come evil thoughts, murders, adulteries, fornications, and the rest. Out of the heart, he says, come evil thoughts. Therefore the ruling part of the soul is not, as Plato holds, in the brain, but, as Christ teaches, in the heart; and this saying refutes those who think that thoughts are sent in by the devil and do not arise from one's own will. The devil can be a helper and an inciter of evil thoughts; their author he cannot be; but if, ever lying in ambush, he has fanned the slight spark of our thoughts into flame with his kindling. We ought not to suppose that he also searches out the hidden things of the heart, but rather that he judges from the bearing and gestures of the body what we are turning over within: for example, if he has seen us often looking back at a beautiful woman, he understands that the heart has been wounded by the dart of love.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.15.21-22":["And Jesus went out from there and withdrew into the parts of Tyre and Sidon. Leaving behind the scribes and Pharisees, his slanderers, he passes over into the parts of Tyre and Sidon in order to heal the Tyrians and Sidonians. And the Canaanite woman goes out from her former territory in order to obtain, by crying out, healing for her daughter. Note that it is in the fifteenth place that the Canaanite daughter is healed.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.15.22":["Have mercy on me, Lord, son of David; my daughter is badly vexed by a demon. She knew to call him son of David because she had already gone out from her own territory and, by a change of place and of faith, had left behind the error of the Tyrians and Sidonians. My daughter is badly vexed by a demon. I think that the daughter of the Church is the souls of believers, which were badly vexed by a demon, not knowing the Creator and worshiping stone.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.15.23":["Who did not answer her a word. Not from Pharisaic pride nor from the haughtiness of the scribes, but so that he might not seem to contradict his own pronouncement, in which he had commanded: Do not go into the way of the Gentiles, and do not enter the cities of the Samaritans. For he did not wish to give an occasion to the slanderers, and he was reserving the full salvation of the Gentiles for the time of his passion and resurrection.\n— Commentary on Matthew","And his disciples came and asked him, saying: Send her away, for she is crying out after us. The disciples, not yet knowing at that time the mysteries of the Lord, were asking on behalf of the Canaanite woman (whom the other evangelist calls a Syrophoenician), either moved by mercy or wishing to be rid of her importunity, because she kept crying out to him, not as to a merciful physician but as to a harsh one.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.15.24":["I was not sent except to the lost sheep of the house of Israel. Not that he was not sent to the Gentiles too, but that he was sent first to Israel, so that, when they did not receive the Gospel, the passing over to the Gentiles might be just. And he said pointedly: to the lost sheep of the house of Israel, so that from this place we may also understand the one straying sheep of the other parable.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.15.25-27":["But she came and worshiped him, saying. Wonderful, under the person of the Canaanite woman, are the faith, the patience and the humility of the Church: faith, by which she believed that her daughter could be healed; patience, by which, though so often scorned, she perseveres in her prayers; humility, by which she compares herself not to dogs but to whelps. Now the heathen are called dogs because of idolatry, for, given over to the eating of blood and to the corpses of the dead, they are carried away into madness. Note that this Canaanite woman, persevering, calls him first son of David, then Lord, and at the last worships him as God.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.15.27-28":["But she said: Yes, Lord, for even the little dogs eat of the crumbs that fall from their masters' table. I know, she says, that I do not deserve the children's bread, and that I cannot take whole food or sit at table with the father; but I am content with the whelps' leavings, so that through the humility of crumbs I may come to the greatness of the whole loaf. O wonderful reversal of things! Israel once a son, we dogs. As faith differs, the order of the names is changed. Of them it is later said: Many dogs have surrounded me; and: Look at the dogs, look at the evil workers, look at the concision. We hear, with the Syrophoenician woman and with the woman who had suffered a flow of blood: Great is your faith, let it be done according to your faith; and: Daughter, your faith has made you whole.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.15.29-31":["And he healed them, so that the crowds marveled, seeing the mute speaking, the lame walking, the blind seeing; and they magnified the God of Israel. About the κυλλούς he was silent, because he had no word to say for their opposite. So much for one word. But let us observe that, once the daughter of the Canaanite woman has been healed, he returns to Judea and to the sea of Galilee, and goes up on the mountain, and, like a bird calling its tender young to fly, sits there; and the crowds run together to him, leading or carrying with them those weighed down by various infirmities; and after he had healed them he gave them food, and when this work was completed he went up into the boat and came into the territory of Magadan. And going up on the mountain he sat there, and crowds came to him. Note that many lame and blind are led to the mountain so as to be healed there by the Lord.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.15.30-31":["And when Jesus had passed on from there, he came beside the sea of Galilee, and going up on the mountain he sat there. And great crowds came to him, having with them the mute, the lame, the blind, the maimed and many others, and they cast them down at his feet. In the place where the Latin translator rendered maimed the Greek has κυλλούς, which is the name not of disability in general but of one particular infirmity: just as he is called lame who limps on one foot, so he is called κυλλός who has one disabled hand. We have no word with the exact meaning of this one. Hence too, in what follows, the evangelist set out the healings of the other disabled, but was silent about these. For what follows?\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.15.32-38":["And the disciples say to him: Where then are we to get so many loaves in the desert as to fill so great a crowd? And Jesus said to them: How many loaves have you? And they said: Seven, and a few little fishes, and the rest. Of this sign we have already spoken above, and to repeat the same things is the mark of an idle man; let us dwell only on the points in which they differ. Above we read: When evening had come, the disciples came to him, saying: The place is a desert, and the rest. Here, having called the disciples together, the Lord himself speaks: I have pity on the crowd, because they have now been staying with me three days. There, there were five loaves and two fishes; here, seven loaves and a few little fishes. There they recline upon the grass, here upon the ground. There those who eat are five thousand, according to the number of the loaves they eat; here, four thousand. There twelve baskets are filled with what is left of the fragments; here, seven hampers. In the former sign, then, because they were near and neighbors to the five senses, it is not the Lord himself who is mindful of them but the disciples, and they are mindful of them at evening, with night near and the sun already sinking. Here, however, the Lord himself is mindful of them, and says that he has pity, and sets out the causes of his pity: Because they have now been staying with me three days, and he does not wish to send them away fasting lest they faint on the way. These who are fed from seven loaves, that is, by a sacred and perfect number, are not five thousand but four thousand, a number that is always set down in praise; and a four-cornered stone does not totter, is not unstable; and for this reason the Gospels too are consecrated in that number.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.15.32":["But Jesus called his disciples together and said: I have pity on the crowd, because they have now been staying with me three days and have nothing to eat; and I am unwilling to send them away fasting, lest they faint on the way. He wishes to feed those whom he has healed: first he takes away their disabilities, then he offers food to the healthy. He also calls his disciples together and tells them what he is about to do, either to give them a master's example that counsels are to be shared with the lesser and with disciples, or so that from the conversation they might understand the greatness of the sign, when they answer that they have no loaves in the desert. I have pity, he says, on the crowd, because they have now been staying with me three days. He has pity on the crowd because, in the number of three days, they believed in the Father, the Son and the Holy Spirit. And they have nothing to eat. The crowd is always hungry and in need of food unless it is filled by the Lord. And I am unwilling to send them away fasting, lest they faint on the way. They were hungry after their great disabilities, and in patience they were awaiting the food to come. Jesus does not wish to send them away fasting, lest they faint on the way. He is in peril, then, who hastens to reach the longed-for dwelling without the heavenly bread. Hence too the angel says to Elijah: Arise and eat, for you are to walk a great way.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.16.2-3":["But he answered and said to them: When evening has come you say: It will be fair, for the sky is red; and in the morning: Today there will be a storm, for the sky is red and lowering. You know, then, how to judge the face of the sky, but the signs of the times you cannot. This is not found in most manuscripts, and the sense is plain: from the order and constancy of the elements days both of fair weather and of rain can be known beforehand; but the scribes and Pharisees, who seemed to be teachers of the law, could not understand from the prophecy of the prophets the coming of the Savior.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.16.4-5":["And leaving them he went away; and when his disciples had come across the water, they had forgotten to take bread. Leaving the scribes and Pharisees, to whom he had said: An evil and adulterous generation seeks a sign, and no sign will be given it except the sign of Jonah, he rightly went away across the water, and the people of the Gentiles followed him. What the sign of Jonah means has already been said above.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.16.6":["Beware of the leaven of the Pharisees and Sadducees. He who bewares of the leaven of the Pharisees and Sadducees does not keep the precepts of the law and of the letter; he neglects the traditions of men in order to do the commandment of God.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.16.8-12":["Why are you reasoning among yourselves, you of little faith, that you have no bread? Do you not yet understand, nor remember the five loaves of the five thousand men, and how many baskets you took up, and the rest. On the occasion of the precept that the Savior had given, saying: Beware of the leaven of the Pharisees and Sadducees, he teaches them what the five loaves and the seven mean, and the five thousand men and the four thousand who were fed in the desert: that, although the greatness of the signs is evident, yet something else as well is shown by spiritual understanding. For if the leaven of the Pharisees and Sadducees signifies not bodily bread but perverse traditions and heretical doctrines, why should not the foods too with which the people of God was nourished signify true and whole doctrine? Someone may ask and say: How did they have no bread, who, right after filling seven hampers, went up into the boat and came into the territory of Magadan, and there, while sailing, hear that they must beware of the leaven of the Pharisees and Sadducees? But Scripture testifies that they had forgotten to take them along. This is the leaven of which the Apostle too speaks: A little leaven corrupts the whole lump. Leaven of this kind, which must be shunned by every means, Marcion had, and Valentinus, and all the heretics. Leaven has this power: if it is mixed with flour, what seemed small grows into something greater and draws the whole mass of dough to its own taste. So too heretical doctrine: if it has cast even a tiny spark into your breast, in a short time it grows up into a huge flame and draws the whole of a man's possession to itself. Finally there follows: Then they understood that he had not said they should beware of the leaven of bread, but of the teaching of the Pharisees and Sadducees.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.16.12":["But now, by this same saying, he teaches that the spiritual bread of the Church, which came down from heaven, ought not to be violated by a Jewish interpretation; and the Lord commanded the very same thing to the disciples, that they should beware of the leaven of the Pharisees (John 6). The evangelist, making this plainer, added: \"Now he had spoken to them of the doctrine of the Pharisees\" (Matt. 16:12). And what is this other doctrine of the Pharisees, if not the observance of the Law according to the flesh?\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"MAT.16.13":["But Jesus came into the parts of Caesarea Philippi. This Philip is the brother of Herod of whom we spoke above, the tetrarch of the regions of Iturea and Trachonitis, who in honor of Tiberius Caesar named Caesarea Philippi (which is now called Paneas), and it is in the province of Phoenicia; he imitated his father Herod, who in honor of Augustus Caesar named Caesarea, which was formerly called Strato's Tower, and who, from the name of his daughter, built Libias beyond the Jordan. This place is Caesarea Philippi, where the Jordan rises at the roots of Lebanon; and it has two springs, one named Ior and the other Dan, which, mingled together, make the name Jordan.\n— Commentary on Matthew","And he asked his disciples, saying: Whom do men say that the Son of Man is? He did not say: Whom do men say that I am, but the Son of Man, lest he seem to inquire boastfully about himself. And note that wherever son of man is written in the Old Testament, in the Hebrew son of Adam is set down; and that which we read in the psalm: Sons of men, how long will you be heavy of heart? in the Hebrew is said sons of Adam. Beautifully, moreover, he asks: Whom do men say that the Son of Man is, because those who speak of the Son of Man are men; but those who understand his divinity are called not men but gods.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.16.14":["And they said: Some John the Baptist, others Elijah, but others Jeremiah or one of the prophets. I wonder at certain interpreters who inquire into the causes of each one's errors and weave a very long discussion of why some thought our Lord Jesus Christ to be John, others Elijah, but others Jeremiah or one of the prophets, when they could have erred thus about Elijah and Jeremiah in the same way that Herod erred about John, saying: John, whom I beheaded, has himself risen from the dead, and mighty works are at work in him.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.16.15-16":["But whom do you say that I am? Simon Peter answered: You are the Christ, the Son of the living God. Prudent reader, observe that from what follows and from the texture of the discourse the apostles are by no means called men but gods. For when he had said: Whom do men say that the Son of Man is, he added: But whom do you say that I am? Since they, being men, think human things, you who are gods, whom do you consider me to be? Peter, in the person of all the apostles, professes: You are the Christ, the Son of the living God. He calls him the living God by comparison with those gods who are thought to be gods but are dead, meaning Saturn, Jupiter, Ceres, Liber, Hercules and the other monstrosities of idols.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.16.17":["Jesus answered and said to him: Blessed are you, Simon Bar Iona, because flesh and blood has not revealed it to you, but my Father who is in heaven. He repays in kind the apostle's testimony about him. Peter had said: You are the Christ, the Son of the living God; the true confession received its reward: Blessed are you, Simon Bar Iona. Why? Because flesh and blood did not reveal it to you, but the Father revealed it. What flesh and blood could not reveal was revealed by the grace of the Holy Spirit. Therefore from his confession he obtains a name signifying that he has his revelation from the Holy Spirit, whose son he is also to be called. For Bar Iona in our language means son of a dove. Others take it simply, that Simon, that is, Peter, is the son of John, according to the question in another place: Simon of John, do you love me? and he answered: Lord, you know, and they hold that it was corrupted by the fault of the copyists, so that Bar Iona was written in place of Bar Iohanna, that is, son of John, one syllable being taken away. And Iohanna is interpreted the grace of the Lord. But both names can be understood mystically, in that the dove signifies the Holy Spirit, and the grace of God the spiritual gift. That also which he says: Because flesh and blood has not revealed it to you, compare with the Apostle's narrative, in which he says: Immediately I did not acquiesce to flesh and blood, there signifying the Jews by flesh and blood; so that here too, under another sense, it is shown that Christ the Son of God was revealed to him not through the teaching of the Pharisees but through the grace of God.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.16.18":["And I say to you. What is it that he says: And I say to you? Because you said to me: You are the Christ, the Son of the living God, and I say to you, not with an empty word that has no effect, but I say to you because for me to have said is to have done.\n— Commentary on Matthew","That you are Peter, and upon this rock I will build my Church. Just as he himself gave light to the apostles, so that they were called the light of the world, and the other names which they obtained from the Lord; so too on Simon, who believed in the rock Christ, he bestowed the name of Peter, and according to the metaphor of the rock it is rightly said to him: I will build my Church upon you.\n— Commentary on Matthew","And the gates of hell shall not prevail against it. I think the gates of hell are vices and sins, or at any rate the doctrines of heretics, by which men are enticed and led to Tartarus. So let no one think it is said of death, as if the apostles were not subject to the condition of death, when he sees their martyrdoms shining.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.16.19":["And to you I will give the keys of the kingdom of heaven, and whatever you bind upon earth shall be bound also in heaven, and whatever you loose upon earth shall be loosed also in heaven. Not understanding this place, bishops and presbyters assume to themselves something of the haughtiness of the Pharisees, so as either to condemn the innocent or to think that they loose the guilty, when with God it is not the sentence of the priests but the life of the accused that is examined. We read in Leviticus about lepers, where they are commanded to show themselves to the priests, and, if they have leprosy, then they are made unclean by the priest; not that the priests make them lepers and unclean, but that they have knowledge of the leper and the non-leper and can discern who is clean or who unclean. Just as, then, there the priest makes the leper unclean, so here too the bishop and presbyter binds or looses, not those who are innocent or guilty, but, according to his office, when he has heard the varieties of sins, he knows who is to be bound, who loosed.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.16.20":["Then he commanded his disciples that they should tell no one that he himself was Jesus Christ. Above, when sending the disciples to preach, he had ordered them to announce his coming; here he commands them not to say that he is Jesus Christ. It seems to me that it is one thing to preach Christ, another to preach Jesus Christ. Christ is a common name of dignity; Jesus is the proper name of the Savior. But it may be that he did not wish to be preached before the passion and resurrection for this reason, that, when the mystery of the blood had afterward been completed, he might more fittingly say to the apostles: Go, teach all nations, and the rest. And lest anyone think that this is only our own understanding and not the sense of the Gospel, what follows sets out the causes of the preaching forbidden at that time.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.16.21":["From then on Jesus began to show his disciples that he must go to Jerusalem and suffer many things from the elders and scribes and chief priests, and be killed, and on the third day rise again. Now the sense is: Preach me then, when I have suffered these things, because it does no good to preach Christ publicly and to spread abroad among the peoples the majesty of him whom a little later they are to see scourged and crucified, suffering many things from the elders and scribes and chief priests. And now Jesus suffers many things from those who crucify the Son of God for themselves again, and who, though they are regarded in the Church as elders and chief priests, following the simple letter, kill the Son of God, who is perceived wholly in the spirit.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.16.22-23":["And Peter, taking him aside, began to rebuke him, saying: Far be it from you, Lord; this shall not happen to you. He turned and said to Peter: Go behind me, Satan; you are a stumbling block to me, because you do not savor the things that are of God but those that are of men. We have often said that Peter had an excessive ardor and the very greatest love toward the Lord Savior. Because, then, after his confession in which he had said: You are the Christ, the Son of the living God, and the Savior's reward which he had heard: Blessed are you, Simon Bar Iona, because flesh and blood has not revealed it to you, but my Father who is in heaven, he suddenly hears from the Lord that he must go to Jerusalem and there suffer many things from the elders and scribes and chief priests and be killed and on the third day rise again, he does not want his confession destroyed, nor does he think it can happen that the Son of God should be killed; and he takes him into his affection, or leads him apart, lest he seem to rebuke his master in the presence of the other fellow disciples, and began to rebuke him with the affection of one who loves, and to say, wishing it: Far be it from you, Lord, or, as it is better had in the Greek, Ἵλεώς σοι κύριε, that is: Be propitious, Lord; this shall not happen, it cannot be, nor do my ears accept it, that the Son of God is to be killed. To him the Lord, turning, says: Get back behind me, Satan; you are a stumbling block to me. Satan is interpreted adversary or opponent. Because, he says, you speak things contrary to my will, you ought to be called an adversary. Many think that it was not Peter who was rebuked, but the adversary spirit who was suggesting to the apostle to say these things. But to me the apostle's error, coming from an affection of piety, will never seem an incitement of the devil. Go behind me, Satan, is said to the devil. Get back, Peter hears, get back behind me, that is, follow my judgment, because you do not savor the things that are of God but those of men. It is my will and the Father's, whose will I came to do, that I should die for the salvation of men; you, considering only your own will, do not want the grain of wheat to fall into the earth so that it may bear many fruits. Let the prudent reader inquire how, after such great blessedness: Blessed are you, Simon Bar Iona, and: You are Peter, and upon this rock I will build my Church, and the gates of hell shall not prevail against it, and to you I will give the keys of the kingdom of heaven, and whatever you bind or loose upon earth shall be bound or loosed in heaven, he now hears: Get back behind me, Satan; you are a stumbling block to me, or what so sudden a turnabout this is, that after such great rewards he is called Satan. But let him who asks this consider that that blessing and blessedness and power and the building of the Church upon him were promised to Peter in the future, not given in the present. I will build, he says, my Church upon you, and the gates of hell shall not prevail against it, and I will give you the keys of the kingdom of heaven. All of it is in the future; and if he had given it to him at once, the error of a wrong confession would never have found a place in him.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.16.24":["Then Jesus said to his disciples: If anyone wishes to come after me, let him deny himself and take up his cross and follow me, and the rest. He who puts off the old man with his deeds denies himself, saying: But I live, now not I, but Christ lives in me, and he takes up his cross and is crucified to the world. And he to whom the world is crucified follows the crucified Lord.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.16.26":["Or what exchange shall a man give for his soul? For Israel, Egypt and Ethiopia and Soene are given as an exchange; for the human soul the only recompense is that which the psalmist sings: What shall I render to the Lord for all that he has rendered to me? I will take the cup of salvation, and I will call upon the name of the Lord.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.16.27":["For the Son of Man is to come in the glory of his Father with his angels, and then he will render to each according to his work. Peter, scandalized at the announcement of the Lord's death, had been rebuked by the Lord's judgment; the disciples were challenged to deny themselves and take up their cross and follow their master in the spirit of men going to their death. Great was the terror of the hearers, and such as could, with the chief of the apostles terrified, cast fear into the others too. Therefore glad things succeed the sad, and he says: The Son of Man is to come in the glory of his Father with his angels. You fear death: hear of the glory of the one triumphing; you dread the cross: listen to the ministries of the angels. And then, he says, he will render to each according to his works. There is no distinction of Jew and heathen, of man and woman, of poor and rich, where not persons but works are considered.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.16.28":["Amen I say to you: There are some of those standing here who shall not taste death until they see the Son of Man coming in his kingdom. He had wished to heal the terror of the apostles with the hope of what was promised, saying: The Son of Man is to come in the glory of his Father with his angels; adding besides the authority of the judge: And he will render to each according to his works. The silent thought of the apostles could have suffered a stumbling block of this sort: slaughter and death you say are coming now; but what you promise, that you will be present in the glory of the Father with the ministries of the angels and the power of a judge, this will be for days to come and is put off to long ages. Foreseeing, then, what they could object, the present fear, the knower of hidden things compensates it with a present reward. For what does he say? There are some of those standing here who shall not taste death until they see the Son of Man coming in his kingdom, so that what he is to be like when he comes afterward may, because of your unbelief, be shown in the present time.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.17.1":["And after six days Jesus took Peter and James and John his brother. Why Peter and James and John are separated from the rest in certain places of the Gospels, or what privilege they have beyond the other apostles, we have often said. Now it is asked how he took them after six days and led them up a high mountain apart, when Luke the evangelist puts the number eight. But the answer is easy: here the middle days are given; there the first and the last are added. For it is not said: After eight days Jesus takes Peter and James and John, but on the eighth day.\n— Commentary on Matthew","And he leads them up a high mountain apart. To lead the disciples up to the mountains is part of the kingdom. They are led apart because many are called, few chosen.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.17.2":["And he was transfigured before them. Such as he is to be at the time of judging, such he appeared to the apostles. But as to his saying: he was transfigured before them, let no one think that he lost his former form and face, or gave up the reality of his body and took on a body either spiritual or airy; but how he was transformed the evangelist shows, saying:\n\nAnd his face shone like the sun, and his garments became white as snow. Where the splendor of the face is shown and the whiteness of the garments is described, the substance is not taken away, but the glory is changed. His face shone like the sun. Certainly the Lord was transformed into that glory with which he is to come afterward in his kingdom. The transformation added splendor; it did not take away the face. Grant that the body was spiritual: were the garments also changed, which were so white that another evangelist said: Such as no fuller on earth can make? But what a fuller on earth can make is bodily and subject to touch, not spiritual and airy, a thing that deceives the eyes and is seen only as a phantasm.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.17.3":["And behold, there appeared to them Moses and Elijah, talking with him. When the scribes and Pharisees were testing him and demanding signs from heaven, he would not give them, but refuted the perverse demand with a prudent answer. Here, however, to increase the faith of the apostles, he gives a sign from heaven, Elijah coming down from the place to which he had ascended, and Moses rising from the underworld; as Ahaz too is instructed through Isaiah to ask for himself a sign from on high or from hell. For as to what was said: There appeared to them Moses and Elijah, talking with him, it is also reported in another Gospel that they told him what he was to suffer at Jerusalem. The Law and the Prophets are shown, which in frequent utterances announced both the passion and the resurrection of the Lord.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.17.4":["But Peter answered and said to Jesus: Lord, it is good for us to be here. Because he had climbed up to the mountains, he does not want to go down to earthly things, but to remain always on the heights.\n— Commentary on Matthew","If you wish, I will make here three tabernacles, one for you, and one for Moses, and one for Elijah. You err, Peter, as another evangelist also testifies: you do not know what you are saying. Do not seek three tabernacles, when there is one tabernacle of the Gospel, in which the Law and the Prophets are to be summed up. But if you seek three tabernacles, by no means compare the servants with the Lord. Rather, make three tabernacles, or rather one, for the Father and the Son and the Holy Spirit, so that those whose divinity is one may also have one tabernacle in your breast.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.17.5":["While he was still speaking, behold, a bright cloud overshadowed them, and a voice from the cloud, saying: This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased; hear him. Because he had asked imprudently, he therefore does not merit the Lord's answer, but the Father answered for the Son, so that the word of the Lord might be fulfilled: I do not bear witness for myself, but the Father who sent me, he bears witness for me. And the cloud is seen to be bright and overshadows them, so that those who were seeking a fleshly tabernacle of boughs or tents might be covered by the shelter of the bright cloud. The voice too of the Father speaking from heaven is heard, to bear witness to the Son and, removing Peter's error, to teach him the truth, or rather, through Peter, the other apostles. This is, he says, my beloved Son: for him a tabernacle is to be made, him you must obey; this is my Son; they are servants; Moses and Elijah ought themselves, with you, to prepare a tabernacle for the Lord in the inner recesses of their heart.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.17.6":["And the disciples, hearing it, fell on their face and were exceedingly afraid. They are struck with dread for a threefold cause: either because they had recognized that they had erred, or because the bright cloud had covered them, or because they had heard the voice of God the Father speaking; human frailty does not endure to bear the sight of a greater glory, and, trembling in its whole soul and body, it falls to the earth. The more anyone seeks greater things, the more he collapses to the lower, if he is ignorant of his own measure.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.17.7":["And Jesus came and touched them. Because they lay there and could not rise, he himself mercifully approaches and touches them, so that by his touch he may put their fear to flight and their weakened limbs may be made firm.\n— Commentary on Matthew","And he said to them: Rise, and do not be afraid. Those whom he had healed with his hand he heals with his command. Do not be afraid. First fear is driven out, so that afterward teaching may be imparted.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.17.8":["And lifting up their eyes they saw no one but Jesus alone. Reasonably, after they had risen they saw no one but Jesus alone, lest, if Moses and Elijah had remained with the Lord, the Father's voice might seem uncertain as to whom above all it bore witness. They see, then, Jesus standing, the cloud taken away, and Moses and Elijah vanished; because after the shadow of the Law and the Prophets, which had covered the apostles with its veil, has departed, both are found in the Gospel.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.17.9":["And as they were coming down from the mountain, Jesus commanded them, saying: Tell the vision to no one until the Son of Man rises from the dead. A foretaste of the kingdom to come and the glory of the one triumphing had been shown on the mountain. He does not wish, therefore, that this be preached among the peoples, lest it both be incredible because of the greatness of the thing and, after such great glory, the cross that followed should cause a stumbling block among untaught minds.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.17.10":["And the disciples asked him, saying: Why then do the scribes say that Elijah must come first? Unless we know the causes why the disciples asked about the name of Elijah, their question seems foolish and out of place. For what has it to do with the things written above, to ask about the coming of Elijah? It is a tradition of the Pharisees, according to the prophet Malachi, who is the last of the twelve, that Elijah will come before the coming of the Savior and turn back the heart of the fathers to the sons and of the sons to the fathers, and restore all things to their ancient state. The disciples therefore suppose that this transformation of glory is the one they had seen on the mountain, and they say: If you have already come in glory, how is it that your forerunner does not appear? especially since they had seen Elijah withdraw. But when they add: The scribes say that Elijah must come first, by saying first they show that, unless Elijah has come, it is not the coming of the Savior according to the Scriptures.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.17.11-12":["But he answered and said to them: Elijah indeed is to come and will restore all things; but I say to you that Elijah has already come, and the rest. He who is to come at the second coming of the Savior in the truth of the body now comes through John in power and spirit; but they did not know him and did to him whatever they wished, that is, they despised and beheaded him.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.17.12":["So also the Son of Man is to suffer by them. Now it is asked, since Herod and Herodias killed John, how they themselves are said to have crucified Jesus as well, when we read that he was killed by the scribes and Pharisees; and the short answer is that both in John's murder the faction of the Pharisees consented, and in the slaying of the Lord Herod joined his will, he who, after mocking and despising him, sent him back to Pilate to crucify him.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.17.15":["Lord, have mercy on my son, for he is a lunatic and suffers badly; for he often falls into the fire, and frequently into the water; and I brought him to your disciples, and they could not cure him. For what reason a demon, watching the course of the moon, seizes men and strives through the creatures to defame the Creator, we have said above. But it seems to me that according to tropology the lunatic is he who is changed toward vices from moment to moment of the hours and does not persist in what he has begun, but waxes and wanes, and is now carried into the fire with which the hearts of adulterers are kindled, now into the waters, which cannot extinguish charity.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.17.16":["But as to his saying: I brought him to your disciples, and they could not cure him, he covertly accuses the apostles, although the impossibility of curing is sometimes to be referred not to the weakness of those who cure but to the faith of those who are to be cured, as the Lord says: Be it done to you according to your faith.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.17.17":["Jesus answered and said: O unbelieving and perverse generation, how long shall I be with you? How long shall I bear with you? Not that he was overcome by weariness, and that he who is meek and gentle, who like a lamb before its shearer did not open his mouth, broke out into words of fury; but that, in the likeness of a physician who sees a sick man conducting himself against his orders, he might say: How long shall I come to your house, how long shall I waste the diligence of my art, when I order one thing and you do another? So little, moreover, is he angry with the man rather than with the vice, and through one man he convicts the Jews of unbelief, that he immediately added: Bring him here to me.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.17.18":["And Jesus rebuked him, and the demon went out of him. Not he who was suffering but the demon ought to have been rebuked. Or else he rebuked the boy, and the demon went out of him, because he had been oppressed by the demon on account of his own sins.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.17.19-20":["And they said: Why could we not cast him out? He said to them: Because of your unbelief. This is what he says in another place: Whatever you ask in my name, believing, you will receive. So whenever we do not receive, it is not the inability of the giver but the fault of those who pray.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.17.20":["And they give as an example in the Gospel the lunatic, whom the apostles had not been able to heal; they ask why they could not heal him, and they hear: Amen I say to you, if you have faith like a grain of mustard seed, you will say to this mountain: Pass from here, and it will pass; and nothing will be impossible to you. But this kind is not cast out except by prayer and fasting. There the mountain is plainly taken to mean the devil.\n— Commentary on Zechariah","If you have faith like a grain of mustard seed, you will say to this mountain: Move from here, and it will move. Some think that faith compared to a grain of mustard seed is said to be small, though the kingdom of heaven is likened to a grain of mustard seed; and since the Apostle says: And if I have all faith, so that I move mountains, great, then, is the faith that is made equal to a grain of mustard seed. The moving of a mountain signifies not that one which we see with the eyes of the flesh, but the one which had been moved by the Lord out of the lunatic. For what does he say? You will say to this mountain: Move from here, and it will move. By this those are to be convicted of folly who contend that the apostles and all believers did not have even a small faith, because none of them moved mountains. For the moving of a mountain from one place to another, and a vain display of signs to be sought, does not profit so much as the moving, for the benefit of all, of that mountain which, through the prophet, is said to corrupt the whole earth.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.17.21":["But this kind is not cast out except through prayer and fasting. While he teaches how a most wicked demon can be expelled, he instructs all for life.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.17.22-23":["And while they were staying in Galilee, Jesus said to them: The Son of Man is to be betrayed into the hands of men, and they will kill him, and on the third day he will rise again. And they were exceedingly saddened. He always mingles sadness with prosperous things, so that when these come suddenly they may not terrify the apostles, but may be borne by minds that have prepared for them beforehand. For if it saddens them that he is to be killed, what is said ought to gladden them: on the third day he will rise again. Moreover, that they are saddened, and saddened exceedingly, does not come from unbelief, for otherwise they also knew that Peter had been rebuked because he did not savor the things that are of God but those of men; but because, out of love for their master, they cannot bear to hear anything adverse and lowly about him.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.17.24":["And when they had come to Capernaum, those who collected the didrachma came to Peter and said: Does your master not pay the didrachma? He said to them: Yes. After Augustus Caesar, Judea was made tributary, and all were registered by a head tax. Hence Joseph too, with Mary his kinswoman, made his declaration in Bethlehem. Again, since he had been brought up in Nazareth, which is a town of Galilee lying near the city of Capernaum, tribute is demanded of him; and because of the greatness of his signs those who were collecting it do not dare to ask it of him himself, but approach the disciple; or else they ask maliciously whether he pays tribute or opposes the will of Caesar, according to what we also read in another place: Is it lawful to pay tribute to Caesar or not?\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.17.25-26":["What do you think, Simon? The kings of the earth, from whom do they take tribute or tax, from their own sons or from strangers? And he said: From strangers. Jesus said to him: Then the sons are free. Our Lord was the son of a king both according to the flesh and according to the spirit, either as begotten of the stock of David or as the Word of the almighty Father. Therefore, as the son of kings, he did not owe tribute; but he who had taken on the humility of the flesh had to fulfill all justice. And we unhappy ones, who are reckoned by the name of Christ and do nothing worthy of so great a majesty: he for our sake both endured the cross and paid tribute; we, for his honor, do not pay tribute, and as though sons of the king we are exempt from taxes.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.17.25":["And when he had entered the house, Jesus forestalled him, saying. Those who collected the didrachma had approached Peter apart; and when he had entered the house, before Peter can bring it up, the Lord asks, lest the disciples be scandalized at the demand for the tribute, when they see that he knows what was done in his absence.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.17.27":["Go to the sea and cast a hook, and take the fish that comes up first, and when you have opened its mouth you will find a stater; take it and give it to them for me and for you. What I should wonder at first in this place I do not know, whether the foreknowledge or the greatness of the Savior: the foreknowledge, in that he knew the fish had a stater in its mouth and that it would be the first to be caught; the greatness and power, if at his word the stater was at once created in the fish's mouth, and he himself made what was to be by speaking it. But it seems to me, according to the mystical understandings, that this fish which was caught first is the one which was in the depth of the sea and dwelt in the salt and bitter whirlpools, so that through the second Adam the first Adam might be freed, and that which had been found in his mouth, that is, in confession, might be paid for Peter and the Lord. And beautifully the very same price is given, but it is divided, because for Peter, as for a sinner, a price was being paid, whereas our Lord had committed no sin, nor was deceit found in his mouth. That is called a stater which holds two didrachmas, so that the likeness of the flesh might be shown, when both lord and servant are freed by the same price. But understood simply too it edifies the hearer, since the Lord was of such poverty that he did not have the means to pay the tribute for himself and the apostle. But if anyone wishes to object: And how did Judas carry money in his purse? we shall answer: he thought it wrong to turn the property of the poor to his own uses, and he gave us the same example.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.18.1":["At that hour the disciples came to Jesus, saying: Who, do you think, is greater in the kingdom of heaven? What I have often advised must be observed now too: the causes of each of the Lord's sayings and deeds are to be sought. After the stater was found, after the tribute was paid, what does this sudden question of the apostles mean? At that hour the disciples came to Jesus, saying: Who, do you think, is greater in the kingdom of heaven? Because they had seen the same tribute paid for Peter and for the Lord, they judged from the equality of the price that Peter had been preferred to all the apostles, since in the payment of the tribute he had been put on a level with the Lord; therefore they ask who is greater in the kingdom of heaven. And Jesus, seeing their thoughts and understanding the causes of their error, wishes to heal the desire for glory by a contest of humility.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.18.2":["And Jesus, calling a little child, set him in their midst. Either simply any little child, so that he might seek out that age and show the likeness of innocence; or else he set himself in their midst as the little child, he who had come not to be served but to serve, so that he might give them an example of humility. Others interpret the little child as the Holy Spirit, whom he placed in the hearts of the disciples so that they might exchange pride for humility.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.18.3":["Amen I say to you: Unless you are turned and become like little children, you will not enter the kingdom of heaven. The apostles are not commanded to have the age of little children but their innocence, and that what those possess by their years, these should possess by effort, so that they may be little children in malice, not in wisdom.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.18.4":["Whoever therefore humbles himself like this little child, he is greater in the kingdom of heaven. Just as this little child, whose example I give you, does not persist in anger, does not remember when he has been hurt, is not delighted at the sight of a beautiful woman, does not think one thing and say another, so you too, unless you have such innocence and purity of mind, will not be able to enter the kingdom of heaven. Or alternatively: Whoever humbles himself like this little child, he is greater in the kingdom of heaven, that is, whoever imitates me and humbles himself after my example, so as to cast himself down as far as I cast myself down when I took the form of a servant, he will enter the kingdom of heaven.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.18.5":["And whoever receives one such little child in my name receives me. Whoever is such that he imitates the humility and innocence of Christ, in him Christ is received. And prudently, lest the apostles, when honor has been paid to them, should think themselves the ones honored, he added that they are to be received not for their own merit but in honor of their master.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.18.6":["But whoever causes one of these little ones to stumble. Note that the one who is scandalized is a little child; for the greater do not admit stumbling blocks.\n— Commentary on Matthew","It is better for him that an ass's millstone be hung on his neck and that he be sunk in the depth of the sea. Although this may be a general sentence against all who cause anyone to stumble, yet according to the sequence of the discourse it can also be understood as spoken against the apostles, who, by asking who was greater in the kingdom of heaven, seemed to be contending among themselves about rank, and, had they persisted in this fault, could have destroyed through their own stumbling block those whom they were calling to the faith, when these saw the apostles fighting among themselves over honor. But when he said: It is better for him that an ass's millstone be hung on his neck, he speaks according to the custom of the province, by which this was the penalty for the greater crimes among the Jews of old, that they were sunk in the deep with a stone tied to them. And it is better for him, because it is much better to receive a brief punishment for a fault than to be kept for eternal torments. For the Lord will not punish twice for the same thing.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.18.7":["Woe to the world because of stumbling blocks. It is necessary that stumbling blocks come, but woe to the man through whom the stumbling block comes. Not that it is necessary for stumbling blocks to come (otherwise those who cause the stumbling block would be without fault), but, since it is necessary that stumbling blocks occur in this world, each one lies open to stumbling blocks by his own fault. And at the same time, by a general sentence, Judas is struck, who had prepared his mind for the betrayal.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.18.8-9":["But if your hand or your foot causes you to stumble, cut it off and throw it from you, and the rest. It is indeed necessary that stumbling blocks come, yet woe to that man who, by his own fault, brings it about that what must necessarily happen in the world happens through him. Therefore every affection is cut off and all kinship is amputated, lest under the pretext of dutiful affection each of the believers lie open to stumbling blocks. If, he says, there is someone so joined to you as hand, foot, eye, and he is useful and attentive and sharp at seeing, but he makes a stumbling block for you and through the discord of his ways drags you into Gehenna, it is better for you to go without his kinship and his fleshly advantages than, while you wish to gain your relatives and close friends, to have a cause of ruin. So let not brother, not wife, not children, not friends, not any affection that can shut us out from the kingdom of heaven be preferred to the love of the Lord. Each of the believers knows what harms him, or in what his mind is solicited and often tempted. It is better to lead a solitary life than to lose eternal life for the necessities of the present life.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.18.10":["See that you do not despise one of these little ones. For I tell you that their angels always see the face of my Father who is in heaven. Above he had said that through the hand and the foot and the eye all kinships and close ties that could cause a stumbling block were to be amputated; so he tempers the severity of the sentence by the precept that follows, saying: See that you do not despise one of these little ones. I so enjoin severity, he says, as to teach clemency mingled with it. As far as lies in you, do not despise them, but through your own salvation seek their healing as well. But if you see them persevering in sins and enslaved to vices, it is better to be saved alone than to perish with many. For their angels in heaven always see the face of the Father. Great is the dignity of souls, that each one has, from the moment of its birth, an angel assigned to its keeping. Hence we read in the Apocalypse of John: To the angel of Ephesus, of Thyatira, and to the angel of Philadelphia, and to the angels of the four remaining churches, write these things. The Apostle too commanded that the heads of women be veiled in the churches because of the angels.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.18.12":["What do you think? If someone has a hundred sheep, and one of them goes astray, does he not leave the ninety-nine on the mountains and go to seek the one that went astray? Following on, he who had said before: See that you do not despise one of these little ones, calls us to clemency, and adds the parable of the ninety-nine sheep left on the mountains and of the one that strayed, which the good shepherd, because it could not walk for its great weakness, carried back on his shoulders to the rest of the flock. Some think that this is the shepherd who, though he was in the form of God, did not think it robbery to be equal with God, but emptied himself, taking the form of a servant, becoming obedient to the Father unto death, even the death of the cross, and that for this reason he came down to earthly things, to save the one little sheep that had perished, that is, the human race. Others, however, think that in the ninety-nine sheep the number of the righteous is understood, and in the one little sheep that of sinners, according to what he said in another place: I did not come to call the righteous but sinners; for those who are well have no need of a physician, but those who are ill. This parable is written in the Gospel according to Luke together with two other parables, of the ten drachmas and of the two sons.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.18.14":["So it is not the will before your Father who is in heaven that one of these little ones should perish. He refers back to the proposition above, about which he had said: See that you do not despise one of these little ones, and teaches that the parable was set down for this reason, that the little ones should not be despised. And in saying: It is not the will before your Father that one of these little ones should perish, it is shown that whenever one of the little ones has perished, he has not perished by the will of the Father.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.18.15-16":["But if your brother sins against you, go and rebuke him between yourself and him, and the rest. If our brother sins against us and has injured us in any matter, we have the power to forgive, nay rather the necessity, since we are commanded to forgive our debtors their debts; but if anyone sins against God, it is not within our discretion. For the divine Scripture says: If a man sins against a man, the priest will pray for him; but if he sins against God, who will pray for him? We, on the contrary, are kindly when the injury is done to God, and over insults to ourselves we give rein to hatreds. A brother is to be rebuked privately, lest, if he has once lost his shame and modesty, he remain in sin; and if indeed he listens, we gain his soul, and through the salvation of another, salvation is gained for us too; but if he will not listen, let a brother be brought in; and if he does not listen to him either, let a third be brought in as well, either with the aim of correcting him or of summoning him before witnesses.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.18.17":["Further, if he will not listen even to them, then it must be told to many, so that they may hold him in detestation, and he who could not be saved by shame may be saved by reproaches. But when it is said: Let him be to you as a heathen and a tax collector, it is shown that he who under the name of a believer does the works of unbelievers deserves greater detestation than those who are openly Gentiles. For according to tropology those are called tax collectors who pursue the gains of the world and exact tolls through trade and frauds and thefts, crimes and perjuries.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.18.18":["Amen I say to you: Whatever you bind on earth shall be bound also in heaven, and whatever you loose on earth shall be loosed also in heaven. Because he had said: But if he does not listen to the Church, let him be to you as a heathen and a tax collector, and the contemptuous brother's hidden reply or silent thought could be this: If you despise me, I too despise you; if you condemn me, you too will be condemned by my sentence, he gave power to the apostles, so that those who are condemned by such men may know that the human sentence is strengthened by the divine sentence, and that whatever has been bound on earth is bound equally in heaven.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.18.19-20":["Again I say to you that if two of you agree on earth about anything whatever that they ask, it will be done for them by my Father who is in heaven. For where two or three are gathered in my name, there I am in their midst. All the discourse above had called us to concord. Therefore he also promises a reward, so that we may hasten more eagerly to peace, since he says that he will be in the midst between two and three, after the example of the tyrant who wished to test two friends he had taken captive in this way, holding the one and letting the other go, when one had gone back to visit his mother and had given his friend as surety for himself; and when he returned on the appointed day, the tyrant, admiring the good faith of both, asked them to take him as a third. We can also understand this spiritually: that where spirit and soul and body agree and do not have among themselves a war of divergent wills, the flesh lusting against the spirit and the spirit against the flesh, they obtain from the Father whatever they ask; and no one can doubt that the request is for good things where the body wishes to have what the spirit wishes.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.18.21-22":["Then Peter, coming to him, said: Lord, how often shall my brother sin against me and I forgive him? Up to seven times? and the rest. The Lord's discourse holds together and, like a threefold cord, cannot be broken. Above he had said: See that you do not despise one of these little ones; and he had added: If your brother sins against you, go and rebuke him between yourself and him; and he had promised a reward, saying: If two of you agree on earth, about anything they will obtain what they ask, and I will be in their midst. Roused by this, the apostle Peter asks how often he ought to forgive a brother who sins against him, and with his question he puts forward a verdict: up to seven times, to whom Jesus answered: Not up to seven times but seventy times seven, that is, four hundred and ninety times, so that he should forgive a brother who sins as many times in a day as the brother could not sin.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.18.23-35":["Therefore the kingdom of heaven is likened to a man, a king, who wished to settle accounts with his servants. It is customary for the Syrians, and especially for the Palestinians, to join parables to all their speech, so that what cannot be held by the hearers through a simple precept may be held through a likeness and examples. So he commanded Peter, under the comparison of the king and Lord and of the servant who, owing ten thousand talents, had obtained pardon by asking it of the Lord, that he too should forgive his fellow servants who sin less. For if that king and Lord so easily forgave the servant who owed ten thousand talents, how much more ought servants to forgive their fellow servants lesser things? To make this clearer, let us put it by an example: if any of us has committed adultery, murder, sacrilege, greater crimes worth ten thousand talents, they are forgiven to those who ask, if they too forgive those who sin less; but if we are implacable because of an insult done to us, and keep up lasting quarrels because of a rather bitter word, do we not see that we are rightly to be thrown into prison, and that by the example of our own deed we bring it about that pardon for our greater offenses is not granted us?\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.18.24-25":["There was brought to him one who owed ten thousand talents. I know that some interpret this man who owed ten thousand talents as the devil; by his wife and children, who are to be sold while he persists in malice, they would have folly and evil thoughts understood; for just as the wife of the righteous man is said to be wisdom, so, they say, the wife of the unrighteous man and sinner is called folly. But how the Lord should forgive him ten thousand talents and he not forgive us, his fellow servants, a hundred denarii, is neither the interpretation of the Church nor one to be accepted by prudent men.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.18.35":["So also my heavenly Father will do to you, if you do not forgive, each of you, his brother from your hearts. A fearful sentence, if the sentence of God is bent and changed according to our mind. If we do not forgive our brothers small things, great things will not be forgiven us by God. And because anyone can say: I have nothing against him, he knows his own affair, he has God for his judge, it is no concern of mine, whatever he wishes to do, I have forgiven him, he confirms his sentence and overturns every pretense of feigned peace, saying: If you do not forgive, each of you, his brother from your hearts.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.19.3":["And the Pharisees came to him, testing him and saying: Is it lawful for a man to put away his wife for any cause? He had come from Galilee to Judea; for that reason the faction of the Pharisees and scribes asks him whether it is lawful for a man to put away his wife for whatever cause, so as to hold him as in a horned syllogism, and whatever he answered would lie open to capture: if he said that a wife is to be put away for any cause whatever and others married, the preacher of purity would seem to teach things contrary to himself; but if he answered that she ought not to be put away for every cause, he would be held as guilty of sacrilege, as acting against the teaching of Moses, and through Moses, of God. So the Lord tempers his answer in such a way as to pass by the snare, bringing in holy Scripture as testimony and opposing the natural law and God's first sentence to the second, which was granted not by the will of God but by the necessity of sinners.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.19.4":["Have you not read that he who made them from the beginning made them male and female? This is written at the beginning of Genesis. But by saying male and female, he shows that second marriages are to be avoided. For he does not say: male and females, which is what was sought by the repudiation of the former wives, but male and female, so that the partnership of a single marriage might be bound together.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.19.5":["For this reason a man leaves father and mother and will cleave to his wife. Likewise he says: will cleave to his wife, not to wives.\n— Commentary on Matthew","And the two shall be in one flesh. The reward of marriage is that from two there comes to be one flesh. Chastity joined to the spirit becomes one spirit.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.19.6":["What therefore God has joined, let man not separate. God has joined them by making one flesh of man and woman; this man cannot separate, unless perhaps God alone. Man separates when, out of desire for a second wife, we put away the first; God, who also had joined, separates when by consent, for the service of God, because the time is short, we so have wives as though we did not have them.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.19.7":["They say to him: Why then did Moses command to give a bill of divorce and to put her away? They lay open the false charge they had prepared. And yet the Lord had not put forward an opinion of his own, but had recalled the ancient history and the commandments of God.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.19.8":["He says to them: Because Moses, in view of the hardness of your heart, permitted you to put away your wives; but from the beginning it was not so. What he says is of this kind: Can God be contrary to himself, so as to have commanded one thing before and then break his own sentence by a new command? That is not to be thought; but Moses, when he saw that, because of the desire for second wives who were richer or younger or more beautiful, the first wives were being killed or leading a wretched life, preferred to allow discord rather than that hatreds and murders should go on. And at the same time consider that he did not say: God permitted you because of the hardness of your heart, but Moses, so that, according to the Apostle, it is the counsel of a man, not the command of God.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.19.9":["But I say to you that whoever puts away his wife, except for fornication, and marries another commits adultery; and he who marries her who was put away commits adultery. Fornication alone is what overcomes the affection owed to a wife; indeed, when she has divided the one flesh into another and by fornication has separated herself from her husband, she ought not to be kept, lest she bring her husband too under a curse, as Scripture says: He who keeps an adulteress is foolish and impious. Wherever, therefore, there is fornication and the suspicion of fornication, the wife is freely put away. And because it could happen that someone might bring a false charge against an innocent woman and, for the sake of a second marriage union, fasten a crime on the former wife, he is commanded to put away the first wife on these terms: that he not have a second while the first is living. For what he says is this: If you put away your wife not out of lust but because of an injury, why, having experienced an unhappy first marriage, do you thrust yourself into the danger of a new one? Moreover, because it could happen that by the same law the wife too might give her husband a bill of divorce, the same caution is enjoined, that she not take a second husband. And because a harlot, and one who had once been an adulteress, did not fear disgrace, it is enjoined on the second husband that if he marries such a woman he is under the charge of adultery.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.19.10":["The disciples say to him: If such is the case of a man with his wife, it is not expedient to marry. Heavy is the burden of wives, if it is not lawful to put them away except for the cause of fornication. For what if she is a drunkard, if irascible, if of bad morals, if wanton, if gluttonous, if a gadabout, if quarrelsome and abusive, must such a one be kept? Willing or unwilling, she must be borne. For when we were free, we subjected ourselves to servitude of our own will. The apostles, then, seeing the heavy yoke of wives, utter the feeling of their mind and say: If such is the case of a man with his wife, it is not expedient to marry.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.19.11":["He said: Not all accept this word, but those to whom it is given. Let no one think that by this word either fate or fortune is brought in, as if those are virgins to whom it has been given by God, or whom some chance has brought to it; rather, it is given to those who have asked, who have willed, who have labored to receive it. For to everyone who asks it will be given, and he who seeks will find, and to him who knocks it will be opened.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.19.12":["Therefore by eunuchs are by no means to be understood those whom the fiery poet describes, saying (Lucan, Book 10): And the unhappy youth too, maimed by the steel, and cut away from manhood........ But those of whom the Lord speaks in the Gospel: Who have castrated themselves for the kingdom of heaven (Matt 19:12); such were the Apostles too, to whom, when they wondered and said because of the difficulty of the thing, Who then can be saved?, the Savior answered: He who can take it, let him take it. Hence the Apostle too testifies that concerning eunuchs of this kind, that is, concerning virgins, he has no commandment of the Lord, but gives counsel as one who has obtained mercy from the Lord, wishing all to be as himself: For the time, he says, is short: it remains that those who have wives be as though they had none (1 Cor 7:29). For he who has been called free from the bondage and duty of marriage is truly the slave of Christ.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","For there are eunuchs who were born so from their mother's womb, and there are eunuchs who were made so by men, and there are eunuchs who castrated themselves for the kingdom of heaven. He who can accept it, let him accept it. There is a threefold kind of eunuchs, two carnal and the third spiritual. Some are those who are born so from their mother's womb; others, those whom captivity makes, or the luxuries of matrons. The third are those who castrated themselves for the kingdom of heaven, who, though they could be men, become eunuchs for Christ. To these the reward is promised; but to the former, for whom chastity is a necessity and not a choice, nothing at all is owed. We can also put it another way: those are eunuchs from their mother's womb who are of a colder nature and do not seek after lust; and others are those made by men, whom either the philosophers make, or who are softened into women for the worship of idols, or who, by heretical persuasion, feign chastity so as to counterfeit the truth of religion. But none of them attains the kingdom of heaven except him who has castrated himself for Christ. Hence he adds: He who can accept it, let him accept it, so that each may consider his own strength, whether he can fulfill the precepts of virginity and purity. For chastity in itself is alluring and draws anyone to itself. But one's strength must be considered, so that he who can accept it, let him accept it. It is the voice of the Lord as of one exhorting and urging his soldiers on to the reward of purity: He who can accept it, let him accept it; he who can fight, let him fight, conquer and triumph.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.19.13":["Then little children were brought to him, that he might lay his hands on them and pray; but the disciples rebuked them. Not that they were unwilling for them to be blessed by both the hand and the voice of the Savior, but because, not yet having the fullest faith, they thought that he, after the manner of men, was being wearied by the importunity of those who brought them.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.19.14":["Let the little children be, and do not forbid them to come to me; for of such is the kingdom of heaven. He said significantly of such, not of these, to show that it is not age that reigns but character, and that the reward is promised to those who have a like innocence and simplicity, the Apostle too agreeing with the same sentence: Brothers, do not become children in your understanding, but be little children in malice; but in understanding, be perfect.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.19.16":["Behold, one came up and said to him: Good Teacher, what good shall I do that I may have eternal life? This man who asks how he may attain eternal life is young and rich and proud, and, according to another evangelist, he asks not with the desire of a learner but as one testing.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.19.17-18":["If you wish to enter into life, keep the commandments. He says to him: Which? And Jesus said: You shall not commit murder, you shall not commit adultery, and the rest, and you shall love your neighbor as yourself. That this young man is a tempter we can prove from this too, that when the Lord said to him: If you wish to come to life, keep the commandments, he again asks deceitfully which those commandments are, as if he had not read them himself, or as if the Lord could command things contrary to God.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.19.17":["Why do you ask me about the good? One is good, God. Because he had called him a good teacher and had not confessed him God or the Son of God, he learns that a man, however holy, is not good in comparison with God, of whom it is said: Confess to the Lord, for he is good. But lest anyone think that, in God's being called good, the Son of God is excluded from goodness, let us read in another place: The good shepherd lays down his life for his sheep; and in the prophet, a good spirit and a good land. So the Savior too did not refuse the testimony to his goodness, but excluded the error of a teacher apart from God.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.19.20":["The young man says to him: All these I have kept; what do I still lack? The young man lies. For if he had fulfilled in deed what is set down in the commandments: You shall love your neighbor as yourself, how is it that afterward, on hearing: Go, sell what you have and give to the poor, he went away sad, because he had many possessions?\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.19.21":["Jesus said to him: If you wish to be perfect, go, sell what you have and give to the poor, and you will have treasure in heaven; and come, follow me. It is in our power whether we wish to be perfect. Yet whoever wishes to be perfect ought to sell what he has, and not to sell in part, as Ananias and Sapphira did, but to sell all, and when he has sold it, to give everything to the poor, and so to prepare for himself a treasure in the kingdom of heaven. Nor is this enough for perfection unless, after despising riches, he follows the Savior, that is, leaving evil things behind, does good. For a purse is more easily despised than the will. Many who leave riches behind do not follow the Lord. But he follows the Lord who is his imitator and walks in his footsteps. For he who says he believes in Christ ought himself also to walk as he walked.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.19.22":["He went away sad, for he had many possessions. This is the sadness that leads to death, and the cause of the sadness is given: that he had many possessions, that is, thorns and thistles, which choked the Lord's seed.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.19.23":["And Jesus said to his disciples: Amen I say to you that a rich man will enter the kingdom of heaven with difficulty. And how did Abraham, Isaac and Jacob, rich men, enter the kingdom of heaven, and how are Matthew and Zacchaeus in the Gospel proclaimed by the Lord's testimony, once they had left their riches? But it must be considered that at the time they entered they had ceased to be rich. So long, then, as they are rich, they will not enter. And yet, because riches are despised with difficulty, he did not say: It is impossible for the rich to enter the kingdom of heaven, but with difficulty. Where difficulty is stated, impossibility is not alleged, but rarity is shown.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.19.24-26":["That rich man in the Gospel who, like a camel, carried the burdens of riches, and was a camel, would not listen to the Lord's counsels nor be unloaded, so that, the pack cast off, he might fly to heaven on the wings of a dove; therefore he went away sad. And of a camel of this kind the Savior speaks: It is easier for a camel to pass through the eye of a needle than for a rich man into the kingdom of heaven (Matt 19:24). He set forth the difficulty of the thing, not its impossibility. Indeed your mother Paula, of holy memory, and your brother Pammachius passed through the eye of a needle, that is, through the strait and narrow way that leads to life, into the kingdom of heaven, leaving behind with its burdens the broad way that leads to Tartarus. Nay, whatever they had they carried into the gifts of the Lord, fulfilling what is written: The ransom of a man's soul is his own riches (Prov 13:8). For the things that are impossible with men are possible with God (Matt 19).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"MAT.19.24":["And again I say to you: It is easier for a camel to pass through the eye of a needle than for a rich man to enter the kingdom of heaven. By this saying it is shown that it is not difficult but impossible. For if, as a camel cannot enter through the eye of a needle, so a rich man cannot enter the kingdom of heaven, none of the rich will be saved. But if we read in Isaiah how the camels of Midian and Ephah come to Jerusalem with gifts and offerings, and how those who were formerly bent and twisted by the perversity of vices enter the gates of Jerusalem, we shall see how these camels too, to which the rich are compared, when they have laid down the heavy burden of sins and the crookedness of the whole body, can enter by the narrow and strait way that leads to life.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.19.25-26":["But when the disciples asked, marveling at the severity of the saying: Who then will be saved? he tempered the severity of the sentence by his clemency, saying: What with men is impossible is possible with God.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.19.27":["Then Peter answered and said to him: Behold, we have left all things and have followed you; what then will there be for us? Great confidence! Peter was a fisherman, he had not been rich, he sought his food by hand and craft, and yet he speaks confidently: we have left all things. And because merely leaving is not enough, he joins what is perfect: and have followed you. We have done what you commanded; what reward, then, will you give us?\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.19.28":["And Jesus said to them: Amen I say to you that you who have followed me, in the regeneration, when the Son of Man sits on the seat of his majesty, will sit on twelve seats, judging the twelve tribes of Israel. He did not say: who have left all things, for this Crates the philosopher also did, and many others have despised riches; but who have followed me, which belongs properly to the apostles and to believers. In the regeneration, when the Son of Man sits on the seat of his majesty, when from the dead they rise incorrupt out of corruption, you too will sit on the thrones of judges, condemning the twelve tribes of Israel, because when you believed they refused to believe.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.19.29":["And everyone who has left house or brothers or sisters or father or mother or wife or children or fields for my name's sake will receive a hundredfold and will possess eternal life. But many who are first will be last, and the last first. This passage agrees with that sentence in which the Savior says: I came not to send peace but a sword; for I came to separate a man from his father, and the mother from the daughter, and the daughter-in-law from the mother-in-law; and a man's enemies are those of his own household. Those, therefore, who for the faith of Christ and the preaching of the Gospel have despised all affections, and riches, and the pleasures of the world, these will receive a hundredfold and will possess eternal life. On the occasion of this sentence some bring in a thousand years after the resurrection, saying that then a hundredfold of all the things we have given up, and eternal life, will be rendered to us, not understanding that, even if in other things the promise is fitting, in the case of wives the baseness is plain: that he who has given up one for the Lord should receive a hundred in the future. This, then, is the sense: He who has given up carnal things for the Savior will receive spiritual things, which in comparison and by their worth will be as if the number one hundred were compared with a small number. Hence the Apostle too, who had given up only one house and the small fields of one province, says: As having nothing and possessing all things.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.20.1-16":["The kingdom of heaven is like a householder who went out early in the morning to hire labourers for his vineyard. And having made an agreement with the labourers for a denarius a day, he sent them into his vineyard. This parable, or likeness of the kingdom of heaven, is understood from what has gone before it. For it is written before it: Many who are first will be last, and the last first, the Lord giving precedence not to time but to faith; and it says that the householder went out early in the morning to hire labourers for his vineyard and fixed a denarius as the price of the work. Then, having gone out about the third hour, he saw others standing idle in the marketplace and promised them by no means a denarius but what is just; at the sixth hour too and at the ninth he did likewise; and at the eleventh he found others standing who had been idle the whole day, and sent them into the vineyard; and when evening had come, he ordered his steward to begin paying from the last, that is, from the labourers of the eleventh hour down to the labourers of the first hour; and all alike, roused to envy against the last, charged the householder with injustice, not because they received less than had been agreed, but because they wanted to receive more than those upon whom the clemency of the hirer had poured itself out. It seems to me that the labourers of the first hour are Samuel and Jeremiah and John the Baptist, who can say with the psalmist: From my mother's womb you are my God. The labourers of the third hour are those who began to serve God from puberty; of the sixth hour, those who took up the yoke of Christ at a mature age; of the ninth, those who did so when already declining toward old age; and of the eleventh, those who did so in extreme old age; and yet all alike receive the reward, though the labour is different. There are those who explain this parable otherwise. They hold that at the first hour Adam and the rest of the patriarchs down to Noah were sent into the vineyard; at the third, Noah himself down to Abraham and the circumcision given to him; at the sixth, from Abraham down to Moses, when the law was given; at the ninth, Moses himself and the prophets; at the eleventh, the apostles and the people of the Gentiles, whom all envy. Hence, understanding this very thing, when it was already past the eleventh hour, near sunset and toward evening, John the evangelist says: My little children, it is the last hour. And consider at the same time that the injustice of the householder, which all alike accuse in the case of the labourers of the eleventh hour, they do not perceive in their own case. For if the householder is unjust, he is unjust not toward one but toward all, since the labourer of the third hour did not labour as much as he who was sent into the vineyard from the first hour; likewise the labourer of the sixth hour laboured less than the labourer of the third hour, and the labourer of the ninth less than the labourer of the sixth hour. So every earlier calling envies the Gentiles and is tormented at the grace of the Gospel. Hence the Savior too, concluding the parable: The first, he says, will be last, and the last first, because the Jews are turned from the head into the tail, and we are changed from the tail into the head.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.20.13":["Friend, I do you no wrong. I have read in someone's book that this friend who is rebuked by the householder, the labourer of the first hour, is to be understood as the first-formed man and those who believed in that time.\n— Commentary on Matthew","Did you not agree with me for a denarius? The denarius bears the figure of the king. So you have received the wage that I had promised you, that is, my image and likeness; what more do you seek? And you desire not so much to receive more yourself as that another should receive nothing, as though the merit of the reward were lessened by being shared with another.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.20.14":["Take what is yours and go. The Jew under the law is saved not by grace but by work. For he who does it shall live in it. Hence it is said to him:\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.20.15-16":["Or is your eye evil because I am good? The same thing is meant by that parable of Luke as well, where the elder son envies the younger and does not want him received back as a penitent, and accuses the father of injustice. And so that we may know that this is the sense we have stated, the title of this parable and its end agree: So, he says, the last will be first and the first last; for many are called but few are chosen.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.20.17-19":["And Jesus, going up to Jerusalem, took his twelve disciples and said to them: Behold, we are going up to Jerusalem, and the Son of Man will be handed over to the chiefs of the elders and to the scribes, and they will condemn him to death and hand him over to the Gentiles to be mocked and scourged and crucified, and on the third day he will rise again. He had often told the disciples this very thing, but because, with many matters discussed in between, what they had heard could slip from their memory, being about to go to Jerusalem and to take the apostles with him, he prepares them for the trial, so that when persecution and the ignominy of the cross come they may not be scandalized.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.20.20-21":["Then the mother of the sons of Zebedee came to him with her sons, worshipping him and asking something of him. He said to her: What do you want? She said to him: Say that these two sons of mine may sit, one at your right hand and one at your left, in your kingdom. Where does the mother of the sons of Zebedee get the notion of a kingdom, that, when the Lord had said: The Son of Man will be handed over to the chief priests and the scribes, and they will condemn him to death and hand him over to the Gentiles to be mocked and scourged and crucified, and had announced the ignominy of the passion to his fearful disciples, she should ask for the glory of one triumphing? For this reason, as I think: because after everything else the Lord had said: and on the third day he will rise again, the woman thought that he would reign immediately after the resurrection, and that what is promised for the second coming was to be fulfilled in the first; and with a woman's eagerness she longs for present things, unmindful of things to come. But that the Lord asks, and answered her request: What do you want? does not come from ignorance; rather it is said in the person of him who was to be scourged and crucified, just as also in the case of the woman with the flow of blood: Who touched me? and of Lazarus: Where have you laid him? and in the Old Testament too: Adam, where are you? And I will go down and see whether they are doing according to the cry that has come to me; and if it is not so, that I may know. The mother of the sons of Zebedee, then, makes her request out of a woman's error and the affection of love, not knowing what she was asking. Nor is it surprising if she is convicted of inexperience, when it is said of Peter, when he wants to make three tabernacles, not knowing what he was saying.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.20.22":["But Jesus answered and said: You do not know what you are asking. The mother makes the request, and the Lord speaks to the disciples, understanding that her prayers came down from the will of her sons.\n— Commentary on Matthew","Can you drink the cup that I am to drink? By the cup in the divine Scriptures we understand the passion: Father, if it is possible, let this cup pass from me; and in the psalm: What shall I render to the Lord for all that he has rendered to me? I will take the cup of salvation and call upon the name of the Lord; and at once he adds what this cup is: Precious in the sight of the Lord is the death of his saints.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.20.23":["He said to them: My cup you will indeed drink, but to sit at my right hand and at my left is not mine to give you, but to those for whom it has been prepared by my Father. It is asked how the sons of Zebedee, namely James and John, drank the cup of martyrdom, when Scripture relates that only James the apostle was beheaded by Herod, while John ended his life by a death of his own. But if we read the histories of the Church, in which it is reported that he too, for the sake of martyrdom, was put into a jar of boiling oil, and came out from there as an athlete of Christ to receive the crown, and was at once banished to the island of Patmos, we shall see that his spirit did not fall short of martyrdom, and that John drank the cup of confession, which the three young men in the furnace of fire also drank, although the persecutor did not shed their blood. But what he says: to sit at my right hand and at my left is not mine to give you, but to those for whom it has been prepared by my Father, is to be understood thus: The kingdom of heaven belongs not to the one who gives but to the one who receives; for there is no respect of persons with God, but whoever shows himself such that he becomes worthy of the kingdom of heaven, he will receive what has been prepared not for a person but for a life; if then you are such as to attain the kingdom of heaven which my Father has prepared for the triumphant and the victors, you too will receive it. Others want it to have been said of Moses and Elijah, whom a little before they had seen on the mountain talking with him, but to me it by no means seems so. For the names of those who sit in the kingdom of heaven are not stated for this reason: lest, if a few were named, the rest should be thought excluded.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.20.24":["And the ten, hearing it, were indignant at the two brothers. The ten apostles are not indignant with the mother of the sons of Zebedee, nor do they lay the boldness of the request to the woman, but to the sons, because, ignorant of their own measure, they had blazed up with immoderate desire; to them the Lord too had said: You do not know what you are asking. But it is understood, either from the Lord's answer or from the indignation of the apostles, that the sons had sent their mother in to ask for great things.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.20.25-27":["But Jesus called them to him and said: You know that the princes of the Gentiles lord it over them, and the rest. The humble and gentle master neither rebukes the two who asked for their immoderate desire nor reproves the other ten for indignation and envy, but sets forth such an example as to teach that he is greater who has been lesser, and that he becomes lord who is the servant of all. In vain therefore did those seek immoderate things, or do these grieve over the desire for greater things, since one comes to the summit of the virtues not by power but by humility. In short, he sets forth his own example, so that, if they held his words cheap, they might be put to shame by his deeds, and he says:\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.20.28":["Even as the Son of Man did not come to be served but to serve. Note what we have often said: that the one who serves is called the Son of Man. And to give his life as a redemption for many, when he took the form of a servant in order to shed his blood for the world. And he did not say to give his life as a redemption for all, but for many, that is, for those who would be willing to believe.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.20.29":["And as they were going out from Jericho, a great crowd followed him. And behold, two blind men sitting by the way heard that Jesus was passing by, and they cried out, saying: Lord, have mercy on us, son of David. But the crowd rebuked them, that they should be silent. But they cried out the more, saying: Lord, have mercy on us, son of David. There were many robbers in Jericho who were accustomed to kill and wound those going out and going down from Jerusalem; therefore the Lord came to Jericho with his disciples, to free the wounded and to draw a great crowd along with him. And indeed, when he wished to go out from Jericho, a great crowd followed him. If he had stayed in Jerusalem and had never come down to lowly places, the crowd would to this day be sitting in darkness and in the shadow of death.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.20.30":["But there were also two blind men by the way. He calls them blind who could not yet say: In your light we shall see light; by the way, because they seemed indeed to have knowledge of the law, but did not know the way, which is Christ. Most take them to be the Pharisees and the Sadducees, but others both peoples, of the Old Testament and of the New, because the one following the written law, the other the natural law, was blind without Christ. These, because they could not see by themselves, heard the proclamations of the Savior and confessed him the son of David.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.20.31":["But if both blind men are referred to the people of the Jews, what follows: the crowd rebuked them, is to be understood of the heathen, whom the Apostle warns not to boast and be proud against their root, but, since they themselves, through the error of the former, have been grafted from the wild olive into the good olive, they ought by no means to envy the salvation of the former.\n— Commentary on Matthew","Have mercy on us, son of David. They are rebuked by the crowds and nonetheless are not silent, but repeat the very same thing all the more often, to show a desire full of the true light.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.20.32":["Jesus stood still and called them and said. They were blind; they did not know where to go; they could not follow the Savior. There are many pits in Jericho, many crags and precipices sloping down into the depths; therefore the Lord stands still so that they may be able to come, and orders them to be called so that the crowds may not hinder them, and asks, as though not knowing, what they want, so that from the blind men's answer the infirmity may be made plain and the power may be recognized from the remedy.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.20.34":["And Jesus, having pity on them, touched their eyes, and at once they saw and followed him. He touches their eyes, and the craftsman supplies what nature had not given, or at any rate what infirmity had taken away; mercy bestows it, and at once they saw and followed him. Those who before sat huddled in Jericho and knew only how to cry out afterward follow Jesus not so much with their feet as with their virtues.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.21.1-2":["And when they drew near to Jerusalem and came to Bethphage, to the Mount of Olives, then Jesus sent two disciples, saying to them: Go into the village that is opposite you, and at once you will find an ass tied and a colt with her; loose them and bring them to me; and if anyone says anything to you, say: The Lord has need of them, and at once he will let them go. He goes out from Jericho, having led out from there very many crowds, and, having restored health to the blind, he draws near to Jerusalem, enriched with great wares; having restored salvation to those who believe, he desires to enter the city of peace and the place of the vision of God and the citadel of the watchman. And when he had drawn near to Jerusalem and had come to Bethphage, to the house of jaws (which was a little village of the priests and bore the type of confession and was situated on the Mount of Olives, where is the light of knowledge, where is rest from labors and sorrows), he sent his two disciples, θεωρητικὴν καὶ πρακτικήν, that is, knowledge and works, to enter the village, and he said to them: Go into the village that is opposite you. For it was opposite the apostles and would not accept the yoke of teachings. And at once, he says, you will find an ass tied and a colt with her; loose them and bring them to me. The ass was tied with many chains of sins; the colt too, frisky and impatient of the bridle, was with its mother; according to the Gospel of Luke it had many masters, being subject not to one error and doctrine; and yet the many masters who claimed for themselves an unlawful power, seeing that the true Lord had come and his servants who had been sent to loose them, do not dare to resist. But what the ass and the foal of the ass are, we say below.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.21.4-5":["The evangelists write that this prophecy was fulfilled when the Lord entered Jerusalem, sitting upon a she-ass and the foal of a she-ass, and a crowd of children with branches of palms came to meet him, crying: Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord, hosanna in the highest; and when the Pharisees rebuked him for not checking the shouting children, he answered: Have you not read: Out of the mouth of infants and sucklings you have perfected praise?\n— Commentary on Zechariah","Now this was done that what was spoken through the prophet might be fulfilled, who says: Say to the daughter of Zion: Behold, your king comes to you, meek, sitting upon an ass and a colt, the foal of a beast of burden. This is written in the prophet Zechariah, of whom it will be spoken more fully in its own place, if span of life is granted. For now we must touch briefly on this: that according to the letter, in the short stretch of the journey, he did not rest sitting upon both animals. For either he sat on the ass and the colt was without a rider, or, if he made use of the colt for sitting (which is more fitting), the ass was led along free. Therefore, since the history involves either impossibility or unseemliness, we are carried over to higher things, so that this ass, which was a beast of burden and tamed and had drawn the yoke of the law, may be understood as the synagogue, and the foal of the ass, frisky and free, as the people of the Gentiles, upon whom Jesus sat, his two disciples having been sent to them, one to the circumcision and the other to the Gentiles.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.21.6-7":["And the disciples went and did as Jesus had commanded them, and they brought the ass and the colt and put their garments upon them and made him sit on top. This colt and this ass, on which the apostles spread their garments so that Jesus may sit more softly, were naked before the coming of the Savior, and, with many claiming dominion over them, they shivered without covering; but after they had received the apostolic garments and had been made more beautiful, they had the Lord as their rider. Now the apostolic garment can be understood either as the teaching of the virtues or as the exposition of the Scriptures, or as the varieties of the doctrines of the Church; unless the soul has been instructed and adorned with these, it does not deserve to have the Lord as its rider.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.21.8":["And a very great crowd spread their garments on the road. See the difference of each person. The apostles put their garments upon the ass; the crowd, which is of lower rank, spreads them beneath the ass's feet, so that it may nowhere strike against a stone, may not tread on a thorn, may not slip into a pit.\n— Commentary on Matthew","And others cut branches from the trees and spread them on the road. From the fruit-bearing trees with which the Mount of Olives is planted they cut branches and spread them on the road, to make the crooked straight and to level the uneven, so that Christ, the conqueror of demons and of vices, might advance more straightly and more safely in the heart of believers.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.21.9":["And the crowds that went before and that followed cried out, saying: Hosanna to the son of David; blessed is he who is to come in the name of the Lord; hosanna in the highest. Since the history is plain, let us follow the spiritual order of discussion. The crowds that had gone out from Jericho and had followed the Savior and his disciples, after they saw the foal of the ass loosed, which before had been tied, and adorned with the garments of the apostles, and the Lord Jesus sitting upon it, laid their garments beneath and spread the road with branches of trees; and when they had done all this in deed, they also bestow the testimony of the voice, and those going before and those following resound not with a brief and silent confession but with the fullest shout: Hosanna to the son of David; blessed is he who is to come in the name of the Lord. And in saying: The crowds that went before and that followed, he shows that both peoples, those who believed in the Lord before the Gospel and those who believed after the Gospel, praise Jesus with the harmonious voice of confession, and that, after the example of the parable above, the workers of different hours receive the one reward of faith. Moreover, what the following words mean: Hosanna to the son of David, I remember that I said many years ago, in a short letter to Damasus, then bishop of the city of Rome, and now I will touch on it briefly. In Psalm 117 (118), which was plainly written about the coming of the Savior, among other things we read this too: The stone which the builders rejected, this has become the head of the corner; this was done by the Lord, this is wonderful in our eyes; this is the day which the Lord has made; let us exult and rejoice in it; and at once there is joined: O Lord, save me; O Lord, prosper well; blessed is he who is to come in the name of the Lord; we have blessed you from the house of the Lord, and the rest. Where the LXX translators have: O Lord, save me, in the Hebrew we read: Anna adonai osi anna, which Symmachus translated more plainly, saying: I beseech you, Lord, save me, I beseech you. Let no one, then, think that the word is composed of two words, namely a Greek and a Hebrew one, but that it is wholly Hebrew and signifies that the coming of Christ is the salvation of the world. Hence there also follows: Blessed is he who is to come in the name of the Lord, the Savior too confirming this very thing in the Gospel: I have come in the name of my Father, and you have not received me; another will come in his own name, and you will receive him. Further, by what is joined: Hosanna (that is, salvation) in the highest, it is clearly shown that the coming of Christ is the salvation not only of men but of the whole world, joining earthly things to heavenly, that every knee should bow to him, of those in heaven and on earth and in the lower world.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.21.10":["And when he had entered Jerusalem, the whole city was stirred, saying: Who is this? As Jesus enters with the crowd, the whole city of Jerusalem is stirred, marveling at the throng, not knowing the truth, and saying: Who is this? We read this in another place too, where the angels say: Who is this king of glory? But while others are either in doubt or asking, the common little crowd confesses, beginning from lesser things so as to arrive at greater, and says:\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.21.11":["This is Jesus, the prophet from Nazareth of Galilee. The prophet whom Moses too had said would come like himself, and who among the Greeks is properly written with the article; and from Nazareth of Galilee, because he had been brought up there, so that the flower of the field might be nourished in the flower of the virtues.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.21.12-13":["And Jesus entered the temple of God and cast out all who sold and bought in the temple, and he overturned the tables of the money-changers and the chairs of those who sold doves, and he says to them: It is written: My house shall be called a house of prayer, but you have made it a den of robbers. Accompanied by the crowd of believers, which had spread its garments so that the colt might go with unhurt foot, Jesus enters the temple and casts out all who sold and bought in the temple, and he overturned the tables of the money-changers and scattered the chairs of those who sold doves, and he said to them, bringing forward testimony from the holy Scriptures, that his Father's house ought to be a house of prayer, not a den of robbers or a house of trade, as is written in another evangelist. And this must first be known: that, in accordance with the commands of the law, since the temple of the Lord was the most august in the whole world, and the people of the Jews flocked there from almost all regions, countless victims were sacrificed, especially on feast days, of bulls, rams and goats, while the poor, so as not to be without a sacrifice, offered young doves and turtledoves; and it often happened that those who had come from far away did not have victims. So the priests devised how they might make plunder of the people, and they sold all the animals that were needed for the sacrifices, so that they might both sell to those who did not have them and themselves receive back again what had been bought. This scheme of theirs was frequently defeated by the poverty of those who came, who lacked the means and not only had no victims but had not even the wherewithal to buy birds and cheap little gifts. So they also set up money-changers to lend money on security; but because it was commanded in the law that no one should take interest, and money lent at interest could be of no use when it brought no profit and sometimes lost the principal, they devised yet another trick: in place of money-changers to make collybists, a word whose proper sense the Latin language does not express. Among them collyba is the name for what we call sweetmeats or cheap little gifts, for example roasted chickpeas and raisins and fruits of various kinds. So, because the collybists who had lent the money could not take interest, in place of interest they took various kinds of goods, so as to exact in these things, which are bought with coins, what was not lawful in coin; as if Ezekiel had not guarded against this very thing, saying: You shall not take interest and increase. The Lord, seeing such trade, or robbery, in his Father's house, roused by the ardor of the spirit, according to what is written in Psalm 68 (69): The zeal of your house has eaten me up, made himself a whip of cords and cast so great a multitude of men out of the temple, saying: It is written: My house shall be called a house of prayer, but you have made it a den of robbers. For he is a robber, and turns the temple of God into a den of robbers, who pursues gain from religion, and whose worship is not so much the worship of God as an occasion for trade. So much according to the history. But according to the mystical senses Jesus daily enters his Father's temple and casts out all, bishops and presbyters and deacons as well as laymen and the whole crowd, from his Church, and holds those who sell and those who buy alike guilty of one crime; for it is written: Freely you have received, freely give. He also overturns the tables of the money-changers. Note that because of the avarice of the priests the altars of God are called the tables of money-changers. And he overturns the chairs of those who sell doves, who sell the grace of the Holy Spirit and do everything to devour the peoples subject to them, of whom it is said: Who devour my people like food of bread. According to the simple understanding, the doves were not on chairs but in cages, unless perhaps the dealers in doves sat on chairs, which is utterly absurd, because in chairs it is rather the dignity of teachers that is indicated, a dignity that is brought to nothing when it has been mixed with gain. What we have said of the churches, let each one understand of himself as well. For the Apostle Paul says: You are the temple of God, and the Holy Spirit dwells in you. Let there be no trade in the house of our heart, no commerce of selling and buying, no greed for gifts, lest Jesus enter angry and stern and cleanse his temple in no other way than by applying the whip, so that out of a den of robbers and a house of trade he may make a house of prayer.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.21.14":["And the blind and the lame came to him in the temple, and he healed them. Had he not overturned the tables of the money-changers and the chairs of those who sold doves, the blind and the lame would not have deserved to receive back their former light and their quick step.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.21.15":["But the chief priests and the scribes, seeing the wonderful things that he did and the children crying out in the temple and saying: Hosanna to the son of David, were indignant and said to him: Do you hear what these are saying? Most people think that the greatest of the signs is that Lazarus was raised, that the man blind from the womb received light, that at the Jordan the voice of the Father was heard, that, transfigured on the mountain, he showed the glory of one triumphing. To me, among all the signs he did, this seems more wonderful: that one man, at that time despised and so lowly that he was afterward crucified, while the scribes and Pharisees raged against him and saw their gains being destroyed, was able, at the blows of a single whip, to cast out so great a multitude, to overturn the tables and break the chairs, and to do other things which an endless army would not have done. For something fiery and starlike shone from his eyes, and the majesty of the divinity gleamed in his face. And though the priests do not dare to lay a hand on him, they nevertheless slander his works, and the testimony of the people and of the children who cried out: Hosanna to the son of David, they turn into slander, namely that this is not said except to the Son of God alone. Let bishops, then, and men however holy, see with how great danger they allow these things to be said to them, if to the Lord, to whom this was truly said, it is charged as a crime because the faith of believers was not yet firm.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.21.16":["But Jesus says to them: Certainly; have you never read: Out of the mouth of infants and sucklings you have perfected praise? How moderately tempered the statement, and the reply leaning both ways and not open to slander! He did not say what the scribes wanted to hear: The children do well to bear witness to me; nor again: They are wrong, they are children, you ought to make allowance for their age; but he brings forward an example from Psalm 8, so that, while the Lord was silent, the testimony of the Scriptures might confirm the words of the children.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.21.17":["And leaving them, he went out outside the city to Bethany and stayed there. He left the unbelieving and, going out of the city of those who contradicted him, went to Bethany, which is interpreted house of obedience, already then prefiguring the calling of the Gentiles; and he stayed there because he could not remain in Israel. This too must be understood: that he was of such poverty and flattered no one to such a degree that in the greatest city he found no host, no lodging, but lived in a tiny field with Lazarus and his sisters; for Bethany is their village.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.21.18":["Now in the morning, returning to the city, he was hungry, and seeing a single fig tree beside the road, he came to it and found nothing on it but leaves only. And he said to it: Let fruit never grow from you forever. And the fig tree withered at once. And the disciples, seeing it, marveled, saying: How did it wither at once? When the darkness of night had been dispelled and the morning light was shining, and midday was near, in which the Lord was to illumine the world with his passion, as he was returning to the city he was hungry, either showing the reality of human flesh, or hungering for the salvation of believers and burning at the unbelief of Israel.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.21.19-20":["But according to the letter, the Lord, being about to suffer among the peoples and to bear the stumbling block of the cross, had to strengthen the minds of the disciples by anticipating it with a sign. Hence the disciples too marvel, saying: How did it wither at once? The Savior, then, could have withered his enemies too by the same power, had he not been awaiting their salvation through repentance.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.21.19":["For the fig tree did not give fruit, the one to which the Lord came hungry in the Gospel, and he did not find fruit on it, and he cursed it, saying: You shall not bear fruit even unto the age. And consider diligently what he said: You shall not bear fruit unto the age; he does not say, unto the ages of ages, but when this age has passed away and the fullness of the Gentiles has entered, then this fig tree too will bear its fruits, and all Israel will be saved.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk","And when he had seen a single tree, which we understand as the synagogue and the assembly of the Jews, beside the road (for it had the law, and it was beside the road because it did not believe in the Way), he came to it, standing as it was and immovable and not having the feet of the Gospel, and he found nothing on it but leaves only: the rustling of promises, the traditions of the Pharisees, boasting of the law, ornaments of words, without any fruits of truth. Hence another evangelist also says: For it was not yet the time, either because the time of Israel's salvation had not yet come, since the people of the Gentiles had not yet come in, or because the time of faith had passed, since, having come to Israel first and been spurned, he had passed over to the nations. And he said to it: Let fruit never grow from you, either forever or to the age; for the Greek word αἰών signifies both. And the fig tree withered, because, when the Lord was hungry, it did not have the food he had desired. But the leaves withered in such a way that the trunk itself remained, and, though the branches were broken, the root stayed green, so that in the last time, if they are willing to believe, it may sprout shoots of faith, and the Scripture may be fulfilled which says: There is hope for a tree.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.21.21":["And Jesus answered and said to them: Amen I say to you: If you have faith and do not hesitate, you will do not only this of the fig tree, but even if you say to a mountain: Lift yourself up and cast yourself into the sea, it will be done. The dogs of the Gentiles bark against us in their volumes, which they have left behind as a memorial of their own impiety, asserting that the apostles did not have faith because they could not move mountains. To them we shall answer that many signs were done by the Lord, according to the testimony of John the evangelist, which, if they had been written, the world could not contain; not that the world could not have contained the volumes, which, however many they may be, one little bookcase or one book-chest can contain, but that it could not bear the greatness of the signs, on account of their wonder and its own unbelief. Therefore we believe that the apostles did these things too, but that they were not written for this reason, lest a greater occasion for contradicting be given to unbelievers. Otherwise, let us ask them whether they believe these signs which are narrated in writing or not; and when we have seen that they are unbelieving, we shall prove as a consequence that those who did not believe the lesser would not have believed the greater either. So much against them. But we, as we have already said before, should understand the mountain as the devil, who is proud and vaunts himself against his creator, who is called by the prophet the corrupt mountain; and when he has possessed a man's soul and has become rooted in it, he can be moved by the apostles, and by those who are like the apostles, into the sea, that is, into places salty and tossing and bitter, which have none of the sweetness of God. This same thing is read in the psalms too: We will not fear while the earth is troubled and the mountains are moved into the heart of the sea.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.21.23":["And when he had come into the temple, the chief priests and the elders of the people came to him as he was teaching, saying: By what power do you do these things, and who gave you this power? In different words they build up the same slander as above, when they said: By Beelzebub, the prince of demons, this man casts out demons. For when they say: By what power do you do these things, they doubt the power of God and want it to be understood that what he does is the devil's; and adding further: who gave you this power, they most plainly deny the Son of God, whom they think does signs not by his own powers but by another's.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.21.24-27":["Jesus answered and said to them: I also will ask you one word, and if you tell it to me, I also will tell you by what power I do these things. The baptism of John, where was it from, from heaven or from men? and the rest. This is what is commonly said: Into a bad knot in a tree a bad nail or wedge must be driven. The Lord could have refuted the slander of those tempting him by an open answer, but he prudently asks a question, so that they may be condemned either by their own silence or by their own statement. For if they had answered that the baptism of John was from heaven, as they, wise in malice, had worked it out among themselves, the reply followed: Why then were you not baptized by John? If they had chosen to say that it was put together by human deceit and had had nothing divine, they feared a riot of the people. For all the assembled multitudes had received the baptism of John and held him thus, as a prophet. So the most impious faction answered and used a word of humility, by which it said that it did not know, to fit together its snares.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.21.27":["He too said to them: Neither do I tell you by what power I do these things. They lied in answering that they did not know; it followed, then, according to their answer, that the Lord too should say: Neither do I know; but the truth cannot lie; and he said: Neither do I tell you. By this he shows both that they knew but would not answer and that he knew and for this reason would not tell, because they kept silent about what they knew; and at once he brings in a parable by which he both convicts them of impiety and teaches that the kingdom of God is to be transferred to the Gentiles.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.21.28-30":["But what do you think? A man had two sons, and coming to the first he said: Go today, work in my vineyard. But he answered and said: I will not; but afterward, moved by repentance, he went. And coming to the other he spoke likewise. And he answered and said: I go, lord, and he did not go. These are the two sons who are also described in Luke's parable, the thrifty one and the prodigal, and of whom the prophet Zechariah speaks: I took for myself two rods; one I called Beauty and the other I called Cord, and I fed the flock. First it is said to the people of the Gentiles, through the knowledge of the natural law: Go and work in my vineyard, that is: what you do not want done to you, do not do to another. It answered proudly: I will not; but afterward, at the coming of the Savior, having repented, it worked in the vineyard of God and corrected the stubbornness of its word by labor. But the second son is the people of the Jews, which answered Moses: All things whatsoever the Lord says we will do, and did not go into the vineyard, because, having killed the householder's son, it thought itself the heir.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.21.31-32":["Others, however, think that the parable is not about the Gentiles and the Jews but simply about sinners and the just, the Lord himself too afterward expounding his proposition: Amen I say to you that tax collectors and harlots go before you in the kingdom of God; because those who by evil works had refused to serve God afterward received the baptism of repentance from John, while the Pharisees, who displayed righteousness and boasted that they did the law of God, having despised the baptism of John, did not do the commandments of God. Hence he says: For John came to you in the way of righteousness, and you did not believe him, but the tax collectors and harlots believed. Moreover, as to what follows: Which of the two did the father's will? and they say: the last, it must be known that the true copies have not the last but the first, so that they are condemned by their own judgment. But if we choose to read the last, the interpretation is plain: we should say that the Jews indeed understand the truth but evade it and will not say what they think, just as they would not say that the baptism of John was from heaven, though they knew it.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.21.33":["Hear another parable: There was a man, a householder, who planted a vineyard and set a hedge around it and dug a winepress in it and built a tower, and he let it out to farmers and went abroad. This is what the Lord signified with a saying taken from a proverb: It is hard to kick against the goad. The chief priests and the elders of the people, who had asked the Lord: By what power do you do these things, and who gave you this power, and had wished to catch Wisdom in a word, are overcome by their own art and hear in parables what they did not deserve to hear openly. This man, the householder, is the same who had two sons, and who in another parable hired workers for his vineyard; who planted the vineyard of which Isaiah too speaks most fully in a song, bringing in at the end: The vineyard of the Lord of hosts is the house of Israel; and in the psalm: A vineyard, he says, you transplanted from Egypt; you cast out the nations and planted it. And he set a hedge around it, either the wall of the city or the help of the angels. And dug a winepress in it, either the altar or those winepresses with which three psalms are marked in their title, the eighth, the eightieth (81st) and the eighty-third (84th). And built a tower, no doubt the temple, of which it is said through Micah: And you, cloudy tower of the daughter of Zion. And he let it out to farmers, whom elsewhere he called the workers of the vineyard, who had been hired at the first, third, sixth and ninth and eleventh hour. And went abroad, not by change of place, for from where can God be absent, by whom all things are filled, and who says through Jeremiah: I am a God who draws near, and not from afar, says the Lord? But he seems to go away from the vineyard so as to leave the vinedressers free choice in their working.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.21.34-35":["And when the time of fruits drew near, he sent his servants to the farmers to receive its fruits; and the farmers, seizing his servants, beat one, killed another, and stoned another. He had given them the law and had ordered them to work in this vineyard, so that they might show the fruit of the law in works. Afterward he sent servants to them, whom they seized and either beat, like Jeremiah, or killed, like Isaiah, or stoned, like Naboth and Zechariah, whom they slew between the temple and the altar. Let us read Paul's letter to the Hebrews, and from it we learn most fully which of the Lord's servants suffered how much.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.21.36-37":["But last of all he sent his son to them, saying: They will revere my son. In what we read above: Again he sent other servants, more than the first, and they did the same to them, he shows the patience of the householder, in that he sent more often, to provoke the wicked tenants to repentance; but they stored up for themselves wrath on the day of wrath. Moreover, what is joined: They will revere my son, does not come from ignorance. For what could the householder not know, who in this place is understood as God the Father? But God is always said to be in doubt, so that free will may be kept for man. Let us question Arius and Eunomius. See, the Father is said not to know, and he tempers his statement, and, as far as lies in you, he is proved to have lied. Whatever they answer on behalf of the Father, they will understand this of the Son, who says that he does not know the day of the consummation.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.21.39":["Seizing him, they cast him out of the vineyard and killed him. The Apostle too says that the Lord was crucified outside the gate. But we can also understand it otherwise: that he was cast out of the vineyard and killed there so that, with the Gentiles receiving him, the vineyard might be let out to others.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.21.40-41":["Something of the kind the Prophet Nathan, sent to David, says too (2 Sam 12), and he questions him through a parable, so that while judging of another he may pronounce sentence on himself. So here too the people is questioned as though about a vineyard, so that it may answer against itself. This place the Savior carries out more fully in the Gospel (Matt 21), and what is passed over here, he puts as a question to the Scribes and Pharisees. For in Isaiah nothing is said of the husbandmen, nor is it indicated what they are to suffer, but the discourse is only of the vineyard; there, however, as though the vineyard were one thing and the husbandmen another, he speaks of the people and of the teachers in such a way that he destroys the wicked wickedly and lets out the vineyard to other husbandmen, signifying the apostles and those who were to succeed the apostles.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","When therefore the lord of the vineyard comes, what will he do to those tenants, and the rest. The Lord asks them, not because he does not know what they will answer, but so that they may be condemned by their own answer. Now the vineyard has been let out to us, and let out on this condition: that we render the Lord the fruit in its seasons and know at each time what we ought either to say or to do.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.21.41-43":["Thus the Savior asks the scribes and Pharisees, the vinedressers and farmers of the Jews, what he will do to the wicked vinedressers and farmers; and when they answered, He will destroy the wicked wickedly, and give the vineyard to other farmers (Matt 21:41), he went on: The kingdom of God will be taken from you and given to a nation producing its fruits (ibid., 43). The matter needs no interpretation. For how few of the princes of the churches are from the Jews, and not from foreigners and men of outside nations! Formerly serving idols, and strangers to the testament of God and aliens from his promises, having no hope and without God in the world, they now preside over the churches, and with the plough of faith they tame the hard and formerly untamed hearts of the Gentiles to bear fruit, so that they may multiply the seed of the Lord's doctrine with an abundance of good works.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"MAT.21.42":["Jesus says to them: Have you never read in the Scriptures: The stone which the builders rejected, this has become the head of the corner; this was done by the Lord, this is wonderful in our eyes? By various parables and different discourses the same things are woven together. For those whom above he had called workers and vinedressers and farmers, he now calls builders, that is, masons. Hence the Apostle too says: You are God's tillage, God's building. These masons, then, just as the vinedressers receive the vineyard, so received the stone, to place it either in the foundations, according to the architect Paul, or in the corner, so that it might join together two walls, that is, both peoples; and this stone, rejected by them, has become the head of the corner; and this was done by the Lord, not by human powers but by the might of God. Of this stone of help Peter too speaks confidently: This is the stone which was rejected by you builders, which has become the head of the corner; and Isaiah: Behold, he says, I will lay in the foundations of Zion a stone, chosen, precious, a cornerstone, and whoever believes in it will not be confounded.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.21.43":["Therefore I say to you that the kingdom of God will be taken from you and given to a nation producing its fruits. I have said several times that the kingdom of God is understood as the holy Scriptures, which the Lord took away from the Jews and handed over to us, so that we may produce their fruits. This is the vineyard that is handed over to farmers and vinedressers; those in it who have not worked, having only the name of the Scriptures, will lose the fruits of the vineyard.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.21.44":["Whoever falls on this stone will be broken, but on whomever it falls it will crush him. It is one thing to offend Christ by evil works, another to deny him. He who is a sinner and yet believes in him falls indeed on the stone and is broken, but is not utterly crushed; for he is kept through patience for salvation. But on whomever it falls, that is, on whom the stone itself rushes down, and who has wholly denied Christ, it will so crush him that not even a potsherd remains with which a little water might be drawn.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.21.45-46":["And when the chief priests and the Pharisees had heard his parables, they knew that he was speaking about them; and seeking to seize him, they feared the crowds, since they held him as a prophet. Although they were hard of heart and dulled by unbelief and impiety toward the Son of God, still they could not deny plain propositions, and they understood that all the Lord's sayings were directed against them. Hence they wished indeed to kill him, but they feared the crowds, because they held him as a prophet. The crowd is always fickle and does not persist in its purposed will, and, like the waves and the various winds, it is drawn this way and that. The one whom they now venerate and honor as a prophet, afterward they shout against: Crucify, crucify such a man.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.22.1-3":["And answering, Jesus spoke to them in parables, saying: The kingdom of heaven has become like a man, a king, who made a wedding for his son, and sent his servant to call those invited to the wedding, and they would not come. The Pharisees, understanding that the parables were spoken about them, sought to seize him and kill him. Knowing this will of theirs, the Lord nonetheless rebukes them in their raging, and he is not so overcome by fear as to refrain from reproving sinners. This king who made a wedding for his son is God almighty. And he makes a wedding for our Lord Jesus Christ and the Church, which has been gathered as much from the Jews as from the Gentiles; and he sends his servant to call those invited to the wedding, no doubt Moses, through whom he gave the law to those invited. But if we read 'servants', as most copies have it, it is to be referred to the prophets, because those invited through them disdained to come.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.22.4":["Again he sent other servants, saying: Tell those invited: Behold, I have prepared my dinner, my bulls and fatlings are killed and all things are ready; come to the wedding. The servants who were sent the second time are better understood as the prophets than as the apostles, provided, however, that 'servant' was written above; but if you read 'servants' there, then here the second servants are to be understood as the apostles. By the dinner prepared and the bulls and fatlings killed, either the king's riches are described by metaphor, so that from carnal things spiritual things may be understood, or else the greatness of the doctrines and the teaching of God, most full of the law, can be perceived.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.22.5-6":["And they went off, one to his farm, another to his business; but others seized his servants and, having treated them with insult, killed them. Among those who do not receive the truth of the Gospel there is great diversity. For those who, occupied with other things, would not come are of lesser guilt than those who, scorning the affection of the one inviting, turned kindness into cruelty and seized the king's servants and either treated them with insults or killed them. In this parable the killing of the bridegroom is passed over in silence, and through the deaths of the servants the contempt for the wedding is shown.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.22.7":["But when the king heard, he was angry. Of him he had said above: The kingdom of heaven has become like a man, a king, when he was inviting to the wedding and doing works of clemency, the name of man was set beside him; now, when he comes to vengeance, 'man' is passed over in silence and he is called only 'king'.\n— Commentary on Matthew","And having sent his armies, he destroyed those murderers and burned their city. By the armies let us understand either the avenging angels, of whom it is written in the psalms: An infliction by most evil angels, or the Romans under the leadership of Vespasian and Titus, who, having slain the peoples of Judea, burned the transgressing city.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.22.9-10":["Then he said to his servants: The wedding indeed is ready, but those who had been invited were not worthy; go therefore to the exits of the roads, and whomever you find, call to the wedding. The people of the Gentiles was not on the roads but at the exits of the roads. But it is asked how, among these who were outside among the wicked, some good men were also found. The Apostle treats this passage more fully to the Romans: that the Gentiles, doing by nature the things that are of the law, condemn the Jews, who did not do the written law. Among the heathen themselves, too, there is infinite diversity, since we know that some are prone to vices and rush headlong into evils, while others, on account of the uprightness of their morals, are devoted to virtues.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.22.11-12":["But the king went in to see those reclining at table, and he saw there a man not clothed in a wedding garment, and he said to him: Friend, how did you come in here not having a wedding garment? But he fell silent. Those who had been invited to the wedding from the hedges and corners and streets and various places had filled the king's supper. But afterward, when the king had come to see those reclining at his banquet, that is, resting, as it were, in his faith, so that on the day of judgment he might visit the guests and discern the merits of each, he found one who was not clothed in a wedding garment. This one man is understood as all who are allied in malice. And the wedding garments are the precepts of the Lord and the works that are fulfilled from the law and the Gospel and make up the clothing of the new man. If anyone, then, at the time of judgment is found, under the Christian name, not to have the wedding garment, that is, the garment of the supercelestial man, but a polluted garment, that is, the cast-off skins of the old man, he is at once rebuked, and it is said to him: Friend, how did you come in here? He calls him friend because he was invited to the wedding; he convicts him of impudence because with a filthy garment he polluted the cleanness of the wedding. But he fell silent. For at that time there will be no place for impudence and no power of denial, since all the angels and the world itself are witness of sins.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.22.13":["Then the king said to the attendants: With his feet and hands bound, cast him into the outer darkness; there will be weeping and gnashing of teeth. The bound hands and feet, and the weeping of the eyes and the gnashing of teeth, understand either as proving the truth of the resurrection, or else the hands and feet are bound for this reason, that they may cease to work evil and to run to shed blood. In the weeping of the eyes, too, and the gnashing of teeth, the greatness of the torments is shown through a metaphor drawn from the members of the body.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.22.14":["Many, however, are called, but few chosen. In a brief little sentence he sums up all the parables: that in the work of the vineyard, and in the building of the house, and in the wedding banquet, it is not the beginning but the end that is sought.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.22.15-16":["Then the Pharisees went off and took counsel how to catch him in his speech, and they send their disciples with the Herodians, saying. Not long before, under Caesar Augustus, Judea, subjected to the Romans when the census was held throughout the whole world, had been made tributary, and there was great sedition among the people: some said that for the security and quiet for which the Romans fought on behalf of all, the tributes ought to be paid; but the Pharisees, who applauded themselves in their righteousness, strove on the contrary that the people of God, which paid tithes and gave firstfruits and the other things that are written in the law, ought not to be subject to human laws. Caesar Augustus had established Herod, the son of Antipater, a foreigner and a proselyte, as king over the Jews, to preside over the tributes and obey the Roman empire. The Pharisees therefore send their disciples with the Herodians, that is, with Herod's soldiers, or with those whom the Pharisees, in mockery, because they paid tributes to the Romans, called Herodians and not men devoted to divine worship. Some of the Latins absurdly think that the Herodians were those who believed Herod to be the Christ, something we read nowhere at all.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.22.16-17":["Teacher, we know that you are truthful and teach the way of God in truth, and you have no care for anyone; for you do not regard the person of men. Tell us therefore what you think: Is it lawful to give tax to Caesar or not? The flattering and deceitful question lures the one answering into fearing God rather than Caesar and saying that the tributes ought not to be paid, so that the Herodians, hearing it, might at once seize him as the leader of a sedition against the Romans.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.22.17-21":["Something of the kind is written to the Romans as well: \"Let every soul be subject to the higher authorities. For there is no authority except from God\" (Rom. 13:1). This precept, both here and there, I think was issued for this reason: that at that time the doctrine of Judas the Galilean was still flourishing and had very many followers. Mention of him is made also in the Acts of the Apostles, where Scripture relates: \"For before these days Theudas rose up, saying that he was someone great; and about three thousand men joined him\" (Acts 5:36); and, \"after him rose up Judas the Galilean in the days of the census\". Among other things he put forward this as if it were plausible from the Law: that no one ought to be called Lord except God alone, and that those who brought tithes to the temple should not pay tribute to Caesar. This heresy had grown so much that it troubled a great part even of the Pharisees and of the people, so that this question was brought even to our Lord: \"Is it lawful to give tribute to Caesar, or not\" (Matt. 22:17)? Answering them prudently and cautiously, the Lord says: \"Render the things that are Caesar's to Caesar, and the things that are God's to God\" (ibid. 21). In agreement with this answer, the apostle Paul teaches that believers ought to be subject to principalities and authorities.\n— Commentary on Titus"],"MAT.22.18":["But Jesus, knowing their wickedness, said: Why do you tempt me, hypocrites? The first virtue of one who answers is to know the mind of those who question him, and to call them not disciples but tempters. A hypocrite, then, is the name for one who is one thing and pretends another, that is, does one thing in deed and puts forward another with his voice.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.22.19":["Show me the coin of the tax. And they brought him a denarius. Wisdom always acts wisely, so that tempters are refuted above all by their own words. Show me, he says, the denarius. This is the kind of coin that was reckoned at ten coins and bore the image of Caesar.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.22.20":["And Jesus said to them: Whose image and superscription is this? Let those who think that the Savior's questioning is ignorance and not dispensation learn from the present passage that Jesus certainly could have known whose image was on the coin; but he asks so that he may answer their words fittingly.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.22.21":["They say to him: Caesar's. Then he said to them: Render therefore the things that are Caesar's to Caesar, and the things that are God's to God. Let us not think that Augustus is signified by Caesar, but Tiberius, his stepson, who had succeeded to his stepfather's place, and under whom the Lord also suffered. But all the Roman kings, from the first, Gaius Caesar, who had seized the empire, were called Caesars. Moreover, as for his saying: Render the things that are Caesar's to Caesar, that is, coin, tribute and money, and the things that are God's to God, let us understand tithes, firstfruits, oblations and victims; just as he himself both renders tributes for himself and Peter, and renders to God the things that are God's, doing the will of the Father.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.22.22":["And hearing it they marveled. Those who ought to have believed marveled at such great wisdom, because their cunning had found no opening for laying a snare.\n— Commentary on Matthew","And leaving him they went away, carrying back unbelief together with their wonder.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.22.23":["On that day the Sadducees, who say that there is no resurrection, came to him. There were two heresies among the Jews, one of the Pharisees and the other of the Sadducees. The Pharisees held up a righteousness of traditions and observances, which they call deuteroses, and hence they were also called 'divided' by the people; while the Sadducees, which is interpreted 'just', themselves also claimed for themselves what they were not. And whereas the former believed and confessed the resurrection of both body and soul, and angels and spirit, the latter, according to the Acts of the Apostles, denied it all. These are the two houses of which Isaiah teaches quite plainly that they would stumble on the stone of stumbling.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.22.24-27":["And they questioned him, saying: Teacher, Moses said: If anyone dies not having a son, his brother is to marry his wife and raise up seed for his brother. Now there were among us seven brothers, and the first, having married a wife, died, and the rest. Those who did not believe in the resurrection of bodies, and thought that the soul perished together with the bodies, aptly invent a fable of this kind, to convict of madness those who assert the resurrection of the dead. But it may be that this really happened at some time among their people.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.22.28":["In the resurrection, therefore, whose wife of the seven will she be? For they all had her. They set the foulness of the fable against it in order to deny the truth of the resurrection.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.22.29":["Jesus answered and said to them: You err, not knowing the Scriptures nor the power of God. They err for this reason, that they do not know the Scriptures; and because they are ignorant of the Scriptures, they consequently do not know the power of God, that is, Christ, who is the power of God and the wisdom of God.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.22.30":["For in the resurrection they will neither marry nor be married. Latin usage does not correspond to the Greek idiom. For properly it is women who are said to wed, and men to take wives. But let us understand it as said simply, so that 'marry' is written of the men and 'be married' of the wives. Thus in the resurrection they will not marry nor be married: therefore bodies will rise which are able to marry and be married. For no one says of a stone or a tree or of those things that have no genital members that they do not marry nor are married, but of those who, though they could marry, yet for another reason do not marry. But as for what is added:\n\nBut they are like the angels of God in heaven, a spiritual way of life is promised.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.22.31-33":["But concerning the resurrection of the dead, have you not read what was spoken by God, who says to you: I am the God of Abraham and the God of Isaac and the God of Jacob? He is not the God of the dead but of the living. To prove the truth of the resurrection he could have used other, much plainer examples, among which is this one: The dead shall be raised, and those who are in the tombs shall rise again, and in another place: Many of those who sleep in the dust of the earth shall arise, some to life, others to reproach and everlasting confusion. It is asked, therefore, what the Lord meant by bringing forward this testimony, which seems ambiguous or not sufficiently pertinent to the truth of the resurrection: I am the God of Abraham and the God of Isaac and the God of Jacob, and why, as though by bringing it forward he had proved what he wished, he at once added: He is not the God of the dead but of the living. The crowds standing around, too, recognizing the mystery of this matter, marveled at the teaching of his answer. We said above that the Sadducees, confessing neither angel nor spirit nor the resurrection of bodies, also preached the destruction of souls. They received only the five books of Moses, rejecting the oracles of the prophets. It would therefore have been foolish to bring forward testimonies from a source whose authority they did not follow. Moreover, to prove the eternity of souls he sets down an example from Moses: I am the God of Abraham and the God of Isaac and the God of Jacob, and at once adds: He is not the God of the dead but of the living, so that, once he had proved that souls endure after death (for it could not be that he was the God of those who did not subsist at all), the resurrection of the bodies too might consequently be introduced, the bodies which together with the souls did good or evil. The apostle Paul pursues this passage more fully in the last part of the first letter to the Corinthians.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.22.34":["When the Pharisees heard that he had imposed silence on the Sadducees, they gathered together; and one of them, a doctor of the law, questioned him, tempting him: Teacher, which is the great commandment in the law? Jesus said to him: You shall love the Lord your God with your whole heart, and the rest. What we read of Herod and Pontius Pilate, that they made concord over the Lord's death, we now see also of the Pharisees and the Sadducees. They are contrary to each other, but to tempt Jesus they agree with one mind. Those, then, who had already been refuted above in the showing of the denarius, and had seen the faction of the opposing party overthrown, ought to have been warned by the example not to contrive snares any further; but ill will and envy nourish impudence.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.22.35-36":["One of the doctors of the law asks, not desiring to know but tempting, whether he, when asked, knew what he was being asked, which is the greater commandment: not asking about the commandments, but which is the first and great commandment, so that, since all that God has commanded is great, whatever he answered, the questioner might have an occasion for slander, asserting that another, out of the many, is great. Whoever, then, knows and asks, not from a wish to learn but from eagerness to find out whether the one who is to answer knows, approaches after the likeness of the Pharisees, not as a disciple but as a tempter.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.22.41-45":["Now when the Pharisees were gathered together, Jesus asked them, saying: What do you think about the Christ? Whose son is he? They say to him: David's. He says to them: How then does David in the Spirit call him Lord, saying: The Lord said to my Lord: Sit at my right until I make your enemies the footstool of your feet. Those who had gathered to tempt Jesus, and were striving to catch the truth with a deceitful question, provided the occasion for their own refutation, and they are asked about the Christ, whose son he is. The question of Jesus profits us against the Jews even to this day. For these too, who confess that the Christ is to come, assert that he is a mere man and a holy man of the line of David. Let us therefore, taught by the Lord, ask them: If he is a mere man and only the son of David, how does David call him his Lord, not in the uncertainty of error nor by his own will, but in the Holy Spirit? Now the testimony that he set down is taken from Psalm 109 (110). David's Lord, therefore, he is called not according to that by which he was born of him, but according to that by which, born of the Father, he always was, going before the very father of his flesh. The Jews, to make sport of the truth of the question, invent many frivolous things, asserting that it was Abraham's home-born servant, whose son was Eliezer of Damascus, and that the psalm was written in his person, because after the slaughter of the five kings the Lord God said to his lord Abraham: Sit at my right hand until I make all your enemies the footstool of your feet. Let us ask them: How did God say to Abraham the words that follow: With you is the beginning in the day of your power, in the splendors of the saints; from the womb before the morning star I begot you; and: The Lord has sworn and will not repent: You are a priest for ever according to the order of Melchizedek, and let us compel them to answer how Abraham was begotten before the morning star and was a priest according to the order of Melchizedek, for whom Melchizedek offered bread and wine, and from whom he received tithes of the spoil.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.22.46":["And no one could answer him a word, nor did anyone dare from that day to ask him any more. The Pharisees and Sadducees, seeking an occasion for false accusation and to find some word that would lie open to their snares, because they have been refuted in argument, ask no further, but seize him quite openly and hand him over to the Roman power. From this we understand that the poisons of envy can indeed be overcome, but with difficulty are laid to rest.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.23.2-3":["Then Jesus spoke to the crowds and to his disciples, saying: The scribes and Pharisees have sat upon the chair of Moses; therefore all that they say to you, observe and do, but do not do according to their works; for they say and do not do. What is gentler, what is kinder than the Lord? He is tested by the Pharisees, their snares are broken, and, according to the psalmist: The arrows of little children have become their wounds, and nonetheless, on account of the priesthood and the dignity of their name, he exhorts the peoples to be subject to them, considering not their works but their teaching. As for his saying: The scribes and Pharisees have sat upon the chair of Moses, by the chair he signifies the teaching of the law. Therefore that also which is said in the psalm: He has not sat in the chair of pestilence; and: He overturned the chairs of those who sold doves, we ought to take as teaching.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.23.4":["But they bind heavy and unbearable burdens and lay them on men's shoulders, but with their own finger they will not move them. This is said generally against all teachers who command great things and do not do even lesser ones. But it must be noted that the shoulders and the finger and the burdens and the bonds with which the burdens are bound are to be understood spiritually.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.23.5":["But they do all their works in order to be seen by men. Whoever, therefore, does anything in such a way as to be seen by men is a scribe and a Pharisee.\n— Commentary on Matthew","For they broaden their phylacteries and enlarge their fringes; they love also the first places at table at dinners and the first chairs in the synagogues and greetings in the marketplace and to be called rabbi by men. Woe to us wretches, to whom the vices of the Pharisees have passed! The Lord, when he had given the commandments of the law through Moses, added at the end: You shall bind them on your hand, and they shall be unmoved before your eyes; and the sense is: Let my precepts be in your hand, that they may be fulfilled in deed; let them be before your eyes, that you may meditate on them day and night. The Pharisees, interpreting this badly, used to write the decalogue of Moses, that is, the ten words of the law, on little strips of parchment, folding them up and binding them on the forehead and making as it were a crown for the head, so that they might always move before their eyes, as the Indians and Babylonians do to this day; and whoever has this is judged among the peoples to be, as it were, religious. Moses had also commanded another thing: that they should make violet-blue fringes on the four corners of their cloaks to distinguish the people of Israel, so that, as circumcision in their bodies gave a sign of the Jewish nation, so their clothing might have some distinction. The superstitious teachers, courting popular favor and pursuing gain from silly women, used to make large fringes and bind very sharp thorns in them, so that, namely, as they walked and sat they might now and then be pricked and, as by this reminder, be drawn back to the duties of God and the ministries of his service. Since, therefore, the Lord had said: They do all their works in order to be seen by men, what he had charged in general he now divides into parts. Those little slips of the decalogue they called phylacteries, because whoever had them would have them as it were for his own guarding and protection, the Pharisees not understanding that these things are to be carried in the heart, not on the body; otherwise bookcases and chests too have books, and do not have the knowledge of God. This, among us, superstitious little women do to this day with little Gospels and with the wood of the cross and things of this kind, women who have indeed a zeal for God, but not according to knowledge, straining out the gnat and swallowing the camel. Of this kind was the fringe, small and short, prescribed by the law, which that woman also who suffered a flow of blood touched on the Lord's cloak; but she was not pricked by the superstitious thorns of the Pharisees, but rather was healed at its touch.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.23.6-7":["And since they broaden their phylacteries beyond measure and make large fringes, courting glory from men, they are rebuked in the rest for seeking the first places at table at dinners and the first chairs in the synagogues, and for pursuing gluttony and glory in public, and for being called rabbi by men, which in the Latin tongue means master. In short, there follows:\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.23.8-10":["But do not you be called rabbi, for one is your master; and do not call anyone your father on earth, for one is your Father, who is in heaven; nor be called masters, because your master is one, Christ. Neither master nor father is anyone else to be called except God the Father and our Lord Jesus Christ: Father, because from him are all things; master, because through him are all things, or because through the dispensation of the flesh we have all been reconciled to God. It is asked why, against this precept, the Apostle said that he was the teacher of the Gentiles, or how, in common speech, especially in the monasteries of Palestine and of Egypt, they call one another fathers. This is solved thus: it is one thing to be a father or a master by nature, another by indulgence. If we call a man father, we pay honor to his age; we do not show him to be the author of our life. A master too is so called from fellowship with the true Master. And, not to unfold endless matters: just as one God by nature and one Son does not prejudice others from being called gods and sons by adoption, so also one Father and Master does not prejudice others from being called fathers and masters in a looser sense.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.23.9":["But since Abba means Father in the Hebrew and Syriac tongue, and our Lord in the Gospel commands that no one is to be called father except God (Matt. 23), I do not know by what license in the monasteries we either call others by this name or consent to be called by it ourselves. And certainly he gave this command who had said that one must not swear (Matt. 5). If we do not swear, let us not name anyone father either. If we interpret the saying about a father otherwise, we shall be compelled to think otherwise about swearing as well.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"MAT.23.13":["Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites, because you shut the kingdom of heaven before men; you yourselves do not enter, nor do you allow those entering to enter. The scribes and Pharisees have knowledge of the law and the prophets; they know that Christ is the Son of God; they are not ignorant that he was born of a virgin; but while they seek plunder from the common people subject to them, they neither enter the kingdoms of heaven themselves nor permit those who could enter. This is what he rebukes in the prophet Hosea: The priests have hidden the way, they have killed Shechem; and again: The priests did not say: Where is the Lord? Or else every teacher who causes his disciples to stumble by evil works shuts the kingdom of heaven before them.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.23.15":["Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites, because you go around sea and dry land to make one proselyte, and when he has been made, you make him a son of Gehenna twice as much as yourselves. We do not keep what we have sought with the same zeal with which we seek it. The scribes and Pharisees, traversing the whole world for the sake of trade and various gains, to be captured as much from their disciples as through a show of holiness, made it their zeal to make a proselyte from the Gentiles, that is, to mingle a stranger, an uncircumcised man, with the people of God. But he who before, while he was a heathen, went astray in simplicity and was a son of Gehenna once, seeing the vices of his teachers and understanding that they destroy by deed what they taught by words, returns to his vomit and, having become a Gentile, will be worthy of greater punishment as a transgressor. He is called a son of Gehenna in the way one is called a son of perdition and a son of this world. For each one is called the son of him whose works he does.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.23.16-19":["Woe to you, blind guides, who say: Whoever swears by the temple, it is nothing; but whoever swears by the gold of the temple is bound. Fools and blind, for which is greater: the gold, or the temple that sanctifies the gold? And: Whoever swears by the altar, it is nothing; but whoever swears by the gift that is upon it is bound. Blind ones, for which is greater: the gift, or the altar that sanctifies the gift? He, therefore, who swears by the altar swears by it and by all things that are upon it; and he who swears by the temple swears by it and by him who dwells in it; and he who swears by heaven swears by the throne of God and by him who sits upon it. Above, as seemed good to us, we explained what was signified by the tradition of the Pharisees who said: Whatever gift is from me will profit you. Now a tradition of the Pharisees is condemned that is double and draws toward the one occasion of avarice, so that they are convicted of doing all for gain and not for the fear of God. For as, in the broadened phylacteries and fringes, the reputation for holiness courted glory, and through the occasion of glory sought gain, so another scheme devised by their tradition convicts the teachers of impiety. If anyone, in a dispute or in some quarrel or in a doubtful case, had sworn by the temple and was afterwards convicted of a lie, he was not held guilty of the crime; but if he had sworn by the gold and the money that was offered to the priests in the temple, he was at once compelled to pay that by which he had sworn. Again, if anyone had sworn by the altar, no one held him guilty of perjury; but if he had perjured himself by the gift or by the offerings, that is, by the sacrifice or by the victims, by fine flour and the other things that are offered to God upon the altar, these they demanded back most zealously. The Lord therefore convicts them both of folly and of fraud, because the temple is much greater than the gold which is sanctified by the temple, and the altar than the sacrifices which are sanctified by the altar. And they did all this not out of the fear of God but out of desire for riches.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.23.23":["Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites, because you tithe mint and dill and cumin and have left aside the weightier things of the law, judgment and mercy and faith; these you ought to have done, and not to omit those. There are many precepts in the law which display types of things to come, but others which are plain, according to the psalmist who says: The commandment of the Lord is bright, enlightening the eyes, which demand works at once, for example: You shall not commit adultery, you shall not steal, you shall not bear false witness, and the rest. But the Pharisees, because the Lord had commanded (to leave aside the mystical senses for the moment) that, for the support of the priests and Levites, whose portion was the Lord, tithes of all things be offered in the temple, had this one zeal: that what had been ordered should be brought in; the other things, which were greater, whether anyone did them or not, they counted of little worth. And so from this passage too he convicts them of avarice, because they zealously exact tithes even of cheap herbs, and pass over judgment in the settling of business, and mercy toward the poor, orphans and widows, and faith in God, which are great things.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.23.24":["Blind guides, straining out the gnat, but swallowing the camel. By the camel I think is meant, according to the sense of the present passage, the greatness of the precepts, judgment and mercy and faith; and by the gnat the tithes of mint and dill and cumin and of the other cheap herbs. These things, which are great, we devour and neglect against the precept of God, and in small things that bring gain we make a show of religion and of diligence.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.23.25-26":["Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites, because you clean what is outside of the cup and the dish, but inside you are full of plunder and uncleanness. Blind Pharisee, first clean what is inside of the cup and the dish, so that what is outside may become clean. In different words, with the same sense as above, he convicts the Pharisees of pretense and falsehood, because they show one thing to men outside and do another at home; not that their superstition dwelt in the cup and the dish, but that outside they showed men holiness in dress, in speech, in phylacteries, in fringes, in the length of their prayers and in other things of this kind, while inwardly they were full of the filth of vices.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.23.27-28":["Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites, because you are like whitewashed tombs, which outside appear beautiful to men, but inside are full of the bones of the dead and of all filthiness. What he had shown in the cup and the dish, in that they were washed outside and filthy within, he now repeats through the example of tombs: that just as tombs are washed outwardly with lime and adorned with marbles and picked out with gold and colors, but within are full of the bones of the dead, so too the perverse teachers, who teach one thing and do another, display cleanness in the fashion of their dress and in humility of words, but inside are full of all filthiness and avarice and lust. In short, he expresses this very thing more plainly, adding:\n\nSo you too outwardly indeed appear just to men, but inside you are full of hypocrisy and iniquity.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.23.29-31":["Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites, because you build the tombs of the prophets and adorn the monuments of the just, and say: If we had been in the days of our fathers, we would not have been their partners in the blood of the prophets. Thus you are witnesses against yourselves that you are sons of those who killed the prophets. With a most prudent syllogism he convicts them of being sons of murderers, since they themselves, for a reputation of goodness and glory among the peoples, build the tombs of the prophets whom their forefathers killed, and say: If we had been at that time, we would not have done the things that our fathers did. And this, even if they do not say it in word, they speak in deed, in that they build, ambitiously and magnificently, memorials of the slain, who, they do not deny, were slaughtered by their fathers.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.23.32":["And you, fill up the measure of your fathers. Having proved by what was said above that they were sons of murderers and of those who had killed the prophets, he now concludes what he had intended and sets down, as it were, the last part of the syllogism: And you, fill up the measure of your fathers, what was lacking in them, you fill up; they killed the servants, you, crucify the Lord; they the prophets, you him who was preached by the prophets.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.23.33":["Serpents, brood of vipers, how will you flee from the judgment of Gehenna? John the Baptist too had said this very thing. Just as, then, vipers are born of vipers, so you, he says, born of murderous fathers, are murderers.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.23.34":["Therefore, behold, I send to you prophets and wise men and scribes; some of them you will kill and crucify, and some of them you will scourge in your synagogues, and you persecute them from city to city. This which we said earlier, Fill up the measure of your fathers, to pertain to the person of the Lord, since he was to be killed by them, can also be referred to his disciples, of whom he now says: Behold, I send to you prophets and wise men and scribes; some of them you will kill and crucify, and you will scourge in your synagogues, and you persecute them from city to city, so that you may fill up the measure of your fathers. At the same time note, according to the Apostle writing to the Corinthians, that the gifts of the disciples of Christ are various: some are prophets, who foretell what is to come; others wise men, who know when they ought to bring forth speech; others scribes, most learned in the law; of whom Stephen was stoned, Paul killed, Peter crucified, the disciples scourged in the Acts of the Apostles; and they persecuted them from city to city, driving them out of Judea, so that they passed over to the people of the Gentiles.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.23.35-36":["Let us say briefly why the blood of Abel the just, up to Zechariah son of Barachiah, is required of that generation, though it killed neither of them. It is a rule of the Scriptures to set forth two generations, of the good or of the evil, that is, one for each. Let us take examples from the good: Who shall ascend into the mountain of the Lord, or who shall rest on his holy mountain? and when he had described the many who are to ascend into the mountain of the Lord, who lived in different ages, he afterwards adds: This is the generation of those who seek the Lord, who seek the face of the God of Jacob; and in another place, of all the saints: The generation of the just shall be blessed; but of the evil, as in the present passage: generation of vipers and all things shall be required of this generation; and in Ezekiel, when he had described the sins of the land, the prophetic word added: If Noah and Job and Daniel are found there, the sins of that land will not be forgiven, wishing all the just who would be like them in virtues to be understood through Noah and Job and Daniel. Therefore these too, who did things against the apostles like those of Cain and Joash, are reported to be of one generation.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.23.35":["That upon you may come all the just blood that has been shed upon the earth, from the blood of Abel the just to the blood of Zechariah son of Barachiah, who was killed between the temple and the altar. Amen I say to you, all these things will come upon this generation. Concerning Abel there is no ambiguity that he is the one whom Cain his brother killed. That he was just is proved not only from the Lord's present judgment but from the testimony of Genesis, where his gifts are related as accepted by God. We ask who this Zechariah son of Barachiah is, since we read of many Zechariahs. And lest a free license for error be granted us, there is added: Whom you killed between the temple and the altar. I have read different things in different writers, and I ought to set down the opinions of each. Some say it is Zechariah son of Barachiah, who is the eleventh among the twelve prophets, and the name of the father agrees in his case; but where he was killed between the temple and the altar Scripture does not say, especially since in his times there were scarcely even ruins of the temple. Others want it understood as Zechariah the father of John, proving from certain dreams of the apocrypha that he was killed because he had preached the coming of the Savior. This, because it has no authority from the Scriptures, is despised with the same ease with which it is proved. Others will have this to be the Zechariah who was killed by Joash king of Judea between the temple and the altar, as the history of Kings relates. But it must be observed that that Zechariah is not the son of Barachiah but the son of Jehoiada the priest. Hence Scripture also relates: Joash did not remember the good things that Jehoiada his father had done for him. Since, then, we have both the Zechariah and a place of killing that agrees, we ask why he is called the son of Barachiah and not of Jehoiada. Barachiah in our language means blessed of the Lord, and the justice of the priest Jehoiada is shown by his Hebrew name. In the Gospel which the Nazarenes use we find written son of Jehoiada in place of son of Barachiah. The simpler brethren, among the ruins of the temple and of the altar, or at the exits of the gates that lead to Siloam, point out red stones and think they were stained with the blood of Zechariah. We do not condemn the error, which comes down from hatred of the Jews and from the piety of faith.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.23.37":["Jerusalem, Jerusalem, you who kill the prophets and stone those who are sent to you, how often I wished to gather your children as a hen gathers her chicks under her wings, and you would not. Jerusalem (he means not the stones and buildings of the city but its inhabitants), which he laments with a father's affection, as we also read in another place that, seeing it, he wept. And in saying, how often I wished to gather your children, he testifies that all the prophets of the past were sent by him. The likeness of the hen gathering her chicks under her wings we read also in the song of Deuteronomy: As an eagle protects its nest and has longed over its young, spreading its wings it took them up and bore them on its pinions.\n— Commentary on Matthew","He calls them mother and sons, not because the mother is one thing and the sons another; but just as the Lord, speaking to the people of the Jews, said: Jerusalem, Jerusalem, who kill the prophets and stone those who are sent to you, how often I wished to gather your sons, as a hen gathered her chicks under her wings, and you would not? Not that Jerusalem was one thing and her people another; for he was not speaking to the rubble of the city and its timbers and stones apart from the people. So the multitude of the people and the whole crowd of the Hebrew nation to which he speaks is called mother; and the sons are either the individuals of the people, or those scattered through the towns and villages.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"MAT.23.38":["Behold, your house will be left to you deserted. He had said this very thing long before, in the person of Jeremiah: I have left my house, I have abandoned my inheritance; my inheritance has become to me like the cave of a hyena. We prove that the house of the Jews, that is, that temple which before shone more majestically to the eyes, is deserted, because it lost Christ its inhabitant and, eager to snatch the inheritance, killed the heir.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.23.39":["For I say to you: You will not see me henceforth until you say: Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord. He speaks to Jerusalem and to the people of the Jews. Now this verse, which the little ones too and the sucklings used at the entry of the Lord Savior into Jerusalem, when they said: Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord, Hosanna in the highest; he took from Psalm 117 (118), which was plainly written about the coming of the Lord; and by what he says he wants this understood: Unless you repent and confess that I myself am he of whom the prophets sang, the Son of the almighty Father, you will not see my face. The Jews have a time of repentance given them; let them confess him blessed who comes in the name of the Lord, and they will behold the face of Christ.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.24.1-2":["And Jesus went out from the temple and was going away. And his disciples came to him to show him the buildings of the temple. But he answered and said to them: Do you see all these things? Amen I say to you: There shall not be left here a stone upon a stone that shall not be destroyed. According to the history the sense is plain. But as the Lord withdrew from the temple, all the buildings of the law and the structure of the commandments were so destroyed that nothing can be fulfilled by the Jews, and, the head having been taken away, all the members fight against one another.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.24.3":["But as he sat upon the Mount of Olives, the disciples came to him privately, saying: Tell us when these things will be, and what is the sign of your coming and of the consummation of the age? He sat on the Mount of Olives, where the true light of knowledge was being born; and the disciples, who longed to know the mysteries and the revelation of things to come, come to him privately and ask three things: at what time Jerusalem is to be destroyed, at what time Christ will come, at what time the consummation of the age will be.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.24.5":["For many will come in my name, saying: I am the Christ, and they will lead many astray. One of these is Simon the Samaritan, of whom we read in the Acts of the Apostles, who said that he was the great power of God, and who left behind, among other things written in his books, these words too: I am the word of God, I am the beautiful one, I the Paraclete, I the almighty, I all things of God. But John the apostle too says in his letter: You have heard that antichrist is to come, but now there are many antichrists. I think that all heresiarchs are antichrists and teach, under the name of Christ, the things that are contrary to Christ. Nor is it any wonder if we see some led astray by them, since the Lord said: and they will lead many astray.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.24.6":["For you are going to hear of battles and rumors of battles; see that you are not troubled; for these things must happen, but the end is not yet. So when we see these things happen, let us not think that the day of judgment is coming in, but that it is reserved for that time whose sign is clearly set out in what follows.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.24.7":["For nation will rise against nation and kingdom against kingdom, and there will be pestilences and famines and earthquakes in various places; all these are the beginnings of sorrows. I do not doubt that these things too which are written will come to pass according to the letter; but it seems to me that kingdom against kingdom, and the pestilences of those whose speech creeps like a cancer, and the famine of hearing the word of God, and the shaking of the whole earth and the separation from the true faith, are rather to be understood of the heretics, who, fighting against one another, bring about a victory for the Church.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.24.8":["But as for what he said: But these are the beginnings of sorrows, it is better translated of birth pangs, so that the coming of antichrist may be understood as a kind of conception, as it were, not the birth.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.24.9":["Then they will deliver you up to tribulation and will kill you. Through the apostles the person of all believers is signified, not that the apostles are signified as going to be found in the body at that time.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.24.12":["And because iniquity has abounded, the love of many will grow cold. It is not of all that he denied the faith, but of many. For many are called, but few chosen. For in the apostles and those like them the love will endure of which it is written: Much water will not be able to quench love; and Paul himself: Who will separate us from the love of Christ? tribulation or distress and the rest.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.24.14":["And this Gospel of the kingdom will be preached in the whole world as a testimony to all nations, and then the consummation will come. The sign of the Lord's coming is that the Gospel be preached in the whole world, so that no one may be excusable; and this we see either already fulfilled or soon to be fulfilled. For I do not think that any nation remains which does not know the name of Christ; and although it may not have had a preacher, yet from the neighboring nations it cannot be ignorant of the report of the faith.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.24.15-18":["Then let those who are in Judea flee to the mountains, and let him who is on the roof not come down to take anything from his house, and let him who is in the field not turn back to take his tunic. The abomination of desolation can also be understood as every perverse doctrine; and when we see it standing in the holy place, that is, in the Church, and showing itself as God, we must flee from Judea to the mountains, that is, leaving behind the letter that kills and Jewish perversity, draw near to the eternal mountains from which God gives light wonderfully; and be on the roof and on the housetop, where the fiery darts of the devil cannot reach; and not come down and take anything from the house of our former way of life, nor seek the things behind, but rather sow in the field of the spiritual Scriptures so that we may gather fruit from it; nor take a second tunic, which the apostles are forbidden to have. On this passage, that is, on the abomination of desolation which was spoken of by Daniel the prophet standing in the holy place, Porphyry uttered many blasphemies against us in the thirteenth volume of his work; Eusebius, bishop of Caesarea, answered him in three volumes, the eighteenth, the nineteenth and the twentieth; Apollinaris too wrote most fully; and it was a superfluous attempt to want to discuss in one passage a matter that has been disputed in so many thousands of lines.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.24.15":["So when you see the abomination of desolation which was spoken of by Daniel the prophet standing in the holy place; let him who reads understand. When we are summoned to understanding, what was said is shown to be mystical. And we read in Daniel in this way: And in the middle of the week my sacrifice and libations will be taken away, and in the temple there will be the abomination of desolations until the consummation of time, and consummation will be given upon the desolation. Of this the Apostle also speaks, saying that the man of iniquity and the adversary is to be lifted up against everything that is called God or that is worshiped, so that he dares to stand in the temple of God and to show that he himself is God; whose coming, according to the working of Satan, is to destroy those who have received him and reduce them to a desolation of God. But it can also be taken simply, either of antichrist, or of the image of Caesar which Pilate set up in the temple, or of the equestrian statue of Hadrian which has stood in the very place of the holy of holies up to the present day. Also, according to the old Scripture, an idol is called an abomination, and for that reason of desolation is added, because the idol was set up in the desolated and destroyed temple.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.24.19":["But woe to those who are pregnant and nursing in those days. Woe to those souls which have not brought their offspring to the perfect man, but have only the beginnings of faith, so that they need the nurture of teachers. This too can be said: that in the persecution of antichrist, or of the Roman captivity, those who were pregnant and nursing, weighed down by the burden of womb and of children, could not have an unencumbered flight.\n— Commentary on Matthew","For they have crushed, or cut asunder, with iron carts the women with child of Gilead. These are the women with child of whom the Lord speaks in the Gospel: Woe to those who are with child, and to those who give suck in those days. The women with child are the souls of believers, who at the beginning of faith can say: From fear of you, Lord, we have conceived and been in labour; and if they have not been cut up and pierced with iron by the carts and the iron and toothed wheels, of which the psalmist says: The sons of men, their teeth are weapons and arrows, they can also say what follows: And we have brought forth; we have made the spirit of your salvation upon the earth. And consider at the same time that they cannot cut up men of mature age, but only those who are still in the womb, or who cannot take solid food and are still nourished with the milk of infancy.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"MAT.24.20":["But pray that your flight may not be in winter or on the Sabbath. If we choose to take this of the captivity of Jerusalem, when it was taken by Titus and Vespasian, they ought to pray that their flight may not be in winter or on the Sabbath, because in the one the harshness of the cold prevents them from going into the wildernesses and hiding in the mountains and deserts, and in the other there is either transgression of the law, if they choose to flee, or imminent death, if they remain. But if it is understood of the consummation of the world, he commands this: that our faith and our love toward Christ may not grow cold, and that we may not, idle in the work of God, grow sluggish in a Sabbath of the virtues.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.24.22":["And unless those days had been shortened, no flesh would be saved; but for the sake of the elect those days will be shortened. We ought to understand the shortened days not according to the ravings of some who think that the measures of the times are changed, and who do not remember that writing: By your ordinance the day continues, but according to the quality of the times; that is, shortened not in measure but in number, so that, just as it is said in the blessing: With length of days I will fill him, so here too the days may be understood as shortened, lest the faith of believers be shaken by the delay of the times.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.24.23":["Then if anyone says to you: Behold, here is the Christ, or there, do not believe it. At the time of the Jewish captivity many leaders arose who said that they were Christs, so much so that, while the Romans were besieging the city, there were three factions within. But it is better understood of the consummation of the world.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.24.24-25":["For false Christs and false prophets will arise and will give great signs and wonders, so that even the elect, if it can be, are led into error. Behold, I have told you beforehand. This passage, as I have already said before, is to be discussed in a threefold way: either of the time of the Roman siege, or of the consummation of the world, or of the fight of the heretics against the Church and of antichrists of this kind, who fight against Christ under the pretense of a false knowledge.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.24.26":["If therefore they say to you: Behold, he is in the desert, do not go out. Behold, in the inner rooms, do not believe it. If anyone promises you that Christ dwells in the desert of the doctrine of the Gentiles and the philosophers, or in the inner rooms of the heretics, who promise the secrets of God, do not go out, do not believe; or else: because in a time of persecution and distress false prophets always find room for deceiving, if anyone wishes to vaunt himself under the name of Christ, do not at once give him credence.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.24.27":["For as the lightning goes out from the east and appears even to the west, so also will be the coming of the Son of Man. Do not go out, do not believe that the Son of Man is either in the desert of the Gentiles or in the inner rooms of the heretics, but believe that from the east even to the west his faith shines in the catholic churches. This too must be said: that the second coming of the Savior is to be shown not in humility as before, but in glory. It is foolish, therefore, to seek in a small or hidden place him who is the light of the whole world.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.24.28":["Wherever the body is, there the eagles will be gathered together. By a natural example which we see daily we are instructed in the mystery of Christ. Eagles and vultures are said to perceive corpses even across the seas and to gather to food of this kind. If, then, irrational birds, by a natural sense, though separated by such great spaces of land and by the waves of the sea, perceive where a small corpse lies, how much more ought we and the whole multitude of believers to hasten to him whose lightning goes out from the east and appears even to the west! But by the body, that is, πτῶμα, which is more expressively called in Latin a cadaver, from the fact that it falls through death, we can understand the passion of Christ, to which we are summoned, so that wherever it is read in the Scriptures we may gather together; and through it we can come to the word of God, as in that saying: They have dug my hands and feet; and in Isaiah: As a sheep led to the slaughter, and other things like these. And the saints are called eagles, whose youth is renewed like the eagle's, and who, according to Isaiah, grow feathers and take on wings so as to come to the passion of Christ.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.24.29":["But immediately after the tribulation of those days the sun will be darkened, and the moon will not give its light, and the stars of heaven will fall from heaven, and the powers of the heavens will be shaken. The sun and moon will be darkened and will not give their light, and the other stars will fall from heaven, and the powers of the heavens will be moved, not by a diminution of light, for we read, after all, that the sun will have sevenfold light, but because in comparison with the true light all things are dark to the sight. If, then, this sun which now glows red over the whole world, and the moon which is the second luminary, and the stars which are kindled for the solace of the night, and all the powers, which we understand as the multitudes of angels, will be reckoned as darkness at the coming of Christ, let the haughty brow of those be struck down who, thinking themselves holy, do not dread the presence of the judge.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.24.30":["And then the sign of the Son of Man will appear in heaven. Let us understand the sign here either as that of the cross, so that, according to Zechariah and John, the Jews may see him whom they had pierced, or as the standard of a triumphant victory.\n— Commentary on Matthew","Then all the tribes of the earth will mourn. Those will mourn who have had no citizenship in heaven but are written on earth.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.24.31":["And he will send his angels with a trumpet. Of this trumpet the Apostle also speaks, and we read of it in the Apocalypse of John; and in the Old Testament trumpets of beaten work are commanded to be made of gold, bronze and silver, so that the sublime mysteries of the teachings may resound.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.24.32-33":["Learn a parable from the fig tree. When its branch has already become tender and the leaves have come out, you know that summer is near, and the rest. Under the example of the tree he taught the coming of the consummation. Just as, he says, when the shoots on the fig tree have become tender, and the bud breaks into flower, and the bark brings forth leaves, you understand the coming of summer and the arrival of the west wind and of spring; so when you see all these things that are written, do not think that the consummation of the world is already present, but that certain preludes and forerunners, as it were, are coming to show that it is near and at the doors.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.24.34":["Amen I say to you, that this generation will not pass away until all these things happen. We said above that the good and, on the contrary, the wicked each form a single generation. Therefore it signifies either the whole race of men or specifically that of the Jews.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.24.35":["Heaven and earth will pass away, but my words will not pass away. Heaven and earth will pass away by a change, not by an abolition of themselves; otherwise how will the sun be darkened and the moon not give its light and the stars fall, if the heaven in which these things are, and the earth, will not exist?\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.24.36":["But of that day and hour no one knows, not even the angels of heaven, but the Father alone. In some Latin codices there is added: nor the Son, whereas in the Greek copies, and especially those of Adamantius and Pierius, this is not found written; but because it is read in some, it seems that it should be discussed. Arius and Eunomius rejoice, as though the ignorance of the master were the glory of the disciples, and they say: He who knows and he who is ignorant cannot be equal. Against them these things must briefly be said: Since Jesus, that is, the Word of God, made all times, for all things indeed were made through him, and without him nothing was made; and since among all the times there is also the day of judgment, by what logic can he be ignorant of a part of that whose whole he knows? This too must be said: Which is greater, the knowledge of the Father or that of the judgment? If he knows the greater, how is he ignorant of what is less? We read it written: All things that are the Father's have been delivered to me. If all things of the Father are the Son's, for what reason did he reserve to himself the knowledge of one day and refuse to share it with the Son? But this too must be inferred: If he is ignorant of the last day of the times, he is ignorant also of the next to last and of all those before it. For it cannot be that he who is ignorant of the first knows what the second is. Therefore, since we have proved that the Son is not ignorant of the day of the consummation, a reason must be given why he is said to be ignorant of it. The Apostle writes concerning the Savior: In whom are hidden all the treasures of wisdom and knowledge. So all the treasures of wisdom and knowledge are in Christ, but they are hidden. Why hidden? After the resurrection, when asked by the apostles about the day, he answered more plainly: It is not yours to know the times and the moments which the Father has set in his own power. When he says: It is not yours to know, he shows that he himself knows, but that it is not expedient for the apostles to know, so that, always uncertain about the coming of the judge, they may live each day as though they were to be judged on the next. Finally, the words of the Gospel that follow also compel the same understanding, saying too that the Father alone knows. In the Father he includes the Son as well; for every father is the name of a son.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.24.37-39":["But as in the days of Noah, so also will be the coming of the Son of Man. For as in the days before the flood they were eating and drinking, marrying and giving in marriage, and the rest. It is asked how it is written above: For nation will rise against nation and kingdom against kingdom, and there will be pestilences and famines and earthquakes, and now those things are mentioned as about to come which are signs of peace. But it must be reckoned, following the Apostle, that after the battles and dissensions, the pestilences, famines and earthquakes, and the other things by which the human race is laid waste, a brief peace will follow, which will promise that all things are quiet, so that the faith of believers may be tested, whether, when the evils have passed, they still hope that the judge is to come. For this is what we read in Paul: When they say: Peace and security, then sudden destruction will come upon them, like the pain of a woman in labor, and they will not escape.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.24.40-41":["Then two will be in the field; one will be taken and one will be left; two women grinding at the mill; one will be taken and the other will be left. Then, he says, two will be in the field, namely when, at the time of the consummation and judgment, two will be found together in the field, having the same labor and, as it were, an equal sowing, but not receiving the fruit of their labor equally; two women also will be grinding together; one will be taken and the other left. In the two who are staying in the field and in the two women who grind together, understand either the synagogue and the Church, because they seem to grind together in the law and to grind the flour of God's commandments from the same Scriptures; or the other heresies, which seem to grind the flour of their doctrines from both Testaments or from one of them, and which, though they have one profession of the Christian name, will not receive the same reward, some being taken and others left behind.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.24.42":["Watch therefore, because you do not know at what hour your Lord is to come. He shows clearly why he said above: But of that day no one knows, neither the Son of Man nor the angels, but the Father alone, namely because it is not expedient for the apostles to know, so that, in the uncertainty of a suspended expectation, they may always believe that he is about to come of whom they do not know when he will come. And he did not say: because we do not know at what hour the Lord is to come, but you do not know, and having set forth the example of the householder, he teaches more plainly why he keeps silent about the day of the consummation, saying:\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.24.44-46":["Be ready, because you do not know at what hour the Son of Man is to come. Who, do you think, is the faithful and prudent servant whom his master has set over his household, to give them food in due season? Blessed is that servant whom his master, when he comes, finds so doing. He presses home more fully and repeats why he foretold that neither the angels nor he himself knows the day and hour of the consummation, but only the Father: because it is not expedient for the apostles to know; and he inserts the example of the householder, that is, of himself, and of the faithful servants, that is, of the apostles, as an exhortation to a watchful mind, so that in hope of rewards they may minister to their fellow servants in due season the food of the teachings.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.24.48-49":["But if that wicked servant says in his heart: My master is delaying his coming, and begins to strike his fellow servants, and the rest. It depends on what precedes: that just as the watchful servant, always awaiting his master's coming, hands out food to his fellow servants in due season and afterward is set over all the goods of the householder, so, on the contrary, he who, in Ezekiel's words, says: This will come to pass in distant times, and does not think that the master will come soon, having grown more careless, gives himself to feasts and luxury, and will find not a gentle householder but a most severe judge.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.24.50-51":["The master of that servant will come on a day when he does not expect and at an hour he does not know, and will divide him and put his portion with the hypocrites. He teaches this very thing: that they should know that the Lord will come at the time when he is not expected; and he admonishes the stewards to vigilance and watchfulness. Moreover, as for his saying: will divide him, it does not mean that he will cut him in two with a sword, but that he will separate him from the fellowship of the saints and put his portion with the hypocrites, namely with those who were in the field and who were grinding and were nonetheless left behind. We have often said that a hypocrite is one who is one thing and shows another, just as both in the field and at the mill he seemed to do the same as the man of the Church, but the outcome revealed a different will.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.25.1-4":["Then the kingdom of heaven will be like ten virgins who, taking their lamps, went out to meet the bridegroom and the bride; but five of them were foolish and five wise, and the rest. This parable, that is, likeness, of the ten foolish and wise virgins some interpret simply of virgins, of whom some, according to the Apostle, are virgins both in body and in mind, while others, preserving only the virginity of their bodies, either have other works not matching their profession, or, kept safe by the guardianship of their parents, have nonetheless married in mind. But to me it seems, from what precedes, that what is said has another sense, and that the comparison pertains not to virginal bodies but to the whole race of men. For just as the two in the field and the two women grinding signify two peoples, of Christians and of Jews, or of saints and of sinners, who, though placed in the Church, seem indeed themselves to plow and to grind but do everything in hypocrisy, so now too the ten virgins embrace all men who seem to believe in God and pride themselves on the holy Scriptures, men of the Church as well as Jews and heretics. They are all called virgins for this reason, that they glory in the knowledge of the one God and their mind is not defiled by the throng of idolatry. Those virgins have oil who, besides their faith, are adorned with works too; those do not have oil who seem indeed to confess the Lord with a like faith but neglect the works of the virtues. We can interpret the five wise and the five foolish virgins as the five senses, of which some hasten toward heavenly things and long for the things above, while others, gaping after the dregs of the earth, do not have the fuel of truth with which to light up their hearts. Of sight and hearing and touch it is said spiritually: What we have seen, what we have heard, what we have looked upon with our eyes and our hands have handled; of taste: Taste and see that the Lord is sweet; of smell: We run in the fragrance of your ointments; and: We are the good fragrance of Christ.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.25.1":["But they also refer to the mystery of this number the parable of the ten virgins in the Gospel, whom we interpret of the senses of the flesh and of the spirit, if they have prepared the oil of good works for their lamps, and have doubled the number five, so as to be holy, according to the Apostle, in body and in spirit.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"MAT.25.5":["But while the bridegroom delayed, they all grew drowsy and slept. For no little time passes between the first and the second coming of the Lord. They all grew drowsy, that is, they died, because the death of the saints is called sleep. But it then says they slept, because they are afterward to be raised.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.25.6":["But at midnight a cry arose: Behold, the bridegroom comes; go out to meet him. For suddenly, as in the dead of night, when all are unconcerned and sleep is at its heaviest, the coming of Christ will resound through the cry of the angels and the trumpets of the powers that go before him. Let us say something that may perhaps be useful to the reader. It is a tradition of the Jews that Christ will come at midnight, after the likeness of the time in Egypt, when the Passover was celebrated and the destroyer came and the Lord passed over the tents and the doorposts of our foreheads were consecrated with the blood of the lamb. Hence I think the apostolic tradition too has persisted, that on the day of the vigil of the Passover it is not permitted to dismiss the people, who are awaiting the coming of Christ, before the middle of the night; and that after that time has passed, confidence being presumed, all keep the day as a feast. Hence the psalmist too said: At midnight I rose to give thanks to you over the judgments of your justice.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.25.7":["Then all those virgins arose and trimmed their lamps. All the virgins arose and each trimmed her lamps, that is, the senses, in which they received the oil of knowledge, so as to nourish the works of the virtues that would shine out before the true judge.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.25.8":["But the foolish said to the wise: Give us some of your oil, because our lamps are going out. Those who complain that their lamps are going out show that they are partly shining, and yet they do not have an unfailing light nor lasting works. If anyone, therefore, has a virginal soul and is a lover of chastity, he must not be content with middling virtues, which quickly fade and wither when the heat comes up, but let him pursue perfect virtues, so that he may have everlasting light.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.25.9":["The prudent answered and said: Lest perhaps there not be enough for us and for you. They answer this not out of avarice but out of fear. For each will receive the wage for his own works, nor on the day of judgment can the virtues of some relieve the vices of others. And just as in the time of the Babylonian captivity Jeremiah could not help the sinners, and it is said to him: Do not pray for this people, so fearful will that day be, when each will be anxious for himself.\n— Commentary on Matthew","Go rather to those who sell and buy for yourselves. This oil is sold, and bought at a great price, and acquired by hard labor; we understand it of almsgiving and all the virtues and the counsels of teachers.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.25.10":["But while they were going to buy, the bridegroom came. They give advice indeed, as prudent ones, that they ought not to go to meet the bridegroom without oil for their lamps. But because the time for buying had already gone by, and with the day of judgment arriving there was no place for repentance, as the psalmist says: But in hell who will give thanks to you, they are compelled not to accomplish new works but to render an account of past ones.\n— Commentary on Matthew","The bridegroom came, and those who were ready went in with him to the wedding, and the door was shut. After the day of judgment no opportunity for good works and justice will be left.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.25.11":["Last of all the other virgins also come, saying: Lord, Lord, open to us. Excellent indeed is the confession in calling him Lord, and its repetition is a sign of faith. But what does it profit to call with the voice on him whom you deny by your works?\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.25.12":["But he answered and said: Amen I say to you: I do not know you. The Lord knows those who are his, and he who does not know will not be known. The Lord does not know the workers of iniquity; and although they are virgins and, according to the twofold understanding, glory both in purity of body and in the confession of the true faith, yet, because they do not have the oil of knowledge, it is enough for their punishment that they are not known by the bridegroom.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.25.13":["Watch therefore, because you know neither the day nor the hour. I always warn the prudent reader not to acquiesce in superstitious interpretations and in things said commatically, phrase by phrase, at the whim of those who invent them, but to consider what comes before, what lies in the middle and what follows, and to bind together for himself all that is written. And so from this which he adds: Watch, because you know neither the day nor the hour, it is understood that all that he said, that is, about the two who are in the field, and the two women grinding, and the householder who entrusted his substance to his servant, and the ten virgins, these parables were set forth beforehand so that, because we men do not know the day of judgment, we may carefully prepare for ourselves the light of good works, lest the judge come while we do not know it.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.25.14-15":["For just as a man setting out on a journey called his servants and handed over his goods to them, and to one he gave five talents, to another two, and to another one. This man, the householder, is beyond doubt Christ, who, ascending as victor to the Father after the resurrection, called the apostles and handed over to them the teaching of the Gospel, giving more to one and less to another, not out of lavishness or stinginess but according to the strength of those who received it, just as the Apostle too says that he gave milk to drink to those who could not take solid food. In short, he receives with like joy both the one who had made ten out of five talents and the one who had made four out of two, considering not the size of the profit but the will behind the effort. In the five and the two and the one talent let us understand either the diverse graces that have been handed over to each, or else in the first all the senses tested, in the second understanding and works, in the third reason, by which we men are set apart from the beasts.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.25.16":["Then he who had received five talents went away and traded with them; he gained five more. Having received earthly senses, he doubled for himself the knowledge of heavenly things, understanding the Creator from creatures, the incorporeal from the corporeal, the invisible from the visible, the eternal from the brief.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.25.17":["He who had received two; he gained two more. This one too, according to his strength, doubled in the Gospel whatever he had learned in the law; or else he understood both the knowledge and the works of the present life as types of the future blessedness.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.25.18":["But he who had received one went off, dug in the earth and hid his lord's money. The wicked servant, by earthly works and the pleasure of the world, neglected and defiled the commandments of God; although in another evangelist it is written that he tied it up in a napkin, that is, that by living softly and delicately he enfeebled the teaching of the householder.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.25.19":["But after a long time the lord of those servants came. A great time lies between the ascension of the Savior and his second coming. But if the apostles are to render an account and to rise again under fear of the judge, what ought we to do?\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.25.21-23":["His lord said to him: Well done, good and faithful servant; because you were faithful over a few things, I will set you over many; enter into the joy of your lord. To both servants, as I have already said before, both the one who had made ten out of five talents and the one who had made four out of two, the same word of the householder speaks kindly. And it is to be noted that all the things we have in the present, though they seem great and very many, are yet small and few in comparison with the things to come. Enter, he says, into the joy of your lord, and receive what neither eye has seen nor ear heard, nor has it ascended into the heart of man. But what greater thing can be given to a faithful servant than to be with his lord and to see the joy of his lord?\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.25.24-25":["And he too who had received one talent came forward and said: Lord, I know that you are a hard man; you reap where you did not sow and gather where you did not scatter; and being afraid I went off and hid your talent in the earth; here you have what is yours. Truly, what is written: To make excuses in sins, befell this servant also, so that to sloth and negligence the charge of pride was added as well. For he who ought simply to have confessed his idleness and entreated the householder, on the contrary slanders him and says that he acted on prudent counsel, lest while he sought profit on the money he should put even the principal at risk.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.25.26-28":["But his lord answered and said to him: Wicked and lazy servant, you knew that I reap where I do not sow and gather where I did not scatter; you ought therefore to have put my money with the money-changers, and on coming I would surely have received what is mine with interest; take therefore the talent from him and give it to him who has ten talents. What he had thought he said by way of excuse is turned into his own guilt. He is called a wicked servant because he slanders his lord, lazy because he would not double the talent, so that in the one he is condemned for pride, in the other for negligence. If, he says, you knew that I am hard and cruel, and that I go after what belongs to others and reap there where I have not sown, why did not a thought of this kind strike fear into you, so that you would know that I would seek out my own more diligently, and would give my money, or silver, to the money-changers? For the Greek word ἀργύριον signifies both. The words, he says, of the Lord are pure words, silver tried by fire, proved, purged of earth sevenfold. The money, then, and the silver is the preaching of the Gospel and the divine word, which ought to have been given to the money-changers and bankers, that is, either to the other teachers, as the apostles also did, ordaining presbyters and bishops in the several provinces, or to all believers who can double the money and return it with interest, so that whatever they had learned by word they might fulfill in deed. Now the talent is taken away and given to him who had made ten talents, so that we may understand that, although the Lord's joy over the labor of both is equal, that is, both of him who had doubled five into ten and of him who had doubled two into four, yet a greater reward is owed to him who had labored more over the Lord's money. Hence the Apostle too says: Honor the presbyters who are truly presbyters, especially those who labor in the word of God. From the fact that the wicked servant dared to say: you reap where you did not sow and gather where you did not scatter, we understand that the Lord accepts the good life even of Gentiles and philosophers, and regards in one way those who act justly and in another those who act unjustly, and that, in comparison with him who serves the natural law, those who neglect the written law are condemned.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.25.29":["For to everyone who has will be given, and he will abound; but from him who does not have, even what he seems to have will be taken away from him. Many, though they are wise by nature and have keenness of mind, if they have been negligent and have spoiled the good of nature by sloth, in comparison with one who is somewhat slower but has made up by labor and diligence for what he had less of, lose the good of nature and see the reward that had been promised them pass to others. It can also be understood thus: to him who has faith and a good will in the Lord, even if, being a man, he has had something less in works, it will be given by the good judge; but he who has not had faith will lose even the other virtues that he seemed to possess by nature. And elegantly too: even what he seems, he says, to have will be taken away from him. For whatever is without faith in Christ ought not to be credited to him who has badly misused it, but to him who granted the good of nature even to a wicked servant.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.25.30":["And cast the useless servant into the outer darkness; there will be weeping and gnashing of teeth. The Lord is light. Whoever is sent out by him lacks the true light. But what the weeping and gnashing of teeth is, we have said above.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.25.31":["But when the Son of Man comes in his majesty, and all the angels with him, then he will sit upon the seat of his majesty, and all the nations will be gathered before him, and he will separate them from one another as a shepherd separates the sheep from the goats, and he will set the sheep on his right, but the goats on his left. About to keep the Passover after two days, and to be handed over to the cross, and to be mocked by men, and to be given vinegar and gall to drink, he rightly promises the glory of one in triumph, so that he might offset the stumbling blocks that were to follow with the reward of the promise. And it is to be noted that he who is to be seen in majesty is the Son of Man.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.25.33":["And what follows: he will set the sheep on his right, but the goats on his left, understand according to what you read elsewhere: The heart of the wise man is in his right hand, and the heart of the fool in his left, and above in this same Gospel: Let your left hand not know what your right hand is doing. The sheep, on the side of the just, are bidden to stand at the right; the goats, that is, sinners, at the left, goats being always offered for sin in the law. Nor did he say she-goats, which can bear young and, when shorn, come up from the washing, all with twin offspring, and there is no barren one among them, but goats, a wanton and butting animal, always burning for coupling.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.25.34":["Come, blessed of my Father, possess the kingdom prepared for you from the foundation of the world. This is to be taken according to the foreknowledge of God, with whom future things are already done.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.25.40":["Amen I say to you: Inasmuch as you did it to one of these least brothers of mine, you did it to me. We would have been free to understand that in every poor man Christ, hungry, is fed; thirsty, is given drink; a stranger, is brought under a roof; naked, is clothed; sick, is visited; shut in prison, has the comfort of one who talks with him. But from what follows: When you did it to one of these least brothers of mine, you did it to me, he does not seem to me to have spoken generally of the poor, but of those who are poor in spirit, toward whom he stretched out his hand and had said: My brothers and my mother are these who do the will of my Father.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.25.46":["And these will go into eternal punishment, but the just into eternal life. Prudent reader, observe that both the punishments are eternal and the perpetual life henceforth has no fear of falling.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.26.2":["And it came to pass, when Jesus had finished all these sayings, he said to his disciples: You know that after two days the Passover will take place, and the Son of Man will be handed over to be crucified. Let those blush who think that the Savior feared death and said from dread of the passion: Father, if it can be done, let this cup pass from me. About to keep the Passover after two days, he knows that he is to be handed over to be crucified, and yet he does not avoid the snares, he does not flee in terror; so much so that, even when the others were unwilling to go, he goes on undaunted, when Thomas says: Let us go, that we too may die with him. And, wishing to put an end to the carnal festival and, as the shadow passed away, to give back the truth of the Passover, he said: With desire I have desired to eat this Passover with you before I suffer. For Christ our Passover has been sacrificed, if only we eat it in the unleavened bread of sincerity and truth. Moreover, as to his saying: After two days the Passover will take place, let us pass over the simple understanding and seek what is hallowed in it. After two days of the most brilliant light of the Old and the New Testament, the true Passover is celebrated for the world. The Passover (pascha), which in Hebrew is called phase, is named not from the passion, as most people think, but from the passing over, because the destroyer, seeing the blood on the doors of the Israelites, passed over and did not strike them, or else the Lord himself, bringing help to his people, walked above them. Read the book of Exodus, of which we shall treat more fully, if life is granted to us. But our passing over, that is, phase, is celebrated in this way: if, leaving behind earthly things and Egypt, we hasten to heavenly things.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.26.3-5":["Then the chief priests and the elders of the people gathered in the court of the chief priest, who was called Caiaphas; and they took counsel to seize Jesus by guile and kill him. Those who, with the Passover near, ought to have been preparing the victims, polishing the walls of the temple, sweeping the pavements, cleaning the vessels, and purifying themselves according to the rite of the law, so that they might become worthy to eat the lamb, gather together and enter into counsel how they may kill the Lord: not fearing sedition, as the simple wording shows, but taking care lest he be taken out of their hands by the help of the people.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.26.6":["Now when Jesus was in Bethany, in the house of Simon the leper. About to suffer for the whole world and to redeem all the nations by his blood, he stays in Bethany, the house of obedience, which once was the house of Simon the leper: not that he still remained a leper at that time, but he who had been a leper before was afterwards cleansed by the Savior, the former name remaining so that the power of the one who healed might appear. For in the catalogue of the apostles too Matthew is called the tax collector, with his former fault and occupation, though he had certainly ceased to be a tax collector. Some want the house of Simon the leper to be understood as that part of the people which believed in the Lord and was healed by him. Simon himself, too, is said to mean obedient; according to another understanding he can be interpreted as pure, and in his house the Church was healed.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.26.7":["A woman came to him having an alabaster of precious ointment, and she poured it over his head as he reclined. Let no one think that she who poured ointment over the head is the same as she who poured it over the feet. For the latter both washes with tears and wipes with her hair and is openly called a harlot, whereas of this one nothing of the kind is written. Nor indeed could a harlot at once become worthy of the Lord's head. Another evangelist, in place of the alabaster of precious ointment (which is a kind of marble), put pistic nard, that is, true and without guile, to show the faith of the Church and of the Gentiles.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.26.8-9":["But the disciples, seeing it, were indignant, saying: To what purpose is this waste? For this could have been sold for much and given to the poor. I know that some cavil at this passage, asking why another evangelist said that Judas alone was saddened, because he held the purse and had been a thief from the beginning, while Matthew writes that all the apostles were indignant; they do not know the figure that is called σύλληψις (syllepsis), by which all are customarily named for one and one for many. For Paul the apostle too, in his letter which is written to the Hebrews (although many of the Latins have doubts about it), when he had described the sufferings and merits of the saints, added: They were stoned, they were tempted, they were sawn asunder, they died by the slaughter of the sword, though the Jews hold that only one, the prophet Isaiah, was sawn. We can also say otherwise: that the apostles were truly indignant on account of the poor, but Judas on account of his own gain. Hence his muttering too is set down together with a charge: that he had no care for the poor but wished to provide for his own theft.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.26.11":["But Jesus, knowing it, said to them: Why do you trouble the woman? She has wrought a good work on me; for you always have the poor with you, but me you will not always have. Another question arises: why the Lord said to the disciples after the resurrection: Behold, I am with you even to the consummation of the world, and now says: but me you will not always have. But it seems to me that in this place he speaks of his bodily presence: that after the resurrection he would by no means be with them in such a way as he is now, in all shared life and familiarity. Mindful of this, the Apostle says: And if we had known Jesus Christ according to the flesh, yet now we know him no longer.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.26.12":["For in putting this ointment on my body, she has done it for my burial. What you think to be a waste of ointment is the office of burial. Nor is it strange if she has given me the good odor of her faith, since I am going to pour out my blood for her.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.26.13":["Amen I say to you: Wherever this Gospel shall be preached in the whole world, what she has done shall also be told in memory of her. In the whole world it is not so much this woman as the Church that is proclaimed, because she buried the Savior, because she anointed his head. And note his knowledge of things to come: that, about to suffer after two days and to die, he knows that his Gospel is to be celebrated in all the world.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.26.15":["And he said to them: What will you give me, and I will hand him over to you? But they fixed for him thirty pieces of silver. Unhappy Judas! The loss which he believed he had suffered from the pouring out of the ointment he wishes to make good with the price of his master. And yet he does not demand a fixed sum, so that the betrayal might at least seem profitable, but, as though handing over a cheap slave, he left it in the power of the buyers to give as much as they wished. Who fixed for him thirty pieces of silver. Joseph was not sold, as many think, for twenty pieces of gold, according to the LXX translators, but, according to the Hebrew truth, for twenty pieces of silver. For the servant could not be more precious than the master.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.26.17":["Now on the first day of the Unleavened Bread the disciples came to Jesus, saying: Where do you wish us to prepare for you to eat the Passover? The first day of the Unleavened Bread is the fourteenth day of the first month, when the lamb is sacrificed and the moon is at its fullest and leaven is cast out. And among those disciples who came to the Lord asking: Where do you wish us to prepare for you to eat the Passover, I reckon that Judas the betrayer was also present.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.26.18":["But Jesus said: Go into the city to a certain man. The new Scripture keeps the custom of the Old Testament. We often read: So-and-so said to so-and-so; and: In such and such a place, which in Hebrew is called phelmoni and helmoni; and yet the name of the persons and of the places is not given. And you will find, he says, a certain man carrying a jar of water. Their names were passed over for this reason: that a free occasion for the festival might be opened to all who are going to keep the Passover.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.26.19":["And the disciples did as Jesus had appointed them, and they prepared the Passover. In another evangelist it is written that they found a large upper room, furnished and cleaned, and there they made ready for him. But it seems to me that the upper room is to be understood as the spiritual law, which, going out from the narrowness of the letter, receives the Savior in a lofty place, Paul saying this very thing: that the things which he formerly reckoned as gain he despised as refuse and rubbish, so that he might prepare a lodging worthy of the Lord.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.26.20":["When evening had come, he reclined with the twelve disciples. Judas does everything so that suspicion of the betrayer may be removed.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.26.21":["And as they were eating he said: Amen I say to you that one of you is going to betray me. He who had foretold his passion foretells the betrayer too, giving room for repentance, so that, when he understood that his thoughts and secret counsels were known, he might repent of his deed; and yet he does not point him out specifically, lest, being openly convicted, he should become more shameless. He casts the charge among the whole number, so that the one who is conscious of it may repent.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.26.22":["And being greatly saddened they began each to say: Is it I, Lord? And surely the eleven apostles knew that they were planning nothing of the kind against the Lord; but they trust the master more than themselves, and, fearing their own frailty, they sorrowfully ask about a sin of which they had no consciousness.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.26.23":["But he answered and said: He who dips his hand with me in the dish, he will betray me. O the wonderful patience of the Lord! First he had said: One of you is going to betray me. The betrayer persists in evil; he reproves him more plainly, and yet he does not point out his name specifically. Judas, while the others were saddened and drew back their hands and forbade food to their mouths, with the same rashness and shamelessness with which he was going to betray him, even puts his hand into the dish with his master, so that by his boldness he might feign a good conscience.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.26.24":["The Son of Man indeed goes as it is written of him; but woe to that man by whom the Son of Man will be betrayed. Reproved neither the first time nor the second, he does not draw back his foot from the betrayal; rather, he feeds his shamelessness on the patience of the Lord and stores up for himself wrath on the day of wrath. Punishment is foretold, so that the torments announced might correct one whom shame had not overcome. But as to what follows:\n\nIt would have been good for him if that man had not been born, he is not on that account to be thought to have existed before he was born, on the ground that it can be well with no one except one who has existed; it is simply said that it is much better not to exist than to exist badly.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.26.25":["And Judas, who betrayed him, answered and said: Is it I, rabbi? He said to him: You have said it. Because the others, sad and very sad, had asked: Is it I, Lord, lest he should seem to give himself away by keeping silent, he too, whom his conscience was gnawing, who had boldly put his hand into the dish, asks in the same way: Is it I, rabbi, and adds the feeling of one who flatters, or else a sign of unbelief. For the others, who were not going to betray him, say: Is it I, Lord; this one, who was going to betray him, calls him not lord but master, as if he would have an excuse if, having denied him as lord, he had at least betrayed a master. And he said to him: You have said it. The betrayer is refuted by the same reply with which he will afterwards answer Pilate.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.26.26-28":["And while they were dining, Jesus took bread and blessed and broke it, and gave it to his disciples and said: Take and eat, this is my body. And taking the cup he gave thanks and gave it to them, saying, and the rest. After the typical Passover had been fulfilled and he had eaten the flesh of the lamb with the apostles, he takes bread, which strengthens the heart of man, and passes over to the true sacrament of the Passover, so that, just as Melchizedek, the priest of the most high God, had done in prefiguration of it when he offered bread and wine, he himself also might represent it in the truth of his own body and blood. In Luke we read of two cups with which he drank to the disciples, one of the first month and the other of the second, so that he who could not eat the lamb among the saints in the first month may eat the kid among the penitents in the second month.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.26.29":["But I say to you: I will not drink from now on of this fruit of the vine until that day when I drink it new with you in the kingdom of my Father. He passes from carnal things to spiritual ones. That the vine transplanted from Egypt is the people of Israel, to whom the Lord speaks through Jeremiah: I planted you a true vine; how have you been changed into the bitterness of a foreign vine, both Isaiah the prophet testifies, in the song which he sings to his beloved, and all Scripture here and there. The Lord therefore says that he will by no means drink of this vine except in the kingdom of his Father. The kingdom of the Father I take to be the faith of believers, the Apostle also confirming this very thing: The kingdom of God is within you. Therefore, when the Jews have received the kingdom of the Father (note that he says Father, not God: every name of Father refers to a son), when, I say, they have believed in God the Father and the Father has brought them to the Son, then the Lord will drink of their wine, and, in the likeness of Joseph reigning in Egypt, he will be made drunk with his brothers.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.26.30":["And when a hymn had been said, they went out to the Mount of Olives. This is what we read in a certain psalm: They have eaten and worshiped, all the fat ones of the earth. According to this example, whoever has been filled and made drunk with the bread and the cup of the Savior can praise the Lord and ascend the Mount of Olives, where there is refreshment from labors, solace for pain, and the knowledge of the true light.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.26.31":["But I am amazed that some wish to thin out with allegorical interpretations this prophecy — which the evangelist Matthew referred to the Lord and Savior after the disciples fled at his passion, and says that it was then fulfilled —, and, while they want to seem to know more than the rest, do not hold to the rule of truth. For the evangelist Matthew relates: Then Jesus says to them: You will all take offense at me this night. For it is written: I will strike the shepherd and the sheep of the flock shall be scattered. And again: But all this was done that the scriptures of the prophets might be fulfilled. Then all the disciples, leaving him, fled. Nor should it be thought that the testimony was taken from another place because in the Gospel God says that the shepherd was struck by himself, while in the present place we read that the sword and the point are commanded: Strike the shepherd, and the sheep shall be scattered.\n— Commentary on Zechariah","Then Jesus says to them: All of you will suffer a stumbling block in me on this night. He foretells what they are going to suffer, so that, when they have suffered it, they may not despair of salvation, but may be set free by repenting. And he pointedly added: on this night you will suffer a stumbling block, because, just as those who get drunk get drunk at night, so also those who suffer a stumbling block undergo it in the night and in darkness. But let us say: The night has passed, and the day has drawn near.\n— Commentary on Matthew","For it is written: I will strike the shepherd, and the sheep of the flock will be scattered. This is written in other words in Zechariah the prophet, and, unless I am mistaken, it is said in the person of the prophet to God: Strike the shepherd, and let the sheep be scattered, Psalm 68 (69) also, which is sung throughout in the Lord's person, agreeing with this sense: For they have persecuted him whom you have struck. And the good shepherd is struck so that he may lay down his life for his sheep, and that out of many flocks of errors there may be made one flock and one shepherd. Of this testimony more has been said in the little book which we wrote on the best kind of translating.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.26.33":["But Peter answered and said to him: Even if all are scandalized, I will never be scandalized. It is not rashness, nor is it a lie; it is the faith of the apostle Peter and his burning affection toward the Lord Savior, of which we spoke above.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.26.34":["Jesus said to him: Amen I say to you that on this night, before the cock crows, you will deny me three times. Peter made his promise out of the ardor of faith, and the Savior, as God, knew what was to come. And note that Peter denies in the night, and denies a third time; but after the cock crowed and, as the darkness waned, the approaching light was announced, he turned and wept bitterly, washing away the filth of the denial with tears.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.26.36":["Then Jesus came with them to an estate which is called Gethsemane, and he said to his disciples: Sit here while I go over there and pray. Gethsemane is interpreted most fertile valley; there he ordered the disciples to sit for a little while and to wait for his return, while the Lord alone prayed for all.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.26.37":["And taking Peter and the two sons of Zebedee, he began to be saddened and to be sorrowful. What we said above about passion and propassion is shown also in the present passage: that the Lord, in order to prove the reality of the man he had assumed, was indeed truly saddened, but, lest passion should rule in his soul, began to be saddened by way of propassion. For it is one thing to be saddened and another to begin to be saddened. Yet he was saddened not by fear of suffering, he who had come for this very purpose, that he might suffer, and who had rebuked Peter for timidity, but on account of the most unhappy Judas, and the stumbling block of all the apostles, and the rejection of the people of the Jews, and the overthrow of wretched Jerusalem. Hence Jonah too is saddened over the withering of the gourd or ivy, not wishing his former shelter to perish. But if the heretics interpret the sadness of soul not as the Savior's affection toward those who would perish but as passion, let them answer how they expound what is said in the person of God through Ezekiel: And in all these things you saddened me.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.26.38":["Then he said to them: My soul is sad even to death; stay here and keep watch with me. What is saddened is the soul; and it is saddened not on account of death but even to death, until he should free the apostles by his passion. But as to his command: stay here and keep watch with me, he does not forbid them sleep, for which it was not the time with the danger at hand, but the sleep of unbelief and the torpor of the mind. Let those who suspect that Jesus took on an irrational soul tell us how he is saddened and knows the time of sadness. For although brute animals too grieve, yet they know neither the causes nor the time up to which they ought to be saddened.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.26.39":["And in the passion: Father, if it be possible, he says, let this cup pass from me (Matt 26:39). This place has this sense: If it can be that the multitude of the nations believe without the destruction of the Jews, I refuse the passion. But if they are to be blinded so that all nations may see, Father, let your will be done.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","But if anyone should object the brook Cherith, from which Elijah drank, and another torrent, from which the Lord drank on the way, for so it is written: He will drink from the torrent on the way, this must be said: that whoever is in Egypt and on the way of this age, though he be Moses and Aaron, though he be Jeremiah and Elijah, must nevertheless drink of the temptations of Egypt and of the wilderness; hence also the Word of the Lord, who had assumed flesh for this, that he might drink from the torrent, considering his majesty, said: Father, if it is possible, let this cup pass from me. Again, seeing that he was in Egypt, and that the waters could not be cleansed unless he himself had drunk, he says: Nevertheless not as I will, but as you will.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk","And going forward a little he fell on his face, worshiping and saying: My Father, if it is possible, let this cup pass from me; yet not as I will, but as you. Having given the apostles the command to stay and keep watch with the Lord, he goes forward a little, falls on his face, and shows the humility of his mind by the posture of his flesh; and he says tenderly: My Father, and asks that, if it is possible, the cup of the passion, of which we spoke above, may pass from him. But he asks this not from fear of suffering but from mercy toward the former people, lest he drink the cup handed to him by them. Hence he pointedly did not say: let the cup pass from me, but this cup, that is, the cup of the people of the Jews, which can have no excuse of ignorance if it kills me, since it has the Law and the Prophets who prophesy of me daily. Nevertheless, returning to himself, what he had shrunk from in trepidation in the person of a man, he confirms in that of God and of the Son: yet not as I will, but as you. Let not this be done, he says, which I speak from human feeling, but that for which I came down to earth by your will.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.26.40":["And he came to the disciples and found them sleeping, and he says to Peter: So you could not keep watch with me one hour? He who had said above: Even if all are scandalized in you, I will never be scandalized, now cannot overcome sleep for the greatness of his sadness.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.26.41":["Keep watch and pray, that you may not enter into temptation. It is impossible for the human soul not to be tempted. Hence in the Lord's prayer too we say: Lead us not into temptation, namely the temptation which we cannot bear, not refusing temptation altogether but praying for the strength to endure in temptations. So here too he does not say: Keep watch and pray that you may not be tempted, but that you may not enter into temptation, that is, lest temptation overcome and conquer you and hold you within its toils. For example, the martyr who shed his blood for confessing the Lord was indeed tempted, but was not bound in the nets of temptations; but he who denies runs into the snares of temptation.\n— Commentary on Matthew","The spirit indeed is willing, but the flesh is weak. This is against the rash, who think that whatever they have believed they can attain. So, as much as we trust in the ardor of the mind, so much let us fear from the frailty of the flesh. And yet, according to the Apostle, the works of the flesh are put to death by the spirit.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.26.42":["Again a second time he went away and prayed, saying: My Father, if this cup cannot pass unless I drink it, your will be done. A second time he prays that, if Nineveh cannot be saved otherwise than by the withering of the gourd, the will of the Father may be done, which is not contrary to the will of the Son, as he himself says through the prophet: To do your will, my God, I have willed.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.26.43":["And he came again and found them sleeping; for their eyes were heavy. He alone prays for all, just as he alone suffers for all. But the eyes of the apostles were growing faint and weighed down, with the denial near.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.26.44-45":["Then he comes to his disciples and says to them: Sleep now and take your rest; behold, the hour has drawn near. After he had prayed a third time, so that in the mouth of two and three witnesses every word might stand, and had obtained that the fear of the apostles should be corrected by the repentance that followed, secure about his passion he goes on to meet the persecutors and offers himself of his own accord to be killed, and he says to his disciples:\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.26.46":["Rise, let us go; behold, he who is going to betray me has drawn near. Let them not find us as though afraid and drawing back; let us go of our own accord to death, so that they may see the confidence and joy of one who is about to suffer.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.26.48":["Now he who betrayed him had given them a sign, saying: Whomever I kiss, he is the one; seize him. Wretched Judas, and yet not to be pitied! With the same unbelief with which he betrayed his master and Lord, he thought that the signs which he had seen the Savior performing were done not by divine majesty but by magic arts; and because he had perhaps heard that he was transfigured on the mountain, he feared that by a similar transformation he might slip out of the hands of the attendants. So he gives a sign, so that they might know that the one he pointed out with a kiss was the man.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.26.49":["And at once coming up to Jesus, he said: Hail, rabbi, and he kissed him. Shameless indeed and wicked is the confidence that calls him master and presses a kiss on the one whom he was betraying. Yet he still has something of a disciple's modesty, since he does not hand him over openly to the persecutors, but through the sign of a kiss. This is the sign which God set on Cain, so that whoever found him should not kill him.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.26.50":["And Jesus said to him: Friend, for what have you come? The word friend is to be understood either κατὰ ἀντίφρασιν (by antiphrasis), or at any rate according to what we read above: Friend, how did you come in here not having a wedding garment.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.26.51":["And behold, one of those who were with Jesus, stretching out his hand, drew his sword, and striking the servant of the chief priest cut off his ear. In another Gospel it is written that Peter did this, with the same ardor of mind as in everything else. The servant of the chief priest, too, is called Malchus; and the ear that is cut off is the right one. It should be said in passing that Malchus, that is, king, the people of the Jews, once became the servant of the impiety and greed of the priests, and lost its right ear, so as to hear the whole meanness of the letter with the left; but the Lord, in those of the Jews who were willing to believe, restored the right ear and made the servant a royal and priestly race.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.26.52":["Then Jesus said to him: Turn your sword back into its place; for all who take the sword will perish by the sword. Although he does not bear the sword in vain who has been appointed the avenger of the Lord's wrath against him who does evil, nevertheless whoever takes up the sword will perish by the sword. By what sword? Surely by that fiery one which turns before paradise, and by the sword of the Spirit which is described in the armor of God.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.26.53":["Or do you think that I cannot ask my Father, and he will furnish me now with more than twelve legions of angels? How then will the Scriptures be fulfilled, that thus it must be done? I do not need the help of twelve apostles, even if they all were to defend me, I who can have twelve legions of the angelic army. Among the ancients one legion was made up of six thousand men. For the shortness of the time we cannot set out the number in full; let it be enough to have stated the type alone: that from twelve legions come seventy-two thousand angels (the number of the nations into which the language of men was divided).\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.26.54":["The following sentence shows a mind ready to suffer: that the prophets would have sung in vain unless the Lord had affirmed by his passion that they had spoken the truth.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.26.55":["At that hour Jesus said: You have come out as against a robber with swords and clubs to arrest me? Daily I sat among you teaching in the temple, and you did not seize me. It is foolish, he says, to seek with swords and clubs one who hands himself over of his own accord into your hands, and to track down at night through a betrayer, as if he were hiding and avoiding your eyes, one who teaches daily in the temple. But you gather against me in darkness for this reason, that your power is in darkness.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.26.56":["But all this was done that the Scriptures of the prophets might be fulfilled. What are the Scriptures of the prophets? They pierced my hands and feet; and elsewhere: He was led like a sheep to the sacrifice; and in another place: By the iniquities of my people he was led to death.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.26.57":["But they, holding Jesus, led him to Caiaphas the chief priest, where the scribes and the elders had gathered. Moses, at God's command, had ordered that the high priests should succeed their fathers and that a line of descent should be woven among the priests. Josephus relates that this Caiaphas bought the high priesthood for one year only from Herod for a price. It is no wonder, then, if an unjust high priest judges unjustly.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.26.58":["But Peter followed him from afar. He who was going to deny the Lord followed from afar.\n— Commentary on Matthew","And going inside he sat with the attendants, to see the end. Either out of a disciple's love or out of human curiosity he wished to know what the high priest would decide about the Lord: whether he would condemn him to death or would release him after beating him with scourges. And in this lies the difference between the ten apostles and Peter: they flee; he, although from a distance, still follows the Savior.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.26.60-61":["At last two false witnesses came and said: This man said: I can destroy the temple of God and after three days build it. How are they false witnesses, if they say the things which we read above that the Lord said? But a false witness is one who does not understand what is said in the same sense in which it is said. For the Lord had spoken of the temple of his body; but they falsely accuse him in the very words too, and by adding or changing a few they make, as it were, a just accusation. The Savior had said: Dissolve this temple; they change it and say: I can destroy the temple of God. You, he says, dissolve, not I, because it is unlawful for us to lay hands on ourselves. Then they twist it: and after three days build it, so that he might seem to have spoken properly of the Jewish temple. But the Lord, to show a living and breathing temple, had said: And I in three days will raise it up. It is one thing to build, another to raise up.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.26.62-63":["And the chief priest, rising up, said to him: Do you answer nothing to the things these men testify against you? But Jesus was silent. Headlong anger and impatience, finding no ground for a false accusation, shake the high priest from his throne, so that he might show the madness of his mind by the movement of his body. The more Jesus kept silent before the false witnesses and the impious priests, unworthy of his reply, the more the high priest, overcome by fury, provokes him to answer, so that from any occasion of speech whatever he might find ground for accusing him. Nevertheless Jesus is silent. For he knew, as God, that whatever he answered would be twisted into a false accusation.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.26.63":["And the chief priest said to him: I adjure you by the living God that you tell us whether you are the Christ, the Son of God. Why do you adjure him, most impious of priests: in order to accuse, or in order to believe? If in order to accuse, others are accusing him: condemn him in his silence; if in order to believe, why did you refuse to believe him when he confessed?\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.26.64":["Jesus says to him: You have said it. Both to Pilate and to Caiaphas the reply is alike, so that they may be condemned by their own sentence.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.26.65":["Then the chief priest tore his garments, saying: He has blasphemed; what further need have we of witnesses? The man whom fury had shaken from the priestly throne the same rage provokes to tear his clothes. And he tears his garments to show that the Jews had lost the glory of the priesthood and that the high priests have an empty seat. But it is also a Jewish custom to tear their garments when they hear anything blasphemous and as it were against God, as we read that Paul and Barnabas also did when in Lycaonia they were being honored with the worship of gods. But Herod, because he did not give honor to God but acquiesced in the immoderate applause of the people, was at once struck by an angel.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.26.67":["Then they spat in his face and struck him with their fists. That what was written might be fulfilled: I gave my cheeks to blows and did not turn my face away from the shame of spitting.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.26.68":["And others struck his face with their palms, saying: Prophesy to us, Christ, who is it that struck you? It was foolish to answer those who were beating him and to prophesy who was striking him, when the madness of the one who struck was plain to see. But just as he did not prophesy this to you, so he foretold that other thing most plainly: that Jerusalem would be surrounded by an army and that not a stone would be left upon a stone in the temple.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.26.69":["But Peter was sitting outside in the court. He sat outside to see the outcome of the affair, and he did not come near Jesus, lest any suspicion should arise among the attendants.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.26.72":["And again he denied with an oath: I do not know the man. I know that some, out of pious affection toward the apostle Peter, have interpreted this passage so as to say that Peter denied not God but a man, and that the sense is: I do not know the man, because I know God. How frivolous this is the prudent reader understands: they so defend the apostle as to make the Lord guilty of a lie. For if he did not deny, then the Lord lied, who had said: Amen I say to you that on this night, before the cock crows, you will deny me three times. See what he says: you will deny me, not the man.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.26.73":["Surely you too are one of them, for your speech also makes you plain. Not that Peter was of another language or of a foreign nation, for all of them were Hebrews, both those who accused and he who was accused, but because each province and region had its own peculiarities and could not avoid its native sound of speech. Hence too the Ephraimites in the book of Judges cannot say the σύνθημα (the password).\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.26.74":["Then he began to curse and to swear that he did not know the man. And immediately the cock crowed. In another Gospel we read that after Peter's denial and the crowing of the cock the Savior looked at Peter and by his gaze moved him to bitter tears; nor could it be that he should remain in the darkness of denial on whom the light of the world had looked.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.26.75":["And going outside he wept bitterly. Sitting in the court of Caiaphas he could not repent. He goes outside, away from the council of the impious, so that he may wash away the filth of a fearful denial with bitter weeping.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.27.1-2":["When morning had come, all the chief priests and the elders of the people took counsel against Jesus, to hand him over to death; and they led him bound and handed him over to Pontius Pilate the governor. He was led not only to Pilate but also to Herod, so that both might mock the Lord. And see the zeal of the priests for evil: they kept watch the whole night in order to commit murder. And they handed him over bound to Pilate. For they had this custom, that whomever they had adjudged to death they handed over bound to the judge.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.27.3-4":["Then Judas, who betrayed him, seeing that he had been condemned, moved by repentance brought back the thirty pieces of silver to the chief priests and the elders, saying: I have sinned in betraying just blood. The weight of his impiety shut out the greatness of his avarice. Judas, seeing the Lord adjudged to death, brought the price back to the priests, as if it were in his power to change the sentence of the persecutors. And so, although he changed his will, yet he did not change the outcome of his first will. But if he sinned who betrayed just blood, how much more did they sin who had bought just blood and, by offering a price, had provoked the disciple to betrayal! Let those who try to introduce different natures, and say that Judas the traitor was of an evil nature and could not have been preserved by his election to the apostleship, answer how an evil nature repented.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.27.5":["But they said: What is that to us? You see to it. And throwing down the pieces of silver in the temple he withdrew, and going away he hanged himself with a noose. It profited him nothing to have repented, since by that repentance he could not correct the crime. If ever a brother so sins against a brother that he is able to amend what he has sinned, it can be forgiven him; but if the works remain, repentance is taken up in vain by the voice. This is what is said in the psalm about this same most unhappy Judas: And let his prayer become sin, so that not only was he unable to amend the wickedness of the betrayal, but to the former crime he added also the charge of murdering himself. The Apostle too says something of the kind in the second letter to the Corinthians: Lest the brother be swallowed up by excessive sorrow.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.27.6":["But the chief priests, having taken the pieces of silver, said: It is not lawful to put them into the corbanan, because it is the price of blood. Truly straining out the gnat and swallowing the camel! For if they do not put the money into the corbanam, that is, into the treasury and the gifts of God, because it is the price of blood, why is the blood itself shed?\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.27.7":["And having taken counsel, they bought with them the potter's field, as a burial place for strangers. They indeed did it with another intention, to leave from the purchase of the field an everlasting monument of their impiety. But we, who were strangers to the Law and the Prophets, have taken up their perverse endeavors for our salvation, and we rest in the price of his blood. And it is called the potter's field because our potter is Christ.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.27.9-10":["And I took, says the Lord through the prophet, as I had been commanded by the Lord, the thirty pieces of silver, and I did not put them down, but cast them into the house of the Lord to the moulder; in the house of the Lord I caused them to be returned by the traitor to the priests and Pharisees, the very man who sold me confessing: I have sinned, betraying just blood. But they, since it was the price of blood, would not put it into the corbona, that is, into the treasury; but with it they bought the potter's field for the burial of strangers. For all of us who were strangers and sojourners from the law, redeemed by his most precious blood, are buried and rest in the house of the potter and Creator of all.\n— Commentary on Zechariah","Then was fulfilled what was spoken through the prophet Jeremiah, saying: And they took the thirty pieces of silver, the price of the one valued, whom they valued from the sons of Israel, and they gave them for the potter's field, as the Lord appointed for me. This testimony is not found in Jeremiah; but in Zechariah, who is almost the last of the twelve prophets, a certain likeness is reported, and although the sense does not differ much, yet both the order and the words are different. I read recently, in a certain Hebrew volume which a Hebrew of the Nazarene sect brought to me, an apocryphal Jeremiah, in which I found these things written word for word. But still it seems to me that the testimony is rather taken from Zechariah, in the common manner of the evangelists and apostles, who, passing over the order of the words, bring forward only the sense from the Old Testament as an example.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.27.11-12":["Jesus says to him: You say so. He answered in such a way that he both spoke the truth and his word lay open to no false accusation. And note that to Pilate, who was pronouncing sentence unwillingly, he answered in some part, but to the priests and rulers he would not answer, judging them unworthy of his speech.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.27.11":["And Jesus stood before the governor. And the governor questioned him, saying: Are you the king of the Jews? Since Pilate questions him on no other charge than whether he is king of the Jews, the Jews are convicted of impiety, because they could not find, even falsely, anything to lay against the Savior.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.27.13":["Then Pilate says to him: Do you not hear how many testimonies they speak against you? He who condemns Jesus is indeed a heathen, but he lays the cause upon the people of the Jews.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.27.14":["Do you not hear how many testimonies they speak against you? But Jesus would answer nothing, lest by refuting the charge he should be released by the governor and the benefit of the cross be put off.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.27.16":["Now he had at that time a notable prisoner, who was called Barabbas. This man, in the Gospel which is written according to the Hebrews, is interpreted as son of their master; he had been condemned for sedition and murder.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.27.17-18":["Pilate offers them the choice of whom they wished him to release, the robber or Jesus, not doubting that Jesus would rather be chosen, since he knew that he had been handed over out of envy. Therefore the cause of the cross is plainly envy.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.27.19":["While he was sitting on the judgment seat, his wife sent to him, saying: Let there be nothing between you and that just man; for I have suffered many things today in a vision because of him. Note that dreams are often revealed by God to Gentiles, and that in Pilate and his wife, who confess the Lord to be just, there is the testimony of the Gentile people.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.27.22-23":["Pilate says to them: What then shall I do with Jesus who is called Christ? They all say: Let him be crucified. The governor says to them: Why, what evil has he done? But they cried out the more, saying: Let him be crucified. Pilate gave many occasions for freeing the Savior: first, by comparing the robber with the just man; then by adding: What then shall I do with Jesus who is called Christ, that is, who is your king; and when they answered: Let him be crucified, he did not acquiesce at once, but, following the suggestion of his wife, who had sent word: Let there be nothing between you and that just man, he too answered: Why, what evil has he done? In saying this Pilate acquitted Jesus. But they cried out the more, saying: Let him be crucified, so that what he had said in Psalm 21 (22) might be fulfilled: Many dogs have surrounded me; the assembly of evildoers has besieged me; and that word of Jeremiah: My inheritance has become to me like a lion in the forest; they have raised their voice against me; Isaiah too agreeing in this sense: And I waited for them to do judgment, but they did iniquity, and not justice but an outcry.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.27.24":["But Pilate, seeing that he was gaining nothing, but that rather a tumult was arising, took water and washed his hands before the people, saying: I am innocent of the blood of this just man; you see to it. Pilate took water according to that prophetic word: I will wash my hands among the innocent, so that in the washing of his hands the works of the Gentiles might be cleansed, and that he might in some way make us strangers to the impiety of the Jews, who cried out: Crucify him, testifying to this and saying: I indeed wished to free an innocent man, but since a sedition is arising and the charge of treason against Caesar is being thrust upon me: I am innocent of the blood of this just man. The judge who is forced to pass sentence against the Lord does not condemn the one brought before him, but convicts those who bring him, pronouncing that he who is to be crucified is just. You, he says, see to it; I am the minister of the laws, your voice sheds the blood.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.27.25":["And all the people answered and said: His blood be upon us and upon our children. This imprecation persists upon the Jews to the present day, and the blood of the Lord will not be taken away from them. Hence he speaks through Isaiah: If you lift up your hands to me, I will not hear you; for your hands are full of blood. An excellent inheritance the Jews have left their children, saying: His blood be upon us and upon our children.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.27.26":["Then he released Barabbas to them; but Jesus, scourged, he handed over to them to be crucified. Barabbas the robber, who stirred up seditions among the crowds, who was the author of murders, was released to the people of the Jews, that is, the devil, who reigns in them to this day, and for that reason they cannot have peace; but Jesus, handed over by the Jews, is acquitted by Pilate's wife and is called just by the governor himself, and the centurion confesses that he is truly the Son of God. Let the learned reader ask how it is consistent that Pilate washed his hands and said: I am innocent of the blood of this just man, and afterwards handed over Jesus, scourged, to be crucified. But it must be known that he was serving the Roman laws, by which it was decreed that one who is crucified should first be beaten with scourges. So Jesus was handed over to the soldiers to be beaten, and scourges cut that most sacred body and the breast that could contain God. And this was done so that, since it was written: Many are the scourges of sinners; by his being scourged we might be freed from blows, Scripture saying to the just man: The scourge has not come near your tent.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.27.27-29":["Then the soldiers of the governor, taking Jesus into the praetorium, gathered the whole cohort to him; and stripping him, they put a scarlet cloak around him, and plaiting a crown of thorns they put it on his head, and a reed in his right hand, and bending the knee before him they mocked him, saying: Hail, king of the Jews. The soldiers indeed, because he had been called king of the Jews, and the scribes and priests had laid this charge against him, that he was usurping rule for himself among the people of Israel, do this in mockery: having stripped him of his former garments, they clothe him in a scarlet cloak in place of the red border which the ancient kings used to wear, and in place of a diadem they put on him a thorny crown, in place of a royal scepter they give him a reed, and they worship him as a king. But let us understand all these things mystically. For just as Caiaphas said: It is necessary that one man die for all, not knowing what he said, so these too, whatever they did, although they did it with another mind, were nevertheless bestowing mysteries on us who believe. In the scarlet cloak he bears up the bloody works of the Gentiles; in the crown of thorns he dissolves the ancient curse; with the reed he kills venomous animals; or else he held the reed in his hand to write down the sacrilege of the Jews.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.27.30":["And spitting on him they took the reed and struck his head. At that time was fulfilled: I did not turn away my face from the shame of spitting. And yet, though they strike his head with a reed, he bears all things patiently, to show true the prophecy of Isaiah, who says: A shaken reed he will not break.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.27.31":["And after they had mocked him, they stripped him of the cloak and clothed him in his own garments, and led him away to crucify him. When Jesus is scourged and spat upon and derided, he does not have his own garments, but those which he had taken on for our sins; but when he is crucified, and the pageant of mockery and derision has passed, then he receives back his former clothes and takes up his own adornment, and at once the elements are thrown into confusion and the creature gives testimony to the Creator.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.27.32":["And going out they found a man of Cyrene named Simon; him they pressed into service to carry his cross. Let no one think that the history of John the evangelist is contrary to this passage. For he says that the Lord, going out of the praetorium, carried his own cross; but Matthew reports that they found a man of Cyrene named Simon, on whom, pressing him into service, they laid the cross of Jesus. But this is to be understood: that Jesus, going out of the praetorium, carried his cross himself, and afterwards met Simon, on whom they laid the cross to be carried. According to anagogy, however, the nations take up the cross of Jesus, and the obedient stranger carries the ignominy of the Savior.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.27.33":["And they came to the place which is called Golgotha, which is the place of Calvary. I have heard someone expound the place of Calvary as the place in which Adam was buried, and say that it was so called because the head of the ancient man was laid there, and that this is what the Apostle says: Arise, you who sleep, and rise from the dead, and Christ will enlighten you. A pleasing interpretation, and soothing to the ear of the people, but not true. For outside the city and beyond the gate there are places in which the heads of the condemned are cut off, and they have taken the name of Calvary, that is, of the beheaded. And the Lord was crucified there for this reason: that where formerly was the ground of the condemned, there the standards of martyrdom might be raised; and just as for us he was made the curse of the cross, and was scourged and crucified, so for the salvation of all he is crucified as a guilty man among the guilty. But if anyone should wish to contend that the Lord was crucified there so that his blood might drip upon the tomb of Adam, let us ask him why other robbers too were crucified in the same place. From this it is evident that Calvary signifies not the sepulcher of the first man but the place of the beheaded, so that where sin abounded, grace might superabound. Indeed, that Adam was buried near Hebron and Arba we read in the book of Joshua son of Nun.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.27.34":["And they gave him wine mixed with gall to drink, and when he had tasted it he would not drink. God speaks to Jerusalem: I planted you a true vine; how have you turned into the bitterness of a foreign vine? A bitter vine makes bitter wine, which it gives the Lord Jesus to drink, so that what is written might be fulfilled: They gave gall for my food, and in my thirst they gave me vinegar to drink. But what is said: when he had tasted it he would not drink, indicates that he tasted indeed the bitterness of death for us, but on the third day he rose again.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.27.35":["After they had crucified him, they divided his garments, casting lots. This too had been prophesied in the same psalm: They divided my garments among themselves, and upon my vesture they cast lots.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.27.36":["And sitting down they kept watch over him. The diligence of the soldiers and priests profits us, so that the power of the one rising again may appear greater and more manifest.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.27.37":["And they set above his head the charge against him, written: This is Jesus the king of the Jews. I cannot sufficiently wonder, as the greatness of the matter deserves, that after false witnesses had been bought and the unhappy people had been stirred up to sedition and clamor, he found no other cause for putting him to death than that he was king of the Jews. And they perhaps did this mocking and laughing. But Pilate answered them, even against their will: What I have written, I have written. Whether you will or not, O Jews, the whole crowd of the Gentiles has answered you: Jesus is king of the Jews, that is, the commander of those who believe and confess.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.27.38":["Then two robbers were crucified with him, one on the right and one on the left. If Golgotha is the tomb of Adam and not the place of the condemned, and the Lord is crucified there in order to raise up Adam, why are two robbers crucified in the same place?\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.27.39-40":["And those passing by blasphemed him, moving their heads. They blasphemed because they were passing by the way and would not walk on the true road of the Scriptures. They moved their heads because they had already moved their feet before and did not stand upon the rock. And the foolish people, insulting him, says the very same thing that the false witnesses had made up.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.27.42":["He saved others; himself he cannot save. Even against their will the scribes and Pharisees confess that he saved others. So your own sentence condemns you. For he who saved others could surely have saved himself too, if he had wished.\n— Commentary on Matthew","Let him come down now from the cross, and we believe him. A deceitful promise! Which is greater: to come down from the cross while still alive, or to rise from the sepulcher when dead? He rose again, and you do not believe. Therefore, even if he had come down from the cross, you would likewise not believe. But it seems to me that the demons put this into them. For as soon as the Lord was crucified, they felt the power of the cross and understood that their strength was broken, and they are working for this, that he should come down from the cross. But the Lord, knowing the snares of his adversaries, remains on the gibbet in order to destroy the devil.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.27.44":["And the robbers too who had been fixed with him reproached him with the same. Here, by the trope which is called σύλληψις (syllepsis), both are presented as having blasphemed, in place of one robber. But Luke asserts that, while one blasphemed, the other confessed and on the contrary rebuked the blasphemer; not that the Gospels disagree, but because at first both blasphemed, and then, when the sun fled, the earth was shaken, the rocks were split and darkness came on, one believed in Jesus and corrected his former denial by his subsequent confession. In the two robbers both peoples, of the Gentiles and of the Jews, at first blasphemed the Lord; afterwards one, terrified by the greatness of the signs, repented, and to this day rebukes the Jews when they blaspheme.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.27.45":["Now from the sixth hour there was darkness over the whole earth until the ninth hour. Those who have written against the Gospels suspect that the disciples of Christ, out of ignorance, interpreted as concerning the resurrection of the Lord an eclipse of the sun, which usually occurs at fixed and appointed times, although a failure of the sun never usually occurs except at the rising of the moon. But no one doubts that at the time of the Passover the moon was at its fullest. And lest perhaps the shadow of the earth, or the disk of the moon set against the sun, should seem to have caused a short and rust-colored darkness, the space of three hours is stated, so that every pretext of the cavilers might be taken away. And I think this was done so that the prophecy might be fulfilled which says: The sun shall go down at midday, and the light shall be darkened over the earth in the day, and in another place: The sun went down while it was still midday. And it seems to me that the brightest light of the world, that is, the greater luminary, drew back its rays, so that it might neither see the Lord hanging nor let the impious blasphemers enjoy its light.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.27.46":["And about the ninth hour Jesus cried out with a loud voice, saying: Heli Heli lema sabacthani, that is: My God, my God, why have you forsaken me? He made use of the beginning of Psalm 21 (22); and what is read in the middle of the little verse: Look upon me, is superfluous. For in the Hebrew it is read: My God, my God, why have you forsaken me? Therefore they are impious who think that the psalm was spoken in the person of David, or of Esther and Mordecai, since even the evangelists understand testimonies taken from it as concerning the Savior, such as this: They divided my garments among themselves, and upon my vesture they cast lots; and another: They dug through my hands and my feet. Nor should you wonder at the lowliness of the words and the complaints of one forsaken, since, knowing the form of a servant, you see the stumbling block of the cross.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.27.47":["But some who were standing there and heard it said: This man is calling Elijah. Not all, but some, whom I judge to have been Roman soldiers who did not understand the idiom of the Hebrew language, but from the fact that he said: Heli Heli, thought that Elijah was being invoked by him. But if you would rather understand that it was Jews who said this, they do this too in their usual manner, to defame the Lord with weakness, as one who begs the help of Elijah.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.27.48":["And at once one of them ran, and taking a sponge he filled it with vinegar and put it on a reed and gave him to drink. And these things were done so that the prophecy might be fulfilled: In my thirst they gave me vinegar to drink. To this day the Jews and all who do not believe in the Lord's resurrection give Jesus vinegar and gall to drink, and give him wine mixed with myrrh to put him to sleep, so that he may not see their evils.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.27.50":["And Jesus, crying out again with a loud voice, sent forth his spirit. It is a sign of divine power to send forth the spirit, as he himself had also said: No one can take my soul from me, but I lay it down of myself, that I may take it up again.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.27.51-52":["The earth was shaken, and the rocks were split, and the tombs were opened. No one doubts what the greatness of the signs signifies according to the letter: that heaven and earth and all things were showing that the crucified was their Lord. But it seems to me that the earthquake and the rest bear the type of believers: that, having left behind the former vices of their errors and having softened the hardness of their heart, those who previously were like the tombs of the dead afterwards came to know the Creator.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.27.51":["And the veil of the temple was torn in two, from the top to the bottom. The veil of the temple was torn, and all the mysteries of the law, which were previously covered, were laid open and passed over to the people of the Gentiles. In the Gospel of which we often make mention, we read that the lintel of the temple, of immense size, was broken and split apart. Josephus also reports that the angelic powers, once the guardians of the temple, then cried out together: Let us depart from these abodes.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.27.52-53":["And many bodies of the saints who had fallen asleep arose, and coming out of the tombs after his resurrection they came into the holy city and appeared to many. As Lazarus, having died, rose again, so also many bodies of the saints rose again, to show the Lord rising. And yet, though the tombs were opened, they did not rise before the Lord rose, so that he might be the firstborn of the resurrection from the dead. As for the holy city in which those rising were seen, let us understand either the heavenly Jerusalem or this earthly one, which formerly was holy. Just as Matthew too is called the tax collector, not because, now an apostle, he still remains a tax collector, but because he keeps his former name, so the city of Jerusalem was called holy because of the temple and the holy of holies, and to distinguish it from the other cities in which idols were worshiped. But when it is said: appeared to many, it is shown that the resurrection was not general, such as to appear to all, but special, to a great many, so that those might see it who deserved to behold it.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.27.54":["But the centurion, and those who were with him keeping watch over Jesus, when they saw the earthquake and the things that were happening, were greatly afraid, saying: Truly this was the Son of God. In another Gospel, after the earthquake, the cause of the centurion's wonder is set forth more plainly: that when he had seen him yield up the spirit, he said: Truly this was the Son of God. For no one has the power of yielding up the spirit except him who is the creator of souls. And let us understand spirit in this place for soul, either because it makes the body spiritual and living, or because the substance of the soul itself is spirit, according to what is written: You will take away their spirit, and they will fail. And this is to be considered, that the centurion, before the cross, in the very stumbling block of the passion, confesses him truly the Son of God, and Arius in the Church preaches him a creature.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.27.55":["And there were many women there at a distance, who had followed Jesus from Galilee, ministering to him. It was a Jewish custom, and by the ancient usage of the nation it was not held to be a fault, that women should supply food and clothing to their teachers from their own substance. Because this could cause a stumbling block among the nations, Paul records that he had cast it aside: Have we not the power to lead about women as sisters, as the other apostles also do? And they ministered to the Lord from their own substance, so that he whose spiritual things they reaped might reap their carnal things; not that the Lord of creatures needed food, but to show a type for teachers, that they ought to be content with food and clothing from their disciples.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.27.56":["But let us see what sort of companions he had: Mary Magdalene, from whom he had cast out seven demons; and Mary the mother of James and Joseph, his aunt, the sister of Mary the mother of the Lord; and the mother of the sons of Zebedee, who a little before had asked for the kingdom for her children; and others, whom we read of in the other Gospels.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.27.57-58":["When it was evening, there came a certain rich man from Arimathea named Joseph, who himself also was a disciple of Jesus. He is said to be rich, not out of the boastfulness of the writer, as if to report that a noble and very wealthy man was a disciple of Jesus, but to show the reason why he was able to obtain the body of Jesus from Pilate. For it was not for a poor and unknown man to approach Pilate, the governor of the Roman power, and obtain the body of one crucified. In another evangelist this Joseph is called βουλευτής, that is, counselor, and some think that the first psalm was composed about him: Blessed is the man who has not walked in the counsel of the impious, and the rest.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.27.59":["And having taken the body of Jesus, he wrapped it in a clean linen cloth. From the simple burial of the Lord the ambition of the rich is condemned, who cannot do without riches even in their tombs. And according to spiritual understanding we can also take this to mean that the body of the Lord is to be wrapped not in gold, not in gems and silk, but in pure linen; although this too may be signified, that he wraps Jesus in a clean linen cloth who has received him with a pure mind.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.27.60":["And he laid it in his own new tomb, which he had hewn in the rock, and he rolled a great stone to the door of the tomb and went away. He is laid in a new tomb, lest after the resurrection, while the other bodies remained, it should be pretended that another had risen. But the new sepulcher can also signify the virginal womb of Mary; and the stone set against the door, and a great stone at that, can show that the sepulcher could not have been opened without the help of many.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.27.61":["And Mary Magdalene was there, and the other Mary, sitting opposite the sepulcher. While the others left the Lord, the women persevere in their duty, waiting for what Jesus had promised; and therefore they deserved to be the first to see him rising, because he who perseveres to the end, he shall be saved.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.27.64-66":["Command therefore that the sepulcher be guarded until the third day, lest perhaps his disciples come and steal him. It was not enough for the chief priests and the scribes and Pharisees to have crucified the Lord Savior, unless they also guarded the sepulcher, received a cohort, sealed the stone and, as far as lay in them, set their hand against the one rising, so that their diligence might profit our faith; for the more closely it is guarded, the more the power of the resurrection is shown. Hence also he was laid in a new tomb which had been hewn in the rock, lest, if it had been built of many stones, he should be said to have been taken away by theft after the foundations of the grave had been dug under. And that he was to be laid in a sepulcher, there is the testimony of the prophet who says: He dwelt in a lofty cave of the strongest rock, and immediately, after two little verses, there follows: You will see the King with glory.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.28.1":["Now in the evening of the Sabbath, as it grows light toward the first day of the week, there came Mary Magdalene and the other Mary to see the sepulchre. That different times are described for these women in the Gospels is not a sign of falsehood, as the impious object, but the service of a diligent visiting, while they often go away and run back and do not bear to be away from the Lord's sepulchre long or far.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.28.2-3":["And behold, there was a great earthquake; for an angel of the Lord came down from heaven, and coming up he rolled back the stone and was sitting upon it; and his appearance was like lightning, and his clothing like snow. Our Lord, one and the same Son of God and Son of Man, according to each nature, of divinity and of flesh, shows now the signs of his greatness, now those of his lowliness. Hence in the present passage too, although it is a man who was crucified, who was buried, who was shut in the tomb, whom the stone set against it holds in, yet the things done outside show the Son of God: the sun fleeing, darkness rushing in, the earth shaken, the veil torn, the rocks split apart, the dead raised, the ministries of angels, which from the very beginning of his birth also proved him God. Gabriel comes to Mary, an angel speaks with Joseph, the same announces it to the shepherds, afterward a choir of angels is heard: Glory to God in the highest, and on earth peace among men of good will, he is tempted in the wilderness, and after the victory angels at once serve him. Now too an angel comes as guardian of the Lord's sepulchre and in white clothing signifies the glory of the one who triumphs; and moreover, as the Lord ascends to heaven, two angels are seen on the Mount of Olives, promising the apostles the second coming of the Savior.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.28.4-5":["But for fear of him the guards were terrified and became like dead men; and the angel answered and said to the women: You, do not be afraid; for I know that you are seeking Jesus who was crucified. The guards, terrified with fear, lie stunned like dead men, and yet the angel consoles not them but the women. You, do not be afraid. Let them, he says, be afraid; let dread persist in those in whom unbelief remains; but you, because you are seeking Jesus crucified, hear that he has risen and has fulfilled what he promised.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.28.6-7":["Come and see where he had been laid, so that, if you do not believe my words, you may believe the empty sepulchre; and with hurried step go and announce to his disciples that he has risen and is going before you into Galilee, that is, into the wallowing-place of the Gentiles, where before there was error and slipperiness, and no footstep was planted with firm and steady foot.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.28.8":["And they went out quickly from the tomb with fear and great joy, running to announce it to his disciples. A twofold feeling, of fear and of joy, held the minds of the women: the one from the greatness of the miracle, the other from longing for the one rising again; and yet both spurred on the women's step. They were going to the apostles so that through them the seed of faith might be scattered.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.28.9":["And behold, Jesus met them, saying: Hail. Those who were seeking in this way, who were running so, deserved to have the risen Lord come to meet them and to be the first to hear: Hail, so that the curse of the woman Eve might be undone in women.\n— Commentary on Matthew","But they came up and took hold of his feet and worshiped him. These women come up and hold his feet because they worshiped him. But that woman who was seeking the living among the dead and did not yet know that the Son of God had risen rightly hears: Do not touch me, for I have not yet ascended to my Father.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.28.10":["Then Jesus said to them: Do not be afraid. In both the Old and the New Testament this must always be observed: that whenever some more august vision has appeared, fear is first driven out, so that, the mind being thus calmed, the things that are said may be heard.\n— Commentary on Matthew","Go, tell my brothers to go into Galilee; there they will see me. To those brothers of whom he also said in another place: I will announce your name to my brothers, who see the Savior not at all in Judea but in the multitude of the Gentiles.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.28.11-13":["And having gathered with the elders and taken counsel, they gave a large sum of money to the soldiers, saying: Say that his disciples came by night and stole him while we were asleep; and if this comes to the governor's hearing, we will persuade him and make you safe. The guards confess the miracle; they hurry back to the city; they report to the chief priests what they had seen, what they had beheld being done. Those who ought to have been turned to repentance and to seek the risen Jesus persist in malice, and they turn the money that had been given for the uses of the temple to the buying of a lie, just as before they had given thirty pieces of silver to Judas the traitor. All, therefore, who misuse the offerings of the temple and the things contributed for the uses of the Church on other things with which to satisfy their own will are like the scribes and priests who bought the lie and the blood of the Savior.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.28.16-17":["But the eleven disciples went into Galilee, to the mountain where Jesus had appointed for them. After the resurrection Jesus is seen on a mountain of Galilee and is worshiped there, although some doubt, and their doubt increases our faith. Then he is shown more plainly to Thomas, and he displays both his side wounded by the lance and his hands pierced with the nails.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.28.18":["Jesus came up and spoke to them, saying: All power has been given to me in heaven and on earth. Power has been given to him who a little before had been crucified, who had been buried in the tomb, who had lain dead, who afterward rose again. But power has been given in heaven and on earth so that he who before reigned in heaven may reign on earth through the faith of believers.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.28.19":["Go therefore, teach all nations, baptizing them in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit. First they teach all nations, then they dip those who have been taught in water. For it cannot be that the body receives the sacrament of baptism unless the soul has first received the truth of the faith. And they are baptized in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit so that, as their divinity is one, the bestowal may be one; and the name of the Trinity is one God.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAT.28.20":["Teaching them to observe all things whatever I have commanded you. A notable order. He commanded the apostles first to teach all the nations, then to dip them in the sacrament of faith, and after faith and baptism to enjoin what was to be observed. And lest we think that what was commanded is light and little, he added: all things whatever I have commanded you, so that those who have believed, who have been baptized in the Trinity, may do all things that have been commanded.\n— Commentary on Matthew","And behold, I am with you all days, even to the consummation of the age. He promises that he will be with the disciples even to the consummation of the age, and shows both that they will always live and that he will never withdraw from those who believe. But he who promises his presence even to the consummation of the world is not ignorant of that day on which he knows he will be with the apostles.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.1.2-3":["Mark the evangelist also, weaving together two testimonies, of Malachi and of Isaiah, under the utterance of one prophet, began thus: The beginning of the Gospel of Jesus Christ, as it is written in Isaiah the prophet: Behold, I send my angel before your face, who will prepare your way. This, though in other words, we read in Malachi. But what follows, The voice of one crying in the desert, prepare the way of the Lord, make his paths straight, is said by Isaiah the prophet; and at once, explaining of whom both testimonies are said: There was, he says, John in the desert, baptizing and preaching the baptism of repentance, for the remission of sins. But the evangelists too interpreted the prophecy of the prophets concerning John, and while they agree in sense, they differ in words. For whereas the LXX rendered: Make the paths of our God straight, Mark and Luke said: Make his paths straight. But John: Make straight, he says, the way of the Lord. From which it is clear that the apostles and evangelists, and the Lord Savior himself, do not follow the authority of the LXX translators, which, having knowledge of the Hebrew tongue, they do not need, but translate from the Hebrew what they had read, not caring about the syllables and points of the words, provided that the truth of the sense is carried over. That they did this in many places we have indeed shown, and especially in that book which we entitled 'On the Best Kind of Translating' it has been proved by many testimonies together.\n— Commentary on Malachi","For this is he who was spoken of through the prophet Isaiah, saying: The voice of one crying in the desert: Prepare the way of the Lord, make his paths straight. He was preparing the souls of believers, in which the Lord was going to walk, so that he, the pure one, might walk in most pure ways, saying: I will dwell in them and walk among them, and I will be their God, and they shall be my people. Porphyry compares this passage with the beginning of the evangelist Mark, in which it is written: The beginning of the Gospel of Jesus Christ, the Son of God; as it is written in the prophet Isaiah: Behold, I send my angel before your face, who shall prepare your way; the voice of one crying in the desert: Prepare the way of the Lord, make his paths straight. For since the testimony is woven together from Malachi and Isaiah, he asks how we can think the example was taken as from Isaiah alone. To him churchmen have answered most fully. We, however, think either that the name of Isaiah was added through the fault of copyists, which we can prove in other places as well, or at any rate that one body has been made out of different testimonies of the Scriptures. Read Psalm 13 (14) and you will find this same thing.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.1.6":["Now John himself had a garment of the hair of camels and a leather girdle about his loins. Of hair, he says, he had, not of wool. One thing is the mark of austere clothing, another of softer luxury. And the leather girdle with which Elijah too was girded is a σύμβολον (symbol) of mortification. Moreover, what follows,\n\nHis food was locusts and wild honey, is fitting for a dweller in the wilderness, so that he might satisfy not the delights of food but the necessity of human flesh.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.1.7":["Whose shoes I am not worthy to carry. In another Gospel: Whose, he says, shoe's strap I am not worthy to loose. Here humility is shown, there a mystery: that Christ is the bridegroom and John does not deserve to loose the bridegroom's strap, lest his house be called, according to the law of Moses and the example of Ruth, the house of the unshod.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.1.8":["He will baptize you in the Holy Spirit and fire. Either because the Holy Spirit is fire, as the Acts of the Apostles teach, at whose descent there sat as it were fire upon the tongues of the believers, and the word of the Lord was fulfilled who said: I came to send fire upon the earth, and how I wish that it may burn; or because in the present we are baptized with the spirit and in the future with fire, the Apostle too agreeing with this sense: The fire will test what sort each one's work is.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.1.9":["Then Jesus came from Galilee to the Jordan to John, to be baptized by him. For a threefold reason the Savior received baptism from John: first, so that, since he had been born a man, he might fulfill all the justice and humility of the law; second, so that by his own baptism he might approve the baptism of John; third, so that, sanctifying the waters of the Jordan by the descent of the dove, he might show the coming of the Holy Spirit in the washing of believers.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.1.10-11":["The heavens were opened to him, and he saw the Spirit descending like a dove, coming upon him, and the rest. The mystery of the Trinity is shown in the baptism. The Lord is baptized, the Spirit descends in the form of a dove, the voice of the Father is heard bearing witness to the Son. And the heavens are opened, not by an unbarring of the elements, but to spiritual eyes, with which Ezekiel too, at the beginning of his volume, records that they were opened. The dove sat, moreover, upon the head of Jesus, lest anyone should think that the voice of the Father was addressed to John and not to the Lord.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.1.12-13":["Then he was led in the desert by the Spirit. No doubt, by the Holy Spirit. For there follows:\n\nTo be tempted by the devil. But he is led not unwillingly, nor as a captive, but by the will to fight.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.1.13":["And when he had fasted forty days and forty nights, afterward he was hungry. In this number the mystery of the forty days is shown to us, in which Moses too fasted on Mount Sinai and Elijah beside Mount Horeb. But the body is allowed to be hungry so that the devil may be given the occasion to tempt.\n— Commentary on Matthew","Then the devil left him, and angels came and were ministering to him. The temptation comes first so that victory may follow. Angels minister so that the dignity of the victor may be proven.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.1.14-15":["From then on Jesus began to preach and to say: Repent, for the kingdom of heaven will draw near. With John handed over, he himself rightly begins to preach. As the law ceases, the Gospel accordingly arises. And if the Savior preaches the same things that John the Baptist had said before, he shows that he is the Son of the same God whose prophet the latter is.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.1.17":["Come after me, and I will make you become fishers of men. These were the first called to follow the Lord; fishermen and unlettered men are sent to preach, lest the faith of believers be thought to come about not by the power of God but by eloquence and learning.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.1.22":["And he was teaching them as one having authority, not as the scribes. For they taught the peoples the things that are written in Moses and the prophets, but Jesus, as God and Lord of Moses himself, by the freedom of his own will either added in the law the things that seemed lacking or, changing them, preached to the peoples, as we also read above: It was said to the ancients; but I say to you.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.1.31":["When Jesus had come into Peter's house, he saw his mother-in-law lying ill with fever, and he touched her hand, and the fever left her, and she rose and served them. The woman's hand is touched, and as her works are healed the infirmity of sins flees. Human nature is such that after a fever bodies are the more wearied, and as health begins they feel the ills of the sickness. But the health that is conferred by the Lord restores everything at once; nor is it enough that she was healed, but so that an ἐπίτασις (intensification) of strength might be shown, it is added: and she rose, served them. That hand served which had been both touched and healed.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.1.32-34":["When evening had come, they brought to him many who had demons, and he cast out the spirits with a word and healed all who were ill. All are healed not in the morning, not at midday, but towards evening, when the sun is about to set, when the grain of wheat dies in the earth that it may bear much fruit.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.1.40":["And when he had come down from the mountain, great crowds followed him. And behold, a leper came and worshiped him. As the Lord comes down from the mountain the crowds meet him, because they had not been able to ascend to the higher places. And the first to meet him is the leper. For with his leprosy he could not yet hear the Savior's so manifold discourse on the mountain. And it must be noted that this one was the first to be healed individually; second, the centurion's servant; third, Peter's mother-in-law, sick with fever, in Capernaum; in the fourth place, those vexed by a demon who were brought to him, whose spirits he cast out with a word, when he also healed all who were ill.\n— Commentary on Matthew","And behold, a leper came and worshiped him, saying. Rightly, after the preaching and teaching, an occasion for a sign is offered, so that through the miracles of mighty works the discourse just past may be confirmed among the hearers.\n— Commentary on Matthew","Lord, if you will, you can cleanse me. He who asks about the will does not doubt the power.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.1.41":["And Jesus, stretching out his hand, touched him, saying: I will, be cleansed. As the Lord stretches out his hand, at once the leprosy flees. At the same time consider how humble and free of boasting the answer is. The one had said: If you will; the Lord answered: I will; the one had put first: you can cleanse me; the Lord joins it and says: be cleansed. So it is not to be joined and read, as most of the Latins think: I will to cleanse, but separately, so that first he says: I will, and then commands: be cleansed.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.1.44":["And Jesus said to him: See that you tell no one. And truly, what need was there for him to boast in words of what he displayed in his body?\n— Commentary on Matthew","But go, show yourself to the priest and offer the gift that Moses commanded, as a testimony to them. He sends him to the priest for various reasons. First, for humility's sake, that he may be seen to defer to the priests. For it was commanded in the law that those who had been cleansed of leprosy should offer gifts to the priests. Next, so that, seeing the leper cleansed, they might either believe the Savior or not believe; if they believed, they would be saved; if they did not believe, they would be made inexcusable; and at the same time, so that he might not seem — a thing they very often charged against him — to break the law.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.2.1":["And he came into his own city. And behold, they were bringing to him a paralytic lying on a bed. And Jesus, seeing their faith, said to the paralytic: Take heart, son, your sins are remitted to you, and the rest. Let us understand his city to be none other than Nazareth; whence he was also called a nazarene.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.2.3-5":["And, as we said above, they brought to him a second paralytic, lying on a little bed, because he himself was not able to walk in. And Jesus, seeing the faith, not of the one who was being brought but of those who were bringing him, said to the paralytic: Take heart, son, your sins are forgiven you. O wonderful humility! He calls son one who was despised and infirm and loosened in all the joints of his limbs, one whom the priests did not deign to touch. Or else he calls him son for this reason, that his sins are forgiven him. According to tropology, sometimes a soul lying in its own body, with all the powers of its limbs loosened, is brought by a perfect teacher to the Lord to be healed; and if it has been healed by his mercy, it receives so much strength that it at once carries its own little bed.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.2.6-8":["And behold, some of the scribes said within themselves: This man blasphemes. We read in the prophet, God saying: I am he who blots out your iniquities. Consistently, therefore, the scribes, because they thought him a man and understood the words of God, accuse him of blasphemy. But the Lord, seeing their thoughts, shows himself to be God, who can know the hidden things of the heart, and, as it were keeping silent, he speaks: By the same majesty and power by which I look upon your thoughts, I can also forgive men their offenses; understand from yourselves what the paralytic obtains.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.2.9":["Which is easier, to say: Your sins are forgiven you; or to say: Rise and walk? Between saying and doing there is a great distance. Whether the sins had been forgiven the paralytic, he alone knew who was forgiving them. Rise, however, and walk could be verified as much by him who was rising as by those who saw him rise. A bodily sign therefore is wrought so that the spiritual one may be proved, although it belongs to the same power to remit the faults both of body and of soul. And we are given to understand that many infirmities of bodies come about on account of sins, and that perhaps for this reason the sins are forgiven first, so that, once the causes of the infirmity have been taken away, health may be restored.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.2.11":["Take up your bed and go into your house. And the paralytic soul too, if it rises, if it recovers its former strength, carries its bed, on which it previously lay loosened, and carries it into the house of its own virtues.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.2.14":["And as Jesus was passing on from there, he saw a man sitting at the tax office, Matthew by name, and he said to him: Follow me. And he rose and followed him. The other evangelists, out of modesty and regard for Matthew, did not wish to call him by his common name, but said Levi; for he had a twofold name. But Matthew himself, according to what is enjoined by Solomon: The just man is his own accuser at the beginning of his speech; and in another place: Tell your sins, that you may be justified, names himself Matthew and a tax collector, to show his readers that no one ought to despair of salvation if he has turned to better things, since he himself was suddenly changed from a tax collector into an apostle. In this place Porphyry and Julian the Augustus charge either the ignorance of a lying historian or the folly of those who at once followed the Savior, as though they had irrationally followed any man at all who called them, when so many mighty works and so many signs had gone before, which there is no doubt the apostles saw before they believed. Certainly the very brightness and majesty of the hidden divinity, which shone forth even in his human face, could draw to himself at first sight those who saw him. For if the magnet stone and amber are said to have this power, that they join rings and straw and bits of chaff to themselves, how much more could the Lord of all creatures draw to himself those whom he willed.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.2.15":["And it came to pass, as he reclined at table in the house, behold, many tax collectors and sinners came and reclined with Jesus, and the rest. They had seen that a tax collector converted from sins to better things had found a place of repentance, and on that account they too do not despair of salvation; nor indeed do they come to Jesus remaining in their former vices, as the Pharisees and scribes murmur, but repenting, as the Lord's following word signifies, saying:\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.2.17":["I desire mercy and not sacrifice. I came not to call the just, but sinners. Now the Lord used to go to the banquets of sinners in order to have an occasion of teaching and to offer spiritual food to those who invited him. In short, though he is frequently described as going to banquets, nothing else is reported except what he did there, what he taught, so that both the humility of the Lord in going to sinners and the power of his teaching in the conversion of the penitent might be shown. And as for what follows: I desire mercy and not sacrifice; and: I came not to call the just, but sinners, bringing forward testimony from the prophet he rebukes the scribes and Pharisees, who, esteeming themselves just, shunned the company of sinners and tax collectors.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.2.18":["Then the disciples of John came to him, saying: Why do we and the Pharisees fast often, but your disciples do not fast? A proud question and full of the haughtiness of the Pharisees. Certainly (not to say anything else) the boasting of their fasting is to be reproved. Nor could the disciples of John but be at fault, since they were slandering him whom they knew to have been proclaimed by their master's voice, and were joining themselves to the Pharisees, whom they knew had been condemned by John: Brood of vipers, who showed you to flee from the wrath to come?\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.2.19-20":["Jesus said to them: Can the sons of the bridegroom mourn as long as the bridegroom is with them? But the days will come when the bridegroom will be taken away from them, and then they will fast. The bridegroom is Christ, the bride is the Church. From this holy and spiritual marriage the apostles were begotten, who cannot mourn as long as they see the bride in the bridal chamber and know that the bridegroom is with the bride. But when the wedding has passed and the time of the passion and resurrection has come, then the sons of the bridegroom will fast. Some think that for this reason fasts ought to be entered upon forty days after the passion, although the day of Pentecost at once, and the Holy Spirit's coming, indicate festivity to us; and taking occasion from this testimony, Montanus, Prisca and Maximilla keep a forty-day fast even after Pentecost, on the ground that, when the bridegroom has been taken away, the sons of the bridegroom ought to fast. But the custom of the Church comes to the passion and resurrection of the Lord through humility of the flesh, so that by the fasting of the body we may be prepared for the spiritual banquet. By the laws of tropology, however, we must know that as long as the bridegroom is with us and we are in gladness, we can neither fast nor mourn; but when on account of our sins he has withdrawn from us and flown away, then a fast must be proclaimed, then mourning must be taken up.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.2.21-22":["No one puts a patch of unworked cloth on an old garment; for it takes its fullness away from the garment, and a worse tear is made. Nor do they put new wine into old wineskins; otherwise the wineskins will burst, and the wine will be poured out, and the wineskins will perish; but they put new wine into new wineskins, and both are preserved. What he says is this: until someone has been reborn and, having put off the old man, has put on the new man through my passion, he cannot bear the stricter fasts and the precepts of continence, lest through excessive severity he lose even the belief that he now seems to have. He set down two examples, then, of the garment and of the wineskins, old and new. By the old we ought to understand the scribes and Pharisees. By the patch of the new garment and the new wine are to be understood the precepts of the Gospel, which the Jews cannot bear, lest a greater tear be made. Some such thing the Galatians too were eager to do, to mix the precepts of the law with the Gospel and to put new wine into old wineskins. But the Apostle says to them: O foolish Galatians, who has bewitched you not to obey the truth? The word of the Gospel, therefore, is to be poured into the apostles rather than into the scribes and Pharisees, who, corrupted by the traditions of their elders, could not keep the purity of the precepts of Christ. For the purity of a virginal soul, polluted by no contagion of former vice, is one thing, and the filth of one who has lain subject to the lust of many is another.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.2.23":["At that time Jesus went through the sown fields on the Sabbath, and his disciples, being hungry, began to pluck ears of grain and to eat. As we read also in another evangelist, because of the excessive importunity they did not even have room to eat, and so, being men, they were hungry. But that they rub the ears of the crops in their hands and so relieve their want of food is a sign of a more austere life, of men who seek not prepared banquets but simple foods.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.2.24":["But the Pharisees, seeing it, said to him: Behold, your disciples are doing what it is not lawful for them to do on the Sabbaths. Note that the first apostles of the Savior destroy the letter of the Sabbath, against the Ebionites, who, though they receive the other apostles, reject Paul as a transgressor of the law.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.2.25-26":["But he said to them: Have you not read what David did when he was hungry, and those who were with him, how he entered the house of God and ate the loaves of the presentation, which it was not lawful for him to eat, nor for those who were with him, but for the priests alone? To refute the false charge of the Pharisees he recalls the old history, when David, fleeing Saul, came to Nob and, received by Ahimelech the priest, asked for food; and he, since he had no common loaves, gave him consecrated ones, which it was not lawful to eat except for the priests and Levites alone, and he asked only this, whether the young men were clean from women; and when David answered, since yesterday and the day before, he did not hesitate to give the loaves, judging it better (as the prophet says: I desire mercy and not sacrifice) to free men from the danger of hunger than to offer sacrifice to God. For the salvation of men is a victim pleasing to God. So the Lord sets this against them and says: If David too is holy, and Ahimelech the high priest is not blamed by you, but both transgressed the command of the law with an acceptable excuse, and hunger is the cause, why do you not approve in the apostles the same hunger that you approve in the others? Although even in this there is a great difference: these rub ears of grain in their hands on the Sabbath; those ate the levitical loaves, and the days of the new moons were added to the solemnity of the Sabbath, on which, being sought at the banquet, he fled from the royal court. Note that neither David nor his young men received the loaves of the presentation before they had answered that they were clean from women.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.3.1":["And when he had passed on from there, he came into their synagogue. And behold, a man having a withered hand. This man, who is healed in the synagogue, is the thirteenth. And it is to be noted that the hand was withered not on the road and out of doors, but in the assembly of the Jews.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.3.2":["And they questioned him, saying: Is it lawful to heal on the Sabbaths? so that they might accuse him. Since he had excused by an acceptable example the destruction of the Sabbath which the Pharisees charged against the disciples, they wish to slander him himself, and they ask whether it is lawful to heal on the Sabbaths, so that, if he did not heal, they might accuse him of cruelty or of weakness, and if he healed, of transgression.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.3.4":["But he said to them: What man of you will there be who has one sheep, and if it falls into a pit on the Sabbaths, will he not take hold of it and lift it out? How much better, then, is a man than a sheep! So it is lawful to do good on the Sabbaths. He so solved the question put to him as to condemn the questioners of avarice. If you, he says, hasten on the Sabbath to pull out a sheep or any other animal that falls into a pit, looking out not for the animal but for your own avarice, how much more ought I to set free a man, who is much better than a sheep!\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.3.5":["Then he said to the man: Stretch out your hand. And he stretched it out, and it was restored to health like the other. In the Gospel which the Nazarenes and the Ebionites use, which we recently translated from the Hebrew language into Greek, and which is called by most the authentic Matthew, this man who has the withered hand is described as a mason, begging for help in words of this kind: I was a mason, seeking my living with my hands; I beg you, Jesus, to restore my health to me, so that I may not shamefully beg for food. Until the coming of the Savior the withered hand was in the synagogue of the Jews, and the works of God were not done in it; after he came upon earth, the right hand was given back in the apostles who believed and was restored to its former work.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.3.6":["But the Pharisees went out and took counsel against him, how they might destroy him. That they lay plots against the Lord, envy is the cause. For what had he done to provoke the Pharisees to kill him? Why, that a man had stretched out his hand. For which of the Pharisees does not stretch out his hand on the Sabbath day, carrying food, holding out a cup, and the other things that are necessary for living? If, then, to stretch out the hand and to lift up food or drink on the Sabbath is no crime, why do they charge in another what they themselves are convicted of doing, especially since this mason carried nothing of the kind, but at the Lord's command only stretched out his hand?\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.3.7":["But Jesus, knowing it, withdrew from there. Knowing their plots, that they wished to destroy their Savior, he withdrew from there, so as to take away from the Pharisees an occasion for impiety against himself.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.3.16-18":["First, Simon, who is called Peter, and Andrew his brother. To set out the order of the apostles and the merit of each belonged to him who searches the secrets of the heart. Simon, surnamed Peter, is written first, to distinguish him from the other Simon, who is called the Cananaean, from Cana, the village of Galilee where the Lord turned water into wine. He also calls James the son of Zebedee, because another James follows as well, the son of Alphaeus. And he joins the apostles together in pairs and yokes. He joins Peter and Andrew, brothers not so much in the flesh as in the spirit; James and John, who, leaving the father of their body, followed the true Father; Philip and Bartholomew; Thomas also and Matthew the tax collector. The other evangelists, in pairing the names, put Matthew first and Thomas after, and do not add the name of tax collector, lest by recalling his former way of life they should seem to disparage the evangelist. But this one, as we said above, both places himself after Thomas and calls himself a tax collector, so that where sin abounded there might superabound grace.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.3.16-19":["Now the names of the twelve apostles are these. A catalogue of the apostles is set down so that those who were going to be false apostles, outside these, might be shut out.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.3.17-19":["Simon the Cananaean. He is the one who in another evangelist is written as zelotes; for Cana is interpreted 'zeal'. The history of the Church relates that the apostle Thaddaeus was sent to Edessa, to Abgar king of Osrhoene; he is called Judas of James by the evangelist Luke, and elsewhere he is named Lebbaeus, which is interpreted 'little heart'; and we must believe that he had three names, just as Simon Peter and the sons of Zebedee, boanerges, were named from the firmness and greatness of their faith. But Judas Scariothes took his name either from the village in which he was born or from the tribe of Issachar, so that by a kind of prophecy he was born to his own condemnation. For Issachar is interpreted 'reward', so that the price of the traitor may be signified.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.3.22-24":["But Jesus, knowing their thoughts, said to them: Every kingdom divided within itself will be laid waste. The crowds were amazed and confessed that he who did such great signs was the son of David; but the Pharisees ascribed the works of God to the prince of the demons; and the Lord answered them not according to what they said but according to what they thought, so that even thus they might be compelled to believe in the power of him who saw the hidden things of the heart.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.3.23-26":["If Satan casts out Satan, he is divided against himself; how then will his kingdom stand? A kingdom and a city divided against itself cannot endure; but as small things grow by concord, so the greatest fall apart by discord. If, then, Satan fights against himself, and demon is the enemy of demon, the consummation of the world ought already to have come, and the adverse powers would have no place in it, whose war among themselves is the peace of men. But if you think, O scribes and Pharisees, that the withdrawal of the demons is obedience to their prince, so that by a fraudulent pretense they may delude ignorant men, what can you say of the healings of bodies which the Lord performed? It is another matter if you ascribe to the demons the infirmities of the limbs too, and the signal marks of spiritual mighty works.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.3.27":["Our Lord and Savior, who had spoken earlier through Isaiah, himself repeats the same sense in the Gospel: how can anyone enter the house of a strong man and plunder his goods, unless he first binds the strong man, and then he will plunder his house (Mark 3)? The strong man and giant is the devil, who had subjugated all the nations to his rule, daring to say to the Savior: All these things have been delivered to me, and I will give them to you, if you will fall down and worship me (Matt 4:9). For the world lies in the evil one (1 John 5), and none of the righteous could conquer him: nor, according to Amos, take the prey from the lion's mouth (Amos 3). The question having been set, then, that no one could conquer the strong man and the giant, nor save what had been seized by him, the Lord answered that all would be accomplished by his coming: namely, that the nations formerly captured would be taken from the giant, and all his furniture and his whole household be divided among the Apostles. So is fulfilled what is written: The king of the powers of the beloved, and to the beauty of the house to divide the spoils. For he it is who, ascending on high, led captivity captive, he gave gifts among men (Ps 67 [68]:13, 19; Eph 4): that is, those who had formerly been taken captive unto death he received unto life. Hence Symmachus translated more plainly: But even the captivity of the strong one shall be taken away, and the plunder of the terrible one shall be safe. He wishes the strong and terrible one to be understood as the devil, who has been overcome by the power of the Lord.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","Or how can anyone enter the house of the strong man and plunder his vessels, unless he first binds the strong man, and then he will plunder his house? We must not be complacent: our adversary is shown to be strong even by the words of the victor; his house is the world, which is set in the evil one, not by the dignity of the Creator but by the greatness of the offender; his vessels we once were. The strong man has been bound and fastened down in Tartarus and crushed by the foot of the Lord, and, the seats of the tyrant having been plundered, captivity has been led captive.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.3.28-30":["Whoever speaks a word against the Son of Man, it will be forgiven him; but whoever speaks against the Holy Spirit, it will not be forgiven him, neither in this age nor in the one to come. And how do some of our people receive bishops and presbyters back into their rank after blasphemy of the Holy Spirit, when the Savior says that every sin and blasphemy is forgiven to men, but that those who have blasphemed against the Holy Spirit are not forgiven, neither in the present time nor in the future; unless perhaps we take that example from Mark the evangelist, who expressed more plainly the causes of such great anger, saying: Because they were saying: He has an unclean spirit. Therefore whoever ascribes the works of the Savior to Beelzebub, the prince of the demons, and says that the Son of God has an unclean spirit, to him the blasphemy will at no time be forgiven. Or else this passage is to be understood thus: He who speaks a word against the Son of Man, scandalized by my flesh and thinking me only a man, because I am the son of a carpenter and have brothers James and Joseph and Jude, and because I am a man who is a glutton and a drinker of wine, such an opinion and blasphemy, although it is not without fault, still has the pardon due to error because of the lowliness of the body; but he who, plainly understanding the works of God, since he cannot deny the mighty work, is goaded by the same envy and slanders them, and says that Christ, the Word of God, and the works of the Holy Spirit are Beelzebub, to him it will not be forgiven, neither in the present age nor in the one to come.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.3.31-32":["While he was still speaking to the crowds, behold, his mother and his brothers were standing outside, seeking to speak to him. And someone said to him: Behold, your mother and your brothers are standing outside seeking you. The Lord was occupied in the work of the word, in the teaching of the peoples, in the duty of preaching; his mother and brothers come and stand outside and desire to speak to him. Then someone announces to the Savior that his mother and brothers are standing outside seeking him. It seems to me that this man who brings the news does not announce it by chance and in simplicity, but is laying a trap, to see whether he would prefer flesh and blood to the spiritual work. Hence the Lord too scorned to go out, not as though he were denying his mother and brothers, but so as to answer the one laying the trap:\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.3.31-35":["Stretching out his hand toward his disciples he said: Behold my mother and my brothers. These are my mother, who daily bring me to birth in the souls of believers; these are my brothers, who do the works of my Father. So he did not, as Marcion and Mani hold, deny his mother, so as to be thought born of a phantasm, but he preferred the apostles to kinship, so that we too, in weighing our love, may prefer the spirit to the flesh. Behold, your mother and your brothers are standing outside seeking you. Some suspect that the brothers of the Lord are sons of Joseph by another wife, following the ravings of the apocrypha and inventing a certain little woman Escha. But we, as is contained in the book which we wrote Against Helvidius, understand the brothers of the Lord to be not sons of Joseph but cousins of the Savior, the children of Mary, the Lord's maternal aunt, who is said to be the mother of James the less, and of Joses and Jude, whom in another place of the Gospel we read called the brothers of the Lord. And all Scripture shows that cousins are called brothers. Let us also speak in another way. The Savior speaks to the crowds; inside he instructs the nations. His mother and brothers, that is, the synagogue and the people of the Jews, stand outside and desire to enter, and become unworthy of his discourse; and when they have asked and sought and sent a messenger, they receive the answer that they are of free will and can enter, if they too were willing to believe; yet they will not be able to enter unless they have asked others.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.4.1":["On that day Jesus, going out of the house, sat beside the sea, and great crowds were gathered to him. The people could not enter the house of Jesus, nor be where the apostles were hearing the mysteries; therefore the compassionate and merciful Lord goes out of his house and sat beside the sea of this world, so that the crowds might be gathered to him and hear on the shore what they did not deserve to hear within.\n— Commentary on Matthew","So that, going up into a little boat, he sat, and the whole crowd stood on the shore. Jesus is in the midst of the waves; he is beaten by the sea on this side and on that, and, secure in his majesty, he makes his little boat draw near to the land; but the people, in no way enduring danger nor surrounded by temptations which they could not bear, stand on the shore with a fixed step to hear what is said.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.4.2":["And he spoke many things to them in parables, saying. The crowd is not of one mind but of wills differing in each person. Hence he speaks to it in many parables, so that according to their various wills they might receive different teachings. And it should be noted that he did not speak all things to them in parables, but many. For if he had said everything in parables, the people would have gone away without profit. He mixes the plain with the obscure, so that through the things they understand they may be roused to a knowledge of the things they do not understand.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.4.3":["Behold, he who sows went out to sow. He was within, he was staying at home, he was speaking mysteries to the disciples. So he who sows the word of God went out of his house to sow among the crowds. Now it is signified that this sower who sows is the Son of God and of the Father, sowing the word among the peoples. And at the same time note that this is the first parable that has been set down with its own interpretation; and we must beware, wherever the Lord expounds his own sayings and, when asked by the disciples, discourses within, lest we wish to understand anything else, or anything more or less, than has been expounded by him.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.4.4-8":["Some fell beside the way, and the birds came and ate them; and others fell on rocky places, where they did not have much earth, and the rest. Valentinus takes up this parable to prove his heresy, bringing in the notion that there are three natures, the spiritual, the animal and the earthly, whereas here there are four: one beside the way, another rocky, the third full of thorns, the fourth good ground. We put off its interpretation for a little, wishing with the disciples to hear in secret what is said:\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.4.9":["He who has ears to hear, let him hear. We are roused to the understanding of what is said whenever we are admonished by these words.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.4.10":["And the disciples came and said to him. It must be asked how the disciples come to him when Jesus is sitting in the ship, unless perhaps we are given to understand that some time before they had gone up into the ship with him and, standing there, had inquired about the interpretation of the parable.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.4.11-12":["Therefore I speak to them in parables, because seeing they do not see and hearing they do not hear. He says these things of those who stand on the shore and are separated from Jesus, and, with the sound of the waves roaring, do not hear clearly what is said, and in them is fulfilled the prophecy of Isaiah: By hearing you shall hear and shall not understand, and seeing you shall see and shall not see. These things were prophesied of the crowds that stand on the shore and do not deserve to hear the word of God. Let us therefore also come to Jesus with the disciples and ask him for the explanation of the parable, lest with the crowds we seem to have ears and eyes in vain.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.4.15":["Everyone who hears the word of the kingdom and does not understand. Putting this first, he urges us to listen more diligently to what is said.\n— Commentary on Matthew","The evil one comes and snatches what has been sown in his heart. The evil one snatches the good seed. And at the same time understand that it was sown in the heart, and that the different kinds of ground are the souls of believers.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.4.17":["But when tribulation and persecution have come because of the word, he is at once scandalized. Pay attention to what is said: he is at once scandalized. There is therefore some difference between the one who is compelled by many tribulations and punishments to deny Christ and the one who at the first persecution is immediately scandalized and falls.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.4.18-19":["But he who sows among thorns, this is he who hears the word, and the care of this world and the deceitfulness of riches chokes the word, and it is made fruitless. It seems to me that what is said to Adam according to the letter, Among thorns and thistles you shall eat your bread, also signifies mystically that whoever gives himself to the pleasures of the world and the cares of this world eats the heavenly bread and the true food among thorns. And he aptly added: the deceitfulness of riches chokes the word. For riches are alluring, doing one thing and promising another. Their possession is slippery, since they are carried about hither and thither, and with unsteady step they either desert those who have them or stuff those who do not have them. Hence the Lord also asserts that the rich enter the kingdom of heaven with difficulty, since riches choke the word of God and soften the rigor of the virtues.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.4.20":["But he who was sown in good ground, this is he who hears the word and understands and bears fruit. Just as in the bad ground there were three different kinds, the wayside and the rocky and the thorny places, so in the good ground there is a threefold diversity: of the hundredfold, the sixtyfold and the thirtyfold fruit. And in the one as in the other it is not the substance that is changed but the will, and it is the hearts of unbelievers as well as of believers that receive the seed. The evil one comes, he says, and snatches what has been sown in his heart, and in the second and third place: this is he, he says, who hears the word. In the exposition of the good ground too, this is he who hears the word. First, therefore, we must hear, then understand, and after understanding render the fruits of the teachings and produce either the hundredfold fruit or the sixtyfold and thirtyfold; of these we spoke more fully in the book Against Jovinian, and now we touch on them briefly, assigning the hundredfold fruit to virgins, the sixtyfold to widows and the continent, the thirtyfold to holy matrimony. Honorable indeed is marriage, and the bed undefiled. Some of our writers refer the hundredfold fruit to the martyrs; but if that is so, the holy partnerships of marriage are shut out from the good fruit.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.4.21":["You are the light of the world. A city set upon a mountain cannot be hidden, nor do they light a lamp and put it under a bushel, and the rest. He teaches boldness in preaching, so that the apostles may not hide themselves out of fear and be like a lamp under a bushel, but may come forward with all freedom, so as to preach on the housetops what they have heard in the chambers.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.4.25":["He who has, it will be given to him, and he will abound; but he who does not have, even what he has will be taken from him. It is not by an equality of judgment that something is added to those who have, and that from those who do not have even what they seem to have is taken away; rather, to the apostles, who have faith in Christ, something is granted, even if they have somewhat fewer virtues, while from the Jews, who did not believe in the Son of God, it is taken away, even if they possess something through the good of nature. For those who do not have the head of wisdom cannot understand anything wisely.\n— Commentary on Matthew","For to everyone who has will be given, and he will abound; but from him who does not have, even what he seems to have will be taken away from him. Many, though they are wise by nature and have keenness of mind, if they have been negligent and have spoiled the good of nature by sloth, in comparison with one who is somewhat slower but has made up by labor and diligence for what he had less of, lose the good of nature and see the reward that had been promised them pass to others. It can also be understood thus: to him who has faith and a good will in the Lord, even if, being a man, he has had something less in works, it will be given by the good judge; but he who has not had faith will lose even the other virtues that he seemed to possess by nature. And elegantly too: even what he seems, he says, to have will be taken away from him. For whatever is without faith in Christ ought not to be credited to him who has badly misused it, but to him who granted the good of nature even to a wicked servant.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.4.30-31":["The kingdom of heaven is like a grain of mustard seed, which a man took and sowed in his field; which indeed is the least of all seeds, but when it has grown it is greater than all garden herbs and becomes a tree, so that the birds of the sky come and dwell in its branches. Let it not be irksome to the reader if we set out whole parables. For things that are obscure must be discussed more fully, lest by excessive brevity the sense be wrapped up rather than explained. The kingdom of heaven is the preaching of the Gospel and the knowledge of the Scriptures, which leads to life, and of which it is said to the Jews: The kingdom of God shall be taken from you and given to a nation producing its fruits. Like, therefore, in this manner, is the kingdom to a grain of mustard seed, which a man took and sowed in his field. The man who sows in his field is understood by most to be the Savior, because he sows in the souls of believers; by others, a man himself sowing in his own field, that is, in himself and in his heart. Who is this who sows but our mind and our soul, which, receiving the grain of preaching and fostering the seed with the moisture of faith, makes it sprout in the field of its own breast?\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.4.31-32":["The preaching of the Gospel is the least of all teachings. For at the first hearing of its doctrine it has no credit of truth, since it preaches a man who is God, Christ dead, and the stumbling block of the cross. Compare a teaching of this kind with the doctrines of the philosophers and with their books, and with the splendor of their eloquence and the composition of their discourses, and you will see how much smaller than the other seeds is the sowing of the Gospel. But those, when they have grown, show nothing pungent, nothing vigorous, nothing vital; the whole of it bubbles up, flabby and withered, into garden herbs and grasses which quickly dry up and collapse. This preaching, however, which seemed small in the beginning, when it has been sown either in the soul of the believer or in the whole world, does not rise up into garden herbs but grows into a tree, so that the birds of the sky, by which we ought to understand either the souls of believers or powers given over to the service of God, come and dwell in its branches. The branches of the Gospel tree that has grown from the grain of mustard seed I take to be the diversities of doctrines, on which each of the birds mentioned above rests. Let us too take up the wings of a dove, so that, flying to higher places, we may dwell in the branches of this tree and make ourselves little nests of teachings, and, fleeing earthly things, hasten toward heavenly ones. Many, reading that the grain of mustard seed is the least of all seeds, and what is said in the Gospel by the disciples, Lord, increase our faith, and the answer given them by the Savior, Amen I say to you: If you had faith like a grain of mustard seed, you would say to this mountain: Move from this place, and it would move, think either that the apostles are asking for a small faith or that the Lord is in doubt about a small faith, whereas the apostle Paul judges faith compared to a grain of mustard seed to be the greatest. For what does he say? If I have such faith that I move mountains, but do not have love, it profits me nothing. Therefore what the Lord said is done by a faith that is compared to a grain of mustard seed, this the Apostle teaches can be done by faith entire.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.4.33-34":["All these things Jesus spoke in parables to the crowds, and without parables he did not speak to them. He spoke through parables not to the disciples but to the crowds; and to this day the crowds hear in parables, while the disciples question the Savior at home.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.4.36":["And as he went up into the boat his disciples followed him, and the rest. The fifth sign he did when, going up into the ship from Capernaum, he commanded the winds and the sea; the sixth, when in the country of the Gerasenes he gave the demons power over the swine; the seventh, when, entering his own city, he healed the second paralytic on his bed; for the first paralytic is the centurion's servant.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.4.38":["But he was sleeping. And they came and woke him, saying: Lord, save us. The type of this sign we read in Jonah, when, while the others are in peril, he himself is untroubled and sleeps, and is woken, and by the command and mystery of his passion frees those who woke him.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.4.39":["Then rising he rebuked the winds and the sea. And from this place we understand that all creatures perceive the Creator. For the things that are rebuked and commanded perceive the one who commands, not by the error of the heretics, who think all things are animate, but by the majesty of the Maker, to whom things insensible to us are sensible.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.4.41":["Moreover the men marveled, saying: What sort of man is this, that the winds and the sea obey him. It was not the disciples but the sailors and the others who were in the ship who marveled. But if anyone should contentiously maintain that those who marveled were the disciples, we shall answer that they were rightly called men, because they did not yet know the power of the Savior.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.5.7":["What have we to do with you, Son of God? Have you come here before the time to torment us? This confession is not one of will, on which the reward of confessing follows, but an extortion of necessity, which compels the unwilling; just as runaway slaves, if after a long time they see their master, beg for nothing else but to be spared the lash, so too the demons, seeing the Lord suddenly dwelling on earth, believed that he had come to judge them. The presence of the Savior is the torment of the demons. And some ridiculously think that the demons know the Son of God and the devil does not, because these are of less malice than he whose henchmen they are. Whereas all the knowledge of the disciples is to be referred to the master, and both the demons and the devil are to be understood rather to suspect the Son of God than to know him. For no one knows the Father except the Son and him to whom the Son wills to reveal him.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.5.13-14":["If you cast us out, send us into the herd of swine. And he said to them: Go. The Savior did not say: Go, as granting the demons what they asked, but so that through the killing of the swine an occasion of salvation might be offered to men. For the herdsmen, seeing these things, at once report them to the city. Let the Manichee blush. If the souls of men and of beasts are of the same substance and from the same author, how are two thousand swine drowned for the salvation of one man?\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.5.13":["But the old man declared to them how the devil, for men's sake, seizes even beasts of burden; that he is inflamed by such intense hatred for men that he desires to destroy not only them but what belongs to them. As an illustration of this he added the fact that before he was permitted to try the saintly Job, he made an end of all his substance. Nor ought it to disturb anyone that by the Lord's command two thousand swine were slain by the agency of demons, since those who witnessed the miracle could not have believed that so great a multitude of demons had gone out of the man unless an equally vast number of swine had rushed to ruin, showing that it was a legion that impelled them.\n— THE LIFE OF S. HILARION 23"],"MRK.5.17":["And behold, the whole city went out to meet Jesus, and when they saw him they asked him to pass from their borders. Those who ask him to pass beyond their borders do this not from pride (as some suppose) but from humility, by which they judged themselves unworthy of the Lord's presence, just as Peter too, at the catch of fish, falling at the Savior's knees, says: Go out from me, Lord, for I am a sinful man.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.5.22-23":["Behold, a certain ruler came and worshiped him, saying: My daughter has just now died; but come, lay your hand upon her, and she will live. And Jesus rose and followed him, and his disciples. This is the eighth sign, in which the ruler asks that his daughter be raised, not wishing to be shut out from the mystery of the true circumcision; but the woman with a flow of blood slips in and is healed in the eighth place, so that the ruler's daughter, shut out from this number, comes to the ninth, according to what is said in the psalms: Ethiopia shall go before with her hands to God; and: When the fullness of the Gentiles has come in, then all Israel will be saved.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.5.25-27":["Behold, a woman who had suffered a flow of blood for twelve years came up behind and touched the fringe of his garment. In the Gospel according to Luke it is written that the ruler's daughter was twelve years of age. Note, therefore, that this woman, that is, the people of the Gentiles, began to be sick at the time when the nation of the Jews believed. For a vice is not shown except by comparison with virtues. Now this woman with a flow of blood approaches the Lord not in a house, not in a city, because according to the law she was excluded from cities, but on the way, as the Lord was walking, so that while he was going to one woman, another might be healed. Whence the apostles too say: It was necessary that the word be preached to you, but since you have judged yourselves unworthy of salvation, we turn to the Gentiles.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.5.28":["For she said within herself: If I only touch the fringe of his garment, I shall be well. According to the law, whoever touches a menstruous woman or one with a flow of blood is unclean. She touches the Lord for this reason, that she too may be healed of the fault of her blood.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.5.34":["Take heart, daughter, your faith has made you well. Daughter, for this reason, that your faith has made you well. Nor did he say: Your faith will make you well, but has made you well; for in that you believed, you have already been made well.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.5.38":["And when Jesus had come into the ruler's house and had seen the flute players and the crowd in an uproar. To this day the girl lies dead in the ruler's house, and those who seem to be teachers are flute players singing a mournful song. The crowd of the Jews, too, is not a crowd of believers but a crowd of people in an uproar.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.5.39":["The girl is not dead, but sleeps, because all things live to God.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.5.40":["And when the crowd had been put out, he went in and took her by the hand. For they were not worthy to see the mystery of her rising, who mocked him that was raising her with unworthy insults.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.5.41-42":["And he took her by the hand, and the girl arose. Unless the hands of the Jews, which are full of blood, are first cleansed, their dead synagogue does not rise.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.6.1-2":["And it came to pass, when Jesus had finished these parables, he passed over from there, and coming into his own land he taught them in their synagogue. After the parables which he spoke to the people, and which the apostles alone understand, he passed over into his own country, so as to teach more openly there.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.6.2":["Whence has this man this wisdom and these mighty works? The amazing folly of the Nazarenes! They wonder whence Wisdom has wisdom and Power mighty works; but their error lies plain to see, for they supposed him to be the son of a carpenter.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.6.3":["Is not this the carpenter's son? Is not his mother called Mary, and his brothers James and Joseph, Simon and Judas, and his sisters, all of them with us? The error of the Jews is our salvation and the condemnation of the heretics. For they so far saw Jesus Christ as a man that they thought him the son of a carpenter: Is not this the carpenter's son? Do you wonder that they err about the brothers when they err about the father? This passage is set out more fully in the little book Against Helvidius mentioned above.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.6.4":["But Jesus said to them: A prophet is not without honor except in his own country and in his own house. It is almost natural for citizens always to envy their fellow citizens; for they do not consider the man's present works but remember his frail infancy, as though they themselves had not come to mature age through the same stages of life.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.6.5-6":["And therefore he did not do many mighty works there, because of their unbelief. Not that he could not have done many mighty works even among them in their unbelief, but lest, by doing many, he should condemn his unbelieving fellow citizens. It can also be understood in another way: that Jesus is despised in his house and in his own country, that is, among the people of the Jews, and therefore did few signs there, lest they be made wholly without excuse. But greater signs he does daily among the Gentiles through the apostles, not so much in the healing of bodies as in the salvation of souls.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.6.6":["And Jesus went about all the cities and villages, teaching in their synagogues and preaching the Gospel of the kingdom and healing every sickness and every infirmity. You see that he preached the Gospel equally to country places and cities and villages, that is, both to great and to small, so that he considered not the power of the noble but the salvation of believers. He went about the cities: having this as his work, which the Father had commanded, and this as his hunger, to save the unbelieving by his teaching. And he taught the Gospel of the kingdom in synagogues and in country places, and after the preaching and the teaching he healed every sickness and every infirmity, so that those whom the word had not convinced the works might persuade. It is said properly of the Lord: healing every sickness and every infirmity; for nothing is impossible to him.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.6.7":["And having called together his twelve disciples, he gave them power over unclean spirits, to cast them out and to heal every sickness and every infirmity. The kind and merciful Lord and Master does not begrudge his servants and disciples his own mighty works, and just as he himself had healed every sickness and every infirmity, so he grants to his apostles too the power to heal every sickness and every infirmity. But there is a great difference between having and granting, between giving and receiving. Whatever he does, he does by the power of the Lord; they, if they do anything, confess their own weakness and the power of the Lord, saying: In the name of Jesus, rise and walk. Note, moreover, that it is in the twelfth place that the power of signs is granted to the apostles.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.6.8-9":["No bag for the road. By this precept he refutes the philosophers who are commonly called bactroperites, because, though despisers of the world who count all things as nothing, they carried their storeroom about with them. Nor two tunics. By the two tunics he seems to me to indicate a double garment; not that in the regions of Scythia, stiff with glacial snow, anyone ought to be content with a single tunic, but that by the tunic we should understand clothing, lest, being clothed in one, we keep another for ourselves out of fear of what is to come. Nor of shoes. Plato too prescribes that the two extremities of the body should not be covered, and that one ought not to grow used to softness of the head and the feet; for when these have firmness, the rest are sturdier. Nor a staff. We who have the help of the Lord, why should we seek the protection of a stick? And because he had sent the apostles to preach as it were naked and unencumbered, and the condition of the teachers seemed to be hard, he tempered the severity of the precept with the sentence that follows, saying: The laborer is worthy of his food. Take only so much, he says, as is necessary for you in food and clothing. Hence the Apostle too repeats: Having food and clothing, with these we are content, and in another place: Let him who is catechized share with him who catechizes him in every good thing, so that disciples may make those whose spiritual things they reap sharers in their own carnal things, not for avarice but for necessity.\n— Commentary on Matthew","These things we have said according to history. But according to anagogy, teachers are not permitted to possess the gold and silver and money that is in the belts. We often read gold for the understanding, silver for speech, bronze for the voice. These we may not receive from others, but must possess them as given by the Lord. Nor may we take up the disciplines of heretics and philosophers and of perverse doctrine, nor be weighed down by the weight of the world, nor be double-minded, nor have our feet bound with deadly bonds, but, entering the holy ground, we must go barefoot, and not have a staff that is turned into a serpent, nor lean on any protection of the flesh, because a staff and stick of this kind is of reed, which, if you press on it a little, breaks and pierces the hand of the one leaning on it.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.6.8":["Do not possess gold or silver or money in your belts, no bag for the road, not two tunics, no shoes, nor a staff in the hand; for the laborer is worthy of his food. Consistently he gave these commands to the preachers of the truth, to whom he had said before: Freely you have received, freely give. For if they preach in such a way that they accept no price, the possession of gold and silver and coins is superfluous. For if they had had gold and silver, they would have seemed to preach not for the sake of men's salvation but for the sake of gain. Nor bronze in purses. He who had cut off riches cuts away almost even the necessities of life, so that the apostles, teachers of the true religion, who taught that all things are governed by providence, might show that they themselves took no thought for the morrow.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.6.10":["Into whatever city or village you enter, inquire who in it is worthy, and stay there until you leave. Concerning the ordination of a bishop and a deacon Paul says: Moreover they must also have a good testimony from those who are outside. The apostles, entering a new city, could not know who was of what sort. Therefore the host is to be chosen by the report of the people and the judgment of the neighbors, lest the dignity of the preaching be disfigured by the ill repute of the one who receives them. Though they ought to preach to all, one host is chosen, not conferring a benefit on him who is going to stay with him, but receiving one. For the words who in it is worthy are said so that he may know that he is receiving grace rather than giving it.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.6.11":["And whoever does not receive you nor hear your words, going outside, out of the house or the city, shake off the dust from your feet. The dust is shaken from the feet as a testimony of their labor, that they entered the city and the apostolic preaching reached even to them; or else the dust is shaken off so that they may accept nothing, not even what is necessary for food, from those who have spurned the Gospel.\n— Commentary on Matthew","Amen I say to you: It will be more tolerable for the land of Sodom and Gomorrah in the day of judgment than for that city. If it will be more tolerable for the land of Sodom and Gomorrah than for that city which has not received the Gospel, and more tolerable for this reason, that it was not preached to Sodom and Gomorrah, but it has been preached to this city and yet it has not received the Gospel, then there are different punishments even among sinners.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.6.14":["At that time Herod the tetrarch heard the report of Jesus and said to his servants: This is John the Baptist; he has risen from the dead, and therefore mighty works are at work in him. A certain one of the Church's interpreters asks the reasons why Herod suspected this, so as to think that John had risen from the dead and that therefore mighty works were at work in him; as though we had to render an account of another's error, or as though the sect of μετεμψυχώσεως (transmigration of souls) could take occasion from these words, when in any case, at the time when John was beheaded, the Lord was thirty years old, whereas μετεμψύχωσις (transmigration of souls) says that souls are introduced into different bodies after many cycles of years.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.6.17-18":["But Herod had seized John and bound him and put him in prison because of Herodias, his brother's wife. For John said to him: It is not lawful for you to have her. An old history relates that Philip, the son of Herod the elder, under whom the Lord fled into Egypt, and the brother of this Herod, under whom Christ suffered, married Herodias, the daughter of King Phetras; but that afterwards her father, his father-in-law, when certain quarrels had arisen against his son-in-law, took back his daughter and, to the grief of her former husband, joined her in marriage to Herod, his enemy. Who this Philip is, the evangelist Luke teaches more fully: In the fifteenth year of the reign of Tiberius Caesar, when Pontius Pilate was governing Judea, and Herod was tetrarch of Galilee, and Philip his brother was tetrarch of Iturea and the region of Trachonitis. So John the Baptist, who had come in the spirit and power of Elijah, with the same authority with which Elijah had rebuked Ahab and Jezebel, reproves Herod and Herodias because they had made an unlawful marriage, and because it is not lawful, while one's own brother is living, to take his wife; for he preferred to be in danger with the king rather than to be forgetful of the commandments of God for the sake of flattery.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.6.19-20":["And wishing to kill him, he feared the people, because they held him as a prophet. He dreaded indeed an uprising of the people on account of John, by whom he knew that very great crowds had been baptized in the Jordan; but he was overcome by love of his wife, out of burning desire for whom he had neglected even the commandments of God.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.6.21":["But on Herod's birthday the daughter of Herodias danced in the middle of the dining hall and pleased Herod. We find no one else who kept his birthday except Herod and Pharaoh, so that those whose impiety was equal might have a single festival.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.6.23":["With an oath he promised to give her whatever she should ask of him. I do not excuse Herod on the ground that he committed murder against his will and unwillingly because of the oath; he perhaps swore for this very purpose, to prepare his engines for an occasion to come. Otherwise, if he says that he did it because of the oath, had she asked for the death of his father, or of his mother, would he have done it or not? What, therefore, he would have refused in his own case, he ought to have disregarded in the case of the prophet as well.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.6.24-25":["Give me, she says, here on a dish the head of John the Baptist. Herodias, fearing that Herod might at some time come to his senses, or become a friend to his brother Philip, and that the unlawful marriage might be dissolved by divorce, advises her daughter to ask at once, at the very banquet, for the head of John: a fitting reward of blood for a fitting work of dancing.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.6.26":["And the king was saddened. It is the custom of the Scriptures that the historian narrates the opinion of many in the way it was believed by all at that time. Just as Joseph is called the father of Jesus even by Mary herself, so now too Herod is said to have been saddened, because those reclining at table thought so. For this dissembler of his own mind and skilled murderer displayed sadness on his face while he had joy in his mind.\n— Commentary on Matthew","But because of the oath and of those who were reclining with him, he ordered it to be given. He excuses the crime by the oath, so that under the pretext of piety he might become impious. And as for what he added, and because of those who were reclining with him, he wants all to be partners in his crime, so that bloody dishes might be served at a luxurious and impure banquet.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.6.28":["And his head was brought on a dish and given to the girl, and she brought it to her mother. We read in Roman history that Flaminius, a Roman general, was expelled from the senate by the censors because, when a little harlot reclining beside him said that she had never seen a man beheaded, he had agreed that a certain man guilty of a capital crime should be beheaded at the banquet: expelled because he had mixed feasting with blood and had offered up the death of a man, guilty though he was, for the delight of another, so that lust and murder were mingled together. How much more wicked are Herod and Herodias, and the girl who danced and asks for the head of the prophet as the price of blood, so as to have in her power the tongue that rebuked the unlawful marriage. Let this have been done according to the letter. But we see to this day, in the head of John the prophet, that the Jews have lost Christ, who is the head of the prophets.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.6.29":["And his disciples came up and took his body and buried it, and they came and told Jesus. Josephus reports that John was beheaded in a certain town of Arabia. And what follows, His disciples came up and took the body, we can understand of the disciples both of John himself and of the Savior. And they came and told Jesus.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.6.32":["When Jesus had heard this, he withdrew from there in a boat to a desert place apart. They announce the Baptist's death to the Savior; having heard of it, he withdraws to a desert place; not, as some think, out of fear of death, but sparing his enemies, lest they add murder to murder; or else putting off his own death to the day of the Passover, on which, because of the mystery, the lamb is to be sacrificed and the doorposts of the believers are to be sprinkled with blood. Or else he withdrew for this reason, to give us an example of avoiding the rashness of those who hand themselves over of their own accord, because not all persevere in torments with the same constancy with which they offer themselves to be tortured. For this reason he also commands in another place: When they persecute you in this city, flee into another. Elegantly too the evangelist does not say, he fled to a desert place, but he withdrew, so that he avoided his persecutors rather than feared them. Alternatively. After the head of the prophet had been cut off by the Jews and by the king of the Jews, and prophecy had lost its tongue and voice among them, Jesus passed over to the desert place of the Church, which before had not had a husband.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.6.33":["And when the crowds had heard, they followed him on foot from the cities. He may also, for another reason, having heard of John's death, have withdrawn into a desert place: to test the faith of the believers. In short, the crowds followed him on foot, not on beasts of burden, not in various vehicles, but by the labor of their own feet, to show the ardor of their minds. If we wish to lay open the reasons of every single word, we exceed the brevity of the work we have undertaken. Yet it must be said in passing that after the Lord had come into the desert, very great crowds followed him; for before he came into the solitudes of the Gentiles, he was worshiped by one people only.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.6.34":["And going out he saw a great crowd. In the words of the Gospel the spirit is always joined to the letter, and whatever seems cold at first sight is warm if you touch it. The Lord was in a desert place; the crowds followed him, leaving their cities, that is, their former ways of life and the variety of doctrines. But Jesus' coming out signifies that the crowds had indeed the will to go, but did not have the strength to arrive; therefore the Savior comes out from his place and goes to meet them, as also in another parable the penitent son; and seeing the crowd he has pity and heals their sicknesses, so that full faith may at once obtain its reward.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.6.35":["But when evening had come, his disciples came to him, saying, and the rest. All things are full of mysteries. He withdraws from Judea, he comes into a desert place, the crowds follow him, having left their cities, Jesus comes out to them, he has pity on the crowd, he heals their sick; and he does this not in the morning, not as the day grows, not at midday, but in the evening, when the sun of justice had set.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.6.37":["But Jesus said to them: They have no need to go; you give them to eat. They have no need to seek various foods and to buy themselves unknown loaves, when they have the heavenly bread with them. You give them to eat. He challenges the apostles to the breaking of bread, so that, when they testify that they have none, the greatness of the sign may become better known.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.6.38":["They answered him: We have nothing here but five loaves and two fishes. In another evangelist we read: There is a certain boy here who has five loaves, and he seems to me to signify Moses; the two fishes we understand either as both Testaments, or because an even number refers to the law. So the apostles, before the passion of the Savior and the flashing forth of the lightning-bright Gospel, had nothing but five loaves and two little fishes, which moved about in salt waters and in bitter waves.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.6.39-40":["And when he had ordered the crowd to recline on the grass. According to the letter the sense is plain; let us lay open the mysteries of the spiritual interpretation. They are ordered to recline on the grass, and according to another evangelist on the ground, by fifties or by hundreds, so that, after they have trodden down their flesh and have put under themselves all its flowers and the pleasures of the world like withered grass, they may then, through the repentance of the number fifty, ascend to the perfect summit of the number one hundred.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.6.41":["Taking the five loaves and the two fishes, looking up to heaven, he blessed and broke and gave the loaves to the disciples. He looks up to heaven to teach that our eyes must be directed there. He took the five loaves and the two little fishes into his hands, and broke them, and handed them to the disciples. As the Lord breaks them, a seedbed of food comes into being. For if they had stayed whole, and had not been torn into pieces nor divided into a manifold harvest, they could not have fed the crowds, and the children, and the women, and so great a multitude. So the law together with the prophets is broken and torn into pieces, and its mysteries are brought out into the open, so that what did not nourish while it was whole and remained in its former state might, divided into parts, nourish the multitude of the Gentiles.\n— Commentary on Matthew","And he gave the loaves to the disciples, and the disciples to the crowds. The crowds receive nourishment from the Lord through the apostles.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.6.43":["And they took up of the remnants twelve baskets full of fragments. Each of the apostles fills his basket from the Savior's remnants, either so that he may have from it something with which afterwards to offer food to the Gentiles, or so that he may teach from the remnants that those were real loaves which were afterwards multiplied. And at the same time ask how, in the desert and in so vast a solitude, no loaves are found but five only, and two little fishes, and yet twelve baskets are found so easily.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.6.44":["Now the number of those who ate was five thousand men, besides women and little children. In keeping with the number of the five loaves, the multitude of the men who ate is also five thousand. For it had not yet come, as in the narrative of another passage, to the number seven, of which those who eat are four thousand, a number close to that of the Gospels. Now five thousand men eat, men who had grown to the perfect man and were following him of whom Zechariah says: Behold a man, the Rising is his name. But women and little children, the frail sex and the lesser age, are unworthy of being numbered. Hence too in the book of Numbers, whenever the priests and the Levites and the army or the bands of fighting men are registered, slaves and women and little children and the undistinguished common people are passed over without number.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.6.45":["And he compelled his disciples to get into the boat and to go ahead of him across the water, until he dismissed the crowds. He commanded the disciples to cross the water, and compels them to get into the boat. By this word it is shown that they withdrew from the Lord unwillingly, since out of love for their teacher they do not want to be separated from him even for a moment of time.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.6.46":["And having dismissed the crowd, he went up the mountain alone to pray. If the disciples Peter and James and John, who had seen the glory of him transfigured, had been with him, perhaps they would have gone up the mountain with him; but the crowd cannot follow to the heights unless he has taught it by the sea on the shore and fed it in the desert. But that he went up alone to pray, do not refer to him who with five loaves satisfied five thousand men, besides little children and women, but to him who, having heard of John's death, withdrew into solitude; not that we separate the person of the Lord, but that his works are divided between God and man.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.6.47-48":["But the boat in the middle of the sea was tossed by the waves. Rightly had the apostles withdrawn from the Lord as though unwilling and reluctant, lest in his absence they should suffer shipwreck. In short, while the Lord was lingering on the summit of the mountain, at once a contrary wind arises and troubles the sea, and the apostles are in danger, and the impending shipwreck lasts until Jesus comes.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.6.48":["But in the fourth watch of the night he came to them walking upon the sea. Military guard-posts and watches are divided into spaces of three hours each. So when he says that the Lord came to them in the fourth watch of the night, he shows that they were in danger the whole night, and that at the very end of the night and at the consummation of the world he will bring them help.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.6.49":["And seeing him walking upon the sea, they were troubled, saying: It is a phantom. If, according to Marcion and Mani, our Lord was not born of a virgin but was seen as a phantom, how is it that the apostles now fear that they are seeing a phantom?\n— Commentary on Matthew","And for fear they cried out. A confused cry and an uncertain voice are the mark of great fear.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.6.50":["And at once Jesus spoke to them, saying: Have confidence, it is I. What was at issue first, that he heals first, and to those who are afraid he gives a command, saying: Have confidence, do not be afraid. And as to what follows, it is I, he does not add who he is: either they could recognize him from a voice known to them, as he spoke through the dark shadows of the night, or they recalled that it was he himself whom they knew to have spoken to Moses: This is what you say to the sons of Israel: He who is has sent me to you.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.6.53":["And when they had crossed the water, they came into the land of Gennesaret. If we knew what Gennesaret sounds like in our language, we would understand how Jesus, through the type of the apostles and of the ship, brings the Church, delivered from the shipwreck of persecution, across to the shore, and makes it rest in a most tranquil harbor.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.6.54-55":["And when the men of that place had recognized him, they sent into all that region. They recognized him by report, not by face; or at any rate, because of the greatness of the signs that he was performing among the peoples, he was also known by face to very many. And see how great is the faith of the men of the land of Gennesaret, that they are not content with the health of those present only, but send to other cities round about, so that all may run to the physician.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.6.56":["And they brought to him all who were ill, and they begged him that they might touch even the fringe of his garment; and whoever touched it were made whole. Let those who are ill touch, not the body of Jesus, nor his whole garment, but the outermost fringe; and whoever touch it will be healed. Understand the fringe of his garment as the least commandment, for whoever transgresses it shall be called least in the kingdom of heaven; or as the assumption of the body, through which we come to the Word of God and afterwards enjoy his majesty.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.7.5":["Why do your disciples transgress the tradition of the elders? Astonishing is the folly of the Pharisees and scribes. They accuse the Son of God of not keeping the traditions and precepts of men.\n— Commentary on Matthew","For they do not wash their hands when they eat bread. The hands, that is, the works, not of the body but of the soul, are what must be washed, so that the word of God may come to be in them.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.7.9":["But he answered and said to them: Why do you also transgress the commandment of God for the sake of your tradition? He refutes a false charge with a true answer. Since, he says, you neglect the precepts of the Lord for the sake of the tradition of men, why do you think my disciples are to be reproved for making light of the commands of the elders in order to keep the decrees of God?\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.7.10-12":["For God said: Honor father and mother, and whoever curses father or mother, let him die the death. But you say: Whoever says to father or mother: Whatever gift is from me will profit you; and he has not honored his father or his mother. Honor in the Scriptures is understood not so much in greetings and the paying of courtesies as in alms and the offering of gifts. Honor, says the Apostle, widows who are truly widows, and here honor is understood as a gift; and in another place: Presbyters are to be honored with double honor, especially those who labor in the word and teaching of God. And by this commandment we are bidden not to close the mouth of the ox that treads out the grain, and that the laborer be worthy of his wages. The Lord, considering either the infirmities or the ages or the poverty of parents, had commanded that sons should honor their parents also by supplying them with the necessities of life. Wishing to overturn this most provident law of God, so as to bring in impiety under the name of piety, the scribes and Pharisees taught the worst of sons that if anyone wished to vow to God, who is the true Father, the things that were to be offered to his parents, the offering to the Lord should take precedence over gifts to parents. Or else the parents themselves, shunning what they saw consecrated to God so as not to incur the charge of sacrilege, were worn down by want; and so it came about that the offering of the children, under pretext of the temple and of God, went to the profit of the priests. This most wicked tradition of the Pharisees came from another occasion. Many who held men bound to them by debt, men unwilling to repay what had been lent them, assigned these debts to the priests, so that the money, once exacted, might serve the ministries of the temple and the priests' own uses. But it can also, briefly, have this sense: The gift that is from me will profit you. You compel sons, he says, to say to their parents: Whatever gift I was going to offer to God I spend on your food, and it profits you, O father and mother; so that they, afraid to accept what they see made over to God, would rather lead a life of want than eat of consecrated things.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.7.15":["Not what enters the mouth makes a man common, but what comes out of the mouth, this makes a man common. The word makes common is proper to the Scriptures and is not worn in public speech. The Jewish people, boasting that they are God's portion, call common the foods that all men use, for example pork, oysters, hares and animals of that kind that do not divide the hoof nor chew the cud, nor, among fish, have scales. Hence it is also written in the Acts of the Apostles: What God has sanctified, do not call common. Common, then, that which lies open to other men and is, as it were, not of God's portion, is used for unclean. Not what enters the mouth defiles a man, but what comes out of the mouth, this defiles a man. The prudent reader may object and say: If what enters the mouth does not defile a man, why do we not eat things sacrificed to idols? And the Apostle writes: You cannot drink the cup of the Lord and the cup of demons. It must be understood, therefore, that the foods themselves, and every creature of God, are clean in themselves, but the invocation of idols and demons makes them unclean.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.7.17-18":["And Peter answered and said to him: Explain this parable to us. And he said: Are you also still without understanding? What had been said openly and lay plain to the hearing, the apostle Peter thinks was spoken in a parable, and in a plain matter he seeks a mystical understanding; and he is rebuked by the Lord for thinking that something was said in parable which he had spoken plainly. From this we observe that a hearer is at fault who wishes to understand either obscure things plainly or things plainly said obscurely.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.7.18-19":["Do you not understand that everything that enters the mouth goes into the belly and is cast out into the privy? All the passages of the Gospels are full of stumbling blocks for heretics and the perverse. And from this little saying some slander the Lord as ignorant of natural science, as though he thought that all foods go into the belly and are passed into the privy, when in fact food, as soon as it is taken in, is diffused through the limbs and veins and marrow and sinews; whence too, they say, many who through a disorder of the stomach suffer constant vomiting throw up what they have taken in immediately after dinners and luncheons, and yet are fleshy, because at the first touch the more liquid food and drink are diffused through the members. But men of this kind, while they wish to censure another's ignorance, display their own. For however thin the moisture and however liquid the food, once it has been concocted and digested in the veins and limbs, it slips down through the hidden passages of the body, which the Greeks call πόρους, to the lower parts, and goes into the privy.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.7.21":["For out of the heart come evil thoughts, murders, adulteries, fornications, and the rest. Out of the heart, he says, come evil thoughts. Therefore the ruling part of the soul is not, as Plato holds, in the brain, but, as Christ teaches, in the heart; and this saying refutes those who think that thoughts are sent in by the devil and do not arise from one's own will. The devil can be a helper and an inciter of evil thoughts; their author he cannot be; but if, ever lying in ambush, he has fanned the slight spark of our thoughts into flame with his kindling. We ought not to suppose that he also searches out the hidden things of the heart, but rather that he judges from the bearing and gestures of the body what we are turning over within: for example, if he has seen us often looking back at a beautiful woman, he understands that the heart has been wounded by the dart of love.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.7.24-25":["And Jesus went out from there and withdrew into the parts of Tyre and Sidon. Leaving behind the scribes and Pharisees, his slanderers, he passes over into the parts of Tyre and Sidon in order to heal the Tyrians and Sidonians. And the Canaanite woman goes out from her former territory in order to obtain, by crying out, healing for her daughter. Note that it is in the fifteenth place that the Canaanite daughter is healed.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.7.25-26":["Have mercy on me, Lord, son of David; my daughter is badly vexed by a demon. She knew to call him son of David because she had already gone out from her own territory and, by a change of place and of faith, had left behind the error of the Tyrians and Sidonians. My daughter is badly vexed by a demon. I think that the daughter of the Church is the souls of believers, which were badly vexed by a demon, not knowing the Creator and worshiping stone.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.7.25-28":["But she came and worshiped him, saying. Wonderful, under the person of the Canaanite woman, are the faith, the patience and the humility of the Church: faith, by which she believed that her daughter could be healed; patience, by which, though so often scorned, she perseveres in her prayers; humility, by which she compares herself not to dogs but to whelps. Now the heathen are called dogs because of idolatry, for, given over to the eating of blood and to the corpses of the dead, they are carried away into madness. Note that this Canaanite woman, persevering, calls him first son of David, then Lord, and at the last worships him as God.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.7.28-29":["But she said: Yes, Lord, for even the little dogs eat of the crumbs that fall from their masters' table. I know, she says, that I do not deserve the children's bread, and that I cannot take whole food or sit at table with the father; but I am content with the whelps' leavings, so that through the humility of crumbs I may come to the greatness of the whole loaf. O wonderful reversal of things! Israel once a son, we dogs. As faith differs, the order of the names is changed. Of them it is later said: Many dogs have surrounded me; and: Look at the dogs, look at the evil workers, look at the concision. We hear, with the Syrophoenician woman and with the woman who had suffered a flow of blood: Great is your faith, let it be done according to your faith; and: Daughter, your faith has made you whole.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.8.1-3":["But Jesus called his disciples together and said: I have pity on the crowd, because they have now been staying with me three days and have nothing to eat; and I am unwilling to send them away fasting, lest they faint on the way. He wishes to feed those whom he has healed: first he takes away their disabilities, then he offers food to the healthy. He also calls his disciples together and tells them what he is about to do, either to give them a master's example that counsels are to be shared with the lesser and with disciples, or so that from the conversation they might understand the greatness of the sign, when they answer that they have no loaves in the desert. I have pity, he says, on the crowd, because they have now been staying with me three days. He has pity on the crowd because, in the number of three days, they believed in the Father, the Son and the Holy Spirit. And they have nothing to eat. The crowd is always hungry and in need of food unless it is filled by the Lord. And I am unwilling to send them away fasting, lest they faint on the way. They were hungry after their great disabilities, and in patience they were awaiting the food to come. Jesus does not wish to send them away fasting, lest they faint on the way. He is in peril, then, who hastens to reach the longed-for dwelling without the heavenly bread. Hence too the angel says to Elijah: Arise and eat, for you are to walk a great way.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.8.1-9":["And the disciples say to him: Where then are we to get so many loaves in the desert as to fill so great a crowd? And Jesus said to them: How many loaves have you? And they said: Seven, and a few little fishes, and the rest. Of this sign we have already spoken above, and to repeat the same things is the mark of an idle man; let us dwell only on the points in which they differ. Above we read: When evening had come, the disciples came to him, saying: The place is a desert, and the rest. Here, having called the disciples together, the Lord himself speaks: I have pity on the crowd, because they have now been staying with me three days. There, there were five loaves and two fishes; here, seven loaves and a few little fishes. There they recline upon the grass, here upon the ground. There those who eat are five thousand, according to the number of the loaves they eat; here, four thousand. There twelve baskets are filled with what is left of the fragments; here, seven hampers. In the former sign, then, because they were near and neighbors to the five senses, it is not the Lord himself who is mindful of them but the disciples, and they are mindful of them at evening, with night near and the sun already sinking. Here, however, the Lord himself is mindful of them, and says that he has pity, and sets out the causes of his pity: Because they have now been staying with me three days, and he does not wish to send them away fasting lest they faint on the way. These who are fed from seven loaves, that is, by a sacred and perfect number, are not five thousand but four thousand, a number that is always set down in praise; and a four-cornered stone does not totter, is not unstable; and for this reason the Gospels too are consecrated in that number.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.8.11":["Then some of the scribes and Pharisees answered him, saying: We want to see a sign from you. They ask for a sign as though the signs they had seen were not signs. But in another evangelist what they ask for is set out more fully: We want to see a sign from you from heaven, they desired either that fire should come down from on high in the manner of Elijah, or that, after the likeness of Samuel, in the summertime thunder should roar, lightning flash and rains pour down; as if they could not slander those things too, and say that they had happened from hidden and various affections of the air. For you who slander the things you see with your eyes, hold in your hand and feel in their benefit, what will you do with things that come from heaven? Surely you will answer that the magicians in Egypt too did many signs from heaven.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.8.13-14":["And leaving them he went away; and when his disciples had come across the water, they had forgotten to take bread. Leaving the scribes and Pharisees, to whom he had said: An evil and adulterous generation seeks a sign, and no sign will be given it except the sign of Jonah, he rightly went away across the water, and the people of the Gentiles followed him. What the sign of Jonah means has already been said above.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.8.15":["Beware of the leaven of the Pharisees and Sadducees. He who bewares of the leaven of the Pharisees and Sadducees does not keep the precepts of the law and of the letter; he neglects the traditions of men in order to do the commandment of God.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.8.17-21":["Why are you reasoning among yourselves, you of little faith, that you have no bread? Do you not yet understand, nor remember the five loaves of the five thousand men, and how many baskets you took up, and the rest. On the occasion of the precept that the Savior had given, saying: Beware of the leaven of the Pharisees and Sadducees, he teaches them what the five loaves and the seven mean, and the five thousand men and the four thousand who were fed in the desert: that, although the greatness of the signs is evident, yet something else as well is shown by spiritual understanding. For if the leaven of the Pharisees and Sadducees signifies not bodily bread but perverse traditions and heretical doctrines, why should not the foods too with which the people of God was nourished signify true and whole doctrine? Someone may ask and say: How did they have no bread, who, right after filling seven hampers, went up into the boat and came into the territory of Magadan, and there, while sailing, hear that they must beware of the leaven of the Pharisees and Sadducees? But Scripture testifies that they had forgotten to take them along. This is the leaven of which the Apostle too speaks: A little leaven corrupts the whole lump. Leaven of this kind, which must be shunned by every means, Marcion had, and Valentinus, and all the heretics. Leaven has this power: if it is mixed with flour, what seemed small grows into something greater and draws the whole mass of dough to its own taste. So too heretical doctrine: if it has cast even a tiny spark into your breast, in a short time it grows up into a huge flame and draws the whole of a man's possession to itself. Finally there follows: Then they understood that he had not said they should beware of the leaven of bread, but of the teaching of the Pharisees and Sadducees.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.8.27":["But Jesus came into the parts of Caesarea Philippi. This Philip is the brother of Herod of whom we spoke above, the tetrarch of the regions of Iturea and Trachonitis, who in honor of Tiberius Caesar named Caesarea Philippi (which is now called Paneas), and it is in the province of Phoenicia; he imitated his father Herod, who in honor of Augustus Caesar named Caesarea, which was formerly called Strato's Tower, and who, from the name of his daughter, built Libias beyond the Jordan. This place is Caesarea Philippi, where the Jordan rises at the roots of Lebanon; and it has two springs, one named Ior and the other Dan, which, mingled together, make the name Jordan.\n— Commentary on Matthew","And he asked his disciples, saying: Whom do men say that the Son of Man is? He did not say: Whom do men say that I am, but the Son of Man, lest he seem to inquire boastfully about himself. And note that wherever son of man is written in the Old Testament, in the Hebrew son of Adam is set down; and that which we read in the psalm: Sons of men, how long will you be heavy of heart? in the Hebrew is said sons of Adam. Beautifully, moreover, he asks: Whom do men say that the Son of Man is, because those who speak of the Son of Man are men; but those who understand his divinity are called not men but gods.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.8.28":["And they said: Some John the Baptist, others Elijah, but others Jeremiah or one of the prophets. I wonder at certain interpreters who inquire into the causes of each one's errors and weave a very long discussion of why some thought our Lord Jesus Christ to be John, others Elijah, but others Jeremiah or one of the prophets, when they could have erred thus about Elijah and Jeremiah in the same way that Herod erred about John, saying: John, whom I beheaded, has himself risen from the dead, and mighty works are at work in him.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.8.29":["But whom do you say that I am? Simon Peter answered: You are the Christ, the Son of the living God. Prudent reader, observe that from what follows and from the texture of the discourse the apostles are by no means called men but gods. For when he had said: Whom do men say that the Son of Man is, he added: But whom do you say that I am? Since they, being men, think human things, you who are gods, whom do you consider me to be? Peter, in the person of all the apostles, professes: You are the Christ, the Son of the living God. He calls him the living God by comparison with those gods who are thought to be gods but are dead, meaning Saturn, Jupiter, Ceres, Liber, Hercules and the other monstrosities of idols.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.8.30":["Then he commanded his disciples that they should tell no one that he himself was Jesus Christ. Above, when sending the disciples to preach, he had ordered them to announce his coming; here he commands them not to say that he is Jesus Christ. It seems to me that it is one thing to preach Christ, another to preach Jesus Christ. Christ is a common name of dignity; Jesus is the proper name of the Savior. But it may be that he did not wish to be preached before the passion and resurrection for this reason, that, when the mystery of the blood had afterward been completed, he might more fittingly say to the apostles: Go, teach all nations, and the rest. And lest anyone think that this is only our own understanding and not the sense of the Gospel, what follows sets out the causes of the preaching forbidden at that time.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.8.31":["From then on Jesus began to show his disciples that he must go to Jerusalem and suffer many things from the elders and scribes and chief priests, and be killed, and on the third day rise again. Now the sense is: Preach me then, when I have suffered these things, because it does no good to preach Christ publicly and to spread abroad among the peoples the majesty of him whom a little later they are to see scourged and crucified, suffering many things from the elders and scribes and chief priests. And now Jesus suffers many things from those who crucify the Son of God for themselves again, and who, though they are regarded in the Church as elders and chief priests, following the simple letter, kill the Son of God, who is perceived wholly in the spirit.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.8.32-33":["And Peter, taking him aside, began to rebuke him, saying: Far be it from you, Lord; this shall not happen to you. He turned and said to Peter: Go behind me, Satan; you are a stumbling block to me, because you do not savor the things that are of God but those that are of men. We have often said that Peter had an excessive ardor and the very greatest love toward the Lord Savior. Because, then, after his confession in which he had said: You are the Christ, the Son of the living God, and the Savior's reward which he had heard: Blessed are you, Simon Bar Iona, because flesh and blood has not revealed it to you, but my Father who is in heaven, he suddenly hears from the Lord that he must go to Jerusalem and there suffer many things from the elders and scribes and chief priests and be killed and on the third day rise again, he does not want his confession destroyed, nor does he think it can happen that the Son of God should be killed; and he takes him into his affection, or leads him apart, lest he seem to rebuke his master in the presence of the other fellow disciples, and began to rebuke him with the affection of one who loves, and to say, wishing it: Far be it from you, Lord, or, as it is better had in the Greek, Ἵλεώς σοι κύριε, that is: Be propitious, Lord; this shall not happen, it cannot be, nor do my ears accept it, that the Son of God is to be killed. To him the Lord, turning, says: Get back behind me, Satan; you are a stumbling block to me. Satan is interpreted adversary or opponent. Because, he says, you speak things contrary to my will, you ought to be called an adversary. Many think that it was not Peter who was rebuked, but the adversary spirit who was suggesting to the apostle to say these things. But to me the apostle's error, coming from an affection of piety, will never seem an incitement of the devil. Go behind me, Satan, is said to the devil. Get back, Peter hears, get back behind me, that is, follow my judgment, because you do not savor the things that are of God but those of men. It is my will and the Father's, whose will I came to do, that I should die for the salvation of men; you, considering only your own will, do not want the grain of wheat to fall into the earth so that it may bear many fruits. Let the prudent reader inquire how, after such great blessedness: Blessed are you, Simon Bar Iona, and: You are Peter, and upon this rock I will build my Church, and the gates of hell shall not prevail against it, and to you I will give the keys of the kingdom of heaven, and whatever you bind or loose upon earth shall be bound or loosed in heaven, he now hears: Get back behind me, Satan; you are a stumbling block to me, or what so sudden a turnabout this is, that after such great rewards he is called Satan. But let him who asks this consider that that blessing and blessedness and power and the building of the Church upon him were promised to Peter in the future, not given in the present. I will build, he says, my Church upon you, and the gates of hell shall not prevail against it, and I will give you the keys of the kingdom of heaven. All of it is in the future; and if he had given it to him at once, the error of a wrong confession would never have found a place in him.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.8.34":["Then Jesus said to his disciples: If anyone wishes to come after me, let him deny himself and take up his cross and follow me, and the rest. He who puts off the old man with his deeds denies himself, saying: But I live, now not I, but Christ lives in me, and he takes up his cross and is crucified to the world. And he to whom the world is crucified follows the crucified Lord.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.8.37":["Or what exchange shall a man give for his soul? For Israel, Egypt and Ethiopia and Soene are given as an exchange; for the human soul the only recompense is that which the psalmist sings: What shall I render to the Lord for all that he has rendered to me? I will take the cup of salvation, and I will call upon the name of the Lord.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.8.38":["For the Son of Man is to come in the glory of his Father with his angels, and then he will render to each according to his work. Peter, scandalized at the announcement of the Lord's death, had been rebuked by the Lord's judgment; the disciples were challenged to deny themselves and take up their cross and follow their master in the spirit of men going to their death. Great was the terror of the hearers, and such as could, with the chief of the apostles terrified, cast fear into the others too. Therefore glad things succeed the sad, and he says: The Son of Man is to come in the glory of his Father with his angels. You fear death: hear of the glory of the one triumphing; you dread the cross: listen to the ministries of the angels. And then, he says, he will render to each according to his works. There is no distinction of Jew and heathen, of man and woman, of poor and rich, where not persons but works are considered.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.9.1":["Amen I say to you: There are some of those standing here who shall not taste death until they see the Son of Man coming in his kingdom. He had wished to heal the terror of the apostles with the hope of what was promised, saying: The Son of Man is to come in the glory of his Father with his angels; adding besides the authority of the judge: And he will render to each according to his works. The silent thought of the apostles could have suffered a stumbling block of this sort: slaughter and death you say are coming now; but what you promise, that you will be present in the glory of the Father with the ministries of the angels and the power of a judge, this will be for days to come and is put off to long ages. Foreseeing, then, what they could object, the present fear, the knower of hidden things compensates it with a present reward. For what does he say? There are some of those standing here who shall not taste death until they see the Son of Man coming in his kingdom, so that what he is to be like when he comes afterward may, because of your unbelief, be shown in the present time.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.9.2-3":["And he was transfigured before them. Such as he is to be at the time of judging, such he appeared to the apostles. But as to his saying: he was transfigured before them, let no one think that he lost his former form and face, or gave up the reality of his body and took on a body either spiritual or airy; but how he was transformed the evangelist shows, saying:\n\nAnd his face shone like the sun, and his garments became white as snow. Where the splendor of the face is shown and the whiteness of the garments is described, the substance is not taken away, but the glory is changed. His face shone like the sun. Certainly the Lord was transformed into that glory with which he is to come afterward in his kingdom. The transformation added splendor; it did not take away the face. Grant that the body was spiritual: were the garments also changed, which were so white that another evangelist said: Such as no fuller on earth can make? But what a fuller on earth can make is bodily and subject to touch, not spiritual and airy, a thing that deceives the eyes and is seen only as a phantasm.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.9.2":["And after six days Jesus took Peter and James and John his brother. Why Peter and James and John are separated from the rest in certain places of the Gospels, or what privilege they have beyond the other apostles, we have often said. Now it is asked how he took them after six days and led them up a high mountain apart, when Luke the evangelist puts the number eight. But the answer is easy: here the middle days are given; there the first and the last are added. For it is not said: After eight days Jesus takes Peter and James and John, but on the eighth day.\n— Commentary on Matthew","And he leads them up a high mountain apart. To lead the disciples up to the mountains is part of the kingdom. They are led apart because many are called, few chosen.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.9.4":["And behold, there appeared to them Moses and Elijah, talking with him. When the scribes and Pharisees were testing him and demanding signs from heaven, he would not give them, but refuted the perverse demand with a prudent answer. Here, however, to increase the faith of the apostles, he gives a sign from heaven, Elijah coming down from the place to which he had ascended, and Moses rising from the underworld; as Ahaz too is instructed through Isaiah to ask for himself a sign from on high or from hell. For as to what was said: There appeared to them Moses and Elijah, talking with him, it is also reported in another Gospel that they told him what he was to suffer at Jerusalem. The Law and the Prophets are shown, which in frequent utterances announced both the passion and the resurrection of the Lord.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.9.5-6":["If you wish, I will make here three tabernacles, one for you, and one for Moses, and one for Elijah. You err, Peter, as another evangelist also testifies: you do not know what you are saying. Do not seek three tabernacles, when there is one tabernacle of the Gospel, in which the Law and the Prophets are to be summed up. But if you seek three tabernacles, by no means compare the servants with the Lord. Rather, make three tabernacles, or rather one, for the Father and the Son and the Holy Spirit, so that those whose divinity is one may also have one tabernacle in your breast.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.9.5":["But Peter answered and said to Jesus: Lord, it is good for us to be here. Because he had climbed up to the mountains, he does not want to go down to earthly things, but to remain always on the heights.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.9.6":["And the disciples, hearing it, fell on their face and were exceedingly afraid. They are struck with dread for a threefold cause: either because they had recognized that they had erred, or because the bright cloud had covered them, or because they had heard the voice of God the Father speaking; human frailty does not endure to bear the sight of a greater glory, and, trembling in its whole soul and body, it falls to the earth. The more anyone seeks greater things, the more he collapses to the lower, if he is ignorant of his own measure.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.9.7":["While he was still speaking, behold, a bright cloud overshadowed them, and a voice from the cloud, saying: This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased; hear him. Because he had asked imprudently, he therefore does not merit the Lord's answer, but the Father answered for the Son, so that the word of the Lord might be fulfilled: I do not bear witness for myself, but the Father who sent me, he bears witness for me. And the cloud is seen to be bright and overshadows them, so that those who were seeking a fleshly tabernacle of boughs or tents might be covered by the shelter of the bright cloud. The voice too of the Father speaking from heaven is heard, to bear witness to the Son and, removing Peter's error, to teach him the truth, or rather, through Peter, the other apostles. This is, he says, my beloved Son: for him a tabernacle is to be made, him you must obey; this is my Son; they are servants; Moses and Elijah ought themselves, with you, to prepare a tabernacle for the Lord in the inner recesses of their heart.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.9.8":["And lifting up their eyes they saw no one but Jesus alone. Reasonably, after they had risen they saw no one but Jesus alone, lest, if Moses and Elijah had remained with the Lord, the Father's voice might seem uncertain as to whom above all it bore witness. They see, then, Jesus standing, the cloud taken away, and Moses and Elijah vanished; because after the shadow of the Law and the Prophets, which had covered the apostles with its veil, has departed, both are found in the Gospel.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.9.9":["And as they were coming down from the mountain, Jesus commanded them, saying: Tell the vision to no one until the Son of Man rises from the dead. A foretaste of the kingdom to come and the glory of the one triumphing had been shown on the mountain. He does not wish, therefore, that this be preached among the peoples, lest it both be incredible because of the greatness of the thing and, after such great glory, the cross that followed should cause a stumbling block among untaught minds.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.9.11":["And the disciples asked him, saying: Why then do the scribes say that Elijah must come first? Unless we know the causes why the disciples asked about the name of Elijah, their question seems foolish and out of place. For what has it to do with the things written above, to ask about the coming of Elijah? It is a tradition of the Pharisees, according to the prophet Malachi, who is the last of the twelve, that Elijah will come before the coming of the Savior and turn back the heart of the fathers to the sons and of the sons to the fathers, and restore all things to their ancient state. The disciples therefore suppose that this transformation of glory is the one they had seen on the mountain, and they say: If you have already come in glory, how is it that your forerunner does not appear? especially since they had seen Elijah withdraw. But when they add: The scribes say that Elijah must come first, by saying first they show that, unless Elijah has come, it is not the coming of the Savior according to the Scriptures.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.9.12-13":["But he answered and said to them: Elijah indeed is to come and will restore all things; but I say to you that Elijah has already come, and the rest. He who is to come at the second coming of the Savior in the truth of the body now comes through John in power and spirit; but they did not know him and did to him whatever they wished, that is, they despised and beheaded him.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.9.12":["So also the Son of Man is to suffer by them. Now it is asked, since Herod and Herodias killed John, how they themselves are said to have crucified Jesus as well, when we read that he was killed by the scribes and Pharisees; and the short answer is that both in John's murder the faction of the Pharisees consented, and in the slaying of the Lord Herod joined his will, he who, after mocking and despising him, sent him back to Pilate to crucify him.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.9.18":["But as to his saying: I brought him to your disciples, and they could not cure him, he covertly accuses the apostles, although the impossibility of curing is sometimes to be referred not to the weakness of those who cure but to the faith of those who are to be cured, as the Lord says: Be it done to you according to your faith.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.9.19":["Jesus answered and said: O unbelieving and perverse generation, how long shall I be with you? How long shall I bear with you? Not that he was overcome by weariness, and that he who is meek and gentle, who like a lamb before its shearer did not open his mouth, broke out into words of fury; but that, in the likeness of a physician who sees a sick man conducting himself against his orders, he might say: How long shall I come to your house, how long shall I waste the diligence of my art, when I order one thing and you do another? So little, moreover, is he angry with the man rather than with the vice, and through one man he convicts the Jews of unbelief, that he immediately added: Bring him here to me.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.9.22":["Lord, have mercy on my son, for he is a lunatic and suffers badly; for he often falls into the fire, and frequently into the water; and I brought him to your disciples, and they could not cure him. For what reason a demon, watching the course of the moon, seizes men and strives through the creatures to defame the Creator, we have said above. But it seems to me that according to tropology the lunatic is he who is changed toward vices from moment to moment of the hours and does not persist in what he has begun, but waxes and wanes, and is now carried into the fire with which the hearts of adulterers are kindled, now into the waters, which cannot extinguish charity.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.9.25-26":["And Jesus rebuked him, and the demon went out of him. Not he who was suffering but the demon ought to have been rebuked. Or else he rebuked the boy, and the demon went out of him, because he had been oppressed by the demon on account of his own sins.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.9.28":["And they said: Why could we not cast him out? He said to them: Because of your unbelief. This is what he says in another place: Whatever you ask in my name, believing, you will receive. So whenever we do not receive, it is not the inability of the giver but the fault of those who pray.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.9.29":["But this kind is not cast out except through prayer and fasting. While he teaches how a most wicked demon can be expelled, he instructs all for life.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.9.30-31":["And while they were staying in Galilee, Jesus said to them: The Son of Man is to be betrayed into the hands of men, and they will kill him, and on the third day he will rise again. And they were exceedingly saddened. He always mingles sadness with prosperous things, so that when these come suddenly they may not terrify the apostles, but may be borne by minds that have prepared for them beforehand. For if it saddens them that he is to be killed, what is said ought to gladden them: on the third day he will rise again. Moreover, that they are saddened, and saddened exceedingly, does not come from unbelief, for otherwise they also knew that Peter had been rebuked because he did not savor the things that are of God but those of men; but because, out of love for their master, they cannot bear to hear anything adverse and lowly about him.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.9.34":["At that hour the disciples came to Jesus, saying: Who, do you think, is greater in the kingdom of heaven? What I have often advised must be observed now too: the causes of each of the Lord's sayings and deeds are to be sought. After the stater was found, after the tribute was paid, what does this sudden question of the apostles mean? At that hour the disciples came to Jesus, saying: Who, do you think, is greater in the kingdom of heaven? Because they had seen the same tribute paid for Peter and for the Lord, they judged from the equality of the price that Peter had been preferred to all the apostles, since in the payment of the tribute he had been put on a level with the Lord; therefore they ask who is greater in the kingdom of heaven. And Jesus, seeing their thoughts and understanding the causes of their error, wishes to heal the desire for glory by a contest of humility.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.9.36":["And Jesus, calling a little child, set him in their midst. Either simply any little child, so that he might seek out that age and show the likeness of innocence; or else he set himself in their midst as the little child, he who had come not to be served but to serve, so that he might give them an example of humility. Others interpret the little child as the Holy Spirit, whom he placed in the hearts of the disciples so that they might exchange pride for humility.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.9.37":["And whoever receives one such little child in my name receives me. Whoever is such that he imitates the humility and innocence of Christ, in him Christ is received. And prudently, lest the apostles, when honor has been paid to them, should think themselves the ones honored, he added that they are to be received not for their own merit but in honor of their master.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.9.41":["And whoever gives one of these least ones a cup of cold water to drink, only in the name of a disciple, amen I say to you, he will not lose his reward. We read in the prophet David: To make excuses in sins, because many put forward, as it were, just pretexts for their sins, so that what they commit by will they may seem to sin by necessity. The Lord, the searcher of heart and reins, beholds the future thoughts in each one. He had said: Whoever receives you receives me. But many false prophets and false preachers could hinder this command. He applied a remedy to this stumbling block too, saying: Whoever receives a just man in the name of a just man will receive a just man's reward. Again, another could plead an excuse and say: I am prevented by poverty; the slenderness of my means holds me back, so that I cannot be hospitable. This excuse too he washed away with a very light command, that we should offer a cup of cold water with our whole heart: cold, he says, water not hot, lest in the case of hot water too poverty and a shortage of firewood should be sought as a pretext. Something of the kind the Apostle too, as we have said already, commands the Galatians: Let him who is catechized share with him who catechizes him in all good things, and he exhorts disciples to the refreshment of their teachers; and since anyone could plead poverty and evade the command, before such a one could put it forward he resolves the impending question, saying: Do not be deceived; God is not mocked; for what a man sows, that he will also reap; and the sense is: in vain do you plead want when your conscience holds something else; you can deceive me as I exhort you, but know that however much you sow, so much you will also reap.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.9.42":["But whoever causes one of these little ones to stumble. Note that the one who is scandalized is a little child; for the greater do not admit stumbling blocks.\n— Commentary on Matthew","It is better for him that an ass's millstone be hung on his neck and that he be sunk in the depth of the sea. Although this may be a general sentence against all who cause anyone to stumble, yet according to the sequence of the discourse it can also be understood as spoken against the apostles, who, by asking who was greater in the kingdom of heaven, seemed to be contending among themselves about rank, and, had they persisted in this fault, could have destroyed through their own stumbling block those whom they were calling to the faith, when these saw the apostles fighting among themselves over honor. But when he said: It is better for him that an ass's millstone be hung on his neck, he speaks according to the custom of the province, by which this was the penalty for the greater crimes among the Jews of old, that they were sunk in the deep with a stone tied to them. And it is better for him, because it is much better to receive a brief punishment for a fault than to be kept for eternal torments. For the Lord will not punish twice for the same thing.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.9.43-47":["But if your right eye causes you to stumble, and the rest. Because he had spoken above of lust for a woman, he now rightly called the thought and the mind flitting in different directions the eye. And by the right hand and the other parts of the body the beginnings of will and affection are shown, so that what we conceive in the mind we may accomplish in deed. We must beware, therefore, lest what is best in us quickly slip into vice. For if the right eye and the right hand cause us to stumble, how much more the things in us that are on the left! If the soul slips, how much more the body, which is more prone to sins! Alternatively: in the right eye and the right hand is shown our affection for brothers and wife and children and kinsmen and relatives; if we see that these are a hindrance to us in contemplating the true light, we ought to cut off such portions, lest, while we wish to gain the others, we ourselves perish forever. Hence it is said also of the high priest, whose soul is dedicated to the worship of God: Over mother and father and children he shall not be defiled, that is, he shall know no affection except for him to whose worship he is dedicated.\n— Commentary on Matthew","But if your hand or your foot causes you to stumble, cut it off and throw it from you, and the rest. It is indeed necessary that stumbling blocks come, yet woe to that man who, by his own fault, brings it about that what must necessarily happen in the world happens through him. Therefore every affection is cut off and all kinship is amputated, lest under the pretext of dutiful affection each of the believers lie open to stumbling blocks. If, he says, there is someone so joined to you as hand, foot, eye, and he is useful and attentive and sharp at seeing, but he makes a stumbling block for you and through the discord of his ways drags you into Gehenna, it is better for you to go without his kinship and his fleshly advantages than, while you wish to gain your relatives and close friends, to have a cause of ruin. So let not brother, not wife, not children, not friends, not any affection that can shut us out from the kingdom of heaven be preferred to the love of the Lord. Each of the believers knows what harms him, or in what his mind is solicited and often tempted. It is better to lead a solitary life than to lose eternal life for the necessities of the present life.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.9.50":["But if the salt has vanished, with what will it be salted? If the teacher has gone astray, by what other teacher will he be corrected?\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.10.2":["And the Pharisees came to him, testing him and saying: Is it lawful for a man to put away his wife for any cause? He had come from Galilee to Judea; for that reason the faction of the Pharisees and scribes asks him whether it is lawful for a man to put away his wife for whatever cause, so as to hold him as in a horned syllogism, and whatever he answered would lie open to capture: if he said that a wife is to be put away for any cause whatever and others married, the preacher of purity would seem to teach things contrary to himself; but if he answered that she ought not to be put away for every cause, he would be held as guilty of sacrilege, as acting against the teaching of Moses, and through Moses, of God. So the Lord tempers his answer in such a way as to pass by the snare, bringing in holy Scripture as testimony and opposing the natural law and God's first sentence to the second, which was granted not by the will of God but by the necessity of sinners.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.10.4":["They say to him: Why then did Moses command to give a bill of divorce and to put her away? They lay open the false charge they had prepared. And yet the Lord had not put forward an opinion of his own, but had recalled the ancient history and the commandments of God.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.10.5":["He says to them: Because Moses, in view of the hardness of your heart, permitted you to put away your wives; but from the beginning it was not so. What he says is of this kind: Can God be contrary to himself, so as to have commanded one thing before and then break his own sentence by a new command? That is not to be thought; but Moses, when he saw that, because of the desire for second wives who were richer or younger or more beautiful, the first wives were being killed or leading a wretched life, preferred to allow discord rather than that hatreds and murders should go on. And at the same time consider that he did not say: God permitted you because of the hardness of your heart, but Moses, so that, according to the Apostle, it is the counsel of a man, not the command of God.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.10.6":["Have you not read that he who made them from the beginning made them male and female? This is written at the beginning of Genesis. But by saying male and female, he shows that second marriages are to be avoided. For he does not say: male and females, which is what was sought by the repudiation of the former wives, but male and female, so that the partnership of a single marriage might be bound together.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.10.7":["For this reason a man leaves father and mother and will cleave to his wife. Likewise he says: will cleave to his wife, not to wives.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.10.8":["And the two shall be in one flesh. The reward of marriage is that from two there comes to be one flesh. Chastity joined to the spirit becomes one spirit.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.10.9":["What therefore God has joined, let man not separate. God has joined them by making one flesh of man and woman; this man cannot separate, unless perhaps God alone. Man separates when, out of desire for a second wife, we put away the first; God, who also had joined, separates when by consent, for the service of God, because the time is short, we so have wives as though we did not have them.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.10.11-12":["But I say to you that whoever puts away his wife, except for fornication, and marries another commits adultery; and he who marries her who was put away commits adultery. Fornication alone is what overcomes the affection owed to a wife; indeed, when she has divided the one flesh into another and by fornication has separated herself from her husband, she ought not to be kept, lest she bring her husband too under a curse, as Scripture says: He who keeps an adulteress is foolish and impious. Wherever, therefore, there is fornication and the suspicion of fornication, the wife is freely put away. And because it could happen that someone might bring a false charge against an innocent woman and, for the sake of a second marriage union, fasten a crime on the former wife, he is commanded to put away the first wife on these terms: that he not have a second while the first is living. For what he says is this: If you put away your wife not out of lust but because of an injury, why, having experienced an unhappy first marriage, do you thrust yourself into the danger of a new one? Moreover, because it could happen that by the same law the wife too might give her husband a bill of divorce, the same caution is enjoined, that she not take a second husband. And because a harlot, and one who had once been an adulteress, did not fear disgrace, it is enjoined on the second husband that if he marries such a woman he is under the charge of adultery.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.10.13":["Then little children were brought to him, that he might lay his hands on them and pray; but the disciples rebuked them. Not that they were unwilling for them to be blessed by both the hand and the voice of the Savior, but because, not yet having the fullest faith, they thought that he, after the manner of men, was being wearied by the importunity of those who brought them.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.10.14":["Let the little children be, and do not forbid them to come to me; for of such is the kingdom of heaven. He said significantly of such, not of these, to show that it is not age that reigns but character, and that the reward is promised to those who have a like innocence and simplicity, the Apostle too agreeing with the same sentence: Brothers, do not become children in your understanding, but be little children in malice; but in understanding, be perfect.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.10.15":["Amen I say to you: Unless you are turned and become like little children, you will not enter the kingdom of heaven. The apostles are not commanded to have the age of little children but their innocence, and that what those possess by their years, these should possess by effort, so that they may be little children in malice, not in wisdom.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.10.17":["Behold, one came up and said to him: Good Teacher, what good shall I do that I may have eternal life? This man who asks how he may attain eternal life is young and rich and proud, and, according to another evangelist, he asks not with the desire of a learner but as one testing.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.10.18":["Why do you ask me about the good? One is good, God. Because he had called him a good teacher and had not confessed him God or the Son of God, he learns that a man, however holy, is not good in comparison with God, of whom it is said: Confess to the Lord, for he is good. But lest anyone think that, in God's being called good, the Son of God is excluded from goodness, let us read in another place: The good shepherd lays down his life for his sheep; and in the prophet, a good spirit and a good land. So the Savior too did not refuse the testimony to his goodness, but excluded the error of a teacher apart from God.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.10.19":["If you wish to enter into life, keep the commandments. He says to him: Which? And Jesus said: You shall not commit murder, you shall not commit adultery, and the rest, and you shall love your neighbor as yourself. That this young man is a tempter we can prove from this too, that when the Lord said to him: If you wish to come to life, keep the commandments, he again asks deceitfully which those commandments are, as if he had not read them himself, or as if the Lord could command things contrary to God.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.10.20":["The young man says to him: All these I have kept; what do I still lack? The young man lies. For if he had fulfilled in deed what is set down in the commandments: You shall love your neighbor as yourself, how is it that afterward, on hearing: Go, sell what you have and give to the poor, he went away sad, because he had many possessions?\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.10.21":["Jesus said to him: If you wish to be perfect, go, sell what you have and give to the poor, and you will have treasure in heaven; and come, follow me. It is in our power whether we wish to be perfect. Yet whoever wishes to be perfect ought to sell what he has, and not to sell in part, as Ananias and Sapphira did, but to sell all, and when he has sold it, to give everything to the poor, and so to prepare for himself a treasure in the kingdom of heaven. Nor is this enough for perfection unless, after despising riches, he follows the Savior, that is, leaving evil things behind, does good. For a purse is more easily despised than the will. Many who leave riches behind do not follow the Lord. But he follows the Lord who is his imitator and walks in his footsteps. For he who says he believes in Christ ought himself also to walk as he walked.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.10.22":["He went away sad, for he had many possessions. This is the sadness that leads to death, and the cause of the sadness is given: that he had many possessions, that is, thorns and thistles, which choked the Lord's seed.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.10.23":["And Jesus said to his disciples: Amen I say to you that a rich man will enter the kingdom of heaven with difficulty. And how did Abraham, Isaac and Jacob, rich men, enter the kingdom of heaven, and how are Matthew and Zacchaeus in the Gospel proclaimed by the Lord's testimony, once they had left their riches? But it must be considered that at the time they entered they had ceased to be rich. So long, then, as they are rich, they will not enter. And yet, because riches are despised with difficulty, he did not say: It is impossible for the rich to enter the kingdom of heaven, but with difficulty. Where difficulty is stated, impossibility is not alleged, but rarity is shown.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.10.25":["And again I say to you: It is easier for a camel to pass through the eye of a needle than for a rich man to enter the kingdom of heaven. By this saying it is shown that it is not difficult but impossible. For if, as a camel cannot enter through the eye of a needle, so a rich man cannot enter the kingdom of heaven, none of the rich will be saved. But if we read in Isaiah how the camels of Midian and Ephah come to Jerusalem with gifts and offerings, and how those who were formerly bent and twisted by the perversity of vices enter the gates of Jerusalem, we shall see how these camels too, to which the rich are compared, when they have laid down the heavy burden of sins and the crookedness of the whole body, can enter by the narrow and strait way that leads to life.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.10.26-27":["But when the disciples asked, marveling at the severity of the saying: Who then will be saved? he tempered the severity of the sentence by his clemency, saying: What with men is impossible is possible with God.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.10.28":["Then Peter answered and said to him: Behold, we have left all things and have followed you; what then will there be for us? Great confidence! Peter was a fisherman, he had not been rich, he sought his food by hand and craft, and yet he speaks confidently: we have left all things. And because merely leaving is not enough, he joins what is perfect: and have followed you. We have done what you commanded; what reward, then, will you give us?\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.10.29-30":["And everyone who has left house or brothers or sisters or father or mother or wife or children or fields for my name's sake will receive a hundredfold and will possess eternal life. But many who are first will be last, and the last first. This passage agrees with that sentence in which the Savior says: I came not to send peace but a sword; for I came to separate a man from his father, and the mother from the daughter, and the daughter-in-law from the mother-in-law; and a man's enemies are those of his own household. Those, therefore, who for the faith of Christ and the preaching of the Gospel have despised all affections, and riches, and the pleasures of the world, these will receive a hundredfold and will possess eternal life. On the occasion of this sentence some bring in a thousand years after the resurrection, saying that then a hundredfold of all the things we have given up, and eternal life, will be rendered to us, not understanding that, even if in other things the promise is fitting, in the case of wives the baseness is plain: that he who has given up one for the Lord should receive a hundred in the future. This, then, is the sense: He who has given up carnal things for the Savior will receive spiritual things, which in comparison and by their worth will be as if the number one hundred were compared with a small number. Hence the Apostle too, who had given up only one house and the small fields of one province, says: As having nothing and possessing all things.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.10.32-34":["And Jesus, going up to Jerusalem, took his twelve disciples and said to them: Behold, we are going up to Jerusalem, and the Son of Man will be handed over to the chiefs of the elders and to the scribes, and they will condemn him to death and hand him over to the Gentiles to be mocked and scourged and crucified, and on the third day he will rise again. He had often told the disciples this very thing, but because, with many matters discussed in between, what they had heard could slip from their memory, being about to go to Jerusalem and to take the apostles with him, he prepares them for the trial, so that when persecution and the ignominy of the cross come they may not be scandalized.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.10.35-37":["Then the mother of the sons of Zebedee came to him with her sons, worshipping him and asking something of him. He said to her: What do you want? She said to him: Say that these two sons of mine may sit, one at your right hand and one at your left, in your kingdom. Where does the mother of the sons of Zebedee get the notion of a kingdom, that, when the Lord had said: The Son of Man will be handed over to the chief priests and the scribes, and they will condemn him to death and hand him over to the Gentiles to be mocked and scourged and crucified, and had announced the ignominy of the passion to his fearful disciples, she should ask for the glory of one triumphing? For this reason, as I think: because after everything else the Lord had said: and on the third day he will rise again, the woman thought that he would reign immediately after the resurrection, and that what is promised for the second coming was to be fulfilled in the first; and with a woman's eagerness she longs for present things, unmindful of things to come. But that the Lord asks, and answered her request: What do you want? does not come from ignorance; rather it is said in the person of him who was to be scourged and crucified, just as also in the case of the woman with the flow of blood: Who touched me? and of Lazarus: Where have you laid him? and in the Old Testament too: Adam, where are you? And I will go down and see whether they are doing according to the cry that has come to me; and if it is not so, that I may know. The mother of the sons of Zebedee, then, makes her request out of a woman's error and the affection of love, not knowing what she was asking. Nor is it surprising if she is convicted of inexperience, when it is said of Peter, when he wants to make three tabernacles, not knowing what he was saying.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.10.38":["But Jesus answered and said: You do not know what you are asking. The mother makes the request, and the Lord speaks to the disciples, understanding that her prayers came down from the will of her sons.\n— Commentary on Matthew","Can you drink the cup that I am to drink? By the cup in the divine Scriptures we understand the passion: Father, if it is possible, let this cup pass from me; and in the psalm: What shall I render to the Lord for all that he has rendered to me? I will take the cup of salvation and call upon the name of the Lord; and at once he adds what this cup is: Precious in the sight of the Lord is the death of his saints.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.10.39-40":["He said to them: My cup you will indeed drink, but to sit at my right hand and at my left is not mine to give you, but to those for whom it has been prepared by my Father. It is asked how the sons of Zebedee, namely James and John, drank the cup of martyrdom, when Scripture relates that only James the apostle was beheaded by Herod, while John ended his life by a death of his own. But if we read the histories of the Church, in which it is reported that he too, for the sake of martyrdom, was put into a jar of boiling oil, and came out from there as an athlete of Christ to receive the crown, and was at once banished to the island of Patmos, we shall see that his spirit did not fall short of martyrdom, and that John drank the cup of confession, which the three young men in the furnace of fire also drank, although the persecutor did not shed their blood. But what he says: to sit at my right hand and at my left is not mine to give you, but to those for whom it has been prepared by my Father, is to be understood thus: The kingdom of heaven belongs not to the one who gives but to the one who receives; for there is no respect of persons with God, but whoever shows himself such that he becomes worthy of the kingdom of heaven, he will receive what has been prepared not for a person but for a life; if then you are such as to attain the kingdom of heaven which my Father has prepared for the triumphant and the victors, you too will receive it. Others want it to have been said of Moses and Elijah, whom a little before they had seen on the mountain talking with him, but to me it by no means seems so. For the names of those who sit in the kingdom of heaven are not stated for this reason: lest, if a few were named, the rest should be thought excluded.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.10.41":["And the ten, hearing it, were indignant at the two brothers. The ten apostles are not indignant with the mother of the sons of Zebedee, nor do they lay the boldness of the request to the woman, but to the sons, because, ignorant of their own measure, they had blazed up with immoderate desire; to them the Lord too had said: You do not know what you are asking. But it is understood, either from the Lord's answer or from the indignation of the apostles, that the sons had sent their mother in to ask for great things.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.10.42-44":["But Jesus called them to him and said: You know that the princes of the Gentiles lord it over them, and the rest. The humble and gentle master neither rebukes the two who asked for their immoderate desire nor reproves the other ten for indignation and envy, but sets forth such an example as to teach that he is greater who has been lesser, and that he becomes lord who is the servant of all. In vain therefore did those seek immoderate things, or do these grieve over the desire for greater things, since one comes to the summit of the virtues not by power but by humility. In short, he sets forth his own example, so that, if they held his words cheap, they might be put to shame by his deeds, and he says:\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.10.45":["Even as the Son of Man did not come to be served but to serve. Note what we have often said: that the one who serves is called the Son of Man. And to give his life as a redemption for many, when he took the form of a servant in order to shed his blood for the world. And he did not say to give his life as a redemption for all, but for many, that is, for those who would be willing to believe.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.10.46-47":["But there were also two blind men by the way. He calls them blind who could not yet say: In your light we shall see light; by the way, because they seemed indeed to have knowledge of the law, but did not know the way, which is Christ. Most take them to be the Pharisees and the Sadducees, but others both peoples, of the Old Testament and of the New, because the one following the written law, the other the natural law, was blind without Christ. These, because they could not see by themselves, heard the proclamations of the Savior and confessed him the son of David.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.10.46":["And as they were going out from Jericho, a great crowd followed him. And behold, two blind men sitting by the way heard that Jesus was passing by, and they cried out, saying: Lord, have mercy on us, son of David. But the crowd rebuked them, that they should be silent. But they cried out the more, saying: Lord, have mercy on us, son of David. There were many robbers in Jericho who were accustomed to kill and wound those going out and going down from Jerusalem; therefore the Lord came to Jericho with his disciples, to free the wounded and to draw a great crowd along with him. And indeed, when he wished to go out from Jericho, a great crowd followed him. If he had stayed in Jerusalem and had never come down to lowly places, the crowd would to this day be sitting in darkness and in the shadow of death.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.10.48":["But if both blind men are referred to the people of the Jews, what follows: the crowd rebuked them, is to be understood of the heathen, whom the Apostle warns not to boast and be proud against their root, but, since they themselves, through the error of the former, have been grafted from the wild olive into the good olive, they ought by no means to envy the salvation of the former.\n— Commentary on Matthew","Have mercy on us, son of David. They are rebuked by the crowds and nonetheless are not silent, but repeat the very same thing all the more often, to show a desire full of the true light.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.10.49-51":["Jesus stood still and called them and said. They were blind; they did not know where to go; they could not follow the Savior. There are many pits in Jericho, many crags and precipices sloping down into the depths; therefore the Lord stands still so that they may be able to come, and orders them to be called so that the crowds may not hinder them, and asks, as though not knowing, what they want, so that from the blind men's answer the infirmity may be made plain and the power may be recognized from the remedy.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.10.52":["And Jesus, having pity on them, touched their eyes, and at once they saw and followed him. He touches their eyes, and the craftsman supplies what nature had not given, or at any rate what infirmity had taken away; mercy bestows it, and at once they saw and followed him. Those who before sat huddled in Jericho and knew only how to cry out afterward follow Jesus not so much with their feet as with their virtues.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.11.1-2":["And when they drew near to Jerusalem and came to Bethphage, to the Mount of Olives, then Jesus sent two disciples, saying to them: Go into the village that is opposite you, and at once you will find an ass tied and a colt with her; loose them and bring them to me; and if anyone says anything to you, say: The Lord has need of them, and at once he will let them go. He goes out from Jericho, having led out from there very many crowds, and, having restored health to the blind, he draws near to Jerusalem, enriched with great wares; having restored salvation to those who believe, he desires to enter the city of peace and the place of the vision of God and the citadel of the watchman. And when he had drawn near to Jerusalem and had come to Bethphage, to the house of jaws (which was a little village of the priests and bore the type of confession and was situated on the Mount of Olives, where is the light of knowledge, where is rest from labors and sorrows), he sent his two disciples, θεωρητικὴν καὶ πρακτικήν, that is, knowledge and works, to enter the village, and he said to them: Go into the village that is opposite you. For it was opposite the apostles and would not accept the yoke of teachings. And at once, he says, you will find an ass tied and a colt with her; loose them and bring them to me. The ass was tied with many chains of sins; the colt too, frisky and impatient of the bridle, was with its mother; according to the Gospel of Luke it had many masters, being subject not to one error and doctrine; and yet the many masters who claimed for themselves an unlawful power, seeing that the true Lord had come and his servants who had been sent to loose them, do not dare to resist. But what the ass and the foal of the ass are, we say below.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.11.7":["And the disciples went and did as Jesus had commanded them, and they brought the ass and the colt and put their garments upon them and made him sit on top. This colt and this ass, on which the apostles spread their garments so that Jesus may sit more softly, were naked before the coming of the Savior, and, with many claiming dominion over them, they shivered without covering; but after they had received the apostolic garments and had been made more beautiful, they had the Lord as their rider. Now the apostolic garment can be understood either as the teaching of the virtues or as the exposition of the Scriptures, or as the varieties of the doctrines of the Church; unless the soul has been instructed and adorned with these, it does not deserve to have the Lord as its rider.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.11.8":["And a very great crowd spread their garments on the road. See the difference of each person. The apostles put their garments upon the ass; the crowd, which is of lower rank, spreads them beneath the ass's feet, so that it may nowhere strike against a stone, may not tread on a thorn, may not slip into a pit.\n— Commentary on Matthew","And others cut branches from the trees and spread them on the road. From the fruit-bearing trees with which the Mount of Olives is planted they cut branches and spread them on the road, to make the crooked straight and to level the uneven, so that Christ, the conqueror of demons and of vices, might advance more straightly and more safely in the heart of believers.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.11.9-10":["And the crowds that went before and that followed cried out, saying: Hosanna to the son of David; blessed is he who is to come in the name of the Lord; hosanna in the highest. Since the history is plain, let us follow the spiritual order of discussion. The crowds that had gone out from Jericho and had followed the Savior and his disciples, after they saw the foal of the ass loosed, which before had been tied, and adorned with the garments of the apostles, and the Lord Jesus sitting upon it, laid their garments beneath and spread the road with branches of trees; and when they had done all this in deed, they also bestow the testimony of the voice, and those going before and those following resound not with a brief and silent confession but with the fullest shout: Hosanna to the son of David; blessed is he who is to come in the name of the Lord. And in saying: The crowds that went before and that followed, he shows that both peoples, those who believed in the Lord before the Gospel and those who believed after the Gospel, praise Jesus with the harmonious voice of confession, and that, after the example of the parable above, the workers of different hours receive the one reward of faith. Moreover, what the following words mean: Hosanna to the son of David, I remember that I said many years ago, in a short letter to Damasus, then bishop of the city of Rome, and now I will touch on it briefly. In Psalm 117 (118), which was plainly written about the coming of the Savior, among other things we read this too: The stone which the builders rejected, this has become the head of the corner; this was done by the Lord, this is wonderful in our eyes; this is the day which the Lord has made; let us exult and rejoice in it; and at once there is joined: O Lord, save me; O Lord, prosper well; blessed is he who is to come in the name of the Lord; we have blessed you from the house of the Lord, and the rest. Where the LXX translators have: O Lord, save me, in the Hebrew we read: Anna adonai osi anna, which Symmachus translated more plainly, saying: I beseech you, Lord, save me, I beseech you. Let no one, then, think that the word is composed of two words, namely a Greek and a Hebrew one, but that it is wholly Hebrew and signifies that the coming of Christ is the salvation of the world. Hence there also follows: Blessed is he who is to come in the name of the Lord, the Savior too confirming this very thing in the Gospel: I have come in the name of my Father, and you have not received me; another will come in his own name, and you will receive him. Further, by what is joined: Hosanna (that is, salvation) in the highest, it is clearly shown that the coming of Christ is the salvation not only of men but of the whole world, joining earthly things to heavenly, that every knee should bow to him, of those in heaven and on earth and in the lower world.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.11.11":["And leaving them, he went out outside the city to Bethany and stayed there. He left the unbelieving and, going out of the city of those who contradicted him, went to Bethany, which is interpreted house of obedience, already then prefiguring the calling of the Gentiles; and he stayed there because he could not remain in Israel. This too must be understood: that he was of such poverty and flattered no one to such a degree that in the greatest city he found no host, no lodging, but lived in a tiny field with Lazarus and his sisters; for Bethany is their village.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.11.12":["Now in the morning, returning to the city, he was hungry, and seeing a single fig tree beside the road, he came to it and found nothing on it but leaves only. And he said to it: Let fruit never grow from you forever. And the fig tree withered at once. And the disciples, seeing it, marveled, saying: How did it wither at once? When the darkness of night had been dispelled and the morning light was shining, and midday was near, in which the Lord was to illumine the world with his passion, as he was returning to the city he was hungry, either showing the reality of human flesh, or hungering for the salvation of believers and burning at the unbelief of Israel.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.11.13-14":["And when he had seen a single tree, which we understand as the synagogue and the assembly of the Jews, beside the road (for it had the law, and it was beside the road because it did not believe in the Way), he came to it, standing as it was and immovable and not having the feet of the Gospel, and he found nothing on it but leaves only: the rustling of promises, the traditions of the Pharisees, boasting of the law, ornaments of words, without any fruits of truth. Hence another evangelist also says: For it was not yet the time, either because the time of Israel's salvation had not yet come, since the people of the Gentiles had not yet come in, or because the time of faith had passed, since, having come to Israel first and been spurned, he had passed over to the nations. And he said to it: Let fruit never grow from you, either forever or to the age; for the Greek word αἰών signifies both. And the fig tree withered, because, when the Lord was hungry, it did not have the food he had desired. But the leaves withered in such a way that the trunk itself remained, and, though the branches were broken, the root stayed green, so that in the last time, if they are willing to believe, it may sprout shoots of faith, and the Scripture may be fulfilled which says: There is hope for a tree.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.11.15-17":["And Jesus entered the temple of God and cast out all who sold and bought in the temple, and he overturned the tables of the money-changers and the chairs of those who sold doves, and he says to them: It is written: My house shall be called a house of prayer, but you have made it a den of robbers. Accompanied by the crowd of believers, which had spread its garments so that the colt might go with unhurt foot, Jesus enters the temple and casts out all who sold and bought in the temple, and he overturned the tables of the money-changers and scattered the chairs of those who sold doves, and he said to them, bringing forward testimony from the holy Scriptures, that his Father's house ought to be a house of prayer, not a den of robbers or a house of trade, as is written in another evangelist. And this must first be known: that, in accordance with the commands of the law, since the temple of the Lord was the most august in the whole world, and the people of the Jews flocked there from almost all regions, countless victims were sacrificed, especially on feast days, of bulls, rams and goats, while the poor, so as not to be without a sacrifice, offered young doves and turtledoves; and it often happened that those who had come from far away did not have victims. So the priests devised how they might make plunder of the people, and they sold all the animals that were needed for the sacrifices, so that they might both sell to those who did not have them and themselves receive back again what had been bought. This scheme of theirs was frequently defeated by the poverty of those who came, who lacked the means and not only had no victims but had not even the wherewithal to buy birds and cheap little gifts. So they also set up money-changers to lend money on security; but because it was commanded in the law that no one should take interest, and money lent at interest could be of no use when it brought no profit and sometimes lost the principal, they devised yet another trick: in place of money-changers to make collybists, a word whose proper sense the Latin language does not express. Among them collyba is the name for what we call sweetmeats or cheap little gifts, for example roasted chickpeas and raisins and fruits of various kinds. So, because the collybists who had lent the money could not take interest, in place of interest they took various kinds of goods, so as to exact in these things, which are bought with coins, what was not lawful in coin; as if Ezekiel had not guarded against this very thing, saying: You shall not take interest and increase. The Lord, seeing such trade, or robbery, in his Father's house, roused by the ardor of the spirit, according to what is written in Psalm 68 (69): The zeal of your house has eaten me up, made himself a whip of cords and cast so great a multitude of men out of the temple, saying: It is written: My house shall be called a house of prayer, but you have made it a den of robbers. For he is a robber, and turns the temple of God into a den of robbers, who pursues gain from religion, and whose worship is not so much the worship of God as an occasion for trade. So much according to the history. But according to the mystical senses Jesus daily enters his Father's temple and casts out all, bishops and presbyters and deacons as well as laymen and the whole crowd, from his Church, and holds those who sell and those who buy alike guilty of one crime; for it is written: Freely you have received, freely give. He also overturns the tables of the money-changers. Note that because of the avarice of the priests the altars of God are called the tables of money-changers. And he overturns the chairs of those who sell doves, who sell the grace of the Holy Spirit and do everything to devour the peoples subject to them, of whom it is said: Who devour my people like food of bread. According to the simple understanding, the doves were not on chairs but in cages, unless perhaps the dealers in doves sat on chairs, which is utterly absurd, because in chairs it is rather the dignity of teachers that is indicated, a dignity that is brought to nothing when it has been mixed with gain. What we have said of the churches, let each one understand of himself as well. For the Apostle Paul says: You are the temple of God, and the Holy Spirit dwells in you. Let there be no trade in the house of our heart, no commerce of selling and buying, no greed for gifts, lest Jesus enter angry and stern and cleanse his temple in no other way than by applying the whip, so that out of a den of robbers and a house of trade he may make a house of prayer.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.11.20-21":["But according to the letter, the Lord, being about to suffer among the peoples and to bear the stumbling block of the cross, had to strengthen the minds of the disciples by anticipating it with a sign. Hence the disciples too marvel, saying: How did it wither at once? The Savior, then, could have withered his enemies too by the same power, had he not been awaiting their salvation through repentance.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.11.22-23":["And Jesus answered and said to them: Amen I say to you: If you have faith and do not hesitate, you will do not only this of the fig tree, but even if you say to a mountain: Lift yourself up and cast yourself into the sea, it will be done. The dogs of the Gentiles bark against us in their volumes, which they have left behind as a memorial of their own impiety, asserting that the apostles did not have faith because they could not move mountains. To them we shall answer that many signs were done by the Lord, according to the testimony of John the evangelist, which, if they had been written, the world could not contain; not that the world could not have contained the volumes, which, however many they may be, one little bookcase or one book-chest can contain, but that it could not bear the greatness of the signs, on account of their wonder and its own unbelief. Therefore we believe that the apostles did these things too, but that they were not written for this reason, lest a greater occasion for contradicting be given to unbelievers. Otherwise, let us ask them whether they believe these signs which are narrated in writing or not; and when we have seen that they are unbelieving, we shall prove as a consequence that those who did not believe the lesser would not have believed the greater either. So much against them. But we, as we have already said before, should understand the mountain as the devil, who is proud and vaunts himself against his creator, who is called by the prophet the corrupt mountain; and when he has possessed a man's soul and has become rooted in it, he can be moved by the apostles, and by those who are like the apostles, into the sea, that is, into places salty and tossing and bitter, which have none of the sweetness of God. This same thing is read in the psalms too: We will not fear while the earth is troubled and the mountains are moved into the heart of the sea.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.11.27-28":["And when he had come into the temple, the chief priests and the elders of the people came to him as he was teaching, saying: By what power do you do these things, and who gave you this power? In different words they build up the same slander as above, when they said: By Beelzebub, the prince of demons, this man casts out demons. For when they say: By what power do you do these things, they doubt the power of God and want it to be understood that what he does is the devil's; and adding further: who gave you this power, they most plainly deny the Son of God, whom they think does signs not by his own powers but by another's.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.11.29-33":["Jesus answered and said to them: I also will ask you one word, and if you tell it to me, I also will tell you by what power I do these things. The baptism of John, where was it from, from heaven or from men? and the rest. This is what is commonly said: Into a bad knot in a tree a bad nail or wedge must be driven. The Lord could have refuted the slander of those tempting him by an open answer, but he prudently asks a question, so that they may be condemned either by their own silence or by their own statement. For if they had answered that the baptism of John was from heaven, as they, wise in malice, had worked it out among themselves, the reply followed: Why then were you not baptized by John? If they had chosen to say that it was put together by human deceit and had had nothing divine, they feared a riot of the people. For all the assembled multitudes had received the baptism of John and held him thus, as a prophet. So the most impious faction answered and used a word of humility, by which it said that it did not know, to fit together its snares.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.11.33":["He too said to them: Neither do I tell you by what power I do these things. They lied in answering that they did not know; it followed, then, according to their answer, that the Lord too should say: Neither do I know; but the truth cannot lie; and he said: Neither do I tell you. By this he shows both that they knew but would not answer and that he knew and for this reason would not tell, because they kept silent about what they knew; and at once he brings in a parable by which he both convicts them of impiety and teaches that the kingdom of God is to be transferred to the Gentiles.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.12.1":["Hear another parable: There was a man, a householder, who planted a vineyard and set a hedge around it and dug a winepress in it and built a tower, and he let it out to farmers and went abroad. This is what the Lord signified with a saying taken from a proverb: It is hard to kick against the goad. The chief priests and the elders of the people, who had asked the Lord: By what power do you do these things, and who gave you this power, and had wished to catch Wisdom in a word, are overcome by their own art and hear in parables what they did not deserve to hear openly. This man, the householder, is the same who had two sons, and who in another parable hired workers for his vineyard; who planted the vineyard of which Isaiah too speaks most fully in a song, bringing in at the end: The vineyard of the Lord of hosts is the house of Israel; and in the psalm: A vineyard, he says, you transplanted from Egypt; you cast out the nations and planted it. And he set a hedge around it, either the wall of the city or the help of the angels. And dug a winepress in it, either the altar or those winepresses with which three psalms are marked in their title, the eighth, the eightieth (81st) and the eighty-third (84th). And built a tower, no doubt the temple, of which it is said through Micah: And you, cloudy tower of the daughter of Zion. And he let it out to farmers, whom elsewhere he called the workers of the vineyard, who had been hired at the first, third, sixth and ninth and eleventh hour. And went abroad, not by change of place, for from where can God be absent, by whom all things are filled, and who says through Jeremiah: I am a God who draws near, and not from afar, says the Lord? But he seems to go away from the vineyard so as to leave the vinedressers free choice in their working.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.12.2-5":["And when the time of fruits drew near, he sent his servants to the farmers to receive its fruits; and the farmers, seizing his servants, beat one, killed another, and stoned another. He had given them the law and had ordered them to work in this vineyard, so that they might show the fruit of the law in works. Afterward he sent servants to them, whom they seized and either beat, like Jeremiah, or killed, like Isaiah, or stoned, like Naboth and Zechariah, whom they slew between the temple and the altar. Let us read Paul's letter to the Hebrews, and from it we learn most fully which of the Lord's servants suffered how much.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.12.5-6":["But last of all he sent his son to them, saying: They will revere my son. In what we read above: Again he sent other servants, more than the first, and they did the same to them, he shows the patience of the householder, in that he sent more often, to provoke the wicked tenants to repentance; but they stored up for themselves wrath on the day of wrath. Moreover, what is joined: They will revere my son, does not come from ignorance. For what could the householder not know, who in this place is understood as God the Father? But God is always said to be in doubt, so that free will may be kept for man. Let us question Arius and Eunomius. See, the Father is said not to know, and he tempers his statement, and, as far as lies in you, he is proved to have lied. Whatever they answer on behalf of the Father, they will understand this of the Son, who says that he does not know the day of the consummation.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.12.8":["Seizing him, they cast him out of the vineyard and killed him. The Apostle too says that the Lord was crucified outside the gate. But we can also understand it otherwise: that he was cast out of the vineyard and killed there so that, with the Gentiles receiving him, the vineyard might be let out to others.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.12.9":["When therefore the lord of the vineyard comes, what will he do to those tenants, and the rest. The Lord asks them, not because he does not know what they will answer, but so that they may be condemned by their own answer. Now the vineyard has been let out to us, and let out on this condition: that we render the Lord the fruit in its seasons and know at each time what we ought either to say or to do.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.12.10-11":["Jesus says to them: Have you never read in the Scriptures: The stone which the builders rejected, this has become the head of the corner; this was done by the Lord, this is wonderful in our eyes? By various parables and different discourses the same things are woven together. For those whom above he had called workers and vinedressers and farmers, he now calls builders, that is, masons. Hence the Apostle too says: You are God's tillage, God's building. These masons, then, just as the vinedressers receive the vineyard, so received the stone, to place it either in the foundations, according to the architect Paul, or in the corner, so that it might join together two walls, that is, both peoples; and this stone, rejected by them, has become the head of the corner; and this was done by the Lord, not by human powers but by the might of God. Of this stone of help Peter too speaks confidently: This is the stone which was rejected by you builders, which has become the head of the corner; and Isaiah: Behold, he says, I will lay in the foundations of Zion a stone, chosen, precious, a cornerstone, and whoever believes in it will not be confounded.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.12.12":["And when the chief priests and the Pharisees had heard his parables, they knew that he was speaking about them; and seeking to seize him, they feared the crowds, since they held him as a prophet. Although they were hard of heart and dulled by unbelief and impiety toward the Son of God, still they could not deny plain propositions, and they understood that all the Lord's sayings were directed against them. Hence they wished indeed to kill him, but they feared the crowds, because they held him as a prophet. The crowd is always fickle and does not persist in its purposed will, and, like the waves and the various winds, it is drawn this way and that. The one whom they now venerate and honor as a prophet, afterward they shout against: Crucify, crucify such a man.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.12.13":["Then the Pharisees went off and took counsel how to catch him in his speech, and they send their disciples with the Herodians, saying. Not long before, under Caesar Augustus, Judea, subjected to the Romans when the census was held throughout the whole world, had been made tributary, and there was great sedition among the people: some said that for the security and quiet for which the Romans fought on behalf of all, the tributes ought to be paid; but the Pharisees, who applauded themselves in their righteousness, strove on the contrary that the people of God, which paid tithes and gave firstfruits and the other things that are written in the law, ought not to be subject to human laws. Caesar Augustus had established Herod, the son of Antipater, a foreigner and a proselyte, as king over the Jews, to preside over the tributes and obey the Roman empire. The Pharisees therefore send their disciples with the Herodians, that is, with Herod's soldiers, or with those whom the Pharisees, in mockery, because they paid tributes to the Romans, called Herodians and not men devoted to divine worship. Some of the Latins absurdly think that the Herodians were those who believed Herod to be the Christ, something we read nowhere at all.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.12.14":["Teacher, we know that you are truthful and teach the way of God in truth, and you have no care for anyone; for you do not regard the person of men. Tell us therefore what you think: Is it lawful to give tax to Caesar or not? The flattering and deceitful question lures the one answering into fearing God rather than Caesar and saying that the tributes ought not to be paid, so that the Herodians, hearing it, might at once seize him as the leader of a sedition against the Romans.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.12.15-16":["Show me the coin of the tax. And they brought him a denarius. Wisdom always acts wisely, so that tempters are refuted above all by their own words. Show me, he says, the denarius. This is the kind of coin that was reckoned at ten coins and bore the image of Caesar.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.12.15":["But Jesus, knowing their wickedness, said: Why do you tempt me, hypocrites? The first virtue of one who answers is to know the mind of those who question him, and to call them not disciples but tempters. A hypocrite, then, is the name for one who is one thing and pretends another, that is, does one thing in deed and puts forward another with his voice.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.12.16-17":["They say to him: Caesar's. Then he said to them: Render therefore the things that are Caesar's to Caesar, and the things that are God's to God. Let us not think that Augustus is signified by Caesar, but Tiberius, his stepson, who had succeeded to his stepfather's place, and under whom the Lord also suffered. But all the Roman kings, from the first, Gaius Caesar, who had seized the empire, were called Caesars. Moreover, as for his saying: Render the things that are Caesar's to Caesar, that is, coin, tribute and money, and the things that are God's to God, let us understand tithes, firstfruits, oblations and victims; just as he himself both renders tributes for himself and Peter, and renders to God the things that are God's, doing the will of the Father.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.12.16":["And Jesus said to them: Whose image and superscription is this? Let those who think that the Savior's questioning is ignorance and not dispensation learn from the present passage that Jesus certainly could have known whose image was on the coin; but he asks so that he may answer their words fittingly.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.12.17":["And hearing it they marveled. Those who ought to have believed marveled at such great wisdom, because their cunning had found no opening for laying a snare.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.12.18":["On that day the Sadducees, who say that there is no resurrection, came to him. There were two heresies among the Jews, one of the Pharisees and the other of the Sadducees. The Pharisees held up a righteousness of traditions and observances, which they call deuteroses, and hence they were also called 'divided' by the people; while the Sadducees, which is interpreted 'just', themselves also claimed for themselves what they were not. And whereas the former believed and confessed the resurrection of both body and soul, and angels and spirit, the latter, according to the Acts of the Apostles, denied it all. These are the two houses of which Isaiah teaches quite plainly that they would stumble on the stone of stumbling.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.12.19-22":["And they questioned him, saying: Teacher, Moses said: If anyone dies not having a son, his brother is to marry his wife and raise up seed for his brother. Now there were among us seven brothers, and the first, having married a wife, died, and the rest. Those who did not believe in the resurrection of bodies, and thought that the soul perished together with the bodies, aptly invent a fable of this kind, to convict of madness those who assert the resurrection of the dead. But it may be that this really happened at some time among their people.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.12.23":["In the resurrection, therefore, whose wife of the seven will she be? For they all had her. They set the foulness of the fable against it in order to deny the truth of the resurrection.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.12.24":["Jesus answered and said to them: You err, not knowing the Scriptures nor the power of God. They err for this reason, that they do not know the Scriptures; and because they are ignorant of the Scriptures, they consequently do not know the power of God, that is, Christ, who is the power of God and the wisdom of God.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.12.25":["For in the resurrection they will neither marry nor be married. Latin usage does not correspond to the Greek idiom. For properly it is women who are said to wed, and men to take wives. But let us understand it as said simply, so that 'marry' is written of the men and 'be married' of the wives. Thus in the resurrection they will not marry nor be married: therefore bodies will rise which are able to marry and be married. For no one says of a stone or a tree or of those things that have no genital members that they do not marry nor are married, but of those who, though they could marry, yet for another reason do not marry. But as for what is added:\n\nBut they are like the angels of God in heaven, a spiritual way of life is promised.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.12.26-27":["But concerning the resurrection of the dead, have you not read what was spoken by God, who says to you: I am the God of Abraham and the God of Isaac and the God of Jacob? He is not the God of the dead but of the living. To prove the truth of the resurrection he could have used other, much plainer examples, among which is this one: The dead shall be raised, and those who are in the tombs shall rise again, and in another place: Many of those who sleep in the dust of the earth shall arise, some to life, others to reproach and everlasting confusion. It is asked, therefore, what the Lord meant by bringing forward this testimony, which seems ambiguous or not sufficiently pertinent to the truth of the resurrection: I am the God of Abraham and the God of Isaac and the God of Jacob, and why, as though by bringing it forward he had proved what he wished, he at once added: He is not the God of the dead but of the living. The crowds standing around, too, recognizing the mystery of this matter, marveled at the teaching of his answer. We said above that the Sadducees, confessing neither angel nor spirit nor the resurrection of bodies, also preached the destruction of souls. They received only the five books of Moses, rejecting the oracles of the prophets. It would therefore have been foolish to bring forward testimonies from a source whose authority they did not follow. Moreover, to prove the eternity of souls he sets down an example from Moses: I am the God of Abraham and the God of Isaac and the God of Jacob, and at once adds: He is not the God of the dead but of the living, so that, once he had proved that souls endure after death (for it could not be that he was the God of those who did not subsist at all), the resurrection of the bodies too might consequently be introduced, the bodies which together with the souls did good or evil. The apostle Paul pursues this passage more fully in the last part of the first letter to the Corinthians.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.12.28":["One of the doctors of the law asks, not desiring to know but tempting, whether he, when asked, knew what he was being asked, which is the greater commandment: not asking about the commandments, but which is the first and great commandment, so that, since all that God has commanded is great, whatever he answered, the questioner might have an occasion for slander, asserting that another, out of the many, is great. Whoever, then, knows and asks, not from a wish to learn but from eagerness to find out whether the one who is to answer knows, approaches after the likeness of the Pharisees, not as a disciple but as a tempter.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.12.34":["And no one could answer him a word, nor did anyone dare from that day to ask him any more. The Pharisees and Sadducees, seeking an occasion for false accusation and to find some word that would lie open to their snares, because they have been refuted in argument, ask no further, but seize him quite openly and hand him over to the Roman power. From this we understand that the poisons of envy can indeed be overcome, but with difficulty are laid to rest.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.12.35-37":["Now when the Pharisees were gathered together, Jesus asked them, saying: What do you think about the Christ? Whose son is he? They say to him: David's. He says to them: How then does David in the Spirit call him Lord, saying: The Lord said to my Lord: Sit at my right until I make your enemies the footstool of your feet. Those who had gathered to tempt Jesus, and were striving to catch the truth with a deceitful question, provided the occasion for their own refutation, and they are asked about the Christ, whose son he is. The question of Jesus profits us against the Jews even to this day. For these too, who confess that the Christ is to come, assert that he is a mere man and a holy man of the line of David. Let us therefore, taught by the Lord, ask them: If he is a mere man and only the son of David, how does David call him his Lord, not in the uncertainty of error nor by his own will, but in the Holy Spirit? Now the testimony that he set down is taken from Psalm 109 (110). David's Lord, therefore, he is called not according to that by which he was born of him, but according to that by which, born of the Father, he always was, going before the very father of his flesh. The Jews, to make sport of the truth of the question, invent many frivolous things, asserting that it was Abraham's home-born servant, whose son was Eliezer of Damascus, and that the psalm was written in his person, because after the slaughter of the five kings the Lord God said to his lord Abraham: Sit at my right hand until I make all your enemies the footstool of your feet. Let us ask them: How did God say to Abraham the words that follow: With you is the beginning in the day of your power, in the splendors of the saints; from the womb before the morning star I begot you; and: The Lord has sworn and will not repent: You are a priest for ever according to the order of Melchizedek, and let us compel them to answer how Abraham was begotten before the morning star and was a priest according to the order of Melchizedek, for whom Melchizedek offered bread and wine, and from whom he received tithes of the spoil.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.12.38-39":["And since they broaden their phylacteries beyond measure and make large fringes, courting glory from men, they are rebuked in the rest for seeking the first places at table at dinners and the first chairs in the synagogues, and for pursuing gluttony and glory in public, and for being called rabbi by men, which in the Latin tongue means master. In short, there follows:\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.12.42":["The quadrans is a kind of coin that contains two mites. Hence in one Gospel that poor widow woman is said to have put a quadrans into the corban, in another two mites; not that the Gospels are at variance, but because one quadrans contains two mites. This, then, is what he says: You will not come out of prison until you have paid even for the least sins.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.13.1-2":["And Jesus went out from the temple and was going away. And his disciples came to him to show him the buildings of the temple. But he answered and said to them: Do you see all these things? Amen I say to you: There shall not be left here a stone upon a stone that shall not be destroyed. According to the history the sense is plain. But as the Lord withdrew from the temple, all the buildings of the law and the structure of the commandments were so destroyed that nothing can be fulfilled by the Jews, and, the head having been taken away, all the members fight against one another.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.13.3-4":["But as he sat upon the Mount of Olives, the disciples came to him privately, saying: Tell us when these things will be, and what is the sign of your coming and of the consummation of the age? He sat on the Mount of Olives, where the true light of knowledge was being born; and the disciples, who longed to know the mysteries and the revelation of things to come, come to him privately and ask three things: at what time Jerusalem is to be destroyed, at what time Christ will come, at what time the consummation of the age will be.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.13.6":["For many will come in my name, saying: I am the Christ, and they will lead many astray. One of these is Simon the Samaritan, of whom we read in the Acts of the Apostles, who said that he was the great power of God, and who left behind, among other things written in his books, these words too: I am the word of God, I am the beautiful one, I the Paraclete, I the almighty, I all things of God. But John the apostle too says in his letter: You have heard that antichrist is to come, but now there are many antichrists. I think that all heresiarchs are antichrists and teach, under the name of Christ, the things that are contrary to Christ. Nor is it any wonder if we see some led astray by them, since the Lord said: and they will lead many astray.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.13.7":["For you are going to hear of battles and rumors of battles; see that you are not troubled; for these things must happen, but the end is not yet. So when we see these things happen, let us not think that the day of judgment is coming in, but that it is reserved for that time whose sign is clearly set out in what follows.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.13.8":["For nation will rise against nation and kingdom against kingdom, and there will be pestilences and famines and earthquakes in various places; all these are the beginnings of sorrows. I do not doubt that these things too which are written will come to pass according to the letter; but it seems to me that kingdom against kingdom, and the pestilences of those whose speech creeps like a cancer, and the famine of hearing the word of God, and the shaking of the whole earth and the separation from the true faith, are rather to be understood of the heretics, who, fighting against one another, bring about a victory for the Church.\n— Commentary on Matthew","But as for what he said: But these are the beginnings of sorrows, it is better translated of birth pangs, so that the coming of antichrist may be understood as a kind of conception, as it were, not the birth.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.13.9":["Then they will deliver you up to tribulation and will kill you. Through the apostles the person of all believers is signified, not that the apostles are signified as going to be found in the body at that time.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.13.10":["And this Gospel of the kingdom will be preached in the whole world as a testimony to all nations, and then the consummation will come. The sign of the Lord's coming is that the Gospel be preached in the whole world, so that no one may be excusable; and this we see either already fulfilled or soon to be fulfilled. For I do not think that any nation remains which does not know the name of Christ; and although it may not have had a preacher, yet from the neighboring nations it cannot be ignorant of the report of the faith.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.13.11":["But when they hand you over, do not think how or what you are to speak. He had said above: For they will hand you over in councils, and in their synagogues they will scourge you, and you will be led before governors and kings for my sake. Since, then, we are led before judges for Christ's sake, we ought to offer only our will for Christ. For the rest, Christ himself, who dwells in us, will speak for himself, and the grace of the Holy Spirit will be supplied in answering.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.13.12":["And brother will hand over brother to death, and father son, and sons will rise up against parents. This we see happen frequently in persecutions, and there is no faithful affection among those whose faith is different.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.13.13":["Whoever has persevered to the end, he will be saved. For it belongs to virtue not to have begun but to have finished.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.13.14-16":["Then let those who are in Judea flee to the mountains, and let him who is on the roof not come down to take anything from his house, and let him who is in the field not turn back to take his tunic. The abomination of desolation can also be understood as every perverse doctrine; and when we see it standing in the holy place, that is, in the Church, and showing itself as God, we must flee from Judea to the mountains, that is, leaving behind the letter that kills and Jewish perversity, draw near to the eternal mountains from which God gives light wonderfully; and be on the roof and on the housetop, where the fiery darts of the devil cannot reach; and not come down and take anything from the house of our former way of life, nor seek the things behind, but rather sow in the field of the spiritual Scriptures so that we may gather fruit from it; nor take a second tunic, which the apostles are forbidden to have. On this passage, that is, on the abomination of desolation which was spoken of by Daniel the prophet standing in the holy place, Porphyry uttered many blasphemies against us in the thirteenth volume of his work; Eusebius, bishop of Caesarea, answered him in three volumes, the eighteenth, the nineteenth and the twentieth; Apollinaris too wrote most fully; and it was a superfluous attempt to want to discuss in one passage a matter that has been disputed in so many thousands of lines.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.13.14":["So when you see the abomination of desolation which was spoken of by Daniel the prophet standing in the holy place; let him who reads understand. When we are summoned to understanding, what was said is shown to be mystical. And we read in Daniel in this way: And in the middle of the week my sacrifice and libations will be taken away, and in the temple there will be the abomination of desolations until the consummation of time, and consummation will be given upon the desolation. Of this the Apostle also speaks, saying that the man of iniquity and the adversary is to be lifted up against everything that is called God or that is worshiped, so that he dares to stand in the temple of God and to show that he himself is God; whose coming, according to the working of Satan, is to destroy those who have received him and reduce them to a desolation of God. But it can also be taken simply, either of antichrist, or of the image of Caesar which Pilate set up in the temple, or of the equestrian statue of Hadrian which has stood in the very place of the holy of holies up to the present day. Also, according to the old Scripture, an idol is called an abomination, and for that reason of desolation is added, because the idol was set up in the desolated and destroyed temple.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.13.17":["But woe to those who are pregnant and nursing in those days. Woe to those souls which have not brought their offspring to the perfect man, but have only the beginnings of faith, so that they need the nurture of teachers. This too can be said: that in the persecution of antichrist, or of the Roman captivity, those who were pregnant and nursing, weighed down by the burden of womb and of children, could not have an unencumbered flight.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.13.18":["But pray that your flight may not be in winter or on the Sabbath. If we choose to take this of the captivity of Jerusalem, when it was taken by Titus and Vespasian, they ought to pray that their flight may not be in winter or on the Sabbath, because in the one the harshness of the cold prevents them from going into the wildernesses and hiding in the mountains and deserts, and in the other there is either transgression of the law, if they choose to flee, or imminent death, if they remain. But if it is understood of the consummation of the world, he commands this: that our faith and our love toward Christ may not grow cold, and that we may not, idle in the work of God, grow sluggish in a Sabbath of the virtues.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.13.20":["And unless those days had been shortened, no flesh would be saved; but for the sake of the elect those days will be shortened. We ought to understand the shortened days not according to the ravings of some who think that the measures of the times are changed, and who do not remember that writing: By your ordinance the day continues, but according to the quality of the times; that is, shortened not in measure but in number, so that, just as it is said in the blessing: With length of days I will fill him, so here too the days may be understood as shortened, lest the faith of believers be shaken by the delay of the times.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.13.21":["Then if anyone says to you: Behold, here is the Christ, or there, do not believe it. At the time of the Jewish captivity many leaders arose who said that they were Christs, so much so that, while the Romans were besieging the city, there were three factions within. But it is better understood of the consummation of the world.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.13.22-23":["For false Christs and false prophets will arise and will give great signs and wonders, so that even the elect, if it can be, are led into error. Behold, I have told you beforehand. This passage, as I have already said before, is to be discussed in a threefold way: either of the time of the Roman siege, or of the consummation of the world, or of the fight of the heretics against the Church and of antichrists of this kind, who fight against Christ under the pretense of a false knowledge.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.13.24-25":["But immediately after the tribulation of those days the sun will be darkened, and the moon will not give its light, and the stars of heaven will fall from heaven, and the powers of the heavens will be shaken. The sun and moon will be darkened and will not give their light, and the other stars will fall from heaven, and the powers of the heavens will be moved, not by a diminution of light, for we read, after all, that the sun will have sevenfold light, but because in comparison with the true light all things are dark to the sight. If, then, this sun which now glows red over the whole world, and the moon which is the second luminary, and the stars which are kindled for the solace of the night, and all the powers, which we understand as the multitudes of angels, will be reckoned as darkness at the coming of Christ, let the haughty brow of those be struck down who, thinking themselves holy, do not dread the presence of the judge.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.13.27":["And he will send his angels with a trumpet. Of this trumpet the Apostle also speaks, and we read of it in the Apocalypse of John; and in the Old Testament trumpets of beaten work are commanded to be made of gold, bronze and silver, so that the sublime mysteries of the teachings may resound.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.13.28-29":["Learn a parable from the fig tree. When its branch has already become tender and the leaves have come out, you know that summer is near, and the rest. Under the example of the tree he taught the coming of the consummation. Just as, he says, when the shoots on the fig tree have become tender, and the bud breaks into flower, and the bark brings forth leaves, you understand the coming of summer and the arrival of the west wind and of spring; so when you see all these things that are written, do not think that the consummation of the world is already present, but that certain preludes and forerunners, as it were, are coming to show that it is near and at the doors.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.13.30":["Amen I say to you, that this generation will not pass away until all these things happen. We said above that the good and, on the contrary, the wicked each form a single generation. Therefore it signifies either the whole race of men or specifically that of the Jews.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.13.31":["Heaven and earth will pass away, but my words will not pass away. Heaven and earth will pass away by a change, not by an abolition of themselves; otherwise how will the sun be darkened and the moon not give its light and the stars fall, if the heaven in which these things are, and the earth, will not exist?\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.13.32":["But of that day and hour no one knows, not even the angels of heaven, but the Father alone. In some Latin codices there is added: nor the Son, whereas in the Greek copies, and especially those of Adamantius and Pierius, this is not found written; but because it is read in some, it seems that it should be discussed. Arius and Eunomius rejoice, as though the ignorance of the master were the glory of the disciples, and they say: He who knows and he who is ignorant cannot be equal. Against them these things must briefly be said: Since Jesus, that is, the Word of God, made all times, for all things indeed were made through him, and without him nothing was made; and since among all the times there is also the day of judgment, by what logic can he be ignorant of a part of that whose whole he knows? This too must be said: Which is greater, the knowledge of the Father or that of the judgment? If he knows the greater, how is he ignorant of what is less? We read it written: All things that are the Father's have been delivered to me. If all things of the Father are the Son's, for what reason did he reserve to himself the knowledge of one day and refuse to share it with the Son? But this too must be inferred: If he is ignorant of the last day of the times, he is ignorant also of the next to last and of all those before it. For it cannot be that he who is ignorant of the first knows what the second is. Therefore, since we have proved that the Son is not ignorant of the day of the consummation, a reason must be given why he is said to be ignorant of it. The Apostle writes concerning the Savior: In whom are hidden all the treasures of wisdom and knowledge. So all the treasures of wisdom and knowledge are in Christ, but they are hidden. Why hidden? After the resurrection, when asked by the apostles about the day, he answered more plainly: It is not yours to know the times and the moments which the Father has set in his own power. When he says: It is not yours to know, he shows that he himself knows, but that it is not expedient for the apostles to know, so that, always uncertain about the coming of the judge, they may live each day as though they were to be judged on the next. Finally, the words of the Gospel that follow also compel the same understanding, saying too that the Father alone knows. In the Father he includes the Son as well; for every father is the name of a son.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.13.34":["For just as a man setting out on a journey called his servants and handed over his goods to them, and to one he gave five talents, to another two, and to another one. This man, the householder, is beyond doubt Christ, who, ascending as victor to the Father after the resurrection, called the apostles and handed over to them the teaching of the Gospel, giving more to one and less to another, not out of lavishness or stinginess but according to the strength of those who received it, just as the Apostle too says that he gave milk to drink to those who could not take solid food. In short, he receives with like joy both the one who had made ten out of five talents and the one who had made four out of two, considering not the size of the profit but the will behind the effort. In the five and the two and the one talent let us understand either the diverse graces that have been handed over to each, or else in the first all the senses tested, in the second understanding and works, in the third reason, by which we men are set apart from the beasts.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.13.35":["Watch therefore, because you do not know at what hour your Lord is to come. He shows clearly why he said above: But of that day no one knows, neither the Son of Man nor the angels, but the Father alone, namely because it is not expedient for the apostles to know, so that, in the uncertainty of a suspended expectation, they may always believe that he is about to come of whom they do not know when he will come. And he did not say: because we do not know at what hour the Lord is to come, but you do not know, and having set forth the example of the householder, he teaches more plainly why he keeps silent about the day of the consummation, saying:\n— Commentary on Matthew","Watch therefore, because you know neither the day nor the hour. I always warn the prudent reader not to acquiesce in superstitious interpretations and in things said commatically, phrase by phrase, at the whim of those who invent them, but to consider what comes before, what lies in the middle and what follows, and to bind together for himself all that is written. And so from this which he adds: Watch, because you know neither the day nor the hour, it is understood that all that he said, that is, about the two who are in the field, and the two women grinding, and the householder who entrusted his substance to his servant, and the ten virgins, these parables were set forth beforehand so that, because we men do not know the day of judgment, we may carefully prepare for ourselves the light of good works, lest the judge come while we do not know it.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.14.1-2":["Then the chief priests and the elders of the people gathered in the court of the chief priest, who was called Caiaphas; and they took counsel to seize Jesus by guile and kill him. Those who, with the Passover near, ought to have been preparing the victims, polishing the walls of the temple, sweeping the pavements, cleaning the vessels, and purifying themselves according to the rite of the law, so that they might become worthy to eat the lamb, gather together and enter into counsel how they may kill the Lord: not fearing sedition, as the simple wording shows, but taking care lest he be taken out of their hands by the help of the people.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.14.1":["And it came to pass, when Jesus had finished all these sayings, he said to his disciples: You know that after two days the Passover will take place, and the Son of Man will be handed over to be crucified. Let those blush who think that the Savior feared death and said from dread of the passion: Father, if it can be done, let this cup pass from me. About to keep the Passover after two days, he knows that he is to be handed over to be crucified, and yet he does not avoid the snares, he does not flee in terror; so much so that, even when the others were unwilling to go, he goes on undaunted, when Thomas says: Let us go, that we too may die with him. And, wishing to put an end to the carnal festival and, as the shadow passed away, to give back the truth of the Passover, he said: With desire I have desired to eat this Passover with you before I suffer. For Christ our Passover has been sacrificed, if only we eat it in the unleavened bread of sincerity and truth. Moreover, as to his saying: After two days the Passover will take place, let us pass over the simple understanding and seek what is hallowed in it. After two days of the most brilliant light of the Old and the New Testament, the true Passover is celebrated for the world. The Passover (pascha), which in Hebrew is called phase, is named not from the passion, as most people think, but from the passing over, because the destroyer, seeing the blood on the doors of the Israelites, passed over and did not strike them, or else the Lord himself, bringing help to his people, walked above them. Read the book of Exodus, of which we shall treat more fully, if life is granted to us. But our passing over, that is, phase, is celebrated in this way: if, leaving behind earthly things and Egypt, we hasten to heavenly things.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.14.3":["Now when Jesus was in Bethany, in the house of Simon the leper. About to suffer for the whole world and to redeem all the nations by his blood, he stays in Bethany, the house of obedience, which once was the house of Simon the leper: not that he still remained a leper at that time, but he who had been a leper before was afterwards cleansed by the Savior, the former name remaining so that the power of the one who healed might appear. For in the catalogue of the apostles too Matthew is called the tax collector, with his former fault and occupation, though he had certainly ceased to be a tax collector. Some want the house of Simon the leper to be understood as that part of the people which believed in the Lord and was healed by him. Simon himself, too, is said to mean obedient; according to another understanding he can be interpreted as pure, and in his house the Church was healed.\n— Commentary on Matthew","A woman came to him having an alabaster of precious ointment, and she poured it over his head as he reclined. Let no one think that she who poured ointment over the head is the same as she who poured it over the feet. For the latter both washes with tears and wipes with her hair and is openly called a harlot, whereas of this one nothing of the kind is written. Nor indeed could a harlot at once become worthy of the Lord's head. Another evangelist, in place of the alabaster of precious ointment (which is a kind of marble), put pistic nard, that is, true and without guile, to show the faith of the Church and of the Gentiles.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.14.4-5":["But the disciples, seeing it, were indignant, saying: To what purpose is this waste? For this could have been sold for much and given to the poor. I know that some cavil at this passage, asking why another evangelist said that Judas alone was saddened, because he held the purse and had been a thief from the beginning, while Matthew writes that all the apostles were indignant; they do not know the figure that is called σύλληψις (syllepsis), by which all are customarily named for one and one for many. For Paul the apostle too, in his letter which is written to the Hebrews (although many of the Latins have doubts about it), when he had described the sufferings and merits of the saints, added: They were stoned, they were tempted, they were sawn asunder, they died by the slaughter of the sword, though the Jews hold that only one, the prophet Isaiah, was sawn. We can also say otherwise: that the apostles were truly indignant on account of the poor, but Judas on account of his own gain. Hence his muttering too is set down together with a charge: that he had no care for the poor but wished to provide for his own theft.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.14.7":["But Jesus, knowing it, said to them: Why do you trouble the woman? She has wrought a good work on me; for you always have the poor with you, but me you will not always have. Another question arises: why the Lord said to the disciples after the resurrection: Behold, I am with you even to the consummation of the world, and now says: but me you will not always have. But it seems to me that in this place he speaks of his bodily presence: that after the resurrection he would by no means be with them in such a way as he is now, in all shared life and familiarity. Mindful of this, the Apostle says: And if we had known Jesus Christ according to the flesh, yet now we know him no longer.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.14.8":["For in putting this ointment on my body, she has done it for my burial. What you think to be a waste of ointment is the office of burial. Nor is it strange if she has given me the good odor of her faith, since I am going to pour out my blood for her.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.14.9":["Amen I say to you: Wherever this Gospel shall be preached in the whole world, what she has done shall also be told in memory of her. In the whole world it is not so much this woman as the Church that is proclaimed, because she buried the Savior, because she anointed his head. And note his knowledge of things to come: that, about to suffer after two days and to die, he knows that his Gospel is to be celebrated in all the world.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.14.10-11":["And he said to them: What will you give me, and I will hand him over to you? But they fixed for him thirty pieces of silver. Unhappy Judas! The loss which he believed he had suffered from the pouring out of the ointment he wishes to make good with the price of his master. And yet he does not demand a fixed sum, so that the betrayal might at least seem profitable, but, as though handing over a cheap slave, he left it in the power of the buyers to give as much as they wished. Who fixed for him thirty pieces of silver. Joseph was not sold, as many think, for twenty pieces of gold, according to the LXX translators, but, according to the Hebrew truth, for twenty pieces of silver. For the servant could not be more precious than the master.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.14.12":["Now on the first day of the Unleavened Bread the disciples came to Jesus, saying: Where do you wish us to prepare for you to eat the Passover? The first day of the Unleavened Bread is the fourteenth day of the first month, when the lamb is sacrificed and the moon is at its fullest and leaven is cast out. And among those disciples who came to the Lord asking: Where do you wish us to prepare for you to eat the Passover, I reckon that Judas the betrayer was also present.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.14.13":["But Jesus said: Go into the city to a certain man. The new Scripture keeps the custom of the Old Testament. We often read: So-and-so said to so-and-so; and: In such and such a place, which in Hebrew is called phelmoni and helmoni; and yet the name of the persons and of the places is not given. And you will find, he says, a certain man carrying a jar of water. Their names were passed over for this reason: that a free occasion for the festival might be opened to all who are going to keep the Passover.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.14.15-16":["And the disciples did as Jesus had appointed them, and they prepared the Passover. In another evangelist it is written that they found a large upper room, furnished and cleaned, and there they made ready for him. But it seems to me that the upper room is to be understood as the spiritual law, which, going out from the narrowness of the letter, receives the Savior in a lofty place, Paul saying this very thing: that the things which he formerly reckoned as gain he despised as refuse and rubbish, so that he might prepare a lodging worthy of the Lord.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.14.17":["When evening had come, he reclined with the twelve disciples. Judas does everything so that suspicion of the betrayer may be removed.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.14.18":["And as they were eating he said: Amen I say to you that one of you is going to betray me. He who had foretold his passion foretells the betrayer too, giving room for repentance, so that, when he understood that his thoughts and secret counsels were known, he might repent of his deed; and yet he does not point him out specifically, lest, being openly convicted, he should become more shameless. He casts the charge among the whole number, so that the one who is conscious of it may repent.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.14.19":["And being greatly saddened they began each to say: Is it I, Lord? And surely the eleven apostles knew that they were planning nothing of the kind against the Lord; but they trust the master more than themselves, and, fearing their own frailty, they sorrowfully ask about a sin of which they had no consciousness.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.14.20":["But he answered and said: He who dips his hand with me in the dish, he will betray me. O the wonderful patience of the Lord! First he had said: One of you is going to betray me. The betrayer persists in evil; he reproves him more plainly, and yet he does not point out his name specifically. Judas, while the others were saddened and drew back their hands and forbade food to their mouths, with the same rashness and shamelessness with which he was going to betray him, even puts his hand into the dish with his master, so that by his boldness he might feign a good conscience.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.14.21":["The Son of Man indeed goes as it is written of him; but woe to that man by whom the Son of Man will be betrayed. Reproved neither the first time nor the second, he does not draw back his foot from the betrayal; rather, he feeds his shamelessness on the patience of the Lord and stores up for himself wrath on the day of wrath. Punishment is foretold, so that the torments announced might correct one whom shame had not overcome. But as to what follows:\n\nIt would have been good for him if that man had not been born, he is not on that account to be thought to have existed before he was born, on the ground that it can be well with no one except one who has existed; it is simply said that it is much better not to exist than to exist badly.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.14.22-24":["And while they were dining, Jesus took bread and blessed and broke it, and gave it to his disciples and said: Take and eat, this is my body. And taking the cup he gave thanks and gave it to them, saying, and the rest. After the typical Passover had been fulfilled and he had eaten the flesh of the lamb with the apostles, he takes bread, which strengthens the heart of man, and passes over to the true sacrament of the Passover, so that, just as Melchizedek, the priest of the most high God, had done in prefiguration of it when he offered bread and wine, he himself also might represent it in the truth of his own body and blood. In Luke we read of two cups with which he drank to the disciples, one of the first month and the other of the second, so that he who could not eat the lamb among the saints in the first month may eat the kid among the penitents in the second month.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.14.25":["But I say to you: I will not drink from now on of this fruit of the vine until that day when I drink it new with you in the kingdom of my Father. He passes from carnal things to spiritual ones. That the vine transplanted from Egypt is the people of Israel, to whom the Lord speaks through Jeremiah: I planted you a true vine; how have you been changed into the bitterness of a foreign vine, both Isaiah the prophet testifies, in the song which he sings to his beloved, and all Scripture here and there. The Lord therefore says that he will by no means drink of this vine except in the kingdom of his Father. The kingdom of the Father I take to be the faith of believers, the Apostle also confirming this very thing: The kingdom of God is within you. Therefore, when the Jews have received the kingdom of the Father (note that he says Father, not God: every name of Father refers to a son), when, I say, they have believed in God the Father and the Father has brought them to the Son, then the Lord will drink of their wine, and, in the likeness of Joseph reigning in Egypt, he will be made drunk with his brothers.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.14.26":["And when a hymn had been said, they went out to the Mount of Olives. This is what we read in a certain psalm: They have eaten and worshiped, all the fat ones of the earth. According to this example, whoever has been filled and made drunk with the bread and the cup of the Savior can praise the Lord and ascend the Mount of Olives, where there is refreshment from labors, solace for pain, and the knowledge of the true light.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.14.27":["Then Jesus says to them: All of you will suffer a stumbling block in me on this night. He foretells what they are going to suffer, so that, when they have suffered it, they may not despair of salvation, but may be set free by repenting. And he pointedly added: on this night you will suffer a stumbling block, because, just as those who get drunk get drunk at night, so also those who suffer a stumbling block undergo it in the night and in darkness. But let us say: The night has passed, and the day has drawn near.\n— Commentary on Matthew","For it is written: I will strike the shepherd, and the sheep of the flock will be scattered. This is written in other words in Zechariah the prophet, and, unless I am mistaken, it is said in the person of the prophet to God: Strike the shepherd, and let the sheep be scattered, Psalm 68 (69) also, which is sung throughout in the Lord's person, agreeing with this sense: For they have persecuted him whom you have struck. And the good shepherd is struck so that he may lay down his life for his sheep, and that out of many flocks of errors there may be made one flock and one shepherd. Of this testimony more has been said in the little book which we wrote on the best kind of translating.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.14.29":["But Peter answered and said to him: Even if all are scandalized, I will never be scandalized. It is not rashness, nor is it a lie; it is the faith of the apostle Peter and his burning affection toward the Lord Savior, of which we spoke above.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.14.30":["Jesus said to him: Amen I say to you that on this night, before the cock crows, you will deny me three times. Peter made his promise out of the ardor of faith, and the Savior, as God, knew what was to come. And note that Peter denies in the night, and denies a third time; but after the cock crowed and, as the darkness waned, the approaching light was announced, he turned and wept bitterly, washing away the filth of the denial with tears.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.14.32":["Then Jesus came with them to an estate which is called Gethsemane, and he said to his disciples: Sit here while I go over there and pray. Gethsemane is interpreted most fertile valley; there he ordered the disciples to sit for a little while and to wait for his return, while the Lord alone prayed for all.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.14.33":["And taking Peter and the two sons of Zebedee, he began to be saddened and to be sorrowful. What we said above about passion and propassion is shown also in the present passage: that the Lord, in order to prove the reality of the man he had assumed, was indeed truly saddened, but, lest passion should rule in his soul, began to be saddened by way of propassion. For it is one thing to be saddened and another to begin to be saddened. Yet he was saddened not by fear of suffering, he who had come for this very purpose, that he might suffer, and who had rebuked Peter for timidity, but on account of the most unhappy Judas, and the stumbling block of all the apostles, and the rejection of the people of the Jews, and the overthrow of wretched Jerusalem. Hence Jonah too is saddened over the withering of the gourd or ivy, not wishing his former shelter to perish. But if the heretics interpret the sadness of soul not as the Savior's affection toward those who would perish but as passion, let them answer how they expound what is said in the person of God through Ezekiel: And in all these things you saddened me.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.14.34":["Then he said to them: My soul is sad even to death; stay here and keep watch with me. What is saddened is the soul; and it is saddened not on account of death but even to death, until he should free the apostles by his passion. But as to his command: stay here and keep watch with me, he does not forbid them sleep, for which it was not the time with the danger at hand, but the sleep of unbelief and the torpor of the mind. Let those who suspect that Jesus took on an irrational soul tell us how he is saddened and knows the time of sadness. For although brute animals too grieve, yet they know neither the causes nor the time up to which they ought to be saddened.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.14.35-36":["And going forward a little he fell on his face, worshiping and saying: My Father, if it is possible, let this cup pass from me; yet not as I will, but as you. Having given the apostles the command to stay and keep watch with the Lord, he goes forward a little, falls on his face, and shows the humility of his mind by the posture of his flesh; and he says tenderly: My Father, and asks that, if it is possible, the cup of the passion, of which we spoke above, may pass from him. But he asks this not from fear of suffering but from mercy toward the former people, lest he drink the cup handed to him by them. Hence he pointedly did not say: let the cup pass from me, but this cup, that is, the cup of the people of the Jews, which can have no excuse of ignorance if it kills me, since it has the Law and the Prophets who prophesy of me daily. Nevertheless, returning to himself, what he had shrunk from in trepidation in the person of a man, he confirms in that of God and of the Son: yet not as I will, but as you. Let not this be done, he says, which I speak from human feeling, but that for which I came down to earth by your will.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.14.37":["And he came to the disciples and found them sleeping, and he says to Peter: So you could not keep watch with me one hour? He who had said above: Even if all are scandalized in you, I will never be scandalized, now cannot overcome sleep for the greatness of his sadness.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.14.38":["Keep watch and pray, that you may not enter into temptation. It is impossible for the human soul not to be tempted. Hence in the Lord's prayer too we say: Lead us not into temptation, namely the temptation which we cannot bear, not refusing temptation altogether but praying for the strength to endure in temptations. So here too he does not say: Keep watch and pray that you may not be tempted, but that you may not enter into temptation, that is, lest temptation overcome and conquer you and hold you within its toils. For example, the martyr who shed his blood for confessing the Lord was indeed tempted, but was not bound in the nets of temptations; but he who denies runs into the snares of temptation.\n— Commentary on Matthew","The spirit indeed is willing, but the flesh is weak. This is against the rash, who think that whatever they have believed they can attain. So, as much as we trust in the ardor of the mind, so much let us fear from the frailty of the flesh. And yet, according to the Apostle, the works of the flesh are put to death by the spirit.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.14.39":["Again a second time he went away and prayed, saying: My Father, if this cup cannot pass unless I drink it, your will be done. A second time he prays that, if Nineveh cannot be saved otherwise than by the withering of the gourd, the will of the Father may be done, which is not contrary to the will of the Son, as he himself says through the prophet: To do your will, my God, I have willed.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.14.40":["And he came again and found them sleeping; for their eyes were heavy. He alone prays for all, just as he alone suffers for all. But the eyes of the apostles were growing faint and weighed down, with the denial near.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.14.41":["Then he comes to his disciples and says to them: Sleep now and take your rest; behold, the hour has drawn near. After he had prayed a third time, so that in the mouth of two and three witnesses every word might stand, and had obtained that the fear of the apostles should be corrected by the repentance that followed, secure about his passion he goes on to meet the persecutors and offers himself of his own accord to be killed, and he says to his disciples:\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.14.42":["Rise, let us go; behold, he who is going to betray me has drawn near. Let them not find us as though afraid and drawing back; let us go of our own accord to death, so that they may see the confidence and joy of one who is about to suffer.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.14.44":["Now he who betrayed him had given them a sign, saying: Whomever I kiss, he is the one; seize him. Wretched Judas, and yet not to be pitied! With the same unbelief with which he betrayed his master and Lord, he thought that the signs which he had seen the Savior performing were done not by divine majesty but by magic arts; and because he had perhaps heard that he was transfigured on the mountain, he feared that by a similar transformation he might slip out of the hands of the attendants. So he gives a sign, so that they might know that the one he pointed out with a kiss was the man.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.14.45":["And at once coming up to Jesus, he said: Hail, rabbi, and he kissed him. Shameless indeed and wicked is the confidence that calls him master and presses a kiss on the one whom he was betraying. Yet he still has something of a disciple's modesty, since he does not hand him over openly to the persecutors, but through the sign of a kiss. This is the sign which God set on Cain, so that whoever found him should not kill him.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.14.47":["And behold, one of those who were with Jesus, stretching out his hand, drew his sword, and striking the servant of the chief priest cut off his ear. In another Gospel it is written that Peter did this, with the same ardor of mind as in everything else. The servant of the chief priest, too, is called Malchus; and the ear that is cut off is the right one. It should be said in passing that Malchus, that is, king, the people of the Jews, once became the servant of the impiety and greed of the priests, and lost its right ear, so as to hear the whole meanness of the letter with the left; but the Lord, in those of the Jews who were willing to believe, restored the right ear and made the servant a royal and priestly race.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.14.48-49":["At that hour Jesus said: You have come out as against a robber with swords and clubs to arrest me? Daily I sat among you teaching in the temple, and you did not seize me. It is foolish, he says, to seek with swords and clubs one who hands himself over of his own accord into your hands, and to track down at night through a betrayer, as if he were hiding and avoiding your eyes, one who teaches daily in the temple. But you gather against me in darkness for this reason, that your power is in darkness.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.14.49":["But all this was done that the Scriptures of the prophets might be fulfilled. What are the Scriptures of the prophets? They pierced my hands and feet; and elsewhere: He was led like a sheep to the sacrifice; and in another place: By the iniquities of my people he was led to death.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.14.53":["But they, holding Jesus, led him to Caiaphas the chief priest, where the scribes and the elders had gathered. Moses, at God's command, had ordered that the high priests should succeed their fathers and that a line of descent should be woven among the priests. Josephus relates that this Caiaphas bought the high priesthood for one year only from Herod for a price. It is no wonder, then, if an unjust high priest judges unjustly.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.14.54":["But Peter followed him from afar. He who was going to deny the Lord followed from afar.\n— Commentary on Matthew","And going inside he sat with the attendants, to see the end. Either out of a disciple's love or out of human curiosity he wished to know what the high priest would decide about the Lord: whether he would condemn him to death or would release him after beating him with scourges. And in this lies the difference between the ten apostles and Peter: they flee; he, although from a distance, still follows the Savior.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.14.57-58":["At last two false witnesses came and said: This man said: I can destroy the temple of God and after three days build it. How are they false witnesses, if they say the things which we read above that the Lord said? But a false witness is one who does not understand what is said in the same sense in which it is said. For the Lord had spoken of the temple of his body; but they falsely accuse him in the very words too, and by adding or changing a few they make, as it were, a just accusation. The Savior had said: Dissolve this temple; they change it and say: I can destroy the temple of God. You, he says, dissolve, not I, because it is unlawful for us to lay hands on ourselves. Then they twist it: and after three days build it, so that he might seem to have spoken properly of the Jewish temple. But the Lord, to show a living and breathing temple, had said: And I in three days will raise it up. It is one thing to build, another to raise up.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.14.60-61":["And the chief priest, rising up, said to him: Do you answer nothing to the things these men testify against you? But Jesus was silent. Headlong anger and impatience, finding no ground for a false accusation, shake the high priest from his throne, so that he might show the madness of his mind by the movement of his body. The more Jesus kept silent before the false witnesses and the impious priests, unworthy of his reply, the more the high priest, overcome by fury, provokes him to answer, so that from any occasion of speech whatever he might find ground for accusing him. Nevertheless Jesus is silent. For he knew, as God, that whatever he answered would be twisted into a false accusation.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.14.61":["And the chief priest said to him: I adjure you by the living God that you tell us whether you are the Christ, the Son of God. Why do you adjure him, most impious of priests: in order to accuse, or in order to believe? If in order to accuse, others are accusing him: condemn him in his silence; if in order to believe, why did you refuse to believe him when he confessed?\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.14.62":["Jesus says to him: You have said it. Both to Pilate and to Caiaphas the reply is alike, so that they may be condemned by their own sentence.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.14.63-64":["Then the chief priest tore his garments, saying: He has blasphemed; what further need have we of witnesses? The man whom fury had shaken from the priestly throne the same rage provokes to tear his clothes. And he tears his garments to show that the Jews had lost the glory of the priesthood and that the high priests have an empty seat. But it is also a Jewish custom to tear their garments when they hear anything blasphemous and as it were against God, as we read that Paul and Barnabas also did when in Lycaonia they were being honored with the worship of gods. But Herod, because he did not give honor to God but acquiesced in the immoderate applause of the people, was at once struck by an angel.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.14.65":["Then they spat in his face and struck him with their fists. That what was written might be fulfilled: I gave my cheeks to blows and did not turn my face away from the shame of spitting.\n— Commentary on Matthew","And others struck his face with their palms, saying: Prophesy to us, Christ, who is it that struck you? It was foolish to answer those who were beating him and to prophesy who was striking him, when the madness of the one who struck was plain to see. But just as he did not prophesy this to you, so he foretold that other thing most plainly: that Jerusalem would be surrounded by an army and that not a stone would be left upon a stone in the temple.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.14.66":["But Peter was sitting outside in the court. He sat outside to see the outcome of the affair, and he did not come near Jesus, lest any suspicion should arise among the attendants.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.14.70":["And again he denied with an oath: I do not know the man. I know that some, out of pious affection toward the apostle Peter, have interpreted this passage so as to say that Peter denied not God but a man, and that the sense is: I do not know the man, because I know God. How frivolous this is the prudent reader understands: they so defend the apostle as to make the Lord guilty of a lie. For if he did not deny, then the Lord lied, who had said: Amen I say to you that on this night, before the cock crows, you will deny me three times. See what he says: you will deny me, not the man.\n— Commentary on Matthew","Surely you too are one of them, for your speech also makes you plain. Not that Peter was of another language or of a foreign nation, for all of them were Hebrews, both those who accused and he who was accused, but because each province and region had its own peculiarities and could not avoid its native sound of speech. Hence too the Ephraimites in the book of Judges cannot say the σύνθημα (the password).\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.14.71-72":["Then he began to curse and to swear that he did not know the man. And immediately the cock crowed. In another Gospel we read that after Peter's denial and the crowing of the cock the Savior looked at Peter and by his gaze moved him to bitter tears; nor could it be that he should remain in the darkness of denial on whom the light of the world had looked.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.14.72":["And going outside he wept bitterly. Sitting in the court of Caiaphas he could not repent. He goes outside, away from the council of the impious, so that he may wash away the filth of a fearful denial with bitter weeping.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.15.1":["When morning had come, all the chief priests and the elders of the people took counsel against Jesus, to hand him over to death; and they led him bound and handed him over to Pontius Pilate the governor. He was led not only to Pilate but also to Herod, so that both might mock the Lord. And see the zeal of the priests for evil: they kept watch the whole night in order to commit murder. And they handed him over bound to Pilate. For they had this custom, that whomever they had adjudged to death they handed over bound to the judge.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.15.2-3":["Jesus says to him: You say so. He answered in such a way that he both spoke the truth and his word lay open to no false accusation. And note that to Pilate, who was pronouncing sentence unwillingly, he answered in some part, but to the priests and rulers he would not answer, judging them unworthy of his speech.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.15.2":["And Jesus stood before the governor. And the governor questioned him, saying: Are you the king of the Jews? Since Pilate questions him on no other charge than whether he is king of the Jews, the Jews are convicted of impiety, because they could not find, even falsely, anything to lay against the Savior.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.15.4":["Then Pilate says to him: Do you not hear how many testimonies they speak against you? He who condemns Jesus is indeed a heathen, but he lays the cause upon the people of the Jews.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.15.5":["Do you not hear how many testimonies they speak against you? But Jesus would answer nothing, lest by refuting the charge he should be released by the governor and the benefit of the cross be put off.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.15.7":["Now he had at that time a notable prisoner, who was called Barabbas. This man, in the Gospel which is written according to the Hebrews, is interpreted as son of their master; he had been condemned for sedition and murder.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.15.9-10":["Pilate offers them the choice of whom they wished him to release, the robber or Jesus, not doubting that Jesus would rather be chosen, since he knew that he had been handed over out of envy. Therefore the cause of the cross is plainly envy.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.15.12-14":["Pilate says to them: What then shall I do with Jesus who is called Christ? They all say: Let him be crucified. The governor says to them: Why, what evil has he done? But they cried out the more, saying: Let him be crucified. Pilate gave many occasions for freeing the Savior: first, by comparing the robber with the just man; then by adding: What then shall I do with Jesus who is called Christ, that is, who is your king; and when they answered: Let him be crucified, he did not acquiesce at once, but, following the suggestion of his wife, who had sent word: Let there be nothing between you and that just man, he too answered: Why, what evil has he done? In saying this Pilate acquitted Jesus. But they cried out the more, saying: Let him be crucified, so that what he had said in Psalm 21 (22) might be fulfilled: Many dogs have surrounded me; the assembly of evildoers has besieged me; and that word of Jeremiah: My inheritance has become to me like a lion in the forest; they have raised their voice against me; Isaiah too agreeing in this sense: And I waited for them to do judgment, but they did iniquity, and not justice but an outcry.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.15.15":["Then he released Barabbas to them; but Jesus, scourged, he handed over to them to be crucified. Barabbas the robber, who stirred up seditions among the crowds, who was the author of murders, was released to the people of the Jews, that is, the devil, who reigns in them to this day, and for that reason they cannot have peace; but Jesus, handed over by the Jews, is acquitted by Pilate's wife and is called just by the governor himself, and the centurion confesses that he is truly the Son of God. Let the learned reader ask how it is consistent that Pilate washed his hands and said: I am innocent of the blood of this just man, and afterwards handed over Jesus, scourged, to be crucified. But it must be known that he was serving the Roman laws, by which it was decreed that one who is crucified should first be beaten with scourges. So Jesus was handed over to the soldiers to be beaten, and scourges cut that most sacred body and the breast that could contain God. And this was done so that, since it was written: Many are the scourges of sinners; by his being scourged we might be freed from blows, Scripture saying to the just man: The scourge has not come near your tent.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.15.16-19":["Then the soldiers of the governor, taking Jesus into the praetorium, gathered the whole cohort to him; and stripping him, they put a scarlet cloak around him, and plaiting a crown of thorns they put it on his head, and a reed in his right hand, and bending the knee before him they mocked him, saying: Hail, king of the Jews. The soldiers indeed, because he had been called king of the Jews, and the scribes and priests had laid this charge against him, that he was usurping rule for himself among the people of Israel, do this in mockery: having stripped him of his former garments, they clothe him in a scarlet cloak in place of the red border which the ancient kings used to wear, and in place of a diadem they put on him a thorny crown, in place of a royal scepter they give him a reed, and they worship him as a king. But let us understand all these things mystically. For just as Caiaphas said: It is necessary that one man die for all, not knowing what he said, so these too, whatever they did, although they did it with another mind, were nevertheless bestowing mysteries on us who believe. In the scarlet cloak he bears up the bloody works of the Gentiles; in the crown of thorns he dissolves the ancient curse; with the reed he kills venomous animals; or else he held the reed in his hand to write down the sacrilege of the Jews.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.15.19":["And spitting on him they took the reed and struck his head. At that time was fulfilled: I did not turn away my face from the shame of spitting. And yet, though they strike his head with a reed, he bears all things patiently, to show true the prophecy of Isaiah, who says: A shaken reed he will not break.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.15.20":["And after they had mocked him, they stripped him of the cloak and clothed him in his own garments, and led him away to crucify him. When Jesus is scourged and spat upon and derided, he does not have his own garments, but those which he had taken on for our sins; but when he is crucified, and the pageant of mockery and derision has passed, then he receives back his former clothes and takes up his own adornment, and at once the elements are thrown into confusion and the creature gives testimony to the Creator.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.15.21":["And going out they found a man of Cyrene named Simon; him they pressed into service to carry his cross. Let no one think that the history of John the evangelist is contrary to this passage. For he says that the Lord, going out of the praetorium, carried his own cross; but Matthew reports that they found a man of Cyrene named Simon, on whom, pressing him into service, they laid the cross of Jesus. But this is to be understood: that Jesus, going out of the praetorium, carried his cross himself, and afterwards met Simon, on whom they laid the cross to be carried. According to anagogy, however, the nations take up the cross of Jesus, and the obedient stranger carries the ignominy of the Savior.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.15.22":["And they came to the place which is called Golgotha, which is the place of Calvary. I have heard someone expound the place of Calvary as the place in which Adam was buried, and say that it was so called because the head of the ancient man was laid there, and that this is what the Apostle says: Arise, you who sleep, and rise from the dead, and Christ will enlighten you. A pleasing interpretation, and soothing to the ear of the people, but not true. For outside the city and beyond the gate there are places in which the heads of the condemned are cut off, and they have taken the name of Calvary, that is, of the beheaded. And the Lord was crucified there for this reason: that where formerly was the ground of the condemned, there the standards of martyrdom might be raised; and just as for us he was made the curse of the cross, and was scourged and crucified, so for the salvation of all he is crucified as a guilty man among the guilty. But if anyone should wish to contend that the Lord was crucified there so that his blood might drip upon the tomb of Adam, let us ask him why other robbers too were crucified in the same place. From this it is evident that Calvary signifies not the sepulcher of the first man but the place of the beheaded, so that where sin abounded, grace might superabound. Indeed, that Adam was buried near Hebron and Arba we read in the book of Joshua son of Nun.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.15.23":["And they gave him wine mixed with gall to drink, and when he had tasted it he would not drink. God speaks to Jerusalem: I planted you a true vine; how have you turned into the bitterness of a foreign vine? A bitter vine makes bitter wine, which it gives the Lord Jesus to drink, so that what is written might be fulfilled: They gave gall for my food, and in my thirst they gave me vinegar to drink. But what is said: when he had tasted it he would not drink, indicates that he tasted indeed the bitterness of death for us, but on the third day he rose again.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.15.24":["After they had crucified him, they divided his garments, casting lots. This too had been prophesied in the same psalm: They divided my garments among themselves, and upon my vesture they cast lots.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.15.26":["And they set above his head the charge against him, written: This is Jesus the king of the Jews. I cannot sufficiently wonder, as the greatness of the matter deserves, that after false witnesses had been bought and the unhappy people had been stirred up to sedition and clamor, he found no other cause for putting him to death than that he was king of the Jews. And they perhaps did this mocking and laughing. But Pilate answered them, even against their will: What I have written, I have written. Whether you will or not, O Jews, the whole crowd of the Gentiles has answered you: Jesus is king of the Jews, that is, the commander of those who believe and confess.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.15.27":["Then two robbers were crucified with him, one on the right and one on the left. If Golgotha is the tomb of Adam and not the place of the condemned, and the Lord is crucified there in order to raise up Adam, why are two robbers crucified in the same place?\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.15.29":["And those passing by blasphemed him, moving their heads. They blasphemed because they were passing by the way and would not walk on the true road of the Scriptures. They moved their heads because they had already moved their feet before and did not stand upon the rock. And the foolish people, insulting him, says the very same thing that the false witnesses had made up.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.15.31":["He saved others; himself he cannot save. Even against their will the scribes and Pharisees confess that he saved others. So your own sentence condemns you. For he who saved others could surely have saved himself too, if he had wished.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.15.32":["Let him come down now from the cross, and we believe him. A deceitful promise! Which is greater: to come down from the cross while still alive, or to rise from the sepulcher when dead? He rose again, and you do not believe. Therefore, even if he had come down from the cross, you would likewise not believe. But it seems to me that the demons put this into them. For as soon as the Lord was crucified, they felt the power of the cross and understood that their strength was broken, and they are working for this, that he should come down from the cross. But the Lord, knowing the snares of his adversaries, remains on the gibbet in order to destroy the devil.\n— Commentary on Matthew","And the robbers too who had been fixed with him reproached him with the same. Here, by the trope which is called σύλληψις (syllepsis), both are presented as having blasphemed, in place of one robber. But Luke asserts that, while one blasphemed, the other confessed and on the contrary rebuked the blasphemer; not that the Gospels disagree, but because at first both blasphemed, and then, when the sun fled, the earth was shaken, the rocks were split and darkness came on, one believed in Jesus and corrected his former denial by his subsequent confession. In the two robbers both peoples, of the Gentiles and of the Jews, at first blasphemed the Lord; afterwards one, terrified by the greatness of the signs, repented, and to this day rebukes the Jews when they blaspheme.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.15.33":["Now from the sixth hour there was darkness over the whole earth until the ninth hour. Those who have written against the Gospels suspect that the disciples of Christ, out of ignorance, interpreted as concerning the resurrection of the Lord an eclipse of the sun, which usually occurs at fixed and appointed times, although a failure of the sun never usually occurs except at the rising of the moon. But no one doubts that at the time of the Passover the moon was at its fullest. And lest perhaps the shadow of the earth, or the disk of the moon set against the sun, should seem to have caused a short and rust-colored darkness, the space of three hours is stated, so that every pretext of the cavilers might be taken away. And I think this was done so that the prophecy might be fulfilled which says: The sun shall go down at midday, and the light shall be darkened over the earth in the day, and in another place: The sun went down while it was still midday. And it seems to me that the brightest light of the world, that is, the greater luminary, drew back its rays, so that it might neither see the Lord hanging nor let the impious blasphemers enjoy its light.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.15.34":["And about the ninth hour Jesus cried out with a loud voice, saying: Heli Heli lema sabacthani, that is: My God, my God, why have you forsaken me? He made use of the beginning of Psalm 21 (22); and what is read in the middle of the little verse: Look upon me, is superfluous. For in the Hebrew it is read: My God, my God, why have you forsaken me? Therefore they are impious who think that the psalm was spoken in the person of David, or of Esther and Mordecai, since even the evangelists understand testimonies taken from it as concerning the Savior, such as this: They divided my garments among themselves, and upon my vesture they cast lots; and another: They dug through my hands and my feet. Nor should you wonder at the lowliness of the words and the complaints of one forsaken, since, knowing the form of a servant, you see the stumbling block of the cross.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.15.35":["But some who were standing there and heard it said: This man is calling Elijah. Not all, but some, whom I judge to have been Roman soldiers who did not understand the idiom of the Hebrew language, but from the fact that he said: Heli Heli, thought that Elijah was being invoked by him. But if you would rather understand that it was Jews who said this, they do this too in their usual manner, to defame the Lord with weakness, as one who begs the help of Elijah.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.15.36":["And at once one of them ran, and taking a sponge he filled it with vinegar and put it on a reed and gave him to drink. And these things were done so that the prophecy might be fulfilled: In my thirst they gave me vinegar to drink. To this day the Jews and all who do not believe in the Lord's resurrection give Jesus vinegar and gall to drink, and give him wine mixed with myrrh to put him to sleep, so that he may not see their evils.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.15.37":["And Jesus, crying out again with a loud voice, sent forth his spirit. It is a sign of divine power to send forth the spirit, as he himself had also said: No one can take my soul from me, but I lay it down of myself, that I may take it up again.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.15.38":["And the veil of the temple was torn in two, from the top to the bottom. The veil of the temple was torn, and all the mysteries of the law, which were previously covered, were laid open and passed over to the people of the Gentiles. In the Gospel of which we often make mention, we read that the lintel of the temple, of immense size, was broken and split apart. Josephus also reports that the angelic powers, once the guardians of the temple, then cried out together: Let us depart from these abodes.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.15.39":["But the centurion, and those who were with him keeping watch over Jesus, when they saw the earthquake and the things that were happening, were greatly afraid, saying: Truly this was the Son of God. In another Gospel, after the earthquake, the cause of the centurion's wonder is set forth more plainly: that when he had seen him yield up the spirit, he said: Truly this was the Son of God. For no one has the power of yielding up the spirit except him who is the creator of souls. And let us understand spirit in this place for soul, either because it makes the body spiritual and living, or because the substance of the soul itself is spirit, according to what is written: You will take away their spirit, and they will fail. And this is to be considered, that the centurion, before the cross, in the very stumbling block of the passion, confesses him truly the Son of God, and Arius in the Church preaches him a creature.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.15.40":["But let us see what sort of companions he had: Mary Magdalene, from whom he had cast out seven demons; and Mary the mother of James and Joseph, his aunt, the sister of Mary the mother of the Lord; and the mother of the sons of Zebedee, who a little before had asked for the kingdom for her children; and others, whom we read of in the other Gospels.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.15.41":["And there were many women there at a distance, who had followed Jesus from Galilee, ministering to him. It was a Jewish custom, and by the ancient usage of the nation it was not held to be a fault, that women should supply food and clothing to their teachers from their own substance. Because this could cause a stumbling block among the nations, Paul records that he had cast it aside: Have we not the power to lead about women as sisters, as the other apostles also do? And they ministered to the Lord from their own substance, so that he whose spiritual things they reaped might reap their carnal things; not that the Lord of creatures needed food, but to show a type for teachers, that they ought to be content with food and clothing from their disciples.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.15.43":["When it was evening, there came a certain rich man from Arimathea named Joseph, who himself also was a disciple of Jesus. He is said to be rich, not out of the boastfulness of the writer, as if to report that a noble and very wealthy man was a disciple of Jesus, but to show the reason why he was able to obtain the body of Jesus from Pilate. For it was not for a poor and unknown man to approach Pilate, the governor of the Roman power, and obtain the body of one crucified. In another evangelist this Joseph is called βουλευτής, that is, counselor, and some think that the first psalm was composed about him: Blessed is the man who has not walked in the counsel of the impious, and the rest.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.15.46":["And having taken the body of Jesus, he wrapped it in a clean linen cloth. From the simple burial of the Lord the ambition of the rich is condemned, who cannot do without riches even in their tombs. And according to spiritual understanding we can also take this to mean that the body of the Lord is to be wrapped not in gold, not in gems and silk, but in pure linen; although this too may be signified, that he wraps Jesus in a clean linen cloth who has received him with a pure mind.\n— Commentary on Matthew","And he laid it in his own new tomb, which he had hewn in the rock, and he rolled a great stone to the door of the tomb and went away. He is laid in a new tomb, lest after the resurrection, while the other bodies remained, it should be pretended that another had risen. But the new sepulcher can also signify the virginal womb of Mary; and the stone set against the door, and a great stone at that, can show that the sepulcher could not have been opened without the help of many.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.15.47":["And Mary Magdalene was there, and the other Mary, sitting opposite the sepulcher. While the others left the Lord, the women persevere in their duty, waiting for what Jesus had promised; and therefore they deserved to be the first to see him rising, because he who perseveres to the end, he shall be saved.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.16.2":["Now in the evening of the Sabbath, as it grows light toward the first day of the week, there came Mary Magdalene and the other Mary to see the sepulchre. That different times are described for these women in the Gospels is not a sign of falsehood, as the impious object, but the service of a diligent visiting, while they often go away and run back and do not bear to be away from the Lord's sepulchre long or far.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.16.4-5":["And behold, there was a great earthquake; for an angel of the Lord came down from heaven, and coming up he rolled back the stone and was sitting upon it; and his appearance was like lightning, and his clothing like snow. Our Lord, one and the same Son of God and Son of Man, according to each nature, of divinity and of flesh, shows now the signs of his greatness, now those of his lowliness. Hence in the present passage too, although it is a man who was crucified, who was buried, who was shut in the tomb, whom the stone set against it holds in, yet the things done outside show the Son of God: the sun fleeing, darkness rushing in, the earth shaken, the veil torn, the rocks split apart, the dead raised, the ministries of angels, which from the very beginning of his birth also proved him God. Gabriel comes to Mary, an angel speaks with Joseph, the same announces it to the shepherds, afterward a choir of angels is heard: Glory to God in the highest, and on earth peace among men of good will, he is tempted in the wilderness, and after the victory angels at once serve him. Now too an angel comes as guardian of the Lord's sepulchre and in white clothing signifies the glory of the one who triumphs; and moreover, as the Lord ascends to heaven, two angels are seen on the Mount of Olives, promising the apostles the second coming of the Savior.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.16.6-7":["Come and see where he had been laid, so that, if you do not believe my words, you may believe the empty sepulchre; and with hurried step go and announce to his disciples that he has risen and is going before you into Galilee, that is, into the wallowing-place of the Gentiles, where before there was error and slipperiness, and no footstep was planted with firm and steady foot.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.16.6":["But for fear of him the guards were terrified and became like dead men; and the angel answered and said to the women: You, do not be afraid; for I know that you are seeking Jesus who was crucified. The guards, terrified with fear, lie stunned like dead men, and yet the angel consoles not them but the women. You, do not be afraid. Let them, he says, be afraid; let dread persist in those in whom unbelief remains; but you, because you are seeking Jesus crucified, hear that he has risen and has fulfilled what he promised.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.16.8":["And they went out quickly from the tomb with fear and great joy, running to announce it to his disciples. A twofold feeling, of fear and of joy, held the minds of the women: the one from the greatness of the miracle, the other from longing for the one rising again; and yet both spurred on the women's step. They were going to the apostles so that through them the seed of faith might be scattered.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MRK.16.15-16":["Go therefore, teach all nations, baptizing them in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit. First they teach all nations, then they dip those who have been taught in water. For it cannot be that the body receives the sacrament of baptism unless the soul has first received the truth of the faith. And they are baptized in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit so that, as their divinity is one, the bestowal may be one; and the name of the Trinity is one God.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.1.1":["That there were many who wrote gospels, the evangelist Luke testifies, saying: Since indeed many have attempted to set in order a narrative of the things that have been fulfilled among us, as those handed them down to us who from the beginning themselves saw the word and ministered to it; and the records that have lasted up to the present time declare it too, records which, published by various authors, were the beginnings of various heresies, such as the one according to the Egyptians, and those of Thomas and of Matthias and of Bartholomew, of the Twelve Apostles also, and of Basilides and of Apelles and of the rest, whom it would take very long to enumerate; since for the present it is necessary to say only this, that there have been certain men who, without the Spirit and grace of God, attempted rather to set in order a narrative than to weave together the truth of history. To them that prophetic word can rightly be fitted: Woe to those who prophesy from their own heart, who walk after their own spirit, who say: The Lord says, and the Lord did not send them; of them the Savior too speaks in the Gospel of John: All who came before me were thieves and robbers. Who came, not who were sent; for he himself says: They came, and I did not send them; in those who come there is the presumption of rashness, in those who are sent the obedience of service.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.1.15":["Drunkenness, moreover, can come about as much from wine as from the other kinds of drink that are prepared in various ways; whence it is also said of the saint: \"He shall not drink wine and strong drink (siceram)\" (Luke 1:15). Sicera means \"drunkenness\". And lest anyone who does not drink wine should think that something else might be drunk by him, the pretext is excluded, since everything that can make drunk is taken away together with wine.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"LUK.1.17":["John, then, is called Elijah not according to the foolish philosophers and certain heretics who introduce a μετεμψύχωσιν (transmigration of souls), but because, according to another testimony of the Gospel, he came in the spirit and power of Elijah and had either the same grace or the same measure of the Holy Spirit. Moreover, the austerity of life and the rigor of mind of Elijah and John are equal. The one was in the desert, the other in the desert; the one was girded with a leather belt, and the other had a similar girdle; the one, because he rebuked King Ahab and Jezebel for impiety, was forced to flee, the other, because he rebuked the unlawful marriage of Herod and Herodias, is beheaded.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.1.27":["When his mother Mary had been betrothed. Why is he conceived not of a simple virgin but of one betrothed? First, so that through the generation of Joseph the origin of Mary might be shown; second, so that she might not be stoned by the Jews as an adulteress; third, so that in fleeing to Egypt she might have a comfort. The martyr Ignatius also added a fourth reason why he was conceived of a betrothed woman: so that, he says, his birth might be hidden from the devil, while he supposes him begotten not of a virgin but of a wife.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.1.53":["And the mother of the Lord, full of the Holy Spirit, prophesied: He has filled the hungry with good things (Luke 1:53), those who before did not have the food of the Lord: and the rich he has sent away empty, those who were given for his burial, and who spat out him whom the Prophets had promised. Of these the Psalmist sings in another place too: The rich have been in want, and have hungered (Ps 33 [34]:11), that is, the people of the Jews. But those who seek the Lord, that is, the crowd of the Gentiles, shall not lack any good thing.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"LUK.2.7":["And Joseph, rising from sleep, did as the angel of the Lord commanded him. He took his wife and did not know her until she bore her firstborn son. From this passage certain people most perversely suspect that Mary had other sons as well, saying that no one is called firstborn unless he also has brothers; whereas this is the usage of the divine Scriptures, that they call firstborn not him whom brothers follow but him who is born first. Read the little book Against Helvidius mentioned above.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.2.23":["All heretics have gone astray by not understanding the mystery of his nativity. The statement \"he who opens the womb shall be called holy to the Lord\" is more applicable to the special nativity of the Savior than to that of all men, for Christ alone opened the closed doors of the womb of virginity, which nevertheless remained permanently closed. This is the closed east door, through which only the high priest enters and leaves, and nevertheless it is always closed.\n— AGAINST THE PELAGIANS 2.4"],"LUK.2.34":["He, then, who by falling had turned aside from the Lord and was of a most wicked building, rightly falls while he whom God strengthened is prophesying; but he who obeyed the precepts of God and was sustained and supported by his help rightly remains in command of the people — we read in Exodus that, when Pharaoh king of Egypt had died, the sons of Israel groaned from the works of clay, of straw and of brick, and cried out to the Lord, upon whom, while he lived, they could not call; Isaiah also, when Uzziah the leprous king had died, saw the Lord sitting upon a throne high and lifted up, and the Seraphim standing around him, and, understanding that he had unclean lips and dwelt among a people having unclean lips, he merits that a coal be sent from the altar and purge his unclean lips; here, however, as the strength of God prophesies, the prince who had turned aside from the service of the Lord falls, so that he may be raised up by Christ: for if the Lord is the resurrection, the devil is the fall; in which it is asked how it is said of the Savior: Behold, this one is set for the fall and the resurrection of many in Israel: for the fall of those who stood most wickedly, and the resurrection of such as had fallen —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"LUK.2.51":["The Savior too was subject to his parents (Luke 2), but when he was still twelve years old and differed in nothing from a slave, though Lord of all; for he had not yet reached the full age of a man that could take up the inheritance. But it is also written of him elsewhere: \"For when all things have been subjected to him, then the Son himself will be subjected to him who subjected all things to himself, that God may be all in all\" (1 Cor. 15:28). And all things will be subjected to him when he has said, \"The Lord to my Lord: Sit at my right hand, until I make your enemies the footstool of your feet\" (Ps. 109:1). In slaves who are subject, the Lord is subject. And just as he is said to be a curse for us, though he is not a curse but true blessing, so for us he is written either as subject or as not subject, according as we are either subject to God or not subject.\n— Commentary on Titus"],"LUK.3.1":["An old history relates that Philip, the son of Herod the elder, under whom the Lord fled into Egypt, and the brother of this Herod, under whom Christ suffered, married Herodias, the daughter of King Phetras; but that afterwards her father, his father-in-law, when certain quarrels had arisen against his son-in-law, took back his daughter and, to the grief of her former husband, joined her in marriage to Herod, his enemy. Who this Philip is, the evangelist Luke teaches more fully: In the fifteenth year of the reign of Tiberius Caesar, when Pontius Pilate was governing Judea, and Herod was tetrarch of Galilee, and Philip his brother was tetrarch of Iturea and the region of Trachonitis.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.3.4":["For this is he who was spoken of through the prophet Isaiah, saying: The voice of one crying in the desert: Prepare the way of the Lord, make his paths straight. He was preparing the souls of believers, in which the Lord was going to walk, so that he, the pure one, might walk in most pure ways, saying: I will dwell in them and walk among them, and I will be their God, and they shall be my people. Porphyry compares this passage with the beginning of the evangelist Mark, in which it is written: The beginning of the Gospel of Jesus Christ, the Son of God; as it is written in the prophet Isaiah: Behold, I send my angel before your face, who shall prepare your way; the voice of one crying in the desert: Prepare the way of the Lord, make his paths straight. For since the testimony is woven together from Malachi and Isaiah, he asks how we can think the example was taken as from Isaiah alone. To him churchmen have answered most fully. We, however, think either that the name of Isaiah was added through the fault of copyists, which we can prove in other places as well, or at any rate that one body has been made out of different testimonies of the Scriptures. Read Psalm 13 (14) and you will find this same thing.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.3.8":["God is able from these stones to raise up sons to Abraham. He calls the heathen stones because of the hardness of their heart. Read Ezekiel: I will take away, he says, from you the heart of stone, and I will give a heart of flesh. In stone hardness is shown, in flesh softness; or else he simply indicates the power of God, that he who made all things out of nothing can also bring forth a people from the hardest rocks.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.3.9":["Behold, the axe is laid to the root of the trees. The preaching of the word of the Gospel, which is a sword sharp on both sides, is called an axe, according to the prophet Jeremiah, who compares the word of the Lord to an axe cutting the rock.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.3.16":["Whose shoes I am not worthy to carry. In another Gospel: Whose, he says, shoe's strap I am not worthy to loose. Here humility is shown, there a mystery: that Christ is the bridegroom and John does not deserve to loose the bridegroom's strap, lest his house be called, according to the law of Moses and the example of Ruth, the house of the unshod.\n— Commentary on Matthew","He will baptize you in the Holy Spirit and fire. Either because the Holy Spirit is fire, as the Acts of the Apostles teach, at whose descent there sat as it were fire upon the tongues of the believers, and the word of the Lord was fulfilled who said: I came to send fire upon the earth, and how I wish that it may burn; or because in the present we are baptized with the spirit and in the future with fire, the Apostle too agreeing with this sense: The fire will test what sort each one's work is.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.3.19-20":["But Herod had seized John and bound him and put him in prison because of Herodias, his brother's wife. For John said to him: It is not lawful for you to have her. An old history relates that Philip, the son of Herod the elder, under whom the Lord fled into Egypt, and the brother of this Herod, under whom Christ suffered, married Herodias, the daughter of King Phetras; but that afterwards her father, his father-in-law, when certain quarrels had arisen against his son-in-law, took back his daughter and, to the grief of her former husband, joined her in marriage to Herod, his enemy. Who this Philip is, the evangelist Luke teaches more fully: In the fifteenth year of the reign of Tiberius Caesar, when Pontius Pilate was governing Judea, and Herod was tetrarch of Galilee, and Philip his brother was tetrarch of Iturea and the region of Trachonitis. So John the Baptist, who had come in the spirit and power of Elijah, with the same authority with which Elijah had rebuked Ahab and Jezebel, reproves Herod and Herodias because they had made an unlawful marriage, and because it is not lawful, while one's own brother is living, to take his wife; for he preferred to be in danger with the king rather than to be forgetful of the commandments of God for the sake of flattery.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.3.21-22":["The heavens were opened to him, and he saw the Spirit descending like a dove, coming upon him, and the rest. The mystery of the Trinity is shown in the baptism. The Lord is baptized, the Spirit descends in the form of a dove, the voice of the Father is heard bearing witness to the Son. And the heavens are opened, not by an unbarring of the elements, but to spiritual eyes, with which Ezekiel too, at the beginning of his volume, records that they were opened. The dove sat, moreover, upon the head of Jesus, lest anyone should think that the voice of the Father was addressed to John and not to the Lord.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.3.21":["Then Jesus came from Galilee to the Jordan to John, to be baptized by him. For a threefold reason the Savior received baptism from John: first, so that, since he had been born a man, he might fulfill all the justice and humility of the law; second, so that by his own baptism he might approve the baptism of John; third, so that, sanctifying the waters of the Jordan by the descent of the dove, he might show the coming of the Holy Spirit in the washing of believers.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.3.23":["And Jacob begot Joseph. This passage Julian the Augustus throws at us as a discrepancy of the evangelists: why the evangelist Matthew called Joseph the son of Jacob, and Luke called him the son of Heli; not understanding the custom of the Scriptures, that one is his father according to nature, the other according to the law. For we know that this was commanded through Moses at God's bidding: that if a brother or a kinsman has died without children, another should take his wife to raise up seed for his brother or kinsman. On this matter both Africanus, the writer of chronicles, and Eusebius of Caesarea have argued more fully in the books on the discrepancies of the Gospels.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.3.32":["And Nahshon begot Salmon. This is Salmon, prince of the tribe of Judah, as we read in Numbers.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.4.1-2":["Then he was led in the desert by the Spirit. No doubt, by the Holy Spirit. For there follows:\n\nTo be tempted by the devil. But he is led not unwillingly, nor as a captive, but by the will to fight.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.4.2":["And when he had fasted forty days and forty nights, afterward he was hungry. In this number the mystery of the forty days is shown to us, in which Moses too fasted on Mount Sinai and Elijah beside Mount Horeb. But the body is allowed to be hungry so that the devil may be given the occasion to tempt.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.4.3":["Say that these stones become loaves. It is fittingly said to one who is hungry: Say that these stones become loaves. But you are held fast by two contraries, devil: if at his command stones can become loaves, then you tempt in vain one who is of such great power; but if he cannot do it, in vain do you suspect him of being the Son of God: If you are the Son of God, say that these stones become loaves.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.4.4":["Who answering said: It is written: Not by bread alone does man live, but by every word that proceeds from the mouth of God. The testimony is taken from Deuteronomy. And the Lord answered thus because his purpose was to overcome the devil by humility, not by power. At the same time it must be noted that, had the Lord not begun to fast, the devil would have had no occasion to tempt, according to that saying: Son, as you come to the service of God, prepare your soul for temptation. But the Savior's answer itself also shows that it was a man who was tempted: Not by bread alone does man live, but by every word that proceeds from the mouth of God. So if anyone does not feed on the word of God, he does not live.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.4.5":["Again the devil takes him up onto a very high mountain and shows him all the kingdoms of the world and their glory. The glory of the world, which is to pass away with the world, is displayed on a mountain and on a height. But the Lord comes down to low places and plains, so as to overcome the devil by humility. The devil, moreover, hurries to lead him to the mountains, so that through the mountains by which he himself had fallen the rest too may fall, according to that saying of the Apostle: Lest, puffed up, he fall into the judgment of the devil.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.4.6-7":["All these I will give you, if falling down you worship me. Arrogant and proud, he says this too out of boastfulness, not because the devil has power in the whole world so as to be able to give all kingdoms, since we know that very many holy men were made kings by God. If falling down, he says, you worship me. So whoever is going to worship the devil has fallen beforehand.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.4.8":["Then Jesus said to him: Go, Satan; it is written: You shall worship the Lord your God and him alone shall you serve. Satan and the apostle Peter are not, as most think, condemned by the same sentence. For to Peter it is said: Go behind me, Satan, that is, follow me, you who are contrary to my will; but here he hears: go, Satan, and behind is not said to him, so that it is understood: go into the eternal fire that has been prepared for you and your angels.\n— Commentary on Matthew","You shall worship the Lord your God. The devil, saying to the Savior: If falling down you worship me, hears on the contrary that it is rather he himself who ought to worship him as his Lord and God.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.4.9":["Then the devil took him up into the holy city. This taking up that is spoken of does not come from any weakness of the Lord, but from the pride of the enemy, who thinks the Savior's will is necessity. From this passage, moreover, it is understood what that is which is written in another place: They went into the holy city and appeared to many.\n— Commentary on Matthew","He set him on the pinnacle of the temple, so that the one he had tempted by hunger he might tempt also by vainglory.\n— Commentary on Matthew","If you are the Son of God, throw yourself down. In all the temptations the devil works at this: to find out whether he is the Son of God. But the Lord so tempers his answer as to leave him in doubt.\n— Commentary on Matthew","Throw yourself down. The voice of the devil, by which he always desires that all should fall down; Throw yourself, he says; he can persuade, he cannot cast down.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.4.10-11":["He has commanded his angels concerning you, that they bear you up in their hands, lest perhaps you strike your foot against a stone. This we read in Psalm 90 (91). But there the prophecy is not about Christ but about a holy man. So the devil interprets the Scriptures badly. Surely, if he really knew it was written of the Savior, he ought also to have quoted what follows in the same psalm against himself: You shall walk upon the asp and the basilisk, and you shall trample the lion and the dragon. Of the help of the angels he speaks as though to a weak man; of his own trampling, like a shifty evader, he is silent.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.4.12":["Jesus said to him: Again it is written: You shall not tempt the Lord your God. The false arrows of the devil, drawn from the Scriptures, he breaks with the true shields of the Scriptures. And it should be noted that he brought forward the necessary testimonies from Deuteronomy only, to show the mysteries of the second law.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.4.13":["Then the devil left him, and angels came and were ministering to him. The temptation comes first so that victory may follow. Angels minister so that the dignity of the victor may be proven.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.4.18-21":["Hence the Savior too, after he was baptized in the Jordan, when he had come to Nazareth, where he had been brought up, entered their synagogue on the sabbath day, according to custom; and when he had stood up to read, the book of the prophet Isaiah was given to him, and on opening it he found written: The Spirit of the Lord is upon me, because he has anointed me; he has sent me to preach the gospel to the poor, to preach remission to the captives and sight to the blind, to set the broken at liberty, to preach the acceptable year of the Lord. Then he rolled up the book, gave it back to the attendant, and sat down; and the eyes of all who were in the synagogue were fixed on him, and he began to say to them: Today this Scripture has been fulfilled in your ears; and all bore witness to him, and marveled at the words of grace that came forth from his mouth. If, then, the prophecy was fulfilled at that time, how do some refer what is said here to the times of the consummation? Unless perhaps we can say this: that what was then fulfilled in part is to be fulfilled more fully when the whole people of God will be righteous. For now we know in part and we prophesy in part; but when that which is perfect has come, the things that are in part will be done away (1 Cor 13).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"LUK.4.22":["Is not this the carpenter's son? Is not his mother called Mary, and his brothers James and Joseph, Simon and Judas, and his sisters, all of them with us? The error of the Jews is our salvation and the condemnation of the heretics. For they so far saw Jesus Christ as a man that they thought him the son of a carpenter: Is not this the carpenter's son? Do you wonder that they err about the brothers when they err about the father? This passage is set out more fully in the little book Against Helvidius mentioned above.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.4.24":["But Jesus said to them: A prophet is not without honor except in his own country and in his own house. It is almost natural for citizens always to envy their fellow citizens; for they do not consider the man's present works but remember his frail infancy, as though they themselves had not come to mature age through the same stages of life.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.4.32":["And he was teaching them as one having authority, not as the scribes. For they taught the peoples the things that are written in Moses and the prophets, but Jesus, as God and Lord of Moses himself, by the freedom of his own will either added in the law the things that seemed lacking or, changing them, preached to the peoples, as we also read above: It was said to the ancients; but I say to you.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.4.39":["When Jesus had come into Peter's house, he saw his mother-in-law lying ill with fever, and he touched her hand, and the fever left her, and she rose and served them. The woman's hand is touched, and as her works are healed the infirmity of sins flees. Human nature is such that after a fever bodies are the more wearied, and as health begins they feel the ills of the sickness. But the health that is conferred by the Lord restores everything at once; nor is it enough that she was healed, but so that an ἐπίτασις (intensification) of strength might be shown, it is added: and she rose, served them. That hand served which had been both touched and healed.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.4.40":["When evening had come, they brought to him many who had demons, and he cast out the spirits with a word and healed all who were ill. All are healed not in the morning, not at midday, but towards evening, when the sun is about to set, when the grain of wheat dies in the earth that it may bear much fruit.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.5.8":["And behold, the whole city went out to meet Jesus, and when they saw him they asked him to pass from their borders. Those who ask him to pass beyond their borders do this not from pride (as some suppose) but from humility, by which they judged themselves unworthy of the Lord's presence, just as Peter too, at the catch of fish, falling at the Savior's knees, says: Go out from me, Lord, for I am a sinful man.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.5.10":["Come after me, and I will make you become fishers of men. These were the first called to follow the Lord; fishermen and unlettered men are sent to preach, lest the faith of believers be thought to come about not by the power of God but by eloquence and learning.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.5.12":["And when he had come down from the mountain, great crowds followed him. And behold, a leper came and worshiped him. As the Lord comes down from the mountain the crowds meet him, because they had not been able to ascend to the higher places. And the first to meet him is the leper. For with his leprosy he could not yet hear the Savior's so manifold discourse on the mountain. And it must be noted that this one was the first to be healed individually; second, the centurion's servant; third, Peter's mother-in-law, sick with fever, in Capernaum; in the fourth place, those vexed by a demon who were brought to him, whose spirits he cast out with a word, when he also healed all who were ill.\n— Commentary on Matthew","And behold, a leper came and worshiped him, saying. Rightly, after the preaching and teaching, an occasion for a sign is offered, so that through the miracles of mighty works the discourse just past may be confirmed among the hearers.\n— Commentary on Matthew","Lord, if you will, you can cleanse me. He who asks about the will does not doubt the power.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.5.13":["And Jesus, stretching out his hand, touched him, saying: I will, be cleansed. As the Lord stretches out his hand, at once the leprosy flees. At the same time consider how humble and free of boasting the answer is. The one had said: If you will; the Lord answered: I will; the one had put first: you can cleanse me; the Lord joins it and says: be cleansed. So it is not to be joined and read, as most of the Latins think: I will to cleanse, but separately, so that first he says: I will, and then commands: be cleansed.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.5.14":["And Jesus said to him: See that you tell no one. And truly, what need was there for him to boast in words of what he displayed in his body?\n— Commentary on Matthew","But go, show yourself to the priest and offer the gift that Moses commanded, as a testimony to them. He sends him to the priest for various reasons. First, for humility's sake, that he may be seen to defer to the priests. For it was commanded in the law that those who had been cleansed of leprosy should offer gifts to the priests. Next, so that, seeing the leper cleansed, they might either believe the Savior or not believe; if they believed, they would be saved; if they did not believe, they would be made inexcusable; and at the same time, so that he might not seem — a thing they very often charged against him — to break the law.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.5.18-20":["And, as we said above, they brought to him a second paralytic, lying on a little bed, because he himself was not able to walk in. And Jesus, seeing the faith, not of the one who was being brought but of those who were bringing him, said to the paralytic: Take heart, son, your sins are forgiven you. O wonderful humility! He calls son one who was despised and infirm and loosened in all the joints of his limbs, one whom the priests did not deign to touch. Or else he calls him son for this reason, that his sins are forgiven him. According to tropology, sometimes a soul lying in its own body, with all the powers of its limbs loosened, is brought by a perfect teacher to the Lord to be healed; and if it has been healed by his mercy, it receives so much strength that it at once carries its own little bed.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.5.21-22":["And behold, some of the scribes said within themselves: This man blasphemes. We read in the prophet, God saying: I am he who blots out your iniquities. Consistently, therefore, the scribes, because they thought him a man and understood the words of God, accuse him of blasphemy. But the Lord, seeing their thoughts, shows himself to be God, who can know the hidden things of the heart, and, as it were keeping silent, he speaks: By the same majesty and power by which I look upon your thoughts, I can also forgive men their offenses; understand from yourselves what the paralytic obtains.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.5.23":["Which is easier, to say: Your sins are forgiven you; or to say: Rise and walk? Between saying and doing there is a great distance. Whether the sins had been forgiven the paralytic, he alone knew who was forgiving them. Rise, however, and walk could be verified as much by him who was rising as by those who saw him rise. A bodily sign therefore is wrought so that the spiritual one may be proved, although it belongs to the same power to remit the faults both of body and of soul. And we are given to understand that many infirmities of bodies come about on account of sins, and that perhaps for this reason the sins are forgiven first, so that, once the causes of the infirmity have been taken away, health may be restored.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.5.24":["Take up your bed and go into your house. And the paralytic soul too, if it rises, if it recovers its former strength, carries its bed, on which it previously lay loosened, and carries it into the house of its own virtues.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.5.27-28":["And as Jesus was passing on from there, he saw a man sitting at the tax office, Matthew by name, and he said to him: Follow me. And he rose and followed him. The other evangelists, out of modesty and regard for Matthew, did not wish to call him by his common name, but said Levi; for he had a twofold name. But Matthew himself, according to what is enjoined by Solomon: The just man is his own accuser at the beginning of his speech; and in another place: Tell your sins, that you may be justified, names himself Matthew and a tax collector, to show his readers that no one ought to despair of salvation if he has turned to better things, since he himself was suddenly changed from a tax collector into an apostle. In this place Porphyry and Julian the Augustus charge either the ignorance of a lying historian or the folly of those who at once followed the Savior, as though they had irrationally followed any man at all who called them, when so many mighty works and so many signs had gone before, which there is no doubt the apostles saw before they believed. Certainly the very brightness and majesty of the hidden divinity, which shone forth even in his human face, could draw to himself at first sight those who saw him. For if the magnet stone and amber are said to have this power, that they join rings and straw and bits of chaff to themselves, how much more could the Lord of all creatures draw to himself those whom he willed.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.5.29":["And it came to pass, as he reclined at table in the house, behold, many tax collectors and sinners came and reclined with Jesus, and the rest. They had seen that a tax collector converted from sins to better things had found a place of repentance, and on that account they too do not despair of salvation; nor indeed do they come to Jesus remaining in their former vices, as the Pharisees and scribes murmur, but repenting, as the Lord's following word signifies, saying:\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.5.32":["I desire mercy and not sacrifice. I came not to call the just, but sinners. Now the Lord used to go to the banquets of sinners in order to have an occasion of teaching and to offer spiritual food to those who invited him. In short, though he is frequently described as going to banquets, nothing else is reported except what he did there, what he taught, so that both the humility of the Lord in going to sinners and the power of his teaching in the conversion of the penitent might be shown. And as for what follows: I desire mercy and not sacrifice; and: I came not to call the just, but sinners, bringing forward testimony from the prophet he rebukes the scribes and Pharisees, who, esteeming themselves just, shunned the company of sinners and tax collectors.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.5.33":["Then the disciples of John came to him, saying: Why do we and the Pharisees fast often, but your disciples do not fast? A proud question and full of the haughtiness of the Pharisees. Certainly (not to say anything else) the boasting of their fasting is to be reproved. Nor could the disciples of John but be at fault, since they were slandering him whom they knew to have been proclaimed by their master's voice, and were joining themselves to the Pharisees, whom they knew had been condemned by John: Brood of vipers, who showed you to flee from the wrath to come?\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.5.34-35":["Jesus said to them: Can the sons of the bridegroom mourn as long as the bridegroom is with them? But the days will come when the bridegroom will be taken away from them, and then they will fast. The bridegroom is Christ, the bride is the Church. From this holy and spiritual marriage the apostles were begotten, who cannot mourn as long as they see the bride in the bridal chamber and know that the bridegroom is with the bride. But when the wedding has passed and the time of the passion and resurrection has come, then the sons of the bridegroom will fast. Some think that for this reason fasts ought to be entered upon forty days after the passion, although the day of Pentecost at once, and the Holy Spirit's coming, indicate festivity to us; and taking occasion from this testimony, Montanus, Prisca and Maximilla keep a forty-day fast even after Pentecost, on the ground that, when the bridegroom has been taken away, the sons of the bridegroom ought to fast. But the custom of the Church comes to the passion and resurrection of the Lord through humility of the flesh, so that by the fasting of the body we may be prepared for the spiritual banquet. By the laws of tropology, however, we must know that as long as the bridegroom is with us and we are in gladness, we can neither fast nor mourn; but when on account of our sins he has withdrawn from us and flown away, then a fast must be proclaimed, then mourning must be taken up.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.5.36-38":["No one puts a patch of unworked cloth on an old garment; for it takes its fullness away from the garment, and a worse tear is made. Nor do they put new wine into old wineskins; otherwise the wineskins will burst, and the wine will be poured out, and the wineskins will perish; but they put new wine into new wineskins, and both are preserved. What he says is this: until someone has been reborn and, having put off the old man, has put on the new man through my passion, he cannot bear the stricter fasts and the precepts of continence, lest through excessive severity he lose even the belief that he now seems to have. He set down two examples, then, of the garment and of the wineskins, old and new. By the old we ought to understand the scribes and Pharisees. By the patch of the new garment and the new wine are to be understood the precepts of the Gospel, which the Jews cannot bear, lest a greater tear be made. Some such thing the Galatians too were eager to do, to mix the precepts of the law with the Gospel and to put new wine into old wineskins. But the Apostle says to them: O foolish Galatians, who has bewitched you not to obey the truth? The word of the Gospel, therefore, is to be poured into the apostles rather than into the scribes and Pharisees, who, corrupted by the traditions of their elders, could not keep the purity of the precepts of Christ. For the purity of a virginal soul, polluted by no contagion of former vice, is one thing, and the filth of one who has lain subject to the lust of many is another.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.6.1":["At that time Jesus went through the sown fields on the Sabbath, and his disciples, being hungry, began to pluck ears of grain and to eat. As we read also in another evangelist, because of the excessive importunity they did not even have room to eat, and so, being men, they were hungry. But that they rub the ears of the crops in their hands and so relieve their want of food is a sign of a more austere life, of men who seek not prepared banquets but simple foods.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.6.2":["But the Pharisees, seeing it, said to him: Behold, your disciples are doing what it is not lawful for them to do on the Sabbaths. Note that the first apostles of the Savior destroy the letter of the Sabbath, against the Ebionites, who, though they receive the other apostles, reject Paul as a transgressor of the law.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.6.3-4":["But he said to them: Have you not read what David did when he was hungry, and those who were with him, how he entered the house of God and ate the loaves of the presentation, which it was not lawful for him to eat, nor for those who were with him, but for the priests alone? To refute the false charge of the Pharisees he recalls the old history, when David, fleeing Saul, came to Nob and, received by Ahimelech the priest, asked for food; and he, since he had no common loaves, gave him consecrated ones, which it was not lawful to eat except for the priests and Levites alone, and he asked only this, whether the young men were clean from women; and when David answered, since yesterday and the day before, he did not hesitate to give the loaves, judging it better (as the prophet says: I desire mercy and not sacrifice) to free men from the danger of hunger than to offer sacrifice to God. For the salvation of men is a victim pleasing to God. So the Lord sets this against them and says: If David too is holy, and Ahimelech the high priest is not blamed by you, but both transgressed the command of the law with an acceptable excuse, and hunger is the cause, why do you not approve in the apostles the same hunger that you approve in the others? Although even in this there is a great difference: these rub ears of grain in their hands on the Sabbath; those ate the levitical loaves, and the days of the new moons were added to the solemnity of the Sabbath, on which, being sought at the banquet, he fled from the royal court. Note that neither David nor his young men received the loaves of the presentation before they had answered that they were clean from women.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.6.6":["And when he had passed on from there, he came into their synagogue. And behold, a man having a withered hand. This man, who is healed in the synagogue, is the thirteenth. And it is to be noted that the hand was withered not on the road and out of doors, but in the assembly of the Jews.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.6.7":["And they questioned him, saying: Is it lawful to heal on the Sabbaths? so that they might accuse him. Since he had excused by an acceptable example the destruction of the Sabbath which the Pharisees charged against the disciples, they wish to slander him himself, and they ask whether it is lawful to heal on the Sabbaths, so that, if he did not heal, they might accuse him of cruelty or of weakness, and if he healed, of transgression.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.6.9":["But he said to them: What man of you will there be who has one sheep, and if it falls into a pit on the Sabbaths, will he not take hold of it and lift it out? How much better, then, is a man than a sheep! So it is lawful to do good on the Sabbaths. He so solved the question put to him as to condemn the questioners of avarice. If you, he says, hasten on the Sabbath to pull out a sheep or any other animal that falls into a pit, looking out not for the animal but for your own avarice, how much more ought I to set free a man, who is much better than a sheep!\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.6.10":["Then he said to the man: Stretch out your hand. And he stretched it out, and it was restored to health like the other. In the Gospel which the Nazarenes and the Ebionites use, which we recently translated from the Hebrew language into Greek, and which is called by most the authentic Matthew, this man who has the withered hand is described as a mason, begging for help in words of this kind: I was a mason, seeking my living with my hands; I beg you, Jesus, to restore my health to me, so that I may not shamefully beg for food. Until the coming of the Savior the withered hand was in the synagogue of the Jews, and the works of God were not done in it; after he came upon earth, the right hand was given back in the apostles who believed and was restored to its former work.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.6.11":["But the Pharisees went out and took counsel against him, how they might destroy him. That they lay plots against the Lord, envy is the cause. For what had he done to provoke the Pharisees to kill him? Why, that a man had stretched out his hand. For which of the Pharisees does not stretch out his hand on the Sabbath day, carrying food, holding out a cup, and the other things that are necessary for living? If, then, to stretch out the hand and to lift up food or drink on the Sabbath is no crime, why do they charge in another what they themselves are convicted of doing, especially since this mason carried nothing of the kind, but at the Lord's command only stretched out his hand?\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.6.14-15":["First, Simon, who is called Peter, and Andrew his brother. To set out the order of the apostles and the merit of each belonged to him who searches the secrets of the heart. Simon, surnamed Peter, is written first, to distinguish him from the other Simon, who is called the Cananaean, from Cana, the village of Galilee where the Lord turned water into wine. He also calls James the son of Zebedee, because another James follows as well, the son of Alphaeus. And he joins the apostles together in pairs and yokes. He joins Peter and Andrew, brothers not so much in the flesh as in the spirit; James and John, who, leaving the father of their body, followed the true Father; Philip and Bartholomew; Thomas also and Matthew the tax collector. The other evangelists, in pairing the names, put Matthew first and Thomas after, and do not add the name of tax collector, lest by recalling his former way of life they should seem to disparage the evangelist. But this one, as we said above, both places himself after Thomas and calls himself a tax collector, so that where sin abounded there might superabound grace.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.6.14-16":["Now the names of the twelve apostles are these. A catalogue of the apostles is set down so that those who were going to be false apostles, outside these, might be shut out.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.6.15-16":["Simon the Cananaean. He is the one who in another evangelist is written as zelotes; for Cana is interpreted 'zeal'. The history of the Church relates that the apostle Thaddaeus was sent to Edessa, to Abgar king of Osrhoene; he is called Judas of James by the evangelist Luke, and elsewhere he is named Lebbaeus, which is interpreted 'little heart'; and we must believe that he had three names, just as Simon Peter and the sons of Zebedee, boanerges, were named from the firmness and greatness of their faith. But Judas Scariothes took his name either from the village in which he was born or from the tribe of Issachar, so that by a kind of prophecy he was born to his own condemnation. For Issachar is interpreted 'reward', so that the price of the traitor may be signified.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.6.20":["Blessed are the poor in spirit. This is what we read elsewhere: And he will save the humble in spirit. But lest anyone should think that poverty, which is sometimes borne of necessity, is being proclaimed by the Lord, he added in spirit, so that you would understand humility, not want. Blessed are the poor in spirit, who for the sake of the Holy Spirit are poor by their own will. Hence the Savior too speaks through Isaiah about poor men of this kind: The Lord has anointed me; for this reason he has sent me to preach the Gospel to the poor.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.6.21":["Blessed are those who mourn, for they shall be comforted. The mourning set down here is not for those dead by the common law of nature, but for those dead in sins and vices. Thus Samuel also wept for Saul, because the Lord had repented that he had anointed him king over Israel; thus the apostle Paul too says that he weeps and mourns for those who after fornication and impurity have not repented.\n— Commentary on Matthew","Blessed are those who hunger and thirst for justice. It is not enough for us to want justice unless we suffer hunger for justice, so that under this example we may understand that we are never just enough, but always hunger for the works of justice.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.6.22":["Blessed are you when they curse you and persecute you and say every evil against you, lying. That curse is to be despised, and creates blessedness, which is uttered by the mouth of one who curses falsely. Hence he also specifically defined what the blessed curse is, saying: every curse against you, lying, for my sake. So where Christ is the cause, there a curse is to be desired.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.6.23":["Rejoice and exult. I do not know which of us could fulfill this: that our reputation be torn with reproaches and we exult in the Lord. He who pursues vainglory cannot fulfill it. We ought therefore to rejoice and exult, so that a reward may be prepared for us in the heavens. We read it elegantly written in a certain book: Do not seek glory, and you will not grieve when you are without glory.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.6.27":["But I say to you: Love your enemies, do good to those who hate you. Many, judging the precepts of God by their own weakness and not by the strength of the saints, think that the things commanded are impossible, and say that it is enough for the virtues not to hate one's enemies; but that to love them is to command more than human nature can bear. It must be known, then, that Christ commands not impossible things but perfect ones, which David did toward Saul and Absalom. Stephen the martyr too prayed for the enemies who were stoning him, and Paul longs to be anathema for his persecutors. And these things Jesus both taught and did, saying: Father, forgive them; for they do not know what they are doing.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.6.29":["If anyone strikes you on your right cheek, offer him the other too; and to him who wishes to contend with you in judgment and take your tunic, let him have your cloak as well. The man of the Church is described, the imitator of him who says: Learn from me, for I am meek and humble of heart; and who, struck with a slap, proves his promise: If I have spoken evil, rebuke me; but if well, why do you strike me? David too said something of the kind in the psalm: If I have repaid with evils those who repaid me; and Jeremiah in the Lamentations: It is good for a man when he has sat from his youth; he will give his cheek to the one who strikes him, he will be filled with reproaches. This is against those who think there is one God of the law and another of the Gospel, since meekness is taught both there and here. According to the mystical understanding, when our right cheek is struck we are commanded to offer not the left but the other, that is, the other right one. For the just man has no left. If a heretic strikes us in disputation and wishes to wound a right doctrine, let another testimony from the Scriptures be set against him, and let us offer to the one who strikes us right cheeks succeeding one another, until the anger of the enemy grows weary.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.6.30":["Give to him who asks of you, and do not turn away from him who wishes to borrow from you. If we understand it as said only of almsgiving, in the case of most poor people this cannot stand. And even the rich, if they always give, will not be able always to give. After the good of almsgiving, then, precepts are given to the apostles, that is, to teachers, so that those who have received freely may bestow freely. Money of this kind never runs out, but the more of it is given, the more it is doubled; and though it waters the fields below it, the water of the spring never dries up.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.6.35":["That you may be sons of your Father who is in heaven. If by keeping the precepts of God someone is made a son of God, then he is a son not by nature but by his own choice.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.6.37":["Do not judge, that you may not be judged. If he forbids judging, by what consistency does Paul in Corinth judge the fornicator, and Peter convict Ananias and Sapphira of lying? But from what follows he shows what he forbade, saying: for as you judge, so will it be judged of you. Thus he did not forbid judging but taught it.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.6.39":["Let them alone; they are blind, leaders of the blind. This is what the Apostle had commanded: Avoid a heretical man after a first and a second correction, knowing that such a one is perverted and condemned by himself. In this sense the Savior too commands that the worst teachers be left to their own choice, knowing that they can hardly be drawn to the truth, that they are blind and drag a blind people into error.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.6.41-42":["But why do you see the mote in your brother's eye and do not see the beam in your own eye, and the rest. He speaks of those who, though they are themselves held liable for a mortal crime, do not pardon lesser sins in their brothers, straining out the gnat and swallowing the camel. Rightly, then, these too, as we said above, are called hypocrites for their pretense of justice, who through the beam in their own eye look at the mote in their brother's eye.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.6.43-44":["Either make the tree good and its fruit good, or make the tree bad and its fruit bad. For the tree is known by its fruits. He binds them with a syllogism which the Greeks call ἄφυκτον, and which we can call inescapable, which, when men are questioned, shuts them in on this side and on that and presses them with either horn. If, he says, the devil is evil, he cannot do good works; but if the things you see done are good, it follows that it is not the devil who does them. For it cannot be that good should arise from evil, or evil from good. But what follows,\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.6.43":["However the proper sense of the names stands, this is to be said: that evil things are turned into good, and virtues spring up in place of vices, that is, righteousness in place of iniquity, fortitude in place of rashness, temperance in place of luxury, prudence in place of folly. Let us give examples of our forebears: Matthew and Zacchaeus and the publicans were saliunca, and στοιβή, and useless stalks, and κονύζη of bitter taste and most loathsome smell, saying: My scars have rotted and are corrupted, because of my folly (Ps 37 [38]:6). These, suddenly changed into Apostles, became cypress and fir and myrtle, of the best fragrance and needful for various works. Paul too, the persecutor of the Church, when he heard from the Lord, Saul, Saul, why do you persecute me? It is hard to kick against the goad (Acts 9:4, 5), was a nettle, having the stings of persecution. But when he preached the Gospel in the whole world and could say, We are the good odor of Christ (2 Cor 2:15), he is rightly called a cypress and a myrtle. Harlots and publicans go before the Pharisees into the kingdom of God, and the thief passes from the cross into paradise (Matt 21). Therefore what is said in the Gospel, A good tree cannot bear evil fruits (Luke 6:43; Matt 7:18), by no means refers to a property of nature, as the heretics would have it, but to the choice of the mind. Finally it is added: Either make the tree good, and its fruits good. From this it is clear that each one by his own will makes his soul a good or an evil tree, whose fruits vary.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","A good tree cannot bear bad fruit, nor a bad tree bear good fruit. Let us ask the heretics who say that there are two natures contrary to each other: if, according to their understanding, a good tree can never bear bad fruit, how did Moses, a good tree, sin at the water of contradiction, and David, after Uriah had been killed, lie with Bathsheba, and Peter too at the Lord's passion deny him, saying: I do not know the man? Or by what consistency did Jethro, Moses' father-in-law, a bad tree, who certainly did not believe in the God of Israel, give Moses good counsel; and Achior say something useful to Holofernes; and the comic poet say what the Apostle approved as well said: Very evil conversations corrupt good morals; and when they find nothing to answer, we add that Judas too, once a good tree, bore bad fruit after he betrayed the Savior, and that Paul, a bad tree at the time when he was persecuting the Church of Christ, afterwards bore good fruit, when from a persecutor he was changed into a vessel of election. So long, then, does a good tree not bear bad fruit as its zeal for goodness persists, and a bad tree remains in the fruits of sins as long as it is not converted to repentance. For no one, while remaining in what he was, begins to be what he has not yet begun to be.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.6.45":["The good man out of the good treasure brings forth good things, and the evil man out of the evil treasure brings forth evil things. Either he shows from what kind of treasure the Jews themselves, blaspheming the Lord, bring forth their blasphemies, or else the saying clings to the earlier question: that as a good man cannot bring forth evil things, nor an evil man good things, so Christ cannot do evil works, nor the devil good ones.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.6.46":["Not everyone who says to me: Lord, Lord, will enter into the kingdom of heaven, but he who does the will of my Father who is in heaven. As above he had said that those who had the garment of a good life were not to be received because of the wickedness of their doctrines, so now, conversely, he asserts that credence is not to be given even to those who, though strong in the integrity of the faith, live shamefully and destroy the integrity of their teaching by evil works. For both are necessary for the servants of God, that the work be approved by the word and the word by works. That saying may seem contrary to this sentence: No one can say: Lord Jesus, except in the Holy Spirit. But it is the custom of the Scriptures to take sayings for deeds, as is proved at once in what follows, where those are refuted who boast of knowledge of the Lord without works, and they hear from the Savior: Depart from me, workers of iniquity, I do not know you; and the Apostle speaks in this sense: They profess that they know God, but in deeds they deny him.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.6.48":["And the rain came down, and the floods came, and the winds blew. That rain which strives to overturn the house is the devil; the floods are all the antichrists who think against Christ; the winds are the spiritual wickednesses in the heavenly places.\n— Commentary on Matthew","And it did not fall; for it was founded upon the rock. Upon this rock the Lord founded the Church; from this rock the apostle Peter too received his name; upon a rock of this kind the traces of the serpent are not found; of this the prophet too speaks with confidence: He has set my feet upon the rock, and in another place: The rock is a refuge for the hares, or for the hedgehogs. For the timid animal takes refuge in the hollows of the rock, and the one whose skin is rough and armed all over with darts keeps itself safe by such protection. Hence it is said to Moses too, at the time when he had fled from Egypt and was the Lord's little hare: Stand in the cleft of the rock, and you will see my back parts.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.6.49":["Who built his house upon the sand. The foundation which the Apostle as architect laid is one, our Lord Jesus Christ. Upon this foundation, stable and firm and grounded on its own strong mass, the Church of Christ is built; but upon the sand, which is fluid and cannot be compacted or brought together into one bond, every discourse of the heretics is built, to this end, that it may fall.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.7.6-7":["A centurion came to him, entreating him and saying: Lord, my servant lies at home paralyzed and is grievously tormented. And Jesus said to him: I will come and heal him and the rest. We ought not to accuse the Lord of boasting because he promises at once that he will go and heal, for he saw the centurion's faith, humility and prudence: his faith, in that, being of the Gentiles, he believed that the leper could be healed by the Savior; his humility, in that he judged himself unworthy that the Lord should enter under his roof; his prudence, in that beyond the covering of the body he saw the hidden divinity, knowing that what would profit him was not what was seen even by unbelievers, but what lay hidden within. Of this prudence he also says this:\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.7.8":["Behold, I, though I am a man, say to this one: Go, and he goes, and the rest. He wishes to show that the Lord too could accomplish what he wished not only through the coming of his body but through the ministries of angels.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.7.9":["And Jesus, hearing it, marveled. He marveled because he saw that the centurion understood his majesty. For both the infirmities of bodies and the opposing powers, to which a man is often given over to be disabled, had to be driven out, both by the word of the Lord and by the ministries of angels.\n— Commentary on Matthew","Amen I say to you: I have not found such faith in Israel. He speaks of those present, not of all the patriarchs and prophets of the past, unless perhaps in the centurion the faith of the Gentiles is set above Israel.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.7.18-19":["Now John, when he had heard in chains of the works of Christ, sending two of his disciples, said to him: Are you he who is to come, or do we look for another? He does not ask as one who does not know, for he himself pointed him out to the others who did not know, saying: Behold the Lamb of God, behold him who takes away the sins of the world, and he had heard the voice of the Father thundering: This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased, but just as the Savior asks where Lazarus has been laid, so that those who were showing the place of the tomb might at least in this way be prepared for faith and see the dead man rising, so John too, about to be killed by Herod, sends his disciples to Christ, so that through this occasion, seeing the signs and mighty works, they might believe in him and, while their master asked, learn for themselves. That the disciples of John were proud against the Lord and had something of a biting spirit from spite and envy, the earlier question also showed, as the evangelist relates: Then the disciples of John came to him, saying: Why do we and the Pharisees fast often, but your disciples do not fast? and elsewhere: Master, he to whom you bore witness at the Jordan, behold, his disciples baptize, and more come to him, as if they said: We are deserted, here there is scarcity, to him the crowd runs together.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.7.19":["Are you he who is to come, or do we look for another? He does not say: Are you he who has come, but Are you he who is to come; and the sense is: Send me word, since I am about to go down to the lower world, whether I ought to announce you to those below as well, I who have announced you to those above, or whether it is not fitting for the Son of God to taste death, and you will send another for these mysteries.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.7.22":["Jesus answered and said to them: Go and report to John what you have heard and seen: the blind see, the lame walk, lepers are cleansed, the deaf hear, the dead rise. John had asked through his disciples: Are you he who is to come, or do we look for another? Christ points to signs, answering not what he had been asked but the stumbling block of the messengers: Go, he says, and tell John the signs that you see, the blind seeing, the lame walking, and the rest, and what is no less than these: the poor have the Gospel preached to them; either the poor in spirit or at any rate the poor in wealth, so that in the preaching there is no difference between the noble and the lowborn, between the rich and the needy. These things prove the rigor of the master, these the truth of the teacher, since with him everyone who can be saved is equal. But as for what he says:\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.7.23":["And blessed is he who has not been scandalized in me, he strikes at the go-betweens, as will be shown in what follows.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.7.24-25":["When they had gone away, Jesus began to say to the crowds about John: What did you go out into the desert for? To see a reed shaken by the wind? But what did you go out for? To see a man clothed in soft garments? and the rest. If the previous sentence had been uttered against John, as most people think, in his saying: Blessed is he who has not been scandalized in me, how is John now proclaimed with such praises? But it is because the crowd standing around did not know the mystery of the question and thought that John was in doubt about Christ, whom he himself had pointed out: so that they might understand that John had asked not for himself but for his disciples. What, he says, did you go out into the desert for? Was it for this, that you might see a man like a reed, who is carried about by every wind and, from lightness of mind, would be in doubt about the one whom he had earlier proclaimed? Or perhaps he is driven by the goads of envy against me, and his preaching pursues vain glory, so that he may seek gain from it? Why should he desire riches so as to abound in feasts? He feeds on locusts and wild honey. Or was it to be clothed in soft garments? Camels' hair is his covering. Food and clothing of this kind are received into the lodging of a prison, and the preaching of truth has such a dwelling. But those who are flatterers and pursue gain and seek riches and abound in delights and are clothed in soft garments, they are in the houses of kings. From this it is shown that a rigorous life and austere preaching ought to avoid the courts of kings and shun the palaces of soft men.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.7.26-27":["But what did you go out for? To see a prophet? Yes, I tell you, and more than a prophet. John is greater than the other prophets in this, that the one whom they had proclaimed was to come, he pointed out with his finger as having come, saying: Behold the Lamb of God, behold him who takes away the sins of the world. And because to the prophetic privilege there was added also the Baptist's reward, that he should baptize his own Lord, he therefore brings in an αὔξησιν (increase) of his merits, producing the testimony from Malachi in which he is even proclaimed an angel. But let us not think that John is called an angel here by a sharing of nature, but by the dignity of his office, that is, a messenger, because he announced the Lord who was to come.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.7.28":["Amen I say to you: There has not arisen among those born of women one greater than John the Baptist. Among those born, he says, of women. He is therefore preferred to those men who were born of women and of union with a man, and not to him who was born of a virgin and the Holy Spirit. And yet in saying: There has not arisen among those born of women one greater than John the Baptist, he did not prefer John to the other prophets and patriarchs and to all men, but made the others equal to John. For it does not straightway follow that, if others are not greater than he, he is greater than the others, but rather that he has equality with the other saints.\n— Commentary on Matthew","But he who is less in the kingdom of heaven is greater than he. Many want this to be understood of the Savior: that he who is less in time is greater in dignity. But let us understand simply that every saint who is already with God is greater than he who still stands in the battle. For it is one thing to possess the crown of victory, another still to fight in the battle line. Some want to take the lowest angel ministering to the Lord in heaven as better than any foremost man who lives on earth.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.7.31-32":["But to what shall I liken this generation? It is like children sitting in the forum, who call out to their companions and say: We sang to you, and you did not dance; we lamented to you, and you did not mourn. For John came neither eating nor drinking, and they say: He has a demon. The Son of Man came eating and drinking, and they say: Look, a gluttonous man and a drinker of wine, a friend of tax collectors and sinners. And wisdom has been justified by her children. To children sitting in the forum and calling out and saying to their companions: We sang to you, and you did not dance; we lamented, and you did not mourn, the generation of the Jews is compared, as Scripture says: What shall I reckon this generation to be like? It is like children sitting in the forum, and the rest. We are not, therefore, granted a free understanding and an allegorical interpretation that ranges at will, but whatever we are going to say about the children must be referred to the likeness of the generation. These children who sit in the forum are those of whom Isaiah speaks: Behold, I and my children whom God has given me; and in Psalm 18 (19): The testimony of God is faithful, giving wisdom to little ones; and elsewhere: Out of the mouth of infants and sucklings you have perfected praise. These children, then, sat in the forum, or ἐν ἀγορᾷ, as it is said more expressively in Greek, where many things are for sale. And because the people of the Jews would not listen, they did not merely speak to it but cried out with full throats: We sang to you, and you did not dance: we urged you to do good works to our song and to dance to our pipe, as David too danced before the ark of the Lord, and you would not; we have lamented, and we urged you to repentance, and you would not do even this, spurning both kinds of preaching, both the exhortation to virtues and the repentance after sins.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.7.33-35":["Nor is it surprising if you have despised the twofold way of salvation, when you have spurned fasting and fullness alike. If fasting pleases you, why did John displease you? If fullness, why the Son of Man? Of these you called the one a man with a demon, the other a glutton and a drunkard. Therefore, because you would not receive either discipline, wisdom has been justified by her children, that is, the dispensation and the doctrine of God; and I, who am the power of God and the wisdom of God, have been proved by my apostles, my children, to have acted justly, to whom the Father has revealed what he had hidden from the wise and from those who are prudent in their own eyes. In certain Gospels it reads: Wisdom has been justified by her works. For wisdom does not seek the testimony of the voice but of works.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.7.37-38":["A woman came to him having an alabaster of precious ointment, and she poured it over his head as he reclined. Let no one think that she who poured ointment over the head is the same as she who poured it over the feet. For the latter both washes with tears and wipes with her hair and is openly called a harlot, whereas of this one nothing of the kind is written. Nor indeed could a harlot at once become worthy of the Lord's head. Another evangelist, in place of the alabaster of precious ointment (which is a kind of marble), put pistic nard, that is, true and without guile, to show the faith of the Church and of the Gentiles.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.7.47-50":["Let that harlot from the Gospel supply an example of the faith which works through love: when, in the house of the Pharisee, she had washed with her tears the feet of the Lord as he reclined at table, wiped them with her hair, and anointed them with ointment, and the Lord had put to the murmuring Pharisee the parable of the debtors of fifty and five hundred denarii, he added: \"Wherefore I say to you: Her many sins are forgiven her, because she loved much\" (Luke 7:47 and 50). And turning to the woman herself, he said: \"Your faith has made you safe: go in peace\". For in this place it is openly shown that this woman had faith working through love, which availed much in Christ.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"LUK.8.4":["And he spoke many things to them in parables, saying. The crowd is not of one mind but of wills differing in each person. Hence he speaks to it in many parables, so that according to their various wills they might receive different teachings. And it should be noted that he did not speak all things to them in parables, but many. For if he had said everything in parables, the people would have gone away without profit. He mixes the plain with the obscure, so that through the things they understand they may be roused to a knowledge of the things they do not understand.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.8.5-8":["Some fell beside the way, and the birds came and ate them; and others fell on rocky places, where they did not have much earth, and the rest. Valentinus takes up this parable to prove his heresy, bringing in the notion that there are three natures, the spiritual, the animal and the earthly, whereas here there are four: one beside the way, another rocky, the third full of thorns, the fourth good ground. We put off its interpretation for a little, wishing with the disciples to hear in secret what is said:\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.8.5":["Behold, he who sows went out to sow. He was within, he was staying at home, he was speaking mysteries to the disciples. So he who sows the word of God went out of his house to sow among the crowds. Now it is signified that this sower who sows is the Son of God and of the Father, sowing the word among the peoples. And at the same time note that this is the first parable that has been set down with its own interpretation; and we must beware, wherever the Lord expounds his own sayings and, when asked by the disciples, discourses within, lest we wish to understand anything else, or anything more or less, than has been expounded by him.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.8.8":["He who has ears to hear, let him hear. We are roused to the understanding of what is said whenever we are admonished by these words.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.8.9":["And the disciples came and said to him. It must be asked how the disciples come to him when Jesus is sitting in the ship, unless perhaps we are given to understand that some time before they had gone up into the ship with him and, standing there, had inquired about the interpretation of the parable.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.8.10":["Therefore I speak to them in parables, because seeing they do not see and hearing they do not hear. He says these things of those who stand on the shore and are separated from Jesus, and, with the sound of the waves roaring, do not hear clearly what is said, and in them is fulfilled the prophecy of Isaiah: By hearing you shall hear and shall not understand, and seeing you shall see and shall not see. These things were prophesied of the crowds that stand on the shore and do not deserve to hear the word of God. Let us therefore also come to Jesus with the disciples and ask him for the explanation of the parable, lest with the crowds we seem to have ears and eyes in vain.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.8.12":["Everyone who hears the word of the kingdom and does not understand. Putting this first, he urges us to listen more diligently to what is said.\n— Commentary on Matthew","The evil one comes and snatches what has been sown in his heart. The evil one snatches the good seed. And at the same time understand that it was sown in the heart, and that the different kinds of ground are the souls of believers.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.8.13":["But when tribulation and persecution have come because of the word, he is at once scandalized. Pay attention to what is said: he is at once scandalized. There is therefore some difference between the one who is compelled by many tribulations and punishments to deny Christ and the one who at the first persecution is immediately scandalized and falls.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.8.14":["But he who sows among thorns, this is he who hears the word, and the care of this world and the deceitfulness of riches chokes the word, and it is made fruitless. It seems to me that what is said to Adam according to the letter, Among thorns and thistles you shall eat your bread, also signifies mystically that whoever gives himself to the pleasures of the world and the cares of this world eats the heavenly bread and the true food among thorns. And he aptly added: the deceitfulness of riches chokes the word. For riches are alluring, doing one thing and promising another. Their possession is slippery, since they are carried about hither and thither, and with unsteady step they either desert those who have them or stuff those who do not have them. Hence the Lord also asserts that the rich enter the kingdom of heaven with difficulty, since riches choke the word of God and soften the rigor of the virtues.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.8.15":["But he who was sown in good ground, this is he who hears the word and understands and bears fruit. Just as in the bad ground there were three different kinds, the wayside and the rocky and the thorny places, so in the good ground there is a threefold diversity: of the hundredfold, the sixtyfold and the thirtyfold fruit. And in the one as in the other it is not the substance that is changed but the will, and it is the hearts of unbelievers as well as of believers that receive the seed. The evil one comes, he says, and snatches what has been sown in his heart, and in the second and third place: this is he, he says, who hears the word. In the exposition of the good ground too, this is he who hears the word. First, therefore, we must hear, then understand, and after understanding render the fruits of the teachings and produce either the hundredfold fruit or the sixtyfold and thirtyfold; of these we spoke more fully in the book Against Jovinian, and now we touch on them briefly, assigning the hundredfold fruit to virgins, the sixtyfold to widows and the continent, the thirtyfold to holy matrimony. Honorable indeed is marriage, and the bed undefiled. Some of our writers refer the hundredfold fruit to the martyrs; but if that is so, the holy partnerships of marriage are shut out from the good fruit.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.8.16":["You are the light of the world. A city set upon a mountain cannot be hidden, nor do they light a lamp and put it under a bushel, and the rest. He teaches boldness in preaching, so that the apostles may not hide themselves out of fear and be like a lamp under a bushel, but may come forward with all freedom, so as to preach on the housetops what they have heard in the chambers.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.8.18":["He who has, it will be given to him, and he will abound; but he who does not have, even what he has will be taken from him. It is not by an equality of judgment that something is added to those who have, and that from those who do not have even what they seem to have is taken away; rather, to the apostles, who have faith in Christ, something is granted, even if they have somewhat fewer virtues, while from the Jews, who did not believe in the Son of God, it is taken away, even if they possess something through the good of nature. For those who do not have the head of wisdom cannot understand anything wisely.\n— Commentary on Matthew","For to everyone who has will be given, and he will abound; but from him who does not have, even what he seems to have will be taken away from him. Many, though they are wise by nature and have keenness of mind, if they have been negligent and have spoiled the good of nature by sloth, in comparison with one who is somewhat slower but has made up by labor and diligence for what he had less of, lose the good of nature and see the reward that had been promised them pass to others. It can also be understood thus: to him who has faith and a good will in the Lord, even if, being a man, he has had something less in works, it will be given by the good judge; but he who has not had faith will lose even the other virtues that he seemed to possess by nature. And elegantly too: even what he seems, he says, to have will be taken away from him. For whatever is without faith in Christ ought not to be credited to him who has badly misused it, but to him who granted the good of nature even to a wicked servant.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.8.19-20":["While he was still speaking to the crowds, behold, his mother and his brothers were standing outside, seeking to speak to him. And someone said to him: Behold, your mother and your brothers are standing outside seeking you. The Lord was occupied in the work of the word, in the teaching of the peoples, in the duty of preaching; his mother and brothers come and stand outside and desire to speak to him. Then someone announces to the Savior that his mother and brothers are standing outside seeking him. It seems to me that this man who brings the news does not announce it by chance and in simplicity, but is laying a trap, to see whether he would prefer flesh and blood to the spiritual work. Hence the Lord too scorned to go out, not as though he were denying his mother and brothers, but so as to answer the one laying the trap:\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.8.19-21":["Stretching out his hand toward his disciples he said: Behold my mother and my brothers. These are my mother, who daily bring me to birth in the souls of believers; these are my brothers, who do the works of my Father. So he did not, as Marcion and Mani hold, deny his mother, so as to be thought born of a phantasm, but he preferred the apostles to kinship, so that we too, in weighing our love, may prefer the spirit to the flesh. Behold, your mother and your brothers are standing outside seeking you. Some suspect that the brothers of the Lord are sons of Joseph by another wife, following the ravings of the apocrypha and inventing a certain little woman Escha. But we, as is contained in the book which we wrote Against Helvidius, understand the brothers of the Lord to be not sons of Joseph but cousins of the Savior, the children of Mary, the Lord's maternal aunt, who is said to be the mother of James the less, and of Joses and Jude, whom in another place of the Gospel we read called the brothers of the Lord. And all Scripture shows that cousins are called brothers. Let us also speak in another way. The Savior speaks to the crowds; inside he instructs the nations. His mother and brothers, that is, the synagogue and the people of the Jews, stand outside and desire to enter, and become unworthy of his discourse; and when they have asked and sought and sent a messenger, they receive the answer that they are of free will and can enter, if they too were willing to believe; yet they will not be able to enter unless they have asked others.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.8.22":["And as he went up into the boat his disciples followed him, and the rest. The fifth sign he did when, going up into the ship from Capernaum, he commanded the winds and the sea; the sixth, when in the country of the Gerasenes he gave the demons power over the swine; the seventh, when, entering his own city, he healed the second paralytic on his bed; for the first paralytic is the centurion's servant.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.8.23-24":["But he was sleeping. And they came and woke him, saying: Lord, save us. The type of this sign we read in Jonah, when, while the others are in peril, he himself is untroubled and sleeps, and is woken, and by the command and mystery of his passion frees those who woke him.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.8.24":["Then rising he rebuked the winds and the sea. And from this place we understand that all creatures perceive the Creator. For the things that are rebuked and commanded perceive the one who commands, not by the error of the heretics, who think all things are animate, but by the majesty of the Maker, to whom things insensible to us are sensible.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.8.25":["Moreover the men marveled, saying: What sort of man is this, that the winds and the sea obey him. It was not the disciples but the sailors and the others who were in the ship who marveled. But if anyone should contentiously maintain that those who marveled were the disciples, we shall answer that they were rightly called men, because they did not yet know the power of the Savior.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.8.28":["What have we to do with you, Son of God? Have you come here before the time to torment us? This confession is not one of will, on which the reward of confessing follows, but an extortion of necessity, which compels the unwilling; just as runaway slaves, if after a long time they see their master, beg for nothing else but to be spared the lash, so too the demons, seeing the Lord suddenly dwelling on earth, believed that he had come to judge them. The presence of the Savior is the torment of the demons. And some ridiculously think that the demons know the Son of God and the devil does not, because these are of less malice than he whose henchmen they are. Whereas all the knowledge of the disciples is to be referred to the master, and both the demons and the devil are to be understood rather to suspect the Son of God than to know him. For no one knows the Father except the Son and him to whom the Son wills to reveal him.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.8.32-34":["If you cast us out, send us into the herd of swine. And he said to them: Go. The Savior did not say: Go, as granting the demons what they asked, but so that through the killing of the swine an occasion of salvation might be offered to men. For the herdsmen, seeing these things, at once report them to the city. Let the Manichee blush. If the souls of men and of beasts are of the same substance and from the same author, how are two thousand swine drowned for the salvation of one man?\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.8.37":["And behold, the whole city went out to meet Jesus, and when they saw him they asked him to pass from their borders. Those who ask him to pass beyond their borders do this not from pride (as some suppose) but from humility, by which they judged themselves unworthy of the Lord's presence, just as Peter too, at the catch of fish, falling at the Savior's knees, says: Go out from me, Lord, for I am a sinful man.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.8.41-42":["Behold, a certain ruler came and worshiped him, saying: My daughter has just now died; but come, lay your hand upon her, and she will live. And Jesus rose and followed him, and his disciples. This is the eighth sign, in which the ruler asks that his daughter be raised, not wishing to be shut out from the mystery of the true circumcision; but the woman with a flow of blood slips in and is healed in the eighth place, so that the ruler's daughter, shut out from this number, comes to the ninth, according to what is said in the psalms: Ethiopia shall go before with her hands to God; and: When the fullness of the Gentiles has come in, then all Israel will be saved.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.8.42-44":["Behold, a woman who had suffered a flow of blood for twelve years came up behind and touched the fringe of his garment. In the Gospel according to Luke it is written that the ruler's daughter was twelve years of age. Note, therefore, that this woman, that is, the people of the Gentiles, began to be sick at the time when the nation of the Jews believed. For a vice is not shown except by comparison with virtues. Now this woman with a flow of blood approaches the Lord not in a house, not in a city, because according to the law she was excluded from cities, but on the way, as the Lord was walking, so that while he was going to one woman, another might be healed. Whence the apostles too say: It was necessary that the word be preached to you, but since you have judged yourselves unworthy of salvation, we turn to the Gentiles.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.8.48":["Take heart, daughter, your faith has made you well. Daughter, for this reason, that your faith has made you well. Nor did he say: Your faith will make you well, but has made you well; for in that you believed, you have already been made well.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.8.52":["And when Jesus had come into the ruler's house and had seen the flute players and the crowd in an uproar. To this day the girl lies dead in the ruler's house, and those who seem to be teachers are flute players singing a mournful song. The crowd of the Jews, too, is not a crowd of believers but a crowd of people in an uproar.\n— Commentary on Matthew","The girl is not dead, but sleeps, because all things live to God.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.8.53-54":["And when the crowd had been put out, he went in and took her by the hand. For they were not worthy to see the mystery of her rising, who mocked him that was raising her with unworthy insults.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.8.54-55":["And he took her by the hand, and the girl arose. Unless the hands of the Jews, which are full of blood, are first cleansed, their dead synagogue does not rise.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.9.1":["And having called together his twelve disciples, he gave them power over unclean spirits, to cast them out and to heal every sickness and every infirmity. The kind and merciful Lord and Master does not begrudge his servants and disciples his own mighty works, and just as he himself had healed every sickness and every infirmity, so he grants to his apostles too the power to heal every sickness and every infirmity. But there is a great difference between having and granting, between giving and receiving. Whatever he does, he does by the power of the Lord; they, if they do anything, confess their own weakness and the power of the Lord, saying: In the name of Jesus, rise and walk. Note, moreover, that it is in the twelfth place that the power of signs is granted to the apostles.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.9.2":["And going, preach, saying that the kingdom of heaven has drawn near; heal the sick, raise the dead, cleanse the lepers, cast out demons; freely you have received, freely give. So that rustic men, without the charm of eloquence, unlearned and unlettered, should not go unbelieved when they promised the kingdoms of heaven, he gives them power: heal the sick, cleanse the lepers, cast out demons, so that the greatness of the signs might prove the greatness of the promises.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.9.3":["Do not possess gold or silver or money in your belts, no bag for the road, not two tunics, no shoes, nor a staff in the hand; for the laborer is worthy of his food. Consistently he gave these commands to the preachers of the truth, to whom he had said before: Freely you have received, freely give. For if they preach in such a way that they accept no price, the possession of gold and silver and coins is superfluous. For if they had had gold and silver, they would have seemed to preach not for the sake of men's salvation but for the sake of gain. Nor bronze in purses. He who had cut off riches cuts away almost even the necessities of life, so that the apostles, teachers of the true religion, who taught that all things are governed by providence, might show that they themselves took no thought for the morrow.\n— Commentary on Matthew","No bag for the road. By this precept he refutes the philosophers who are commonly called bactroperites, because, though despisers of the world who count all things as nothing, they carried their storeroom about with them. Nor two tunics. By the two tunics he seems to me to indicate a double garment; not that in the regions of Scythia, stiff with glacial snow, anyone ought to be content with a single tunic, but that by the tunic we should understand clothing, lest, being clothed in one, we keep another for ourselves out of fear of what is to come. Nor of shoes. Plato too prescribes that the two extremities of the body should not be covered, and that one ought not to grow used to softness of the head and the feet; for when these have firmness, the rest are sturdier. Nor a staff. We who have the help of the Lord, why should we seek the protection of a stick? And because he had sent the apostles to preach as it were naked and unencumbered, and the condition of the teachers seemed to be hard, he tempered the severity of the precept with the sentence that follows, saying: The laborer is worthy of his food. Take only so much, he says, as is necessary for you in food and clothing. Hence the Apostle too repeats: Having food and clothing, with these we are content, and in another place: Let him who is catechized share with him who catechizes him in every good thing, so that disciples may make those whose spiritual things they reap sharers in their own carnal things, not for avarice but for necessity.\n— Commentary on Matthew","These things we have said according to history. But according to anagogy, teachers are not permitted to possess the gold and silver and money that is in the belts. We often read gold for the understanding, silver for speech, bronze for the voice. These we may not receive from others, but must possess them as given by the Lord. Nor may we take up the disciplines of heretics and philosophers and of perverse doctrine, nor be weighed down by the weight of the world, nor be double-minded, nor have our feet bound with deadly bonds, but, entering the holy ground, we must go barefoot, and not have a staff that is turned into a serpent, nor lean on any protection of the flesh, because a staff and stick of this kind is of reed, which, if you press on it a little, breaks and pierces the hand of the one leaning on it.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.9.4":["Into whatever city or village you enter, inquire who in it is worthy, and stay there until you leave. Concerning the ordination of a bishop and a deacon Paul says: Moreover they must also have a good testimony from those who are outside. The apostles, entering a new city, could not know who was of what sort. Therefore the host is to be chosen by the report of the people and the judgment of the neighbors, lest the dignity of the preaching be disfigured by the ill repute of the one who receives them. Though they ought to preach to all, one host is chosen, not conferring a benefit on him who is going to stay with him, but receiving one. For the words who in it is worthy are said so that he may know that he is receiving grace rather than giving it.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.9.5":["And whoever does not receive you nor hear your words, going outside, out of the house or the city, shake off the dust from your feet. The dust is shaken from the feet as a testimony of their labor, that they entered the city and the apostolic preaching reached even to them; or else the dust is shaken off so that they may accept nothing, not even what is necessary for food, from those who have spurned the Gospel.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.9.7":["At that time Herod the tetrarch heard the report of Jesus and said to his servants: This is John the Baptist; he has risen from the dead, and therefore mighty works are at work in him. A certain one of the Church's interpreters asks the reasons why Herod suspected this, so as to think that John had risen from the dead and that therefore mighty works were at work in him; as though we had to render an account of another's error, or as though the sect of μετεμψυχώσεως (transmigration of souls) could take occasion from these words, when in any case, at the time when John was beheaded, the Lord was thirty years old, whereas μετεμψύχωσις (transmigration of souls) says that souls are introduced into different bodies after many cycles of years.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.9.10":["When Jesus had heard this, he withdrew from there in a boat to a desert place apart. They announce the Baptist's death to the Savior; having heard of it, he withdraws to a desert place; not, as some think, out of fear of death, but sparing his enemies, lest they add murder to murder; or else putting off his own death to the day of the Passover, on which, because of the mystery, the lamb is to be sacrificed and the doorposts of the believers are to be sprinkled with blood. Or else he withdrew for this reason, to give us an example of avoiding the rashness of those who hand themselves over of their own accord, because not all persevere in torments with the same constancy with which they offer themselves to be tortured. For this reason he also commands in another place: When they persecute you in this city, flee into another. Elegantly too the evangelist does not say, he fled to a desert place, but he withdrew, so that he avoided his persecutors rather than feared them. Alternatively. After the head of the prophet had been cut off by the Jews and by the king of the Jews, and prophecy had lost its tongue and voice among them, Jesus passed over to the desert place of the Church, which before had not had a husband.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.9.11":["And when the crowds had heard, they followed him on foot from the cities. He may also, for another reason, having heard of John's death, have withdrawn into a desert place: to test the faith of the believers. In short, the crowds followed him on foot, not on beasts of burden, not in various vehicles, but by the labor of their own feet, to show the ardor of their minds. If we wish to lay open the reasons of every single word, we exceed the brevity of the work we have undertaken. Yet it must be said in passing that after the Lord had come into the desert, very great crowds followed him; for before he came into the solitudes of the Gentiles, he was worshiped by one people only.\n— Commentary on Matthew","And going out he saw a great crowd. In the words of the Gospel the spirit is always joined to the letter, and whatever seems cold at first sight is warm if you touch it. The Lord was in a desert place; the crowds followed him, leaving their cities, that is, their former ways of life and the variety of doctrines. But Jesus' coming out signifies that the crowds had indeed the will to go, but did not have the strength to arrive; therefore the Savior comes out from his place and goes to meet them, as also in another parable the penitent son; and seeing the crowd he has pity and heals their sicknesses, so that full faith may at once obtain its reward.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.9.12":["But when evening had come, his disciples came to him, saying, and the rest. All things are full of mysteries. He withdraws from Judea, he comes into a desert place, the crowds follow him, having left their cities, Jesus comes out to them, he has pity on the crowd, he heals their sick; and he does this not in the morning, not as the day grows, not at midday, but in the evening, when the sun of justice had set.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.9.13":["But Jesus said to them: They have no need to go; you give them to eat. They have no need to seek various foods and to buy themselves unknown loaves, when they have the heavenly bread with them. You give them to eat. He challenges the apostles to the breaking of bread, so that, when they testify that they have none, the greatness of the sign may become better known.\n— Commentary on Matthew","They answered him: We have nothing here but five loaves and two fishes. In another evangelist we read: There is a certain boy here who has five loaves, and he seems to me to signify Moses; the two fishes we understand either as both Testaments, or because an even number refers to the law. So the apostles, before the passion of the Savior and the flashing forth of the lightning-bright Gospel, had nothing but five loaves and two little fishes, which moved about in salt waters and in bitter waves.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.9.14":["And when he had ordered the crowd to recline on the grass. According to the letter the sense is plain; let us lay open the mysteries of the spiritual interpretation. They are ordered to recline on the grass, and according to another evangelist on the ground, by fifties or by hundreds, so that, after they have trodden down their flesh and have put under themselves all its flowers and the pleasures of the world like withered grass, they may then, through the repentance of the number fifty, ascend to the perfect summit of the number one hundred.\n— Commentary on Matthew","Now the number of those who ate was five thousand men, besides women and little children. In keeping with the number of the five loaves, the multitude of the men who ate is also five thousand. For it had not yet come, as in the narrative of another passage, to the number seven, of which those who eat are four thousand, a number close to that of the Gospels. Now five thousand men eat, men who had grown to the perfect man and were following him of whom Zechariah says: Behold a man, the Rising is his name. But women and little children, the frail sex and the lesser age, are unworthy of being numbered. Hence too in the book of Numbers, whenever the priests and the Levites and the army or the bands of fighting men are registered, slaves and women and little children and the undistinguished common people are passed over without number.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.9.16":["Taking the five loaves and the two fishes, looking up to heaven, he blessed and broke and gave the loaves to the disciples. He looks up to heaven to teach that our eyes must be directed there. He took the five loaves and the two little fishes into his hands, and broke them, and handed them to the disciples. As the Lord breaks them, a seedbed of food comes into being. For if they had stayed whole, and had not been torn into pieces nor divided into a manifold harvest, they could not have fed the crowds, and the children, and the women, and so great a multitude. So the law together with the prophets is broken and torn into pieces, and its mysteries are brought out into the open, so that what did not nourish while it was whole and remained in its former state might, divided into parts, nourish the multitude of the Gentiles.\n— Commentary on Matthew","And he gave the loaves to the disciples, and the disciples to the crowds. The crowds receive nourishment from the Lord through the apostles.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.9.17":["And they took up of the remnants twelve baskets full of fragments. Each of the apostles fills his basket from the Savior's remnants, either so that he may have from it something with which afterwards to offer food to the Gentiles, or so that he may teach from the remnants that those were real loaves which were afterwards multiplied. And at the same time ask how, in the desert and in so vast a solitude, no loaves are found but five only, and two little fishes, and yet twelve baskets are found so easily.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.9.18":["And he asked his disciples, saying: Whom do men say that the Son of Man is? He did not say: Whom do men say that I am, but the Son of Man, lest he seem to inquire boastfully about himself. And note that wherever son of man is written in the Old Testament, in the Hebrew son of Adam is set down; and that which we read in the psalm: Sons of men, how long will you be heavy of heart? in the Hebrew is said sons of Adam. Beautifully, moreover, he asks: Whom do men say that the Son of Man is, because those who speak of the Son of Man are men; but those who understand his divinity are called not men but gods.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.9.19":["And they said: Some John the Baptist, others Elijah, but others Jeremiah or one of the prophets. I wonder at certain interpreters who inquire into the causes of each one's errors and weave a very long discussion of why some thought our Lord Jesus Christ to be John, others Elijah, but others Jeremiah or one of the prophets, when they could have erred thus about Elijah and Jeremiah in the same way that Herod erred about John, saying: John, whom I beheaded, has himself risen from the dead, and mighty works are at work in him.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.9.20":["But whom do you say that I am? Simon Peter answered: You are the Christ, the Son of the living God. Prudent reader, observe that from what follows and from the texture of the discourse the apostles are by no means called men but gods. For when he had said: Whom do men say that the Son of Man is, he added: But whom do you say that I am? Since they, being men, think human things, you who are gods, whom do you consider me to be? Peter, in the person of all the apostles, professes: You are the Christ, the Son of the living God. He calls him the living God by comparison with those gods who are thought to be gods but are dead, meaning Saturn, Jupiter, Ceres, Liber, Hercules and the other monstrosities of idols.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.9.21":["Then he commanded his disciples that they should tell no one that he himself was Jesus Christ. Above, when sending the disciples to preach, he had ordered them to announce his coming; here he commands them not to say that he is Jesus Christ. It seems to me that it is one thing to preach Christ, another to preach Jesus Christ. Christ is a common name of dignity; Jesus is the proper name of the Savior. But it may be that he did not wish to be preached before the passion and resurrection for this reason, that, when the mystery of the blood had afterward been completed, he might more fittingly say to the apostles: Go, teach all nations, and the rest. And lest anyone think that this is only our own understanding and not the sense of the Gospel, what follows sets out the causes of the preaching forbidden at that time.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.9.22":["From then on Jesus began to show his disciples that he must go to Jerusalem and suffer many things from the elders and scribes and chief priests, and be killed, and on the third day rise again. Now the sense is: Preach me then, when I have suffered these things, because it does no good to preach Christ publicly and to spread abroad among the peoples the majesty of him whom a little later they are to see scourged and crucified, suffering many things from the elders and scribes and chief priests. And now Jesus suffers many things from those who crucify the Son of God for themselves again, and who, though they are regarded in the Church as elders and chief priests, following the simple letter, kill the Son of God, who is perceived wholly in the spirit.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.9.23":["There is also a certain praiseworthy denial, of which the Apostle himself says: \"That, denying impiety and worldly desires, we may live purely and justly and piously in this present age, awaiting the blessed hope and the coming of our Savior God\" (Titus 2:12). Whoever denies with this denial, and is willing to follow the Savior when he says, \"Whoever will come to me, let him deny himself\" (Luke 9:23), having put off the old man with his works and put on the new, will follow his God. But how one denies himself must be considered. The chaste man denies the fornicator that he formerly was; the wise man the imprudent, the just man the unjust, the brave man the weak. And, to speak of all in common, we deny ourselves as often as, trampling on our former vices, we cease to be what we were and begin to be what we were not before.\n— Commentary on Titus","And he who does not take up his cross and follow me is not worthy of me. In another Gospel it is written: He who has not taken up his cross daily. Lest we think that ardor of faith shown once can suffice, the cross must always be carried, so that we may show that we always love Christ.\n— Commentary on Matthew","Then Jesus said to his disciples: If anyone wishes to come after me, let him deny himself and take up his cross and follow me, and the rest. He who puts off the old man with his deeds denies himself, saying: But I live, now not I, but Christ lives in me, and he takes up his cross and is crucified to the world. And he to whom the world is crucified follows the crucified Lord.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.9.26":["For the Son of Man is to come in the glory of his Father with his angels, and then he will render to each according to his work. Peter, scandalized at the announcement of the Lord's death, had been rebuked by the Lord's judgment; the disciples were challenged to deny themselves and take up their cross and follow their master in the spirit of men going to their death. Great was the terror of the hearers, and such as could, with the chief of the apostles terrified, cast fear into the others too. Therefore glad things succeed the sad, and he says: The Son of Man is to come in the glory of his Father with his angels. You fear death: hear of the glory of the one triumphing; you dread the cross: listen to the ministries of the angels. And then, he says, he will render to each according to his works. There is no distinction of Jew and heathen, of man and woman, of poor and rich, where not persons but works are considered.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.9.27":["Amen I say to you: There are some of those standing here who shall not taste death until they see the Son of Man coming in his kingdom. He had wished to heal the terror of the apostles with the hope of what was promised, saying: The Son of Man is to come in the glory of his Father with his angels; adding besides the authority of the judge: And he will render to each according to his works. The silent thought of the apostles could have suffered a stumbling block of this sort: slaughter and death you say are coming now; but what you promise, that you will be present in the glory of the Father with the ministries of the angels and the power of a judge, this will be for days to come and is put off to long ages. Foreseeing, then, what they could object, the present fear, the knower of hidden things compensates it with a present reward. For what does he say? There are some of those standing here who shall not taste death until they see the Son of Man coming in his kingdom, so that what he is to be like when he comes afterward may, because of your unbelief, be shown in the present time.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.9.28":["And after six days Jesus took Peter and James and John his brother. Why Peter and James and John are separated from the rest in certain places of the Gospels, or what privilege they have beyond the other apostles, we have often said. Now it is asked how he took them after six days and led them up a high mountain apart, when Luke the evangelist puts the number eight. But the answer is easy: here the middle days are given; there the first and the last are added. For it is not said: After eight days Jesus takes Peter and James and John, but on the eighth day.\n— Commentary on Matthew","And he leads them up a high mountain apart. To lead the disciples up to the mountains is part of the kingdom. They are led apart because many are called, few chosen.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.9.29":["And he was transfigured before them. Such as he is to be at the time of judging, such he appeared to the apostles. But as to his saying: he was transfigured before them, let no one think that he lost his former form and face, or gave up the reality of his body and took on a body either spiritual or airy; but how he was transformed the evangelist shows, saying:\n\nAnd his face shone like the sun, and his garments became white as snow. Where the splendor of the face is shown and the whiteness of the garments is described, the substance is not taken away, but the glory is changed. His face shone like the sun. Certainly the Lord was transformed into that glory with which he is to come afterward in his kingdom. The transformation added splendor; it did not take away the face. Grant that the body was spiritual: were the garments also changed, which were so white that another evangelist said: Such as no fuller on earth can make? But what a fuller on earth can make is bodily and subject to touch, not spiritual and airy, a thing that deceives the eyes and is seen only as a phantasm.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.9.30-31":["And behold, there appeared to them Moses and Elijah, talking with him. When the scribes and Pharisees were testing him and demanding signs from heaven, he would not give them, but refuted the perverse demand with a prudent answer. Here, however, to increase the faith of the apostles, he gives a sign from heaven, Elijah coming down from the place to which he had ascended, and Moses rising from the underworld; as Ahaz too is instructed through Isaiah to ask for himself a sign from on high or from hell. For as to what was said: There appeared to them Moses and Elijah, talking with him, it is also reported in another Gospel that they told him what he was to suffer at Jerusalem. The Law and the Prophets are shown, which in frequent utterances announced both the passion and the resurrection of the Lord.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.9.33":["But Peter answered and said to Jesus: Lord, it is good for us to be here. Because he had climbed up to the mountains, he does not want to go down to earthly things, but to remain always on the heights.\n— Commentary on Matthew","If you wish, I will make here three tabernacles, one for you, and one for Moses, and one for Elijah. You err, Peter, as another evangelist also testifies: you do not know what you are saying. Do not seek three tabernacles, when there is one tabernacle of the Gospel, in which the Law and the Prophets are to be summed up. But if you seek three tabernacles, by no means compare the servants with the Lord. Rather, make three tabernacles, or rather one, for the Father and the Son and the Holy Spirit, so that those whose divinity is one may also have one tabernacle in your breast.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.9.34-35":["While he was still speaking, behold, a bright cloud overshadowed them, and a voice from the cloud, saying: This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased; hear him. Because he had asked imprudently, he therefore does not merit the Lord's answer, but the Father answered for the Son, so that the word of the Lord might be fulfilled: I do not bear witness for myself, but the Father who sent me, he bears witness for me. And the cloud is seen to be bright and overshadows them, so that those who were seeking a fleshly tabernacle of boughs or tents might be covered by the shelter of the bright cloud. The voice too of the Father speaking from heaven is heard, to bear witness to the Son and, removing Peter's error, to teach him the truth, or rather, through Peter, the other apostles. This is, he says, my beloved Son: for him a tabernacle is to be made, him you must obey; this is my Son; they are servants; Moses and Elijah ought themselves, with you, to prepare a tabernacle for the Lord in the inner recesses of their heart.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.9.34":["And the disciples, hearing it, fell on their face and were exceedingly afraid. They are struck with dread for a threefold cause: either because they had recognized that they had erred, or because the bright cloud had covered them, or because they had heard the voice of God the Father speaking; human frailty does not endure to bear the sight of a greater glory, and, trembling in its whole soul and body, it falls to the earth. The more anyone seeks greater things, the more he collapses to the lower, if he is ignorant of his own measure.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.9.36":["And lifting up their eyes they saw no one but Jesus alone. Reasonably, after they had risen they saw no one but Jesus alone, lest, if Moses and Elijah had remained with the Lord, the Father's voice might seem uncertain as to whom above all it bore witness. They see, then, Jesus standing, the cloud taken away, and Moses and Elijah vanished; because after the shadow of the Law and the Prophets, which had covered the apostles with its veil, has departed, both are found in the Gospel.\n— Commentary on Matthew","And as they were coming down from the mountain, Jesus commanded them, saying: Tell the vision to no one until the Son of Man rises from the dead. A foretaste of the kingdom to come and the glory of the one triumphing had been shown on the mountain. He does not wish, therefore, that this be preached among the peoples, lest it both be incredible because of the greatness of the thing and, after such great glory, the cross that followed should cause a stumbling block among untaught minds.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.9.38-39":["Lord, have mercy on my son, for he is a lunatic and suffers badly; for he often falls into the fire, and frequently into the water; and I brought him to your disciples, and they could not cure him. For what reason a demon, watching the course of the moon, seizes men and strives through the creatures to defame the Creator, we have said above. But it seems to me that according to tropology the lunatic is he who is changed toward vices from moment to moment of the hours and does not persist in what he has begun, but waxes and wanes, and is now carried into the fire with which the hearts of adulterers are kindled, now into the waters, which cannot extinguish charity.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.9.40":["But as to his saying: I brought him to your disciples, and they could not cure him, he covertly accuses the apostles, although the impossibility of curing is sometimes to be referred not to the weakness of those who cure but to the faith of those who are to be cured, as the Lord says: Be it done to you according to your faith.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.9.41":["Jesus answered and said: O unbelieving and perverse generation, how long shall I be with you? How long shall I bear with you? Not that he was overcome by weariness, and that he who is meek and gentle, who like a lamb before its shearer did not open his mouth, broke out into words of fury; but that, in the likeness of a physician who sees a sick man conducting himself against his orders, he might say: How long shall I come to your house, how long shall I waste the diligence of my art, when I order one thing and you do another? So little, moreover, is he angry with the man rather than with the vice, and through one man he convicts the Jews of unbelief, that he immediately added: Bring him here to me.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.9.42":["And Jesus rebuked him, and the demon went out of him. Not he who was suffering but the demon ought to have been rebuked. Or else he rebuked the boy, and the demon went out of him, because he had been oppressed by the demon on account of his own sins.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.9.44":["And while they were staying in Galilee, Jesus said to them: The Son of Man is to be betrayed into the hands of men, and they will kill him, and on the third day he will rise again. And they were exceedingly saddened. He always mingles sadness with prosperous things, so that when these come suddenly they may not terrify the apostles, but may be borne by minds that have prepared for them beforehand. For if it saddens them that he is to be killed, what is said ought to gladden them: on the third day he will rise again. Moreover, that they are saddened, and saddened exceedingly, does not come from unbelief, for otherwise they also knew that Peter had been rebuked because he did not savor the things that are of God but those of men; but because, out of love for their master, they cannot bear to hear anything adverse and lowly about him.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.9.46":["At that hour the disciples came to Jesus, saying: Who, do you think, is greater in the kingdom of heaven? What I have often advised must be observed now too: the causes of each of the Lord's sayings and deeds are to be sought. After the stater was found, after the tribute was paid, what does this sudden question of the apostles mean? At that hour the disciples came to Jesus, saying: Who, do you think, is greater in the kingdom of heaven? Because they had seen the same tribute paid for Peter and for the Lord, they judged from the equality of the price that Peter had been preferred to all the apostles, since in the payment of the tribute he had been put on a level with the Lord; therefore they ask who is greater in the kingdom of heaven. And Jesus, seeing their thoughts and understanding the causes of their error, wishes to heal the desire for glory by a contest of humility.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.9.47":["And Jesus, calling a little child, set him in their midst. Either simply any little child, so that he might seek out that age and show the likeness of innocence; or else he set himself in their midst as the little child, he who had come not to be served but to serve, so that he might give them an example of humility. Others interpret the little child as the Holy Spirit, whom he placed in the hearts of the disciples so that they might exchange pride for humility.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.9.48":["Whoever therefore humbles himself like this little child, he is greater in the kingdom of heaven. Just as this little child, whose example I give you, does not persist in anger, does not remember when he has been hurt, is not delighted at the sight of a beautiful woman, does not think one thing and say another, so you too, unless you have such innocence and purity of mind, will not be able to enter the kingdom of heaven. Or alternatively: Whoever humbles himself like this little child, he is greater in the kingdom of heaven, that is, whoever imitates me and humbles himself after my example, so as to cast himself down as far as I cast myself down when I took the form of a servant, he will enter the kingdom of heaven.\n— Commentary on Matthew","And whoever receives one such little child in my name receives me. Whoever is such that he imitates the humility and innocence of Christ, in him Christ is received. And prudently, lest the apostles, when honor has been paid to them, should think themselves the ones honored, he added that they are to be received not for their own merit but in honor of their master.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.9.57-58":["And a scribe came up and said to him: Master, I will follow you wherever you go. And Jesus says to him: Foxes have holes and the birds of the air nests, but the Son of Man has nowhere to lay his head. This scribe of the law, who knew only the letter that kills, if he had said: Lord, I will follow you wherever you go, would not have been rejected by the Lord; but because he reckoned him one master among many, and was a man of letters (which is more expressively said in Greek γραμματεύς, and not a spiritual hearer), therefore Jesus has no place where he can lay his head. It is shown to us, moreover, that the scribe was rejected for this reason too, that, seeing the greatness of the signs, he wished to follow the Savior in order to seek gain from the miracles of his works, desiring the very thing that Simon Magus too had wished to buy from Peter. Such faith, then, is justly condemned by the Lord's sentence, and it is said to him: Why do you desire to follow me for riches and the gains of the world, when I am of such poverty that I do not even have a little lodging and do not use a roof of my own?\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.9.59":["And another of his disciples said to him: Lord, let me first go and bury my father. What likeness is there between the scribe and the disciple? The one calls him master, the other confesses him Lord; the one, on the occasion of piety, desires to go to bury his father, the other promises that he will follow wherever he goes, seeking not the master but gain from the master.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.9.60":["Jesus said to him: Follow me, and leave the dead to bury their own dead. Whoever does not believe is dead. But if the dead buries the dead, we ought to have care not for the dead but for the living, lest, while we are anxious about the dead, we too be called dead.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.10.2":["The harvest indeed is great, but the laborers are few. The great harvest signifies the multitude of the peoples, the few laborers the scarcity of teachers. And he commands them to ask the Lord of the harvest to send out laborers into his harvest. These are the laborers of whom the psalmist speaks: Those who sow in tears shall reap in joy; going they went and wept, carrying their seeds, but coming they shall come with exultation, carrying their sheaves. And to speak more plainly: the great harvest is the whole crowd of believers; the few laborers are the apostles and their imitators, who are sent to the harvest.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.10.3":["Behold, I send you as sheep in the midst of wolves. He calls the scribes and Pharisees, who are the clergy of the Jews, wolves.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.10.4":["Do not possess gold or silver or money in your belts, no bag for the road, not two tunics, no shoes, nor a staff in the hand; for the laborer is worthy of his food. Consistently he gave these commands to the preachers of the truth, to whom he had said before: Freely you have received, freely give. For if they preach in such a way that they accept no price, the possession of gold and silver and coins is superfluous. For if they had had gold and silver, they would have seemed to preach not for the sake of men's salvation but for the sake of gain. Nor bronze in purses. He who had cut off riches cuts away almost even the necessities of life, so that the apostles, teachers of the true religion, who taught that all things are governed by providence, might show that they themselves took no thought for the morrow.\n— Commentary on Matthew","No bag for the road. By this precept he refutes the philosophers who are commonly called bactroperites, because, though despisers of the world who count all things as nothing, they carried their storeroom about with them. Nor two tunics. By the two tunics he seems to me to indicate a double garment; not that in the regions of Scythia, stiff with glacial snow, anyone ought to be content with a single tunic, but that by the tunic we should understand clothing, lest, being clothed in one, we keep another for ourselves out of fear of what is to come. Nor of shoes. Plato too prescribes that the two extremities of the body should not be covered, and that one ought not to grow used to softness of the head and the feet; for when these have firmness, the rest are sturdier. Nor a staff. We who have the help of the Lord, why should we seek the protection of a stick? And because he had sent the apostles to preach as it were naked and unencumbered, and the condition of the teachers seemed to be hard, he tempered the severity of the precept with the sentence that follows, saying: The laborer is worthy of his food. Take only so much, he says, as is necessary for you in food and clothing. Hence the Apostle too repeats: Having food and clothing, with these we are content, and in another place: Let him who is catechized share with him who catechizes him in every good thing, so that disciples may make those whose spiritual things they reap sharers in their own carnal things, not for avarice but for necessity.\n— Commentary on Matthew","These things we have said according to history. But according to anagogy, teachers are not permitted to possess the gold and silver and money that is in the belts. We often read gold for the understanding, silver for speech, bronze for the voice. These we may not receive from others, but must possess them as given by the Lord. Nor may we take up the disciplines of heretics and philosophers and of perverse doctrine, nor be weighed down by the weight of the world, nor be double-minded, nor have our feet bound with deadly bonds, but, entering the holy ground, we must go barefoot, and not have a staff that is turned into a serpent, nor lean on any protection of the flesh, because a staff and stick of this kind is of reed, which, if you press on it a little, breaks and pierces the hand of the one leaning on it.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.10.5-6":["And entering into the house, greet it; and if the house is worthy, your peace will come upon it; but if it is not worthy, your peace will return to you. He has expressed, in a hidden way, the greeting of the Hebrew and Syriac language. For what in Greek is said χαῖρε and in Latin haue, this in the Hebrew and Syriac language is called salom lach or salama lach, that is, peace be with you. What he commanded, then, is this: entering a house, call down peace upon your host, and, as far as lies in you, calm the wars of discord; but if contradiction arises, you will have the reward of the peace you offered, and they will possess the war they wished to have.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.10.7":["No bag for the road. By this precept he refutes the philosophers who are commonly called bactroperites, because, though despisers of the world who count all things as nothing, they carried their storeroom about with them. Nor two tunics. By the two tunics he seems to me to indicate a double garment; not that in the regions of Scythia, stiff with glacial snow, anyone ought to be content with a single tunic, but that by the tunic we should understand clothing, lest, being clothed in one, we keep another for ourselves out of fear of what is to come. Nor of shoes. Plato too prescribes that the two extremities of the body should not be covered, and that one ought not to grow used to softness of the head and the feet; for when these have firmness, the rest are sturdier. Nor a staff. We who have the help of the Lord, why should we seek the protection of a stick? And because he had sent the apostles to preach as it were naked and unencumbered, and the condition of the teachers seemed to be hard, he tempered the severity of the precept with the sentence that follows, saying: The laborer is worthy of his food. Take only so much, he says, as is necessary for you in food and clothing. Hence the Apostle too repeats: Having food and clothing, with these we are content, and in another place: Let him who is catechized share with him who catechizes him in every good thing, so that disciples may make those whose spiritual things they reap sharers in their own carnal things, not for avarice but for necessity.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.10.9":["And going, preach, saying that the kingdom of heaven has drawn near; heal the sick, raise the dead, cleanse the lepers, cast out demons; freely you have received, freely give. So that rustic men, without the charm of eloquence, unlearned and unlettered, should not go unbelieved when they promised the kingdoms of heaven, he gives them power: heal the sick, cleanse the lepers, cast out demons, so that the greatness of the signs might prove the greatness of the promises.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.10.10-11":["And whoever does not receive you nor hear your words, going outside, out of the house or the city, shake off the dust from your feet. The dust is shaken from the feet as a testimony of their labor, that they entered the city and the apostolic preaching reached even to them; or else the dust is shaken off so that they may accept nothing, not even what is necessary for food, from those who have spurned the Gospel.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.10.12":["Amen I say to you: It will be more tolerable for the land of Sodom and Gomorrah in the day of judgment than for that city. If it will be more tolerable for the land of Sodom and Gomorrah than for that city which has not received the Gospel, and more tolerable for this reason, that it was not preached to Sodom and Gomorrah, but it has been preached to this city and yet it has not received the Gospel, then there are different punishments even among sinners.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.10.13-14":["Woe to you, Chorazin; woe to you, Bethsaida; for if the mighty works that have been done in you had been done in Tyre and Sidon, they would long ago have repented in sackcloth and ashes. Nevertheless I say to you: it will be more tolerable for Tyre and Sidon on the day of judgment than for you. Chorazin and Bethsaida, cities of Galilee, are lamented by the Savior because after so many signs and mighty works they did not repent, and Tyre and Sidon, cities given over to idolatry and vices, are preferred to them. And they are preferred for this reason, that Tyre and Sidon had trampled only the natural law, whereas these, after transgressing the natural and the written law, also made light of the signs that had been done among them. We ask where it is written that the Lord did signs in Chorazin and Bethsaida. Above we read: And he went about all the cities and villages, healing every infirmity, and the rest. Among the other cities and little villages, therefore, we must suppose that the Lord did signs in Chorazin and Bethsaida too.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.10.13":["For if the mighty works that have been done in you had been done in Sodom, perhaps it would have remained until this day. The prudent reader may ask and say: If Tyre and Sidon and Sodom could have repented at the preaching of the Savior and the miracles of his signs, they are not at fault for not having believed; rather, the fault of silence lies in him who would not preach to those who would have repented. To this the answer is easy and plain: we do not know the judgments of God and are ignorant of the mysteries of each of his dispensations. The Lord had purposed not to go beyond the borders of Judea, so as not to give the Pharisees and the priests a just occasion for persecution. Hence he also commanded the apostles before the passion: Do not go off into the way of the Gentiles, and do not enter the cities of the Samaritans. Chorazin and Bethsaida, therefore, are condemned because they would not believe the Lord when he was present; Tyre and Sidon are justified because they believed his apostles. Do not ask about the times when you behold the salvation of believers. But in Capernaum, whose name is interpreted most beautiful village, unbelieving Jerusalem is condemned, to which it is said through Ezekiel: Sodom has been justified by you.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.10.15":["And you, Capernaum, will you be exalted even to heaven? You will go down even to hell. In another copy we have found: And you, Capernaum, who have been exalted even to heaven, you will go down even to the lower regions, and the understanding is twofold: either you will go down to the lower regions because you resisted my preaching most proudly, or because, having been exalted even to heaven by my lodging with you and by my signs and mighty works, possessing so great a privilege, you are struck with greater punishments, since you would not believe even these.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.10.16":["Whoever receives you receives me, and whoever receives me receives him who sent me. A most beautiful order. He sends them to preach, teaches that dangers are not to be feared, subordinates affection to religion; he had taken away gold above, he had shaken bronze out of the belt. Hard is the condition of the evangelists. Whence, then, their expenses, whence the necessities of food? He tempers the austerity of the commands with the hope of the promises: Whoever receives, he says, you receives me, and whoever receives me receives him who sent me, so that in receiving the apostles each of the believers might think that he had received Christ.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.10.18-20":["For Lucifer, who in Hebrew is called ELIL (הילל), Aquila translated howling son of the dawn. For truly he ought to howl and wail, who because of his pride was cast headlong from heaven to earth and crushed. Hence the Savior too says to the disciples: I saw Satan falling like lightning from heaven (Luke 10:18). Not, I see now; but I saw before, when he fell. And if he fell from so great a height because of pride, you too ought not to glory because the demons are subject to you, but because your names are written in heaven; so that whence he fell through pride, you may ascend through humility.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"LUK.10.18":["Moreover, as to what is brought in, For evils have come down from the Lord upon the gates of Jerusalem, the Marcionites and the Manichaeans too use this text, on the ground that the God of the law is the author of evils. Let us say that evils came down from the Lord in the way that the Savior also speaks in the Gospel: I saw Satan like lightning falling from heaven. For as there Lucifer, who rose in the morning, fell from heaven and was crushed upon the earth, he who used to send to the nations, so also these evils, which fell from the Lord and came to the gates of Jerusalem, were not evils before they fell, but because they fell headlong from the Lord, for that reason they became evils.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"LUK.10.21":["At that time Jesus answered and said: I confess to you, Father, Lord of heaven and earth. Confession does not always signify repentance but also thanksgiving, as we very often read in the psalms. Let those hear who slanderously claim that the Savior was not born but created, that he calls him his Father, but Lord of heaven and earth. For if he too is a creature, and a creature can call its maker Father, it was foolish not to call him likewise Lord or Father both of himself and of heaven and earth.\n— Commentary on Matthew","Because you have hidden these things from the wise and prudent and have revealed them to little ones. He gives thanks and exults in the Father because he has opened to the apostles the mysteries of his coming, of which the scribes and Pharisees were ignorant, who seem wise to themselves and prudent in their own sight. Wisdom has been justified by her children.\n— Commentary on Matthew","Yes, Father, for so it was pleasing before you. He speaks to the Father with the feeling of one who coaxes, that the kindness begun toward the apostles may be completed.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.10.22":["All things have been handed over to me by my Father. Understand mystically both the Father who hands over and the Son who receives. Otherwise, if we choose to think according to our frailty: when he who received begins to have, he who gave will begin not to have. And by the all things handed over to him are to be understood not heaven and earth and the elements and the other things that he himself made and founded, but those who through the Son have access to the Father and, rebels before, afterwards began to perceive God.\n— Commentary on Matthew","And no one knows the Son except the Father, nor does anyone know the Father except the Son and he to whom the Son wishes to reveal him. Let Eunomius blush, who boasts that he has as much knowledge of the Father and the Son as they have of each other. But suppose he argues from this and consoles his madness because there follows: and he to whom the Son wishes to reveal him. It is one thing to know what one knows by equality of nature, another by the condescension of one who reveals.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.10.23":["But blessed are your eyes, because they see, and your ears, because they hear. Had we not read above that the hearers were roused to understanding when the Savior said, He who has ears to hear, let him hear, we would think that the eyes and ears which now receive the beatitude are to be understood as those of the flesh. But to me those eyes seem blessed which can know the mysteries of Christ, and which Jesus commanded to be lifted up on high so that they might behold the white harvest fields; and those ears blessed of which Isaiah speaks: The Lord has given me an ear.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.10.24":["Amen indeed I say to you that many prophets and just men desired to see what you see, and did not see it, and to hear what you hear, and did not hear it. There seems to be, contrary to this passage, what is said elsewhere: Abraham longed to see my day, and he saw it and rejoiced. But he did not say: all prophets and just men desired to see what you see, but many. Among many it may be that some saw and others did not see, although in this too the interpretation is dangerous, since we may seem to make some distinction or other among the merits of the saints. Therefore Abraham saw in an enigma, saw in a form; but you hold and have your Lord in the present, and you question him at your will, and you eat with him.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.11.2":["Our Father, who are in heaven. By saying Father they confess themselves sons.\n— Commentary on Matthew","Hallowed be your name, not in you but in us. For if because of sinners the name of God is blasphemed among the Gentiles, conversely because of the just it is hallowed.\n— Commentary on Matthew","Your kingdom come. He asks either generally for the kingdom of the whole world, that the devil may cease to reign in the world, or that God may reign in each one, and that sin may not reign in the mortal body of men. At the same time this too must be noted, that it belongs to great boldness and a pure conscience to ask for the kingdom of God and not to fear the judgment.\n— Commentary on Matthew","Your will be done, as in heaven, also on earth, that as the angels serve you blamelessly in the heavens, so men may serve you on earth. Let those blush at this sentence who falsely claim that falls take place daily in heaven. For what does likeness to the heavens profit us, if there is sin in heaven too?\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.11.3":["Our supersubstantial bread give us today. What we have expressed as supersubstantial is in the Greek ἐπιούσιον, the word which the LXX translators very often rendered περιούσιον (special). We looked, therefore, in the Hebrew, and wherever they had expressed περιούσιον (special), we found sogolla, which Symmachus translated ἐξαίρετον, that is, choice or excellent, although in one place he rendered it peculiar. When therefore we ask God to grant us the peculiar or choice bread, we ask for that bread which says: I am the bread that came down from heaven. In the Gospel that is called according to the Hebrews, for supersubstantial bread I found maar, which means of tomorrow, so that the sense is: Our bread of tomorrow, that is, of the future, give us today. We can also understand the supersubstantial bread in another way, as that which is above all substances and surpasses all creatures. Others think simply, following the word of the Apostle who says: Having food and clothing, with these we are content, that the saints take care only for present food; whence it is also commanded later on: Do not think about tomorrow.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.11.9-10":["Ask and it will be given to you, seek and you will find, knock and it will be opened to you. He who above had forbidden fleshly things to be asked for shows what we ought to seek. If it is given to him who asks, and he who seeks finds, and to him who knocks it will be opened, then of him to whom it is not given, and who does not find, and to whom it will not be opened, it is plain that he has not asked, sought or knocked well. Let us knock, therefore, at the door of Christ, of which it is said: This is the gate of the Lord, the just shall enter by it, so that when we have entered there may be opened to us the hidden and dark treasures in Christ Jesus, in whom is all knowledge.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.11.13":["If you then, though you are evil, know how to give good things to your children. It must be noted that he called the apostles evil, unless perhaps under the person of the apostles the whole human race is condemned, whose heart, in comparison with the divine clemency, is set on evil from infancy. Read Genesis. Nor is it strange that the men of this world are called evil, since the Apostle Paul too recalls: Redeeming the time, because the days are evil.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.11.14":["Then there was brought to him one who had a demon, blind and mute, and he healed him, so that he spoke and saw. Three signs were performed at once in one man: the blind man sees, the mute man speaks, the man possessed by a demon is set free. This was then indeed done in the flesh, but it is also fulfilled daily in the lives of believers, so that, once the demon has been driven out, they first behold the light of faith, and then mouths that were formerly silent are loosed in the praises of God.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.11.16":["Then some of the scribes and Pharisees answered him, saying: We want to see a sign from you. They ask for a sign as though the signs they had seen were not signs. But in another evangelist what they ask for is set out more fully: We want to see a sign from you from heaven, they desired either that fire should come down from on high in the manner of Elijah, or that, after the likeness of Samuel, in the summertime thunder should roar, lightning flash and rains pour down; as if they could not slander those things too, and say that they had happened from hidden and various affections of the air. For you who slander the things you see with your eyes, hold in your hand and feel in their benefit, what will you do with things that come from heaven? Surely you will answer that the magicians in Egypt too did many signs from heaven.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.11.17-18":["If Satan casts out Satan, he is divided against himself; how then will his kingdom stand? A kingdom and a city divided against itself cannot endure; but as small things grow by concord, so the greatest fall apart by discord. If, then, Satan fights against himself, and demon is the enemy of demon, the consummation of the world ought already to have come, and the adverse powers would have no place in it, whose war among themselves is the peace of men. But if you think, O scribes and Pharisees, that the withdrawal of the demons is obedience to their prince, so that by a fraudulent pretense they may delude ignorant men, what can you say of the healings of bodies which the Lord performed? It is another matter if you ascribe to the demons the infirmities of the limbs too, and the signal marks of spiritual mighty works.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.11.17":["But Jesus, knowing their thoughts, said to them: Every kingdom divided within itself will be laid waste. The crowds were amazed and confessed that he who did such great signs was the son of David; but the Pharisees ascribed the works of God to the prince of the demons; and the Lord answered them not according to what they said but according to what they thought, so that even thus they might be compelled to believe in the power of him who saw the hidden things of the heart.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.11.19":["If I cast out demons by Beelzebub, by whom do your sons cast them out? Therefore they shall be your judges. By the sons of the Jews he means either, after the custom, the exorcists of that nation, or the apostles, born of their stock. If the exorcists, who used to cast out demons at the invocation of God, he constrains them with a prudent question, so that they confess it to be the work of the Holy Spirit. If, he says, the expulsion of demons by your sons is ascribed to God and not to demons, why should the same work in me not also have the same cause? Therefore they shall be your judges, not by power but by comparison, since they ascribe the expulsion of demons to God, and you to Beelzebub, the prince of the demons. But if it was said of the apostles, which is rather what we ought to understand, they will be their judges because they will sit on twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Israel.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.11.20":["But if I cast out demons by the Spirit of God, then the kingdom of God has come upon you. In Luke we read this passage written thus: But if I cast out demons by the finger of God. This is the finger which even the magicians, who performed signs against Moses and Aaron, confess, saying: This is the finger of God, the finger by which the tablets of stone were written on Mount Sinai. If, then, the hand and the arm of God is the Son, and his finger the Holy Spirit, the substance of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit is one; let not the inequality of the members cause you to stumble, since the unity of the body edifies. The kingdom of God has come upon you: he means either himself, of whom it is written in another place: The kingdom of God is within you, and: In your midst stands one whom you do not know, or else that kingdom which both John and the Lord himself had preached: Repent, for the kingdom of heaven has drawn near. There is also a third kingdom, that of holy Scripture, which is taken away from the Jews and given to a nation that produces its fruits.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.11.21-22":["Or how can anyone enter the house of the strong man and plunder his vessels, unless he first binds the strong man, and then he will plunder his house? We must not be complacent: our adversary is shown to be strong even by the words of the victor; his house is the world, which is set in the evil one, not by the dignity of the Creator but by the greatness of the offender; his vessels we once were. The strong man has been bound and fastened down in Tartarus and crushed by the foot of the Lord, and, the seats of the tyrant having been plundered, captivity has been led captive.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.11.23":["He who is not with me is against me, and he who does not gather with me scatters. Let no one think that this was said of heretics and schismatics, although it can also be understood so, as something over and above; but from what follows and from the texture of the discourse it is referred to the devil, in that the works of the Savior cannot be compared with the works of Beelzebub: he desires to hold the souls of men captive, the Lord to set them free; he preaches idols, this one the knowledge of the one God; he draws men to vices, this one calls them back to virtues. How then can those whose works are opposite have concord between them?\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.11.24-26":["But when the unclean spirit has gone out of a man, it walks through dry places seeking rest and does not find it, and the rest. Some think this passage was said of heretics: that the unclean spirit which had formerly dwelt in them when they were Gentiles is cast out at the confession of the true faith, but that afterwards, when they have gone over to heresy and adorned their house with feigned virtues, then the devil, having joined to himself seven other wicked spirits, returns to them and dwells in them, and their last state becomes worse than the first; for heretics are in a much worse condition than Gentiles, because in the latter there is hope of faith, in the former the fight of discord. Although this interpretation displays a certain appeal and color of learning, I do not know whether it has the truth. For from the fact that, once the parable or example is finished, there follows: So shall it be also with this most wicked generation, we are compelled to refer the parable not to heretics and to any men whatever but to the people of the Jews, so that the context of the passage may not drift loosely and vaguely in different directions and be thrown into confusion in the manner of the foolish, but, cohering with itself, may answer either to what precedes or to what follows. The unclean spirit went out from the Jews when they received the law, and it walked through dry places seeking rest for itself. Driven out, that is, from the Jews, it walked through the solitudes of the Gentiles; and when these afterwards believed in the Lord, it, finding no place among the nations, said:\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.11.24":["I will return to my former house from which I went out, I go off to the Jews whom I had formerly left.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.11.25-26":["And coming, it finds the house vacant, swept with brooms. For the temple of the Jews was vacant and did not have Christ as its guest, who says: Rise, let us go from here; and in another place: Your house will be left to you deserted. Since, therefore, they had neither the protection of God nor that of the angels, and were adorned with the superfluous observances of the law and the traditions of the Pharisees, the devil returns to his former seat and, adding to himself the sevenfold number of demons, inhabits his former house, and the later things of that people become worse than the first. For now they are possessed by a much greater number of demons, blaspheming Christ Jesus in their synagogues, than they had been possessed in Egypt before the knowledge of the law; for it is one thing not to believe in one who is to come, and another not to have received him who has come. But understand the sevenfold number joined to the devil either on account of the Sabbath or on account of the number of the Holy Spirit, so that, as in Isaiah the seven spirits of the virtues are told to have descended upon the rod from the root of Jesse and upon the flower that rises up from the root, so, on the contrary, the number of the vices is consecrated in the devil.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.11.31":["The queen of the south will rise in the judgment with this generation and will condemn it, because she came from the ends of the earth to hear the wisdom of Solomon. The queen of the south will condemn the people of the Jews in the same way as the men of Nineveh will condemn unbelieving Israel. Now this is the queen of Sheba, of whom we read in the book of Kings and in Chronicles, who through such great difficulties, leaving her nation and her empire behind, came into Judea to hear the wisdom of Solomon, and brought him many gifts. And in Nineveh and in the queen of Sheba the hidden faith of the nations is preferred to Israel.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.11.32":["The men of Nineveh will rise in the judgment with this generation and will condemn it, not by the power of a sentence but by the example of comparison.\n— Commentary on Matthew","And behold, here is more than Jonah. Here you must take as an adverb of place, not a pronoun. Jonah, according to the LXX translators, preached for three days, I for so long a time; he to the Assyrians, an unbelieving nation, I to the Jews, the people of God; he to foreigners, I to fellow citizens; he spoke with a simple voice, doing no signs, I, doing such great signs, endure the slander of Beelzebub. So more than Jonah is here, that is, at present among you.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.11.33":["You are the light of the world. A city set upon a mountain cannot be hidden, nor do they light a lamp and put it under a bushel, and the rest. He teaches boldness in preaching, so that the apostles may not hide themselves out of fear and be like a lamp under a bushel, but may come forward with all freedom, so as to preach on the housetops what they have heard in the chambers.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.11.34":["If your eye is single, your whole body will be full of light. Those with bleary eyes commonly see many lamps. The single and pure eye looks upon things single and pure. He transfers all this to the understanding. For just as the whole body is in darkness if the eye is lacking, so if the soul has lost its principal brightness, the whole understanding will linger in darkness.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.11.35":["If then the light that is in you is darkness, how great will the darkness itself be? If the understanding, which is the light of the soul, is darkened by vice, with what darkness, do you think, is the gloom itself enveloped?\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.11.39-41":["Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites, because you clean what is outside of the cup and the dish, but inside you are full of plunder and uncleanness. Blind Pharisee, first clean what is inside of the cup and the dish, so that what is outside may become clean. In different words, with the same sense as above, he convicts the Pharisees of pretense and falsehood, because they show one thing to men outside and do another at home; not that their superstition dwelt in the cup and the dish, but that outside they showed men holiness in dress, in speech, in phylacteries, in fringes, in the length of their prayers and in other things of this kind, while inwardly they were full of the filth of vices.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.11.42":["Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites, because you tithe mint and dill and cumin and have left aside the weightier things of the law, judgment and mercy and faith; these you ought to have done, and not to omit those. There are many precepts in the law which display types of things to come, but others which are plain, according to the psalmist who says: The commandment of the Lord is bright, enlightening the eyes, which demand works at once, for example: You shall not commit adultery, you shall not steal, you shall not bear false witness, and the rest. But the Pharisees, because the Lord had commanded (to leave aside the mystical senses for the moment) that, for the support of the priests and Levites, whose portion was the Lord, tithes of all things be offered in the temple, had this one zeal: that what had been ordered should be brought in; the other things, which were greater, whether anyone did them or not, they counted of little worth. And so from this passage too he convicts them of avarice, because they zealously exact tithes even of cheap herbs, and pass over judgment in the settling of business, and mercy toward the poor, orphans and widows, and faith in God, which are great things.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.11.43":["And since they broaden their phylacteries beyond measure and make large fringes, courting glory from men, they are rebuked in the rest for seeking the first places at table at dinners and the first chairs in the synagogues, and for pursuing gluttony and glory in public, and for being called rabbi by men, which in the Latin tongue means master. In short, there follows:\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.11.46":["But they bind heavy and unbearable burdens and lay them on men's shoulders, but with their own finger they will not move them. This is said generally against all teachers who command great things and do not do even lesser ones. But it must be noted that the shoulders and the finger and the burdens and the bonds with which the burdens are bound are to be understood spiritually.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.11.47-48":["Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites, because you build the tombs of the prophets and adorn the monuments of the just, and say: If we had been in the days of our fathers, we would not have been their partners in the blood of the prophets. Thus you are witnesses against yourselves that you are sons of those who killed the prophets. With a most prudent syllogism he convicts them of being sons of murderers, since they themselves, for a reputation of goodness and glory among the peoples, build the tombs of the prophets whom their forefathers killed, and say: If we had been at that time, we would not have done the things that our fathers did. And this, even if they do not say it in word, they speak in deed, in that they build, ambitiously and magnificently, memorials of the slain, who, they do not deny, were slaughtered by their fathers.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.11.49":["Therefore, behold, I send to you prophets and wise men and scribes; some of them you will kill and crucify, and some of them you will scourge in your synagogues, and you persecute them from city to city. This which we said earlier, Fill up the measure of your fathers, to pertain to the person of the Lord, since he was to be killed by them, can also be referred to his disciples, of whom he now says: Behold, I send to you prophets and wise men and scribes; some of them you will kill and crucify, and you will scourge in your synagogues, and you persecute them from city to city, so that you may fill up the measure of your fathers. At the same time note, according to the Apostle writing to the Corinthians, that the gifts of the disciples of Christ are various: some are prophets, who foretell what is to come; others wise men, who know when they ought to bring forth speech; others scribes, most learned in the law; of whom Stephen was stoned, Paul killed, Peter crucified, the disciples scourged in the Acts of the Apostles; and they persecuted them from city to city, driving them out of Judea, so that they passed over to the people of the Gentiles.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.11.50-51":["Let us say briefly why the blood of Abel the just, up to Zechariah son of Barachiah, is required of that generation, though it killed neither of them. It is a rule of the Scriptures to set forth two generations, of the good or of the evil, that is, one for each. Let us take examples from the good: Who shall ascend into the mountain of the Lord, or who shall rest on his holy mountain? and when he had described the many who are to ascend into the mountain of the Lord, who lived in different ages, he afterwards adds: This is the generation of those who seek the Lord, who seek the face of the God of Jacob; and in another place, of all the saints: The generation of the just shall be blessed; but of the evil, as in the present passage: generation of vipers and all things shall be required of this generation; and in Ezekiel, when he had described the sins of the land, the prophetic word added: If Noah and Job and Daniel are found there, the sins of that land will not be forgiven, wishing all the just who would be like them in virtues to be understood through Noah and Job and Daniel. Therefore these too, who did things against the apostles like those of Cain and Joash, are reported to be of one generation.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.11.51":["That upon you may come all the just blood that has been shed upon the earth, from the blood of Abel the just to the blood of Zechariah son of Barachiah, who was killed between the temple and the altar. Amen I say to you, all these things will come upon this generation. Concerning Abel there is no ambiguity that he is the one whom Cain his brother killed. That he was just is proved not only from the Lord's present judgment but from the testimony of Genesis, where his gifts are related as accepted by God. We ask who this Zechariah son of Barachiah is, since we read of many Zechariahs. And lest a free license for error be granted us, there is added: Whom you killed between the temple and the altar. I have read different things in different writers, and I ought to set down the opinions of each. Some say it is Zechariah son of Barachiah, who is the eleventh among the twelve prophets, and the name of the father agrees in his case; but where he was killed between the temple and the altar Scripture does not say, especially since in his times there were scarcely even ruins of the temple. Others want it understood as Zechariah the father of John, proving from certain dreams of the apocrypha that he was killed because he had preached the coming of the Savior. This, because it has no authority from the Scriptures, is despised with the same ease with which it is proved. Others will have this to be the Zechariah who was killed by Joash king of Judea between the temple and the altar, as the history of Kings relates. But it must be observed that that Zechariah is not the son of Barachiah but the son of Jehoiada the priest. Hence Scripture also relates: Joash did not remember the good things that Jehoiada his father had done for him. Since, then, we have both the Zechariah and a place of killing that agrees, we ask why he is called the son of Barachiah and not of Jehoiada. Barachiah in our language means blessed of the Lord, and the justice of the priest Jehoiada is shown by his Hebrew name. In the Gospel which the Nazarenes use we find written son of Jehoiada in place of son of Barachiah. The simpler brethren, among the ruins of the temple and of the altar, or at the exits of the gates that lead to Siloam, point out red stones and think they were stained with the blood of Zechariah. We do not condemn the error, which comes down from hatred of the Jews and from the piety of faith.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.11.52":["Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites, because you shut the kingdom of heaven before men; you yourselves do not enter, nor do you allow those entering to enter. The scribes and Pharisees have knowledge of the law and the prophets; they know that Christ is the Son of God; they are not ignorant that he was born of a virgin; but while they seek plunder from the common people subject to them, they neither enter the kingdoms of heaven themselves nor permit those who could enter. This is what he rebukes in the prophet Hosea: The priests have hidden the way, they have killed Shechem; and again: The priests did not say: Where is the Lord? Or else every teacher who causes his disciples to stumble by evil works shuts the kingdom of heaven before them.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.12.1":["Beware of the leaven of the Pharisees and Sadducees. He who bewares of the leaven of the Pharisees and Sadducees does not keep the precepts of the law and of the letter; he neglects the traditions of men in order to do the commandment of God.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.12.2":["For nothing is covered that will not be revealed, and hidden that will not be known. And how is it that in the present age the vices of many are not known? But this is written of the time to come, when God will judge the hidden things of men and will illumine the hiding places of darkness and will make manifest the counsels of hearts; and the sense is: do not fear the savagery of the persecutors and the rage of the blasphemers, because the day of judgment will come in which both your virtue and their wickedness will be shown.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.12.3":["What I say to you in darkness, speak in the light, and what you hear in the ear, preach upon the housetops. What you have heard in mystery, preach more openly; what you have learned in secret, speak publicly; what I have taught you in a little place of Judea, say boldly in all the cities and in the whole world.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.12.4":["Do not fear those who kill the body but cannot kill the soul. If those who kill the body cannot kill the soul, then the soul is invisible and incorporeal; I mean, in comparison with the grosser substance of our body. Or at any rate it will be punished and will feel torments at that time when it has received its former body, so that it may be punished together with that with which it sinned.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.12.5":["But fear him who can destroy both soul and body in Gehenna. The name of Gehenna is not found in the old books, but is first used by the Savior. Let us ask, then, what the occasion of this word is. We read more than once that there was an idol of Baal near Jerusalem, at the foot of Mount Moriah, where Siloam flows. This valley and small level stretch of plain was well watered and wooded and full of delights, and in it was a grove consecrated to the idol. But the people of Israel had come to such madness that, forsaking the neighborhood of the temple, they offered victims there, and delights overcame the rigor of religion, and they burned their sons to the demon or initiated them; and the place itself was called gehennon, that is, the valley of the sons of Ennon. The book of Kings and Chronicles and Jeremiah write of this most fully. And God threatens that he will fill that very place with the corpses of the dead, so that it will no longer be called Topeth and Baal but will be called Polyandrum, that is, the burial mound of the dead. Therefore the future torments and everlasting punishments with which sinners are to be tormented are denoted by the name of this place. That there is a twofold Gehenna, of excessive fire and of cold, we read most fully in Job.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.12.6-7":["Are not two sparrows sold for an as? And one of them will not fall upon the ground without your Father. But even the hairs of your head are numbered. Do not fear, therefore; you are worth more than many sparrows. The Lord's discourse holds together, and what follows hangs upon what went before. Prudent reader, always beware of a superstitious understanding, so that you do not adjust the Scriptures to your own sense, but join your sense to the Scriptures and understand what follows. He had said above: Do not fear those who kill the body but cannot kill the soul, now he says, in consequence: Are not two sparrows sold for an as, and one of them will not fall upon the ground without your Father; and the sense is: if small and cheap animals do not die without God as their author, and there is providence in all things, and those among them that are to perish do not perish without the will of God, you who are eternal ought not to fear that you live without the providence of God. This sense was also stated above: Look at the birds of the sky, for they neither sow nor reap nor gather into their barns, and your heavenly Father feeds them; are you not worth more than they? and then: Consider the lilies of the field, how they grow, and the rest. But if God so clothes the grass of the field, which is today and tomorrow is thrown into the oven, how much more you, O you of very little faith? Some, in a forced way, interpret the two sparrows as the soul and the body; and the five sparrows according to Luke, which are sold for two asses, they refer to the senses. But how that understanding can be fitted to the whole body of the Gospel discourse is a matter of no small difficulty: But even the hairs of your head are all numbered; do not fear, therefore; you are better than many sparrows. More plainly is expressed the earlier sense of our exposition, that they ought not to fear those who can kill the body and cannot kill the soul, since, if even small animals do not die without God's knowledge, how much more a man who is supported by apostolic dignity.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.12.7":["And when he says: But even the hairs of your head are all numbered, he shows the immense providence of God toward men and signifies his ineffable affection, in that nothing of ours is hidden from God, and even small and idle words do not escape his knowledge. Those who deny the resurrection of the flesh mock the Church's understanding of this place, as though we said that even the hairs that have been numbered and cut off by the barber all rise again, when the Savior did not say: But even all your hairs are to be saved, but are numbered. Where there is number, knowledge of the number is shown, not the preservation of that same number.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.12.10":["Whoever speaks a word against the Son of Man, it will be forgiven him; but whoever speaks against the Holy Spirit, it will not be forgiven him, neither in this age nor in the one to come. And how do some of our people receive bishops and presbyters back into their rank after blasphemy of the Holy Spirit, when the Savior says that every sin and blasphemy is forgiven to men, but that those who have blasphemed against the Holy Spirit are not forgiven, neither in the present time nor in the future; unless perhaps we take that example from Mark the evangelist, who expressed more plainly the causes of such great anger, saying: Because they were saying: He has an unclean spirit. Therefore whoever ascribes the works of the Savior to Beelzebub, the prince of the demons, and says that the Son of God has an unclean spirit, to him the blasphemy will at no time be forgiven. Or else this passage is to be understood thus: He who speaks a word against the Son of Man, scandalized by my flesh and thinking me only a man, because I am the son of a carpenter and have brothers James and Joseph and Jude, and because I am a man who is a glutton and a drinker of wine, such an opinion and blasphemy, although it is not without fault, still has the pardon due to error because of the lowliness of the body; but he who, plainly understanding the works of God, since he cannot deny the mighty work, is goaded by the same envy and slanders them, and says that Christ, the Word of God, and the works of the Holy Spirit are Beelzebub, to him it will not be forgiven, neither in the present age nor in the one to come.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.12.11-12":["But when they hand you over, do not think how or what you are to speak. He had said above: For they will hand you over in councils, and in their synagogues they will scourge you, and you will be led before governors and kings for my sake. Since, then, we are led before judges for Christ's sake, we ought to offer only our will for Christ. For the rest, Christ himself, who dwells in us, will speak for himself, and the grace of the Holy Spirit will be supplied in answering.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.12.22":["Do not be anxious for your soul, what you shall eat, nor for your body, what you shall put on. In some manuscripts there is added: nor what you shall drink. So from care for that which nature has granted to all, and which is common to beasts of burden and wild beasts and men, we are wholly set free. But we are commanded not to be anxious about what we shall eat, because in the sweat of our face we prepare bread for ourselves. Labor must be exercised, anxiety removed. This which is said: Do not be anxious for your soul, what you shall eat, nor for your body, what you shall put on, let us take of fleshly food and clothing. But for spiritual food and clothing we ought always to be anxious.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.12.23":["Is not the soul more than food, and the body more than clothing? What he says is of this kind: He who has granted the greater will surely grant the lesser too.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.12.24":["Look at the birds of the air, that they neither sow nor reap nor gather into their barns, and your heavenly Father feeds them. Are you not worth more than they? The Apostle commands that we not be wiser than it behooves us to be wise. This testimony must be kept in the present passage too. For there are some who, while they wish to overstep the boundaries of the fathers and to fly up to the heights, are plunged into the depths, saying that the birds of the air are the angels and the other powers in the ministry of God, which are fed without care of their own by God's providence. If this is so, as they wish it to be understood, how does what is said to men follow: Are you not worth more than they? It is to be taken simply, therefore: if the birds, which are today and tomorrow will not be, whose soul is mortal and which, once they have ceased to be, will never be again, are fed by God's providence without care and toil, how much more will men, to whom eternity is promised, be ruled by God's governance.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.12.27":["Which of you can add one cubit to his stature? And why are you anxious about clothing? As he showed by the comparison of the birds that the soul is more than food, so from the things that follow he shows that the body is more than clothing, saying:\n\nConsider the lilies of the field, how they grow, and the rest. And truly, what silk, what purple of kings, what picture of weavers can be compared with the flowers? What is as red as the rose? What is as white as the lily? But that the purple of the violet is surpassed by no murex dye is a matter for the judgment of the eyes rather than of words.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.12.34":["Where your treasure is, there is your heart also. This is to be understood not only of money but of all the passions. The glutton's god is his belly; so he has his heart there where he has his treasure too. The treasure of the luxurious is feasting, of the wanton amusements, of the lover lust. Each serves that by which he is overcome.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.12.40-43":["Be ready, because you do not know at what hour the Son of Man is to come. Who, do you think, is the faithful and prudent servant whom his master has set over his household, to give them food in due season? Blessed is that servant whom his master, when he comes, finds so doing. He presses home more fully and repeats why he foretold that neither the angels nor he himself knows the day and hour of the consummation, but only the Father: because it is not expedient for the apostles to know; and he inserts the example of the householder, that is, of himself, and of the faithful servants, that is, of the apostles, as an exhortation to a watchful mind, so that in hope of rewards they may minister to their fellow servants in due season the food of the teachings.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.12.45":["But if that wicked servant says in his heart: My master is delaying his coming, and begins to strike his fellow servants, and the rest. It depends on what precedes: that just as the watchful servant, always awaiting his master's coming, hands out food to his fellow servants in due season and afterward is set over all the goods of the householder, so, on the contrary, he who, in Ezekiel's words, says: This will come to pass in distant times, and does not think that the master will come soon, having grown more careless, gives himself to feasts and luxury, and will find not a gentle householder but a most severe judge.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.12.46":["The master of that servant will come on a day when he does not expect and at an hour he does not know, and will divide him and put his portion with the hypocrites. He teaches this very thing: that they should know that the Lord will come at the time when he is not expected; and he admonishes the stewards to vigilance and watchfulness. Moreover, as for his saying: will divide him, it does not mean that he will cut him in two with a sword, but that he will separate him from the fellowship of the saints and put his portion with the hypocrites, namely with those who were in the field and who were grinding and were nonetheless left behind. We have often said that a hypocrite is one who is one thing and shows another, just as both in the field and at the mill he seemed to do the same as the man of the Church, but the outcome revealed a different will.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.12.51":["Do not think that I have come to send peace upon the earth; I have not come to send peace but a sword. He had said above: What I say to you in darkness, speak in the light, and what you hear in the ear, preach upon the housetops. Now he adds what follows after the preaching. Over the faith of Christ the whole world was divided against itself; every house had both unbelievers and believers, and therefore a good war was sent so that an evil peace might be broken. Something of the kind God is written in Genesis to have done against the rebellious men who had moved from the east and were hastening to build a tower by which they might penetrate the heights of heaven: he divided their tongues. Hence also in the psalm David prays: Scatter, O Lord, the nations that want wars.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.12.53":["For I have come to separate a man against his father, and a daughter against her mother, and a daughter-in-law against her mother-in-law; and a man's enemies are those of his household. This passage is written in nearly the same words in the prophet Micah. And note, wherever a testimony is set down from the Old Testament, whether the sense alone agrees or the wording as well.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.12.54-56":["But he answered and said to them: When evening has come you say: It will be fair, for the sky is red; and in the morning: Today there will be a storm, for the sky is red and lowering. You know, then, how to judge the face of the sky, but the signs of the times you cannot. This is not found in most manuscripts, and the sense is plain: from the order and constancy of the elements days both of fair weather and of rain can be known beforehand; but the scribes and Pharisees, who seemed to be teachers of the law, could not understand from the prophecy of the prophets the coming of the Savior.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.12.58-59":["Be consenting to your adversary quickly, while you are with him on the way, lest perhaps the adversary hand you over to the judge, and the judge hand you over to the officer, and you be put into prison. Amen I say to you: You will not go out from there until you pay the last quadrans. Where we have consenting in the Latin codices, in the Greek is written εὐνοῶν, which is interpreted well-disposed or kind. Now from what precedes and what follows the sense is plain: that our Lord and Savior, while we run along the way of this world, exhorts us to peace and concord, according to the Apostle, who says: If it can be done, as far as lies in you, having peace with all men. For in the preceding passage too he had said: If you are offering your gift at the altar and there remember that your brother has something against you, and having finished this he immediately adds: Be well-disposed or kind to your adversary, and the rest. And in what follows he commands: Love your enemies, do good to those who hate you, and pray for your persecutors. Although this understanding is plain and follows from the context, very many think it was said of the flesh and the soul, or of the soul and the spirit, which does not stand at all. For how will the flesh be put into prison if it has not consented to the soul, since both soul and flesh are to be shut up alike, and the flesh can do nothing except what the mind commanded? Or how could the Holy Spirit dwelling in us hand over to the judge either the flesh or the soul resisting him, since he himself is the judge? Others, following the letter of Peter, who says: Our adversary the devil goes about like a roaring lion, interpret the adversary as the devil, and hold that the Savior commands that, while it is in our power, we should be well-disposed toward the devil, who is our enemy and avenger, and not make him undergo punishments on our account; for since he himself supplies the incitements to the vices, and we too sin by our own will, if we consent to him when he suggests the vices, he too must be tormented on our account; and they say that each of the saints is well-disposed to his adversary if he does not make him undergo torments on his account. Some argue in a more forced way that in baptism each one enters into a pact with the devil and says: I renounce you, devil, and your pomp and your vices and your world, which lies in the evil one. If, then, we keep the pact, we are well-disposed and consenting to our adversary and are by no means to be shut up in prison. But if we transgress in anything of what we had pledged to the devil, we shall be handed over to the judge and the officer and put into prison, and we shall not go out of it until we pay the last quadrans.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.12.59":["The quadrans is a kind of coin that contains two mites. Hence in one Gospel that poor widow woman is said to have put a quadrans into the corban, in another two mites; not that the Gospels are at variance, but because one quadrans contains two mites. This, then, is what he says: You will not come out of prison until you have paid even for the least sins.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.13.6-9":["This is the fig tree to which the householder came for the third time, and which he wishes to cut down as not producing fruit; for it the farmer, to whom it had been entrusted, pleads, that he may give it time, and he says: Lord, let it alone this year also, until I dig around it and put on manure, and if indeed it bears fruit; but if not, then you shall cut it down. That farmer is either Gabriel or Michael, to whom the people of the Jews was entrusted, who pleads with the Lord at the passion and says: Lord, give it time for repentance, and do not overthrow them, and if indeed they bear fruit; but if not, then you shall overthrow them. If they bear fruit, he says: he did not say what they would undergo; nor did he say: If they bear fruit, they will remain as they were; but, if they bear fruit: the sentence is left suspended, so that this is understood: you will transfer them into the church of the Gentiles, and transplant them into another vineyard. The Lord came for the third time and did not find fruit in them. First he gave the law through Moses, the second time he spoke through the prophets, the third time he himself came down. And after the passion, when forty-two years had been given for repentance, because they did not bear fruit, the fourth time they were overthrown. This, however, is left to our understanding. For in the parable it is not written what the householder did afterward, but only what the farmer pleaded for. From this we perceive that those of this fig tree who bore fruit were transferred to the people of the Gentiles, to which the prophet too goes up, saying: I shall rest in the day of tribulation, that I may go up to the people of my sojourning; but that those who did not bear fruit and remained in their hardness were overthrown.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"LUK.13.16":["The same thing is signified also by that daughter of Abraham in the Gospel, a noble soul, whom Satan had bound and bent over, and who could not be raised up on high before the coming of the Lord, nor look up at her Creator. Hence the Savior says: This woman, he says, a daughter of Abraham, whom Satan had bound, ought she not to have been loosed from this bond on the sabbath day?\n— Commentary on Micah"],"LUK.13.18-19":["The kingdom of heaven is like a grain of mustard seed, which a man took and sowed in his field; which indeed is the least of all seeds, but when it has grown it is greater than all garden herbs and becomes a tree, so that the birds of the sky come and dwell in its branches. Let it not be irksome to the reader if we set out whole parables. For things that are obscure must be discussed more fully, lest by excessive brevity the sense be wrapped up rather than explained. The kingdom of heaven is the preaching of the Gospel and the knowledge of the Scriptures, which leads to life, and of which it is said to the Jews: The kingdom of God shall be taken from you and given to a nation producing its fruits. Like, therefore, in this manner, is the kingdom to a grain of mustard seed, which a man took and sowed in his field. The man who sows in his field is understood by most to be the Savior, because he sows in the souls of believers; by others, a man himself sowing in his own field, that is, in himself and in his heart. Who is this who sows but our mind and our soul, which, receiving the grain of preaching and fostering the seed with the moisture of faith, makes it sprout in the field of its own breast?\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.13.19":["The preaching of the Gospel is the least of all teachings. For at the first hearing of its doctrine it has no credit of truth, since it preaches a man who is God, Christ dead, and the stumbling block of the cross. Compare a teaching of this kind with the doctrines of the philosophers and with their books, and with the splendor of their eloquence and the composition of their discourses, and you will see how much smaller than the other seeds is the sowing of the Gospel. But those, when they have grown, show nothing pungent, nothing vigorous, nothing vital; the whole of it bubbles up, flabby and withered, into garden herbs and grasses which quickly dry up and collapse. This preaching, however, which seemed small in the beginning, when it has been sown either in the soul of the believer or in the whole world, does not rise up into garden herbs but grows into a tree, so that the birds of the sky, by which we ought to understand either the souls of believers or powers given over to the service of God, come and dwell in its branches. The branches of the Gospel tree that has grown from the grain of mustard seed I take to be the diversities of doctrines, on which each of the birds mentioned above rests. Let us too take up the wings of a dove, so that, flying to higher places, we may dwell in the branches of this tree and make ourselves little nests of teachings, and, fleeing earthly things, hasten toward heavenly ones. Many, reading that the grain of mustard seed is the least of all seeds, and what is said in the Gospel by the disciples, Lord, increase our faith, and the answer given them by the Savior, Amen I say to you: If you had faith like a grain of mustard seed, you would say to this mountain: Move from this place, and it would move, think either that the apostles are asking for a small faith or that the Lord is in doubt about a small faith, whereas the apostle Paul judges faith compared to a grain of mustard seed to be the greatest. For what does he say? If I have such faith that I move mountains, but do not have love, it profits me nothing. Therefore what the Lord said is done by a faith that is compared to a grain of mustard seed, this the Apostle teaches can be done by faith entire.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.13.20-21":["Another parable he spoke to them: The kingdom of heaven is like leaven, which a woman took and hid in three measures of meal until the whole was leavened. The stomach of men varies: some delight in bitter foods, others in sweet, these in harsher, those in milder. So the Lord, as we have already said above, sets forth different parables, so that according to the variety of the wounds the medicine too may vary. This woman who took the leaven and hid it in three measures of meal until the whole is leavened seems to me to be either the apostolic preaching or the Church, which has been gathered from different nations. She takes the leaven, namely the knowledge and understanding of the Scriptures, and hides it in three measures of meal, so that spirit, soul and body, brought into one, may not be at variance with one another, but, when they have agreed with two and three, may obtain from the Father whatever they have asked. This passage is also explained in another way. We read in Plato, and it is a widespread doctrine of the philosophers, that there are three passions in the human soul: τὸ λογιστικόν, which we can render as the rational; τὸ θυμικόν, which we call full of anger, or irascible; τὸ ἐπιθυμητικόν, which we name concupiscible; and that philosopher thinks that our rational part dwells in the brain, anger in the gall, desire in the liver. And so we too, if we have received the Gospel leaven of the holy Scriptures of which we spoke above, shall have the three passions of the human soul brought into one, so that in reason we possess prudence, in anger hatred against vices, in desire a longing for virtues; and all this will come about through the Gospel teaching which mother Church has furnished to us. I will also give a third understanding, that of certain people, so that the curious reader may choose from several whichever pleases him. They too interpret this woman as the Church, which has mixed the faith of man in three measures of meal, namely belief in the Father and the Son and the Holy Spirit, and, when it has been leavened into one, leads us not to a threefold God but to the knowledge of one divinity. The three measures of meal also, since there is no different nature in each, draw toward the unity of the substance. A pious sense indeed; but parables and the doubtful understanding of enigmas can never avail for the authority of doctrines. Now the satum is a kind of measure, according to the custom of the province of Palestine, holding one and a half modii. Other things too are said about this parable, but it is not for the present matter to say everything about everything.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.13.24":["Enter by the narrow gate; how wide is the gate and broad the way that leads to destruction, and many are those who enter by it; how narrow is the gate and strait the way that leads to life, and few are those who find it. The wide way is the pleasures of the world, which men seek after; the narrow one is that which is opened up through labors and fasts, which the Apostle too entered upon, and he urges Timothy to enter by it. At the same time consider how expressively he has spoken of both ways. Many walk by the wide one; few find the narrow. The wide one we do not seek, nor is there need of finding it: it offers itself of its own accord and is the way of those who go astray; but the narrow one neither do all find, nor do those who have found it enter by it at once. Indeed many, having found the way of truth, are taken captive by the pleasures of the world and turn back from the middle of the journey.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.13.25":["Last of all the other virgins also come, saying: Lord, Lord, open to us. Excellent indeed is the confession in calling him Lord, and its repetition is a sign of faith. But what does it profit to call with the voice on him whom you deny by your works?\n— Commentary on Matthew","But he answered and said: Amen I say to you: I do not know you. The Lord knows those who are his, and he who does not know will not be known. The Lord does not know the workers of iniquity; and although they are virgins and, according to the twofold understanding, glory both in purity of body and in the confession of the true faith, yet, because they do not have the oil of knowledge, it is enough for their punishment that they are not known by the bridegroom.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.13.27":["Then I will confess to them. He said expressively: I will confess, because for a long time before he had refrained from saying: I do not know you. The Lord does not know those who perish. But note why he added: I never knew you, if, as some hold, all men have always dwelt among the rational creatures.\n— Commentary on Matthew","Withdraw from me, you who work iniquity. He did not say: you who have worked iniquity, lest he seem to take away repentance, but you who work, that is, you who up to the present hour, when the time of judgment has come, although you no longer have the opportunity of sinning, still have the inclination.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.13.28-29":["Many will come from the east and the west and will recline with Abraham and Isaac and Jacob in the kingdom of heaven. Because the God of Abraham, the maker of heaven, is the Father of Christ, therefore Abraham too is in the kingdom of heaven, and with him the nations that have believed in Christ, the Son of the Creator, are to recline. And that sense too is likewise fulfilled of which we spoke above, that in the centurion's faith the precedence of the Gentiles is established, since the peoples who will believe from east and west are mentioned with reference to his belief.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.13.28":["But the sons of the kingdom. By the sons of the kingdom he means the Jews, over whom God reigned before.\n— Commentary on Matthew","Will be cast out into the outer darkness. Darkness is always inner, not outer. But since he who is driven out of doors by the Lord leaves the light behind, therefore it has been named the outer darkness.\n— Commentary on Matthew","There will be weeping and gnashing of teeth. If weeping belongs to the eyes, and gnashing of teeth points to bones, then the resurrection of bodies, and of the same members that had fallen, is real.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.13.34":["Jerusalem, Jerusalem, you who kill the prophets and stone those who are sent to you, how often I wished to gather your children as a hen gathers her chicks under her wings, and you would not. Jerusalem (he means not the stones and buildings of the city but its inhabitants), which he laments with a father's affection, as we also read in another place that, seeing it, he wept. And in saying, how often I wished to gather your children, he testifies that all the prophets of the past were sent by him. The likeness of the hen gathering her chicks under her wings we read also in the song of Deuteronomy: As an eagle protects its nest and has longed over its young, spreading its wings it took them up and bore them on its pinions.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.13.35":["Behold, your house will be left to you deserted. He had said this very thing long before, in the person of Jeremiah: I have left my house, I have abandoned my inheritance; my inheritance has become to me like the cave of a hyena. We prove that the house of the Jews, that is, that temple which before shone more majestically to the eyes, is deserted, because it lost Christ its inhabitant and, eager to snatch the inheritance, killed the heir.\n— Commentary on Matthew","For I say to you: You will not see me henceforth until you say: Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord. He speaks to Jerusalem and to the people of the Jews. Now this verse, which the little ones too and the sucklings used at the entry of the Lord Savior into Jerusalem, when they said: Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord, Hosanna in the highest; he took from Psalm 117 (118), which was plainly written about the coming of the Lord; and by what he says he wants this understood: Unless you repent and confess that I myself am he of whom the prophets sang, the Son of the almighty Father, you will not see my face. The Jews have a time of repentance given them; let them confess him blessed who comes in the name of the Lord, and they will behold the face of Christ.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.14.16-17":["And answering, Jesus spoke to them in parables, saying: The kingdom of heaven has become like a man, a king, who made a wedding for his son, and sent his servant to call those invited to the wedding, and they would not come. The Pharisees, understanding that the parables were spoken about them, sought to seize him and kill him. Knowing this will of theirs, the Lord nonetheless rebukes them in their raging, and he is not so overcome by fear as to refrain from reproving sinners. This king who made a wedding for his son is God almighty. And he makes a wedding for our Lord Jesus Christ and the Church, which has been gathered as much from the Jews as from the Gentiles; and he sends his servant to call those invited to the wedding, no doubt Moses, through whom he gave the law to those invited. But if we read 'servants', as most copies have it, it is to be referred to the prophets, because those invited through them disdained to come.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.14.17":["Again he sent other servants, saying: Tell those invited: Behold, I have prepared my dinner, my bulls and fatlings are killed and all things are ready; come to the wedding. The servants who were sent the second time are better understood as the prophets than as the apostles, provided, however, that 'servant' was written above; but if you read 'servants' there, then here the second servants are to be understood as the apostles. By the dinner prepared and the bulls and fatlings killed, either the king's riches are described by metaphor, so that from carnal things spiritual things may be understood, or else the greatness of the doctrines and the teaching of God, most full of the law, can be perceived.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.14.18-20":["And they went off, one to his farm, another to his business; but others seized his servants and, having treated them with insult, killed them. Among those who do not receive the truth of the Gospel there is great diversity. For those who, occupied with other things, would not come are of lesser guilt than those who, scorning the affection of the one inviting, turned kindness into cruelty and seized the king's servants and either treated them with insults or killed them. In this parable the killing of the bridegroom is passed over in silence, and through the deaths of the servants the contempt for the wedding is shown.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.14.21-23":["Then he said to his servants: The wedding indeed is ready, but those who had been invited were not worthy; go therefore to the exits of the roads, and whomever you find, call to the wedding. The people of the Gentiles was not on the roads but at the exits of the roads. But it is asked how, among these who were outside among the wicked, some good men were also found. The Apostle treats this passage more fully to the Romans: that the Gentiles, doing by nature the things that are of the law, condemn the Jews, who did not do the written law. Among the heathen themselves, too, there is infinite diversity, since we know that some are prone to vices and rush headlong into evils, while others, on account of the uprightness of their morals, are devoted to virtues.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.14.21":["But when the king heard, he was angry. Of him he had said above: The kingdom of heaven has become like a man, a king, when he was inviting to the wedding and doing works of clemency, the name of man was set beside him; now, when he comes to vengeance, 'man' is passed over in silence and he is called only 'king'.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.14.26":["He who loves father or mother more than me is not worthy of me, and the rest. Because he had said before: I have not come to send peace but a sword, and to divide men against father and mother and mother-in-law, lest anyone should put filial devotion before religion, he went on, saying: He who loves father or mother more than me; and in the Song of Songs we read: Set charity in order in me. This order is necessary in every affection. After God love your father, love your mother, love your children; but if a necessity comes such that the love of parents and children is weighed against the love of God, and both cannot be kept, hatred of one's own is devotion toward God. So he did not forbid loving father or mother, but pointedly added: He who loves father or mother more than me.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.14.27":["And he who does not take up his cross and follow me is not worthy of me. In another Gospel it is written: He who has not taken up his cross daily. Lest we think that ardor of faith shown once can suffice, the cross must always be carried, so that we may show that we always love Christ.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.14.34":["But if the salt has vanished, with what will it be salted? If the teacher has gone astray, by what other teacher will he be corrected?\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.14.35":["It is good for nothing any more except to be cast out and trodden underfoot by men. The example is taken from agriculture. For salt, as it is necessary for seasoning foods and for drying meats, so it has no other use. Indeed, we read in the Scriptures that certain cities were sown with salt by the anger of their conquerors, so that no shoot might spring up in them. Let teachers and bishops therefore beware, and see that the mighty mightily endure torments and that there is no remedy, but that the falls of the greater lead down to Tartarus.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.15.4-5":["What do you think? If someone has a hundred sheep, and one of them goes astray, does he not leave the ninety-nine on the mountains and go to seek the one that went astray? Following on, he who had said before: See that you do not despise one of these little ones, calls us to clemency, and adds the parable of the ninety-nine sheep left on the mountains and of the one that strayed, which the good shepherd, because it could not walk for its great weakness, carried back on his shoulders to the rest of the flock. Some think that this is the shepherd who, though he was in the form of God, did not think it robbery to be equal with God, but emptied himself, taking the form of a servant, becoming obedient to the Father unto death, even the death of the cross, and that for this reason he came down to earthly things, to save the one little sheep that had perished, that is, the human race. Others, however, think that in the ninety-nine sheep the number of the righteous is understood, and in the one little sheep that of sinners, according to what he said in another place: I did not come to call the righteous but sinners; for those who are well have no need of a physician, but those who are ill. This parable is written in the Gospel according to Luke together with two other parables, of the ten drachmas and of the two sons.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.16.13":["You cannot serve God and mammon. In the Syriac language riches are called mammona. You cannot serve God and mammon. Let the avaricious man hear this, let him hear who is enrolled under the Christian name, that one cannot serve riches and Christ at the same time. And yet he did not say: he who has riches, but: he who serves riches. For he who is the slave of riches guards his riches as a slave; but he who has shaken off the yoke of slavery distributes them as a master.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.16.15":["Finally it is said to the Scribes and to the Pharisees: You are those who justify yourselves before men: but God knows your hearts (Luke 16:15). In this it is to be considered that he did not say Father, according to the blasphemies of the heretics, lest he should seem to exclude the Son, but God, which is common to the Father and the Son. In the beginning indeed was the Word, and the Word was with God, and the Word was God: he was in the beginning with God (John 1:1, 2).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"LUK.16.16":["From the days of John the Baptist until now the kingdom of heaven suffers violence. If John, as we said above, was the first to announce repentance to the peoples, saying: Repent, for the kingdom of heaven has drawn near, it follows that from his days the kingdom of heaven suffers violence, and the violent plunder it. For it is great violence that we, born on earth, should seek the seat of heaven and possess by virtue what we did not hold by nature.\n— Commentary on Matthew","For all the prophets and the law prophesied until John. Not that he excludes prophets after John (for we read in the Acts of the Apostles that both Agabus prophesied and the four virgin daughters of Philip), but that the law and the prophets whom we read in writing, whatever they prophesied, foretold of the Lord. When therefore it is said: All the prophets and the law prophesied until John, the time of Christ is shown, in that the one whom they said was to come, John showed had come.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.16.17":["Until heaven and earth pass away. New heavens and a new earth are promised to us, which the Lord God will make. If, then, new things are to be created, it follows that the old will pass away. But as for what follows:\n\nOne iota or one apex shall not pass from the law until all things come to pass, it is shown by the figure of the letter that even the things thought least in the law are full of spiritual mysteries, and that all things are summed up in the Gospel. Of what learning, then, and of what doctrine is it to show that even the various sacrifices, and the things in the victims that seem superstitious, are fulfilled daily?\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.16.18":["It was said: Whoever puts away his wife, let him give her a bill of divorce; but I say to you that everyone who puts away his wife, and the rest. In a later part the Savior explains this passage more fully: that Moses ordered a bill of divorce to be given on account of the hardness of heart of the husbands, not granting separation but taking away murder. For it is much better that discord, however grievous, should come about than that blood should be shed through hatred.\n— Commentary on Matthew","But I say to you that whoever puts away his wife, except for fornication, and marries another commits adultery; and he who marries her who was put away commits adultery. Fornication alone is what overcomes the affection owed to a wife; indeed, when she has divided the one flesh into another and by fornication has separated herself from her husband, she ought not to be kept, lest she bring her husband too under a curse, as Scripture says: He who keeps an adulteress is foolish and impious. Wherever, therefore, there is fornication and the suspicion of fornication, the wife is freely put away. And because it could happen that someone might bring a false charge against an innocent woman and, for the sake of a second marriage union, fasten a crime on the former wife, he is commanded to put away the first wife on these terms: that he not have a second while the first is living. For what he says is this: If you put away your wife not out of lust but because of an injury, why, having experienced an unhappy first marriage, do you thrust yourself into the danger of a new one? Moreover, because it could happen that by the same law the wife too might give her husband a bill of divorce, the same caution is enjoined, that she not take a second husband. And because a harlot, and one who had once been an adulteress, did not fear disgrace, it is enjoined on the second husband that if he marries such a woman he is under the charge of adultery.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.16.25":["In short, Abraham did not have good things because he was rich, but because he used his riches well (Gen 13). And Lazarus, who afterward rested in his bosom, did not endure evils because he suffered the torments of diseases together with want; rather, through what were thought evils in the world, he obtained true goods. Hence too that rich man clad in purple received his good things in his lifetime, which were good to him who thought them good (Luke 16); and of Lazarus it is not said, conversely: He received his evil things in his lifetime; but: he received evil things in his lifetime, which seemed evil not to him who suffered them but to others.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"LUK.17.1":["Woe to the world because of stumbling blocks. It is necessary that stumbling blocks come, but woe to the man through whom the stumbling block comes. Not that it is necessary for stumbling blocks to come (otherwise those who cause the stumbling block would be without fault), but, since it is necessary that stumbling blocks occur in this world, each one lies open to stumbling blocks by his own fault. And at the same time, by a general sentence, Judas is struck, who had prepared his mind for the betrayal.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.17.2":["But whoever causes one of these little ones to stumble. Note that the one who is scandalized is a little child; for the greater do not admit stumbling blocks.\n— Commentary on Matthew","It is better for him that an ass's millstone be hung on his neck and that he be sunk in the depth of the sea. Although this may be a general sentence against all who cause anyone to stumble, yet according to the sequence of the discourse it can also be understood as spoken against the apostles, who, by asking who was greater in the kingdom of heaven, seemed to be contending among themselves about rank, and, had they persisted in this fault, could have destroyed through their own stumbling block those whom they were calling to the faith, when these saw the apostles fighting among themselves over honor. But when he said: It is better for him that an ass's millstone be hung on his neck, he speaks according to the custom of the province, by which this was the penalty for the greater crimes among the Jews of old, that they were sunk in the deep with a stone tied to them. And it is better for him, because it is much better to receive a brief punishment for a fault than to be kept for eternal torments. For the Lord will not punish twice for the same thing.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.17.3":["But if your brother sins against you, go and rebuke him between yourself and him, and the rest. If our brother sins against us and has injured us in any matter, we have the power to forgive, nay rather the necessity, since we are commanded to forgive our debtors their debts; but if anyone sins against God, it is not within our discretion. For the divine Scripture says: If a man sins against a man, the priest will pray for him; but if he sins against God, who will pray for him? We, on the contrary, are kindly when the injury is done to God, and over insults to ourselves we give rein to hatreds. A brother is to be rebuked privately, lest, if he has once lost his shame and modesty, he remain in sin; and if indeed he listens, we gain his soul, and through the salvation of another, salvation is gained for us too; but if he will not listen, let a brother be brought in; and if he does not listen to him either, let a third be brought in as well, either with the aim of correcting him or of summoning him before witnesses.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.17.4":["Then Peter, coming to him, said: Lord, how often shall my brother sin against me and I forgive him? Up to seven times? and the rest. The Lord's discourse holds together and, like a threefold cord, cannot be broken. Above he had said: See that you do not despise one of these little ones; and he had added: If your brother sins against you, go and rebuke him between yourself and him; and he had promised a reward, saying: If two of you agree on earth, about anything they will obtain what they ask, and I will be in their midst. Roused by this, the apostle Peter asks how often he ought to forgive a brother who sins against him, and with his question he puts forward a verdict: up to seven times, to whom Jesus answered: Not up to seven times but seventy times seven, that is, four hundred and ninety times, so that he should forgive a brother who sins as many times in a day as the brother could not sin.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.17.5-6":["Many, reading that the grain of mustard seed is the least of all seeds, and what is said in the Gospel by the disciples, Lord, increase our faith, and the answer given them by the Savior, Amen I say to you: If you had faith like a grain of mustard seed, you would say to this mountain: Move from this place, and it would move, think either that the apostles are asking for a small faith or that the Lord is in doubt about a small faith, whereas the apostle Paul judges faith compared to a grain of mustard seed to be the greatest. For what does he say? If I have such faith that I move mountains, but do not have love, it profits me nothing. Therefore what the Lord said is done by a faith that is compared to a grain of mustard seed, this the Apostle teaches can be done by faith entire.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.17.23":["Then if anyone says to you: Behold, here is the Christ, or there, do not believe it. At the time of the Jewish captivity many leaders arose who said that they were Christs, so much so that, while the Romans were besieging the city, there were three factions within. But it is better understood of the consummation of the world.\n— Commentary on Matthew","If therefore they say to you: Behold, he is in the desert, do not go out. Behold, in the inner rooms, do not believe it. If anyone promises you that Christ dwells in the desert of the doctrine of the Gentiles and the philosophers, or in the inner rooms of the heretics, who promise the secrets of God, do not go out, do not believe; or else: because in a time of persecution and distress false prophets always find room for deceiving, if anyone wishes to vaunt himself under the name of Christ, do not at once give him credence.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.17.24":["For as the lightning goes out from the east and appears even to the west, so also will be the coming of the Son of Man. Do not go out, do not believe that the Son of Man is either in the desert of the Gentiles or in the inner rooms of the heretics, but believe that from the east even to the west his faith shines in the catholic churches. This too must be said: that the second coming of the Savior is to be shown not in humility as before, but in glory. It is foolish, therefore, to seek in a small or hidden place him who is the light of the whole world.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.17.26-27":["But as in the days of Noah, so also will be the coming of the Son of Man. For as in the days before the flood they were eating and drinking, marrying and giving in marriage, and the rest. It is asked how it is written above: For nation will rise against nation and kingdom against kingdom, and there will be pestilences and famines and earthquakes, and now those things are mentioned as about to come which are signs of peace. But it must be reckoned, following the Apostle, that after the battles and dissensions, the pestilences, famines and earthquakes, and the other things by which the human race is laid waste, a brief peace will follow, which will promise that all things are quiet, so that the faith of believers may be tested, whether, when the evils have passed, they still hope that the judge is to come. For this is what we read in Paul: When they say: Peace and security, then sudden destruction will come upon them, like the pain of a woman in labor, and they will not escape.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.17.31":["Then let those who are in Judea flee to the mountains, and let him who is on the roof not come down to take anything from his house, and let him who is in the field not turn back to take his tunic. The abomination of desolation can also be understood as every perverse doctrine; and when we see it standing in the holy place, that is, in the Church, and showing itself as God, we must flee from Judea to the mountains, that is, leaving behind the letter that kills and Jewish perversity, draw near to the eternal mountains from which God gives light wonderfully; and be on the roof and on the housetop, where the fiery darts of the devil cannot reach; and not come down and take anything from the house of our former way of life, nor seek the things behind, but rather sow in the field of the spiritual Scriptures so that we may gather fruit from it; nor take a second tunic, which the apostles are forbidden to have. On this passage, that is, on the abomination of desolation which was spoken of by Daniel the prophet standing in the holy place, Porphyry uttered many blasphemies against us in the thirteenth volume of his work; Eusebius, bishop of Caesarea, answered him in three volumes, the eighteenth, the nineteenth and the twentieth; Apollinaris too wrote most fully; and it was a superfluous attempt to want to discuss in one passage a matter that has been disputed in so many thousands of lines.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.17.35-36":["Then two will be in the field; one will be taken and one will be left; two women grinding at the mill; one will be taken and the other will be left. Then, he says, two will be in the field, namely when, at the time of the consummation and judgment, two will be found together in the field, having the same labor and, as it were, an equal sowing, but not receiving the fruit of their labor equally; two women also will be grinding together; one will be taken and the other left. In the two who are staying in the field and in the two women who grind together, understand either the synagogue and the Church, because they seem to grind together in the law and to grind the flour of God's commandments from the same Scriptures; or the other heresies, which seem to grind the flour of their doctrines from both Testaments or from one of them, and which, though they have one profession of the Christian name, will not receive the same reward, some being taken and others left behind.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.17.37":["Wherever the body is, there the eagles will be gathered together. By a natural example which we see daily we are instructed in the mystery of Christ. Eagles and vultures are said to perceive corpses even across the seas and to gather to food of this kind. If, then, irrational birds, by a natural sense, though separated by such great spaces of land and by the waves of the sea, perceive where a small corpse lies, how much more ought we and the whole multitude of believers to hasten to him whose lightning goes out from the east and appears even to the west! But by the body, that is, πτῶμα, which is more expressively called in Latin a cadaver, from the fact that it falls through death, we can understand the passion of Christ, to which we are summoned, so that wherever it is read in the Scriptures we may gather together; and through it we can come to the word of God, as in that saying: They have dug my hands and feet; and in Isaiah: As a sheep led to the slaughter, and other things like these. And the saints are called eagles, whose youth is renewed like the eagle's, and who, according to Isaiah, grow feathers and take on wings so as to come to the passion of Christ.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.18.15":["Then little children were brought to him, that he might lay his hands on them and pray; but the disciples rebuked them. Not that they were unwilling for them to be blessed by both the hand and the voice of the Savior, but because, not yet having the fullest faith, they thought that he, after the manner of men, was being wearied by the importunity of those who brought them.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.18.16":["Let the little children be, and do not forbid them to come to me; for of such is the kingdom of heaven. He said significantly of such, not of these, to show that it is not age that reigns but character, and that the reward is promised to those who have a like innocence and simplicity, the Apostle too agreeing with the same sentence: Brothers, do not become children in your understanding, but be little children in malice; but in understanding, be perfect.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.18.17":["Amen I say to you: Unless you are turned and become like little children, you will not enter the kingdom of heaven. The apostles are not commanded to have the age of little children but their innocence, and that what those possess by their years, these should possess by effort, so that they may be little children in malice, not in wisdom.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.18.18":["Behold, one came up and said to him: Good Teacher, what good shall I do that I may have eternal life? This man who asks how he may attain eternal life is young and rich and proud, and, according to another evangelist, he asks not with the desire of a learner but as one testing.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.18.19":["Why do you ask me about the good? One is good, God. Because he had called him a good teacher and had not confessed him God or the Son of God, he learns that a man, however holy, is not good in comparison with God, of whom it is said: Confess to the Lord, for he is good. But lest anyone think that, in God's being called good, the Son of God is excluded from goodness, let us read in another place: The good shepherd lays down his life for his sheep; and in the prophet, a good spirit and a good land. So the Savior too did not refuse the testimony to his goodness, but excluded the error of a teacher apart from God.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.18.20":["If you wish to enter into life, keep the commandments. He says to him: Which? And Jesus said: You shall not commit murder, you shall not commit adultery, and the rest, and you shall love your neighbor as yourself. That this young man is a tempter we can prove from this too, that when the Lord said to him: If you wish to come to life, keep the commandments, he again asks deceitfully which those commandments are, as if he had not read them himself, or as if the Lord could command things contrary to God.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.18.21":["The young man says to him: All these I have kept; what do I still lack? The young man lies. For if he had fulfilled in deed what is set down in the commandments: You shall love your neighbor as yourself, how is it that afterward, on hearing: Go, sell what you have and give to the poor, he went away sad, because he had many possessions?\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.18.22":["Jesus said to him: If you wish to be perfect, go, sell what you have and give to the poor, and you will have treasure in heaven; and come, follow me. It is in our power whether we wish to be perfect. Yet whoever wishes to be perfect ought to sell what he has, and not to sell in part, as Ananias and Sapphira did, but to sell all, and when he has sold it, to give everything to the poor, and so to prepare for himself a treasure in the kingdom of heaven. Nor is this enough for perfection unless, after despising riches, he follows the Savior, that is, leaving evil things behind, does good. For a purse is more easily despised than the will. Many who leave riches behind do not follow the Lord. But he follows the Lord who is his imitator and walks in his footsteps. For he who says he believes in Christ ought himself also to walk as he walked.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.18.23":["He went away sad, for he had many possessions. This is the sadness that leads to death, and the cause of the sadness is given: that he had many possessions, that is, thorns and thistles, which choked the Lord's seed.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.18.24":["And Jesus said to his disciples: Amen I say to you that a rich man will enter the kingdom of heaven with difficulty. And how did Abraham, Isaac and Jacob, rich men, enter the kingdom of heaven, and how are Matthew and Zacchaeus in the Gospel proclaimed by the Lord's testimony, once they had left their riches? But it must be considered that at the time they entered they had ceased to be rich. So long, then, as they are rich, they will not enter. And yet, because riches are despised with difficulty, he did not say: It is impossible for the rich to enter the kingdom of heaven, but with difficulty. Where difficulty is stated, impossibility is not alleged, but rarity is shown.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.18.25":["And again I say to you: It is easier for a camel to pass through the eye of a needle than for a rich man to enter the kingdom of heaven. By this saying it is shown that it is not difficult but impossible. For if, as a camel cannot enter through the eye of a needle, so a rich man cannot enter the kingdom of heaven, none of the rich will be saved. But if we read in Isaiah how the camels of Midian and Ephah come to Jerusalem with gifts and offerings, and how those who were formerly bent and twisted by the perversity of vices enter the gates of Jerusalem, we shall see how these camels too, to which the rich are compared, when they have laid down the heavy burden of sins and the crookedness of the whole body, can enter by the narrow and strait way that leads to life.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.18.26-27":["But when the disciples asked, marveling at the severity of the saying: Who then will be saved? he tempered the severity of the sentence by his clemency, saying: What with men is impossible is possible with God.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.18.28":["Then Peter answered and said to him: Behold, we have left all things and have followed you; what then will there be for us? Great confidence! Peter was a fisherman, he had not been rich, he sought his food by hand and craft, and yet he speaks confidently: we have left all things. And because merely leaving is not enough, he joins what is perfect: and have followed you. We have done what you commanded; what reward, then, will you give us?\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.18.29-30":["And everyone who has left house or brothers or sisters or father or mother or wife or children or fields for my name's sake will receive a hundredfold and will possess eternal life. But many who are first will be last, and the last first. This passage agrees with that sentence in which the Savior says: I came not to send peace but a sword; for I came to separate a man from his father, and the mother from the daughter, and the daughter-in-law from the mother-in-law; and a man's enemies are those of his own household. Those, therefore, who for the faith of Christ and the preaching of the Gospel have despised all affections, and riches, and the pleasures of the world, these will receive a hundredfold and will possess eternal life. On the occasion of this sentence some bring in a thousand years after the resurrection, saying that then a hundredfold of all the things we have given up, and eternal life, will be rendered to us, not understanding that, even if in other things the promise is fitting, in the case of wives the baseness is plain: that he who has given up one for the Lord should receive a hundred in the future. This, then, is the sense: He who has given up carnal things for the Savior will receive spiritual things, which in comparison and by their worth will be as if the number one hundred were compared with a small number. Hence the Apostle too, who had given up only one house and the small fields of one province, says: As having nothing and possessing all things.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.18.31-33":["And Jesus, going up to Jerusalem, took his twelve disciples and said to them: Behold, we are going up to Jerusalem, and the Son of Man will be handed over to the chiefs of the elders and to the scribes, and they will condemn him to death and hand him over to the Gentiles to be mocked and scourged and crucified, and on the third day he will rise again. He had often told the disciples this very thing, but because, with many matters discussed in between, what they had heard could slip from their memory, being about to go to Jerusalem and to take the apostles with him, he prepares them for the trial, so that when persecution and the ignominy of the cross come they may not be scandalized.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.18.35-38":["But there were also two blind men by the way. He calls them blind who could not yet say: In your light we shall see light; by the way, because they seemed indeed to have knowledge of the law, but did not know the way, which is Christ. Most take them to be the Pharisees and the Sadducees, but others both peoples, of the Old Testament and of the New, because the one following the written law, the other the natural law, was blind without Christ. These, because they could not see by themselves, heard the proclamations of the Savior and confessed him the son of David.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.18.35":["And as they were going out from Jericho, a great crowd followed him. And behold, two blind men sitting by the way heard that Jesus was passing by, and they cried out, saying: Lord, have mercy on us, son of David. But the crowd rebuked them, that they should be silent. But they cried out the more, saying: Lord, have mercy on us, son of David. There were many robbers in Jericho who were accustomed to kill and wound those going out and going down from Jerusalem; therefore the Lord came to Jericho with his disciples, to free the wounded and to draw a great crowd along with him. And indeed, when he wished to go out from Jericho, a great crowd followed him. If he had stayed in Jerusalem and had never come down to lowly places, the crowd would to this day be sitting in darkness and in the shadow of death.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.18.39":["But if both blind men are referred to the people of the Jews, what follows: the crowd rebuked them, is to be understood of the heathen, whom the Apostle warns not to boast and be proud against their root, but, since they themselves, through the error of the former, have been grafted from the wild olive into the good olive, they ought by no means to envy the salvation of the former.\n— Commentary on Matthew","Have mercy on us, son of David. They are rebuked by the crowds and nonetheless are not silent, but repeat the very same thing all the more often, to show a desire full of the true light.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.18.40-41":["Jesus stood still and called them and said. They were blind; they did not know where to go; they could not follow the Savior. There are many pits in Jericho, many crags and precipices sloping down into the depths; therefore the Lord stands still so that they may be able to come, and orders them to be called so that the crowds may not hinder them, and asks, as though not knowing, what they want, so that from the blind men's answer the infirmity may be made plain and the power may be recognized from the remedy.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.18.43":["And Jesus, having pity on them, touched their eyes, and at once they saw and followed him. He touches their eyes, and the craftsman supplies what nature had not given, or at any rate what infirmity had taken away; mercy bestows it, and at once they saw and followed him. Those who before sat huddled in Jericho and knew only how to cry out afterward follow Jesus not so much with their feet as with their virtues.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.19.12-13":["For just as a man setting out on a journey called his servants and handed over his goods to them, and to one he gave five talents, to another two, and to another one. This man, the householder, is beyond doubt Christ, who, ascending as victor to the Father after the resurrection, called the apostles and handed over to them the teaching of the Gospel, giving more to one and less to another, not out of lavishness or stinginess but according to the strength of those who received it, just as the Apostle too says that he gave milk to drink to those who could not take solid food. In short, he receives with like joy both the one who had made ten out of five talents and the one who had made four out of two, considering not the size of the profit but the will behind the effort. In the five and the two and the one talent let us understand either the diverse graces that have been handed over to each, or else in the first all the senses tested, in the second understanding and works, in the third reason, by which we men are set apart from the beasts.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.19.15":["But after a long time the lord of those servants came. A great time lies between the ascension of the Savior and his second coming. But if the apostles are to render an account and to rise again under fear of the judge, what ought we to do?\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.19.17":["His lord said to him: Well done, good and faithful servant; because you were faithful over a few things, I will set you over many; enter into the joy of your lord. To both servants, as I have already said before, both the one who had made ten out of five talents and the one who had made four out of two, the same word of the householder speaks kindly. And it is to be noted that all the things we have in the present, though they seem great and very many, are yet small and few in comparison with the things to come. Enter, he says, into the joy of your lord, and receive what neither eye has seen nor ear heard, nor has it ascended into the heart of man. But what greater thing can be given to a faithful servant than to be with his lord and to see the joy of his lord?\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.19.20-21":["And he too who had received one talent came forward and said: Lord, I know that you are a hard man; you reap where you did not sow and gather where you did not scatter; and being afraid I went off and hid your talent in the earth; here you have what is yours. Truly, what is written: To make excuses in sins, befell this servant also, so that to sloth and negligence the charge of pride was added as well. For he who ought simply to have confessed his idleness and entreated the householder, on the contrary slanders him and says that he acted on prudent counsel, lest while he sought profit on the money he should put even the principal at risk.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.19.20":["But he who had received one went off, dug in the earth and hid his lord's money. The wicked servant, by earthly works and the pleasure of the world, neglected and defiled the commandments of God; although in another evangelist it is written that he tied it up in a napkin, that is, that by living softly and delicately he enfeebled the teaching of the householder.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.19.22-24":["But his lord answered and said to him: Wicked and lazy servant, you knew that I reap where I do not sow and gather where I did not scatter; you ought therefore to have put my money with the money-changers, and on coming I would surely have received what is mine with interest; take therefore the talent from him and give it to him who has ten talents. What he had thought he said by way of excuse is turned into his own guilt. He is called a wicked servant because he slanders his lord, lazy because he would not double the talent, so that in the one he is condemned for pride, in the other for negligence. If, he says, you knew that I am hard and cruel, and that I go after what belongs to others and reap there where I have not sown, why did not a thought of this kind strike fear into you, so that you would know that I would seek out my own more diligently, and would give my money, or silver, to the money-changers? For the Greek word ἀργύριον signifies both. The words, he says, of the Lord are pure words, silver tried by fire, proved, purged of earth sevenfold. The money, then, and the silver is the preaching of the Gospel and the divine word, which ought to have been given to the money-changers and bankers, that is, either to the other teachers, as the apostles also did, ordaining presbyters and bishops in the several provinces, or to all believers who can double the money and return it with interest, so that whatever they had learned by word they might fulfill in deed. Now the talent is taken away and given to him who had made ten talents, so that we may understand that, although the Lord's joy over the labor of both is equal, that is, both of him who had doubled five into ten and of him who had doubled two into four, yet a greater reward is owed to him who had labored more over the Lord's money. Hence the Apostle too says: Honor the presbyters who are truly presbyters, especially those who labor in the word of God. From the fact that the wicked servant dared to say: you reap where you did not sow and gather where you did not scatter, we understand that the Lord accepts the good life even of Gentiles and philosophers, and regards in one way those who act justly and in another those who act unjustly, and that, in comparison with him who serves the natural law, those who neglect the written law are condemned.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.19.26":["For to everyone who has will be given, and he will abound; but from him who does not have, even what he seems to have will be taken away from him. Many, though they are wise by nature and have keenness of mind, if they have been negligent and have spoiled the good of nature by sloth, in comparison with one who is somewhat slower but has made up by labor and diligence for what he had less of, lose the good of nature and see the reward that had been promised them pass to others. It can also be understood thus: to him who has faith and a good will in the Lord, even if, being a man, he has had something less in works, it will be given by the good judge; but he who has not had faith will lose even the other virtues that he seemed to possess by nature. And elegantly too: even what he seems, he says, to have will be taken away from him. For whatever is without faith in Christ ought not to be credited to him who has badly misused it, but to him who granted the good of nature even to a wicked servant.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.19.29-33":["And when they drew near to Jerusalem and came to Bethphage, to the Mount of Olives, then Jesus sent two disciples, saying to them: Go into the village that is opposite you, and at once you will find an ass tied and a colt with her; loose them and bring them to me; and if anyone says anything to you, say: The Lord has need of them, and at once he will let them go. He goes out from Jericho, having led out from there very many crowds, and, having restored health to the blind, he draws near to Jerusalem, enriched with great wares; having restored salvation to those who believe, he desires to enter the city of peace and the place of the vision of God and the citadel of the watchman. And when he had drawn near to Jerusalem and had come to Bethphage, to the house of jaws (which was a little village of the priests and bore the type of confession and was situated on the Mount of Olives, where is the light of knowledge, where is rest from labors and sorrows), he sent his two disciples, θεωρητικὴν καὶ πρακτικήν, that is, knowledge and works, to enter the village, and he said to them: Go into the village that is opposite you. For it was opposite the apostles and would not accept the yoke of teachings. And at once, he says, you will find an ass tied and a colt with her; loose them and bring them to me. The ass was tied with many chains of sins; the colt too, frisky and impatient of the bridle, was with its mother; according to the Gospel of Luke it had many masters, being subject not to one error and doctrine; and yet the many masters who claimed for themselves an unlawful power, seeing that the true Lord had come and his servants who had been sent to loose them, do not dare to resist. But what the ass and the foal of the ass are, we say below.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.19.35":["And the disciples went and did as Jesus had commanded them, and they brought the ass and the colt and put their garments upon them and made him sit on top. This colt and this ass, on which the apostles spread their garments so that Jesus may sit more softly, were naked before the coming of the Savior, and, with many claiming dominion over them, they shivered without covering; but after they had received the apostolic garments and had been made more beautiful, they had the Lord as their rider. Now the apostolic garment can be understood either as the teaching of the virtues or as the exposition of the Scriptures, or as the varieties of the doctrines of the Church; unless the soul has been instructed and adorned with these, it does not deserve to have the Lord as its rider.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.19.36":["And a very great crowd spread their garments on the road. See the difference of each person. The apostles put their garments upon the ass; the crowd, which is of lower rank, spreads them beneath the ass's feet, so that it may nowhere strike against a stone, may not tread on a thorn, may not slip into a pit.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.19.37-38":["And the crowds that went before and that followed cried out, saying: Hosanna to the son of David; blessed is he who is to come in the name of the Lord; hosanna in the highest. Since the history is plain, let us follow the spiritual order of discussion. The crowds that had gone out from Jericho and had followed the Savior and his disciples, after they saw the foal of the ass loosed, which before had been tied, and adorned with the garments of the apostles, and the Lord Jesus sitting upon it, laid their garments beneath and spread the road with branches of trees; and when they had done all this in deed, they also bestow the testimony of the voice, and those going before and those following resound not with a brief and silent confession but with the fullest shout: Hosanna to the son of David; blessed is he who is to come in the name of the Lord. And in saying: The crowds that went before and that followed, he shows that both peoples, those who believed in the Lord before the Gospel and those who believed after the Gospel, praise Jesus with the harmonious voice of confession, and that, after the example of the parable above, the workers of different hours receive the one reward of faith. Moreover, what the following words mean: Hosanna to the son of David, I remember that I said many years ago, in a short letter to Damasus, then bishop of the city of Rome, and now I will touch on it briefly. In Psalm 117 (118), which was plainly written about the coming of the Savior, among other things we read this too: The stone which the builders rejected, this has become the head of the corner; this was done by the Lord, this is wonderful in our eyes; this is the day which the Lord has made; let us exult and rejoice in it; and at once there is joined: O Lord, save me; O Lord, prosper well; blessed is he who is to come in the name of the Lord; we have blessed you from the house of the Lord, and the rest. Where the LXX translators have: O Lord, save me, in the Hebrew we read: Anna adonai osi anna, which Symmachus translated more plainly, saying: I beseech you, Lord, save me, I beseech you. Let no one, then, think that the word is composed of two words, namely a Greek and a Hebrew one, but that it is wholly Hebrew and signifies that the coming of Christ is the salvation of the world. Hence there also follows: Blessed is he who is to come in the name of the Lord, the Savior too confirming this very thing in the Gospel: I have come in the name of my Father, and you have not received me; another will come in his own name, and you will receive him. Further, by what is joined: Hosanna (that is, salvation) in the highest, it is clearly shown that the coming of Christ is the salvation not only of men but of the whole world, joining earthly things to heavenly, that every knee should bow to him, of those in heaven and on earth and in the lower world.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.19.40":["Something of this kind the Lord too said in the Gospel against the Pharisees, who reproached him for not rebuking the children who acclaimed him: Hosanna in the highest to the son of David, blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord; Hosanna in the highest. Have you not read, he says, that it is written: Out of the mouth of infants and sucklings you have perfected praise? And if these are silent, the stones will cry out. For although very many think it is to be understood thus, if the Jews are silent, the multitude of the nations will confess me, yet this is the more expressive and truer understanding: even if men were silent, and the envious tongue would not speak of the multitude of my signs, yet the stones themselves, and the foundations of the walls, and the structure of the walls will be able to proclaim aloud my greatness.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"LUK.19.45-46":["And Jesus entered the temple of God and cast out all who sold and bought in the temple, and he overturned the tables of the money-changers and the chairs of those who sold doves, and he says to them: It is written: My house shall be called a house of prayer, but you have made it a den of robbers. Accompanied by the crowd of believers, which had spread its garments so that the colt might go with unhurt foot, Jesus enters the temple and casts out all who sold and bought in the temple, and he overturned the tables of the money-changers and scattered the chairs of those who sold doves, and he said to them, bringing forward testimony from the holy Scriptures, that his Father's house ought to be a house of prayer, not a den of robbers or a house of trade, as is written in another evangelist. And this must first be known: that, in accordance with the commands of the law, since the temple of the Lord was the most august in the whole world, and the people of the Jews flocked there from almost all regions, countless victims were sacrificed, especially on feast days, of bulls, rams and goats, while the poor, so as not to be without a sacrifice, offered young doves and turtledoves; and it often happened that those who had come from far away did not have victims. So the priests devised how they might make plunder of the people, and they sold all the animals that were needed for the sacrifices, so that they might both sell to those who did not have them and themselves receive back again what had been bought. This scheme of theirs was frequently defeated by the poverty of those who came, who lacked the means and not only had no victims but had not even the wherewithal to buy birds and cheap little gifts. So they also set up money-changers to lend money on security; but because it was commanded in the law that no one should take interest, and money lent at interest could be of no use when it brought no profit and sometimes lost the principal, they devised yet another trick: in place of money-changers to make collybists, a word whose proper sense the Latin language does not express. Among them collyba is the name for what we call sweetmeats or cheap little gifts, for example roasted chickpeas and raisins and fruits of various kinds. So, because the collybists who had lent the money could not take interest, in place of interest they took various kinds of goods, so as to exact in these things, which are bought with coins, what was not lawful in coin; as if Ezekiel had not guarded against this very thing, saying: You shall not take interest and increase. The Lord, seeing such trade, or robbery, in his Father's house, roused by the ardor of the spirit, according to what is written in Psalm 68 (69): The zeal of your house has eaten me up, made himself a whip of cords and cast so great a multitude of men out of the temple, saying: It is written: My house shall be called a house of prayer, but you have made it a den of robbers. For he is a robber, and turns the temple of God into a den of robbers, who pursues gain from religion, and whose worship is not so much the worship of God as an occasion for trade. So much according to the history. But according to the mystical senses Jesus daily enters his Father's temple and casts out all, bishops and presbyters and deacons as well as laymen and the whole crowd, from his Church, and holds those who sell and those who buy alike guilty of one crime; for it is written: Freely you have received, freely give. He also overturns the tables of the money-changers. Note that because of the avarice of the priests the altars of God are called the tables of money-changers. And he overturns the chairs of those who sell doves, who sell the grace of the Holy Spirit and do everything to devour the peoples subject to them, of whom it is said: Who devour my people like food of bread. According to the simple understanding, the doves were not on chairs but in cages, unless perhaps the dealers in doves sat on chairs, which is utterly absurd, because in chairs it is rather the dignity of teachers that is indicated, a dignity that is brought to nothing when it has been mixed with gain. What we have said of the churches, let each one understand of himself as well. For the Apostle Paul says: You are the temple of God, and the Holy Spirit dwells in you. Let there be no trade in the house of our heart, no commerce of selling and buying, no greed for gifts, lest Jesus enter angry and stern and cleanse his temple in no other way than by applying the whip, so that out of a den of robbers and a house of trade he may make a house of prayer.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.20.1-2":["And when he had come into the temple, the chief priests and the elders of the people came to him as he was teaching, saying: By what power do you do these things, and who gave you this power? In different words they build up the same slander as above, when they said: By Beelzebub, the prince of demons, this man casts out demons. For when they say: By what power do you do these things, they doubt the power of God and want it to be understood that what he does is the devil's; and adding further: who gave you this power, they most plainly deny the Son of God, whom they think does signs not by his own powers but by another's.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.20.3-7":["Jesus answered and said to them: I also will ask you one word, and if you tell it to me, I also will tell you by what power I do these things. The baptism of John, where was it from, from heaven or from men? and the rest. This is what is commonly said: Into a bad knot in a tree a bad nail or wedge must be driven. The Lord could have refuted the slander of those tempting him by an open answer, but he prudently asks a question, so that they may be condemned either by their own silence or by their own statement. For if they had answered that the baptism of John was from heaven, as they, wise in malice, had worked it out among themselves, the reply followed: Why then were you not baptized by John? If they had chosen to say that it was put together by human deceit and had had nothing divine, they feared a riot of the people. For all the assembled multitudes had received the baptism of John and held him thus, as a prophet. So the most impious faction answered and used a word of humility, by which it said that it did not know, to fit together its snares.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.20.8":["He too said to them: Neither do I tell you by what power I do these things. They lied in answering that they did not know; it followed, then, according to their answer, that the Lord too should say: Neither do I know; but the truth cannot lie; and he said: Neither do I tell you. By this he shows both that they knew but would not answer and that he knew and for this reason would not tell, because they kept silent about what they knew; and at once he brings in a parable by which he both convicts them of impiety and teaches that the kingdom of God is to be transferred to the Gentiles.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.20.9":["Hear another parable: There was a man, a householder, who planted a vineyard and set a hedge around it and dug a winepress in it and built a tower, and he let it out to farmers and went abroad. This is what the Lord signified with a saying taken from a proverb: It is hard to kick against the goad. The chief priests and the elders of the people, who had asked the Lord: By what power do you do these things, and who gave you this power, and had wished to catch Wisdom in a word, are overcome by their own art and hear in parables what they did not deserve to hear openly. This man, the householder, is the same who had two sons, and who in another parable hired workers for his vineyard; who planted the vineyard of which Isaiah too speaks most fully in a song, bringing in at the end: The vineyard of the Lord of hosts is the house of Israel; and in the psalm: A vineyard, he says, you transplanted from Egypt; you cast out the nations and planted it. And he set a hedge around it, either the wall of the city or the help of the angels. And dug a winepress in it, either the altar or those winepresses with which three psalms are marked in their title, the eighth, the eightieth (81st) and the eighty-third (84th). And built a tower, no doubt the temple, of which it is said through Micah: And you, cloudy tower of the daughter of Zion. And he let it out to farmers, whom elsewhere he called the workers of the vineyard, who had been hired at the first, third, sixth and ninth and eleventh hour. And went abroad, not by change of place, for from where can God be absent, by whom all things are filled, and who says through Jeremiah: I am a God who draws near, and not from afar, says the Lord? But he seems to go away from the vineyard so as to leave the vinedressers free choice in their working.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.20.10-12":["And when the time of fruits drew near, he sent his servants to the farmers to receive its fruits; and the farmers, seizing his servants, beat one, killed another, and stoned another. He had given them the law and had ordered them to work in this vineyard, so that they might show the fruit of the law in works. Afterward he sent servants to them, whom they seized and either beat, like Jeremiah, or killed, like Isaiah, or stoned, like Naboth and Zechariah, whom they slew between the temple and the altar. Let us read Paul's letter to the Hebrews, and from it we learn most fully which of the Lord's servants suffered how much.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.20.12-13":["But last of all he sent his son to them, saying: They will revere my son. In what we read above: Again he sent other servants, more than the first, and they did the same to them, he shows the patience of the householder, in that he sent more often, to provoke the wicked tenants to repentance; but they stored up for themselves wrath on the day of wrath. Moreover, what is joined: They will revere my son, does not come from ignorance. For what could the householder not know, who in this place is understood as God the Father? But God is always said to be in doubt, so that free will may be kept for man. Let us question Arius and Eunomius. See, the Father is said not to know, and he tempers his statement, and, as far as lies in you, he is proved to have lied. Whatever they answer on behalf of the Father, they will understand this of the Son, who says that he does not know the day of the consummation.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.20.13":["And you shall say to them: Thus says the Lord God; if perhaps even they will hear, and if perhaps they will be quiet. Something similar is written in Jeremiah too: If perhaps they will hear and repent, and in the Gospel: Perhaps they will respect my son. Now God speaks these words with the disposition of one who is in doubt, so that they may show the free choice of man, lest the foreknowledge of future things make immutable, whether for evil or for good, what God knew would come to be: for it is not because he knows what is to come that we must necessarily do what he foreknew; rather, what we are going to do by our own will, he, as God, knows will come to be.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"LUK.20.15-16":["When therefore the lord of the vineyard comes, what will he do to those tenants, and the rest. The Lord asks them, not because he does not know what they will answer, but so that they may be condemned by their own answer. Now the vineyard has been let out to us, and let out on this condition: that we render the Lord the fruit in its seasons and know at each time what we ought either to say or to do.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.20.15":["Seizing him, they cast him out of the vineyard and killed him. The Apostle too says that the Lord was crucified outside the gate. But we can also understand it otherwise: that he was cast out of the vineyard and killed there so that, with the Gentiles receiving him, the vineyard might be let out to others.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.20.17":["Jesus says to them: Have you never read in the Scriptures: The stone which the builders rejected, this has become the head of the corner; this was done by the Lord, this is wonderful in our eyes? By various parables and different discourses the same things are woven together. For those whom above he had called workers and vinedressers and farmers, he now calls builders, that is, masons. Hence the Apostle too says: You are God's tillage, God's building. These masons, then, just as the vinedressers receive the vineyard, so received the stone, to place it either in the foundations, according to the architect Paul, or in the corner, so that it might join together two walls, that is, both peoples; and this stone, rejected by them, has become the head of the corner; and this was done by the Lord, not by human powers but by the might of God. Of this stone of help Peter too speaks confidently: This is the stone which was rejected by you builders, which has become the head of the corner; and Isaiah: Behold, he says, I will lay in the foundations of Zion a stone, chosen, precious, a cornerstone, and whoever believes in it will not be confounded.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.20.18":["Whoever falls on this stone will be broken, but on whomever it falls it will crush him. It is one thing to offend Christ by evil works, another to deny him. He who is a sinner and yet believes in him falls indeed on the stone and is broken, but is not utterly crushed; for he is kept through patience for salvation. But on whomever it falls, that is, on whom the stone itself rushes down, and who has wholly denied Christ, it will so crush him that not even a potsherd remains with which a little water might be drawn.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.20.19":["And when the chief priests and the Pharisees had heard his parables, they knew that he was speaking about them; and seeking to seize him, they feared the crowds, since they held him as a prophet. Although they were hard of heart and dulled by unbelief and impiety toward the Son of God, still they could not deny plain propositions, and they understood that all the Lord's sayings were directed against them. Hence they wished indeed to kill him, but they feared the crowds, because they held him as a prophet. The crowd is always fickle and does not persist in its purposed will, and, like the waves and the various winds, it is drawn this way and that. The one whom they now venerate and honor as a prophet, afterward they shout against: Crucify, crucify such a man.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.20.20":["Then the Pharisees went off and took counsel how to catch him in his speech, and they send their disciples with the Herodians, saying. Not long before, under Caesar Augustus, Judea, subjected to the Romans when the census was held throughout the whole world, had been made tributary, and there was great sedition among the people: some said that for the security and quiet for which the Romans fought on behalf of all, the tributes ought to be paid; but the Pharisees, who applauded themselves in their righteousness, strove on the contrary that the people of God, which paid tithes and gave firstfruits and the other things that are written in the law, ought not to be subject to human laws. Caesar Augustus had established Herod, the son of Antipater, a foreigner and a proselyte, as king over the Jews, to preside over the tributes and obey the Roman empire. The Pharisees therefore send their disciples with the Herodians, that is, with Herod's soldiers, or with those whom the Pharisees, in mockery, because they paid tributes to the Romans, called Herodians and not men devoted to divine worship. Some of the Latins absurdly think that the Herodians were those who believed Herod to be the Christ, something we read nowhere at all.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.20.21-22":["Teacher, we know that you are truthful and teach the way of God in truth, and you have no care for anyone; for you do not regard the person of men. Tell us therefore what you think: Is it lawful to give tax to Caesar or not? The flattering and deceitful question lures the one answering into fearing God rather than Caesar and saying that the tributes ought not to be paid, so that the Herodians, hearing it, might at once seize him as the leader of a sedition against the Romans.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.20.23":["But Jesus, knowing their wickedness, said: Why do you tempt me, hypocrites? The first virtue of one who answers is to know the mind of those who question him, and to call them not disciples but tempters. A hypocrite, then, is the name for one who is one thing and pretends another, that is, does one thing in deed and puts forward another with his voice.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.20.24-25":["They say to him: Caesar's. Then he said to them: Render therefore the things that are Caesar's to Caesar, and the things that are God's to God. Let us not think that Augustus is signified by Caesar, but Tiberius, his stepson, who had succeeded to his stepfather's place, and under whom the Lord also suffered. But all the Roman kings, from the first, Gaius Caesar, who had seized the empire, were called Caesars. Moreover, as for his saying: Render the things that are Caesar's to Caesar, that is, coin, tribute and money, and the things that are God's to God, let us understand tithes, firstfruits, oblations and victims; just as he himself both renders tributes for himself and Peter, and renders to God the things that are God's, doing the will of the Father.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.20.24":["Show me the coin of the tax. And they brought him a denarius. Wisdom always acts wisely, so that tempters are refuted above all by their own words. Show me, he says, the denarius. This is the kind of coin that was reckoned at ten coins and bore the image of Caesar.\n— Commentary on Matthew","And Jesus said to them: Whose image and superscription is this? Let those who think that the Savior's questioning is ignorance and not dispensation learn from the present passage that Jesus certainly could have known whose image was on the coin; but he asks so that he may answer their words fittingly.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.20.26":["And hearing it they marveled. Those who ought to have believed marveled at such great wisdom, because their cunning had found no opening for laying a snare.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.20.27":["On that day the Sadducees, who say that there is no resurrection, came to him. There were two heresies among the Jews, one of the Pharisees and the other of the Sadducees. The Pharisees held up a righteousness of traditions and observances, which they call deuteroses, and hence they were also called 'divided' by the people; while the Sadducees, which is interpreted 'just', themselves also claimed for themselves what they were not. And whereas the former believed and confessed the resurrection of both body and soul, and angels and spirit, the latter, according to the Acts of the Apostles, denied it all. These are the two houses of which Isaiah teaches quite plainly that they would stumble on the stone of stumbling.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.20.28-32":["And they questioned him, saying: Teacher, Moses said: If anyone dies not having a son, his brother is to marry his wife and raise up seed for his brother. Now there were among us seven brothers, and the first, having married a wife, died, and the rest. Those who did not believe in the resurrection of bodies, and thought that the soul perished together with the bodies, aptly invent a fable of this kind, to convict of madness those who assert the resurrection of the dead. But it may be that this really happened at some time among their people.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.20.33":["In the resurrection, therefore, whose wife of the seven will she be? For they all had her. They set the foulness of the fable against it in order to deny the truth of the resurrection.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.20.35-36":["For in the resurrection they will neither marry nor be married. Latin usage does not correspond to the Greek idiom. For properly it is women who are said to wed, and men to take wives. But let us understand it as said simply, so that 'marry' is written of the men and 'be married' of the wives. Thus in the resurrection they will not marry nor be married: therefore bodies will rise which are able to marry and be married. For no one says of a stone or a tree or of those things that have no genital members that they do not marry nor are married, but of those who, though they could marry, yet for another reason do not marry. But as for what is added:\n\nBut they are like the angels of God in heaven, a spiritual way of life is promised.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.20.37-38":["But concerning the resurrection of the dead, have you not read what was spoken by God, who says to you: I am the God of Abraham and the God of Isaac and the God of Jacob? He is not the God of the dead but of the living. To prove the truth of the resurrection he could have used other, much plainer examples, among which is this one: The dead shall be raised, and those who are in the tombs shall rise again, and in another place: Many of those who sleep in the dust of the earth shall arise, some to life, others to reproach and everlasting confusion. It is asked, therefore, what the Lord meant by bringing forward this testimony, which seems ambiguous or not sufficiently pertinent to the truth of the resurrection: I am the God of Abraham and the God of Isaac and the God of Jacob, and why, as though by bringing it forward he had proved what he wished, he at once added: He is not the God of the dead but of the living. The crowds standing around, too, recognizing the mystery of this matter, marveled at the teaching of his answer. We said above that the Sadducees, confessing neither angel nor spirit nor the resurrection of bodies, also preached the destruction of souls. They received only the five books of Moses, rejecting the oracles of the prophets. It would therefore have been foolish to bring forward testimonies from a source whose authority they did not follow. Moreover, to prove the eternity of souls he sets down an example from Moses: I am the God of Abraham and the God of Isaac and the God of Jacob, and at once adds: He is not the God of the dead but of the living, so that, once he had proved that souls endure after death (for it could not be that he was the God of those who did not subsist at all), the resurrection of the bodies too might consequently be introduced, the bodies which together with the souls did good or evil. The apostle Paul pursues this passage more fully in the last part of the first letter to the Corinthians.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.20.40":["And no one could answer him a word, nor did anyone dare from that day to ask him any more. The Pharisees and Sadducees, seeking an occasion for false accusation and to find some word that would lie open to their snares, because they have been refuted in argument, ask no further, but seize him quite openly and hand him over to the Roman power. From this we understand that the poisons of envy can indeed be overcome, but with difficulty are laid to rest.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.20.41-44":["Now when the Pharisees were gathered together, Jesus asked them, saying: What do you think about the Christ? Whose son is he? They say to him: David's. He says to them: How then does David in the Spirit call him Lord, saying: The Lord said to my Lord: Sit at my right until I make your enemies the footstool of your feet. Those who had gathered to tempt Jesus, and were striving to catch the truth with a deceitful question, provided the occasion for their own refutation, and they are asked about the Christ, whose son he is. The question of Jesus profits us against the Jews even to this day. For these too, who confess that the Christ is to come, assert that he is a mere man and a holy man of the line of David. Let us therefore, taught by the Lord, ask them: If he is a mere man and only the son of David, how does David call him his Lord, not in the uncertainty of error nor by his own will, but in the Holy Spirit? Now the testimony that he set down is taken from Psalm 109 (110). David's Lord, therefore, he is called not according to that by which he was born of him, but according to that by which, born of the Father, he always was, going before the very father of his flesh. The Jews, to make sport of the truth of the question, invent many frivolous things, asserting that it was Abraham's home-born servant, whose son was Eliezer of Damascus, and that the psalm was written in his person, because after the slaughter of the five kings the Lord God said to his lord Abraham: Sit at my right hand until I make all your enemies the footstool of your feet. Let us ask them: How did God say to Abraham the words that follow: With you is the beginning in the day of your power, in the splendors of the saints; from the womb before the morning star I begot you; and: The Lord has sworn and will not repent: You are a priest for ever according to the order of Melchizedek, and let us compel them to answer how Abraham was begotten before the morning star and was a priest according to the order of Melchizedek, for whom Melchizedek offered bread and wine, and from whom he received tithes of the spoil.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.20.46":["And since they broaden their phylacteries beyond measure and make large fringes, courting glory from men, they are rebuked in the rest for seeking the first places at table at dinners and the first chairs in the synagogues, and for pursuing gluttony and glory in public, and for being called rabbi by men, which in the Latin tongue means master. In short, there follows:\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.21.2":["The quadrans is a kind of coin that contains two mites. Hence in one Gospel that poor widow woman is said to have put a quadrans into the corban, in another two mites; not that the Gospels are at variance, but because one quadrans contains two mites. This, then, is what he says: You will not come out of prison until you have paid even for the least sins.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.21.3-4":["And the workers, he says (or those who serve in the works of the city), shall be from all the tribes of Israel, so that no one may think himself exempt from the works of the city, but all may vie with one another in building up the Church — which we read was done in the tabernacle too, so that, according to the variety of their means, one offered gold and silver, scarlet, fine linen and purple, and various and diverse woofs, others skins and goats' hair; and, though the gifts were diverse according to the measure of their substance, the reward of the givers was one, nay rather greater for those who offered small things indeed but greater in proportion to their means, after the example of the poor widow who put two mites into the gifts of the treasury and was praised by the judgment of the Lord, who said: Truly I say to you that this poor little widow has put in more than all; for all these out of their abundance have put into the gifts of God, but she out of that which she lacks has put in all the living that she had —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"LUK.21.5-6":["And Jesus went out from the temple and was going away. And his disciples came to him to show him the buildings of the temple. But he answered and said to them: Do you see all these things? Amen I say to you: There shall not be left here a stone upon a stone that shall not be destroyed. According to the history the sense is plain. But as the Lord withdrew from the temple, all the buildings of the law and the structure of the commandments were so destroyed that nothing can be fulfilled by the Jews, and, the head having been taken away, all the members fight against one another.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.21.7":["But as he sat upon the Mount of Olives, the disciples came to him privately, saying: Tell us when these things will be, and what is the sign of your coming and of the consummation of the age? He sat on the Mount of Olives, where the true light of knowledge was being born; and the disciples, who longed to know the mysteries and the revelation of things to come, come to him privately and ask three things: at what time Jerusalem is to be destroyed, at what time Christ will come, at what time the consummation of the age will be.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.21.8":["For many will come in my name, saying: I am the Christ, and they will lead many astray. One of these is Simon the Samaritan, of whom we read in the Acts of the Apostles, who said that he was the great power of God, and who left behind, among other things written in his books, these words too: I am the word of God, I am the beautiful one, I the Paraclete, I the almighty, I all things of God. But John the apostle too says in his letter: You have heard that antichrist is to come, but now there are many antichrists. I think that all heresiarchs are antichrists and teach, under the name of Christ, the things that are contrary to Christ. Nor is it any wonder if we see some led astray by them, since the Lord said: and they will lead many astray.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.21.9":["For you are going to hear of battles and rumors of battles; see that you are not troubled; for these things must happen, but the end is not yet. So when we see these things happen, let us not think that the day of judgment is coming in, but that it is reserved for that time whose sign is clearly set out in what follows.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.21.10-11":["For nation will rise against nation and kingdom against kingdom, and there will be pestilences and famines and earthquakes in various places; all these are the beginnings of sorrows. I do not doubt that these things too which are written will come to pass according to the letter; but it seems to me that kingdom against kingdom, and the pestilences of those whose speech creeps like a cancer, and the famine of hearing the word of God, and the shaking of the whole earth and the separation from the true faith, are rather to be understood of the heretics, who, fighting against one another, bring about a victory for the Church.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.21.12":["Then they will deliver you up to tribulation and will kill you. Through the apostles the person of all believers is signified, not that the apostles are signified as going to be found in the body at that time.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.21.14-15":["But when they hand you over, do not think how or what you are to speak. He had said above: For they will hand you over in councils, and in their synagogues they will scourge you, and you will be led before governors and kings for my sake. Since, then, we are led before judges for Christ's sake, we ought to offer only our will for Christ. For the rest, Christ himself, who dwells in us, will speak for himself, and the grace of the Holy Spirit will be supplied in answering.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.21.16":["And brother will hand over brother to death, and father son, and sons will rise up against parents. This we see happen frequently in persecutions, and there is no faithful affection among those whose faith is different.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.21.21":["Then let those who are in Judea flee to the mountains, and let him who is on the roof not come down to take anything from his house, and let him who is in the field not turn back to take his tunic. The abomination of desolation can also be understood as every perverse doctrine; and when we see it standing in the holy place, that is, in the Church, and showing itself as God, we must flee from Judea to the mountains, that is, leaving behind the letter that kills and Jewish perversity, draw near to the eternal mountains from which God gives light wonderfully; and be on the roof and on the housetop, where the fiery darts of the devil cannot reach; and not come down and take anything from the house of our former way of life, nor seek the things behind, but rather sow in the field of the spiritual Scriptures so that we may gather fruit from it; nor take a second tunic, which the apostles are forbidden to have. On this passage, that is, on the abomination of desolation which was spoken of by Daniel the prophet standing in the holy place, Porphyry uttered many blasphemies against us in the thirteenth volume of his work; Eusebius, bishop of Caesarea, answered him in three volumes, the eighteenth, the nineteenth and the twentieth; Apollinaris too wrote most fully; and it was a superfluous attempt to want to discuss in one passage a matter that has been disputed in so many thousands of lines.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.21.23":["But woe to those who are pregnant and nursing in those days. Woe to those souls which have not brought their offspring to the perfect man, but have only the beginnings of faith, so that they need the nurture of teachers. This too can be said: that in the persecution of antichrist, or of the Roman captivity, those who were pregnant and nursing, weighed down by the burden of womb and of children, could not have an unencumbered flight.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.21.25-26":["But immediately after the tribulation of those days the sun will be darkened, and the moon will not give its light, and the stars of heaven will fall from heaven, and the powers of the heavens will be shaken. The sun and moon will be darkened and will not give their light, and the other stars will fall from heaven, and the powers of the heavens will be moved, not by a diminution of light, for we read, after all, that the sun will have sevenfold light, but because in comparison with the true light all things are dark to the sight. If, then, this sun which now glows red over the whole world, and the moon which is the second luminary, and the stars which are kindled for the solace of the night, and all the powers, which we understand as the multitudes of angels, will be reckoned as darkness at the coming of Christ, let the haughty brow of those be struck down who, thinking themselves holy, do not dread the presence of the judge.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.21.29-31":["Learn a parable from the fig tree. When its branch has already become tender and the leaves have come out, you know that summer is near, and the rest. Under the example of the tree he taught the coming of the consummation. Just as, he says, when the shoots on the fig tree have become tender, and the bud breaks into flower, and the bark brings forth leaves, you understand the coming of summer and the arrival of the west wind and of spring; so when you see all these things that are written, do not think that the consummation of the world is already present, but that certain preludes and forerunners, as it were, are coming to show that it is near and at the doors.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.21.32":["Amen I say to you, that this generation will not pass away until all these things happen. We said above that the good and, on the contrary, the wicked each form a single generation. Therefore it signifies either the whole race of men or specifically that of the Jews.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.21.33":["Heaven and earth will pass away, but my words will not pass away. Heaven and earth will pass away by a change, not by an abolition of themselves; otherwise how will the sun be darkened and the moon not give its light and the stars fall, if the heaven in which these things are, and the earth, will not exist?\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.22.1":["And it came to pass, when Jesus had finished all these sayings, he said to his disciples: You know that after two days the Passover will take place, and the Son of Man will be handed over to be crucified. Let those blush who think that the Savior feared death and said from dread of the passion: Father, if it can be done, let this cup pass from me. About to keep the Passover after two days, he knows that he is to be handed over to be crucified, and yet he does not avoid the snares, he does not flee in terror; so much so that, even when the others were unwilling to go, he goes on undaunted, when Thomas says: Let us go, that we too may die with him. And, wishing to put an end to the carnal festival and, as the shadow passed away, to give back the truth of the Passover, he said: With desire I have desired to eat this Passover with you before I suffer. For Christ our Passover has been sacrificed, if only we eat it in the unleavened bread of sincerity and truth. Moreover, as to his saying: After two days the Passover will take place, let us pass over the simple understanding and seek what is hallowed in it. After two days of the most brilliant light of the Old and the New Testament, the true Passover is celebrated for the world. The Passover (pascha), which in Hebrew is called phase, is named not from the passion, as most people think, but from the passing over, because the destroyer, seeing the blood on the doors of the Israelites, passed over and did not strike them, or else the Lord himself, bringing help to his people, walked above them. Read the book of Exodus, of which we shall treat more fully, if life is granted to us. But our passing over, that is, phase, is celebrated in this way: if, leaving behind earthly things and Egypt, we hasten to heavenly things.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.22.2":["Then the chief priests and the elders of the people gathered in the court of the chief priest, who was called Caiaphas; and they took counsel to seize Jesus by guile and kill him. Those who, with the Passover near, ought to have been preparing the victims, polishing the walls of the temple, sweeping the pavements, cleaning the vessels, and purifying themselves according to the rite of the law, so that they might become worthy to eat the lamb, gather together and enter into counsel how they may kill the Lord: not fearing sedition, as the simple wording shows, but taking care lest he be taken out of their hands by the help of the people.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.22.4-5":["And he said to them: What will you give me, and I will hand him over to you? But they fixed for him thirty pieces of silver. Unhappy Judas! The loss which he believed he had suffered from the pouring out of the ointment he wishes to make good with the price of his master. And yet he does not demand a fixed sum, so that the betrayal might at least seem profitable, but, as though handing over a cheap slave, he left it in the power of the buyers to give as much as they wished. Who fixed for him thirty pieces of silver. Joseph was not sold, as many think, for twenty pieces of gold, according to the LXX translators, but, according to the Hebrew truth, for twenty pieces of silver. For the servant could not be more precious than the master.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.22.7-9":["Now on the first day of the Unleavened Bread the disciples came to Jesus, saying: Where do you wish us to prepare for you to eat the Passover? The first day of the Unleavened Bread is the fourteenth day of the first month, when the lamb is sacrificed and the moon is at its fullest and leaven is cast out. And among those disciples who came to the Lord asking: Where do you wish us to prepare for you to eat the Passover, I reckon that Judas the betrayer was also present.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.22.10":["But Jesus said: Go into the city to a certain man. The new Scripture keeps the custom of the Old Testament. We often read: So-and-so said to so-and-so; and: In such and such a place, which in Hebrew is called phelmoni and helmoni; and yet the name of the persons and of the places is not given. And you will find, he says, a certain man carrying a jar of water. Their names were passed over for this reason: that a free occasion for the festival might be opened to all who are going to keep the Passover.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.22.12-13":["And the disciples did as Jesus had appointed them, and they prepared the Passover. In another evangelist it is written that they found a large upper room, furnished and cleaned, and there they made ready for him. But it seems to me that the upper room is to be understood as the spiritual law, which, going out from the narrowness of the letter, receives the Savior in a lofty place, Paul saying this very thing: that the things which he formerly reckoned as gain he despised as refuse and rubbish, so that he might prepare a lodging worthy of the Lord.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.22.14":["When evening had come, he reclined with the twelve disciples. Judas does everything so that suspicion of the betrayer may be removed.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.22.17-20":["And while they were dining, Jesus took bread and blessed and broke it, and gave it to his disciples and said: Take and eat, this is my body. And taking the cup he gave thanks and gave it to them, saying, and the rest. After the typical Passover had been fulfilled and he had eaten the flesh of the lamb with the apostles, he takes bread, which strengthens the heart of man, and passes over to the true sacrament of the Passover, so that, just as Melchizedek, the priest of the most high God, had done in prefiguration of it when he offered bread and wine, he himself also might represent it in the truth of his own body and blood. In Luke we read of two cups with which he drank to the disciples, one of the first month and the other of the second, so that he who could not eat the lamb among the saints in the first month may eat the kid among the penitents in the second month.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.22.18":["But I say to you: I will not drink from now on of this fruit of the vine until that day when I drink it new with you in the kingdom of my Father. He passes from carnal things to spiritual ones. That the vine transplanted from Egypt is the people of Israel, to whom the Lord speaks through Jeremiah: I planted you a true vine; how have you been changed into the bitterness of a foreign vine, both Isaiah the prophet testifies, in the song which he sings to his beloved, and all Scripture here and there. The Lord therefore says that he will by no means drink of this vine except in the kingdom of his Father. The kingdom of the Father I take to be the faith of believers, the Apostle also confirming this very thing: The kingdom of God is within you. Therefore, when the Jews have received the kingdom of the Father (note that he says Father, not God: every name of Father refers to a son), when, I say, they have believed in God the Father and the Father has brought them to the Son, then the Lord will drink of their wine, and, in the likeness of Joseph reigning in Egypt, he will be made drunk with his brothers.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.22.21":["And as they were eating he said: Amen I say to you that one of you is going to betray me. He who had foretold his passion foretells the betrayer too, giving room for repentance, so that, when he understood that his thoughts and secret counsels were known, he might repent of his deed; and yet he does not point him out specifically, lest, being openly convicted, he should become more shameless. He casts the charge among the whole number, so that the one who is conscious of it may repent.\n— Commentary on Matthew","But he answered and said: He who dips his hand with me in the dish, he will betray me. O the wonderful patience of the Lord! First he had said: One of you is going to betray me. The betrayer persists in evil; he reproves him more plainly, and yet he does not point out his name specifically. Judas, while the others were saddened and drew back their hands and forbade food to their mouths, with the same rashness and shamelessness with which he was going to betray him, even puts his hand into the dish with his master, so that by his boldness he might feign a good conscience.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.22.22":["The Son of Man indeed goes as it is written of him; but woe to that man by whom the Son of Man will be betrayed. Reproved neither the first time nor the second, he does not draw back his foot from the betrayal; rather, he feeds his shamelessness on the patience of the Lord and stores up for himself wrath on the day of wrath. Punishment is foretold, so that the torments announced might correct one whom shame had not overcome. But as to what follows:\n\nIt would have been good for him if that man had not been born, he is not on that account to be thought to have existed before he was born, on the ground that it can be well with no one except one who has existed; it is simply said that it is much better not to exist than to exist badly.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.22.23":["And being greatly saddened they began each to say: Is it I, Lord? And surely the eleven apostles knew that they were planning nothing of the kind against the Lord; but they trust the master more than themselves, and, fearing their own frailty, they sorrowfully ask about a sin of which they had no consciousness.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.22.25-26":["But Jesus called them to him and said: You know that the princes of the Gentiles lord it over them, and the rest. The humble and gentle master neither rebukes the two who asked for their immoderate desire nor reproves the other ten for indignation and envy, but sets forth such an example as to teach that he is greater who has been lesser, and that he becomes lord who is the servant of all. In vain therefore did those seek immoderate things, or do these grieve over the desire for greater things, since one comes to the summit of the virtues not by power but by humility. In short, he sets forth his own example, so that, if they held his words cheap, they might be put to shame by his deeds, and he says:\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.22.28-30":["And Jesus said to them: Amen I say to you that you who have followed me, in the regeneration, when the Son of Man sits on the seat of his majesty, will sit on twelve seats, judging the twelve tribes of Israel. He did not say: who have left all things, for this Crates the philosopher also did, and many others have despised riches; but who have followed me, which belongs properly to the apostles and to believers. In the regeneration, when the Son of Man sits on the seat of his majesty, when from the dead they rise incorrupt out of corruption, you too will sit on the thrones of judges, condemning the twelve tribes of Israel, because when you believed they refused to believe.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.22.28":["Be a friend, like the apostles, to whom the Savior says: No longer will I call you servants, because the servant does not know what his master does, but I will call you friends, because you have persevered with me in all my temptations. Dainty is the friendship that follows the good fortune and the riches of friends. Men of this kind seem to me to love not their friends but themselves. Let us consider more attentively the words of the Lord: But I will call you, he says, friends. And he gives the reasons why he calls them friends: Because you have persevered with me in temptation, and he did not stop there, but in all, he says, my temptations. For it sometimes happens that one who persevered with us in one temptation is overcome by others and withdraws.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"LUK.22.33":["But Peter answered and said to him: Even if all are scandalized, I will never be scandalized. It is not rashness, nor is it a lie; it is the faith of the apostle Peter and his burning affection toward the Lord Savior, of which we spoke above.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.22.34":["Jesus said to him: Amen I say to you that on this night, before the cock crows, you will deny me three times. Peter made his promise out of the ardor of faith, and the Savior, as God, knew what was to come. And note that Peter denies in the night, and denies a third time; but after the cock crowed and, as the darkness waned, the approaching light was announced, he turned and wept bitterly, washing away the filth of the denial with tears.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.22.37":["One will resolve this question briefly if one uses that example: \"and he was reckoned with those who were without the Law\". For although in the Latin manuscripts, owing to the simplicity of the translators, it has been badly rendered: \"And he was reckoned with the iniquitous\" (Luke 22:37), yet one must know that among the Greeks ἄνομον, which is written here, means one thing, and ἄδικον, which is found in the Latin volumes, another. For ἄνομος is said of one who is without law and is bound by no right, whereas ἄδικος means iniquitous, or unjust. Hence the Apostle himself too, in another place: \"since I was not\", he says, \"without the Law of God, but was in the Law of Christ\" (1 Cor. 9); and certainly in this testimony also ἄνομος is written in the Greek; and he who translated well here could have translated the same word likewise there too, had the ambiguity not deceived him.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"LUK.22.39":["And when a hymn had been said, they went out to the Mount of Olives. This is what we read in a certain psalm: They have eaten and worshiped, all the fat ones of the earth. According to this example, whoever has been filled and made drunk with the bread and the cup of the Savior can praise the Lord and ascend the Mount of Olives, where there is refreshment from labors, solace for pain, and the knowledge of the true light.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.22.41-42":["And going forward a little he fell on his face, worshiping and saying: My Father, if it is possible, let this cup pass from me; yet not as I will, but as you. Having given the apostles the command to stay and keep watch with the Lord, he goes forward a little, falls on his face, and shows the humility of his mind by the posture of his flesh; and he says tenderly: My Father, and asks that, if it is possible, the cup of the passion, of which we spoke above, may pass from him. But he asks this not from fear of suffering but from mercy toward the former people, lest he drink the cup handed to him by them. Hence he pointedly did not say: let the cup pass from me, but this cup, that is, the cup of the people of the Jews, which can have no excuse of ignorance if it kills me, since it has the Law and the Prophets who prophesy of me daily. Nevertheless, returning to himself, what he had shrunk from in trepidation in the person of a man, he confirms in that of God and of the Son: yet not as I will, but as you. Let not this be done, he says, which I speak from human feeling, but that for which I came down to earth by your will.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.22.45":["And he came to the disciples and found them sleeping, and he says to Peter: So you could not keep watch with me one hour? He who had said above: Even if all are scandalized in you, I will never be scandalized, now cannot overcome sleep for the greatness of his sadness.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.22.46":["Keep watch and pray, that you may not enter into temptation. It is impossible for the human soul not to be tempted. Hence in the Lord's prayer too we say: Lead us not into temptation, namely the temptation which we cannot bear, not refusing temptation altogether but praying for the strength to endure in temptations. So here too he does not say: Keep watch and pray that you may not be tempted, but that you may not enter into temptation, that is, lest temptation overcome and conquer you and hold you within its toils. For example, the martyr who shed his blood for confessing the Lord was indeed tempted, but was not bound in the nets of temptations; but he who denies runs into the snares of temptation.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.22.47":["And at once coming up to Jesus, he said: Hail, rabbi, and he kissed him. Shameless indeed and wicked is the confidence that calls him master and presses a kiss on the one whom he was betraying. Yet he still has something of a disciple's modesty, since he does not hand him over openly to the persecutors, but through the sign of a kiss. This is the sign which God set on Cain, so that whoever found him should not kill him.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.22.50-51":["And behold, one of those who were with Jesus, stretching out his hand, drew his sword, and striking the servant of the chief priest cut off his ear. In another Gospel it is written that Peter did this, with the same ardor of mind as in everything else. The servant of the chief priest, too, is called Malchus; and the ear that is cut off is the right one. It should be said in passing that Malchus, that is, king, the people of the Jews, once became the servant of the impiety and greed of the priests, and lost its right ear, so as to hear the whole meanness of the letter with the left; but the Lord, in those of the Jews who were willing to believe, restored the right ear and made the servant a royal and priestly race.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.22.52-53":["At that hour Jesus said: You have come out as against a robber with swords and clubs to arrest me? Daily I sat among you teaching in the temple, and you did not seize me. It is foolish, he says, to seek with swords and clubs one who hands himself over of his own accord into your hands, and to track down at night through a betrayer, as if he were hiding and avoiding your eyes, one who teaches daily in the temple. But you gather against me in darkness for this reason, that your power is in darkness.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.22.54":["But they, holding Jesus, led him to Caiaphas the chief priest, where the scribes and the elders had gathered. Moses, at God's command, had ordered that the high priests should succeed their fathers and that a line of descent should be woven among the priests. Josephus relates that this Caiaphas bought the high priesthood for one year only from Herod for a price. It is no wonder, then, if an unjust high priest judges unjustly.\n— Commentary on Matthew","But Peter followed him from afar. He who was going to deny the Lord followed from afar.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.22.55":["And going inside he sat with the attendants, to see the end. Either out of a disciple's love or out of human curiosity he wished to know what the high priest would decide about the Lord: whether he would condemn him to death or would release him after beating him with scourges. And in this lies the difference between the ten apostles and Peter: they flee; he, although from a distance, still follows the Savior.\n— Commentary on Matthew","But Peter was sitting outside in the court. He sat outside to see the outcome of the affair, and he did not come near Jesus, lest any suspicion should arise among the attendants.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.22.58":["And again he denied with an oath: I do not know the man. I know that some, out of pious affection toward the apostle Peter, have interpreted this passage so as to say that Peter denied not God but a man, and that the sense is: I do not know the man, because I know God. How frivolous this is the prudent reader understands: they so defend the apostle as to make the Lord guilty of a lie. For if he did not deny, then the Lord lied, who had said: Amen I say to you that on this night, before the cock crows, you will deny me three times. See what he says: you will deny me, not the man.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.22.59":["Surely you too are one of them, for your speech also makes you plain. Not that Peter was of another language or of a foreign nation, for all of them were Hebrews, both those who accused and he who was accused, but because each province and region had its own peculiarities and could not avoid its native sound of speech. Hence too the Ephraimites in the book of Judges cannot say the σύνθημα (the password).\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.22.60-62":["Then he began to curse and to swear that he did not know the man. And immediately the cock crowed. In another Gospel we read that after Peter's denial and the crowing of the cock the Savior looked at Peter and by his gaze moved him to bitter tears; nor could it be that he should remain in the darkness of denial on whom the light of the world had looked.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.22.62":["And going outside he wept bitterly. Sitting in the court of Caiaphas he could not repent. He goes outside, away from the council of the impious, so that he may wash away the filth of a fearful denial with bitter weeping.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.22.64":["And others struck his face with their palms, saying: Prophesy to us, Christ, who is it that struck you? It was foolish to answer those who were beating him and to prophesy who was striking him, when the madness of the one who struck was plain to see. But just as he did not prophesy this to you, so he foretold that other thing most plainly: that Jerusalem would be surrounded by an army and that not a stone would be left upon a stone in the temple.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.22.67":["And the chief priest said to him: I adjure you by the living God that you tell us whether you are the Christ, the Son of God. Why do you adjure him, most impious of priests: in order to accuse, or in order to believe? If in order to accuse, others are accusing him: condemn him in his silence; if in order to believe, why did you refuse to believe him when he confessed?\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.22.70":["Jesus says to him: You have said it. Both to Pilate and to Caiaphas the reply is alike, so that they may be condemned by their own sentence.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.23.1":["When morning had come, all the chief priests and the elders of the people took counsel against Jesus, to hand him over to death; and they led him bound and handed him over to Pontius Pilate the governor. He was led not only to Pilate but also to Herod, so that both might mock the Lord. And see the zeal of the priests for evil: they kept watch the whole night in order to commit murder. And they handed him over bound to Pilate. For they had this custom, that whomever they had adjudged to death they handed over bound to the judge.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.23.3":["And Jesus stood before the governor. And the governor questioned him, saying: Are you the king of the Jews? Since Pilate questions him on no other charge than whether he is king of the Jews, the Jews are convicted of impiety, because they could not find, even falsely, anything to lay against the Savior.\n— Commentary on Matthew","Jesus says to him: You say so. He answered in such a way that he both spoke the truth and his word lay open to no false accusation. And note that to Pilate, who was pronouncing sentence unwillingly, he answered in some part, but to the priests and rulers he would not answer, judging them unworthy of his speech.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.23.19":["Now he had at that time a notable prisoner, who was called Barabbas. This man, in the Gospel which is written according to the Hebrews, is interpreted as son of their master; he had been condemned for sedition and murder.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.23.20-23":["Pilate says to them: What then shall I do with Jesus who is called Christ? They all say: Let him be crucified. The governor says to them: Why, what evil has he done? But they cried out the more, saying: Let him be crucified. Pilate gave many occasions for freeing the Savior: first, by comparing the robber with the just man; then by adding: What then shall I do with Jesus who is called Christ, that is, who is your king; and when they answered: Let him be crucified, he did not acquiesce at once, but, following the suggestion of his wife, who had sent word: Let there be nothing between you and that just man, he too answered: Why, what evil has he done? In saying this Pilate acquitted Jesus. But they cried out the more, saying: Let him be crucified, so that what he had said in Psalm 21 (22) might be fulfilled: Many dogs have surrounded me; the assembly of evildoers has besieged me; and that word of Jeremiah: My inheritance has become to me like a lion in the forest; they have raised their voice against me; Isaiah too agreeing in this sense: And I waited for them to do judgment, but they did iniquity, and not justice but an outcry.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.23.25":["Then he released Barabbas to them; but Jesus, scourged, he handed over to them to be crucified. Barabbas the robber, who stirred up seditions among the crowds, who was the author of murders, was released to the people of the Jews, that is, the devil, who reigns in them to this day, and for that reason they cannot have peace; but Jesus, handed over by the Jews, is acquitted by Pilate's wife and is called just by the governor himself, and the centurion confesses that he is truly the Son of God. Let the learned reader ask how it is consistent that Pilate washed his hands and said: I am innocent of the blood of this just man, and afterwards handed over Jesus, scourged, to be crucified. But it must be known that he was serving the Roman laws, by which it was decreed that one who is crucified should first be beaten with scourges. So Jesus was handed over to the soldiers to be beaten, and scourges cut that most sacred body and the breast that could contain God. And this was done so that, since it was written: Many are the scourges of sinners; by his being scourged we might be freed from blows, Scripture saying to the just man: The scourge has not come near your tent.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.23.26":["And going out they found a man of Cyrene named Simon; him they pressed into service to carry his cross. Let no one think that the history of John the evangelist is contrary to this passage. For he says that the Lord, going out of the praetorium, carried his own cross; but Matthew reports that they found a man of Cyrene named Simon, on whom, pressing him into service, they laid the cross of Jesus. But this is to be understood: that Jesus, going out of the praetorium, carried his cross himself, and afterwards met Simon, on whom they laid the cross to be carried. According to anagogy, however, the nations take up the cross of Jesus, and the obedient stranger carries the ignominy of the Savior.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.23.30":["At that time they will say to the mountains: Cover us; and to the hills: Fall upon us. This the Lord says is to be fulfilled in the last time of the Jewish captivity. Whatever, then, is now said against the ten tribes, or against all Israel, let us know that it can also be transferred τυπικώς (typologically) to the whole people, so that when the Romans had taken Jerusalem and overthrown the temple, or when the day of judgment comes, as others suppose, they say, fearing with great horror, to the mountains: Cover us; and to the hills: Fall upon us, preferring to die rather than to see the things that bring death.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"LUK.23.33":["And they came to the place which is called Golgotha, which is the place of Calvary. I have heard someone expound the place of Calvary as the place in which Adam was buried, and say that it was so called because the head of the ancient man was laid there, and that this is what the Apostle says: Arise, you who sleep, and rise from the dead, and Christ will enlighten you. A pleasing interpretation, and soothing to the ear of the people, but not true. For outside the city and beyond the gate there are places in which the heads of the condemned are cut off, and they have taken the name of Calvary, that is, of the beheaded. And the Lord was crucified there for this reason: that where formerly was the ground of the condemned, there the standards of martyrdom might be raised; and just as for us he was made the curse of the cross, and was scourged and crucified, so for the salvation of all he is crucified as a guilty man among the guilty. But if anyone should wish to contend that the Lord was crucified there so that his blood might drip upon the tomb of Adam, let us ask him why other robbers too were crucified in the same place. From this it is evident that Calvary signifies not the sepulcher of the first man but the place of the beheaded, so that where sin abounded, grace might superabound. Indeed, that Adam was buried near Hebron and Arba we read in the book of Joshua son of Nun.\n— Commentary on Matthew","Then two robbers were crucified with him, one on the right and one on the left. If Golgotha is the tomb of Adam and not the place of the condemned, and the Lord is crucified there in order to raise up Adam, why are two robbers crucified in the same place?\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.23.34":["After they had crucified him, they divided his garments, casting lots. This too had been prophesied in the same psalm: They divided my garments among themselves, and upon my vesture they cast lots.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.23.35":["He saved others; himself he cannot save. Even against their will the scribes and Pharisees confess that he saved others. So your own sentence condemns you. For he who saved others could surely have saved himself too, if he had wished.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.23.38":["And they set above his head the charge against him, written: This is Jesus the king of the Jews. I cannot sufficiently wonder, as the greatness of the matter deserves, that after false witnesses had been bought and the unhappy people had been stirred up to sedition and clamor, he found no other cause for putting him to death than that he was king of the Jews. And they perhaps did this mocking and laughing. But Pilate answered them, even against their will: What I have written, I have written. Whether you will or not, O Jews, the whole crowd of the Gentiles has answered you: Jesus is king of the Jews, that is, the commander of those who believe and confess.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.23.39-42":["And the robbers too who had been fixed with him reproached him with the same. Here, by the trope which is called σύλληψις (syllepsis), both are presented as having blasphemed, in place of one robber. But Luke asserts that, while one blasphemed, the other confessed and on the contrary rebuked the blasphemer; not that the Gospels disagree, but because at first both blasphemed, and then, when the sun fled, the earth was shaken, the rocks were split and darkness came on, one believed in Jesus and corrected his former denial by his subsequent confession. In the two robbers both peoples, of the Gentiles and of the Jews, at first blasphemed the Lord; afterwards one, terrified by the greatness of the signs, repented, and to this day rebukes the Jews when they blaspheme.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.23.44-45":["Now from the sixth hour there was darkness over the whole earth until the ninth hour. Those who have written against the Gospels suspect that the disciples of Christ, out of ignorance, interpreted as concerning the resurrection of the Lord an eclipse of the sun, which usually occurs at fixed and appointed times, although a failure of the sun never usually occurs except at the rising of the moon. But no one doubts that at the time of the Passover the moon was at its fullest. And lest perhaps the shadow of the earth, or the disk of the moon set against the sun, should seem to have caused a short and rust-colored darkness, the space of three hours is stated, so that every pretext of the cavilers might be taken away. And I think this was done so that the prophecy might be fulfilled which says: The sun shall go down at midday, and the light shall be darkened over the earth in the day, and in another place: The sun went down while it was still midday. And it seems to me that the brightest light of the world, that is, the greater luminary, drew back its rays, so that it might neither see the Lord hanging nor let the impious blasphemers enjoy its light.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.23.45":["And the veil of the temple was torn in two, from the top to the bottom. The veil of the temple was torn, and all the mysteries of the law, which were previously covered, were laid open and passed over to the people of the Gentiles. In the Gospel of which we often make mention, we read that the lintel of the temple, of immense size, was broken and split apart. Josephus also reports that the angelic powers, once the guardians of the temple, then cried out together: Let us depart from these abodes.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.23.46":["Let us say this too, lest we seem to have hidden from the reader what we know: that spirit and wind are called among the Hebrews by the same word, that is, rua, and that according to the sense of the passage it is customarily taken either as spirit or as wind. Therefore in this place too we can take spirit either for wind, in that nothing breathes in idols, or for the soul, in that the images are animated. And that spirit is taken for the soul the Savior's prayer clearly signifies: Father, into your hands I commend my spirit. For Jesus could not commend to the Father either a perverse spirit, which it is impious even to think, or the Holy Spirit, who is himself also God, rather than his own soul, of which he had said: My soul is sorrowful even to death, and: No one can take my soul from me, but by my will I lay down my soul, and of my own accord I take it up again.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk","And Jesus, crying out again with a loud voice, sent forth his spirit. It is a sign of divine power to send forth the spirit, as he himself had also said: No one can take my soul from me, but I lay it down of myself, that I may take it up again.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.23.47":["But the centurion, and those who were with him keeping watch over Jesus, when they saw the earthquake and the things that were happening, were greatly afraid, saying: Truly this was the Son of God. In another Gospel, after the earthquake, the cause of the centurion's wonder is set forth more plainly: that when he had seen him yield up the spirit, he said: Truly this was the Son of God. For no one has the power of yielding up the spirit except him who is the creator of souls. And let us understand spirit in this place for soul, either because it makes the body spiritual and living, or because the substance of the soul itself is spirit, according to what is written: You will take away their spirit, and they will fail. And this is to be considered, that the centurion, before the cross, in the very stumbling block of the passion, confesses him truly the Son of God, and Arius in the Church preaches him a creature.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.23.49":["And there were many women there at a distance, who had followed Jesus from Galilee, ministering to him. It was a Jewish custom, and by the ancient usage of the nation it was not held to be a fault, that women should supply food and clothing to their teachers from their own substance. Because this could cause a stumbling block among the nations, Paul records that he had cast it aside: Have we not the power to lead about women as sisters, as the other apostles also do? And they ministered to the Lord from their own substance, so that he whose spiritual things they reaped might reap their carnal things; not that the Lord of creatures needed food, but to show a type for teachers, that they ought to be content with food and clothing from their disciples.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.23.50-52":["When it was evening, there came a certain rich man from Arimathea named Joseph, who himself also was a disciple of Jesus. He is said to be rich, not out of the boastfulness of the writer, as if to report that a noble and very wealthy man was a disciple of Jesus, but to show the reason why he was able to obtain the body of Jesus from Pilate. For it was not for a poor and unknown man to approach Pilate, the governor of the Roman power, and obtain the body of one crucified. In another evangelist this Joseph is called βουλευτής, that is, counselor, and some think that the first psalm was composed about him: Blessed is the man who has not walked in the counsel of the impious, and the rest.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.23.53":["And having taken the body of Jesus, he wrapped it in a clean linen cloth. From the simple burial of the Lord the ambition of the rich is condemned, who cannot do without riches even in their tombs. And according to spiritual understanding we can also take this to mean that the body of the Lord is to be wrapped not in gold, not in gems and silk, but in pure linen; although this too may be signified, that he wraps Jesus in a clean linen cloth who has received him with a pure mind.\n— Commentary on Matthew","And he laid it in his own new tomb, which he had hewn in the rock, and he rolled a great stone to the door of the tomb and went away. He is laid in a new tomb, lest after the resurrection, while the other bodies remained, it should be pretended that another had risen. But the new sepulcher can also signify the virginal womb of Mary; and the stone set against the door, and a great stone at that, can show that the sepulcher could not have been opened without the help of many.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.23.55":["And Mary Magdalene was there, and the other Mary, sitting opposite the sepulcher. While the others left the Lord, the women persevere in their duty, waiting for what Jesus had promised; and therefore they deserved to be the first to see him rising, because he who perseveres to the end, he shall be saved.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.24.1":["Now in the evening of the Sabbath, as it grows light toward the first day of the week, there came Mary Magdalene and the other Mary to see the sepulchre. That different times are described for these women in the Gospels is not a sign of falsehood, as the impious object, but the service of a diligent visiting, while they often go away and run back and do not bear to be away from the Lord's sepulchre long or far.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.24.2-4":["And behold, there was a great earthquake; for an angel of the Lord came down from heaven, and coming up he rolled back the stone and was sitting upon it; and his appearance was like lightning, and his clothing like snow. Our Lord, one and the same Son of God and Son of Man, according to each nature, of divinity and of flesh, shows now the signs of his greatness, now those of his lowliness. Hence in the present passage too, although it is a man who was crucified, who was buried, who was shut in the tomb, whom the stone set against it holds in, yet the things done outside show the Son of God: the sun fleeing, darkness rushing in, the earth shaken, the veil torn, the rocks split apart, the dead raised, the ministries of angels, which from the very beginning of his birth also proved him God. Gabriel comes to Mary, an angel speaks with Joseph, the same announces it to the shepherds, afterward a choir of angels is heard: Glory to God in the highest, and on earth peace among men of good will, he is tempted in the wilderness, and after the victory angels at once serve him. Now too an angel comes as guardian of the Lord's sepulchre and in white clothing signifies the glory of the one who triumphs; and moreover, as the Lord ascends to heaven, two angels are seen on the Mount of Olives, promising the apostles the second coming of the Savior.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.24.5-6":["But for fear of him the guards were terrified and became like dead men; and the angel answered and said to the women: You, do not be afraid; for I know that you are seeking Jesus who was crucified. The guards, terrified with fear, lie stunned like dead men, and yet the angel consoles not them but the women. You, do not be afraid. Let them, he says, be afraid; let dread persist in those in whom unbelief remains; but you, because you are seeking Jesus crucified, hear that he has risen and has fulfilled what he promised.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"LUK.24.9":["And they went out quickly from the tomb with fear and great joy, running to announce it to his disciples. A twofold feeling, of fear and of joy, held the minds of the women: the one from the greatness of the miracle, the other from longing for the one rising again; and yet both spurred on the women's step. They were going to the apostles so that through them the seed of faith might be scattered.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"JHN.1.3":["I know that I have read in the commentaries of a certain man expounding the beginning of John the Evangelist, All things were made through him, and without him was made nothing, that he referred what is said, nothing, to wickedness; and again that he interpreted wickedness itself as the devil, and by this step, as it were, understood the very nothing that was made without Christ to be the devil. If, then, wickedness, or the devil, is nothing, and those who have been corrupted with corruption fled with no one, that is, nothing, pursuing, the devil pursued them in the guise of nothing. But if this seems to anyone too forced, and argued against the simplicity of Scripture by an artifice of eloquence rather than by true interpretation, let him follow either the former exposition or one he has found himself.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"JHN.1.9":["But to reveal his Son in me is not the same as if he were to say to reveal his Son to me. For to whom something is revealed, to him that can be revealed which was not in him before. But in whom something is revealed, that is revealed which was in him before and afterwards has been revealed. Similar is that saying in the Gospel: \"He stands in the midst of you, whom you do not know\" (John 1:26). And elsewhere: \"That was the true light, which enlightens every man coming into the world\" (ibid., 9). From this it becomes clear that the knowledge of God is present in all by nature, and that no one is born without Christ and without having in himself the seeds of wisdom and of righteousness and of the other virtues. Hence many, without faith and the Gospel of Christ, do some things either wisely or in a holy manner, such as obeying their parents, stretching out a hand to the needy, not oppressing their neighbors, not plundering what belongs to others; and they become all the more liable to the judgment of God, because, having in themselves the beginnings of the virtues and the seeds of God, they do not believe in him without whom they cannot exist.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"JHN.1.16":["And what he says: He shall make equal the grace of its grace, signifies this: Of his fullness we have all received, and grace for grace, that is, for the grace of the law, the grace of the Gospel, so that both those who believe from Israel and the people of the heathen may receive an equal grace and a like gift.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"JHN.1.18":["Someone may ask how the Prophet now says that he has seen the Lord, and not simply the Lord but the Lord Sabaoth, as he himself testifies in what follows; when the evangelist John has said: No one has ever seen God (John 1:18). And God speaks to Moses: You cannot see my face; for man shall not see my face and live (Exod 33:20). To which we shall answer that not only the divinity of the Father, but not even that of the Son and of the Holy Spirit, since in the Trinity there is one nature, can the eyes of the flesh behold; but the eyes of the mind, of which the Savior himself says: Blessed are the pure in heart, for they shall see God (Matt 5:8). We read that the Lord was seen by Abraham in the figure of a man; and that he who was God wrestled with Jacob as a man. Hence that very place was called Phanuel, that is, the face of God: For I have seen, he says, the Lord face to face, and my soul has been saved (Gen 32). Ezekiel too saw the Lord in the form of a man sitting upon the Cherubim; from his loins and downward there was as it were fire, and the upper parts had the appearance of electrum (Ezek 1). The nature of God, then, is not discerned, but he is seen by men as he wills.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"JHN.1.23":["Mindful of this voice, the Scribes and Pharisees and the princes of the Jews, when they heard that John was preaching in the wilderness a baptism of penance and teaching the people, send men to ask him whether he was the Christ, or Elijah, or the Prophet; and when he had answered that he was none of these, they ask again: Tell us then, who are you? that we may give an answer to those who sent us. What do you say of yourself? And he answered: I am the voice of one crying in the desert: Prepare the way of the Lord; as Isaiah the Prophet said (John 1:22, 23). In this it is to be noticed that the straight ways of the Lord and the paths of our God, the filling of the valleys, and the lowering of the mountains and hills, and the correction of the crooked, and the level plains made of rough places and rocks, and the glory of the Lord and the salvation of our God, are proclaimed not in Jerusalem but in the wilderness of the Church and in the deserted multitude of the nations, of which we read above (cf. Isa 35:1): Rejoice, thirsting desert: let the wilderness exult and flower like the lily. For this multitude was deserted of the knowledge of God, and was held by idols: low in confession, lifted up in pride, rough and intractable in savagery. But after the glory of the Lord appeared, and all flesh saw the salvation of God, all things were suddenly changed and the way of the Lord prepared, so that the glory of God appeared in the wilderness: when the Lord was baptized in the Jordan, and the heavens were opened, and the Holy Spirit descended in the form of a dove and remained on him; and the voice of the Father thundering from above was heard: This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased: hear him (Matt 3:17). And all flesh saw the salvation of God. It was called flesh for this reason, that before it did not have the Holy Spirit. Of it the Lord also says: My spirit shall not remain in these men, because they are flesh (Gen 6:3). But that flesh will see the salvation of God of which the same Lord spoke through Joel: I will pour out of my spirit upon all flesh, and they shall prophesy (Joel 2:28). This was cried out not only at that time by John, who, as the forerunner and precursor of the Word of God, is rightly called a voice (Matt 3); but to this day in the desert of the Gentiles the teachers of the Churches cry out: that we should make straight ways and paths for God in our hearts, and be filled with virtues, and be bowed down by humility; that we should change the crooked into straight and turn the rough into smooth: and so deserve to see the glory of the Lord and the salvation of God.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","For this is he who was spoken of through the prophet Isaiah, saying: The voice of one crying in the desert: Prepare the way of the Lord, make his paths straight. He was preparing the souls of believers, in which the Lord was going to walk, so that he, the pure one, might walk in most pure ways, saying: I will dwell in them and walk among them, and I will be their God, and they shall be my people. Porphyry compares this passage with the beginning of the evangelist Mark, in which it is written: The beginning of the Gospel of Jesus Christ, the Son of God; as it is written in the prophet Isaiah: Behold, I send my angel before your face, who shall prepare your way; the voice of one crying in the desert: Prepare the way of the Lord, make his paths straight. For since the testimony is woven together from Malachi and Isaiah, he asks how we can think the example was taken as from Isaiah alone. To him churchmen have answered most fully. We, however, think either that the name of Isaiah was added through the fault of copyists, which we can prove in other places as well, or at any rate that one body has been made out of different testimonies of the Scriptures. Read Psalm 13 (14) and you will find this same thing.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"JHN.1.27":["Whose shoes I am not worthy to carry. In another Gospel: Whose, he says, shoe's strap I am not worthy to loose. Here humility is shown, there a mystery: that Christ is the bridegroom and John does not deserve to loose the bridegroom's strap, lest his house be called, according to the law of Moses and the example of Ruth, the house of the unshod.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"JHN.1.32":["The heavens were opened to him, and he saw the Spirit descending like a dove, coming upon him, and the rest. The mystery of the Trinity is shown in the baptism. The Lord is baptized, the Spirit descends in the form of a dove, the voice of the Father is heard bearing witness to the Son. And the heavens are opened, not by an unbarring of the elements, but to spiritual eyes, with which Ezekiel too, at the beginning of his volume, records that they were opened. The dove sat, moreover, upon the head of Jesus, lest anyone should think that the voice of the Father was addressed to John and not to the Lord.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"JHN.1.33":["If the word of God were always in the prophets and had a constant lodging in their breast, Ezekiel would never so often set down: 'And the word of the Lord came to me, saying'; but it was because, on account of human frailty and the necessities of this life, it at times withdrew from them; therefore John the Baptist says: He who sent me to baptize, he said to me: He on whom you see the Holy Spirit descending in the form of a dove and remaining in him, he it is — for what is added, And remaining in him, would never be something proper to Christ, unless it sometimes withdrew from others —; besides, God also says to Moses: I will take away from the spirit that is in you — which he certainly would not have had unless it had descended to him from the Lord —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"JHN.1.46-47":["Nor shall they bring forth in trouble, or for a curse, or, as is put more expressly in the Hebrew, εἰς ἀνυπαρξίαν, which in their language is said LABALA (לבהלה), that is, so that they cease to be and have an end of their substance: for which Aquila, Symmachus and Theodotion translated haste: so that they may by no means hasten to believe without reason, but may imitate Nathanael, who was praised by the voice of the Lord: Behold, truly an Israelite, in whom there is no guile (John 1:47), who sought Christ by the authority of the Scriptures and longed to recognize him from the Prophets, saying: Can anything good come out of Nazareth? And the sense is: How do you bring me a Messiah from Galilee and Nazareth, whom I know to have been promised from Bethlehem of Judah?\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"JHN.2.14-17":["And Jesus entered the temple of God and cast out all who sold and bought in the temple, and he overturned the tables of the money-changers and the chairs of those who sold doves, and he says to them: It is written: My house shall be called a house of prayer, but you have made it a den of robbers. Accompanied by the crowd of believers, which had spread its garments so that the colt might go with unhurt foot, Jesus enters the temple and casts out all who sold and bought in the temple, and he overturned the tables of the money-changers and scattered the chairs of those who sold doves, and he said to them, bringing forward testimony from the holy Scriptures, that his Father's house ought to be a house of prayer, not a den of robbers or a house of trade, as is written in another evangelist. And this must first be known: that, in accordance with the commands of the law, since the temple of the Lord was the most august in the whole world, and the people of the Jews flocked there from almost all regions, countless victims were sacrificed, especially on feast days, of bulls, rams and goats, while the poor, so as not to be without a sacrifice, offered young doves and turtledoves; and it often happened that those who had come from far away did not have victims. So the priests devised how they might make plunder of the people, and they sold all the animals that were needed for the sacrifices, so that they might both sell to those who did not have them and themselves receive back again what had been bought. This scheme of theirs was frequently defeated by the poverty of those who came, who lacked the means and not only had no victims but had not even the wherewithal to buy birds and cheap little gifts. So they also set up money-changers to lend money on security; but because it was commanded in the law that no one should take interest, and money lent at interest could be of no use when it brought no profit and sometimes lost the principal, they devised yet another trick: in place of money-changers to make collybists, a word whose proper sense the Latin language does not express. Among them collyba is the name for what we call sweetmeats or cheap little gifts, for example roasted chickpeas and raisins and fruits of various kinds. So, because the collybists who had lent the money could not take interest, in place of interest they took various kinds of goods, so as to exact in these things, which are bought with coins, what was not lawful in coin; as if Ezekiel had not guarded against this very thing, saying: You shall not take interest and increase. The Lord, seeing such trade, or robbery, in his Father's house, roused by the ardor of the spirit, according to what is written in Psalm 68 (69): The zeal of your house has eaten me up, made himself a whip of cords and cast so great a multitude of men out of the temple, saying: It is written: My house shall be called a house of prayer, but you have made it a den of robbers. For he is a robber, and turns the temple of God into a den of robbers, who pursues gain from religion, and whose worship is not so much the worship of God as an occasion for trade. So much according to the history. But according to the mystical senses Jesus daily enters his Father's temple and casts out all, bishops and presbyters and deacons as well as laymen and the whole crowd, from his Church, and holds those who sell and those who buy alike guilty of one crime; for it is written: Freely you have received, freely give. He also overturns the tables of the money-changers. Note that because of the avarice of the priests the altars of God are called the tables of money-changers. And he overturns the chairs of those who sell doves, who sell the grace of the Holy Spirit and do everything to devour the peoples subject to them, of whom it is said: Who devour my people like food of bread. According to the simple understanding, the doves were not on chairs but in cages, unless perhaps the dealers in doves sat on chairs, which is utterly absurd, because in chairs it is rather the dignity of teachers that is indicated, a dignity that is brought to nothing when it has been mixed with gain. What we have said of the churches, let each one understand of himself as well. For the Apostle Paul says: You are the temple of God, and the Holy Spirit dwells in you. Let there be no trade in the house of our heart, no commerce of selling and buying, no greed for gifts, lest Jesus enter angry and stern and cleanse his temple in no other way than by applying the whip, so that out of a den of robbers and a house of trade he may make a house of prayer.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"JHN.2.15-16":["In the Song of Songs the bride praises her bridegroom: His cheeks are like bowls of spices. By the cheeks is understood speech, which, uttered by the Lord, brings forth various ointments; and so great will be the fragrance of its good odor that the Lord makes himself a scourge woven from the testimonies of the scriptures, and casts out of the temple those who sell and buy, and says to them: It is written, My Father's house shall be called a house of prayer for all nations; but you have made it a house of trade.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"JHN.2.19":["At last two false witnesses came and said: This man said: I can destroy the temple of God and after three days build it. How are they false witnesses, if they say the things which we read above that the Lord said? But a false witness is one who does not understand what is said in the same sense in which it is said. For the Lord had spoken of the temple of his body; but they falsely accuse him in the very words too, and by adding or changing a few they make, as it were, a just accusation. The Savior had said: Dissolve this temple; they change it and say: I can destroy the temple of God. You, he says, dissolve, not I, because it is unlawful for us to lay hands on ourselves. Then they twist it: and after three days build it, so that he might seem to have spoken properly of the Jewish temple. But the Lord, to show a living and breathing temple, had said: And I in three days will raise it up. It is one thing to build, another to raise up.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"JHN.3.18":["Since we have already discussed at length the just man and his likeness to the tree, also the wicked man and his likeness, and have talked about the present world, it remains for us to meditate on the future life and on eternity. Therefore in judgment the wicked shall not stand, nor shall sinners, in the assembly of the just.' [Psalm 1:5] In the Gospel according to John, we read: 'He who believes in me is not judged; but he who does not believe in me is already judged.' [John 3:18] Who is left to be judged if both he who believes will no be judged and he who does not believe is already judged? Who will be judged on the day of judgment?\n\nLet us reflect upon the one who stands between the believer and the non-believer, the one that is to be judged. He who believes will not be judged. Now he who believes does not sin; he who believes according to truth does not sin; he who has true faith does not sin. Actually, when we commit sin, it is because our mind is wavering in faith. When we are giving way to anger, when we are detracting from the reputation of another, when we are committing murder, when we are yielding to fornication, just where is our faith? Hence, the words: 'He who believes in me will not be judged'; there is no need to judge him, he is already blessed. Further, he who does not believe is already judged; he has already been judged un to punishment. Who, therefore, is to be judged? The one who indeed believes and yet yields to sin; he who has goodness, but has evil too; he who performs good acts at the time when he believes, but commits sin when his faith is weak.\n\nLet us at this point consider the meaning of the words: 'Therefore in judgment the wicked shall not stand.' They shall not rise to be judged because they have already been judged, for 'he who does not believe in Me is already judged.' 'Nor shall sinners in the assembly of the just.' It does not say that sinners shall not rise again; but that they shall not stand in the assembly of the just; they do not deserve to stand with those who are not to be judged. If they believed in Me, says the Lord, they would rise up with those who do not have to be judged.\n— Homily 1, on Psalm 1"],"JHN.3.26":["That the disciples of John were proud against the Lord and had something of a biting spirit from spite and envy, the earlier question also showed, as the evangelist relates: Then the disciples of John came to him, saying: Why do we and the Pharisees fast often, but your disciples do not fast? and elsewhere: Master, he to whom you bore witness at the Jordan, behold, his disciples baptize, and more come to him, as if they said: We are deserted, here there is scarcity, to him the crowd runs together.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"JHN.3.30":["In short, before the Gospel of Christ shone out in the whole world, the precepts of the Law had their own brightness; but after the greater light of the grace of the Gospel shone forth, and the sun of righteousness showed itself to the whole world, the light of the stars was hidden and their rays grew dim, so that the Apostle says in another place: \"For what was glorified in this respect was not glorified, because of the surpassing glory\" (2 Cor. 3). This he now says in other words, meaning that the Law of Moses, which before the Gospel was rich and wealthy and splendid, after the coming of Christ, by comparison with him, as being weak and needy, has been diminished and destroyed by him who was greater than Solomon, and the temple, and Jonah. For what is written, \"He must increase, but I must decrease\" (John 3:30), I think was said not so much in the person of John as in the person of the Law, because lesser things always give way to greater, and perfect things are set before beginnings.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"JHN.4.13-14":["But that we may know that waters and cups signify teaching, the Lord speaks to the Samaritan woman: Everyone who drinks of this water will thirst again; but whoever drinks of the water that I shall give him will not thirst for ever. Therefore whoever drinks of the waters of the Samaritan woman, that is, of the heretics, will always thirst and will not be able to cool the burning of a parched throat, as Isaiah cries aloud about this very thing: As one who is thirsty dreams that he drinks, and when he has risen he still thirsts, and his soul has hoped in vain; so shall all the nations be that fight against Jerusalem. For truly whoever drinks of the waters of the heretics, and fights against Jerusalem, the Church of God, drinks in his sleep, and his soul is mocked by empty images. And when he thinks himself satisfied, then he will have the beginning of thirst.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"JHN.4.31-34":["Because, then, he labored, he shall see the Churches rise up in the whole world, and shall be satisfied with their faith. Indeed, when, hungry and thirsty, he had sat down by the well of Jacob (John 4) at midday under the scorching sun, he would not use the food that had been bought, because he was already satisfied with the faith of the Samaritan woman and of the city of Shechem coming out to him.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"JHN.4.44":["But Jesus said to them: A prophet is not without honor except in his own country and in his own house. It is almost natural for citizens always to envy their fellow citizens; for they do not consider the man's present works but remember his frail infancy, as though they themselves had not come to mature age through the same stages of life.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"JHN.6.1":["When Jesus had heard this, he withdrew from there in a boat to a desert place apart. They announce the Baptist's death to the Savior; having heard of it, he withdraws to a desert place; not, as some think, out of fear of death, but sparing his enemies, lest they add murder to murder; or else putting off his own death to the day of the Passover, on which, because of the mystery, the lamb is to be sacrificed and the doorposts of the believers are to be sprinkled with blood. Or else he withdrew for this reason, to give us an example of avoiding the rashness of those who hand themselves over of their own accord, because not all persevere in torments with the same constancy with which they offer themselves to be tortured. For this reason he also commands in another place: When they persecute you in this city, flee into another. Elegantly too the evangelist does not say, he fled to a desert place, but he withdrew, so that he avoided his persecutors rather than feared them. Alternatively. After the head of the prophet had been cut off by the Jews and by the king of the Jews, and prophecy had lost its tongue and voice among them, Jesus passed over to the desert place of the Church, which before had not had a husband.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"JHN.6.2":["And when the crowds had heard, they followed him on foot from the cities. He may also, for another reason, having heard of John's death, have withdrawn into a desert place: to test the faith of the believers. In short, the crowds followed him on foot, not on beasts of burden, not in various vehicles, but by the labor of their own feet, to show the ardor of their minds. If we wish to lay open the reasons of every single word, we exceed the brevity of the work we have undertaken. Yet it must be said in passing that after the Lord had come into the desert, very great crowds followed him; for before he came into the solitudes of the Gentiles, he was worshiped by one people only.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"JHN.6.9":["They answered him: We have nothing here but five loaves and two fishes. In another evangelist we read: There is a certain boy here who has five loaves, and he seems to me to signify Moses; the two fishes we understand either as both Testaments, or because an even number refers to the law. So the apostles, before the passion of the Savior and the flashing forth of the lightning-bright Gospel, had nothing but five loaves and two little fishes, which moved about in salt waters and in bitter waves.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"JHN.6.10":["And when he had ordered the crowd to recline on the grass. According to the letter the sense is plain; let us lay open the mysteries of the spiritual interpretation. They are ordered to recline on the grass, and according to another evangelist on the ground, by fifties or by hundreds, so that, after they have trodden down their flesh and have put under themselves all its flowers and the pleasures of the world like withered grass, they may then, through the repentance of the number fifty, ascend to the perfect summit of the number one hundred.\n— Commentary on Matthew","Now the number of those who ate was five thousand men, besides women and little children. In keeping with the number of the five loaves, the multitude of the men who ate is also five thousand. For it had not yet come, as in the narrative of another passage, to the number seven, of which those who eat are four thousand, a number close to that of the Gospels. Now five thousand men eat, men who had grown to the perfect man and were following him of whom Zechariah says: Behold a man, the Rising is his name. But women and little children, the frail sex and the lesser age, are unworthy of being numbered. Hence too in the book of Numbers, whenever the priests and the Levites and the army or the bands of fighting men are registered, slaves and women and little children and the undistinguished common people are passed over without number.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"JHN.6.11":["Taking the five loaves and the two fishes, looking up to heaven, he blessed and broke and gave the loaves to the disciples. He looks up to heaven to teach that our eyes must be directed there. He took the five loaves and the two little fishes into his hands, and broke them, and handed them to the disciples. As the Lord breaks them, a seedbed of food comes into being. For if they had stayed whole, and had not been torn into pieces nor divided into a manifold harvest, they could not have fed the crowds, and the children, and the women, and so great a multitude. So the law together with the prophets is broken and torn into pieces, and its mysteries are brought out into the open, so that what did not nourish while it was whole and remained in its former state might, divided into parts, nourish the multitude of the Gentiles.\n— Commentary on Matthew","And he gave the loaves to the disciples, and the disciples to the crowds. The crowds receive nourishment from the Lord through the apostles.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"JHN.6.13":["And they took up of the remnants twelve baskets full of fragments. Each of the apostles fills his basket from the Savior's remnants, either so that he may have from it something with which afterwards to offer food to the Gentiles, or so that he may teach from the remnants that those were real loaves which were afterwards multiplied. And at the same time ask how, in the desert and in so vast a solitude, no loaves are found but five only, and two little fishes, and yet twelve baskets are found so easily.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"JHN.6.15":["And having dismissed the crowd, he went up the mountain alone to pray. If the disciples Peter and James and John, who had seen the glory of him transfigured, had been with him, perhaps they would have gone up the mountain with him; but the crowd cannot follow to the heights unless he has taught it by the sea on the shore and fed it in the desert. But that he went up alone to pray, do not refer to him who with five loaves satisfied five thousand men, besides little children and women, but to him who, having heard of John's death, withdrew into solitude; not that we separate the person of the Lord, but that his works are divided between God and man.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"JHN.6.17-18":["But the boat in the middle of the sea was tossed by the waves. Rightly had the apostles withdrawn from the Lord as though unwilling and reluctant, lest in his absence they should suffer shipwreck. In short, while the Lord was lingering on the summit of the mountain, at once a contrary wind arises and troubles the sea, and the apostles are in danger, and the impending shipwreck lasts until Jesus comes.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"JHN.6.19":["But in the fourth watch of the night he came to them walking upon the sea. Military guard-posts and watches are divided into spaces of three hours each. So when he says that the Lord came to them in the fourth watch of the night, he shows that they were in danger the whole night, and that at the very end of the night and at the consummation of the world he will bring them help.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"JHN.6.20":["And at once Jesus spoke to them, saying: Have confidence, it is I. What was at issue first, that he heals first, and to those who are afraid he gives a command, saying: Have confidence, do not be afraid. And as to what follows, it is I, he does not add who he is: either they could recognize him from a voice known to them, as he spoke through the dark shadows of the night, or they recalled that it was he himself whom they knew to have spoken to Moses: This is what you say to the sons of Israel: He who is has sent me to you.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"JHN.8.31-32":["Among the elect themselves, too, there is great diversity according to the variety of their works, minds and words. Nor does whoever is elect of God at once possess faith according to his election, or have knowledge of the truth according to his faith. Hence the Savior also spoke to the Jews who had believed in him: \"If you remain in my word, you will know the truth, and the truth will set you free\" (John 8:31, 32). And the evangelist testifies that he said these things to those who believed indeed, but did not know the truth, which they could attain if they remained in his word, and, being made free, would cease to be servants.\n— Commentary on Titus"],"JHN.8.31-35":["The Lord speaks to the Jews, who still preferred to be sons of the bondwoman: \"If you remain in my word, you will truly be my disciples, and you will know the truth, and the truth will make you free\" (John 8:31, 32). Hence they too, not knowing that what was said was mystical, say: \"We are the seed of Abraham, and we have never been slaves to anyone: how do you say, You will be free? Jesus answered them: Amen, amen I say to you, that everyone who commits sin is the slave of sin. But the slave does not remain in the house forever; but the son remains forever\". If we are slaves of sin, Hagar the Egyptian has begotten us; if sin does not reign in our mortal body, we are truly sons of God.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"JHN.8.56":["Amen indeed I say to you that many prophets and just men desired to see what you see, and did not see it, and to hear what you hear, and did not hear it. There seems to be, contrary to this passage, what is said elsewhere: Abraham longed to see my day, and he saw it and rejoiced. But he did not say: all prophets and just men desired to see what you see, but many. Among many it may be that some saw and others did not see, although in this too the interpretation is dangerous, since we may seem to make some distinction or other among the merits of the saints. Therefore Abraham saw in an enigma, saw in a form; but you hold and have your Lord in the present, and you question him at your will, and you eat with him.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"JHN.9.6-7":["We read in the Gospel according to John (John 9) that the Lord sent the man blind from birth, whose eyes he had anointed with clay made from his spittle, to the waters of Siloam, and when the blind man had washed off the clay in them, his blindness was wiped away and he received the clear light of his eyes: which, beyond the greatness of the sign, indicates that the blindness of the Jews and of every unbeliever cannot be healed otherwise than if the darkness of former error is put to flight by the doctrine of the waters of Christ, which flows gently without the din and clamor of words.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"JHN.9.39":["Hence the Lord too: For judgment, he says, I have come into this world, so that those who do not see may see, and those who see may become blind. The people of the Gentiles did not see, and after faith in Christ began to behold the light of the truth, which speaks through the prophet: The Spirit of the Lord is upon me, because he has anointed me; he has sent me to preach the gospel to the poor, to proclaim release to the captives and to the blind that they may see, and be enlightened with wisdom, of which it is written: A man's wisdom enlightens his face, and in the psalms we read: The Lord enlightens the blind, or makes them wise. For that is rather what σοφοῖ signifies. The Jews saw, and because they would not receive the light, they were covered with eternal blindness.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"JHN.10.8":["And you dwell with scorpions. Such as can strike, such as can wound both with an arched blow and with a hollow sting, so that the same stroke both opens the skin and pours in the venom. Each one receives his name according to his character: to the scribes and Pharisees it is said: Brood of vipers, and of Herod, who was a shifty turncoat: Go and tell this fox; and the false prophets in this same prophet are compared to foxes, and now, for their untamable heart, they are said to be scorpions; whence in the Gospel too we read: All who came before me were thieves and robbers, and the sheep did not hear them — and how did the lost sheep of the house of Israel hear the adversaries? —, from which it is shown that those who heard the thieves and robbers lost the name of sheep and took on other names, so that they did not perish as sheep but as vipers, foxes and scorpions.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","But this too can be said: that when the Lord and Savior, after the shedding of his blood and his Church gathered from Jews as well as from Gentiles, wished to heal the sins of the people and to bring them back to repentance, suddenly Ephraim, who promise an abundance of false dogmas, and the people of Samaria, who say that they keep the commandments of God, rose up and wrought the idol of false dogmas; and through them the thief and robber, the devil, entered the Church; or else the very doctrine of the heretics entered, like a thief and a robber, of whom the Savior too says in the Gospel: All who came before me were thieves and robbers. Thieves lie in wait and deceive by hidden fraud; robbers boldly carry off what belongs to others. For those who steal, steal by night and in darkness. Hence he says pointedly that the thief entered secretly, and that the robber plunders outside. For they cannot strip those whom they have taught of the garment of Christ unless they have led them out of the Church and made them walk in the perverse way of their doctrines. By the thieves and robbers who came before the Lord we ought to understand not Moses and the prophets, who are always praised by the mouth of the Savior, but the false prophets; and afterwards the heretics, who were not sent by the Lord but came of their own will.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"JHN.10.9":["For the Lord himself is both king and shepherd, both the way and the gate, and he says: I am the gate: whoever enters through me will be saved; he will go in and go out, and will find pasture. Of this gate it is prophesied elsewhere too: This is the gate of the Lord, the just will enter by it. But whoever has entered ought not to remain in the state in which he entered; let him go out to the pastures, so that in entering there is the beginning, and in going out and finding pasture, the perfection of the virtues. He who enters is still in the world, and understands the Creator from the creatures. But he who goes out transcends the whole creation, and, counting as nothing all things that can be seen, will find pasture above the heavens, and will feed on the word of God, and will say: The Lord shepherds me, and I shall lack nothing. This is said so that we might understand the testimony of the Gospel, He will go in and go out, and will find pasture, and what is now said through the prophet, And they passed through the gate, and went out by it; yet this passing through and going out cannot be granted without Christ our king, who is king and Lord.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"JHN.10.16":["After this it is written: And he brought me out into the outer court and led me around through the four corners of the court, and behold, there was a little court in a corner of the court, a little court in each corner of the court; in the four little courts of the corner, little courts laid out; that man whom he often mentions brought him out of the place of the priests which sloped toward the north, or which was separated, where the priests cook for trespass and for sin or ignorance; and he brought him out into the outer court: from which we understand that the court from which he went out was the inner one, and that there are many courts in the holy Scriptures — of which we read: You who stand in the house of the Lord, in the courts of the house of our God, and in John: And other sheep I have, which are not 'of this court'; and them too I must bring: and they will hear my voice, and there will be one 'court' and one shepherd — for this is what the Greek αὐλή means, which Latin simplicity translated as 'fold' —: for when he says 'of this court', he shows that there is another as well, which is pointed out either in the throng of the nations, as distinct from Israel, or in the heavens, as separate from the earthly court, and that to each office a court has been assigned according to the quality of merits, a subject that was argued more fully earlier —;\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"JHN.10.28-29":["Hence also the sheep of the Lord which follow him are not seized from his hands, and he himself says: What the Father has given me is greater than all, and no one can seize it from the hand of the Father; from which is seen the one power, might and substance of the Father and the Son: for if no one can seize from the Son's hand what the Father has given, and these same things are in the Father's hand and are not seized from him, it is clearly proved that all things are common to the Father and the Son, and that the Father holds in the Son's hand, just as the things that are the Son's are held in the Father's hand.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"JHN.10.29":["It is asked: if, when God seizes, carries off and holds, no one can snatch from his hands, according to what is written: No one can snatch out of the hand of my Father, how was Judas snatched from the hand of God by his betrayal? To which we will briefly answer that no one can snatch from the hand of God; but that he who is held can, by his own will, fall out of the hand of God.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"JHN.12.3":["A woman came to him having an alabaster of precious ointment, and she poured it over his head as he reclined. Let no one think that she who poured ointment over the head is the same as she who poured it over the feet. For the latter both washes with tears and wipes with her hair and is openly called a harlot, whereas of this one nothing of the kind is written. Nor indeed could a harlot at once become worthy of the Lord's head. Another evangelist, in place of the alabaster of precious ointment (which is a kind of marble), put pistic nard, that is, true and without guile, to show the faith of the Church and of the Gentiles.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"JHN.12.4-6":["But the disciples, seeing it, were indignant, saying: To what purpose is this waste? For this could have been sold for much and given to the poor. I know that some cavil at this passage, asking why another evangelist said that Judas alone was saddened, because he held the purse and had been a thief from the beginning, while Matthew writes that all the apostles were indignant; they do not know the figure that is called σύλληψις (syllepsis), by which all are customarily named for one and one for many. For Paul the apostle too, in his letter which is written to the Hebrews (although many of the Latins have doubts about it), when he had described the sufferings and merits of the saints, added: They were stoned, they were tempted, they were sawn asunder, they died by the slaughter of the sword, though the Jews hold that only one, the prophet Isaiah, was sawn. We can also say otherwise: that the apostles were truly indignant on account of the poor, but Judas on account of his own gain. Hence his muttering too is set down together with a charge: that he had no care for the poor but wished to provide for his own theft.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"JHN.12.7":["For in putting this ointment on my body, she has done it for my burial. What you think to be a waste of ointment is the office of burial. Nor is it strange if she has given me the good odor of her faith, since I am going to pour out my blood for her.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"JHN.12.8":["But Jesus, knowing it, said to them: Why do you trouble the woman? She has wrought a good work on me; for you always have the poor with you, but me you will not always have. Another question arises: why the Lord said to the disciples after the resurrection: Behold, I am with you even to the consummation of the world, and now says: but me you will not always have. But it seems to me that in this place he speaks of his bodily presence: that after the resurrection he would by no means be with them in such a way as he is now, in all shared life and familiarity. Mindful of this, the Apostle says: And if we had known Jesus Christ according to the flesh, yet now we know him no longer.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"JHN.12.13":["And others cut branches from the trees and spread them on the road. From the fruit-bearing trees with which the Mount of Olives is planted they cut branches and spread them on the road, to make the crooked straight and to level the uneven, so that Christ, the conqueror of demons and of vices, might advance more straightly and more safely in the heart of believers.\n— Commentary on Matthew","And the crowds that went before and that followed cried out, saying: Hosanna to the son of David; blessed is he who is to come in the name of the Lord; hosanna in the highest. Since the history is plain, let us follow the spiritual order of discussion. The crowds that had gone out from Jericho and had followed the Savior and his disciples, after they saw the foal of the ass loosed, which before had been tied, and adorned with the garments of the apostles, and the Lord Jesus sitting upon it, laid their garments beneath and spread the road with branches of trees; and when they had done all this in deed, they also bestow the testimony of the voice, and those going before and those following resound not with a brief and silent confession but with the fullest shout: Hosanna to the son of David; blessed is he who is to come in the name of the Lord. And in saying: The crowds that went before and that followed, he shows that both peoples, those who believed in the Lord before the Gospel and those who believed after the Gospel, praise Jesus with the harmonious voice of confession, and that, after the example of the parable above, the workers of different hours receive the one reward of faith. Moreover, what the following words mean: Hosanna to the son of David, I remember that I said many years ago, in a short letter to Damasus, then bishop of the city of Rome, and now I will touch on it briefly. In Psalm 117 (118), which was plainly written about the coming of the Savior, among other things we read this too: The stone which the builders rejected, this has become the head of the corner; this was done by the Lord, this is wonderful in our eyes; this is the day which the Lord has made; let us exult and rejoice in it; and at once there is joined: O Lord, save me; O Lord, prosper well; blessed is he who is to come in the name of the Lord; we have blessed you from the house of the Lord, and the rest. Where the LXX translators have: O Lord, save me, in the Hebrew we read: Anna adonai osi anna, which Symmachus translated more plainly, saying: I beseech you, Lord, save me, I beseech you. Let no one, then, think that the word is composed of two words, namely a Greek and a Hebrew one, but that it is wholly Hebrew and signifies that the coming of Christ is the salvation of the world. Hence there also follows: Blessed is he who is to come in the name of the Lord, the Savior too confirming this very thing in the Gospel: I have come in the name of my Father, and you have not received me; another will come in his own name, and you will receive him. Further, by what is joined: Hosanna (that is, salvation) in the highest, it is clearly shown that the coming of Christ is the salvation not only of men but of the whole world, joining earthly things to heavenly, that every knee should bow to him, of those in heaven and on earth and in the lower world.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"JHN.12.14-15":["Now this was done that what was spoken through the prophet might be fulfilled, who says: Say to the daughter of Zion: Behold, your king comes to you, meek, sitting upon an ass and a colt, the foal of a beast of burden. This is written in the prophet Zechariah, of whom it will be spoken more fully in its own place, if span of life is granted. For now we must touch briefly on this: that according to the letter, in the short stretch of the journey, he did not rest sitting upon both animals. For either he sat on the ass and the colt was without a rider, or, if he made use of the colt for sitting (which is more fitting), the ass was led along free. Therefore, since the history involves either impossibility or unseemliness, we are carried over to higher things, so that this ass, which was a beast of burden and tamed and had drawn the yoke of the law, may be understood as the synagogue, and the foal of the ass, frisky and free, as the people of the Gentiles, upon whom Jesus sat, his two disciples having been sent to them, one to the circumcision and the other to the Gentiles.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"JHN.12.31-32":["I know certain men for whom the king of Nineveh, (who is the last to hear the proclamation and who descends from his throne, and forgoes the ornaments of his former vices and dressed in sackcloth sits on the ground, he is not content with his own conversion, preaches penitence to others with his leaders, saying, \"let the men and beasts, big and small of size, be tortured by hunger, let them put on sackcloth, condemn their former sins and betake themselves without reservation to penitence!) is the symbol of the devil, who at the end of the world, (because no spiritual creature that is made reasoning by God will perish), will descend from his pride and do penitence and will be restored to his former position. To support this opinion they use this example of Daniel in which Nebuchadnezzar after seven years of penitence is returned to his former reign. [Dan. 4:24, 29, 33] But because this idea is not in the Holy Scripture and since it completely destroys the fear of God, (for men will slide easily into vices if they believe that even the devil, the creator of wickedness and the source of all sins, can be saved if he does penitence), we must eradicate this from our spirits. Let us remember though that the sinners in the Gospel are sent to the eternal fire [Mt. 25:41], which is prepared for the devil and his angels, about whom is said, \"their worm will not die and their fire will not be extinguished\" [Is. 66:24]... Moreover if all spiritual creatures are equal and if they raise themselves up by their virtues to heaven, or by their vices take themselves to the depths, then after a long circuit and infinite centuries, if all are returned to their original state with the same worthiness to all conflicting, what difference will there be between the virgin and the prostitute? What distinction will there be between the mother of the Lord and (it is wicked to say) the victims of public pleasures? Will Gabriel be like the devil? Will the apostles be as demons? Will the prophets be as pseudoprophets? Martyrs as their persecutors? Imagine all that you will, increase by two-fold the years and the time, take infinite time for torture: if the end for all is the same, all the past is then nothing, for what is of importance to us is not what we are at any given moment, but what we will be forever more.\n— Commentary on Jonah, Chapter 3, Verses 6-9"],"JHN.12.41":["But who this Lord is who is seen, we learn more fully in the evangelist John and in the Acts of the Apostles. Of whom John says: These things Isaiah said when he saw his glory, and spoke of him (John 12:41), meaning without doubt Christ. Again Paul in the Acts of the Apostles, where at Rome he speaks to the Jews: Well, he says, did the Holy Spirit speak through Isaiah the prophet to our fathers, saying: Go to this people and say: With the ear you shall hear and shall not understand, and seeing you shall see and shall not perceive; for the heart of this people has been made gross, and with their ears they have heard heavily; and they have shut their eyes, lest perhaps they should see with their eyes, and hear with their ears, and understand with their heart, and turn, and I should heal them (Acts 28:26-27). Now the Son was seen in the bearing of one who reigns, and the Holy Spirit spoke, on account of the fellowship of majesty and the unity of substance.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"JHN.13.20":["Whoever receives you receives me, and whoever receives me receives him who sent me. A most beautiful order. He sends them to preach, teaches that dangers are not to be feared, subordinates affection to religion; he had taken away gold above, he had shaken bronze out of the belt. Hard is the condition of the evangelists. Whence, then, their expenses, whence the necessities of food? He tempers the austerity of the commands with the hope of the promises: Whoever receives, he says, you receives me, and whoever receives me receives him who sent me, so that in receiving the apostles each of the believers might think that he had received Christ.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"JHN.13.21":["And as they were eating he said: Amen I say to you that one of you is going to betray me. He who had foretold his passion foretells the betrayer too, giving room for repentance, so that, when he understood that his thoughts and secret counsels were known, he might repent of his deed; and yet he does not point him out specifically, lest, being openly convicted, he should become more shameless. He casts the charge among the whole number, so that the one who is conscious of it may repent.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"JHN.13.22":["And being greatly saddened they began each to say: Is it I, Lord? And surely the eleven apostles knew that they were planning nothing of the kind against the Lord; but they trust the master more than themselves, and, fearing their own frailty, they sorrowfully ask about a sin of which they had no consciousness.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"JHN.13.37":["But Peter answered and said to him: Even if all are scandalized, I will never be scandalized. It is not rashness, nor is it a lie; it is the faith of the apostle Peter and his burning affection toward the Lord Savior, of which we spoke above.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"JHN.13.38":["Jesus said to him: Amen I say to you that on this night, before the cock crows, you will deny me three times. Peter made his promise out of the ardor of faith, and the Savior, as God, knew what was to come. And note that Peter denies in the night, and denies a third time; but after the cock crowed and, as the darkness waned, the approaching light was announced, he turned and wept bitterly, washing away the filth of the denial with tears.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"JHN.15.1-2":["The Lord too says something of the kind in the Gospel according to John: I am the true vine, and my Father is the husbandman. Every branch that abides in me and does not bear fruit, the Father will take it away; and every one that bears fruit he will cleanse, that it may bear more fruit; but if the Savior is the vine and we the branches and God the Father the husbandman, as long as we keep God's commandments we are continually cultivated, and our cultivation is a cleansing: for it is not of him who wills, nor of him who runs, but of God who shows mercy; and if, bearing fruit, we are cleansed by the Father so that we may bear more abundant fruit, it is shown that all our effort, by God's help, reaches its end and yields the fruit of fullness; but the branch that has not borne fruit will be taken away by the Father, and once it has been taken away, what the Father does with it Scripture does not say, unless perhaps separation from the body of Christ is everlasting punishment and destruction.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"JHN.15.2":["The vine shall give its fruit, the vine that says in the Gospel: I am the vine, you are the branches. Every branch that abides in me, the Father cleanses, that it may bear greater fruit. And when its branches have been cleansed, that is, its shoots and layers, and with budding eyes have begun to promise the hope of fruits to come, so that what is written may be fulfilled: The flowering vines have given forth their fragrance, then the sun of justice has colored the hanging clusters, so that, sent into the winepresses of Psalms 8 and 83 (84), which are entitled for the winepresses, they may be trodden by the foot of the Lord, who comes up from Bozrah, that he may pour out the wine that gladdens the heart of man.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"JHN.15.15":["Be a friend, like the apostles, to whom the Savior says: No longer will I call you servants, because the servant does not know what his master does, but I will call you friends, because you have persevered with me in all my temptations. Dainty is the friendship that follows the good fortune and the riches of friends. Men of this kind seem to me to love not their friends but themselves. Let us consider more attentively the words of the Lord: But I will call you, he says, friends. And he gives the reasons why he calls them friends: Because you have persevered with me in temptation, and he did not stop there, but in all, he says, my temptations. For it sometimes happens that one who persevered with us in one temptation is overcome by others and withdraws.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"JHN.18.10":["And behold, one of those who were with Jesus, stretching out his hand, drew his sword, and striking the servant of the chief priest cut off his ear. In another Gospel it is written that Peter did this, with the same ardor of mind as in everything else. The servant of the chief priest, too, is called Malchus; and the ear that is cut off is the right one. It should be said in passing that Malchus, that is, king, the people of the Jews, once became the servant of the impiety and greed of the priests, and lost its right ear, so as to hear the whole meanness of the letter with the left; but the Lord, in those of the Jews who were willing to believe, restored the right ear and made the servant a royal and priestly race.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"JHN.18.11":["Then Jesus said to him: Turn your sword back into its place; for all who take the sword will perish by the sword. Although he does not bear the sword in vain who has been appointed the avenger of the Lord's wrath against him who does evil, nevertheless whoever takes up the sword will perish by the sword. By what sword? Surely by that fiery one which turns before paradise, and by the sword of the Spirit which is described in the armor of God.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"JHN.18.15-16":["And going inside he sat with the attendants, to see the end. Either out of a disciple's love or out of human curiosity he wished to know what the high priest would decide about the Lord: whether he would condemn him to death or would release him after beating him with scourges. And in this lies the difference between the ten apostles and Peter: they flee; he, although from a distance, still follows the Savior.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"JHN.18.15":["But Peter followed him from afar. He who was going to deny the Lord followed from afar.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"JHN.18.24":["But they, holding Jesus, led him to Caiaphas the chief priest, where the scribes and the elders had gathered. Moses, at God's command, had ordered that the high priests should succeed their fathers and that a line of descent should be woven among the priests. Josephus relates that this Caiaphas bought the high priesthood for one year only from Herod for a price. It is no wonder, then, if an unjust high priest judges unjustly.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"JHN.18.25":["And again he denied with an oath: I do not know the man. I know that some, out of pious affection toward the apostle Peter, have interpreted this passage so as to say that Peter denied not God but a man, and that the sense is: I do not know the man, because I know God. How frivolous this is the prudent reader understands: they so defend the apostle as to make the Lord guilty of a lie. For if he did not deny, then the Lord lied, who had said: Amen I say to you that on this night, before the cock crows, you will deny me three times. See what he says: you will deny me, not the man.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"JHN.18.27":["Then he began to curse and to swear that he did not know the man. And immediately the cock crowed. In another Gospel we read that after Peter's denial and the crowing of the cock the Savior looked at Peter and by his gaze moved him to bitter tears; nor could it be that he should remain in the darkness of denial on whom the light of the world had looked.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"JHN.18.28":["When morning had come, all the chief priests and the elders of the people took counsel against Jesus, to hand him over to death; and they led him bound and handed him over to Pontius Pilate the governor. He was led not only to Pilate but also to Herod, so that both might mock the Lord. And see the zeal of the priests for evil: they kept watch the whole night in order to commit murder. And they handed him over bound to Pilate. For they had this custom, that whomever they had adjudged to death they handed over bound to the judge.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"JHN.18.33":["And Jesus stood before the governor. And the governor questioned him, saying: Are you the king of the Jews? Since Pilate questions him on no other charge than whether he is king of the Jews, the Jews are convicted of impiety, because they could not find, even falsely, anything to lay against the Savior.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"JHN.18.37":["Jesus says to him: You say so. He answered in such a way that he both spoke the truth and his word lay open to no false accusation. And note that to Pilate, who was pronouncing sentence unwillingly, he answered in some part, but to the priests and rulers he would not answer, judging them unworthy of his speech.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"JHN.18.39":["Pilate offers them the choice of whom they wished him to release, the robber or Jesus, not doubting that Jesus would rather be chosen, since he knew that he had been handed over out of envy. Therefore the cause of the cross is plainly envy.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"JHN.18.40":["Now he had at that time a notable prisoner, who was called Barabbas. This man, in the Gospel which is written according to the Hebrews, is interpreted as son of their master; he had been condemned for sedition and murder.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"JHN.19.1":["Then he released Barabbas to them; but Jesus, scourged, he handed over to them to be crucified. Barabbas the robber, who stirred up seditions among the crowds, who was the author of murders, was released to the people of the Jews, that is, the devil, who reigns in them to this day, and for that reason they cannot have peace; but Jesus, handed over by the Jews, is acquitted by Pilate's wife and is called just by the governor himself, and the centurion confesses that he is truly the Son of God. Let the learned reader ask how it is consistent that Pilate washed his hands and said: I am innocent of the blood of this just man, and afterwards handed over Jesus, scourged, to be crucified. But it must be known that he was serving the Roman laws, by which it was decreed that one who is crucified should first be beaten with scourges. So Jesus was handed over to the soldiers to be beaten, and scourges cut that most sacred body and the breast that could contain God. And this was done so that, since it was written: Many are the scourges of sinners; by his being scourged we might be freed from blows, Scripture saying to the just man: The scourge has not come near your tent.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"JHN.19.2-3":["Then the soldiers of the governor, taking Jesus into the praetorium, gathered the whole cohort to him; and stripping him, they put a scarlet cloak around him, and plaiting a crown of thorns they put it on his head, and a reed in his right hand, and bending the knee before him they mocked him, saying: Hail, king of the Jews. The soldiers indeed, because he had been called king of the Jews, and the scribes and priests had laid this charge against him, that he was usurping rule for himself among the people of Israel, do this in mockery: having stripped him of his former garments, they clothe him in a scarlet cloak in place of the red border which the ancient kings used to wear, and in place of a diadem they put on him a thorny crown, in place of a royal scepter they give him a reed, and they worship him as a king. But let us understand all these things mystically. For just as Caiaphas said: It is necessary that one man die for all, not knowing what he said, so these too, whatever they did, although they did it with another mind, were nevertheless bestowing mysteries on us who believe. In the scarlet cloak he bears up the bloody works of the Gentiles; in the crown of thorns he dissolves the ancient curse; with the reed he kills venomous animals; or else he held the reed in his hand to write down the sacrilege of the Jews.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"JHN.19.17":["And going out they found a man of Cyrene named Simon; him they pressed into service to carry his cross. Let no one think that the history of John the evangelist is contrary to this passage. For he says that the Lord, going out of the praetorium, carried his own cross; but Matthew reports that they found a man of Cyrene named Simon, on whom, pressing him into service, they laid the cross of Jesus. But this is to be understood: that Jesus, going out of the praetorium, carried his cross himself, and afterwards met Simon, on whom they laid the cross to be carried. According to anagogy, however, the nations take up the cross of Jesus, and the obedient stranger carries the ignominy of the Savior.\n— Commentary on Matthew","And they came to the place which is called Golgotha, which is the place of Calvary. I have heard someone expound the place of Calvary as the place in which Adam was buried, and say that it was so called because the head of the ancient man was laid there, and that this is what the Apostle says: Arise, you who sleep, and rise from the dead, and Christ will enlighten you. A pleasing interpretation, and soothing to the ear of the people, but not true. For outside the city and beyond the gate there are places in which the heads of the condemned are cut off, and they have taken the name of Calvary, that is, of the beheaded. And the Lord was crucified there for this reason: that where formerly was the ground of the condemned, there the standards of martyrdom might be raised; and just as for us he was made the curse of the cross, and was scourged and crucified, so for the salvation of all he is crucified as a guilty man among the guilty. But if anyone should wish to contend that the Lord was crucified there so that his blood might drip upon the tomb of Adam, let us ask him why other robbers too were crucified in the same place. From this it is evident that Calvary signifies not the sepulcher of the first man but the place of the beheaded, so that where sin abounded, grace might superabound. Indeed, that Adam was buried near Hebron and Arba we read in the book of Joshua son of Nun.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"JHN.19.18":["Then two robbers were crucified with him, one on the right and one on the left. If Golgotha is the tomb of Adam and not the place of the condemned, and the Lord is crucified there in order to raise up Adam, why are two robbers crucified in the same place?\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"JHN.19.19-22":["And they set above his head the charge against him, written: This is Jesus the king of the Jews. I cannot sufficiently wonder, as the greatness of the matter deserves, that after false witnesses had been bought and the unhappy people had been stirred up to sedition and clamor, he found no other cause for putting him to death than that he was king of the Jews. And they perhaps did this mocking and laughing. But Pilate answered them, even against their will: What I have written, I have written. Whether you will or not, O Jews, the whole crowd of the Gentiles has answered you: Jesus is king of the Jews, that is, the commander of those who believe and confess.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"JHN.19.23-24":["After they had crucified him, they divided his garments, casting lots. This too had been prophesied in the same psalm: They divided my garments among themselves, and upon my vesture they cast lots.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"JHN.19.25":["But let us see what sort of companions he had: Mary Magdalene, from whom he had cast out seven demons; and Mary the mother of James and Joseph, his aunt, the sister of Mary the mother of the Lord; and the mother of the sons of Zebedee, who a little before had asked for the kingdom for her children; and others, whom we read of in the other Gospels.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"JHN.19.29":["And at once one of them ran, and taking a sponge he filled it with vinegar and put it on a reed and gave him to drink. And these things were done so that the prophecy might be fulfilled: In my thirst they gave me vinegar to drink. To this day the Jews and all who do not believe in the Lord's resurrection give Jesus vinegar and gall to drink, and give him wine mixed with myrrh to put him to sleep, so that he may not see their evils.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"JHN.19.30":["And Jesus, crying out again with a loud voice, sent forth his spirit. It is a sign of divine power to send forth the spirit, as he himself had also said: No one can take my soul from me, but I lay it down of myself, that I may take it up again.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"JHN.19.37":["The Hebrew letters daleth and res, that is, d and r, are alike and are distinguished only by a small stroke. From this it comes about that those who read the same word differently translate it now one way, now another. For the sake of understanding let us give one example: And Samuel, it says, was clothed in an ephod bad, that is, in a linen garment; for linen is called bad; hence also linens are called baddim. In place of this Hebrew and Latin expression some wrongly read ephod bar; for bar means either a son, or a sheaf of grain, or chosen, or οὖλος, that is, curly. What happened there by an error of interpretation we detect has happened here too. For if dacaru is read, it is taken as ἐξεκέντησαν, that is, they pricked or they pierced; but if, in the reverse order, with the letters exchanged, racadu, it is understood as ὠρχήσαντο, that is, they danced. And the error was born from the likeness of the letters. But John the evangelist, who drew wisdom from the breast of the Lord, a Hebrew of the Hebrews whom the Savior loved most, did not greatly care what the Greek letters contained, but rendered word from word, as he had read it in the Hebrew, and said that it was fulfilled at the time of the Lord's passion. But if anyone does not accept this, let him bring evidence from what passage of the holy Scriptures John brought forth these words, and when he has not found it, he will be forced against his will to accept the truth.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"JHN.19.38":["When it was evening, there came a certain rich man from Arimathea named Joseph, who himself also was a disciple of Jesus. He is said to be rich, not out of the boastfulness of the writer, as if to report that a noble and very wealthy man was a disciple of Jesus, but to show the reason why he was able to obtain the body of Jesus from Pilate. For it was not for a poor and unknown man to approach Pilate, the governor of the Roman power, and obtain the body of one crucified. In another evangelist this Joseph is called βουλευτής, that is, counselor, and some think that the first psalm was composed about him: Blessed is the man who has not walked in the counsel of the impious, and the rest.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"JHN.19.40":["And having taken the body of Jesus, he wrapped it in a clean linen cloth. From the simple burial of the Lord the ambition of the rich is condemned, who cannot do without riches even in their tombs. And according to spiritual understanding we can also take this to mean that the body of the Lord is to be wrapped not in gold, not in gems and silk, but in pure linen; although this too may be signified, that he wraps Jesus in a clean linen cloth who has received him with a pure mind.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"JHN.19.41":["And he laid it in his own new tomb, which he had hewn in the rock, and he rolled a great stone to the door of the tomb and went away. He is laid in a new tomb, lest after the resurrection, while the other bodies remained, it should be pretended that another had risen. But the new sepulcher can also signify the virginal womb of Mary; and the stone set against the door, and a great stone at that, can show that the sepulcher could not have been opened without the help of many.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"JHN.20.1":["Now in the evening of the Sabbath, as it grows light toward the first day of the week, there came Mary Magdalene and the other Mary to see the sepulchre. That different times are described for these women in the Gospels is not a sign of falsehood, as the impious object, but the service of a diligent visiting, while they often go away and run back and do not bear to be away from the Lord's sepulchre long or far.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"JHN.20.17":["But they came up and took hold of his feet and worshiped him. These women come up and hold his feet because they worshiped him. But that woman who was seeking the living among the dead and did not yet know that the Son of God had risen rightly hears: Do not touch me, for I have not yet ascended to my Father.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"JHN.21.11":["and very many species, or rather kinds, of fish will be in the sea once dead, which fish Peter drew out on the right-hand side at the Lord's command, and they were one hundred and fifty-three, so that the nets were breaking because of their multitude — and those who have written on the natures of animals, and especially those who have published ἁλιευτικά [works on fishing] in the Latin as well as the Greek tongue — of whom Opianicus the Cilician is a most learned poet —, say that there are one hundred and fifty-three kinds of fish; all of which were caught by the apostles, and nothing remained uncaught, while both noble and ignoble, both rich and poor, and every kind of men is drawn out of the sea of this world to salvation —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"JHN.21.15":["Others take it simply, that Simon, that is, Peter, is the son of John, according to the question in another place: Simon of John, do you love me? and he answered: Lord, you know, and they hold that it was corrupted by the fault of the copyists, so that Bar Iona was written in place of Bar Iohanna, that is, son of John, one syllable being taken away. And Iohanna is interpreted the grace of the Lord. But both names can be understood mystically, in that the dove signifies the Holy Spirit, and the grace of God the spiritual gift.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"JHN.21.25":["The dogs of the Gentiles bark against us in their volumes, which they have left behind as a memorial of their own impiety, asserting that the apostles did not have faith because they could not move mountains. To them we shall answer that many signs were done by the Lord, according to the testimony of John the evangelist, which, if they had been written, the world could not contain; not that the world could not have contained the volumes, which, however many they may be, one little bookcase or one book-chest can contain, but that it could not bear the greatness of the signs, on account of their wonder and its own unbelief.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"ACT.1.7":["The Apostle writes concerning the Savior: In whom are hidden all the treasures of wisdom and knowledge. So all the treasures of wisdom and knowledge are in Christ, but they are hidden. Why hidden? After the resurrection, when asked by the apostles about the day, he answered more plainly: It is not yours to know the times and the moments which the Father has set in his own power. When he says: It is not yours to know, he shows that he himself knows, but that it is not expedient for the apostles to know, so that, always uncertain about the coming of the judge, they may live each day as though they were to be judged on the next.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"ACT.2.16-21":["This passage the blessed apostle Peter explained as fulfilled at the time of the Lord's passion, when on the day of Pentecost the Holy Spirit came down upon the believers, and all spoke as the Holy Spirit gave them, so that those who were present marveled and said: Are not all these who are speaking Galileans? And how have we heard our own language in which we were born? Parthians, and Medes, and Elamites, and the rest; but others said: What does this mean? And when others mocked and said: They are full of new wine. Peter, standing with the eleven, lifted up his voice and said: Men of the Jews, and all you who dwell in Jerusalem, let this be known to you, and give ear to my words; for these men are not drunk, as you suppose, since it is the third hour of the day, but this is what was spoken by the prophet Joel. And it shall be after these things, says the Lord: I will pour out of my spirit upon all flesh, and they shall prophesy, and the rest, up to the place which we set out in the section. Since, therefore, Peter records that the prophecy of Joel was fulfilled at the time when the Lord suffered, we ask only this: how the earlier parts and the middle, and what follows up to the end of the volume, can cohere with one another, lest under one text and sequence of discourse a diverse explanation, dissonant with itself, should seem to gush forth? From the beginning, then, up to the place where it is written: Great is the day of the Lord and very terrible, and who shall endure it, it is a threat and a description of what the Lord is going to bring upon the sinful people. But from the place where we read: Turn to me with your whole heart, and in fasting, and in weeping, and in mourning, up to the place where he says: Why do they say among the peoples: Where is their God, it is the exhortation of the Lord, calling to repentance after punishments and torments. Again, from the place which follows: The Lord was jealous for his land and spared his people, and the Lord answering said, up to the place where it is written: And my people shall not be confounded forever, it is a promise of things to come, of the good things which they ought to wait for after they have done penance. And after many things which it would be long to go over now, these too are said in the promise, which at present we are trying to expound: I will pour out of my spirit upon all flesh, and your sons and your daughters shall prophesy, and the rest. I seem to myself to have kept the thread of the discourse from the beginning up to this place. It is a matter of the greatest labor how what follows is to be fitted to what we are now discussing. One says that what was promised generally for the last time has now been fulfilled in part; and that, because the apostles perceived the reward of things to come at the first coming of the Lord, they recorded that certain foretastes and first fruits of the wonders had been fulfilled, so that until what is perfect comes we might perceive what had gone before in part. But another asserts that it was the custom of the apostles, according to what is written of the holy man: He will dispose his words in judgment, that whatever they saw to be useful to their hearers, and not in conflict with the present, they confirmed by testimonies of another time; not that they abused the simplicity and inexperience of their hearers, as the impious Porphyry slanders, but that, according to the apostle Paul, they preached in season, out of season. And following the rule of the prophets, whatever the Jews promise themselves carnally in the last time, they said had been fulfilled spiritually in the first coming of the Lord the Savior; especially since both they and we say that what has been promised is to be fulfilled in Christ; differing only in this, that they contend it is future, we prove it already done; and of this we must speak more fully in what follows.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"ACT.4.36":["Barnabas means, 'Son of a prophet', or 'son of one coming', or, as many think, son of consolation. All the derivations, as 'Bar' (son of) either 'Nabi' (a prophet), or a derivative from 'Bo' (to come) or from 'Bee' (to console), as by the Syriac interpreter it is also rendered, 'Son of consolation.'\n— Book on Hebrew Names, On the Acts of the Apostles, B"],"ACT.5.1":["Ananias: Grace of the Lord.\n— On Hebrew Names"],"ACT.5.37":["Something of the kind is written to the Romans as well: \"Let every soul be subject to the higher authorities. For there is no authority except from God\" (Rom. 13:1). This precept, both here and there, I think was issued for this reason: that at that time the doctrine of Judas the Galilean was still flourishing and had very many followers. Mention of him is made also in the Acts of the Apostles, where Scripture relates: \"For before these days Theudas rose up, saying that he was someone great; and about three thousand men joined him\" (Acts 5:36); and, \"after him rose up Judas the Galilean in the days of the census\". Among other things he put forward this as if it were plausible from the Law: that no one ought to be called Lord except God alone, and that those who brought tithes to the temple should not pay tribute to Caesar. This heresy had grown so much that it troubled a great part even of the Pharisees and of the people, so that this question was brought even to our Lord: \"Is it lawful to give tribute to Caesar, or not\" (Matt. 22:17)? Answering them prudently and cautiously, the Lord says: \"Render the things that are Caesar's to Caesar, and the things that are God's to God\" (ibid. 21). In agreement with this answer, the apostle Paul teaches that believers ought to be subject to principalities and authorities.\n— Commentary on Titus"],"ACT.7.41-43":["That we may understand it so, Stephen, the first martyr of the Gospel, worthy of his name, relates in the history of the apostles: And they made a calf in those days, and offered a victim to the image, and rejoiced in the works of their hands. But God turned, and gave them up to serve the host of heaven, as it is written in the book of the prophets: Did you offer sacrificial beasts or victims to me for forty years in the desert, house of Israel? And you took up the tabernacle of Moloch, and the star of your god Rhepham, the figures which you made to worship them, and I will carry you over beyond Babylon. Nor must the first martyr be thought to have erred because, where it is written in the prophet, Beyond Damascus, he said, Beyond Babylon. For he set down the sense rather than the word, since they were led beyond Damascus into Babylon, or beyond Babylon. But in the place where Luke put Μολὸχ and where in the Hebrew is written Melchechem, Aquila and the LXX translated Μελχὸμ, Symmachus and Theodotion, of your king. Likewise, where Repham is read in the Seventy, Aquila and Symmachus, transferring the Hebrew word itself, put Chion, Theodotion ἀμαύρωσιν, that is, obscurity. Again, for Sochoth Aquila translated συσκιασμοὺς, that is, tabernacles, Symmachus and the Seventy, tabernacle, Theodotion, vision. And this is to be observed in all the holy Scriptures: that the apostles and apostolic men, in setting down testimonies from the Old Testament, consider not the words but the sense, and do not tread in the same footprints of the expressions, provided they do not depart from the meaning.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"ACT.7.47-50":["Thus says the Lord: Heaven is my seat, and earth the footstool of my feet. What is this house that you will build for me, and what is this place of my rest? All these things my hand has made, and all these things have come to be, says the Lord. LXX likewise. Lest we should understand the holy mountain as Zion and be covered with the Jewish error, who think that Jerusalem is to be built up and that all the things the Lord promises are to be fulfilled there carnally, he takes this suspicion away from us, and sets down the testimony which Stephen, the first martyr in Christ, used against the contentiousness of the Jews (Acts 7). Solomon built him a house, without doubt for God; but the Most High does not dwell in things made with hands, as the Prophet says: Heaven is my throne: but earth is the footstool of my feet. And Paul in the same volume: God, who made the world and those who are in it, by no means dwells in temples made with hands (Acts 17:24). For if, like one sitting on a throne and reigning, heaven is his throne and earth the footstool of his feet, how shall he be shut up in a small place who fills all things, and in whom all things are? Hence Moses too: Do not say, he says, in your heart, He is far off: for God is in heaven above and upon the earth beneath: and there is no other God besides him (Deut 4:39). And the Psalmist: Where shall I go from your spirit, and where shall I flee from your face? If I go up into heaven, you are there: if I go down to hell, you are present (Ps 138 [139]:7). With him Jeremiah too agrees, speaking in the person of God: I am a God at hand, and not a God from afar. Can anyone hide in secret places, and I not see him? Do I not fill heaven and earth (Jer 23:23, 24)? For in him we all are and move (Acts 17). And he says this to refute the Jewish error, who think that the invisible, incorporeal and incomprehensible God can be shut up in the temple of Jerusalem. This indeed Solomon himself, the builder of the Temple, confesses at length in his prayer to the Lord (1 Kgs 8). And lest we think that the greatness of God is to be measured even by heaven and earth, we read of him in another place: Who holds heaven in his palm, and the earth in his fist (Isa 40:12). By these things God is shown to be both outside and within, both poured into and poured around, since he is not shut in by the throne that surrounds him, and he shuts in with his fist and his palm. He is the creator not only of heaven and earth, but also of the invisible Angels and Archangels, Dominions, Powers, and of all men, of whom the Apostle speaks (Col 1): all of which the hand of God has worked. Of this hand both Job and the Psalmist make mention: Your hand has made me and fashioned me (Job 10:18; Ps 118 [119]:73). For all things were made through him, and without him nothing was made. Of whom John says again: He was in the world, he says, and the world was made through him (John 1). For he spoke, and they were made; he commanded, and they were created (Ps 32 [33]). And this is what the Scripture of Genesis shows in mystical speech: God said, and God made (Gen 1). For by the Word of the Lord the heavens were made firm, and all their power by the spirit of his mouth. And there is no place of the Lord's rest except the one which the Prophet adds next.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ACT.8.27":["We read also in Jeremiah that Ebed-melech the Ethiopian eunuch pleased God; and in the Acts of the Apostles that the Ethiopian eunuch of Queen Candace had such zeal for the Scriptures and the law of God that he read in his carriage, and came to Jerusalem to worship the Lord in his temple. Hence such faith is crowned with a worthy reward, and Philip the evangelist is sent to him, and at once he is taught, believes, is baptized and is saved. And he is not set down only as a eunuch, but with the addition of man: a eunuch, a man, an Ethiopian. For because he was a eunuch of Christ, and had made himself a eunuch for the sake of the kingdom of heaven, for that reason he had not lost the name of man.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"ACT.8.39":["By Spirit here we ought to understand the angel who was the leader of the people of Israel, according to what is written: Who makes his angels spirits, and his ministers a burning fire (Ps 103 [104]:4). And in the Epistle to the Hebrews: These are ministering spirits, sent forth to serve for the sake of those who are to possess salvation (Heb 1:14). Let us consider whether what is written in the Acts of the Apostles, The Spirit of the Lord caught away Philip, and the eunuch saw him no more (Acts 8:39), ought to be taken of an angel. There are some who maintain that the angel did these things in the Holy Spirit.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ACT.9.19-30":["But I went away into Arabia: and again I returned to Damascus. The order of the history does not seem to agree with itself, since Luke relates in the Acts of the Apostles (Acts 9) that when Paul, after his faith in Christ, had for many days been speaking the Gospel boldly at Damascus, a plot was laid against him, and he was let down through the wall by night in a basket, and came to Jerusalem trying to join himself to the disciples; and that, when they avoided him and were afraid to approach him, he was brought by Barnabas to the apostles, and there related how he had seen the Lord on the road, and how he had acted with confidence at Damascus in the name of the Lord. \"He was\", he says, \"with them, going in and going out in Jerusalem, acting with confidence in the name of the Lord. He also spoke and disputed with the Greeks: but they sought to kill him. And when the brothers had learned this, they brought him down to Caesarea, and sent him off to Tarsus\" (Acts 9:28-30). Here, however, he says that he first went into Arabia; and that, having returned again to Damascus, he came after three years to Jerusalem, saw Peter, and stayed with him fifteen days, and met no one else besides James, the brother of the Lord. And so that these things may be believed true (for they could seem doubtful to those who were absent), he confirms them under oath, saying: \"But the things that I say to you, behold, before God, I do not lie.\" We can therefore judge that Paul was indeed at Jerusalem, according to Luke's history, not as though going to the apostles who were his predecessors in order to learn something from them, but in order to escape the onslaught of persecution that had been stirred up against him at Damascus on account of the Gospel of Christ; and that he thus came to Jerusalem as he would to any other city. That from there he at once withdrew because of the plots, and came to Arabia, or to Damascus; and from there, after three years, returned to Jerusalem to see Peter. Or certainly thus: that as soon as he was baptized, and strengthened by taking food, he was with the disciples who were at Damascus for some days; and, while all were astonished, he at once preached in the synagogues of the Jews that Jesus was the Son of God; and that he then went into Arabia, and returned from Arabia to Damascus, and spent three years there: and these are the many days to which Scripture testifies, saying: \"But when many days were fulfilled, the Jews took counsel to kill him\" (Acts 9:23). But their plot became known to Saul: and they watched the gates day and night, in order to kill him. So his disciples took him by night and let him down through the wall, lowering him in a basket. And when he had come to Jerusalem, he tried to join himself to the disciples. And that Luke passed over Arabia for this reason, because perhaps he had accomplished nothing worthy of his apostleship in Arabia; and that he chose rather to tell, in a concise narrative, those things that seemed worthy of the Gospel of Christ. Nor is it to be counted as sloth on the Apostle's part if he was in Arabia to no purpose: rather, there was some dispensation and a command of God that he should keep silent. For afterward too we read that Paul, having set out with Silas, was forbidden by the Holy Spirit to speak the word in Asia (Acts 16).\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"ACT.15.13-18":["Where the authority of the apostles goes before, above all of Peter and James, whom the vessel of election calls pillars of the Church, there every suspicion of a differing explanation must be removed, and what is expounded by such great men, that must be followed. In the Acts of the Apostles, when a question had been raised among the apostles as to why Paul and Barnabas had received men from the Gentiles without circumcision and the observance of the sabbath, Peter answered as he ought; and James, approving his judgment, spoke thus: Men, brothers, hear me: Simon has related how God first visited to take from the Gentiles a people, for his name, and with this the words of the prophets agree; as it is written: After these things I will return, and I will build the tabernacle of David that has fallen, and its ruins I will rebuild, and I will set it up; that the rest of men may seek the Lord, and all the Gentiles upon whom my name has been invoked, says the Lord who does these things; known to the Lord from eternity is his work.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"ACT.17.23":["Nor is it any wonder if, as the occasion offered, he made use of the verses of Gentile poets, since, changing some things in the inscription of an altar, he even spoke to the Athenians: \"For passing through,\" he says, \"and beholding your objects of worship, I found also an altar on which was written: To an unknown God. What, therefore, you worship without knowing, this I announce to you\". But the inscription of the altar was not as Paul declared, To an unknown God, but thus: To the gods of Asia and Europe and Africa: to unknown and foreign gods. But because Paul did not need many unknown gods, but only one unknown God, he used the singular word, to teach that his God was the one whom the Athenians had inscribed in the title of the altar, and that they ought rightly to worship, knowing him, the one whom they venerated without knowing, and whom they could not fail to know.\n— Commentary on Titus"],"ACT.17.28":["And Paul is found to have done this not only in this passage, but in others as well. For in the Acts of the Apostles, when he was addressing the people and disputing in the Areopagus, which is the senate house of the Athenians, he says among other things: \"As certain of your own poets also have said: For we are also his offspring\" (Acts 17), a half-line which is read in the Phaenomena of Aratus, whom Cicero translated into the Latin tongue, and Germanicus Caesar, and lately Avienus, and many whom it would take very long to enumerate.\n— Commentary on Titus","That Paul knew secular letters, though not to perfection, his own words testify: \"One of them, a prophet of their own, said: Cretans are always liars, evil beasts, idle bellies\" (Titus 1:12). This heroic verse is by the poet Epimenides, whom both Plato and the other ancient writers recall. Among the Athenians also, when he was making his defense in the Areopagus, he added this: \"As also certain of your poets have said: For we are also his offspring\" (Acts 17:28). This half-line is found in Aratus, who wrote about the heaven and the stars. And also this: \"Very evil conversations corrupt good morals\" (1 Cor. 15:33); it is an iambic trimeter taken from a comedy of Menander. From these and other passages it is evident that Paul was not ignorant of secular letters, and that what he here called allegory he elsewhere called spiritual understanding.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"ACT.20.28":["Should anyone think that the view that bishop and presbyter are one, and that the one (presbyter, that is, \"elder\") is a name of age, the other a name of office, is not that of the Scriptures but our own, let him read again the words of the Apostle to the Philippians, saying: \"Paul and Timothy, servants of Jesus Christ, to all the saints in Christ Jesus who are at Philippi, with the bishops and deacons: grace to you and peace\" (Phil. 1:1, 2), and the rest. Philippi is a single city of Macedonia, and certainly in one city there could not have been several bishops, as they are called. But because at that time they called the same men bishops whom they also called presbyters, for that reason he spoke of bishops indifferently as of presbyters. This may still seem doubtful to someone, unless it is proved by a second testimony. In the Acts of the Apostles it is written that when the Apostle had come to Miletus, he sent to Ephesus and called the presbyters of that church, to whom he afterwards said, among other things: \"Take heed to yourselves and to all the flock, in which the Holy Spirit has placed you as bishops, to feed the Church of the Lord, which he acquired by his own blood\" (Acts 20:28). And here observe more carefully how, calling the presbyters of the single city of Ephesus, he afterwards called the same men bishops.\n— Commentary on Titus"],"ACT.28.25-28":["The Seventy translated this place as the evangelist Luke set it down in the Acts of the Apostles: And when they did not agree among themselves, without doubt the Jews, they departed, Paul having said one word: Well did the Holy Spirit speak through Isaiah the prophet to our fathers, saying: go to this people and say: With the ear you shall hear and shall not understand, and seeing you shall see and shall not perceive; for the heart of this people has been made gross, and with their ears they have heard heavily, and they have shut their eyes, lest perhaps they should see with their eyes, and hear with their ears, and understand with their heart, and turn, and I should heal them (Acts 28:25ff.). And at what time this prophecy was fulfilled, the Apostle Paul himself says in what follows: Let it be known to you, therefore, that this salvation of God has been sent to the nations, and they will hear (Acts 28:28). Hence too we read in the same Acts of the Apostles that Paul and Barnabas, when the Jews would not believe, said: It was necessary that the word of God be spoken first to you; but since you have thrust it away and judged yourselves unworthy of eternal life, behold, we turn to the Gentiles (Acts 13:46-47). For thus the Lord has commanded us: I have set you for a light to the nations, that you may be for salvation to the end of the earth (Isa 49:6). So according to the Seventy the interpretation is easy: that Isaiah the prophet, at the Lord's command, foretells what the people is going to do. In the Hebrew there is a difficulty: how God himself commands the people to hear with hearing and not understand, and seeing to see and not recognize, and afterward the prophet is introduced speaking, and he entreats the Lord and says: Blind the heart of this people, and make its ears heavy, and shut its eyes, lest perhaps it see with its eyes, and hear with its ears, and understand with its heart, and be converted, and be healed (Isa 6:10). And first we must resolve the question that can be put to us: why did the apostle Paul, disputing with Hebrews, speak not according to the Hebrew, which he knew to be correct, but according to the Seventy? The ancient expositors of the Church relate that the evangelist Luke was most learned in the art of medicine, and knew Greek letters better than Hebrew. Hence his speech too, both in the Gospel and in the Acts of the Apostles, that is, in both volumes, is more polished and has the scent of secular eloquence, and he uses Greek testimonies more than Hebrew. But Matthew and John, of whom the one wove his Gospel in the Hebrew tongue, the other in Greek, bring forward testimonies from the Hebrew, such as this: Out of Egypt I have called my son (Hos 11:1). And: For he shall be called a Nazarene (Matt 2:23). And: Rivers of living water shall flow from his belly (John 7:38). And: They shall look on him whom they pierced (Zech 12:10; John 19:37), and other things like these. The Epistle of Paul to the Hebrews too is gainsaid for this reason, that in writing to Hebrews he uses testimonies that are not found in the Hebrew volumes. But if anyone should say that the Hebrew books were afterward falsified by the Jews, let him hear what Origen answers to this little question in the eighth volume of his Explanations of Isaiah: that the Lord and the Apostles, who rebuke the other crimes in the Scribes and Pharisees, would never have kept silent about this crime, which was the greatest. But if they should say that the Hebrew books were falsified after the coming of the Lord and Savior and the preaching of the Apostles, I shall not be able to hold back my laughter, that the Savior and the Evangelists and the Apostles should have brought forward testimonies in such a way as the Jews were afterward going to falsify them.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ROM.1.1":["Paul, a servant of God, but an apostle of Jesus Christ. In the Epistle to the Romans he began thus: \"Paul, a servant of Jesus Christ, called an apostle\". In this one, however, he calls himself a servant of God, but an apostle of Jesus Christ. For if the Father and the Son are one, and whoever has believed in the Son believes also in the Father, then the servitude of the apostle Paul too is to be referred without distinction either to the Father or to the Son. But this servitude is not the one of which the Apostle himself says: \"For you did not receive the spirit of servitude again in fear, but you received the spirit of adoption, in which we cry, Abba, Father\" (Rom. 8:15); rather, it is a noble servitude, of which David also speaks to God: \"I am your servant, the son of your handmaid\" (Ps. 115:16). And blessed Mary to the angel: \"Behold the handmaid of the Lord; be it done to me according to your word\" (Luke 1:38). Moses too had this servitude, of whom the Lord says to Joshua the son of Nun: \"Moses,\" he says, \"my servant is dead\" (Josh. 1:2). And in another place: \"Moses the servant of the Lord died in the land of Moab by the word of the Lord\" (Deut. 34:5). God forbid, indeed, that we should believe that Moses and Mary had the spirit of servitude in fear, and not in the love of God. Nor is it any wonder that men, holy though they are, are nobly called servants of God, since through the prophet Isaiah the Father speaks to the Son: \"It is a great thing for you to be called my child (puer)\" (Isa. 49:6), which in Greek is said: μέγα σοί ἐστι τοῦ κληθῆναί σε παῖδά μου. Now because \"child\" (puer), that is, παῖς, can according to the Greeks signify both servant (famulus) and son, we inquired in the Hebrew, and found written not \"my son\" but \"my servant\", that is, ABDI (עבדי). Hence also the prophet Obadiah (Abdias), whose name means \"servant of the Lord\", received his name from his service to God. If it disturbs anyone that the Lord and Savior, who is the founder of the universe, is called a servant of God, he will not be disturbed if he hears him speaking to the apostles: \"Whoever wishes to be greater among you, let him be the servant of all\"; and: \"The Son of man came, not to be served, but to serve\" (Matt. 20:27, 28); and, lest he should seem to teach this by words alone, he showed it by example. For taking a towel, he girded himself, and filling a basin with water, he washed the feet of the disciples (John 13). It is therefore not impious to believe that he who had taken the form of a servant did those things which belong to a servant, so that he may be said to have served his Father's will, since he himself served his own servants. Now this servitude belongs to love, through which we are commanded to serve one another. And the Apostle himself, though he was free from all, made himself the servant of all (1 Cor. 9). And in another place: \"Your servant,\" he says, \"for Christ's sake\". He is a servant of God who is not a servant of sin. \"For everyone who commits sin is a servant of sin\" (John 8:34). The Apostle, therefore, who was not a servant of sin, is rightly called a servant of God the Father and of Christ. Further, his saying but an apostle of Jesus Christ seems to me to be just as if he had said: prefect of the praetorium of Augustus Caesar, master of the army of the emperor Tiberius. For just as the judges of this age, in order to appear more noble, take their titles from the kings whom they serve and from the dignity with which they swell: so too the Apostle, claiming for himself a great dignity among Christians, inscribed himself beforehand with the title of an apostle of Christ, so as to awe his future readers by the very authority of the name, indicating that all who believed in Christ ought to be subject to him. Moreover, what we set down a little before as written to the Romans, \"A servant of Jesus Christ\", does not differ from his having said: a servant of wisdom, a servant of righteousness, a servant of sanctification, a servant of redemption: \"For Christ was made for us by God the Father wisdom, righteousness, sanctification and redemption\" (1 Cor. 1:30).\n— Commentary on Titus"],"ROM.1.17":["Moreover, why the Apostle, writing to the Romans, made use rather of the testimony of the LXX, But the just shall live by my faith, and not of what is found in the Hebrew, the reason is plain. For he was writing to Romans, who did not know the Hebrew Scriptures; nor was he concerned about the words, since the sense was safe, and the present argument suffered no harm from it. Otherwise, wherever the sense is different, and it is written one way in the Hebrew and another in the LXX, note that he uses those testimonies which he had learned from Gamaliel, the teacher of the law.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"ROM.1.18":["And at the same time observe the clemency of God: he did not say: When you inflict punishments, you will remember mercy, but: When you are angry. Now he who is angry sometimes does not strike, but only threatens. The Apostle too, perceiving this, says: For the wrath of God is revealed from heaven against all ungodliness and iniquity of men. But where it is revealed, it is not inflicted, it does not strike, but it is revealed to terrify, and so that it may not be inflicted on those who are terrified.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"ROM.1.22-25":["It is a grave displeasure, after you have sinned, not to merit the wrath of the Lord. Israel had played the harlot away from the Lord his God and been led astray by a spirit of fornication; therefore his daughters and brides also play the harlot and are left in their crime without any punishment, so that what the sons and the wife had done to their true parent and husband, they may feel in their own children and wives, and from their own pain understand the pain of God, who is so angry that he does not strike the offenders at all. The Apostle too, writing to the Romans, testifies to this in mystical speech: For, saying that they were wise, they became fools; and they changed the glory of the incorruptible God into the likeness of the image of corruptible man, and of birds, and of four-footed beasts, and of serpents. And because they had played the harlot with idols, they are struck with such a sentence: For this reason God gave them up to the desires of their heart, to uncleanness, so that they dishonor their bodies among themselves, who exchanged the truth of God for a lie; and they worshipped and served the creature rather than the Creator, who is blessed for ever. Amen.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"ROM.1.24-28":["But if the heretic who does not receive the Old Testament is scandalized that the Lord is said to mingle a spirit of error or of giddiness, let him hear what is written in the Apostle, that is, in the New Testament: God delivered them up in the desires of their heart to uncleanness (Rom 1:24). And again: For this reason God delivered them up to passions of ignominy. And again: God delivered them up to a reprobate mind, to do those things which are not fitting. Now they are delivered up in the desires of their heart because they changed the glory of the incorruptible God into the likeness of an image of corruptible man, and of birds, and of four-footed beasts, and of creeping things. This we read not only in the Epistle to the Romans, but also in that to the Thessalonians concerning Antichrist: Because they did not receive the love of the truth, that they might be saved, God shall send them the working of error, that they may believe a lie, and that all may be judged who did not believe the truth, but took pleasure in the lie (2 Thess 2:10); which I think the apostle Paul took from the present reading of Isaiah, in which he says: The Lord has mingled for them a spirit of error, and they have made Egypt err in all its works, as, he says, a drunken man errs and vomits, one who is drunk with vices.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ROM.2.25":["Someone may say: Contrary to this passage is what is written to the Romans: \"Circumcision indeed profits, if you keep the Law\" (Rom. 2:25); and below: \"What then has the Jew more, or what is the profit of circumcision? Much in every way. First indeed, because the oracles of God were entrusted to them\" (Rom. 3:1-2). For if Christ profits nothing to those who have been circumcised, how does circumcision profit those who keep the Law? This indeed will be resolved by this answer, that we say that the Epistle written to the Romans was dictated to those who had believed from among the Jews and the Gentiles, and that Paul acted so that neither people would be offended: namely, so that each people might possess its own privilege, so that neither would the Gentiles be circumcised, nor would the circumcised draw forward a foreskin. But writing to the Galatians he used another argument. For they were not of the circumcision, but of the Gentiles who had believed. Nor could circumcision profit those who, after the grace of the Gospel, were turning back again to the elements of the Law. And in the Acts of the Apostles the history relates (Acts 15) that, when certain men of the circumcision had risen up and asserted that those who had believed from among the Gentiles ought to be circumcised and to keep the law of Moses, the elders who were at Jerusalem, and the apostles gathered together with them, decreed by letter that the yoke of the Law should not be laid upon them, and that they should observe nothing further, except only to keep themselves from things sacrificed to idols, and from blood, and from fornication, or, as is written in some copies, \"and from things strangled\". And lest any doubt remain that circumcision profits nothing, but that it was on account of those who had believed from among the Jews that he tempered his judgment on circumcision to the Romans, coming down little by little to the later parts of the same Epistle he showed that neither circumcision nor uncircumcision avails anything, saying: \"Circumcision therefore is nothing, and uncircumcision is nothing, but the keeping of the commandments of God\" (1 Cor. 7:19).\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"ROM.3.4":["Thou wilt destroy all who speak a lie. I see a new thing. He who works evil is held in hatred; he who lies, perishes. Let us see which is more, to be held in hatred by the Lord, or to perish. He who is held in hatred by God is indeed unhappy, because he is held in hatred by the Lord: but he still lives. But he who speaks a lie and perishes, has already ceased to be. Therefore he who speaks a lie is more unhappy than he who works iniquity: for he who works iniquity is held in hatred; but he who speaks a lie, perishes. 'For the mouth that lies kills the soul.' And how is it written, 'That God may be truthful, but every man a liar'? But if whoever speaks a lie already perishes, and kills his own soul, and yet we all lie, then shall we all perish? Therefore, from what he says here, Thou wilt destroy all who speak a lie, and from what follows in the psalm, and from the very order, we can understand that he is speaking of heretics. For he who works iniquity kills only his own soul; but he who is a heretic, and speaks a lie, kills as many men as he has led astray.\n— Homily 2, on Psalm 5","Before he laid the foundations of the world, before he poured out the seas, set up the mountains, hung up the sky, and made the earth firm with its mass cast down, God, in whom there is no lie, promised these things. Not because he could lie and is unwilling to break out into words of falsehood, but because he who is the father of truth has no lie in himself, according to that saying: \"But let God be true, and every man a liar\" (Rom. 3:4). Now God is called \"who does not lie\" for this reason: for he promises certain things with an oath in the prophets, so that we, made more secure, may hope the more that the things foretold will come to be, and, believing with our whole mind, may be prepared to attain the things that are to come.\n\nIt seems not beside the point to touch briefly on why God alone is called true, and every man a liar, by the voice of the Apostle. And unless I am mistaken, just as he alone is said to have immortality, although he has made both angels and many rational creatures to whom he has given immortality, so too he alone is said to be true: not that the others are not also immortal and lovers of truth, but that he alone is, by nature, both immortal and true. The others, however, obtain immortality and truth from his bounty, and it is one thing to be true, to have something through oneself; another to have what you have in the power of the giver.\n— Commentary on Titus"],"ROM.3.13-18":["It is an excellent saying of a most eloquent orator: The arts would be happy if only the artists judged them. And lest I seem to take my example only from profane writers, this is surely what the Prophet shows in other words: Blessed is he who speaks into the ears of those who listen (Sir 25:12). You, my daughter Eustochium, have made me a sharer in that happiness. For when you read the little preface of the previous book, in which I asserted that the Apostles and Evangelists, whether in their own words or in those of the Seventy Translators, set down from the Seventy only those testimonies that agree with the Hebrew, and wholly passed over whatever had been added by others, you at once brought me no small little question: namely, that the Apostle, writing to the Romans, made use of eight verses of the thirteenth psalm which are read in the churches but are not found in the Hebrew: Their throat is an open sepulcher; with their tongues they have dealt deceitfully; the poison of asps is under their lips. Whose mouth is full of cursing and bitterness; their feet are swift to shed blood; destruction and misery are in their ways; and the way of peace they have not known; there is no fear of God before their eyes (Rom 3:13ff.). When I heard this, as though I had been struck by a most powerful boxer, I began to seethe in silence and to betray the stupor of my mind by the pallor of my face. A Hebrew, I said, of the Hebrews, as to the law a Pharisee, and taught at the feet of Gamaliel, either did not know these things, or abused the ignorance of those who were going to read him (Phil 3:5; Acts 22:3). The one of these belongs to an unlearned man, the other to one cunning in malice, and neither to him who said: Though I be unskilled in speech, yet not in knowledge (2 Cor 11:6); and again: in simplicity and sincerity I have announced the word to you (2 Cor 1:12). At last, coming back to myself, I asked for the space of one day, so that my answer might in no way be the product of human wit, but the fruit of constant reading. And so, running through the whole of Scripture in my mind, I noticed that, just as almost the whole Epistle to the Romans is built out of the Old Instrument, so this testimony too has been woven together from the Psalms and Isaiah (Verse 11). For the first two verses, Their throat is an open sepulcher; with their tongues they have dealt deceitfully, belong to the fifth psalm (Verse 11). What follows, The poison of asps is under their lips, belongs to the hundred and thirty-ninth psalm (Verse 4). Again, what is said, Whose mouth is full of cursing and bitterness, is taken from the ninth psalm (Verse 29). The three little verses that follow, Their feet are swift to shed blood; destruction and misery are in their ways; and the way of peace they have not known, I found in the prophet Isaiah, and I am going to expound them in the sixteenth book of my explanation of him, which I now wish to dictate. The last verse, that is, the eighth, There is no fear of God before their eyes, is at the beginning of the thirty-fifth psalm. Nor should it seem to anyone a discrepancy if what is said in the singular number in its own places is said in the plural by the Apostle, who was writing to many and was drawing many examples together into one sense. I think your question is solved, and that our rule about the translation of the Old Instrument has been shaken rather than moved. And it is not so much that the Apostle took from the thirteenth psalm what is not found in the Hebrew, as that those who did not know the Apostle's art of weaving the Scriptures together sought a fitting place in which to set the testimony he had taken up, since they did not think it had been set down in Scripture without authority. In short, all the Greek commentators who have left us commentaries on the Psalms from their learning mark these little verses with an obelus and pass them by, plainly confessing that they are not found in the Hebrew, nor are they in the Seventy Translators, but in the common edition, which in Greek is called κοινὴ, and which differs throughout the whole world. But now it is time for us to apply ourselves to the work we have begun and to pursue what remains.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ROM.3.15-17":["What follows, Their thoughts are the thoughts of fools; destruction and misery are in their ways, and the way of peace they have not known, and, above, Their feet are swift to shed blood, the Apostle set down in Romans (ch. 3). Many, not knowing this, think it was taken from the thirteenth psalm, in which the verses were added in the common edition and are not found in the Hebrew; about this we spoke more fully at the beginning of this volume.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ROM.5.14":["Nor do I say this, but I say it according to the apostle, who affirms that we were all, in the transgression of Adam, cast down from paradise through sin. This the apostle says, that Adam fell even in us, who were afterward to be. Therefore now in Christ, that is, the heavenly Adam, we believe, so that we who through the sin of the first Adam fell from paradise, may now through the righteousness of the second Adam return to paradise.\n— Homily 66, on Psalm 88 (Alternate Series)"],"ROM.5.18-19":["I know certain men for whom the king of Nineveh, (who is the last to hear the proclamation and who descends from his throne, and forgoes the ornaments of his former vices and dressed in sackcloth sits on the ground, he is not content with his own conversion, preaches penitence to others with his leaders, saying, \"let the men and beasts, big and small of size, be tortured by hunger, let them put on sackcloth, condemn their former sins and betake themselves without reservation to penitence!) is the symbol of the devil, who at the end of the world, (because no spiritual creature that is made reasoning by God will perish), will descend from his pride and do penitence and will be restored to his former position. To support this opinion they use this example of Daniel in which Nebuchadnezzar after seven years of penitence is returned to his former reign. [Dan. 4:24, 29, 33] But because this idea is not in the Holy Scripture and since it completely destroys the fear of God, (for men will slide easily into vices if they believe that even the devil, the creator of wickedness and the source of all sins, can be saved if he does penitence), we must eradicate this from our spirits. Let us remember though that the sinners in the Gospel are sent to the eternal fire [Mt. 25:41], which is prepared for the devil and his angels, about whom is said, \"their worm will not die and their fire will not be extinguished\" [Is. 66:24]... Moreover if all spiritual creatures are equal and if they raise themselves up by their virtues to heaven, or by their vices take themselves to the depths, then after a long circuit and infinite centuries, if all are returned to their original state with the same worthiness to all conflicting, what difference will there be between the virgin and the prostitute? What distinction will there be between the mother of the Lord and (it is wicked to say) the victims of public pleasures? Will Gabriel be like the devil? Will the apostles be as demons? Will the prophets be as pseudoprophets? Martyrs as their persecutors? Imagine all that you will, increase by two-fold the years and the time, take infinite time for torture: if the end for all is the same, all the past is then nothing, for what is of importance to us is not what we are at any given moment, but what we will be forever more.\n— Commentary on Jonah, Chapter 3, Verses 6-9"],"ROM.6.12":["I am your servant. It seems to be a thing of humility, what is said. It is a thing of great dignity and merit, to be the servant of the Lord, and not the servant of sin. Because I do not have, he says, men as masters, because I do not have sin as master: for sin does not reign in my mortal body: therefore I am your servant. A great merit.\n— Homily 40, on Psalm 115"],"ROM.7.23":["If Paul the apostle, the vessel of election, chastised his body and brought it into subjection, lest, having preached to others, he himself should be found a castaway: if he says 'But I see another law resisting the law of my mind and leading me into captivity,' and afterward says 'Unhappy man that I am, who shall deliver me from the body of this death?' — if he fears the snares of the body, are we secure?\n— Homily 41, on Psalm 119"],"ROM.7.24":["But what follows: The length of the vestibule twenty cubits and the breadth eleven cubits, seems to raise a question: how the dual number, which is referred to two decades, and the eleventh, which lacks one to fill up the sacred number twelve, can be said to be in the vestibule of the temple; which most people solve thus: although the priests are holy and placed in the ministries of the temple and daily offer victims to Christ, yet, because they dwell in this mortal and frail little body — and one which is subject to sicknesses and vices —, they have the number twenty and the eleventh — whence the apostle too said: Wretched man that I am! Who will deliver me from the body of this death?, not that we should believe, according to a most savage heresy, that bodies are to be abolished, but that it is for us to be clothed over, not stripped, and for this mortal to receive immortality and for the corruptible to put on incorruption —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"ROM.8.8":["Let the apostle too give his testimony: \"Whoever are in the flesh cannot please God.\" If all who are in the flesh cannot please God, how did Paul himself, who is speaking, please God? How did Peter or the rest of the apostles and the saints please him, whom we cannot deny to have been in the flesh? Just as the same apostle again says: \"But you are not in the flesh, but in the spirit.\" They were in the flesh: and how does he say, You are not in the flesh? Because you do not live according to the flesh, because \"our citizenship is in heaven.\" For we who in this age \"are pilgrims away from the Lord\" do indeed walk on the earth, but we hasten toward the things of heaven: because here we have no home of our own, but are strangers and pilgrims, just as all our fathers were.\n— Homily 63, on Psalm 83 (Alternate Series)"],"ROM.8.21":["The sun therefore and the moon and the stars are for our sake, they serve us. The apostle too says that the very creation groans together and is in travail for the deliverance of the sons of God. But when the sons of God shall have been taken up, then the creation itself also is set free from this servitude.\n— Homily 58, on Psalm 148"],"ROM.8.28":["\"But to those who love God all things work together for good.\" Job, the holy man, was tempted: he lost his sons and daughters, his house collapsed, he lost everything, he lost all things suddenly — neither father nor master; for nothing sound remained to him in his body, except only his tongue with which he might blaspheme. See the tempter, the devil. \"From the sole,\" he says, \"of his foot to the crown of his head he struck him with a most grievous wound\" — that is, with elephantiasis. From it worms flowed over his whole body, and gore, and rottenness. His tongue alone he kept intact for him, that he might be able to blaspheme his God. \"In all these things Job did not sin with his lips.\" See the greatness of the temptation, see the greatness of his virtue. See and consider that the apostle's saying is fulfilled: But to those who love God all things work together for good. At the time when he was losing his possessions, when he was losing his sons, the things that had happened did indeed seem to him to be adverse: but because he loved the Lord, those evils, which he brings upon him, work together to his good. Those worms of his body were preparing for him the crown of heaven. Before he was tempted, God had never spoken to him: but after he was tempted, God came to him, and speaks familiarly, as a friend with his friend. Let the plagues come, every kind of punishment, so long as after the plagues Christ arrives.\n— Homily 6, on Psalm 66"],"ROM.9.2-5":["And when your seed shall seem to flower, and to have some shadow of piety, while you draw men away from idols, yet when you come to gather the fruits in the day of inheritance you shall grieve grievously, seeing the people of the nations preferred to you. Whence the Apostle also says: I have great sorrow and unceasing grief in my heart. For I myself wished to be anathema from Christ for my brethren, who are my kinsmen according to the flesh, who are Israelites, to whom belong the adoption of sons, and the giving of the law, and the glory, and the covenant, and the promises; whose are the fathers and so on (Rom 9:2ff.). This not only Paul but every saint says, wishing that, together with the branches of the wild olive, the root too may be saved.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ROM.9.10-13":["The apostle Paul, sifting this passage in a mystical disputation, writes to the Romans, joining together two testimonies, from Genesis and from Malachi: But Rebekah too, having conceived by one act of intercourse with Isaac our father. For when they were not yet born, nor had done anything good or evil, so that the purpose of God according to election might stand, not from works but from him who calls, it was said to her: The elder shall serve the younger; as it is written: Jacob I have loved, but Esau I have hated. For what is said: As it is written, refers both to the book of Genesis and to the prophet Malachi.\n— Commentary on Malachi"],"ROM.9.11-13":["And in both directions, before the little ones were born: \"God loved Jacob, but Esau he held in hatred\" (Mal. 1:1, 2). Here the heretics find an opening, who claim that there are diverse natures, namely the spiritual and the animal and the χοϊκὴν (earthy), and that one is saved, another perishes, and another stands between the two; for, they say, neither would the righteous man ever be chosen before he had done anything good, nor the sinner be hated before his sin, unless the nature of those who perish and of those who are to be saved were different. To this it can be answered simply that this comes about from the foreknowledge of God: that the one whom he knows will be righteous he loves before he comes forth from the womb, and the one whom he knows will be a sinner he hates before he sins; not that there is injustice in God, either in his love or in his hatred, but that he ought not to regard them otherwise, because he knows that they will be either sinners or righteous; we, as men, judge only of present things, whereas he, for whom future things are already done, passes sentence on the end of things, not on their beginnings. And these things indeed have been said rather simply, and without a loftier discussion they can in some measure satisfy the reader. But those who strive to assert that God is unjust, after the words we set down a little earlier, \"sinners are estranged from the womb\", also bring in the rest that follows: \"They have gone astray from the womb, they have spoken falsehoods\". And they say: how did sinners go astray straight from the womb and speak falsehoods, when they could have neither speech nor understanding? Or what is this righteousness of the foreknowledge of God, to love and guard one before he is born, and to detest another? And they refer the causes of this to a former life, holding that each one, according to his merit, is assigned from his very first birth to good or to evil angels. And that whole passage about Jacob and Esau, which we have just mentioned, as it is written to the Romans (Rom. 9), they toss about in such a way that they cannot be answered without sweat, and even the hellebore of Chrysippus.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"ROM.9.13":["This is what the Apostle too argues about, writing: Jacob I loved, but Esau I hated; and he loves Jacob because he supplants and overcomes what is fleshly and earthly, and deserves to receive its blessings; for first we live according to the flesh and afterwards according to the spirit. First the vices, then the virtues by which the vices are undermined, because the heart of man is set on malice from boyhood, and mature age condemns the errors of youth; hence David too: The offenses, he says, of my youth and my ignorances do not remember. And yet it must be known that Jacob receives the first blessing, Esau the second, and at the last it is said of him: You shall be the servant of your brother, so that, after the flesh has been made subject to the spirit and joined to it, it may cease to be flesh, and Idumaea may pass over into Judea.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"ROM.9.20-21":["This thought of yours, he says, is perverse: that you should think the maker does not know what he has made, and the Creator is ignorant of his creature; as if the clay and the work should say to the potter and its maker: You did not make me, or you did not make me well, nor do you understand your handiwork. The Apostle uses this testimony in other words in writing to the Romans, where he discusses the deep knowledge of God and seeks to refute the false charge of men: O man, who are you to answer back to God? Does the thing formed say to him who formed it: Why have you made me thus? Or has not the potter power over the same clay to make one vessel unto honor and another unto dishonor (Rom 9:20-21). In all these that rule must always be observed: the Evangelists and Apostles translated from the Hebrew into Greek as seemed good to them, without loss to the sense.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ROM.9.25-26":["To one who is in doubt and wavering among various opinions there comes to mind that passage of Paul the Apostle writing to the Romans: What if God, wishing to show his wrath and to make known his power, endured with much patience the vessels of wrath fitted for destruction, so that he might show the riches of his glory upon the vessels of mercy which he prepared for glory; us whom he also called, not only from the Jews but also from the Gentiles, as he says in Hosea: I called not my people, my people, and: Not Obtained Mercy, Obtained Mercy. And it will be in the place where it was said to them: Not my people are you, there they will be called sons of the living God. But Isaiah cries out on behalf of Israel: If the number of the sons of Israel be as the sand of the sea, a remnant will be saved. For he is completing and cutting short the word in equity, because the Lord will make a shortened word upon the earth. And as Isaiah foretold: Unless the Lord Sabaoth had left us a seed, we would have become like Sodom, and we would have been like Gomorrah. What then shall we say? That the Gentiles, who did not pursue righteousness, have laid hold of righteousness, but the righteousness that is from faith; but Israel, pursuing a law of righteousness, has not attained to the law of righteousness. Therefore the blessed Apostle, taking up the testimony of the prophet Hosea and expounding it of the calling of the Gentiles and of the faith of those of the Jews who were willing to believe, has cut away for us every difficulty of interpretation, asserting that it was fulfilled in the times of Christ; namely, that in Israel the twelve tribes are chosen, that is, the whole people of the Jews, and in Judah those of the Gentiles who confess the rule of the Lord.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"ROM.9.27-28":["And because he had said that a remnant would be saved, he passes to later times, and says that the full salvation will come under Christ. Understanding this, the apostle Paul too writes to the Romans: But Isaiah cries out concerning Israel: Though the number of the sons of Israel be as the sand of the sea, a remnant will be saved. For he is finishing the word and cutting it short in equity, because a shortened word will the Lord make upon the earth (Rom 9:27). And as Isaiah said: Unless the Lord Sabaoth had left us seed, we would have become as Sodom, and would have been like Gomorrah (Isa 1:9). Where, then, the authority of so great a man goes before, let every other interpretation cease. And indeed, if we read Josephus (bk. 10, ch. 2), and how great a multitude of men there was in Jerusalem and in Judea when the Lord suffered, we understand that scarcely a few of the Jews were saved, among the Apostles and apostolic men. Now the shortened and perfect word is the word of the Gospel, which in place of all the ceremonies of the long-winded Law gave a very brief precept of love and faith: that what we would not have done to ourselves, we should not do to another. Hence the Lord too in the Gospel: On these two commandments, he says, hangs all the Law and the Prophets (Matt 22:40).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ROM.9.31-33":["From this place the Apostle too takes a testimony, that Christ became an offense to the Jews, but to the Gentiles foolishness (1 Cor 1:23). And again: Israel, by pursuing the law of righteousness, has not attained to the law of righteousness (Rom 9:31). Why? Because not from faith, but as if from the works of the law, they stumbled against the stone of stumbling, as it is written: Behold, I will lay in Zion a stone of stumbling and a rock of offense: everyone who believes in him shall not be put to shame (ibid., 33). So those who did not receive Emmanuel, but for whom he became a stone of stumbling and a rock of offense, will be broken and led captive.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ROM.10.15":["What the Seventy translated, as ὥρα, that is, an hour upon the mountains: so the feet of him who brings good news of the hearing of peace, and the rest, Paul, following the sense of the Hebrew Truth, sets down in the Epistle to the Romans: How beautiful are the feet of those who bring good news of good things, who bring good news of peace (Rom 10:15): wishing the Apostles to be understood, whose feet the Lord washed (John 13), that they might be clean and beautiful for preaching, and might run through the whole world, and in a short time fill the world with the doctrine of Christ.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ROM.10.17":["Someone may ask: if faith is not except by hearing, how can those who were born deaf become Christians? For anyone can understand God the Father from the greatness and beauty of creatures, and the Creator is consequently recognized from the things created; but the birth, the cross, the death and the resurrection of Christ cannot be known except by hearing. Either, then, the deaf are not Christians; or, if the deaf are Christians, what is said elsewhere by the apostle is false: \"So faith is by hearing, and hearing through the word of God\". To this, one who is content with a simple answer says that he did not say generally, \"The faith of all is by hearing,\" but \"faith is by hearing,\" which can be taken both in part and in whole: that is, the faith of those who hear, who believe, is by hearing. But one who tries to satisfy this scruple will first attempt to assert that the deaf can learn the Gospel by nods too, and by daily intercourse, and, so to speak, by the speaking gesture of the whole body; and then also this, that the word of God, to whom nothing is deaf, speaks rather to those ears of which he himself says in the Gospel: \"He who has ears to hear, let him hear\" (Luke 8:8). And in the Apocalypse: \"He who has an ear, let him hear what the spirit says to the churches\" (Rev. 2:11). And Isaiah: \"The Lord added to me an ear\" (Isa. 50:4-5). This is the other man, to whom God speaks in secret, who cries in the heart of the believer: \"Abba, Father\" (Rom. 8:15); and (as we have often explained) just as the body has all its members and senses, so the soul too has all the senses and members, and among the rest ears as well; and whoever has these will not greatly need these ears of the body for coming to know the Gospel of Christ.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"ROM.10.21":["It can also be understood of the Savior, that upon His cross He protected us with His wings. And under His feathers you shall hope. \"All the day long I have stretched forth my hands to a people that did not believe and that contradicted me.\" The hands of the Lord raised to heaven did not ask for help, but were protecting us wretches.\n— Homily 68, on Psalm 90 (Alternate Series)","This which was said above: I appeared to those who did not ask for me: I was found by those who did not seek me, the apostle Paul, writing to the Romans, when he had expounded it of the person of the nations, added: But to Israel he says: I have spread out my hands to an unbelieving and contradicting people (Rom 10:21); a people which, when it was overcome by the benefits of the Lord and saw signs beyond what a man can do, said: This man does not cast out demons except by Beelzebub, the prince of the demons (Luke 11:15). And again: You are a Samaritan and have a demon (John 8:48). And again: though you are a man, you make yourself God (John 10:33). And elsewhere: This man is not from God, for he does not keep the sabbath (John 9:16). And many other things, which it would take long to string together. Finally, when he on the cross stretched out his hands to the unbelieving people and said: Father, forgive them, for they do not know what they do (Luke 23:34), they on the other side said: Ah! you who destroy the Temple and in three days build it up: let him come down now from the cross, and we believe him. He saved others, himself he cannot save (Matt 27:40). Of him Simeon too, holding the little child in his arms, prophesied: Behold, this one is set for the fall and for the rising of many, and for a sign that will be spoken against (Luke 2:34). At Rome the Jews say to Paul: Concerning this sect it is known to us that everywhere it is spoken against (Acts 28:22).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ROM.11.18":["Believe me: as often as I see synagogues, I always have in my mind that which is said by the apostle, that we ought not to exult over the olive tree whose branches were broken, but to fear; since if the natural branches were cut off, how much more should we, who are grafted in from the wild olive, fear lest we become like our fathers, \"a generation crooked and provoking, a generation which did not direct its heart, and whose spirit was not held faithful with God.\"\n— Homily 11, on Psalm 77"],"ROM.11.25-27":["Paul, explaining this place carefully, writes to the Romans that there is no distinction of Jew and Greek, but the same Lord of all, rich toward all who call upon him (Rom 10); and that the nations did not believe in such a way that Israel was wholly driven out. For I too, he says, am an Israelite, of the seed of Abraham, of the tribe of Benjamin. God has not cast off his people whom he foreknew (Rom 11:1, 2). And a little after, when he had set down the example of Elijah, complaining to the Lord that he alone was left, and hearing from him that the Lord had left for himself seven thousand men who had not bent the knee before Baal (1 Kgs 19), he added: So then at this present time also a remnant has been saved according to the election of grace. And if by grace, it is no longer of works; otherwise grace is no longer grace. What then? What Israel was seeking, this it has obtained; but the election has obtained it, and the rest have been blinded. And they, if they do not remain in unbelief, will be grafted in. For God is able, he says, to graft them in again (Rom 11:5ff.): not against nature from the wild olive, as the multitude of the nations was grafted in, but according to nature into the good olive, that is, into their own. And at the end he set down, so as to arrive at the present chapter: For I would not have you ignorant, brothers, of this mystery, lest you be wise in your own eyes: that blindness has befallen Israel in part, until the fullness of the nations should come in, and so all Israel should be saved, as it is written: He who delivers shall come from Zion, and shall turn away impieties from Jacob; and this is my testament to them, when I take away their sins (Rom 11:25ff.). We have pursued these things at greater length so that we may understand that whatever promises we have read, and are going to speak of, concerning Zion and Jerusalem, are said not generally to all the Jews, but specially to those of Israel who were chosen in the Apostles and through the Apostles.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ROM.11.32":["The blessed apostle Paul explains this place more fully to the Romans, and we should be doing something superfluous if we wished to comprise in a brief discourse what he pursued through almost the whole epistle. For he says, after many things: God has shut up all things in unbelief, that he may have mercy on all (Rom 11:32). And marveling at the mysteries of the Lord he added: O depth of the riches of the wisdom and knowledge of God, how inscrutable are his judgments, and how untraceable his ways. And again, disputing about the unbelief of the Jews, he says: Have they so sinned that they should fall? God forbid; but by their offense salvation came to the Gentiles, to provoke them to emulation (Rom 11:11). And a little after: For if their casting away is the reconciliation of the world, what will their taking up be? Is it not life from the dead? And again: For I would not have you ignorant, brothers, of this mystery, lest you be wise in yourselves, that blindness has come in part upon Israel, until the fullness of the Gentiles comes in, and then all Israel should be saved. And a little after (Rom 11:25): As concerning the Gospel they are enemies for your sakes, but as concerning the election they are beloved for the sake of the fathers: for the gifts and the calling of God are without repentance. For as you, he says, once did not believe God, but now have obtained mercy because of their unbelief: so these too have now not believed in your mercy, that they too may obtain mercy. For God has shut up all things under sin, that he may have mercy on all. It is therefore not the cruelty of God but his mercy that one nation should perish so that all may be saved; that a part of the Jews should not see, so that the whole world may behold.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ROM.12.20":["Therefore the apostle too says that we should give our enemies to eat and to drink. And what does he say? \"Doing this,\" he says, \"you heap coals of fire upon his head.\" Someone may say: Enemies do this; do I therefore do good to my adversary in order to heap coals of fire upon his head? But something else is meant. If he has done you evil, and you have done him good, you heap coals of fire upon his head: that is, you cleanse his vices, and you boil away his malice, so that he may turn to repentance.\n— Homily 41, on Psalm 119"],"ROM.13.1":["Something of the kind is written to the Romans as well: \"Let every soul be subject to the higher authorities. For there is no authority except from God\" (Rom. 13:1). This precept, both here and there, I think was issued for this reason: that at that time the doctrine of Judas the Galilean was still flourishing and had very many followers. Mention of him is made also in the Acts of the Apostles, where Scripture relates: \"For before these days Theudas rose up, saying that he was someone great; and about three thousand men joined him\" (Acts 5:36); and, \"after him rose up Judas the Galilean in the days of the census\". Among other things he put forward this as if it were plausible from the Law: that no one ought to be called Lord except God alone, and that those who brought tithes to the temple should not pay tribute to Caesar. This heresy had grown so much that it troubled a great part even of the Pharisees and of the people, so that this question was brought even to our Lord: \"Is it lawful to give tribute to Caesar, or not\" (Matt. 22:17)? Answering them prudently and cautiously, the Lord says: \"Render the things that are Caesar's to Caesar, and the things that are God's to God\" (ibid. 21). In agreement with this answer, the apostle Paul teaches that believers ought to be subject to principalities and authorities.\n— Commentary on Titus"],"ROM.13.13":["Whence the apostle also says: \"The night is past, and the day is at hand.\" Therefore this present age is the night: but the age to come is the day. Whence the apostle says: \"The night is past, and the day is at hand. Let us walk honestly, as in the day,\" he says. As we are to live in the day, that is, in the age to come, so let us now live.\n— Homily 46, on Psalm 133"],"ROM.13.14":["That Christ is a garment, moreover, is proved not only from the present passage but also from another, where the same Paul exhorts: \"Put on the Lord Jesus Christ\" (Rom. 13:14). If, therefore, those who have been baptized in Christ have put on Christ, it is plain that those who have not put on Christ were not baptized in Christ. For it was to those who were thought to be believers and to have obtained the baptism of Christ that it was said: \"Put on the Lord Jesus Christ\". If anyone receives only the bodily washing of water, the one that is seen with the eyes of the flesh, he has not put on the Lord Jesus Christ. For Simon too, the one in the Acts of the Apostles, had received the washing of water; but because he did not have the Holy Spirit, he had not put on Christ (Acts 8). And heretics or hypocrites, and those who live sordidly, seem indeed to receive baptism, but I do not know whether they have the garment of Christ. Let us therefore take care lest perhaps someone be found among us too who, from the fact that he does not have the garment of Christ, is shown not to have been baptized in Christ.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"ROM.15.28":["One poor man, already old, preached from Jerusalem all the way to Illyricum, and swiftly runs his word. Since, then, it had come from Jerusalem all the way to Illyricum, and his word had run swiftly, next it follows, and that very word which had said, From Jerusalem all the way to Illyricum, says: But I wish to go even to the Spains. This the apostle Paul speaks. See the swiftness of the word. He is not content with the east: he longs to run to the west.\n— Homily 57, on Psalm 147"],"1CO.1.19":["We read in another place: I will destroy the wisdom of the wise, and the understanding of the prudent I will reject (1 Cor 1:19). Not that true wisdom and true prudence are destroyed by the Lord, but the knowledge falsely so called: those who treasure up for themselves a lying tongue, and fail searching with searching, and find nothing, speaking iniquity on high and setting their mouth against heaven. Hence the pride of heretics is properly called insolence against the truth.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","From this place the Apostle Paul, writing to the Corinthians, set down a testimony: I will destroy the wisdom of the wise, and the understanding of the prudent I will reject (1 Cor 1:19): setting out the same sense in other words; not that God is an enemy of wisdom, of which he is the father, and through whom all wisdom among men exists; but he set it down with an addition, as it is in the Hebrew: of the wise of the people and of its prudent, that is, of the people of the Jews.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","Or he is the one who trusts in his own strength and not in the mercy of God, according to what is written: I will destroy the wisdom of the wise, and the understanding of the prudent I will reject; not that true wisdom can perish and the understanding of the truth be rejected, but that the wisdom of those perishes who seem wise in their own eyes and trust in their own learning.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"1CO.1.23-25":["And that the cross is a stumbling block to the Jews and foolishness to the Gentiles, our Lord himself shows, who is called a stone of offense and a rock of stumbling (Matt. 21; Luke 20): for no other reason, I think, than that preaching, when it has advanced toward its hearers under full sail, strikes against the cross as soon as it comes to it, and can by no means proceed further on a free course. But this cross, which among the Jews is a stumbling block and among the Gentiles foolishness, is to us who believe power and wisdom. \"For Christ is the power of God and the wisdom of God\" (1 Cor. 1:24), so that, because of that which was called foolishness, the foolishness of God might become wiser than men; and because of that which was called weakness and a stumbling block, the weakness of God might become stronger than men.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"1CO.2.9":["From of old they have not heard, nor perceived with their ears; the eye has not seen, O God, besides you, what you have prepared for those who wait for you. You have met him who rejoices and does righteousness in your ways; they will remember you. LXX: From of old we have not heard, nor have our eyes seen a God besides you, and your works, which you will do for those who wait for mercy; for he meets those who do righteousness, and they will remember your ways. A paraphrase of this testimony the apostle Paul, as a Hebrew of the Hebrews, takes from the authentic books in the Epistle which he writes to the Corinthians, not rendering word for word, which he altogether disdains to do, but expressing the truth of the sense, which he uses to support what he wished (1 Cor 2). Let the ravings of the apocrypha, then, fall silent, which on the pretext of this testimony are thrust upon the churches of Christ. Of them it can truly be said that the devil sits in ambush with the rich in the apocrypha, to kill the innocent. And again: He lies in wait in the hidden place like a lion in his den; he lies in wait to seize the poor (Ps 9 [10]:8). For the Ascension of Isaiah and the Apocalypse of Elijah contain this testimony. And on this pretext, and through many things of this kind, silly women of the Spains, and above all of Lusitania, laden with sins, led away by various desires, always learning and never coming to the knowledge of the truth (2 Tim 3), were deceived into taking up the monstrosities of Basilides, of Balsamus and Thesaurus, of Barbelo too and Leusibora, and of the remaining names. Of these the apostolic man Irenaeus, bishop of Lyons and martyr, writes most carefully, explaining the origins of many heresies, and above all of the Gnostics, who through Mark the Egyptian deceived noble women, first of the Gauls about the Rhône, then of the Spains, mixing pleasure with fables and claiming for their ignorance the name of knowledge (Irenaeus, Against Heresies, book 1).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"1CO.2.14":["It is a matter of great labor and of excessive disputation, once the works of the flesh and of the spirit have been set out, to find some middle things which seem to belong neither to the flesh nor to the spirit. We are called carnal when we give ourselves wholly to pleasures; spiritual, when we follow the Holy Spirit going before us, that is, when we are wise with Him instructing us, and are taught with Him as our authority. The animal men (animales), I think, are the philosophers, who suppose their own thoughts to be wisdom, of whom it is rightly said: \"But the animal man does not receive the things that are of the spirit. For it is foolishness to him\". To make this clearer, let us take an example: let the flesh be called earth, the soul gold, the spirit fire. As long as the gold is in the earth, it loses its own name, and is called after the earth with which it is mixed. But when, separated from the soil, it has received both the appearance and the name of gold, it is indeed called gold, but not yet tested. If, however, it has been smelted and purged through fire, then it receives the splendor of gold and the dignity of its own adornment. So too the soul, standing between soil and fire, that is, between flesh and spirit, when it has handed itself over to the flesh, is called flesh; when to the spirit, it is called spirit. But if it has trusted its own thought, and has supposed that it finds the truth without the grace of the Holy Spirit, it is marked, like impure gold, with the name of the animal man.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"1CO.2.15":["I know what can be objected on the contrary: \"Brothers, if a man has been overtaken in some fault, you who are spiritual instruct such a one in the spirit of gentleness\" (below, 6:1). And in another place: \"But the spiritual man judges all things, yet he himself is judged by no one\" (1 Cor. 2:15): namely, that the word spiritual sounds something other than what we said above. But we call that man spiritual, in that he judges all things and is himself judged by no one, who, knowing all the mysteries of the Scriptures, understands them in a lofty way, and, seeing Christ in the divine books, admits in them nothing of Jewish tradition.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"1CO.4.6":["And he says these things, not because the prophet himself thinks so, as I have testified above, but because he expresses human impatience in his own person; just as we often see the Apostle take upon himself the various opinions of men, and now say: But I see another law in my members, fighting against the law of my mind, and taking me captive in the law of sin, which is in my members. And, as though he were a beginner: Brothers, I do not consider that I have laid hold of it, and: We know in part, and we prophesy in part. And again, as though he were perfect: As many, therefore, as are perfect, let us be of this mind, when surely it does not belong to one and the same man to say that he knows in part and that he is perfect. And lest perhaps you think that this is not the Apostle's custom but our own argument, he himself says to the Corinthians: But these things, brothers, I have transferred to myself and Apollos for your sakes, that you may learn in us.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"1CO.4.21":["There may be set against us what is written to the Corinthians: \"What do you want? Shall I come to you with a rod, or in love and the spirit of gentleness\" (1 Cor. 4:21)? For if there he says that he comes to sinners not in the spirit of gentleness but with a rod, how is it that here, to those who have been overtaken in some sin, he applies not the rod but the spirit of gentleness? But there it is said to those who, after their sin, not perceiving their error, were unwilling to be subject to their superiors and to be corrected by repentance. Where, however, the sinner, understanding his wound, hands himself over to the physician to be cured, there the rod is not necessary, but the spirit of mildness.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"1CO.5.6-8":["Now Paul uses this very same saying to the Corinthians as well, where he commanded that the man who had his father's wife should be taken away from their midst and handed over to penance, for the destruction and affliction of the flesh through fasts and sicknesses, so that the spirit might be saved in the day of the Lord Jesus Christ. For he says: \"Your glorying is not good. Do you not know that a little leaven corrupts the whole mass\" (1 Cor. 5:5, 6 ff.)? or (as we have already corrected it) leavens the whole lump? And at once he added: \"Purge out the old leaven, that you may be a new lump, as you are unleavened; for Christ our pasch has been sacrificed. Therefore let us feast, not in the old leaven, nor in the leaven of malice and wickedness, but in the unleavened bread of sincerity and truth.\"\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"1CO.6.15-20":["So the first work of the flesh is fornication. He put the manifest ones at the beginning, so that we should not be in doubt about those in the middle. For whatever a man does is outside the body, but he who commits fornication sins against his own body. And we are not our own: for we have been bought with a price; let us glorify and carry God in our body. In this the fornicator is of greater guilt: because he takes the members of Christ and makes them the members of a harlot. For the two shall be in one flesh. He who is not a believer and does not believe in Christ makes his own members the members of a harlot; he who believes and commits fornication makes the members of Christ the members of a harlot. On the other hand, whether the unbeliever in his fornication violates a temple or builds a temple to an idol, I do not know. For demons are worshiped above all through vices. This one thing I know: that he who commits fornication after faith in Christ violates the temple of God.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"1CO.6.20":["Another, however, will look more keenly at the very word might redeem, and will say that those are signified as redeemed who were first on God's side and afterwards ceased to be so, while those who had not been under the Law are not so much redeemed as bought. Hence to the Corinthians too, among whom fornication was heard of, and such fornication as is not even among the Gentiles (ibid. 5), it is written: \"You have been bought at a price\", not redeemed; for they had not been under the Law.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"1CO.7.5":["But what follows: 'So that they do not carry it out into the outer court and the people be sanctified, the people who had not been prepared for sanctification', signifies this: that we should not readily give holy things to the unsanctified; that we should not, according to the Gospel, cast pearls before swine nor give what is holy to dogs — indeed Moses wishes the people to be sanctified for three days from all uncleanness and from the embrace of wives, so that they may approach Mount Sinai and, sanctified, receive holy things. But if those about to approach to hear and receive the words of God are commanded to be sanctified from their wives, it is manifest that that apostolic saying too is contained in the law: Do not defraud one another, except perhaps by consent for a time, that you may be free for prayer; and that by consent one must abstain from wives, that we may be free for prayer —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"1CO.7.18-22":["And the blessed Apostle, disputing of virginity and continence and marriage, threw in, in the midst: Was anyone called circumcised? Let him not draw on a foreskin. Was he called in foreskin? Let him not be circumcised (1 Cor 7:18). This seems to me to say in other words: Has he been called without a wife, and believed? let him not take a wife. Or on the contrary: having a wife, has he believed in Christ? let him by no means make a divorce. And he keeps this sense not only in circumcision and foreskin, but in freedom and slavery, so as to call virgins and the continent free, and slaves those who render the debt to a wife. For it is not in our power to draw on a foreskin after circumcision, as those in the book of Maccabees are said to have made foreskins for themselves: which is said of the sons being born, and not of the fathers. Or he who has been called free is rather the slave of Christ (ibid. 7:22), since in the baptism of Christ there is no difference of Jew, pagan, Greek and Barbarian, man and woman, free and slave.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"1CO.7.31":["That this may be understood, let us set down examples from our own condition: when an infant has grown into a boy, and a boy into a youth, and a youth into a man, and a man into an old man, he by no means perishes through the several ages. For he is the same who was before; but he is changed little by little, and is said to have perished to his former age. Understanding this, the apostle Paul too said: For the fashion of this world passes away (1 Cor 7:31). Let us consider what he said: the fashion passes away, not the substance.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","The time has come, the day has drawn near. It is not only to Jerusalem that it is said that the time of its captivity has come and the day has drawn near on which it is to be walled in by the Babylonian army; but to him too who exulted over his barns built and enlarged, the Lord speaks: Fool, this night your soul will be taken from you; and the things you have prepared, whose will they be?; whence the apostle too: The time, he says, has been shortened; and in another place: For the figure of this world is passing away; and it must be noted that he did not defer it to the future but spoke of the present, 'is passing away', and that daily 'the figure of the world' slips by: for it never remains in the same state, but the figure of things growing and diminishing ever flies past and is changed; whence the Lord too: Heaven, he says, and earth will pass away. But if these things, by which all that is in the world is contained, pass away and go by, what in human affairs can be everlasting?\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"1CO.9.5":["And there were many women there at a distance, who had followed Jesus from Galilee, ministering to him. It was a Jewish custom, and by the ancient usage of the nation it was not held to be a fault, that women should supply food and clothing to their teachers from their own substance. Because this could cause a stumbling block among the nations, Paul records that he had cast it aside: Have we not the power to lead about women as sisters, as the other apostles also do? And they ministered to the Lord from their own substance, so that he whose spiritual things they reaped might reap their carnal things; not that the Lord of creatures needed food, but to show a type for teachers, that they ought to be content with food and clothing from their disciples.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"1CO.9.20":["The question is raised in this place, if whoever is led by the spirit is not under the Law, whether Moses and the prophets were both driven by the spirit and lived under the Law, which the Apostle denies; or, having the spirit, were not under the Law, which the Apostle here affirms; or, which is the third possibility, living under the Law, did not have the spirit, which it is impious to believe of such great men. To this we shall answer briefly: it is not the same thing to be under the Law and to be as if under the Law, just as it is not the very same thing to be in the likeness of the flesh of sin and to be in the flesh of sin. Nor does a true serpent mean the same as the likeness of a bronze serpent, which Moses hung up in the desert (Num. 21). So, therefore, the holy prophets too, and Moses, walking by the spirit and living by the spirit, lived not under the Law but as if under the Law, so that they themselves seemed indeed to be under the Law, but might gain those who were under the Law and call them forth from the lowliness of the letter to the height of the spirit. For Paul too, who became a Jew to the Jews, and all things to all, that he might gain all (1 Cor. 9), did not say \"made under the Law\", but \"made as if under the Law\", to show that he had kept not the truth of the Law but its likeness. We seem to ourselves to have solved the question proposed.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"1CO.9.21":["One will resolve this question briefly if one uses that example: \"and he was reckoned with those who were without the Law\". For although in the Latin manuscripts, owing to the simplicity of the translators, it has been badly rendered: \"And he was reckoned with the iniquitous\" (Luke 22:37), yet one must know that among the Greeks ἄνομον, which is written here, means one thing, and ἄδικον, which is found in the Latin volumes, another. For ἄνομος is said of one who is without law and is bound by no right, whereas ἄδικος means iniquitous, or unjust. Hence the Apostle himself too, in another place: \"since I was not\", he says, \"without the Law of God, but was in the Law of Christ\" (1 Cor. 9); and certainly in this testimony also ἄνομος is written in the Greek; and he who translated well here could have translated the same word likewise there too, had the ambiguity not deceived him.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"1CO.10.4":["For the Lord redeemed his servant Jacob with his most precious blood, and led him through the desert of the world, and split water from the rock. Concerning which the Apostle too says: And the rock was Christ (1 Cor 10:4). And the divine word is split, and divided into many parts, so that what we cannot receive whole we may take in parts. And lest this preaching should be thought to be spoken to all the seed of Jacob, and not only to those who were to believe through the Apostles, there is added and joined: There is no peace for the wicked, says the Lord: that is, to those who remained in their former error; who did not deserve to drink from the rock. Whose side, to speak in a new way, wounded by the lance, flowed with water and blood (John 19), consecrating for us baptism and martyrdom.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"1CO.10.31":["'And meditates on his law day and night.' [Psalm 1:2] The psalmist has already mentioned three things one must not do: follow the Counsel of the wicked; stand in the way of sinners; sit in the company of the insolent. These three things we must not do, but there are also two things that we must do, for it will not be sufficient for us to shun evil unless we seek good. 'But delights in the law of the Lord.' The psalm does not say fears the law, but delights in the law. There are many who observe the law through fear, but fear as a motive for action is far from meritorious. 'But delights in the law of the Lord' - wholeheartedly he obeys the Lord's command.\n\n'And meditates on his law day and night.' Mere words cannot express adequately what the mind conceives. 'But delights in the law of the Lord.' Some one may say: 'Look, I want to obey the law of God, and so because I want to obey, I am happy.' But consider the words that follow. It is not enough to want the law of God, but one must meditate on His law day and night. 'Meditate day and night.' Someone else may object: 'This is too much for human nature to endure, for one must walk, and drink, and eat, and sleep, and perform all the other necessities of life. How, then, meditate on the law of God day and night, and especially since the Apostle says: \"Pray without ceasing\"? [1 Thess 5:17] How can I be praying during the time that I am sleeping?'\n\nMeditation on the law does not consist in reading, but in doing, just as the Apostle says in another place: 'Whether you eat or drink, or do anything else, do all for the glory of God. [1 Cor. 10:31] Even if I merely stretch forth my hand in alms giving, I am meditating on the law of God; if I visit the sick, my feet are meditating on the law of God; if I do what is prescribed, I am praying with my whole body what others are praying with their lips. The Jews, indeed, prayed with their lips, but our prayer is works.\n— Homily 1, on Psalm 1"],"1CO.10.32-33":["Let us not suppose that we are being taught by the Apostle to despise the judgments of men after his example, since in another place he said: \"Knowing therefore the fear of the Lord, we persuade men; but to God we are manifest\" (2 Cor. 5:11); and this: \"Be without offense to the Jews, and to the Gentiles, and to the Church of God, just as I also please all men in all things, not seeking what is useful to me, but what is useful to many, that they may be saved\" (1 Cor. 10). But if it can be that we please God and men alike, men too must be pleased. If, however, we do not please men otherwise than by displeasing God, we ought to please God rather than men. Besides, he himself also adds why he pleases all men in all things: \"Not seeking\", he says, \"what is useful to me, but what is useful to many, that they may be saved\". Now he who, out of that love which does not seek the things that are its own but the things that are another's, pleases all men that they may be saved, certainly pleases God first, to whom the salvation of men is a care.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"1CO.11.16":["He may also be forestalling the contentiousness of those, if any should wish to contradict him hereafter, as he also did to the Corinthians concerning the veiling of a woman's head and the man's not veiling, when after much argument he concluded, saying: \"But if anyone seems to be contentious: we have no such custom, nor has the Church of God\" (1 Cor. 11:16); that is, we have said what seemed to us honorable and just; but if anyone, unwilling to acquiesce in the truth, seeks what to answer, and on what grounds they may strive against it, let him know that he is not worthy of a response, since he is more ready to contend than to be taught.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"1CO.12.3":["But what he commands: Say to your brothers (Matt 28:10), is not to be taken simply; otherwise many call others brothers and do not keep love in their heart, of whom the Apostle writes: They profess that they know God, but in their works they deny him (Titus 1:16). Of these the Lord too was speaking: Not everyone who says to me: Lord, Lord, shall enter the kingdom of heaven: but he who does the will of my Father who is in heaven (Matt 7:21). And so that sentence can stand: No one says, Lord Jesus, except in the Holy Spirit (1 Cor 12:3). For though the heretics say Lord Jesus, and many in the resurrection will say: Lord, did we not prophesy in your name, and do many mighty works (Matt 7:22)? the Lord will answer them: Depart from me, I do not know you, workers of iniquity. Therefore saying is proved not by speech, which is easy, but by disposition and works.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","Not everyone who says to me: Lord, Lord, will enter into the kingdom of heaven, but he who does the will of my Father who is in heaven. As above he had said that those who had the garment of a good life were not to be received because of the wickedness of their doctrines, so now, conversely, he asserts that credence is not to be given even to those who, though strong in the integrity of the faith, live shamefully and destroy the integrity of their teaching by evil works. For both are necessary for the servants of God, that the work be approved by the word and the word by works. That saying may seem contrary to this sentence: No one can say: Lord Jesus, except in the Holy Spirit. But it is the custom of the Scriptures to take sayings for deeds, as is proved at once in what follows, where those are refuted who boast of knowledge of the Lord without works, and they hear from the Savior: Depart from me, workers of iniquity, I do not know you; and the Apostle speaks in this sense: They profess that they know God, but in deeds they deny him.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"1CO.13.2":["Many, reading that the grain of mustard seed is the least of all seeds, and what is said in the Gospel by the disciples, Lord, increase our faith, and the answer given them by the Savior, Amen I say to you: If you had faith like a grain of mustard seed, you would say to this mountain: Move from this place, and it would move, think either that the apostles are asking for a small faith or that the Lord is in doubt about a small faith, whereas the apostle Paul judges faith compared to a grain of mustard seed to be the greatest. For what does he say? If I have such faith that I move mountains, but do not have love, it profits me nothing. Therefore what the Lord said is done by a faith that is compared to a grain of mustard seed, this the Apostle teaches can be done by faith entire.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"1CO.13.3":["Chastity itself too, in marriage, in widowhood, in virgins, often seeks human applause; and, what I have long been afraid to say, yet it must be said, martyrdom itself, if it is undergone so that we may be held in admiration and praise by the brothers: the blood has been shed in vain. Let the Apostle speak, let the vessel of election speak: \"If I hand over my body that I may glory, but have not love, it profits me nothing\" (1 Cor. 13:3).\n— Commentary on Galatians","I know that in the Latin codices, in the testimony we set down above, \"If I hand over my body that I may glory\", they have \"that I may burn\" (ardeam) in place of \"that I may glory\" (glorier); but because of the similarity of the word, by which among the Greeks \"I may burn\" and \"I may glory\", that is, καυθήσομαι and καυχήσομαι, are distinguished by part of a single letter, the error has grown up among our people. But among the Greeks themselves, too, the copies differ.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"1CO.14.16":["And that Amen signifies the assent of the hearer and is a seal of truth, the first Epistle to the Corinthians also teaches us, in which Paul says: \"But if you bless with the spirit, who fills the place of the unlearned? How will he say amen over your blessing, since indeed he does not know what you are saying\" (1 Cor. 14:16)? From which he shows that the unlearned man cannot answer that what is said is true unless he has understood what is being taught.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"1CO.14.21":["We read in the Apostle: In other tongues and with other lips I will speak to this people, and not even so will they hear me, says the Lord (1 Cor 14:21). This seems to me taken from the present chapter according to the Hebrew; and we have observed this in the Old Testament (apart from a few testimonies which Luke alone uses, who had a greater knowledge of the Greek language): wherever anything is cited from the old instrument, they set it down not according to the Septuagint but according to the Hebrew, following no one's translation, but rendering the Hebrew sense in their own language.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"1CO.14.30-33":["This too must be noticed: that the assumption or weight, which we have already said are heavy things, is the vision of the prophet; and, against the perverse doctrine of Montanus, he understands what he sees, and he neither speaks like a madman nor, in the manner of raving women, gives forth sound without mind. Hence the Apostle also commands that, if while others are prophesying something has been revealed to another, those who were speaking first should be silent. And immediately: For God, he says, is not of dissension, but of peace. From this it is understood that when someone falls silent by his own will and gives place to another to speak, he can both speak and be silent when he wishes. But he who speaks in ecstasy, that is, unwillingly, has neither silence nor speech in his own power.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"1CO.15.28":["The Savior too was subject to his parents (Luke 2), but when he was still twelve years old and differed in nothing from a slave, though Lord of all; for he had not yet reached the full age of a man that could take up the inheritance. But it is also written of him elsewhere: \"For when all things have been subjected to him, then the Son himself will be subjected to him who subjected all things to himself, that God may be all in all\" (1 Cor. 15:28). And all things will be subjected to him when he has said, \"The Lord to my Lord: Sit at my right hand, until I make your enemies the footstool of your feet\" (Ps. 109:1). In slaves who are subject, the Lord is subject. And just as he is said to be a curse for us, though he is not a curse but true blessing, so for us he is written either as subject or as not subject, according as we are either subject to God or not subject.\n— Commentary on Titus"],"1CO.15.33":["To the Corinthians too (1 Cor. 15:33), who were themselves polished with Attic eloquence and, because of the nearness of the places, seasoned with the savor of the Athenians, he took an iambic verse from a comedy of Menander: Evil conversations corrupt good morals.\n— Commentary on Titus","That Paul knew secular letters, though not to perfection, his own words testify: \"One of them, a prophet of their own, said: Cretans are always liars, evil beasts, idle bellies\" (Titus 1:12). This heroic verse is by the poet Epimenides, whom both Plato and the other ancient writers recall. Among the Athenians also, when he was making his defense in the Areopagus, he added this: \"As also certain of your poets have said: For we are also his offspring\" (Acts 17:28). This half-line is found in Aratus, who wrote about the heaven and the stars. And also this: \"Very evil conversations corrupt good morals\" (1 Cor. 15:33); it is an iambic trimeter taken from a comedy of Menander. From these and other passages it is evident that Paul was not ignorant of secular letters, and that what he here called allegory he elsewhere called spiritual understanding.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"1CO.15.47-50":["The earth, therefore, will be scattered, and all earthly works will be reduced to nothing, so that, the image of the χοϊκοῦ having been abolished, the image of the heavenly may remain. For the first man was from the earth, earthly, and the second from heaven, heavenly: as is the earthly, such also are the earthly; and as is the heavenly, such also are the heavenly; so that, as we have borne the image of the earthly, we may bear also the image of the heavenly (1 Cor 15:47-49). Hence the same Apostle says: Flesh and blood shall not possess the kingdom of God (ibid., 50). Not that, as the heretics say, the nature of bodies perishes, but that this corruptible puts on incorruption, and this mortal puts on immortality.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"1CO.15.50":["And again, where we rendered: And their whole 'body', with 'of the wheels' understood, in the Hebrew it is written: And all their 'fleshes', and necks and hands and wings and rims; note that flesh is spoken of in the heavenly powers, so that, when we read: Flesh and blood shall not possess the kingdom of God, nor corruption incorruption, we may know that not the substance of the flesh but its works are condemned: for 'basar', which is written in this place, signifies not 'body' but 'flesh'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"1CO.15.54-57":["Where we have translated: I will be your death, O death; I will be your bite, O hell, the LXX rendered: Where is your cause, O death? where is your goad, O hell? In place of which the Apostle put: Death is swallowed up in strife? Where, O death, is your strife? Where, O death, is your sting? And expounding the force of the testimony he added: Now the sting of death is sin, and the power of sin is the law; but thanks be to God, who has given us the victory through our Lord Jesus Christ. Therefore what he interpreted of the resurrection of the Lord, we neither can nor dare interpret otherwise.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"2CO.3.10":["In short, before the Gospel of Christ shone out in the whole world, the precepts of the Law had their own brightness; but after the greater light of the grace of the Gospel shone forth, and the sun of righteousness showed itself to the whole world, the light of the stars was hidden and their rays grew dim, so that the Apostle says in another place: \"For what was glorified in this respect was not glorified, because of the surpassing glory\" (2 Cor. 3). This he now says in other words, meaning that the Law of Moses, which before the Gospel was rich and wealthy and splendid, after the coming of Christ, by comparison with him, as being weak and needy, has been diminished and destroyed by him who was greater than Solomon, and the temple, and Jonah. For what is written, \"He must increase, but I must decrease\" (John 3:30), I think was said not so much in the person of John as in the person of the Law, because lesser things always give way to greater, and perfect things are set before beginnings.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"2CO.4.16":["But now, because he says, You have been corrupted with corruption, he seems to me to have said it to distinguish it from a salutary corruption, of which the Apostle also speaks: And though our man who is outside is corrupted, yet he who is inside is renewed from day to day. He who always carries the dying of Jesus in his body, and corrupts the outer man, and subjects the flesh to the rule of the soul, is indeed corrupted, but not with corruption, because his corruption is salutary.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"2CO.6.2":["The Apostle Paul used this testimony in the second Epistle to the Corinthians, saying: In an acceptable time I have heard you: and in the day of salvation I have helped you. Behold, now is the acceptable time, etc. (2 Cor 6:2). If, then, the vessel of election refers what is said to the understanding of the first coming, let us too follow the footsteps of his exposition, and, like little children, press our letters upon the lines our teacher has traced. The pleasing and acceptable time, and the day of salvation, is the passion and resurrection of the Savior, when he prayed on the cross: My God, my God, why have you forsaken me (Matt 27:46)?\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"2CO.10.3":["Notice carefully that he who follows the Scriptures according to the letter is said to be made perfect in the flesh. Wherefore that which is written to the Corinthians: \"Living in the flesh, we do not wage war according to the flesh\" (2 Cor. 10:3), can better be understood thus, that those are said to wage war in the flesh who expound the Old Testament in a lowly manner. But those who follow the spiritual understanding may indeed be in the flesh, because they have the same letter as the Jews, yet they do not wage war according to the flesh, since they pass over from the flesh to the spirit.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"2CO.10.4-5":["And it says: He will make sport of every fortress. But what other fortress is there, except that of which the Apostle speaks: For the weapons of our warfare are not carnal, but mighty for God for the destruction of fortresses, destroying thoughts, and every height that lifts itself up against the knowledge of God. If therefore there are any fortifications, either in discourses which promise that they have a height and greatness contrary to the truth, or in all the glory and riches and strength that is praised in the world, all will be destroyed, and the word of God will make sport of every fortress. And it will cast up a mound, and will take it by the mound and the earthly things it brings forward, convicting it of fragility in those things in which it formerly seemed to itself to have some strength.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"2CO.11.23-26":["Let us set out an example, so that what we are saying may become plainer. The Assyrian once came upon the land of Paul and went up upon his country, when he was in labors beyond measure, in blows more abundantly, in prisons exceedingly, in deaths often, when he received from the Jews five times forty lashes less one, was beaten with rods three times, was stoned once, suffered shipwreck three times, was a night and a day in the depth of the sea, in perils from robbers, in perils among false brothers, in perils from my own race, in perils from the Gentiles; but in all these he conquered because of him who had loved him. And therefore he was filled with peace, because he had been filled with insults.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"2CO.12.7":["Or surely we can suspect that the Apostle, at the time when he first came to the Galatians, was sick; and that, though held back by some infirmity of his poor body, he nevertheless did not cease nor hold back his voice in silence from preaching the Gospel he had begun. For they relate that he often suffered a most severe pain of the head, and that this is the angel of Satan who was set upon him to buffet him in the flesh, lest he be lifted up. This infirmity and this sickness of the body was a temptation for those to whom the Gospel was proclaimed: whether they would despise him who promised sublime things, whom they saw subject to the sicknesses of the body.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.1.1":["Very few days have passed since, having interpreted Paul's epistle to Philemon, I had gone on to the Galatians, passing over many epistles in between, going backward; and behold, suddenly letters were brought to me from the City, announcing both that Albina, the venerable old woman, had been returned to the presence of the Lord, and that the holy Marcella, bereft of her mother's companionship, now more than ever begs for your consolation, O Paula and Eustochium. And since this cannot be done for the present, because of the great expanses of sea and land between us, I would wish at least to heal the wound so suddenly inflicted with the medicine of the Scriptures. I know, indeed, her ardor; I know her faith, what a flame she always has in her breast, how she rises above her sex, forgets the human, and, with the timbrel of the divine volumes sounding, crosses the Red Sea of this age. Certainly, when I was at Rome, she was never in such haste when she saw me that she did not ask me something about the Scriptures. Nor indeed, in the Pythagorean manner, did she think that whatever I had answered was right; nor did prejudged authority carry weight with her without reason; but she examined everything, and weighed all things with a shrewd mind, so that I felt I had not so much a disciple as a judge. Therefore, since I judge that it will be most welcome to her in her absence, and useful to you who are present, I shall undertake a work not attempted before me by the writers of our language, and taken up even by the Greeks themselves by scarcely a few, in the way the dignity of the subject demanded. Not that I am unaware that Gaius Marius Victorinus, who taught rhetoric at Rome when I was a boy, published Commentaries on the Apostle; but because he, occupied with the learning of secular letters, was wholly ignorant of the holy Scriptures; and no one, however eloquent, can discuss well what he does not know. What then, am I foolish or rash, who promise what he could not do? By no means. Rather, in this, as it seems to me, I am more cautious and more timid, in that, feeling the weakness of my powers, I have followed the Commentaries of Origen. For that man wrote five volumes properly on Paul's epistle to the Galatians, and filled the tenth book of his Stromata with a commatic discourse on its explanation; he also composed various Treatises and Excerpts, which even by themselves could suffice. I pass over Didymus, my seer, and the Laodicean (Apollinaris), who has lately gone out from the Church, and Alexander, the old heretic, and also Eusebius of Emesa and Theodore of Heraclea, who likewise left some little commentaries on this matter. If I were to pluck even a few things from these, something would result that would not be utterly despised. And so, to confess it plainly, I have read all these things, and, heaping up a great many of them in my mind, I summoned a secretary and dictated either my own thoughts or those of others, retaining a memory sometimes neither of the order, nor of the words, nor of the meanings. It now rests only with the Lord's mercy that the things others have said well should not perish through our unskillfulness, and that what pleases among its own people should not fail to please among strangers. Therefore, briefly summarizing the argument of this Epistle, I remind you by this preface, so that you may know that the subject matter of Paul's Epistle to the Galatians and of the one written to the Romans is the same. But the difference between the two is this: that in the latter he used a loftier sense and deeper arguments, while here, as though writing to those of whom he says in what follows, \"O senseless Galatians\", and, \"So unwise are you\", he tempered himself to such a style that he rebuked rather than taught, and used a style which even the foolish could understand, so as to clothe common thoughts in common speech, and so that authority might call back those whom reason could not persuade. There is indeed no discourse of the Apostle, whether by letter or in person, in which he does not labor to teach that the burdens of the ancient Law have been laid aside, and that all those things which went before in types and images, that is, the rest of the sabbath, the injury of circumcision, the recurrences of the Kalends and of the three solemn feasts in the year, the scrupulousness about foods, and the washings day after day that were to be soiled again, have ceased as the grace of the Gospel stole in, which not the blood of victims but the faith of the believing soul fulfills. But elsewhere this is discussed in part, and from the side, as this question presented itself to him while he was dealing with something else, and it is barely touched upon. In these two Epistles, however, as I have said, the cessation of the ancient law and the introduction of the new are specifically contained. But the Epistle to the Galatians has this peculiar to it, that he does not write to those who had believed in Christ from among the Jews and thought that their fathers' ceremonies ought to be observed, but to those who had received the faith of the Gospel from among the Gentiles and, having slipped back again, had been frightened off by the authority of certain men who asserted that Peter too, and James, and the whole churches of Judaea had mixed the Gospel of Christ with the old law; that Paul himself also did one thing in Judaea and preached another to the nations; and that they believed in the Crucified in vain if they thought that what the chiefs of the apostles observed should be neglected. For this reason he walks so cautiously between the two, and down the middle, that he neither betrays the grace of the Gospel, pressed by the weight and authority of his elders, nor does injury to his predecessors while he is the champion of grace; but going obliquely and as if secretly through tunnels, so as to teach that Peter did this for the sake of the people of the circumcision entrusted to him, lest, suddenly departing from their ancient way of life, they should be caused to stumble and not believe in the Cross; and that he himself, to whom the preaching to the Gentiles had been entrusted, judged it right to defend for the sake of truth what the other pretended for the sake of dispensation. Not understanding this at all, the Batanean, that wicked Porphyry, in the first book of his work against us, objected that Peter had been rebuked by Paul because he did not walk with a straight foot in preaching the gospel, wishing to brand the one with the stain of error and the other with that of insolence, and to accuse both in common of the falsehood of a fabricated doctrine, since the chiefs of the Churches disagree among themselves. These things, in what sense they were said, we touch on lightly even now at your prayers, and we will treat them more fully in their proper places. But it is now time to set down the words of the Apostle himself and to unfold each of them one by one.\n— Commentary on Galatians","Paul an apostle, not from men, nor through man, but through Jesus Christ and God the Father, who raised him from the dead.\n\nNot out of pride, as some think, but out of necessity, he declares that he is an Apostle neither from men nor through man, but through Jesus Christ and God the Father, so that by this authority he might confound those who were bandying Paul about as being outside the twelve apostles, and were alleging that he had burst forth suddenly from I know not where, or had been ordained by those senior to him. It can also be taken as said obliquely against Peter and the rest, that the Gospel was not handed down to him by the apostles but by Jesus Christ himself, who had also chosen those apostles. All this, moreover, is prepared for this reason: that no one might be able to object to him, as he argues against the burdens of the Law on behalf of the grace of the Gospel: \"But Peter said this,\" \"But the apostles decreed this,\" \"But your predecessors decided otherwise.\" This, which he now preludes as if in veiled language, he makes more plain in what follows, when he reports that nothing was conferred upon him by those who seemed to be something, and writes that he withstood Peter himself to his face, saying that he was compelled by no necessity to yield to the hypocrisy of the Jews. But if it seems rash to some that he should have spoken against the apostles, however covertly, he who went to Jerusalem for this very purpose, to confer with them about the Gospel, lest perhaps he should run or had run in vain, let us transfer the understanding to this: that to this day apostles are sent out by the patriarchs of the Jews, and by such men, I think, the Galatians were even then corrupted and began to observe the Law; or at any rate that others from among the Jews who believed in Christ went to Galatia, asserting that Peter too, the chief of the apostles, and James the brother of the Lord, kept the ceremonies of the Law. So, to distinguish between those who are sent by men and himself, who was sent by Christ, he made this his opening: Paul an apostle, not from men, nor through man. Now \"apostle,\" that is, \"sent,\" is properly a word of the Hebrews, and Silas also has this sound, he to whom the name \"sent\" was given from sending. The Hebrews say that among the prophets themselves and the holy men there are some who are both prophets and apostles, but others who are only prophets. Thus Moses, to whom it is said: \"And I will send you to Pharaoh\" (Exod. 3:10, 11); and who answers: \"Provide another whom you may send\"; and Isaiah, to whom God speaks: \"Whom shall I send, and who will go to this people\" (Isa. 6:8)? are both apostles and prophets. Hence we too can understand that John the Baptist also should be called both prophet and apostle, since indeed Scripture says: \"There was a man sent from God, whose name was John\" (John 1:6). And we can understand that in the Epistle to the Hebrews (Heb. 3) Paul, contrary to his usual custom, set at its head neither his own name nor the title of apostle for this reason, that he was going to say of Christ: \"Having therefore the chief of priests and the apostle of our confession, Jesus\"; and that it was not fitting that where Christ was to be called an apostle, Paul should also be set down there as an apostle. Now there are four kinds of apostles. One, which is neither from men nor through man, but through Jesus Christ and God the Father; another, which is indeed from God, but through man; a third, which is from man and not from God; a fourth, which is neither from God, nor through man, nor from man, but from oneself. Of the first kind can be Isaiah and the other prophets, and the apostle Paul himself, who was sent neither from men nor through man, but by God the Father and Christ. Of the second, Joshua the son of Nun, who was indeed appointed an apostle by God, but through a man, Moses. The third kind is when someone is ordained by the favor and zeal of men, as we now see very many put into the priesthood not by the judgment of God but by the purchased favor of the crowd. The fourth is that of the false prophets and false apostles, of whom the apostle says: \"Such men\", he says, \"false apostles, workers of iniquity, transforming themselves into apostles of Christ, who say: Thus says the Lord; and the Lord has not sent them\" (2 Cor. 11:13). But the apostle Paul is not such a one, he who was sent neither from men nor through man, but from God the Father through Jesus Christ. From this it is proved that the heresy of Ebion and Photinus is to be beaten back from this place too, because our Lord Jesus Christ is God: since the Apostle, because he was sent by Christ to preach the Gospel, denies that he was sent by a man. At this place the other heresies creep in, which, claiming for Christ a putative flesh, say that Christ is God and not man; and also the new heresy, which asserts a halved dispensation of Christ. And so the faith of the Church is set amid such great shipwrecks of false doctrines: if it confesses Christ to be man, Ebion and Photinus creep in; if it contends that he is God, Manichaeus and Marcion, the author of a novel doctrine, bubble up. Let them therefore hear in common that Christ is both God and man: not that God is one and man another, but that he who was always God deigned to become man for our salvation. It should also be known that in Marcion's Apostle the words and through God the Father are not written, since he wished to explain that Christ was raised not by God the Father but by himself, as in that saying: \"Destroy this temple, and in three days I will raise it up\" (John 2:19); and also elsewhere: \"No one takes my soul from me; but I lay it down of myself. I have power to lay it down, and again I have power to take it up\" (ibid., 10:18).\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.1.2":["And all the brothers who are with me, to the churches of Galatia.\n\nIn the other Epistles Sosthenes and Silvanus, and sometimes Timothy as well, are placed first in the opening; in this one alone, because the authority of a great many was needed, the name of all the brothers is taken up. These too were perhaps of the circumcision, and were not held in contempt by the Galatians. For the judgment and agreement of many on one matter does a very great deal toward correcting a people. But as to his saying, to the churches of Galatia: this too is to be noted, that here alone he writes in general terms, not to one church of one city, but to the churches of a whole province, and that he calls churches those which he afterwards accuses of having been corrupted by error. From this we are to learn that the Church can be spoken of in two ways: both as that which has no spot or wrinkle and is truly the body of Christ, and as that which is gathered in the name of Christ without full and perfect virtues (Eph. 5). In the same way the wise are named in two senses: both those who are of full and perfect virtue, and those who are beginning and are set on the way of progress. Of the perfect it is said: \"I will send wise men to you\" (Luke 11:49). Of the unwise: \"Rebuke a wise man, and he will love you\" (Prov. 9:8). For he who is of full and consummate virtue does not need reproof. According to this sense the other virtues too are to be understood: namely, that brave and prudent, pious, chaste, righteous and temperate are taken sometimes in the full sense, sometimes loosely.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.1.3":["Grace to you, and peace from God the Father, and our Lord Jesus Christ.\n\nNot as in the other Epistles does he set down the grace and the peace of God the Father and of our Lord Jesus Christ, through which, without the merit of works, both our former sins have been forgiven us and peace has been granted after pardon; but he is now prudently pleading the case against those who had been forestalled by the Law and thought that they could be justified by works, so that they might know that they had been saved by grace and ought to persevere in that which they had begun.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.1.4":["Who gave himself for our sins, to deliver us from the present evil age, according to the will of God and our Father, to whom is glory unto the ages of ages. Amen.\n\nNeither did the Son give himself for our sins without the will of the Father, nor did the Father hand over the Son without the Son's will; rather, this is the will of the Son: to fulfill the will of the Father, as he himself says in the psalm: \"That I might do your will, my God, I have willed\" (Ps. 39:9). Now the Son gave himself in order that he, Righteousness, might overthrow the unrighteousness that was in us. Wisdom handed itself over to conquer foolishness. Holiness and strength offered itself to blot out filth and weakness. And so he has delivered us not only in the age to come, according to the promised hope by which we believe, but also here, from the present age: while, having died together with Christ, we are transfigured into newness of mind, and are not of this world, by which, deservedly, we are not loved either. It is asked how the present age has been called evil. For heretics are accustomed to seize occasions from this passage to assert that there is one creator of light and of the age to come, and another of darkness and of the present age. We, however, say that it is not so much the age itself, which runs on by day and night, by years and months, that is called evil, as, homonymously (ὁμωνύμως), the things that are done in the age: just as the day is said to have enough of its own evil (Matt. 6), and the days of Jacob are written to be few and very evil (Gen. 47). Not that the span of time in which Jacob lived was evil, but that the things he endured exercised him through various trials. Indeed, at the very time when he was serving for his wives and was struggling with many hardships (Gen. 29), Esau was at rest; and so the same span of time was good for one and evil for the other. Nor would it have been written in Ecclesiastes: \"Do not say that my former days were good beyond these\" (Eccles. 7:11), except with a view to the difference of evils. Hence John says: \"The whole world is set in the wicked one\" (1 John 5:19). Not that the world itself is evil, but that evils are done in the world by men who say, \"Let us eat and drink\", \"for tomorrow we shall die\" (Isa. 22:13). And the Apostle himself: \"Redeeming\", he says, \"the time, because the days are evil\" (Eph. 5:16). Woodlands too are given a bad name when they are full of robberies, not because the earth and the forests sin, but because the places too have drawn upon themselves the infamy of murder. We also detest the sword by which human blood has been shed, and the cup in which poison has been mixed, not for any sin of the sword or of the cup, but because those who have used them ill deserve hatred. So also the age, which is a span of times, is not in itself either good or evil, but is called good or evil through those who are in it. For this reason the ravings and fables of Valentinus are to be despised, who fabricated his thirty aeons (αἰῶνας) from the fact that \"ages\" are read of in the Scriptures, saying that they are living beings, and that by tetrads and ogdoads, and also decads and dodecads, they produced as many numbers of ages as the young that the sow of Aeneas brought forth.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.1.5":["We must also inquire what difference there is between \"age\" and \"age of the age,\" or \"ages of ages,\" and where it is used for a short span of time, and where for eternity: for in Hebrew \"age,\" that is, OLAM (עלם), when it has the letter VAV placed in it, signifies eternity, but when it is written without VAV, the fiftieth year, which they call the Jubilee. For this reason too that Hebrew who, because of his wife and children, loving his master, is subjected to servitude with his ear pierced, is ordered to serve \"for ever\" (in sæculum) (Exod. 21), that is, up to the fiftieth year. And the Moabites and Ammonites (Deut. 23) do not enter the church of the Lord up to the fifteenth generation, and \"for ever\": because every hard condition was dissolved at the coming of the Jubilee. Some say that there is the same sense in \"ages of ages\" as in \"holy of holies,\" in \"heavens of heavens,\" in \"works of works,\" in \"Song of Songs\": and that the difference the heavens have with respect to those whose heavens they are, and the holy things which, in comparison with holy things, are holier, and the works which, preferred above works, are better, and the Songs which excel among all songs, the age too has the same difference with respect to those which, in comparison with ages, are ages. Accordingly they explain the present age by saying that it is to be counted from the time when heaven and earth were founded, and that it runs up to the consummation of the world, when Christ is to judge all things. They also go back beyond this and advance their step into earlier things, disputing about past and future ages, whether they were or will be good or evil: and they fall into such deep questions that they have even composed books and endless volumes on this discussion. As for the fact that Paul's prologue concludes with AMEN, a word of the Hebrew tongue: AMEN (אמן) the Seventy translated γένοιτο, that is, \"let it be\"; Aquila πεπιστωμένος, \"truly\" or \"faithfully.\" This the Savior too always uses in the Gospel, confirming his own words by AMEN.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.1.6":["I marvel that you are so quickly transferred from him who called you into the grace of Christ Jesus, to another Gospel which is not another, unless there are some who trouble you and wish to overturn the Gospel of Christ.\n\nWe first read the word \"transfer\" in Genesis (Gen. 5), where \"God transferred Enoch, and he was not found\". And afterwards in the books of Kingdoms (1 Kings 21), when his wife Jezebel transferred Ahab from the worship of God to the veneration of idols, so that he did according to all that the Amorite did, whom the Lord destroyed from before the face of the children of Israel. But although each is a transfer, the one is God's, the other the devil's. He who is transferred by God is not found by his enemies, nor can the one who lies in wait creep up on him. This indeed is what I judge \"and he was not found\" to signify. But he who is transferred by the devil is transferred into that which seems to be, but is not. The wise men of the age, too, call those who are transferred from one doctrine to another \"transferred\": thus that Dionysius (whose opinion had formerly been that pain is not an evil, but who, after he was overwhelmed by calamities and tormented by pain, began to affirm that pain was the greatest of all evils) was called by them the Transposed (Transpositus), or the Transferred (Translatus), because, namely, departing from his former tenet, he had fallen back into its contrary. Paul therefore marvels: first, that they have been transferred from the freedom of the Gospel into the slavery of legal works; next, that they were transferred so quickly, since it is not the same guilt to be transferred by someone with difficulty and to be transferred quickly; just as in martyrdom he who without struggle and torments leapt forward at once to deny is not punished with the same penalty as he who, twisted amid the rack, the cords and the fires, was compelled to deny what he believed. The preaching of the Gospel was still recent; no great time lay in between since the Apostle had brought the Galatians over from idols to Christ. He marvels therefore how they had so quickly departed from him in whose name they had lately been made Christians. The passage itself also has a ὑπέρβατον (hyperbaton, a transposition of word order), which can be read in its proper order thus: I marvel that you are so quickly transferred from Christ Jesus, who called you into grace, saying: \"I came not to call the righteous, but sinners\" to repentance (Mark 2:17). For by grace we have been saved, and not through the law (Eph. 2:8).\n— Commentary on Galatians","And this too is fitting, that the word \"transfer\" has been fitted to the Galatians: for Galatia in our language means \"transfer\".\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.1.7":["\"But you have been transferred,\" he says, \"to another Gospel, which is not another\": because everything that is false does not subsist, and what is contrary to the truth is not, as in that verse: \"Give not, O Lord, your scepter to those who are not\" (Esther 14:11). And God called the things which were not, so as to make to be that which was not. Now if this is said of those who believed in the same God and had the same Scriptures, that they were transferred to another Gospel which is not a Gospel, what ought we to think of Marcion and the other heretics, who reject the Creator and feign a Christ of another God? They do not slip and fall in the interpretation of the law and the letter, or in the struggle of flesh and spirit, but they are at variance with the whole order of the Church. And he says finely: Unless there are some who trouble you, and wish to overturn the Gospel of Christ. \"They wish,\" he says, \"to change, to overturn, to disturb the Gospel of Christ\": but they are not able. For its nature is such that it cannot be other than what is true. Everyone who interprets the Gospel in another spirit and mind than it was written troubles believers and overturns the Gospel of Christ, so as to put behind the back what is in front, and to turn into the front what is behind the back. If anyone follows only the letter, he puts the hinder parts in front. If anyone acquiesces in the interpretations of the Jews, he casts behind his back the things which by their own nature were set in front.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.1.8-9":["And for this reason he pronounced anathema both on himself, whom they accused of doing one thing in Judea and teaching another among the Gentiles, and on an angel, who was admittedly greater even than the apostles who preceded him: so that the authority of Peter and John might not be thought great, since neither he himself, who had taught them before, nor an angel was permitted to preach otherwise than they had once learned. So he named himself and the angel expressly, but the others without a name: If anyone, he says, has preached the gospel to you, so that by a general term he might do no injury to his predecessors, and yet might covertly point to their names.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.1.8":["But though we, or an angel from heaven, preach the gospel to you other than what we have preached to you, let him be anathema. As we said before, and now I say again: if anyone has preached the gospel to you other than what you have received, let him be anathema.\n\nIt can also be taken as said hyperbolically: not that either an apostle or an angel could have preached otherwise than they had once spoken, but that, even if it could come about that both apostles and angels were changed, still one must not depart from what had once been received; especially since the Apostle himself in another place shows the firmness of his faith, saying: \"I know that neither death, nor life, nor angels, nor principalities, nor things present, nor things to come, nor strength, nor height, nor depth, nor any other creature, will be able to separate us from the love of God, which is in Christ Jesus our Lord\" (Rom. 8:38). \"I speak the truth, I do not lie, my conscience bearing me witness\" (Rom. 9:1). These indeed are not the words of one who could at some time depart from the faith and love of Christ. But those who will not have this said καθ᾽ ὑπόθεσιν (hypothetically), but in earnest, namely that both apostles and angels can be turned to worse things, object that Paul himself also knew that he could fall if he acted too slackly, saying: \"But I subdue my body and bring it into slavery, lest, while preaching to others, I myself be found reprobate\" (1 Cor. 9:27); that angels too are changeable, who did not keep their principality but, leaving their own dwelling, are reserved under darkness in eternal chains for the judgment of the great day (Jude 6); that the nature of God alone is unchangeable, of whom it is written: \"But you are the same\" (Ps. 101:28). And he says of himself: \"I am your God, and I do not change\" (Mal. 3:6). That Lucifer fell, who rose in the morning, and that he who once sent to all the nations was crushed upon the earth. Elegantly, on this passage, that most learned man Tertullian, writing against Apelles and his virgin Philumene, whom a certain perverse angel of a devilish spirit had filled, writes that this is the angel on whom, long before Apelles was born, anathema was prophesied by the Apostle through the foretelling of the Holy Spirit. Moreover, ἀνάθεμα is properly a word of the Jews, and it is set down both in Joshua son of Nun (Josh. 6:17) and in Numbers (Num. 21), when the Lord commanded that all the things that were in Jericho, and those of the Midianites, be held as objects of detestation and anathema. Let us ask those who assert that Christ and the apostle Paul are respectively the son and the slave of the good God, who was unknown until that time, a God who does not know how to curse and has never learned to condemn anyone: how is it that his Apostle now uses a word of the Jews, that is, of the Creator, and wishes either an angel or an apostle to perish, when he himself is not accustomed to take vengeance?\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.1.9":["But in adding as we said before, and now I say again, he shows that at the beginning too, guarding against this very thing, he had pronounced anathema on those who were going to preach otherwise, and that now, after the preaching has been done, he decrees the anathema which he had foretold before.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.1.10":["For do I now persuade men, or God? Or do I seek to please men? If I were still pleasing men, I would not be the slave of Christ.\n\nLet us not suppose that we are being taught by the Apostle to despise the judgments of men after his example, since in another place he said: \"Knowing therefore the fear of the Lord, we persuade men; but to God we are manifest\" (2 Cor. 5:11); and this: \"Be without offense to the Jews, and to the Gentiles, and to the Church of God, just as I also please all men in all things, not seeking what is useful to me, but what is useful to many, that they may be saved\" (1 Cor. 10). But if it can be that we please God and men alike, men too must be pleased. If, however, we do not please men otherwise than by displeasing God, we ought to please God rather than men. Besides, he himself also adds why he pleases all men in all things: \"Not seeking\", he says, \"what is useful to me, but what is useful to many, that they may be saved\". Now he who, out of that love which does not seek the things that are its own but the things that are another's, pleases all men that they may be saved, certainly pleases God first, to whom the salvation of men is a care. Moreover, the word that has been specially added here, now, also implies that men are either to be pleased or to be displeased according to the time: so that he who now does not please on account of the truth of the Gospel may once have pleased for the salvation of very many. Paul had at one time pleased the Jews, when, being a zealot for the ancestral traditions, he had formerly lived in the law without reproach; and he had so much ardor and faith in the ceremonies of the forefathers that he was a partaker in the killing of Stephen, and went on to Damascus to bind those who had fallen away from the law (Acts 9). But after he was transferred from a persecutor into a vessel of election and began to preach the faith which he had once fought against, he began likewise to displease the Jews whom he had pleased before. This, then, is what he says: Do I seek to please the Jews, by displeasing whom I have pleased God? For if I were pleasing them, I would still not be the slave of Christ. For I would be upholding the law and destroying the grace of the Gospel. But now, for this reason, I am not induced even into a pretense of observing the law: because I am not able to please God and the Jews alike; for whoever pleases them displeases God. The very word to persuade, too, is taken from human usage, when someone tries to implant in others as well what he himself holds and has once imbibed; and it is read in very many places of the Scriptures, among which is this: \"The persuasion is not from him who called you\" (Gal. 5:8). And also in the Acts of the Apostles: \"So many Jews came to him at his lodging, to whom he expounded, testifying to them the kingdom of God, and persuading them concerning Jesus from the Law of Moses and the Prophets until evening\". Now all this was because it had been disseminated concerning him that he secretly kept the Law, and that at Jerusalem he had entered into fellowship with those who were Judaizing.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.1.11-12":["For I make known to you, brothers, the Gospel which was preached by me: that it is not according to man; for neither did I receive it from man, nor did I learn it, but through the revelation of Jesus Christ.\n\nBy this passage the doctrine of Ebion and Photinus is crushed, in that Christ is God, and not only a man. For if the Gospel of Paul is not according to man, and he neither received it from man nor learned it, but through the revelation of Jesus Christ, then Jesus Christ, who revealed the Gospel to Paul, is certainly not a man. And if he is not a man, it follows that he is God. Not that we deny that a man was assumed, but that we reject that he is only a man. It is asked whether the Churches of the whole world have received the Gospel of God, or that of man: for how few of us have learned through the revelation of Christ, and have not come to know it through a man preaching? To this we shall answer that those who can say: \"Do you seek a proof of him who speaks in me, Christ\" (2 Cor. 13:3)? and: \"But I live, now not I, but Christ lives in me\" (Gal. 2:20), are not so much themselves the ones teaching as God in them, who says to the saints: \"I have said, You are gods, and all of you sons of the Most High\" (Ps. 81:6); and at once, of sinners: \"But you shall die like men, and shall fall like one of the princes\". Since, then, Paul speaks, and Peter, who do not die like men nor fall like one of the princes, it is manifest that they are gods. And those who are gods hand on the Gospel of God, and not of man. Marcion and Basilides and the other plagues of the heretics do not have the Gospel of God, because they do not have the Holy Spirit, without whom the Gospel that is taught becomes human. Nor let us think that the Gospel lies in the words of the Scriptures, but in the sense; not on the surface, but in the marrow; not in the leaves of the words, but in the root of the meaning. It is said in the prophet concerning God: \"His words are good with him\" (Mic. 2:7). Scripture is profitable to those who hear it only when it is not spoken without Christ, when it is not brought forward without the Father, when he who preaches does not instill it without the Spirit. Otherwise both the devil, who speaks from the Scriptures, and all the heresies, according to Ezekiel (ch. 13), sew for themselves from them pillows to place under the elbow of every age. I too, who am speaking, if I have Christ in me, do not have the Gospel of man; but if I am a sinner, it is said to me: \"To the sinner God said: Why do you declare my righteous deeds, and take my Testament upon your lips? But you have hated discipline, and have cast my words behind you\" (Ps. 49:16, 17), and the rest that follows. It is a great danger to speak in the Church, lest by a perverse interpretation the Gospel of Christ should become the gospel of man, or, what is worse, of the devil.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.1.12":["Between receiving and learning, moreover, there is this difference: he receives the Gospel to whom it is first instilled and who is led to faith in it, so that he believes that the things written are yours. But he learns who comes to know, explained and set out in discourse, the things which are figured in it through enigmas and parables; and he comes to know them not by a man revealing them, but by Christ, who revealed them to Paul, or through Paul, in whom Christ speaks. The very word ἀποκαλύψεως, that is, of revelation, also belongs properly to the Scriptures, and was used by none of the wise men of the world among the Greeks. Hence it seems to me that, as with other words which the Seventy translators carried over from Hebrew into Greek, so with this one too they took great pains to express the proper character of a foreign tongue, fashioning new words for new things; and that it signifies when something covered and veiled, its covering taken off from above, is shown and brought forth into the light. That this may become plainer, take an example. Moses used to speak with God with face revealed and open (Exod. 33, 34), that is, without a veil; but when speaking to the people, because they were not able to look upon his face, he put a veil on his face (Num. 4). Before the Ark of the Testament, too, a veil had been spread: and when it had been drawn back, the things which before had been hidden were brought forth, and, to use the very word, were revealed. If, therefore, those who are accustomed to read the eloquent writers of the world begin to mock us for the novelty and meanness of our language, let us send them to the books of Cicero which are entitled On Questions of Philosophy, and let them see by how great a necessity he was compelled there to bring forth such monstrous words as the ear of a Latin man never heard; and this when he was translating into our tongue from Greek, which is a neighboring language. What do they endure who try to express the proper sense of Hebrew difficulties? And yet in such great volumes of the Scriptures there are far fewer things that have a ring of novelty than those which he gathered together in one small work. It can also be taken, as we said at the beginning when we were expounding \"Paul an apostle, not from men, nor through man\", so in the present passage too, as aimed obliquely at Peter and the others who preceded him: that he who has Christ alone as the teacher of the Gospel is moved by no one as by Law and authority. Moreover, he means that revelation when, going to Damascus, he was found worthy to hear the voice of Christ on the way, and with his eyes blinded beheld the true light of the world.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.1.13-14":["For you have heard of my conduct formerly in Judaism: that beyond measure I persecuted the Church of God, and fought against it; and I advanced in Judaism beyond many of my contemporaries in my race, being more abundantly a zealot for the traditions of my fathers.\n\nThis narrative is of very great profit to the Galatians: how Paul, once the ravager of the Church and the fiercest defender of Judaism, was suddenly converted to the faith of Christ. And this at the time when the Crucified was first being proclaimed in the world, when the new doctrine was being driven from the bounds of the whole earth by both the Gentiles and the Jews. For they could say: If he who from an early age was trained in the disciplines of the Pharisees, who surpassed all his contemporaries in the Jewish tradition, now defends the Church which he once persecuted most vehemently, and would rather have the grace and newness of Christ with the ill will of all than the oldness of the Law with the praise of many, what ought we to do, who have begun to be Christians out of heathenism?\n— Commentary on Galatians","But to this very day those who understand the Scriptures in a Jewish sense persecute the Church of Christ and lay it waste, corrupted not by zeal for the Law of God but by the traditions of men.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.1.13":["And he fittingly added: Beyond measure I persecuted the Church of God, so that from this too admiration might arise, because it was not just anyone of those who persecuted the Church lightly, but he who outdid the rest in persecution, who was converted to the faith.\n— Commentary on Galatians","And how fine is the observation and the weight of the words: You have heard, he says, of my conduct formerly in Judaism: conduct, not grace; formerly, not now; in Judaism, not in the law of God. That beyond measure I persecuted the Church of God, and laid waste to it. He did not persecute as the others did, but beyond measure. Nor was a persecution, however vehement, enough for him: but like some brigand and robber he ravaged the Church. Nor does he say the Church of Christ, as he then thought, Christ whom he held in contempt, whom he persecuted; but, as he now believes, the Church of God: signifying either that Christ himself is God, or that the Church belongs to the same God who was once the giver of the Law.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.1.14":["And prudently, while narrating something else, he inserts that he had served not so much the Law of God as the ancestral traditions, that is, those of the Pharisees, who teach the doctrines and commandments of men (Matt. 15; Mark 7) and reject the Law of God in order to establish their own traditions.\n— Commentary on Galatians","And I advanced, he says, in Judaism beyond many of my contemporaries in my race, being more abundantly a zealot for the traditions of my fathers. Again he calls it an advance not in the Law of God, but in Judaism. And not beyond all, but beyond very many; and not beyond the old, but beyond his contemporaries; so as both to relate his zeal in the Law and to avoid boasting. And in mentioning the traditions of the fathers, not the commandments of the Lord, he showed both that he was a Pharisee of Pharisees, and that he had indeed had a zeal for God, but not according to knowledge.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.1.15":["But when it pleased him who set me apart from my mother's womb, and called me through his grace, to reveal his Son in me, that I might preach him among the Gentiles.\n\nNot only in this place, but also in writing to the Romans, Paul says that he was set apart for the Gospel of God. And Jeremiah, before he was formed in the womb and conceived in his mother's belly, is said to have been known to God and sanctified (Jer. 1). And in the person of the righteous man, or, as some think, in the person of the Savior, it is said (Ps. 21:11): \"Upon you I was cast from the womb: from my mother's womb you are my God\". On the other hand, David sings of sinners: \"For behold, I was conceived in iniquities, and in sins my mother conceived me\" (Ps. 50:7). And in another place: \"Sinners are estranged from the womb\" (Ps. 57:4). And in both directions, before the little ones were born: \"God loved Jacob, but Esau he held in hatred\" (Mal. 1:1, 2). Here the heretics find an opening, who claim that there are diverse natures, namely the spiritual and the animal and the χοϊκὴν (earthy), and that one is saved, another perishes, and another stands between the two; for, they say, neither would the righteous man ever be chosen before he had done anything good, nor the sinner be hated before his sin, unless the nature of those who perish and of those who are to be saved were different. To this it can be answered simply that this comes about from the foreknowledge of God: that the one whom he knows will be righteous he loves before he comes forth from the womb, and the one whom he knows will be a sinner he hates before he sins; not that there is injustice in God, either in his love or in his hatred, but that he ought not to regard them otherwise, because he knows that they will be either sinners or righteous; we, as men, judge only of present things, whereas he, for whom future things are already done, passes sentence on the end of things, not on their beginnings. And these things indeed have been said rather simply, and without a loftier discussion they can in some measure satisfy the reader. But those who strive to assert that God is unjust, after the words we set down a little earlier, \"sinners are estranged from the womb\", also bring in the rest that follows: \"They have gone astray from the womb, they have spoken falsehoods\". And they say: how did sinners go astray straight from the womb and speak falsehoods, when they could have neither speech nor understanding? Or what is this righteousness of the foreknowledge of God, to love and guard one before he is born, and to detest another? And they refer the causes of this to a former life, holding that each one, according to his merit, is assigned from his very first birth to good or to evil angels. And that whole passage about Jacob and Esau, which we have just mentioned, as it is written to the Romans (Rom. 9), they toss about in such a way that they cannot be answered without sweat, and even the hellebore of Chrysippus.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.1.16":["But to reveal his Son in me is not the same as if he were to say to reveal his Son to me. For to whom something is revealed, to him that can be revealed which was not in him before. But in whom something is revealed, that is revealed which was in him before and afterwards has been revealed. Similar is that saying in the Gospel: \"He stands in the midst of you, whom you do not know\" (John 1:26). And elsewhere: \"That was the true light, which enlightens every man coming into the world\" (ibid., 9). From this it becomes clear that the knowledge of God is present in all by nature, and that no one is born without Christ and without having in himself the seeds of wisdom and of righteousness and of the other virtues. Hence many, without faith and the Gospel of Christ, do some things either wisely or in a holy manner, such as obeying their parents, stretching out a hand to the needy, not oppressing their neighbors, not plundering what belongs to others; and they become all the more liable to the judgment of God, because, having in themselves the beginnings of the virtues and the seeds of God, they do not believe in him without whom they cannot exist. The Son of God revealed in Paul can also be understood otherwise: that, through his preaching, he was recognized by the Gentiles, who had not known him before.\n— Commentary on Galatians","Immediately I did not acquiesce in flesh and blood.\n\nOr, as it stands better in the Greek: \"I did not confer with flesh and blood\". I know that most people think this was said of the apostles. For Porphyry too raises the objection that after the revelation of Christ he did not deign to go to men and confer in discourse with them, lest, that is to say, after being taught by God he should be instructed by flesh and blood. But far be it from me to think Peter, John and James flesh and blood, which cannot possess the kingdom of God (1 Cor. 15:50). If the spiritual apostles are flesh and blood, what are we to think of the earthy (χοϊκοῖς)? Plainly, Paul did not confer with flesh and blood after the revelation of Christ, because he did not wish to cast pearls before swine, nor to give what is holy to dogs (Matt. 7). See what is written of sinners: \"My spirit shall not remain in these men, because they are flesh\" (Gen. 6:3). With such as were flesh and blood, which also did not reveal the Son of God to Peter (Matt. 16), the Apostle did not confer the Gospel that had been revealed to him, but little by little he turned them from flesh and blood into spirit, and only then did he entrust to them the hidden mysteries of the Gospel. Someone may say: If he did not immediately confer the Gospel with flesh and blood, yet it is understood by implication that he conferred with blood and flesh afterward; and this sense, by which the apostles are excused from being flesh and blood, will not be able to stand, since he who at the beginning did not confer with flesh and blood nonetheless afterward, as I said, conferred with flesh and blood. This placing of the word before constrains us to divide the sentence so that we do not join at once (statim), or immediately (continuo), with flesh and blood, but make it cling to what precedes, and it is read: \"But when it pleased him who set me apart from my mother's womb\"; and then: \"To reveal his Son in me\"; and finally: \"That I might preach him among the Gentiles immediately\"; so that \"I did not confer with flesh and blood\" begins from its own opening. And we should rather judge that the sense stands thus: that he who had been sent immediately after the revelation of Christ to announce the Gospel to the Gentiles did not stand still, nor drag out the time with certain delays by going to the apostles and conferring the Lord's revelation with men, but went away into Arabia, and having returned again to Damascus, preached the Gospel after three years, and only then, coming to Jerusalem, saw Peter, John and James.\n— Commentary on Galatians","That also which he says: Because flesh and blood has not revealed it to you, compare with the Apostle's narrative, in which he says: Immediately I did not acquiesce to flesh and blood, there signifying the Jews by flesh and blood; so that here too, under another sense, it is shown that Christ the Son of God was revealed to him not through the teaching of the Pharisees but through the grace of God.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"GAL.1.17":["Nor did I come to Jerusalem to the apostles, my predecessors.\n\nIf he had said of the apostles, \"I did not confer with flesh and blood\", what need had there been to repeat the very same thing by saying: Nor did I come to Jerusalem to the apostles, my predecessors? So the sense that we set out above must be held.\n\nBut I went away into Arabia: and again I returned to Damascus. The order of the history does not seem to agree with itself, since Luke relates in the Acts of the Apostles (Acts 9) that when Paul, after his faith in Christ, had for many days been speaking the Gospel boldly at Damascus, a plot was laid against him, and he was let down through the wall by night in a basket, and came to Jerusalem trying to join himself to the disciples; and that, when they avoided him and were afraid to approach him, he was brought by Barnabas to the apostles, and there related how he had seen the Lord on the road, and how he had acted with confidence at Damascus in the name of the Lord. \"He was\", he says, \"with them, going in and going out in Jerusalem, acting with confidence in the name of the Lord. He also spoke and disputed with the Greeks: but they sought to kill him. And when the brothers had learned this, they brought him down to Caesarea, and sent him off to Tarsus\" (Acts 9:28-30). Here, however, he says that he first went into Arabia; and that, having returned again to Damascus, he came after three years to Jerusalem, saw Peter, and stayed with him fifteen days, and met no one else besides James, the brother of the Lord. And so that these things may be believed true (for they could seem doubtful to those who were absent), he confirms them under oath, saying: \"But the things that I say to you, behold, before God, I do not lie.\" We can therefore judge that Paul was indeed at Jerusalem, according to Luke's history, not as though going to the apostles who were his predecessors in order to learn something from them, but in order to escape the onslaught of persecution that had been stirred up against him at Damascus on account of the Gospel of Christ; and that he thus came to Jerusalem as he would to any other city. That from there he at once withdrew because of the plots, and came to Arabia, or to Damascus; and from there, after three years, returned to Jerusalem to see Peter. Or certainly thus: that as soon as he was baptized, and strengthened by taking food, he was with the disciples who were at Damascus for some days; and, while all were astonished, he at once preached in the synagogues of the Jews that Jesus was the Son of God; and that he then went into Arabia, and returned from Arabia to Damascus, and spent three years there: and these are the many days to which Scripture testifies, saying: \"But when many days were fulfilled, the Jews took counsel to kill him\" (Acts 9:23). But their plot became known to Saul: and they watched the gates day and night, in order to kill him. So his disciples took him by night and let him down through the wall, lowering him in a basket. And when he had come to Jerusalem, he tried to join himself to the disciples. And that Luke passed over Arabia for this reason, because perhaps he had accomplished nothing worthy of his apostleship in Arabia; and that he chose rather to tell, in a concise narrative, those things that seemed worthy of the Gospel of Christ. Nor is it to be counted as sloth on the Apostle's part if he was in Arabia to no purpose: rather, there was some dispensation and a command of God that he should keep silent. For afterward too we read that Paul, having set out with Silas, was forbidden by the Holy Spirit to speak the word in Asia (Acts 16). Otherwise: \"But I went away into Arabia; and again I returned to Damascus.\" What does this account profit me, if I read that Paul, after the revelation of Christ, at once went into Arabia, and from Arabia at once returned to Damascus, and yet I do not know what he did there, or what usefulness the going and the returning had? The Apostle himself, in this same Epistle, gives me occasion for a higher understanding, when, disputing about Abraham, Hagar and Sarah: \"These things indeed\", he says, \"are spoken through allegory. For these are the two Testaments: the one from Mount Sinai, bearing children into slavery, which is Hagar. For Sinai is a mountain in Arabia, which is joined to that which is now Jerusalem\" (Gal. 4:24, 25). And he teaches that the Old Testament, that is, the son of the handmaid, is set in Arabia (which means \"humble and setting\"). As soon, then, as Paul believed, he turned to the Law, to the Prophets, to the mysteries of the Old Testament, already placed in its setting, and sought Christ in them, whom he had been commanded to preach among the Gentiles: and having found him, he did not stay there longer, but returned to Damascus, that is, to the blood and passion of Christ; and from there, strengthened by the reading of the prophets, he goes on to Jerusalem, the place of vision and of peace, not so much to learn something from the apostles as to confer with them about the Gospel that he had taught.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.1.18":["Then after three years I came to Jerusalem to see Peter.\n\nNot to look at his eyes, his cheeks and his face, whether he was lean or stout, whether his nose was hooked or straight, and whether hair covered his forehead or (as Clement relates in the Periodi of Peter) he had a bald head. Nor do I think it would have befitted apostolic gravity that, after so great a preparation of three years, he should have wished to look at something human in Peter. But he looked at him with those eyes with which he too is now seen in his Epistles. With those eyes Paul saw Cephas with which Paul himself is now beheld by all the wise. But if anyone does not see it so, let him join all these things with the earlier sense: that the apostles conferred nothing on him. For the fact that he appears to have gone to Jerusalem means that he went there in order to see the apostle, not from a desire to learn, since he himself had the same author of his preaching, but to pay honor to the earlier apostle.\n\nAnd I remained with him fifteen days. He who had prepared himself for so long a time to see Peter did not need much instruction. And although to some it may seem superfluous to observe even the numbers that are in the Scriptures, yet I think it not beside the point that the fifteen days during which Paul stayed with Peter signify full knowledge and perfected doctrine. For there are fifteen songs in the Psalter, and fifteen steps by which they go up to sing to God, and by which the righteous man goes up to stand in his courts. Hezekiah too, when a span of fifteen years of life was granted him, deserves to receive a sign on the steps (Isa. 38); and the solemn feasts of God begin from the fifteenth day (Exod. 12). Moreover (since we follow a twofold understanding), he sets down fifteen days for this reason: to show that it was not a long time in which he could have learned anything from Peter, so that everything may be referred back to the sense with which he began: that he was taught not by man but by God.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.1.19":["But of the other apostles I saw none, except James the brother of the Lord.\n\nI remember that while I was at Rome, at the urging of the brothers, I published a book on the perpetual virginity of holy Mary. In it I had to argue at some length about those who are called the brothers of the Lord. Hence, whatever the things we wrote there are worth, we ought to be content with them. For now let this suffice: that because of his excellent character, his incomparable faith and his uncommon wisdom he was called the brother of the Lord; and because he was the first to preside over the Church which, the first to believe in Christ, had been gathered from the Jews. The other apostles too are indeed called brothers of the Lord, as in the Gospel: \"Go, tell my brothers: I go to my Father and to your Father, and to my God and to your God\" (John 20:17). And in the psalm: \"I will declare your name to my brothers; in the midst of the Church I will sing to you\" (Ps. 21:22). But this man is called brother in a special way, to whom the Lord, as he was going to the Father, had entrusted the sons of his mother. And just as Job and the other patriarchs were indeed called servants of God, yet Moses had something as it were outstanding, so that it was written of him, \"But not as Moses my servant\" (Heb. 3:5): so too blessed James was specially called the brother of the Lord (as we said before). As for the fact that, apart from the twelve, certain men are called apostles, the reason is this: all who had seen the Lord and afterwards preached him were called apostles, as is written to the Corinthians: \"That he was seen by Cephas, after this by the eleven; then he was seen by more than five hundred brothers at once, of whom many remain until now, but some have fallen asleep; then he was seen by James, then by all the apostles\" (1 Cor. 15:5 ff.). But gradually, as time went on, and elsewhere, by those whom the Lord had chosen, apostles were ordained; as that passage to the Philippians declares, saying: \"But I thought it necessary to send to you Epaphroditus, my brother, fellow worker and fellow soldier, but your apostle and the minister of my need\" (Phil. 2:25). And to the Corinthians it is written of such men: \"Or the apostles of the churches, the glory of God\" (2 Cor. 8:23). Silas also and Judas were named apostles by the apostles. Hence he erred greatly who thought that this James was the one in the Gospel, the apostle, the brother of John, who, it is agreed, shed his blood for Christ after Stephen, according to the testimony of the Acts of the Apostles (Acts 12). This James, however, was the first bishop of Jerusalem, surnamed the Just: a man of such holiness and of such renown among the people that they vied with one another in their eagerness to touch the fringe of his garment. He too was afterwards thrown down from the temple by the Jews, and had as his successor Simon, who also, they relate, was crucified for the Lord. He therefore denies that he met any of the apostles besides these, lest a hidden objection should arise: \"Even if you were not taught by Peter, you had other apostles as your teachers.\" And he did not see them, not because he held them in contempt, but because they had been scattered over the whole world to preach the Gospel.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.1.20":["But the things I write to you: behold, before God, I do not lie.\n\nThis may be taken either simply, so that the sense is: The things I write to you are true, and I confirm with God as witness that they are colored by no art of words and by no falsehood. Or more deeply, so that it is read: The things I write to you are before God, that is, worthy of God's sight. But why worthy of God's sight? Because, namely, I do not lie. And just as the eyes of the Lord are upon the righteous (Ps. 33), but he turns away his face from the sight of the ungodly, so now the things that are written are before the Lord, since I who write do not lie; they would not be before the Lord if I were lying. Now this can be taken not only of the things he now writes to the Galatians, but also generally of all his Epistles: since the things he writes are not false, and his heart and his words do not disagree.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.1.21":["Then I came into the regions of Syria and Cilicia.\n\nAfter seeing Jerusalem he came to Syria, which in our tongue is called \"lofty and sublime.\" And from there he passed over to Cilicia, which he desired to take up into the faith of Christ, proclaiming to it the calling of repentance: for Cilicia means \"taken up,\" or \"lamentable calling.\"\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.1.22-24":["But I was unknown by face to the churches of Judaea which were in Christ Jesus. But they had only heard that he who once persecuted us now preaches the faith which he formerly fought against; and they glorified God in me.\n\nThe churches that were in Judaea had known Paul only by report. Of these, most had seen him as a persecutor rather than as an apostle. But Syria and the regions of Cilicia, Arabia and Damascus had perhaps known him by face as well, because the Teacher of the Gentiles was preaching the Gospel of Christ not to the Jews but to the Gentiles. Now the whole of what he is doing is this: to teach that he could never, from being a persecutor, have become glorious among those very people whom he had previously persecuted, unless his preaching had been approved by the judgment even of those who had earlier known him as a wicked man. And he covertly returns to his purpose, confirming that he had spent so short a time in Judaea that he was unknown even by face to the believers. From this he shows that he had not had Peter, not James, not John as his teachers, but Christ, who had revealed the Gospel to him. At the same time it should be noted that above he is said to have fought against the Church, here against the faith: there men, here a thing; so that now it might more fittingly be added: \"He preaches the faith which he once fought against.\" For the words could not have sounded the same way of the Church.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.2.1-2":["Then after fourteen years I went up again to Jerusalem with Barnabas, taking Titus along also. And I went up according to revelation; and I conferred with them the Gospel which I preach among the Gentiles, but privately with those who seemed, lest perhaps I should run, or had run, in vain.\n\nWhat the Latin translator had rendered above as \"I acquiesced\" (acquievi), in the place where it is written: \"Immediately I did not acquiesce to flesh and blood\", he has in the present place translated I conferred (contuli) rather than \"I acquiesced\" (acquievi). And to speak more truly, the Greek word ἀνεθέμην means something other than is understood among us: namely, when we confer with a friend the things we know, and as it were lay them up in his bosom and his conscience, so that by a shared counsel the things we know may be either approved or disapproved. He went up, therefore, to Jerusalem after fourteen years; and he who earlier had gone only to see Peter, and had stayed with him fifteen days, now says that he went for this reason, that he might confer the Gospel with the apostles, taking along Barnabas, who was circumcised, and Titus, from the Gentiles, who had a foreskin: so that at the mouth of two and three witnesses every word might stand (Deut. 19:15). But it is one thing to confer, another to learn. Among those who confer there is equality; between one who teaches and one who learns, the one who learns is the lesser. At the beginning of his faith he saw the apostles in passing. After (as he himself says) seventeen years, he speaks with them fully, and humbles himself, and inquires whether perhaps he might be running or might have run in vain. This was for a twofold reason: so that both the humility of Paul might be shown, who, though already the teacher of the Gentiles in the whole world, had run to the apostles who preceded him; and so that the Galatians might learn that even those who presided over the churches in Judaea had not rejected his Gospel. At the same time he also teaches this: that for the faith of Christ and the freedom of the Gospel he dared to bring Titus, an uncircumcised man, before the very men who had come to know many things about him, namely that he was breaking the Law, destroying Moses and doing away with circumcision entirely. And amid so great a multitude of Jews and of his own enemies, who, as if out of zeal for the Law, longed to drain his blood, neither he himself nor Titus was overcome by any terror into yielding to necessity. Such a yielding could have had pardon, whether on account of the place, or of the authority of the elders, or of the number of the churches which, from among the Jews, believed in Christ, or on account of the time, lest they should bear so great an ill will all at once. Some say that he went up to Jerusalem after fourteen years at the time when, as the Acts of the Apostles relate, a dissension arose among the believers at Antioch over questions of keeping or setting aside the Law, and it was decided to go to Jerusalem and await the judgment of the elders, when Paul himself too and Barnabas were sent; and that this is what is read in the Latin codices: \"To whom we yielded in subjection for an hour, that the truth of the Gospel might continue with you.\" That is to say, for this reason Paul and Barnabas, about a clear matter as though it were doubtful, allowed themselves to be sent to Jerusalem: so that the grace of the Gospel, confirmed also by the judgment of the elders, might be proved to the believers, and that no doubt about the abandonment of circumcision might remain any longer, since it had been commanded by the letter of the apostles that the yoke of the Law was to be removed from those of the Gentiles who had believed in Christ.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.2.2":["But what he says: \"I conferred with them the Gospel which I preach among the Gentiles, but privately with those who seemed, lest perhaps I should run, or had run, in vain\", can also be understood thus: that he conferred secretly with the apostles on the grace of evangelical freedom and the oldness of the abolished Law, on account of the multitude of believing Jews, who could not yet bear to hear that Christ is the fulfillment and end of the Law, and who, even in Paul's absence, had loudly declared at Jerusalem that he was running, or had run, in vain, since he thought that the old Law was not to be followed. Not that Paul feared that for seventeen years he had preached a false Gospel among the Gentiles, but so that he might show his predecessors that he was not running, nor had run, in vain, as they in their ignorance had supposed.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.2.3-5":["But neither was Titus, who was with me, compelled to be circumcised, though he was of the Gentiles. But because of the false brothers brought in by stealth, who stole in to spy out our freedom, which we have in Christ Jesus, that they might reduce us to slavery: to whom we did not yield in subjection even for an hour, that the truth of the Gospel may remain with you.\n\nIf Titus, though he was of the Gentiles, could be compelled by no terror to be circumcised at Jerusalem, in the metropolis of the Jews, in which Paul was inflamed by such great odium for blasphemy against Moses that he was afterwards almost killed by the Jews, when he is freed by the tribune and sent in chains to Rome to Caesar: how is it that some think it should be read, \"to whom we yielded in subjection for an hour, that the truth of the Gospel may remain with you\", and understood to mean that Titus himself, who could not be compelled to circumcision before, was afterward circumcised and made subject? Or what is this truth of the Gospel: to yield to the hypocrisy of the Jews, and to observe the things which you formerly reckoned as dung and despised as losses, and to reckon them to be something, when they are nothing? But it is also strongly opposed to the sense of the Epistle itself to call the Galatians back to circumcision. Through the whole fabric of his discourse he works at this: to teach that he, a Hebrew of Hebrews, who once kept all the works of the Law, circumcised on the eighth day according to the Law of the Pharisees, nevertheless despises all things for the sake of the grace of Christ. For when he had gone to Jerusalem, and the false brothers who had believed from the circumcision wished to drive him to circumcise Titus, he teaches that neither Titus nor he himself yielded to violence so as to fail to guard the truth of the Gospel. But if he says that he was overcome by necessity so as to circumcise Titus, how does he call the Galatians back from circumcision, from which he could not exempt even Titus, who had been with him, one of the Gentiles, at Jerusalem? Therefore either we must read according to the Greek manuscripts, \"To whom we did not yield in subjection even for an hour\", so that what follows can be understood consistently: \"that the truth of the Gospel may remain with you.\" Or, if anyone is satisfied with the reliability of the Latin copy, we ought to take it according to the sense given above: that the yielding for an hour was not one of circumcising Titus, but of going to Jerusalem. In this, namely, Paul and Barnabas yielded to the subjection of going to Jerusalem, for this reason, when a sedition had been stirred up at Antioch over the Law: that through the Epistle of the apostles their own view might be confirmed, and the truth of the Gospel might remain with the Galatians, a truth which would not be in the letter but in the spirit; not in a carnal sense but in spiritual understanding; nor in manifest Judaism but in the hidden. It should be known, moreover, that the conjunction but (autem), which is placed in the present passage, But because of the false brothers brought in by stealth, is superfluous; and if it is read, it has nothing to answer to it and to complete it; rather, the order of the reading and of the sense is: But neither was Titus, who was with me, compelled to be circumcised, though he was of the Gentiles. And at once he adds what the cause was that he was being driven to circumcision against his will: Because of the false brothers brought in by stealth, he says, who stole in to spy out our freedom, which we have in Christ Jesus, that they might reduce us to slavery. Though they terrified us with threats and wished by their numbers to lead us over from the freedom of Christ into the slavery of the Law, we did not yield to them even for a time, so as to circumcise Titus; especially since some necessity, for the sake of the Church's peace, could also have excused us; and we did all this so that no occasion might arise for you to withdraw from the grace of the Gospel. If, then, we at Jerusalem, among so many Jews, with false brothers pressing upon us from this side and that, and with those who were greater holding us back in some measure, could be compelled by no force or reasoning to observe the circumcision which we knew to be ended: you, from the Gentiles, you in Galatia, you on whom no force can be brought to bear, withdrawing of your own accord from grace, have passed over to the oldness of the Law, which is already abolished.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.2.6":["But from those who seemed to be something, what they once were makes no difference to me: God does not accept the person of man.\n\nAlthough, he says, the Lord had Peter and John with Him as apostles, and they saw Him transfigured on the mountain (Matt. 17; Mark 9), and the foundation of the Church has been laid upon them, yet to me it matters nothing, because I am not speaking against those who followed the Lord at that time, but I am speaking against those who now set the Law before grace. I do not disparage my predecessors, nor do I in any respect accuse my elders, but I say this: that God does not accept the person of man. For He did not accept that of Moses, He did not accept that of David, He did not accept that of others; nor, therefore, will He accept that of those who seem to yield to certain people for a time, even though they themselves agree with me, since Peter too says: \"In truth I perceive that God is not an acceptor of persons, but in every place he who fears Him and works righteousness is acceptable to Him\" (Acts 10:34, 35). And so the very argument which the holy apostle Peter himself uses against those who found a stumbling block in Cornelius, baptized from among the Gentiles and not circumcised, when he placates them by saying that he could not deny water to those who had received the Holy Spirit, is the same one by which the holy apostle Paul now disputes against Peter himself: that God is not an acceptor of persons, but judges each one according to the truth. And so cautiously and step by step does he walk a middle way between praise and rebuke of Peter, that he both defers to the apostle who preceded him and nonetheless boldly withstands him to his face, compelled by the truth.\n\nFor to me those who seemed conferred nothing. He himself, above, conferred with them and reported to them many things that he was accomplishing among the Gentiles; they conferred nothing on him, but, merely approving what had been said by him, gave the right hands of fellowship and confirmed their own Gospel and Paul's as one. Again it must be noted that \"conferred\" (contulerunt) is in the Greek the very word which we discussed before.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.2.7-9":["But on the contrary, when they had seen that the Gospel of the uncircumcision was entrusted to me, as that of the circumcision to Peter. For he who worked in Peter for the apostleship of the circumcision worked also in me among the Gentiles. And when they had recognized the grace that was given to me, Peter and James and John, who seemed to be pillars, gave to me and to Barnabas the right hands of fellowship: that we should go to the Gentiles, and they themselves to the circumcision.\n\nIt is a ὑπέρβατον (hyperbaton, a transposition of word order), and once the many things inserted in the middle are removed, it can be read briefly thus: For those who seemed to be conferred nothing on me; but on the contrary they gave to me and to Barnabas the right hands of fellowship. Or certainly that hidden sense, free of any boasting of himself, is this: Those who seemed to be something conferred nothing on me; but on the contrary something was conferred on them by me, while they become firmer in the grace of the Gospel. But the whole of what he says is this: one and the same entrusted the Gospel of the uncircumcision to me, and that of the circumcision to Peter. He sent me to the Gentiles; he placed him in Judaea. For neither could the Gentiles, already of adult age, be tormented by the pain of circumcision, which would profit them nothing, and abstain from foods to which they had always been accustomed and which God had created for use; nor could those who had believed from among the Jews and were circumcised, and who, out of custom, as if it were a second nature, thought that they had something more than the other nations, easily despise the things in which they gloried. And so by the providence of God one apostle was given to the circumcised, who might seem to acquiesce in the shadows of the Law, and another to those placed in uncircumcision, who would not think the grace of the Gospel to be slavery, but free faith; lest on some pretext a hindrance to faith should arise, and on account of circumcision or uncircumcision men should not believe in Christ. Nor do we say this as though Peter, who also himself testified in the Acts of the Apostles that no man is common (Acts 10), and who was taught in that vessel which he had seen let down from heaven by its four corners that it makes no difference whether someone is a Jew or a Gentile, had, as if forgetful of his former understanding, thought the Law was to be observed over and above the grace of the Gospel; but rather that he too, pretending to keep the Law, might little by little lead the Jews away from their ancient manner of living. For they could not suddenly despise, as refuse and loss, so great a labor of observance and the most scrupulous conduct of their old life. From this we perceive that the right hands of fellowship were given to Paul and Barnabas by Peter, James and John for this reason: lest, through a differing observance, the Gospel of Christ should be thought to be diverse; but that there should be one communion both of the circumcised and of those who have the uncircumcision. And he has prepared the ground beautifully, saying: For he who worked in Peter for the apostleship of the circumcision: lest anyone should think that he was disparaging Peter; but rather so that, his praises having been set forth beforehand, it might be understood that Peter for this reason took up circumcision in some measure, that he might gain those from among the Jews who had been entrusted to him, and keep them in the faith and Gospel of Christ. He also implies that if Peter did this without fault, even by observing for a time what is not lawful, lest he lose those entrusted to him, all the more ought he himself, for the truth of the Gospel, to do what was entrusted to him in the uncircumcision, lest the Gentiles, frightened off by the burden and difficulty of the Law, should depart from the faith and belief of Christ. Here a hidden question arises: What then? If Peter had found any from among the Gentiles, did he not bring them to the faith? Or if Paul had come upon some from the circumcision, did he not call them to the baptism of Christ? It is resolved thus: we say that to each one a principal charge was given, toward the Jews and toward the Gentiles, so that those who defended the Law should have someone to follow, and those who preferred grace to the Law should not lack a teacher and guide. But they had this purpose in common, to gather a Church for Christ out of all the nations. For we read both that the Gentile Cornelius was baptized by holy Peter, and that Christ was very often preached by Paul in the synagogues of the Jews.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.2.9":["\"Peter, and John, and James, who seemed to be pillars\" (Acts 10, 13, 17). Three times above we read concerning the apostles: \"But privately to those who seemed.\" And: \"By those who seemed to be something.\" And: \"For those who seemed to be something conferred nothing on me.\" And so I was anxiously asking what it might be that he meant by \"those who seemed\"; but now he has freed me from every scruple by adding, who seemed to be pillars. The apostles, therefore, are the pillars of the Church, and especially Peter, James and John, two of whom were found worthy to go up the mountain with the Lord, and one of whom in the Apocalypse introduces the Savior speaking: \"Whoever conquers, I will make him a pillar in the temple of my God\" (Rev. 3:12), teaching that all believers who conquer the adversary can become pillars of the Church. And Paul, writing to Timothy, says: \"That you may know how you ought to conduct yourself in the house of God, which is the Church of the living God, the pillar and foundation of the truth\" (1 Tim. 3:15). By these and other passages we are instructed that in the Scriptures the apostles and all believers, as well as the Church itself, are called a pillar, and that it makes no difference whether something is said of the body or of the members, since the body too is divided into members, and the members belong to the body. So Peter and James and John, who seemed to be pillars, gave to Paul and Barnabas the right hands of fellowship; but to Titus, who was with them, they did not give their right hands. For he had not yet come to such a measure that the merchandise of Christ could be entrusted to him on equal terms with his elders, and that he could hold the same place in the trade that Barnabas held, and Paul.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.2.10":["Only that we should be mindful of the poor, which I also was anxious to do, this very thing.\n\nThe holy poor, whose care is enjoined especially by the apostles upon Paul and Barnabas, are those who, believing from among the Jews, brought the prices of their possessions to the feet of the apostles to be bestowed upon the needy, or because they were held in detestation and as an abomination by their fellow tribesmen, their kinsmen and their parents, as deserters of the Law and as believers in a crucified man. With how much labor the holy apostle Paul toiled in ministering to these, his Epistles are witnesses, as he writes to the Corinthians and to the Thessalonians, and to all the churches of the Gentiles, that they should prepare this gift, to be carried to Jerusalem by themselves or by others who pleased them. Hence he now says confidently, which I also was anxious to do, this very thing. But the poor can also be taken in another sense, those of whom it is said in the Gospel: \"Blessed are the poor in spirit: for theirs is the kingdom of heaven\" (Matt. 5:3). Such men indeed deserve to be in the memory of the apostles. And also those poor of whom it is written in Solomon: \"The ransom of a man's soul is his own riches\" (Prov. 13:8). But the poor man does not bear the threat. For he cannot hear the future terror of punishments, poor in faith, poor in grace, not having spiritual riches, nor the knowledge of the Scriptures, which are compared to gold and silver and precious stone. Since, therefore, it is not the healthy who need a physician, but those who are sick, for this reason it was agreed with the apostles also, in the giving of right hands, that they should not spurn the poor, should not despise sinners, but should always be mindful of them, just as Paul was mindful of that Corinthian whom, after he had grieved him for a time in the first Epistle, so that, the body laboring through penitence, he might be saved in spirit (1 Cor. 5), in the second he calls back to the Church, lest he be swallowed up by excessive grief. And he asks all of them to confirm their love toward him, and to forgive the brother, just as he himself had forgiven each one of them in the face of Christ, fulfilling the pact which he had made at Jerusalem, that he would always remember the poor.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.2.11-13":["But when Peter had come to Antioch, I withstood him to his face, because he was reprehensible. For before certain men came from James, he used to eat with the Gentiles. But when they had come, he withdrew and separated himself, fearing those who were of the circumcision; and the other Jews also consented to his pretense, so that even Barnabas was led by them into that pretense.\n\nFrom the fact that Peter, before certain men came from Jerusalem to Antioch, used to eat with the Gentiles, it is shown that he had not forgotten the precept: to call no man common and unclean. But because, on account of those who still thought that the Law must be observed, he had withdrawn himself a little from table-fellowship with the Gentiles (so that even the rest who were of the Jews did likewise, and Barnabas, who was preaching the Gospel among the Gentiles together with Paul, was compelled to do this), those at Antioch who had believed from among the Gentiles and had not been circumcised were being driven to cross over to the burdens of the Law, not understanding the dispensation of Peter, by which he wished the Jews to be saved, but thinking that this was how the principle of the Gospel stood. And so, when the apostle Paul saw that the grace of Christ was in peril, the old warrior used a new art of fighting, so that he himself might correct the dispensation of Peter, by which Peter wished the Jews to be saved, by a new dispensation of contradiction, and might withstand him to his face: not censuring his purpose, but contradicting him as it were in public, so that, through the fact that Paul resisted him and censured him, those who had believed from among the Gentiles might be preserved. But if anyone thinks that Paul truly withstood the apostle Peter, and for the truth of the Gospel fearlessly did injury to his predecessor, his view will not stand against the fact that Paul himself also became a Jew to the Jews, in order to win the Jews; and Paul will be held guilty of the same pretense, when he shaved his head at Cenchreae and, having made himself bald, offered an offering in Jerusalem (Acts 18), and circumcised Timothy (ibid., 16), and practiced the barefoot rites, which certainly belong most manifestly to the ceremonies of the Jews. If, therefore, he who had been sent to preach to the Gentiles did not think it beside the point to say: \"Be without offense to the Jews and to the Church of God\" (1 Cor. 10:32); just as I too please all men in all things, not seeking what is profitable to me, but what is profitable to many, that they may be saved; and if he did some things that were contrary to the freedom of the Gospel, so as not to cause the Jews to stumble: by what authority, with what face does he dare to reprehend in Peter, who was the apostle of the circumcision, what he himself, the apostle of the Gentiles, is charged with having committed? But, as we have already said before, he withstood Peter and the others before the public face, so that the hypocrisy of observing the Law, which was harming those who had believed from among the Gentiles, might be corrected by the hypocrisy of rebuke, and both peoples might be saved, while those who praise circumcision follow Peter, and those who are unwilling to be circumcised proclaim the freedom of Paul. As for his saying he was reprehensible, he therefore moderated the abstinence from food, so that we may understand that Peter was reprehensible not so much to Paul as to those brothers with whom he had formerly eaten and from whom he afterwards separated himself. That pretense is useful, and to be adopted at the right time, let the example of Jehu, king of Israel, teach us: he could not have killed the priests of Baal unless he had feigned that he wished to worship the idol, saying: \"Gather to me all the priests of Baal: for if Ahab served Baal in a few things, I will serve him in many\" (2 Kings 10:18). And David, when he changed his face before Abimelech, and he dismissed him, and he went away (1 Sam. 21). Nor is it surprising that men, although righteous, should nevertheless pretend some things for a time, for their own salvation and that of others, since even our Lord himself, having no sin, nor the flesh of sin, took on the pretense of sinful flesh, so that, condemning sin in the flesh, he might make us in himself the righteousness of God. Paul had certainly read in the Gospel the Lord commanding: \"But if your brother sins against you, go and rebuke him between you and him alone. If he hears you, you have gained your brother\" (Matt. 18:15). And how, when the Lord had commanded that this be done even concerning the least of the brothers, did he dare to censure the greatest of the apostles to his public face so impudently, so resolutely, unless it had pleased Peter too to be censured in this way, and Paul was not doing injury to him of whom he had said before: \"I came to Jerusalem to see Peter, and stayed with him fifteen days: but other of the apostles I saw none\"? And again: \"For he who worked in Peter for the apostleship of the circumcision\". And below: \"Peter and James and John, who seemed to be pillars\", and the rest that he declaims in his praise. Several times, when as a young man at Rome I was declaiming controversies and exercising myself for real contests with fictitious lawsuits, I used to run to the tribunals of the judges, and I saw the most eloquent of the orators contending with one another with such bitterness that, often leaving the cases aside, they turned to insults of their own and bit one another with a jesting tooth. If they do this so as to incur no suspicion of collusion in the eyes of the defendants, and deceive the people standing around, what do we think such great pillars of the Church, Peter and Paul, such great vessels of wisdom, ought to have done between the disagreeing Jews and Gentiles, except that their pretended contention should become the peace of the believers, and the faith of the Church should be brought into harmony by a holy quarrel between them? There are those who do not think that Cephas, whom Paul here writes that he withstood to his face, is the apostle Peter, but another of the seventy disciples called by this name. And they say that Peter could by no means have withdrawn himself from table-fellowship with the Gentiles, since he had even baptized the centurion Cornelius; and, when he had gone up to Jerusalem, as those who were of the Circumcision disputed against him and said: \"Why did you go in to men who have the foreskin, and eat with them\" (Acts 11:3)? he concluded his response, after the narration of the vision, with this ending: \"If therefore God gave them the same grace as he gave us also, who believed in the Lord Jesus Christ, who was I, that I could forbid God? When they heard these things they were silent, and glorified God, saying: Then God has given repentance unto life to the Gentiles also\"; especially since Luke, the writer of the history, makes no mention of this dissension, nor ever says that Peter was at Antioch with Paul; and that room would be given to Porphyry in his blasphemy if Peter is believed to have erred, or Paul to have impudently refuted the prince of the apostles. To these we must reply, first, that we do not know the name of some other Cephas, except him who in the Gospel, and in the other Epistles of Paul, and in this one too, is written now Cephas, now Peter. Not that Peter signifies one thing and Cephas another, but that what we in Latin and Greek call petra, \"rock\", the Hebrews and Syrians, because of the nearness of their languages to one another, call Cephas. Next, that the whole argument of the epistle, in what is said obliquely about Peter, James and John, is opposed to this understanding. Nor is it surprising if Luke kept silent about this matter, since by the historian's license he also passed over many other things which Paul recounts that he endured; and it is not at once a contradiction if one writer thought something worth relating for some reason, while another left it out among other things. Finally, we have received that Peter was the first bishop of the Church of Antioch, and was translated from there to Rome, which Luke omitted entirely. Lastly, if on account of Porphyry's blasphemy another Cephas must be invented by us, so that Peter may not be thought to have erred, countless things will have to be erased from the divine Scriptures which he attacks because he does not understand them. But against Porphyry too we shall fight in another work, if Christ commands; now let us pursue the rest.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.2.14":["But when I had seen that they do not walk with an upright foot toward the truth of the Gospel, I said to Peter before all.\n\nJust as those who, though their steps are sound, pretend to limp have no defect in their feet, but there is some reason why they limp, so also Peter, knowing that circumcision and uncircumcision are nothing, but rather the observance of the commandments of God, did indeed eat with the Gentiles before, but for the time being withdrew himself from them, lest he should lose the Jews from the faith of Christ. Hence Paul too, by the same art with which Peter was pretending, withstood him to his face, and speaks before all; not so much in order to rebuke Peter as in order that those on whose account Peter had pretended might be corrected, or also that pride might be taken away from the Jews and despair from the Gentiles. But if this sense does not please someone, a sense in which Peter is shown neither to have sinned nor Paul to have impudently rebuked his senior, he must explain by what consistency Paul censures in another what he himself committed.\n\nIf you, though you are a Jew, live like a Gentile and not like a Jew, how do you compel the Gentiles to Judaize? He binds Peter with an indissoluble argument, or rather, through Peter, binds those who were compelling him to do things that conflicted with one another. If, he says, O Peter, you are a Jew by nature, circumcised from an early age and keeping all the precepts of the Law, and now, on account of the grace of Christ, you know that they have no usefulness in themselves, but are patterns and images of things to come, and you take food with those who are from the Gentiles, no longer superstitiously as before, but living freely and without distinction: how is it that now, withdrawing from those who have believed from among the Gentiles, and as it were separating and setting yourself apart from the contaminated, you compel them to Judaize? For if those from whom you withdraw are unclean, and you withdraw for this reason, because they do not have circumcision, you compel them to be circumcised and to become Jews, although you yourself, born a Jew, have lived like a Gentile. And he gladly shows the reason why he disputed against him: namely, because by his pretense he was compelling the Gentiles to Judaize, while they desire to imitate him.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.2.15-21":["But to take in the whole argument in a brief discourse, this is the sense that is being woven: We, that is, I and you, Peter (for he joined his own person to Peter's, so as not to seem to do him an injury), since we were, he says, Jews by nature, doing those things which are commanded in the law, and not sinners from among the Gentiles (who either are sinners generally, because they serve idols, or are those whom we now regard as unclean), knowing that we cannot be saved by the work of the Law but by faith in Christ, have believed in Christ, so that what the Law had not given us, the faith which we had in Christ might bestow. But if, withdrawing from the Law, in which we could not be saved, we have crossed over to faith, in which it is not circumcision of the flesh that is sought but the devotion of a pure heart; and if now, by withdrawing from the Gentiles, we bring it about that whoever is not circumcised is unclean: then faith in Christ, in which we formerly thought we were saved, is the minister of sin rather than of righteousness, since it takes away circumcision, and whoever does not have circumcision is unclean. But far be it from me to lay claim again to what I once destroyed and knew had been of no profit to me. Departing once for all from the Law, I died to the Law, that I might live in Christ, and, fastened to his cross and reborn into the new man, might subsist by faith rather than by the flesh, and might go forth from the world with Christ. What I once undertook, I hold fast. Christ did not die for me for nothing: in him I believed in vain, if I could have been saved in the old Law without faith in him.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.2.15":["We, Jews by nature, and not sinners from among the Gentiles.\n\nAt this place the heretics creep in, who, inventing certain ridiculous and silly notions, say that a spiritual nature cannot sin, nor an earthly nature do anything righteously. Let us ask them why branches were broken off from the good olive, and why branches from the wild olive were grafted into the root of the good olive, if nothing can either fall from the good or rise from the bad? Or how Paul either persecuted the Church earlier, if he was of a spiritual nature, or was afterward made an Apostle, if he was begotten of earthly dregs. But if they contend that he was not earthly, let us set down his own words: \"We were by nature children of wrath, even as the rest\" (Eph. 2:3). He is a Jew by nature who is of the race of Abraham and was circumcised by his parents on the eighth day. He is not a Jew by nature who was afterward made one from among the Gentiles.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.2.16-18":["On account of which, by the works of the Law all flesh shall not be justified. But if, seeking to be justified in Christ, we ourselves also are found sinners, is Christ then the minister of sin? God forbid. For if I build up again the things which I destroyed, I make myself a transgressor.\n\nNot justified by the works of the Law shall be that flesh of which it is written: \"All flesh is grass, and all its glory as the flower of the field\" (Isa. 40:6). But justified by the faith of Jesus Christ is that flesh of which it is said in the mystery of the resurrection: \"All flesh shall see the salvation of God\" (Luke 3:6). But also, according to a humbler understanding, formerly not all flesh was justified by the Law, but only those men who were in Palestine; now, however, all flesh is justified by the faith of Jesus Christ, while His Church is being founded in the whole world.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.2.16":["But knowing that a man is not justified by the works of the Law, except through faith in Jesus Christ: we too have believed in Christ Jesus, that we may be justified in faith in Christ, and not by the works of the Law.\n\nSome say: if what Paul affirms is true, that no one is justified by the works of the Law but by faith in Jesus Christ, then the patriarchs and prophets and the saints who lived before the coming of Christ were imperfect. We must remind them that those are here said not to have attained righteousness who believe that they can be justified by works alone; but that the saints who lived in ancient times were justified by faith in Christ. For indeed Abraham saw the day of Christ, and rejoiced. And Moses esteemed the reproach of Christ greater riches than the treasure of the Egyptians; for he looked to the reward. And Isaiah saw the glory of Christ (Isa. 6), as John the Evangelist records; and Jude speaks of all in general: \"I would remind you,\" he says, \"since you once knew all things, that Jesus, saving the people out of the land of Egypt, afterward destroyed those who did not believe\" (Jude 5). Hence it is not so much the works of the Law that are condemned as those who trust that they can be justified by works alone, since the Savior too says to his disciples: \"Unless your righteousness abounds more than that of the Scribes and Pharisees, you shall not enter into the kingdom of heaven\" (Matt. 5:20). At this point we must gather how many precepts there are in the Law which no one is able to fulfill. And on the other hand it must be said that some things of the Law are done even by those who do not know it; but that those who do them are not justified, for this reason, that they are done without faith in Christ: for example, not to lie with a man as one lies with a woman, not to commit adultery, not to steal, but rather to honor father and mother, and to do the other things that have been commanded. But if they bring forward to us examples from holy men, that, being versed in the Law, they performed the things that belonged to the Law, we shall say: Because the Law is not laid down for the righteous, but for the unjust and the insubordinate, for the ungodly and sinners, for the defiled and unclean (1 Tim. 1). And that one who has been taught by God has no need to be taught even about love, as Paul says: \"But concerning love I have no need to write to you: for you yourselves are taught by God to love one another\" (1 Thess. 4:9).\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.2.19":["For I through the Law have died to the Law, that I may live to God.\n\nIt is one thing to die through the Law, another to die to the Law. He who dies to the Law was living to it before he died, observing sabbaths and new moons and feast days, and the typological scrupulousness about victims, and Jewish fables and genealogies. But after Christ came, and the Law of which it is written: \"But we know that the Law is spiritual\" (Rom. 7:14), through the Gospel law he died to the former Law: and the soul which, according to what is written to the Romans, was called an adulteress if it had married while the husband was living, now that its husband, that is, the old law, is dead, has married the spiritual Law, so that it might bear fruit to God. Hence in Hosea too it is said to her: \"From me your fruit has been found\" (Hos. 14:9). To which that mystical saying is beautifully added: \"Who is wise, and will understand these things: understanding, and will know them?\" He, therefore, who through the spiritual law dies to the Law of the letter lives to God, since he is not without the law of God, but is in the law of Christ. But he who dies to the law on account of sins is indeed dead; but what follows cannot be said of him, that he may live to God. And that there is another, spiritual law besides the Law of the letter, the Apostle teaches elsewhere too, saying: \"So the law indeed is holy, and the commandment holy, and just, and good\" (Rom. 7:12). And Ezekiel, in the person of God: \"I led out\", he says, \"them\", that is, the people of the Jews, \"from the land of Egypt: and I led them into the desert: and I gave them my precepts, and showed them my justifications: which a man shall do, and live in them\" (Ezek. 20:10). But concerning that law which works wrath, to which the Apostle too died, he adds afterward: \"And I gave them precepts that were not good, and justifications in which they shall not live in them\" (ibid., 25). The very same thing is signified in the Psalter as well: \"Because I have not known letter-learning, I will enter into the strength of the Lord\" (Ps. 70:15).\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.2.20":["I am fastened to the cross with Christ. Since he had said that he had died to the law through the law, he shows how he died: I am fastened to the cross with Christ, taking up his cross and following Christ, and in the very passion beseeching: \"Remember me when you come into your kingdom\" (Luke 23:42, 43). And at once hearing: \"Today you will be with me in paradise\". If anyone, having put to death his members which are upon the earth, and being dead to the world, has been conformed to the death of Jesus Christ, he is crucified with Jesus, and fixes the trophy of his own mortification on the wood of the Lord's passion.\n\nAnd I live, now not I: but Christ lives in me.\n\nHe no longer lives who once lived in the Law, he, namely, who persecuted the Church. But Christ lives in him: wisdom, strength, the Word, peace, joy and the other virtues; and whoever does not have these cannot say: But Christ lives in me. All this, however, he argues in his own person against Peter, about Peter.\n\nAnd that I now live in the flesh. It is one thing to be in the flesh, and another to live in the flesh. \"For those who are in the flesh cannot please God\" (Rom. 8:8, 9). Hence it is said to those who live well: \"But you are not in the flesh\".\n\nI live in the faith of the Son of God, who loved me and delivered himself up for me. To the Romans he speaks of God, that he did not spare his own Son, but delivered him up for us. But now he speaks of Christ, that he delivered himself up: Who loved me, he says, and delivered himself up for me. In the Gospel, moreover, where the apostles are enumerated, it is added: \"And Judas Iscariot, who also delivered him up\" (Luke 6:16). Again in the same: \"Behold, he who will deliver me up has drawn near\" (Matt. 26:46). And of the chief priests and the elders of the people, Scripture records that they condemned Jesus to death, and binding him, led him away and delivered him up to Pilate the governor (ibid. 27, and Mark 15). And next, of Pilate: \"He released Barabbas to them, but Jesus, when he had been scourged, he delivered up to them, that they might crucify him\" (John 19). Therefore both the Father delivered up the Son, and the Son delivered himself up, and Judas and the priests delivered him up to the rulers, and finally Pilate himself delivered up the one who had been delivered up to him. But the Father delivered him up in order to save a lost world; Jesus delivered himself up in order to do the will of the Father and his own; but Judas and the priests and the elders of the people, and Pilate, unknowingly delivered up Life to death. And since Life itself also delivered itself up for our salvation, blessed and very happy is he who, with Christ living in him, can say through each of his thoughts and works: I live in the faith of the Son of God, who loved me, and delivered himself up for me.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.2.21":["I do not cast away the grace of God: for if righteousness is through the Law, then Christ died for nothing.\n\nHe casts away the grace of God, both he who after the Gospel lives in the Law and he who after baptism is defiled by sins. But he who can say, in accordance with the Apostle, \"His grace in me was not void\" (1 Cor. 15:10), that man also says this with confidence: I do not cast away the grace of God. And what follows is very necessary against those who think that, after faith in Christ, the precepts of the Law must be kept. For one must say to them: If righteousness is through the law, then Christ died for nothing. Or at any rate let them show how Christ did not die for nothing, if works justify. But however dull they may be, they will not dare to say that Christ died without cause. Therefore, to the part of the syllogism which is put forward here, that is, For if righteousness is through the Law, then Christ died for nothing, we must add as the assumption what follows by consequence and cannot be denied, But Christ did not die for nothing; and conclude, Therefore righteousness is not through the Law. Thus far against Peter; now he returns to the Galatians.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.3.1":["O senseless Galatians, who has bewitched you?\n\nThis passage can be understood in two ways. Either the Galatians were called senseless for this reason, that coming from greater things to lesser they had begun in the spirit and were being finished in the flesh; or on account of this, that each province has its own peculiar characteristics. The Apostle confirms that the Cretans are always liars, evil beasts, lazy bellies, as was truly said by the poet Epimenides. The Latin historian lashes the vain Moors and the fierce Dalmatians. All the poets tear at the timid Phrygians. The philosophers boast that readier minds are born at Athens. Tully, before Gaius Caesar, taunts the Greeks as fickle, saying: Either of the fickle Greeks or of the savage barbarians. And in the speech for Flaccus: Inborn, he says, fickleness, and learned vanity. All the Scriptures reprove Israel itself for its heavy heart and stiff neck. In this way, then, I think that the Apostle too lashed the Galatians with the peculiar characteristic of their region. Some, it is true, thrusting themselves into deep questions, as if under the pretext of avoiding the heresy that introduces diverse natures, say that the Tyrians and Sidonians too, the Moabites and Ammonites and Edomites, the Babylonians and Egyptians, and all the peoples that are named in the Scriptures, have certain peculiarities from preceding causes and by the merit of former works, so that the righteousness of God may not come into doubt, since each nation is asserted to have some good or some evil that another does not have. We, declining these depths, shall follow what was said above: either that they are charged with folly, by which they cannot discern the spirit of the Law from the letter; or that they are rebuked for the fault of their nation, in that they are unteachable and witless and rather slow to wisdom. But what follows, Who has bewitched you, we ought to explain in a manner worthy of Paul (who, though he is unskilled in speech, is not so in knowledge [2 Cor. 11:6]): not that he knew that there is such a thing as the evil eye, which is commonly thought to do harm, but that he used the speech of the crossroads, and, as elsewhere, so in this place too he took up a word of everyday conversation. We read in Proverbs: \"The gift of the envious man torments the eyes\". What with us is \"envious\" is put more expressively in the Greek as \"bewitcher\"; and in the Wisdom that is ascribed to Solomon: \"The bewitching of malice obscures good things\" (Wisd. 4:12). By these examples we are taught either that the envious man is tormented by another's happiness, or that he in whom there are some good things is harmed when another bewitches him, that is, envies him. The evil eye is said properly to harm infants and those of tender age, and those who do not yet plant their step with a firm footing. Hence also one of the Gentiles says: Some eye or other bewitches my tender lambs. (Virgil, Eclogues 3.) Whether this is true or not, God will see; for it may be that demons too serve this sin, and turn away from good works whomever they know either to have begun or to have made progress in the work of God. For now, the point at issue is that we think the example was taken from the opinion of the common people: so that, just as tender age is said to be harmed by the evil eye, so also the Galatians, newly born in the faith of Christ and nourished with milk and not with solid food (1 Cor. 3), were harmed as though by someone bewitching them, and, with the stomach of their faith nauseated, vomited up the food of the Holy Spirit. But if anyone objects, let him explain how it is taken from common opinion that there is the valley of the Titans in the books of Kingdoms (2 Kings 23), Sirens and Onocentaurs in Isaiah (ch. 34), Arcturus and Orion and the Pleiades in Job (ch. 9), and other things like these, which names certainly have both the causes and the origins of the fables of the Gentiles. Let us ask Marcion, then, who rejects the prophets, how he interprets in this place what follows.\n\nBefore whose eyes Jesus Christ was publicly portrayed (proscriptus), crucified among you. For Christ was rightly portrayed beforehand (præscriptus) for us, of whose gibbet and passion, blows and scourgings the whole choir of the prophets foretells, so that we have known his cross not from the Gospel alone, in which he is related to have been crucified, but long before he deigned to descend to the earth and to take up the man who was crucified. Nor is it small praise of the Galatians that they believed in the Crucified in such a way as he had been portrayed beforehand (præscriptus) to them: namely, that by reading the prophets again and again and knowing all the mysteries of the old Law, they came to believe by a way and an order. In some codices is read: \"Who has bewitched you, not to believe the truth?\" But because this is not found in the copies of Adamantius, we have omitted it.\n— Commentary on Galatians","What I had left untouched in the first book of the Commentaries on Galatians, when I was discussing the characteristics of the nations, it now seems I should render in the second: who the Galatians are, or whither and whence they crossed over; whether the land they now inhabit poured them forth as natives or received them as newcomers; and whether they lost their language through intermarriage, or both learned a new one and did not lose their own. Marcus Varro, the most diligent investigator of all antiquities, and the others who have imitated him, have handed down many things about this people, and things worthy of memory. But since it is our purpose not to bring uncircumcised men into the temple of God (and, to confess it plainly, it is many years now since we ceased to read these things), we shall set down the words of our Lactantius, what he held about this people in the third volume to Probus: \"The Gauls,\" he says, \"were anciently called Galatae from the whiteness of their bodies; and the Sibyl calls them so. This is what the poet meant to signify when he says: Then their milk-white necks — are entwined with gold (Virgil, Aeneid 8): when he could have said 'white.' Hence, certainly, the province of Galatia, into which the Gauls came at one time and mingled themselves with the Greeks. Whence that region was first named Gallograecia, afterward Galatia.\" Nor is it surprising if he said this of the Galatians, and recorded that Western peoples, passing over such great spaces of the lands in between, settled in the region of the East, since it is established that swarms from the East and from Greece, conversely, reached the farthest bounds of the West. The Phocaeans founded Marseilles, and Varro says they are trilingual, because they speak Greek and Latin and Gallic. Colonists of the Rhodians founded the town of Rhoda, from which the river Rhone received its name. I pass over the Tyrians, founders of Carthage, and the city of Agenor; I pass by the Thebes of Liber, which he founded in Africa, a city now called Thebestis. I leave aside that part of Libya which is full of Greek cities. I cross over to the Spains: did not Greeks setting out from the island of Zacynthus found Saguntum, and are not the Ionians, Greek men, reported to have founded the town of Tartessus, which is now called Carteia? The mountains of the Spains too, Calpe, Idrus, Pyrene, and likewise the islands Aphrodisiades and Gymnesiae, which are called the Balearics, do they not show traces of the Greek tongue? Italy itself, occupied by Greek peoples, was once called Greater Greece. Certainly, and this cannot be denied, the Romans were begotten of the stock of Aeneas, a man of Asia. From this it comes about that in the West talents of Greek sharpness are often found, and in the East they smack of barbarian stupidity. Nor do we say this as though diverse things were not born in each place according to the region, but because for the most part even the other things that are not alike are so named. Therefore it is not surprising that the Galatians were called foolish and slower of understanding, since even Hilary, the Rhone of Latin eloquence, himself a Gaul and born at Poitiers, in his Song of Hymns calls the Gauls unteachable. And that they are now fertile in orators pertains not so much to the diligence of the region as to the clamor of the rhetoricians, especially since Aquitaine boasts of a Greek origin, and the Galatians set out not from that part of the lands but from the fiercer Gauls. Do you wish to know, O Paula and Eustochium, how the Apostle marked each province with its own characteristics? To this very day the same traces remain, whether of virtues or of errors. The faith of the Roman people is praised (Rom. 1). Where else do people run with such zeal and in such crowds to the churches and to the tombs of the martyrs? Where does the Amen so resound, like heavenly thunder, that the empty temples of the idols are shaken? Not that the Romans have another faith than this which all the churches of Christ have, but that devotion is greater in them, and simplicity in believing. Again, they are accused of facility and of pride. Of facility, as there: \"I beseech you, brothers, that you watch those who cause dissensions and offenses, contrary to the doctrine which you have learned; and turn aside from them; for such men do not serve Christ the Lord, but their own belly; and by sweet words and blessings they seduce the hearts of the innocent. For your obedience is spread abroad to every place. I rejoice therefore in you, and I wish you to be wise in good and simple in evil\" (Rom. 16:17 ff.). And of pride: \"Be not high-minded, but fear\" (Rom. 11:20, 25). And: \"I do not wish you to be ignorant, brothers, of this mystery, that you may not be wise in yourselves\". And in what follows: \"For I say, through the grace that has been given to me, to all who are among you: not to be wise beyond what one ought to be wise, but to be wise unto sobriety\" (Rom. 12:3). And more openly: \"To rejoice with those who rejoice; to weep with those who weep. Being of the same mind toward one another. Not minding high things, but consenting to the humble. Do not be prudent in your own sight\" (Rom. 12:15-16). He also marks the Corinthians, because their women are with uncovered head and the men let their hair grow (1 Cor. 11), and they eat indifferently in temples (1 Cor. 8:10), and, puffed up with secular wisdom, they deny the resurrection of the flesh. That these things in part remain to this day, no one who has seen Achaia can doubt (1 Cor. 4). The Macedonians are praised for love, and for hospitality and the reception of the brothers. Hence it is written to them: \"But concerning the love of the brotherhood we have no need to write to you. For you yourselves have learned from God to love one another. And indeed you do it toward all the brothers in all Macedonia\" (1 Thess. 4:9). But they are rebuked because they go idly round the houses, and, looking for another's food, while they desire to please each one and run about hither and thither, they report what is done at each person's house. For it follows: \"But we beseech you, brothers, that you abound more, and take pains to be quiet, and that you do your own business, and work with your hands, as we commanded you, so that you may walk honestly toward those who are outside, and may desire nothing of anyone\" (1 Thess. 4:10-11). And lest anyone think this was urged more from the duty of a teacher than because of a fault of the people, in the second letter to the same he presses and repeats it, saying: \"For even when we were with you, we declared this to you, that if anyone will not work, neither let him eat. For we have heard that some among you walk restlessly, doing no work, but acting as busybodies. Now those who are such we charge and beseech in the Lord Jesus Christ, that, working in silence, they eat their own bread\" (2 Thess. 3:10 ff.). It would be long if I wished to note, from the Apostle and from all the Scriptures, either the virtues or the vices of the individual nations, since we have come down to these very things we have said from the fact that the Galatians were pronounced foolish and senseless. Whoever has seen Ancyra, the metropolitan city of Galatia, knows with me by how many schisms it is torn even now, by how many varieties of doctrines it is defiled. I pass over the Cataphrygians, Ophites, Borborites and Manichaeans; for these are by now familiar names of human calamity. Who has ever heard, in any part of the Roman world, of the Passalorynchites and Ascodrobi and Artotyrites, and the rest, monsters rather than names? Traces of the ancient folly remain to this day. One thing we add, and so pay what was promised at the beginning: that the Galatians, apart from the Greek speech which the whole East speaks, have their own language, almost the same as the Treveri, and it does not matter if they have corrupted some things in it, since the Africans too have in some part changed the language of the Phoenicians, and Latinity itself changes daily both by regions and by time. But now let us return to our purpose.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.3.2":["This only I wish to learn from you: did you receive the spirit by works of the Law, or by the hearing of faith?\n\nThere are indeed, he says, many things that could compel you by questioning to admit that the Gospel is to be preferred to the Law; but since you are senseless and can by no means hear them, I speak with you in plain language and ask about what is ready to hand: the Holy Spirit whom you received, did the works of the Law, the observance of the sabbath, the superstition of circumcision and of new moons give him to you, or the hearing of faith, through which you believed, coming from the Gentiles? But if it cannot be denied that the Holy Spirit, and the powers which at the beginning of faith followed the Spirit once received, were given not by works of the Law but by faith in Christ, it is manifest that you, having begun from better things, are collapsing into worse. Let us consider carefully, however, that he did not say: I wish to learn from you whether you received the Spirit by works; but added: by works of the Law. For he knew that Cornelius the centurion too had received the Spirit by works (Acts 10), but not by works of the Law, which he did not know. But if it is said on the contrary, \"Then the Spirit can be received even without the hearing of faith,\" we shall answer that he did indeed receive the Spirit, but by the hearing of faith and by the natural law, which speaks in our hearts that every good thing is to be done and evil things avoided; by which, as we have related before, Abraham, Moses and the other saints were also justified; and which the observance of works and the righteousness of the Law can afterwards increase: not, however, of the carnal law, which has passed away, but of the spiritual, because the Law is spiritual. Nor indeed, because we prefer faith, do we destroy the works of the Law (Rom. 3), nor do we say, as certain people do: \"Let us do evil things until good things come\" (whose condemnation is just); but we set grace before slavery. And we say that what the Jews do out of fear, we do out of love: that they are slaves, we sons; that they are compelled to the good, we take up the good of our own accord. Therefore from faith in Christ there is not born a license to sin; rather, from the love of faith the will to do good works is increased, since we do good things not because we dread the judge, but because we know that they please him in whom we believe. Someone may ask: if faith is not except by hearing, how can those who were born deaf become Christians? For anyone can understand God the Father from the greatness and beauty of creatures, and the Creator is consequently recognized from the things created; but the birth, the cross, the death and the resurrection of Christ cannot be known except by hearing. Either, then, the deaf are not Christians; or, if the deaf are Christians, what is said elsewhere by the apostle is false: \"So faith is by hearing, and hearing through the word of God\". To this, one who is content with a simple answer says that he did not say generally, \"The faith of all is by hearing,\" but \"faith is by hearing,\" which can be taken both in part and in whole: that is, the faith of those who hear, who believe, is by hearing. But one who tries to satisfy this scruple will first attempt to assert that the deaf can learn the Gospel by nods too, and by daily intercourse, and, so to speak, by the speaking gesture of the whole body; and then also this, that the word of God, to whom nothing is deaf, speaks rather to those ears of which he himself says in the Gospel: \"He who has ears to hear, let him hear\" (Luke 8:8). And in the Apocalypse: \"He who has an ear, let him hear what the spirit says to the churches\" (Rev. 2:11). And Isaiah: \"The Lord added to me an ear\" (Isa. 50:4-5). This is the other man, to whom God speaks in secret, who cries in the heart of the believer: \"Abba, Father\" (Rom. 8:15); and (as we have often explained) just as the body has all its members and senses, so the soul too has all the senses and members, and among the rest ears as well; and whoever has these will not greatly need these ears of the body for coming to know the Gospel of Christ. At the same time, observe this too, that here the Spirit, without any addition, is understood as the Holy Spirit, whom we obtain by the gift of God, and not as the spirit of man, of which it is written elsewhere: \"The incorruptible Spirit is in all things\" (Wisd. 12:1). And: \"The Spirit himself bears witness to our spirit\" (Rom. 8:16). And in another place: \"No one knows the things that are in a man except the spirit of the man that is in him\" (1 Cor. 2:11). And in Daniel: \"Bless the Lord, you spirits and souls of the righteous\" (Dan. 3:86).\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.3.3":["Are you so foolish that, though you began with the Spirit, you are now made perfect in the flesh?\n\nIf the Galatians had received the Holy Spirit, how were they foolish? But this is at once resolved: they had indeed begun with the Spirit, but when they were being made perfect in the flesh, the Spirit was taken away from them. Hence they suffered so many things in vain, as many as they suffered. That this might not befall him after his sin, David prays, saying: \"Take not your holy Spirit from me\" (Ps. 50:13). Notice carefully that he who follows the Scriptures according to the letter is said to be made perfect in the flesh. Wherefore that which is written to the Corinthians: \"Living in the flesh, we do not wage war according to the flesh\" (2 Cor. 10:3), can better be understood thus, that those are said to wage war in the flesh who expound the Old Testament in a lowly manner. But those who follow the spiritual understanding may indeed be in the flesh, because they have the same letter as the Jews, yet they do not wage war according to the flesh, since they pass over from the flesh to the spirit. When you see one who first believes from among the Gentiles, and puts his hand to the plough of Christ, and, with some prudent teacher going before him, so advances along the road of the Law to the Gospel that he understands all those things which are written there, about the sabbath, about unleavened bread, about circumcision, about victims, in a manner worthy of God; and then, after the reading of the Gospel, is persuaded by some Jew, or by an associate of the Jews, to abandon the shadows and the clouds of allegory and to interpret the Scriptures just as they are written: of him you can say: Are you so foolish that, though you began with the spirit, you are now made perfect in the flesh?\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.3.4":["Have you suffered so much without cause, if indeed it is without cause?\n\nLet us consider the unhappy Jews, with how great superstition and labor of observance they live among the other nations, saying, touch not, taste not, handle not; and we shall prove that what is said is true: Have you suffered so much without cause; but the sentence is not at once tempered toward them, and it becomes doubtful, if indeed it is without cause, because it is said of those who are able to return from the Law to the Gospel. But it can better be understood thus: that the Galatians, when they first believed in the Crucified, suffered many reproaches both from the Jews and from the Gentiles, and endured no small persecutions. These they are charged with having suffered in vain, if they withdraw from the grace of Christ, for the sake of which they suffered so much. At the same time there is also this hope: that whoever has labored for the faith of Christ and has afterward fallen into sin, just as he is said to have suffered his former things without cause while he sins, so in turn he does not lose them, if he returns to his original faith and to his former zeal. Otherwise: If, he says, you think that circumcision ought to be followed after grace, then all that you have suffered while living until the present time without circumcision has been brought to nothing. And indeed you seem to me not to have endured these things in vain, for I know that the Law has no force after the Gospel. Or certainly thus: It would have been no light loss if, by following circumcision, you had lost only your former labor of faith; but now the penalty of transgression is also joined to this harm, so that you have both suffered the past things without cause and will be tormented in the future. Some understand it in a more forced way, thus: Consider the former freedom of grace and the burdens of the present observance in the Law, and you will see how many things you have done with fruitless zeal; although the fruit of this error is not to be wholly despaired of, since you were led to this very thing by zeal for God. For pardon can be granted to the ignorant, if, turned to better things, you show that it was your knowledge that wavered in you, not your zeal.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.3.5":["He therefore who gives you the Spirit and works miracles among you, does he do it by the works of the Law, or by the hearing of faith?\n\nGives (tribuit), that is, ministers (administrat), is to be read in the present tense, so as to show that the Holy Spirit is ministered to the worthy always, every hour and moment; and that the more anyone has advanced in the work and love of God, the more he has in himself the powers of the Holy Spirit, which the hearing of faith, and not the works of the Law, brings to completion. Not that the works of the Law are to be despised, and simple faith sought without them; but that the works themselves are to be adorned by faith in Christ. For shrewd is that saying of a wise man: \"Not that the faithful man lives by righteousness, but that the righteous man lives by faith.\" At the same time it is shown that the Galatians, having received the Holy Spirit after they believed, had gifts of powers, that is, prophecy, kinds of tongues, healings of diseases, and the rest that are enumerated to the Corinthians among the spiritual gifts (1 Cor. 12). And yet after such great things (because perhaps they did not have the grace of discerning spirits) they were ensnared by false teachers. It must also be observed that miracles are said to be worked among those who do not hold the truth of the Gospel: as among those who, not following the Lord, were doing signs in his name, John especially complaining: \"Master, we saw someone casting out demons in your name, and we forbade him, because he does not follow with us\" (Mark 9:37). This is against the heretics, who think the proof of their faith lies in whether they have done some sign. Though they have eaten and drunk in the name of the Lord (for they too have a sacrilegious altar), and boast that they have done many signs by invoking the Savior, on the day of judgment they will deserve to hear: \"I do not know you; depart from me, you who work iniquity\" (Matt. 7:23).\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.3.6":["Even as Abraham believed God, and it was reckoned to him as righteousness.\n\nFrom this place up to the one where it is written: \"Those who are of faith shall be blessed with faithful Abraham\", Marcion erased the text from his own Apostle. But what did it profit him to have removed this, when the rest that he left is opposed to his madness? Now Abraham believed God when he went out from his homeland into a land he did not know (Gen. 12 ff.); when he trusted that Sarah, ninety years old and barren, would give birth; and when, having heard God's promise that in Isaac his seed would be called, he offered that very Isaac as a victim, and yet did not doubt the Lord's promise. Rightly is faith reckoned as righteousness to such a man, who, having gone beyond the works of the Law, won the favor of God not by fear but by love.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.3.7":["You know, therefore, that those who are of faith, these are the sons of Abraham.\n\nHe argues this more fully in the Epistle to the Romans: that faith was reckoned to Abraham for righteousness, not in circumcision but in uncircumcision (Rom. 4). And, observing it carefully, he teaches that those are sons of Abraham, whoever have believed with the same mind with which Abraham believed while uncircumcised, he who rejoiced to see the day of the Lord, and saw it, and was glad (John 8). Hence it is also said to the unbelieving Jews: \"If you were sons of Abraham, you would do the works of Abraham\" (ibid. 39). But what other works (at that time, when these things were being said) did the Lord seek from them, except belief in the Son of God, whom the Father had sent, and who says: \"He who believes in me, does not believe in me, but in him who sent me\" (John 12:44)? Hence also in another place, to those who were applauding themselves on the antiquity and nobility of their race, the answer is given: \"And do not say, We have Abraham as our father. For God is able from these stones to raise up sons to Abraham\" (Matt. 3:9). No one doubts that the stones there signify the hard hearts of the Gentiles, which were afterwards softened and received the seal of faith. Count up, diligent reader, the virtues of Abraham by which he pleased God before circumcision, and whomever you find in like work, say that they are sons of Abraham, who was justified in uncircumcision and who received circumcision not on account of the merit of works, but as a sign of his earlier faith. For since Christ was to spring from his seed (in whom the blessing of all the nations had been promised, and many ages were to pass between Abraham and Christ), God, providing that the offspring of beloved Abraham should not be mingled with the other nations and his family little by little become uncertain, marked the Israelite flock with a kind of brand of circumcision, so that, living among the Egyptians, among the Assyrians, Babylonians and Chaldeans, they might be distinguished by this seal. Accordingly, for forty years in the desert no one was circumcised: for they were living alone, without any admixture of another nation; as soon as the people had crossed the bank of the Jordan and the Jewish swarm had poured itself into the land of Palestine, circumcision, now necessary, provided against the confusion that would come from mingling with the nations. But that the people is written to have been circumcised a second time by Joshua (Jesu) their leader (Josh. 5) signifies both that circumcision, which had been reasonably practiced in Egypt, ceased in the desert, and that believers were to be cleansed by the Lord Jesus Christ with a spiritual circumcision.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.3.8":["But the Scripture, foreseeing that God justifies the Gentiles by faith, announced beforehand to Abraham that all the Gentiles shall be blessed in you. Therefore those who are of faith shall be blessed with faithful Abraham.\n\nNot that Scripture itself, that is, the ink and the parchment, which are without sense, can foreknow future things; but that the Holy Spirit, and the sense which lies hidden in the letter, foretold things that were to come many ages later. Moreover, the example that has been taken from Genesis is contained thus in its own book: \"And in your seed shall all the nations of the earth be blessed\" (Gen. 26:4). Interpreting this of Christ, the Apostle says: \"It is not written 'of seeds,' as in many, but as in one, 'and your seed,' which is Christ\". And this we ought to observe in almost all the testimonies that have been taken from the old books into the New Testament: that the evangelists or apostles trusted to memory, and, setting out only the sense, often changed the order, and sometimes either took words away or added them. But no one doubts that it was not in Isaac and Jacob, or in the twelve patriarchs, and the others who descend from the stock of Abraham, that all the nations were blessed, but in Christ Jesus, through whom all the Gentiles praise God, and a new name is blessed upon the earth. It can also be understood that the Apostle took the example of the seed from another place of Genesis, where it is written: \"Now there led out of doors\" (no doubt, Abraham) \"God, and said to him: Look into heaven and number the stars, if you shall be able to number them. And he said to him: So shall your seed be; and Abraham believed God, and it was reckoned to him for righteousness\" (Gen. 15:5, 6).\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.3.9":["Whoever therefore believe shall be blessed with faithful Abraham, who, because of his outstanding faith in God, is related to have been the first to believe in him; just as Enosh, because of his preeminent hope in God, one that stood out among the rest, is written to have hoped to call upon the Lord God (Gen. 4:26). Not that Abel also, of whom the Lord says: \"The voice of your brother's blood cries to me\" (ibid. 4:10), and the others after him, did not hope to call upon God; but that each one is named from that quality which he possesses most of all.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.3.10":["For whoever are of the works of the Law are under the curse. For it is written: Cursed is everyone who does not continue in all the things that are written in the book of the Law, that he may do them.\n\nI have this custom: whenever something is taken by the apostles from the Old Instrument, I go back to the original books and examine carefully how it is written in its own place. And so I found this very passage set down in Deuteronomy by the Seventy translators thus: \"Cursed is every man who does not continue in all the words of this Law, that he may do them: and all the people shall say, So be it\" (Deut. 27:26). In Aquila, however, thus: \"Cursed is he who does not establish the words of this Law, that he may do them: and all the people shall say, Truly\". Symmachus: \"Cursed is he who does not make firm the words of that Law, that he may do them, and all the people shall say, Amen\". Theodotion, moreover, translated it thus: \"Cursed is he who does not raise up the words of this Law, to do them, and all the people shall say, Amen\". From this we understand that the Apostle, as in other places, set down the sense of the testimony rather than the words; and we hold it uncertain whether the Seventy Translators added \"every man\" (omnis homo) and \"in all\" (in omnibus), or whether it was so in the old Hebrew and was afterwards deleted by the Jews. Now what spurs me to this suspicion is this: the word \"every\" (omnis) and \"in all\" (in omnibus), as though necessary to his sense for proving that whoever are of the works of the Law are under the curse, the Apostle, a man of Hebrew learning and most learned in the Law, would never have brought forward unless it were found in the Hebrew volumes. For this reason, reading through the Hebrew volumes of the Samaritans, I found that CHOL, which means \"every\" or \"all\", is written there and agrees with the Seventy translators. In vain, therefore, did the Jews remove it, so as not to seem to be under the curse if they could not fulfill all the things that are written, since the more ancient letters of another nation too testify that it was set down. Now that no one can fulfill the Law and do all the things that are commanded, the Apostle testifies elsewhere as well, saying: \"For what was impossible to the Law, in that it was weak through the flesh, God, sending his Son in the likeness of the flesh of sin, from sin condemned sin in the flesh\" (Rom. 8). If this is true, it can be objected to us: Then are Moses too, and Isaiah, and the other prophets, who were under the works of the Law, under the curse? He will not fear to grant this who has read the Apostle saying: \"Because Christ redeemed us from the curse of the Law, being made a curse for us\"; and to answer that each one of the saints was in his own time made a curse for the people. Nor, in granting this to righteous men as well, will he at once seem to detract from the Savior, as though he had nothing special and excellent in being made a curse for us, since the others too were made a curse for others. For none of them, although he himself was made a curse, freed anyone from the curse, save only the Lord Jesus Christ, who with his precious blood redeemed from the curse of the Law both all of us and them too, I mean Moses and Aaron and all the prophets and patriarchs. And do not think that this is said from my own sense; Scripture is witness: that \"Christ by the grace of God\", or, as is read in some copies, \"without God, died for all\" (2 Cor. 5). But if for all, then also for Moses and for all the prophets, none of whom was able to blot out the old handwriting that was written against us and fix it to the cross (Col. 2): \"All have sinned, and lack the glory [that is, grace] of God\" (Rom. 3:23). Ecclesiastes too confirms this judgment: \"There is not a righteous man on earth who does good and does not sin\" (Eccles. 7:21). Finally, the Apostle's statement further below also plainly teaches that neither Moses nor any illustrious man among the ancients could be justified before God through the Law. For there follows:\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.3.11":["But since in the Law no one is justified before God, it is manifest that the righteous lives by faith. But the Law is not of faith, but he who has done these things shall live in them.\n\nThe example by which it is proved that the righteous lives by faith, and not by works, he took from Habakkuk, which the Seventy translators rendered thus: \"But the righteous lives by my faith\" (Hab. 2:4). Aquila and Theodotion: \"But the righteous lives by his faith\", that is, God's. It must therefore be considered that he did not say \"a human being\" or \"a man lives by faith\", lest he should give occasion for despising the works of the virtues; but the righteous lives by faith: so that whoever was faithful and was to live through faith could not otherwise come to faith, or live in it, unless he had first been righteous, and had ascended by purity of life as by certain steps to faith. It can therefore happen that someone is righteous, and yet does not live, being without faith in Christ. If a scruple is stirred in the reader, let him take up the words of Paul, in which he says of himself: \"As to the righteousness which is in the Law, without reproach\" (Phil. 3:6). Paul therefore was at that time righteous in the Law, but could not yet live, because he did not have within himself Christ, who says: \"I am the life\" (John 11:25). Believing in him afterwards, he began also to live. Let us too make something similar to what is said, the righteous lives by faith; and let us say: the chaste lives by faith, the wise lives by faith, the brave lives by faith, and from the remaining parts of the virtues let us bring forward a like sentence against those who, not believing in Christ, think themselves brave and wise, temperate or righteous: so that they may know that no one lives without Christ, without whom every virtue is in vice. The present testimony can also be read thus: that the righteous by faith, so that there is then added, lives.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.3.12":["But in what he says, The Law is not of faith, but he who has done these things shall live in them, it is most manifestly shown that it is not life simply that is meant, but that life which is referred to something. For the righteous lives by faith: and there is not added \"in these\" or \"in them\". But he who lives in the Law, who has done these things, lives in them, that is, in the things which he did, which he thought good: having as the reward of his labor only those works which he did, whether length of life (as the Jews think) or escape from the penalty by which the transgressor of the Law is put to death. But let us not think that to live in them are the words of the Apostle, but of the prophet Ezekiel, who says: \"And I led them out into the desert, and I gave them my precepts, and showed them my ordinances, which a man shall do, and shall live in them\" (Ezek. 20:10, 11). And when he had said that those who had walked in the precepts and ordinances live, he added: \"And I gave them precepts not good, and ordinances in which they should not live in them\" (ibid., 25). How much care in the words! Where he said: \"I gave them precepts and ordinances in which they should live in them\", he did not add \"good\". But where he put \"in which they should not live in them\", he added: \"And I gave them precepts not good, and ordinances in which they shall not live in them\". But these things more fully in Ezekiel: now let us return to the order of the Epistle.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.3.13":["Christ redeemed us from the curse of the Law, made a curse for us.\n\nIn this place Marcion creeps in concerning the power of the Creator, whom he defames as bloodthirsty, cruel and an avenger, asserting that we have been redeemed by Christ, who is the Son of another, good God. If he understood how buying and redeeming differ (for he who buys, buys what belongs to another; but he who redeems buys, in the proper sense, that which was his own and had ceased to be his own), he would never twist the simple words of the Scriptures into a calumny in support of his doctrine. Christ therefore redeemed us from the curse of the Law, which was established for those who sin, whom he himself rebukes through the prophet, saying: \"Behold, you have been sold for your sins, and in your iniquities I have put away your mother\" (Isa. 50:1). And the Apostle repeats this very thing, saying: \"But I am carnal, sold under sin\" (Rom. 7:14). The curses of the Law too, which are written in Leviticus and Deuteronomy, are not fulfilled with God as their author; rather, by a prophetic spirit, the things that are to come upon those who were going to sin are announced to them. But if he should wish to hem us in with the testimony of the Apostle, who says: \"Whoever are of the works of the Law are under a curse: for it is written: Cursed is everyone who does not remain in all things that are written in the book of the Law, to do them\" (Deut. 27:26), and to assert that all who were under the Law were cursed, let us ask him whether those who are under the Gospel of Christ and do not do its precepts are cursed or not. If he says they are cursed, he will have in the Gospel what we have in the Law. If he denies that they are cursed, then the precepts of the Gospel have been laid down in vain, and those who have fulfilled them will be without reward. Both points, therefore, are resolved in this way: just as Christ Jesus freed us from the curse of the Law, made a curse for us, so also from the curse of the Gospel, which has been established over those who have not done its precepts, he has rescued us, himself made a curse for us, knowing how not to remit even the smallest portion of a talent, and to exact the last farthing (Matt. 5, and Mark 12).\n— Commentary on Galatians","Because it is written: Cursed is everyone who hangs on a tree: that the blessing of Abraham might come among the Gentiles in Christ Jesus, that we might receive the promise of the spirit through faith.\n\nBefore we discuss the sense and the words of the Apostle, it seems right to go over briefly the testimony of Deuteronomy, from which the Apostle also took these words (Deut. 21:22, 23), and to compare it with the other editions. The Seventy translators, then, rendered this passage thus: \"But if there be in anyone a sin and a judgment of death, and he has died, and you hang him on a tree, his body shall not sleep upon the tree, but burying you shall bury him on that day; because cursed by God is everyone who hangs on a tree: and you shall not defile your land, which the Lord your God will give you for an inheritance\". Aquila: \"And when there is in a man a sin unto a judgment of death, and he has been slain, and you hang him upon a tree, his carcass shall not remain upon the tree, but burying you shall bury him on that day, because he who is hanged is the curse of God: and you shall not defile your ground, which the Lord your God will give you as an inheritance\". Symmachus: \"But if there be in a man a sin bringing a judgment of death, and he has been slain, and you hang him upon a tree: his corpse shall not stay the night upon the tree, but with burial you shall bury him on that same day, because he was hanged on account of blasphemy of God: and you shall not defile your land, which the Lord your God will give you for an inheritance\". Theodotion: \"And because there will be in a man a sin unto a judgment of death, and he will die, and you will hang him on a tree, his carcass shall not sleep upon the tree, because with burial you shall bury him on that same day, because the one hanged is the curse of God: and you shall not defile your adama, which the Lord your God shall have given you as an inheritance\". Now ADAMA (אדמה) is the word in the Hebrew language for \"earth\" or \"ground\". But in the place where Aquila and Theodotion translated alike, saying: \"because the one hanged is the curse of God\", the Hebrew has it thus: CHI CALALATH ELOIM THALUI. These words Ebion, that heresiarch, half-Christian and half-Jew, interpreted thus: ὅτι ὕβρις Θεοῦ ὁ κρεμάμενος, that is, \"because the one hanged is an insult to God\". I remember that in the Dispute of Jason and Papiscus, which is written in the Greek language, I found it thus: λοιδορία Θεοῦ ὁ κρεμάμενος, that is, \"a curse of God is he who is hung up\". The Hebrew who instructed me in some measure in the Scriptures used to tell me that it can also be read thus: \"because God was hanged shamefully\". We have gathered these things together for this reason: because it is a most notorious question, and the Jews are accustomed to throw it at us as a disgrace that our Savior and Lord was under the curse of God. First, then, we must observe that not whoever has hung on a tree is cursed before God, but he who has sinned, and on account of a crime has been sentenced to death and raised up on a cross: he is cursed not because he was crucified, but because he fell into such guilt that he deserved to be crucified. Next we must set against them this point: that further on the cause of the gallows is set out more fully, Scripture relating that he was crucified on account of a curse and blasphemy of God. Symmachus translated this more plainly, saying: \"because he was hanged on account of blasphemy of God\". Lastly, let us ask them whether, if Ananias and Azarias and Misael, refusing to worship the idol of Nebuchadnezzar, had been hanged on a tree (Dan. 3), and Eleazar too, a man of ninety under Antiochus king of Syria, and the glorious mother with her seven sons (2 Macc. 7), they would have reckoned them cursed, or most worthy of every blessing? Surely, if Haman had not himself mounted, as he deserved, the cross that he had prepared for Mordecai (Esther 7), I think Mordecai would have ascended it not as one cursed, but as a holy man. By these and other similar examples it is proved that he is cursed who has committed a crime worthy of the gallows, not he who has been crucified through the iniquity of judges and the power of enemies, or the clamor of the mob, or envy of his virtues, or the anger of a king. Naboth too was once condemned to death by the whole city of Jezreel at the letters of Jezebel (1 Kings 21); but his blood, as a type of Christ, is avenged many ages later, the Lord saying to Hosea: \"Call his name Jezreel, for yet a little while, and I will avenge the blood of Jezreel upon the house of Jehu.\" (Hos. 1:4). So much against the Jews. But to bring the discussion back to ourselves, I cannot know why the Apostle, in what is written: \"Cursed by God is everyone who hangs on a tree\" (Deut. 21:23), has either taken something away or added something. For if he was once following the authority of the Seventy translators, he ought, as it was published by them, to have added the name of God as well. But if, as a Hebrew of the Hebrews, he thought that what he had read in his own language was most true, he ought not to have taken up either \"everyone\" or \"on a tree\", which are not found in the Hebrew. From this it seems to me either that the ancient books of the Hebrews had it otherwise than they have it now; or that the Apostle (as I have already said before) set down the sense of the Scriptures, not the words; or, what is rather to be supposed, that after the passion of Christ the name of God was added by someone, both in the Hebrew codices and in ours, so as to brand with infamy us who believe in a Christ cursed by God. With a bold foot, therefore, I advance to this contest, to appeal to the books: that in no place is it written that anyone is cursed by God, and wherever a curse is set down, the name of God is never added. \"Cursed are you by all the beasts\" (Gen. 3:14), is said to the serpent. And to Adam: \"Cursed is the ground in your works\" (ibid., 17). And to Cain: \"Cursed are you upon the earth\" (Gen. 4:11). And elsewhere: \"Cursed be Canaan the boy, he shall be a servant to his brothers\" (Gen. 9:25). And also in another place: \"Cursed be their fury, because it is bold, and their anger, because it is harsh\" (Gen. 49:7). It would be long if I were to list all the curses that are written in Leviticus, and in Deuteronomy, and in Joshua son of Nun; and yet in none of them is the name of God added: so much so that even Satan himself, when he was promising concerning Job that, if he were pressed by great distresses, he would blaspheme, expressed this by the better word, saying: \"If he does not bless you to your face\" (Job 1:11). And in the books of Kingdoms Naboth is reported to have been stoned for this reason: \"because he blessed God and the king\" (1 Kings 21). But no one ought to be troubled that Christ was made a curse for us, since God also, who is said to have made him a curse, himself (although Christ knew no sin) made him sin for us; and the Savior emptied himself from the fullness of the Father, taking the form of a slave (Phil. 2): Life died, and the wisdom of God was called foolishness, so that what was foolish of God might become wiser than men (1 Cor. 1). And in the sixty-eighth psalm he speaks of himself: \"O God, you know my foolishness, and my offenses are not hidden from you\" (Ps. 68:7). The Lord's insult, therefore, is our glory. He died, that we might live. He went down to the underworld, that we might ascend to heaven. He was made foolishness, that we might be made wisdom. He emptied himself of the fullness and the form of God, taking the form of a slave, that the fullness of divinity might dwell in us, and that from slaves we might become the Lord's. He hung on a tree, that, hung upon a tree, he might blot out the sin which we had committed by the tree of the knowledge of good and evil. His cross turned bitter waters into a sweet taste, and, cast into the streams of the Jordan, it raised the axe that had been lost and sunk into the depths (2 Kings 6).\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.3.14":["Finally, he was made a curse, made, I say, not born: so that the blessings which had been promised to Abraham might, through him as author and forerunner, be transferred to the Gentiles, and the promise of the spirit might be fulfilled in us through faith in him: which we ought to take in two ways, either of the spiritual gifts of the virtues, or of the spiritual understanding of the Scriptures (1 Cor. 9).\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.3.15-17":["Some think that he said Brothers, I speak according to man because he was about to discuss a man's testament, the death of the testator, and other examples drawn from human likeness; but it seems to me that it was set before partly, indeed, on account of what they suppose, but chiefly on account of what follows, that is: He does not say, \"and to seeds,\" as of many, but as of one, \"and to your seed,\" who is Christ. Ranging through all the Scriptures in mind and memory, I have never found \"seeds\" written in the plural number, but, whether in a good sense or in a bad, always in the singular number. So too with what he adds: But this I say: the testament confirmed by God; if anyone carefully compares the Hebrew volumes and the other editions with the translation of the Seventy translators, he will find that where testament is written, it does not sound \"testament\" (testamentum) but \"pact\" (pactum), which in the Hebrew tongue is called BERITH (ברית). From this it is plain that the Apostle did what he promised, and did not use hidden meanings with the Galatians, but everyday and cheap ones, such as could (had he not set before them I speak according to man) displease the prudent.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.3.15":["Brothers, I speak according to man: yet a man's testament, once confirmed, no one despises or adds to: to Abraham were the promises spoken, and to his seed. He does not say, \"and to seeds,\" as of many, but as of one, \"and to your seed,\" who is Christ. But this I say: the testament confirmed by God, the Law, which was made after four hundred and thirty years, does not make void so as to annul the promise; for if the inheritance is from the Law, it is no longer from promise. But God gave it to Abraham through promise.\n\nThe Apostle, who became all things to all men that he might gain all, a debtor to Greeks and Barbarians, to the wise and the unwise, became foolish to the Galatians too, whom a little before he had called foolish. For he did not use with them the arguments he used with the Romans, but simpler ones, such as the foolish could understand, and almost from the street corner. And lest he seem to have done this from lack of skill and not by art, he first placates the prudent reader, and tempers what he is about to say with a preface set before it: Brothers, I speak according to man. For what I am about to say, I do not say according to God; I do not say it according to hidden wisdom and to those who can feed on solid food, but according to those who, because of the tenderness of their stomach, are nourished on a milky dew and are by no means able to hear things that are great (1 Cor. 5). Hence also to the Corinthians, among whom fornication was reported, and such fornication as is not even among the Gentiles, he says: \"I say, and not the Lord\" (1 Cor. 7:12). And to the same in the second letter: \"What I speak, I do not speak according to the Lord, but as it were in foolishness\" (2 Cor. 11:17).\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.3.17-18":["But the simple sense woven in this place has this force: the Apostle teaches that the promises made earlier to Abraham cannot be destroyed through the Law, which was given afterward, nor can later things prejudice earlier ones, since the promises were given to Abraham four hundred and thirty years before, that all the nations would be blessed in him; whereas the observance of the Law, that whoever had done it should live in it, was given to Moses on Mount Sinai four hundred and thirty years later. On the contrary, it could be said here: What need was there, then, for the Law to be given so long a time after the promise, since, once the Law was given, a suspicion that the pledge had been destroyed could arise, and since, while the promise remained, a Law that would profit nothing was given? Foreseeing this question, the Apostle himself puts it to himself in what follows and unravels it, saying:\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.3.17":["In this place the years must be reckoned from the time when the Lord spoke to Abraham, saying: \"And in your seed shall all the nations be blessed\" (Gen. 22:18), down to Moses the lawgiver: whether they are four hundred and thirty, or how in Genesis the Lord promises Abraham himself that after four hundred years his sons would go out from the land of slavery. For it is no small matter, and though it has been sought by many, I do not know whether it has been found by anyone. That too which is read in the same book about Tamar and her two little ones (Gen. 38), namely that the first, who is called Zerah, put out his hand, and the midwife tied a scarlet thread on it, and then, as he drew his hand back inside, the later one, who is called Perez, stretched out his hand, fits the present place: that Israel showed its hand in the work of the Law, and drew it back defiled with the blood of the prophets and of the Savior himself. But afterward the people of the Gentiles broke forth, on whose account it is said to have been destroyed many times, and the middle wall which had stood between the Jews and the Gentiles to have been broken down, so that there might be one flock and one shepherd, and glory and honor and peace to everyone who works good, to the Jew first and to the Greek.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.3.19-20":["What then? The Law was set down because of transgressions, until the seed should come to whom it had been promised, ordained through angels in the hand of a mediator. Now a mediator is not of one, but God is one.\n\nSince, while the promise that had been made to Abraham still stood, the Law given afterwards through Moses seemed to have been brought in to no purpose, he explains why it was given: because of transgressions, he says. For after the offense of the people in the desert, after the worship of the calf and the murmuring against the Lord, the Law followed to forbid transgressions. \"For the law is not set down for the righteous man, but for the unjust and the unsubmissive, for the ungodly and sinners\" (1 Tim. 1:9). And, to go back further: after the idolatry to which they had been enslaved in Egypt, so that they forgot the God of their fathers and afterwards said, \"These are your gods, Israel, who brought you out of the land of Egypt\", the rite of worshipping God and the punishment of offenders were ordained in the hand of the mediator Christ Jesus, because all things were made through him, and without him nothing was made: not only heaven, earth, sea and all the things that we see, but also those things that were laid through Moses upon a hard people as the yoke of the Law (John 1). It is also written to Timothy: \"For there is one God, and one mediator also of God and men, the man Christ Jesus\" (1 Tim. 2:5). After the mediator of God and men, the man Christ Jesus, deigned for our salvation to be born from the Virgin's womb, he was called the go-between (sequester). But before he took up a human body, while he was with the Father in the beginning as God the Word, toward all the saints to whom the word of God came, namely Enoch, Noah, Abraham, Isaac and Jacob, and afterwards Moses and all the Prophets whom Scripture records, he is called only mediator, without the addition of the man, whom he had not yet assumed. And when he says, the Law ordained through angels, he wants this to be understood: that throughout the Old Testament, where an angel is first reported to have been seen and is afterwards introduced as if God were speaking, an angel indeed, whichever one of the many ministers it may be, was truly seen, but in him the mediator speaks, who says: \"I am the God of Abraham, the God of Isaac, and the God of Jacob\" (Exod. 3:6). Nor is it surprising if God speaks in angels, since God also speaks in the prophets through the angels who are in men, as Zechariah says: \"And the angel who spoke in me said\" (Zech. 2:3); and next adds: \"Thus says the Lord almighty\". For the angel who had been said to be in the prophet did not dare to say in his own person: \"Thus says the Lord almighty\". By the hand of the mediator we ought to understand his power and might. Though according to God he is himself one with the Father, according to the office of mediator he is understood as other than he.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.3.19":["But since the order of the reading is confused and thrown into disorder by a hyperbaton, it seems to us that it should be restored thus: The Law was set down through angels in the hand of a mediator, ordained through angels because of transgressions, until the seed should come to whom the promise had been made. Now there is no doubt that the seed signifies Christ, who is shown by the opening of Matthew also to be the son of Abraham, as Scripture relates: \"The book of the generation of Jesus Christ, the son of David, the son of Abraham\".\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.3.21":["Is the Law then against the promises of God? God forbid. For if a Law had been given which could give life, righteousness would truly be from the Law. But Scripture has shut up all things under sin, that the promise by the faith of Jesus Christ might be given to those who believe. But before faith came, we were kept under the law, shut up unto that faith which was to be revealed.\n\nJust as the mediator of God and men stood in the middle between the one who gave the Law and the one who received it, so the Law itself, which was given after the promise, crept in as something in the middle between the promise and its fulfillment. It must not be thought to exclude the promise on the ground that, having followed afterwards, it seems to abolish that which came before; rather, from the fact that it could not give life, nor bestow what the first promise had pledged, it is manifest that it was given for the safekeeping of the promise, not for its overthrow. For if a Law had been given which could furnish life, and deliver what the promise had pledged, then truly the promise would be thought to have been excluded through the Law.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.3.22-23":["But now, having been laid down because of transgressions, as we said above, it rather convicts as sinners those to whom, after the promise, it was given as a custody and, so to speak, as a prison, so that, since through the freedom of their will they had been unwilling to await the promise in innocence, they might be held fast by the bonds of the Law and brought into the slavery of the commandments, and so be kept against the coming of the faith that was to be in Christ, which would bring the promise to its fulfillment.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.3.22":["Nor indeed should it be supposed that Scripture is the author of sin because it is said to have shut up all things under sin, since the commandment which is enjoined by right rather shows and convicts sin than is the cause of sin. So too a judge is not the author of a crime by binding wicked men: rather he shuts them up and, by the authority of his sentence, pronounces them guilty, so that afterwards, if he so wills, the sovereign may by his indulgence absolve those who are liable to punishment.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.3.24-26":["And so the law of Moses too was set over a wanton people after the likeness of a rather severe pedagogue, to guard them and to prepare them for the faith to come; and after this came, and we believed in Christ, we are no longer under a pedagogue, the guardian and the trustee depart from us, and, entering upon the lawful time of our age, we are called true sons of God, to whom not the Law, now abolished, but mother Faith, which is in Christ Jesus, begets us.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.3.24":["Therefore the Law was our pedagogue in Christ, that we might be justified by faith. But when faith came, we are no longer under a pedagogue. For you are all sons of God through faith, which is in Christ Jesus.\n\nA pedagogue is assigned to little children, so that a wanton age may be reined in and hearts prone to vices may be held in check, while tender infancy is trained in studies and, restrained by fear of punishment, is prepared for the greater disciplines of philosophy and of governing the commonwealth. Yet the pedagogue is not the master and father, nor does the one who is being trained expect the inheritance and the knowledge of the pedagogue; rather, the pedagogue guards another man's son, and is to withdraw from him once he has arrived at the lawful time for taking up the inheritance. Indeed, the very name of pedagogue signifies this, and it is compounded from the fact that he leads (agat) boys, that is, conducts them.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.3.25":["But if anyone, after the time of his age has been completed, when he is now called heir and free and son, should wish to be under a pedagogue, let him know that he cannot live by the laws of a little child. For where now can that be fulfilled: \"Three times in the year all your males shall appear in the sight of the Lord your God\" (Exod. 23:17), now that Jerusalem has been overthrown and the temple scattered even to ashes? Where are the peace offerings and the victims for sin? Where is the eternal fire of the burnt offerings, after the likeness of the heavenly stars, now that the altar has been utterly destroyed? And what punishment will be able to be decreed for the guilty, when Scripture says: \"You shall take away the evil from the midst of you\" (Deut. 13:5), while the Jews are in servitude and the Romans reign? And so it will come about that they live neither under a father nor under a pedagogue: since the Law cannot be fulfilled after faith has succeeded it, and faith, while the Law as pedagogue is sought after, is not held.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.3.27":["For as many of you as have been baptized in Christ have put on Christ. There is neither Jew nor Greek. There is neither slave nor free. There is neither male nor female. For you are all one in Christ Jesus.\n\nHe shows how we are born sons of God through the faith which is in Christ Jesus, saying: For as many of you as have been baptized in Christ have put on Christ. That Christ is a garment, moreover, is proved not only from the present passage but also from another, where the same Paul exhorts: \"Put on the Lord Jesus Christ\" (Rom. 13:14). If, therefore, those who have been baptized in Christ have put on Christ, it is plain that those who have not put on Christ were not baptized in Christ. For it was to those who were thought to be believers and to have obtained the baptism of Christ that it was said: \"Put on the Lord Jesus Christ\". If anyone receives only the bodily washing of water, the one that is seen with the eyes of the flesh, he has not put on the Lord Jesus Christ. For Simon too, the one in the Acts of the Apostles, had received the washing of water; but because he did not have the Holy Spirit, he had not put on Christ (Acts 8). And heretics or hypocrites, and those who live sordidly, seem indeed to receive baptism, but I do not know whether they have the garment of Christ. Let us therefore take care lest perhaps someone be found among us too who, from the fact that he does not have the garment of Christ, is shown not to have been baptized in Christ.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.3.28":["But when someone has once put on Christ and, cast into the flame, has glowed white with the ardor of the Holy Spirit, one cannot tell whether he is gold or silver. So long as the heat holds the mass in this way, there is a single fiery color, and every difference of race, condition and bodies is taken away by a garment of this kind. For there is neither Jew nor Greek. By \"Greek\" we ought to understand Gentile, because Ἕλλην signifies both a Greek and a heathen. Nor is the Jew better for being circumcised, nor the Gentile worse for being uncircumcised; rather, it is according to the quality of their faith that either the Jew or the Greek is better or worse. Slave also and free are separated not by condition but by faith, since a slave can be better than a free man, and a free man can surpass a slave in the quality of his faith. Male and female likewise are separated by the strength and weakness of their bodies. But faith is reckoned according to the devotion of the mind, and it often happens both that a woman becomes the cause of salvation for a man and that a man goes before a woman in religion. Since, then, this is how the matter stands, and every difference of race, condition and bodies is taken away by the baptism of Christ and by his garment, we are all one in Christ Jesus: so that, as the Father and the Son are one in each other, so we too may be one in them.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.3.29":["But if you are Christ's, then you are Abraham's seed, heirs according to the promise.\n\nSince the promises were made to Abraham and to his seed, which is Christ Jesus, it follows that those who are sons of Christ, that is, his seed, are also called the seed of Abraham, whose seed they are from his seed. But whenever our Lord Jesus is named the seed of Abraham, it is to be understood bodily, in that he is begotten of his stock. Whenever, however, it is we who are so named, we who, having received the Savior's word, have believed in him and have taken up the nobility of the lineage of Abraham, to whom the promise was made, then we ought to take it spiritually as the seed of faith and of preaching. Next, this too must be considered: that when he speaks of the Lord, \"Now to Abraham were the promises spoken, and to his seed\", that is, to Christ Jesus, he puts \"promises\" in the plural; but when it concerns those who through Christ are the seed of Abraham, it is called \"promise\" in the singular, as in the present place: Then you are Abraham's seed, heirs according to the promise. For it was fitting that what was spoken in the plural in the one Christ should be put in the singular among many men. There follows:\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.4.1-2":["Now I say, as long as the heir is a little child, he differs nothing from a slave, though he is Lord of all, but he is under guardians and stewards until the time appointed beforehand by the father.\n\nThis little heir, who differs nothing from a slave though he is Lord of all, but is under guardians and stewards until the time appointed beforehand by the father, signifies the whole human race up to the coming of Christ, and, to say more, up to the consummation of the world. For just as all die in Adam, the first-formed man, though not yet born, so also all, even those who were born before the coming of Christ, are made alive in the second Adam. And so it comes about both that we served the Law in the fathers, and that they are saved by grace in the sons. This understanding accords with the Catholic Church, which asserts one providence of both the Old and the New Testament, and does not separate in time those whom it has joined in condition. We are all built upon the foundation of the apostles and prophets, Jesus Christ our Lord, the cornerstone, holding us together, who made both one, and, breaking down the middle wall, destroyed in his flesh the enmity of both peoples, and changed the difficulty of the ancient Law by the integrity of the Gospel doctrines (Eph. 2:20). Truly in Christ we are all one bread, and we two have agreed upon earth. And just as we are founded upon the prophets, so too the patriarchs have stood upon the foundation of the apostles. Now the guardians and stewards can also be taken to be the prophets, by whose words we were daily instructed toward the coming of the Savior, just as the Law of Moses was discussed above as a pedagogue; and the angels of the little ones, who daily see the face of the Father and intercede for them. Of these it was said: \"He will send the angels of the Lord round about those who fear him, and will deliver them\" (Ps. 33:8). The priests and princes too can be understood, who then ruled over the people and are now held to provide a pattern. And rightly are those said to be under guardians and stewards who, having the spirit of fear, have not yet deserved to receive the spirit of freedom and adoption. For the age of infancy trembles at sins, fears the pedagogue, and does not trust that it is free, although by nature it is mistress. And according to both understandings, by which we have said that the guardians and stewards are either the prophets or the angels, this little one is under stewards and guardians for as long as it takes him to complete the lawful time of the perfect man. Now just as the lawful time under the Roman laws is bounded by a span of twenty-five years, so for the perfection of the human race the coming of Christ is reckoned as that time. As soon as he has come, and we have all grown up into the perfect man, the pedagogue and guardian depart from us. Then we shall fully enjoy the authority of the Lord and the possession of the inheritance, in which we, born earlier, were in a manner regarded as strangers.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.4.3":["So we also, when we were little children, were in bondage under the elements of this world.\n\nHe has called the elements of the world the same ones whom above he had called guardians and stewards: because, set first under these very overseers, since we could not yet grasp the coming of the Son of God to us, we were being instructed in the meantime. Some think that they are angels who preside over the four elements of the world, namely earth, water, fire and air, and that it is necessary that, before anyone believes in Christ, he be governed by those as his overseers. Many think that heaven and earth, and the things that are within them, are called the elements of the world: because, namely, the wise men of Greece, and the barbarian nations, and the Romans, the cesspool of all superstitions, worship the sun, the moon, the seas, and the gods of the forests and the mountains; from which we are set free when Christ has come, understanding that they are creatures, not divinities. Others interpret the elements of the world as the Law of Moses and the utterances of the prophets: because through these, as through the beginnings and first rudiments of letters, we take up the fear of God, which is the beginning of wisdom. Finally, to those who ought already to have been perfect, and who, having neglected the truth, still clung to the first principles of instruction, the Apostle writes in the Epistle to the Hebrews: \"For when by reason of the time you ought to be teachers, you have need again to be taught what are the elements of the beginning of the words of God\" (Heb. 5:12). On the contrary, it can be objected to us that Paul the apostle, writing to the Colossians, has named other things the elements of the world: \"See\", he says, \"that no one plunder you through philosophy and empty deceit, according to the tradition of men, according to the elements of the world, and not according to Christ\" (Col. 2:8). But from what he added, \"according to the tradition of men, and empty deceit\", he shows that the same elements are not named to the Colossians and to the Galatians. For from these elements, after the fullness of time has come, we are redeemed, and, advancing to greater things, we receive the adoption of sons. But of those nothing of the kind is said as something that follows; rather, the elements are taken simply for letters. Therefore, as we have said, the Law of Moses and the prophets can be taken for the elements of letters, because through them syllables and names are joined, and they are learned not so much for their own usefulness as for that of something else: so that we may be able to read connected discourse, in which the sense and the order of the words, rather than the first principles of the letters, are considered. And as for our having interpreted the Law and the prophets as the elements of the world, the world is commonly taken for those who are in the world, as the same Paul says: \"God was in Christ, reconciling the world to himself\" (2 Cor. 5:19). And in the Gospel: \"And the world was made through him, and the world does not receive him\" (John 1:10). Some, moreover, range more freely in this matter: so that, since the Law has a shadow of good things to come, they inquire whether in another world, of which the Savior says: \"I am not of this world\" (John 8:23), we are first little children and, set under the elements of the beginnings, advance little by little to the highest point, and receive again the place of adoption which we once lost.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.4.4-5":["But when the fullness of time came, God sent his Son, made of a woman, made under the Law, that he might redeem those who were under the Law, that we might receive back the adoption of sons.\n\nPay careful attention to the fact that he did not say made through a woman, as Marcion and the other heresies would have it, which pretend that the flesh of Christ was merely apparent, but of a woman, so that he may be believed to have been born not through her but of her. And as for his calling the holy and blessed Mother of the Lord woman (mulierem), not Virgin (Virginem), this same thing is written also in the Gospel κατὰ Ματθαῖον (according to Matthew), where she is called the wife of Joseph (Luke 2), and where she is rebuked by the Lord himself as a woman (John 2). For it was not necessary always to say Virgin, as if cautiously and timidly, since woman (mulier) signifies the sex rather than union with a man; and according to the sense of the Greek language, γυνή can be translated as either wife or woman. But to pass over all this: just as he was made under the law that he might redeem those who were under the law, so for the sake of those who had been born of a woman he willed to be born of a woman. For this is also why he received baptism in the stream of the Jordan as if he were a penitent, though he was free from sins: to teach the rest that they must be cleansed through baptism and be begotten as sons by the new adoption of the Spirit. John the Baptist, by no means understanding this, tried to keep him from coming to the washing, saying: \"I ought to be baptized by you\" (Matt. 3:14). And at once the mystery is taught: \"Permit it now; for thus it befits us to fulfill all righteousness\", so that he who had come for the salvation of men should not pass over anything of the manner of life of men. Someone may ask and say: If he was made under the Law for this reason, that he might redeem those who were under the Law (since, evidently, it would have been impossible for those who were under the Law to be redeemed unless he himself had been made under the Law), then either he was made without the Law, that he might redeem those who were not under the Law; or, if he himself was not made without the Law, he does not redeem those who had not been under the Law. But if it was possible for those who were without the Law to be redeemed in such a way that he himself did not become without the Law, then he was made under the Law superfluously in order to redeem those who were under the Law. One will resolve this question briefly if one uses that example: \"and he was reckoned with those who were without the Law\". For although in the Latin manuscripts, owing to the simplicity of the translators, it has been badly rendered: \"And he was reckoned with the iniquitous\" (Luke 22:37), yet one must know that among the Greeks ἄνομον, which is written here, means one thing, and ἄδικον, which is found in the Latin volumes, another. For ἄνομος is said of one who is without law and is bound by no right, whereas ἄδικος means iniquitous, or unjust. Hence the Apostle himself too, in another place: \"since I was not\", he says, \"without the Law of God, but was in the Law of Christ\" (1 Cor. 9); and certainly in this testimony also ἄνομος is written in the Greek; and he who translated well here could have translated the same word likewise there too, had the ambiguity not deceived him.\n— Commentary on Galatians","But what shall we do about that passage of Paul, where he says: \"But when the fullness of time came, God sent his Son, made of a woman, made under the Law, that he might redeem those who were under the Law\" (above)? For if Christ was under the Law, and not as if under the Law, that whole preceding discussion will become empty. But this objection too will be resolved from its own passage. For he who was made under the Law for this reason, that he might redeem those who were under the Law, surely, since he was free from the Law, subjected himself to the Law of his own will; and he was far freer than Paul, who testified that he had been not under the Law but as if under the Law. And just as he went down into the mire and abyss of death for our sake, who were praying and saying: \"Who will deliver me from the body of this death\" (Rom. 7:24)? so too he willed to be born of a woman and to be under the Law, that he might save those who had been born of a woman and under the Law. And certainly he was not born of a woman, that is, of a married woman, but of a virgin. But the virgin was called a woman by a loose use of the word, on account of those who did not know that she had been a virgin. So, then, just as \"woman\" was put for \"virgin\" on account of those who thought that holy Mary had a husband, so, on account of those who supposed that Christ was under the Law, not knowing that to those who were under the Law he had become as if under the Law, he too is said to have been made under the Law.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.4.5":["Another, however, will look more keenly at the very word might redeem, and will say that those are signified as redeemed who were first on God's side and afterwards ceased to be so, while those who had not been under the Law are not so much redeemed as bought. Hence to the Corinthians too, among whom fornication was heard of, and such fornication as is not even among the Gentiles (ibid. 5), it is written: \"You have been bought at a price\", not redeemed; for they had not been under the Law. We therefore receive back the adoption of the sons of God, and, redeemed by Christ, we cease to be under the slavery of the elements of the world and the power of guardians. And just as we have shown what difference there is between redeeming (redimere) and buying (emere), so let us consider what difference there is between receiving (accipere) and receiving back (recipere) the adoption of sons.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.4.6":["And because you are sons of God, God sent the spirit of his Son into our hearts, crying, Abba, Father.\n\nThe apostle Paul plainly names three spirits: the Spirit of the Son of God, as in the present passage, \"God sent the Spirit of the Son into our hearts\" (Rom. 8:14); and the Spirit of God, as in that passage: \"As many as are led by the Spirit of God, these are sons of God\"; and the Holy Spirit, as there: \"Your bodies are a temple of the Holy Spirit, who is in you\" (1 Cor. 6:19). That the Holy Spirit is other than the Son of God is plainly proved in the Gospel as well: \"Whoever speaks a word against the Son of man, it will be forgiven him. But whoever speaks against the Holy Spirit, it will not be forgiven him, neither here nor in the age to come\" (Luke 12:10). I say this for the following reason: because many, through ignorance of the Scriptures (as Firmianus also does in the eighth book of his Letters to Demetrianus), assert that the Holy Spirit is often named the Father, often the Son. And although we clearly believe in the Trinity, they take away the third Person and will have him to be not a substance but a name. But not to be long (for it is a commentary, not a dialogue, that is being written), I shall show briefly from the fiftieth psalm that three spirits are named, the prophet saying: \"Create a clean heart in me, O God, and renew a right spirit in my inward parts. Do not cast me away from your face, and do not take your Holy Spirit from me. Restore to me the joy of your salvation, and strengthen me with a princely spirit\". By the princely spirit he means the Father, because the Son is from the Father, and not the Father from the Son. But by the right spirit, the spirit of truth and righteousness, he signifies Christ the Lord: \"For the Father has given all judgment to the Son\" (John 5:22), as David says: \"O God, give your judgment to the king, and your power to the king's son\" (Ps. 71:1). Further, he calls the Holy Spirit by his open name. And though these differ in names and persons, they are joined in substance and nature; and, because of the fellowship of nature, the same Spirit is said without distinction to be now of the Father, now of the Son. Now the argument by which he is trying to assert that we are no longer under the Law but under the grace of the Lord Jesus, he concludes with an ending of this kind. Above he had said, \"that we might receive the adoption of sons\"; now he proves that we are sons of God from the spirit which we have within us. For we should never dare, he says, to say: \"Our Father, who are in heaven, hallowed be your name\", except from the consciousness of the Spirit dwelling in us and crying with a great voice of understanding and of doctrines: \"Abba, Father\" (Rom. 8:15). Abba is Hebrew, meaning the very same thing as Father. And Scripture keeps this custom in many places, setting a Hebrew word together with its interpretation: Bartimaeus, \"son of Timaeus\"; Asher, \"riches\"; Tabitha, \"Dorcas\" (Acts 9); and in Genesis, Mesech, \"home-born slave\" (Gen. 15), and other things like these. But since Abba means Father in the Hebrew and Syriac tongue, and our Lord in the Gospel commands that no one is to be called father except God (Matt. 23), I do not know by what license in the monasteries we either call others by this name or consent to be called by it ourselves. And certainly he gave this command who had said that one must not swear (Matt. 5). If we do not swear, let us not name anyone father either. If we interpret the saying about a father otherwise, we shall be compelled to think otherwise about swearing as well. It should be noted also that in the Scriptures a cry is to be understood not as the emitting of a loud voice but as the greatness of knowledge and of doctrines. For in Exodus too the Lord answered Moses: \"Why do you cry to me\" (Exod. 14:15)? although no voice of Moses at all had gone before. But Scripture called a cry his heart, pierced with compunction and groaning tearfully for the people. Just as, therefore, he who has the Spirit of the Son of God is a son of God, so conversely he who does not have the spirit of the Son of God cannot be called a son of God.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.4.7":["Therefore he is now not a slave, but a son. And if a son, an heir also through Christ.\n\nHaving, he says, the spirit of the Son of God in you crying, Abba, Father, you have begun to be not slaves but sons. For before, you differed in nothing from a slave, although you were indeed God's by nature; but you lived as little ones under guardians and stewards. But if you are sons, the inheritance is consequently owed to you, so that, just as by receiving the spirit of the Son of God you have been made sons of God, so, changed from slavery into freedom, you may be heirs with the Father's heir, Christ Jesus, who speaks in the psalm in the person of the man he assumed: \"The Lord said to me, You are my Son, today I have begotten you. Ask of me, and I will give you the nations as your inheritance, and the ends of the earth as your possession\" (Ps. 2:7, 8). Now what we say in this place we ought to observe in the other places as well: that the whole human race is being discussed in the singular number. For all of us who believe are one in Christ Jesus, and members of his body, and, brought to a perfect man, we have him as our head, because the head of the man is Christ (1 Cor. 11:3).\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.4.8-9":["But then indeed, not knowing God, you served those who by nature were not gods. But now, knowing God, or rather being known by him, how is it that you turn again to the weak and needy elements, which you wish to serve once more?\n\nHe rebukes the Galatians, whom they had brought over from the worship of idols to faith in the true God: how is it that, having abandoned the idols, which by nature were not gods, and knowing God, or rather being known by him, and having also received the spirit of adoption, they are once more, as if they were little children, and desiring to be under guardians and a pedagogue, going back to the weak and poor elements, which were given in the desert to the people for this reason, because of its weak and poor understanding, since it could not receive and bear greater things.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.4.9":["Now the same elements which he has now called weak and needy he set down above simply as the elements of the world. And where they were called elements of the world, weak and needy was not added there. Here again, where they are called weak, the name \"of the world\", as we said above, is passed over in silence. I think, therefore, that as long as someone is a little child and has not completed the time fixed by the father, so that he can be called son and heir, he is under the elements of the world, that is to say, the Law of Moses. But when, after the freedom owed to the son, he has gone back again to the Law, wishing to be circumcised and to follow the whole letter of the Jewish superstition, then the things which before had been for him only the elements of the world are also called weak and needy beginnings. For they are of so little profit to their worshippers that they cannot even offer them what they had provided before, since Jerusalem, the temple and the altar have been destroyed. Someone may answer and say: If the law and the precepts written in the Law are weak and needy elements, and those who have known God, or rather have been known by him, ought not to observe the Law (lest they begin not so much to worship the God by whom they have been known as to go back to those who by nature are not gods), then either Moses and the prophets observed the Law and did not know God, nor were known by him; or, if they knew God, they did not at all fulfill the commandments of the Law. Either of these is dangerous to say: either that they did not do the things of the Law, and so knew God; or that they did not know God, while they kept the weak and needy elements of the Law. This can indeed be resolved in this way, by saying that, just as Paul became a Jew to the Jews in order to win the Jews (1 Cor. 9), and at Cenchreae cut his hair in fulfillment of a vow (Acts 18), and at Jerusalem in the temple practiced the going barefoot and the shaving of the head, in order to calm the ill will of those who had been instructed about him that he was acting against the Law of Moses and the God of the prophets: so also the holy men did indeed do the things that belonged to the Law, but followed the sense of the Law rather than the letter. They, no less than Abraham, with the veil taken from their face, desired to see the day of Christ, and they saw it and were glad: they became weak to a weak people, in order to win the weak (1 Cor. 9), and to those who were under the Law as though they themselves were under the Law, in order to separate them from the idols to which they had grown accustomed in Egypt. For it is absurd that Moses and the others who conversed with God should have been in such a condition that we do not believe that for them too the time fixed by the Father had come, and that they were redeemed from legal slavery, and obtained the adoption of sons, and received the inheritance with Christ. For whatever the wisdom of God has granted to the whole human race as to one Son, this same it has always bestowed on each of the saints in his own order and dispensation. When we call the Law of Moses weak and needy elements, the heretics find an occasion to disparage the Creator, because he founded the world and established the Law. To them we shall answer what we said above: that the elements are weak and needy for those who go back to them after the grace of the Gospel. But before the time fixed by the Father came, the elements were called not so much weak and needy as elements of the world. In short, before the Gospel of Christ shone out in the whole world, the precepts of the Law had their own brightness; but after the greater light of the grace of the Gospel shone forth, and the sun of righteousness showed itself to the whole world, the light of the stars was hidden and their rays grew dim, so that the Apostle says in another place: \"For what was glorified in this respect was not glorified, because of the surpassing glory\" (2 Cor. 3). This he now says in other words, meaning that the Law of Moses, which before the Gospel was rich and wealthy and splendid, after the coming of Christ, by comparison with him, as being weak and needy, has been diminished and destroyed by him who was greater than Solomon, and the temple, and Jonah. For what is written, \"He must increase, but I must decrease\" (John 3:30), I think was said not so much in the person of John as in the person of the Law, because lesser things always give way to greater, and perfect things are set before beginnings. In another way, however, we shall affirm that the weak and needy elements are the traditions of the Jews and the base understanding according to the letter, which are statutes that are not good and precepts that are not good. For the spiritual understanding of the Law is strong and rich (Ezek. 20), so that either it ought not to be called an element at all, or it is indeed an element by comparison with the age to come and with the life in Christ Jesus, by which the angels and the heavenly powers now live. But compared with the Jewish sense, it should be called not so much an element, that is, a beginning, as perfection. And when he says: But now, knowing God, or rather being known by him, this shows that after the worship of idols the Galatians came to understand God, or rather that they themselves were judged worthy of his knowledge. Not that God, the Creator of all things, is ignorant of anything; but that he is said to know only those who have exchanged error for piety. \"The Lord knows those who are his\" (2 Tim. 2:19). And the Savior in the Gospel: \"I am\", he says, \"the good shepherd, and I know mine, and mine know me\" (John 10:14). On the contrary, to the ungodly: \"I do not know you; depart from me, workers of iniquity\" (Luke 13:27). And to the foolish virgins: \"I do not know who you are\" (Matt. 25:12).\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.4.10-11":["You observe days, and months, and times, and years; I fear you, lest perhaps I have labored in vain among you.\n\nHe who does not worship the Father in spirit and truth does not know the sabbath rest laid up for the saints, of which God speaks: \"If they shall enter into my rest\" (Ps. 94:11); and he does not remember those times of which it is written: \"Remember the days of the age\" (Isa. 46:9). And elsewhere: \"I remembered the ancient days, and I had the eternal years in mind\" (Ps. 76:6). He observes Jewish days, and months, and times, and years. Days, such as those of the sabbath and of the new moon, and from the tenth of the first month to the fourteenth, on which the bodily lamb is kept for sacrifice, and from the fourteenth to the twenty-first of the same month, when unleavened bread is eaten, not that of sincerity and truth, but in the old leaven of the malice and wickedness of the Pharisees. He too who, by the Jewish rite, counts seven weeks after the unleavened bread keeps the days of the Israelite Pentecost; and likewise the blast of trumpets in the seventh month, on the first of the month. Those too who keep the tenth of the same month, the expiation and the fast, and who pitch the booths of the Feast of Tabernacles according to custom, observe Jewish days. And they keep months who observe the first and the seventh month, not understanding the mystery of the truth. They also keep times who, coming to Jerusalem three times every year, think they are fulfilling the precept of the Lord, who says: \"Three times in the year you shall keep a feast day to me: the solemnity of unleavened bread, and the solemnity of the harvest of the firstfruits, and the solemnity of the completion at the going out of the year\" (Exod. 23:14 ff.). And elsewhere: \"Three times in the year your male shall appear in the sight of the Lord your God\" (Exod. 23:17). But as for his saying and years, I think it is said of the seventh year, that of remission, and of the fiftieth, which they call the Jubilee. The Apostle explains this passage more fully to the Colossians, saying: \"Let no one therefore judge you in food, or in drink, or in part of a feast day, or of a new moon, or of sabbaths, which are a shadow of things to come\" (Col. 2:16). He put \"part of a feast day\" here to distinguish it from the perpetual festival, so that we may hold not a brief one and, so to speak, some small particle of the whole body, but the whole span of our life as a perpetual solemnity in Christ. And, to connect the earlier with the later, what he thinks of the law of Moses and of superfluous fussiness about foods in this same Epistle he at once adds, saying: \"For if you have died with Christ from the elements of this world, why do you still, as though living in this world, lay down decrees? Do not touch, nor taste, nor handle, which things all are for destruction by the very use, according to the precepts and doctrines of men\". Someone may say: If it is not permitted to observe days, and months, and times, and years, we too incur a similar charge when we observe the fourth day of the week, and the Preparation, and the Lord's day, and the fast of Lent, and the festival of the Pasch, and the gladness of Pentecost, and the various times appointed, according to the variety of regions, in honor of the martyrs. He who answers this simply will say that the days of the Jewish observance are not the same as ours. For we do not celebrate a pasch of unleavened bread, but of the resurrection and of the cross. Nor do we number seven weeks at Pentecost after the custom of Israel, but we venerate the coming of the Holy Spirit. And lest a disorderly gathering of the people should diminish faith in Christ, for that reason certain days have been appointed, so that we might all come together into one place. Not that the day on which we assemble is more festive, but that, on whatever day we are to assemble, a greater joy may arise from the sight of one another. He, however, who tries to answer the opposing question more acutely affirms this: that all days are equal, and that Christ is not crucified only on the Preparation and does not rise only on the Lord's day, but that it is always the holy day of the resurrection and always one may feed on the Lord's flesh; and that fasts and assemblies on days in between were appointed by prudent men on account of those who are more free for the world than for God, and who cannot, or rather will not, gather in the church through the whole time of their life and, before their human business, offer to God the sacrifice of their prayers. For how many are there who always practice even these few things that have been appointed, the times either of praying or of fasting? And so, just as it is permitted to us either to fast always or always to pray, and, rejoicing, to celebrate the Lord's day unceasingly, having received the Lord's body, it is not likewise lawful for the Jews to sacrifice the lamb at all times, to keep Pentecost, to pitch tabernacles, to fast daily.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.4.11":["Quite cautiously, however, has he tempered his words between the authority of the Apostle and the gentleness of a holy man, adding: I fear you, lest perhaps I have labored in vain among you. For had he wished to condemn them abruptly, he would certainly have said: I fear you: for I have labored in vain among you. But now, seeing that they have a zeal for God, but not according to knowledge, he neither wholly despaired of the salvation of those who had been deceived by a pious error, nor, on the other hand, did he leave them unreproved, lest he should give both to them an occasion of persevering in error and to the rest likewise an occasion of erring. But I fear you, he set down in place of I fear for you. A teacher labors in vain when he himself calls his disciples on to greater things, and they, slipping back, roll down again to lesser and lowly things.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.4.12-13":["Brothers, I beseech you, you have done me no injury. And you know that through infirmity of the flesh I preached the gospel to you some time ago.\n\nJoin what follows to the preceding sentence; and to make this clearer, let the order be the one we construct: I beseech you, brothers, be as I am, because I also am as you are. Like this is that saying: \"We entreat on behalf of Christ, be reconciled to God\" (2 Cor. 5:20). And elsewhere too: \"I beseech that first of all supplications, prayers, petitions, thanksgivings be made\" (1 Tim. 2:1). So also the words of Peter, who says: \"The elders among you I entreat, who am myself a fellow elder and a witness of the sufferings of Christ\" (1 Pet. 5:1). These words indeed both summon us to humility and strike down the haughty brow of bishops who, as though set upon some lofty watchtower, scarcely deign to look at mortals and to speak to their fellow slaves. Let them learn from the Apostle that the erring and unwise Galatians are called brothers. Let them learn from him who, after rebuke, speaks gentle words: \"I beseech you\" (1 Cor. 11). And what he beseeches is this, that they be imitators of him, as he himself is of Christ; or rather, to keep to the present passage, what he asks is nothing great: that just as he himself for their sake was made lesser from greater, so they should climb up from lesser things to greater. In nothing, he says, have you injured me. A disciple injures his master if through his own negligence he wastes his precepts and his labor. The Galatians had not injured the Apostle, since up to the present time they were keeping his Gospel and his commandments. Or certainly thus: When I first announced the Gospel to you, and on account of the infirmity of your flesh, because you could not receive the greater mysteries, I preached to you as to little children, and pretended that I myself was weak, so that I might gain you who were weak: did you not receive me as an angel, as Christ Jesus? Since, then, you did me no injury in anything at that time, and thought me, humble and cast down for your sake, like the Son of God, how is it that, when I summon you to greater things, I am injured by you, through the waste of my labor, and that you now mourn, with the work made void, that dispensation by which I had pretended to be a little one?\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.4.12":["Be as I also am, because I also am as you.\n\nWhat he says is this: just as I became weak to you who were weak, and could not speak to you as to spiritual men but as to carnal men and little ones in Christ, and, because you were not yet able to feed on solid food, gave you only the milk of the Gospel to drink, not wishing you to remain forever in the age of infancy but to lead you little by little on to adolescence and youth, so that you might be able to take solid food: so you too ought to be as I also am, namely to have a taste for more perfect things and, leaving milk behind, to pass on to stronger foods and to greater pastures. And he says this as an imitator of the Savior, who did not think it robbery to be equal to God, but emptied himself, taking the form of a slave; and was found in habit as a man, so that we might become gods from men and no longer die; but, rising together with Christ, might be called his friends and brothers, and so that the disciple might be as the teacher, and the slave as the master. But it can also be understood thus: I beseech you, he says, brothers, that, despising the Jewish observance of days, months, seasons and years, which are shadows of things to come, you imitate me, who, having lived without reproach in the Law, counted all things as refuse and rubbish, that I might gain Christ. For I too was as you now are, when I was held bound by the same observances, and, because the Church of Christ did not do these things, I persecuted it and laid it waste.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.4.13":["Now Paul announces the Gospel to the Galatians through the infirmity of the flesh, not his own but that of his hearers, who could not subject the flesh to the word of God, but, being as it were fleshly, received nothing of spiritual understanding. To make this more evident, let us set out an example. He teaches through the infirmity of the flesh who says: \"If they do not contain themselves, let them marry\". And: \"A woman, if her husband has died, is free: let her marry whom she will, only in the Lord\" (1 Cor. 7:9, 39). But he by no means teaches through the infirmity of the flesh when he records these things: \"You are loosed from a wife: do not seek a wife\". And: \"It is time that those who have wives be as though they had none\" (ibid. 27, 29). For some precepts are given to the spiritual, others to the fleshly. And it is one thing that is commanded by way of command, another by way of indulgence.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.4.14":["And your temptation, which was in my flesh, you did not despise nor reject; but you received me as an angel of God, as Christ Jesus.\n\nAn obscure passage, and one to be attended to more keenly. I, he says, long ago preached the Gospel to you as to little ones and sucklings, because of the weakness of your flesh, beginning from the lesser things and (so to speak) almost stammering among you. This dispensation and pretense of a weak preaching was indeed my management; but it was your temptation, to see whether those things would please you and seem great which in their own condition were lesser, and were brought forth by me as if lowly. And you, taking them not as small but as great, admired them so much that you welcomed me, who spoke them, as an angel and, to say more, as the Son of God. This temptation of yours, then, by which I was tempting you in the carnal proclamation of my discourse, was not despised, nor was it held cheap; but it had more dignity than I reckoned. This passage can also be explained thus: when I came to you, I did not come in the word of wisdom, but as a lowly and despised man, bringing nothing great: the Crucified. When, therefore, you saw me placed in a body subject to infirmities, promising the heavenly kingdoms, you did not laugh at me, nor did you reckon me worthy of contempt: for you understood that the lowliness of my flesh and the meanness of my very bearing came about for your temptation, namely, whether you would despise him who was thought pitiable by unbelievers; but on the contrary you welcomed that lowly, mean and despised man as an angel, and more than an angel. Or surely we can suspect that the Apostle, at the time when he first came to the Galatians, was sick; and that, though held back by some infirmity of his poor body, he nevertheless did not cease nor hold back his voice in silence from preaching the Gospel he had begun. For they relate that he often suffered a most severe pain of the head, and that this is the angel of Satan who was set upon him to buffet him in the flesh, lest he be lifted up. This infirmity and this sickness of the body was a temptation for those to whom the Gospel was proclaimed: whether they would despise him who promised sublime things, whom they saw subject to the sicknesses of the body. It can also be said that at the beginning of his coming to the Galatians he endured insults and persecutions and blows on his body from those who opposed the Gospel; and that this was the very greatest temptation for the Galatians, seeing the Apostle of Christ beaten. But when he says, as an angel, as Christ Jesus you welcomed me, he both shows that Christ is greater than an angel, Christ whom the Psalmist had sung as lesser according to the dispensation of the body, saying: \"You have made him a little less than the angels\" (Ps. 8:6); and he demonstrates that his own words at the beginning had such force that they were thought to be those of an angel and of Christ.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.4.15-16":["Where then is your blessedness? For I bear you witness that, if it could have been done, you would have plucked out your eyes and given them to me. Have I then become your enemy, telling you the truth?\n\nBlessed is he who walks in the way of the virtues, but only if he arrives all the way to the virtues. Nor does it profit to have withdrawn from vices unless you lay hold of the best things. For in good pursuits it is not so much the beginnings that are to be praised as the end. For just as in the vineyard there are many stages of the grape up to the press: first the vine must bud into leafy shoots and hold out hope in its blossoms; then, once the flower has fallen, the form of the future cluster is shaped, and little by little the grape, swelling, comes to its birth, so that, pressed in the winepresses, it may sweat out sweet must. So also in teaching there are single advances of blessedness: that someone hears the word of God, that he conceives it, that it grows in the womb of his soul and comes all the way to birth; that, when he has brought it forth, he nourishes it with milk and leads it through infancy, boyhood, adolescence and youth all the way to the perfect man. Since, then, the single stages, as we have said, have their blessedness according to their advances, if the end, and, so to speak, the finishing hand is lacking to the work, all the labor will become void; and it will be said: Where then is your blessedness? Although, he says, at the time when you received the Gospel according to the flesh I called you blessed because you were fervent in the beginnings, yet now, because I do not see the roof set upon the building, and the foundations hardly laid at all, I am compelled to say: Where then is your blessedness, for which, judging you blessed, I used to praise you before? For truly I myself also confess that you so loved me in the beginning, when I preached lowly things to you and was beset by persecutions, that, if it could have been done (and what he says is to be taken as hyperbole), you would have plucked out your eyes and given them to me, so that I might see more with the lights of all of you. For you wished to be blind yourselves through an ineffable love toward me, so that the light of the Gospel might rise more fully in my heart; you wished my gain to grow by your losses: and this at that time when, whether because of the weakness of your flesh I announced small and lowly things to you as to little ones and sucklings, or because of the injuries of my own flesh, I did not seem worthy of belief.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.4.16":["But now, because I have begun to call you on from the elements and syllables and the reading of boys to greater studies, so that you may hold books in your hands and may learn words full of learning and of meanings, you kick back, you are angry, the perfection of teachings seems burdensome to you; and you are changed to other feelings to such a degree that me, whom you had received as an angel and as Christ, to whom you wished to hand over your eyes, you now hold as an enemy, because I announce to you the full truth. And he has elegantly ended the sentence, saying: Have I then become your enemy, telling you the truth? in order to show that the beginnings of the preaching were not so much the truth as the shadow and image of the truth. Like this is that famous sentence of the poet among the Romans (Terence, Andria 1.1): Compliance begets friends, truth hatred. But see how much better the one here is than that one; for the Apostle tempered this sentence for those whom he had called foolish, whom he had named little ones, and made it particular, since he directed it properly to the persons and to the Galatians. But that one, declaring it both general and as holding so among all men, erred badly. For compliance, by which he thought friends are made, once truth is taken away, is not so much compliance as flattery and fawning; and it is plain that these ought to be called hidden enmities rather than friendships. At the same time this too must be considered, that today also, so long as we explain the Scriptures according to the letter to little ones and sucklings, and to those in whose hearts Christ never grows up nor advances in age and wisdom and grace with God and men, we are praised, looked up to, held in admiration. But when we have begun a little to call them on to pass over to greater things, from being our heralds they become enemies, and they would rather follow the Jews than the apostles, who, withdrawing from the teaching and traditions of the Pharisees, entered into Christ himself, the propitiatory, and the perfection of the Law; nor do they deign to accept the divine word, which bids the teachers of the Church to climb to loftier doctrines, to raise the voice with all their strength, and not to fear the clamor of the little ones barking around them, saying: \"Go up on a lofty mountain, you who bring good tidings to Sion. Lift up your voice in strength, you who bring good tidings to Jerusalem. Lift it up, do not fear\" (Isa. 40:9).\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.4.17-18":["They emulate you not well: but they wish to shut you out, that you may emulate them. But emulate the good in a good thing always: and not only when I am present with you.\n\nThey emulate well who, when they see graces, gifts and powers in certain people, themselves desire to be such: and they strive to imitate the faith, life and diligence of those people, through which they merited those things, so that they too may be able to attain the things that are good and worthy of emulation. Of these the Apostle also says: \"Emulate spiritual things: but rather that you may prophesy\". And next: \"So you also, since you are emulators of spiritual things, seek to abound for the edification of the Church\". And again: \"Therefore, brothers, emulate to prophesy, and do not forbid to speak in tongues\". But they emulate not well who do not so much desire to be better themselves, by imitating those who are worthy of emulation, as wish to make those very people worse, and to drag them backward by a perverse emulation. Let it be said by way of example: someone is a Christian; he reads Moses and the prophets; he knows that all things went before for that people in shadow and in image, but were written for our sake, upon whom the ends of the ages have come down. He understands circumcision not so much of the foreskin as of the ears and of the heart. He has risen with Christ: he seeks the things that are above. He has been freed from the burden and slavery of the Law, which commands: touch not, taste not, defile not. If anyone should wish to persuade this man by the words of the Scriptures to receive the things that are written not through tropology but according to the killing letter, so that he becomes a Jew outwardly, not inwardly, that person emulates him not well: rather he hastens to drag back one who is advancing at a swift pace toward greater things, so that the other may instead emulate him who is going backward; or at the least he keeps him from advancing much further. He speaks, therefore, to the Galatians, who had been induced by the champions of the Law to imitate them, when those men ought rather to have imitated the Galatians.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.4.17":["This is said of that zeal and perverse emulation of which it is also said elsewhere: \"Do not emulate among the evildoers\" (Ps. 36:1); and here: They emulate you not well. But we find also another zeal, with which the sons of Jacob were zealous against their brother Joseph (Gen. 37 ff.), and Miriam and Aaron against Moses, the friend of the Lord (Num. 12). For neither the former nor the latter were stirred to zeal in order to become better than Joseph and Moses, but because they grieved that those men were better. This zeal is a near neighbor of envy. It would be a long task if I wished to bring forth from the treasury of the Scriptures all the kinds of zeal, whether good or bad. We read of the good zeal of Phinehas (Num. 25), of Elijah (1 Kings 19), of Mattathias (1 Macc. 2), and of the apostle Judas (but not the traitor), who on account of the remarkable power of zeal in him received also the name of Zealot (Acts 1). And bad zeal, as that of Cain against Abel (Gen. 4), and of others against others. And the zeal of a husband, of which it is written: \"And the spirit of zeal come upon him\" (Num. 5). Unless perhaps this zeal is intermediate, and can be taken neither in a good nor in a bad sense, but, standing between the two, is rather called jealousy. Otherwise: those who were of the circumcision, seeing that the Galatians from the Gentiles abounded in the powers of the Holy Spirit, while they themselves did not speak in tongues, did not have gifts of healings, nor the grace of prophecy, were spurred on by the goads of zeal and desired to bring them over to the burdens of the Law, so that they too might begin to become like them.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.4.18":["And since it is natural that the greater should be made from the lesser, not the lesser from the greater, he also says: Emulate the good in a good thing, that is, do not imitate the champions of Jewish observance, but imitate the things that are good. For just as one who imitates someone's riches, power and rank emulates not so much good things as things that ought to be shunned, so you, on the contrary, emulate the good in a good thing, seeking spiritual things rather than carnal ones, so that it is not they who teach you to be Jews, but you who teach them to be Christians. And do this always, so that with persevering step you may be able to reach the end of the good work. For indeed you emulated the good in a good thing formerly, when I was with you; but after I departed from you, you lost everything I had handed on to you, drawn away from a sure anchorage and a trusty harbor out again into the deep by the ebbing wave. Nor is it any wonder if, when the Apostle departed, the vessel of election, in whom Christ the Lord spoke, the Galatians were changed, since even now we see the very same thing happen in the churches too. For if ever there happens to be in a church a teacher adorned in speech and in life, who rouses his hearers to virtues as if with certain goads, we see the whole people hasten, burn with fervor and run to and fro over almsgiving, fasting, chastity, the receiving of the poor, the burial of the dead and other such things. But when he has departed, we see it little by little wither away, and, its food withdrawn, grow thin, turn pale and languish, and the ruin follow of everything that had flourished before. Wherefore, since the harvest is great but the laborers are few (Matt. 9:37), let us pray the Lord of the harvest to send laborers to reap, who may reap and gather the ears of the Christian people that stand in the Church, made ready to be the wheat to come, and, carrying them into the barns, may by no means allow them to perish.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.4.19":["My little children, of whom I travail again, until Christ be formed in you.\n\nWith what great difficulties and pain offspring are brought forth from the womb, the first curse declares, saying: \"In sorrow you shall bear children\" (Gen. 3:16). Wishing therefore to show the solicitude of teachers for their disciples, and what feelings they suffer lest their followers fall away from salvation, Paul says: My little children, of whom I travail again. For he who in another place had said as a father: \"Though you have ten thousand pedagogues in Christ, yet not many fathers\" (1 Cor. 4:15), now speaks in Christ no longer as a father but as a mother, so that they may recognize in him the anxiety and the tender devotion of both parents. Something of the sort Moses too said of the people: \"Did I receive all this people in my womb\" (Num. 11:12)? Which of us, do you think, is so anxious for the salvation of his disciples that he is tormented not for a few hours, or at most for two or three days, but for the whole time of his life, until Christ is formed in them? The example he has taken of a pregnant woman, conceiving and forming the seed within herself, must be carefully held to, so that we may understand what is said. Nature is not to be blushed at, but revered. For just as the seed is first cast without form into a woman's womb, so that it clings to its furrows and floor as if with a kind of glue (and the prophet too, remembering his own beginning, says of it: \"Your eyes saw my unformed state\" (Ps. 138:16)); then through nine months, the blood being held back, the future man is curdled, given a body, nourished and articulated into parts; so that, after it has stirred in the womb, at the appointed time it is poured forth into the light, and is born with as great difficulties as those with which it is afterwards nursed lest it perish: so too the seed of the word of Christ, when it has fallen into the soul of the hearer, grows by its own stages, and, to pass over many things (for we can easily transfer the bodily description to the spiritual understanding), it remains in a precarious state for as long as it takes until he who conceived gives birth. Nor is the end of the effort reached at once in having brought forth; rather, then comes the beginning of another labor: to lead the suckling infancy, with diligent nourishment and pains, up to the full age of Christ. And just as in marriage the husband's seed is often the cause that children are not begotten, sometimes a barren wife does not retain the seed, frequently neither is fit for begetting, and on the contrary both are fruitful: so too among those who sow the word of God this fourfold division holds: either the teacher indeed fulfills his office, but the hearer is barren; or the hearer is of good disposition, but through the teacher's want of skill the seed of the word perishes; or surely the one who is taught is as senseless as the one who instructs; and rarely does it happen that master and disciple are in accord with each other, namely that the one teaches as much as the other can draw in, or that the one taught takes in as much as the teacher pours in. But now we are all judges. We do not know what number a psalm is, what part of prophecy, what chapter of the Law, and by mere facility of speech we boldly interpret what we in no way understand. It is no concern of ours that Christ be formed in the people, that each one, returning to his own home, may have the seed of the word of God, so that when he has conceived it he may say with the prophet: \"From fear of you, O Lord, we have conceived and brought forth; we have made children of your salvation upon the earth\" (Isa. 26:17, 18). Such people pass over into the apostles, and deserve to hear from the Savior: \"Whoever shall do the will of my Father, he is my brother, and sister, and mother\" (Matt. 12:50): the diversity of their progress being shown in the different names. Christ is also formed in the heart of believers when all mysteries are laid open to them, and the things that seemed obscure become clear. But this too must be observed: that he who through sin had in a certain manner ceased to be a man is conceived by the master through penitence, and the forming of Christ in him is promised once again. This is against the Novatians, who will not have those whom sins have once crushed formed anew.\n— Commentary on Galatians","Let us give an example, so that what is commanded to the daughter of Zion, when it is said: Be in pain, and act manfully, like a woman giving birth, we may refer not to her punishment but to her benefit. Paul says to the Galatians: My little children, with whom I am in labor again, until Christ be formed in you. And he was in pain so long, and in labor so long, until he begot again through repentance those who had perished through sin.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"GAL.4.20":["But I would wish to be with you now, and to change my voice, because I am confounded in you.\n\nDivine Scripture edifies even when it is read; but it profits much more if it is turned from letters into voice, so that he who had taught through the Epistle may, being present, instruct his hearers. For the living voice has great force: a voice resounding from the mouth of its author, which is uttered and articulated with the very delivery with which it was generated in its own man's heart. The Apostle, therefore, knowing that speech addressed to those who are present has greater force, desires to change the epistolary voice, the voice confined in letters, into presence: and, because this was more expedient for those who had been perverted in error, to draw them back to the truth by living speech. And this for the reason that he was confounded in them: which indeed is said more properly in Greek. For Ἀποροῦμαι signifies not so much confusion, which among them is called αἰσχύνη or σύγχυσις, as need and want. This, then, is the sense: I would wish to be with you now, and to utter in person, being present myself, the voice of my letters, because I am in need in you. For I do not have the fruits that teachers are accustomed to have from their disciples; and the seed of teachings has been sown in vain, since I suffer utter destitution in you: so that I could break out in the words of Jeremiah: \"I have not profited, nor has anyone profited me\" (Jer. 15:10). This passage can also be interpreted another way: Paul the apostle, who had become a Jew to the Jews, that he might gain the Jews (1 Cor. 9), and to those who were under the Law as though he himself were under the Law, and weak to the weak, that he might gain the weak: according to the character of those whom he desired to save, he would change his voice, and, in the manner of actors (for indeed he was made a spectacle to the world, and to angels, and to men [1 Cor. 4]), he would turn his bearing and his voices into various shapes. Not that he was what he pretended to be; but that he only seemed to be what was of profit to the others. He perceives that the Galatians need another teaching, that they must be saved by another way, not by that by which they had first been brought over from heathenism to the faith of Christ: and he is compelled to say: I would wish now to be with you, and to change my voice, because I am confounded in you. I do not see, he says, that I am of profit if I speak the things that I spoke before; because of which, not knowing what to do, and drawn in different directions, I am torn, confounded and rent apart. And just as physicians, when they have seen that the force of their art does not prevail with the first remedy, pass on to another, and go on trying for so long which of several may help, until they arrive at a cure, so that what could by no means be healed by the softness of some plaster may be healed by a more biting powder and a harsher treatment: so I too, because I am confounded in you and am drawn hither and thither by not knowing, would wish, being present, to utter the voice of my letters from my own mouth, so that I myself might rebuke you more severely than usual; since a letter cannot express the voice of one who rebukes; it cannot make the outcry of one who is angry resound, nor unfold the pain of the breast in strokes of the pen. But it can also be understood more simply: I have just now used gentle words with you, saying: \"Brothers, I beseech you\". And: \"My little children, of whom I am again in labor until Christ be formed in you\"; but I, gentle and mild, who spoke to you as a father, out of that love by which I do not suffer my sons to perish and to wander perpetually, would wish now to be present, if the chains of my confession did not confine me, and to change my gentle voice into the words of one who rebukes. Nor is it fickleness if I now coax, now grow angry; love impels me, sorrow impels me, to speak with differing feelings. For I do not know into what words I should first break out, and with what remedy I ought to heal you, because I am confounded in you.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.4.21":["Tell me, you who wish to be under the Law, have you not heard the Law?\n\nIt must be noted that here the history of Genesis is called the Law: not, as people commonly suppose, the things that are to be done or are to be avoided, but the whole account that is woven together about Abraham and his wives and children is called law (John 15). We read in another place too that the Prophets are also called the Law. He therefore hears the Law who, according to Paul, looks not into its surface but into its marrow. He does not hear the Law who, like the Galatians, follows only the outer bark.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.4.22-23":["For it is written that Abraham had two sons, one by the bondwoman and one by the free woman. But he who was by the bondwoman was born according to the flesh; but he who was by the free woman, through the promise.\n\nIt is a matter of very great difficulty to show that only Isaac, who was born of Sarah, was begotten by promise, and not Ishmael too, who sprang from Hagar, the Egyptian bondwoman. For Scripture relates that when, with Sarah persecuting her, Hagar had fled while with child, and an angel had come to her in the desert and was warning her to submit herself to her mistress's authority, that same angel also spoke these words: \"Multiplying I will multiply your seed, and it shall not be numbered for multitude\" (Gen. 16:10). And afterward, concerning Ishmael (and no one would doubt that these are certainly words of promise): \"He shall be a rustic man, his hand over all, and the hands of all over him, and he shall dwell over against the face of all his brothers\". But it can be answered that the promise of an angel is of lesser authority than that of God himself. For just as a star does not glow when the sun has risen, so too the words of the angel, in comparison with the promise of God, are obscured, and vanish, and are counted as nothing. This answer does indeed seem to have some weight; but it is at once crushed by the authority of the Scripture that follows. For it is written: \"But Abraham said to God: Let this Ishmael live in your sight\" (ibid., 17:18 ff.); and God answered him thus: \"Behold, Sarah your wife shall bear you a son, and you shall call his name Isaac, and I will establish my testament with him, for an everlasting testament, and with his seed after him. But concerning Ishmael: Behold, I have heard you, and behold, I have blessed him, and I will increase him, and I will multiply him exceedingly. He shall beget twelve nations, and I will make him a great nation; but my testament I will establish with Isaac, whom Sarah shall bear to you at this time, in the coming year\". From these words it is evident, by the very speech of God as well, that Ishmael was begotten according to promise. But this too is resolved thus: the promise is properly fulfilled in the giving of the testament, and it is one thing to bless, to increase, to multiply exceedingly, which is written of Ishmael, and another to make an heir through a testament, which is said of Isaac: \"I will establish my testament with him, for an everlasting testament, and with his seed after him\". And in what follows: \"But my testament I will establish with Isaac, whom Sarah shall bear to you\" (Gen. 17:19). And just as gifts are one thing and the substance another, legacies one thing and the inheritance another (for we read that gifts were handed over to the sons of Abraham's concubines, but to the son of Sarah was left the inheritance of the whole substance), so, as we have said, blessing and legacies are one thing, the testament another. And this too can be said: that concerning Ishmael either the angel or God spoke after he had been conceived, whereas concerning Isaac God made the promise before he was conceived in Sarah's womb. Let this be said for the present, as far as the mediocrity of our talent allows. But if anyone can find something greater, as to how Ishmael, who was born of the bondwoman, is not the son of promise, but Isaac, who was born of the free woman, is, he should rather be listened to. \"And if\", says the Apostle, \"you think anything otherwise, God has revealed this also to you\". Now we must briefly reach toward higher things, so as to say that each one of us is at first not born according to promise, as long as he is instructed by the simple words of the Scriptures and still delights in Jewish expositions; but when he has crossed over to more sublime things and has understood the Law as spiritual, then he is begotten of promise; and, to speak more plainly, those who do the works of Abraham are born of Abraham daily. But those who have the spirit of slavery again in fear are begotten of the Egyptian bondwoman, while those who have received the spirit of adoption are begotten of Sarah, the free woman: the freedom with which we have been endowed by Christ. The Lord speaks to the Jews, who still preferred to be sons of the bondwoman: \"If you remain in my word, you will truly be my disciples, and you will know the truth, and the truth will make you free\" (John 8:31, 32). Hence they too, not knowing that what was said was mystical, say: \"We are the seed of Abraham, and we have never been slaves to anyone: how do you say, You will be free? Jesus answered them: Amen, amen I say to you, that everyone who commits sin is the slave of sin. But the slave does not remain in the house forever; but the son remains forever\". If we are slaves of sin, Hagar the Egyptian has begotten us; if sin does not reign in our mortal body, we are truly sons of God.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.4.24-26":["For these are the two testaments, one indeed from Mount Sinai, bearing children into slavery, which is Hagar. For Sinai is a mountain in Arabia, which borders on that which is now Jerusalem, and is in slavery with her sons. But that Jerusalem which is above is free, which is the mother of us all.\n\nThe explanation of nearly everyone on this passage is this: they interpret Hagar, the slave woman, as the Law and the people of the Jews, but Sarah, the free woman, as the Church, which has been gathered from the Gentiles and which is the mother of the saints, as Paul says: Which is the mother of us all. She did not give birth for a long time, before Christ was born of the Virgin, and she was barren, since Isaac, the laughter of the world, was not yet resounding from the chosen father with the voice of sublime doctrines; for indeed Abraham too is rendered in our language \"chosen father with sound.\" But Hagar, which means παροικία, that is, \"sojourning,\" or \"pilgrimage,\" or \"tarrying,\" begets Ishmael, who only hears the precepts of God and does not do them, a rustic man, bloodthirsty, haunting the deserts, who is an enemy to all his brothers born of the free woman and resists them with a hostile front. Nor is it any wonder that the Old Testament, which was established and written down on Mount Sinai, which is in Arabia and borders on that which is now Jerusalem, is not perpetual: since sojourning, too, is different from perpetual possession, and the name of Mount Sinai sounds \"temptation,\" and Arabia signifies \"setting\"; and, on the contrary, that Jerusalem which is above, which is free and the mother of the saints, shows that this Jerusalem which exists at present is below, and sunk in what is low and lowest. There are some who understand the two Testaments in another way as well: so that they interpret divine Scripture, the old as well as the new, either as the slave woman or as the free woman, according to the diversity of understanding and the judgment of those who read it; and they would have those who still serve the letter and have the spirit of fear unto slavery be begotten of Hagar the Egyptian, but those who ascend to higher things and are willing to understand allegorically what is written be the sons of Sarah, whose name in our language means ἄρχουσα, that is, \"princess,\" in the feminine gender. And they assert that they adopt this view out of the following necessity: because it would be unjust to understand Moses and all the prophets as born of the slave woman, but any of the Gentiles whatever as born of the free woman. Hence, they say, it is better that we should judge not only, concerning those who are in the Church, some to be slaves and others free according to the diversity of their understandings (as we said above), but also, concerning one and the same man, that as long as he follows the history he is the son of the slave woman; but when, as Jesus opens the Scriptures, his heart has been set on fire, and in the breaking of the bread he has looked upon him whom before he did not see (Luke 24), then he too is called a son of Sarah. Marcion and Manichaeus were unwilling to remove from their codex this passage in which the Apostle said: \"Which things indeed are allegorical\", and the rest that follows, thinking that it was left there against us: namely, that the Law is to be understood otherwise than it is written; whereas, certainly, even if it is to be taken allegorically (which we too confess, and Paul teaches), it was so composed not at the will of the reader, but by the authority of the writer; and by the very thing which they seemed to keep against us they are crushed: that Moses, the servant of God the Creator, wrote spiritual things, their Apostle too teaching that very thing, he whom they themselves assert to be the preacher of another Christ and of a better God.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.4.24":["Which things are said through allegory.\n\nAllegory belongs properly to the art of grammar, and how it differs from metaphor or the other tropes, we learn as little children in the schools. It puts forward one thing in the words and signifies another in the sense. The books of the orators (supply: of allegories) and of the poets are full of it. Divine Scripture too is woven together through it in no small part. Understanding this, Paul the apostle (since he had touched secular letters to some extent) used the very word of the figure, so as to call it allegory, as it is called among his own people: namely, so that by this sense he might show more fully the loose use (abusionem) of the Greek word in this place. That Paul knew secular letters, though not to perfection, his own words testify: \"One of them, a prophet of their own, said: Cretans are always liars, evil beasts, idle bellies\" (Titus 1:12). This heroic verse is by the poet Epimenides, whom both Plato and the other ancient writers recall. Among the Athenians also, when he was making his defense in the Areopagus, he added this: \"As also certain of your poets have said: For we are also his offspring\" (Acts 17:28). This half-line is found in Aratus, who wrote about the heaven and the stars. And also this: \"Very evil conversations corrupt good morals\" (1 Cor. 15:33); it is an iambic trimeter taken from a comedy of Menander. From these and other passages it is evident that Paul was not ignorant of secular letters, and that what he here called allegory he elsewhere called spiritual understanding. As there: \"For we know that the Law is spiritual\" (Rom. 7:14), in place of saying allegory, or figured allegorically. And elsewhere: \"All ate the same spiritual food, and drank the same spiritual drink. And they drank of the spiritual rock that followed them, and the rock was Christ\" (1 Cor. 10:3-4). There is no one who doubts that the manna here, and the eruption of the sudden spring, and the rock itself that follows, are to be taken allegorically. I know what can be objected on the contrary: \"Brothers, if a man has been overtaken in some fault, you who are spiritual instruct such a one in the spirit of gentleness\" (below, 6:1). And in another place: \"But the spiritual man judges all things, yet he himself is judged by no one\" (1 Cor. 2:15): namely, that the word spiritual sounds something other than what we said above. But we call that man spiritual, in that he judges all things and is himself judged by no one, who, knowing all the mysteries of the Scriptures, understands them in a lofty way, and, seeing Christ in the divine books, admits in them nothing of Jewish tradition.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.4.27":["For it is written: Rejoice, barren one, you who do not bear; break forth and cry out, you who do not travail: for many more are the children of the desolate than of her who has a husband (Isa. 54:1.)\n\nThe synagogue had the Law as her husband; and, according also to the prophecy of Hannah, she was once fruitful in children (1 Sam. 1). But the barren Church, without Christ as husband, without any address of the Bridegroom's word, lay long in the desert. But after the synagogue received the bill of divorce into her hands, and turned all her husband's ornaments into the ornament of an idol, then the husband, his former girdle rotting, wove another belt for his loins, another loincloth out of the Gentiles; and she, as soon as she was joined to her husband, conceived and gave birth. And in Isaac the Lord cries out through the prophet: \"Is a nation born all at once?\" (Isa. 66:8): when in a single day, in the Acts of the Apostles, three thousand and five thousand men believed (Acts 3 ff.). Nor do I think it necessary for us to speak of the Christian multitude and of the fewness of the Jews, since the banners of the cross shine forth in the whole world, and a Jew scarcely appears in the cities, rare and conspicuous.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.4.28":["But we, brothers, are children of promise according to Isaac.\n\nThat the Apostle and those like him are children of promise according to Isaac presents no difficulty of understanding. But since Origen, in expounding this passage, set down the Apostle's text thus: \"But you, brothers, are children of promise according to Isaac\", the question is raised how he now calls the Galatians, whom he had called senseless, and of whom he had said that they had begun in the spirit and were finishing in the flesh, children of the promise according to Isaac. We say, therefore, that the Apostle calls them children of the promise according to Isaac for this reason: because he does not utterly despair of their salvation, and he reckons that they will once more return to the spirit in which they had begun, and become children of the free woman. But if they are brought to completion in the flesh, they are children of the slave woman.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.4.29-30":["But as then the one who had been born according to the flesh persecuted the one according to the spirit, so it is now also. But what does the Scripture say? Cast out the bondwoman and her son: for the son of the bondwoman shall not be heir with my son Isaac. Therefore, brothers, we are not sons of the bondwoman, but of the free woman. With which freedom Christ has made us free.\n\nI do not think that we can find where Ishmael persecuted Isaac, but only this: that when the son of the Egyptian woman, who was the elder by birth, was playing with Isaac, Sarah was indignant and said to Abraham: \"Cast out the bondwoman and her son: For the son of the bondwoman shall not inherit with my son Isaac\" (Gen. 21:10). And surely simple play among infants does not deserve expulsion and disinheritance. But the Apostle, as a Hebrew of the Hebrews, and taught at the feet of the master Gamaliel (who once restrained in council the Pharisees raging against the Lord), understood from the words of Sarah, who said: \"for the son of the bondwoman shall not inherit with my son Isaac\", that that play had not been simple. But because perhaps Ishmael, as the elder by birth, and circumcised at a time when he could already understand and feel what he suffered, was claiming the birthright for himself, Scripture called the quarrel of the little ones play. Hence Sarah too, not bearing these words, and not enduring the habit of the bondwoman's son, who from an early age claimed the birthright for himself, broke out in speech: \"Cast out the bondwoman with her son: For the son of the bondwoman shall not inherit with my son Isaac\". And when it seemed hard to Abraham (for more is always owed to the firstborn) that Ishmael should not only cease to be the first, but should not even receive an equal portion with his younger brother, God, who willed that the free woman be within and the bondwoman be driven out of doors, confirms Sarah's words and speaks to Abraham: \"Let it not be hard in your sight concerning the boy and the bondwoman. In all that Sarah shall say to you, hear her voice: for in Isaac shall your seed be called\". As then, therefore, the elder brother Ishmael persecuted Isaac, who was still nursing and little, claiming for himself the prerogative of circumcision, claiming for himself the birthright: so now also Israel according to the flesh is raised up, is puffed up, is exalted against the younger brother, the Christian people from the Gentiles. Let us consider the madness of the Jews, who killed both the Lord and the prophets, and persecuted the apostles, and oppose the will of God: and we shall see that far greater persecutions, as histories also teach us, have been stirred up by the Jews against the Christians than by the Gentiles. Do we wonder at the Jews? Today also those who are little ones in Christ and live carnally persecute those who have been born of water and the spirit, and who, rising with Christ, seek the things that are above, not below. Let them do what they will: let them with Ishmael persecute Isaac: they will be cast out of doors with their mother, the Egyptian bondwoman, nor will they receive the inheritance, which he alone who was born of the promise shall obtain. And elegant too is this, that he who was born according to the flesh persecutes the spiritual one. For the spiritual man never persecutes the carnal, but pardons him as a rustic brother: he knows that he can make progress in time. And if ever he sees the son of the Egyptian woman angry, he remembers the one father, who created light, oxen and the gnat, and that in a great house there are not only vessels of gold and silver, but also of wood and earthenware.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.4.31":["Therefore let us say with the apostle Paul: We are not sons of the bondwoman, but of the free woman (2 Tim. 2); and, renewed in Christ, let us hear the words of the Lord speaking to the Jews: \"If you abide in my word, you shall know the truth, and the truth shall make you free\" (John 8:31, 32). Made free by this freedom, the Apostle too said: For though I am free from all. \"He who commits sin is the slave of sin\" (1 Cor. 9:19). He, because he knew himself free from all vices and a stranger to all concupiscence and error, rightly rejoiced in the freedom of Christ, saying: We are not sons of the bondwoman, but of the free woman: with which freedom Christ has made us free (John 8:34).\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.5.1":["Stand, and do not be held again by the yoke of slavery.\n\nFrom this too it is shown that he who clings to the yoke of slavery does not stand; and also that he who has been granted freedom by Christ was under the yoke for as long as he had the spirit of slavery in fear and followed the beginnings of the Law. When he says, stand, he urges a firm and stable faith in Christ, so that the churches of Galatia may remain with foot fixed upon the Savior. Of this the righteous man also speaks in another place: \"He set my feet upon the rock\" (Ps. 39:3), in place of what is, \"upon Christ\": namely, that they may not be carried about by every wind of doctrine and swept off in different directions (Eph. 4). Hence it is also said to those who stand: \"And he who stands, let him see that he does not fall\" (1 Cor. 10:12). And in another place: \"Stand, act manfully, be strengthened\" (ibid. 16:13), so that they may stand with him whom Stephen, persevering in martyrdom, saw standing at the right hand of the Father (Acts 7), and who spoke to Moses: \"But you, stand with me\" (Exod. 34:2). Now by the yoke of slavery he means the Law, hard, difficult, laborious, which day and night wears out its worshippers with heavy toil. As Peter too says in the Acts of the Apostles: \"Why do you try\", he says, \"to put a heavy yoke upon the neck of the brothers, which neither we nor our fathers have been able to bear\" (Acts 15:10)? And when he added, not again, it is not that the Galatians had kept the Law before; but that the yoke of idolatry too is heavy, the yoke by which the people of the Egyptians was oppressed and sank like lead in the Red Sea (Exod. 15). In this sense he had also said above: \"How are you turning again to the weak and needy elements, which you wish to serve once more, observing days, and months, and times, and years?\" For the Galatians, who at the preaching of the apostle Paul had abandoned idols and at once risen to the grace of the Gospel, were not returning to the slavery of the Jewish Law, which they had never known before; but in wishing to observe times, to be circumcised in the flesh and to offer bodily sacrifices, they were in a manner returning to the same rites which they had served before in idolatry. For they say that the priests of Egypt, and the Ishmaelites, and the Midianites have no foreskin. And that the nations observe days, months and years, would that we did not know, so that there might never be a festivity shared by us with them.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.5.2":["Behold, I Paul say to you that if you be circumcised, Christ profits you nothing.\n\nIn the Gospel the Savior speaks to the disciples: \"He who hears you hears me; he who receives you receives me\" (Luke 10:16). And the Apostle testifies, saying: \"And I live, now not I; but Christ lives in me\" (above, 2:20); and elsewhere: \"Or do you seek a proof of him who speaks in me, Christ\" (2 Cor. 13:3)? From this it is clearly proved that what he now says, Behold, I Paul say to you, is to be received not as the words of Paul only, but of the Lord. For when in the first Epistle to the Corinthians he had first set down: \"But to those who are married I give charge, not I, but the Lord\" (1 Cor. 7:10); and had at once added: \"but to the rest I command\" (ibid., 12), then, lest his authority be thought cheap, \"I think,\" he says, \"that I too have the spirit of God\": so that, with the spirit and Christ speaking in him, he who, imitating the prophets, might say, \"Thus says the Lord almighty\", would not be held in contempt. But what has been said, Behold, I Paul say to you that if you be circumcised, Christ profits you nothing, will become greater if it is joined with the beginning, where he says: \"Paul an apostle, not from men, nor through man, but through Jesus Christ\", and the rest: so that the hearers may be moved not so much by the authority of the one sent as by that of the one sending. Someone may say: Contrary to this passage is what is written to the Romans: \"Circumcision indeed profits, if you keep the Law\" (Rom. 2:25); and below: \"What then has the Jew more, or what is the profit of circumcision? Much in every way. First indeed, because the oracles of God were entrusted to them\" (Rom. 3:1-2). For if Christ profits nothing to those who have been circumcised, how does circumcision profit those who keep the Law? This indeed will be resolved by this answer, that we say that the Epistle written to the Romans was dictated to those who had believed from among the Jews and the Gentiles, and that Paul acted so that neither people would be offended: namely, so that each people might possess its own privilege, so that neither would the Gentiles be circumcised, nor would the circumcised draw forward a foreskin. But writing to the Galatians he used another argument. For they were not of the circumcision, but of the Gentiles who had believed. Nor could circumcision profit those who, after the grace of the Gospel, were turning back again to the elements of the Law. And in the Acts of the Apostles the history relates (Acts 15) that, when certain men of the circumcision had risen up and asserted that those who had believed from among the Gentiles ought to be circumcised and to keep the law of Moses, the elders who were at Jerusalem, and the apostles gathered together with them, decreed by letter that the yoke of the Law should not be laid upon them, and that they should observe nothing further, except only to keep themselves from things sacrificed to idols, and from blood, and from fornication, or, as is written in some copies, \"and from things strangled\". And lest any doubt remain that circumcision profits nothing, but that it was on account of those who had believed from among the Jews that he tempered his judgment on circumcision to the Romans, coming down little by little to the later parts of the same Epistle he showed that neither circumcision nor uncircumcision avails anything, saying: \"Circumcision therefore is nothing, and uncircumcision is nothing, but the keeping of the commandments of God\" (1 Cor. 7:19). For circumcision is nothing to such a degree that it profited nothing even the house of Israel, which boasted of circumcision, as the prophet records: \"All the nations are uncircumcised in flesh, but the house of Israel is uncircumcised in heart\" (Ezek. 44:9); and the uncircumcised Melchizedek blessed the circumcised Abraham. For what he says, If you be circumcised (Gen. 14), is as if he had meant to say: if you be circumcised in the flesh. This in another place he calls not circumcision but mutilation, saying: \"See the mutilation. For we are the circumcision, who serve by the spirit of God, and glory in Christ, and do not trust in the flesh\" (Phil. 3:2-3). He does not trust in the flesh who expects every benefit from Christ, and does not sow in the flesh, so as to reap corruption from the flesh, but in the spirit, from which eternal life is brought forth. The sentence must be examined more closely: If you be circumcised, Christ profits you nothing. That is, not only that, if they are circumcised, circumcision itself does not profit them; but that, even if they seem to have the other virtues in Christ apart from circumcision, all of them perish once they have been circumcised after faith in Christ. What then? Did circumcision profit Timothy nothing? Much in every way. For he was circumcised not so much in order that he might think he could gain some advantage from circumcision itself, as that he might win the rest (1 Cor. 9). He became a Jew to the Jews, so that by his circumcision he might bring the Jews over to the faith of Christ. For circumcision does not profit precisely when it is thought to bring some benefit of itself.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.5.3":["And I solemnly testify to every man who circumcises himself, that he is a debtor to do the whole Law.\n\nGod, who commanded circumcision first to Abraham and then through Moses in the Law, appointed not circumcision alone but many other things as well to be observed: feast days to be attended at Jerusalem; whole burnt offerings of victims, always morning and evening; the sacrifice of the lamb in one place only; rest for the land in its seventh season; the fiftieth year of release; and the rest, which it is easy for every reader to gather for himself from the Scriptures. We shall therefore hem in Ebion and his followers, who think that those who believe in Christ after the Gospel must be circumcised, so that either they practice circumcision together with the other things that are commanded in the Law, or else, if it is impossible for all of them to be done, circumcision too should cease, since it has been passed over along with the rest as useless. But if they answer that they are bound to do only what is possible (for God, they say, does not demand of us the things we cannot fulfill, but those we can), we shall tell them that it does not belong to the same God both to wish the Law to be kept and to abandon those who keep the Law. Or how could he make them guilty for leaving the Law undone, when, even if they wish it, they cannot fulfill it all? We, for our part, follow the spiritual law, which says: \"You shall not bridle the mouth of the ox that treads out the grain\" (Deut. 25:4), and with the Apostle we understand: \"Does God care about oxen\" (1 Tim. 5:18; 1 Cor. 9:9)? Surely he says it for our sake; and we keep delightful sabbaths (Isa. 58:13), not so that our ox and ass and our worthless cattle may rejoice on the sabbath, but those men and beasts of whom it is written: \"Men and beasts you will save, O Lord\" (Ps. 35:7). By men we mean all who are rational and spiritual; by beasts, those who are of slower wit and are taught by the spiritual to keep the sabbaths of the Lord. Nor is what was said above, \"If you are circumcised, Christ will profit you nothing\", and what follows, \"I testify to every man who circumcises himself, that he is a debtor to do the whole Law\", contrary to what we are now bringing forward. For it is not the hearers of the Law who are righteous before God, but the doers of the Law will be justified. For the doer of the Law is the one who can say: \"We are not the circumcision\"; and, \"A Jew in secret\"; and, \"We know that the Law is spiritual\". But whoever follows the mutilation and the letter that kills is not a doer of the Law, but truly an enemy of the law, above all after the coming of the Savior, who takes away the veil from the heart of those who turn to him, so that all of us, beholding the glory of the Lord with unveiled face, may be transfigured from the oldness of the letter into the newness of the spirit.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.5.4":["You have been made void from Christ: you who are justified in the Law have fallen away from grace.\n\nJust as no one can serve two masters (Matt. 6), so it is difficult to fulfil at one and the same time both the shadow and the truth of the Law. The shadow is in the old Law, until the day breathes and the shadows are removed; the truth is in the Gospel of Christ: \"For grace and truth were made through Jesus Christ\" (John 1:17). Therefore he who thinks that he is justified in some observance of the Law loses the grace of Christ, and lets go the Gospel which he had held; and when he has lost grace, he is deprived of faith in Christ, and ceases in His work. For κατηργήθητε ἀπὸ τοῦ Χριστοῦ is rather to be understood, not as it has been badly translated in the Latin, You have been made void from Christ (Evacuati estis a Christo), but as \"you have ceased in the work of Christ\" (in Christi opere cessastis), so that what he had commanded above specifically about circumcision, saying, \"if you are circumcised, Christ profits you nothing\", he now applies generally to the whole Law: that those make no progress in the work of Christ who have believed that they are to be justified in any observance whatever of the Law.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.5.5":["For we through the spirit, by faith, wait for the hope of righteousness.\n\nHe put \"spirit\" to distinguish it from the letter. But by the hope of righteousness Christ is to be understood, because He Himself is truth, patience, hope, righteousness and all the virtues; whose second coming we await, since He is to judge all things, and will then be present no longer in patience but in righteousness, to render to each one according to his works. Awaiting the presence of this God, the apostle, and those who are like him, say: \"Thy kingdom come\" (Matt. 6:10), so that when the Son has delivered the kingdom to God and the Father, and He Himself also has been subjected in all who are subjected, then the head may be subjected in the body, and God may be all in all (1 Cor. 15). For He who now is in part, through individuals, will then begin to be wholly through all.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.5.6":["For in Christ Jesus neither circumcision avails anything, nor uncircumcision, but faith which works through love.\n\nFor those who wish to live in Christ Jesus, virtues are to be sought, vices to be shunned; but the intermediate things, which lie between virtues and vices, are neither to be shunned nor to be sought, such as circumcision and uncircumcision and other things like them. Circumcision indeed profits, if you keep the Law. It was useful for this reason to those who lived under the Law: not because they were circumcised, but because the oracles of God were entrusted to them, and turning these into works they were not strangers to salvation. Nor should it trouble us that it was Zipporah who, taking a stone, circumcised her son and kept the strangling angel away from her husband (Exod. 4), or however else it is reported to be written in the Hebrew; for he has now testified not so much that circumcision profits nothing at all, as that it does not avail in Christ Jesus, since indeed from the time when the Gospel shone out over the whole world, the injury of circumcision is superfluous. It availed then, as did the other things of the Law as well, when carnal blessings too were promised to those who kept the Law: namely, that if they fulfilled it, they would be blessed in the city, blessed in the field, would have full barns, and many other things that are contained in the promises (Deut. 28). We, however, wish to avail and be strengthened in Christ Jesus, that is, in the true circumcision, and not in the Jewish mutilation. \"For he is not a Jew who is one openly, nor is circumcision that which is manifest in the flesh; but he is a Jew who is one in secret, and circumcision is of the heart, in the spirit, not the letter\" (Rom. 2:28, 29). Circumcision of the flesh, therefore, profits nothing in Christ, but circumcision of the heart and of the ears does, which takes away that reproach of the Jews: \"Behold, your ears are uncircumcised, and you cannot hear\" (Exod. 6:12). Circumcision of the lips profits, which Moses, in his humility, pleaded that he did not yet have, as is written in the Hebrew: \"But I am one having uncircumcision in my lips\". Circumcision also affords much benefit in matters of sex, when unchastity is cut away through chastity. Therefore in Christ Jesus neither circumcision avails nor bodily uncircumcision, because they are placed in the middle, that is, between vices and virtues; but faith, which works through love, so that both the faith which was reckoned to Abraham for righteousness may be approved, and every work of faith may be set in love, since the whole Law and the Prophets hang on love. For in these two commandments, \"You shall love your God, and you shall love your neighbor\", the Savior declared that the Law and the Prophets consist. And Paul in another place: \"For you shall not commit adultery, you shall not steal, you shall not covet, and if there is any other commandment, it is summed up in this word: You shall love your neighbor as yourself\" (Rom. 13:9). If, then, every commandment is summed up in what was said, \"You shall love your neighbor as yourself\", and faith working through love avails very much, it is manifest that the working of faith through love contains the fullness of all the commandments. But just as, according to the apostle James, faith without works is dead (Jas. 2:17, 20), so without faith, even though works be good, they are reckoned dead. Those, therefore, who do not believe in Christ and are of upright morals, what more do they have than works of virtue? Let that harlot from the Gospel supply an example of the faith which works through love: when, in the house of the Pharisee, she had washed with her tears the feet of the Lord as he reclined at table, wiped them with her hair, and anointed them with ointment, and the Lord had put to the murmuring Pharisee the parable of the debtors of fifty and five hundred denarii, he added: \"Wherefore I say to you: Her many sins are forgiven her, because she loved much\" (Luke 7:47 and 50). And turning to the woman herself, he said: \"Your faith has made you safe: go in peace\". For in this place it is openly shown that this woman had faith working through love, which availed much in Christ. Suppose someone should say: He rightly showed that circumcision avails nothing in Christ, since he knew that it had once availed; but was anyone in doubt about uncircumcision too, that he should say, nor uncircumcision? But if we consider that very many Christians, that is, of our own number, who from the wild olive have been grafted into the root of the good olive (Rom. 11), exult against the broken branches of the Jewish people and say that uncircumcision, in which Abraham pleased God and faith was reckoned to him for righteousness, avails more than circumcision, which was given as a sign of faith and did not profit Israel who had it: we shall see that this presumption of certain people too is now most carefully excluded.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.5.7":["You were running well; who hindered you from obeying the truth?\n\nWhat the Latin translator has now set down, not to obey the truth (veritati non obedire), which in the Greek is written τῇ ἀληθείᾳ μὴ πείθεσθαι, he rendered in an earlier passage thus: \"not to believe the truth\" (non credere veritati). This, indeed, we noted in its proper place is not found in the old manuscripts, although the Greek copies too have been confused by this error. But the sense is this: You were worshiping the Father in spirit and truth, and, receiving of the fullness of Christ, you knew that the Law was only given to the people through Moses, and not also made; but grace and truth were not only given but also made through Jesus Christ. Since, therefore, you were running so well, serving the truth rather than images, by what perverse teacher have you been hindered, that you follow the shadow of the Law and abandon the truth of the Gospel? There follows:\n\nConsent to no one. But since we have found this written neither in the Greek books nor in those who have commented on the Apostle, it seems it should be passed over.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.5.8":["Your persuasion is not from him who called you.\n\nIn the Latin manuscripts I have found it written thus: \"Your persuasion is from God, who called you.\" I think, indeed, that this was originally from him (ex eo), and that, not being understood, \"from God\" (ex Deo) gradually became common because of the similarity, in place of what is from him (ex eo). But not even so can the sense stand, namely that he should now, on the contrary, assert that the persuasion and obedience of those whom he had just accused of not obeying the truth, showing that it lay in their own choice either to obey or not to obey, is not so much from those who are called as from him who calls. It is therefore better and more truly read thus: Your persuasion is not from him who called you. For the work of God is one thing, the work of men another. It is the work of God to call; it is the work of men either to believe or not to believe. And if anywhere in the Scriptures the free will of man is affirmed, as there: \"If you will be willing and will hear me\" (Exod. 19:5); and again: \"And now, Israel, what does the Lord your God ask of you?\" (Deut. 10:12), it is also proved most of all from this passage. But certain of the simpler sort, thinking that they were deferring to God by making our persuasion also lie in his power, have taken away the part of speech not and rendered a sense contrary to the Apostle. Whether, therefore, in the good sense or in the bad, neither God nor the devil is the cause, because our persuasion is not from him who called us, but from ourselves, who either consent or do not consent to the one who calls. Otherwise: This persuasion which you now follow is not from God, who called you in the beginning, but from those who afterwards troubled you.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.5.9":["A little leaven leavens the whole lump.\n\nIt is badly rendered in our codices: \"A little leaven corrupts the whole mass\", and the translator has rendered his own sense rather than the words of the Apostle. Now Paul uses this very same saying to the Corinthians as well, where he commanded that the man who had his father's wife should be taken away from their midst and handed over to penance, for the destruction and affliction of the flesh through fasts and sicknesses, so that the spirit might be saved in the day of the Lord Jesus Christ. For he says: \"Your glorying is not good. Do you not know that a little leaven corrupts the whole mass\" (1 Cor. 5:5, 6 ff.)? or (as we have already corrected it) leavens the whole lump? And at once he added: \"Purge out the old leaven, that you may be a new lump, as you are unleavened; for Christ our pasch has been sacrificed. Therefore let us feast, not in the old leaven, nor in the leaven of malice and wickedness, but in the unleavened bread of sincerity and truth.\" But now, by this same saying, he teaches that the spiritual bread of the Church, which came down from heaven, ought not to be violated by a Jewish interpretation; and the Lord commanded the very same thing to the disciples, that they should beware of the leaven of the Pharisees (John 6). The evangelist, making this plainer, added: \"Now he had spoken to them of the doctrine of the Pharisees\" (Matt. 16:12). And what is this other doctrine of the Pharisees, if not the observance of the Law according to the flesh? So this is the sense: Do not think that the snares of a few men, who come from Judea and teach something different, are to be despised. A spark is a small thing, and while it is being looked at it is almost not seen; but if it catches hold of tinder, and the fire, however small, finds what feeds it, it consumes walls, cities, the widest forests and whole regions. Leaven too, whose parable in the Gospel is turned to the other side (Luke 13), seems a small thing and of no account; but when it has been mixed into flour and has corrupted the whole mass by its strength, everything that has been mixed with it passes over into its power. So too a perverse doctrine, beginning from one man, at first, in its beginning, scarcely finds two or three hearers; but little by little it creeps through the body like a cancer, and, as the common proverb has it, the scab of one beast befouls the whole flock. Therefore the spark must be put out as soon as it appears, and the leaven removed from the neighborhood of the mass, the rotting flesh cut away, and the scabby animal driven from the sheepfolds, lest the whole house, mass, body and flock burn, be corrupted, rot and perish. Arius in Alexandria was a single spark; but because it was not put out at once, its flame laid waste the whole world.\n— Commentary on Galatians","This is the leaven of which the Apostle too speaks: A little leaven corrupts the whole lump. Leaven of this kind, which must be shunned by every means, Marcion had, and Valentinus, and all the heretics. Leaven has this power: if it is mixed with flour, what seemed small grows into something greater and draws the whole mass of dough to its own taste. So too heretical doctrine: if it has cast even a tiny spark into your breast, in a short time it grows up into a huge flame and draws the whole of a man's possession to itself.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"GAL.5.10":["I have confidence in you in the Lord that you will think nothing else.\n\nNot by conjecture, as some would have it, but by the prophetic spirit Paul declares that the Galatians will return to the way of truth which they had lost (1 Cor. 12). For he who exhorted others to be zealous for spiritual gifts, but rather that they might prophesy, himself also, full of the same grace, said: \"We know in part, and we prophesy in part\" (ibid., 13:9). Foreseeing therefore in the spirit that they would believe nothing else than what they were being taught through the Epistle, he says: I have confidence in you in the Lord that you will think nothing else. For the addition of the Lord's name too signifies this very thing. For if he had been reckoning this by conjecture, he could have said: I have confidence in you. But now, by adding in the Lord, trusting in a certain divine spirit, he prophesied what he had known would come to pass.\n— Commentary on Galatians","But he who troubles you shall bear judgment, whoever he is.\n\nHe is covertly tearing at Peter, they say, whom he writes above that he withstood to his face because he did not walk with a straight foot toward the truth of the Gospel. But neither would Paul speak with so insolent a curse about the prince of the Church (Gal. 2), nor did Peter deserve to be made guilty of troubling the Church. From this it must be judged that it is said of some other person, who either had been with the apostles, or had come from Judea, or had believed from among the Pharisees, or at any rate was esteemed great among the Galatians, so that he bears the judgment for the troubled Church, whoever he is. Now \"to bear judgment\" is what he said in other words in what follows: \"Each one shall bear his own burden.\" And I think that in the Scriptures \"burden\" can be taken both in a good and in a bad sense, that is, both of those who are weighed down by grave sins, and of those who sustain the light burdens of the virtues. Of sins the penitent speaks in the psalm: \"My iniquities are lifted up over my head; like a heavy burden they have weighed heavily upon me\" (Ps. 37:5). Of the virtues, and of the teaching of the virtues, the Savior says: \"For my yoke is sweet, and my burden is light\" (Matt. 11:30). And that teaching too is taken for a burden is made plain in the Gospel. For the Pharisees bind heavy burdens, and such as cannot be borne, and lay them upon the shoulders of men, but they themselves are unwilling to touch them with one finger (ibid. 23). How grave a thing it is to disturb someone out of tranquility, and to stir up the calm hearts of men as if with waves, the words of the Savior to the apostles testify, when he says: \"Let not your heart be troubled, nor be afraid\" (John 14). For it is better for him who troubles anyone in the Church and causes him to stumble that a millstone be put around his neck and he be cast with it into the sea, than that he cause to stumble one of these least ones who are pointed out by the Savior (Luke 17). The Galatians, then, had been troubled between spirit and letter, circumcision and mutilation, hidden and manifest Judaism, not knowing what to do. It can also be taken more briefly in this way: Whoever he is who draws you back to the teaching of the Pharisees and desires to be circumcised in the flesh; though he be eloquent and boast of his learning in the Law, I say nothing more except this (which even you cannot deny): that he shall bear judgment for this work, and shall receive the reward for his labor.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.5.11":["But I, brothers, if I preach circumcision, why do I still suffer persecution? Then has been made void (or, as the Greek has it better, has ceased) the stumbling block of the cross.\n\nWe read in the Acts of the Apostles, and the apostle Paul himself also often mentions in his Epistles, that he endured very frequent persecutions from the Jews, because he taught that those from among the Gentiles who had believed in Christ ought not to be circumcised. These, then, of whom he said above: \"But he who troubles you shall bear judgment, whoever he is\", in order to deceive the Galatians, added this too: Not only do Peter and James and John and the other apostles in Judea observe circumcision and the other precepts of the Law, but Paul himself as well, who taught you otherwise than the truth of the matter stands, circumcised Timothy, and frequently became a Jew to the Jews, the truth compelling him. Wishing now to remove this opinion from the minds of the Galatians, Paul says: But I, brothers, if I preach circumcision, why do I still suffer persecution? All the hatred of the Jews against me, he says, and the madness with which they rage against me, is on account of nothing else than that I teach the Gentiles that they ought not to be circumcised and to keep the burdens of the Law, which are superfluous and already abolished. But since I suffer persecution, it is manifest that I do not preach circumcision, which I destroy. For I do not suffer persecution from the Jews so much because I preach the Crucified, and say that Jesus is the Christ whom the Law and the prophets foretold, as because I teach that the Law has been fulfilled. And that the cross is a stumbling block to the Jews and foolishness to the Gentiles, our Lord himself shows, who is called a stone of offense and a rock of stumbling (Matt. 21; Luke 20): for no other reason, I think, than that preaching, when it has advanced toward its hearers under full sail, strikes against the cross as soon as it comes to it, and can by no means proceed further on a free course. But this cross, which among the Jews is a stumbling block and among the Gentiles foolishness, is to us who believe power and wisdom. \"For Christ is the power of God and the wisdom of God\" (1 Cor. 1:24), so that, because of that which was called foolishness, the foolishness of God might become wiser than men; and because of that which was called weakness and a stumbling block, the weakness of God might become stronger than men. But since, he says, the stumbling block of the cross of Christ remains, and I suffer persecution, which I would not suffer if the stumbling block did not remain, in vain do certain people keep boasting that I preach circumcision, by attacking which I endure persecution.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.5.12":["Would that they were even cut off who trouble you.\n\nIt is asked how Paul, the disciple of him who says: \"Bless those who curse you,\" and who himself says: \"Bless, and do not curse\" (Rom. 12:14), and in another place: \"Nor shall cursers possess the kingdom of God\" (1 Cor. 6:10), has now both cursed those who trouble the churches of Galatia, and cursed them with the prayer of one who wishes it: Would that they were even cut off who trouble you. For the suffering of castration is so detestable that both he who has inflicted it on the unwilling is punished by the public laws, and he who has castrated himself is held infamous. For granted, they say, that this is true: \"Christ lives in me\"; and this: \"Or do you seek a proof of Christ who speaks in me?\" (2 Cor. 13:3), certainly a word of cursing cannot be understood as coming from him who says: \"Learn from me, because I am humble, and meek, and gentle of heart\" (Matt. 11:29). And it is thought rather that he was unable to restrain himself out of Jewish fury and a kind of unbridled madness than that he imitated him who, like a lamb before its shearer, did not open his mouth, and did not curse in return those who cursed him (Isa. 53). But, condemned, he handed himself over to death. To this, whoever answers on Paul's behalf will say the following: that the words he spoke were not so much of fury against his adversaries as of love for the churches of God. For he saw that the whole province, which he himself, with his own blood and his perils, had brought over from idolatry to the faith of Christ, was thrown into confusion by a sudden persuasion, and with an apostle's grief, a father's grief, he could not contain himself: he changed his voice, and was angry with those whom he had coaxed, so that those whom he had been unable to hold by gentleness he might at least hold by rebuke. Nor is it to be wondered at if the Apostle, as a man, and one still shut up in a weak little vessel, and seeing another law in his body taking him captive and leading him in the law of sin, said this once, a thing into which we see that holy men frequently fall. But this too can be said (although to some it may seem superfluous): that Paul did not so much curse them as pray for them, that they might lose those parts of the body through which they were driven to sin. And just as it was said in the Gospel that it is better for someone to enter the kingdom of heaven without an eye, and without a hand, and without a foot, and without any other part of the members, than to go whole into Gehenna (Matt. 18), so now too he wishes them rather to lose one part of the body than, by occasion of the whole body, to be condemned to everlasting fire. If ever this passage is censured by the heathen, we have shown how they can be answered. Now let it be brought forward by us against the heretics, namely Marcion and Valentinus and all who bark against the Old Testament: by what reasoning can those who accuse the Creator of being bloodthirsty, a stern warrior and only a judge, excuse this in the Apostle of the good God? And certainly I think there is no sentence anywhere in the old Law so savage, so bloody, as would that they were cut off who trouble you. Nor can they say that the Apostle prayed for the enemies of Christ, who were troubling his churches. Nor that it was uttered out of love, since the very weight of the words shows it to be full of swelling anger and indignation. Whatever excuse, therefore, they bring forward for the Apostle, this we shall not plead in defense of the old Law.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.5.13-14":["But through love serve one another, for the whole law is fulfilled in one word: You shall love your neighbor as yourself.\n\nHe who, though he was free from all, made himself the slave of all for love's sake, that he might win the more (1 Cor. 9:19), rightly exhorts the others too to serve one another through love, which seeks not what is its own but what is its neighbor's. For he who wishes to become first shall be the slave of all (Mark 10:44); so that, just as the Savior, being in the form of God, did not think it robbery to be equal with God, but emptied himself, taking the form of a slave, and being found in fashion as a man, he humbled himself, becoming obedient even unto death, and the death of the cross (Phil. 2), so we too should now know that whatever things we formerly seemed to do under the compulsion of the Law, we, being free, must do rather through love.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.5.13-18":["This passage can be better explained thus, and become as it were one sequence and one body, linking itself together and not disagreeing with itself. Brothers, you have been called from the slavery of the Law into the freedom of the Gospel. But I beseech you not to abuse freedom as license, and think that all things which are lawful are expedient for you, and give occasion to the flesh and to licentiousness. Rather, learn that this freedom is a greater slavery, so that the obedience which formerly the Law extorted from the unwilling, you now render by serving one another through love. For indeed all that burden of the Law, and its manifold precepts, have not so much been shut out by the grace of the Gospel as abridged in the one word of love, that we love our neighbor as ourselves. For he who loves his neighbor fulfills the whole law (Matt. 22), bestowing good things on him and not inflicting evils. But if affection ceases, and there is no love, through which the whole law is fulfilled, there will be a kind of public brigandage among men, so that, raging against one another and biting one another, they are consumed by one another. But you, brothers, ought for this reason to live according to the spiritual law, so that you do not accomplish the desires that belong to the flesh. For the flesh fears cold, spurns hunger, is wasted by vigils, burns with lusts, desires whatever is soft and pleasant. The spirit, on the contrary, seeks the things that are contrary to the flesh and that can weaken it. And so it comes about that you should not think yourselves free just because you have ceased to be under the slavery of the Law, but should rather know that you are held fast by the law of nature; because it does not follow at once that, if the law does not command, nature too has ceased, so that, namely, works should not follow your will; but you are often compelled, the flesh fighting against the spirit, to do the things you do not will to do. Therefore, brothers, I beseech you not to give your freedom as an occasion for the flesh, but rather to serve the spirit, so that you may begin to do the things you will, and to owe nothing to the law, that is, not to be under the flesh. For you will truly be able to have in the Gospel the freedom of the abolished law when the flesh in no way compels you to do what you do not will, but, serving the spirit, you have shown that you are not under the Law.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.5.13-23":["For you have been called to freedom, brothers: only do not (it is understood) give freedom as an occasion to the flesh:\n\nbecause this is not found in the Greek, the Latin translator put it in. Since this passage is very obscure, I have decided to translate it word for word from the tenth book of the Stromata. Not that the individual parts cannot be explained in their own places and senses; but because, separated from the preceding subject matter, they form a single difficult body: and if they are understood just as they sound, they seem to clash with one another inconsequently and abruptly, and to be all in a ferment. These, then, are Origen's words: \"The passage is difficult, and it seems to us that it should be discussed thus. He who is free, and in a higher sense follows the spirit and the truth, despises both the preceding types and the letter: he ought not on that account to look down on the lesser, and give those who cannot perceive more sublimely an occasion to despair of themselves utterly. For although they are weak and are called flesh in comparison with the spirit, yet they are the flesh of Christ. For if he understands the mystery of love that serves the weaker, let him do something for the sake of the weak, lest in his knowledge a brother perish, for whom Christ died. Pay careful attention, therefore, whether this sense is woven together from what follows. You, he says, brothers, have been called to freedom: perhaps for this reason, that not all were able to grasp the calling of freedom. That is why you now hear: Only do not give freedom as an occasion to the flesh. For through love the greater ought to serve the lesser: because he who wishes to be greater will be the slave of all (Matt. 20; Mark 10). So let the spiritual man neither tear the flesh of Christ, nor give them an occasion to bite back at him when he provokes them, lest they be consumed by one another. He, then, who walks by the spirit, and pursues the words of the Scriptures by the spirit, must not fulfill the desire of their flesh. But if we understand what is said, 'Walk by the spirit, and you will not fulfill the desire of the flesh', in the simple way (as most people think), Paul will suddenly break out into these things against the argument and the hypothesis of the whole Epistle; for immediately there follows: 'But if you are led by the spirit, you are not under the Law.' And although up to this point the discourse holds together in some measure, again, if we follow the simple understanding, it suddenly carries us over to disordered precepts, discussing flesh and spirit, that is: 'Now the works of the flesh are manifest, this one and that one.' And on the other hand: 'But the fruit of the spirit is love', and the rest. But neither in these should we despair of the sequence; for the history of the divine volumes contains the works of the flesh, and it does not greatly help those who understand it just as it is written. For who will not be taught to serve licentiousness and to count fornication as nothing, when he has read of Judah going in to a harlot (Gen. 38), and that the patriarchs had many wives at the same time? How will he not be provoked to idolatry who supposes that the blood of bulls and the other victims of Leviticus signify nothing more than what sounds in the letter? And that the word of Scripture, set out in the open, teaches enmities is proved also from this passage: 'O wretched daughter of Babylon, blessed is he who will repay you your repayment with which you repaid us. Blessed is he who will seize and dash your little ones against the rock' (Ps. 136:8, 9). And from that one: 'In the morning I killed all the sinners of the land' (Ps. 100:8), and other things like these: namely, concerning strifes, jealousy, anger, quarrels, dissensions. To these things (unless we perceive something higher) the examples of the history provoke us more than they forbid them. Heresies too have arisen more from the carnal understanding of Scripture than from the work of our flesh, as very many suppose. Envy and drunkenness, moreover, we learn through the letter of the Law. Noah gets drunk after the flood, and the patriarchs with their brother Joseph in Egypt (Gen. 9 and 43). And carousings too are written in the book of Kingdoms, with David dancing and beating the timbrels before the Ark of the covenant of God (1 Sam. 6), and things like these. It is asked how the simple word of divine Scripture, which is called flesh, provokes us to sorceries and to evil arts, unless we cross over to the spirit of that same Scripture. I think this is what is signified by Daniel and the three youths being found ten times wiser than the magi, the enchanters, the Gazarenes and the Chaldeans, and by Moses being learned in all the wisdom and teaching of the Egyptians. It is therefore the occasion of many evils if anyone remains in the flesh of Scripture. Those who do such things will not obtain the kingdom of God. Wherefore let us seek the spirit of Scripture and its fruits, which are not said to be manifest. For it is with much labor and sweat, and with fitting cultivation, that the fruit of the spirit is found in the Scriptures. Hence I judge that Paul spoke carefully and cautiously when he said of the carnal senses of Scripture: 'Now the works of the flesh are manifest' (Gal. 5:19). But of the spiritual he did not set down, as there, 'the fruit of the spirit is manifest'; but thus: 'But the fruit of the Spirit is love, joy, peace', and the rest (ibid., 22). But if, leaving the types behind, we pass over to the truth and the spirit of Scripture, at once love is first opened to us, and stepping on from there to joy, we arrive at peace, through which we attain patience. And who would not be instructed in mercy and goodness, when he has understood that even those things in the Law which some think grim (I mean punishments and battles, and the overthrow of nations, and the threats against peoples through the prophets) are remedies rather than penalties? For the Lord will not be angry forever (Isa. 57). When these things, then, have been opened to us, we shall have a more reasonable faith, and temperance will accompany our corrected morals, and self-control and chastity will follow it: and after all these things the Law will begin to be for us.\" Thus far Origen.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.5.13":["To which we can add, saying that those called from the slavery of the Law to the freedom of the Gospel (to whom it was said above: \"Stand, and do not cling again to the yoke of slavery\") are even now admonished that, following the light yoke of Christ and the delightful precepts of the Gospel, they should by no means think it permitted to them to use this very freedom of living as an occasion of the flesh: that is, to live according to the flesh, to be circumcised according to the flesh; but rather to stand in the spirit, to cut off the foreskin of the flesh in the spirit, and, stretching toward the higher things of the spirit, to leave behind the lowliness of the letter. It can also be understood otherwise. Let someone learn: If I have ceased, O Paul, to be under the Law, and I have been called from slavery to freedom, then I ought to live as befits freedom, and not be bound by any precepts, but whatever has pleased me and my will has suggested, this I should do, fulfill, pursue. To this the Apostle replied: that we have indeed been called into the freedom of the spirit, but in such a way that freedom itself does not serve the flesh. Nor let us think that, because all things are lawful for us, all things are expedient: rather, because we have ceased to be slaves of the Law, having been made free, let us all the more serve one another through love, so that the precepts of the sprawling Law may be summed up in the single heading of love.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.5.14":["And love is so great a good that the whole law is summed up in it. In another place also the Apostle enumerates the good things of love, saying: \"It is not jealous, it does not act wrongly\" (1 Cor. 13:4). And having unfolded many things in between, at the end he concludes: \"It hopes all things, it endures all things, love never fails.\" And the Savior in the Gospel says that this is the sign of his disciple: if he loves his neighbor (Matt. 22). This indeed, I think, applies not only to men but also to angels. The same thing is said in other words: \"What you do not wish to be done to you, do not do to others; and whatever you wish that men should do to you, do you also likewise the same to them\" (ibid. 7:12). I do not wish my wife to be defiled by adultery, I do not wish my property to be plundered, I do not wish to be crushed by false testimony, and, to comprise everything in a brief word, I take it ill that anything unjust should be done to me. If, through love working in me, I either do or wish these same things for another, the whole law is fulfilled. Nor is it difficult to show how all the precepts, you shall not kill, you shall not commit adultery, you shall not steal, you shall not bear false witness, and the rest like these, are held together by the single observance of love. What is hard to show is how the sacrifices too that are prescribed in Leviticus, and the abstinence from foods or the permission of them, since some are called clean and others unclean, and also the continual recurrence of the solemn feasts through the years, are summed up in the one precept of love. Unless perhaps one should go over to this position, so as to affirm that the Law is spiritual, and that we served the images and patterns of heavenly things before the true High Priest came; and that after he, offering himself once as a victim, redeemed us by his own blood, all that variety and difficulty of the ancient Law was fulfilled in his love toward men. For the Father so loved the world that he gave his most dear and only-begotten Son for us. But for him who, once living by the spirit, has put to death the works of the flesh, and, being loved by the Savior, is called no longer a slave but a friend, the Law is no longer laid down, which was established for the ungodly and sinners, and for the insubordinate and the wicked. But now, while we do all the things that are more difficult, if only in small part, this one thing alone we do not do, which is both easier to do and without which all that we do is void. The body feels the injury of fasting; vigils waste the flesh; alms are sought by labor; blood in martyrdom, however much faith burns, is still not shed without pain and fear. There are those who do all these things: love alone is without labor. And because it alone makes the heart clean, it is assailed in us by the devil, so that we may not see God with a pure mind. For when I sit and speak against my brother, and lay a stumbling block against my mother's son (Ps. 49:20), when I am tormented by another's happiness and make another's good my evil, is not what follows fulfilled in me: \"If you bite and devour one another, see that you are not consumed by one another\"? The possession of love is rare. Who, following the Apostle, is willing himself to be anathema from Christ for his brothers? Who, mourning with those who mourn and rejoicing with those who rejoice, is wounded by another's wound? Who is slain by a brother's death? We are all lovers of ourselves rather than lovers of God. See how great is the good of love. If we undergo martyrdom in such a way that we wish our relics to be honored by men; if, pursuing the good opinion of the crowd, we fearlessly shed our blood and give away our substance even to the point of our own beggary, to this work is owed not so much a reward as a penalty, and these are the torments of faithlessness rather than a crown of victory.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.5.15":["But if you bite and devour one another, see that you are not consumed by one another.\n\nThis can also be taken simply: that we should not disparage one another, nor think to avenge ourselves with a curse, nor, when grieved, desire to grieve in return, and, like beasts, bite and likewise be bitten back, so that after the bites there follow destruction and consumption. But it is better, lest Paul, against reason and the sequence of the whole Epistle, should suddenly break out into extraneous precepts, to understand it in such a way that we refer everything to circumcision and the observance of the Law. If others, he says, trouble you, you for your part are thrown into turmoil. If, reading the whole old Scripture, you understand it just as it is written: \"An eye for an eye: a tooth for a tooth\" (Deut. 19:21), and anger desires vengeance, while vengeance inflicts pain (which the Law not only does not forbid but even commands, restoring righteousness through retaliation), it follows that the one stripped strips in turn, the one wounded wounds in turn, the one devoured bites back, and what seems to be righteousness is consumption, avenging not one but consuming both.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.5.16":["But I say: Walk in the Spirit, and you will not fulfil the desire of the flesh.\n\nThis too, in keeping with what precedes, is to be taken in two ways, so that we say that those who by the spirit have put to death the works of the flesh and have sown in the spirit, so as to reap eternal life from the spirit, whenever they feel the pleasure of the flesh tickling them, do not fulfil its desire (which indeed, if it has been satisfied, seems to flatter for a time), but rein it in by the spirit, and, according to the maxim of the Historian (Sallust), live rather by the command of the mind and the service of the body. And this as well: since the Law is spiritual (Rom. 7), and he is not a Jew who is one openly, but he who is one in secret, and circumcision is of the heart, in the spirit, not in the letter, we say that those walk in the spirit and do not fulfil the desire of the flesh who go out of Egypt spiritually, and draw spiritual food and drink from the spiritual rock, who are not judged in food, or in drink, or in respect of a feast day, or of the new moon and the sabbath, but walk spiritually in all things, not fulfilling the desire of the carnal law or of the letter, but reaping the fruits of spiritual understanding. A third interpretation too has been put forward by some on this passage, though one that does not differ much from the second: they assert that the desire of the flesh is in those who are little ones in Christ, but the way of the spirit in perfect men, and that the sense is: Walk in the gravity of the spirit, that is, in the way of the perfect man, and you will not do the desires of little ones.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.5.17":["For the flesh lusts against the spirit, but the spirit against the flesh. For these are opposed to one another, so that you do not do whatever things you will.\n\nThe flesh delights in things present and brief; the spirit in things perpetual and future. In the midst of this quarrel the soul stands in the middle, having indeed in its own power good and evil, to will and not to will, but not having this very willing and not willing as something perpetual: because it can happen that, when it has consented to the flesh and done its works, it again stings itself through repentance, is joined to the spirit and accomplishes its works. This, then, is what he says: For these are opposed to one another, that is, the flesh and the spirit; so that you do not do whatever things you will. Not that he has taken away from us our own free choice, by which we assent either to the flesh or to the spirit; but because what we do is not properly ours, but the work itself is reckoned either to the flesh or to the spirit. It is a matter of great labor and of excessive disputation, once the works of the flesh and of the spirit have been set out, to find some middle things which seem to belong neither to the flesh nor to the spirit. We are called carnal when we give ourselves wholly to pleasures; spiritual, when we follow the Holy Spirit going before us, that is, when we are wise with Him instructing us, and are taught with Him as our authority. The animal men (animales), I think, are the philosophers, who suppose their own thoughts to be wisdom, of whom it is rightly said: \"But the animal man does not receive the things that are of the spirit. For it is foolishness to him\". To make this clearer, let us take an example: let the flesh be called earth, the soul gold, the spirit fire. As long as the gold is in the earth, it loses its own name, and is called after the earth with which it is mixed. But when, separated from the soil, it has received both the appearance and the name of gold, it is indeed called gold, but not yet tested. If, however, it has been smelted and purged through fire, then it receives the splendor of gold and the dignity of its own adornment. So too the soul, standing between soil and fire, that is, between flesh and spirit, when it has handed itself over to the flesh, is called flesh; when to the spirit, it is called spirit. But if it has trusted its own thought, and has supposed that it finds the truth without the grace of the Holy Spirit, it is marked, like impure gold, with the name of the animal man.\n— Commentary on Galatians","And since above we had begun to expound this passage according to a twofold understanding, we must render what we omitted. The flesh lusts against the spirit, that is, the history and the fleshly understanding of Scripture fight against allegory and spiritual teaching. But the spirit against the flesh, that is, the more sublime things resist the lowly, the eternal the brief, the truth the shadow. And the fleshly sense of Scripture, which cannot be fulfilled (for we are not able to do all the things that are written), shows that we are not placed in our own power to fulfill the Law, since, even if we wish to follow the letter, impossibility does not allow it.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.5.18":["But if you are led by the spirit, you are not under the Law.\n\nBy spirit he means not that one of which the Apostle speaks in another place: \"The Spirit himself bears witness to our spirit that we are sons of God\" (Rom. 8:16), that is, not the spirit of the man which is in him, but the Holy Spirit: following him, we are made spiritual and cease to be under the Law. It must be noted, therefore, that here spirit is not named with the ἄρθρῳ (article) and with some addition, as we read in other places, the spirit of gentleness and the spirit of faith, but is called simply Spirit: and these minutiae, observed more in Greek than in our own language (for we have no ἄρθρα at all), seem to carry some weight. The question is raised in this place, if whoever is led by the spirit is not under the Law, whether Moses and the prophets were both driven by the spirit and lived under the Law, which the Apostle denies; or, having the spirit, were not under the Law, which the Apostle here affirms; or, which is the third possibility, living under the Law, did not have the spirit, which it is impious to believe of such great men. To this we shall answer briefly: it is not the same thing to be under the Law and to be as if under the Law, just as it is not the very same thing to be in the likeness of the flesh of sin and to be in the flesh of sin. Nor does a true serpent mean the same as the likeness of a bronze serpent, which Moses hung up in the desert (Num. 21). So, therefore, the holy prophets too, and Moses, walking by the spirit and living by the spirit, lived not under the Law but as if under the Law, so that they themselves seemed indeed to be under the Law, but might gain those who were under the Law and call them forth from the lowliness of the letter to the height of the spirit. For Paul too, who became a Jew to the Jews, and all things to all, that he might gain all (1 Cor. 9), did not say \"made under the Law\", but \"made as if under the Law\", to show that he had kept not the truth of the Law but its likeness. We seem to ourselves to have solved the question proposed. But what shall we do about that passage of Paul, where he says: \"But when the fullness of time came, God sent his Son, made of a woman, made under the Law, that he might redeem those who were under the Law\" (above)? For if Christ was under the Law, and not as if under the Law, that whole preceding discussion will become empty. But this objection too will be resolved from its own passage. For he who was made under the Law for this reason, that he might redeem those who were under the Law, surely, since he was free from the Law, subjected himself to the Law of his own will; and he was far freer than Paul, who testified that he had been not under the Law but as if under the Law. And just as he went down into the mire and abyss of death for our sake, who were praying and saying: \"Who will deliver me from the body of this death\" (Rom. 7:24)? so too he willed to be born of a woman and to be under the Law, that he might save those who had been born of a woman and under the Law. And certainly he was not born of a woman, that is, of a married woman, but of a virgin. But the virgin was called a woman by a loose use of the word, on account of those who did not know that she had been a virgin. So, then, just as \"woman\" was put for \"virgin\" on account of those who thought that holy Mary had a husband, so, on account of those who supposed that Christ was under the Law, not knowing that to those who were under the Law he had become as if under the Law, he too is said to have been made under the Law.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.5.19-21":["And to the Galatians it is numbered among the works of the flesh: \"Now the works of the flesh are manifest, which are fornication, uncleanness, licentiousness, idolatry, sorceries, enmities, contentions, rivalries, angers, quarrels, dissensions, heresies, envyings, drunkennesses, revellings, and other things like these, which I foretell to you as I have foretold: that those who do such things will not possess the kingdom of God\" (Gal. 5:19 ff.). In these it must be carefully observed that, just as the other vices which are numbered among the works of the flesh exclude us from the kingdom of God, so heresies too take the kingdom of God away from us; and it makes no difference in what way anyone is excluded from the kingdom, so long as he is excluded.\n— Commentary on Titus"],"GAL.5.19":["Now the works of the flesh are manifest, which are: fornication, impurity, licentiousness, idolatry, sorceries, enmities, strifes, jealousies, angers, quarrels, dissensions, heresies, envyings, drunkenness, carousings, and things like these, which I foretell to you, as I have also foretold: that those who do these things shall not possess the kingdom of God.\n\nAbove, when we were expounding about the flesh and the spirit, we had spoken of a threefold understanding: either that those are fleshly who, being little ones and bodily, could not take solid food in Christ and the nourishment of full age; or that those are carnal who, in the Jewish manner, follow only the history and the letter; or certainly, according to the simple sense, that flesh and spirit subsist in the fashioning of man, and that according to the difference of substance there are works either of the flesh or of the spirit. Now therefore the works of the flesh which are named here, namely fornication, impurity, licentiousness and the rest that follow, seem to me to refer rather to the simple understanding of flesh and spirit than to the flesh of the Law and to the little ones in Christ, although in that place above, where we translated word for word from the tenth book of Origen's Stromata, what may also be thought about these things has been set out. And when he says: Now the works of the flesh are manifest, he shows either that they are known to all, because they are plainly evil of themselves and to be shunned, so much so that even those who do them wish to hide what they do; or certainly that they are manifest only to those who have believed in Christ. For very many of the Gentiles glory in their shames, and think that if they have satisfied their pleasure, they have won a kind of victory of disgraceful deeds. But this too is elegantly done, that he placed works in the flesh and fruits in the spirit: because vices come to an end in themselves and perish, while virtues sprout with fruits and overflow. Nor let us think that the soul has no work, if vices are assigned to the flesh and virtues to the spirit. For the soul (as we said above), placed in a kind of middle ground, is either joined to the flesh, and it is said of it: \"My spirit shall not remain in these men, because they are flesh\" (Gen. 6:3); or it is united to the spirit and passes into the name of spirit. \"For he who cleaves to the Lord is one spirit\" (1 Cor. 6:17). So the first work of the flesh is fornication. He put the manifest ones at the beginning, so that we should not be in doubt about those in the middle. For whatever a man does is outside the body, but he who commits fornication sins against his own body. And we are not our own: for we have been bought with a price; let us glorify and carry God in our body. In this the fornicator is of greater guilt: because he takes the members of Christ and makes them the members of a harlot. For the two shall be in one flesh. He who is not a believer and does not believe in Christ makes his own members the members of a harlot; he who believes and commits fornication makes the members of Christ the members of a harlot. On the other hand, whether the unbeliever in his fornication violates a temple or builds a temple to an idol, I do not know. For demons are worshiped above all through vices. This one thing I know: that he who commits fornication after faith in Christ violates the temple of God. The second work of the flesh is called impurity, and licentiousness follows it as its companion. For just as in the old Law Scripture embraced in general terms the abominable crimes which are done in secret, and which it is most shameful even to name (lest both the mouth of the speaker and the ears of the hearers be defiled), saying: \"Modest\", or \"reverent, make the sons of Israel, away from all impurity\" (Lev. 15:31): so in this place he has named as impurity and licentiousness the other extraordinary pleasures, and even the works of marriage itself, if they are not done modestly and with decency, as under the eyes of God, so that only the begetting of children is served.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.5.20":["Fourth in the catalogue of the works of the flesh, idolatry holds its place. For he who has once given himself over to licentiousness and pleasure does not look back to the Creator. Besides, all idolatry delights in festivity, gluttony, the belly, and the things that are below the belly. And lest sorceries and the arts of witchcraft should perhaps seem not to be forbidden in the New Testament, they too are named among the works of the flesh. For it often happens to the wretched through magic arts both to love and to be loved. Enmity too, which is placed after sorceries: what guilt it carries is declared by its being placed right after a manifest crime. For as far as lies in us, we ought to be no one's enemy, but to have peace with all. But if, by speaking the truth, we earn some enemies, it is not so much we who are their enemies as they who are enemies of the truth. For what is also said to Abraham in Genesis: \"I will be an enemy to your enemies, and I will oppose those who oppose you\", is to be understood as above: not so much that Abraham was an enemy to them, as that they were enemies to the virtues and religion of Abraham, by which, having trampled on idols, he worshiped the God he had come to know. That too which is commanded to the people of Israel, that with everlasting hatred and a discord handed down to their descendants they should be enemies to the Midianites (Num. 31), is said as to those who were under the pedagogue, and who in another place deserved to hear: \"You shall hate your enemy\" (Matt. 5:43). Or certainly the dissension was made not so much between persons as between ways of life: so that, just as God usefully put enmities between the serpent and the woman, lest their friendship, through which man was cast out of paradise, should be harmful to man, so also in the Israelites and the Midianites it was rather a dissimilar way of life than one nation that was condemned. The seventh place among the works of the flesh is held by strife, set as it were in a sacred and eminent number among the vices. \"But the slave of the Lord ought not to quarrel, but to be gentle toward all, a teacher, long-suffering, instructing with gentleness even those who argue against him\" (2 Tim. 2:24, 25). After strife there follows, eighth, jealousy, which in the Greek language is called more expressively and more familiarly ζῆλος. From this evil, indeed, I do not know which of us is free. For both the patriarchs were jealous of their brother Joseph, and Miriam and Aaron, the prophetess of God and the priest, were deceived by such a passion against Moses (Gen. 37; Num. 12): so much so that she of whom Scripture had told, saying: \"And Miriam the prophetess, taking a timbrel\" (Exod. 15:20), and the rest, was afterwards cast out of the camp, defiled with the disease of leprosy, and marked a longer penance by a separation of seven days (Num. 12). Then anger follows, which does not work the righteousness of God (Jas. 1), and is a species of fury. Between irascibility and anger, however, there is this difference: that the irascible man is always angry, while the angry man is roused for a time. And I do not know who could possess the kingdom of God, when he who is angry is separated from the kingdom (Matt. 5). Quarrels too, which the Greeks, signifying something else, call ἐριθείας (since a quarrel is called μάχη), keep men from the kingdom of God. Now ἐριθεία is when someone, always ready to contradict, delights in another's vexation, and contends with womanish wrangling, and provokes the one who contends with him. Among the Greeks this is called by another name, φιλονεικία (love of contention). Dissensions too are works of the flesh: when someone, being by no means perfect in the same mind and the same judgment, says: \"I am of Paul, and I of Apollos, and I of Cephas, and I of Christ\" (1 Cor. 1:12). But this same dissension is also found within households among themselves: namely of husband against wife, father against son, brother against brother, fellow slave against fellow slave, soldier against tent-mate, craftsman against craftsman of the same trade. Sometimes it happens that dissension arises even in the expositions of the Scriptures: from which the heresies too, which are now placed among the works of the flesh, bubble up. For if the wisdom of the flesh is hostile to God (Rom. 8) (and all doctrines of falsehood that fight against God are hostile to him), consequently heresies also, being hostile to God, are referred to the works of the flesh. Now Αἵρεσις in Greek is named from choice: because, namely, each one chooses for himself that discipline which he thinks is better. Whoever, therefore, understands Scripture otherwise than the sense of the Holy Spirit, by which it was written, demands, although he has not withdrawn from the Church, can nevertheless be called a heretic, and is of the works of the flesh, choosing the things that are worse.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.5.21":["Envy follows heresies, which let us not think to be the same as zeal. For zeal can also be taken in a good sense, when someone strives to emulate the things that are better. But envy is tormented by another's happiness, and is split into a twofold passion: when either one is something oneself in that in which one does not wish another to be, or, seeing another to be better, one grieves that one is not like him. One of the moderns, translating a Greek verse, played finely in elegiac meter on envy, saying: Nothing is more just than envy, which straightway gnaws its own author and tortures his mind. The blessed Cyprian also wrote a very excellent book On Zeal and Envy, and whoever reads it will not hesitate to number envy among the works of the flesh. Between the envious man and the envied man there is this difference: that the envious man envies one more fortunate, while the envied man is he who suffers envy from another. The fourteenth place among the works of the flesh is held by drunkenness. For drunkards shall not possess the kingdom of God. And the Lord to the disciples: \"Take heed\", he says, \"lest perhaps your hearts be weighed down with wine and debauchery\" (Luke 21:34). By wine a man's senses are overturned, his feet stumble, his mind wavers, lust is kindled. Hence the Apostle cries out: \"And wine, in which is licentiousness\" (Eph. 5:18). Each one has the power of his own opinion. I follow the Apostle: that in wine there is licentiousness, in wine drunkenness. And that drunkenness and licentiousness are numbered among the works of the flesh, not even he who is overcome by these same passions can deny. And although some think I deserve blame in that book which I wrote on preserving virginity, because I said that young girls ought to flee wine like poison, I shall not repent of the opinion. For there it was rather the work of wine than a creature of God that was condemned by us; and we took away license from a virgin glowing with the natural heat of her age, lest under the pretext of drinking a little she should drink more and perish. Otherwise, we knew both that wine is consecrated into the blood of Christ and that Timothy was commanded to drink wine. Drunkenness, moreover, can come about as much from wine as from the other kinds of drink that are prepared in various ways; whence it is also said of the saint: \"He shall not drink wine and strong drink (siceram)\" (Luke 1:15). Sicera means \"drunkenness\". And lest anyone who does not drink wine should think that something else might be drunk by him, the pretext is excluded, since everything that can make drunk is taken away together with wine. The fifteenth, which is also the last among the works of the flesh, is carousing. \"For the people ate and drank, and rose up to play\" (Exod. 32:6). Licentiousness is always joined to drunkenness. A certain not undistinguished orator, describing a drunken man roused from sleep, said finely: \"Once roused, he could not sleep, and being drunk, he could not stay awake.\" By this saying he expressed that in a certain way he was neither dead nor alive. It would be long to unfold all the works of the flesh and make a catalogue of the vices. So he included them all in one word, saying: and things like these. Would that we could avoid them as easily as we understand them! \"I foretell\", he says, \"to you, as I foretold: that those who do such things shall not attain the kingdom of God\". Where he had foretold it before: \"Let not sin reign\", he says, \"in your mortal body, so as to obey its desires\" (Rom. 6:12). Sin has all these species, on distinguishing which we have perhaps lingered now longer than was fitting. Therefore in a soul in which sin has reigned, the kingdom of God cannot reign. \"For what partnership has righteousness with iniquity? What communion has light with darkness? What agreement has Christ with Belial?\" (2 Cor. 6:14, 15). And we think that we attain the kingdom of God if we are free from fornication, idolatry and sorceries. Behold, enmities, strife, anger, quarrel, dissension, drunkenness too, and the rest which we consider small, exclude us from the kingdom of God. Nor does it matter whether one is excluded from blessedness by one or by several, since all alike exclude. In the Latin codices adultery also, and unchastity, and murders are reported as written in this catalogue of vices. But it must be known that no more than fifteen works of the flesh are named, and these we have also discussed.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.5.22":["But the fruit of the spirit is love, joy, peace, long-suffering, kindness, goodness, faith, gentleness, self-control; against such there is no Law.\n\nAnd what other among the fruits of the spirit ought to hold the first place, if not love, without which the other virtues are not reckoned to be virtues, and from which are born all things that are good? Indeed, both in the Law and in the Gospel it holds the chief place: \"You shall love the Lord your God from your whole heart, and from your whole soul, and from your whole strength; and you shall love your neighbor as yourself\" (Deut. 6:5; Matt. 22:37). With how many good things love is filled we have both touched on briefly above, and now let it suffice to have said even a little: that love does not seek the things that are its own, but those of another (1 Cor. 13). And although someone, by his own fault, is an enemy to the one who loves, and strives to stir up his tranquillity into the waves of hatred, yet he is never disturbed; never does he judge a creature of God worthy of hatred. For love covers a multitude of sins (1 Pet. 4:8). That saying too which is spoken by the Savior, \"A good tree cannot bring forth evil fruits, nor an evil tree bring forth good fruits\" (Matt. 7:18), I think was pronounced not so much about men as about the fruits of the flesh and of the spirit: because neither can the spirit ever commit those vices which have been enumerated among the works of the flesh, nor can the flesh abound in these fruits which arise from the spirit. It can happen, however, through the negligence of the one who possesses it, that the spirit which dwells in a man does not have its fruits; and, on the contrary, that the flesh, its works having been put to death, ceases to sin. Yet they do not go so far that the tree of the spirit, when neglected, bears the works of the flesh, and the tree of the flesh, when cultivated, puts forth spiritual fruits. In the second place among the spiritual fruits joy has been set, which the Stoics too, who distinguish between words rather subtly, judge to be something other than gladness. For joy, they say, is the elation of a mind exulting over things that are worthy; but gladness is an unbridled elation of the mind, which knows no measure, and rejoices even in things that are mixed with vice. Others set pleasure over against joy: not that pleasure which stirs the body to lust, tickles the senses, and flatters with a sweet feeling, but another bearing the same name as this (ὁμώνυμον), which without moderation and without any seemliness of gladness lifts up its voice in laughter. But if this is true, and their distinction between words neither deceives nor is deceived, let us consider whether perhaps it was for this reason that it was said: \"There is no rejoicing for the ungodly, says the Lord\" (Isa. 57:21). At the same time this too must be noted, that joy follows after love. For he who loves anyone always rejoices in his happiness. And if he sees him deceived by some error and fallen on the slippery ground of sins, he will grieve indeed, and will hasten to rescue him; but sadness will not be able to change his joy, since he knows that none of the rational creatures perishes forever before God. The third fruit of the spirit is peace, from which Solomon too, who went before as a type of Christ, received his name. And concerning the Church the Psalmist sings: \"His place has been made in peace\" (Ps. 75:2). And among the eight blessings of the Gospel it is written: \"Blessed are the peacemakers, for they shall be called sons of God\" (Matt. 5:9). In the first psalm of the steps, too, it is sung: \"With those who hated peace I was peaceable\" (Ps. 119:6). And let us not think that peace is to be sought only in this, that we do not quarrel with another; rather, the peace of Christ, that is, our inheritance, is with us then, if a tranquil mind is disturbed by no passions. After peace follows long-suffering (longanimitas), or patience (patientia), because we can render μακροθυμίαν in either way. Contrary to this is faint-heartedness, of which it is written: \"The faint-hearted man is exceedingly foolish; but he who is patient and bears all things is a wise man\" (Sir. 7). And with intensification (ἐπιτάσει) he is called \"very wise\", as is also written in Proverbs: \"A long-suffering man is great in prudence\" (Prov. 14:29). Kindness (benignitas) also, or sweetness (suavitas) (since among the Greeks χρηστότης sounds as both), is a mild virtue, winning, tranquil, and fitted for the fellowship of all good men, inviting to familiarity with itself, sweet in speech, temperate in manners. Finally the Stoics too define it thus: Kindness is a virtue disposed of its own accord to doing good. Goodness is not much different from kindness, because it too seems disposed to doing good. But it differs in this, that goodness can be rather sombre, and with a brow furrowed by severe manners it can indeed do good and grant what is asked, yet not be sweet in fellowship and invite all by its own sweetness. This too the followers of Zeno define thus: Goodness is a virtue that profits; or, a virtue from which usefulness arises; or, a virtue for its own sake; or, a disposition which is the fountain of usefulnesses.\n\nAmong the fruits of the spirit, faith possesses the seventh and more sacred place, which elsewhere too is set among three: hope, faith, love. Nor is it any wonder if hope is not mentioned in this catalogue, since what is hoped for is in faith, and the Apostle, writing to the Hebrews, defines it thus: \"Now faith is the substance of things to be hoped for, the proof of things not yet appearing\" (Heb. 11:1). For that which we hope will come, and which is not yet in the present, we possess by faith, hoping that we hold what we believe. It is asked also how faith has been set in love. He who loves never judges himself to be wronged; he never suspects anything except that he loves and is loved. But when love has gone far away, faith departs along with it.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.5.23":["After faith gentleness is counted, which is opposed to anger, quarrels and dissensions, and is never provoked to its contraries, truly sprouting forth good fruits from the good tree of the spirit. Through this Moses, the servant of God, deserved to receive the testimony of Scripture, which says: \"Moses was gentle, beyond all men upon the earth\" (Num. 12:3). Upon the earth, he says. For he could not be beyond those who saw God face to face, because we are often compelled to do many things through the weakness of the flesh. Concerning David too (although many judge that it was prophesied of our Lord, which we also do not deny), the Holy Spirit sings, as a type of the one to come: \"Remember, O Lord, David, and all his gentleness\" (Ps. 131:1). Whose gentleness shone forth more than all toward Saul, Absalom and Shimei (1 Sam. 24; 2 Sam. 15)? When the one wished to kill him; the other, plotting a revolution, strove to cheat him of his rule; the other, throwing stones at him and tossing up dust, shouted and said: \"Go out, go out, you wicked man\" (ibid., 16:7). Self-control is placed last among the fruits of the spirit. This we ought to understand not only of chastity, but also of food and drink, of anger too and agitation of mind, and of the lust for disparaging others. Between modesty and self-control, however, there is this difference: that modesty is found in perfect men and those of consummate virtue, of whom the Savior says: \"Blessed are the meek, for they shall possess the earth\" (Matt. 5:4); and of himself: \"Learn from me, for I am meek, and humble, and gentle of heart\" (Matt. 11:29). Self-control, on the other hand, is indeed on the road of virtue, but has not yet reached the goal, because desires still arise in the thought of the one who controls himself and pollute the ruling part of the mind, although they do not overcome him nor drag the one who thinks them into the deed. And self-control is necessary not only in desires and cupidity, but also in the three remaining passions, namely grief, gladness and fear. Against such fruits of the spirit there is no Law. \"For the Law is not laid down for the righteous man, but for the wicked and the insubordinate, for the ungodly and sinners\" (1 Tim. 1:9). The Law says to me: \"You shall not commit adultery; you shall not kill; you shall not speak false testimony; you shall not defraud; you shall not desire what belongs to others; you shall not swear falsely; you shall not steal\" (Exod. 20:12 ff.). If I do none of these things, because love, the fruit of the spirit, reigns in me, the precepts of the Law are superfluous to me. Finally, the wise men of the world too held this opinion about philosophy: that what the public laws compel men to do by necessity, philosophy persuades them to do by will.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.5.24":["But those who are Christ's have crucified the flesh with vices and lusts.\n\nOrigen, linking this passage with the preceding ones, reads it thus: \"Against such there is no Law, who have crucified the flesh of Christ with vices and lusts\": so that he is not saying, as it sounds in the Latin, that those who are Christ's have crucified their own flesh with vices and lusts, but that the flesh of Christ has been crucified by them with vices and lusts. And he asks how, in the case of those who have the fruits of the spirit, and against whom the Law has ceased to be, the crucifixion of the Lord's flesh is set down as praise, when in Hebrews it is set down as reproach: \"Crucifying again in themselves the Son of God, and holding him up to display\" (Heb. 6:6). For \"crucifying again\" there is in the Greek, better, a single compound word, ἀνασταυροῦντες, which we can render \"re-crucifying\" (recrucifigentes). First, then, it must be noted that to crucify is one thing, to re-crucify another. Next, that to re-crucify the Son of God is not the same thing as to crucify the flesh of Christ with vices and lusts. For the flesh of Christ is not principally and properly the Son of God, but Jesus Christ, who, though he was in the beginning with the Father, God the Word, was made flesh and emptied himself, taking the form of a slave, so that he might crucify the flesh and despoil the principalities and powers, triumphing over them on the tree (Phil. 2), so that that saying of the Apostle might be fulfilled: \"For in that he died, he died to sin once\" (Rom. 6:10). Therefore, if our bodies are members of Christ, it follows that our flesh too is the flesh of Christ, which we crucify, mortifying through it, upon the earth, fornication, impurity, passion, evil desire and avarice; and it is now set down in praise of us, who have crucified the flesh of Christ Jesus with vices and lusts, and always carry about the mortification of Jesus in our body, so that his life also may be made manifest in our flesh. Yet it is no small labor so to live in the present age that even now the life of Jesus is made manifest in our flesh. For thus will our mortal bodies also be quickened through the spirit dwelling in us. Where the Latin translator put \"vices\", in the Greek παθήματα, that is, \"passions\", is read. And since passion can signify both pain and the other necessities of the poor body, the Apostle cautiously added \"desires\", so that he might not seem to deny the nature of the body in spiritual men, but the vices. And let this warning be given thus if we follow the common edition (Vulgatam editionem), reading: But those who are Christ's have crucified the flesh with vices and lusts: so that we say they have crucified not the flesh of Christ but their own. I had almost forgotten the second interpretation. For I had said beforehand that all that follows is to be referred to the Law and to circumcision. The sense, therefore, is this: those in whom is the fruit of the spirit, love, joy and the rest, have crucified the bodily understanding of Scripture, which is now called the flesh of Christ, with its passions and desires, which breed fuel for vices in little ones and sucklings. He has crucified the flesh of Christ who does not wage war according to the flesh of history, but follows the spirit of allegory going before him.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.5.25":["If we live by the spirit, let us also walk by the spirit.\n\nLet us use this testimony against those who refuse to understand the Scriptures spiritually. But who is it that lives by the spirit, if not our hidden man, who is also at times accustomed to live according to the flesh? But when he has lived by the spirit, he walks by the spirit. When he has chosen to walk in the flesh, though living he is dead. The man perfect in Christ always lives in the spirit: he obeys the spirit, he never lives in the flesh. And on the contrary: whoever has given himself wholly to the flesh and has consigned himself to the passions never lives in the spirit. Between these are those in the middle, whom we can call neither spiritual nor fleshly; rather, those who, wavering between virtues and vices, are now drawn back to better things, and are spirit; now are tripped up by the slipperiness of the flesh, and are flesh.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.5.26":["Let us not become desirous of empty glory, provoking one another, envying one another.\n\nThe single word κενόδοξοι (vainglorious) among the Greeks the Latin translator has expressed by a circumlocution of three words. How many definitions and meanings glory has, both the innumerable books of the philosophers and the two volumes that Cicero wrote on glory give evidence. But since we are attempting to discuss not the etymologies of words but the sense of Scripture, we will connect this passage with what precedes in this way: If we live by the spirit, let us obey the spirit, serving one another not at all through the Law but through love. We ought not to contend about the interpretation of the Scriptures and say: \"Circumcision is better\"; \"No, rather uncircumcision\"; \"History is to be despised and allegory followed\"; \"Nay, allegory is empty and shadowy, and fixed by no roots of truth.\" From this it comes about that envyings arise against one another. \"For they wish\", he says, \"to shut you out, that you may emulate them\": wishing not so much to teach the truth of the Law as to win. But so that we may not pass over the word glory entirely untouched, leaving the philosophers their trifles, let us go over some points from the Scriptures. The name of glory denotes the opinion of the crowd and praise sought through the favor of men, where it is said: \"But they do all things that they may be glorified by men\" (Matt. 23:5). And elsewhere: \"How can you believe, seeking glory from one another\" (John 5:44)? Further, in a good sense, in the same place: \"And not seeking the glory that is from him who alone is\". From this we understand that the same word signifies now a virtue, now a vice. If I seek glory from men, it is a vice; if from God, it is a virtue, for he also exhorts us to true glory, saying: \"But those who glorify me I will glorify\". Glory also signifies something else in the divine Scriptures, when something more august and more divine presents itself to the gaze of men. The glory of the Lord was seen in the tabernacle (Exod. 40), and in the temple that was built by Solomon there was glory (1 Kings 8:10-11), and in the face of Moses, when he did not know that his countenance had been glorified; and of this glory of the countenance I think the Apostle too speaks: \"But we all, contemplating the glory of the Lord with unveiled face, are transformed into the same image from glory to glory, as by the spirit of God\" (2 Cor. 3:18). And the Savior himself has been called the splendor of glory and the figure of the substance of God (Heb. 1). Stephen too saw the glory of God, and Jesus standing at his right hand (Acts 7). Now, that we too may take upon ourselves the license of coining names (for new things, as someone says, new names must be coined): since here it is said, Let us not become desirous of empty, or of vacant glory, let us assert that those are desirous of full glory who desire the glory of God, and the praise worthy of virtue, and a sight showing forth something more divine. Hence too in very many places our translators have rendered \"majesty\" in place of glory. For a long time now I have been longing to burst out into words, but I am held back by fear of speaking. Yet I will speak, and I will not keep silent about my own passion, a passion almost common to all: not about riches, not about power, not about beauty and comeliness of body, for these are plainly named among the works of the flesh. Almsgiving, if it is done for the sake of praise, is empty glory; so is long prayer, and the pallor that follows from fasting. These are not my words, but those of the Savior thundering in the Gospel (Matt. 6). Chastity itself too, in marriage, in widowhood, in virgins, often seeks human applause; and, what I have long been afraid to say, yet it must be said, martyrdom itself, if it is undergone so that we may be held in admiration and praise by the brothers: the blood has been shed in vain. Let the Apostle speak, let the vessel of election speak: \"If I hand over my body that I may glory, but have not love, it profits me nothing\" (1 Cor. 13:3). To him who had said: \"I know a man in Christ fourteen years ago, whether in the body, I do not know, or out of the body, I do not know, God knows, caught up as far as the third heaven\" (2 Cor. 12:2); and a little after: \"Caught up into paradise, he heard secret words which it is not lawful for a man to speak\": to him, I say, who labored more than all, lest the greatness of the revelations should exalt him, there was given a goad of his flesh, an angel of Satan, to buffet him, so that he might not be lifted up. And indeed three times he asked the Lord that it might depart from him; but it was said to him: \"My grace is sufficient for you: for power is perfected in weakness\". What is so much the work of God as to read the Scriptures, to preach in the Church, to desire the priesthood, to minister before the altar of the Lord? Yet even these, unless one guards his heart with all diligence, spring from the desire for praise. You may see very many (as Tully too says) inscribing their books \"On despising glory\", and, for the sake of glory, setting the titles of their own name at the head. We interpret the Scriptures; we often turn the stylus; we write things worthy to be read; and unless these are done for the sake of Christ, but for remembrance among posterity and fame among the peoples, the whole labor will be made void, and we shall be like a sounding drum and a clanging cymbal (1 Cor. 13). You may see very many contending among themselves about the Scriptures and making the word of God an athletic arena: they provoke one another, and if they are beaten, they envy; for they are desirous of empty glory. I know that in the Latin codices, in the testimony we set down above, \"If I hand over my body that I may glory\", they have \"that I may burn\" (ardeam) in place of \"that I may glory\" (glorier); but because of the similarity of the word, by which among the Greeks \"I may burn\" and \"I may glory\", that is, καυθήσομαι and καυχήσομαι, are distinguished by part of a single letter, the error has grown up among our people. But among the Greeks themselves, too, the copies differ.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.6.1":["Brothers, and if a man has been overtaken in some fault, you who are spiritual, instruct such a one in the spirit of gentleness, considering yourself, lest you too be tempted.\n\nPaul knew that he was a worshiper of that God who does not want the death of the sinner, but his repentance (Ezek. 18, 33), and that, the Trinity excepted, every creature, even if it does not sin, nevertheless can sin; so he exhorts even the one who is spiritual, through fear for himself lest he sin, to stretch out his hand to one who is falling. And he fittingly calls the one overtaken in a fault a \"man,\" one who can die, showing by the very name the frailty of his condition, so that he who, as a man deceived by error and plunged into a whirlpool, cannot raise himself up without help and a helper may be worthy of pardon. But to the spiritual one \"man\" is not added; rather, it is commanded as though to God, that he should instruct the man overtaken in a fault, or (as the Greek has it better) perfect him, in the spirit of mildness. Now he who is being perfected does not lack everything, but some things. In short, if he has gone astray overtaken not by many sins but by some vice, let the spiritual man apply the spirit of mildness and gentleness in correcting the sinner, lest he wish to correct the one who errs while rigid, while angry and severe; but let him call him forth, pledging salvation, promising pardon; let him bring forward the testimony of Christ, that he invites those weighed down by the heavy burden of the law and of sins to his sweet yoke and light burden, that they may learn that he is humble and meek and gentle of heart (Matt. 11), and may find rest for their souls. Let us use this testimony against the heretics who, inventing fables of diverse natures, say that the spiritual is a good tree and never bears bad fruit. Behold, the Apostle, whose authority they themselves also follow, says that those who are spiritual can sin, if through the loftiness of their heart they are puffed up and fall. This we too likewise confess; and also that the χοϊκοὺς (\"earthly\") become spiritual, if they turn back to better things. There may be set against us what is written to the Corinthians: \"What do you want? Shall I come to you with a rod, or in love and the spirit of gentleness\" (1 Cor. 4:21)? For if there he says that he comes to sinners not in the spirit of gentleness but with a rod, how is it that here, to those who have been overtaken in some sin, he applies not the rod but the spirit of gentleness? But there it is said to those who, after their sin, not perceiving their error, were unwilling to be subject to their superiors and to be corrected by repentance. Where, however, the sinner, understanding his wound, hands himself over to the physician to be cured, there the rod is not necessary, but the spirit of mildness. But perhaps this too may be asked: if someone ought to instruct a sinner in the spirit of mildness for this reason, that he considers himself lest he too be tempted, then the righteous man, who is certain of his own mind, who knows that he cannot fall, ought not to instruct the sinner in the spirit of mildness? To this we shall say that even if the righteous man has conquered, knowing with how much labor he overcame, he will all the more grant pardon to the one who sins. For the Savior too was tempted for this reason, in all things like us, without sin, so that he might be able to suffer with us and to grieve with our infirmities, taught by his own example how difficult victory is in the flesh. If anyone has remained a virgin right up to old age, let him pardon the one who was once deceived by the heat of youth, knowing with what difficulties he passed through that age. If anyone, tortured for confessing the name of Christ, sees another, in torments, deny, let him have compassion on the wounds of the denier, and let him marvel not so much that the other was conquered as that he himself conquered. Notice also the caution of the writer, in that he did not say: Considering yourself, lest you fall; but, lest you too be tempted. For to be conquered or to conquer is sometimes in our power; but to be tempted is in the power of the tempter. For if the Savior was tempted, who can be secure that he will cross the seas of this life untempted? Let those who think that Paul said out of humility, and not truly, \"Even if I am unskilled in speech, yet not in knowledge\", defend the coherence of this passage. For according to proper order he ought to have said: \"You who are spiritual, instruct such a one in the spirit of mildness, considering yourselves, lest you too be tempted\"; and not to bring in the singular number after the plural. A Hebrew of the Hebrews, then, and one who was most learned in his native tongue, was not able to express deep meanings in a foreign language; nor did he care greatly about words, since he had the sense safe. These things according to the simple understanding. But, so that we may also pursue the order of the second exposition, this passage is to be opened up from the end of the Epistle to the Romans. For there, when he was likewise writing about the foods and observances of the Jews, and was describing those who despised the precepts of the Law according to the letter as strong and perfect, but those who were still led by the ancient custom as weak and little ones, and saw that there was a quarrel between the spiritual and the fleshly, he admonished the spiritual not to despise the fleshly, and said: \"But take up the one who is weak in faith, not in disputes over thoughts. For one believes he may eat all things; but he who is weak, let him eat vegetables. Let him who eats not despise him who does not eat; and let him who does not eat not judge him who eats, for God has taken him up. Who are you to judge another's slave? To his own master he stands or falls. But he will stand: for God is able to make him stand\" (Rom. 14:1 ff.). And after discussing much in between on this sense, he added at the end: \"Do not, for the sake of food, destroy the work of God\". And again: \"But we who are stronger ought to bear the weaknesses of the weak, and not to please ourselves. Let each of you please his neighbor for good edification\" (Rom. 15:1, 2).\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.6.2":["Bear one another's burdens, and so you will fulfil the law of Christ.\n\nThat sin is a burden, the Psalmist too testifies, saying: \"My iniquities are lifted up over my head; like a heavy burden they have weighed heavily upon me\" (Ps. 37:5). And Zechariah saw iniquity, in the form of a woman, sitting upon a talent of lead (Zech. 5). This burden the Savior bore for us, teaching us by his own example what we ought to do. For he himself carries our iniquities and grieves for us (Isa. 53), and those who are weighed down by the burden of sins and of the Law he invites to the light burden of virtue, saying: \"My yoke is sweet, and my burden light\" (Matt. 11:30). Whoever, therefore, does not despair of a brother's salvation, but stretches out his hand to him as he pleads, and, as far as lies in him, weeps with the one who weeps, is weak with the weak, and judges the sins of others to be his own, he through love fulfils the law of Christ. What is the law of Christ? \"This is my commandment, that you love one another\" (John 13:34). What is the law of the Son of God? \"Love one another, as I also have loved you\". How did the Son of God love us? \"There is no greater love than this, that someone lay down his life for his friends\" (John 15:13). Whoever has no clemency, and has not put on the bowels of mercy and of tears, though he be spiritual, will not fulfil the law of Christ. But let us also join this passage with what went before. For we follow a twofold understanding. If anyone is weak in the faith, and is still nourished on the milk of infancy, and cannot so quickly pass from the observance of the Law to the spiritual mysteries, you who are stronger, bear his burdens, so that the brother for whom Christ died may not perish through your knowledge. He also bears a brother's need who helps a poor man weighed down by the burden of want, and makes himself friends of the mammon of iniquity (Luke 16); and him Christ addresses after the resurrection: \"Come to me, you blessed of my Father, possess the kingdom prepared for you from the foundation of the world. For I was hungry, and you gave me to eat; I was thirsty, and you gave me to drink\" (Matt. 25:34, 35). In accordance with this sense Paul also, teaching Timothy in another Epistle, added: \"Command the rich of this age not to be high-minded\" (that is to say, \"not to be proud\"), \"nor to hope in the uncertainty of riches, but in God, who furnishes all things abundantly for enjoyment; to do good, to be rich in good works, to give readily, to share, to lay up for themselves a good foundation for the future, that they may lay hold of the true life\" (1 Tim. 6:17-19). Whoever has laid hold of the true life, that is, of him who says: \"I am the life\" (John 14:6), has fulfilled the law of Christ, which tends toward life.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.6.3":["For if anyone thinks himself to be something, when he is nothing, he leads himself astray.\n\nIf anyone will not carry the burdens of others, and, without mercy, is content with his own work and virtue alone, seeking not the things that are another's but the things that are his own, that is, a lover of himself alone and not also of God, he himself leads himself astray. Now this can be read and punctuated in two ways: either, \"if anyone thinks himself to be something, when he is nothing\"; or thus: \"If anyone thinks himself to be something\", so that we afterwards add, \"when he is nothing, he leads himself astray\". And this difference resounds more in the Greek than in the Latin. The sense of the first punctuation is this: He who thinks himself to be something, and is nothing, deceives himself. The second sense is loftier, and is the one that pleases us more: If anyone thinks himself to be something, in that he supposes himself to be something, and judges himself not by clemency toward his neighbor but by his own work and labor, content with his own virtue alone, that man by this very arrogance becomes nothing, and he himself deceives himself: which is better said in the Greek, φρεναπατᾷ, that is, he deceives his own mind; for which the Latin translator put \"he leads himself astray\" (se ipse seducit). And he deceives his own mind who supposes himself to be wise, and, according to Isaiah, is wise in himself and understanding in his own sight (Isa. 5). With reference to circumcision and the Law, the understanding of this passage is connected thus: He who is spiritual, and has no mercy toward his neighbor, despising the lowly because he himself is loftier, he himself deceives himself, not knowing that this is the law of the spirit, that we love one another.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.6.4":["But let every man prove his own work: and so he shall have glory in himself only, and not in another.\n\nThis is the sense: You who reckon yourself spiritual, and are stronger by reason of another's weakness, ought not to consider the feebleness of the one who lies fallen, but your own strength. For if another cannot pass perfectly from Judaism to Christianity, it does not follow that you are therefore a perfect Christian; but if your own conscience does not reproach you, you have glory in yourself and not in another. An athlete is not strong because he has defeated a weak man and overcome the feeble limbs of his opponent; but if he is sturdy, and glories in his own strength, not in the weakness of another. It can also be understood in another way: he who has the consciousness of a good work, and, considering himself, finds no fault with his own work, ought not to glory about this before another, and pour out his praise abroad, and share it with everyone, and seek a boast from the favor of men; but let him have glory in himself, and say: \"But far be it from me to glory, except in the cross of our Lord Jesus Christ, through whom the world is crucified to me, and I to the world\". He who seeks glory from another, to him the world has not been crucified, nor has he himself been crucified with Christ. For he has received his reward, what he was seeking from men.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.6.5":["For each one shall bear his own burden.\n\nThis seems to contradict what went before, where he says: \"Bear one another's burdens\": for if each one shall bear his own burden, one will not be able to bear another's burdens. But we must see that there he commanded that in this life we should support one another when we sin, and be a help to each other in the present age; here, however, he speaks of the Lord's judgment upon us: that it is not by another's sin and by comparison with someone worse, but according to our own work, that we are judged by him either sinners or saints, each one receiving according to his own work. Though obscurely, we are taught through this little saying a new doctrine that lies hidden: that while we are in the present age we can help one another, whether by prayers or by counsels; but when we have come before the judgment seat of Christ, neither Job nor Daniel nor Noah can intercede for anyone, but each one bears his own work (Ezek. 14).\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.6.6-7":["Something of the kind the Apostle too, as we have said already, commands the Galatians: Let him who is catechized share with him who catechizes him in all good things, and he exhorts disciples to the refreshment of their teachers; and since anyone could plead poverty and evade the command, before such a one could put it forward he resolves the impending question, saying: Do not be deceived; God is not mocked; for what a man sows, that he will also reap; and the sense is: in vain do you plead want when your conscience holds something else; you can deceive me as I exhort you, but know that however much you sow, so much you will also reap.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"GAL.6.6":["But let him who is catechized in the word share with him who catechizes him in all good things.\n\nMarcion interpreted this passage in such a way as to think that the faithful and the catechumens ought to pray together, and that the teacher ought to share in prayer with his disciples; he was especially carried away by the fact that there follows in all good things. Yet surely, if the discourse had been about prayer, the command ought not to have been given to him who is catechized, but to him who catechizes him, that is, not to the disciple, but to the teacher. Then, too, the other things that follow do not agree with his exposition: \"Whatever a man has sown, that he will also reap\". And: \"But in doing good let us not fail: for in its own time we shall reap, not failing\". This, therefore, is the sense: since above he had commanded the spiritual to instruct in a spirit of gentleness those who had been overtaken in some fault, and to bear one another's burdens, fulfilling the law of Christ, now, on the contrary, he commands those who were still weaker, and disciples, and carnal, that, just as they themselves reap spiritual things from their teachers, so they should furnish carnal things to their teachers, who, giving themselves wholly to divine learning and study, are in need of the necessities of this life; and so that may come to pass which is written of the manna: \"He who had much did not have too much; and he who had little did not have too little\" (2 Cor. 8:15). Now in the present passage he has called good things, according to the custom of the crowd and common usage, food and clothing and the other things that men count among good things. \"For having food and clothing, with these we are content\" (1 Tim. 6:8). Nor is it surprising if Paul designated the things that were necessary to the body by the name of good, since even our Savior, to those who had not yet ascended to the summit of the virtues but were still walking on a lower level, and who were asking that faith be added to them, said: \"If therefore you, though you are evil, know how to give good gifts to your children, how much more will your heavenly Father give good things to those who ask him\" (Matt. 7:11)! I think that Job too, when he was speaking to his wife as to one of the foolish women, spoke of bodily riches with regard to her, who thought so: \"If we have received good things from the hand of the Lord\" (Job 2:10); and again, of the straits and afflictions and temptation, which bring victory: \"Why should we not endure evil?\" Whereas, surely, evil and good are placed not in riches and afflictions, but in virtues and vices, as the righteous man says in the psalm: \"Who is the man who wants life, desires to see good days? Keep your tongue from evil, and let your lips not speak deceit. Turn away from evil, and do good\" (Ps. 33:13 ff., and 36:27). Properly, evil is called that which must be avoided; good, that which we ought to do. That rich man too in the Gospel, who did not have the knowledge of evil and good, rightly reckoned the fruitfulness of his fields to be good things, saying: \"Soul, you have good things laid up for many years: rest, eat, drink, and be merry\" (Luke 12:19). And the other, who lay in purple and abounded in delights, hears from Abraham in the underworld: \"You received good things in your life\" (Luke 16:25). This also must be considered, whether perhaps it may also be understood that a command is given to the disciples to share the word with those who instruct them, to obey them, and to show themselves teachable and compliant; yet in those things that are good, that are spiritual, and not perverted by heretical or Jewish depravity.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.6.7":["Be not deceived, God is not mocked: for what things a man sows, these he shall also reap.\n\nForeseeing in the spirit that those who are taught, and who ought to supply their teachers with expenses and the necessities of life, could plead poverty and say: My field has dried up this year with drought; hail has crushed my vineyard; the taxes have carried off the revenues that there could have been; I have nothing from which to give what is commanded, he added: Be not deceived, God is not mocked. He knows, he says, your hearts; he is not ignorant of your means. A plausible excuse can somehow placate a man; it cannot deceive God. And at the same time he exhorts them to render what has been commanded, calling it seed, so that one should not think lost what he is going to receive back with multiplied interest. To the Corinthians too he taught the reckoning of what is given and received by a similar example: \"He who sows sparingly shall also reap sparingly: and he who sows in blessing shall also reap of blessing. Each one as he has purposed in his heart, not out of sadness or of necessity: for God loves a cheerful giver\" (2 Cor. 9:6-7).\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.6.8":["For he who sows in his own flesh, from the flesh shall also reap corruption. But he who sows in the spirit, from the spirit shall reap life everlasting.\n\nEverything that we speak, do and think is sown in two fields, the flesh and the spirit. If the things brought forth from hand, mouth and heart are good, sown in the spirit, they will overflow with the fruits of eternal life. If they are evil, received by the field of the flesh, they will sprout for us a harvest of corruption. Another way: he who understands the law carnally also expects carnal promises, and things that are corrupted in the present age. But he who is a spiritual hearer sows in the spirit, and from the spirit shall reap life everlasting. At the same time let us note the sequence of the discourse and link it with what went before: the one who sows in the spirit is called a man, and when he has begun to reap life everlasting, he perhaps ceases to be a man. Cassianus, who, bringing in a putative (merely apparent) flesh of Christ, judges every union of male with female unclean, the most vehement heresiarch of the Encratites, used against us, on the occasion of the present testimony, an argument of this sort: If anyone sows in the flesh, from the flesh he shall reap corruption; but he who is joined to a woman sows in the flesh; therefore he too who has intercourse with a wife, and sows in her flesh, from the flesh shall reap corruption. He will be answered, first, that Paul did not say, \"he who sows in the flesh,\" but, in his own flesh. Yet no one lies with himself and sows in his own flesh. Next, even if we grant him, over and above, this observation which we have noted, in his own flesh, it must be added that, by his reckoning, those also who eat and drink and sleep and do anything for the refreshment of the body sow in the flesh, and from it reap corruption. But if he takes refuge in saying that those who, whether they drink or eat or sleep, nevertheless do everything with reason in the name of the Lord do not sow in the flesh but in the spirit, we too will answer him in like manner that those also who follow God's first sentence, acting with reason: \"Increase and multiply, and fill the earth\" (Gen. 1:22), sow not in the flesh but in the spirit. And so his syllogism, futile and tottering, deceives the hearer at first by a sophism; but, carefully inspected, it is easily resolved. For we cannot say that Abraham, Isaac and Jacob, and the other holy men who were born of the promise, and even the Lord's forerunner himself, because he was born in the flesh, sprouted from a seed of corruption. This also must likewise be observed, that he who sows in the flesh is set down with the addition \"of his own flesh,\" whereas he who sows in the spirit is not said to sow in his own spirit, but simply in the spirit. For he who sows good things sows not in anything of his own, but in the spirit of God, from which he is also to reap life eternal.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.6.9":["But in doing good let us not fail: for in its own time we shall reap, if we do not fail.\n\nHe exhorts to zeal for perseverance those who expect the reward of a good work in this life, not knowing that, just as with seed there is one time for sowing and another for harvest, so also in the present life the works are the sowing (which are reaped either in the spirit or in the flesh), while the harvest is the judgment to come; and that according to the quality or the diversity of the sowing we make diverse harvests, the hundredfold, and the sixtyfold, and the thirtyfold fruit, a crop which no one who fails can reap. \"For he who has persevered to the end, he shall be saved\" (Matt. 10:22). As it is also commanded in another place: \"Be not failing\" (Isa. 5). But what sort of thing is it that, while sinners daily increase in evil works, we should grow weary in good work?\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.6.10":["Therefore, while we have time, let us work good toward all, but most of all toward those of the household of faith.\n\nThe time of sowing, as we have said, is the present time and the life which we run. In this life we may sow what we will; when this life has passed, the time for working is taken away. Hence the Savior too says: \"Work while it is day: the night will come, when no one will any longer be able to work\" (John 9:4). The Word of God, the true sun, has risen for us, and the beasts have gathered together, withdrawing into their dens: let us go forth as men to our work, and let us labor until the evening, as is mystically sung in the psalm: \"You have set darkness, and it became night. In it the beasts of the forest will pass through, the young lions roaring to seize their prey and to seek their food from God. The sun has risen, and they have gathered together, and in their dens they have slept. Man shall go forth to his work, and to his labor until the evening\" (Ps. 103:20 ff.). Whether we are sick or whether we are healthy, lowly or powerful, poor, rich, of low birth, honored, hungry or whether well fed, let us do all things in the name of the Lord with patience and equanimity, and there will be fulfilled in us that which is written: \"But for those who love the Lord, all things work together for good\". Anger itself and lust, and the injury that desires vengeance, if I rein myself in, if for God's sake I keep silent, if at each of the goads of agitation and the incitements of the vices I remember God looking upon me from above, become for me an occasion of triumphs. Let us not say in giving: That one is a friend, this one I do not know; this one ought to receive, that one to be despised. Let us imitate our Father, who makes his sun rise upon the good and the evil, and rains upon the righteous and the unrighteous (Matt. 5). The fountain of goodness lies open to all. Slave and free, commoner and king, rich and poor drink from it alike. A lamp, when it has been lit in a house, gives light to all equally. But if the reins of liberality are slackened toward all without distinction, how much more toward those of the household of faith, and toward Christians, who have the same Father and are reckoned by the name of his Master! It seems to me, however, that this passage can also cohere with what precedes, so that by those of the household of faith he means the teachers, whom he had commanded above to be ministered to by their hearers in all things that are reckoned good. Short is the course of this life. This very thing that I speak, that I dictate, that I write, that I correct, that I read over, is either added to me or lost from my time. Titus, the son of Vespasian, who, after overthrowing Jerusalem in vengeance for the Lord's blood, entered Rome as victor, is said to have been of such great goodness that, when one night late at dinner he recalled that he had done nothing good that day, he said to his friends: Today I have lost a day. Do we think that no hour, day, moments, time, ages are lost to us when we speak an idle word, for which we are to render an account on the day of judgment (Matt. 12)? But if he, without the Law, without the Gospel, without the teaching of the Savior and of the apostles, both said and did this by nature, what ought we to do, to whose condemnation Juno has her women once married, and Vesta her virgins, and other idols their continent votaries? The blessed John the Evangelist, when he was staying at Ephesus until extreme old age, and could scarcely be carried to the church in the arms of his disciples, and could not join his voice to many words, used to utter nothing else at each of the gatherings but this: Little children, love one another. At length the disciples and brothers who were present, wearied because they always heard the same things, said: Master, why do you always say this? He gave an answer worthy of John: Because it is the Lord's command, and if it alone is done, it is enough. This on account of the present command of the Apostle: Let us work good toward all: but most of all toward those of the household of faith.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.6.11":["See with what letters I have written to you with my own hand.\n\nThose who wanted the Galatians to be circumcised had spread it about that Paul did one thing and preached another, and destroyed his word by his own deed, since he who asserted that the Law was abolished was himself found in the Law. Because Paul could not overturn this opinion by being present in person among them all (for he was hindered by the chains which he was enduring for the witness of Christ), he makes himself present through a letter. And so that no suspicion of a forged Epistle might arise, from this place to the end he wrote it out with his own hand, showing that what came before had been written down by another. That Epistles were being sent under his name by false teachers he also shows when writing to the Thessalonians: \"But we beseech you, brothers, by the coming of our Lord Jesus Christ, and by our gathering together unto him, that you be not quickly moved from your mind, nor be terrified, neither by spirit, nor by word, nor by an Epistle as if sent by us, as though the day of the Lord were at hand; let no one deceive you in any way\" (2 Thess. 2:1 ff.). And to rescue the whole Epistle he was sending from the suspicion of falsity, he signed it at the end with his own hand, saying: \"The greeting with my own hand, Paul's, which is the sign in every Epistle: thus I write: The grace of our Lord Jesus Christ be with you all\" (2 Thess. 3:17, 18). The letter to the Colossians too, which he had dictated, he likewise signed underneath with his own hand: \"The greeting with my own hand, Paul's: Be mindful of my chains\". And wherever he knew that false teachers were present who might sow new doctrines by means of the Apostle's authority, he signed the Epistle with his own hand. Finally, writing also to the Corinthians, among whom there were schisms and heresies, each one saying: \"I am of Paul, and I of Apollos, but I of Cephas\" (1 Cor. 1:12), he sealed his letter with such an annotation: \"The greeting with my own hand, Paul's: If anyone does not love the Lord Jesus Christ, let him be anathema, maranatha\", and the rest. For this reason, therefore, wishing to take away every occasion from the false teachers, who had also perverted the Galatians from the truth of the Gospel, he completed the end of the letter with an annotation in his own hand, saying: See with what letters I have written to you; not that the letters were large (for that is indeed what πηλίκοις conveys in the Greek), but that the traces of his hand were known to them, so that while they recognized the strokes of the letters, they might think they saw the very man who had written. On this passage a man exceedingly learned in our times, I wonder how he came to say something ridiculous. Paul, he says, was a Hebrew and did not know Greek letters; and because necessity required that he sign the letter with his own hand, contrary to his habit he could scarcely trace the curving paths of the letters, in large strokes, showing even in this the tokens of his love for the Galatians, that for their sake he tried to do even what he could not do. Paul therefore wrote the letter in large letters because the sense in the letters was large, and it had been written down by the spirit of the living God, not with ink nor with a reed pen. And as for his adding with my own hand, let us understand works by \"hands.\" For this reason it is also often written in the prophets: \"The word of God which came in the hand of Jeremiah\", or \"of Haggai\", so that by this likeness too we may know that the word of God came in the hand of Paul. Paul writes large letters not only then to the Galatians but also today to all, and although the strokes with which his Epistles are written are small, yet the letters are great, because in the letters the sense is great.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.6.12":["Whoever wish to please in the flesh, these compel you to be circumcised, only so that they may not suffer the persecution of the cross of Christ.\n\nAbove he showed from what point he had subscribed with his own hand; now he goes back over what he wrote. Gaius Caesar, and Octavian Augustus, and Tiberius the successor of Augustus, had promulgated laws that the Jews who were dispersed throughout the whole world of the Roman Empire should live by their own rite and devote themselves to their ancestral ceremonies. Whoever, therefore, was circumcised, even though he believed in Christ, was regarded by the Gentiles as a Jew. But whoever, being without circumcision, displayed by his uncircumcision that he was not a Jew, became liable to persecutions both of the Gentiles and of the Jews. Therefore those who had corrupted the Galatians, desiring to escape these persecutions, urged circumcision upon their disciples as a defense; and this the Apostle now calls confidence in the flesh, namely because they held out circumcision in persecution as much to the Gentiles, whom they feared, as to the Jews, who wished to please. For neither the Jews could persecute them, nor the Gentiles, since they saw them both circumcising proselytes and themselves keeping the precepts of the Law.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.6.13":["For neither do those who are circumcised, these, keep the Law; but they wish you to be circumcised, that they may glory in your flesh. Because of the weakness of the flesh, he says, the Law cannot be fulfilled. Hence the Jews too keep the precepts and doctrines of men rather than the commandments of God, doing neither the bodily Law (for that indeed is impossible) nor the spiritual Law, which they do not understand. Hence this is all that they are eager for, all that they do, all that they strive after: that among the Jews they may glory over the injury done to your flesh, and boast that Gentiles have been circumcised under their own teaching. And all this they do so that they may please the Jews, and the ill will over the fight against the Law may die down.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.6.14":["But far be it from me to glory, except in the cross of our Lord Jesus Christ, through whom the world is crucified to me, and I to the world.\n\nHe alone can glory in the cross of Christ who takes it up and follows the Savior, who has crucified his flesh with its vices and lusts, who has died to the world and does not contemplate the things that are seen, but those that are not seen: seeing the world crucified, and its form passing away. Now that world is crucified to the righteous man of which the Savior says: \"I have overcome the world\" (John 16:33). And: \"Do not love the world\". And: \"You have not received the spirit from the world\". He to whom the world is crucified, to him the world is also dead; and already the consummation of the world has come for him, and, having been made worthy of a new heaven and a new earth and a new Testament, he sings a new song and receives a new name written on a pebble, which no one knows except him who has received it. It is asked how Paul now says: But far be it from me to glory, except in the cross of our Lord Jesus Christ, when in another place he glories in other things, as he says there: \"By your glory, which I have in Christ Jesus\" (1 Cor. 15:31). And again: \"Gladly will I glory in my infirmities, that the power of Christ may dwell in me\" (2 Cor. 12:9). And in another place: \"But it is good for me rather to die than that anyone should make my glory void\" (1 Cor. 9:15), and the other things written in this manner. But it must be understood that all that glorying, being referred to the cross, is the glory of the cross; and whatever worthy thing is accomplished in the virtues, this is done on account of the Lord's passion.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.6.15":["For neither is circumcision anything, nor uncircumcision, but a new creature.\n\nJust as the faithful man and the unbeliever, though one in substance, are divided into two according to the difference of their understanding, as the Apostle says: \"Stripping yourselves of the old man with his works, and putting on the new, who is renewed unto knowledge according to the image of the Creator\" (Col. 3:9, 10): so also the world, though it is one according to substance, becomes now one thing, now another, according to the mind. To the sinner the world is old; to the saint, new. For when the world has been crucified to the saint, there is for him no circumcision and uncircumcision, no Jew and no Gentile, but a new creature, into which the body of our lowliness is transfigured, conformed to the body of the glory of Christ: for the old things have passed away; behold, all things are made new (2 Cor. 5). And as there is one glory of the sun, another of the moon, another of the stars: \"For star differs from star in glory: so also is the resurrection of the dead\" (1 Cor. 15:41, 42). Concerning which Daniel too agrees with one voice, saying: \"Very many of those who sleep shall rise from the dust of the earth: these to eternal life, and these to reproach and to eternal confusion\" (Dan. 12:2); and: \"Those who understand shall shine like the splendor of the firmament\". And of the righteous: \"Many like the stars for ever\". For neither in the sun and moon, the firmament and the stars, does circumcision avail anything, or uncircumcision: but there is a new creation, without those parts of bodies that can be cut. So therefore we too, who love God, and for whom have been prepared the things that eye has not seen, nor ear heard, nor have they ascended into the heart of man (1 Cor. 2), when we have been transformed from the body of lowliness into the body of the glory of the Lord Jesus Christ, shall have that body which neither the Jew could cut, nor the Gentile keep with its foreskin. Not that it is something other according to substance, but that it is different according to glory. \"For this mortal must put on immortality, and this corruptible be clothed with incorruption\" (1 Cor. 15:53). Perceiving something similar to this, the blessed evangelist John also spoke: \"Dearly beloved, now we are sons of God: and it is not yet manifest what we shall be. We know that if it is made manifest, we shall be like him: because we shall see him as he is\" (1 John 3:2). Since, then, that body of the glory of Jesus Christ has not yet been made manifest — the body which both had the marks of the nails after the resurrection and entered through closed doors — let us, who even now in baptism have risen again with Christ, reborn into the new man, serve neither circumcision nor uncircumcision, but let us believe that even now we are what we shall be.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.6.16":["And whoever follow this rule, peace upon them, and mercy, and upon the Israel of God.\n\nAll things are directed by a norm; and whether they are crooked or straight is shown up once the rule has been laid against them. So too the teaching of God is, as it were, a kind of norm of speech, which judges between what is righteous and what is unrighteous: whoever follows it will have in himself the peace which surpasses all understanding; and after peace, mercy, which is preeminent in the Israel of God. Now he is called the Israel of God to distinguish him from the one who has ceased to be the Israel of God. For they say that they are Jews, and are not, but they lie, since they are of the synagogue of Satan. And do not wonder if, in imitation of the spiritual Israel, there is a carnal Israel, which has neither peace nor mercy; of which it is also written to the Corinthians: \"See Israel according to the flesh\" (1 Cor. 10:18); since, in imitation of God and the Lord as well, there are many gods and many lords, whether in heaven or on earth. And beautifully, so as to end the epistle in keeping with the argument he had set out, he called it in a single phrase the Israel of God; so that, namely, all the things said above may be shown to have been argued not beside the case, but from the case.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.6.17":["From now on let no man be troublesome to me.\n\nNot as though he had grown weary in teaching; but so that the farmer too may have this toil if the young shoots he has planted dry up, and the shepherd anxiety if the flocks which he had gathered are scattered and torn to pieces. It is therefore better read in the Greek, \"From now on let no one cause me labors\"; that is, lest I should again have the need of laboring among you. He causes labor to his teacher who both lives and thinks otherwise than the teacher has both taught and done. He may also be forestalling the contentiousness of those, if any should wish to contradict him hereafter, as he also did to the Corinthians concerning the veiling of a woman's head and the man's not veiling, when after much argument he concluded, saying: \"But if anyone seems to be contentious: we have no such custom, nor has the Church of God\" (1 Cor. 11:16); that is, we have said what seemed to us honorable and just; but if anyone, unwilling to acquiesce in the truth, seeks what to answer, and on what grounds they may strive against it, let him know that he is not worthy of a response, since he is more ready to contend than to be taught.\n\nBut I bear the marks of the Lord Jesus in my body. He who after the coming of Christ is circumcised in the flesh does not bear the marks of the Lord Jesus, but has glory in his shame. But he who was in stripes above measure, in prisons frequently, was three times beaten with rods, once stoned, and the rest that are written in the catalogue of his glorying, he bears the marks of the Lord Jesus in his body. Perhaps also he who chastens his body and brings it into subjection, lest while preaching to others he himself should be found reprobate, bears the marks of the Lord Jesus in his body (1 Cor. 9). The apostles too rejoiced that they had been counted worthy to suffer insult for the name of Jesus (Acts 5).\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GAL.6.18":["The grace of our Lord Jesus Christ be with your spirit, brothers. Amen.\n\nNot dissension, not slavery of the Law, not quarreling, not wrangling, but the grace of the Lord Jesus Christ be with your spirit. By no means with the flesh, by no means with the soul; either because, having been made spiritual, you cease to be flesh and soul, or because in the principal part those things too that are lesser are comprehended. For the soul and the flesh are subject to the spirit. Of which Ecclesiastes also speaks: \"The spirit shall return to him who gave it\" (Eccles. 12:7). And Paul in another place: \"The Spirit himself bears witness to our spirit\" (Rom. 8:16). But this grace of the Lord Jesus is not with all, but with those who deserve to be called brothers by the apostle, faithful brothers and true (germani) brothers, which is what the Hebrew word AMEN signifies. For the Seventy translators rendered Amen as \"so be it\" (fiat); Aquila, Symmachus and Theodotion as \"faithfully\" (fideliter), or \"truly\" (vere). And just as in the Old Testament God confirms his words by a certain custom of swearing, saying: \"I live, says the Lord\" (Num. 14:28), and one also swears by the saints: \"As your soul lives\"; so too our Savior in the Gospel shows by the word Amen that the things he speaks are true. And that Amen signifies the assent of the hearer and is a seal of truth, the first Epistle to the Corinthians also teaches us, in which Paul says: \"But if you bless with the spirit, who fills the place of the unlearned? How will he say amen over your blessing, since indeed he does not know what you are saying\" (1 Cor. 14:16)? From which he shows that the unlearned man cannot answer that what is said is true unless he has understood what is being taught.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"EPH.1.1":["Those whom he has called saintly he also calls faithful, because faith derives from the choice of our own minds, but sanctification we receive meanwhile from the abundance of the Sanctifier, not from our own will. As for his saying \"faithful in Jesus Christ,\" this is aimed at drawing a distinction that should be carefully noted. For there are those who have genuine faith but not faith in Jesus Christ. Someone who returns a deposit and does not deny another's trust shows himself a faithful friend.… This person is indeed faithful but not \"in Christ.\"\n— Commentary on Ephesians 1:1"],"EPH.1.2":["It could be argued that both should be referred to both, that is, both grace and peace apply no less to God the Father than to our Lord Jesus Christ. Or it could be argued that each should be referred to each, so that grace is referred to God the Father and peace to Christ. It is more likely the latter, since the words immediately following are to the praise of the glory of God's grace. Thus the \"grace\" of the Father lies in his willingness to send the Son for our salvation, while the \"peace\" of the Son lies in the fact that we are reconciled to the Father through him.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 1:2"],"EPH.1.3":["Now the phrase \"blessed be the God and Father of our Lord Jesus Christ\" is to be read in a double sense. It first means that God is blessed as the maker of all things, this being the main clause. To this is then added \"who is also the Father of our Lord Jesus Christ.\" It means that both God and Father are to be referred in common to our Lord. Blessed is the God of the man who has been assumed and the father of him who was the Word of God with God in the beginning! Not that the assumed one is other than the Word who assumed him, but that he who is one and the same is spoken of now by sublime and now by humble titles, according to what circumstances demand.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 1:3","Now God has blessed us not with this or that blessing but with every blessing. It is not as though we all obtain them all at once, but singly we obtain particular ones in due time or some of the whole number. Thereby we possess their fullness through these singular blessings. He speaks not only of earthly blessings but of spiritual—there are indeed earthly blessings, as when someone has children, affluence in riches, the pleasure of honor and health.… But spiritual blessings are in the heavens because the earth is too small to circumscribe a spiritual blessing.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 1:3"],"EPH.1.4":["Paul, wishing to show that God made all things out of nothing, ascribed to him not the \"composition,\" the \"creation\" or the \"making\" but the katabolē, that is, the inception of the foundation.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 1:4","Between saintly and unblemished there is this difference, that one who is saintly is ipso facto understood also to be unblemished, but one who is at some point unblemished is not by that fact itself saintly. Infants, after all, are spotless because their bodies are pure and they have committed no sin; and yet they are not saintly, because sanctity is not acquired without will and effort. Moreover, he who has done no sin can be called unblemished, but the saintly person is the one who is full of virtues.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 1:4","It is asked how anyone can be saintly and unblemished in God's sight.… We must reply [that] Paul does not say he chose us before the foundation of the world on account of our being saintly and unblemished. He chose us that we might become saintly and unblemished, that is, that we who were not formerly saintly and unblemished should subsequently be so.… So understood it provides a counterargument to one who says that souls were elected before the world came to be because of their sanctity and freedom from any sinful vice.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 1:4"],"EPH.1.5":["The former [verse] refers to those saints who did not previously exist and who before they came into being were thought of and subsequently acquired existence. This [verse] speaks of God, who was preceded by no thought or willing but always existed and never had a beginning for his existence. Therefore he rightly used the term destined for those who, having once not existed, subsequently acquired existence. But of the Son, that is, of our Lord Jesus Christ, he wrote ordained in another place also.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 1:5","Christ, as we have often said already, is wisdom, justice, peace, joy, temperance and the rest. Note that the names of all these virtues are loved even by those who do not pursue them! No one is such a brazen criminal that he does not claim to love wisdom and justice.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 1:6"],"EPH.1.7":["The one who is yet to be redeemed is a captive. He has ceased to be free by coming under the power of the enemy. So we are captives in this world and bound by the yoke of slavery to the principalities and powers, unable to release our hands from our chains. So we raise our eyes upward until the Redeemer arrives.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 1:7"],"EPH.1.9":["The Stoics also hold that there is a distinction between wisdom and insight. They say, \"Wisdom is the knowledge of things divine and human, insight only of that which is mortal.\" According to this distinction we might apply Paul's term wisdom to the invisible and visible and insight only to the visible.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 1:9","Some attentive reader might object: \"If Paul knows in part and prophesies in part and now sees as through a glass darkly, how is the mystery of God revealed either to him or to the Ephesians \"in all wisdom and insight?\" … It is not that they by themselves have learned this mystery \"in all wisdom and insight,\" but God \"in all wisdom and insight\" has revealed the mystery to us, so far as we are able to grasp it.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 1:9"],"EPH.1.13":["It is no small praise for the Ephesians that they have heard not preaching as such but \"the word of truth.\" Remember that we read in another letter that there is a great distance between preaching and the word of truth.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 1:13"],"EPH.1.14":["A guarantee (arrabōn, \"earnest\") is not the same as a token or pledge. For a guarantee is given as an affidavit and bond for a future purchase. But a pledge … is an expression of a present reciprocal transaction. Thus when the money is returned the pledge is restored by the creditor to the one who has repaid the debt.… So from the guarantee the majestic scope of the future inheritance may be grasped.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 1:14"],"EPH.1.17":["It is this God of the incarnate man who is the Father of glory, wisdom and truth, who gives the Spirit of wisdom and revelation to those who believe in his Son so that they may become wise and contemplate the glory of the Lord with unveiled face. When this wisdom and revelation have made them wise and opened to them the mysteries that were hidden, it follows at once that they have \"the eyes of their heart enlightened.\"\n— Commentary on Ephesians 1:15-17","He is the witness of all that the Father promised and bestowed on the world, whose mystery Paul gathers up in a brief word to the Ephesians: The God, he says, of our Lord Jesus Christ, the Father of glory (Eph 1:17); of whom it is written now as well: Because of the Lord your God, and the Holy One of Israel, who has glorified you, and that with the glory which He had before the world was made. Of it the Prophet also testifies: The glory of God shall appear (Heb 1), He who is the brightness of glory and the form of His substance, when every tongue shall confess that the Lord Jesus is in the glory of the Father (Phil 2). He answered the Son, when He asked for the glory which He had had before: I have both glorified it, and will glorify it. Of which the apostle John also says: We saw, he says, His glory, the glory as of the only-begotten of the Father, full of grace and truth (John 12:28; 1:14). Therefore, when he speaks of Him who in the beginning was with God, God the Word, He is called the Father of glory; but when of Him who says in the Gospel, Why do you persecute me, a man who speaks the truth to you (John 8:40)? He is called the God of our Lord Jesus Christ. Not that there is one and another (which is the very bad error of many), but because one and the same Son of God speaks now with the feelings of the glory of His divinity, now with those of our nature, which He deigned to take up.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"EPH.1.18":["His phrase \"eyes of the heart\" clearly refers to those things we cannot understand without sense and intelligence.… Faith sees beyond what the physical eyes see. Physical eyes are in the heads of not only the wise but the unwise.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 1:18-20","It is not without effort that we come to \"know the hope of our calling and the riches of God's inheritance in the saints.\" This effort in fact comes in response to that renewing gift which God himself gives in the glorious resurrection of his own Son. This gift he gives not once but continually.… Every day Christ rises from the dead. Every day he is raised in the penitent.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 1:18-20"],"EPH.1.20":["He demonstrates the power of God through a human image. It is not that a material throne is set up and God the Father is physically seated on it and has the Son seated above with him. Rather he communicates with this metaphor because we could not understand his role as incomparable governor and judge except in our own terms.… Being on the right or left of God is to be understood as meaning that saints are on his right but sinners on his left. … The very word sits denotes the power of kingship, through which God confers benefits on those above whom he is seated. He has reined them in and has them in his service, guiding those who had previously strayed.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 1:21"],"EPH.1.21":["Now we must ask where the apostle found these four names—principalities, powers, forces and dominions? From what sources did he bring them into the open? It would be dishonorable to imagine that Paul, who had been schooled in godly literature, might be quoting this from pagan sources. I therefore suggest that he has brought into the open some of the Hebrew traditions which are secret. Or better, it might have been that once he understood that the law is spiritual, he grasped a higher meaning in those things that are written in the guise of history. He could have known, for example, that there was a symbol of other powers and authorities in what is said in the books of Numbers and Kings about kings, princes, captains and leaders of tribes and ages.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 1:21"],"EPH.1.22-23":["In the same way as a hand has many members subject to it, of which some are diseased and weak, so too our Lord Jesus Christ, being the head of the church, has as his members the whole congregation of the church, the saints and also the sinners. But the saints are in voluntary subjection to him, while the sinners are under compulsion.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 1:22-23"],"EPH.1.22":["By his foreknowledge he is celebrating what is to come as though it were done already, as I explained above when he says \"he has blessed us.\" … Either this interpretation, or a better one might be: If we are to take account of what has gone before, we should take this to mean that even those things whose will is not subject to him serve him because of their natural condition. So demons, Gentiles and Jews all serve him. Even if they do not freely serve Christ nor are they put under his feet, yet, because they have been created by him for good, they are unwillingly subject to his power, even if they strive against him with the volition of their free judgment.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 1:22-23","Why \"all things\"? Why is it said that angels, thrones, dominions, powers and the other forces that were never opposed to God should be \"put under his feet\"? It seems obscure. But it could be said in reply that none is without sin. The \"stars themselves are not clean in God's sight,\" and every creature dreads the advent of the Lord.… But another explanation refers the word all not to everything but only to those things that are in dispute. It is as if one says \"all the citizens cried out,\" not meaning that there was no one in the city who was silent but that what is said of the majority covers the minority also.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 1:22-23"],"EPH.2.1":["[The Greeks] speak of trespass as the first step toward sin. It is when a secret thought steals in, and, though we offer a measure of collusion, it does not yet drive us on to ruin.… But sin is something else. It is when the collusion is actually completed and reaches its goal.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 2:1-5"],"EPH.2.3":["So that he would not appear to have exempted himself through pride when he said \"your sins in which you walked,\" he now adds \"in which we also lived.\" However, the one who says he has lived confesses past, not present, transgressions.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 2:1-5","There is a difference between sin of the flesh and sin of the mind. The sin of the flesh is indecency and profligacy and whatever might act as instrument to its lusts. The transgression of the mind pertains to doctrine contrary to truth and to the baseness of heretics.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 2:1-5"],"EPH.2.5":["The sufferings of the present time are not worthy to be compared with the future glory that will be revealed in us. If so, we are saved by grace rather than works, for we can give God nothing in return for what he has bestowed on us.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 2:1"],"EPH.2.6":["Above he said that God raised Christ from the dead and seated him at his right hand.… Some may ask how God who has saved us and raised us with him has also made us sit with Christ. A simple response would be indeed that, in the light of God's foreknowledge, Paul is speaking of what is to come as though it had already been done.… One who understands the resurrection and the kingdom of Christ spiritually does not scruple to say that the saints already sit and reign with Christ! Just as a person may become truly holy even in the flesh, when he lives in the flesh and has his conversation in heaven, when he walks on earth and, ceasing to be flesh, is wholly converted into spirit, so he also is seated in heaven with Christ. For indeed \"the kingdom of God is within us.\"\n— Commentary on Ephesians 2:1-5","How abundant is his grace and how multi-faceted is the glory in which God has caused us to be seated and reign with Christ, after freeing us from the tribulations of the age! This is shown above all by the fact that in the ages to come he will shed his glory upon us in the sight not of some but of all rational creatures.… But an attentive reader might inquire: \"Are you saying then that the human arena is greater than the angels and all the heavenly powers?\" No.… Some might conceivably argue that \"he made us sit with him in heavenly places\" refers not to the good angels but to the bad angels, the banished angels and the prince of this world, and Lucifer who rises in the morning, over whom the saints will be enthroned with Christ.… But a better argument will translate [the reference to Christ's grace] as meaning that we are saved not by our own merit but by his grace and that it is a proof of greater goodness to die for sinners rather than for the just.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 2:7"],"EPH.2.8":["Paul says this in case the secret thought should steal upon us that \"if we are not saved by our own works, at least we are saved by our own faith, and so in another way our salvation is of ourselves.\" Thus he added the statement that faith too is not in our own will but in God's gift. Not that he means to take away free choice from humanity … but that even this very freedom of choice has God as its author, and all things are to be referred to his generosity, in that he has even allowed us to will the good.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 2:8-9"],"EPH.2.10":["We are his creation. This means that it is from him that we live, breathe, understand and are able to believe, because he is the One who made us. And note carefully that he did not say \"we are his fashioning and molding\" but \"we are his creation.\" Molding starts with the mud of the earth, but creation from the outset is \"according to the image and likeness of God.\"\n— Commentary on Ephesians 2:10"],"EPH.2.11":["By calling the Ephesians \"Gentiles in the flesh,\" he shows that in the spirit they are not Gentiles, just as conversely the Jews are Gentiles in spirit and Israelites in the flesh. Therefore the Jews and Gentiles are subject to a fourfold division: Some are circumcised in spirit and flesh, as were Moses and Aaron.… Some have been circumcised neither in spirit nor in flesh, as were Nebuchadnezzar and Pharaoh.… A third group are circumcised only in the flesh.… Lastly come those of whom he now speaks, … believers such as today we see in the whole host of believing Gentiles around the world.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 2:12"],"EPH.2.12":["When he says \"having no hope, without God in the world,\" he does not deny that the Ephesians had many gods before they believed in Christ. His point is that one who is without the true God has no god worthy of the name. And the next phrase, \"without God in the world,\" is significant: The Gentiles in a sense already had God indeed in the form of anticipation, because God knew beforehand that he would have them. In God's foreknowledge they were never without God. But enmeshed in the world they were without God.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 2:12"],"EPH.2.13-18":["And this is what he now says: Peace upon peace; not to one nation only, but to the whole world: namely to those who were far off and to those who were near, that is, first to the nations, and afterward to those of Israel who were willing to believe. Of these the Apostle too says: To you indeed it was necessary that the word of God be preached; but since you judge yourselves unworthy of salvation, behold, we turn to the nations (Acts 13:46). He, writing to the Ephesians and discussing most fully the people of the Circumcision and of the Gentiles, adds: But now in Christ Jesus you who once were far off have been brought near in the Blood of Christ. For he is our peace, who made both one, and broke down the middle wall of partition, dissolving the enmity in his flesh, and so on (Eph 2:13, 14); and again: And coming, he announced peace to you who were far off, and peace to those who were near (ibid., 17). For through him we both have access in one spirit to the Father: giving peace upon peace to those who had been far off and to those who were near, both those who had not had the Law and the Prophets and those who had received them before, he received them and healed them.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"EPH.2.13":["God in his entirety is everywhere. Who can be separated from him when all things are in him?… He is, however, said to be far away from the unrighteous, according to Proverbs [15:29].… Just as far as the unrighteous are away from him, so close is he to the saints. Just when God seemed to be furthest from the Ephesians, he was coming close to them by the blood of Jesus.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 2:13-14"],"EPH.2.18":["However, it should not be thought possible to achieve perfect and complete reconciliation in this world.… The making of the new person in Christ will be fully consummated when earthly and heavenly things have been reconciled, when we come to the Father in one Spirit and with one affection and understanding.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 2:1-5"],"EPH.2.19-22":["The apostle Paul, wishing the Ephesians to know the more sacred things, prays for them 'that they may be filled with the wisdom and love of the Lord, and, being rooted, may be able to know and comprehend what is the breadth and length and height and depth of his riches'; and to the same he says: So then you are by no means strangers and sojourners, but fellow citizens of the saints and members of the household of God, built upon the foundation of the apostles and prophets, Jesus Christ himself being the cornerstone; in whom the whole building, fitted together, grows into a holy temple for the Lord, in whom you too are built up into a dwelling place of God in the Holy Spirit. Peter too, in almost the same words, because with the same spirit, says in his epistle: If indeed you have believed that the Lord is good. Coming to him, a living stone, rejected indeed by men, but chosen and honored by God, you yourselves too, as living stones, are built up as a spiritual house, into a holy priesthood, offering spiritual victims acceptable to God through Jesus Christ. From this it is clear that the Lord Savior, who is the true architect, tests the stones: which can be called together into the foundation of the temple, which into the middle, which into the higher places, and which, being of lesser measure or of not so solid firmness, into the wall and outside the temple, into the περιβολῇ round about — for it is a thing of no small strength nor of the least firmness to bear the stones laid on from above; whence the apostle writes to believers: Bearing one another's burdens, and in another place it is written that we ought not to take up a weight which we cannot carry —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EPH.2.21":["It is maintained by some that the whole edifice built on the foundation of the apostles and prophets comprises not only human souls but also angelic powers, so that all equally will become the abode of God. They argue that it would be absurd if angels and all the blessed forces who serve God in heaven would have no part in this blessedness. For in this is a building, put together harmoniously, that is growing into a holy temple of God to be an abode of God in the Spirit.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 2:1-5"],"EPH.3.1":["After a diligent search I have found nothing that answers to his prior clause.… For he does not say, \"For this reason I, Paul, have done this or that or have taught this or that.\" Instead, leaving the thought in suspense, he goes on to other matters. Perhaps we ought to pardon him for what he himself has admitted when he said, \"if unschooled in speech, at least not in knowledge,\" and look for order in his meaning rather than in his words. This can be rendered as follows: \"I, Paul, in the chains of Jesus Christ and in chains for you Gentiles, have learned the mystery so that I may hand it on to you.\"\n— Commentary on Ephesians 3:1","We often read that the body is called the prison of the soul. The soul is confined as if in a cage. Paul, for example, was constrained by the ties of the body and did not return to be with Christ so that the preaching to the Gentiles might be perfectly accomplished through him. But I grant that there are some who introduce another meaning here: Paul before his birth was predestined and sanctified from his mother's womb for the purpose of preaching to the Gentiles. For this vocation he took on the bonds of flesh.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 3:1"],"EPH.3.6":["The Gentiles are fellow heirs with Israel. Put more precisely, they are fellow heirs with Christ. … It is not that some possession is divided among us but that God himself in his fullness is our inheritance and possession.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 3:5-7","Now the meaning of fellow heirs is this: Just as there are many members in one body … and these, though in one body, have their differences and feel their own joy and grief in turn, so those who have believed in Christ, even if they have different gifts, are bonded together in the one body of Christ.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 3:5-7"],"EPH.3.8":["Although it is a token of humility to call oneself \"the least of all the saints,\" one is guilty of deceit if one conceals the truth in the heart and says something else with the tongue. We must therefore look for an argument showing how Paul truly was \"the least of all the saints\" and yet did not fall from the rank of an apostle. The Lord says in the Gospel. \"He who would be great among you, let him be less than all.\" … Paul demonstrates this in his actions. … Therefore the apostle Paul was meekest of all who sought to be weak on Christ's account.… Because of this humility, amazing grace was given to him. In this way he became \"the least of all the saints\" in order \"to preach the unsearchable mysteries of God.\"\n— Commentary on Ephesians 3:8-9","Unsearchable and hidden can be given two senses. The riches were previously unable to be searched out. They are now laid open after the Lord's passion. Another sense, perhaps even better: Those things which by nature were unsearchable to humanity are the ones that have been made known by God's revelation.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 3:8-9"],"EPH.3.9":["These riches of his generosity were \"hidden from all past ages in God,\" the Creator of all. Where are Marcion, Valentinus and the other heretics who say that there is one creator of the visible world and another creator of the invisible?… However, the phrase \"mystery hidden from the ages\" could also be understood to mean that the very ages of time remained ignorant of his generosity when all spiritual and rational creatures who inhabited all previous ages remained unenlightened.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 3:9"],"EPH.3.12":["Nothing can give us such trust in God and purity of conscience … as the Word, the truth, the wisdom and the righteousness received in Christ.… One who thinks in an orderly manner now has the Word and reason as the content of his faith. One who can comprehend wisdom has wisdom as the content of his faith. One who understands truth has truth as the content of his faith. One who lives righteously now has righteousness as the content of his faith.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 3:12"],"EPH.3.15":["Before any discussion we must note that he did not say \"from whom every fatherhood in heaven and earth is born\" but \"from whom every fatherhood in heaven and earth is named.\" For it is one thing to merit the name of father, another to have a natural relation [as eternal Father to all creatures].… I have searched about in Scripture asking whether the word fatherhood is ever applied to the Gentiles. I have found nothing except the twenty-first psalm, \"and all the fatherhoods of the Gentiles shall adore in his sight,\" and the twenty-eighth, \"Give to the Lord, fatherhoods of the Gentiles, bring him the young of rams.\" Think by this analogy: As God exists, God allows the term existence to be applied to creatures as well. So we say that creatures exist and subsist, not so as to imply that they exist in and of themselves [as God exists] but as a derived existence enabled by God.… According to this same argument, God allows the term fatherhood to be given to creatures. So by analogy to his fathering we can understand creaturely fathering.… Similarly, as the only good One he makes others good. As the only immortal One God has bestowed immortality on others. As the only true One he imparts the name of truth. So also the Father alone, being Creator of all and the cause of the subsistence of all things, makes it possible for other creatures to be called fathers.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 3:14","We who are not of Abraham's race are called Abraham's children if we possess his faith. Similarly, I think that the angels and other invisible powers have something like princes of their own in heaven whom they rejoice to call fathers.… Our term fatherhood may now be used in the light of the awareness that God is Father of our Lord Jesus Christ. For the only begotten Son is so not by adoption but by nature. It is by adoption that creatures also are allowed to participate in fathering and hence are given the name of fathers. And remember that whatever we say of the Father and Son we say also of the Holy Spirit.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 2.3"],"EPH.3.18":["Let us think first about physical \"breadth and length, depth and height\" in order that we may be able to pass through these physical dimensions to their spiritual dimensions. For the sake of argument, let the physical length be that of heaven and earth, that is, of the whole world, from east to west. Let the breadth be from south to north. Let the depth be from the abyss and the infernal regions. Let the height be to all that is elevated above the heavens. But they say that the earth is round and rotates as a sphere. Roundness has no breadth and length, height and depth, but is proportional in all dimensions. Hence we are necessarily forced to understand spiritually by height the angels and forces above and by depth those powers below and what is beneath them. By length and breadth we speak spiritually of that which occupies the middle place between those above and those below. The consequence is that one draws near as a neighbor either to those things above or to those below. Whatever begins to advance one's path toward better things so as to rise to the heavenly height, that is what Paul is calling length. Whatever brings one to the lower things as one lapses toward vice he is calling breadth.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 3:16"],"EPH.3.21":["This glory does not extend over the present time only, as if terminating in the age to come. Rather it extends throughout all generations and all ages. It is eternally ineffable. It abides, develops and increases.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 3:20-21"],"EPH.4.1":["Those who love Christ follow him. They are bonded with him in the ties of love. There is also another explanation [i.e., Origen's], which it is the reader's prerogative to accept or not: Suppose what is called here the prison is the body. Because Paul has taken on the body for the ministry of the gospel, he is consequently said to be in bondage to Christ.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 4:1"],"EPH.4.2":["Anyone who understands what it is \"forbear one another in love\" will understand that this is a precept appropriate to the faithful. It is not indeed saints who have any need to \"forbear one another.\" Rather it is those in the earlier stages of Christian life, who being human are still under the control of some passion. Nor is it strange that this should be said to the Ephesians. Among them there were surely some who still had to bear patiently with others.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 4:2"],"EPH.4.4":["His words one body and one Spirit can be taken most simply to mean the one body of Christ, which is the church. Or it could refer to the humanity of the Lord, which he assumed from the Virgin.… Yet indeed the one body can also refer to life and the works that are called in Greek \"the practical life.\" These are distinguished from the oneness of the Spirit in the heart that finds its unity in contemplation.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 4:3-4","If the Father's house has many mansions, how are we to say that we are called to one hope? One reply is that the one hope of the calling is the kingdom of God. It is as though we were to speak of the one house of God or say that in one house are many mansions. … Or again, this subtler meaning may be implied: at the end and consummation of all things everything is to be restored to its original condition, when we are all made one body and formed anew into a perfect man.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 4:3-4"],"EPH.4.5":["There is one Lord and one God, because the dominion of Father and Son is a single Godhead. The faith is said to be one because we believe similarly in Father, Son and Holy Spirit. Baptism is one. We are all baptized in the same way in the name of the Father, Son and Holy Spirit. We are immersed three times so that the sacrament of the Trinity may be apparent.… There is one baptism in the Spirit, in water and in fire.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 4:5-6"],"EPH.4.8":["Therefore, he says: 'Ascending on high, he led captivity captive, he gave gifts to men.' For he had said above, 'To each one of us grace has been given according to the measure of the gift of Christ,' so that he might confirm these very gifts, which he also enumerates a little later, saying: 'And he gave some to be apostles, others to be prophets, others to be evangelists, others to be pastors and teachers,' and the rest. From the Savior, who gave them, he took testimony from the sixty-seventh Psalm, that we might know that the spoils that Christ the conqueror purchased are distributed to men. For ascending on high, he led captivity captive. We believers in Christ, gathered out of the Gentiles, when we were God's creatures, were captured by the devil and distributed among his followers. Then our Lord Jesus Christ came, bearing with him the vessels of captivity spoken of by Ezekiel (Ch. IX and XII), and with his head veiled so as not to be recognized by his adversaries, he preached remission to the captives and release to them who were held in chains. And he freed us from the chains of the enemy and the fetters of the old captivity, and led us with himself into heaven. And to those whom he delivered from the hand of the enemy as the victor, he gave different gifts of grace. And elegantly, he placed here, 'he gave gifts to men,' whereas in the Psalter it is written, 'he received gifts from men' (Ps. LXVII, 19). But there, because it had not yet happened, but was promised for the future, it is said, 'he received.' Here, however, since the Apostle writes that he had already given, and the Church was established throughout the world, he does not write 'received,' but 'gave.' Some have explained this passage such that our Lord Jesus Christ ascended victoriously to heaven so that he might send from there angels and other powers to guard his churches. And while this place on earth is unworthy to have the presence of sublime powers, in a certain way they endured captivity. For he ascended on high, that he might captive captivity and give gifts to men. And the Apostle repeats this whole thing to teach that since he had already said, 'bearing with one another in love, eager to keep the unity of the Spirit in the bond of peace,' he might show that in the various gifts of the Church, there might be harmony, and that there might not be immediately given occasion for schisms and dissensions, because according to the measure of the gift of Christ, each one of us received gifts, not the same but in one body and in one spirit, all of us called, that is, so that just as there is one Lord, one faith, one baptism, and one God the Father, so also we might be one in charity, keeping the unity of the spirit in the bond of peace.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 4:8","Now all this was done that what was spoken by the Lord through the prophet might be fulfilled, saying: Behold, a virgin shall have in the womb and shall bear. Where the evangelist Matthew says: shall have in the womb, in the prophet it is written: shall receive in the womb. But the prophet, because he foretells things to come, signifies what is to be and writes shall receive; the evangelist, because he narrates history not of the future but of the past, changed shall receive and put shall have. For one who has is by no means going to receive. Something of the kind we read in the psalms too: Ascending on high he led captivity captive, he received gifts among men. The Apostle, setting down this testimony, did not say received, but gave, because there it was signified of the future that he would receive, here it is told of him who had already given what he had received.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"EPH.4.9":["But what is it that ascends except that which also descends into the lower parts of the earth? He who is said to have ascended, ascended because he had descended before. This means: 'But what is it that ascends except that which has also descended.' Therefore, we must look into that which is written elsewhere: 'No one has ascended into heaven except he who descended from heaven, the Son of Man' (John 3:13), how did he ascend before descending? For when he spoke these words, he had already descended from heaven and was showing how he had once ascended to heaven. Similarly, we must consider how the descent and ascent are to be understood. Whether it was a physical descent and ascent or a spiritual one, or perhaps both. The lower parts of the earth refer to hell, into which our Lord and Savior descended in order to bring with him the souls of the saints who were held captive there, triumphantly leading them to heaven. This is why, after his resurrection, many bodies of the righteous were seen in the holy city (Matthew 27:52-53). That hell is located in the lower part of the earth is confirmed by the Psalmist who says: 'The earth opened and swallowed up Dathan, and covered the company of Abiram' (Psalm 106:17). This is further explained in the Book of Numbers (Chapter 16). We also read elsewhere: 'Let death come deceitfully upon them; and let them go down alive into Hell' (Psalm 55:15).\n— Commentary on Ephesians 4:9"],"EPH.4.10":["Could he possibly have passed through and beyond all the heavens and all the supernal regions and the heavenly orbits which philosophers call the spheres to take his place in the highest heaven, in its topmost location? Or should we rather believe that, transcending and spurning everything corporeal and contemplating the eternal, he has taken his place above the heavens, that is, above all that is visible? I think this the better opinion. Therefore the Son of God descended to the lower parts of the earth and ascended above all the heavens, so that he might fulfill not only the Law and the Prophets but also certain hidden dispensations which only the Father knew. He also descended to the lower parts and ascended to heaven, so that he might bring fulfillment to those who were in those regions, so far as they were able to receive. From this we know that before Christ descended and ascended everything was void and in need of his fullness.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 4:10"],"EPH.4.11":["From this passage Paul clearly confirms the divinity of the Father and Son. What Christ is here said to have bestowed [as in his first letter to the Corinthians] is nothing less than the gift of God. … Failing to understand this, Sabellius confused the Father and the Son, not grasping that, though distinguishable, they work together with single intent.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 4:11-12","It is not to be supposed that as with the first three … he has allotted different offices to shepherds and teachers. For he does not say \"some shepherds, some teachers\" but \"some shepherds and teachers,\" meaning that he who is a shepherd should at the same time be a teacher. No one in the church, even a saintly person, should take to himself the name of shepherd unless he can teach those whom he feeds.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 4:11-12"],"EPH.4.13":["We must ask: Who are the all whom he speaks of \"coming together in the unity of faith\"? Does he mean all the people of God or all saints? Or rather all who are capable of reason? He seems to me to be speaking of all the people of God because there are so \"many winds of doctrine\" blowing about them. When these blasts and billows are aroused, people in general are carried here and there by diverse errors, uncertain of their course.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 4:13"],"EPH.4.14":["Was Paul referring to himself as one who was tossed to and fro and drifting? According to one view, he was saying this in humility, aware that we see in part and know in part. He is aware of how far he is from perfect knowledge and bursts into an expression of his own awareness. If so, anyone who might think himself to be humble should look to Paul as an example. … But another will respond to this that, by comparison with the majority, the apostle had already reached \"mature manhood,\" even though he still might be here regarded as an infant in relation to those eternal blessings that are stored up for the saints.… The exposition must proceed very carefully after this to meet the possible claim that the apostle really is speaking in humility when he prays that we \"may no longer be children, drifting and carried away in different directions by every blast of doctrine.\" … Maybe it is out of good conscience and not some false humility that Paul is confessing his own limitations. For he was a man of acute and sharp intellect.… He could see that there was often in the manner of speaking on both sides some distorted motives, such that there seemed to be so much truth in contrary assertions as to cause doubt in the listener. Thus, as a human being and still in his fragile little body, he was at times carried about by every wind of doctrine, though he was not cast against the rocks.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 4:13"],"EPH.4.16":["This entire upbuilding, by which the body of the church increases cell by cell, is being accomplished through the mutual love of one for another.… This does not imply that to each member will be distributed the same level of maturity. It is an error to assume, for example, that all human beings will be formed anew into angels. Rather every member will be perfected according to its own distinctive measure and function. Humanity, which has been expelled from paradise, will be restored to the cultivation of paradise again.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 4:16"],"EPH.4.21":["If all who seem to hear Christ did indeed hear him, the apostle would never had said this to the Ephesians. They were those to whom he had already revealed the promises of Christ. Why would he then say conditionally: \"if indeed you have heard him?\" To know Christ is the same thing already as knowing virtue. To hear of Christ rightly is the same as being attentive to all the virtues: wisdom, justice, temperance, fortitude and the other names by which Christ is called. Therefore if anyone has indeed heard and learned Christ, he would not be living \"in the futility of his mind\" nor \"be darkened in understanding\" nor be \"alienated from the life of God.\" He would already have practical knowledge, since his ignorance would have been dispelled, his darkness illuminated and every blindness lifted from the eyes of his heart.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 4:20","On the one hand the name Jesus refers to the man who was assumed by the Word, the man born from the Virgin. … Then again it refers to the Word of God: \"for to us there is one Lord Jesus Christ, through whom are all things\" … When Paul says \"as truth is in Jesus,\" he is speaking of the temple of God in which God the Word dwells. The Word was made flesh and dwelt among us. God is the Word. As life dwells in him, so he also is life. … In this same way the Son too may be called the truth, and truth may be said to dwell in him. In saying this we do not separate God the Word from the humanity he assumed. The man he assumed is not someone else. According to our understanding of certain passages we give different titles to him whom we believe to be the one Son of Man and Son of God, both before and after the virgin birth.… In none of the patriarchs, in none of the prophets, in none of the apostles did truth reside as it did in Jesus. For others know in part and prophesy in part and see \"as through a glass darkly.\" In Jesus alone the truth of God has appeared. He confidently asserts \"I am the truth.\"\n— Commentary on Ephesians 4:21"],"EPH.4.22":["The \"old nature\" whom he tells them to put off has, in my opinion, been aged by wickedness. For, going constantly astray in his former way of life and in the desires of waywardness and acting like a beast in works of corruption, he himself suffers corruption and decay.… But the Word of God kills in such a way as to make the dead one come alive. He then seeks the Lord whom he did not know before his death. He does not corrupt but kills the old man. … As the outer man decays the inner man is renewed.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 4:22"],"EPH.4.23":["We are not being renewed in our thinking process apart from the renewal of our spirits. Nor are we renewed in our spirits without thinking. We are being jointly renewed \"in the spirit of our mind.\" Hence as we sing psalms in the spirit, so we also sing them in our thoughts. As we pray in the spirit, so we also pray in our thoughts. The renewal of \"the spirit of our mind\" means that when the thought is clear and pure … then the spirit is rightly joined to it. They are so coupled as if by a cohesive glue that we no longer speak simply of spirit but of \"the spirit of our mind.\"\n— Commentary on Ephesians 4:23-24"],"EPH.4.24":["The metaphors of creating and establishing are never spoken of in Scripture except in great works. The world is created. A city is established. But observe that a house, however grand it may be, is more commonly said to be built than established or created. Note then that it is a great work of God when it is said that \"the new person is created by God in Christ.\" This creature towers over the other creatures. This creature alone is said to have been established in the same way as the world was established, from \"the beginning of God's ways,\" when all the elements first came into being.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 4:23-24"],"EPH.4.25":["To be members one of another points to a great mystery. He is speaking of those who are very close to us in faith. For people are not generally \"members one of another.\" But the faithful indeed are members of the faithful. Christians are members of the body of Christ. We are members with the saints who embody purity of heart and consummate goodness.… Hence we are being instructed to speak intimately of the truth of this mystery with the neighbor—to speak of the fullness of God's truth.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 4:25","Speak the truth with your neighbors. Let us take neighbor to mean the whole race of men, since we have been begotten of one parent; otherwise, if neighbor is taken to mean kinsman, we must lie to strangers and foreigners. The Apostle says this same thing: Putting away lying, speak the truth, each one with his neighbor.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"EPH.4.26":["This is taken from the fourth psalm, as I am sure no one doubts. It may seem contrary to what is said of anger elsewhere, that we must put away all anger. … It is an oversimplified interpretation that does harm, especially when people imagine that the constraints against anger are being here relaxed. It is not only among us but among philosophers too that anger is spoken of in a double sense. Anger emerges first when we are understandably aroused by a natural stimulus after being wounded by an injury. Or it arises when, after the impulse has abated and our rage has been restrained, the mind is capable of judgment but nonetheless we find ourselves desiring vengeance upon the one who is thought to have inflicted the wound. In this [verse] Paul is speaking of the first kind of anger. He is allowing to us as vulnerable humans that in the face of some undeserved event we may be moved to some level of annoyance, as if a light breeze were disturbing the serenity of the mind. But on no account are we to be carried into swelling rapids by the impulse of rage.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 4:26"],"EPH.4.28":["Those who live in the midst of this life's intense business appear to be forced, for the sake of food and necessary provisions, to buy and sell certain things and to seek unfair profit from business. It is difficult even for those who have been set free from the other passions—namely fornication, idolatry, adultery and murder—to escape being caught by this subtle vice.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 4:28"],"EPH.4.29":["A good word is one that serves to build upon the occasion, communicating grace to the hearers because it teaches them to pursue virtues and shun vices. An evil word is one that prompts them to sin and rather drives them headlong into disaster.… Whenever we say what is not in season or inappropriate for the context, or that which does not contribute to the good of the hearers, an evil word proceeds from our mouth.… Even if we do no direct harm, yet we are not thereby building up. We shall pay the penalty of an evil word.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 4:29"],"EPH.4.30":["That we have been \"sealed\" with the Holy Spirit means that both our spirit and our soul are impressed with God's own seal, signifying that we belong to him. By this we receive in ourselves that image and likeness in which we were created at the outset.… You are sealed so that you may be preserved to the end. You may show that seal on the day of redemption, pure and unblemished and not damaged in any part. You are thereby ready to be counted with those who are redeemed.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 4:30","As for what follows: Her prophets πνευματοφόροι, that is, bearing the spirit, men who are despisers, let it disturb no one that, interpreting this of teachers, we call the same men both prophets and despisers, since the Apostle too commands: Do not grieve the Holy Spirit of God, in whom you were sealed in the day of redemption. And David says in Psalm 50 (51): Do not take your Holy Spirit from me. For unless the Holy Spirit, when grieved, were accustomed to flee his former lodging and to abandon his dwelling, Paul would never have commanded what I said above, nor would David after his adultery have dreaded lest he lose what he had received; of which it is also written to the Hebrews: How much worse punishments do you think he deserves who has trampled on the Son of God, and has held unclean the blood of the covenant, in which he was sanctified, and has done insult to the spirit of grace?\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"EPH.4.31":["Wrath is the outspewing of indignation in the mind when anger overflows. Bitterness and wrath are varieties of anger. Anger desires vengeance after rage has been subdued. Anger wishes to harm the one by whom it believes itself injured.… Vengeance wants to return evil to the one it considers guilty of injury. A Christian ought not to return evil for evil but \"overcome evil with good.\"\n— Commentary on Ephesians 4:31"],"EPH.4.32":["Paul wants us to be gentle, approachable people, people who have left anger, bitterness, wrath and slander behind. If we are merciful and serene, taking the initiative in reaching out to others, our very approachability will overcome the shyness and fear of those for whom we reach out.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 5:1"],"EPH.5.1":["When he wrote to the Corinthians, indeed, he said \"be imitators\" of me … for though they could not instantaneously become imitators of Christ, it was still a great thing for them if they could be imitators of the imitator. But to the Ephesians, since they are those to whom he has revealed such great mysteries, he neither says \"be imitators of me\" nor \"be imitators of Christ\" but \"be imitators of God.\" This does not imply that it is less to be an imitator of Christ than of God, for Christ is God.… Admittedly much that God has done we humans can hardly be said to imitate. But in the way that he is merciful to all and rains on good and bad, so we may pour out mercy upon all we meet. When we do this, we shall be beloved children. We shall be imitating either Paul or, as I rather think, God himself.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 5:1"],"EPH.5.2":["Who is it that truly walks in love? The one who, for the salvation of others, contends against sin to the point of shedding blood, so as even to give up his soul for them. That is the one who walks in love, imitating Christ.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 5:2"],"EPH.5.3":["Even one who is in fact free of fornication is not holy if he remains mentally preoccupied with some uncleanness or with the avaricious pursuit of the pleasures that have once delighted him.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 5:3-4"],"EPH.5.4":["The \"silly talk\" to which Paul refers occurs not only among those who tell dirty jokes to get a cheap laugh. He is also referring to those who put on frivolous airs and to those who manipulate whoever they are trying to please. There is another kind of silly talk that occurs among those reckoned to be the intellectuals of the age who, when disputing on matters of natural science, imagine that they have fully comprehended the sands of the shore, the drops of ocean, the extent of heavens and the minuteness of earth.… Note that levity follows silly talk. The intent here is to speak of frivolous and inappropriate stories. The difference between silliness and levity is this: silliness has nothing in it that is wise or worthy of the human heart. Levity devolves from a clever mind and deliberately seeks out certain words, be they witty, vulgar, obscene or facetious, in a jocularity the sole aim of which is to get a laugh.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 5:3-4"],"EPH.5.5":["Are those merely guilty of silliness and inordinate levity to be kept out of the kingdom of God? Are they excluded on the same basis as those sins that he has marked off specifically? It would seem a cruel sentence not to pardon the weakness of human frailty, so that our words condemned us even when said in jest.… Yet in making this distinction [between lesser and more serious sins] we are not making excuses for silliness and levity. They do not exclude from the kingdom. But they are not negligible and remind us that just as \"the Father has many mansions\" and \"one star differs from another in glory,\" so too will it be in the resurrection of the dead.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 5:5"],"EPH.5.8":["The darkness is being turned into light. There is not, as some heretics argue, a nature so alienated that it cannot receive salvation.… Those who receive salvation—the righteous—are \"the light of the world.\" Those who refuse, the unrighteous, are in consequence called darkness.… The difference and distance between one and the other is clearly seen by their own fruits.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 5:8"],"EPH.5.9":["Christ himself is rightly called goodness, righteousness and truth. He is goodness in that he gives grace to those who believe in him, not according to their works but according to his mercy. He is himself righteousness in that he gives to each what he deserves. He is himself truth in that he is the one who knows the causes of all creatures and all things.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 5:9"],"EPH.5.11":["Paul uses the term fruits in the case of the Spirit, works in the case of the sin nature. … In the present case he indeed is saying that the works of darkness are unfruitful. Those who do these works share an association with darkness.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 5:11","No one is prepared to admonish sinners except one who does not deserve to be called a hypocrite [as with the account in Lk 6:42].… Only those prophets who were themselves unpolluted by any stain of sin were in a position to upbraid others for their wrongdoing. From this we learn that the one who is in the best position to reprimand is the one who cannot himself rightly be reprimanded in turn.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 5:11"],"EPH.5.14":["The one who is content with a simple answer will say indeed that Paul must have read this phrase in some arcane prophet or in the writings called apocryphal. He then brought the text into the open, as he manifestly does in other places—not to substantiate the apocryphal texts but in the same way that he makes use of verses elsewhere from Aratus, Epimenides and Menander to substantiate what he says on other occasions.… Someone less content with this simple answer might argue that the apostle said this as an exhortation to penitence. It is as if he were assuming the voice of the Holy Spirit. For my part, scanty as my knowledge is, I have nowhere found this written after diligently scouring all the editions of the ancient Scriptures and the texts of the Hebrews themselves.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 5:14"],"EPH.5.16":["Christ, the Sun of Righteousness, has risen. Rise up from the sleep of the age. Walk cautiously and prudently. Cast off folly. Take hold of wisdom. In this way you will be able to avoid changing yourself constantly as you walk through the vicissitudes of the times. Rather you will find a unity within yourself even amid the diversity of the times.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 5:16"],"EPH.5.18":["The fourteenth place among the works of the flesh is held by drunkenness. For drunkards shall not possess the kingdom of God. And the Lord to the disciples: \"Take heed\", he says, \"lest perhaps your hearts be weighed down with wine and debauchery\" (Luke 21:34). By wine a man's senses are overturned, his feet stumble, his mind wavers, lust is kindled. Hence the Apostle cries out: \"And wine, in which is licentiousness\" (Eph. 5:18). Each one has the power of his own opinion. I follow the Apostle: that in wine there is licentiousness, in wine drunkenness. And that drunkenness and licentiousness are numbered among the works of the flesh, not even he who is overcome by these same passions can deny. And although some think I deserve blame in that book which I wrote on preserving virginity, because I said that young girls ought to flee wine like poison, I shall not repent of the opinion. For there it was rather the work of wine than a creature of God that was condemned by us; and we took away license from a virgin glowing with the natural heat of her age, lest under the pretext of drinking a little she should drink more and perish. Otherwise, we knew both that wine is consecrated into the blood of Christ and that Timothy was commanded to drink wine.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"EPH.5.19":["Our hymns declare the strength and majesty of God. They express gratitude for his benefits and his deeds. Our psalms convey this gratitude also, since the word Alleluia is either prefaced or appended to them. Our psalms properly belong to the domain of ethics, teaching us what is to be done and avoided. The domain of the psalms is the body as an instrument of grace. But the domain of the spiritual canticles is the mind. As we sing spiritual canticles we hear discourses on things above, on the harmony of the world, on the subtly ordered concord of all creatures. These spiritual songs help us express our meaning more plainly for the sake of simple folk. It is more with the mind than with the voice that we sing, offer psalms and praise God.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 5:19"],"EPH.5.20":["Paul now calls us to \"give thanks always and in everything.\" This is to be understood in a double sense, both in adversity and in good times.… In this way the mind rejoices and bursts out in gratitude to God, not only for what we think good but for what troubles us and happens against our will.… It is obvious that generally we are called to give thanks to God for the sun that rises, for the day that goes by and for the night that brings rest … for the rains that come, for the earth that brings forth fruit and for the elements in their course.… Finally, we are thankful that we are born, that we have being, that our wants are sufficiently taken care of in the world, as if we lived in the house of an extremely powerful family patriarch, knowing that whatever is in the world has been created on our account. In this way we give thanks when we are grateful for the benefits that come to us from God. All these things, however, the heathen also does, and the Jew and the publican and the Gentile. But the second sense of giving thanks is seen in the special gift of Christians to give thanks to God even in seeming adversity.… Those who are saintly in their own eyes are prone to give thanks to God because they have been released from dangers and afflictions. But according to the apostle the greater virtue is to give thanks to God precisely amid those very dangers and afflictions.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 5:20"],"EPH.5.21":["Let bishops hear this, let priests hear, let every rank of learning get this clear: In the church, leaders are servants. Let them imitate the apostle.… The difference between secular rulers and Christian leaders is that the former love to boss their subordinates whereas the latter serve them. We are that much greater if we are considered least of all.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 5:21"],"EPH.5.22":["The union of Christ and the church is holy. So is the proper union of husband and wife holy. Just as a congregation of heretics, however, cannot rightly be called the church of Christ and cannot have Christ as its head, so it is that a union between husband and wife cannot be truly called holy if there is a disregard for the way of life taught by Christ.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 5:22-23"],"EPH.5.23":["Note that the church is never called the flesh but always the body of Christ. Whatever lives according to the flesh must necessarily be embodied. That is true. But it is not true that whatever is body is consequently living according to the flesh.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 5:22-23"],"EPH.5.31":["The same allegorical interpretation applies both to Christ and to the church, that Adam is to prefigure Christ and Eve the church. For \"the last Adam was made a lifegiving spirit.\" Just as the whole human race is born from Adam and his wife, so the whole multitude of believers has been born of Christ and the church.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 5:31"],"EPH.5.32":["Gregory of Nazianzus, a very eloquent man and outstandingly versed in the Scriptures, used to say while discussing this passage with me: See how great the promise in this passage is! The apostle, interpreting it as an analogy of Christ and the church, does not himself even profess to have expounded it as the dignity of the idea demanded. He is in effect saying: \"I know that this analogy is full of ineffable promises. It requires a divine heart in its interpretation. But in the weakness of my understanding I can only say that in the meantime it should be interpreted as Christ in relation to the church. Nothing is greater than Christ and the church. Even all that is said of Adam and Eve is to be interpreted with reference to Christ and the church.\"\n— Commentary on Ephesians 5:32"],"EPH.6.1":["It is not clear whether this saying means that children are to obey their \"parents in the Lord\" or that in the Lord children are to obey their parents. I take it both ways. We are to obey those parents who gave birth to us in the Lord, as through Paul and the apostles they were spiritually born, and do what they say. And we should submit in the Lord to our own parents, from whom we were born according to the flesh, performing all their commands that are not contrary to the Lord's will.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 6:1"],"EPH.6.2":["A promise is attached to this commandment.… It is found in the Decalogue. It is the first command in the second table of the law. It was given to the people as they were leaving Egypt.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 6:1"],"EPH.6.3":["What is promised in honoring one's father and mother is not intended for the Jews alone, nor was it intended as an outright financial exchange.… For there surely have been many who, even while being obedient to their parents, died abruptly. Others who have been irreverent to their parents have reached extreme old age.… Rightly interpreted, the command looks for the land that the Lord promises to Israel. It is offered to those who have left the spiritual Egypt. It calls us to patience as we traverse the vast and terrible wilderness of this life, as we overcome great challengers whom the Lord strikes down and as we enter into the Judea that flows with milk and honey.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 6:1"],"EPH.6.4":["Instead of asking parents to give their children a fancy education in secular literature, instead of making them read comedies and recite the obscene writings of the theater, he asks the Ephesian laypersons, many of whom, as is common in a population, were engaged in the ordinary occupations of this life, that they should \"educate their children in every doctrine and counsel of the Lord.\" Overseers and elders ought to take note of this.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 6:4"],"EPH.6.9":["Serving is to be done \"in singleness of heart.\" One is called to \"do the will of God\" and to do it \"with a good will,\" that is, with benevolence from the heart. Everyone will finally receive just judgment from the Lord for whatever good he has done. If he has served as a lowly one in the household, he will be judged justly according to his responsibility. The master of the household is not to be overbearing, not quick to punish. He should know that he too has a master in heaven who will judge justly, who will judge fairly according to our own willing, who will judge by deeds, not status.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 6:9"],"EPH.6.11":["From what we read of the Lord our Savior throughout the Scriptures, it is manifestly clear that the whole armor of Christ is the Savior himself. It is he whom we are asked to \"put on.\" It is one and the same thing to say \"Put on the whole armor of God\" and \"Put on the Lord Jesus Christ.\" Our belt is truth and our breastplate is righteousness. The Savior is also called both \"truth\" and \"righteousness.\" So no one can doubt that he himself is that very belt and breastplate. On this principle he is also to be understood as the \"preparation of the gospel of peace.\" He himself is the \"shield of faith\" and the \"helmet of salvation.\" He is the \"sword of the Spirit,\" because he is the Word of God, living and efficacious, the utterance of which is stronger than any helmet and sharp on both sides.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 6:11"],"EPH.6.12":["The battle is not against flesh and blood or ordinary temptations. The scene is the war of flesh against spirit. We are being incited to become entrapped in the works of the flesh. … But this is not merely a physical temptation. It is not merely the inward struggle against flesh and blood as such. Rather Satan has cleverly transformed himself into an angel of light. He is striving to persuade us to regard him as a messenger of goodness. This is how he throws his full might into the struggle. He employs deceptive signs and lying omens. He sets before us every possible ruse of evil. Then, when he has so ensnared us that we trust him, he says to us, \"Thus says the Lord.\" This is not flesh and blood deceiving us. It is not a typical human temptation. It is the work of principalities and powers, the ruler of darkness and spiritual wickedness.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 6:11","We have elsewhere read the expression world rulers nowhere in the Old or the New Testament—only here. Paul the apostle employs this name because it was necessary for him, in addressing the Ephesians, to apply new terms to new and invisible subjects.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 6:11","\"Hosts of wickedness in heavenly places?\" This does not mean that demons dwell in heaven but that the air above has received this name, as birds who fly through the air are called \"fowls of heaven.\" … It is indeed impious to suppose that the spirits of wickedness in the heavens occupy the same heaven of which God says, \"It is my throne.\"\n— Commentary on Ephesians 6:11","There is need, therefore, of the lifting up of the eyes and of spiritual understanding, that we may see the opposing powers which have lifted up their horn against us, and of which the Apostle speaks: Our wrestling is not against flesh and blood, but against principalities, against powers, against the rulers of this world, of this darkness, against the spiritual forces of wickedness in the heavenly places. These four adversaries, who were always fighting against the saints, Christ crushed at his coming. And of these the psalmist says over again: You shall walk upon the asp and the basilisk, and you shall trample the lion and the dragon.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"EPH.6.13":["The \"evil day\" may arguably signify the present time.… But the better interpretation is that \"to stand in the evil day\" is a reference to the final consummation and judgment. Then the devil, our enemy and our adversary, will struggle to keep us in his clutches. Who will be freed from him? One who understands what is said of the poor and needy: \"the Lord will deliver him in the evil day.\" … Still another interpretation, however, expounds this more simply: The Ephesians are now being encouraged to prepare for future temptations and persecutions. Paul the apostle in his prophetic spirit saw them as coming at a later time. They are being counseled to do anything they can that might enable them to stand in the faith of the gospel and not to lapse under persecution.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 6:13"],"EPH.6.14":["\"Stand therefore, having your loins girt about with truth.\" Concerning the members of the soul mentioned in scripture, which are all referred to as members of the body, there is no doubt. I believe that one such member is now mentioned, the loins, which we are commanded to gird with truth. It is also written in the Gospel of Luke: \"Let your loins be girded about, and your lights burning\" (Luke 12:35). Therefore, the loins are always mentioned in regards to generation and seed, according to: \"I will set fruit of your loins upon your throne\" (Psalm 132:11). And elsewhere: \"For he was still in the loins of his father Abraham, when Melchisedech met him\" (Hebrews 7:10). It seems to us that those who do not render what is due to their wife or serve their lust, but instead imitate the innate God and do not participate in matters of generation, have girded their loins with truth. I believe that this is also signified in the case of John the Baptist, who had a leather girdle around his loins (Matthew 3), and was not one of the unclean who, for reasons of seminal flux, are banished outside the camp and cannot dwell with the ark of the Lord (Leviticus 13). Nor is he like those written about in Numbers: \"Let his garments be torn\" (Numbers 8). But whoever is girded with truth in Christ, raises these garments up high, and draws them upwards. He covers, tightens, and includes the filthiness of naked sides with the spiritual belt. He is prepared for battle, and has shining works, which are referred to as \"burning lights.\" And having put on the breastplate of righteousness. Just as it is difficult to be injured in those places that hold life, where one, wearing armor made of tightly joined links and iron circles, is protected by the breastplate of virtue, so too one who is surrounded by a diverse garment of righteousness will not accept an arrow in his liver like a deer nor will he fall into desires and frenzies, but will have a pure heart, with God as the maker of this breastplate, who crafts all the weapons for each of the saints and does not allow him to be struck by a flying dart or burned by flaming arrows.\n— Commentary on Ephesians","It would take long now to tell how the strength of the devil is above all in the loins, and how the promise is made to David: Of the fruit of your loin I will set upon your throne, and that saying of the Apostle: For he was still in the loin of his father Abraham, Levi was, when Melchizedek went to meet Abraham. And how John is girded with a leather girdle, and how the disciples are commanded by the Savior: Let your loins be girded. And the Apostle to the Ephesians: Stand therefore, having your loins girded in truth; for although ἄσκησις (ascetic discipline) and continence of life avail very much toward the mortifying of the loins, yet nothing so mortifies them as the knowledge of the truth. Hence it is said: Gird your loins in truth. For if Christ is the truth, he who has believed in Christ with his whole mind has mortified his loins in Christ.\n— Commentary on Nahum"],"EPH.6.15":["It is asked whether he says \"the gospel of peace\" to distinguish it from another gospel. Or is it rather the distinguishing property of the gospel that it may be called \"the gospel of peace?\" If anyone therefore has peace, he is shod with the gospel of Christ. With this footwear he is prepared to walk. Being prepared, however, he does well not to imagine himself already perfect. Rather he merely is prepared to press on and by pressing on hopes to arrive at the goal.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 6:15"],"EPH.6.17":["Because of this helmet of salvation all the senses in our head remain intact. It especially protects the eyes. Solomon in Ecclesiastes notes that \"the eyes of the wise are in the head.\" Paul understood the importance of headship. He knew why the eyes are located in the head. If Christ is the head of a person of faith and \"the eyes of the wise are in the head,\" it follows that all our senses, mind, thought, speech and counsel (if, that is, we are wise) are in Christ.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 6:17"],"EPH.6.19":["This is to be understood as if he said, \"Let the treasuries be opened. Let the promises hidden from ages be revealed. Let the Spirit enter to bring forth those things that have been concealed.\" That this is indeed the meaning of this passage … is clarified by what follows: \"in confidence,\" he says, \"to make known the mystery of the gospel.\"\n— Commentary on Ephesians 6:20"],"EPH.6.21":["This might be understood in two ways: Either Tychicus was sent to Ephesus to announce to them that Paul's chains had become famous in the whole praetorium and his imprisonment had been profitable to the faith of the gospel … or Tychicus was sent to tell them more about Paul's life and daily work, of which they were not aware, so as to give them a clearer pattern of how to live.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 6:21-22"],"EPH.6.23":["There are many gifts bestowed by God the Father and our Lord Jesus Christ, but of all these peace has a crucial place. This peace passes all understanding. It preserves the body and mind of the saints. It is a certain serenity and tranquillity of a mind at rest. It is protected from the universal storm and maelstrom of perturbations. So also is \"love with faith,\" which at the same time is given to us by God the Father and the Holy Spirit, so that we may love God from the whole heart and our neighbor as ourselves.\n— Commentary on Ephesians 6:23"],"PHP.1.1":["Should anyone think that the view that bishop and presbyter are one, and that the one (presbyter, that is, \"elder\") is a name of age, the other a name of office, is not that of the Scriptures but our own, let him read again the words of the Apostle to the Philippians, saying: \"Paul and Timothy, servants of Jesus Christ, to all the saints in Christ Jesus who are at Philippi, with the bishops and deacons: grace to you and peace\" (Phil. 1:1, 2), and the rest. Philippi is a single city of Macedonia, and certainly in one city there could not have been several bishops, as they are called. But because at that time they called the same men bishops whom they also called presbyters, for that reason he spoke of bishops indifferently as of presbyters. This may still seem doubtful to someone, unless it is proved by a second testimony.\n— Commentary on Titus"],"PHP.2.10-11":["And he swears by himself that the sentence of his mouth, and the word he has once uttered concerning the salvation of the nations, will by no means be made void; but that the promise of him who said above: Be converted to me, and you will be saved, all the ends of the earth, will be fulfilled in deed. And he swears, according to the Apostle (Heb 6), so that through two immovable things, in which it is impossible for God to lie, we may have firm consolation; but he also swears this: that, idols abandoned, every knee should bow to him, of those in heaven, on earth, and under the earth, and every tongue of mortals should swear by him (Phil 2). In this the Christian people is plainly signified. For it is the custom of the Church to bend the knee to Christ: which the Jews, displaying the pride of their mind, do not do at all. And every tongue too of all the barbarian nations confesses God, not in synagogues but in the Churches of Christ.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"PHP.3.8":["I have read in someone's volume that the cantharus is understood of the heretics for this reason, that they hold doctrines to be compared with dung. Hence the Apostle too says that he counted the error of the old teaching as scybala, that is, as dung. Not that the old law, as the Manichaeans think, is reckoned as scybala in comparison with the Gospel — which it is impious to say, since both Testaments belong to the one God —, but that the teachings of the Pharisees, and the precepts of men, and the Deuterosis of the Jews are called dung by the Apostle.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"PHP.3.12-15":["Hence in another place too the Apostle himself says that he is imperfect and again perfect, which, if it lacks interpretation, seems to be a contradiction; for he says: Not that I have already received, or am already perfect. Brethren, I do not reckon that I have laid hold; but one thing: forgetting what lies behind, stretching forward to what lies ahead, I pursue the appointed goal, toward the prize of the upward calling of God; and when we would think that by his own profession he is not perfect, and that he is seeking rather than has found what is true, he says that not only he himself but others with him are perfect: As many then as are perfect, let us be of this mind; and the sense of this place is: 'In comparison with the rest of men, who have no care for the knowledge of the Scriptures nor of the mysteries of God, I confess that I am perfect; but as regards the knowledge of the divine majesty, I now see in an enigma, and through mist and darkness, and I say with the prophet: Your knowledge has become wonderful to me; it is too strong, and I shall not be able to reach it' —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","Then too, he does not seek market days or solemn feasts, nor does he observe any difference of days, because every time is a festival as well; but the people enter and go out only on market days and on solemn feasts, and not by the same way, but by one way and then another: for it is written: And when the people of the land enter in the sight of the Lord on the solemn feasts, he who enters by the gate of the north to worship, let him go out by the way of the southern gate; and he who enters by the way of the southern gate, let him go out by the way of the gate of the north: he shall not return by the way of the gate by which he entered, but shall go out opposite it — which is commanded not only to those who go out of Sodom, and to whom it is said: Do not look back, nor only to those who put their hand to the plow, that they not look back at the things that are behind their back, but those too who are set in the house of the Lord are commanded not to look behind their backs and return to the weak and beggarly elements, and, having begun in the spirit, be finished in the flesh, but to advance from spiritual things also to greater ones and to say with the Apostle: Forgetting the things past, and stretching myself out to the things before, so that they may by no means know in part and prophesy in part, but come to the perfect: yet according to that perfection which human nature can contain, so that we may understand that saying of the Gospel: And you, when you have done all things, say: We are unprofitable servants; what we ought to do, we have done; whence the Apostle too, in the same epistle, speaks of two perfections: for as one perfect he writes: Not that I have already received, and that I am already perfect; but I pursue, if I may lay hold of that in which I also have been laid hold of by Christ, and at once he speaks as one perfect: But one thing: forgetting the things behind, stretching myself out to the things that are before, I pursue according to the purpose toward the prize of the upward calling. As many therefore as are perfect, we are of this mind — for how can the statement stand, that he who had said: Not that I have already received or that I am already perfect, should dare to say: As many therefore as are perfect, we are of this mind? —, from which it is plain that every human being and the whole creation, although it has come to perfection, nevertheless needs the mercy of God and possesses full perfection from grace, not from merit —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"COL.1.22":["For flesh is defined one way, the body another; all flesh is body, but not every body is flesh. Flesh is properly what is comprised in blood, veins, bones, and sinews. Although the body is also called flesh, yet sometimes it is designated ethereal or aërial, because it is not subject to touch and sight; and yet it is frequently both visible and tangible. A wall is a body, but is not flesh; a stone is a body, but it is not said to be flesh. Wherefore the Apostle calls some bodies celestial, some terrestrial. A celestial body is that of the sun, moon, stars; a terrestrial body is that of fire, air, water, and the rest, which bodies being inanimate are known as consisting of material elements. ... Lastly, the Apostle, in his Epistle to the Colossians, wishing to show that the body of Christ was made of flesh, and was not spiritual, aërial, attenuated, said significantly, \"And you, when you were some time alienated from Christ and enemies of His spirit in evil works, He has reconciled in the body of His flesh through death.\" And again in the same Epistle: \"In whom ye were circumcised with a circumcision made without hands in the putting off of the body of the flesh.\" If by body is meant flesh only, and the word is not ambiguous, nor capable of diverse significations, it was quite superfluous to use both expressions—bodily and of flesh—as though body did not imply flesh.\n— To Pammachius Against John of Jerusalem"],"COL.2.3":["The Apostle writes concerning the Savior: In whom are hidden all the treasures of wisdom and knowledge. So all the treasures of wisdom and knowledge are in Christ, but they are hidden. Why hidden? After the resurrection, when asked by the apostles about the day, he answered more plainly: It is not yours to know the times and the moments which the Father has set in his own power. When he says: It is not yours to know, he shows that he himself knows, but that it is not expedient for the apostles to know, so that, always uncertain about the coming of the judge, they may live each day as though they were to be judged on the next.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"COL.2.8":["On the contrary, it can be objected to us that Paul the apostle, writing to the Colossians, has named other things the elements of the world: \"See\", he says, \"that no one plunder you through philosophy and empty deceit, according to the tradition of men, according to the elements of the world, and not according to Christ\" (Col. 2:8). But from what he added, \"according to the tradition of men, and empty deceit\", he shows that the same elements are not named to the Colossians and to the Galatians. For from these elements, after the fullness of time has come, we are redeemed, and, advancing to greater things, we receive the adoption of sons. But of those nothing of the kind is said as something that follows; rather, the elements are taken simply for letters.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"COL.2.13-15":["For he bore our sins in his body (1 Pet 2), fastening them to the wood of the cross, that he might blot out the handwriting which we had made to the devil and his angels, written by the hands of the soul, that is, by its works. Of these the apostle Paul speaks: And you, when you were dead in sins and in the desires of the flesh, he made alive with Christ, forgiving us all sins. And blotting out the handwriting that was against us, which was contrary to us, he took it out of the way, fastening it to the cross: despoiling the principalities, he put the powers to shame, triumphing over them confidently (Eph 1:2; Col 2:14, 15).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"COL.2.16":["The Apostle explains this passage more fully to the Colossians, saying: \"Let no one therefore judge you in food, or in drink, or in part of a feast day, or of a new moon, or of sabbaths, which are a shadow of things to come\" (Col. 2:16). He put \"part of a feast day\" here to distinguish it from the perpetual festival, so that we may hold not a brief one and, so to speak, some small particle of the whole body, but the whole span of our life as a perpetual solemnity in Christ.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"COL.3.19":["To husbands it belongs only to love, because love is of the perfect: Husbands, he says, love your wives and do not be bitter towards them, even though they provoke you to anger and do such things as to deserve to endure bitterness. For this is what παραπικραίνεσθαι means; yet do not repay them in kind with bitterness.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"1TH.4.9-12":["The Macedonians are praised for love, and for hospitality and the reception of the brothers. Hence it is written to them: \"But concerning the love of the brotherhood we have no need to write to you. For you yourselves have learned from God to love one another. And indeed you do it toward all the brothers in all Macedonia\" (1 Thess. 4:9). But they are rebuked because they go idly round the houses, and, looking for another's food, while they desire to please each one and run about hither and thither, they report what is done at each person's house. For it follows: \"But we beseech you, brothers, that you abound more, and take pains to be quiet, and that you do your own business, and work with your hands, as we commanded you, so that you may walk honestly toward those who are outside, and may desire nothing of anyone\" (1 Thess. 4:10-11). And lest anyone think this was urged more from the duty of a teacher than because of a fault of the people, in the second letter to the same he presses and repeats it, saying: \"For even when we were with you, we declared this to you, that if anyone will not work, neither let him eat. For we have heard that some among you walk restlessly, doing no work, but acting as busybodies. Now those who are such we charge and beseech in the Lord Jesus Christ, that, working in silence, they eat their own bread\" (2 Thess. 3:10 ff.).\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"1TH.5.3":["But it must be reckoned, following the Apostle, that after the battles and dissensions, the pestilences, famines and earthquakes, and the other things by which the human race is laid waste, a brief peace will follow, which will promise that all things are quiet, so that the faith of believers may be tested, whether, when the evils have passed, they still hope that the judge is to come. For this is what we read in Paul: When they say: Peace and security, then sudden destruction will come upon them, like the pain of a woman in labor, and they will not escape.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"1TH.5.7":["Let each one call his conscience to mind and recall the works of darkness in darkness, and let him know that it is written: Everyone who does evil hates the light, lest he be reproved by the light, and he will understand that this has been fulfilled in himself: He who is drunken is drunken by night, which cannot stand according to the letter: for how many are drunken at luncheons and banquets by day?; but because the drunkenness of the heart, which offends God, belongs to darkness and not to light, therefore: Whoever is drunken is drunken in the night.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"1TH.5.17":["'And meditates on his law day and night.' [Psalm 1:2] The psalmist has already mentioned three things one must not do: follow the Counsel of the wicked; stand in the way of sinners; sit in the company of the insolent. These three things we must not do, but there are also two things that we must do, for it will not be sufficient for us to shun evil unless we seek good. 'But delights in the law of the Lord.' The psalm does not say fears the law, but delights in the law. There are many who observe the law through fear, but fear as a motive for action is far from meritorious. 'But delights in the law of the Lord' - wholeheartedly he obeys the Lord's command.\n\n'And meditates on his law day and night.' Mere words cannot express adequately what the mind conceives. 'But delights in the law of the Lord.' Some one may say: 'Look, I want to obey the law of God, and so because I want to obey, I am happy.' But consider the words that follow. It is not enough to want the law of God, but one must meditate on His law day and night. 'Meditate day and night.' Someone else may object: 'This is too much for human nature to endure, for one must walk, and drink, and eat, and sleep, and perform all the other necessities of life. How, then, meditate on the law of God day and night, and especially since the Apostle says: \"Pray without ceasing\"? [1 Thess 5:17] How can I be praying during the time that I am sleeping?'\n\nMeditation on the law does not consist in reading, but in doing, just as the Apostle says in another place: 'Whether you eat or drink, or do anything else, do all for the glory of God. [1 Cor. 10:31] Even if I merely stretch forth my hand in alms giving, I am meditating on the law of God; if I visit the sick, my feet are meditating on the law of God; if I do what is prescribed, I am praying with my whole body what others are praying with their lips. The Jews, indeed, prayed with their lips, but our prayer is works.\n— Homily 1, on Psalm 1"],"2TH.2.8":["And all his enemies will be destroyed, not that the enemies perish and cease to exist, but that they perish in that they are enemies. As it is written to the Thessalonians: Whom the Lord Jesus will kill with the spirit of his mouth. This killing signifies not abolition but the cessation of the most wicked life by which they had lived badly before. In short, he adds: And he will destroy him by the brightness of his coming. But he would never destroy him if killing meant abolition, since he would already have ceased to exist; so here too the consuming of the enemies is to be understood according to the Proverbs of Solomon, in which it is written: Destruction will lodge with the ungodly: it will not be perpetual, it will not reduce them to nothing, but it will lodge with them as long as it takes for the ungodliness that is in them to be consumed. For God created man that he should not perish, and he did not make death.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"2TH.2.10-12":["But if the heretic who does not receive the Old Testament is scandalized that the Lord is said to mingle a spirit of error or of giddiness, let him hear what is written in the Apostle, that is, in the New Testament: God delivered them up in the desires of their heart to uncleanness (Rom 1:24). And again: For this reason God delivered them up to passions of ignominy. And again: God delivered them up to a reprobate mind, to do those things which are not fitting. Now they are delivered up in the desires of their heart because they changed the glory of the incorruptible God into the likeness of an image of corruptible man, and of birds, and of four-footed beasts, and of creeping things. This we read not only in the Epistle to the Romans, but also in that to the Thessalonians concerning Antichrist: Because they did not receive the love of the truth, that they might be saved, God shall send them the working of error, that they may believe a lie, and that all may be judged who did not believe the truth, but took pleasure in the lie (2 Thess 2:10); which I think the apostle Paul took from the present reading of Isaiah, in which he says: The Lord has mingled for them a spirit of error, and they have made Egypt err in all its works, as, he says, a drunken man errs and vomits, one who is drunk with vices.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"2TH.3.17":["And so that no suspicion of a forged Epistle might arise, from this place to the end he wrote it out with his own hand, showing that what came before had been written down by another. That Epistles were being sent under his name by false teachers he also shows when writing to the Thessalonians: \"But we beseech you, brothers, by the coming of our Lord Jesus Christ, and by our gathering together unto him, that you be not quickly moved from your mind, nor be terrified, neither by spirit, nor by word, nor by an Epistle as if sent by us, as though the day of the Lord were at hand; let no one deceive you in any way\" (2 Thess. 2:1 ff.). And to rescue the whole Epistle he was sending from the suspicion of falsity, he signed it at the end with his own hand, saying: \"The greeting with my own hand, Paul's, which is the sign in every Epistle: thus I write: The grace of our Lord Jesus Christ be with you all\" (2 Thess. 3:17, 18). The letter to the Colossians too, which he had dictated, he likewise signed underneath with his own hand: \"The greeting with my own hand, Paul's: Be mindful of my chains\". And wherever he knew that false teachers were present who might sow new doctrines by means of the Apostle's authority, he signed the Epistle with his own hand.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"1TI.2.12":["Since, then, he taught first what the old women ought to be, and after the things they have in common with the old men set out also the things proper to them, that they be full of an honorable and holy demeanor and of all seemliness: not accusers, not disparaging others, not with their senses seized by wine; now, accordingly, he hands them the reins of teaching, so that, when they are such, they may have freedom to teach, namely to teach the things that are good. For although in another place he has said, \"But I do not permit women to teach\" (1 Tim. 2:12), it must be understood thus: that teaching directed at men is taken from them. For the rest, let them teach the young women as their daughters.\n— Commentary on Titus"],"1TI.3.1":["For it is not rank and the names of rank, but the work of the rank, that is accustomed to save princes, and judges, and prophets, and priests: He who desires the episcopate, he says, desires a good work. See what he said: He desires a good work, not a rank. But if, despising the work, one looks only at the rank, swiftly the tower in Siloam falls, and the heights of the cedar are struck by lightning, and the upraised neck is broken, and the swan, with neck outstretched and straining aloft, is reckoned among the unclean birds.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"1TI.3.15":["The apostles, therefore, are the pillars of the Church, and especially Peter, James and John, two of whom were found worthy to go up the mountain with the Lord, and one of whom in the Apocalypse introduces the Savior speaking: \"Whoever conquers, I will make him a pillar in the temple of my God\" (Rev. 3:12), teaching that all believers who conquer the adversary can become pillars of the Church. And Paul, writing to Timothy, says: \"That you may know how you ought to conduct yourself in the house of God, which is the Church of the living God, the pillar and foundation of the truth\" (1 Tim. 3:15). By these and other passages we are instructed that in the Scriptures the apostles and all believers, as well as the Church itself, are called a pillar, and that it makes no difference whether something is said of the body or of the members, since the body too is divided into members, and the members belong to the body.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"1TI.3.16":["He appeared in the flesh, was justified in the spirit. Of him Judas the traitor too confesses: I have sinned, betraying righteous blood (Matt 27:4). And Pilate's wife: Have nothing to do with that righteous man: for I have suffered many things today in dreams because of him. And it is to be noted that he was not justified so that from being unrighteous he might become righteous; but the righteous one is justified, not so that he might begin to be what he was not, but so that what he was might appear to all.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"1TI.4.1-2":["On that day, which he mentions so often, all idols shall be taken away from the land: whether those idols of which the psalmist says: The images of the nations are silver and gold, the works of men's hands, so that there may be no other religion but only that of the Christian name, and of which it is written in the prophet: Howl, you carved images in Jerusalem and in Samaria. For as I have done to Samaria and to her carved images, so will I do to Jerusalem and to her idols; or those idols of which the Apostle speaks: But the Spirit says manifestly that in the last times some shall depart from the faith, giving heed to seducing spirits and doctrines of demons, in the hypocrisy of liars who have their conscience cauterized. For just as idols are made by the hand of a craftsman, so the perverse doctrine of the heretics turns into an idol whatever it has feigned, and causes the antichrist to be worshipped in place of Christ.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"1TI.4.12":["And what he says, \"in incorruption\", is to be taken thus: that incorruption (incorruptio) properly means virginity. Indeed, those who are virgins are commonly called incorrupt (incorrupti), and those who have ceased to be virgins are named corrupted (corrupti); and we say: She who was once a virgin has been corrupted. Hence I think that Titus, believing the Gospel before he was taken up with the work of the flesh, received baptism and remained a virgin, and is now admonished by the Apostle to show a pattern of himself in incorruption: an incorruption which, indeed, we do not see in Timothy. For when he said to him, \"Let no one despise your youth, but be a pattern of the faithful in speech, in conduct, in love, in faith, in purity\" (1 Tim. 4:12), he was silent about incorruption and set down only \"purity\". But purity can be understood also in celibacy without virginity. Unless perhaps we take purity as in the mind, and incorruption as in the body, according to what is written elsewhere in the definition of a virgin: \"That she may be holy in body and in spirit\" (1 Cor. 7). And he himself has now added in sequence: \"in doctrine, in incorruption, in chastity\".\n— Commentary on Titus","Let no one despise you. Someone might think that this is the very same thing now written to Titus that was said to Timothy: \"Let no one despise your youth\" (1 Tim. 4:12). But we, following the distinction of the Greek language, think that περιφρονείτω, which is written here, means one thing, and καταφρονείτω, which was said to Timothy, another, and that the prepositions περὶ and κατὰ make a different sense. And that the apostle Paul uses, not by chance and at whim, different nouns and verbs, and even different prepositions, according to the variety of the matters, can be made plain from what he says: \"For the woman is from the man, but the man through the woman\" (1 Cor. 11:12). And elsewhere: \"For from him and through him and in him are all things\" (Rom. 11:36). And also this: \"Paul an apostle, not from men, nor through man\" (Gal. 1:1). We think, therefore, that καταφρόνησιν properly pertains to contempt, as when someone, stretched on the rack and among the hot plates, despises pain, and fears neither the threats of the judge nor the roar of the crowd standing around, but for the confession of martyrdom despises and disdains all torments. On the other hand there is also an evil contempt, of which Habakkuk too, the Holy Spirit speaking in him, testifies: \"Behold, you despisers, and look, and wonder at wonders, and perish\" (Hab. 1:5). According to which we have said that it was written to Timothy also: \"Let no one despise your youth\" (1 Tim. 4:12), that is: I do not want you to show yourself such that you could deservedly be despised by anyone.\n— Commentary on Titus"],"1TI.6.17-19":["He also bears a brother's need who helps a poor man weighed down by the burden of want, and makes himself friends of the mammon of iniquity (Luke 16); and him Christ addresses after the resurrection: \"Come to me, you blessed of my Father, possess the kingdom prepared for you from the foundation of the world. For I was hungry, and you gave me to eat; I was thirsty, and you gave me to drink\" (Matt. 25:34, 35). In accordance with this sense Paul also, teaching Timothy in another Epistle, added: \"Command the rich of this age not to be high-minded\" (that is to say, \"not to be proud\"), \"nor to hope in the uncertainty of riches, but in God, who furnishes all things abundantly for enjoyment; to do good, to be rich in good works, to give readily, to share, to lay up for themselves a good foundation for the future, that they may lay hold of the true life\" (1 Tim. 6:17-19). Whoever has laid hold of the true life, that is, of him who says: \"I am the life\" (John 14:6), has fulfilled the law of Christ, which tends toward life.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"2TI.4.8":["And the crowns, or the crown, are set upon Joshua son of Jehozadak, the high priest, because, as we advance and return to better things, the Lord is crowned through each of our virtues; or rather, as we are crowned through the virtues of repentance, the Savior receives a crown in each one, according to what Paul also says: For the rest, there is laid up for me a crown of justice, which the Lord, the just judge, will render to me; and not to me only, but also to all who love the coming of our Lord Savior Jesus Christ. Whence the father of the Lord Savior also took his name from the crown of justice. For Jehozadak is interpreted Lord of justice, because he renders to each according to his works. Of this crown James too speaks in his epistle: Blessed is the man who endures temptation, for when he has been proved he will receive the crown of life, which God has promised to those who love him.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"TIT.1.1":["Although those who have made void their first faith are not worthy of faith (I speak of Marcion and Basilides, and of all the heretics who tear apart the Old Testament), yet we would bear with them in some measure if they at least kept their hands off the New, and did not dare to violate either the evangelists or the apostles of Christ, the Son (as they themselves boast) of the good God. But now, since they have both scattered his Gospels and made the epistles of the apostles to be not the apostles' of Christ but their own, I wonder how they dare to claim for themselves the name of Christians. For, to say nothing of the other Epistles, from which they erased whatever they had seen to be contrary to their doctrine, some they believed should be rejected entire: namely both the Epistles to Timothy, the one to the Hebrews, and the one to Titus, which we are now attempting to expound. And if indeed they gave reasons why they did not think them the Apostle's, we would try to make some reply, and perhaps to satisfy the reader. But now, since they pronounce with heretical authority and say, \"That epistle is Paul's, this one is not,\" let them understand that they are refuted, on behalf of the truth, by the same authority by which they themselves do not blush to feign falsehoods. But Tatian, patriarch of the Encratites, who himself also rejected some epistles of Paul, believed that this one above all, that is, the one to Titus, must be pronounced the Apostle's, setting little store by the assertion of Marcion and of the others who agree with him on this point. The Apostle, then, O Paula and Eustochium, writes from Nicopolis, which, situated on the shore of Actium, is now by far the greatest part of your property; and he writes to Titus, his disciple and his son in Christ, whom he had left in Crete to instruct the churches; and he commanded him that, when one of the two, Artemas or Tychicus, had landed in Crete, he himself should come to Nicopolis. For it was just that he who had said, \"my care for all the churches\", and who, setting out from Jerusalem, had founded the Gospel of Christ as far as Illyricum, should not allow the Cretans, from whom the seeds of idolatry first sprouted, to be left deserted by his own absence and that of Titus, but should send them, in place of himself and Titus, Artemas or Tychicus, by whose teaching and consolation they might be cherished.\n— Commentary on Titus","Paul, a servant of God, but an apostle of Jesus Christ. In the Epistle to the Romans he began thus: \"Paul, a servant of Jesus Christ, called an apostle\". In this one, however, he calls himself a servant of God, but an apostle of Jesus Christ. For if the Father and the Son are one, and whoever has believed in the Son believes also in the Father, then the servitude of the apostle Paul too is to be referred without distinction either to the Father or to the Son. But this servitude is not the one of which the Apostle himself says: \"For you did not receive the spirit of servitude again in fear, but you received the spirit of adoption, in which we cry, Abba, Father\" (Rom. 8:15); rather, it is a noble servitude, of which David also speaks to God: \"I am your servant, the son of your handmaid\" (Ps. 115:16). And blessed Mary to the angel: \"Behold the handmaid of the Lord; be it done to me according to your word\" (Luke 1:38). Moses too had this servitude, of whom the Lord says to Joshua the son of Nun: \"Moses,\" he says, \"my servant is dead\" (Josh. 1:2). And in another place: \"Moses the servant of the Lord died in the land of Moab by the word of the Lord\" (Deut. 34:5). God forbid, indeed, that we should believe that Moses and Mary had the spirit of servitude in fear, and not in the love of God. Nor is it any wonder that men, holy though they are, are nobly called servants of God, since through the prophet Isaiah the Father speaks to the Son: \"It is a great thing for you to be called my child (puer)\" (Isa. 49:6), which in Greek is said: μέγα σοί ἐστι τοῦ κληθῆναί σε παῖδά μου. Now because \"child\" (puer), that is, παῖς, can according to the Greeks signify both servant (famulus) and son, we inquired in the Hebrew, and found written not \"my son\" but \"my servant\", that is, ABDI (עבדי). Hence also the prophet Obadiah (Abdias), whose name means \"servant of the Lord\", received his name from his service to God. If it disturbs anyone that the Lord and Savior, who is the founder of the universe, is called a servant of God, he will not be disturbed if he hears him speaking to the apostles: \"Whoever wishes to be greater among you, let him be the servant of all\"; and: \"The Son of man came, not to be served, but to serve\" (Matt. 20:27, 28); and, lest he should seem to teach this by words alone, he showed it by example. For taking a towel, he girded himself, and filling a basin with water, he washed the feet of the disciples (John 13). It is therefore not impious to believe that he who had taken the form of a servant did those things which belong to a servant, so that he may be said to have served his Father's will, since he himself served his own servants. Now this servitude belongs to love, through which we are commanded to serve one another. And the Apostle himself, though he was free from all, made himself the servant of all (1 Cor. 9). And in another place: \"Your servant,\" he says, \"for Christ's sake\". He is a servant of God who is not a servant of sin. \"For everyone who commits sin is a servant of sin\" (John 8:34). The Apostle, therefore, who was not a servant of sin, is rightly called a servant of God the Father and of Christ. Further, his saying but an apostle of Jesus Christ seems to me to be just as if he had said: prefect of the praetorium of Augustus Caesar, master of the army of the emperor Tiberius. For just as the judges of this age, in order to appear more noble, take their titles from the kings whom they serve and from the dignity with which they swell: so too the Apostle, claiming for himself a great dignity among Christians, inscribed himself beforehand with the title of an apostle of Christ, so as to awe his future readers by the very authority of the name, indicating that all who believed in Christ ought to be subject to him. Moreover, what we set down a little before as written to the Romans, \"A servant of Jesus Christ\", does not differ from his having said: a servant of wisdom, a servant of righteousness, a servant of sanctification, a servant of redemption: \"For Christ was made for us by God the Father wisdom, righteousness, sanctification and redemption\" (1 Cor. 1:30).\n— Commentary on Titus","According to the faith of the elect of God, and the knowledge of the truth which is according to piety, in the hope of eternal life, which God, who does not lie, promised before eternal ages; but he has manifested in his own times his word in the preaching which has been entrusted to me according to the command of our Savior God. To Titus, dearest son, according to the common faith: Grace and peace from God the Father, and Jesus Christ our Savior. He who had said, not out of humility, as most suppose, but truly: \"Even if I am unskilled in speech, yet not in knowledge\", a Hebrew of the Hebrews, as to the Law a Pharisee, does not unfold his profound meanings in the Greek tongue, and scarcely brings out in words what he thinks. Let us therefore discuss each point as it is written, following the order of the meanings and the sequence of the matters rather than of the words. According to the faith, he says, of the elect of God: refer what he has brought in to what precedes: Paul, a servant of God, but an apostle of Jesus Christ according to the faith of the elect of God, that is, of those who are not only called but also elect. Among the elect themselves, too, there is great diversity according to the variety of their works, minds and words. Nor does whoever is elect of God at once possess faith according to his election, or have knowledge of the truth according to his faith. Hence the Savior also spoke to the Jews who had believed in him: \"If you remain in my word, you will know the truth, and the truth will set you free\" (John 8:31, 32). And the evangelist testifies that he said these things to those who believed indeed, but did not know the truth, which they could attain if they remained in his word, and, being made free, would cease to be servants. The question is asked why, to his saying according to the faith of the elect of God and the knowledge of the truth, he joined which is according to piety: whether there is some truth that is not set in piety, and the knowledge of the truth which is according to piety is now brought in to distinguish it from that other. There plainly is a truth that does not have piety: if someone knows the art of grammar, or dialectic, so as to possess the method of speaking correctly and to judge between false and true. Geometry also and arithmetic and music have truth in their knowledge; but that knowledge is not of piety. The knowledge of piety is to know the Law, to understand the prophets, to believe the Gospel, not to be ignorant of the apostles. And on the contrary there are many who have a true knowledge of piety, but not at once the truth of the other arts as well, and of those which we mentioned above.\n— Commentary on Titus"],"TIT.1.2-3":["Attend carefully to the sequence and order of the reading: for the eternal life which God, who does not lie, promised before eternal ages, is nothing other than the Word of God. For he has manifested, he says, in his own times his word: therefore the eternal life which he had promised is itself his Word, which in the beginning was with the Father: and the Word was God, and the Word was made flesh, and dwelt in us (John 1). And that the Word of God, that is, Christ himself, is life, he testifies in another place, saying: \"I am the life\" (John 14:6). A life, moreover, not brief, not bounded by any times, but perpetual, but eternal: which has been manifested in the last ages, in the preaching which was entrusted to Paul, the teacher and master of the Gentiles, that it might be announced in the world and become known to men, according to the command of God the Savior, who willed us to be saved, by fulfilling what he had promised.\n— Commentary on Titus"],"TIT.1.2":["This truth, then, the knowledge of which is according to piety, is set in the hope of eternal life, because it at once bestows the reward of immortality on him who has come to know it. Without piety, however, the knowledge of truth delights for the present, but does not have the eternity of rewards, which God, who does not lie, promised before eternal ages, and manifested in his own times in Christ Jesus. But to whom did he promise beforehand, and afterwards make it plain, if not to his own Wisdom, which was always with the Father, when it rejoiced at the completed world and was glad over the sons of men; and did he not promise that whoever would believe in it would have eternal life? Before he laid the foundations of the world, before he poured out the seas, set up the mountains, hung up the sky, and made the earth firm with its mass cast down, God, in whom there is no lie, promised these things. Not because he could lie and is unwilling to break out into words of falsehood, but because he who is the father of truth has no lie in himself, according to that saying: \"But let God be true, and every man a liar\" (Rom. 3:4). Now God is called \"who does not lie\" for this reason: for he promises certain things with an oath in the prophets, so that we, made more secure, may hope the more that the things foretold will come to be, and, believing with our whole mind, may be prepared to attain the things that are to come.\n\nIt seems not beside the point to touch briefly on why God alone is called true, and every man a liar, by the voice of the Apostle. And unless I am mistaken, just as he alone is said to have immortality, although he has made both angels and many rational creatures to whom he has given immortality, so too he alone is said to be true: not that the others are not also immortal and lovers of truth, but that he alone is, by nature, both immortal and true. The others, however, obtain immortality and truth from his bounty, and it is one thing to be true, to have something through oneself; another to have what you have in the power of the giver. But neither do I think this should be passed over in silence: how God, who does not lie, pledged eternal life before eternal ages, from the time the world was made, according to the history of Genesis, and the times were established through the succession of nights and days, of months and years alike. In this course and wheel of the world, times glide by and come, and either are to be or have been. Hence certain of the philosophers think that there is no present time, but only past or future, because everything we say, do or think either passes while it is being done, or, if it has not yet been done, we await it. Before these times of the world, then, we must believe there was a certain eternity of ages, in which the Father was always with the Son and the Holy Spirit; and, so to speak, the one time of God is all eternity; nay rather, the times are innumerable, since he is himself infinite who, before times, exceeds all time. But not yet a thousand years of our world are fulfilled: and how many eternities before, how many times, how many origins of ages must we think there were, in which angels, thrones, dominions and the other powers served God, and, without the successions and measures of times, subsisted at God's command! Before all these times, then, which speech cannot utter, nor mind comprehend, nor silent thought dare to touch, God the Father promised to his Wisdom his Word, and that his very Wisdom and the life of those who were going to believe would come into the world.\n— Commentary on Titus"],"TIT.1.4":["Now the Apostle writes to Titus, dearest son, which in Greek is said γνησίῳ τέκνῳ (true-born son), and cannot be expressed in the Latin tongue: for γνήσιος rather conveys this, when someone is called faithful and one's own, and (so to speak) legitimate or genuine, beyond comparison with another. From this we understand that there was also a very great difference among Paul's sons: that some he had as γνησίους, that is, most genuine, and joined to himself, and begotten of a true marriage and of a free woman; but others as if of a handmaid and of Hagar, who cannot receive the inheritance with Isaac, the son of the free woman. For the speech and wisdom and doctrine with which Titus instructed the churches of Christ made him the Apostle's own son, and set him apart from all fellowship with others. Let us see after this what follows: According to the common faith: whether he called the faith common to all who believed in Christ, or common only to himself and Titus? It seems better to me that the faith was common to the apostle Paul and Titus than to all believers, in whom, according to the variety of their minds, the faith also could not be common, but diverse. Finally, the preface of the Epistle and the greeting of the Apostle's preface to Titus are completed with this ending: Grace and peace from God the Father and Christ Jesus our Savior: whether because both grace and peace come as much from God the Father as from Christ Jesus, and both may be understood as given by each; or because grace is referred to the Father, and peace to the Son. We must not pass over without scruple that the Apostle prayed for some that grace and peace might be multiplied to them, whereas now, to Titus, peace and grace are set down without multiplication. Noah, a just man, alone preserved when the world was shipwrecked, is said to have found not many graces but one grace in the sight of God. And Moses to the Lord: \"If I have found,\" he says, \"grace with you\" (Exod. 33:13). And wherever else grace is set down in the person of the saints, seek and you will find that they found not graces, but grace. That merchant in the Gospel who had many pearls at last found one precious one, which alone he bought with the many pearls (Matt. 13). For it belongs to the perfect to buy one pearl and one treasure in exchange for all their pearls and their whole substance; but to beginners, and those still set upon the road, to have not just one and only one, but many pearls.\n— Commentary on Titus"],"TIT.1.5-7":["Let us attend carefully to the words of the Apostle, who says: That you might appoint presbyters throughout the cities, as I arranged for you. Discussing in what follows what kind of presbyter ought to be ordained, he says this: \"If anyone is without crime, the husband of one wife\", and the rest; and afterwards he added: \"For a bishop must be without crime, as the steward of God\". A presbyter, therefore, is the same as a bishop; and before, at the devil's instigation, factions arose in religion, and it was said among the peoples, \"I am of Paul, I of Apollos, but I of Cephas\" (1 Cor. 1:12), the churches were governed by the common council of the presbyters. But after each one thought that those whom he had baptized were his own and not Christ's, it was decreed throughout the whole world that one chosen from among the presbyters should be set over the rest, to whom the whole care of the church should belong, and the seeds of schisms be removed. Should anyone think that the view that bishop and presbyter are one, and that the one (presbyter, that is, \"elder\") is a name of age, the other a name of office, is not that of the Scriptures but our own, let him read again the words of the Apostle to the Philippians, saying: \"Paul and Timothy, servants of Jesus Christ, to all the saints in Christ Jesus who are at Philippi, with the bishops and deacons: grace to you and peace\" (Phil. 1:1, 2), and the rest. Philippi is a single city of Macedonia, and certainly in one city there could not have been several bishops, as they are called. But because at that time they called the same men bishops whom they also called presbyters, for that reason he spoke of bishops indifferently as of presbyters. This may still seem doubtful to someone, unless it is proved by a second testimony. In the Acts of the Apostles it is written that when the Apostle had come to Miletus, he sent to Ephesus and called the presbyters of that church, to whom he afterwards said, among other things: \"Take heed to yourselves and to all the flock, in which the Holy Spirit has placed you as bishops, to feed the Church of the Lord, which he acquired by his own blood\" (Acts 20:28). And here observe more carefully how, calling the presbyters of the single city of Ephesus, he afterwards called the same men bishops. If anyone is willing to receive the Epistle written under the name of Paul to the Hebrews, there too the care of the church is divided equally among several. For he writes to the people: \"Obey your rulers and be subject to them: for they are the ones who keep watch over your souls, as those who will give an account, so that they may not do this with sighing; for this is useful for you\" (Heb. 13:17). And Peter, who received his name from the firmness of faith, speaks in his Epistle, saying: \"The presbyters among you, therefore, I beseech, as a fellow presbyter and a witness of the sufferings of Christ, who am also a partaker of his glory which is to be revealed in the future: feed the flock of the Lord which is among you, not as under necessity, but willingly\" (1 Pet. 5:1, 2). This we have said in order to show that among the ancients presbyters were the same as bishops, but that little by little, so that the seedlings of dissensions might be rooted out, the whole responsibility was conferred upon one. Just as the presbyters, then, know that by the custom of the Church they are subject to him who has been set over them, so let the bishops know that they are greater than the presbyters more by custom than by the truth of the Lord's arrangement, and that they ought to rule the church in common, imitating Moses, who, though he had it in his power to preside alone over the people of Israel, chose seventy with whom to judge the people (Num. 11). Let us see, therefore, what kind of presbyter, or bishop, is to be ordained.\n— Commentary on Titus"],"TIT.1.5":["For this reason I left you in Crete, that you might correct the things that were lacking. It belongs to the apostolic dignity to lay the foundation of the Church, which no one can lay except the master builder. \"But there is no other foundation than Christ Jesus\" (1 Cor. 3:11). Those who are lesser craftsmen can build houses upon the foundations. Paul, therefore, as a wise master builder, striving with all labor not to boast in things already prepared, but to preach where Christ had not yet been announced, after he had softened the hard hearts of the Cretans to the faith of Christ, and had tamed them as much by word as by signs, and had taught them to believe not in their native Jupiter but in God the Father and in Christ, left his disciple Titus in Crete to strengthen the rudiments of the newborn Church, and to correct anything that seemed lacking, while he himself went on to other nations, to lay the foundation of Christ among them in turn. Now his saying that you might correct the things that were lacking shows that they had not yet come to the full knowledge of the truth, and that, although they had been corrected by the Apostle, they still needed correction. For everything that is corrected is imperfect. For in the Greek too the addition of the preposition, by which ἐπιδιορθώσῃ is written, does not convey the same as διορθώσῃ, that is, you might correct (corrigeres), but, so to speak, you might correct further (supercorrigeres): namely, that the things which have been corrected by me, and have not yet been drawn back to the full line of truth, may be corrected by you and receive the rule of evenness.\n\nAnd you might appoint presbyters throughout the cities, as I arranged for you. Let the bishops hear, who have the power of appointing presbyters in each city, under what law the order of the Church's constitution is held; and let them not think these to be the words of the Apostle, but of Christ, who says to the disciples: \"He who rejects you rejects me; and he who rejects me rejects him who sent me\" (Luke 10:16). So also he who hears you hears me; and he who hears me hears him who sent me. From this it is plain that those who, scorning the Apostle's law, have chosen to confer an ecclesiastical rank on someone not on merit but by favor act against Christ, who through his Apostle has set out in what follows what kind of presbyter is to be appointed in the Church. Moses, the friend of God, to whom God spoke face to face (Deut. 5 and 31), could certainly have made his own sons the successors of his rule and left his own dignity to his descendants; but Joshua, an outsider from another tribe, is chosen, so that we might know that rule over the peoples is to be conferred not on blood but on life. But now we see very many making this matter a favor, so that they do not seek to raise up as pillars of the Church those who can be of more benefit to the Church, but those whom they themselves love, or by whose services they have been soothed, or for whom one of the great has asked, and (to say nothing of worse things) those who have obtained by gifts that they should become clerics.\n— Commentary on Titus"],"TIT.1.6":["If anyone is without crime, the husband of one wife, having faithful children, not under accusation of licentiousness, or not submissive. First, then, let him be without crime, which I think is named by another word to Timothy, \"irreproachable\" (1 Tim. 3): not that he be without any crime only at the time when he is to be ordained, having washed away past stains by a new manner of life, but that from the time he was reborn in Christ he be gnawed by no consciousness of sin. For how can the head of the Church remove evil from its midst, if he has fallen into a like offense? Or with what freedom can he reprove a sinner, when he silently answers himself that he has committed the same things that he reproves? Thus whoever desires the episcopate desires a good work. \"A work\", he says, not an honor, not glory. \"But he must also have a good testimony from those who are outside, so that he may not fall into reproach and into the snare of the devil\". Now what he says, the husband of one wife, we ought to understand thus: not that we should think every once-married man better than a twice-married man, but that he can exhort to monogamy and continence who holds out his own example in teaching. For suppose that someone in early youth has lost his wife and, overcome by the necessity of the flesh, has taken a second wife, whom he too has at once lost, and from then on has lived continently; and that another has stayed married into old age, and, enjoying the use of a wife, which most people consider happiness, has never ceased from the work of the flesh: which of the two seems to you to be better, more chaste, more continent? Surely the one who was unfortunate even in his second marriage, and afterwards lived chastely and holily, and not the one who was not separated from his wife's embrace even in old age. Let not anyone, then, who is chosen as a once-married man applaud himself as being better than every twice-married man, since in him it is rather good fortune than will that has been chosen. Some take this passage thus: It was the custom of the Jews, they say, to have two wives or more, which we also read of Abraham and Jacob in the old Law; and they will have this now to be the precept, that he who is to be chosen bishop should not have two wives together at one time. Many, more superstitiously than truly, think that even those who, when they were Gentiles, had one wife, and when she was lost married another after the baptism of Christ, are not to be chosen for the priesthood: whereas, surely, if this is to be observed, those ought rather to be kept from the episcopate who, having before indulged a roving lust among harlots, took one wife after being regenerated; and it is much more detestable to have fornicated with many than to be found a twice-married man, since in the one there is the misfortune of marriage, in the other a wantonness prone to sin. Montanus, and those who follow the schism of Novatus, have presumed to claim for themselves the name of purity, and think that second marriages should be barred from the communion of the Church: whereas the Apostle, in commanding this of bishops and presbyters, surely relaxed it in the case of the rest: not that he exhorts to second marriages, but that he makes allowance for the necessity of the flesh. Tertullian too wrote a heretical book On Monogamy, which no one who has read the Apostle will fail to recognize as contrary to the Apostle. And indeed, for a bishop, or presbyter, to be without crime and to have one wife lies within our power. But what follows, To have faithful children, not under accusation of licentiousness, and not submissive, is outside our will. For suppose that parents have brought up their children well and from an early age have always instructed them in the Lord's precepts: if these afterwards give themselves to licentiousness and, overcome by vices, give the reins to lust, will the blame flow back upon the parents, and will the sins of the sons stain the holiness of the father? If anyone instructed his sons well, among these I think was Isaac too, who surely must be believed to have brought up his son Esau well. Yet Esau, a fornicator and profane, sold his birthright for one meal (Gen. 25). Samuel also, who was such that he called upon the Lord and the Lord heard him, and obtained winter rain at the time of harvest, had sons who turned aside after avarice and took gifts, and proved such unjust judges that the people, unable to bear it, demanded a king for themselves in the likeness of the other nations (1 Sam. 12). If, therefore, there were an election of priests, both Isaac because of Esau and Samuel because of his sons would be thought unworthy of the priesthood. And since the sins of the parents are not imputed to the sons, now that the saying, \"The parents ate the sour grape, and the teeth of the sons were set on edge\" (Ezek. 18:2), is no longer in force, shall the vices of the sons tell against the parents? First, then, it must be said that the name of the priesthood is so holy that even things set outside us are reckoned to us: not that we are not to be made bishops because of our own vices, but that we are to be kept from this rank because of the incontinence of our sons. For with what freedom can we reprove other people's sons and teach what is right, when the one who has been reproved can at once throw at us: First teach your own sons? Or with what face do I reprove a stranger who fornicates, when my own conscience answers me: Then disinherit your fornicating son; cast off your sons who are slaves to vices? But when a worthless son lives with you in one house, do you dare to take the mote from another's eye, not seeing the beam in your own (Matt. 7 and Luke 6)? So the just man is not polluted by the vices of his sons; but freedom is reserved by the Apostle for the leader of the Church, in that he should be such a one as does not fear to rebuke strangers because of the vices of his children. Next, this too must be brought against those who swell with pride over the episcopate and think they have obtained not a stewardship of Christ but a command: that those who have not been ordained bishops are not at once worse than all these, nor should they think themselves the more approved from the fact that they were chosen; but let them understand that certain men have been removed from the priesthood for this reason, that the vices of their children hindered them. But if the sins of the sons debar a just man from the episcopate, how much more will each one, considering himself and knowing that the mighty shall suffer torments mightily (Wisd. 6), draw back from this, which is not so much an honor as a burden, and not seek to occupy the place of others who are more worthy! Lastly this must be said: that in the Scriptures by sons are understood λογισμοὺς, that is, thoughts, and by daughters πράξεις, that is, works; and that he is now commanded to be made bishop who has both his thoughts and his works in his power, and believes truly in Christ, and is stained by no blot of creeping vices.\n— Commentary on Titus"],"TIT.1.7":["For a bishop must be without crime, as the steward of God: not insolent, not irascible, not given to wine, not a striker, not a seeker of shameful gain. It is required, then, among stewards that one be found faithful, and not, eating and drinking with drunkards, strike the menservants and maidservants; but let him await the uncertain coming of God, and give his fellow servants their food in due season. Now between the steward (of the estate) and the household there is this sole difference: that a fellow servant has been set over his fellow servants. Let the bishop and the presbyter know, then, that the people are his fellow servants, not his servants. The other things that follow are set within our power: Not insolent, that is, not swelling and pleased with himself because he is a bishop, but, like a good steward, seeking what benefits the greater number. Not irascible. He is irascible who is always angry, and is stirred by the slight breeze of a reply or of an offense, like a leaf by the wind. And truly nothing is more foul than a raging teacher, who, though he ought to be gentle (and according to what is written (2 Tim. 2): \"But the servant of the Lord must not quarrel, but be humble toward all, a teacher, patient, instructing in gentleness those who oppose\"), on the contrary, with grim look, trembling lips, wrinkled brow, unbridled abuse, a face changing between pallor and redness, roars with shouting, and does not so much draw back the erring to the good as hurl them headlong into evil by his savagery. Hence it is that Solomon also says: \"Anger destroys even the wise\" (Prov. 15); and: \"The anger of man does not work the righteousness of God\" (Jas. 1:20). Nor yet is he who is sometimes angry irascible; but he is called irascible who is frequently overcome by this passion. He also forbids the bishop to be given to wine, as is written to Timothy: \"Not given to much wine\" (1 Tim. 3:8). But what a thing it is to see a bishop given to wine, so that, his senses seized, he either raises a laugh against the seemliness of gravity and guffaws with slack lips, or, if he has a little sadly remembered something, bursts out among the cups into sobs and tears. It would be long to go through each point and unfold the madnesses that drunkenness prompts. You may see some turning their cups into weapons, throwing a goblet in the face of a fellow guest; others, with torn clothes, rushing to wound others; some shouting; some dozing; he who has drunk more is counted the braver; it is a ground for accusation, when adjured by the king's name, not to have drunk the more often. They vomit in order to drink, they drink in order to vomit. The digestion of the belly and the throat are taken up with one office. Let it suffice to have said this now: that according to the Apostle, in wine there is licentiousness. And wherever fullness and drunkenness have been, there lust holds sway. Look at the belly and the genitals: the order of the members follows the character of the vices. I shall never think a drunk man pure; for even if, put to sleep by wine, he has slept, he could still have sinned through wine. Yet we marvel that the Apostle condemned fondness for wine in bishops or presbyters, when in the old Law too it was commanded that the priests, when they enter the temple to minister to God, should drink no wine at all (Lev. 10); and that the Nazirite, as long as he grows his holy hair and looks on nothing defiled, nothing dead, should abstain both from wine and from raisins and from the weaker drink that is usually made from grape skins, and from all strong drink, which turns the mind away from its whole soundness (Num. 6). Let each say what he will: I speak my own conscience: I know that abstinence, when interrupted, has harmed me, and when resumed has helped me. After fondness for wine he commands this, \"that he be not a striker\": which, even understood simply, edifies the hearer, that he should not readily stretch out his hand to strike, nor burst out like a madman to beat another's face. But it is better that we call him not a striker who, gentle and patient, knows in due time what is to be said or what kept silent, and does not strike the conscience of the weak with useless speech. For the Apostle, in forming the leader of the church, does not forbid him to be a boxer and a pancratiast [παγκρατιαστὴν, that is, an athlete] (which is censured even in a common man and in any Gentile whatever, if it is found in him), but this, as I said: that he should not, being insulting and garrulous, destroy one whom he could have corrected by modesty and mildness. A craving for shameful gain also ought to be foreign to one who is to be a bishop. For there are many teaching what they ought not, for the sake of shameful gain, who overturn whole houses and think piety to be a source of profit. \"But better is\", according to Solomon, \"a modest receiving with righteousness than many fruits with iniquity\" (Prov. 16:8); and a good name in poverty is rather to be chosen than a very bad name in riches. A bishop who desires to be an imitator of the Apostle, having food and clothing, ought to be content with these alone (1 Tim. 6). Those who serve the altar should live from the altar (1 Cor. 9). Let them live, he says, not grow rich. Hence also the copper is shaken out of our belt, and we are clothed in a single tunic only (Matt. 10 and Mark 6), and we take no thought for the morrow. It is a craving for shameful gain to take thought for more than the present. Up to this point it has been commanded by the Apostle's word what a bishop or presbyter ought not to have; now, on the contrary, what he ought to have is set out.\n— Commentary on Titus"],"TIT.1.8":["But hospitable, a lover of good things, pure, just, holy, self-controlled, or abstinent, holding fast the faithful word which is according to doctrine, that he may be able to comfort in sound doctrine, and to refute those who contradict. Before all things hospitality is enjoined on the future bishop. For if all desire to hear that word of the Gospel, \"I was a stranger, and you took me in\" (Matt. 25:35), how much more the bishop, whose house ought to be the common lodging of all! For a layman, receiving one or two or a few, will fulfill the duty of hospitality; a bishop, unless he receives all, is inhuman. But I fear that, just as the queen of the South, who came from the ends of the earth to hear the wisdom of Solomon (Matt. 12), will judge the men of her time, and the men of Nineveh, who did penance at the preaching of Jonah, will condemn those who scorned to hear the Savior, greater than Jonah, so very many among the peoples may judge the bishops, withdrawing themselves from the ecclesiastical rank and practicing what does not befit a bishop. Of these I think John too writes to Gaius: \"Dearest, you act faithfully in whatever you do for the brethren, and that for strangers, who have borne witness to your love before the Church: you will do very well if you send them on their way worthily of God: for they went out for the name of the Lord, accepting nothing from the Gentiles\" (3 John 5 ff.). And truly, the Holy Spirit speaking in him, he even then rebuked what was to come in the churches, saying: \"I wrote also to the Church: but Diotrephes, who loves to hold the first place among them, does not receive us. Therefore, when I come, I will call to mind his works which he does, slandering us with evil words; and it is not enough for him that he does not himself receive the brethren, but he also forbids those who wish to, and casts them out of the Church.\" Truly one can now see what was foretold: in very many cities, bishops, or presbyters, if they see laymen hospitable, lovers of good things, envy them, rage, excommunicate them, expel them from the Church, as though it were not lawful to do what the bishop does not do, and as though for laymen to be such were a condemnation of the priests. So they find them burdensome, and as though set upon their own necks, and harass them with various persecutions in order to draw them away from good work. Let the bishop also be chaste, whom the Greeks call σώφρονα; and the Latin translator, deceived by the ambiguity of the word, rendered \"prudent\" (prudentem) instead of \"chaste\" (pudicum). Now if laymen are commanded to abstain from intercourse with their wives for the sake of prayer, what are we to think of a bishop, who is every day to offer to God unblemished victims for his own sins and those of the people? Let us read again the books of Kings, and we shall find that the priest Abimelech was unwilling to give of the loaves of the presentation to David and his young men before he asked whether the young men were clean from a woman: not, of course, another man's, but a wife (1 Sam. 21). And had he not heard that they had kept away from the marital act yesterday and the day before, he would by no means have granted the loaves which he had at first refused. There is as great a difference between the loaves of the presentation and the body of Christ as between the shadow and the bodies, between the image and the truth, between the patterns of things to come and those very things which were prefigured through the patterns. Just as, therefore, gentleness, patience, sobriety, moderation, abstinence from gain, and also hospitality and kindness ought to be found especially in the bishop, and to stand out among all the laymen, so too a purity of his own and (so to speak) a priestly chastity, so that he abstain not only from the unclean act, but also from the glance of the eye and the straying of thought, so that the mind that is to consecrate the body of Christ may be free. The bishop ought also to be just and holy, so as to exercise justice among the peoples over whom he presides, rendering to each what he deserves, and not to respect persons in judgment. Now between the justice of a layman and of a bishop there is this difference: that a layman can appear just in a few things, but a bishop can exercise justice in as many as he has subjects. Now holy, which in Greek is said ὅσιος, signifies rather this: when holiness itself is mixed with piety and is referred to God. For whom we call holy (sanctus), the Greeks call ἅγιον; but whom they call ὅσιον, we can call devout toward God (pium in Deum). Let the bishop also be abstinent: not only (as some think) from lust and from his wife's embrace, but from all the disturbances of the soul: that he not be roused to irascibility, that sadness not cast him down, that fear not agitate him, that immoderate joy not lift him up. Now abstinence is numbered by the Apostle among the fruits of the spirit. And if it is required of all, how much more of a bishop, who, patient and gentle, ought to bear the vices of those who sin, comfort the fainthearted, support the weak, render to no one evil for evil, but overcome evil with good.\n— Commentary on Titus"],"TIT.1.9":["Finally, let him hold fast the faithful word which is according to doctrine, so that, just as the word of God is faithful and worthy of all acceptance, so he too may show himself such that everything he says is deemed worthy of faith, and his words are the rule of truth. Let him be able also to comfort those who are tossed by the whirlwinds of this age, and through sound doctrine to destroy weak precepts. Now doctrine is called sound to distinguish it from languid and weak doctrine. Let him also be such as to refute those who contradict: the heretics or the Jews, and the wise men of this age. And indeed the earlier qualities which he set down among the virtues of a bishop pertain to life. But this which he says, That he may be able to comfort in sound doctrine, and to refute those who contradict, is to be referred to knowledge. For if a bishop has only a holy life, by living so he can benefit himself. But if he is learned in doctrine and speech, he can instruct himself and the others; and not only instruct and teach his own, but also beat back the adversaries, who, unless they are refuted and convicted, can easily pervert the hearts of the simple. This passage tells against those who, giving themselves to sloth and leisure and sleep, think it a sin to read the Scriptures, and despise as garrulous and useless those who meditate on the Law of the Lord day and night, not noticing that the Apostle, after the catalogue of the bishop's manner of life, likewise commanded doctrine too.\n— Commentary on Titus"],"TIT.1.10":["For there are many who are also not submissive, empty talkers and deceivers of minds, especially those who are of the circumcision, on whom silence must be imposed; who overturn whole houses, teaching what they ought not, for the sake of shameful gain. Let him who is to be the leader of the Church have eloquence joined to integrity of life, so that his works may not be silent without speech, nor his words blush for lack of deeds; especially since there are not few but many, and not submissive but insolent, who do not care to say that word of the Psalmist, \"Is not my soul subject to God?\" (Ps. 61:1), but by empty persuasion pervert the good seed of minds, which by nature has knowledge of God. For this is what Paul seems to me to have meant when he says φρεναπάται: not, as the Latin translator simply rendered it, deceivers (deceptores), but deceivers of minds (mentium deceptores). And indeed, without the authority of the Scriptures their garrulity would win no belief, unless they seemed to strengthen their perverse doctrine with divine testimonies as well. These are the Jews of the circumcision, who at that time were striving to overturn the newborn Church of Christ and to bring in the precepts of the Law; of whom Paul treats more fully both to the Romans and to the Galatians. And we, a few months ago, dictated three volumes in the explanation of the Epistle to the Galatians.\n— Commentary on Titus"],"TIT.1.11":["Such men the teacher of the Church, to whom the souls of the peoples have been entrusted, ought to overcome by the reasoning of the Scriptures, and impose silence on them by the weight of testimony: men who overturn not one or a few houses, but whole houses, with their masters and households, teaching about the distinctions of foods, about the long since abolished sabbath, about the injury of circumcision. And would that they did this very thing out of zeal for the faith! It could be pardoned in some measure, since the Apostle says: \"I confess, they have a zeal for God, but not according to knowledge\" (Rom. 10:2). But because their belly is their God, they wish for the sake of shameful gain to make disciples their own, so that, as masters, they may be fed by their followers. We can, however, also interpret otherwise what was said, for the sake of shameful gain: so as to think that the Apostle used a common word, by which all heretics, when they teach perverse things, are wont to declare themselves gainers of men, whereas it is not gain but perdition to kill the souls of those they deceive. On the contrary, whoever has reproved his erring brother according to the Gospel, if he has been converted, has gained him. For what greater gain can there be, or what more precious, than if one gains a human soul? Every teacher of the Church, therefore, who persuades to the faith of Christ by right reasoning is an honorable gainer. And every heretic, who deceives men by certain tricks and is deceived, speaks what he ought not, for the sake of shameful gain.\n— Commentary on Titus"],"TIT.1.12-13":["Some think the Apostle should be censured for having slipped imprudently, and that, while refuting false teachers, he approved that little verse which says that the Cretans are called deceitful for this reason, that they built an empty tomb of Jupiter. For if, they say, Epimenides or Callimachus charge the Cretans with being deceitful, evil beasts and idle bellies for this reason, that they do not perceive divine things and imagine that Jupiter, who reigns in heaven, is buried in their island; and if what they said is proved true by the Apostle's judgment, it follows that Jupiter is not dead but alive. Ignorantly, then, did Paul, the destroyer of idolatry, while dealing with perverse teachers, assert the gods whom he was attacking. To these we must reply briefly: just as in his saying, Depraved conversations corrupt good morals, and in that other, For we are also his offspring, he did not at once approve the whole comedy of Menander and the book of Aratus, but made use of the fitness of the little verse; so too in the present passage he did not, through one little verse, confirm the whole work of Callimachus or Epimenides, of whom the one sings the praises of Jupiter, the other writes about Oracles; but he only rebuked the Cretans as liars for the vice of their race: not because of that opinion for which they were charged by the poets, but because of their inborn readiness to lie, confuting them by the race's own author. But those who think that one who has used a part of a book must follow the whole book seem to me also to receive among the Scriptures of the Church the apocryphon of Enoch, from which the apostle Jude set down a testimony in his Epistle, and many other things which the apostle Paul spoke from hidden writings. For by this argument we could say that, because he said that among the Athenians he worshipped the unknown God whom they had noted on the altar, Paul ought also to follow the other things which had been written on the altar, and to do the things the Athenians did, since he had agreed in part with the Athenians in the worship of the unknown God. Far be it from me to drag an argument and scholastic elegance into calumny. No one is such an assassin, such a parricide, such a poisoner, as not to have done something good at some time. If, then, seeing one good deed of his, I approve it, will the necessity also lie on me of approving his other deeds, which were evil? If an enemy wrangles against us and shouts, will he not speak something of truth amid the words of enmity and quarrel? which is not altogether censured even by us, against whom he speaks. So too Callimachus and Epimenides did not speak truly that Jupiter is a god, and the rest that is contained in their poems, because the Cretans are deceitful; but they spoke truly only in this, that they expressed the inborn vice of lying of the Cretans, who, because they are deceitful, have not therefore never spoken the truth at some time. For Jupiter would not on that account not be a god, if the Cretans spoke truly; but yet, even if they were silent, one who was dead would not have the title of god. Finally, so that we may know that the Apostle spoke against the Cretans not by chance and casually, as one pleases (as those men think), but with consideration and circumspection, and guarding himself on every side: This testimony, he says, is true: not the whole poem from which the testimony was taken, not the entire work, but only this testimony, this little verse in which they are called liars. And surely he who agreed with only one part of a poem must be believed to have refuted the rest. But how the Cretans are marked as liars, and the Galatians as foolish, and Israel as stiff-necked, and each province by its own vice, we have discussed in the Epistle of Paul to the Galatians; and because there is nothing more that we can bring forward here, we are content with that.\n— Commentary on Titus"],"TIT.1.12":["One of them, their own prophet, said: Cretans are always liars, evil beasts, idle bellies. This testimony is true: for which cause rebuke them sharply, that they may be sound in the faith, not attending to Jewish fables and the commandments of men who turn themselves away from the truth. As far as the sequence of the discourse and the context of the passage are concerned, what he says, One of them, their own prophet, said, seems to be referred to those of whom he spoke above: \"especially those who are of the circumcision, who must be restrained, who overturn whole houses, teaching what they ought not, for the sake of shameful gain\", so that there follows: one of them, their own prophet, said. But because this little hexameter verse is found in none of the prophets who prophesied in Judea, it seems to me that it should be read in two ways: so that what he says, One of them, their own prophet, said, is joined with the earlier words, \"for this reason I left you in Crete, that you might correct the things that were lacking\", and there follows: one of them, their own Prophet, said, that is, of the Cretans. But because much lies in between, and this seems absurd, and perhaps no one would accept it, it must therefore be fitted otherwise to the earlier words, which are nearer, so that we read: \"For there are many who are also not submissive, empty talkers and deceivers of minds, but especially those who are of the circumcision\"; these many, both not submissive, empty talkers and deceivers of minds, together with those who are of the circumcision, must be restrained, who overturn whole houses, teaching what they ought not, for the sake of shameful gain: one of them, their own Prophet, said; so that what he says, their own Prophet, is referred not specially to the Jews, and to those especially who are of the circumcision, but to the many who are not submissive, and empty talkers, and deceivers of minds, who, since they were certainly in Crete, must be believed to have been Cretans. Now this little verse is said to be found in the Oracles of Epimenides, the Cretan poet; whom he here called a prophet either mocking, namely that such Christians deserve to have such prophets, just as there were also prophets of Baal, and prophets of confusion, and others of stumblings, and whatever corrupt prophets Scripture mentions; or truly, because he wrote about Oracles and responses, which themselves foretell the future and proclaim long beforehand the things that are to come. Indeed the book itself is headed with the title Oracles; and because it seemed to promise something divine, I think the Apostle looked into it on that account, to see what the divination of the Gentiles promised, and at the right time made use of the little verse, writing to Titus, who was in Crete, so as to convict the false teachers of the Cretans by the island's own teacher. And Paul is found to have done this not only in this passage, but in others as well. For in the Acts of the Apostles, when he was addressing the people and disputing in the Areopagus, which is the senate house of the Athenians, he says among other things: \"As certain of your own poets also have said: For we are also his offspring\" (Acts 17), a half-line which is read in the Phaenomena of Aratus, whom Cicero translated into the Latin tongue, and Germanicus Caesar, and lately Avienus, and many whom it would take very long to enumerate. To the Corinthians too (1 Cor. 15:33), who were themselves polished with Attic eloquence and, because of the nearness of the places, seasoned with the savor of the Athenians, he took an iambic verse from a comedy of Menander: Evil conversations corrupt good morals. Nor is it any wonder if, as the occasion offered, he made use of the verses of Gentile poets, since, changing some things in the inscription of an altar, he even spoke to the Athenians: \"For passing through,\" he says, \"and beholding your objects of worship, I found also an altar on which was written: To an unknown God. What, therefore, you worship without knowing, this I announce to you\". But the inscription of the altar was not as Paul declared, To an unknown God, but thus: To the gods of Asia and Europe and Africa: to unknown and foreign gods. But because Paul did not need many unknown gods, but only one unknown God, he used the singular word, to teach that his God was the one whom the Athenians had inscribed in the title of the altar, and that they ought rightly to worship, knowing him, the one whom they venerated without knowing, and whom they could not fail to know. But Paul did this rarely, and as the occasion of the place rather than of display required, in the manner of bees, which are wont to make honey from various flowers and fit together the cells of the combs. There are those who think this verse was taken from Callimachus, the poet of Cyrene, and in some measure they are not mistaken. For he too, writing in praise of Jupiter against the Cretans, who boasted that they could show his tomb, says: Cretans are always liars, who even with sacrilegious mind built his tomb. But, as we said above, the whole verse was taken by the Apostle from the poet Epimenides, and Callimachus used its opening in his own poem; or else he put into meter, without theft of another's work, a common proverb by which the Cretans were called deceitful.\n— Commentary on Titus","That Paul knew secular letters, though not to perfection, his own words testify: \"One of them, a prophet of their own, said: Cretans are always liars, evil beasts, idle bellies\" (Titus 1:12). This heroic verse is by the poet Epimenides, whom both Plato and the other ancient writers recall. Among the Athenians also, when he was making his defense in the Areopagus, he added this: \"As also certain of your poets have said: For we are also his offspring\" (Acts 17:28). This half-line is found in Aratus, who wrote about the heaven and the stars. And also this: \"Very evil conversations corrupt good morals\" (1 Cor. 15:33); it is an iambic trimeter taken from a comedy of Menander. From these and other passages it is evident that Paul was not ignorant of secular letters, and that what he here called allegory he elsewhere called spiritual understanding.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"TIT.1.13":["Therefore rebuke them, he says, sharply: for they are liars, and evil beasts, and idle bellies; who persuade men of falsehoods, who after the manner of wild beasts thirst for the blood of those they deceive, and do not eat their own bread working in quietness; whose god is their belly, and their glory is in their shame; and rebuke them, that they may be sound in the faith. Of this soundness of faith he speaks also in what follows: that the old men be sober, honorable, chaste, sound in faith, and in love, and in patience; and after the likeness of faith, soundness is also ascribed to doctrine. \"There will be\", he says, \"a time when they will not acquiesce in sound doctrine\". There are also words of soundness, of which he speaks to Timothy in the first Epistle: \"If anyone teaches otherwise, and does not acquiesce in the sound words of our Lord Jesus Christ, and in the doctrine which is according to piety\" (1 Tim. 6:3). And in the second: \"Having a pattern of sound words which you have heard from me\" (2 Tim. 1:13).\n— Commentary on Titus"],"TIT.1.14":["Those who have this soundness of faith and doctrine and words will not attend to Jewish fables and the commandments of men who turn themselves away from the truth. Let us humor the Jews for a little while, and listen patiently to the absurdities of those among them who are called wise; and then we shall understand what are the Jewish fables, of men who, without the authority of Scripture, without any support of reason, invent certain fabulous animals; of whom Isaiah prophesied, saying: \"This people honors me with their lips, but their heart is far from me. But they worship me in vain, teaching the doctrines and commandments of men\" (Isa. 29:13). Approving this testimony in the Gospel, the Savior convicts them of having preferred the commandments of men to the Law of God. \"For God says: Honor father and mother\"; but they handed down: \"That whoever says to father and mother, Whatever gift is from me will profit you, shall not honor his father and his mother\" (Matt. 15:3 ff.). If anyone after the coming of Christ is cut, and not circumcised, he serves Jewish fables and the commandments of men who turn themselves away from the truth. For he is not a Jew who is one outwardly, but he who is one inwardly; and circumcision is of the heart, in the spirit, not in the letter. If anyone keeps the Pasch, not in the unleavened bread of sincerity and truth, so as to purge from his soul all the old leaven of malice and wickedness, he attends to fables and follows shadows, neglecting the truth. If anyone, not rising with Christ, nor seeking the things that are above but those that are below, says, \"Do not touch, do not taste, do not handle the things that are in corruption\", by their very use, according to the precepts and doctrines of men, he follows righteousnesses that are not good, and precepts that are not good. But where there is truth, and the spiritual law, there are good justifications, and the precepts are the best: whoever does them shall live in them.\n— Commentary on Titus"],"TIT.1.15":["All things are clean to the clean, but to the defiled and to unbelievers nothing is clean: but both their mind and their conscience are polluted. Because he had said above, \"For there are many who are also not submissive, empty talkers and deceivers, especially those who are of the circumcision\", and of those who had been deceived by them he had consequently added, \"Rebuke them sharply, that they may be sound in the faith, not giving heed to Jewish fables and the commandments of men who turn themselves away from the truth\"; and because, as is argued most fully to the Galatians and to the Romans, they thought there was a distinction among foods, since some seemed clean and some unclean, he therefore now adds: All things are clean to the clean, that is, to those who believe in Christ and know that every creature is good, and that nothing is to be rejected which is received with thanksgiving. But to the defiled and to unbelievers nothing is clean: because both their mind and their conscience are polluted; and for this reason even the things which are clean by nature become unclean to them. Not that anything is either clean or unclean, but that, according to the character of those who eat, it becomes both clean to the clean and unclean to the defiled. Otherwise, even the bread of blessing and the Lord's cup do not help any who are unbelievers and polluted; because whoever eats of that bread and drinks of the cup unworthily eats and drinks judgment to himself (1 Cor. 11). By the coming of Christ all things have been purified. What he has cleansed, we cannot make common. But we must take care, in treating these things, not to give occasion to that heresy which, according to the Apocalypse (ch. 2) and the apostle Paul himself writing to the Corinthians, thinks one may eat of things sacrificed to idols, because all things are clean to the clean (1 Cor. 8). For the Apostle's purpose here was not to discuss the things that are sacrificed to demons, but to argue against the Jews, who, according to the discipline of the abolished Law, thought some things clean and some unclean. \"For we cannot,\" he says, \"partake of the table of the Lord and of the table of demons; nor can we drink at once the cup of the Lord and the cup of demons\" (1 Cor. 10:20, 21). It lies with us, then, to eat things either clean or unclean. For if we are clean, the creature is clean to us. But if we are unclean and unbelievers, all things become common to us, whether through heresy dwelling in our hearts, or through the consciousness of offenses. But if our conscience does not reproach us, and we have the confidence of piety toward the Lord, we will pray with the spirit, we will pray also with the mind; we will sing with the spirit, we will sing also with the mind (1 Cor. 14); and we shall be far from those of whom it is now written: Both their mind and their conscience are polluted.\n— Commentary on Titus"],"TIT.1.16":["They confess that they know God, but in deeds they deny him: detestable, and disobedient, and reprobate for every good work. These, whose mind and conscience are polluted, confess that they know God, but in deeds deny him, according to that which is said in Isaiah: \"This people honors me with their lips, but their heart is far from me\" (Isa. 29:13). Just as, therefore, one who honors with the lips and withdraws far off with the heart, so one who confesses God in speech denies him by works. And one who denies God by works, with a feigned confession, is rightly detestable and profane; and, persuaded by no reasoning of the truth, he is called disobedient and unbelieving. Hence it comes about that he is reprobate for every good work: namely, that even the things that, overcome by natural goodness, he may perhaps have done well are not good, since they are reprobate through the perversity of his mind. Some think that God is denied only if someone, seized by the Gentiles in persecution, refuses to own himself a Christian. But see, the Apostle declares that God is denied by all deeds that are perverse. Christ is wisdom, righteousness, truth, holiness, fortitude. Wisdom is denied by folly, righteousness by iniquity, truth by lying, holiness by shamefulness, fortitude by weakness of soul. And as often as we are overcome by vices and sins, so often do we deny God; so that, on the contrary, as often as we do something well, we confess God. Nor should it be thought that on the day of judgment only those will be denied by the Son of God who denied Christ in martyrdom; but through all works, words and thoughts, Christ either, being denied, denies, or, being confessed, confesses. Of this confession, I think, he also gave command to the disciples, saying: \"You shall be my witnesses in Jerusalem, and in all Judea and Samaria, and to the end of the earth\" (Acts 1:8), so that in all good works and words the mind devoted to him may confess Christ. There is also a certain praiseworthy denial, of which the Apostle himself says: \"That, denying impiety and worldly desires, we may live purely and justly and piously in this present age, awaiting the blessed hope and the coming of our Savior God\" (Titus 2:12). Whoever denies with this denial, and is willing to follow the Savior when he says, \"Whoever will come to me, let him deny himself\" (Luke 9:23), having put off the old man with his works and put on the new, will follow his God. But how one denies himself must be considered. The chaste man denies the fornicator that he formerly was; the wise man the imprudent, the just man the unjust, the brave man the weak. And, to speak of all in common, we deny ourselves as often as, trampling on our former vices, we cease to be what we were and begin to be what we were not before.\n— Commentary on Titus"],"TIT.2.1":["But you, speak the things that befit sound doctrine. It is one thing to speak sound doctrine, another to teach the things that suit sound doctrine: for in the one there is simple instruction only; in the other, together with the teaching, there is also the correction of life. \"For whoever breaks one of these least commandments, and teaches men so, shall be called least in the kingdom of heaven\" (Matt. 5:19): the Lord commanding the disciples that they ought not only to labor in meditation on the Scriptures, so as to go over what is written and store it up in the treasury of memory, but first to do what has been commanded. \"But whoever does and teaches, he shall be called great in the kingdom of heaven.\" For unless our righteousness surpasses the righteousness of the scribes and of the Pharisees, we cannot enter the kingdoms of heaven (Matt. 5). They, sitting upon the chair of Moses, say and do not do, and bind unbearable burdens which they lay upon men's necks, and will not themselves touch them with their least finger. This, then, is what the Apostle now teaches Titus, his son in Christ and his disciple: to speak the things that suit sound doctrine; because doctrine has soundness then, when the teacher's doctrine and his life agree alike.\n— Commentary on Titus"],"TIT.2.2":["That old men be sober, honorable, chaste, sound in faith, and in love, and in patience. Having first commanded Titus in general what he himself ought to say to all, where he says, \"But you, speak the things that befit sound doctrine\", he now sets out, class by class, what befits each age. First what suits old men, then what befits old women, third what is fitting for the young, both male, that is, and female; although within the precept for the old women he has set down the commands concerning young women, so that he did not so much teach the young women himself as set out what they should be taught by the old women. Lastly he speaks of slaves; and through each age and condition he lays down his precepts so fittingly that his speech is a rule of life and of morals. Old men, then, are to be sober, or watchful, because νηφάλιοι among the Greeks means both; honorable, so that gravity of morals may adorn the gravity of age; chaste, lest they run riot at an age not suited to it, lest, with blood already cold toward lust, they be an example to the young unto ruin. Sound in faith: of this soundness of faith we spoke above. And sound not only in faith but also in love and in patience, so that, when they have first the soundness of faith, they may hear from the Savior: \"Your faith has made you whole\" (Mark 10:52). And elsewhere: \"For not even in Israel have I found such faith\" (Matt. 8:10). And because of this same soundness of faith let them become sons of Abraham, of whom it is written: \"Abraham believed God, and it was reckoned to him for righteousness\" (Gen. 15:6). And Habakkuk, recalling this soundness of faith, says: \"But the just man lives by my faith\" (Hab. 2:4). Read again the Epistle of the apostle Paul to the Hebrews (or of whoever else you think it is, since it has now been received among the ecclesiastical writings), count through that whole catalogue of faith in which it is written: \"By faith Abel offered to God a greater sacrifice than Cain\" (Heb. 11:4 ff.). And: \"Enoch was translated, that he should not see death\"; and Noah, believing God about the things he did not yet see, built the ark; and Abraham went out into a land he did not know. And lest Scripture should seem to give women no example of faith, it is written in the same Epistle that Sarah too received power to conceive seed when the time of her age was already past it, because she judged him faithful who had promised. There the faith of Isaac and of Jacob, of Joseph, of Moses too and of Rahab, and of the rest, is praised, whom he can know better who has read the Epistle itself. As there is, then, a soundness of faith, so the same soundness is also in love. And who possesses the soundness of love but he who has first loved God with all his soul, and with all his heart, and with all his strength? Then, hearing Christ's precept concerning his neighbor, \"You shall love your neighbor as yourself\" (Matt. 5), he has divided his love: because on these two commandments hang all the Law and the Prophets. He who has the soundness of love is not envious, is not puffed up, does not act wrongly, not dishonorably, is not provoked to anger, thinks no evil, does not rejoice over iniquity, but rejoices with the truth, bears all things, believes all things, hopes all things, awaits all things (1 Cor. 13). And because love never falls away, he who is in the soundness of love never himself falls either. For neither tribulation, nor distress, nor famine, nor persecution, nor nakedness, nor peril, nor sword will be able to separate him from the soundness of love which he has in Christ Jesus. Why should I speak of the sword and the other lesser things, which cannot divide him who possesses the love of soundness, when neither death, nor life, nor angels, nor principalities, nor things present, nor things to come, nor strength, nor height, nor depth, nor any other creature whatever can separate him who has the love of soundness in Christ Jesus? If the soundness of love has been understood by us, let us take from the Scriptures some example of those who are in weakness of love. The Savior says of the last times: \"Because when iniquity has been multiplied, the love of many will grow cold\" (Matt. 24). For love is warm in those who are fervent in spirit, but cold, icy and frozen in those who have taken in the harshest blasts of the North wind. \"For from the North evils will blaze forth upon all who inhabit the land\" (Jer. 1:14). With this coldness of love Amnon too had grown stiff toward his sister Tamar (2 Sam. 13). We must fear, then, lest we too at some time be overcome by this weakness of love. For it sometimes happens that at first our love toward a virgin, or toward any woman, is holy; and when the mind has been softened into affections, little by little the soundness of love grows pale with languor, and begins to weaken, and carries the lover to final death. Hence the Apostle too, cautiously and prudently, commands Timothy to exhort the young women in all purity (1 Tim. 5). And all purity is in flesh and spirit and soul: that the eye be not made to stumble; that we not hang clinging to the beauty of a woman's face; that it not delight us to hear flattering words; that the mind, hard before, not wilt at feigned words. Let them beware, then, as we have said, young men as well as old, young women as well as old women, and keep their heart with all diligence, lest through the soundness of love the sickness of love enter in, and through holy love there come to be an unholy love which drags them down to Gehenna. Let him who is sound in faith, who is sound in love, be sound also in patience: and in patience, which is proved above all in temptations; because it profits nothing to have had the things we enumerated above, unless all the riches and merchandise with which the ship is laden are preserved in the storm, and, while the winds blow from this side and that, what has been well gained is delivered without shipwreck. \"He who\", indeed, \"has persevered to the end, he shall be saved\" (Matt. 24:13).\n— Commentary on Titus"],"TIT.2.3":["Old women likewise in holy demeanor, not inciters, not enslaved to much wine; teaching well, that they may instruct the young women in purity, that they may love their husbands, that they may love their children: chaste, pure, having care of the house, kind, subject to their husbands, that the word of God be not blasphemed. Although the apostle Peter has commanded that husbands give honor to their wives as to the weaker vessel, it is not, however, to be supposed that a wife, who has a weak vessel of the body, is at once weaker in soul as well. Hence it is now commanded them, so that in them too that saying of the Apostle may be fulfilled: \"Power is made perfect in weakness\" (2 Cor. 12:9); and it is said that they also are to have all the things that were commanded in common to the old men, namely in that he says, Old women likewise, that is, just as the old men, honorable in all things, sober, chaste, sound in faith, and in love, and in patience; and, for their sex, they are to have this as their own, that they be in holy demeanor, or, as it reads better in the Greek, ἐν καταστήματι ἱεροπρεπεῖς (in a bearing befitting what is sacred), so that their very gait and movement, face, speech and silence may display a certain dignity of sacred seemliness. And because this kind of little women is usually talkative, according to that saying: \"And at the same time, being idle, they learn to go about from house to house; and not only idle, but also wordy and busybodies, speaking things they ought not\" (1 Tim. 5:13): therefore he wants them to be not inciters (incentrices), that is, not accusers, not such as disparage some in order to please others. Or certainly, not such as, because they themselves have already passed beyond youth, discuss the ages of the young women and say: That one adorns herself so, that one does her hair so, so that one goes out; she loves him, she is loved by him: whereas, even if these things are true, they ought not so much to accuse her before the others as to reprove the girl herself in private with the love of Christ, and rather to teach her not to do it than to accuse in public what she has done. Women of this age, because they have grown cold to the licentiousness of the body (although there are very many who are not ashamed of their gray hairs, and, trembling, are dressed up as little maidens before a flock of grandchildren), are accustomed to give themselves to wine in place of lust; and when among their cups they have seemed to themselves wise and eloquent, to put on a kind of austerity of morals, speaking as what they seem to themselves to be, and not remembering what they were. So let old women be kept also from too much drinking of wine, since what lust is in the young, drunkenness is in the old. Or how can an old woman teach young women purity, when, if a young woman has imitated the drunkenness of an old woman, she cannot be chaste? And he expressed it pointedly: not enslaved to much wine. For it is a kind of servitude, and the lowest condition, for a man's senses to be seized by wine, and for him to be not his own but wine's. Since, then, he taught first what the old women ought to be, and after the things they have in common with the old men set out also the things proper to them, that they be full of an honorable and holy demeanor and of all seemliness: not accusers, not disparaging others, not with their senses seized by wine; now, accordingly, he hands them the reins of teaching, so that, when they are such, they may have freedom to teach, namely to teach the things that are good. For although in another place he has said, \"But I do not permit women to teach\" (1 Tim. 2:12), it must be understood thus: that teaching directed at men is taken from them. For the rest, let them teach the young women as their daughters.\n— Commentary on Titus"],"TIT.2.4":["First purity: because the enemy fights more against this in the flowering age, and all his power against women is in the navel of the belly; then that they love their husbands and love their children. What teaching is there in loving husbands, since this is established not in the eloquence of one who teaches but in the heart of one who loves? He wants them to love their husbands purely: he wants there to be a chaste love between husband and wife, so that with modesty and reserve, and as if by the necessity of her sex, she rather renders the debt to her husband than herself demands it of him, and believes that she performs the work of begetting children before the eyes of God and of the angels: thus she will not blush even at the secret couch, and the darkness of the night, and her closed chamber, since she will have reflected that all things lie open to the eyes of God. And they love their children thus, if they instruct them in the discipline of God. But to be unwilling to sadden them by teaching the things that are good, and to grant them the freedom to sin, is not to love children but to hate them.\n— Commentary on Titus"],"TIT.2.5":["Let the young women also be instructed to have care of the house. And because it could happen that the care of the house was governed with harshness, and through this precept of the Apostle the matron might become too severe toward the household servants, he therefore joined to it kind: so that she might believe that she rules her husband's house well when she commands the servants with kindness, not with terror. And also subject to their husbands: lest, puffed up by riches and rank, they not remember the sentence of God by which they are subjected to their husbands. For he says to the woman: \"Your turning shall be to your husband, and he shall rule over you\" (Gen. 3:16). In this the prudence of holy Scripture is to be considered: that the Lord did not say this to the man, saying, \"You shall rule over your wife\", but to the woman herself, so as to leave her the reward of compliance, since it is in her own power, if she wishes to obey the precepts of God, to serve the man and to be subject to her husband, so that it might in some way be a free servitude, and full of love; she is therefore serving her husband while she fears to offend him. For man was not created for the woman, but woman for the man. And since the man is the head of the woman, and Christ the head of the man, any wife who is not subject to her husband, that is, to her head, is guilty of the same crime as the man is if he is not subject to Christ, his head. And the word of the Lord is blasphemed either when the first sentence of God is despised and counted for nothing, or when the Gospel of Christ is defamed, while she who is a Christian, and subject by the law of God, desires, against the law and the faith of nature, to command her husband: when even Gentile women serve their husbands by the common law of nature.\n— Commentary on Titus"],"TIT.2.6":["Exhort the young men likewise to be chaste in all things, showing yourself an example of good works, in doctrine, in integrity and purity, in sound and irreprehensible speech, so that he who is on the opposing side may be abashed, having nothing evil to say of us. Just as, in what he had commanded above, saying, \"Old women likewise in holy demeanor\", we had said that the likeness of the old women was to be referred to the old men, so now, in what he has added, \"Urge the young men likewise to be chaste\", we judge that the likeness of the young men is to be fitted to the old women, and through the old women to the old men: so that they have the sobriety of the old men, and be honorable and chaste, and sound in faith, and in love, and in patience; and that of the old women in holiness of demeanor, that they be not accusers, not enslaved to much wine, teaching well, and the rest. And he has set down this as proper to the young men, that they be chaste in all things, namely in mind as well as in body, in deed as well as in thought, so that there be in a young man no suspicion of shamefulness. And although some of the Latins think it should be read thus: \"Urge the young men likewise to be chaste\", and afterward add, \"in all things showing yourself a pattern of good works\", yet let us know that in all things is to be referred to what precedes, that is: urge them to be chaste in all things. It must also be known that continence is necessary not only in the work of the flesh and in the desire of the mind, but in all things: that we may not seek undue honors, that we may not be inflamed by avarice, that we may not be overcome by any passion.\n— Commentary on Titus"],"TIT.2.7":["\"Showing yourself\", he says, \"a pattern of good works\". It profits nothing for someone to be practiced in speaking and to have worn his tongue smooth for talking, unless he teaches more by example than by word. In short, one who is unchaste, however eloquent he may be, if he exhorts his hearers to purity, his speech is weak and has no authority to exhort. And conversely, however rustic and slow of speech he may be, if he is pure, he can by his example drive men to the likeness of his life. And what he says, \"in incorruption\", is to be taken thus: that incorruption (incorruptio) properly means virginity. Indeed, those who are virgins are commonly called incorrupt (incorrupti), and those who have ceased to be virgins are named corrupted (corrupti); and we say: She who was once a virgin has been corrupted. Hence I think that Titus, believing the Gospel before he was taken up with the work of the flesh, received baptism and remained a virgin, and is now admonished by the Apostle to show a pattern of himself in incorruption: an incorruption which, indeed, we do not see in Timothy. For when he said to him, \"Let no one despise your youth, but be a pattern of the faithful in speech, in conduct, in love, in faith, in purity\" (1 Tim. 4:12), he was silent about incorruption and set down only \"purity\". But purity can be understood also in celibacy without virginity. Unless perhaps we take purity as in the mind, and incorruption as in the body, according to what is written elsewhere in the definition of a virgin: \"That she may be holy in body and in spirit\" (1 Cor. 7). And he himself has now added in sequence: \"in doctrine, in incorruption, in chastity\". We could interpret both chastity and incorruption as the integrity of doctrine too, were it not that what follows specifically, sound and irreprehensible speech, would then hold a precept of its own concerning instruction in doctrine.\n— Commentary on Titus"],"TIT.2.8":["And as for his saying irreprehensible speech: not that anyone is of such eloquence and prudence as to be reprehended by no one (for even the apostles and evangelists are reprehended by heretics and Gentiles), but that he says or does nothing worthy of reprehension, even though adversaries are ready to reprehend. And because there are many who are not subject, empty talkers and deceivers of minds, who hate him who reproves in the gates and abhor holy speech, therefore let us show ourselves an example in all things, in doctrine, in integrity, in chastity, in sound and irreprehensible speech, so that our adversaries, thoroughly frightened by the soundness of our life and doctrine, may not dare to accuse, that is, may fabricate nothing plausible in accusation. And indeed to this day we see some in the churches (although this is a rare bird) of such gravity and continence that they have testimony even from their adversaries, and it is said: That man is great, and of holy conduct and upright morals, were he not a heretic. For no one is of such unbounded impudence that he could accuse the rays of the sun of being dark, and overspread its clear light with the gloom of night. Hence the Apostle too, guarding against these same things, says: \"That I may cut off occasion from those who desire occasion\" (2 Cor. 11:12). He who is on the opposing side can also be understood as the devil, who is the accuser of our brethren, as John the Evangelist proclaims: who, when he has nothing evil to lay against us, blushes, and the slanderer will not be able to slander. And diabolus in the Latin language means \"slanderer\" (criminator).\n— Commentary on Titus"],"TIT.2.9":["Let slaves be subject to their masters in all things: let them be pleasing, not contradicting, not stealing, but showing all good faith, that they may adorn the doctrine of our Savior, God, in all things. Since our Lord and Savior, who says in the Gospel, \"Come to me, all you who labor and are burdened, and I will refresh you\" (Matt. 11:28), considers no condition, age or sex a stranger to blessedness, therefore the Apostle now lays down precepts for slaves too, namely as for a part of the Church and members of the body of Christ. And just as above he taught what Titus ought to instruct the old men, the old women, the young women and the young men, so now he lays down precepts suited to slaves. First, that they be subject to their masters in all things. But in all those things which are not contrary to God: so that if the master commands things that are not opposed to the holy Scriptures, the slave is to be subject to the master; but if he commands things contrary to them, let him obey the master of the spirit rather than the master of the body. Pay careful attention to how he decrees precepts suited to the persons. \"Let slaves\", he says, \"be subject to their masters in all things\" (Eph. 6:5). In another place, treating of children, he says: \"Children, obey your parents\" (Col. 3:20). For it befits children to obey their parents, but slaves to be subject to a master who commands. Nor let us think it contradictory that in another Epistle \"Let wives\", he says, \"be subject to their husbands\" (Eph. 5:22; Col. 3:18), and in this one has declared that wives are to be subject to their husbands, as though he used the same word both of slaves and of wives. For in a certain way the husband is the master of the wife. \"He\", it says, \"shall rule over you\" (Gen. 3:16). The Savior too was subject to his parents (Luke 2), but when he was still twelve years old and differed in nothing from a slave, though Lord of all; for he had not yet reached the full age of a man that could take up the inheritance. But it is also written of him elsewhere: \"For when all things have been subjected to him, then the Son himself will be subjected to him who subjected all things to himself, that God may be all in all\" (1 Cor. 15:28). And all things will be subjected to him when he has said, \"The Lord to my Lord: Sit at my right hand, until I make your enemies the footstool of your feet\" (Ps. 109:1). In slaves who are subject, the Lord is subject. And just as he is said to be a curse for us, though he is not a curse but true blessing, so for us he is written either as subject or as not subject, according as we are either subject to God or not subject. Some read this passage thus: Let slaves be subject to their masters, and after they have punctuated up to here, they add, in all things let them please: whereas in the Greek the order of the reading is different, that is, let slaves be subject to their masters in all things; so that there follows εὐαρέστους εἶναι, which we can render, though not fully, yet in some measure, that they be well content with themselves: namely, lest the sentence of God on their condition should seem unjust to them. But just as the poor man can be saved according to his measure, and the woman in the weakness of her sex is not excluded from the kingdom of God, and every condition can attain blessedness according to its rank, so let slaves too be well content with themselves that they are slaves, and not think that they cannot serve God because they are subject to men; but rather that they please the will of God in this, if they are both subject to their masters in all things and well content with themselves in their condition; and let them carry out what the Apostle commands next, that they be not contradicting, not stealing. Indeed the greatest vice of slaves is to contradict their masters, and, when these have commanded anything, to mutter to themselves. Therefore he admonishes Titus to take away, through sound doctrine, a passion of this kind from those who are Christian slaves. For if the slave must carry out what the master commands, why should he not do this very thing with good will, rather than both offend his master and still do what he is told: especially since God too was offended at the water of contradiction? And in another place he speaks of the murmuring people: \"Let their murmuring cease from me, and they shall not die\" (Num. 17).\n— Commentary on Titus"],"TIT.2.10":["After contradiction, let the doctrine of Christ correct another vice of slaves too, \"that they be not thieves\". Now a thief is judged one not only in greater things but also in lesser. For it is not what has been carried off by theft but the mind of the one stealing that is considered. Just as in fornication and adultery, the fornication or adultery is not different on this account, if the woman is beautiful or rich, ugly or poor, a harlot or an adulteress, but whatever she may be, it is one fornication, or adultery: so also in theft, however much a slave has carried off, he incurs the crime of theft. Hence in the law of Moses thieves are compelled sometimes to restore sevenfold, sometimes fourfold, and at times the thief himself is cut down, at times he is sold for his theft, matters which I recall that I recently expounded to you in Leviticus. But if this is forbidden in a slave, how much more in a free man, lest either a judge plunder, or a soldier not content with his pay lay waste what belongs to others? Finely did a certain man of the greatest gravity, when the integrity of a certain judge was being praised to him and the one praising said of him, \"He is not a thief\", reply: \"He would make an excellent slave, if he were not a runaway as well\": so far should the suspicion of theft be from every free man. So let slaves be subject to their masters in all things, let them be well content with their condition, so that they do not bear slavery bitterly, do not contradict their masters, do not steal, and besides this show good faith in all things, that they may adorn the doctrine of the Savior, our God, in all things. For if they have been faithful in the least with their fleshly masters, greater things will begin to be entrusted to them with God. And he adorns the doctrine of the Lord who does the things that are fitting to his condition. And on the other hand he puts it to confusion who is not subject in all things, whose condition displeases him, who, a contradicter and a defrauder, shows good faith in nothing. For how can he be faithful with the goods of God who could not show faith to a fleshly master?\n— Commentary on Titus"],"TIT.2.11":["For the grace of God the Savior has shone forth to all men, instructing us that, denying impiety and worldly desires, we should live chastely, and justly, and piously in this age, awaiting the blessed hope and the coming of the glory of the great God and our Savior Jesus Christ, who gave himself for us, that he might redeem us from all iniquity and cleanse for himself a choice people, an emulator of good works. After the catalogue of doctrine to Titus, of what he ought to instruct the old men, what the old women, what the young women and the young men, what, lastly, the slaves, he now rightly adds: For the grace of God the Savior has shone forth to all men. For there is no difference at all between free and slave, Greek and barbarian, the circumcised and the one who has a foreskin, woman and man: but we are all one in Christ, we are all called to the kingdom of God, all of us after the offense are to be reconciled to our Father; not through our merits, but through the grace of the Savior: either because Christ himself is the living and subsisting grace of God the Father, or because this is the grace of Christ, God the Savior; and we have been saved not by our own merit, according to what is said in another place: \"For nothing you will save them\" (Ps. 55:8).\n— Commentary on Titus"],"TIT.2.12":["This grace has shone forth to all men for this reason: to instruct us that, denying impiety and worldly desires, we should live chastely and justly and piously in this age. And what it is to deny impiety and worldly desires, I trust can be understood from what we expounded above, \"They confess that they know God, but in deeds they deny him\", opposites being explained through their opposites. Worldly desires, then, are those which are suggested by the prince of this world: and since they belong to the age, they pass away with the cloud of this age. But when we have lived chastely and justly in Christ, sinning, that is, neither in body nor in mind, we shall also live piously in this age: and this piety awaits the blessed hope and the coming of the glory of the great God and our Savior Jesus Christ.\n— Commentary on Titus"],"TIT.2.13":["For as impiety dreads the coming of the great God, so piety, secure about its work and about its faith, awaits him. Where is Arius the serpent? Where Eunomius the snake? The great God Jesus Christ is called Savior; not the firstborn of every creature, not the Word of God and wisdom, but Jesus Christ: names which belong to the man assumed. Nor indeed do we call Jesus Christ one and the Word another, as a new heresy falsely charges: but the same one, both before the ages and after the ages, both before the world and after Mary, nay rather from Mary, we call the great God, our Savior Jesus Christ, who gave himself for us, that with his precious blood he might redeem us from all iniquity, and cleanse for himself a people περιούσιον (for so it stands in the Greek) and make it an emulator of good works.\n— Commentary on Titus"],"TIT.2.14":["Often considering with myself what the word περιούσιον might mean, and asking the wise men of this age whether they had perhaps read it anywhere, I could never find anyone to explain to me what it signified. Therefore I was compelled to have recourse to the old instrument, from which I supposed the Apostle too had taken what he had said. For a Hebrew of the Hebrews, and as to the law a Pharisee, he was surely setting down in his Epistle what he knew he had read in the Old Testament. So in Deuteronomy I found: \"For you are a holy people to the Lord your God, and it has pleased the Lord your God in you that you should be to him a people περιούσιον out of all the peoples that are upon the face of the earth\" (Deut. 7:6). And in the hundred and thirty-fourth Psalm, where we have, \"Sing psalms to his name, for it is sweet: for the Lord has chosen Jacob for himself, Israel for a possession (in possessione) for himself\" (Ps. 134:3–4): for what is \"for a possession\" (in possessione), in the Greek it is written εἰς περιουσιασμὸν, which Aquila and the Fifth Edition indeed expressed as εἰς περιούσιον. But the Seventy and Theodotion, translating περιουσιασμὸν, made a change of syllable, not of sense. Symmachus, then, for what is περιούσιον in the Greek, SGOLLA (סגלה) in the Hebrew, expressed ἐξαίρετον, that is, \"choice\" (egregium), or \"special\" (præcipuum); for which word, using the Latin language in another volume, he rendered \"peculiar\" (peculiarem). Rightly, then, did Christ Jesus, our great God and Savior, redeem us with his blood, to make the Christian people peculiar to himself, a people that could then be peculiar if it proved an emulator of good works. Hence also what is written in the Gospel according to the Latin translators, \"Give us this day our daily (quotidianum) bread\" (Matt. 6:11), is better rendered in the Greek \"our bread ἐπιούσιον\", that is, \"special\", \"choice\", \"peculiar\": namely him who, coming down from heaven, says, \"I am the bread that came down from heaven\" (John 6:51). Far be it, indeed, that we, who are forbidden to take thought for the morrow, should be commanded in the Lord's prayer to ask for that bread which in a little while is to be digested and cast out into the drain. Nor is there much difference between ἐπιούσιον and περιούσιον: for only the preposition has been changed, not the word. Some think that the bread in the Lord's prayer is called ἐπιούσιον because it is above all οὐσίας, that is, above all substances. If this is accepted, it does not differ much from the sense we have set out. For whatever is choice and special is outside all things and above all things.\n— Commentary on Titus"],"TIT.2.15":["Speak these things and exhort, and rebuke with all authority. He has set down three things: speak, exhort, and rebuke. And what he says, speak, seems to refer to doctrine. But what he has added, exhort (exhortare), that is, παρακάλει, means something different in the Greek from what it means in the Latin: for παράκλησις means comfort rather than exhortation. He used this word above too about the young men: \"Comfort (consolare) the young men likewise to be chaste in all things\". In its own place we rendered it as it is read in the Latin, as though \"exhort\" were written. He, then, comforts his hearer who says, \"We beseech you on behalf of Christ, be reconciled to God\" (2 Cor. 5:20), and humbles and subjects himself, to gain the one he comforts. But the third thing, rebuke, seems to me to be the opposite of comfort, so that whoever has despised comfort is worthy of rebuke, and deserves to hear: \"You have forgotten the comfort which speaks to you as to sons\". To Timothy also we read of comfort as one thing and rebuke as another, as the Apostle says: \"Be instant in season, out of season; reprove, rebuke, comfort\" (2 Tim. 4:2). And there, indeed, rebuke is taken up first, and afterward severity is tempered by comfort. Here, however, he wants the disciples first to be comforted, and, if they have not profited by the comforting, then to be reproved, and reproved with all authority. For so I understand what has been said, rebuke with all authority: that it refers specifically to rebuke, and not to the two preceding in common. For it is not fitting to say, comfort with all authority, and speak with all authority, but only, rebuke with all authority.\n\nLet no one despise you. Someone might think that this is the very same thing now written to Titus that was said to Timothy: \"Let no one despise your youth\" (1 Tim. 4:12). But we, following the distinction of the Greek language, think that περιφρονείτω, which is written here, means one thing, and καταφρονείτω, which was said to Timothy, another, and that the prepositions περὶ and κατὰ make a different sense. And that the apostle Paul uses, not by chance and at whim, different nouns and verbs, and even different prepositions, according to the variety of the matters, can be made plain from what he says: \"For the woman is from the man, but the man through the woman\" (1 Cor. 11:12). And elsewhere: \"For from him and through him and in him are all things\" (Rom. 11:36). And also this: \"Paul an apostle, not from men, nor through man\" (Gal. 1:1). We think, therefore, that καταφρόνησιν properly pertains to contempt, as when someone, stretched on the rack and among the hot plates, despises pain, and fears neither the threats of the judge nor the roar of the crowd standing around, but for the confession of martyrdom despises and disdains all torments. On the other hand there is also an evil contempt, of which Habakkuk too, the Holy Spirit speaking in him, testifies: \"Behold, you despisers, and look, and wonder at wonders, and perish\" (Hab. 1:5). According to which we have said that it was written to Timothy also: \"Let no one despise your youth\" (1 Tim. 4:12), that is: I do not want you to show yourself such that you could deservedly be despised by anyone. Περιφρόνησις, however, means this, as the Stoics at any rate assert, who distinguish subtly between words: when someone, trusting in himself that he is better than another, looks down on the one he thinks inferior, and, being above, that is, wiser, judges the humbler man worthy of contempt. Something of this kind a certain person among the Greeks, swelling with the vanity of pride and looking down on heaven itself and the sun, is mocked for having said: Ἀεροβατῶ καὶ περιφρονῶ τὸν ἥλιον, which we can say in Latin: I climb through the air, and I know myself to be worth more than the sun. Περιφρόνησις, then, which is now set down to Titus, has this sense: Let no one of those who are in the churches, while you act sluggishly, so live as to think himself the better. For what sort of edification will there be for the disciple if he perceives that he is greater than his master? Hence not only bishops, presbyters and deacons ought to take great care to go before all the people over whom they preside in conduct and in speech, but also the lower rank: exorcists, readers, sacristans, and absolutely all who serve the house of God. For it greatly destroys the Church of Christ that laymen should be better than clerics.\n— Commentary on Titus"],"TIT.3.1":["Remind them to be subject to rulers and authorities, to obey; to be ready for every good work; to blaspheme no one, not to be quarrelsome; to be modest, showing all gentleness toward all men. Something of the kind is written to the Romans as well: \"Let every soul be subject to the higher authorities. For there is no authority except from God\" (Rom. 13:1). This precept, both here and there, I think was issued for this reason: that at that time the doctrine of Judas the Galilean was still flourishing and had very many followers. Mention of him is made also in the Acts of the Apostles, where Scripture relates: \"For before these days Theudas rose up, saying that he was someone great; and about three thousand men joined him\" (Acts 5:36); and, \"after him rose up Judas the Galilean in the days of the census\". Among other things he put forward this as if it were plausible from the Law: that no one ought to be called Lord except God alone, and that those who brought tithes to the temple should not pay tribute to Caesar. This heresy had grown so much that it troubled a great part even of the Pharisees and of the people, so that this question was brought even to our Lord: \"Is it lawful to give tribute to Caesar, or not\" (Matt. 22:17)? Answering them prudently and cautiously, the Lord says: \"Render the things that are Caesar's to Caesar, and the things that are God's to God\" (ibid. 21). In agreement with this answer, the apostle Paul teaches that believers ought to be subject to principalities and authorities. For Ἀρχαὶ, which is read in the Greek, sounds more like \"principalities\" (principatus) than \"rulers\" (principes), and signifies the authority itself, not the men who are in authority. But because he had said, \"Remind them to be subject to principalities and authorities\", an occasion for denial could be given to those who dread torments: they might claim, according to the Apostle's saying, that they were subject to principalities and authorities and were doing what these commanded; therefore he added: To obey for every good work. If what the emperor and the governor command is good, comply with the will of the one who commands. But if it is evil and savors of what is against God, answer him with that saying from the Acts of the apostles: \"One must obey God rather than men\" (Acts 5:29). Let us understand this same thing of servants toward their masters, of wives toward their husbands, and of children toward their parents: that they ought to be subject to masters, husbands and parents only in those things that do not go against the commandments of God. What follows, To be ready, is to be read in two ways: either so that \"to be ready for every good work\" is understood; or else, joining what he says, to obey for every good work, with what precedes and ending it there, to be ready is, as it were, another precept of its own and a special one, in keeping with what is written in Leviticus: \"The goat that is sent away, bearing the curses of the people, is to be handed over into the hands of a ready man\" (Lev. 16:21). If, then, anyone is ready to hold the ἀποπομπαῖον (the scapegoat), and lead it out into the desert, and there destroy it, and, so far as in him lies, banish it by the lot of the curse, he, when he has obeyed, will also be ready for every good work. To be ready can also be taken in another way: that they should picture to themselves beforehand in their minds everything that can happen, so that when it does happen they suffer nothing as if it were new, but all things have been prepared for them in advance.\n— Commentary on Titus"],"TIT.3.2":["No one, too, to blaspheme, is not taken simply. For he does not say, to blaspheme no man, but absolutely, no one: not an angel, not any creature of God. For all things that were made by God are very good. \"When Michael the archangel was disputing with the devil about the body of Moses, he did not dare to bring a judgment of blasphemy, but said, May God command you\" (Jude 9). If, then, Michael did not dare to bring a judgment of blasphemy against the devil, who was surely most deserving of a curse, how much more ought we to be pure of every curse? The devil deserved a curse; but blasphemy ought not to come out through the archangel's mouth. Read the old books again, and see which tribes were stationed on Mount Gerizim to bless the people, and which on the other mount to curse. Reuben, who had defiled his father's bed, and Zebulun, the last son of Leah, and the children of the handmaids are placed on Mount Ebal to curse those who are worthy of cursing (Deut. 27). It would take long if I were now to recount how Jacob, who had called his sons to a blessing (Gen. 49:1), saying, \"that I may bless you\", afterward joins, as if within the blessing, \"cursed be their fury, because it was headstrong\"; and how the Lord himself speaks in Genesis: \"Cursed is the ground in your works\" (Gen. 3:17). Let it suffice now to have said only this: that the disciples of Christ ought not to blaspheme, nor, as is added, to be quarrelsome. For if we are sons of peace, and wish peace to rest upon us, and have come to the heavenly Jerusalem, which took its name from peace, let us have peace with those who hate peace, and, so far as in us lies, let us be peaceable with all men: not only with the modest but also with brawlers, since there is no virtue in bearing with the gentle; and let us give place to wrath, showing all gentleness toward all men: not that we ought, from a desire for vainglory, to show ourselves gentle to all men, but that, while we bear with all and do not repay injury in kind, our very works may become better known to all. Someone can, for the sake of boasting and the opinion of the crowd and popular favor, feign gentleness before certain people and pretend goodness. But where there is no true, genuine and solid gentleness, I do not know whether all can be persuaded that he is gentle.\n— Commentary on Titus"],"TIT.3.3":["For we too were once foolish, disobedient, erring, serving various desires and pleasures, living in malice and in envy, hateful, hating one another. But when the goodness and kindness of our Savior God shone forth, not from works of righteousness which we did, but according to his mercy he saved us, through the washing of regeneration and of the renewal of the Holy Spirit, whom he poured out upon us richly through Jesus Christ our Savior, that, justified by his grace, we may become heirs according to the hope of eternal life. Someone may ask how Paul was foolish, unbelieving, erring, and serving various desires and pleasures, in malice and in envy, hateful and hating, before the goodness and clemency of our Savior saved him through the washing of the second regeneration: not from the works of righteousness which he had done, but out of his mercy, the Holy Spirit having been poured out abundantly and generously upon the apostles and believers through Jesus Christ, so that, having obtained the inheritance of grace, they might possess the hope of life forever. And indeed we read that, according to the righteousness which is in the Law, he was without reproach, circumcised on the eighth day (Phil. 3:5): a Hebrew of the Hebrews, according to the Law a Pharisee, of the tribe of Benjamin, educated at the feet of Gamaliel, and instructed in the sacred Letters from infancy (Acts 22). To this the answer is that the Jews who were versed in the Law before the coming of the Savior and his passion and resurrection had righteousness, if not full, yet in some part: as Simeon too did, and Anna the prophetess, who was found serving in the temple of God. But after the people cried out together, \"Crucify, crucify him; we have no king but Caesar\" (John 19:15), and, \"His blood be upon us and upon our children\" (Matt. 27:25), and the kingdom of God was taken from them and given to a nation producing its fruits: from that time whoever did not believe in Christ was foolish, wandering, unbelieving, and serving various pleasures. Or does it not seem to us that Paul was foolish when he had a zeal for God, but not according to knowledge, and persecuted the Church, and kept the garments of those who were stoning Stephen? when he blazed up, goaded into such hatred against the Savior, that he received letters from the priests and went to Damascus to bind those who had believed in Christ? Or could he have any virtues without the power of God, Christ Jesus, or quench the raging flame of pleasures, when he was not a temple of God? And what greater malice and envy can there be than to take up letters against people who are absent, and everywhere to lay waste the disciples of Christ; to be unwilling to be saved oneself, and to envy others who could be saved; to hate Christians and consequently to earn the hatred of all? And what greater error, and disobedience, and madness is there than, after the day has breathed and the shadows have passed away, to wish to keep the abolished Law and to say, \"Do not handle, do not touch, do not taste\", and, when solid and manly food has appeared, to desire to be given the milk of infancy to drink?\n— Commentary on Titus","Others understand this place in such a way as to think it said not of Paul and the apostles, but, under the person of the apostles, of others: so that, just as he spoke in his own person and in that of Apollos and of Cephas about the dissension and schism which he was rebuking among the Corinthians, so also in the present place, naming himself and the apostles, he showed what all who had believed in Christ were like before the regeneration of the life-giving washing. At the same time his humility too is to be admired: that he who despised all the humility and righteousness of the Law as refuse and offscourings rightly recalls that without Christ he had served all vices.\n— Commentary on Titus"],"TIT.3.4-7":["Let us attend more carefully, and we shall find in the present passage the Trinity most manifest. For the benignity and clemency of our Savior God, of none other than God the Father, through the washing of regeneration and the renewal of the Holy Spirit, whom he poured out upon us abundantly through Jesus Christ our Savior, justified us unto eternal life. The salvation of believers is the mystery of the Trinity.\n— Commentary on Titus"],"TIT.3.8":["A faithful word, and concerning these things I want you to confirm, that those who believe in the Lord may take care to be foremost in good works. These things are good and useful to men. What he says, a faithful word, is to be joined to what precedes, where he had set down first, \"that, justified by his grace, we may become heirs according to the hope of eternal life\". For the word about the inheritance of God and about the hope of eternal life is worthy of faith. Hence one ought, not doubting, not fearing, both to believe these things oneself and to confirm them so that others may believe; and not only this, but this too is to be confirmed, together with these others who wish to believe: wherefore he says, and concerning these things I want you to confirm. And those who have believed that these things are truly so must take care of good works, through which the inheritance of God and the hope of eternal life are prepared. And beautifully, to produce greater faith, he did not say, who believe men, but who believe God. For it is necessary that they take care of good works, which, when fulfilled and carried out with all zeal, are good and useful to believers.\n— Commentary on Titus"],"TIT.3.9":["But avoid foolish questions, and genealogies, and contentions, and fights that come from the Law: for they are useless and vain. Because questions are manifold and diverse, Solomon therefore spoke about those who seek God, saying: \"But those who seek him rightly have found peace\" (Prov. 16:8). Those, then, who do not seek God rightly cannot find peace. There are very many examples of those who seek God not rightly. The Jews seek God perversely, hoping that they can find him without Christ. The heretics, clattering with an empty din of words, seek one whom they cannot find. Philosophers too, and barbarians, holding various views about God, have sought God. But because they did not seek rightly, their questions were foolish, since they thought that God could be comprehended by human senses. From these questions, then, Paul calls us back. But to wise questions, and to those propped up by the authority of the Scriptures, he rather exhorts and summons us, not unaware of the precepts of the Savior, in which he says: \"Seek and you will find, knock and it will be opened to you, ask and it will be given to you. For everyone who seeks finds, and who asks receives, and to him who knocks it will be opened\" (Luke 11); only let our body not be subject to sins, and wisdom will enter into us. Let the understanding be exercised, let the mind be fed daily with divine reading, and our questions will not be foolish questions. And what he says, \"Avoid genealogies and contentions and quarrels that come from the Law\", strikes properly at the Jews, who boast in this and think they have knowledge of the Law if they hold the names of individuals: names which, because they are barbarous and we do not know their etymologies, are generally pronounced corruptly by us. And if perhaps we have erred in accent, in the length and shortness of a syllable, either lengthening short ones or shortening long ones, they are wont to laugh at us for our lack of skill, especially in the aspirates and in pronouncing certain letters with a scraping of the throat. This has come about because the Seventy translators, through whom the divine Law was translated into the Greek tongue, expressed in particular the letter HETH, and AIN, and others of this kind (because they could not carry them over into the Greek language with a double aspiration), by adding other letters. For example, so that for Rahel they said Rachel, and for Jeriho, Jericho, and for Hebron, Chebron, and for Seor, Segor; but in other cases this attempt fails them. For we and the Greeks have only one letter s, but they have three, SAMECH, SADE and SIN, which have different sounds. Isaac and Sion are written with SADE; Israel with SIN, and yet it does not sound as it is written. Seon, king of the Amorites, is both pronounced and written with the letter SAMECH. If, then, these idioms of the names and of the language, being of course barbarous, have not been expressed by us as they are expressed by the Hebrews, they are wont to raise a guffaw and to swear that they have no idea at all what we are saying. Hence it has also been our care to correct, from the originals themselves, all the books of the old Law which the learned man Adamantius had arranged in the Hexapla, copied from the library of Caesarea; in these both the Hebrew words themselves are written out in their own characters and they are expressed in Greek letters in the adjoining column. Aquila also and Symmachus, the Seventy too and Theodotion keep their own order. Some books, however, and especially those which among the Hebrews are composed in verse, have three other editions added, which they call the fifth, sixth and seventh translation, editions that have gained authority without the names of their translators. That immortal genius bestowed these things on us by his own labor, so that we need not greatly fear the haughtiness of the Jews, who delight in loosened lips, a twisted tongue, hissing saliva and a scraped throat. They have also another occasion for pride: just as we who are Latins more easily commit to memory Latin names having their origins from our own tongue, so they from an early age, with their innermost senses, have drunk in the native words of their speech, and from the beginning, Adam, to the last, Zerubbabel, they run through the generations of all so readily and swiftly from memory that you would think they were reciting their own name. We, who either learned other letters, or certainly believed in Christ late, or, even if we were given over to the Church as infants, pursue the sense of the Scriptures rather than the words: if perhaps we do not know this so well, they think themselves more learned in reciting names, in reckoning years, and in grandsons and great-great-grandsons, grandfathers, great-grandfathers and great-great-great-grandfathers. I heard a certain one of the Hebrews, who pretended at Rome to have believed in Christ, raise a question about the genealogies of our Lord Jesus Christ written in Matthew and Luke: namely, that from Solomon to Joseph they agree with each other neither in number nor in the correspondence of the names. When he had perverted the hearts of the simple, he would bring forth, as if from the inner sanctuary and an oracle, certain solutions, as they seemed to him, when he ought rather to have sought righteousness and mercy and the love of God, and after those things, if it happened to come up, to dispute about names and numbers. Perhaps we have said enough, and more than was necessary, about the haughtiness of the Hebrews; but an occasion was given us to discourse on the genealogies and contention and quarrels that come from the Law. The dialecticians, whose chief is Aristotle, are wont to spread nets of argumentation and to shut up the roving freedom of rhetoric within the thorn-thickets of syllogisms. These men, then, who wear away whole days and nights in asking or answering, in giving a proposition or accepting it, in assuming, confirming and concluding, call certain people contentious, those who dispute as they please, not by reason but with the temper of litigants. If, then, those whose art is properly contention do this, what ought the Christian to do but flee contention altogether? Quarrels about the Law, too, are to be utterly rejected and left to the folly of the Jews. For they are useless and vain, having only the appearance of knowledge; but they profit neither those who speak nor those who hear. For what does it profit me to know how many years Methuselah lived, or in what year of his age Solomon took a wife, lest perhaps Rehoboam be believed to have been born in the eleventh year of Solomon's age? and many things of this kind, which are either hard to find because of the variety of the books and the errors that have grown in (while uncorrected copies are gradually written from uncorrected copies), or, even if we should find them with great zeal and labor, we know will profit nothing. It often happens that we have fights over the Law, not from a desire for truth but from boasting of glory, while we wish to be thought learned among those who hear; or at any rate we pursue shameful gain from this petty bit of fame. For what does it profit to chatter with foaming lips and with the barking of dogs, when a simple and moderate answer can either appease you, if it is true, or, if false, be corrected gently and peaceably?\n— Commentary on Titus"],"TIT.3.10":["Avoid a heretical man after one and a second reproof, knowing that such a one is subverted, and sins, being condemned by himself. The name of heresy is used also in the Epistle to the Corinthians: \"For there must also be heresies among us, so that those who are approved may become manifest\" (1 Cor. 11:19). And to the Galatians it is numbered among the works of the flesh: \"Now the works of the flesh are manifest, which are fornication, uncleanness, licentiousness, idolatry, sorceries, enmities, contentions, rivalries, angers, quarrels, dissensions, heresies, envyings, drunkennesses, revellings, and other things like these, which I foretell to you as I have foretold: that those who do such things will not possess the kingdom of God\" (Gal. 5:19 ff.). In these it must be carefully observed that, just as the other vices which are numbered among the works of the flesh exclude us from the kingdom of God, so heresies too take the kingdom of God away from us; and it makes no difference in what way anyone is excluded from the kingdom, so long as he is excluded. And, what may be more wonderful, that passage from the Acts of the apostles also seems worth reading again, where our faith in Christ and the discipline of the Church were already then called a heresy by perverse men. \"For we\", say the Jews to the apostle Paul, \"have neither received letters from you from Judea, nor has any of the brethren come and reported to us, or anyone spoken evil of you. But we ask to hear from you what you think; for concerning this heresy it is known to us that it is everywhere spoken against\" (Acts 28:21, 22). And although the name of heresy is not spoken at Miletus, yet its works are named by Paul when he speaks to the presbyters of the church: \"I know that after my departure grievous wolves will come in among you, not sparing the flock; and from among yourselves men will rise up speaking perverse things, to draw away disciples after them\" (Acts 20:29, 30). Let these things be said in passing, about where else too heresy is so named. Now the name itself seems to need to be sifted most fully. Heresy is named in Greek \"from choice\" (electio), because each one chooses for himself what seems to him better. The philosophers too, Stoics, Peripatetics, Academics, Epicureans, are said to be of this or that heresy. It is superfluous to go through them one by one and to enumerate Marcion, Valentinus, Apelles, Ebion, Montanus and Manichaeus with their doctrines, since it is very easy for anyone to know by what errors each is led. Arius and Eunomius, and the author of a new heresy: would that they were not so well known; perhaps they would have deceived fewer! Avoid, then, a heretical man after one reproof, or, as the Greek has it better, νουθεσίαν; and νουθεσία signifies rather admonition and teaching without rebuke. In the Latin manuscripts is read (and Pope Athanasius too approved it as true): \"After one and a second reproof\"; namely that it is not enough for one who is depraved by some error to be reproved or admonished only once, but a second teaching too must be applied to him, so that in the mouth of two or three witnesses every word may stand.\n— Commentary on Titus"],"TIT.3.11":["And why he is to be avoided after a first and second reproof, he gives the reasons, saying: \"that such a one is subverted, and sins, since he is condemned by himself\". For he who, reproved once and twice, having heard his error, does not wish to be corrected, thinks the one correcting him is in error; and on the contrary, preparing himself for fights and wranglings of words, he wants to win over the one by whom he is taught. He is said to be condemned by himself for this reason: because the fornicator, the adulterer, the murderer and the other vices are driven out of the Church through the priests; but heretics pass sentence on themselves, withdrawing from the Church by their own choice; and this withdrawal seems to be a condemnation by their own conscience. Between heresy and schism they think the difference is this: that heresy holds a perverse doctrine, while schism separates from the Church on account of episcopal dissension; which indeed can be understood in some measure at the beginning. But there is no schism that does not invent some heresy for itself, so that it may seem to have withdrawn from the Church rightly.\n— Commentary on Titus"],"TIT.3.12":["When I send Artemas to you, or Tychicus, hasten to come to me at Nicopolis; for there I have decided to winter. We read at the beginning of this Epistle: \"For this reason I left you in Crete, that you may correct the things that were lacking, and appoint presbyters in the cities, as I arranged for you\". This was so that, since the Cretans had recently believed, they should not be left orphans when Paul withdrew and passed on to other churches, but should have an apostolic man to correct the things that seemed to be lacking. Since, then, after the foundation of other churches Titus was needed to build the structure upon it, he writes to him that when he had sent Artemas or Tychicus, that is, one of the two who had been with him, to Crete, to fill his place, he himself should come to Nicopolis, declaring that he would winter there. From this we prove Paul's fatherly affection toward the Cretans. He needs Titus for the ministry of the Gospel; yet he does not want him to come to him before Artemas or Tychicus has arrived as successor in his place. Nicopolis is the city which received its name from the victory of Augustus, because there he overcame Antony and Cleopatra.\n— Commentary on Titus"],"TIT.3.13":["Send Zenas, the teacher of the law, and Apollos ahead with care, so that nothing may be lacking to them. This is the Apollos about whom it is written to the Corinthians too: \"Each of you says: I am of Paul, but I of Apollos, and I of Cephas\" (1 Cor. 1:12). He was a man of Alexandria, of the Jews, very eloquent and perfect in the Law, bishop of the Corinthians. Because of the dissensions that were at Corinth, it is to be supposed that he crossed over with Zenas, the teacher of the law, to the neighboring island of Crete, and that, when the dissensions that had arisen at Corinth were calmed by Paul's Epistle, he returned again to Corinth. As for Zenas, the teacher of the law, we cannot say from any other place of Scripture who he was, except only this: that he too, an apostolic man, had the same work that Apollos was doing, that of building up the churches of Christ. He therefore instructs Titus that, since they were about to sail from Crete to Greece, he should not let them be in need of provisions, but should see that they have what is necessary for provisions for the journey.\n— Commentary on Titus"],"TIT.3.14":["But let ours also learn to be foremost in good works for necessary uses, so that they may not be unfruitful. Above he had said: \"Send Zenas, the teacher of the Law, and Apollos ahead with care, so that nothing may be lacking to them\". Since, then, a hidden reply could arise, so that not so much Titus as any reader of the Epistle might say this: \"And from what means is Titus to bestow on them, since they have no provisions for the journey?\", he resolves this question and, as if no objection were put to him, strikes it down by saying: But let ours also learn to be foremost in good works for necessary uses, so that they may not be unfruitful. He calls his own \"ours\", those who had believed in Christ: because they were Christ's, they rightly deserved to be called both Paul's and Titus's. You have, he says, authority over the disciples; teach them not to be unfruitful, but to minister to the evangelists and apostolic men who serve good works; and to minister not in any matters whatever, but for necessary uses: \"For having food and clothing, let us be content with these\" (1 Tim. 6:8); and let those who serve the altar live from the altar; and those who have become partakers of our spiritual things ought to share their carnal things with us. And lest perhaps they should easily despise either Paul's letter or Titus's precept, he calls unfruitful all who have not ministered to the evangelists. Solomon too says in Proverbs: \"But almsgiving is fruit\" (Prov. 3:9). And Paul himself calls love the first fruit of the spirit (Gal. 5). Love, however, is proved above all in sharing and in ministry. So that they may not, he says, be unfruitful. \"For every tree that does not bear good fruit will be cut down and cast into the fire\" (Matt. 3:10). Now I say this because he who sows sparingly will also reap sparingly. To be unwilling to give to apostolic men and to the evangelizers of Christ for necessary uses is to condemn oneself of barrenness.\n— Commentary on Titus"],"TIT.3.15":["All who are with me greet you. Either he used the customary formula, saying that Titus was greeted by all who were with him; or else he says it properly of Titus, because he was such as to deserve the love of all who were with Paul. And it is great praise of Titus to be greeted through Paul by all.\n\nGreet those who love us in faith. If everyone who loves loved in faith, and there were not others who love without faith, Paul would never have added faith to love, saying: Greet those who love us in faith. For mothers too love their sons, so much that they are ready to meet death for them; but they do not love in faith. And wives love their husbands, with whom they very often die; but that love is not of faith. Only the love of the saints loves in faith, so much so that, even if the one who is loved is an unbeliever, the saint nevertheless loves him in faith, according to that saying: \"Let all your things be done in faith\" (1 Cor. 16:14). And elsewhere: \"Love your enemies\" (Luke 6:35). The saint loves his enemies, and therefore loves in faith, because he believes in him who promised that he would render a reward for the fulfilling of the commandment.\n\nThe grace of our Lord be with you all. It should be known that in the Greek manuscripts it is written thus: \"Grace be with you all\", so that neither \"of the Lord\" nor \"our\" is found in the authentic books. So he invokes grace in common upon the saints and believers, upon Titus and the others who were with him. And just as the patriarch Isaac blessed his son Jacob (Gen. 27), and he himself the twelve Patriarchs (ibid. 49), the apostles too, entering a house, used to say: \"Peace to this house\" (Matt. 10:12). And if the house was worthy, their peace rested upon it; but if it showed itself unworthy, it returned to those who had invoked it. So now too, at the end of his Epistle, the Apostle invokes grace upon believers: a grace which had its effect together with the prayer, and lay in the power of the believers, if the one blessed was willing to show himself such as the one who blessed him.\n— Commentary on Titus"],"PHM.1.1-24":["According to the Jews, Paul means \"admirable\"; Timothy, \"beneficent\"; Philemon, \"wonderfully gifted\" or \"mouth of bread\", from os \"mouth\", not os \"bone\"; Apphia, \"continent\" or \"liberty\"; Archippus, \"length of work\"; Onesimus, \"answering\"; Epaphras, \"fruitful\" and \"seeing\", or \"growing up\"; Mark, \"exalted by commandment\"; Aristarchus, \"mountain of greater work\"; Demas, \"silent\"; Luke, \"himself rising up\". If you wish to understand these names according to their meaning, it is not difficult to see that the admirable and beneficent one writes above all to him to whom all faults have been forgiven and whose mouth lies open to the heavenly bread. Next he writes to the continent woman, the free woman, and to the length of work, because he never desists from holy labour. He writes on behalf of him who answers to his own testimony. He writes also that greeting is given to him to whom the Epistle is specially dedicated, by growing fruitfulness. And the one who has been made more exalted through the commandments sends greeting, and the greeting comes from him who through greater works has grown up even into a mountain. It comes also from him who set a guard on his mouth and a fortified door on his lips, who perhaps kept silent for this reason, that he had for a little while abandoned the Apostle. And last, it comes from him who, rising up through himself, is daily increased and makes progress, while the world is filled with his Gospel, and he grows as often as, being heard and read, he edifies.\n— Commentary on Philemon"],"PHM.1.1":["Those who refuse to receive among the Epistles of Paul the one which is written to Philemon say that the Apostle did not always, nor in all things, speak with Christ speaking in him. For, they argue, human weakness could not have borne the one unbroken course of the Holy Spirit; nor would the necessities of this poor body always be fulfilled in the presence of the Lord: such as arranging a meal, taking food, being hungry, being filled, digesting what has been taken in, replenishing what has been used up. I say nothing of the other things which they unfold in an elaborate and forced manner, so as to affirm that there was some time in which Paul would not dare to say: \"I live, now not I, but Christ lives in me\" (Gal. 2:20); and this: \"Or do you seek a proof of him who speaks in me, Christ\" (2 Cor. 13:3)? What sort of proof of Christ is it, they say, to hear: \"The cloak which I left at Troas with Carpus, when you come bring with you\" (2 Tim. 4:13)? And this to the Galatians: \"Would that they were even cut off who trouble you\" (Gal. 5:12). And in this very Epistle: \"But at the same time also prepare me a lodging\". And this, they say, happened in like manner not only to the apostles but also to the prophets. Hence it is quite often found written: \"The word of the Lord came to Ezekiel\" (Ezek. 22), or to any other of the prophets, because after the prophecy had been completed, returning again into himself, he became an ordinary man instead of a prophet; and in no one except our Lord Jesus Christ did the Holy Spirit abide permanently. This sign John the Baptist too had received: that he upon whom he saw the Holy Spirit descending and remaining in him, he should know to be the one (John 1). From this he shows that the Holy Spirit indeed descends upon many, but that this is the distinctive mark of the Saviour, that he abides permanently in him. By these and other arguments of this kind they hold either that the Epistle which is written to Philemon is not Paul's, or that, even if it is Paul's, it has nothing that can edify us; and that it was rejected by most of the ancients, since it was written merely for the purpose of commending, not of teaching. But on the contrary, those who defend it as of genuine authority say that it would never have been received throughout the whole world by all the churches unless it were believed to be Paul the apostle's. And by this rule, they say, those men ought not to receive even the second Epistle to Timothy, and that to the Galatians, from which he himself also published examples of human weakness: \"The cloak which I left at Troas with Carpus, when you come bring with you\"; and: \"Would that they were cut off who trouble you\". Very many things are found both in Romans and in the letters to the other churches, and especially to the Corinthians, dictated in a more relaxed way and in almost everyday speech, in which the Apostle says: \"But to the rest I say, not the Lord\". These too, because they contain something of the sort, must either not be thought epistles of Paul; or, if these are received, that to Philemon must also be received, on the precedent of similar letters already received. And those men, they say, err greatly and naively if they think that buying food, preparing a lodging, procuring clothes, is a sin, and assert that the Holy Spirit is put to flight if for a little while we serve the necessities of the poor body. \"Do not\", says the Apostle, \"grieve the Holy Spirit, in whom you were sealed in the day of redemption\" (Eph. 4:30). By what works the Holy Spirit is grieved the prophet records, having set out many vices and sins in order, and adding at the end: \"In all these you grieved me\". Otherwise, would offering a cup of cold water, washing feet, sacrificing a calf, preparing a meal, be a sin, when we know that through these things certain people are adopted as sons of God? This is not the time to answer everything, since we have not brought forward everything that they are accustomed to propose. But if they do not think that small things belong to those who own the great things as well, then, according to Valentinus, Marcion and Apelles, they must bring in for me one Creator of the ant, of worms, gnats and locusts, and another of heaven, earth, sea and the angels. Or rather, is it not a mark of the same power not to withhold in lesser things also the skill which you have exercised in greater? And since we have made mention of Marcion, let them be taught that the Epistle to Philemon is Paul's at least on Marcion's authority. Although he either did not receive the other Epistles of the same Apostle, or changed and gnawed away certain things in them, on this one alone he did not dare to lay hands, because its brevity defended it. But they seem to me, while they accuse the epistle of simplicity, to betray their own lack of skill, not understanding what power and wisdom lies hidden in its individual words. These things, while you pray, and with the Holy Spirit himself, by whom they were written, prompting us, we shall try to explain in their places. But if brevity is held in contempt, let Obadiah, Nahum, Zephaniah and the others of the twelve prophets be despised, in whom the things that are related are so wonderful and so great that you would not know whether you ought to admire in them the brevity of the words or the greatness of the meanings. But if those who reject the Epistle to Philemon understood this, they would never despise brevity, which, in place of the long-trailing burdens of the Law, has been written with the beauty of the Gospel, since the Lord makes a word shortened and completed upon the earth. But now the very words of the Apostle are to be set down, which begin thus.\n— Commentary on Philemon","Paul, a prisoner of Christ Jesus, and Timothy the brother, to Philemon the beloved and our fellow worker, and to Apphia the sister, and to Archippus our fellow soldier, and to the church that is in your house: grace to you and peace from God our Father and our Lord Jesus Christ.\n\nYou have been pleased to have me dictate to you on the Epistles of Paul in a back-to-front and inverted order. For when you, O Paula and Eustochium, often asked me to do it, and I stubbornly refused, you compelled me at least to discuss a short one, and one which seemed to you the last in sense and order as well as in the number of its verses. I shall therefore attempt at the end what belongs to the beginning. Another commentator would ask at once, in his introduction to the Apostle, why, or at what time, or by whose naming, he received the name of Paul in place of Saul. I am now compelled to do this, although before this day I had never dared to utter even a mu about him, as the saying goes. Nor indeed should it be thought, as the simpler Latins read it, that he was formerly called Saulus and not Saul; for he was of the tribe of Benjamin, in which this name was quite familiar. Indeed, that well-known Saul, king of Judea, the persecutor of David, was also of the tribe of Benjamin. But as for his being called Saulus by us, it is no wonder that Hebrew names are declined after the likeness of the Greek and Roman cases, so that just as Josephus is said for Joseph, and Jacobus for Jacob, so Saulus too is said for Saul in our language and speech. The question, then, is why, or at whose command, he either lost his old name or took a new one. That Abram should be called Abraham was accomplished by God's bidding (Gen. 17, etc.); that the name Sarai should become Sarah was likewise God's command. And to come to the New Testament: that Simon should receive the name of Peter (Mark 3), and that the sons of Zebedee should be called BANE REEM (בני רעם), that is, \"sons of thunder\", was ordained by the voice of our Lord Jesus Christ (ibid.). But why he was called Paul instead of Saul, no Scripture records. I shall therefore act boldly, but perhaps truly, in setting out my conjectures from the Acts of the Apostles. We read in them that the Holy Spirit said at Antioch: \"Set apart for me Barnabas and Saul for the work to which I have taken them up. Then, fasting and praying and laying hands on them, they sent them away\" (Acts 13:3 ff.). They went down to Seleucia of Syria and sailed to Salamis of Cyprus, which is now called Constantia. They had John with them in the ministry (on whose account a quarrel edifying to the Church was later stirred up). When they had travelled through the whole island and come as far as Paphos, they found a certain magician, a false prophet named Bar-Jesus, with the proconsul Sergius Paulus, a prudent man. The proconsul summoned Barnabas and Saul and desired to hear the word of God from them. So when the magician resisted and was perverting Sergius from the right faith, \"Saul\" (says Scripture) \"who is also Paul, filled with the Holy Spirit, looking upon him said: O full of all guile and all deceit, son of the devil, enemy of all justice, do you not cease to subvert the right ways of the Lord? And now behold, the hand of the Lord is upon you, and you shall be blind, not seeing the sun for a time. And immediately a mist and darkness fell upon him, and going about he sought someone to give him his hand. Then the proconsul, when he had seen what was done, believed, marvelling at the teaching of the Lord. And when Paul and those who were with him had sailed from Paphos, they came to Perga of Pamphylia\". Note carefully that it is here that he first received the name of Paul. Scipio, having subdued Africa, took for himself the name of Africanus; Metellus, having subjugated the island of Crete, brought back to his family the distinction of Creticus; and Roman emperors down to the present day are styled Adiabenicus, Parthicus and Sarmaticus from the nations they have subdued. In the same way Saul, sent to preach to the Gentiles, brought back the trophies of his victory from the first spoil of the Church, the proconsul Sergius Paulus, and raised his standard, so that he was called Paul instead of Saul. But if the meaning of the name is also sought, Paul in Hebrew means \"wonderful\". And truly it is a wonder that after being Saul, which means \"sought after\" (because he had been demanded by the devil to harass the Church), he should become, from a persecutor, a vessel of election. This has been discussed at greater length perhaps than was fitting, but necessarily. As for what follows, prisoner of Jesus Christ: in no Epistle has he used this title, although in the body of the Epistles, namely those to the Ephesians, the Philippians and the Colossians, he testifies that he is in chains for his confession. And it seems to me a mark of greater loftiness to call himself a prisoner of Jesus Christ than an apostle. For the apostles gloried that they had been worthy to suffer insult for the name of Jesus Christ (Acts 5:41); but the authority of the chains was necessary. Since he was about to plead for Onesimus, he had to plead as one who could obtain what he asked. Happy indeed is he who glories not in wisdom, not in riches, not in eloquence and worldly power, but in the sufferings of Christ. With such words he also concludes his Epistle to the Galatians: \"For the rest,\" he says, \"let no one trouble me; for I bear the marks of our Lord Jesus Christ in my body\" (Gal. 6). Not everyone who is a prisoner, however, is a prisoner of Christ. Whoever is bound for the name of Christ and for confessing him, he is truly called a prisoner of Jesus Christ; and only that shed blood makes a martyr which is poured out for the name of Christ. He writes to Philemon, then, as a prisoner in prison at Rome, at the time when, it seems to me, the Epistles to the Philippians, Colossians and Ephesians were dictated. I place the Epistle to the Philippians there for this reason: first, because he writes with Timothy alone, as he also does in this Epistle. Next, because he says that his chains have become manifest for Christ throughout the whole praetorium. What the praetorium is, he indicates at the end of that same Epistle: \"All the saints greet you, but especially those who are of Caesar's household\". Sent to prison by Caesar and having become better known to his household, he made the persecutor's house a church of Christ. Then he says: \"But some proclaim Christ out of contention, not sincerely, thinking to stir up affliction for my chains\" (Phil. 1:17). Further, the opening to the Colossians is also similar: \"Paul, an apostle of Jesus Christ by the will of God, and Timothy the brother\". And in what follows: \"Of which I, Paul, have been made a minister, who now rejoice in sufferings for you, and fill up in my flesh what is lacking of the sufferings of Christ, for his body, which is the Church\" (Col. 1:2, 23, 24). And at the end: \"My greeting by the hand of Paul. Be mindful of my chains\" (Col. 4:18). I cite this so that we may know that these Epistles too were dictated from prison and in chains. But the Epistle to the Colossians has this peculiar feature, that the same Onesimus who is now commended to Philemon was also the bearer of that letter. Accordingly he says: \"All that concerns me Tychicus will make known to you, the dearest brother and faithful minister and fellow servant in the Lord, whom I have sent to you for this purpose, that he may know what concerns you and comfort your hearts, with Onesimus, the dearest and faithful brother, who is one of you\" (ibid. 7, 8). Now Philemon, to whom this epistle is written, is the master of Onesimus, or rather has begun to be his brother in the Lord. And the Colossians are told that Onesimus is one of them. If so, reason itself and the order of things lead us to conclude two things: that Philemon too was a Colossian, and that Onesimus carried the letter common to the whole church at the same time as he took up the private letter commending him to his master. There is also another indication: in this same epistle Archippus too is named, to whom the present letter is written along with Philemon. \"Say\", he says, \"to Archippus: See to the ministry which you have received from the Lord, that you fulfil it\". What is the ministry which Archippus received from the Lord? In the letter to Philemon we read: \"And to Archippus our fellow soldier, and to the church that is in your house\". From this I think that he was bishop of the Colossian church and is admonished to preside over it zealously and diligently, as a preacher of the Gospel. Or, if that is not so, this suffices me for the present: that Philemon and Archippus, and Onesimus himself who carried the letters, were Colossians, and that the four Epistles (as we said before) were written at the same time. I place the Epistle to the Ephesians there for this reason. Here too he says that he is a prisoner for Christ. At the end of this Epistle he also enjoins the same things he had commanded the Colossians: that wives be subject to their husbands, and husbands love their wives; that children obey their parents; that fathers not provoke their children to anger; that slaves obey their masters according to the flesh; that masters lay aside threats and grant their slaves what is just. And at the very end he concludes his Epistle thus: \"What I am doing Tychicus will make known to you, the dearest brother and faithful minister in the Lord, whom I have sent to you for this very purpose, that you may know what concerns me, and that he may comfort your hearts\". Now Tychicus is the one who is also sent to the Colossians with Onesimus, and he had Onesimus as his travelling companion at the time when Onesimus was carrying the letter to Philemon.\n\nAnd Timothy the brother. In other epistles Sosthenes and Silvanus are associated with him, and sometimes Timothy the brother. In only four is Timothy named alone, because they were dictated either at the same time, or with Timothy and the others present. I think this was done for a twofold reason. First, so that the Epistle might have greater authority, since it was not written by one man. Second, there was no rivalry among the apostles: if perhaps, while Paul was dictating, the Spirit had suggested something to another, Paul added it without any vexation to the letter he was dictating. He did so in accordance with what he himself enjoined on the Corinthians: if, while one is prophesying, something is revealed to another, he who was prophesying first should be silent (1 Cor. 14). Thus he too fulfilled his own precept in deed, and because of the few things he had added at another's suggestion, he headed the epistle as another's as well as his own. To Philemon, he says, the beloved. The Greek does not have ἠγαπημένῳ, which is rendered \"beloved\" (dilectus), but ἀγαπητῷ, that is, \"lovable\" (diligibilis). Between beloved (dilectus) and lovable (diligibilis) there is this difference: even one who does not deserve love can be called beloved, but only one who is loved for his merit is lovable. Thus we are commanded to love even our enemies, who are beloved, but not lovable. For we love them, not because they deserve to be loved, but because we are commanded not to hold them in hatred. Again, what is set down in the title of the forty-fourth Psalm, \"for the beloved\", is better in the Greek, \"for the lovable\"; and this passage is most plainly understood of Christ. The Jews think that Solomon is called IDIDIA (ידידיה), that is, \"loved by God\", because that name was given him by God on account of his wisdom (2 Sam. 12). Yet who can more truly be called loved by God than he of whom the Father speaks in the Gospel: \"This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased; hear him\" (Luke 9:36)? So also in Isaiah it is written thus: \"I will sing to the beloved a song of the lovable one for my vineyard. A vineyard was made for the beloved\" (Isa. 5:1). The circumcision thought that this was to be understood of the Jewish people, and so struck against the stone of stumbling and the rock of scandal. They did not reflect that the vineyard transplanted from Egypt is the house of Israel, and that it is Christ who is here called either lovable or beloved. For he both deserves to be loved by the saints, and the saints love him, offering him love rather than demanding any rewards for their love. Paul and Timothy therefore write to Philemon, dearest and fellow worker; he is called dearest for this reason, that he is engaged in the same work of Christ.\n— Commentary on Philemon"],"PHM.1.2":["They write also to Apphia the sister, who had in her nothing of a false and feigned sisterhood. And they write to Archippus the fellow soldier: I think that, fighting with Paul and Timothy against adversaries for the name of Christ, he proved victorious, and is therefore now called fellow soldier because he prevailed in the same contest and war. It is written also to the church that is in his house. But this is ambiguous: is it the church in the house of Archippus, or the one in the house of Philemon? It seems to me that it should be referred not to the person of Archippus but to that of Philemon, to whom the epistle itself is assigned. For although Paul and Timothy write jointly to Philemon, Apphia, Archippus and the church, yet what follows proves that Paul alone is writing to Philemon, one conversing with one: \"I give thanks to my God always, and making remembrance of you\". You will be able to find this manner of writing in a number of his epistles: several persons, writing to several, are set down in the preface, but afterwards, throughout the whole body of the Epistle, one alone is brought in as discoursing. Writing to the Galatians, the Apostle says that in the faith of Christ it makes no difference whether one is a Gentile or a Jew, man or woman, slave or free (Gal. 3:28); this too becomes clear in this place. For between two men, and apostolic men, between Paul's fellow worker and his fellow soldier, the name of Apphia is inserted in the middle, so that, supported on either side by such company, she may be seen to hold her rank by merit, not by sex.\n— Commentary on Philemon"],"PHM.1.3":["As for his saying, Grace to you and peace from God our Father and the Lord Jesus Christ, it is still written by two to several. In almost all the Epistles the opening is the same, calling down upon them grace and peace from God the Father and Christ the Lord. From this it is shown that the nature of the Son and of the Father is one, since the Son can bestow what the Father bestows, and the Father is said to bestow what the Son bestows. Now grace is that by which we are saved through no merit or work. Peace is that by which we have been reconciled to God through Christ, as in the passage: \"We beseech you on behalf of Christ, be reconciled to God\" (2 Cor. 5:20).\n— Commentary on Philemon"],"PHM.1.4":["I give thanks to my God always, making remembrance of you in my prayers, hearing of your love and the faith which you have in the Lord Jesus and in all his saints: that the sharing of your faith may become evident in the full knowledge of every good which is in you in Christ.\n\nThese words he now speaks not as Paul and Timothy to Philemon and the rest, but as Paul alone to Philemon alone: I give thanks, he says, to my God always making remembrance of you in my prayers. It is said ambiguously, however, whether he gives thanks to his God always, or makes remembrance of him in his prayers always. And both can be understood. For he who instructs others to give thanks to God in all things can be hemmed in by no straits so tight that he does not himself render thanks to God always. But if Paul always prayed for the saints and for all the better sort (and Philemon too is holy, having in himself such faith and love that he was known to him not only by report but also by deed), it is credible that Paul also prayed always for Philemon. He prayed, namely, that the faith and love which Philemon had in Christ and in all his saints might be preserved by Christ's mercy, through the sharing of faith and the operation of full knowledge in every good.\n— Commentary on Philemon"],"PHM.1.5":["And concerning the love which he had in Christ Jesus and in all his saints, the interpretation is indeed not difficult: it is the love by which, after God, we bid [men] to love our neighbors too. Now the question is this: how can anyone have the same faith in Christ Jesus and in his saints? For, by ἀπὸ κοινοῦ, the phrase sounds both ways: the love which you have in the Lord Jesus and in all his saints, and the faith which you have in the Lord Jesus and in all his saints. To expound this passage let us take an example from Exodus: \"The people believed God and Moses his servant\" (Exod. 19). One and the same belief is directed to Moses and to God, so that the people who believed in the Lord is said to have believed equally in the servant. And this holds not only in Moses but in all his saints, so that whoever has believed God cannot receive faith in him in any other way unless he also believes in his saints. For that love and faith toward God is not perfect which is thinned by hatred and unbelief toward his ministers. What I mean is this. Someone has believed in God the Creator. He cannot believe unless he has first believed that what is written about his saints is true: that Adam was formed by God; that Eve was fashioned from his rib and side; that Enoch was translated; that Noah alone was saved when the world was shipwrecked; that Abraham, the first to be commanded to go out from his land and his kindred, left to his descendants the circumcision which he had received as a sign of the offspring to come; that Isaac was offered as a victim, and that a ram, sacrificed in his place and crowned with thorns, foreshadowed the Lord's passion; that Moses and Aaron afflicted Egypt with ten plagues; that at the voice and prayers of Joshua the son of Nun the sun stood still in Gibeon, and the moon in the valley of Aijalon. It would be long to run through all the deeds of the Judges, and to draw the whole story of Samson to the mystery of the true sun (for that is what his name signifies). I shall come to the books of Kings: when at harvest time, at Samuel's entreaty, rains and streams suddenly poured down from heaven; and David was anointed king; and Nathan and Gad prophesied mysteries; when Elijah was caught up in a fiery chariot, and Elisha, with a double spirit, though dead raised the dead. Unless one has believed all these things and the rest that are written about the saints, he will not be able to believe in the God of the saints. Nor can he be brought to faith in the Old Testament unless he has accepted as true whatever the history narrates about the patriarchs and prophets and other distinguished men: so that from the faith of the Law he may come to the faith of the Gospel, and the righteousness of God may be revealed in him from faith to faith, as it is written: \"But the just lives by faith\" (Hab. 2). It is also commanded in another place: \"Be holy, because I am holy, says the Lord your God\" (Lev. 19:2). The same holiness is owed to the servants and to the Lord: for both he who sanctifies and those who are sanctified are all from one. Let us not think it slight praise of Philemon that he has the same faith in the saints as in God. He who believes God to be holy certainly does not err. But if anyone believes a man who is not holy to be holy, and joins him to fellowship with God, he violates Christ, of whose body we are all members. \"He who says\", he says, \"that the just is unjust and the unjust just, both are abominable before God\" (Prov. 7:15). Likewise he who says that the holy is not holy, and again asserts that the unholy is holy, is abominable before God. All believers, according to the Apostle, are made the body of Christ (1 Cor. 6). Whoever errs and slips with regard to the body of Christ, asserting that a member of it is holy when it is not, or not holy when it is holy, see what a charge he becomes liable to: \"Woe\", says Isaiah, \"to those who call sweet bitter and bitter sweet, making darkness light and light darkness\" (Isa. 5:20). Sweet, I think, is holiness; bitter is what is contrary to holiness. Likewise holiness can be understood as light, and darkness as what is contrary to holiness. Which of us, do you think, though a moneychanger skilled in testing coins, will not err in discerning the saints?\n— Commentary on Philemon"],"PHM.1.6":["He who has equal love and faith in God and in his saints ought to have a sharing of that faith which is not unequal: so that as he believes and loves, so he may complete his love and faith in deed. Hence he says: May become evident in the full knowledge of every good; or, as it stands better in the Greek, \"effective\". For ἐνεργής can properly be rendered effective or operative: so that we may not merely believe that faith and love in God and in his saints can suffice for us, but that what we believe may be fulfilled in deed. It can happen, however, that someone has faith and completes it in deed, but simply and barely, without having full knowledge or knowledge of it, according to that saying of the Apostle: \"I confess that they have a zeal of God, but not according to knowledge\" (Rom. 10:2). There are today many among the simple who do works of righteousness and have no knowledge of the things they themselves do. Hence he added: That the sharing of your faith may become operative in the full knowledge of every good. By what great steps and what great leaps does the apostolic word stretch itself toward higher things? Someone has love and faith in God and in his saints, but perhaps he does not share it with all in an equal balance. Perhaps he shares it with all, but does not carry it out in deed. Someone may fulfil it in will and in deed, yet he cannot have a perfect understanding of his own actions. Let there also be such a one as has both the work and the knowledge, yet he does not have the full knowledge of every good. For though he performs many things justly, gently and zealously, he is unequal in some part to his own virtues. Is not Philemon such a one? For he has the sharing of an operative faith and love in the full knowledge of every good. And since this is in the apostles, let us not think it perfect merely because it is in them, but entire in this, that it is Christ's. So whatever good is both praised in Philemon and taken from the example of the apostles is good for this reason, that it is drawn from the fountain of Christ.\n— Commentary on Philemon"],"PHM.1.7":["For we have had great joy and consolation in your love, because the bowels of the saints have found rest through you, brother.\n\nHe drives home and explains more fully why he had said: \"I give thanks to my God, always making remembrance of you in my prayers\". For it was indeed fitting to give thanks to God for the love of Philemon, who, by receiving them, had refreshed the inner affection of the heart and the deep recesses of the saints' souls. And this is an apostolic idiom: he always says \"bowels\" when he wishes to show the full love of the mind. Therefore, rejoicing with those who rejoice, and believing that he himself had been refreshed along with those who had found rest, he has a joy that is not passing and light, nor such as might come about by chance. It is great, and as surpassing as the love that was in Philemon. That joy was increased by the consolation over Philemon's love, a consolation coming down, full, from the Father of mercies and the God of all consolation.\n— Commentary on Philemon"],"PHM.1.8-9":["Wherefore, having much confidence in Christ Jesus to command you what is profitable for love's sake, I rather beseech, since you are such a one, as Paul an old man, and now also a prisoner of Jesus Christ.\n\nMany praises of Philemon have been set out beforehand. The matter for which he is asked is also such as to be useful both to the one who grants it and to the one who asks. Paul therefore could have commanded rather than requested. This came from that confidence: one who had accomplished such great works for Christ's sake could surely not be unequal to himself in other matters. But he prefers to ask rather than to order, having first set forth the great authority of the one who asks, by which it is at once the Apostle who beseeches, and an old man, and a prisoner of Jesus Christ. Now the whole matter for which he asks is this. Onesimus, Philemon's slave, heaping theft upon flight, had pilfered certain things belonging to the household. Setting out from there for Italy, so that he could not be caught so easily close at hand, he had squandered his master's money in riotous living. Lest anyone think this rashly said and invented by us at our pleasure, let him learn it from what follows. For Paul would never say: \"If he has done you any harm or owes you anything, charge this to me: I Paul have written it with my own hand, I will repay.\" Nor would he become guarantor for property taken away unless what had been taken had been squandered. This man, then, when Paul was in prison at Rome for confessing Christ, believed in Christ. Baptized by him, he wiped away the stains of his former life by a worthy penitence, so much so that the witness of his conversion was that apostle who had once rebuked Peter for not walking with a straight foot in the truth of the Gospel (Gal. 2). So far, therefore, as concerns the sin and the crime by which he had wronged his master, he does not deserve pardon. But so far as concerns the testimony of the Apostle, who knows him to be fully converted, great weight presses upon the one who is asked, that the man who from a runaway slave and robber had been made a minister of the Apostle (and what other ministry does the Apostle have but that of the Gospel of Christ Jesus?) should now be pardoned, no longer as by a master but as by a fellow servant and fellow evangelist, since he was likewise a servant and minister of Christ.\n— Commentary on Philemon"],"PHM.1.10-13":["I beseech you for my son, whom I have begotten in chains, Onesimus, who was once unprofitable to you, but now is profitable both to you and to me: whom I have sent back to you. But do you receive him, that is, my bowels, whom I had wished to keep with me, so that in your stead he might minister to me in the chains of the Gospel.\n\nWishing to obtain what he asks, he declares that he is now pleading not for Philemon's slave but for his own son, and for that son whom he had begotten in the chains of the Gospel, that is, the chains he was enduring for the Gospel of Christ. Before, this man had been unprofitable only to his master (for a slave who is a thief and a runaway has harmed no one but his master). Now, on the contrary, he is useful to his master himself and to Paul, and through Paul to the rest. By this compensation of usefulness he deserves more love than he had before deserved hatred. Hence he says: Who was once unprofitable to you. To you, he says, alone, not to the rest; but now profitable both to me and to you. He is profitable to his master in this, that he could also serve Paul in his master's stead; and profitable to Paul in this, that while Paul was held in prison and in chains, he could minister to him in the Gospel. At the same time we must marvel at the greatness of soul of the Apostle, and at his mind burning toward Christ. He is held in prison, he is bound tight with chains, he is hemmed in by the squalor of his body, by separation from those dear to him, by the darkness of punishment; and yet he does not feel the injury, he is not tormented by pain, he knows nothing else but to think about the Gospel of Christ. He knew that a slave, he knew that a runaway, he knew that one who had once been a robber had been converted to the faith of Christ. It is a matter of great labor for such a man to persevere in what he has begun. Therefore he presses home and repeats \"his son,\" and \"the son of his chains,\" and \"the minister of the Gospel to one placed in chains.\" He does this so that Philemon, whom he had prudently and by way of accommodation praised so highly in the preface, would not dare to refuse, lest he seem unworthy of his own praises.\n— Commentary on Philemon"],"PHM.1.12":["As for his saying: But do you receive him, that is, my bowels, this is what I said a little earlier: bowels signify the inner affection of the heart and a full and heartfelt will, when the one who is asked receives everything whatever that is in us. Otherwise, all children are the bowels of their parents.\n— Commentary on Philemon"],"PHM.1.14":["But without your counsel I wished to do nothing, so that your good might not be as it were of necessity, but voluntary.\n\nThe question asked by very many, and very often taken up again, is why God, in making man, did not create him good and upright. It can be resolved from the present passage. For if God is good voluntarily, and not of necessity, then in making man he ought to have made him after his own image and likeness, that is, so that man too would be good voluntarily, and not of necessity. But those who assert that he ought to have been made such that he could not admit evil are saying this: he ought to have been made such that he would be good by necessity, and not by will. Yet if he had been made such as to accomplish good not by will but by necessity, he would not be like God, who is good because he wills it, not because he is compelled. From this it is manifest that they are demanding two things contrary to each other. For when they say, 'Man ought to have been made like God,' they are asking that he be made with free will, as God himself is. But when they infer, 'He ought to have been made such that he could not admit evil,' they impose upon him a necessity of good, and so they want man not to be made like God. So the Apostle Paul too could have kept Onesimus with him for his ministry without Philemon's will. But if he had done this without Philemon's will, it would indeed have been good, but not voluntary. And what was not voluntary would be shown on another ground not to be good. For nothing can be called good except what is spontaneous. From this we should consider the prudence of the Apostle: he sends the runaway slave back to his master for this reason, that he may be of profit to his master. He could not be of profit if he were kept while his master was absent. The question above, then, is resolved thus: God could have made man good without man's will. But if he had done this, the good would not have been voluntary, but of necessity. And what is good by necessity is not good, and on another ground is shown to be evil. Therefore, by leaving us to our own free choice, he made us all the more after his own image and likeness. And to be like God is absolutely good.\n— Commentary on Philemon"],"PHM.1.15-16":["That you might receive him eternal. No one is the eternal master of his slave: for his power, and the condition of each of them, is ended by death. But Onesimus, who was made eternal through the faith of Christ, having received the spirit of liberty, began, from a slave, to be no longer a slave but a brother to Philemon, who is eternal because he too had believed in Christ: a dearest brother, an eternal brother; also to the eternal Apostle himself and to his master, to whom, as the condition of the flesh joined Onesimus before, so afterward the spirit joins him. And indeed then, when he was subject to him in the flesh, he was not joined to him in the Lord; but now he is joined to him both in the flesh and in the Lord. From this we understand that a slave who has believed in Christ is bound to his master by a twofold law: he is joined to him for a time by the necessity of the flesh, and united to him forever by the spirit.\n— Commentary on Philemon"],"PHM.1.15":["For perhaps he therefore departed from you for an hour, that you might receive him eternal. No longer as a slave, but more than a slave, a dearest brother, especially to me: but how much more to you, both in the flesh and in the Lord.\n\nSometimes evil becomes the occasion of good things, and God turns the crooked counsels of men to what is right. What I say will be made clearer by an example. Joseph's brothers, spurred on by the goads of their jealousy, sold him to the Ishmaelites for twenty gold pieces (Gen. 37). This was the beginning of all good things for his father, for his brothers, and for all Egypt. Finally, he himself afterward said to his brothers: \"You\", he says, \"thought evil things concerning me: and God thought good things concerning me\" (Gen. 45:8). We can understand something similar in Onesimus too: evil beginnings were the occasions of a good thing. For if he had not run away from his master, he would never have come to Rome, where Paul was a prisoner in prison. If he had not seen Paul in chains, he would not have received faith in Christ. If he had not had the faith of Christ, he would never have been made Paul's son and sent into the work of the Gospel. From this, little by little and step by step, with the reasoning running back in reverse, Onesimus was made a minister of the Gospel precisely because he ran away from his master. But by adding perhaps he finely tempered his statement. For the judgments of God are hidden, and it is rash to pronounce as if on a certainty what is doubtful. Perhaps, he says, he therefore departed: cautiously, timidly, with trembling, and not with the whole foot firmly planted. For if he had not put perhaps, all slaves would have to run away in order to become apostolic men. As for his adding for an hour, we ought to take \"hour\" for \"time\". For in comparison with eternity, all time is short.\n— Commentary on Philemon"],"PHM.1.17":["If therefore you have me as a partner, receive him as me.\n\nPhilemon desired to have Paul as a partner, and, believing in Christ, he certainly wished to make such progress as to become like Paul and to share with him in his chains. Let us consider, therefore, how greatly Onesimus is praised here, and how far he is said to have progressed: for he is to be received as the Apostle is, and his master ought to desire his companionship just as he desires Paul's. In brief, what he says is this: If you wish to have me as a companion, have Onesimus too, whom I hold as my companion, and my son, and my bowels; if you do not receive him and are unwilling to have him, you yourself understand that you cannot have me.\n— Commentary on Philemon"],"PHM.1.18":["But if he has harmed you in anything, or owes you, charge this to me.\n\nHe imitates his Lord and has Christ speaking in him, so he ought to do, as far as his strength allows, the things that Christ does. For if He bore our infirmities and grieved over our wounds, the Apostle rightly puts himself forward on behalf of Onesimus and pledges to pay what Onesimus owed. But, as we said above, the whole of it is what was taken by theft and squandered in luxury and could not be repaid. Philemon weighed this against a great price: in return for a runaway slave and money lost, he would receive both a dearest brother and an eternal brother, and through him he would make the Apostle his debtor.\n— Commentary on Philemon"],"PHM.1.19":["I Paul have written with my own hand. I will repay, not to say to you that you owe me even your own self.\n\nWhat he says amounts to this: What Onesimus carried off by theft, I pledge that I will repay. Of this pledge this Epistle is witness, and so is my own hand, for I did not dictate it in the usual way but wrote it out myself with my own hand. Believe me, therefore, when I make a promise on behalf of Onesimus. But I say this as though speaking to a stranger. Otherwise, if I return to my own right: because of the word of Christ which I preached to you as the Gospel, and you were made a Christian, you owe me your own self. But if you are mine, then all that is yours is also mine: Onesimus too, who is yours, is mine. I could therefore have used him as my own; but I leave it to your will, so that you may have a reward by pardoning.\n— Commentary on Philemon"],"PHM.1.20":["Yes, brother, may I enjoy you in the Lord.\n\nThe Latin tongue does not render the Greek idiom. For in what he says, ναὶ, ἀδελφέ, ναὶ is a kind of adverb, as it were, of one who speaks coaxingly. But we, in translating it yes (ita), brother, give out a sound that is somehow more watery and diluted, something rather other than what is written. For just as that Hebrew ANNA (אנה), for which the Seventy translators frequently rendered ὦ δή, signifies in its own language the feeling of one who entreats (whence Symmachus sometimes rendered ANNA as δέομαι, that is, \"I beseech\"), so we too suffer the same strain with the Greek language that the Greeks endure with the Hebrew. But what he says, may I enjoy you in the Lord, is understood as something far different from what is supposed. The Apostle enjoys no one except him who has in himself many virtues in harmony with one another, and all that Christ is called according to the variety of occasions: namely wisdom, justice, continence, gentleness, temperance, chastity. These he invokes upon Philemon, so that when he abounds in them, Paul himself, enjoying him to the full, may be filled. And lest you think that the enjoyment meant is that by which we often delight in one another's presence, he added, in the Lord: so that from the fact that the name of the Lord was joined to it, it might be understood that there is also another enjoyment, by which one may enjoy apart from the Lord.\n\nRefresh my bowels in Christ. Just as he himself wishes to enjoy Philemon in the Lord, so he wishes his own bowels, Onesimus, whom he also called by the same name above, to be refreshed through Philemon. And it is said ambiguously: whether Onesimus is Paul's bowels in Christ, or whether Paul's bowels, Onesimus, are to be refreshed in Christ through Philemon. If you choose to take the former, Onesimus will rightly be called Paul's bowels in Christ, since Paul begot him for Christ in chains. If the latter, Onesimus is to be refreshed in Christ by Philemon, while he is instructed in Christ by Philemon's words.\n— Commentary on Philemon"],"PHM.1.21":["Confident of your obedience, I have written to you, knowing that you will do even beyond what I say.\n\nHe who presumes upon the one he is about to ask in some way forestalls him by that very presumption, so that he is not free to refuse what is asked. Moreover, if the one who makes the request knows that the person asked will do more than he asked, he asks for lesser things for this reason: so that the one asked may have a voluntary reward, and one made greater by what he performs. But if Philemon does these things at a man's command, how much more will he do them for the love of God? Hence he is deservedly praised by the voice of the Apostle, because he anticipates his commands in deed and can say: \"Let the voluntary offerings of my mouth be pleasing to you, O Lord\" (Ps. 118:108); and because, doing more than was commanded, he surpasses those who did only what they were ordered, and who are bidden to say: \"We are unprofitable servants; what we ought to have done, we have done\" (Luke 17:10). For this reason virginity too is crowned with a greater reward: it has no commandment of the Lord, and it reaches beyond what is commanded.\n— Commentary on Philemon"],"PHM.1.22":["At the same time, also prepare me a lodging.\n\nI do not think the Apostle was so rich, or so encumbered with baggage, that he needed a lodging prepared for him. Nor do I think he was not content with a single little cell, he who would think a house small by the measure of his body most spacious. He says this rather so that, while Philemon expects him to come to him, he may all the more do what he has been asked. But if anyone thinks that this was commanded not by way of accommodation but in earnest, that a lodging should be prepared for him, then the lodging must be prepared for the apostle rather than for Paul. He was about to come to a new city, to preach the Crucified, and to bring doctrines never heard before, and he knew that very many would flock to him. It was therefore necessary, first, that the house be in a frequented part of the city, where people could easily gather. Next, that it be free of every disturbance; that it be ample, to hold a great many hearers; that it not be next to the places of the public shows; that it not be detestable for a shameful neighborhood; and lastly, that it be situated on the ground floor rather than in an upper room. For this reason I think that at Rome too he stayed two years in a rented house. Nor, I suppose, was the dwelling small to which crowds of Jews flocked daily.\n\nFor I hope that through your prayers I shall be given to you. God, when asked, grants a son to his father, and a brother is often saved by a brother's prayer. The Apostle, however, is granted at the prayers of the whole Church, for the benefit of those who are going to hear him. And this gift is said to be not so much toward him who, though prepared for martyrdom, is brought back from martyrdom, as toward those to whom the Apostle is sent. Paul himself says in another place that he was often in prison and was set free from chains: \"In prisons frequently\" (2 Cor. 11). He was released from these sometimes by the Lord's help, and frequently because the persecutors themselves found nothing in him worthy of death. For no decrees of the senate had yet been passed concerning the Christian name, and Nero's sword had not yet consecrated Christian blood. But because the preaching was new, the peoples were stirred to fury, either by the Jews out of envy or by those who saw their idols being destroyed. Then the preachers were sent to prison, and when the violence and fury had subsided they were let go again. The Acts of the Apostles testify that this is as we say, for there Felix too speaks to Agrippa: Paul could have been released if he had not appealed to Caesar (Acts 25). Felix also says that he had found no charge except certain questions about their own religion and about a certain Jesus, whom Paul preached to be alive. From this we observe that they could likewise have been released by the other judges too, the Lord bringing this about so that the new preaching might be spread through the whole world.\n— Commentary on Philemon"],"PHM.1.23-24":["Epaphras my fellow captive in Christ Jesus greets you: Mark, Aristarchus, Demas, Luke, my fellow workers.\n\nWhat we were saying at the beginning, that the Epistle to the Colossians was written at the same time and through the same bearer of the letters as the letter to Philemon, is also shown by the names of those who are introduced as sending greetings. For in that Epistle too it is written thus: \"Aristarchus my fellow captive greets you, and Mark, the cousin of Barnabas, and Epaphras, who is one of you, a servant of Christ\" (Col. 4:10, 12); and a little further on: \"Luke the dearest physician greets you, and Demas; and say to Archippus: See the ministry which you have received in the Lord, that you fulfil it\" (ibid. 14, 17); and: \"Be mindful of my chains\" (ibid. 18). But suppose someone thinks that they were not written together, because the Epistle to the Colossians has a few names that are not found written here. Let him know that not everyone is either a friend or an acquaintance of everyone, and that it is one thing for a private letter to be written to one man, and another for a public letter to be written to a whole church.\n— Commentary on Philemon"],"PHM.1.23":["Epaphras my fellow captive in Christ Jesus greets you, he says. As to who Epaphras, Paul's fellow captive, may be, we have received the following story. They say that the parents of the Apostle Paul were from the district of Gischala in Judea. When the whole province was being laid waste by the Roman hand and the Jews were being scattered across the world, they were removed to Tarsus, a city of Cilicia, and the young Paul followed his parents' lot. In this way, they say, what he testifies of himself can stand: \"They are Hebrews, so am I: they are Israelites, so am I: they are the seed of Abraham, so am I\" (2 Cor. 11:22). And again elsewhere: \"A Hebrew of Hebrews\" (Phil. 3:5), and the other things which mark him as a Jew rather than a man of Tarsus. If this is so, we can suppose that Epaphras too was taken captive at the same time that Paul was taken, and that, having been settled with his parents at Colossae, a city of Asia, he afterwards received the word of Christ. Hence, as we said above, it is written to the Colossians: \"Epaphras greets you, who is one of you, a servant of Christ, always anxious for you in prayers\" (Col. 4:12). If this is the case, Aristarchus too, who in the same Epistle is called his fellow captive, will be brought to the same understanding. That holds unless perhaps something hidden and sacred, as some think, is shown in the word captivity: namely, that captured and bound alike, they were brought into this valley of tears. But if neither explanation is accepted, then from the fact that in Christ Jesus has been added here, we can suppose that he endured at Rome the same chains for Christ as Paul did, and that, just as he could be called a prisoner of Christ, so he could also be called His captive. Or at any rate it may be in this sense: that he too is noble among the apostles, like Andronicus and Julia, of whom it is written to the Romans: \"Greet Andronicus and Julia, my kinsmen and my fellow captives, who are noble among the apostles, who also were in Christ Jesus before me\" (Rom. 16:7). So much about Epaphras.\n— Commentary on Philemon"],"PHM.1.24":["As for the rest, in writing the letter to Philemon he names as fellow workers in the Gospel and in his chains Mark, whom I take to be the writer of the Gospel; and Aristarchus, whom we mentioned above; and Demas, of whom he complains in another place: \"Demas has forsaken me, loving the present world, and has gone to Thessalonica\". And he names Luke the physician, who, in leaving the Gospel and the Acts of the Apostles to the churches, was turned from a physician of bodies into a physician of souls, just as the apostles were made from fishers of fish into fishers of men (Matt. 4). Of him it is said in another place also: \"I have sent\", he says, \"with him the brother whose praise is in the Gospel through all the churches\" (2 Cor. 8:18). As often as his book is read in the churches, so often his medicine does not cease.\n— Commentary on Philemon"],"PHM.1.25":["Grace of our Lord Jesus Christ be with your spirit.\n\nJust as the people of Israel is numbered by the better part of man, the head, as Scripture says, according to their heads (Num. 1:2), so the grace of the Lord Jesus Christ is indeed in the whole man and in every part of the saints. But by synecdoche it is said of the whole man, from the greater and better part, that is, the spirit: Grace of our Lord Jesus Christ be with your spirit. And when grace is in the spirit, it makes the whole man spiritual, so that the flesh too serves the spirit, and the soul is not overcome by the flesh, and, brought together into a spiritual substance, cleaves to the Lord; for he who cleaves to the Lord is one spirit (1 Cor. 6).\n— Commentary on Philemon"],"HEB.2.12-14":["But the blessed Apostle, in the Epistle which is written to the Hebrews (though Latin custom does not receive it among the canonical Scriptures), teaches that this testimony ought to be understood in the person of the Lord and Savior: For which reason he is not ashamed to call them brothers, saying: I will announce your name to my brothers, in the midst of the Church I will praise you (Heb 2:12); and again: I will put my trust in him (ibid., 13); and once more: Behold, I and my children, whom God has given me. Since, then, the children have shared in flesh and blood, he himself likewise became partaker of the same sufferings (Isa 8:18). And how these children were for a sign and a portent to the wisdom of the world and to the pride of the Jews, the same Apostle teaches, who says that the Lord and Savior chose the foolish and weak things of the world, to confound the wise and the strong things (1 Cor 1). Hence the Savior too said to the Apostles: Unless you are converted as one of the children, you shall not enter into the kingdom of heaven (Matt 18:3). And the preacher of the new Gospel becomes a child, who puts off the old man, who is corrupted in the desires of seduction, and puts on the new, who is renewed unto knowledge after the image of the Creator.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"HEB.3.1":["Now \"apostle,\" that is, \"sent,\" is properly a word of the Hebrews, and Silas also has this sound, he to whom the name \"sent\" was given from sending. The Hebrews say that among the prophets themselves and the holy men there are some who are both prophets and apostles, but others who are only prophets. Thus Moses, to whom it is said: \"And I will send you to Pharaoh\" (Exod. 3:10, 11); and who answers: \"Provide another whom you may send\"; and Isaiah, to whom God speaks: \"Whom shall I send, and who will go to this people\" (Isa. 6:8)? are both apostles and prophets. Hence we too can understand that John the Baptist also should be called both prophet and apostle, since indeed Scripture says: \"There was a man sent from God, whose name was John\" (John 1:6). And we can understand that in the Epistle to the Hebrews (Heb. 3) Paul, contrary to his usual custom, set at its head neither his own name nor the title of apostle for this reason, that he was going to say of Christ: \"Having therefore the chief of priests and the apostle of our confession, Jesus\"; and that it was not fitting that where Christ was to be called an apostle, Paul should also be set down there as an apostle.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"HEB.6.6":["And he asks how, in the case of those who have the fruits of the spirit, and against whom the Law has ceased to be, the crucifixion of the Lord's flesh is set down as praise, when in Hebrews it is set down as reproach: \"Crucifying again in themselves the Son of God, and holding him up to display\" (Heb. 6:6). For \"crucifying again\" there is in the Greek, better, a single compound word, ἀνασταυροῦντες, which we can render \"re-crucifying\" (recrucifigentes). First, then, it must be noted that to crucify is one thing, to re-crucify another. Next, that to re-crucify the Son of God is not the same thing as to crucify the flesh of Christ with vices and lusts. For the flesh of Christ is not principally and properly the Son of God, but Jesus Christ, who, though he was in the beginning with the Father, God the Word, was made flesh and emptied himself, taking the form of a slave, so that he might crucify the flesh and despoil the principalities and powers, triumphing over them on the tree (Phil. 2), so that that saying of the Apostle might be fulfilled: \"For in that he died, he died to sin once\" (Rom. 6:10).\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"HEB.7.3":["And Melchizedek, king of Salem, brought forth bread and wine; and he was the priest of the most high God. And he blessed him. Because our work, either the Hebrew Questions or the Collection of Traditions, is once for all, therefore let us infer what the Hebrews think of this. They say that he is the son of Shem, Noah's son; and by calculating the years of his life, they show that he lived until Isaac, and that all the firstborn of Noah, until Aaron assumed the priesthood, were priests. Furthermore, Jerusalem is called the king of Salem, which was previously called Salem. However, blessed Apostle to the Hebrews, mentioning one without a father or mother, refers to Christ, and through Christ to the Church of the Gentiles. For all the glory of the head is referred to the members, because he, having a foreskin, blessed the circumcised Abraham, and in Abraham the Levites, and through the Levites Aaron: from whom later the priesthood. From this he wants to gather that the priesthood of the Church, having a foreskin, blessed the synagogue with the circumcised priesthood. But as he says: You are a priest forever, according to the order of Melchizedek: our mystery is signified in the word of the order: not by offering irrational victims through Aaron, but by offering bread and wine: that is, the body and blood of the Lord Jesus.\n— HEBREW QUESTIONS ON GENESIS 14.18-19"],"HEB.11.1":["Nor is it any wonder if hope is not mentioned in this catalogue, since what is hoped for is in faith, and the Apostle, writing to the Hebrews, defines it thus: \"Now faith is the substance of things to be hoped for, the proof of things not yet appearing\" (Heb. 11:1). For that which we hope will come, and which is not yet in the present, we possess by faith, hoping that we hold what we believe.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"HEB.11.37":["The Jews think that both this and the rest that follow are to be understood either generally of all the righteous, whose blood Manasseh shed, and filled Jerusalem from gate to gate; or else that Isaiah prophesies of his own death, because he was to be sawn by Manasseh with a wooden saw, which is a most certain tradition among them. Hence most of our own writers also refer what is set down in the Epistle to the Hebrews concerning the passion of the Saints, They were sawn asunder (Heb 11:37), to the passion of Isaiah.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","But the disciples, seeing it, were indignant, saying: To what purpose is this waste? For this could have been sold for much and given to the poor. I know that some cavil at this passage, asking why another evangelist said that Judas alone was saddened, because he held the purse and had been a thief from the beginning, while Matthew writes that all the apostles were indignant; they do not know the figure that is called σύλληψις (syllepsis), by which all are customarily named for one and one for many. For Paul the apostle too, in his letter which is written to the Hebrews (although many of the Latins have doubts about it), when he had described the sufferings and merits of the saints, added: They were stoned, they were tempted, they were sawn asunder, they died by the slaughter of the sword, though the Jews hold that only one, the prophet Isaiah, was sawn. We can also say otherwise: that the apostles were truly indignant on account of the poor, but Judas on account of his own gain. Hence his muttering too is set down together with a charge: that he had no care for the poor but wished to provide for his own theft.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"HEB.12.11":["Vengeance will fall upon these, who did not keep the Lord's charges, and say in their hearts: The Lord will not do good, and he will not do evil; not that God does evil, but that punishment seems evil to him who suffers it. Otherwise the physician's scalpel too will be evil, because it cuts open wounds and amputates putrid flesh; and a father will be evil when he beats his son to correct him from his vices, and a teacher evil when he rebukes his pupil to instruct him: For all discipline for the present seems to be not of joy, but of sorrow; but afterwards it will yield the peaceful fruit to those who have been trained by it.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"1PE.2.3-5":["The apostle Paul, wishing the Ephesians to know the more sacred things, prays for them 'that they may be filled with the wisdom and love of the Lord, and, being rooted, may be able to know and comprehend what is the breadth and length and height and depth of his riches'; and to the same he says: So then you are by no means strangers and sojourners, but fellow citizens of the saints and members of the household of God, built upon the foundation of the apostles and prophets, Jesus Christ himself being the cornerstone; in whom the whole building, fitted together, grows into a holy temple for the Lord, in whom you too are built up into a dwelling place of God in the Holy Spirit. Peter too, in almost the same words, because with the same spirit, says in his epistle: If indeed you have believed that the Lord is good. Coming to him, a living stone, rejected indeed by men, but chosen and honored by God, you yourselves too, as living stones, are built up as a spiritual house, into a holy priesthood, offering spiritual victims acceptable to God through Jesus Christ. From this it is clear that the Lord Savior, who is the true architect, tests the stones: which can be called together into the foundation of the temple, which into the middle, which into the higher places, and which, being of lesser measure or of not so solid firmness, into the wall and outside the temple, into the περιβολῇ round about — for it is a thing of no small strength nor of the least firmness to bear the stones laid on from above; whence the apostle writes to believers: Bearing one another's burdens, and in another place it is written that we ought not to take up a weight which we cannot carry —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"1PE.2.22":["He for whose burial and death the ungodly and the rich were handed over did no iniquity, nor was deceit found in his mouth. This can be understood of no man at all, that he sinned neither in deed nor in word, as Scripture says, No one is clean from filth, not even if his life has been of a single day, and, all like sheep have gone astray, each one has turned aside in his own way (Job 25), except of him who bore our sins, and suffers pain for us, and was wounded for our iniquities, and afflicted for our crimes, by whose bruise we have been healed. Expounding this testimony about him, Peter the prince of the Apostles also says: For to this you were called, because Christ also suffered for us, leaving you an example, that you should follow in his steps; who did no sin, nor was deceit found in his mouth; who when he was cursed did not curse, and when he suffered did not threaten (1 Pet 2:21ff.).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"1PE.3.7":["Although the apostle Peter has commanded that husbands give honor to their wives as to the weaker vessel, it is not, however, to be supposed that a wife, who has a weak vessel of the body, is at once weaker in soul as well. Hence it is now commanded them, so that in them too that saying of the Apostle may be fulfilled: \"Power is made perfect in weakness\" (2 Cor. 12:9); and it is said that they also are to have all the things that were commanded in common to the old men, namely in that he says, Old women likewise, that is, just as the old men, honorable in all things, sober, chaste, sound in faith, and in love, and in patience; and, for their sex, they are to have this as their own, that they be in holy demeanor, or, as it reads better in the Greek, ἐν καταστήματι ἱεροπρεπεῖς (in a bearing befitting what is sacred), so that their very gait and movement, face, speech and silence may display a certain dignity of sacred seemliness.\n— Commentary on Titus"],"1PE.5.8":["But if they are separated by discord, at once the roaring lion will attack them, of whom the apostle Peter also says: Your adversary the devil, like a roaring lion, goes about seeking whom he may devour. This roaring lion does not move about in sown fields, not in vineyards, not in olive groves, nor among the fruit-bearing trees, which praise God, but where there are forests and untilled ground and wild beasts dwell.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"2PE.3.13":["Peter too signifies the same thing: This escapes them, willingly: that the heavens were from the beginning, and the earth out of water and through water subsists by the word of God; through which the former world, flooded by the deluge, perished. But the heavens and earth which are now are by the same principle kept for fire (2 Pet 3:5ff.); in what sense this is to be taken, he teaches afterwards: But we see new heavens and a new earth, and his promise (ibid. 13): he did not say, we shall see other heavens and another earth, but the old and ancient ones changed for the better.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"1JN.2.18":["But this mountain of the Lord was shown in the last of days, when the kingdom of heaven drew near. For indeed at the consummation of the ages, for the rejection of sins, our Savior appeared through his own victim, and came at the eleventh hour to hire laborers. And when his passion was accomplished, John says: It is the last hour; for in six thousand years, if five hundred years are allotted to each hour of the day, the time of the faith of the Gentiles will consequently be called the last hour.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"1JN.3.2":["So therefore we too, who love God, and for whom have been prepared the things that eye has not seen, nor ear heard, nor have they ascended into the heart of man (1 Cor. 2), when we have been transformed from the body of lowliness into the body of the glory of the Lord Jesus Christ, shall have that body which neither the Jew could cut, nor the Gentile keep with its foreskin. Not that it is something other according to substance, but that it is different according to glory. \"For this mortal must put on immortality, and this corruptible be clothed with incorruption\" (1 Cor. 15:53). Perceiving something similar to this, the blessed evangelist John also spoke: \"Dearly beloved, now we are sons of God: and it is not yet manifest what we shall be. We know that if it is made manifest, we shall be like him: because we shall see him as he is\" (1 John 3:2). Since, then, that body of the glory of Jesus Christ has not yet been made manifest — the body which both had the marks of the nails after the resurrection and entered through closed doors — let us, who even now in baptism have risen again with Christ, reborn into the new man, serve neither circumcision nor uncircumcision, but let us believe that even now we are what we shall be.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"1JN.5.19":["It is asked how the present age has been called evil. For heretics are accustomed to seize occasions from this passage to assert that there is one creator of light and of the age to come, and another of darkness and of the present age. We, however, say that it is not so much the age itself, which runs on by day and night, by years and months, that is called evil, as, homonymously (ὁμωνύμως), the things that are done in the age: just as the day is said to have enough of its own evil (Matt. 6), and the days of Jacob are written to be few and very evil (Gen. 47). Not that the span of time in which Jacob lived was evil, but that the things he endured exercised him through various trials. Indeed, at the very time when he was serving for his wives and was struggling with many hardships (Gen. 29), Esau was at rest; and so the same span of time was good for one and evil for the other. Nor would it have been written in Ecclesiastes: \"Do not say that my former days were good beyond these\" (Eccles. 7:11), except with a view to the difference of evils. Hence John says: \"The whole world is set in the wicked one\" (1 John 5:19). Not that the world itself is evil, but that evils are done in the world by men who say, \"Let us eat and drink\", \"for tomorrow we shall die\" (Isa. 22:13). And the Apostle himself: \"Redeeming\", he says, \"the time, because the days are evil\" (Eph. 5:16). Woodlands too are given a bad name when they are full of robberies, not because the earth and the forests sin, but because the places too have drawn upon themselves the infamy of murder. We also detest the sword by which human blood has been shed, and the cup in which poison has been mixed, not for any sin of the sword or of the cup, but because those who have used them ill deserve hatred. So also the age, which is a span of times, is not in itself either good or evil, but is called good or evil through those who are in it.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"1JN.5.20":["Further, according to the Seventy, who for amen rendered the true God, so that the true God is blessed, and those who swear upon the earth swear by the true God: we do not, according to the error of the Arians, refer this only to the person of God the Father, of whom it is written: That they may know you, the only true God, and Jesus Christ whom you have sent (John 17:3), but to the Son, who is himself also true God, as the evangelist John says: The Son of God has come, and has given us understanding, that we may know the true one, and may be in his true Son Jesus Christ (1 John 5:20). This is the true God and eternal life. For if the Savior says of himself: I am the truth (John 14:6), consequently the true God received his name from truth, so that he is by no means called God after the manner of false gods, but after the manner of the true God the Father, and is himself true God. Otherwise, if he is not true, he will be like an idol, which recoils upon the heads of those who deny that Christ is true God.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"3JN.1.5-10":["But I fear that, just as the queen of the South, who came from the ends of the earth to hear the wisdom of Solomon (Matt. 12), will judge the men of her time, and the men of Nineveh, who did penance at the preaching of Jonah, will condemn those who scorned to hear the Savior, greater than Jonah, so very many among the peoples may judge the bishops, withdrawing themselves from the ecclesiastical rank and practicing what does not befit a bishop. Of these I think John too writes to Gaius: \"Dearest, you act faithfully in whatever you do for the brethren, and that for strangers, who have borne witness to your love before the Church: you will do very well if you send them on their way worthily of God: for they went out for the name of the Lord, accepting nothing from the Gentiles\" (3 John 5 ff.). And truly, the Holy Spirit speaking in him, he even then rebuked what was to come in the churches, saying: \"I wrote also to the Church: but Diotrephes, who loves to hold the first place among them, does not receive us. Therefore, when I come, I will call to mind his works which he does, slandering us with evil words; and it is not enough for him that he does not himself receive the brethren, but he also forbids those who wish to, and casts them out of the Church.\" Truly one can now see what was foretold: in very many cities, bishops, or presbyters, if they see laymen hospitable, lovers of good things, envy them, rage, excommunicate them, expel them from the Church, as though it were not lawful to do what the bishop does not do, and as though for laymen to be such were a condemnation of the priests. So they find them burdensome, and as though set upon their own necks, and harass them with various persecutions in order to draw them away from good work.\n— Commentary on Titus"],"JUD.1.9":["No one, too, to blaspheme, is not taken simply. For he does not say, to blaspheme no man, but absolutely, no one: not an angel, not any creature of God. For all things that were made by God are very good. \"When Michael the archangel was disputing with the devil about the body of Moses, he did not dare to bring a judgment of blasphemy, but said, May God command you\" (Jude 9). If, then, Michael did not dare to bring a judgment of blasphemy against the devil, who was surely most deserving of a curse, how much more ought we to be pure of every curse? The devil deserved a curse; but blasphemy ought not to come out through the archangel's mouth.\n— Commentary on Titus"],"JUD.1.12":["And to this locust, and to the morning cloud and the dew and the dust, the heretics are compared, of whom it is also said in the catholic epistle: These are clouds without water. For they have the appearance of prophets and of apostolic clouds, to which the truth of God comes; but they do not have waters, that is, the grace of the Holy Spirit, as the Lord says in the Gospel: He who believes in me, as the scripture says, out of his belly shall flow rivers of living water. But this, he says, he said of the Spirit which those who believed in him were to receive.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"REV.1.1":["We read in the Apocalypse of John (a book which, although rejected in these regions, we ought nevertheless to know, because it is accepted and held as canonical throughout the west, and in other Phoenician provinces, and in Egypt, for the ancient churchmen, including Irenaeus, Polycarp, Dionysius, and other Roman expounders of Sacred Scripture, among whom is holy Cyprian, accept and interpret it)...\n— Homily 1, on Psalm 1"],"REV.1.7":["Plainly the second coming of the Savior is foretold, of which John too speaks in his Apocalypse: Behold, he shall come with the clouds, and every eye shall see him, and those who pierced him. And the Lord declares in the Gospel that the Son of man will come on the clouds of heaven with power and great glory. He will come with the clouds, that is, with the angels, who are ministering spirits and are sent to various offices, and with the prophets and apostles, of whom it is written: Your truth reaches to the clouds.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"REV.1.16":["And two-edged swords are in their hands. These who exult in their beds—it is said of the saints, it is said of perfect men—what else do they have? And two-edged swords are in their hands. Two-edged swords. The swords of the saints are two-edged. We read in the Apocalypse of John, which is read in the churches and is received—for it is not held among the apocryphal scriptures, but among the ecclesiastical—it is said there, then, of the Lord Savior: \"And a sword, he says, sharp on both sides went forth from his mouth.\" You see, therefore, that these saints received those two-edged swords, which they have in their hands, from the mouth of the Lord. The Lord, therefore, gives a sword from his mouth to his disciples: a two-edged sword, that is, the word of his teaching. A two-edged sword: both according to history and according to allegory, both according to the letter and according to the spirit. A two-edged sword: which both kills adversaries and defends its own. A two-edged sword. This two-edged sword has two edges: it speaks both of the present age and of the age to come: and here it kills adversaries, and in heaven it opens the kingdoms of heaven.\n— Homily 59, on Psalm 149"],"REV.3.7":["I will give him, he says, the key of the house of David, he who opens and no one shuts, who shuts and no one opens (Rev 3). And this very key shall be upon his shoulder, that is, in his passion, according to what is written in another place: Whose government is upon his shoulder (Isa 9:6). For what he has unlocked by his passion cannot be shut; and what he has shut in the ceremonies of the Jews shall be opened by no one else.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"REV.3.12":["The apostles, therefore, are the pillars of the Church, and especially Peter, James and John, two of whom were found worthy to go up the mountain with the Lord, and one of whom in the Apocalypse introduces the Savior speaking: \"Whoever conquers, I will make him a pillar in the temple of my God\" (Rev. 3:12), teaching that all believers who conquer the adversary can become pillars of the Church. And Paul, writing to Timothy, says: \"That you may know how you ought to conduct yourself in the house of God, which is the Church of the living God, the pillar and foundation of the truth\" (1 Tim. 3:15). By these and other passages we are instructed that in the Scriptures the apostles and all believers, as well as the Church itself, are called a pillar, and that it makes no difference whether something is said of the body or of the members, since the body too is divided into members, and the members belong to the body.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"REV.3.20":["To the terrible one among the kings of the earth. This is understood also according to the letter. But may God grant to us also that we may be kings of the earth: kings of the earth, that we may rule over our flesh. Whence the apostle also says: \"Let not sin reign in your mortal body.\" And in another place it is written: \"The heart of the king is in the hand of God.\" … Kings, then, are the saints, and the heart of these is in the hand of the Lord. Let us therefore also ask God that we may be kings, and may rule over our flesh, that it may be subject to us. Whence the apostle also says: \"Therefore I chastise my body and bring it into servitude, lest by chance, while preaching to others, I myself should become a castaway.\" Let our soul rule, let the body serve: and at once Christ will come, and will dwell in us. Indeed, what does he himself say in the Gospel? \"I will come,\" he says, \"and will stand before the door, and will knock: and if anyone shall open to me, I will come in,\" he says, \"and will sup with him.\" Every day Christ stands before the door of our breast, he desires to enter. Let us open, he enters into us, and he is our guest and our fellow-diner: that is, he both dwells in us and eats with us.\n— Homily 9, on Psalm 75"],"REV.4.7":["That these four Gospels, then, were foretold long before, the book of Ezekiel too proves, in which the first vision is woven together thus: And in the midst as it were the likeness of four living creatures, and their countenance the face of a man and the face of a lion and the face of a calf and the face of an eagle. The first, the face of a man, signifies Matthew, who began to write as if about a man: The book of the generation of Jesus Christ, the son of David, the son of Abraham; the second signifies Mark, in whom is heard the voice of a lion roaring in the desert: The voice of one crying in the desert: Prepare the way of the Lord, make his paths straight; the third, that of the calf, prefigures that the evangelist Luke took his beginning from Zechariah the priest; the fourth signifies John the evangelist, who, taking up the wings of an eagle and hastening to higher things, discourses on the Word of God. The rest that follows tends to the same sense: their legs straight, and their feet winged, and wherever the spirit went, they went and did not turn back, and their backs full of eyes, and sparks and lamps running to and fro in the midst, and a wheel within a wheel, and in each one four faces. Hence the Apocalypse of John too, after its account of the twenty-four elders, who, holding harps and bowls, worship the Lamb of God, brings in lightnings and thunders and seven spirits running to and fro and a sea of glass and four living creatures full of eyes, saying: The first living creature like a lion, and the second like a calf, and the third like a man, and the fourth like a flying eagle; and a little after: They were full, it says, of eyes, and they had no rest day and night, saying: Holy, holy, holy, Lord God almighty, who was and who is and who is to come. By all these things it is plainly shown that only four Gospels ought to be received, and that all the dirges of the apocrypha are to be sung by dead heretics rather than by living churchmen.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"REV.5.1-5":["And all holy Scripture, he says, shall be to you closed and sealed, so that you, who suppose you know the letters of the Law and the oracles of the prophets, and meditate day and night with unwearied mouth on the volumes of the Scriptures, may not understand what you read; as it is written in the Apocalypse of John: Who is worthy to open the book and to loose its seals (Rev 5:2)? And when no one had been found to open the seals, he says that he wept, and that at last he was granted a word of God to him: Do not weep: behold, the lion of the tribe of Judah, the root of David, has prevailed to open the book and to loose its seals. The lion of the tribe of Judah is the Lord Jesus Christ, who loosed the seals of the book, not, properly, of one book, as many think, the psalms of David, but of all the Scriptures, which were written by one Holy Spirit; and therefore they are called one book. Of this Ezekiel testifies in mystical speech (Ezek 2) that it was written within and without: in the sense and in the letter. Of this the Savior too speaks in the psalms: At the head of the book it is written of me (Ps 39 [40]:8); not of Jeremiah, not of Isaiah, but in all holy Scripture, which is called one book.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","In which was a rolled-up book, and he spread it out before me, and it was written within and without; there were written in it lamentations and song and woe. For 'rolled-up book' the Seventy translated 'the heading of the book', in the hand of the Lord who sits upon the throne, the hand which was sent forth and stretched out; of this book it is also said in the Psalms: In the heading of the book it is written of me — let us understand 'heading' as the beginning —. This book, rolled up and sealed, and written within and without, or before and behind, was of such difficulty that no one could, either in heaven or on earth or under the earth, open it or read it, except only the lion of the tribe of Judah, the root of David and of Jesse; the book which John in the Revelation said he had devoured, not whole but in its heading, that is, in its beginning. For it cannot be that human nature should devour all the treasures of this book.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"REV.11.8":["And that you may know that whenever Sodom and Gomorrah and Egypt are named, it is not said of these provinces which we see with our eyes but of other, spiritual ones, which the prophetic word threatens, read in the Apocalypse of John: But the place in which the Lord was crucified is called spiritually Sodom and Egypt. If, then, Jerusalem, in which the Lord was crucified, is called spiritually Sodom and Egypt, why should not, conversely, Egypt and Sodom and Gomorrah, if they have done the works of Jerusalem and of the land of Judah, be transferred into the land of the Lord's lot?\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"REV.20.7-10":["Accordingly the Jews and our own Judaizers think that Gog is the Scythian nations, savage and innumerable, which stretch beyond the Caucasus mountain and the Maeotic marsh and near the Caspian Sea as far as India — and that these are to be stirred up by the devil after the reign of a thousand years —, and that they are to come into the land of Israel to fight against the saints, with many nations gathered together with them: first Meshech, whom Josephus interprets as the Cappadocians; then Tubal, whom the same man supposes to be the Iberians or Spaniards, and the Hebrews the Italians, having with them in their army Persians, Ethiopians and Libyans; also Gomer and Togarmah, whom they interpret as the Galatians and Phrygians; also the Sabaeans and Dedan and the Carthaginians, or Tarshish; and that this is what John too sets down in his Revelation: And when the thousand years are finished, Satan will be loosed from his prison, and he will go out to seduce the nations in the four corners of the earth, Gog and Magog, to gather them for war: whose number is as the sand of the sea. And they went up over the breadth of the earth, and surrounded the camp of the saints and the beloved city. And fire came down from God out of heaven and devoured them; and the devil who seduced them was cast into the lake of fire and sulphur, where the beast and the false prophet were, and they will be tormented day and night for ages of ages; not understanding that the whole volume of John, which is headed with the title of Revelation, is mystical, and that we need revelation so that we may be able to say with the prophet: Unveil my eyes, and I will consider the wonders of your law. Others, however, abandoning the earthly sense and the Jewish and old-wives' fables, which are harmful and drag down into the depths those who acquiesce in them, climb up too far to the heights and invent much worse trifles, so that they describe the wars of the devil and of all his army in the heavenly Jerusalem, and under the ἐτυμολογίαις [etymologies] of the individual nations interpret the spiritual forces of wickedness in the heavenly places. Leaving all these things to the reader's judgment, we shall try not so much to condemn the views of others as to affirm the Church's explanation. 'Gog' is called in the Greek tongue δῶμα, in the Latin 'roof'; 'Magog', moreover, means 'from the roof' — so all pride, and the knowledge falsely so called which raises itself against the knowledge of the truth, is shown by these names —; these are the roofs of which Isaiah too speaks in the vision against the valley of Zion: What has happened to you now, that you all go up onto the empty roofs?; and by 'roof' we shall interpret the princes of the heretics, and by 'from the roof' those who have taken up their doctrines; and fittingly, after the many mystical prophecies in this volume, the last prophecy is against Gog and Magog — for if It is the time of judgment, according to Peter, that it should begin from the house of God, and according to this same Ezekiel, who says: And begin from my saints, and: The last enemy, death, is destroyed, in Isaiah too the first word is directed against Judea, in which is the confession of the Lord, and the last against the four-footed beasts which are in the desert; rightly here too the last word is directed against Gog and Magog, who assail the city of God, which the rush of the river makes glad, and which says in Isaiah: I am a strong city which is besieged, and of which it is written in the Gospel: A city set on a mountain cannot be hidden, and more fully in the psalm: Jerusalem, which is built as a city whose sharing of it is in the selfsame. Mountains are round about it, and the Lord is round about his people —. Moreover, as to the fact that in the army of Gog or Magog, who according to the Seventy, Symmachus and Theodotion is prince of Ros, Meshech and Tubal, Aquila translates the first nation, 'Ros', as 'head', and we too have followed him — so that the sense is: The prince of the head of Meshech and Tubal; and in truth neither in Genesis, nor in any other place of Scripture, nor even in Josephus, who in the first book of the Antiquities sets out the names of all the Hebrew nations, have we been able to find this nation, from which it is clear that 'Ros' signifies not 'a nation' but 'head'; this too must be briefly noted, that in Ezekiel Gog is said to be the prince of the land of Magog, whereas Revelation records that both Gog and Magog are nations which go out from the four corners of the earth; and that just as from Jacob, who was afterwards called Israel, the whole people of the Hebrews received the name Israel, and from Aram Syria, from Mesraim Egypt, whose names are written in Genesis, so too from the prince Gog all who are subject to him are called Magog —; 'Mosoch', however, means 'madness', and 'Thubal' 'whole' or 'all things': so the 'prince' and 'head of proud madness and of all evils' — according to what is written: The world is set in the evil one — are called 'Gog' and 'Magog' — nations which, hostile and adverse to the saints, go out from the corners of the earth, abandoning the straight line and the arrangement of the camps of God which is related in Numbers, and not knowing that testimony: Many will come from the east and the west and the north and the south, and will recline with Abraham, Isaac and Jacob in the kingdom of God, and in another place: I will say to the north: Bring; and to the south-west wind: Do not hinder, bring my sons from a distant land and my daughters from the end of the earth, all who are called by my name; but not such are the corners of the earth from which Gog and Magog go out: beside such corners the harlot stands in Proverbs, who hastens to deceive the foolish young men passing by through the streets, that is, through the broad and spacious way which leads to death: the scribes and Pharisees too pray at the corners of the streets so as to be seen by men, who have received their reward —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"REV.20.12":["That too which we read in the Apocalypse of John (Chapter 20), that after the resurrection small and great are brought into the presence of the judge, signifies differences not of ages but of merits. For he who is small, according to the sentence of Solomon, is worthy of mercy: but the mighty shall mightily suffer torments (Wis 6:7). With this sense the words of the Lord also agree: The servant who knows the will of his lord and does not do it shall be beaten with many stripes (Luke 12:47). But he who does not know, and has done things worthy of stripes, shall be beaten with few.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"REV.22.1-2":["'And whose leaves never fade.' [Psalm 1:3] The leaves of this tree are by no means useless. Even if one understands Holy Writ only as history, he has something useful for his soul. We read in the Apocalypse of John (a book which, although rejected in these regions, we ought nevertheless to know, because it is accepted and held as canonical throughout the west, and in other Phoenician provinces, and in Egypt, for the ancient churchmen, including Irenaeus, Polycarp, Dionysius, and other Roman expounders of Sacred Scripture, among whom is holy Cyprian, accept and interpret it): 'Behold, I saw a throne set up, and one Lamb and a tree alongside a river, and on both sides of the river was that tree.' [Revelation 22:1-2] This means that the tree 'was both on this side and on that side of the river. 'And this tree,' he says, 'bore fruit and was yielding its twelve fruits for the year according to each month. And it had lean's, too, and the leaves for the healing of the nations.' [Revelation 22:2]\n\n'I saw,' he says, 'a single throne set up.' We believe in the Father, and the Son, and the Holy Spirit, that is true, and that they are a Trinity; nevertheless the kingship is one. 'I saw a single throne set lip, and I saw a single Lamb standing in the presence of the throne.' [Revelation 5:6] This refers to the Incarnation of the Savior. Scripture says: 'Behold the lamb of God, who takes away the sin of the world' [John 1:29] 'And there was a fountain of water coming forth from beneath the middle of the throne' [Revelation 22:1] Notice that it is from the midst of the throne that there issues forth a river of graces. That river does not issue forth from the throne unless the Lamb is standing before it, [Revelation 7:17] for unless we believe in the Incarnation of Christ, we do not receive those graces.\n\nA tree, he says, one lofty tree had been set up. He did not say trees, but only one tree. If there is but one tree how can it be on both sides of the river? If he had said, I saw trees, it would have been possible for some trees to be on one side of the river and other trees on the other side. Actually, one tree is said to be on both sides of the river. One river comes forth from the throne of God - the grace of the Holy Spirit - and this grace of the Holy Spirit is found in the river of the Sacred Scriptures. This river, moreover, has two banks, the Old Testament and the New Testament, and the tree planted on both sides is Christ. During the year, this tree yields twelve fruits, one for each month, but we are unable to receive the fruits except through the apostles. If one approaches the tree through the apostles, he must receive the fruit; he gathers the fruit from the Sacred Scriptures; he grasps the divine meaning abiding within the words. If, therefore, one comes to this tree through the apostles, he gathers its fruit just as we have said. If, indeed, he cannot pluck the fruit, it is because he is still too weak; he is not yet a disciple, but belongs to the throng; he is an outsider, a stranger from the nations. Because he cannot pluck the fruit, he plucks only words, the leaves for the healing of the nations, for it is written: 'and the leaves are for the healing of the nations.' One who belongs to the nations, who is not a disciple, who is as yet only one of the crowd, gathers only leaves from the tree; he receives from Scripture plain words for a healing remedy. Briefly, then, the Scripture says: 'and the leaves of the tree are for the healing of the nations'; in other words, the leaves are medicine. Why have we digressed on the Apocalypse? Simply became of that tree 'that yields its fruit in due season, and whose leaves never fade. Whatever he does, prospers.'\n— Homily 1, on Psalm 1"]}});
